('1704567e-a6d5-5879-9ceb-780c66b0e599'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'A 25-year-old male presents with palpitations, weight loss, and prominent bulging of the eyes (exophthalmos). Labs: suppressed TSH, elevated free T4. (Endocrine Case #82)', '["Anti-thyroglobulin antibody resulting in decreased functional activity","Antinuclear antibody accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","TSH receptor stimulating antibody (thyroid-stimulating immunoglobulin)","Anti-thyroid peroxidase (anti-TPO) antibody without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anti-smooth muscle antibody leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'TSH receptor stimulating antibody (thyroid-stimulating immunoglobulin)', 'Graves'' disease is caused by thyroid-stimulating immunoglobulins (TSI) activating the TSH receptor.'),
('3c39436e-ee0d-5ca1-9a35-dd03cedd83ef'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'A 45-year-old nurse complains of heat intolerance, hand tremors, and weight loss. TSH is suppressed. (Endocrine Case #83)', '["Anti-smooth muscle antibody resulting in decreased functional activity","Antinuclear antibody accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anti-thyroid peroxidase (anti-TPO) antibody mediated by specific receptor activation","TSH receptor stimulating antibody (thyroid-stimulating immunoglobulin)","Anti-thyroglobulin antibody leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'TSH receptor stimulating antibody (thyroid-stimulating immunoglobulin)', 'Graves'' disease is caused by thyroid-stimulating immunoglobulins (TSI) activating the TSH receptor.'),
('e1573819-e1bb-53d7-ac68-7635d320ef7c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'A 35-year-old female presents with palpitations, weight loss, and prominent bulging of the eyes (exophthalmos). Labs: suppressed TSH, elevated free T4. (Endocrine Case #84)', '["Anti-smooth muscle antibody resulting in decreased functional activity","TSH receptor stimulating antibody (thyroid-stimulating immunoglobulin)","Anti-thyroid peroxidase (anti-TPO) antibody mediated by specific receptor activation","Antinuclear antibody without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anti-thyroglobulin antibody leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'TSH receptor stimulating antibody (thyroid-stimulating immunoglobulin)', 'Graves'' disease is caused by thyroid-stimulating immunoglobulins (TSI) activating the TSH receptor.'),
('9a652299-560b-584c-ada8-d2d5e36cacb6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Hypercalcemia causes:', '["Increased vitamin D","Increased PTH","Decreased PTH (via CaSR)","Increased calcitonin only","Unchanged PTH"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased PTH (via CaSR)', 'High extracellular calcium activates the calcium-sensing receptor on chief cells and suppresses PTH secretion. Calcitonin is also increased in response to hypercalcemia, but PTH is reduced.'),
('e167de5e-1096-5b64-8035-99b2a54d6752'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'In response to hypocalcemia, the parathyroid glands:', '["Decreased PTH","Decreased calcitonin","Decreased vitamin D","Increased calcitonin","Increased PTH"]'::jsonb, 'Increased PTH', 'Hypocalcemia stimulates the parathyroid glands to increase PTH secretion. Elevated PTH then raises serum calcium through bone resorption, renal calcium retention, and vitamin D activation.'),
('d0932e41-026e-518a-8a8d-58eda0aee28f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'PTH-related peptide (PTHrP) is produced by:', '["Parathyroid resulting in decreased functional activity","Adrenal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thyroid mediated by specific receptor activation","Kidney without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Various tumors causing humoral hypercalcemia of malignancy"]'::jsonb, 'Various tumors causing humoral hypercalcemia of malignancy', 'PTHrP is secreted by various malignant tumors (e.g., squamous cell carcinoma of the lung, renal cell carcinoma) and causes humoral hypercalcemia of malignancy by activating PTH receptors. PTHrP also has normal roles in fetal calcium handling.'),
('690f8647-9ecf-59dd-bb17-54d7c5113c78'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'A 60-year-old female presents with fatigue, weight gain, and cold intolerance. Which condition is the most common cause of primary hyperparathyroidism?', '["Parathyroid adenoma (single)","Parathyroid carcinoma","Parathyroid hyperplasia","Ectopic PTHrP","Renal failure"]'::jsonb, 'Parathyroid adenoma (single)', 'A single parathyroid adenoma accounts for about 80% of primary hyperparathyroidism. Parathyroid carcinoma is rare, and renal failure causes secondary rather than primary hyperparathyroidism.'),
('00530f17-9afd-5356-aaaf-47a4cf6b34a1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Which of the following is NOT a clinical feature of primary hyperparathyroidism?', '["Kidney stones","Depression","Bone pain","Polyuria","Hypocalcemia"]'::jsonb, 'Hypocalcemia', 'Primary hyperparathyroidism produces hypercalcemia, which manifests as kidney stones, bone pain, polyuria, and depression. Hypocalcemia is characteristic of hypoparathyroidism, not primary hyperparathyroidism.'),
('74ca7677-d3a7-578e-9dd3-0527f84d5ee0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'A 25-year-old male presents with bone pain and kidney stones. Which etiological factor is primarily responsible for this presentation?', '["Parathyroid cancer","Parathyroid adenoma","Vitamin D deficiency or chronic kidney disease (low Ca2+)","PTHrP","Calcium excess"]'::jsonb, 'Parathyroid adenoma', 'Bone pain and kidney stones in a young adult are typical of primary hyperparathyroidism, most commonly caused by a single parathyroid adenoma. Vitamin D deficiency and CKD produce secondary hyperparathyroidism without the hypercalcemia that causes stones.'),
('009f2ce4-fcc1-5a85-9812-4cf2f003057e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'A 45-year-old male presents with bone pain and kidney stones. Which laboratory finding most likely explains this presentation?', '["Low phosphate","High Ca2+","High PTH","Low Ca2+","High phosphate"]'::jsonb, 'High Ca2+', 'Kidney stones with bone pain are the classic presentation of primary hyperparathyroidism. Excess PTH raises serum calcium, which promotes nephrolithiasis and demineralizes bone, so hypercalcemia explains both findings.'),
('7914df71-885b-5d63-b18a-3b68fced50e4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'A 50-year-old male presents with muscle cramps and paresthesias. What is the most likely cause of this condition?', '["Genetic resulting in decreased functional activity","Autoimmune accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Parathyroid removal during thyroidectomy","Radiation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Idiopathic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Parathyroid removal during thyroidectomy', 'Muscle cramps and paresthesias suggest hypocalcemia from acute loss of PTH. Accidental removal of the parathyroid glands during thyroidectomy is the most common iatrogenic cause of hypocalcemia and tetany.'),
('1f361c52-fb4a-5843-a540-85a650dd76bf'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Chvostek sign is best described as:', '["Nystagmus resulting in decreased functional activity","Facial muscle twitching on tapping over facial nerve","Muscle cramps mediated by specific receptor activation","Carpopedal spasm with BP cuff without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Positive Babinski leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Facial muscle twitching on tapping over facial nerve', 'The Chvostek sign is elicited by tapping over the facial nerve in front of the ear and is positive when it triggers ipsilateral facial muscle twitching. It is a bedside indicator of latent tetany in hypocalcemia.'),
('4376b193-7384-5fc4-b1a0-29269ff0f758'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Endocrinology Pt2: Leptin acts primarily on which brain region to suppress appetite?', '["Liver","Hypothalamus","Adipose tissue (white fat)","Pancreas","Stomach"]'::jsonb, 'Hypothalamus', 'Leptin crosses the blood-brain barrier and acts on POMC neurons in the arcuate nucleus of the hypothalamus to suppress appetite and increase energy expenditure. It is produced by adipose tissue, so the hypothalamus is the receptor site.'),
('8352f766-9fad-5260-a92d-b64a4c016caa'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Pseudohypoparathyroidism is due to:', '["Renal failure resulting in decreased functional activity","Resistance to PTH (Gs alpha mutation)","PTH deficiency mediated by specific receptor activation","PTH excess without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vitamin D deficiency leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Resistance to PTH (Gs alpha mutation)', 'Pseudohypoparathyroidism features hypocalcemia and hyperphosphatemia despite elevated PTH, because of target-organ resistance to PTH action. It results from inactivating mutations in the Gs alpha subunit (GNAS).'),
('494a6f5c-f222-529b-a325-201d0b7622b0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Endocrinology Pt2: Codeine converted to morphine by:', '["CYP2D6","CYP3A4","CYP2C9","CYP2C19","CYP1A2"]'::jsonb, 'CYP2D6', 'CYP2D6 O-demethylates codeine. Poor metabolizers get no benefit.'),
('aabf975b-ea70-5612-a944-99796a4693a7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'What is the primary biochemical disturbance in hypoparathyroidism?', '["No change in serum calcium resulting in decreased functional activity","Hypercalcemia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Seizures mediated by specific receptor activation","Tetany without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hypocalcemia (due to PTH deficiency/resistance)"]'::jsonb, 'Hypocalcemia (due to PTH deficiency/resistance)', 'PTH maintains serum calcium by stimulating bone resorption, renal calcium reabsorption, and vitamin D activation. Its deficiency or resistance lowers serum calcium and can precipitate tetany.'),
('73af6a81-2476-5d3b-a377-34030fe9853a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Vitamin D is best classified as:', '["A hormone (secosteroid)","An enzyme","A fatty acid","A carbohydrate","A vitamin"]'::jsonb, 'A hormone (secosteroid)', 'Vitamin D is a secosteroid hormone that acts through the vitamin D receptor to regulate calcium and phosphate homeostasis. Although named a vitamin, its endogenous synthesis and receptor-mediated action make it a hormone.'),
('6d9453ed-f36b-5dc4-99c1-a42306ca510d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Which is the biologically active form of vitamin D?', '["25-OH vitamin D","Vitamin D2","1,25-(OH)2 Vitamin D (calcitriol)","7-dehydrocholesterol","Vitamin D3 (cholecalciferol)"]'::jsonb, '1,25-(OH)2 Vitamin D (calcitriol)', 'Calcitriol (1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D) is the biologically active form, synthesized in the kidney by 1-alpha-hydroxylase. The hepatic metabolite 25-OH vitamin D is the main circulating form but is not active.'),
('aa731a2c-bb89-50f5-88ec-378938faefb9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'In the study of Endocrinology Pt2, Which hormone primarily stimulates milk synthesis (lactogenesis)?', '["Breast development at puberty","Milk synthesis (lactogenesis)","Sexual behavior","Uterine contraction","Milk ejection"]'::jsonb, 'Milk synthesis (lactogenesis)', 'Prolactin acts on mammary alveolar cells to stimulate milk synthesis and secretion. Milk ejection is instead driven by oxytocin triggered by suckling.'),
('3eae4ea9-af48-57c6-8d7e-d3e00cbdef8a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'What is the first step in vitamin D activation?', '["Lung resulting in decreased functional activity","Liver (25-hydroxylase) to 25-OH D","Skin mediated by specific receptor activation","Gut without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Kidney (1-alpha-hydroxylase) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Liver (25-hydroxylase) to 25-OH D', 'The first activation step is hepatic 25-hydroxylation, in which 25-hydroxylase converts cholecalciferol to 25-hydroxyvitamin D (calcidiol), the major circulating form.'),
('e641cf49-f3ef-5ad3-a8a9-d2464cacad80'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'The second hydroxylation of vitamin D occurs in:', '["Bone resulting in decreased functional activity","25-(OH)2 D accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Skin mediated by specific receptor activation","Liver without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Kidney (1-alpha-hydroxylase) to form calcitriol"]'::jsonb, 'Kidney (1-alpha-hydroxylase) to form calcitriol', 'The second hydroxylation occurs in renal proximal tubular cells, where 1-alpha-hydroxylase converts 25-OH vitamin D into the active calcitriol. Chronic kidney disease disrupts this step, causing vitamin D deficiency.'),
('57d2b0a6-321e-54ce-a20d-7fc015c312e5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'The main function of vitamin D is to:', '["Increase bone resorption","Decrease Ca2+ absorption","Decrease PTH","Increase intestinal Ca2+ and PO4- absorption","Decrease Ca2+ reabsorption"]'::jsonb, 'Increase intestinal Ca2+ and PO4- absorption', 'Calcitriol acts through the vitamin D receptor in enterocytes to upregulate intestinal calcium and phosphate absorption. This mineral absorption is central to maintaining calcium balance and bone mineralization.'),
('40d8be55-c71e-5720-9496-64e76d70dd78'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Endocrinology Pt2: DAG (diacylglycerol) activates which of the following?', '["PKG","Tyrosine kinase","PKA","CaM kinase","Protein kinase C (PKC)"]'::jsonb, 'Protein kinase C (PKC)', 'DAG remains in the plasma membrane and activates protein kinase C, often together with the calcium released by IP3. PKC then phosphorylates downstream targets to propagate the Gq signaling cascade.'),
('dbdbf72c-9e5c-5286-816a-3522bce18eca'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'In the study of Endocrinology Pt2, TSH stimulates all thyroid processes EXCEPT:', '["Calcitonin secretion","Thyroglobulin synthesis","Thyroid hormone release","Iodide organification","Iodide uptake"]'::jsonb, 'Calcitonin secretion', 'TSH stimulates iodide uptake, organification, thyroglobulin synthesis, and hormone release by follicular cells, but it does not stimulate C cells to secrete calcitonin.'),
('0e12e098-f1ad-5b8f-92da-cc51961f2c8e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Endocrinology Pt2: What is the typical plasma half-life of steroid hormones?', '["Very long (weeks)","Years","Short (minutes)","Relatively long (hours)","Seconds"]'::jsonb, 'Relatively long (hours)', 'Steroid hormones are lipid-soluble and circulate bound to carrier proteins, which slows their degradation and clearance. They therefore have relatively long half-lives of hours to days.'),
('51c170ae-b9de-5995-a363-f3fcbaf75aa4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Which is the most characteristic presenting symptom of osteomalacia?', '["Height loss only","Proximal muscle weakness","Kyphosis alone","Bone pain","Fractures"]'::jsonb, 'Bone pain', 'Osteomalacia classically presents with diffuse bone pain and tenderness caused by unmineralized osteoid. Proximal muscle weakness and insufficiency fractures are associated features, but bone pain is the characteristic complaint.'),
('b853ff31-de26-5a8e-a3a2-dfd3879dee97'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Which is the primary metabolic disturbance in vitamin D toxicity?', '["Hypercalcemia","Osteomalacia","Hypocalcemia","Kidney stones","Hypercalciuria"]'::jsonb, 'Hypercalcemia', 'Vitamin D toxicity increases intestinal calcium absorption beyond physiologic need, producing hypercalcemia. Hypercalciuria and nephrolithiasis are secondary complications of the elevated calcium.'),
('e0e78b53-16fa-5064-882e-973691c4ad8b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Endocrinology Pt2: Myxedema coma is best described as:', '["Adrenal crisis","Life-threatening hypothyroidism","Hyperthyroidism crisis","Thyroid storm","Pituitary apoplexy"]'::jsonb, 'Life-threatening hypothyroidism', 'Myxedema coma is a life-threatening complication of severe hypothyroidism characterized by hypothermia, bradycardia, hypoventilation, and altered mental status.'),
('63d1e59c-55d7-5df0-9cd3-2d6e6603939e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'What are the two main renal actions of parathyroid hormone (PTH)?', '["Phosphate excretion and vitamin D metabolism","PTH secretion","Bone formation","Suppression of phosphate excretion","Calcium excretion"]'::jsonb, 'Phosphate excretion and vitamin D metabolism', 'In the kidney, PTH increases urinary phosphate excretion (phosphaturia) and stimulates 1-alpha-hydroxylase to generate active vitamin D. It simultaneously increases renal calcium reabsorption.'),
('91e14c9b-148d-5659-8775-fc2e98527bcf'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Endocrinology Pt2: From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Endocrinology Pt1: What is the therapeutic index of a drug?', '["TD50 / ED50","ED50 / TD50","LD50 / ED50","ED50 / LD50","TD50 / LD50"]'::jsonb, 'TD50 / ED50', 'TI = TD50/ED50. Wider TI = safer drug.'),
('16047d2f-5c90-51b2-9848-09af44728234'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Albright hereditary osteodystrophy is associated with:', '["Gs alpha mutation","None of the above","Pseudohypoparathyroidism","Pseudopseudohypoparathyroidism","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Albright hereditary osteodystrophy is caused by Gs alpha (GNAS) mutations and is seen in both pseudohypoparathyroidism and pseudopseudohypoparathyroidism. Its features include short stature, round face, and brachydactyly.'),
('3d0d5796-3513-5759-b008-e0eba38636ac'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of Endocrinology Pt2, CYP enzyme metabolising most drugs:', '["CYP3A4","CYP2D6","CYP2C9","CYP2C19","CYP1A2"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4', 'CYP3A4 metabolises 50% of clinically used drugs.'),
('de3e736b-b476-556d-afdf-0eb8288e3746'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Osteoporosis differs from osteomalacia by:', '["Decreased bone mass with normal mineralization","High turnover accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Low PTH mediated by specific receptor activation","Normal bone density without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Defective mineralization leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased bone mass with normal mineralization', 'Osteoporosis is characterized by reduced bone mass with normally mineralized bone, whereas osteomalacia features defective mineralization with unmineralized osteoid. Bone density is low in both, but the mineralization differs.'),
('f5b7322e-7a74-5911-bdde-b6557009aa2d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Paget disease of bone is characterized by:', '["Osteomalacia resulting in decreased functional activity","Low turnover accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Excessive bone resorption followed by disorganized formation","Rickets without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Normal bone turnover leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Excessive bone resorption followed by disorganized formation', 'Paget disease begins with excessive osteoclastic resorption followed by disorganized, woven bone formation. The resulting structurally weak, enlarged bone predisposes to deformity, pain, and fractures.'),
('6247cc0e-9aba-5585-a442-2b35f4e1db5a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Endocrinology Pt2: How are peptide hormones transported in the blood?', '["Bound to carrier proteins","Bound to albumin","Bound to globulins","In RBCs","Freely dissolved (unbound)"]'::jsonb, 'Freely dissolved (unbound)', 'Peptide hormones are water-soluble and travel freely dissolved in plasma without carrier proteins. By contrast, steroid and thyroid hormones circulate bound to plasma transport proteins.'),
('e4fa3319-96ad-56e5-8055-65e624fbf531'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Endocrinology Pt2: What is the pathogenic antibody in Graves'' disease?', '["TSI (thyroid-stimulating immunoglobulin)","TSH-blocking antibodies","Anti-TPO antibodies","Anti-thyroglobulin antibodies","Anti-GAD antibodies"]'::jsonb, 'TSI (thyroid-stimulating immunoglobulin)', 'In Graves disease, thyroid-stimulating immunoglobulin (TSI) binds to and activates the TSH receptor, driving unregulated thyroid hormone production.'),
('0a1d50e0-078c-560b-aa63-216f7e9b4946'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Insulin is synthesized as:', '["Preproinsulin -> insulin","Proinsulin only","C-peptide and insulin separately","Proinsulin -> insulin","Preproinsulin -> proinsulin -> insulin + C-peptide"]'::jsonb, 'Preproinsulin -> proinsulin -> insulin + C-peptide', 'Preproinsulin is cleaved to proinsulin, which is then processed into insulin and C-peptide. Proinsulin and C-peptide are co-secreted with insulin in equimolar amounts.'),
('834906ba-2fa4-5e56-a362-08d017a8ac52'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Endocrinology Pt2: Hyperthyroidism features include all EXCEPT:', '["Exophthalmos","Weight loss","Tachycardia","Bradycardia","Heat intolerance"]'::jsonb, 'Bradycardia', 'Hyperthyroidism causes tachycardia, not bradycardia; exophthalmos, weight loss, and heat intolerance are characteristic hyperthyroid features.'),
('56d003c1-18fc-5d48-b28c-5c0dd1edc5b1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'In the study of Endocrinology Pt2, GH increases blood glucose by:', '["Stimulating insulin","Decreasing glucose uptake (anti-insulin effect)","Increasing glucose uptake","Stimulating glycogenesis","Inhibiting gluconeogenesis"]'::jsonb, 'Decreasing glucose uptake (anti-insulin effect)', 'GH opposes the action of insulin by reducing glucose uptake in peripheral tissues and promoting lipolysis, thereby raising blood glucose.'),
('9fd87ad9-d654-5bd5-9ebd-7544ceaa0058'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'What sequence of events couples a rise in blood glucose to insulin secretion from pancreatic beta cells?', '["cAMP only resulting in decreased functional activity","Nuclear receptor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","GLUT2 -> glucokinase -> ATP -> KATP channel closure -> depolarization -> Ca2+ influx","Direct GPCR activation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tyrosine kinase leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'GLUT2 -> glucokinase -> ATP -> KATP channel closure -> depolarization -> Ca2+ influx', 'Glucose enters beta cells via GLUT2 and is phosphorylated by glucokinase; the resulting ATP closes KATP channels. Channel closure depolarizes the cell and opens voltage-gated calcium channels, triggering insulin exocytosis.'),
('593ba4a8-f6b5-5627-a991-81ab1e6c1027'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Endocrinology Pt2: Anterior pituitary hormones are all:', '["Steroids","Amines","Peptides/proteins","Nucleotides","Eicosanoids"]'::jsonb, 'Peptides/proteins', 'Every anterior pituitary hormone—GH, prolactin, ACTH, TSH, FSH, and LH—is a peptide or protein hormone. Because they cannot cross the cell membrane, they signal through cell surface receptors.'),
('9f250ac5-2a4c-52b7-b3c3-6f3d86b3bae1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Repaglinide and nateglinide (meglitinides) stimulate insulin secretion by:', '["Decrease glucagon","Inhibit DPP-4","Open KATP channels","Close KATP channels (rapid/short action)","Increase insulin sensitivity"]'::jsonb, 'Close KATP channels (rapid/short action)', 'Meglitinides block beta-cell KATP channels to stimulate insulin release, with rapid onset and a short duration of action. They are most useful for controlling postprandial glucose.'),
('0b3f3406-bbb8-5566-8293-8ed4fd8d64b9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Endocrinology Pt2: Which brain structure serves as the master circadian clock?', '["Medulla resulting in decreased functional activity","Thalamus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pineal gland mediated by specific receptor activation","Hypothalamic suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN)","Pituitary leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hypothalamic suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN)', 'The suprachiasmatic nucleus receives direct retinal input through the retinohypothalamic tract and coordinates circadian rhythms across the body, making it the master clock.'),
('7f937f9c-d871-5bd5-8e96-119b86a036ce'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'GIP (gastric inhibitory polypeptide) is produced by:', '["K cells of duodenum/jejunum","PP cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","G cells mediated by specific receptor activation","L cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","D cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'K cells of duodenum/jejunum', 'GIP is released from K cells in the duodenum and jejunum in response to ingested glucose and fat. It potentiates insulin secretion as part of the incretin response.'),
('8cc81f40-0963-53dc-841e-4776922992ed'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'The incretin effect accounts for:', '["Direct glucose effect resulting in decreased functional activity","Insulin resistance accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Greater insulin response to oral vs IV glucose","Insulin sensitivity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Glucagon suppression leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Greater insulin response to oral vs IV glucose', 'The incretin effect describes a larger insulin response to oral than to equivalent intravenous glucose matched for glycemia. It is mediated by GIP and GLP-1 released from the gut during meals.'),
('f4c39ed5-9360-5e7f-945d-e60a130b65dc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'DPP-4 inhibitors (e.g., sitagliptin) work by:', '["Preventing GLP-1/GIP degradation","Stimulating insulin","Blocking KATP","Blocking glucose absorption","Increasing glucagon"]'::jsonb, 'Preventing GLP-1/GIP degradation', 'DPP-4 rapidly inactivates GLP-1 and GIP; inhibitors preserve these incretins, enhancing glucose-dependent insulin secretion. Their effects are modest and they rarely cause hypoglycemia when used alone.'),
('cf0ae36a-59ab-57d5-a256-acafc3c19b34'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'GLP-1 receptor agonists (e.g., exenatide) produce all of the following effects EXCEPT:', '["Stimulate glucose-dependent insulin secretion","Suppress glucagon secretion","Slow gastric emptying","Reduce postprandial blood glucose","Increase glucagon secretion"]'::jsonb, 'Increase glucagon secretion', 'GLP-1 receptor agonists enhance glucose-dependent insulin secretion, suppress glucagon, and delay gastric emptying, lowering postprandial glucose. They do not raise glucagon; they inhibit its release, making that option the exception.'),
('f52caf17-f581-50d4-9c82-626fa2d1f2b8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Endocrinology Pt2: Somatostatin exists in:', '["50 aa form","100 aa form","10 aa form","Single 41 aa form","14 aa and 28 aa forms"]'::jsonb, '14 aa and 28 aa forms', 'Somatostatin is cleaved from preprosomatostatin into two active forms: a 14-amino-acid peptide (SS-14) and a 28-amino-acid form (SS-28). Both inhibit growth hormone, TSH, insulin, and glucagon secretion.'),
('ce61cdea-d36d-523b-9c41-bc4dedee0e0d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'In the study of Endocrinology Pt2, Iodide is trapped in the thyroid via:', '["Simple diffusion","Iodide channels","Na+/I- symporter (NIS)","Facilitated diffusion","Active transport by ATPase"]'::jsonb, 'Na+/I- symporter (NIS)', 'The Na+/I- symporter (NIS) on the basolateral membrane of follicular cells actively transports iodide into the thyroid against its concentration gradient.'),
('0bbf88f6-3b9a-58be-bd67-faf6056a824b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Endocrinology Pt2: A 55-year-old male with a chronic illness presents with fatigue and cold intolerance. Labs show low T3 with normal TSH. This pattern is most consistent with:', '["True hypothyroidism resulting in decreased functional activity","Thyroiditis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Low T3 with normal TSH in non-thyroidal illness","TSH deficiency without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hyperthyroidism leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Low T3 with normal TSH in non-thyroidal illness', 'In non-thyroidal illness (euthyroid sick syndrome), T4-to-T3 conversion is impaired, giving low T3 with normal TSH; true hypothyroidism would show a raised TSH.'),
('395a5893-41f2-5b73-9336-fe5ef54d0477'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Which signaling pathway mediates most of insulin''s metabolic actions after receptor activation?', '["IP3/Ca2+ resulting in decreased functional activity","Autophosphorylation -> IRS -> PI3K/Akt pathway","cGMP mediated by specific receptor activation","cAMP increase without affecting the primary tissue pathway","JAK-STAT leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Autophosphorylation -> IRS -> PI3K/Akt pathway', 'After insulin binds its receptor, autophosphorylation recruits insulin receptor substrates (IRS), activating PI3K and Akt. This cascade drives glucose uptake, glycogen synthesis, and protein synthesis.'),
('610ef480-d806-59f3-b7c2-6a79c10f7a22'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'A 60-year-old male has muscle weakness and serum potassium of 5.8 mEq/L. Which agent most effectively shifts potassium into cells?', '["Insulin (in muscle and adipose)","Glucagon accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Epinephrine mediated by specific receptor activation","Glucose without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cortisol leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Insulin (in muscle and adipose)', 'Insulin activates the sodium-potassium ATPase in muscle and adipose tissue, driving potassium into cells, so it is first-line therapy for hyperkalemia. Glucose is co-administered to prevent hypoglycemia but does not itself lower potassium.'),
('e0853921-26e6-524a-8a10-2e91e20593c0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'During fasting, which metabolic process is stimulated to raise blood glucose?', '["Increased glucose uptake","Increased protein synthesis","Increased lipogenesis","Increased glycogenesis","Increased gluconeogenesis"]'::jsonb, 'Increased gluconeogenesis', 'During fasting, glucagon and cortisol stimulate hepatic gluconeogenesis to maintain blood glucose while insulin falls. This is the key counter-regulatory response that supplies glucose between meals.'),
('b3715c56-c02a-58df-ab1d-00549387d6e3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'In the study of Endocrinology Pt2, Hormonal half-life depends primarily on:', '["Storage","Rate of secretion","Synthesis rate","Binding proteins","Rate of clearance/degradation"]'::jsonb, 'Binding proteins', 'Carrier proteins shield hormones from rapid hepatic and renal clearance, so protein-bound hormones such as cortisol and T4 have long half-lives. Peptide hormones lacking carriers are degraded within minutes.'),
('5fcae2ca-140f-5eae-9e8e-a49805e6e65c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Endocrinology Pt2: Activin stimulates:', '["GH secretion","LH secretion","TSH secretion","Prolactin","FSH secretion"]'::jsonb, 'FSH secretion', 'Activin is produced in the pituitary and gonads and acts on gonadotrophs to increase FSH synthesis and secretion. Inhibin and follistatin oppose this action.'),
('be5f4886-71b5-5cb9-a797-9f7ff008970c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'What is the effect of insulin on fat metabolism?', '["Decreases fat storage","Increases lipogenesis (fat storage)","Increases ketogenesis","Increases lipolysis","Increases fat oxidation"]'::jsonb, 'Increases lipogenesis (fat storage)', 'Insulin promotes fat storage by stimulating lipogenesis and inhibiting lipolysis and ketogenesis. In insulin deficiency, lipolysis and ketogenesis increase, producing ketone bodies and weight loss.'),
('bbd047b1-ac66-55bf-a4f4-e4a9b2af16f9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of Endocrinology Pt2, Thyroid hormones are transported in blood bound to:', '["All of the above","Albumin","Transthyretin","Thyroid-binding globulin (TBG)","Only TBG"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Circulating T4 and T3 bind chiefly to thyroid-binding globulin (TBG) and also to transthyretin and albumin.'),
('dc591ed8-6d95-5be3-bcee-c983ac496d61'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Endocrinology Pt2: Lithium can cause:', '["Hyperthyroidism","Thyroid storm","No significant effect","Hypothyroidism","Goiter only"]'::jsonb, 'Hypothyroidism', 'Lithium inhibits thyroid hormone release and organification, commonly causing hypothyroidism.'),
('30ff277a-e31e-58d3-a3eb-b86e556f9247'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Glucagon acts primarily on which organ?', '["Kidney","Liver","Muscle","Adipose tissue","Brain"]'::jsonb, 'Liver', 'Glucagon acts mainly on hepatocytes through GPCRs coupled to Gs, raising cAMP and activating PKA to drive glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis. Muscle and adipose tissue lack significant glucagon receptors.'),
('16f6b44e-7a0e-5c19-aa6f-24931c6eba06'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Endocrinology Pt2: Which of the following is the precursor for steroid hormone synthesis?', '["Nucleotides","Amino acids","Fatty acids","Cholesterol","Glucose"]'::jsonb, 'Cholesterol', 'All steroid hormones are synthesized from cholesterol, which is converted to pregnenolone as the first committed step. This occurs in steroidogenic tissues such as the adrenal cortex and the gonads.'),
('a7012892-93a4-53d9-8ea5-2a99f23b295a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Endocrinology Pt2: Which hypothalamic hormone inhibits growth hormone (GH) release from the anterior pituitary?', '["GHRH","Somatostatin","TRH","Dopamine","GnRH"]'::jsonb, 'Somatostatin', 'Somatostatin, also called growth hormone-inhibiting hormone, is released by periventricular hypothalamic neurons and suppresses GH secretion. GHRH provides the opposing stimulatory signal.'),
('63d89945-fee7-52b4-b025-ed696d42c83b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of Endocrinology Pt2, TSH secretion is inhibited by:', '["TRH resulting in decreased functional activity","ACTH accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Gonadotropins mediated by specific receptor activation","Both T3/T4 and somatostatin/dopamine","Growth hormone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Both T3/T4 and somatostatin/dopamine', 'TSH secretion is suppressed by thyroid hormones via negative feedback and by somatostatin and dopamine; TRH, in contrast, stimulates TSH release.'),
('66bf8c81-4993-56da-9d4c-d1f799b793bf'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Endocrinology Pt2: Calcitonin acts to:', '["Increase blood calcium resulting in decreased functional activity","Decrease vitamin D accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Decrease blood calcium (inhibits osteoclasts)","Increase phosphate without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Increase PTH leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Decrease blood calcium (inhibits osteoclasts)', 'Calcitonin lowers blood calcium primarily by inhibiting osteoclast-mediated bone resorption.'),
('fac1700d-670f-54ad-8e65-382c17c38574'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Diabetes mellitus type 1 is due to:', '["Beta cell failure in obesity resulting in decreased functional activity","Insulin resistance accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Genetic defect mediated by specific receptor activation","Autoimmune destruction of beta cells (absolute insulin deficiency)","Pancreatitis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Autoimmune destruction of beta cells (absolute insulin deficiency)', 'Type 1 diabetes results from autoimmune destruction of pancreatic beta cells, producing absolute insulin deficiency. Insulin resistance with relative deficiency and beta-cell failure in obesity characterize type 2 diabetes.'),
('6ed2e333-c6f7-524b-8467-fb2c75350d23'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Endocrinology Pt2: Anterior pituitary hormones are all:', '["Steroids","Amines","Peptides/proteins","Nucleotides","Eicosanoids"]'::jsonb, 'Peptides/proteins', 'Every anterior pituitary hormone—GH, prolactin, ACTH, TSH, FSH, and LH—is a peptide or protein hormone. Because they cannot cross the cell membrane, they signal through cell surface receptors.'),
('4e3a114e-6f58-5db8-ba92-e27c45e3a7ec'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Metabolic syndrome includes all of the following EXCEPT:', '["Dyslipidemia","Hypertension","Central obesity","Hypotension","Hyperglycemia"]'::jsonb, 'Hypotension', 'Metabolic syndrome comprises central obesity, dyslipidemia, hypertension, and hyperglycemia with insulin resistance. Hypotension is not a component; hypertension is a defining feature.'),
('64c97fc5-fa13-530b-bcf0-066d67d43d3d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Which of the following is the most important modifiable risk factor for developing type 2 diabetes mellitus?', '["Family history","High HDL cholesterol","Obesity","Low body weight","Tall stature"]'::jsonb, 'Obesity', 'Obesity, particularly central obesity, is the strongest modifiable risk factor for type 2 diabetes because of its association with insulin resistance. Family history is non-modifiable, and high HDL cholesterol is protective.'),
('36c3a54c-c360-50b2-adf6-7cbc83f90800'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA) is most commonly associated with:', '["IGT resulting in decreased functional activity","Type 2 DM accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Prediabetes mediated by specific receptor activation","Type 1 DM (insulin deficiency -> lipolysis -> ketogenesis)","Metabolic syndrome leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Type 1 DM (insulin deficiency -> lipolysis -> ketogenesis)', 'DKA is most common in type 1 diabetes, where absolute insulin deficiency leads to unopposed lipolysis and hepatic ketogenesis. In type 2 diabetes, residual insulin usually limits ketone production.'),
('0bf44932-3f11-5943-9e42-6fc5424e33b9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'In diabetic ketoacidosis, the respiratory compensation for metabolic acidosis is:', '["Hyperglycemia","Hyperkalemia initially then total body K+ depletion","Ketonuria","Metabolic acidosis","Hyperventilation"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperventilation', 'In DKA, metabolic acidosis is compensated by Kussmaul breathing, a pattern of deep rapid hyperventilation that blows off carbon dioxide. The ketone-driven acidosis itself is the metabolic, not respiratory, abnormality.'),
('79b111eb-939d-55a9-8451-8f350b1b3bff'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Endocrinology Pt2: From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Endocrinology Pt1: Efficacy refers to:', '["Ability to produce maximal response","Dose for effect accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Absorption rate mediated by specific receptor activation","Duration without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Safety leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ability to produce maximal response', 'Emax = maximum response a drug can produce.'),
('1521af84-72c6-5305-8a12-654916438a0c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Which of the following is the most characteristic laboratory finding of hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state (HHS)?', '["Extreme hyperglycemia","Dehydration","Ketoacidosis","Altered mental status","Kussmaul breathing"]'::jsonb, 'Extreme hyperglycemia', 'HHS is defined by extreme hyperglycemia, typically above 600 mg/dL, accompanied by severe dehydration and minimal or absent ketosis. Ketoacidosis and Kussmaul breathing are characteristic of DKA rather than HHS.'),
('a177c015-a17f-5743-b38e-15455bec59cb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Insulin therapy is indicated in which of the following situations?', '["Type 2 DM with pancreatic beta-cell failure","Type 1 DM","Gestational DM (if required)","All of the above","Prediabetes"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Insulin is essential for type 1 diabetes and is also used in type 2 diabetes once beta-cell failure develops and in gestational diabetes when needed. It is not indicated for prediabetes, where lifestyle modification is first-line.'),
('ee6d8570-f315-5fce-bf0a-875776731de0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Endocrinology Pt2: The posterior pituitary is derived from which of the following?', '["Mesoderm resulting in decreased functional activity","Oral ectoderm (Rathke pouch) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Endoderm mediated by specific receptor activation","Neural crest without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Neural ectoderm (down growth from hypothalamus)"]'::jsonb, 'Neural ectoderm (down growth from hypothalamus)', 'The posterior pituitary (neurohypophysis) develops as a down-growth from the hypothalamus and is therefore of neural ectodermal origin. The anterior pituitary, by contrast, arises from oral ectoderm of Rathke''s pouch.'),
('4827d7da-271f-548d-907a-e1c095b9c179'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'In the study of Endocrinology Pt2, The JAK-STAT signaling pathway is utilized by which of the following hormones?', '["Catecholamines","Insulin","Growth hormone and prolactin","Thyroid hormone","Steroid hormones"]'::jsonb, 'Growth hormone and prolactin', 'Growth hormone and prolactin signal through receptors that associate with JAK tyrosine kinases, activating the JAK-STAT pathway. Insulin uses receptor tyrosine kinases, while steroid and thyroid hormones act through nuclear receptors.'),
('d101202d-d6af-53ab-ab5a-30c60e715229'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'SGLT2 inhibitors (e.g. empagliflozin) work by:', '["Blocking glucose reabsorption in kidney (glycosuria)","Increasing GLP-1 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Stimulating insulin mediated by specific receptor activation","Decreasing glucagon without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Blocking KATP leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Blocking glucose reabsorption in kidney (glycosuria)', 'SGLT2 inhibitors block sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 in the proximal tubule, reducing renal glucose reabsorption and producing glycosuria with lower blood glucose. They do not stimulate insulin secretion.'),
('de9fd42b-3f92-57d4-8597-486da82a8d32'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'The incretin effect is:', '["Delayed gastric emptying","Glucagon suppression","Greater insulin response to oral vs IV glucose","Satiety","Greater insulin response to IV glucose"]'::jsonb, 'Greater insulin response to oral vs IV glucose', 'The incretin effect is the amplified insulin response after oral glucose compared with intravenous glucose at the same glycemia, mediated by the gut hormones GIP and GLP-1. It is diminished in type 2 diabetes.'),
('6d201407-557f-5163-9141-e3889b859a71'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'GLP-1 is rapidly degraded by which enzyme?', '["Pepsin","Trypsin","Carboxypeptidase","Aminopeptidase","DPP-4 (half-life 2 min)"]'::jsonb, 'DPP-4 (half-life 2 min)', 'GLP-1 is rapidly inactivated by dipeptidyl peptidase-4 (DPP-4), giving it a plasma half-life of about 2 minutes. DPP-4 inhibitors prolong incretin action by preventing this degradation.'),
('6ac3ee4c-4166-52f5-9842-08547a4e7cfd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Endocrinology Pt2: Primary hyperaldosteronism (Conn syndrome) produces which combination of findings?', '["Normal BP with hypernatremia","Hypertension and hypokalemia","Hypotension and hyperkalemia","Hypertension and hyperkalemia","Hypotension and hypokalemia"]'::jsonb, 'Hypertension and hypokalemia', 'Primary hyperaldosteronism causes excessive sodium retention and potassium excretion, producing hypertension with hypokalemia.'),
('2d540820-2222-58b0-ad95-f151bfb027c9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Which of the following most strongly stimulates release of the incretin hormones GIP and GLP-1?', '["Intravenous glucose","Glucose and fat in the duodenum","Fasting","Low blood glucose","Sleep"]'::jsonb, 'Glucose and fat in the duodenum', 'Incretins are released when nutrients, especially glucose and fat, are present in the intestinal lumen, enhancing glucose-stimulated insulin secretion. This underlies the incretin effect, in which oral glucose releases more insulin than intravenous glucose at the same glycemia.'),
('b9b3f737-d093-5e7b-947f-3eef2f419567'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Endocrinology Pt2: LH in females causes:', '["Estrogen production","FSH release","Follicular growth","Prolactin release","Ovulation and luteinization"]'::jsonb, 'Ovulation and luteinization', 'The LH surge triggers rupture of the mature follicle and ovulation, and the remnant is luteinized into the corpus luteum, which secretes progesterone.'),
('20a5c6b5-42fa-5e25-ba50-fd91e7a1b85e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Endocrinology Pt2: Which of the following is not a chemical class of hormones?', '["Peptide/protein","Steroid","Nucleotide","Eicosanoid","Amine"]'::jsonb, 'Nucleotide', 'Peptide/protein, steroid, amine, and eicosanoid molecules constitute recognized hormone classes, whereas nucleotides are nucleic acid building blocks and are not classified as hormones.'),
('997efd68-5415-512d-a81e-8e602a668353'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Somatostatin from pancreatic delta cells acts:', '["Autocrine resulting in decreased functional activity","Paracrine (locally on adjacent alpha and beta cells)","Neurocrine mediated by specific receptor activation","Juxtacrine without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Endocrine (systemically) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Paracrine (locally on adjacent alpha and beta cells)', 'Pancreatic delta cells release somatostatin into the local interstitium, where it inhibits adjacent alpha and beta cells, a paracrine action. It does not circulate at concentrations high enough to act systemically.'),
('6c6febc6-6873-52d5-9d40-653ff3681f24'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Endocrinology Pt2: Adiponectin levels are:', '["Decreased in fasting resulting in decreased functional activity","Increased in diabetes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Decreased in obesity and insulin resistance","Increased in obesity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Unchanged leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased in obesity and insulin resistance', 'Adiponectin levels fall in obesity and insulin resistance and are inversely related to visceral adiposity; levels rise with weight loss.'),
('ee82c7c4-4b9d-5477-b660-672f46910ecb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Recombinant EPO is used to treat:', '["Hemophilia","Anemia of chronic kidney disease","Leukemia","Thrombocytopenia","Polycythemia vera"]'::jsonb, 'Anemia of chronic kidney disease', 'Recombinant EPO replaces the hormone lost as renal EPO production declines, correcting the normocytic anemia of chronic kidney disease. It is not used for hemophilia, leukemia, or thrombocytopenia.'),
('e067e7e3-e9bf-5862-a039-6f6f81a72e15'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'EPO receptor is:', '["Ion channel resulting in decreased functional activity","GPCR accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Tyrosine kinase mediated by specific receptor activation","Nuclear receptor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A cytokine receptor (JAK-STAT signaling)"]'::jsonb, 'A cytokine receptor (JAK-STAT signaling)', 'The EPO receptor is a type I cytokine receptor that signals through JAK2 and the STAT pathway. It is not a GPCR, ligand-gated ion channel, or nuclear receptor.'),
('a0e6a2ac-d645-5bce-bfe0-07c4264617f8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Androgens stimulate:', '["Cortisol","Aldosterone","Erythropoiesis (increase EPO)","Prolactin","Insulin secretion"]'::jsonb, 'Erythropoiesis (increase EPO)', 'Androgens stimulate erythropoiesis in part by increasing EPO production, which explains the higher hemoglobin levels in males. This is the basis for using androgens to treat some anemias.'),
('4a8a33c2-2f05-500d-95d4-59f7b08cf0d8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of Endocrinology Pt2, Codeine converted to morphine by:', '["CYP2D6","CYP3A4","CYP2C9","CYP2C19","CYP1A2"]'::jsonb, 'CYP2D6', 'CYP2D6 O-demethylates codeine. Poor metabolizers get no benefit.'),
('609496b4-619f-5b87-9aa6-e4613e27bf8f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Endocrinology Pt2: Warfarin primarily metabolised by:', '["CYP2C9","CYP3A4","CYP2D6","CYP2C19","CYP1A2"]'::jsonb, 'CYP2C9', 'CYP2C9 metabolises the more potent S-warfarin.'),
('2cd8420f-6c1f-519d-bd60-bf6ff36bb0af'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'In the study of Endocrinology Pt2, Leptin acts primarily on which brain region to suppress appetite?', '["Liver","Hypothalamus","Adipose tissue (white fat)","Pancreas","Stomach"]'::jsonb, 'Hypothalamus', 'Leptin crosses the blood-brain barrier and acts on POMC neurons in the arcuate nucleus of the hypothalamus to suppress appetite and increase energy expenditure. It is produced by adipose tissue, so the hypothalamus is the receptor site.'),
('8c6ff7eb-0da4-5431-bf78-1f8bef6b823e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Endocrinology Pt2: Maintenance dose primarily depends on:', '["Clearance","Volume of distribution","Half-life","Bioavailability","Protein binding"]'::jsonb, 'Clearance', 'Maintenance dose = CL x Css x tau / F.'),
('474c82f2-7f7d-5794-8b37-41073f7d312c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'The zona fasciculata of the adrenal cortex produces which hormone?', '["Aldosterone","Androgens","Epinephrine","Norepinephrine","Cortisol"]'::jsonb, 'Cortisol', 'The zona fasciculata of the adrenal cortex produces cortisol (glucocorticoids). The zona glomerulosa makes aldosterone, the zona reticularis makes androgens, and the adrenal medulla makes catecholamines.'),
('5f3e1afe-82f8-5e24-aa0f-4e7a941f55ef'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of Endocrinology Pt2, From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Endocrinology Pt1: What is the therapeutic index of a drug?', '["TD50 / ED50","ED50 / TD50","LD50 / ED50","ED50 / LD50","TD50 / LD50"]'::jsonb, 'TD50 / ED50', 'TI = TD50/ED50. Wider TI = safer drug.'),
('0ef3b922-f932-5b43-ad62-95c8f7a7da88'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Which stage of organ dysfunction is characterized by depletion of functional reserve?', '["Decompensated function","Impaired homeostatic regulation","Subclinical abnormality","Moderate impairment","Loss of feedback control"]'::jsonb, 'Moderate impairment', 'Moderate impairment is the stage at which functional reserve is depleted and homeostatic regulation becomes impaired, approaching clinical decompensation. Decompensated function is the later stage with overt clinical failure.'),
('b0967810-b26a-5569-b26b-6a571df255f5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'In the study of Endocrinology Pt2, Peripheral conversion of T4 to T3 is via:', '["5-deiodinase type 1 and 2","Monoamine oxidase","5-deiodinase type 3","Thyroid peroxidase","Catechol-O-methyltransferase"]'::jsonb, '5-deiodinase type 1 and 2', 'Type 1 and type 2 iodothyronine deiodinases convert T4 to T3 in peripheral tissues; type 3 deiodinase instead inactivates T4 to reverse T3.'),
('70ec419a-79e0-5b36-b9e6-39312e849ab9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Endocrinology Pt2: What is the normal fasting blood glucose level?', '["<70 mg/dL","<100 mg/dL","<140 mg/dL","<126 mg/dL","<200 mg/dL"]'::jsonb, '<100 mg/dL', 'Normal fasting plasma glucose is below 100 mg/dL. Values of 100-125 mg/dL indicate impaired fasting glucose, and a fasting level of 126 mg/dL or higher suggests diabetes mellitus.'),
('c8ae440c-a49c-5be7-9295-2963bdc01ba9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Endocrinology Pt2: Protein binding fraction (fu) ranges:', '["0 to 1","-1 to 0","0 to 100","-100 to 100","1 to 100"]'::jsonb, '0 to 1', 'Only unbound (free) drug is pharmacologically active.'),
('bad43884-2d90-5352-aaf9-2b2ee0bc1673'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of Endocrinology Pt2, EC50 is concentration producing:', '["50% max effect","100% max effect","No effect","Toxic effects","Lethal in 50%"]'::jsonb, '50% max effect', 'EC50 = concentration at 50% of maximal response.'),
('f1b21392-6082-5d54-b591-8b404b87e5cd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Endocrinology Pt2: A cyst found at the midline of the neck that moves with swallowing is most likely:', '["Thyroglossal duct cyst (midline neck)","Parathyroid cyst","Branchial cleft cyst","Lymph node","Thyroid cancer"]'::jsonb, 'Thyroglossal duct cyst (midline neck)', 'A midline neck mass that moves with swallowing or tongue protrusion is a thyroglossal duct cyst, the most common congenital neck mass.'),
('eb683840-4648-5367-a9cd-6e96128f6ef2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'In the study of Endocrinology Pt2, In the study of Endocrinology Pt1, Fick''s law: diffusion rate proportional to:', '["Surface area x concentration gradient / thickness","Molecular weight x concentration accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Protein binding x pH mediated by specific receptor activation","Vd x clearance without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ionisation x pKa leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Surface area x concentration gradient / thickness', 'Rate = SA xD x dC/dx.'),
('b8bf1b41-8981-5dd6-82b0-5f4227aace82'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Endocrinology Pt2: Thyrotoxic crisis (thyroid storm) is typically precipitated by:', '["TSH excess","Exercise","Low dietary iodine intake","Parathyroid surgery in a euthyroid patient","Stress"]'::jsonb, 'Stress', 'Thyroid storm is typically precipitated by infection, surgery, trauma, iodine load, or stress in a patient with uncontrolled hyperthyroidism.'),
('0dd3f25f-bdb3-56b3-9147-0ed7728dafe9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Which hormone is primarily responsible for basal metabolic rate?', '["Cortisol","Insulin","Growth hormone","Thyroxine","Glucagon"]'::jsonb, 'Thyroxine', 'Thyroid hormones, principally thyroxine (T4), set the basal metabolic rate by stimulating cellular oxygen consumption and thermogenesis across most tissues.'),
('f7746a22-4a5c-50eb-9f27-33a53658e6c4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of Endocrinology Pt2, The preoptic area contains:', '["Feeding center resulting in decreased functional activity","ADH neurons accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thermoregulatory center (heat loss)","Satiety center without affecting the primary tissue pathway","GnRH neurons leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thermoregulatory center (heat loss)', 'The preoptic/anterior hypothalamic area contains the heat-loss center, which promotes sweating and cutaneous vasodilation to dissipate heat. The posterior hypothalamus mediates heat conservation.'),
('1ac53cf9-58de-58a7-a28f-40e36af732b7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Endocrinology Pt2: What is the primary effect of IP3 on intracellular calcium?', '["Activation of PKA resulting in decreased functional activity","Release of Ca2+ from endoplasmic reticulum","cGMP increase mediated by specific receptor activation","Activation of PKC without affecting the primary tissue pathway","cAMP increase leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Release of Ca2+ from endoplasmic reticulum', 'IP3 binds IP3 receptors on the endoplasmic reticulum and triggers release of stored calcium into the cytosol. The resulting rise in cytosolic calcium drives calcium-dependent cellular responses.'),
('27585b44-5624-5b33-8843-5da6bef1097a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'In the study of Endocrinology Pt2, During therapeutic management of Endocrinology Pt1, P-glycoprotein is an efflux transporter that:', '["Pumps drugs out of cells","Enhances absorption","Binds to proteins","Metabolises drugs","Excretes in bile only"]'::jsonb, 'Pumps drugs out of cells', 'P-gp efflux at intestine, kidney, and BBB limits drug absorption.'),
('6d2f6e5c-79f3-5dc3-8c0c-d6877c58bcfc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Which hormone stimulates milk production?', '["Prolactin","Estrogen","hCG","Progesterone","Oxytocin"]'::jsonb, 'Prolactin', 'Prolactin acts on the mammary alveolar epithelium to stimulate milk synthesis (lactogenesis), whereas oxytocin triggers milk ejection rather than production.'),
('eb24f4d0-ef27-5645-8009-31ac3115076a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Endocrinology Pt2: Acromegaly features include all EXCEPT:', '["Coarse facial features","Proportionate tall stature","Prognathism","Enlarged hands and feet","Organomegaly"]'::jsonb, 'Proportionate tall stature', 'Acromegaly develops after epiphyseal closure, so height does not increase; the other listed features reflect bony and soft-tissue overgrowth.'),
('d229c85d-047e-52bc-a938-2d72afe1f7e8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Which hormone deficiency causes diabetes insipidus?', '["Aldosterone","Insulin","ADH (vasopressin)","Glucagon","Cortisol"]'::jsonb, 'ADH (vasopressin)', 'Diabetes insipidus results from deficiency of ADH (vasopressin), which causes loss of renal water reabsorption and dilute polyuria; central DI reflects reduced hypothalamic ADH production.'),
('fdafefca-8e22-5831-8f74-39810af34bbd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Endocrinology Pt2: Thyroglobulin is synthesized in:', '["Hypothalamus resulting in decreased functional activity","Liver accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pituitary mediated by specific receptor activation","Parafollicular cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thyroid follicular cells (released into colloid)"]'::jsonb, 'Thyroid follicular cells (released into colloid)', 'Thyroglobulin is produced by thyroid follicular cells and secreted into the colloid, where iodination and coupling produce the thyroid hormones.'),
('39847054-5916-599d-8303-645fff68e0a9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'In the study of Endocrinology Pt2, Somatostatin exists in:', '["50 aa form","100 aa form","10 aa form","Single 41 aa form","14 aa and 28 aa forms"]'::jsonb, '14 aa and 28 aa forms', 'Somatostatin is cleaved from preprosomatostatin into two active forms: a 14-amino-acid peptide (SS-14) and a 28-amino-acid form (SS-28). Both inhibit growth hormone, TSH, insulin, and glucagon secretion.'),
('dd982d37-cd76-5384-a249-10e7566f914a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Endocrinology Pt2: In the study of Endocrinology Pt1, CYP enzyme metabolising most drugs:', '["CYP3A4","CYP2D6","CYP2C9","CYP2C19","CYP1A2"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4', 'CYP3A4 metabolises 50% of clinically used drugs.'),
('71b9127f-c815-539c-8bd0-7e8068df9a5d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'The islets of Langerhans are located in which organ?', '["Liver","Pancreas","Adrenal gland","Spleen","Thyroid"]'::jsonb, 'Pancreas', 'The islets of Langerhans, containing alpha (glucagon), beta (insulin), and delta (somatostatin) cells, are the endocrine component of the pancreas.'),
('599f5717-967b-5bdb-80a5-2880e46dbb4e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of Endocrinology Pt2, Hyperglycemia causes:', '["Increased IGF-1","Decreased GH (inhibition)","Increased GHRH","Increased prolactin secretion","Increased GH"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased GH (inhibition)', 'Elevated blood glucose inhibits GH secretion and suppresses GHRH, whereas hypoglycemia stimulates GH release.'),
('56eb0b5c-0518-5940-b0ca-87b7a6586871'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Endocrinology Pt2: Hyperprolactinemia in a female most characteristically produces:', '["Increased fertility","Hypergonadism","Precocious puberty","Early menopause","Galactorrhea and hypogonadism"]'::jsonb, 'Galactorrhea and hypogonadism', 'Hyperprolactinemia stimulates lactogenesis, causing galactorrhea, while suppressing GnRH and gonadotropin secretion, which produces hypogonadism with amenorrhea and infertility.'),
('d4461fa2-f501-5807-afd6-9e3360e1277e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'In the study of Endocrinology Pt2, Regarding clinical cases in Endocrinology Pt1: St. John''s Wort induces:', '["CYP3A4 and P-gp","CYP2D6","CYP2C9","CYP1A2","CYP2C19"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4 and P-gp', 'St. John''s Wort induces CYP3A4 and P-gp, reducing drug levels.'),
('3d1cdadc-fa99-5b49-8be2-5b2873934026'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Endocrinology Pt2: Hashimoto thyroiditis is characterized as which of the following?', '["Viral thyroiditis","Autoimmune hyperthyroidism","Autoimmune hypothyroidism","Bacterial thyroiditis","Subacute thyroiditis"]'::jsonb, 'Autoimmune hypothyroidism', 'Hashimoto thyroiditis is an autoimmune disorder in which autoantibodies progressively destroy the thyroid gland, causing hypothyroidism. It is the most common cause of hypothyroidism in iodine-sufficient regions.'),
('e73e0f5f-54b0-5738-87d0-d28127027bd2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Endocrinology Pt2: Androgen-binding protein (ABP) is produced by:', '["Sertoli cells (under FSH)","Spermatogonia","Leydig cells","Epididymis","Prostate"]'::jsonb, 'Sertoli cells (under FSH)', 'Androgen-binding protein is produced by Sertoli cells in response to FSH. It binds testosterone and concentrates it within the seminiferous tubules for spermatogenesis.'),
('f3f696ee-9135-5914-b523-3997139485f5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of Endocrinology Pt2, Radioactive iodine (I-131) is used to treat:', '["Hashimoto resulting in decreased functional activity","Thyroid cancer accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hyperthyroidism (Graves) mediated by specific receptor activation","Both hyperthyroidism and thyroid cancer","Hypothyroidism leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Both hyperthyroidism and thyroid cancer', 'I-131 is concentrated by thyroid tissue and emits beta radiation that destroys follicular cells, treating both hyperthyroidism and differentiated thyroid cancer.'),
('a25ae4b0-ff09-5197-b8cb-28484103f019'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Endocrinology Pt2: Type 2 diabetes mellitus is characterized by which of the following?', '["Insulin resistance with relative insulin deficiency","Absolute insulin deficiency","Pancreatic enzyme deficiency","Autoimmune beta cell destruction","Glucagon excess"]'::jsonb, 'Insulin resistance with relative insulin deficiency', 'Type 2 diabetes is characterized by insulin resistance in peripheral tissues combined with relative insulin deficiency as beta-cell function declines.'),
('b5b731b5-2365-562b-a66e-3617f62727d9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'The thyroid gland produces which hormone?', '["Cortisol","Insulin","Thyroxine (T4)","Growth hormone","Prolactin"]'::jsonb, 'Thyroxine (T4)', 'Thyroid follicular cells synthesize thyroxine (T4) and triiodothyronine (T3); cortisol comes from the adrenal cortex, insulin from pancreatic beta cells, and growth hormone and prolactin from the anterior pituitary.'),
('9bcfe983-94c3-5dff-9bb1-2d637d822bb2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Endocrinology Pt2: Thyroid hormone actions include all EXCEPT:', '["Increased thermogenesis","Increased protein catabolism","Increased heart rate and contractility","Increased basal metabolic rate","Decreased oxygen consumption"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased oxygen consumption', 'Thyroid hormone increases oxygen consumption in most tissues, so decreased oxygen consumption is not one of its actions; the other listed effects are all produced by thyroid hormone.'),
('6dcd8fb2-4fd2-5167-8f0b-b04d4f3d53ae'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Endocrinology Pt2: Addison disease is caused by which of the following?', '["Adrenal medulla insufficiency","Hypothalamic insufficiency","Adrenal cortex insufficiency","Pituitary insufficiency","Thyroid insufficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Adrenal cortex insufficiency', 'Addison disease (primary adrenal insufficiency) is caused by destruction or dysfunction of the adrenal cortex, leading to deficiency of cortisol and aldosterone.'),
('d7dfb0aa-d551-5fc2-b481-03748eb12fa1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of Endocrinology Pt2, From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Endocrinology Pt1: Acetylation polymorphism affects:', '["Isoniazid, hydralazine, procainamide","Warfarin, phenytoin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Codeine, tramadol mediated by specific receptor activation","Omeprazole without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Diazepam leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Isoniazid, hydralazine, procainamide', 'NAT2 polymorphism: slow vs fast acetylators.'),
('7b5390ea-3b2c-5426-a097-98b5841055f5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Endocrinology Pt2: Which hypothalamic hormone stimulates the release of both FSH and LH from the anterior pituitary?', '["GnRH (Gonadotropin-releasing hormone)","TRH (Thyrotropin-releasing hormone)","CRH (Corticotropin-releasing hormone)","GHRH (Growth hormone-releasing hormone)","Somatostatin"]'::jsonb, 'GnRH (Gonadotropin-releasing hormone)', 'GnRH from the hypothalamus stimulates gonadotrophs of the anterior pituitary to release both FSH and LH, which then regulate gonadal steroidogenesis and gametogenesis.'),
('7acd7842-e7ce-5c44-9e43-c2527048e92a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Which gland is known as the master gland of the endocrine system?', '["Adrenal gland","Thyroid gland","Pineal gland","Hypothalamus","Pituitary gland"]'::jsonb, 'Pituitary gland', 'The anterior pituitary is called the master gland because it secretes tropic hormones (e.g., TSH, ACTH, FSH, LH) that regulate the activity of other endocrine glands.'),
('41de4729-02fe-5099-ab57-68cd0540e624'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Endocrinology Pt2: Large Vd means drug is:', '["Distributed into tissues","Confined to plasma","Rapidly excreted","Bound to albumin","Hepatically metabolised"]'::jsonb, 'Distributed into tissues', 'Large Vd >1 L/kg indicates extensive tissue distribution.'),
('ba7f9be1-fc6c-57c7-a569-6d4ea0077ae6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'The adrenal medulla secretes which substance?', '["Aldosterone","ACTH","Renin","Epinephrine","Cortisol"]'::jsonb, 'Epinephrine', 'The adrenal medulla is part of the sympathetic nervous system and secretes catecholamines, chiefly epinephrine with smaller amounts of norepinephrine. Aldosterone and cortisol come from the adrenal cortex.'),
('689d7377-65de-53b1-b264-8ccc8c4655fe'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of Endocrinology Pt2, Each of the following is a chemical class of hormones except:', '["Peptide/protein","Steroid","Nucleotide","Eicosanoid","Amine"]'::jsonb, 'Nucleotide', 'Peptide/protein, steroid, and amine hormones, along with eicosanoid lipid signaling molecules, are recognized chemical classes of hormones. Nucleotides are components of nucleic acids and do not function as hormones.'),
('503df4a9-e419-5aae-8d89-80543b187a6a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Antidiuretic hormone (ADH) is produced in which part of the brain?', '["Cerebellum","Posterior pituitary","Hypothalamus","Anterior pituitary","Medulla oblongata"]'::jsonb, 'Hypothalamus', 'ADH (vasopressin) is synthesized in the supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei of the hypothalamus and stored in the posterior pituitary before release.'),
('25e446e6-3bcd-56af-99ed-0fb7f8c1f845'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Which hormone increases blood calcium levels?', '["Thyroxine","Renin","Estrogen","Calcitonin","Parathyroid hormone"]'::jsonb, 'Parathyroid hormone', 'Parathyroid hormone raises serum calcium by stimulating osteoclast resorption, renal calcium reabsorption, and renal activation of vitamin D. Calcitonin lowers serum calcium.'),
('f9e12678-d1c7-5475-a321-1fca1d43f89e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Endocrinology Pt2: GH excess in adults causes:', '["Gigantism","Cushing syndrome","Acromegaly","Dwarfism","Hyperthyroidism"]'::jsonb, 'Acromegaly', 'GH excess after epiphyseal closure cannot increase height but causes acromegaly, characterized by growth of the hands, feet, and facial bones.'),
('05ebb92b-918a-5601-bde5-1c43fd387a5c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Endocrinology Pt2: Thyroid hormone is essential for which of the following?', '["Reproduction only","Digestion","Muscle growth only","Bone growth only","Fetal brain development"]'::jsonb, 'Fetal brain development', 'Thyroid hormone is indispensable for fetal and early postnatal brain development; deficiency during this period causes irreversible intellectual disability.'),
('e4a72143-bac6-54b2-902d-e3d1f4ff3d63'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of Endocrinology Pt2, GH excess in children causes:', '["Acromegaly","Dwarfism","Precocious puberty","Cushing syndrome","Gigantism"]'::jsonb, 'Gigantism', 'Excess GH before epiphyseal closure causes proportionate overgrowth of long bones, producing gigantism.'),
('52f6879b-8138-5dfd-adb1-2965f0803821'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'The most common cause of primary hypothyroidism is:', '["Radiation","Post-surgical","Congenital","Hashimoto thyroiditis","Iodine deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Hashimoto thyroiditis', 'In iodine-sufficient regions, autoimmune Hashimoto thyroiditis is the most common cause of primary hypothyroidism; iodine deficiency predominates only where dietary iodine is inadequate.'),
('666564e2-e90f-5c85-81e2-fb9ed25ff16e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'In the study of Endocrinology Pt2, During therapeutic management of Endocrinology Pt1, Loading dose primarily depends on:', '["Volume of distribution","Clearance","Half-life","Bioavailability","Renal function"]'::jsonb, 'Volume of distribution', 'Loading dose = Vd x Css / F.'),
('ce5106b1-8a23-59dc-afbe-66cf209efc8e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Endocrinology Pt2: Which hypothalamic factor normally inhibits prolactin secretion?', '["VIP resulting in decreased functional activity","Serotonin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dopamine from hypothalamus","Estrogen without affecting the primary tissue pathway","TRH leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dopamine from hypothalamus', 'Dopamine released by tuberoinfundibular neurons of the hypothalamus is the principal prolactin-inhibiting factor (PIF). Loss of this inhibition raises prolactin and can cause galactorrhea.'),
('31e6c5bc-aa4b-5a89-bfdd-0fdfba1bb750'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Endocrinology Pt2: The liver degrades most:', '["Steroid hormones (conjugation)","Peptide hormones (proteolysis)","Thyroid hormones (deiodination)","None of the above","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'The liver is the principal site of hormone inactivation: it conjugates steroids, proteolytically degrades peptide hormones, and deiodinates thyroid hormones during their clearance.'),
('005ccdcc-1edf-529b-9300-5552e95e7d22'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Which of the following statements about corticosteroid synthesis is correct?', '["21-hydroxylase converts progesterone to aldosterone","17-alpha-hydroxylase converts cholesterol to pregnenolone","Side-chain cleavage (CYP11A1) converts 11-deoxycortisol to cortisol","11-beta-hydroxylase converts 11-deoxycorticosterone to corticosterone","11-beta-hydroxylase converts 11-deoxycortisol to cortisol"]'::jsonb, '11-beta-hydroxylase converts 11-deoxycortisol to cortisol', '11-beta-hydroxylase (CYP11B1) catalyzes the final step of cortisol synthesis, converting 11-deoxycortisol to cortisol; defects produce virilization with hypertension.'),
('44407d6e-e15e-5f66-af2f-b7f8326c3cb4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Endocrinology Pt2: Which hormone is released by the posterior pituitary?', '["FSH and LH","ACTH","Oxytocin and ADH","TSH","Growth hormone"]'::jsonb, 'Oxytocin and ADH', 'The posterior pituitary stores and releases oxytocin and vasopressin (ADH), which are synthesized in the hypothalamus. FSH, LH, ACTH, TSH, and growth hormone are secreted by the anterior pituitary.'),
('2cfb32db-af3e-5d7e-abff-49a2339649f3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Which enzyme catalyzes the conversion of cholesterol to pregnenolone in steroidogenesis?', '["11-beta-hydroxylase","Side-chain cleavage (CYP11A1)","17-alpha-hydroxylase","21-hydroxylase","3-beta-HSD"]'::jsonb, 'Side-chain cleavage (CYP11A1)', 'Cholesterol side-chain cleavage (CYP11A1) removes a six-carbon fragment from cholesterol to form pregnenolone, the rate-limiting first step shared by all steroidogenic tissues.'),
('08891e3a-8517-568f-8be2-0d44c83183ce'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'A 30-year-old female presents with central obesity, moon facies, and purple striae. Which finding best distinguishes Cushing disease from an adrenal adenoma?', '["Elevated serum calcium","Low ACTH with a cortisol-producing adenoma","Elevated ACTH of pituitary origin","Suppressed TSH","Elevated potassium"]'::jsonb, 'Elevated ACTH of pituitary origin', 'Cushing disease is ACTH-dependent pituitary hypercortisolism, so ACTH is normal or elevated; an adrenal adenoma autonomously secretes cortisol and suppresses ACTH to low or undetectable levels.'),
('59ef2e63-933d-5efc-9a4d-ea7e47f009b4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Which cholesterol side-chain cleavage product initiates the steroidogenic pathway in the adrenal cortex?', '["21-hydroxylase","17-alpha-hydroxylase","Pregnenolone","11-beta-hydroxylase","3-beta-HSD"]'::jsonb, 'Pregnenolone', 'CYP11A1 side-chain cleavage converts cholesterol to pregnenolone, the common precursor from which all adrenal mineralocorticoids, glucocorticoids, and androgens are derived.'),
('649505c2-a39c-5455-831d-be75d35bddce'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Cholesterol side-chain cleavage (CYP11A1) primarily acts:', '["21-hydroxylase","17-alpha-hydroxylase","In the mitochondrial inner membrane","11-beta-hydroxylase","3-beta-HSD"]'::jsonb, 'In the mitochondrial inner membrane', 'CYP11A1, part of the StAR-facilitated cholesterol import pathway, is located on the inner mitochondrial membrane, where it converts cholesterol to pregnenolone.'),
('1ccc3916-ecfe-5347-b187-f2196a9be3e4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Cholesterol side-chain cleavage (CYP11A1) is the rate-limiting step directly regulated by:', '["Thyroxine","Prolactin","ACTH and LH","Oxytocin","Calcitonin"]'::jsonb, 'ACTH and LH', 'ACTH stimulates adrenal steroidogenesis and LH stimulates gonadal steroidogenesis by increasing StAR expression and CYP11A1 activity, the rate-limiting step.'),
('acf8d633-d493-574b-adfa-7365d0d41ddd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'In the study of Endocrinology Pt2, Which of the following is the molecular structure of human growth hormone?', '["Single chain polypeptide (191 aa)","Dipeptide accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Disulfide-linked dimer mediated by specific receptor activation","Glycoprotein without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Steroid leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Single chain polypeptide (191 aa)', 'Human growth hormone is a single-chain, non-glycosylated polypeptide of 191 amino acids secreted by somatotrophs. It is structurally homologous to prolactin and human placental lactogen.'),
('4e8325d1-64b7-54d1-bd76-5d3e224c36b2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'A 55-year-old male presents with central obesity, moon facies, and purple striae. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Albumin","Cushing syndrome","Corticosteroid-binding globulin (CBG/transcortin)","TBG","SHBG"]'::jsonb, 'Cushing syndrome', 'Central obesity, moon facies, and purple striae are classic clinical features of Cushing syndrome, which results from chronic glucocorticoid excess. The other options are plasma steroid-binding proteins and not diagnoses.'),
('e7914dcc-c122-54d4-a8e3-a9a17b813608'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Endocrinology Pt2: TSH is secreted by which cell type of the anterior pituitary?', '["Lactotrophs","Corticotrophs","Thyrotrophs","Gonadotrophs","Somatotrophs"]'::jsonb, 'Thyrotrophs', 'Thyrotrophs are the anterior pituitary cells that synthesize and secrete TSH in response to TRH from the hypothalamus. TSH then stimulates the thyroid gland to produce and release thyroid hormones.'),
('b2a45ecb-9514-5c16-9130-d09e077177a1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Which organ produces corticosteroid-binding globulin (CBG)?', '["Pituitary","Adrenal","Hypothalamus","Kidney","Liver"]'::jsonb, 'Liver', 'CBG is synthesized and secreted by the liver, like most plasma-binding proteins. The pituitary, adrenal, hypothalamus, and kidney do not produce CBG.'),
('25321919-3b1e-542b-838b-259512e5449f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Endocrinology Pt2: V1 receptors for ADH mediate:', '["Vasodilation (relaxation of vascular smooth muscle)","Water reabsorption","Vasoconstriction (smooth muscle contraction)","Uterine contraction","Milk ejection"]'::jsonb, 'Vasoconstriction (smooth muscle contraction)', 'V1 receptors couple to the IP3/calcium pathway in vascular smooth muscle, producing vasoconstriction, whereas V2 receptors mediate water reabsorption.'),
('bec3b738-c62a-5f6d-b1d0-e889df52518c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'In the study of Endocrinology Pt2, Congenital hypothyroidism causes:', '["Cretinism (mental retardation + dwarfism)","Graves disease accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Myxedema mediated by specific receptor activation","Hashimoto without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Goiter leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cretinism (mental retardation + dwarfism)', 'Untreated congenital hypothyroidism causes cretinism, characterized by intellectual disability and impaired growth (dwarfism).'),
('24c8a17d-49c7-50d7-9efd-477662aeaf09'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Endocrinology Pt2: Highest capacity Phase II reaction:', '["Glucuronidation","Sulfation","Acetylation","Methylation","Glutathione conjugation"]'::jsonb, 'Glucuronidation', 'Due to abundant UDP-glucuronic acid cofactor.'),
('505e0b13-abbb-590e-8bf1-22ab430d012a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Endocrinology Pt2: TSH acts on thyroid via:', '["Tyrosine kinase","JAK-STAT","Guanylyl cyclase","GPCR -> Gs -> cAMP pathway","Phospholipase C"]'::jsonb, 'GPCR -> Gs -> cAMP pathway', 'The TSH receptor is aG protein-coupled receptor coupled to Gs, which activates adenylyl cyclase and the cAMP pathway in thyroid follicular cells.'),
('503f639d-5e12-581b-9a35-4b446104a809'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of Endocrinology Pt2, Major binding protein for acidic drugs:', '["Albumin","Alpha-1 acid glycoprotein","Globulin","Transferrin","Ceruloplasmin"]'::jsonb, 'Albumin', 'Albumin binds acidic (anionic) drugs.'),
('7a06a93b-448d-5a26-aa6f-f29bb9c5824f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Endocrinology Pt2: The liver degrades most:', '["Steroid hormones (conjugation)","Peptide hormones (proteolysis)","Thyroid hormones (deiodination)","None of the above","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'The liver is the principal site of hormone inactivation: it conjugates steroids, proteolytically degrades peptide hormones, and deiodinates thyroid hormones during their clearance.'),
('ef1f353c-659e-56d2-aeae-f28036c29a60'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'In the study of Endocrinology Pt2, During therapeutic management of Endocrinology Pt1, Phase I metabolism reaction:', '["Oxidation","Glucuronidation","Sulfation","Acetylation","Glutathione conjugation"]'::jsonb, 'Oxidation', 'Phase I: oxidation, reduction, hydrolysis.'),
('2fd4208f-5c4b-54db-90b8-65b0de87f1d0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Endocrinology Pt2: The hypothalamus is connected to the posterior pituitary via which structure?', '["Blood vessels resulting in decreased functional activity","Capillaries accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hypothalamic-hypophyseal tract (axons)","Portal veins without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lymphatics leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hypothalamic-hypophyseal tract (axons)', 'The hypothalamus projects axons through the infundibulum to form the hypothalamic-hypophyseal tract, which transports ADH and oxytocin to the posterior pituitary for storage and release.'),
('d2ec472d-681c-5956-a38f-ad1d0b4f537d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Endocrinology Pt2: FSH in males stimulates:', '["Erection","Semen production","Testosterone production","Leydig cell function","Spermatogenesis (via Sertoli cells)"]'::jsonb, 'Spermatogenesis (via Sertoli cells)', 'FSH binds receptors on Sertoli cells to support spermatogenesis and stimulate secretion of androgen-binding protein and inhibin. It does not directly stimulate Leydig cells.'),
('bc912b9b-0707-56ec-9203-af23bf30cb6d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of Endocrinology Pt2, What is the earliest stage of organ dysfunction in the endocrine dysfunction continuum?', '["Decompensated function","Early dysfunction","Functional reserve depletion","Moderate impairment","Maladaptive response"]'::jsonb, 'Early dysfunction', 'Early dysfunction is the first stage of the continuum, marked by impaired homeostatic regulation with subclinical changes before overt symptoms.'),
('0aab902c-49f9-5280-bcac-4a217a900271'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'What is the pituitary source of excess ACTH in Cushing disease?', '["Adrenal adenoma resulting in decreased functional activity","Ectopic ACTH accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pituitary adenoma (Cushing disease)","Adrenal carcinoma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Iatrogenic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pituitary adenoma (Cushing disease)', 'The excess ACTH in Cushing disease is produced by a corticotroph adenoma of the anterior pituitary. Adrenal adenoma and carcinoma produce cortisol rather than ACTH, and ectopic ACTH arises outside the pituitary.'),
('a4d5b7ee-0fb0-5222-a9dd-288502006fe3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 2', 'In the study of Endocrinology Pt2, V1 receptors for ADH mediate:', '["Vasodilation (relaxation of vascular smooth muscle)","Water reabsorption","Vasoconstriction (smooth muscle contraction)","Uterine contraction","Milk ejection"]'::jsonb, 'Vasoconstriction (smooth muscle contraction)', 'V1 receptors couple to the IP3/calcium pathway in vascular smooth muscle, producing vasoconstriction, whereas V2 receptors mediate water reabsorption.'),
('e4b38896-2edd-5318-a6a2-8d54a6306a38'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Spermatogenesis occurs in:', '["Seminal vesicles","Prostate","Epididymis","Seminiferous tubules of testes","Vas deferens"]'::jsonb, 'Seminiferous tubules of testes', 'Spermatogenesis occurs within the seminiferous tubules of the testes, where Sertoli cells support the developing germ cells.'),
('b4718c93-de90-529b-b369-cd0064d26637'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'In the study of Exercise Environment, Warfarin primarily metabolised by:', '["CYP2C9","CYP3A4","CYP2D6","CYP2C19","CYP1A2"]'::jsonb, 'CYP2C9', 'CYP2C9 metabolises the more potent S-warfarin.'),
('11e12c01-a6c3-5fc0-8c0e-5b0378d692a3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Spermatogonia are:', '["Spermatozoa","Haploid germ cells","Diploid stem cells (2n)","Leydig cells","Spermatids"]'::jsonb, 'Diploid stem cells (2n)', 'Spermatogonia are the diploid (2n) germline stem cells of the seminiferous tubules that undergo mitosis to maintain the germ cell population.'),
('32a04860-22b6-5185-ace1-96790e27f9df'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'What is the correct sequence of germ cell divisions from spermatogonium to mature spermatozoon?', '["Meiosis only resulting in decreased functional activity","Meiosis I only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mitosis -> Meiosis I only mediated by specific receptor activation","Mitosis -> Meiosis I -> Meiosis II -> Spermiogenesis","Mitosis only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mitosis -> Meiosis I -> Meiosis II -> Spermiogenesis', 'Spermatogonia proliferate by mitosis, then undergo meiosis I and meiosis II to form haploid spermatids, which are remodeled into spermatozoa by spermiogenesis.'),
('c22222df-3f94-5e41-b893-c481da9cb86c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Spermatids are:', '["Stem cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Diploid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Secondary spermatocytes mediated by specific receptor activation","Haploid cells that undergo spermiogenesis (no division)","Spermatozoa leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Haploid cells that undergo spermiogenesis (no division)', 'Spermatids are haploid cells that mature into spermatozoa through spermiogenesis without undergoing further cell division.'),
('2c74b9c7-1f50-5d44-a657-ea88f24025db'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Spermiogenesis is the process of:', '["Sperm motility resulting in decreased functional activity","Spermatid differentiation into mature sperm","Sperm cell division mediated by specific receptor activation","Sperm capacitation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sperm storage leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Spermatid differentiation into mature sperm', 'Spermiogenesis is the differentiation of spermatids into mature spermatozoa, involving acrosome formation and flagellar development.'),
('86d02160-274a-5a60-9683-050c6049d395'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Sertoli cells, the supporting cells of the testis, are located:', '["In epididymis resulting in decreased functional activity","In seminal vesicles accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Between tubules mediated by specific receptor activation","In seminiferous tubules (supporting cells)","In prostate leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'In seminiferous tubules (supporting cells)', 'Sertoli cells are the supporting cells lining the seminiferous tubules, where they nurture developing germ cells.'),
('8d3029dd-018f-5d27-846c-bf997556ffb7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Which of the following is a function of Leydig cells?', '["Secrete inhibin and androgen-binding protein (ABP)","Produce testosterone","Phagocytose residual bodies","Form blood-testis barrier","Support spermatogenesis"]'::jsonb, 'Produce testosterone', 'Leydig cells, stimulated by LH, synthesize testosterone; the remaining functions are carried out by Sertoli cells.'),
('aea6ecff-a4b9-571b-b793-e539b39a2150'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'The blood-testis barrier is formed by:', '["Tight junctions between Sertoli cells","Endothelial cells","Basement membrane","Peritubular myoid cells","Leydig cells"]'::jsonb, 'Tight junctions between Sertoli cells', 'Tight junctions between adjacent Sertoli cells create the blood-testis barrier, protecting developing germ cells from the immune system.'),
('16dd104e-1698-56d9-8d34-2532ccd5508c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Which cells in the seminiferous tubule support developing germ cells and provide immune protection?', '["Epididymis","Leydig cells","Prostate","Spermatogonia","Sertoli cells"]'::jsonb, 'Sertoli cells', 'Sertoli cells support developing germ cells and form the blood-testis barrier, protecting them from immune attack.'),
('f6541bd6-d57f-56ba-b8d6-1d38fdc43de7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Where are Leydig cells located?', '["In epididymis","In seminiferous tubules","In interstitial space between tubules","In prostate","In vas deferens"]'::jsonb, 'In interstitial space between tubules', 'Leydig cells occupy the interstitial space between seminiferous tubules, where they secrete testosterone.'),
('12e636b0-83ba-5e8c-a90c-30de51f561db'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'LH stimulates testosterone production via:', '["Tyrosine kinase","Nuclear receptor","IP3/Ca2+","JAK-STAT","GPCR -> Gs -> cAMP"]'::jsonb, 'GPCR -> Gs -> cAMP', 'LH binds its G-protein-coupled receptor on Leydig cells, activating Gs to raise cAMP, which drives steroidogenesis.'),
('24e70035-0d42-59b7-83ba-5e1136126b26'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'FSH acts on Sertoli cells to:', '["Secrete seminal fluid resulting in decreased functional activity","Produce sperm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Support spermatogenesis and produce ABP/inhibin","Store sperm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Produce testosterone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Support spermatogenesis and produce ABP/inhibin', 'FSH stimulates Sertoli cells to support spermatogenesis and to secrete androgen-binding protein and inhibin.'),
('d7dc6e22-b3c8-5aea-a68b-2f0481715ef6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Testosterone is converted to DHT (dihydrotestosterone) by:', '["P450c17","3-beta-HSD","Aromatase","17-beta-HSD","5-alpha-reductase"]'::jsonb, '5-alpha-reductase', '5-alpha-reductase converts testosterone to dihydrotestosterone (DHT) in target tissues.'),
('358a9e44-2704-521e-a385-5be2fce96d29'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'DHT is more potent than testosterone because of its:', '["Higher affinity for androgen receptor","More secretion","Higher concentration","Less protein binding","Longer half-life"]'::jsonb, 'Higher affinity for androgen receptor', 'DHT binds the androgen receptor with higher affinity than testosterone, accounting for its greater potency at target tissues.'),
('a762c2f7-dc4b-58a9-a23b-8dbc5508b54a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Testosterone is also converted to estradiol by:', '["P450c17","Aromatase (P450arom)","3-beta-HSD","17-beta-HSD","5-alpha-reductase"]'::jsonb, 'Aromatase (P450arom)', 'Aromatase (P450arom) catalyzes the conversion of testosterone to estradiol.'),
('fdeb980d-00b1-5398-b422-0c31bb133988'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'SHBG is produced by:', '["Liver","Hypothalamus","Testes","Kidney","Adrenal"]'::jsonb, 'Liver', 'Sex hormone-binding globulin (SHBG) is synthesized by the liver.'),
('73ca937e-be55-5636-b577-02e2fdef0db3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Which of the following correctly describes the hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal (HPG) axis in males?', '["GnRH -> LH (Leydig cells) and FSH (Sertoli cells) -> testosterone -> feedback","GnRH -> prolactin -> testosterone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","GnRH -> ACTH -> testosterone mediated by specific receptor activation","GnRH -> TSH -> testosterone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Direct neural control leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'GnRH -> LH (Leydig cells) and FSH (Sertoli cells) -> testosterone -> feedback', 'In males, GnRH stimulates LH (acting on Leydig cells to produce testosterone) and FSH (acting on Sertoli cells); testosterone and inhibin then exert negative feedback.'),
('c833ae4b-8b41-5747-ab7c-a57f0fb83848'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Testosterone exerts negative feedback on:', '["Hypothalamus (GnRH) and anterior pituitary (LH)","Only on gonads","Only on hypothalamus","Only on the anterior pituitary","Only on the adrenal cortex"]'::jsonb, 'Hypothalamus (GnRH) and anterior pituitary (LH)', 'Testosterone inhibits GnRH release from the hypothalamus and LH secretion from the anterior pituitary.'),
('22f7a9f6-a153-53c7-8ad3-18455b4bae62'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'What hormone from Sertoli cells inhibits FSH secretion via negative feedback?', '["Inhibin B","Testosterone","Anti-Müllerian hormone","Estradiol","Activin"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibin B', 'Inhibin B, secreted by Sertoli cells, selectively inhibits FSH release from the anterior pituitary.'),
('29f4fd66-7234-5a1b-b450-f64d283f53eb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'What is the master switch gene for male sex determination?', '["Autosomal genes resulting in decreased functional activity","X chromosome accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","SRY gene on Y chromosome -> testis-determining factor","Maternal hormones without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Placental hormones leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'SRY gene on Y chromosome -> testis-determining factor', 'SRY on the Y chromosome encodes testis-determining factor (TDF), which commits the indifferent gonad to testis development.'),
('c035cad9-3535-5fd6-8d66-0db2b3e6c784'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'What is the first step in male sex differentiation during embryonic development?', '["Development of testes from indifferent gonad","Leydig cell function","Sertoli cell function","Formation of external genitalia","Development of ovaries"]'::jsonb, 'Development of testes from indifferent gonad', 'The indifferent gonad develops into testes under SRY influence, with subsequent hormone-driven steps following.'),
('b4de4ee7-1a11-546c-bd9e-483932abcd66'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Anti-Müllerian hormone (AMH) is secreted by:', '["Adrenal resulting in decreased functional activity","Leydig cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ovaries mediated by specific receptor activation","Sertoli cells (fetal testes)","Placenta leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sertoli cells (fetal testes)', 'Fetal Sertoli cells secrete AMH, which induces regression of the paramesonephric (Müllerian) ducts in male embryos.'),
('f0db5f0a-a69e-5709-bb5f-45f5b0a1284c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'In male sexual differentiation, testosterone directly drives:', '["Neither","Mullerian regression","Wolffian duct development","External genitalia virilization (via DHT)","AMH secretion"]'::jsonb, 'Wolffian duct development', 'Testosterone promotes Wolffian (mesonephric) duct development into the epididymis, vas deferens, and seminal vesicles; Mullerian regression is driven by AMH, and virilization of the external genitalia is DHT-mediated.'),
('7a853809-74c9-5504-bd29-ca0947950d28'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'DHT is responsible for:', '["Testicular descent resulting in decreased functional activity","Development of male external genitalia and prostate","Spermatogenesis mediated by specific receptor activation","Wolffian duct development without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mullerian regression leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Development of male external genitalia and prostate', 'DHT mediates development of the male external genitalia and prostate, while testosterone acting directly promotes Wolffian duct structures.'),
('97239a68-7372-5dcf-b23c-292dab1e80c5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Androgen insensitivity syndrome (complete AIS) is due to:', '["SRY mutation","5-alpha-reductase deficiency","AMH deficiency","LH receptor mutation","Androgen receptor mutation (XR)"]'::jsonb, 'Androgen receptor mutation (XR)', 'Complete AIS results from an X-linked mutation of the androgen receptor, causing end-organ androgen resistance.'),
('8e0bbe02-7321-5b41-aaca-10be7f50b57e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Cryptorchidism refers to:', '["Undescended testis","Absent testis","Varicocele","Hydrocele","Testicular tumor"]'::jsonb, 'Undescended testis', 'Cryptorchidism is failure of one or both testes to descend into the scrotum.'),
('a0be8158-c089-560e-b74d-d9bd2d917c0f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'What is the embryological origin of the fallopian tubes and uterus?', '["Vas deferens resulting in decreased functional activity","Epididymis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Testis mediated by specific receptor activation","Seminal vesicles without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Paramesonephric (Müllerian) ducts"]'::jsonb, 'Paramesonephric (Müllerian) ducts', 'The fallopian tubes, uterus, and upper vagina develop from the paramesonephric (Müllerian) ducts.'),
('d83a4d20-b727-5a96-8089-a3a2861d1e55'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Which accessory gland contributes approximately 60% of the ejaculate volume?', '["Epididymis","Prostate","Seminal vesicles","Bulbourethral glands","Testis"]'::jsonb, 'Seminal vesicles', 'The seminal vesicles contribute about 60% of the ejaculate volume, with the prostate contributing approximately 30%.'),
('20e96eab-17d6-52c6-9c72-63a0093b0ab0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'PDE5 inhibitors (sildenafil) work by:', '["Blocking PDE5 -> increased cGMP","Increasing cAMP","Increasing PDE5 activity","Blocking nitric oxide release","Blocking calcium"]'::jsonb, 'Blocking PDE5 -> increased cGMP', 'Sildenafil inhibits PDE5, the enzyme that degrades cGMP, thereby increasing cGMP and relaxing penile vascular smooth muscle.'),
('3da72aa5-65ec-50e4-aa5e-bc3215ea2515'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'What is the function of sympathetic nerves (T10-L2) in the male reproductive system?', '["Contraction of reproductive organs during ejaculation","Erection of the penis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sperm production in testes mediated by specific receptor activation","Testosterone secretion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Prostate gland growth leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Contraction of reproductive organs during ejaculation', 'Sympathetic fibers cause contraction of the vas deferens, seminal vesicles, and prostate to expel semen during ejaculation.'),
('991f930b-ff4f-5fed-b547-7e6b3e4fc603'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Male fertility requires all EXCEPT:', '["Normal spermatogenesis","High testosterone only","Sperm motility","Normal accessory gland function","Patent reproductive ducts"]'::jsonb, 'High testosterone only', 'Fertility requires normal spermatogenesis, sperm motility, patent ducts, and accessory gland function; an elevated testosterone level alone is not sufficient.'),
('ecbc5165-3d8c-5c20-8edc-d14ce5ea9945'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Sperm count normally is:', '["5 million/mL resulting in decreased functional activity",">1 million/mL accompanied by associated clinical symptoms",">15 million/mL (>39 million per ejaculate)",">100 million/mL without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Any number leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '>15 million/mL (>39 million per ejaculate)', 'The WHO defines normal sperm concentration as at least 15 million/mL, corresponding to at least 39 million sperm per ejaculate.'),
('6d4c9032-fbb4-5020-b91a-1b5dad2a22ba'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Testosterone therapy causes:', '["Increased endogenous LH secretion","Increased testis size","Suppression of endogenous LH","Increased spermatogenesis","Increased fertility"]'::jsonb, 'Suppression of endogenous LH', 'Exogenous testosterone suppresses endogenous LH (and FSH) via negative feedback on the HPG axis, reducing testicular size and spermatogenesis.'),
('31aeed5e-d72a-59fb-92c5-e9c32a692a28'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'What is the mechanism of clomiphene in ovulation induction?', '["Androgen resulting in decreased functional activity","Aromatase inhibitor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Progestin mediated by specific receptor activation","SERM (estrogen antagonist) increasing GnRH/LH/FSH","GnRH agonist leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'SERM (estrogen antagonist) increasing GnRH/LH/FSH', 'Clomiphene blocks estrogen negative feedback at the hypothalamus, increasing GnRH, LH, and FSH to stimulate follicular development.'),
('ca2bb75a-ea24-5596-aa00-8b228c1376be'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'The female reproductive cycle lasts approximately:', '["40 days","28 days (21-35 days)","7 days","14 days","90 days"]'::jsonb, '28 days (21-35 days)', 'The female reproductive cycle averages 28 days, with a normal range of about 21-35 days.'),
('5a1e907b-5899-5d1c-b2b6-584e1a8f65f3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Which hormone is dominant during the follicular phase of the menstrual cycle?', '["Estrogen","Progesterone","FSH","LH","Inhibin"]'::jsonb, 'Estrogen', 'Estrogen dominates the follicular phase, promoting endometrial proliferation as follicles mature.'),
('90e615db-4329-5b37-a5e8-7306ac635acf'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'What happens during the menstrual phase of the menstrual cycle?', '["Shedding of the endometrial lining","Ovulation occurs","Endometrial proliferation","Corpus luteum formation","Peak LH surge"]'::jsonb, 'Shedding of the endometrial lining', 'The menstrual phase is marked by shedding of the functional endometrial layer due to withdrawal of progesterone and estrogen.'),
('e9a9c885-583d-52fe-a04b-f1d54630ba46'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'The follicular phase is characterized by:', '["Menstruation","Low estrogen","Growth of ovarian follicles under FSH","Corpus luteum formation","High progesterone"]'::jsonb, 'Growth of ovarian follicles under FSH', 'The follicular phase is characterized by FSH-driven growth of ovarian follicles with progressively rising estrogen.'),
('5e7ccf19-9612-5b39-b811-f95f35059d14'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'FSH stimulates:', '["Corpus luteum resulting in decreased functional activity","Androgens accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ovulation mediated by specific receptor activation","Progesterone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Follicle growth and estrogen production (granulosa cells)"]'::jsonb, 'Follicle growth and estrogen production (granulosa cells)', 'FSH stimulates follicular growth and estrogen production by granulosa cells.'),
('12e204f3-4a44-5155-9ab8-8f71896fa797'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Ovulation occurs approximately:', '["Day 21 resulting in decreased functional activity","Day 28 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Day 1 of cycle mediated by specific receptor activation","Day 7 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Day 14 (in 28-day cycle)"]'::jsonb, 'Day 14 (in 28-day cycle)', 'In a 28-day cycle, ovulation occurs around day 14, following the LH surge.'),
('72b291c8-b8c6-51ad-848c-1170a4e04eb0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'The LH surge is caused by:', '["Low FSH resulting in decreased functional activity","High progesterone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","High inhibin mediated by specific receptor activation","Low estrogen without affecting the primary tissue pathway","High estrogen positive feedback (from dominant follicle)"]'::jsonb, 'High estrogen positive feedback (from dominant follicle)', 'Rising estrogen from the dominant follicle, exceeding about 200 pg/mL for 36-48 hours, switches hypothalamic-pituitary feedback to positive and triggers the mid-cycle LH surge that initiates ovulation.'),
('e23bf093-dea2-5b80-a2b3-3c35c3400891'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'A 25-year-old woman with premature ovarian failure develops hot flushes. These vasomotor symptoms are most directly due to:', '["Estrogen remains high for 36-48 hours above 200 pg/mL","Progesterone is high","LH is low","Prolactin is high","Estrogen is low"]'::jsonb, 'Estrogen is low', 'Hot flushes result from estrogen withdrawal, which destabilizes the hypothalamic thermoregulatory center. In premature ovarian failure, low estrogen with compensatory high gonadotropins produces these vasomotor symptoms.'),
('a1e69c10-d39a-59ec-a796-7286cf00c714'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'The luteal phase is characterized by:', '["Follicle growth resulting in decreased functional activity","Estrogen dominance accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ovulation mediated by specific receptor activation","Corpus luteum secretion of progesterone (> estradiol)","Menstruation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Corpus luteum secretion of progesterone (> estradiol)', 'After ovulation the corpus luteum secretes progesterone in greater amounts than estradiol. This progesterone dominance defines the luteal (secretory) phase of the cycle.'),
('dcf9b476-730d-558f-905d-71a0e502f8d1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'The corpus luteum has a lifespan of:', '["28 days resulting in decreased functional activity","14 days (unless pregnancy occurs)","7 days mediated by specific receptor activation","1 day without affecting the primary tissue pathway","3 days leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '14 days (unless pregnancy occurs)', 'The corpus luteum functions for about 14 days. If pregnancy occurs, hCG rescues it; otherwise it regresses and menstruation follows.'),
('1f51b37b-f05c-5370-b874-a3c8ddf7b29e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Corpus luteum regression leads to:', '["Falling estrogen and progesterone -> menstruation","LH surge accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Continued cycle mediated by specific receptor activation","Ovulation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pregnancy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Falling estrogen and progesterone -> menstruation', 'Luteal regression removes progesterone and estradiol support, so the endometrium is shed. Withdrawal of these hormones triggers menstruation.'),
('e1f225e3-b516-5415-a0d7-fda5fed57a34'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Estrogen actions include all of the following EXCEPT:', '["Proliferative phase of endometrium","Increased HDL","Increased bone density","Uterine contraction inhibition (labor)","Breast duct growth"]'::jsonb, 'Uterine contraction inhibition (labor)', 'Estrogen stimulates endometrial proliferation, raises HDL, supports bone density, and promotes breast duct growth. Inhibition of uterine contractions is a progesterone effect, not an estrogen action.'),
('9113ea8d-38ba-5eaa-84a4-4d068cae2934'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Estrogen effects on the endometrium:', '["No significant effect resulting in decreased functional activity","Secretion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Shedding mediated by specific receptor activation","Proliferation (growth of glands and stroma)","Decidualization leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Proliferation (growth of glands and stroma)', 'Estrogen drives mitotic growth of the endometrial glands and stroma during the proliferative phase. Secretion and decidualization are progesterone effects, and shedding follows hormone withdrawal.'),
('68048638-7583-5fa8-b8e9-d7681ebf751f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Estrogen effects on the cervical mucus include:', '["Thick, sticky mucus","Scanty mucus","Ferning mucus (spinnbarkeit)","Non-ferning mucus","Opaque, cellular mucus"]'::jsonb, 'Ferning mucus (spinnbarkeit)', 'Estrogen makes cervical mucus thin, abundant, and clear, with stretchable, ferning properties (spinnbarkeit). The ferning pattern is characteristic of estrogen-dominated mucus, whereas progesterone produces thick, non-ferning mucus.'),
('f33d61ad-4e76-55d1-8780-896fe8d76fb2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Estrogen promotes which of the following effects on the breast?', '["Milk ejection","Alveolar (lobular) growth","Milk secretion","Montgomery gland secretion","Ductal growth and development"]'::jsonb, 'Ductal growth and development', 'Estrogen promotes proliferation of the breast ductal system. Progesterone supports alveolar (lobular) growth, prolactin drives milk secretion, and oxytocin mediates milk ejection.'),
('b57b3766-1988-5f98-a0c5-3e2f4ecda03c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Estrogen feedback on the hypothalamus/pituitary:', '["Positive resulting in decreased functional activity","Negative accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only negative mediated by specific receptor activation","At low levels: negative feedback; at high levels: positive feedback (LH surge)","No significant effect leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'At low levels: negative feedback; at high levels: positive feedback (LH surge)', 'At low circulating levels, estrogen exerts negative feedback on gonadotropin secretion. At the preovulatory peak, feedback becomes positive and triggers the LH surge.'),
('d02eed26-572a-52e4-a9b4-0b530dc0f62e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Progesterone actions include all of the following EXCEPT:', '["Inhibition of uterine contractions","Increased basal body temperature","Alveolar (lobular) breast development","Secretory phase endometrium","Proliferative endometrium"]'::jsonb, 'Proliferative endometrium', 'Progesterone inhibits uterine contractions, raises basal body temperature, supports alveolar breast development, and induces a secretory endometrium. Endometrial proliferation is an estrogen effect, so it is the exception.'),
('3626b045-0173-540f-8a88-181dfe27bc94'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Progesterone effects on the cervical mucus:', '["Thin and watery","Thick","Increased spinnbarkeit","Abundant, clear mucus","Ferning mucus"]'::jsonb, 'Thick', 'Progesterone makes cervical mucus thick, scant, and tacky, abolishing ferning and spinnbarkeit. This contrasts with the thin, stretchy mucus produced under estrogen dominance.'),
('49f8104f-c484-57f9-a22f-291a9ab31bc2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Progesterone effects on body temperature:', '["Decreases then increases resulting in decreased functional activity","Increases basal body temperature (0.5C)","Irregular mediated by specific receptor activation","Decreases it without affecting the primary tissue pathway","No change leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Increases basal body temperature (0.5C)', 'Progesterone acts on the hypothalamic thermoregulatory center to raise basal body temperature by about 0.5 degrees C. The sustained rise after ovulation is a marker of the luteal phase.'),
('daa741a3-6cab-5043-9912-21e56638c7c1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Progesterone effects on uterine contractility:', '["Stimulates contractions","Increases frequency","No significant effect","Inhibits contractions (quiets uterus)","Causes labor"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibits contractions (quiets uterus)', 'Progesterone relaxes myometrial smooth muscle and reduces uterine contractility, helping to maintain pregnancy. Withdrawal of progesterone at term contributes to the onset of labor.'),
('cfdd17c0-96a3-5dfd-8dc0-b42e5ffd9025'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'In the study of Reproduction, P-glycoprotein is an efflux transporter that:', '["Pumps drugs out of cells","Enhances absorption","Binds to proteins","Metabolises drugs","Excretes in bile only"]'::jsonb, 'Pumps drugs out of cells', 'P-gp efflux at intestine, kidney, and BBB limits drug absorption.'),
('ca523258-58c4-57c0-bc72-b10f5eea7ed1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Which of the following selectively inhibits FSH without suppressing LH?', '["Clomiphene citrate","Tamoxifen","Letrozole","GnRH agonist","Inhibin"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibin', 'Inhibin, secreted by granulosa cells, selectively suppresses FSH release from the anterior pituitary with little effect on LH. The listed drugs instead raise or suppress both gonadotropins.'),
('4266b255-8215-5aec-a3f0-d494551b6f02'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Which hormone stimulates follicular development and estrogen production?', '["FSH (Follicle-stimulating hormone)","LH (Luteinizing hormone) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Prolactin mediated by specific receptor activation","Oxytocin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Progesterone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'FSH (Follicle-stimulating hormone)', 'FSH binds granulosa cells of growing follicles, stimulating aromatization of androgens to estrogen and promoting follicular maturation toward ovulation.'),
('108037a6-a245-5833-a067-905f483b6f0f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'The development of a primordial follicle to the preovulatory stage takes approximately:', '["74 days","About 1 month","300 days (multiple cycles)","14 days","About 1 year"]'::jsonb, '300 days (multiple cycles)', 'Folliculogenesis from the primordial follicle to the preovulatory stage spans roughly 300 days (about 10 months), during which the follicle passes through several menstrual cycles.'),
('d1628fd6-d54f-56e8-a710-49d7a4796be1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Primordial follicles consist of:', '["Multiple granulosa layers resulting in decreased functional activity","Corona radiata accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Zona pellucida mediated by specific receptor activation","Oocyte surrounded by a single layer of flat granulosa cells","Antrum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Oocyte surrounded by a single layer of flat granulosa cells', 'Primordial follicles contain an oocyte arrested in prophase of meiosis I surrounded by a single layer of flattened granulosa cells.'),
('0cf48352-648d-5dd9-9304-30da0d7dad4c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'A primary follicle is characterized by:', '["Cumulus oophorus resulting in decreased functional activity","Corona radiata accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Theca externa mediated by specific receptor activation","Cuboidal granulosa cells (single or multiple layers)","Antrum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cuboidal granulosa cells (single or multiple layers)', 'A primary follicle is distinguished by cuboidal granulosa cells that may form one or more layers around the oocyte, replacing the flattened layer of the primordial follicle.'),
('bd0f9675-88a8-551b-b6e0-a8a768e98130'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'The antrum of a developing ovarian follicle is:', '["A fluid-filled cavity within the granulosa cell layers","Zona pellucida formation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Corpus luteum mediated by specific receptor activation","A solid cluster of theca cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Atresia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A fluid-filled cavity within the granulosa cell layers', 'The antrum is a fluid-filled cavity that appears as granulosa cells proliferate in a growing antral follicle, producing follicular fluid that contains the oocyte.'),
('5a6abaed-2e86-58ae-aa7c-93c4fee99de8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'What characterizes a large antral follicle ready to ovulate?', '["Dominated by a single follicle 20mm with high estrogen","Multiple small follicles accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Atretic follicles only mediated by specific receptor activation","Corpus luteum present without affecting the primary tissue pathway","No follicular development leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dominated by a single follicle 20mm with high estrogen', 'A preovulatory antral follicle grows to roughly 18-25 mm, secretes high estrogen, and becomes the single dominant follicle. This estrogen peak drives the LH surge that triggers ovulation.'),
('d67f3894-b5e0-5045-9bb9-db51d7a9509e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'What is the zona pellucida and what is its function?', '["Cumulus cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Jelly coat accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cellular layer mediated by specific receptor activation","Mucopolysaccharide without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Glycoprotein layer surrounding the oocyte"]'::jsonb, 'Glycoprotein layer surrounding the oocyte', 'The zona pellucida is a glycoprotein layer between the oocyte and the corona radiata that mediates sperm binding and triggers the acrosome reaction.'),
('a6d8cbf8-9e9d-5736-ae29-52c92094bd42'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'The cumulus oophorus is:', '["Antral fluid resulting in decreased functional activity","Zona pellucida accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Granulosa cells surrounding the oocyte","Theca cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Corona radiata leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Granulosa cells surrounding the oocyte', 'The cumulus oophorus is a mass of granulosa cells that surrounds and supports the oocyte within the antral follicle; its innermost cells form the corona radiata.'),
('64a2b6d3-5763-5a19-bb68-42eedda38caa'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'The corona radiata is:', '["Corpus luteum resulting in decreased functional activity","Innermost layer of cumulus cells directly investing the oocyte","Theca layer mediated by specific receptor activation","Antrum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Oolemma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Innermost layer of cumulus cells directly investing the oocyte', 'The corona radiata is the innermost layer of cumulus granulosa cells that directly invests the oocyte and is expelled with it at ovulation.'),
('e97f0e40-933b-5738-9416-02e9a39ec47b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'What is the process of ovarian follicle loss from birth to menopause?', '["Follicle death/degeneration (occurs throughout life)","Luteinization accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Follicle growth mediated by specific receptor activation","Ovulation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Follicle maturation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Follicle death/degeneration (occurs throughout life)', 'Most ovarian follicles undergo atresia, a process of degeneration, so that only about 400 oocytes ovulate during a reproductive lifetime. Atresia accounts for the continuous decline in the ovarian reserve.'),
('2f5439e8-aea7-5228-83f9-660ea6c1a898'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'At birth, the female ovary contains approximately how many oocytes?', '["About 400","About 1-2 million","About 10","About 400,000","About 10 million"]'::jsonb, 'About 1-2 million', 'At birth, each ovary contains about 1-2 million oocytes within primordial follicles. This number declines continuously through atresia to about 400,000 at menarche.'),
('408a17dd-9396-5c6b-aabc-3b9bd6f83c83'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Menarche is:', '["First menstruation (usually 12-13 years)","First ovulation","First follicle development","Puberty onset","Menopause onset"]'::jsonb, 'First menstruation (usually 12-13 years)', 'Menarche is the first menstrual period, typically occurring around 12-13 years of age, and marks the completion of pubertal maturation in girls.'),
('26ccded4-1aa0-58f2-adc3-da8074d9a5f0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Menopause is:', '["Loss of FSH resulting in decreased functional activity","Last ovulation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pregnancy loss mediated by specific receptor activation","Cessation of menstruation (average age 51 years)","Loss of LH leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cessation of menstruation (average age 51 years)', 'Menopause is the permanent cessation of menstruation resulting from ovarian follicular depletion, occurring at an average age of about 51 years.'),
('c6cbdedd-580f-53bf-b02e-551a7e06e7ff'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Perimenopause is:', '["Postpartum period resulting in decreased functional activity","Transition to menopause with irregular cycles","Pregnancy mediated by specific receptor activation","Sudden menopause without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Menarche leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Transition to menopause with irregular cycles', 'Perimenopause is the transitional period leading to menopause, marked by irregular cycles and fluctuating gonadotropin and steroid hormone levels.'),
('3ce2e174-5534-582b-b28f-7f4e62e569a5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'What causes the rise in basal body temperature during the luteal phase?', '["Progesterone from the corpus luteum","Estrogen from the follicle","LH surge","FSH elevation","Inhibin B"]'::jsonb, 'Progesterone from the corpus luteum', 'Progesterone secreted by the corpus luteum raises the set point of the hypothalamic thermoregulatory center, producing the rise in basal body temperature during the luteal phase.'),
('eff2bb6f-190c-5681-a85d-c964449dd9ca'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'A 45-year-old woman with confirmed menopause asks about hormone therapy. What is the primary indication for this treatment?', '["Fertility resulting in decreased functional activity","Menopausal symptom relief and osteoporosis prevention","Breast cancer prevention mediated by specific receptor activation","Contraception without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pregnancy maintenance leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Menopausal symptom relief and osteoporosis prevention', 'In menopausal women, hormone therapy is most effective for relieving vasomotor symptoms and preventing osteoporosis. It is not a treatment for infertility and does not prevent breast cancer.'),
('1a599f18-162c-5d7c-a783-42f2b25d0cbe'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Pubertal development in females begins with:', '["Adrenarche","Menarche","Growth spurt","Thelarche (breast budding)","Pubarche"]'::jsonb, 'Thelarche (breast budding)', 'In girls, puberty typically begins with thelarche, the appearance of breast buds, followed by adrenarche/pubarche, the growth spurt, and finally menarche.'),
('90e2812b-7777-5172-87b1-7b82c526b1bf'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'The correct pubertal sequence in females is:', '["Thelarche -> adrenarche/pubarche -> growth spurt -> menarche","Growth spurt -> thelarche accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pubarche -> menarche mediated by specific receptor activation","Adrenarche -> thelarche without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Menarche -> thelarche leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thelarche -> adrenarche/pubarche -> growth spurt -> menarche', 'The normal female pubertal sequence is thelarche, followed by adrenarche/pubarche, then the growth spurt, with menarche occurring last, usually about 2-2.5 years after thelarche.'),
('d01fff68-7af6-5051-a6e7-792ee99dd102'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Kallmann syndrome (hypogonadotropic hypogonadism) is due to:', '["Gonadal failure resulting in decreased functional activity","GnRH deficiency (failure of GnRH neuron migration)","Pituitary tumor mediated by specific receptor activation","LH deficiency without affecting the primary tissue pathway","FSH deficiency leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'GnRH deficiency (failure of GnRH neuron migration)', 'Kallmann syndrome results from failed migration of GnRH neurons to the hypothalamus, causing GnRH deficiency and hypogonadotropic hypogonadism, often accompanied by anosmia.'),
('06fa170d-38e2-5222-a4c7-02ef2981eb6a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'The most characteristic ovarian finding in ovarian dysgenesis (e.g., Turner syndrome) is:', '["Webbed neck","Streak gonads","Short stature","Normal ovaries","Male infertility"]'::jsonb, 'Streak gonads', 'Ovarian dysgenesis is characterized by streak gonads, which consist of fibrous stroma without follicles and cause hypergonadotropic hypogonadism. Webbed neck and short stature are associated somatic features.'),
('1dce2cfb-f367-57ac-8157-c3d422b89e07'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Which of the following is NOT a feature of polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS)?', '["Hyperandrogenism","Chronic anovulation","Polycystic ovarian morphology","Premature ovarian failure","Elevated LH:FSH ratio"]'::jsonb, 'Premature ovarian failure', 'PCOS is marked by hyperandrogenism, chronic anovulation, polycystic ovarian morphology, and an elevated LH:FSH ratio. Premature ovarian failure is a separate disorder with low estrogen and high gonadotropins.'),
('1a947ef2-5a18-537b-bb37-0f01ecf1b22f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Which condition is characterized by insulin resistance and hyperinsulinemia?', '["Polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS)","Turner syndrome","Klinefelter syndrome","Premature ovarian failure","Asherman syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'Polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS)', 'PCOS is frequently associated with insulin resistance and compensatory hyperinsulinemia, which stimulates ovarian androgen production and worsens the syndrome''s metabolic and reproductive features.'),
('44930e1b-ee28-5933-ad67-c7f308a0d760'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'The FSH:LH ratio in PCOS is:', '["Normal ratio","Equal","LH > FSH (ratio > 2-3:1)","LH < FSH","FSH > LH"]'::jsonb, 'LH > FSH (ratio > 2-3:1)', 'In PCOS, LH is elevated relative to FSH, often with an LH:FSH ratio greater than 2-3:1. The relatively low FSH impairs follicular maturation despite chronic anovulation.'),
('b102e2a9-f723-5e55-a3f7-2304707296a9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Premature ovarian failure (premature ovarian insufficiency) is:', '["PCOS","Menopause at normal age","Anovulation","Pregnancy loss","Ovarian failure before age 40"]'::jsonb, 'Ovarian failure before age 40', 'Premature ovarian insufficiency is defined as loss of ovarian function before age 40, presenting with amenorrhea, low estrogen, and elevated gonadotropins.'),
('5b5d6b80-c296-5cd9-bdf1-74df7cc38ce2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Fertilization of the oocyte normally occurs in the:', '["Uterus resulting in decreased functional activity","Vagina accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cervix mediated by specific receptor activation","Ovary without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ampulla of fallopian tube"]'::jsonb, 'Ampulla of fallopian tube', 'Fertilization normally occurs in the ampulla, the widest segment of the fallopian tube. The other listed structures are not the usual site of fertilization.'),
('b1e8c44f-9116-502b-af01-5fec0419b16c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'The acrosome reaction is triggered by:', '["Binding to zona pellucida (ZP3)","pH accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sperm motility mediated by specific receptor activation","Progesterone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Capacitation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Binding to zona pellucida (ZP3)', 'Binding of the sperm head to the ZP3 glycoprotein of the zona pellucida induces the acrosome reaction, releasing enzymes that allow the sperm to penetrate the zona. Capacitation occurs earlier, in the female tract, and merely renders the sperm capable of undergoing the reaction.'),
('9803cfdf-210a-5103-b6ca-c6c2bae4304d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'The cortical reaction prevents:', '["Polyspermy (blocks additional sperm entry)","Fertilization","Acrosome reaction","Zona pellucida formation","Sperm capacitation"]'::jsonb, 'Polyspermy (blocks additional sperm entry)', 'After the first sperm enters, cortical granules release enzymes that alter the zona pellucida so that no further sperm can bind or penetrate. This zona block prevents polyspermy, which would produce a nonviable embryo.'),
('48b66f47-9dee-55a9-b90b-775846ab8bff'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'The zona pellucida block to further sperm entry (cortical reaction) is established:', '["At ovulation resulting in decreased functional activity","At menopause accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","At birth mediated by specific receptor activation","After sperm penetration (fertilization)","At puberty leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'After sperm penetration (fertilization)', 'The zona pellucida block is established after the first sperm penetrates the oocyte, when the cortical reaction alters ZP3 so additional sperm cannot bind. This prevents polyspermy.'),
('fc985928-412e-50c7-9a8a-007e947c73f3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'When does the first cleavage division of the zygote normally occur?', '["After 1 hour","After 7 days","Immediately","After 3 weeks","24-30 hours after fertilization"]'::jsonb, '24-30 hours after fertilization', 'The zygote completes its first mitotic cleavage about 24-30 hours after fertilization, producing a 2-cell embryo. Subsequent divisions follow at progressively shorter intervals.'),
('4ef2ef44-4387-5956-9394-faf2bd4f9969'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'The morula stage is:', '["Embryo resulting in decreased functional activity","Blastocyst accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Solid ball of 16-32 cells (3-4 days after fertilization)","Gastrula without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fetus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Solid ball of 16-32 cells (3-4 days after fertilization)', 'The morula is a solid ball of 16-32 blastomeres that forms approximately 3-4 days after fertilization as cleavages proceed during passage down the uterine tube. It develops into the blastocyst.'),
('67faa0a0-9887-5edd-86ac-d05f0c3e10aa'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'The blastocyst consists of:', '["Only trophoblast resulting in decreased functional activity","Amnion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inner cell mass (embryoblast) and outer trophoblast","Only inner cell mass without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Embryonic disc leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inner cell mass (embryoblast) and outer trophoblast', 'The blastocyst is a hollow sphere composed of an outer trophoblast layer, which forms the placenta, and an inner cell mass (embryoblast), which develops into the embryo.'),
('be37c056-410a-5112-8873-ef0043ccff5a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'When does implantation of the blastocyst normally occur?', '["Day 6-7 after fertilization (endometrial attachment)","Day 1-2 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Day 21 mediated by specific receptor activation","Immediately without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Day 14 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Day 6-7 after fertilization (endometrial attachment)', 'Implantation begins about day 6-7 after fertilization, when the blastocyst hatches and the trophoblast attaches to and invades the endometrial lining. This coincides with the period when the endometrium is receptive.'),
('5d2de123-8940-5130-b68f-651ed0fd1414'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Which two layers of the trophoblast are important for early embryo implantation?', '["Embryo resulting in decreased functional activity","Endometrium accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cytotrophoblast and syncytiotrophoblast","Inner cell mass without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Decidua leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cytotrophoblast and syncytiotrophoblast', 'The inner cytotrophoblast proliferates to form the outer syncytiotrophoblast, which invades the endometrium to anchor the embryo and establish the placenta. Together they mediate implantation.'),
('6be059fd-9abf-5cf7-bf1f-7aec2d815328'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Syncytiotrophoblast produces:', '["hCG (human chorionic gonadotropin)","Estrogen only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Prolactin mediated by specific receptor activation","LH without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Progesterone only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'hCG (human chorionic gonadotropin)', 'The syncytiotrophoblast secretes hCG, the hormone that maintains the corpus luteum and rescues progesterone production in early pregnancy. hCG is also the basis of pregnancy tests.'),
('c92ec33a-f8d9-56c8-b139-8457957970c8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'hCG peaks at:', '["8-10 weeks of pregnancy","4 weeks","Second trimester","First week","Term"]'::jsonb, '8-10 weeks of pregnancy', 'Maternal serum hCG rises rapidly after implantation, doubling about every 48 hours, and reaches a peak at approximately 8-10 weeks of gestation before declining to a lower plateau.'),
('380b4681-754f-57c0-bb09-49ffcf86ba78'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Which trophoblast layer produces human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG)?', '["Fetus","Decidua","Syncytiotrophoblast","Maternal pituitary","Corpus luteum"]'::jsonb, 'Syncytiotrophoblast', 'The syncytiotrophoblast is the trophoblast layer that secretes hCG, which stimulates the corpus luteum to continue progesterone production in early pregnancy.'),
('9860099d-fb14-57d5-a579-83995365a457'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Human placental lactogen (hPL) acts in the mother to:', '["Anti-insulin (diabetogenic)","Increase insulin sensitivity","Lower maternal blood glucose","Inhibit maternal lipolysis","Increase glucose uptake by maternal tissues"]'::jsonb, 'Anti-insulin (diabetogenic)', 'hPL opposes insulin action, raising maternal blood glucose, and promotes maternal lipolysis, sparing glucose and ketone substrates for fetal use. Its anti-insulin effect contributes to the diabetogenic state of late pregnancy.'),
('5d1daaeb-cccb-5bfc-ab34-7a254cea14f1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Placental estrogen increases during pregnancy to promote:', '["Stimulate uterine growth","Initiate labor","Prevent labor","Maintain the corpus luteum","Stimulate uterine relaxation"]'::jsonb, 'Stimulate uterine growth', 'Rising placental estrogen promotes uterine (myometrial) growth and breast development during pregnancy. The corpus luteum is maintained by hCG, and labor is initiated mainly by oxytocin and prostaglandins.'),
('7632817a-1446-57e1-a5b1-3da372b945f4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Which of the following crosses the placenta from mother to fetus?', '["Maternal antibodies (IgG)","Maternal immunoglobulin M (IgM)","Maternal red blood cells","Carbon dioxide (toward the fetus)","IgA as the dominant transferred antibody"]'::jsonb, 'Maternal antibodies (IgG)', 'IgG crosses the placenta via FcRn-mediated transcytosis, conferring passive humoral immunity. IgM, IgA, and maternal red blood cells do not normally cross; carbon dioxide diffuses in the opposite direction, from fetus to mother.'),
('dadeafa2-7402-5d2f-884f-57d6a26cfc14'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'The onset of menstrual bleeding in a non-conception cycle is precipitated by:', '["Progesterone withdrawal","An hCG surge","A rise in estrogen alone","A prostaglandin-stimulated corpus luteum","Oxytocin release from the posterior pituitary"]'::jsonb, 'Progesterone withdrawal', 'At the end of the luteal phase, regression of the corpus luteum causes progesterone withdrawal, which triggers endometrial vasoconstriction, ischemia, and shedding of the functional layer. hCG prevents this when pregnancy occurs.'),
('c7acf653-5b60-57f5-847e-7c29632f4a24'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Oxytocin released during labor:', '["Stimulates uterine contractions (positive feedback)","Stops uterine contractions","Causes cervical ripening without contraction","Relaxes the myometrium","Suppresses prostaglandin release"]'::jsonb, 'Stimulates uterine contractions (positive feedback)', 'Oxytocin from the posterior pituitary stimulates rhythmic myometrial contractions during delivery. Cervical distension from each contraction reflexively releases more oxytocin, a positive-feedback loop.'),
('50710582-2440-57f4-81ec-9b280182e7c5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'The Ferguson reflex is:', '["Positive feedback: uterine contraction -> oxytocin release -> stronger contractions","Negative feedback accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inhibition of oxytocin secretion mediated by specific receptor activation","Uterine relaxation in response to cervical stretch without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Suppression of uterine contractions leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Positive feedback: uterine contraction -> oxytocin release -> stronger contractions', 'The Ferguson reflex is a positive-feedback loop in which uterine contractions distend the cervix, triggering oxytocin release that intensifies further contractions. It boosts uterine force during the second stage of labor.'),
('ef635bdc-3e0e-5b95-ac5d-394d74ff7a3a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Prostaglandins contribute to labor by:', '["Causing uterine contractions and cervical ripening","Stimulating milk production","Preventing uterine contractions","Relaxing the uterus","Inhibiting cervical ripening"]'::jsonb, 'Causing uterine contractions and cervical ripening', 'Prostaglandins stimulate uterine contractions and soften and dilate the cervix, which is why exogenous PGE and PGF analogues are used to induce or augment labor.'),
('06f19525-b9a1-58c7-b01d-6156d25001fb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'What triggers the degradation of collagen in the endometrium during menstruation?', '["Progesterone withdrawal and matrix metalloproteinases","Estrogen elevation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","FSH surge mediated by specific receptor activation","LH peak without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Oxytocin release leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Progesterone withdrawal and matrix metalloproteinases', 'Progesterone withdrawal removes its inhibition, allowing matrix metalloproteinases (MMPs) to degrade endometrial collagen and break down the functional layer. This leads to tissue shedding and menstrual bleeding.'),
('fc5d066f-cd13-5667-b7d0-6fa7cef5932e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Which of the following correctly describes the clinical stages of labor?', '["Stage 1 (cervical dilation)","Stage 1 (delivery of the fetus)","Stage 2 (delivery of the placenta)","Stage 2 (cervical dilation)","Stage 3 (onset of regular contractions)"]'::jsonb, 'Stage 1 (cervical dilation)', 'Labor has three stages: stage 1 is cervical dilation, stage 2 is delivery of the fetus, and stage 3 is delivery of the placenta. Only the option matching stage 1 to cervical dilation is correct.'),
('767d21c4-1151-5834-a865-d7b01f41ddcc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Lactation is initiated by:', '["Oxytocin alone resulting in decreased functional activity","Postpartum drop in estrogen and progesterone (prolactin surge)","Estrogen rise mediated by specific receptor activation","hCG without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Progesterone rise leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Postpartum drop in estrogen and progesterone (prolactin surge)', 'During pregnancy, estrogen and progesterone inhibit lactogenesis. Their sharp postpartum fall removes this inhibition, allowing prolactin to initiate and sustain milk secretion.'),
('d45ef0a7-920b-5e6d-bba5-501ffc023e13'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'During therapeutic management of Reproduction, Acetaminophen antidote:', '["N-acetylcysteine","Flumazenil","Naloxone","Fomepizole","Deferoxamine"]'::jsonb, 'N-acetylcysteine', 'NAC replenishes glutathione stores.'),
('2db0234d-3f5b-5047-b803-d0389363aaa7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Milk ejection from the mammary gland is mediated by:', '["Oxytocin (myoepithelial cell contraction)","Prolactin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","hCG mediated by specific receptor activation","Estrogen without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Progesterone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Oxytocin (myoepithelial cell contraction)', 'Oxytocin released by the posterior pituitary contracts myoepithelial cells surrounding the alveoli, squeezing milk into the ducts for ejection. Prolactin regulates milk synthesis rather than ejection.'),
('be4fe341-b5fb-5365-bc38-2cdd866636ea'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'The milk ejection reflex can be inhibited by:', '["Feeding resulting in decreased functional activity","Warmth accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sleep mediated by specific receptor activation","Suckling without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Stress (sympathetic activation)"]'::jsonb, 'Stress (sympathetic activation)', 'Stress activates the sympathetic nervous system, which inhibits oxytocin release and thereby suppresses the milk ejection reflex. Suckling, warmth, and feeding promote the reflex.'),
('bf4419d8-6daf-542c-aab8-437b5e8e54bd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Which of the following is true about the release of prolactin and oxytocin during breastfeeding?', '["Prolactin and oxytocin release (neuroendocrine reflex)","Only oxytocin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only prolactin mediated by specific receptor activation","Estrogen release without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Progesterone release leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Prolactin and oxytocin release (neuroendocrine reflex)', 'Suckling triggers a neuroendocrine reflex releasing both prolactin (anterior pituitary, for milk synthesis) and oxytocin (posterior pituitary, for milk ejection). Neither estrogen nor progesterone is released by the reflex.'),
('daa32ea4-4aa9-577a-a295-50cb08d3621f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Lactational amenorrhea during exclusive breastfeeding results from:', '["Suckling -> high prolactin -> suppress GnRH -> anovulation","Oxytocin surge accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Progesterone release mediated by specific receptor activation","LH surge without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Estrogen surge leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Suckling -> high prolactin -> suppress GnRH -> anovulation', 'Frequent suckling maintains high prolactin levels, which suppress GnRH secretion and hence gonadotropins. The resulting anovulation produces lactational amenorrhea.'),
('7f4a187b-5803-521b-a9c5-0300501c9f80'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'In breast development, which of the following is correctly matched?', '["Estrogen (ductal growth) and progesterone (alveolar growth)","Prolactin (ductal growth) and oxytocin (alveolar growth)","Estrogen (alveolar growth) and progesterone (ductal growth)","Cortisol (ductal growth) and GH (alveolar growth)","Oxytocin (ductal growth) and prolactin (alveolar growth)"]'::jsonb, 'Estrogen (ductal growth) and progesterone (alveolar growth)', 'Estrogen promotes ductal growth and branching, while progesterone stimulates alveolar (lobuloalveolar) development of the mammary gland at puberty and during pregnancy.'),
('230ec515-493c-5ad4-a024-ed006615b744'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Plasma levels of estrogen and progesterone are jointly elevated during which period?', '["During mid-pregnancy","After delivery","During menstruation","At puberty","At menopause"]'::jsonb, 'During mid-pregnancy', 'After the luteal-placental shift, the placenta secretes large amounts of both estrogen and progesterone, so their levels remain markedly elevated through mid-pregnancy. Both fall sharply only after delivery.'),
('9c3abfe6-101f-5e4a-aa39-83ab5b397856'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'What happens to estrogen and progesterone levels 2-4 days postpartum?', '["Both drop sharply","Estrogen rises, progesterone drops","Both remain elevated","Progesterone rises, estrogen drops","No significant change"]'::jsonb, 'Both drop sharply', 'After delivery of the placenta, the source of placental steroids is removed, so both estrogen and progesterone fall rapidly over 2-4 days. This withdrawal permits lactogenesis.'),
('9d0e4de8-df59-5c95-b55a-fe337cc49b82'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Galactorrhea is:', '["Inappropriate milk secretion (non-postpartum)","Mastitis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Blood in milk mediated by specific receptor activation","Milk stasis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Suppression of lactation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inappropriate milk secretion (non-postpartum)', 'Galactorrhea is the inappropriate secretion of milk in the non-postpartum state, typically from hyperprolactinemia or other lactogenic stimuli. It must be distinguished from mastitis and milk stasis.'),
('86424143-b9b5-5477-82da-3dbe6f0f1761'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Hyperprolactinemia in females characteristically causes:', '["Galactorrhea","Normal ovulatory cycles","Mastitis","Uterine hypertrophy","Polycythemia"]'::jsonb, 'Galactorrhea', 'Hyperprolactinemia characteristically produces galactorrhea and, by suppressing GnRH, menstrual disturbance with anovulatory infertility. Normal ovulatory cycles, mastitis, uterine hypertrophy, and polycythemia are not causal effects of a high prolactin level.'),
('202750cd-3ea1-52e3-953f-cbb41231253c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'The transition from fetal to neonatal circulation involves:', '["Closure of ductus arteriosus","Opening of all fetal shunts","Pulmonary vasoconstriction","Decreased pulmonary blood flow","Persistence of high pulmonary vascular resistance"]'::jsonb, 'Closure of ductus arteriosus', 'At birth, rising oxygen and falling prostaglandins constrict the ductus arteriosus, while pulmonary vasodilation opens the lungs'' circulation and subsequently the foramen ovale and ductus venosus functionally close. The shunts progressively close rather than open.'),
('36c5cb90-4721-5a79-8435-a2dfea4f6423'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'The first breath of a newborn is triggered by:', '["Hypoxia","A fully oxygenated environment","Warm ambient temperature","Deep maternal sedation","Absence of tactile stimulation"]'::jsonb, 'Hypoxia', 'The first breath is stimulated chiefly by asphyxia generated during delivery, i.e., hypoxia, hypercapnia, and acidosis acting on peripheral and central chemoreceptors. Cooling and tactile stimuli provide secondary reinforcement; an oxygen-rich environment does not.'),
('2b07eafd-18b9-5ff5-94d1-cc22c8f12b35'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'At birth, pulmonary vascular resistance:', '["Falls markedly with the first breath","Increases markedly","Remains unchanged","Is sustained by vasoconstriction","Rises as the lungs expand"]'::jsonb, 'Falls markedly with the first breath', 'Lung expansion and the rise in alveolar oxygen at the first breath dilate pulmonary vessels, sharply lowering pulmonary vascular resistance and increasing pulmonary blood flow. This establishes the neonatal pulmonary circulation.'),
('b55e6cac-8b80-5238-8d4c-08bf89fb3300'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Foramen ovale is a shunt between:', '["Liver and IVC","Pulmonary artery and aorta","Right ventricle and left ventricle","Vena cava and aorta","Right atrium and left atrium"]'::jsonb, 'Right atrium and left atrium', 'The foramen ovale is an opening in the interatrial septum that permits blood to pass from the right atrium to the left atrium, bypassing the fetal lungs.'),
('d96de8e3-dcb0-5654-aa88-1e88843f6359'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'The ductus arteriosus connects:', '["Pulmonary artery to descending aorta","Right atrium to left atrium","Pulmonary vein to aorta","Aorta to vena cava","Liver to IVC"]'::jsonb, 'Pulmonary artery to descending aorta', 'The ductus arteriosus connects the pulmonary artery to the descending aorta, allowing most right ventricular output to bypass the unexpanded fetal lungs.'),
('aa8b912f-4f1c-54df-99c3-3de43fc3fccd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Which fetal shunt carries oxygenated blood from the placenta to the IVC?', '["Umbilical vein to inferior vena cava (bypasses liver)","Portal vein to IVC accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Umbilical artery to aorta mediated by specific receptor activation","Aorta to vena cava without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Liver to heart leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Umbilical vein to inferior vena cava (bypasses liver)', 'The ductus venosus shunts well-oxygenated blood from the umbilical vein directly to the inferior vena cava, bypassing the hepatic portal circulation.'),
('2107c43d-6022-5191-a4a8-9a3ae2929a3a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Patent ductus arteriosus (PDA) is treated with:', '["Oxygen resulting in decreased functional activity","Indomethacin (prostaglandin synthesis inhibitor)","Surfactant mediated by specific receptor activation","Antibiotics without affecting the primary tissue pathway","PGE1 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Indomethacin (prostaglandin synthesis inhibitor)', 'Indomethacin inhibits prostaglandin synthesis, allowing the ductus arteriosus to constrict and close. PGE1 has the opposite effect and is used to keep the ductus patent.'),
('714f5267-801b-52c7-9b47-066233b75bae'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'PGE1 (alprostadil) is used to:', '["Treat hypertension resulting in decreased functional activity","Close PDA accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Prevent preterm labor mediated by specific receptor activation","Keep ductus arteriosus open in ductal-dependent congenital heart disease","Induce labor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Keep ductus arteriosus open in ductal-dependent congenital heart disease', 'PGE1 maintains patency of the ductus arteriosus, which is life-saving in ductal-dependent lesions by preserving pulmonary or systemic blood flow until surgery. It is given before definitive repair.'),
('804528fe-2527-5699-ab15-9e678d05cd85'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Which vessel of the fetal circulation carries blood with the highest oxygen saturation?', '["Umbilical vein (80%)","Descending aorta","Pulmonary artery","All vessels equally","Umbilical artery"]'::jsonb, 'Umbilical vein (80%)', 'Oxygenated blood returns from the placenta through the umbilical vein at about 80% saturation, the highest in the fetal circulation. The umbilical artery carries deoxygenated blood to the placenta.'),
('9b0df793-658a-5d16-9557-b6ce44c1bc71'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'After birth, the foramen ovale closes due to:', '["Increased left atrial pressure (from increased pulmonary flow)","Decreased right atrial pressure","Oxygen","Both increased LA pressure and decreased RA pressure","Increased pulmonary vascular resistance"]'::jsonb, 'Both increased LA pressure and decreased RA pressure', 'At birth, falling pulmonary resistance increases left atrial pressure while decreased venous return lowers right atrial pressure, so left atrial pressure exceeds right atrial pressure and the flap valve functionally closes. Anatomic closure follows.'),
('6c87c20f-1338-599b-b372-738946daecb7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'A 55-year-old male presents with altered mental status. Laboratories show a serum calcium of 6.5 mg/dL. Which of the following is the most likely cause of the hypocalcemia?', '["Hypoparathyroidism","Primary hyperparathyroidism","Vitamin D deficiency","Chronic kidney disease","Acute pancreatitis"]'::jsonb, 'Hypoparathyroidism', 'A total serum calcium of 6.5 mg/dL is marked hypocalcemia, which can cause tetany and altered mental status. Hypoparathyroidism is a classic cause of sustained hypocalcemia; vitamin D deficiency, chronic kidney disease, and pancreatitis are other possibilities.'),
('0c59e9a9-c56d-5332-8f07-34db66270cf3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Brown adipose tissue (BAT) generates heat via:', '["UCP1-mediated dissipation of the proton gradient as heat","Glycolysis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Shivering mediated by specific receptor activation","Lipolysis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","ATP synthesis via oxidative phosphorylation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'UCP1-mediated dissipation of the proton gradient as heat', 'Uncoupling protein 1 (UCP1) in the inner mitochondrial membrane of brown adipocytes allows protons to leak back across the membrane, generating heat instead of ATP. This non-shivering thermogenic pathway is the defining mechanism of BAT heat production.'),
('ce021b26-8771-5ff8-b007-d29cfd8a6a28'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Which population relies most heavily on brown adipose tissue (BAT) for thermoregulation?', '["Neonates","Children","All ages equally","Elderly","Adults"]'::jsonb, 'Neonates', 'Neonates have abundant brown adipose tissue and little capacity for shivering, so non-shivering thermogenesis through BAT is their principal heat-generating mechanism.'),
('3326cc47-e3b8-5855-a6f7-b4d16b95a267'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Neonatal jaundice is often due to:', '["Infections resulting in decreased functional activity","Immature hepatic conjugation (low UDP-glucuronyl transferase activity)","Hemolysis mediated by specific receptor activation","Biliary atresia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hepatitis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Immature hepatic conjugation (low UDP-glucuronyl transferase activity)', 'Physiologic neonatal jaundice results from immature hepatic bilirubin conjugation due to low UDP-glucuronyl transferase activity, compounded by increased red cell turnover in the newborn. It characteristically appears after 24 hours and resolves within the first week.'),
('36931905-ea1c-5753-bfb4-af473e6f3eec'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Which of the following is NOT a component of the Apgar score?', '["Activity (muscle tone)","Appearance (color)","Grimace (reflex irritability)","Pulse (heart rate)","Urine output"]'::jsonb, 'Urine output', 'The Apgar score evaluates Appearance, Pulse, Grimace, Activity, and Respiration at 1 and 5 minutes after birth. Urine output is not assessed by the score.'),
('dfc18285-d071-5716-88f3-2b7ac10cdcd0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Which component of the Apgar assessment is the most important indicator of neonatal wellbeing?', '["Muscle tone","Respiratory effort","Reflex irritability","Heart rate","Color"]'::jsonb, 'Heart rate', 'Heart rate is the most critical component of the Apgar score, reflecting the adequacy of circulation. A severely depressed or absent heart rate requires immediate resuscitation regardless of the other findings.'),
('e2ed2d8a-672a-5920-8546-7dd45bdcac12'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'A preterm neonate develops tachypnea, grunting, and hypoxemia within hours of birth. Which condition most likely explains these findings?', '["Congenital heart disease","Infection","Surfactant deficiency","Diaphragmatic hernia","Meconium aspiration"]'::jsonb, 'Surfactant deficiency', 'In preterm infants, inadequate pulmonary surfactant causes alveolar collapse and neonatal respiratory distress syndrome. Tachypnea, grunting, and hypoxemia appearing shortly after birth are its classic manifestations.'),
('b93edcb2-8a2e-5f09-8d64-c1f38ac9e377'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Surfactant production by type II pneumocytes begins at:', '["24-28 weeks of gestation; mature by about 34-35 weeks","Conception accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","20 weeks mediated by specific receptor activation","Term without affecting the primary tissue pathway","First trimester leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '24-28 weeks of gestation; mature by about 34-35 weeks', 'Type II pneumocytes begin producing surfactant around 24-28 weeks of gestation. Functional lung maturity is reached by approximately 34-35 weeks, after which the risk of respiratory distress syndrome falls sharply.'),
('bc1cbf7b-9683-530b-877e-7a7aaf82886a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Antenatal betamethasone is administered to a woman at risk of imminent preterm delivery in order to:', '["Decrease fetal growth resulting in decreased functional activity","Accelerate fetal lung maturity (surfactant production)","Prevent infection mediated by specific receptor activation","Treat hypertension without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Induce labor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Accelerate fetal lung maturity (surfactant production)', 'Betamethasone crosses the placenta and accelerates surfactant production by fetal type II pneumocytes. This reduces the incidence and severity of neonatal respiratory distress syndrome in preterm infants.'),
('93ef3c4c-8c39-5385-96ac-8d62b08b8d7c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'The lecithin-to-sphingomyelin (L/S) ratio in amniotic fluid is useful in assessing:', '["Fetal liver maturity resulting in decreased functional activity","Placental function accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fetal kidney function mediated by specific receptor activation","Amniotic fluid volume without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fetal lung maturity (L/S > 2 suggests mature lungs)"]'::jsonb, 'Fetal lung maturity (L/S > 2 suggests mature lungs)', 'Lecithin rises as the fetal lungs mature while sphingomyelin remains relatively constant, so the L/S ratio tracks pulmonary surfactant production. An L/S ratio greater than 2 indicates functionally mature fetal lungs.'),
('825b8a20-d829-5333-9ed3-f1d5b19cca8a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Which sequence best describes the normal transition from fetal to neonatal circulation?', '["First breath → lung expansion → decreased pulmonary vascular resistance → increased pulmonary blood flow → shunt closure","Decreased heart rate accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pulmonary vasoconstriction after the first breath mediated by specific receptor activation","Persistent elevation of pulmonary vascular resistance without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Decreased oxygen saturations leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'First breath → lung expansion → decreased pulmonary vascular resistance → increased pulmonary blood flow → shunt closure', 'The first breath expands the lungs and lowers pulmonary vascular resistance, increasing pulmonary blood flow. The foramen ovale and ductus arteriosus then close functionally as left atrial pressure rises and ductal flow diminishes.'),
('e98ec9fe-413a-5979-afc9-feba6da14018'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'A 50-year-old male recently started on a beta-blocker for hypertension develops erectile dysfunction. What is the most likely cause?', '["Medication-induced ED","Smoking-induced ED","Parkinson disease-induced ED","Neurogenic ED","Alcohol-induced ED"]'::jsonb, 'Medication-induced ED', 'Antihypertensive drugs, particularly beta-blockers and thiazide diuretics, are common reversible causes of erectile dysfunction. The temporal relationship with starting the medication supports this diagnosis.'),
('eff2da6f-b004-570f-9480-e6e93dd34071'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'A 35-year-old female presents with secondary amenorrhea and galactorrhea. Which diagnosis is most likely?', '["Weight loss-related amenorrhea","17α-hydroxylase deficiency","Hyperprolactinemia","Androgen insensitivity syndrome","Cushing syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperprolactinemia', 'Elevated prolactin inhibits GnRH-driven gonadotropin secretion, suppressing ovulation and causing secondary amenorrhea. Amenorrhea together with galactorrhea is a hallmark of hyperprolactinemia.'),
('8383096e-f8de-5410-9796-fd6368542ecb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'A 25-year-old female presents with hirsutism, obesity, and irregular cycles with secondary amenorrhea. Which condition most likely explains these findings?', '["Turner syndrome","Polycystic ovary syndrome","Premature ovarian failure","Hypothyroidism","Hyperprolactinemia"]'::jsonb, 'Polycystic ovary syndrome', 'PCOS is characterized by chronic anovulation and hyperandrogenism, producing amenorrhea or oligomenorrhea with hirsutism and obesity. It is the most common cause of anovulatory amenorrhea in reproductive-age women.'),
('dad48a83-882d-558e-8744-834dd3f1c2cc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'A 30-year-old male presents with azoospermia and small, firm testes. Which diagnosis is most consistent with this presentation?', '["Premature ovarian failure","Uterine factor infertility","Klinefelter syndrome","Kartagener syndrome","Tubal factor infertility"]'::jsonb, 'Klinefelter syndrome', 'Klinefelter syndrome (47,XXY) causes primary testicular failure with small, firm testes, elevated FSH, and azoospermia. It is the most common genetic cause of male infertility.'),
('da9dd002-9d4d-5dc4-b0b6-1c5152f0ea05'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'A 30-year-old male presents with infertility, and examination reveals a left-sided scrotal mass described as a "bag of worms." Which diagnosis is most likely?', '["Luteal phase defect","Endometriosis","Varicocele","Unexplained infertility","Immunologic infertility"]'::jsonb, 'Varicocele', 'A varicocele is a dilatation of the pampiniform plexus of veins that raises scrotal temperature and impairs spermatogenesis. It is the most common surgically correctable cause of male infertility.'),
('b8029822-fada-5858-a556-6829ac8483b6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'A 22-year-old female athlete with heavy training and restricted caloric intake presents with amenorrhea. Which mechanism most likely explains the amenorrhea?', '["Pituitary tumor","Hyperprolactinemia","Androgen insensitivity syndrome","Hypothalamic dysfunction","Klinefelter syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'Hypothalamic dysfunction', 'Low energy availability from intense exercise and caloric restriction suppresses the hypothalamic GnRH pulse generator. The resulting hypogonadotropic state causes functional hypothalamic amenorrhea.'),
('33bff582-1e5b-5a53-9342-d89512ec2bfe'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'A 30-year-old female with anorexia nervosa presents with secondary amenorrhea. Which mechanism most likely accounts for the amenorrhea?', '["Hypothalamic dysfunction","Hyperprolactinemia","Hypogonadism","Klinefelter syndrome","Androgen insensitivity syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'Hypothalamic dysfunction', 'Severe caloric restriction depresses hypothalamic GnRH pulsatility, lowering LH and FSH secretion. The resulting hypogonadotropic hypogonadism underlies the amenorrhea and typically reverses with weight restoration.'),
('550fa4e1-c07c-5a60-bfef-0dac7d5c8368'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'A 25-year-old overweight female with acne and hirsutism presents with irregular menstrual cycles. Which condition best explains the presentation?', '["Adenomyosis","Obesity-related irregularity","Cushing syndrome","Polycystic ovary syndrome","Endometrial polyp"]'::jsonb, 'Polycystic ovary syndrome', 'PCOS combines chronic anovulation with hyperandrogenism, producing irregular cycles, acne, and hirsutism. It is the leading cause of anovulatory menstrual irregularity in reproductive-age women.'),
('dc90779f-9033-59bb-bca8-9000c19bdaa2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'A 50-year-old male presents with painful penile curvature and difficulty achieving erections. Which diagnosis is most likely?', '["Peyronie disease","Hyperprolactinemia","Spinal cord injury","Arterial insufficiency","Post-surgical ED"]'::jsonb, 'Peyronie disease', 'Peyronie disease is fibrotic plaque formation in the tunica albuginea that produces penile curvature and erectile dysfunction. Curvature with pain during erection is its characteristic presentation.'),
('5ffd802c-2d4c-5a11-be22-624811456eb0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'A 30-year-old female with significant recent weight loss develops amenorrhea. Which condition best explains the amenorrhea?', '["Functional hypothalamic amenorrhea","Endometrial destruction","Turner syndrome","Polycystic ovary syndrome","Primary ovarian insufficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Functional hypothalamic amenorrhea', 'Energy deficit suppresses GnRH release, producing hypogonadotropic amenorrhea. Functional hypothalamic amenorrhea is the diagnosis when weight loss or stress disrupts the GnRH pulse generator.'),
('c24da524-fbf1-5500-a95a-1ced7b976e2d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'A 35-year-old male presents with severe azoospermia and a family history of infertility in paternal relatives. Which genetic cause is most likely?', '["Endometriosis","Y chromosome microdeletion","Klinefelter syndrome","Hypogonadotropic hypogonadism","Genetic infertility"]'::jsonb, 'Y chromosome microdeletion', 'Microdeletions in the AZF regions of the Y chromosome cause spermatogenic arrest and azoospermia. They are among the most common identifiable genetic causes of male infertility.'),
('4b5d5626-9103-56cf-8395-496ad8853707'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'A 16-year-old female presents with primary amenorrhea, short stature, and a webbed neck. Which diagnosis is most likely?', '["Turner syndrome","Androgen insensitivity syndrome","Functional hypothalamic amenorrhea","Hyperprolactinemia","Endometrial destruction"]'::jsonb, 'Turner syndrome', 'Turner syndrome (45,X) features streak gonads that fail to produce estrogen, causing hypergonadotropic hypogonadism and primary amenorrhea. Short stature and a webbed neck are characteristic features.'),
('36063991-998a-5ca4-ab18-cffbd442748c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'A 25-year-old female presents with central obesity, violaceous striae, and irregular menstrual cycles. Which diagnosis is most likely?', '["von Willebrand disease","Uterine fibroid","Cushing syndrome","Obesity-related irregularity","Polycystic ovary syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'Cushing syndrome', 'Chronic cortisol excess in Cushing syndrome disrupts gonadotropin secretion, causing irregular menses. Central obesity, violaceous striae, and hyperglycemia support the diagnosis.'),
('8040105f-ddf2-5cdf-92f6-e7c0a49b2ca1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'A 30-year-old female with fatigue, weight gain, and heavy irregular menstrual bleeding has abnormal thyroid function tests. Which condition best explains the menstrual disturbance?', '["Thyroid dysfunction","Anovulatory cycles","Uterine fibroid","Adrenal hyperplasia","Polycystic ovary syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'Thyroid dysfunction', 'Both hypothyroidism and hyperthyroidism disturb estrogen metabolism and ovulation, producing menstrual irregularity. Treating the underlying thyroid disorder restores normal cycles.'),
('25cf289d-cb49-561e-9ccd-af14cf656876'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'An 18-year-old female presents with primary amenorrhea and anosmia. Which diagnosis is most likely?', '["Kallmann syndrome","Androgen insensitivity syndrome","Hypogonadism","Orchitis","Hyperprolactinemia"]'::jsonb, 'Kallmann syndrome', 'Kallmann syndrome combines congenital GnRH deficiency with anosmia due to failed migration of GnRH neurons. It causes hypogonadotropic hypogonadism and primary amenorrhea.'),
('0906ae07-aec4-5560-a1d5-c2f1276b61ae'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'A 32-year-old female with polycystic ovary syndrome presents with infertility. Which factor most likely explains the infertility?', '["Tubal factor infertility","Anovulation","Antisperm antibodies","Male factor infertility","Y chromosome microdeletion"]'::jsonb, 'Anovulation', 'Chronic anovulation is the most common cause of female infertility. In PCOS, follicles fail to ovulate regularly, preventing conception.'),
('e29ad365-f6d4-5012-900c-1478180b71a8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'A 30-year-old female with galactorrhea presents with irregular menstrual cycles. Which condition best explains the findings?', '["Hyperprolactinemia","Ovarian tumor","Hypothalamic dysfunction","Perimenopause","Anovulatory cycles"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperprolactinemia', 'Elevated prolactin inhibits GnRH-driven gonadotropin secretion, disrupting ovulation and the menstrual cycle. Irregular menses together with galactorrhea strongly suggests hyperprolactinemia.'),
('6f1bd112-3b88-5289-9323-0844233c210a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'A couple is evaluated for infertility. The female partner''s evaluation is normal, but semen analysis reveals severe oligospermia. To which category of infertility do these findings belong?', '["Tubal factor infertility","Kartagener syndrome","Male factor infertility","Premature ovarian failure","Varicocele"]'::jsonb, 'Male factor infertility', 'Abnormalities of sperm count, motility, or morphology account for roughly half of couples'' infertility. Semen analysis is the first-line test for the male partner.'),
('59cc5f74-42a4-5007-bdc3-b194806a5cfd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'A 32-year-old male presents with infertility; semen analysis shows a normal sperm count and morphology but markedly reduced sperm motility. Which diagnosis best explains the finding?', '["Varicocele","Premature ovarian failure","Anovulation","Klinefelter syndrome","Sperm motility disorder"]'::jsonb, 'Sperm motility disorder', 'Impaired sperm motility (asthenozoospermia) prevents sperm from reaching and penetrating the oocyte. Causes include varicocele, genital infection, and ciliary disorders.'),
('d3afb594-ddc2-5c54-b3f6-802773e9c55c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'A 40-year-old male develops erectile dysfunction during a period of intense stress, while nocturnal erections remain intact. Which diagnosis best explains the findings?', '["Psychogenic ED","Venous leak","Alcohol-induced ED","Peyronie disease","Parkinson disease-induced ED"]'::jsonb, 'Psychogenic ED', 'Psychogenic erectile dysfunction typically arises abruptly under stress while nocturnal and morning erections remain preserved. Preservation of nocturnal erections argues against an organic cause.'),
('86727e21-3b27-50ef-95e8-1e8a3e00e62f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'A 42-year-old female develops amenorrhea after a dilation and curettage performed for a missed miscarriage. Which diagnosis is most likely?', '["Pregnancy","Adrenal tumors","Premature ovarian failure","Sheehan syndrome","Asherman syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'Asherman syndrome', 'Asherman syndrome is intrauterine adhesion formation after uterine instrumentation, which obliterates the endometrial cavity and causes secondary amenorrhea. The preceding dilation and curettage is the key clue.'),
('fa980d52-b9ca-5d1d-9fbd-6a5322cc7472'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'A 45-year-old female presents with galactorrhea and secondary amenorrhea. Which diagnosis is most likely?', '["Orchitis","Hypogonadism","Pituitary tumor","Hyperprolactinemia","Hypothalamic dysfunction"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperprolactinemia', 'Hyperprolactinemia suppresses GnRH, lowering gonadotropins and causing secondary amenorrhea. Galactorrhea accompanying amenorrhea is characteristic of elevated prolactin.'),
('9276ecbe-0908-596a-b98a-36f98ebf3abb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'A 25-year-old obese female with acne and hirsutism presents with irregular menstrual cycles. Which condition is most likely?', '["Polycystic ovary syndrome","Coagulation disorder","Obesity-related irregularity","Luteal phase insufficiency","Hyperprolactinemia"]'::jsonb, 'Polycystic ovary syndrome', 'PCOS combines hyperandrogenism with chronic anovulation, producing irregular cycles, acne, and hirsutism. It is the most common cause of anovulatory menstrual irregularity in young women.'),
('0bc65506-580f-5ed2-9a60-91a15b95a4ba'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'A 45-year-old female presents with secondary amenorrhea, galactorrhea, and headaches. Which diagnosis best explains these findings?', '["Endometrial destruction","Asherman syndrome","Kallmann syndrome","Hyperprolactinemia","Turner syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperprolactinemia', 'Amenorrhea with galactorrhea and headaches suggests a prolactin-secreting pituitary adenoma. Elevated prolactin inhibits GnRH, suppressing ovulation and gonadotropin secretion.'),
('c81f01e5-deb8-5007-b9d4-6e6b22a9f591'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'A 25-year-old female presents with galactorrhea and secondary amenorrhea. Which diagnosis is most likely?', '["Varicocele","Hyperprolactinemia","Kallmann syndrome","Orchitis","Androgen insensitivity syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperprolactinemia', 'Galactorrhea with secondary amenorrhea is a classic presentation of hyperprolactinemia. Prolactin excess suppresses GnRH-driven gonadotropin release and ovulation.'),
('a209395d-57aa-587b-927a-3a27f6ad2813'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'A 40-year-old male with significant life stressors develops erectile dysfunction while nocturnal erections remain normal. Which diagnosis best explains the condition?', '["Peyronie disease","Alcohol-induced ED","Psychogenic ED","Neurogenic ED","Spinal cord injury"]'::jsonb, 'Psychogenic ED', 'Normal nocturnal erections together with erectile failure under stress characterize psychogenic ED. Organic vascular, neurologic, or endocrine causes would typically abolish both waking and nocturnal erections.'),
('29d15b27-bf9b-5b02-88a9-1e9bcce16e93'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'A 70-year-old male with diabetes and a history of smoking presents with erectile dysfunction. Which mechanism is most likely responsible?', '["Vascular disease","Medication-induced ED","Spinal cord injury","Hypogonadism","Arterial insufficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Arterial insufficiency', 'Atherosclerosis of the cavernosal arteries is the most common cause of erectile dysfunction in older men with vascular risk factors. Reduced arterial inflow prevents adequate cavernosal filling.'),
('7666e855-0e09-5fe3-85dc-9c447e852d10'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'A 30-year-old female presents with infertility after surgery for uterine fibroids; hysteroscopy reveals intrauterine adhesions. Which diagnosis best explains the infertility?', '["Uterine factor infertility","Klinefelter syndrome","Hypogonadotropic hypogonadism","Azoospermia","Varicocele"]'::jsonb, 'Uterine factor infertility', 'Uterine abnormalities such as fibroids, adhesions, or Müllerian anomalies impair implantation and are a recognized cause of female infertility. The intrauterine adhesions confirmed on hysteroscopy support this diagnosis.'),
('cd020a32-4591-5faa-b300-1dcde6841357'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'A 35-year-old male presents with infertility after a previous genital infection; semen analysis shows sperm agglutination. Which diagnosis best explains the finding?', '["Immunologic infertility","Endometriosis","Kartagener syndrome","Azoospermia","Varicocele"]'::jsonb, 'Immunologic infertility', 'Antisperm antibodies can agglutinate sperm and impair motility and fertilization. Genital infection or trauma can breach the blood-testis barrier and trigger their formation.'),
('876d7ba8-b28a-51ad-a71f-24b0e818eb9a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'A 60-year-old male reports erections that cannot be maintained despite adequate initial rigidity. Which diagnosis best explains the finding?', '["Hypogonadism","Medication-induced ED","Alcohol-induced ED","Neurogenic ED","Venous leak"]'::jsonb, 'Venous leak', 'Veno-occlusive dysfunction (venous leak) allows blood to escape the corpora cavernosa, so rigidity cannot be sustained. It can follow penile trauma or structural damage and is a recognized cause of erectile dysfunction.'),
('541ba1e3-4f85-5702-9934-9b805374e4fd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Regarding clinical cases in Reproduction: Loading dose formula:', '["Vd x Css / F","CL x Css x tau / F","Vd x CL / Css","Css x tau / CL","Fx Css / Vd"]'::jsonb, 'Vd x Css / F', 'Loading dose depends on Vd and target concentration.'),
('e79e1822-d888-5616-bc01-618a9399ae77'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'In the study of Reproduction, Maintenance dose formula:', '["CL x Css x tau / F","Vd x Css / F","Vd x Css x tau","CL x Vd / Css","Css x tau / Vd"]'::jsonb, 'CL x Css x tau / F', 'Maintenance dose depends on clearance and target concentration.'),
('b74ebd13-8af5-587c-84cd-ab870fe4136d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Reproduction: Steady state reached after:', '["4-5 half-lives","1 half-life","2 half-lives","6-7 half-lives","10 half-lives"]'::jsonb, '4-5 half-lives', 'Css reached after 4-5 half-lives.'),
('e2224204-bf96-56e4-a4f9-0f32118f420b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'A 55-year-old male with a spinal cord injury presents with erectile dysfunction. What is the most likely cause?', '["Diabetes mellitus induced ED","Neurogenic ED","Cushing syndrome induced ED","Psychogenic ED","Alcohol-induced ED"]'::jsonb, 'Neurogenic ED', 'Spinal cord injury disrupts the sacral parasympathetic and thoracolumbar sympathetic pathways that mediate erection. Loss of these neural inputs produces neurogenic erectile dysfunction.'),
('35d5f836-a26d-579f-9ec6-ffe14b0e7d39'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'A 25-year-old male who recently underwent radical prostatectomy presents with erectile dysfunction. What is the most likely cause?', '["Psychogenic ED","Cirrhosis induced ED","Neurogenic ED","Post-surgical ED","Arterial insufficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Post-surgical ED', 'Radical prostatectomy frequently damages the cavernous nerves and pelvic vasculature. Erectile dysfunction arising after the procedure is therefore classified as post-surgical.'),
('6ce39651-99b3-5de4-811a-45edb9c8ed65'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Regarding clinical cases in Reproduction: Large Vd means drug is:', '["Distributed into tissues","Confined to plasma","Rapidly excreted","Bound to albumin","Hepatically metabolised"]'::jsonb, 'Distributed into tissues', 'Large Vd >1 L/kg indicates extensive tissue distribution.'),
('ae087e48-d4db-5578-8051-ef2f2356f25f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'A 65-year-old male presents with decreased libido, fatigue, and reduced body and facial hair. Which diagnosis is most suggested by this presentation?', '["Chromosomal abnormality","Varicocele","Hypogonadism","Klinefelter syndrome","Testicular failure"]'::jsonb, 'Hypogonadism', 'Hypogonadism is reduced testicular hormone production, characteristically producing decreased libido, fatigue, and loss of secondary sexual characteristics in older men.'),
('dcf298de-6ab6-50ef-91de-9645a1312d98'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Reproduction: Loading dose primarily depends on:', '["Volume of distribution","Clearance","Half-life","Bioavailability","Renal function"]'::jsonb, 'Volume of distribution', 'Loading dose = Vd x Css / F.'),
('e8bec80e-94a5-5dbb-ac6c-7c8d61bd3087'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'A 35-year-old female presents with irregular menstrual cycles after significant weight loss and intense exercise. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Cushing syndrome","Polycystic ovary syndrome","Hypothalamic dysfunction","Endometriosis","von Willebrand disease"]'::jsonb, 'Hypothalamic dysfunction', 'Hypothalamic dysfunction suppresses GnRH pulsatility, reducing gonadotropin secretion and causing anovulation and menstrual irregularity. Energy deficit from weight loss and excessive exercise is a common trigger.'),
('e6e4cc26-7ba5-580f-ad3e-1841375efdff'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'What is the primary female sex hormone?', '["LH","hCG","Estrogen","Progesterone","FSH"]'::jsonb, 'Estrogen', 'Estrogen, chiefly estradiol, is the principal female sex hormone and governs the development of female secondary sexual characteristics and cyclic endometrial changes.'),
('14d17d5d-9087-5859-8e75-7ca8dafa34eb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Regarding clinical cases in Reproduction: Acetaminophen toxic metabolite:', '["NAPQI","MPP+","MPTP","N-oxide","Sulfate conjugate"]'::jsonb, 'NAPQI', 'NAPQI normally detoxified by glutathione. Overdose depletes glutathione.'),
('744387df-d383-58c5-a97d-4884836a8dd6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'In the study of Reproduction, Acetaminophen antidote:', '["N-acetylcysteine","Flumazenil","Naloxone","Fomepizole","Deferoxamine"]'::jsonb, 'N-acetylcysteine', 'NAC replenishes glutathione stores.'),
('930c6be2-fd73-5bfb-85ee-aa472473a3ce'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Which gland produces the largest fraction of seminal fluid volume?', '["Seminal vesicles","Bulbourethral gland","Testes","Prostate","Epididymis"]'::jsonb, 'Seminal vesicles', 'The seminal vesicles contribute approximately 60-70% of the seminal fluid volume, secreting a fructose-rich fluid that supports sperm survival.'),
('001c5552-600f-569c-b91c-7d815ddf5eb2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'What is the process of male gamete formation called?', '["Spermatogenesis","Fertilization","Oogenesis","Meiosis","Mitosis"]'::jsonb, 'Spermatogenesis', 'Spermatogenesis is the production of mature spermatozoa from spermatogonia within the seminiferous tubules of the testes.'),
('213971d5-1c53-5593-9206-81c85b610524'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'A 30-year-old male presents with infertility, reduced libido, and galactorrhea. Which diagnosis is most suggested by this presentation?', '["Hyperprolactinemia","Hypogonadism","Hypothalamic dysfunction","Epididymitis","Pituitary tumor"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperprolactinemia', 'Hyperprolactinemia suppresses GnRH and gonadotropin secretion, producing hypogonadism with reduced libido, infertility, and galactorrhea.'),
('fa11f87f-f0b3-57a6-8e44-78e75fa009fb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Regarding clinical cases in Reproduction: Efficacy refers to:', '["Ability to produce maximal response","Dose for effect accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Absorption rate mediated by specific receptor activation","Duration without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Safety leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ability to produce maximal response', 'Emax = maximum response a drug can produce.'),
('efe4e29d-2256-551b-bffd-12b01441426b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'In the study of Reproduction, What is the therapeutic index of a drug?', '["TD50 / ED50","ED50 / TD50","LD50 / ED50","ED50 / LD50","TD50 / LD50"]'::jsonb, 'TD50 / ED50', 'TI = TD50/ED50. Wider TI = safer drug.'),
('cbff3d9e-7ac9-547e-81aa-bf8998d2f601'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Reproduction: Bioavailability is defined as:', '["Fraction of unchanged drug reaching systemic circulation","Fraction absorbed from GI tract accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Total drug in plasma mediated by specific receptor activation","Rate of elimination without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Volume of distribution leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fraction of unchanged drug reaching systemic circulation', 'F = fraction of dose reaching systemic circulation unchanged.'),
('3bce5ab0-d2b5-5485-8c75-2bb282227214'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Which hormone is secreted by the corpus luteum?', '["LH","FSH","hCG","Estrogen","Progesterone"]'::jsonb, 'Progesterone', 'The corpus luteum principally secretes progesterone, which converts the endometrium to a secretory state and supports early pregnancy until placental steroid production takes over.'),
('31a0dc5d-86f5-59be-8dcf-75d9a77ee595'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'During therapeutic management of Reproduction, Volume of distribution (Vd) equals:', '["Amount of drug / Plasma concentration","Clearance / Rate","Half-life x Clearance","Dose / F","Absorption rate"]'::jsonb, 'Amount of drug / Plasma concentration', 'Vd = Amount / Concentration. Large Vd = extensive tissue distribution.'),
('bd4f1f41-15b2-5860-bc75-02b2fc1da09c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Regarding clinical cases in Reproduction: CYP enzyme metabolising most drugs:', '["CYP3A4","CYP2D6","CYP2C9","CYP2C19","CYP1A2"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4', 'CYP3A4 metabolises 50% of clinically used drugs.'),
('7f6b6ee1-336c-5feb-b28d-7dc710ceead8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Around which day of a 28-day menstrual cycle does ovulation occur?', '["Day 1","Day 28","Day 14","Day 21","Day 7"]'::jsonb, 'Day 14', 'Ovulation occurs around day 14 of a 28-day cycle, triggered by the mid-cycle surge of LH.'),
('e1926c90-0682-56f0-9cfd-e20ef22524e1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'What is the approximate length of the menstrual cycle?', '["35 days","21 days","28 days","14 days","30 days"]'::jsonb, '28 days', 'The average menstrual cycle lasts about 28 days, although normal cycles range from 21 to 35 days.'),
('1e7a58c8-21a3-5260-9561-154541e19006'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Reproduction: St. John''s Wort induces:', '["CYP3A4 and P-gp","CYP2D6","CYP2C9","CYP1A2","CYP2C19"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4 and P-gp', 'St. John''s Wort induces CYP3A4 and P-gp, reducing drug levels.'),
('866d80b6-ebe2-5528-8c37-cce441a0b64b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'During therapeutic management of Reproduction, Grapefruit juice inhibits:', '["Intestinal CYP3A4","Hepatic CYP3A4","CYP2D6","Renal excretion","Protein binding"]'::jsonb, 'Intestinal CYP3A4', 'Furanocoumarins in grapefruit inhibit intestinal CYP3A4.'),
('a269c36c-46f1-5fcc-ac62-e5a17c834995'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Regarding clinical cases in Reproduction: Weak acids are trapped in:', '["Alkaline urine","Acidic stomach","Blood pH 7.4","Duodenum","Jejunum"]'::jsonb, 'Alkaline urine', 'Weak acids become ionised (trapped) in alkaline environments.'),
('08bf8f02-664c-51d6-810b-0d991c1a061d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'In the study of Reproduction, Zero-order kinetics means:', '["Constant amount eliminated/time","Constant fraction/time","Dose-dependent elimination","First-order at high doses","Saturable absorption"]'::jsonb, 'Constant amount eliminated/time', 'Zero-order: constant amount eliminated regardless of concentration.'),
('0db7255e-fab0-525a-b4f1-8a397d53fefc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Which stage of labor is characterized by cervical dilation?', '["First stage","Latent phase","Second stage","Third stage","Fourth stage"]'::jsonb, 'First stage', 'The first stage of labor is defined by progressive cervical dilation and effacement, ending when the cervix is fully dilated.'),
('5f62bc4f-8e9c-55f9-8343-e522d13d5263'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'What is the primary male sex hormone?', '["Estrogen","DHT","FSH","Testosterone","LH"]'::jsonb, 'Testosterone', 'Testosterone is the principal male sex hormone, responsible for spermatogenesis and the development and maintenance of male secondary sexual characteristics.'),
('01a74a15-07bc-599e-a2d5-a10838d1eae6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'What is the function of the acrosome?', '["Regulates sperm motility","Provides energy for sperm","Stores genetic material","Produces testosterone","Contains enzymes for fertilization"]'::jsonb, 'Contains enzymes for fertilization', 'The acrosome is a cap-like vesicle over the sperm head that releases hydrolytic enzymes, enabling the sperm to penetrate the zona pellucida during fertilization.'),
('ee64ac8d-4afe-5283-8a0a-9b76e115b6de'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Which structure produces hCG in early pregnancy?', '["Endometrium","Anterior pituitary","Ovary","Corpus luteum","Placenta (syncytiotrophoblast)"]'::jsonb, 'Placenta (syncytiotrophoblast)', 'Human chorionic gonadotropin is secreted by the placental syncytiotrophoblast soon after implantation and maintains the corpus luteum in early pregnancy.'),
('d4c22ece-9fba-5c0e-bc28-109f6309ed2a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Approximately when does the fertile window of the menstrual cycle occur?', '["Days 14-21 resulting in decreased functional activity","Days 20-28 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Days 5-9 mediated by specific receptor activation","Days 1-7 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Days 10-17 (5 days before to 2 days after ovulation)"]'::jsonb, 'Days 10-17 (5 days before to 2 days after ovulation)', 'Because sperm can survive about 5 days and the ovum about 24 hours after ovulation, the fertile window spans roughly days 10 to 17 of a 28-day cycle.'),
('827a9aee-14ab-5559-b56a-389978396249'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'What is the process of sperm formation called?', '["Oogenesis","Fertilization","Ovulation","Implantation","Spermatogenesis"]'::jsonb, 'Spermatogenesis', 'Spermatogenesis is the formation of mature spermatozoa from spermatogonia in the seminiferous tubules.'),
('604ba50e-fde1-565b-bafc-579473822524'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'In females, FSH primarily stimulates:', '["Corpus luteum formation","Ovulation","Follicle development","Milk production","Uterine contraction"]'::jsonb, 'Follicle development', 'In females, FSH promotes antral follicle development and stimulates granulosa cells to produce estrogen during the follicular phase.'),
('72cfb972-6a54-54d3-b86a-9c6f15aadcba'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'The LH surge triggers:', '["Follicle development","Fertilization","Ovulation","Menstruation","Implantation"]'::jsonb, 'Ovulation', 'The mid-cycle LH surge triggers ovulation by inducing rupture of the mature Graafian follicle.'),
('8e19bdef-3c48-5219-be33-3d7c3eae2cce'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Which hormones does the placenta produce?', '["TSH resulting in decreased functional activity","hCG, estrogen, progesterone","FSH and LH mediated by specific receptor activation","GnRH without affecting the primary tissue pathway","ACTH leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'hCG, estrogen, progesterone', 'The placenta produces hCG, estrogen, and progesterone; hCG sustains the corpus luteum, and the steroid hormones maintain pregnancy.'),
('9b13fa6d-7f11-57d5-adf8-a531ef6ab03e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Into how many trimesters is pregnancy divided?', '["Two trimesters resulting in decreased functional activity","Four trimesters accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Three trimesters (13-14 weeks each)","Four quarters without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Two halves leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Three trimesters (13-14 weeks each)', 'Pregnancy is divided into three trimesters, each lasting roughly 13 to 14 weeks, with distinct fetal and maternal changes in each.'),
('31bf3703-df82-5d7f-afc9-66916bcd0c0c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'What is the normal duration of pregnancy?', '["32 weeks","42 weeks","36 weeks","40 weeks from LMP","38 weeks"]'::jsonb, '40 weeks from LMP', 'A normal pregnancy lasts about 40 weeks, calculated from the first day of the last menstrual period.'),
('839be3fe-8466-5af1-89f9-1f51ade651f8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Which vessels does the normal umbilical cord contain?', '["One artery and two veins","Two arteries and two veins","Two arteries and one vein","One artery and one vein","Three arteries and one vein"]'::jsonb, 'Two arteries and one vein', 'The normal umbilical cord contains two umbilical arteries carrying deoxygenated blood to the placenta and one umbilical vein returning oxygenated blood to the fetus.'),
('6d24b852-ceaa-5593-91a8-8ad9b1b411c4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Lactation is stimulated by:', '["Progesterone only resulting in decreased functional activity","hCG only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Estrogen only mediated by specific receptor activation","Prolactin (for milk production) and oxytocin (for ejection)","FSH and LH leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Prolactin (for milk production) and oxytocin (for ejection)', 'Prolactin stimulates milk synthesis by the mammary alveoli, and oxytocin triggers milk ejection through the milk-let-down reflex.'),
('6a3b6fd8-8af1-5bfc-a7c6-9924096f56ba'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'How is menopause defined?', '["12 consecutive months without menstruation","6 months without menstruation","Irregular menstruation","Cessation of menstruation at age 50","Absence of ovulation"]'::jsonb, '12 consecutive months without menstruation', 'Menopause is defined retrospectively as 12 consecutive months of amenorrhea without other causes, marking the permanent cessation of ovarian function.'),
('cb077c1c-2f1b-5d0e-91e6-d78114555c0b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'The average age of menopause is:', '["40 years","51 years","55 years","45 years","60 years"]'::jsonb, '51 years', 'Menopause, the permanent cessation of menstruation, typically occurs at a mean age of about 51 years. Age 51 represents the population average despite a wide normal range.'),
('32f4bd65-90d4-5294-bd85-09a8fea9dc7c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'The corpus luteum functions for:', '["7 days resulting in decreased functional activity","Entire menstrual cycle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","21 days mediated by specific receptor activation","28 days without affecting the primary tissue pathway","About 14 days (if no pregnancy)"]'::jsonb, 'About 14 days (if no pregnancy)', 'In the absence of pregnancy, the corpus luteum degenerates about 14 days after ovulation, and the resulting progesterone withdrawal triggers menstruation. With hCG support from a fertilized ovum, it persists beyond this period.'),
('dde7aec0-2e41-5e94-9237-c7f9a6df281f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'The endometrium is at its thickest during:', '["Ovulation","Follicular phase","Proliferative phase","Secretory phase (luteal phase)","Menstrual phase"]'::jsonb, 'Secretory phase (luteal phase)', 'Progesterone from the corpus luteum drives glandular secretion and vascular proliferation, making the endometrium thickest in the secretory (luteal) phase. During the proliferative phase it is still relatively thin.'),
('44bd7e5f-9613-563d-8577-70f7bbdba921'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'The cervix produces mucus that is most favorable to sperm during:', '["During pregnancy","Menstruation","Ovulation (estrogen peak)","Luteal phase","After menopause"]'::jsonb, 'Ovulation (estrogen peak)', 'The mid-cycle estrogen peak makes cervical mucus copious, thin, and watery, creating an environment that favors sperm transport. Under progesterone dominance the mucus becomes thick and impenetrable.'),
('4d0f3357-caa2-5c93-b9ea-14b380c1df35'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Fertilization normally occurs in the:', '["Ampulla of fallopian tube","Vagina accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cervix mediated by specific receptor activation","Uterus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ovary leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ampulla of fallopian tube', 'Sperm and the secondary oocyte normally meet and fuse in the ampulla, the widest lateral portion of the fallopian tube. Implantation then follows in the uterus.'),
('04f95e0a-1c53-549f-aaf1-0230da6c7f7f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Implantation normally occurs in the:', '["Cervix","Vagina","Ovary","Fallopian tube","Uterus (endometrium)"]'::jsonb, 'Uterus (endometrium)', 'The blastocyst implants in the endometrium of the uterus, typically along the posterior-superior wall. Implantation at any other site is ectopic and nonsurvivable.'),
('c02c567c-50d6-5c1c-aeab-09688149ac86'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'The blastocyst implants about:', '["6-7 days after fertilization","14 days after fertilization","10 days after fertilization","3 days after fertilization","Immediately after fertilization"]'::jsonb, '6-7 days after fertilization', 'The blastocyst reaches the uterine cavity around day 4-5 and begins implantation about 6-7 days after fertilization. Implantation is usually complete within the following days.'),
('c2d953fe-d89c-534c-b0f0-4c67168079b9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'The sex of the baby is determined by:', '["Sperm (X vs Y chromosome)","Maternal diet","Both egg and sperm equally","Egg (X chromosome)","Environmental factors"]'::jsonb, 'Sperm (X vs Y chromosome)', 'Males are the heterogametic sex, so the sperm''sX or Y chromosome determines the offspring''s sex. The egg always contributes an X chromosome.'),
('4d9a8828-f356-5531-b68a-8f4f5d96f388'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'The SRY gene is located on:', '["Autosome 1","Autosome 22","Mitochondrial DNA","X chromosome","Y chromosome"]'::jsonb, 'Y chromosome', 'SRY, the testis-determining gene, resides on the short arm of the Y chromosome. Its presence drives differentiation of the indifferent gonad into a testis.'),
('f2ec7d04-fa05-5aef-aa69-04e173835779'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Testosterone is produced by:', '["Sertoli cells","Prostate","Leydig cells (testes)","Seminiferous tubules","Epididymis"]'::jsonb, 'Leydig cells (testes)', 'Leydig cells, located in the interstitial tissue of the testes, synthesize testosterone under LH stimulation. FSH acts on Sertoli cells rather than producing testosterone.'),
('922b5a2b-3ebd-51aa-a4a3-af43a0303532'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Sertoli cells function to:', '["Produce seminal fluid resulting in decreased functional activity","Produce testosterone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Support spermatogenesis and produce inhibin","Store sperm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Secrete FSH leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Support spermatogenesis and produce inhibin', 'Sertoli cells nurture developing germ cells, support spermatogenesis, and secrete inhibin and androgen-binding protein. They do not produce testosterone, FSH, or seminal fluid.'),
('530ecd2d-01d9-55d1-b26a-86cd86a4ca50'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Inhibin inhibits:', '["Testosterone production","LH secretion","FSH secretion","Progesterone production","Estrogen production"]'::jsonb, 'FSH secretion', 'Inhibin, released by Sertoli cells and ovarian granulosa cells, feeds back to selectively suppress FSH secretion from the anterior pituitary. LH and gonadal steroidogenesis are largely unaffected.'),
('e5b1beea-3ae4-58c8-befe-91dfd3b3b731'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'The male gonad is the:', '["Testis","Seminal vesicle","Prostate","Vas deferens","Epididymis"]'::jsonb, 'Testis', 'The testis is the male gonad, producing sperm and testosterone. The remaining structures are accessory glands or ducts of the reproductive tract.'),
('ae9e46af-fb3c-5e0f-81e5-bcfc235e4e68'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'The female gonad is the:', '["Ovary","Uterus","Fallopian tube","Vagina","Mammary gland"]'::jsonb, 'Ovary', 'The ovary is the female gonad, producing ova and the steroids estrogen and progesterone. The other listed structures are organs of the reproductive tract or accessory tissue.'),
('9545e765-0b97-5eec-9c0f-cdaeca7720f7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'The process of egg formation is called:', '["Implantation","Ovulation","Oogenesis","Spermatogenesis","Fertilization"]'::jsonb, 'Oogenesis', 'Oogenesis is the formation of the female gamete (egg) within the ovary. Ovulation is the release of the oocyte, while fertilization is its union with a sperm.'),
('817da3db-3bf5-51c2-8e98-b411a9143398'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'In the study of Reproduction, CYP enzyme metabolising most drugs:', '["CYP3A4","CYP2D6","CYP2C9","CYP2C19","CYP1A2"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4', 'CYP3A4 metabolises 50% of clinically used drugs.'),
('6991ab19-05a4-503a-8566-56854fa1255a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'The female gamete is called:', '["Embryo","Sperm","Zygote","Fetus","Ovum"]'::jsonb, 'Ovum', 'The ovum (mature oocyte) is the female gamete. A zygote forms only after fertilization, while embryo and fetus denote later developmental stages.'),
('74aa8dc9-11dd-5e1d-9c48-7cde84331807'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Which option correctly describes Spermatogonia?', '["Spermatozoa","Haploid germ cells","Diploid stem cells (2n)","Leydig cells","Spermatids"]'::jsonb, 'Diploid stem cells (2n)', 'Spermatogonia are diploid (2n) germline stem cells on the basement membrane of the seminiferous tubules. They divide mitotically and give rise to primary spermatocytes.'),
('20a9bc4f-7f6a-58ef-9cac-f8539f8dd461'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Which cells undergo mitosis in spermatogenesis?', '["Spermatozoa","Haploid germ cells","Diploid stem cells (2n)","Leydig cells","Spermatids"]'::jsonb, 'Diploid stem cells (2n)', 'In spermatogenesis, only spermatogonia, the diploid (2n) stem cells, divide mitotically to preserve the germline pool. Haploid cells such as spermatids do not divide.'),
('221f9910-5054-5033-b324-bb16e42cb790'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Which option correctly describes Spermatids?', '["Stem cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Diploid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Secondary spermatocytes mediated by specific receptor activation","Haploid cells that undergo spermiogenesis (no division)","Spermatozoa leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Haploid cells that undergo spermiogenesis (no division)', 'Spermatids are haploid cells formed when meiosis is complete. Rather than dividing, they differentiate through spermiogenesis into spermatozoa.'),
('2e7e8c88-1bdc-5471-8517-6e1289ecfd1d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'What is the process of spermiogenesis?', '["Sperm motility resulting in decreased functional activity","Spermatid differentiation into mature sperm","Sperm cell division mediated by specific receptor activation","Sperm capacitation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sperm storage leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Spermatid differentiation into mature sperm', 'Spermiogenesis converts spermatids into structurally mature, flagellated spermatozoa. It is a differentiation step, not a cell division.'),
('1ba6be3a-dcd6-5dd7-b737-059841ae2e17'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'What is the primary function of Leydig cells?', '["Secrete inhibin and androgen-binding protein (ABP)","Produce testosterone","Phagocytose residual bodies","Form blood-testis barrier","Support spermatogenesis"]'::jsonb, 'Produce testosterone', 'Leydig cells are the interstitial endocrine cells of the testis, producing testosterone under LH stimulation. The remaining options are functions performed by Sertoli cells.'),
('d32137f4-053a-5acf-9a19-57b84f0d2e84'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction - Reproduction', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Reproduction: Phenytoin zero-order kinetics because:', '["CYP enzymes are saturated","Renal secretion saturated","Protein binding saturated","Gut absorption saturated","Biliary excretion saturated"]'::jsonb, 'CYP enzymes are saturated', 'CYP2C9/CYP3A4 become saturated at therapeutic concentrations.'),
('6644991a-bb9c-520f-8ee6-eec396026185'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Regarding clinical cases in Exercise Environment: What is the therapeutic index of a drug?', '["TD50 / ED50","ED50 / TD50","LD50 / ED50","ED50 / LD50","TD50 / LD50"]'::jsonb, 'TD50 / ED50', 'TI = TD50/ED50. Wider TI = safer drug.'),
('92dc780f-0d62-51b4-b163-8ee181423153'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Stroke volume increases during exercise up to:', '["Resting levels","50-60% of VO2max","100% of VO2max","Maximal exercise intensity","Anaerobic threshold"]'::jsonb, '50-60% of VO2max', 'Stroke volume rises progressively and then plateaus at approximately 50-60% of VO2max. Beyond this intensity, further increases in cardiac output are achieved mainly by a rising heart rate.'),
('2d6bc8b5-54c3-5559-ab39-63620f42663f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Muscle blood flow during exercise can increase:', '["2-fold resulting in decreased functional activity","10-fold accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","5-fold mediated by specific receptor activation","No change without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Up to 100-fold (functional vasodilation)"]'::jsonb, 'Up to 100-fold (functional vasodilation)', 'Active skeletal muscle blood flow can rise up to about 100-fold because of metabolic vasodilation (functional hyperemia). This matches the large increase in oxygen demand during heavy exercise.'),
('1bc114e9-e434-5842-a5fd-260a516977ae'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Sympathetic vasoconstriction in non-exercising tissues is mediated by:', '["Beta-2 receptors","Dopamine receptors","Histamine","Muscarinic receptors","Alpha-1 adrenergic receptors"]'::jsonb, 'Alpha-1 adrenergic receptors', 'Norepinephrine released by sympathetic nerves acts on alpha-1 adrenergic receptors to constrict arterioles in resting tissues. This diverts cardiac output to working skeletal muscle during exercise.'),
('047bb51b-3621-5d3f-b601-c8c3a9f6a024'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'During therapeutic management of Exercise Environment, CYP enzyme metabolising most drugs:', '["CYP3A4","CYP2D6","CYP2C9","CYP2C19","CYP1A2"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4', 'CYP3A4 metabolises 50% of clinically used drugs.'),
('ac3e944f-c19a-544f-aa7c-491a3ecda8cf'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'The cardiovascular drift phenomenon refers to:', '["Increased stroke volume resulting in decreased functional activity","Gradual increase in HR and decrease in SV during prolonged exercise at constant workload","Sudden drop in blood pressure mediated by specific receptor activation","Decreased heart rate without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Stable heart rate and stroke volume leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Gradual increase in HR and decrease in SV during prolonged exercise at constant workload', 'During prolonged constant-load exercise, heart rate slowly rises while stroke volume falls, a combination called cardiovascular drift. It is driven by rising core temperature, dehydration, and fluid loss.'),
('f0ff0468-901b-5c8c-bd1c-151f739db3e9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'VO2max is:', '["Maximum rate of oxygen consumption (measure of aerobic fitness)","Anaerobic threshold accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Maximum ventilation mediated by specific receptor activation","Maximal minute ventilation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Maximum heart rate leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Maximum rate of oxygen consumption (measure of aerobic fitness)', 'VO2max is the highest rate of oxygen consumption achievable during maximal whole-body exercise. It is the standard index of aerobic fitness.'),
('a9926c08-b2f8-5592-ab83-4123b171e568'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'VO2max is determined by:', '["Hemoglobin concentration resulting in decreased functional activity","Muscle mass accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cardiac output and arteriovenous O2 difference (Fick principle)","Ventilation only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Heart rate only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cardiac output and arteriovenous O2 difference (Fick principle)', 'By the Fick principle, VO2max equals cardiac output multiplied by the arteriovenous oxygen difference. Maximal cardiac output and tissue oxygen extraction are therefore the primary determinants.'),
('7600fd35-ef6c-5a9b-895f-fc5f5acd6186'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Exercise Environment: Grapefruit juice inhibits:', '["Intestinal CYP3A4","Hepatic CYP3A4","CYP2D6","Renal excretion","Protein binding"]'::jsonb, 'Intestinal CYP3A4', 'Furanocoumarins in grapefruit inhibit intestinal CYP3A4.'),
('350d2926-0cd7-57e5-b42a-6020643e006d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'The Fick equation is:', '["VO2 = Cardiac output x hemoglobin","VO2 = Cardiac output x (arterial O2 content - venous O2 content)","VO2 = Heart rate x blood pressure","VO2 = Stroke volume x heart rate","VO2 = Ventilation x O2%"]'::jsonb, 'VO2 = Cardiac output x (arterial O2 content - venous O2 content)', 'The Fick equation states that oxygen consumption equals cardiac output multiplied by the arteriovenous oxygen content difference. It links oxygen delivery to extraction by the tissues.'),
('e37a0e37-8930-52b8-93b1-a8573bfaa7b0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Regarding clinical cases in Exercise Environment: Zero-order kinetics means:', '["Constant amount eliminated/time","Constant fraction/time","Dose-dependent elimination","First-order at high doses","Saturable absorption"]'::jsonb, 'Constant amount eliminated/time', 'Zero-order: constant amount eliminated regardless of concentration.'),
('1d6cf624-96b5-59f4-b34f-dc5c0d1ca02f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'RER >1.0 during exercise indicates:', '["Fat utilization resulting in decreased functional activity","Aerobic metabolism accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hyperventilation (blowing off CO2) or lactic acidosis compensation","Carbohydrate utilization without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Steady state leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperventilation (blowing off CO2) or lactic acidosis compensation', 'A respiratory exchange ratio above 1.0 reflects carbon dioxide production exceeding oxygen consumption. This occurs during hyperventilation or when bicarbonate buffers lactic acid, generating extra CO2.'),
('95928dc9-c81e-5fd6-962d-2acb41538049'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Ventilation increases during exercise via:', '["Only neural resulting in decreased functional activity","Only CO2 increase accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only chemical mediated by specific receptor activation","Only O2 decrease without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Neural (central command) and humoral (chemoreflex) mechanisms"]'::jsonb, 'Neural (central command) and humoral (chemoreflex) mechanisms', 'Exercise hyperventilation is driven by feedforward signals from motor areas (central command) and by humoral chemoreflex input from arterial chemoreceptors sensing CO2, O2, and pH. Both neural and humoral mechanisms raise ventilation in proportion to work intensity.'),
('05a7426d-c36d-5391-8837-ed7d7661faac'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'The ventilatory equivalent for O2 (VE/VO2) is:', '["O2 pulse resulting in decreased functional activity","Minute ventilation per unit O2 consumption; used to detect anaerobic threshold","HR reserve mediated by specific receptor activation","Breathing reserve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tidal volume leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Minute ventilation per unit O2 consumption; used to detect anaerobic threshold', 'The ventilatory equivalent for O2 (VE/VO2) is the minute ventilation required per unit of oxygen consumed. A sharp rise in this ratio during incremental exercise marks the anaerobic threshold.'),
('4cd1df17-2725-52e0-ba2a-1a7a5dd3c5cd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Climbing many flights of stairs predominantly relies on skeletal muscle fibers characterized by:', '["Fast contraction and early fatigue","High oxidative capacity and fatigue resistance","Low mitochondrial content","High glycolytic capacity without mitochondria","Rapid fatigability with poor capillarization"]'::jsonb, 'High oxidative capacity and fatigue resistance', 'Prolonged submaximal activity such as stair climbing recruits slow-twitch type I fibers, which have high oxidative capacity and fatigue resistance. These fibers rely on abundant mitochondria, myoglobin, and capillaries for sustained aerobic ATP production.'),
('b0599f13-ec81-5add-a5ac-daf0e5974066'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Fast twitch (Type IIa) fibers are:', '["Very fatigable resulting in decreased functional activity","Fast oxidative-glycolytic (intermediate fatigue resistance)","Slow oxidative mediated by specific receptor activation","Found only in cardiac muscle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pure glycolytic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fast oxidative-glycolytic (intermediate fatigue resistance)', 'Type IIa fibers are fast-twitch fibers that use both oxidative and glycolytic metabolism, giving them intermediate fatigue resistance. They contrast with type IIb fibers, which are pure glycolytic and fatigue rapidly.'),
('06c2ca3f-485c-5c62-856e-7e29bcc9b60e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'In the study of Exercise Environment, Major binding protein for acidic drugs:', '["Albumin","Alpha-1 acid glycoprotein","Globulin","Transferrin","Ceruloplasmin"]'::jsonb, 'Albumin', 'Albumin binds acidic (anionic) drugs.'),
('623ab12c-b1ff-50a9-871f-f97682d8c18b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Aerobic training adaptations include all EXCEPT:', '["Increased stroke volume","Increased mitochondria and oxidative enzymes","Increased capillary density","Decreased resting HR","Increased fast glycolytic capacity"]'::jsonb, 'Increased fast glycolytic capacity', 'Aerobic training increases cardiac stroke volume, muscle capillaries, mitochondrial density, and oxidative enzyme content, and it lowers resting heart rate. Fast glycolytic capacity is an adaptation of anaerobic sprint or resistance training, not endurance exercise.'),
('03423f55-5fb2-5a78-ba8e-0d61c276362a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Resistance training primarily increases:', '["Mitochondrial density resulting in decreased functional activity","Muscle strength and muscle fiber cross-sectional area (hypertrophy)","VO2max mediated by specific receptor activation","Capillary density without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Oxidative enzymes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Muscle strength and muscle fiber cross-sectional area (hypertrophy)', 'Resistance training promotes muscle fiber hypertrophy and neural strength gains, increasing cross-sectional area and maximal force production. Aerobic measures such as VO2max, capillary density, and mitochondrial content are minimally affected.'),
('3ca68959-e2fa-5ee1-b61c-db9683e2a1d7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'What is the principle of specificity in exercise training?', '["Training adaptations are specific to the mode, intensity, and duration of exercise","All training modalities produce identical gains","Adaptations are confined entirely to the nervous system","Adaptations are independent of the muscles trained","Only cardiovascular, never muscular, adaptations occur"]'::jsonb, 'Training adaptations are specific to the mode, intensity, and duration of exercise', 'The specificity principle holds that physiological adaptations mirror the exact training stimulus: mode, intensity, duration, and the muscle groups used. Endurance work produces different adaptations than high-load strength training.'),
('ca50b2aa-fcdd-58ca-bd25-ba5ede2d728f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Detraining leads to:', '["Loss of training adaptations (reversible)","Increased fitness accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Enhanced performance mediated by specific receptor activation","No change without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Maintained gains leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Loss of training adaptations (reversible)', 'Cessation of training causes partial reversal of exercise adaptations such as decreased VO2max, reduced capillary density, and reduced oxidative enzyme activity. These losses are reversible with resumption of training.'),
('d5082899-eeb7-535a-8f8f-2b9024695a19'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'What is the primary consequence of overtraining syndrome?', '["Increased infection risk","Fatigue","Improved performance","Mood disturbances","Decreased performance"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased performance', 'Overtraining syndrome is characterized by a persistent decline in performance despite continued training. Fatigue, mood changes, and increased infection risk accompany the performance decrement.'),
('47cf379e-b399-53e3-aa91-da5fe27a5d98'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'The O2 deficit at exercise onset is:', '["Steady state resulting in decreased functional activity","Oxygen debt accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","EPOC mediated by specific receptor activation","Lag in O2 consumption vs demand at start of exercise (anaerobic component)","VO2max leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lag in O2 consumption vs demand at start of exercise (anaerobic component)', 'At exercise onset, oxygen uptake rises gradually while demand increases immediately, creating an O2 deficit met by anaerobic energy sources. The excess oxygen consumed during recovery, repaying this deficit, is termed oxygen debt or EPOC.'),
('2053b1b0-621d-55da-857f-093e11167087'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'EPOC is increased by:', '["Sleep resulting in decreased functional activity","High intensity and long duration exercise","Low intensity exercise mediated by specific receptor activation","Rest without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Short-duration exercise leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'High intensity and long duration exercise', 'Excess post-exercise oxygen consumption (EPOC) is larger after high-intensity and prolonged exercise because of greater disturbances in body temperature, catecholamines, and energy store restoration. Short, low-intensity efforts produce negligible EPOC.'),
('f7a62831-c961-51eb-8166-07f69826f789'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'What cardiovascular changes occur during prolonged steady-state exercise?', '["Decreasing HR resulting in decreased functional activity","Stable HR accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Increasing stroke volume mediated by specific receptor activation","Increasing HR and decreasing SV during prolonged steady-state exercise","Decreasing CO leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Increasing HR and decreasing SV during prolonged steady-state exercise', 'During prolonged constant-load exercise, heart rate gradually rises while stroke volume slowly falls, a pattern known as cardiovascular drift. These reciprocal changes help maintain cardiac output as dehydration and rising core temperature develop.'),
('6642dfe9-c37f-5656-ae9b-d735ad49f633'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Endothelium-derived nitric oxide (NO) causes:', '["Vasodilation (increases blood flow)","Platelet aggregation","Bronchoconstriction","Vasoconstriction","Smooth muscle contraction"]'::jsonb, 'Vasodilation (increases blood flow)', 'Nitric oxide diffuses into vascular smooth muscle and activates guanylyl cyclase, raising cGMP and causing vasodilation. It thereby increases regional blood flow and also inhibits platelet adhesion.'),
('3faddd4e-beb6-53f0-a70f-6f191b77cf01'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Exercise training improves:', '["Endothelial dysfunction","Oxidative stress","Endothelial function (NO bioavailability)","Insulin resistance","Inflammation"]'::jsonb, 'Endothelial function (NO bioavailability)', 'Regular exercise increases shear stress on the endothelium, upregulating eNOS and improving nitric oxide bioavailability. The resulting improvement in endothelium-dependent vasodilation underlies enhanced blood flow and vascular health.'),
('a8e2f4f0-3385-5b99-9d04-43488224a9a9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'The exercise pressor reflex originates from:', '["Chemoreceptors resulting in decreased functional activity","Cold receptors accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Group III and IV afferents in contracting muscle","Central command without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Baroreceptors leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Group III and IV afferents in contracting muscle', 'The exercise pressor reflex arises from mechanically and chemically sensitive group III and IV afferents in the working muscle. Their activation raises arterial pressure and heart rate in proportion to muscle work.'),
('82271385-c4fc-5f7b-b3d6-7e44bcc4434f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Central command refers to:', '["Chemoreflex resulting in decreased functional activity","Peripheral afferents accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Spinal reflex mediated by specific receptor activation","Baroreflex without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Feedforward neural signals from motor cortex to CV and respiratory centers"]'::jsonb, 'Feedforward neural signals from motor cortex to CV and respiratory centers', 'Central command is the feedforward neural signal from motor areas of the cortex that activates cardiovascular and respiratory centers in parallel with muscle contraction. It anticipates the metabolic needs of exercise before peripheral feedback develops.'),
('e3e627a0-752f-5203-8ccf-3c79c4719ced'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'A 30-year-old female complains of easy fatigability and muscle cramps during exercise. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["McArdle disease (myophosphorylase deficiency)","Myasthenia gravis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Multiple sclerosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Fibromyalgia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Rheumatoid arthritis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'McArdle disease (myophosphorylase deficiency)', 'Easy fatigability with muscle cramps on exertion suggests a metabolic myopathy, and McArdle disease (myophosphorylase deficiency) is the classic cause. Inability to break down muscle glycogen impairs anaerobic ATP production during exercise.'),
('e631ac9f-cc6f-5b6b-b390-004cfff447ca'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Exercise in a hot environment causes:', '["Hypothermia","Cutaneous vasoconstriction","Decreased sweating","Bradycardia","Increased skin blood flow"]'::jsonb, 'Increased skin blood flow', 'Heat stress during exercise causes cutaneous vasodilation, increasing skin blood flow to dissipate heat. This redirects cardiac output toward the skin and can limit blood flow available to exercising muscle.'),
('f6a1a306-c764-5365-8635-0720ed80b56e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'In the study of Exercise Environment, Acetaminophen antidote:', '["N-acetylcysteine","Flumazenil","Naloxone","Fomepizole","Deferoxamine"]'::jsonb, 'N-acetylcysteine', 'NAC replenishes glutathione stores.'),
('13393431-b275-563b-a132-e2702d1e8b36'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Exercise Environment: Protein binding fraction (fu) ranges:', '["0 to 1","-1 to 0","0 to 100","-100 to 100","1 to 100"]'::jsonb, '0 to 1', 'Only unbound (free) drug is pharmacologically active.'),
('d12dad96-72f8-5eac-80f7-6624a4b75895'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Acute exposure to high altitude causes:', '["Apnea resulting in decreased functional activity","Decreased ventilation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Increased ventilation (hypoxic ventilatory response)","Hypoventilation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Decreased heart rate leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Increased ventilation (hypoxic ventilatory response)', 'Acute hypoxia at altitude stimulates peripheral chemoreceptors, producing the hypoxic ventilatory response with increased ventilation. This raises alveolar PO2 and helps preserve arterial oxygen saturation.'),
('aafcab88-1738-5bfc-b0bd-c6dbc5e77641'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Acclimatization to high altitude includes:', '["Increased ventilation","Decreased ventilation","Reduced erythrocyte production","Decreased 2,3-BPG levels","Reduced capillary density"]'::jsonb, 'Increased ventilation', 'Acclimatization augments ventilation through the hypoxic ventilatory response and, over days to weeks, raises red blood cell mass, 2,3-BPG, and capillary density to improve oxygen delivery.'),
('9806592d-8dcc-5fe8-8945-3396c0c1e07f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'A 35-year-old male recently traveled to a high-altitude area and presents with headache and dyspnea. Which etiological factor is primarily responsible for the adaptive increase in red blood cell production?', '["Renal hypoxia -> HIF-1alpha -> EPO production","3-BPG","Increased blood volume","Increased renal oxygen delivery","Decreased ventilatory drive"]'::jsonb, 'Renal hypoxia -> HIF-1alpha -> EPO production', 'The fall in arterial PO2 at altitude is sensed as renal hypoxia by peritubular cells, which stabilize HIF-1alpha and upregulate EPO transcription. The resulting rise in EPO stimulates erythropoiesis and increases oxygen-carrying capacity.'),
('c5b96008-ef35-5238-9ae6-3c9da0fd56f6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'A 25-year-old male presents with cyanosis and dyspnea after arrival at high altitude. What is the likely cause?', '["Pulmonary vasoconstriction (which can cause pulmonary hypertension)","Pulmonary vasodilation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Decreased pulmonary pressure mediated by specific receptor activation","Bronchodilation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","No change leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pulmonary vasoconstriction (which can cause pulmonary hypertension)', 'Alveolar hypoxia triggers pulmonary arteriolar vasoconstriction via the Euler-Liljestrand reflex. When generalized and sustained, this raises pulmonary vascular resistance and pressure, causing pulmonary hypertension at altitude.'),
('d6f9f326-de10-5f15-990d-1c6f98209a35'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'What is the most common symptom of altitude sickness?', '["Nausea","Dizziness","Fatigue","Headache","Cough"]'::jsonb, 'Headache', 'Headache is the most frequent symptom of acute mountain sickness, typically appearing within hours of ascent above 2500 m. Nausea, dizziness, fatigue, and cough occur less consistently.'),
('5438cbc0-b1fe-5783-a704-d00b5bd6fdf7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Exercise Environment: Primary site of first-pass metabolism:', '["Liver","Kidney","Lung","Intestine","Spleen"]'::jsonb, 'Liver', 'Orally absorbed drugs enter portal circulation and are metabolised by the liver first.'),
('671ee603-84e0-5ac2-a552-e25381313f17'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'A 35-year-old male recently traveled to a high-altitude area and presents with headache and dyspnea. Which finding most strongly suggests progression to high-altitude cerebral edema (HACE)?', '["Papilledema","Nausea","Ataxia","Mild headache","Altered consciousness"]'::jsonb, 'Ataxia', 'Truncal ataxia is the classic early sign of progression to high-altitude cerebral edema (HACE), preceding frank altered consciousness and papilledema. Nausea and mild headache are features of ordinary acute mountain sickness.'),
('0927c41e-02dc-5b2e-a151-fb8cabc2625e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'A 25-year-old male recently traveled to a high-altitude area and presents with headache and dyspnea. What mechanism sustains the ventilatory response during acclimatization?', '["Blocking EPO resulting in decreased functional activity","Decreasing ventilation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Direct vasodilation mediated by specific receptor activation","Increasing HR without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Causing metabolic acidosis (increases HCO3- excretion) which stimulates ventilation"]'::jsonb, 'Causing metabolic acidosis (increases HCO3- excretion) which stimulates ventilation', 'The kidneys excrete bicarbonate to compensate for the respiratory alkalosis of hypoxic hyperventilation. The resulting fall in plasma HCO3- produces a metabolic acidosis that removes the ventilatory brake and maintains the increased ventilation.'),
('30bd7cc5-8b6c-5754-b94c-a877e9bd21de'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Dexamethasone is used for:', '["Hypertension resulting in decreased functional activity","HAPE accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bronchospasm mediated by specific receptor activation","Severe AMS and HACE (reduces cerebral edema)","Polycythemia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Severe AMS and HACE (reduces cerebral edema)', 'Dexamethasone treats severe acute mountain sickness and high-altitude cerebral edema by reducing cerebral vasogenic edema. It is not indicated for HAPE or polycythemia.'),
('bb5d7318-da9e-58b5-941e-f1b3b5adcb50'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Nifedipine is used for:', '["HAPE (pulmonary vasodilator)","AMS accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hypertension mediated by specific receptor activation","HACE without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Polycythemia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'HAPE (pulmonary vasodilator)', 'Nifedipine treats and prevents high-altitude pulmonary edema by lowering pulmonary artery pressure through pulmonary vasodilation. It is not first-line for AMS, HACE, or polycythemia.'),
('1a8a6fb3-6f01-538e-9607-3fd1db5eaa0f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Chronic mountain sickness (Monge disease) is characterized by:', '["Excessive polycythemia","Pulmonary hypertension","Acute mountain sickness","Right heart failure","Decreased hematocrit"]'::jsonb, 'Excessive polycythemia', 'Monge disease is marked by excessive erythrocytosis, with hemoglobin usually above 19 g/dL, driven by a blunted ventilatory response and sustained EPO stimulation. The resultant blood hyperviscosity causes headache, fatigue, and thromboembolic risk.'),
('68f74458-97b3-5408-8898-414a45cba4f6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Rapid ascent to altitude without acclimatization can cause:', '["Improved performance","Acute mountain sickness","Increased plasma volume","Chronic mountain sickness","Increased arterial PO2"]'::jsonb, 'Acute mountain sickness', 'Ascending too rapidly overwhelms compensatory mechanisms and precipitates acute mountain sickness within hours, with headache, anorexia, and nausea. Staged ascent with gradual gain is the main preventive measure.'),
('267ed12d-1190-5c42-bc09-bfbad78008de'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'A 45-year-old female presents with fatigue and pallor due to anemia. Which compensatory change in oxygen-hemoglobin affinity would you expect?', '["Increased 2,3-BPG","Decreased 2,3-BPG","Increased temperature","Increased H+ concentration","Decreased cardiac output"]'::jsonb, 'Increased 2,3-BPG', 'In anemia and chronic hypoxia, increased 2,3-BPG shifts the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve rightward, promoting oxygen unloading in tissues. Reduced hemoglobin mass lowers total oxygen-carrying capacity, but right-shifting improves tissue delivery.'),
('0264f5ec-2a73-52ea-aa01-4f1b307026c9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'The Bohr effect refers to:', '["Temperature effect resulting in decreased functional activity","Hb binding to CO accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Increased O2 affinity with acidosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Decreased O2 affinity with alkalosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Decreased Hb O2 affinity with decreased pH (increased CO2/H+)"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased Hb O2 affinity with decreased pH (increased CO2/H+)', 'The Bohr effect is the rightward shift of the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve when CO2 and H+ rise and pH falls. The reduced O2 affinity promotes oxygen unloading in metabolically active tissues.'),
('0425580c-cba3-5532-bcba-ff6be2270cc6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'The Haldane effect refers to:', '["Increased O2 transport","Decreased CO2 transport","Increased CO2 affinity of oxygenated Hb","Increased CO2 transport with deoxygenated Hb","Bohr effect"]'::jsonb, 'Increased CO2 transport with deoxygenated Hb', 'The Haldane effect is the greater CO2-carrying capacity of deoxygenated compared with oxygenated hemoglobin. Deoxyhemoglobin binds H+ and forms carbamino compounds more readily, so at the same PCO2 venous blood carries more CO2.'),
('be4d8caf-72d7-52ad-a4b3-9975ac5d099f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'For each 1000 m gained above 1500 m, by how much does maximal oxygen consumption (VO2max) typically fall on acute exposure to altitude?', '["No change resulting in decreased functional activity","About 10% per 1000 m above 1500 m","About 25% per 1000 m mediated by specific receptor activation","About 50% without affecting the primary tissue pathway","About 1% leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'About 10% per 1000 m above 1500 m', 'Above 1500 m, VO2max falls by roughly 10% per 1000 m of gained elevation because lower barometric pressure reduces ambient and arterial PO2, limiting maximal oxygen delivery. The reduction is greater during acute, unacclimatized exposure.'),
('c3b6c052-62af-59d0-b863-b5cc83535cd7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'During therapeutic management of Exercise Environment, Phase II conjugation reaction:', '["Glucuronidation","Oxidation","Reduction","Hydrolysis","Deamination"]'::jsonb, 'Glucuronidation', 'Phase II: glucuronidation, sulfation, acetylation, methylation, glutathione conjugation.'),
('2cef7de0-abd1-525a-9bd3-985cea6520ab'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'What is the best description of physiologic acclimatization to altitude?', '["Gradual physiologic adaptation to hypoxia over days to weeks","Genetic mutation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Permanent change mediated by specific receptor activation","Immediate adaptation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Acute reflex response leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Gradual physiologic adaptation to hypoxia over days to weeks', 'Acclimatization is a reversible set of physiological adjustments to chronic hypoxia that develops over days to weeks, including hyperventilation, polycythemia, increased 2,3-BPG, and capillary proliferation. It is distinct from immediate reflex responses such as the acute ventilatory increase.'),
('278964d7-d03f-5725-ad58-a27bfde93987'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Pulmonary hypertension at altitude is due to:', '["Pulmonary emboli resulting in decreased functional activity","Hypercapnia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Left heart failure mediated by specific receptor activation","Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction (Euler-Liljestrand mechanism)","Increased flow leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction (Euler-Liljestrand mechanism)', 'Hypoxia constricts small pulmonary arterioles through the Euler-Liljestrand mechanism, raising pulmonary vascular resistance and pressure. Sustained hypoxic vasoconstriction during altitude residence produces pulmonary hypertension.'),
('ec85fcaf-cf5e-5444-81eb-00610bd0cb3b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Increased capillary density at altitude:', '["Decreases CO","Decreases O2 delivery","Improves O2 diffusion to tissues","No significant effect","Increases blood pressure"]'::jsonb, 'Improves O2 diffusion to tissues', 'Angiogenesis at altitude increases capillary density and shortens the diffusion distance between blood and tissue. This improves oxygen diffusion and delivery to exercising muscle.'),
('d9e63beb-c2e3-5fe7-9052-24045c42ae5d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'At altitude, chronic hypoxia stabilizes HIF-1alpha. Which of its target genes is chiefly responsible for raising the oxygen-carrying capacity of blood?', '["EPO","Glycolytic enzymes","VEGF","Pulmonary surfactant","Corticotropin"]'::jsonb, 'EPO', 'Chronic hypoxia stabilizes HIF-1alpha, which transcriptionally induces erythropoietin (EPO) in renal interstitial cells. EPO drives erythropoiesis, raising hemoglobin and arterial oxygen-carrying capacity.'),
('0800568a-8b3e-5dc5-af7c-1466fadeedf1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'What is the molecular response to hypoxia that promotes new blood vessel formation?', '["Hyperoxia resulting in decreased functional activity","Alkalosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","HIF-1alpha (increases angiogenesis)","Acidosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hypocapnia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'HIF-1alpha (increases angiogenesis)', 'HIF-1alpha is the central hypoxia-inducible transcription factor that upregulates VEGF and promotes angiogenesis. It also induces EPO and glycolytic enzymes as part of the coordinated hypoxic response.'),
('f185c1c1-1382-5665-b128-67fc35b5a147'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Decompression sickness (DCS) is due to:', '["Nitrogen narcosis resulting in decreased functional activity","Barotrauma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Formation of nitrogen bubbles in tissues during ascent","Oxygen toxicity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CO2 narcosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Formation of nitrogen bubbles in tissues during ascent', 'Rapid ascent lowers ambient pressure so dissolved nitrogen comes out of solution and forms bubbles in tissues and venous blood. These bubbles obstruct vessels and produce the bends, pulmonary chokes, and neurologic sequelae.'),
('b1ff261f-3283-5f63-a740-6bc58605abec'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'What is a beneficial effect of moderate exercise on joints?', '["Pulmonary chokes","Improved joint function","Skin rash (cutis marmorata)","Joint pain (bends)","Neurologic symptoms (paralysis)"]'::jsonb, 'Improved joint function', 'Moderate exercise promotes synovial fluid circulation, cartilage nutrition, and strengthening of surrounding muscles and ligaments, improving joint function. Joint pain and skin rash are manifestations of decompression sickness, not benefits of exercise.'),
('48e8fc5a-af89-55c5-99de-39117d998eaf'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'The bends refers to:', '["Ear pain resulting in decreased functional activity","Decompression sickness in lungs accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Joint and limb pain from nitrogen bubbles","Neurologic DCS without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Skin bends leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Joint and limb pain from nitrogen bubbles', 'The bends is the joint and limb pain caused by nitrogen bubbles lodging in and around joints during ascent. It is the most common musculoskeletal manifestation of decompression sickness.'),
('40925945-7ebc-538d-a804-aa96145bde01'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Decompression sickness treatment is:', '["100% O2 and recompression in a hyperbaric chamber","Antibiotics accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","IV fluids only mediated by specific receptor activation","Bed rest without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Analgesics leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '100% O2 and recompression in a hyperbaric chamber', 'Definitive treatment of decompression sickness is 100% oxygen plus recompression in a hyperbaric chamber, which shrinks bubbles and forces nitrogen back into solution. Adjunctive fluids and analgesics do not replace recompression.'),
('29ce4cc6-6e09-53ad-81c3-b9706476fbe0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Nitrogen narcosis occurs at depths:', '["At surface resulting in decreased functional activity","Any depth accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Shallow depth mediated by specific receptor activation","Below 100 m without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Below 30-40 m (mental impairment like alcohol intoxication)"]'::jsonb, 'Below 30-40 m (mental impairment like alcohol intoxication)', 'Nitrogen narcosis begins at depths below roughly 30-40 m when breathing air because dissolved nitrogen impairs neuronal function. The resulting euphoria and cognitive impairment resemble alcohol intoxication.'),
('a4998ddf-beb0-578c-8b1b-13a67392e7e9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Symptoms of nitrogen narcosis include:', '["Euphoria","Seizures","Cough","Abdominal pain","Chest pain"]'::jsonb, 'Euphoria', 'Nitrogen narcosis produces euphoria and cognitive impairment resembling alcohol intoxication at depth, with progressive slowing of thought and coordination. These effects resolve promptly with ascent.'),
('ebd3f746-4aa4-57c7-a660-11dd2332952b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Nitrogen narcosis is:', '["Reversible upon ascent (no treatment needed except ascent)","Requires recompression accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fatal mediated by specific receptor activation","Painless without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Permanent leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Reversible upon ascent (no treatment needed except ascent)', 'Nitrogen narcosis reverses rapidly on ascent as tissue nitrogen partial pressure falls, so no treatment beyond ascent is required. It does not require recompression and is not permanent.'),
('aa087760-ebd5-5bb4-91e5-8ec0db740e17'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'O2 toxicity is prevented by:', '["Carbon monoxide resulting in decreased functional activity","Rapid ascent accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Deep diving mediated by specific receptor activation","Limiting PO2 exposure (using appropriate gas mixtures)","Breathing pure O2 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Limiting PO2 exposure (using appropriate gas mixtures)', 'Oxygen toxicity is avoided by keeping inspired PO2 below a safe threshold, using tailored gas mixtures and depth-time limits. Breathing pure O2 or deep diving raises oxygen exposure and the hazard.'),
('4a0eac25-491b-5085-a419-ece64ad5310c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'In deep diving gas mixtures, what is the principal benefit of substituting helium for nitrogen?', '["Reduce N2 narcosis (helium is less narcotic) and reduce density","Increase O2 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Increase N2 mediated by specific receptor activation","No significant effect without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Decrease O2 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Reduce N2 narcosis (helium is less narcotic) and reduce density', 'Helium is far less lipid-soluble than nitrogen and has a lower gas density, so replacing nitrogen with helium reduces narcosis and breathing work at depth. This allows deeper dives with better ventilation.'),
('554818f6-232f-59f4-9391-445b54db275d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Trimix (He-N2-O2) is used for:', '["Deep diving to balance narcosis","Shallow diving","Surface swimming","Prolonged breath-hold diving","Snorkeling"]'::jsonb, 'Deep diving to balance narcosis', 'Trimix is used for deep diving because replacing much of the nitrogen with helium limits nitrogen narcosis, while a reduced oxygen fraction avoids oxygen toxicity at depth. It is not needed for shallow or surface activities.'),
('99dbf5fc-f61f-5c95-9745-08908b385f66'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Which measure is most important for preventing decompression sickness during diving?', '["Oxygen use resulting in decreased functional activity","Safe ascent rates and decompression stops to prevent DCS","Depth limits mediated by specific receptor activation","Gas mixing without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Descent rates leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Safe ascent rates and decompression stops to prevent DCS', 'Decompression sickness is prevented by observing safe ascent rates and scheduled decompression stops, which allow inert gas (nitrogen) to be eliminated gradually through the lungs. Rapid ascent promotes bubble formation.'),
('6ac56a05-645e-5fd1-9b10-dd6c670d5909'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Decompression stops are needed to:', '["Prevent O2 toxicity resulting in decreased functional activity","Allow N2 to be eliminated gradually via lungs","Increase bottom time mediated by specific receptor activation","Prevent HPNS without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Reduce N2 narcosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Allow N2 to be eliminated gradually via lungs', 'Decompression stops allow staged, gradual ascent so nitrogen is washed out of tissues incrementally and eliminated via the lungs without forming bubbles. Skipping stops raises the risk of decompression sickness.'),
('4919e984-ae3e-54a7-88d8-b7a7cf451103'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'The no-decompression limit is:', '["Maximum time at depth without requiring decompression stops","Time to N2 narcosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Maximum depth mediated by specific receptor activation","Time to O2 toxicity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Minimum depth leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Maximum time at depth without requiring decompression stops', 'The no-decompression limit is the maximum time a diver can remain at a given depth and still return directly to the surface without mandatory decompression stops. Exceeding it requires staged ascent or stops.'),
('37a93fc9-85e9-53ae-9c0b-0852224090b5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Pulmonary barotrauma during ascent is due to:', '["Breath-holding -> lung overexpansion -> pneumothorax or air embolism","N2 bubbles accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ear squeeze mediated by specific receptor activation","No significant effect without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Boyle''s law: gas expands during ascent leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Breath-holding -> lung overexpansion -> pneumothorax or air embolism', 'During ascent ambient pressure falls, so gas volume expands (Boyle''s law); if the diver breath-holds, the expanding gas overinflates the lungs and can rupture alveoli, producing pneumothorax or pneumomediastinum. Entry of air into the pulmonary veins then risks arterial gas embolism.'),
('80f23b67-a635-5ff5-9c87-9d19ca8ca756'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Which statement best describes arterial gas embolism (AGE) during ascent?', '["N2 bubbles in tissues resulting in decreased functional activity","Air enters pulmonary veins -> coronary/cerebral arteries -> stroke/MI","CO2 bubbles mediated by specific receptor activation","O2 bubbles without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Same as decompression sickness leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Air enters pulmonary veins -> coronary/cerebral arteries -> stroke/MI', 'Ruptured alveoli allow air to enter the pulmonary veins and left heart, from which it travels to the coronary and cerebral arteries. Occlusion of these vessels produces focal ischemia presenting as stroke or myocardial infarction.'),
('ac43ebb4-d02a-5c7e-85f4-400f4ea9c872'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'The treatment of arterial gas embolism (AGE) is:', '["Beta blockers resulting in decreased functional activity","Antibiotics accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Immediate recompression (hyperbaric O2)","IV fluids without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Aspirin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Immediate recompression (hyperbaric O2)', 'AGE is a life-threatening diving emergency treated by immediate recompression in a hyperbaric chamber at surface, which shrinks the air bubbles and restores blood flow. Delaying recompression worsens ischemic injury.'),
('5d171dc9-0a90-5e60-bdbc-76fcb3e1fe61'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Which statement best describes carbon monoxide (CO) poisoning during diving?', '["Increased arterial pH resulting in decreased functional activity","Increased oxygen delivery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","No significant effect mediated by specific receptor activation","Decreased CO2 production without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CO has 200-250x higher Hb affinity than O2 -> tissue hypoxia"]'::jsonb, 'CO has 200-250x higher Hb affinity than O2 -> tissue hypoxia', 'CO binds hemoglobin with roughly 200-250 times the affinity of oxygen and shifts the oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve left, so tissue hypoxia occurs even when arterial O2 tension is normal. Carboxyhemoglobin also impairs O2 unloading in tissues.'),
('9407de13-4cb1-5daa-9312-3aa7bb755786'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'The most effective treatment for carbon monoxide poisoning is:', '["Helium resulting in decreased functional activity","Inhaled CO2 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","100% normobaric O2 or hyperbaric O2","Room air without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Nitrogen leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '100% normobaric O2 or hyperbaric O2', '100% oxygen displaces CO from hemoglobin by mass action, shortening carboxyhemoglobin half-life from about 4-5 hours to roughly an hour; hyperbaric oxygen accelerates this further and is used for severe cases.'),
('93be13ab-213e-59f0-b2df-454b7ec3708c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'A 45-year-old woman presents with joint pain and neurological symptoms shortly after scuba diving. Which etiological factor is primarily responsible?', '["Cardiac arrhythmias resulting in decreased functional activity","Nitrogen bubble formation in tissues during ascent","Muscle incoordination mediated by specific receptor activation","Death without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Impaired cognition leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Nitrogen bubble formation in tissues during ascent', 'Pain and neurological symptoms appearing after a dive indicate decompression sickness, caused by nitrogen bubbles forming in blood and tissues when ambient pressure falls during ascent. Bubble growth obstructs vessels and causes local ischemia.'),
('fd5989ee-f3be-54f7-819f-4ecf4cf4f10a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'A 60-year-old woman presents with hypothermia after cold exposure. Hypothermia is defined as a core body temperature of:', '["<30°C","<37°C","<35°C (95°F)","<36°C","<32°C"]'::jsonb, '<35°C (95°F)', 'Hypothermia is a core temperature below 35°C (95°F), at which heat loss exceeds heat production and thermoregulatory defenses become overwhelmed. Temperatures below 30°C and 28°C mark increasingly severe stages.'),
('db7d0967-add1-531d-be64-cfe0a74d3e3b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Which is the primary immediate physiological response to cold exposure?', '["Sweating","Vasoconstriction","Shivering","Vasodilation","Increased metabolic rate"]'::jsonb, 'Vasoconstriction', 'The immediate response to cold is cutaneous vasoconstriction, which redirects blood away from the skin to reduce heat loss. Shivering and increased metabolic rate follow only if heat conservation alone cannot maintain core temperature.'),
('6e74f28e-d949-5555-bec0-c27666735751'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Severe hypothermia (below 28°C) causes:', '["Hypotension","Loss of shivering","Bradycardia","Coma","Shivering"]'::jsonb, 'Loss of shivering', 'Below about 30°C shivering ceases because muscle metabolism and neuromuscular activity are depressed, removing the body''s main heat-generating mechanism. This marks the transition to severe hypothermia along with bradycardia, hypotension, and coma.'),
('04138f5f-45ba-5fa0-864d-4c270a84894a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'What is the critical body temperature below which ventricular fibrillation and cardiac arrest are common in hypothermia?', '["32°C (89.6°F)","30°C (86°F)","28°C (82.4°F)","35°C (95°F)","37°C (98.6°F)"]'::jsonb, '28°C (82.4°F)', 'Below about 28°C myocardial irritability rises sharply and ventricular fibrillation becomes common, so cardiac arrest is frequent without active rewarming. This is the level at which death is expected without intervention.'),
('c29d96ee-7526-54cc-befa-7e6ca939ec51'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'When rewarming a patient with prolonged hypothermia, which approach should be avoided?', '["Warm blankets resulting in decreased functional activity","Passive rewarming accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Slow rewarming mediated by specific receptor activation","Heated IV fluids without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Rapid rewarming (may cause afterdrop and arrhythmias)"]'::jsonb, 'Rapid rewarming (may cause afterdrop and arrhythmias)', 'Rapid aggressive rewarming of a chronically hypothermic patient can cause afterdrop (further central cooling) and provoke ventricular arrhythmias. Slow, controlled rewarming is preferred in prolonged exposure.'),
('383ed151-3fb0-55e5-8f33-fb7f09fbef62'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Hyperthermia is defined as a core temperature of:', '[">42°C",">40.5°C (105°F)",">38°C",">37°C",">39°C"]'::jsonb, '>40.5°C (105°F)', 'Hyperthermia is a core temperature above 40.5°C (105°F) caused by failure of heat dissipation, as in heat stroke. It differs from fever, in which the hypothalamic set point is raised.'),
('a420b3b6-8c0e-5cf4-88e8-2e9553259c7f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'A 65-year-old woman presents with cardiac arrhythmia. Electrolytes show K+ 3.0 mEq/L. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Hypokalemia","Hyperkalemia","Hyponatremia","Hypercalcemia","Hypoglycemia"]'::jsonb, 'Hypokalemia', 'A serum potassium of 3.0 mEq/L (normal 3.5-5.0) defines hypokalemia, which increases myocardial automaticity and predisposes to arrhythmias including ectopy and ventricular tachycardia.'),
('58732d6c-a288-523d-9423-76266502db18'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Crush syndrome occurs after:', '["Hemorrhage resulting in decreased functional activity","Minor injury accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Burns mediated by specific receptor activation","Prolonged compression of muscle tissue (e.g. earthquake entrapment)","Dehydration leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Prolonged compression of muscle tissue (e.g. earthquake entrapment)', 'Crush syndrome follows prolonged compression of muscle, as in entrapment under debris, in which muscle ischemia and necrosis on release release myoglobin, potassium, and phosphate into the circulation.'),
('91f896d8-6683-5905-bea8-eb976b717b68'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'The central pathophysiologic process underlying crush syndrome is:', '["Rhabdomyolysis","Hyperkalemia","Acute kidney injury","Hypocalcemia","Hypercalcemia"]'::jsonb, 'Rhabdomyolysis', 'Crush syndrome is fundamentally skeletal muscle breakdown (rhabdomyolysis) with leakage of myoglobin, potassium, and phosphate from damaged cells. The hyperkalemia, hypocalcemia, and acute kidney injury are downstream consequences.'),
('14271513-a215-5c6d-8f73-36b06cb87a05'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Which electrolyte disturbance is a life-threatening complication of severe hypothermia?', '["Hypoglycemia resulting in decreased functional activity","Hyponatremia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hypokalemia mediated by specific receptor activation","Hypercalcemia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hyperkalemia (can cause cardiac arrest)"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperkalemia (can cause cardiac arrest)', 'In severe hypothermia, acidosis and altered membrane function allow potassium to shift out of cells, and the resulting hyperkalemia can trigger fatal ventricular arrhythmias. It is the electrolyte disturbance most likely to cause cardiac arrest.'),
('00579c72-3ab1-560c-a7ad-3cdac3ff5878'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'During therapeutic management of Exercise Environment, Phase I metabolism reaction:', '["Oxidation","Glucuronidation","Sulfation","Acetylation","Glutathione conjugation"]'::jsonb, 'Oxidation', 'Phase I: oxidation, reduction, hydrolysis.'),
('11f14cbd-e5ec-573e-886d-ca4e1ce485c3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'The most critical early complication of a major burn is:', '["Infection","Multi-organ failure","Hypermetabolism","Fluid loss","Respiratory injury"]'::jsonb, 'Fluid loss', 'In the first 48 hours, damaged capillaries leak fluid and protein into tissues and evaporative loss is massive, so hypovolemic (burn) shock from fluid loss is the immediate threat. Infection and multi-organ failure dominate in the later phase.'),
('b2ad5388-2358-56a3-8076-ed0a9e4239f3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'The hypermetabolic response to burns is characterized by:', '["Decreased metabolism","Protein catabolism","Increased metabolic rate","Insulin resistance","Anabolism"]'::jsonb, 'Increased metabolic rate', 'Major burns markedly raise resting energy expenditure, the hallmark of the hypermetabolic burn response. Protein catabolism and insulin resistance accompany this increased metabolic rate.'),
('b5d3ef92-aba2-5ade-ab39-3f3f08e8adba'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'A 40-year-old man presents with altered mental status. Electrolytes show Na+ 120 mEq/L. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Hyponatremia","Hypernatremia","Hypokalemia","Hyperkalemia","Hypocalcemia"]'::jsonb, 'Hyponatremia', 'A serum sodium of 120 mEq/L (normal 135-145) indicates severe hyponatremia. The resulting fall in plasma osmolality causes cerebral edema, presenting as confusion, lethargy, or seizures.'),
('28ad67f0-0ef9-5cc3-8019-1dd2f2a69eb2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'In the ATLS classification of hemorrhagic shock, a blood volume loss of less than 15% corresponds to:', '["Class I (<15% blood volume loss)","Class II (15-30% blood volume loss)","Class III (30-40% blood volume loss)","Class IV (>40% blood volume loss)","Class V (>50% blood volume loss)"]'::jsonb, 'Class I (<15% blood volume loss)', 'The ATLS classification grades hemorrhagic shock by blood loss: Class I <15%, Class II 15-30%, Class III 30-40%, and Class IV >40% of blood volume. Class I produces minimal signs because compensatory mechanisms are adequate.'),
('e9fe777b-53f7-5759-9912-fcf952a518ea'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Which hormonal response is primarily responsible for water conservation during hemorrhage?', '["Cortisol","Epinephrine","Renin","Increased ADH","Aldosterone"]'::jsonb, 'Increased ADH', 'Hemorrhage lowers blood volume and arterial pressure, strongly stimulating ADH (vasopressin) release, which increases renal water reabsorption to restore circulating volume. Renin, aldosterone, epinephrine, and cortisol also rise but are not the primary water-conserving mechanism.'),
('fff4b5ba-41d4-5c82-8c5e-833f6fe6b920'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'What is the hormonal response of the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis during exercise?', '["Cortisol","GH; decreased insulin","Glucagon","Increased ACTH","Catecholamines"]'::jsonb, 'Increased ACTH', 'Exercise activates the HPA axis, increasing ACTH release from the anterior pituitary, which in turn stimulates adrenal cortisol secretion to provide metabolic support. This is the defining HPA-axis response during exercise.'),
('18a3d2f7-9dd0-5d12-8df6-241d579dfca2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Which one of the following is NOT a SIRS criterion?', '["Temperature >38°C or <36°C","Heart rate >90/min","Respiratory rate >20 breaths/min","PaO2 <60 mmHg","WBC >12,000 or <4,000/mm3"]'::jsonb, 'PaO2 <60 mmHg', 'SIRS is defined by two or more of: temperature >38°C or <36°C, heart rate >90/min, respiratory rate >20/min, or WBC >12,000 or <4,000/mm3. Hypoxemia (PaO2 <60 mmHg) is not one of the SIRS criteria.'),
('01650b40-6cff-52e0-86d8-8d53633e24ad'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Sepsis is:', '["SIRS alone","SIRS with documented infection","Bacteremia","Fever alone","Infection without symptoms"]'::jsonb, 'SIRS with documented infection', 'In the classic SIRS-based definition, sepsis is the systemic inflammatory response syndrome triggered by a documented infection. Bacteremia alone or a simple fever is insufficient without the systemic response.'),
('3b7ef505-bf8c-51c6-9adb-be0e88722a9f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Multiple organ dysfunction syndrome (MODS) is:', '["Mild, self-limited illness","Recovery phase of critical illness","Progressive organ dysfunction in severe sepsis/shock","A syndrome limited to the lungs","Single organ failure"]'::jsonb, 'Progressive organ dysfunction in severe sepsis/shock', 'MODS is progressive dysfunction of two or more organs in critically ill patients, most often the culmination of severe sepsis or shock. It carries high mortality when sustained organ failure develops.'),
('abf3c1f3-a0ed-5b5c-b65a-70b7277c0394'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'The acute phase response includes:', '["Increased CRP","Fibrinogen and decreased albumin","Ferritin","Decreased synthesis of all liver proteins","Transferrin"]'::jsonb, 'Increased CRP', 'During the acute phase response the liver raises synthesis of CRP, fibrinogen, ferritin, and haptoglobin while albumin and transferrin fall. Increased CRP is the hallmark positive acute-phase reactant.'),
('14cdfdc3-44ef-5000-b1ec-10023ddc229d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'What is the effect of elevated cortisol during prolonged exercise?', '["Stimulates gluconeogenesis and protein catabolism","Suppresses hepatic glucose production","Increases insulin secretion","Lowers blood glucose","Promotes glycogen synthesis"]'::jsonb, 'Stimulates gluconeogenesis and protein catabolism', 'Cortisol rises with prolonged exercise and stimulates gluconeogenesis, protein catabolism, and lipolysis while promoting insulin resistance, ensuring glucose availability for working muscle.'),
('fe606835-ba0e-5eae-8826-9a46e1bf76b8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'In the study of Exercise Environment, A 25-year-old overweight female with acne and hirsutism presents with irregular menstrual cycles. Which condition best explains the presentation?', '["Adenomyosis","Obesity-related irregularity","Cushing syndrome","Polycystic ovary syndrome","Endometrial polyp"]'::jsonb, 'Polycystic ovary syndrome', 'PCOS combines chronic anovulation with hyperandrogenism, producing irregular cycles, acne, and hirsutism. It is the leading cause of anovulatory menstrual irregularity in reproductive-age women.'),
('5554ea13-5de6-534a-bfdf-73a896585f91'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'The metabolic response to surgery is characterized by:', '["Immune modulation","Decreased anabolism","Increased catabolism","Anabolism only","Decreased metabolic rate"]'::jsonb, 'Increased catabolism', 'Surgical trauma provokes a stress response driven by cortisol, catecholamines, and cytokines that increases catabolism (protein breakdown, lipolysis, and glycogenolysis) to mobilize fuel for recovery.'),
('35c826c3-8aa7-50db-aa78-1aba9c5595dc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Which sequence best describes the processing of pain?', '["Only chemical stimuli resulting in decreased functional activity","Only mechanical stimuli accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Neurotransmitters mediated by specific receptor activation","Nociceptor activation -> transduction -> transmission -> modulation -> perception","Only thermal stimuli leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Nociceptor activation -> transduction -> transmission -> modulation -> perception', 'Pain processing begins with nociceptor activation by a noxious stimulus, followed by transduction into electrical activity, transmission to the CNS, spinal and supraspinal modulation, and finally conscious perception.'),
('1ee67bc9-14ef-5663-9c9f-439bc3917fae'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'The gate control theory of pain modulation involves:', '["Cortical gate resulting in decreased functional activity","Substantia gelatinosa (dorsal horn) gating pain transmission","Beta-endorphin release mediated by specific receptor activation","Spinal cord alone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thalamic gate leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Substantia gelatinosa (dorsal horn) gating pain transmission', 'Gate control theory holds that large-diameter (A-beta) afferents can inhibit nociceptive transmission through interneurons in the substantia gelatinosa of the dorsal horn, effectively closing the gate on pain signals.'),
('18ab2802-203c-5783-a2a3-d4901ecf8505'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Which laboratory marker is most sensitive for diagnosing rhabdomyolysis?', '["Hypocalcemia","Hyperkalemia","Myoglobinuria","Elevated CK","Acute kidney injury"]'::jsonb, 'Elevated CK', 'Creatine kinase leaks in large amounts from damaged muscle, so a markedly elevated serum CK is the most sensitive biochemical marker for rhabdomyolysis. Myoglobinuria, hyperkalemia, and hypocalcemia are secondary features.'),
('197bbbf9-392d-5178-839e-e54e2b13543b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Which statement best describes tissue injury from electrical current?', '["Deep tissue damage (often worse than visible)","Arrhythmias accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Superficial burns only mediated by specific receptor activation","Tetany without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Injury confined to the entry wound leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Deep tissue damage (often worse than visible)', 'Electrical current travels along low-resistance pathways such as vessels and nerves, heating tissue internally, so deep muscle and vascular damage is often far worse than the small visible entry and exit wounds.'),
('0656f383-ae20-56e4-9e74-0380d919b0c9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Which cardiac arrhythmia is characteristically produced by a lightning strike?', '["Burns only","Asystole","Neurologic injury","Tympanic membrane rupture","Rhabdomyolysis"]'::jsonb, 'Asystole', 'A lightning strike delivers a massive direct current through the body, depolarizing the myocardium and characteristically producing asystole. Cardiac rhythm often returns spontaneously, but neurologic injury, burns, and tympanic membrane rupture are also recognized lightning-related injuries.'),
('3f09de0d-a374-5dc8-a589-776430258691'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Which sequence best describes the usual pathophysiology of drowning?', '["Laryngospasm -> hypoxia -> aspiration -> asphyxia","Aspiration without preceding hypoxia","Pure hyperoxia followed by respiratory alkalosis","No significant physiologic effect in either fresh or salt water","Hypoxia without subsequent aspiration"]'::jsonb, 'Laryngospasm -> hypoxia -> aspiration -> asphyxia', 'Drowning begins with reflex laryngospasm that produces hypoxia; as the victim loses consciousness the laryngospasm relaxes and water is aspirated, culminating in asphyxia. The physicochemical differences between fresh and salt water influence the volume and electrolyte shifts that follow.'),
('c50cde38-631d-5435-9186-c68f17e78818'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'What change in blood osmolality occurs when large amounts of fresh water are aspirated in a near-drowning episode?', '["Freshwater: hypotonic -> hemodilution","Seawater: hypertonic -> net water shift into the blood","Freshwater: hypertonic -> plasma concentration","Any aspiration leaves plasma osmolality unchanged","Freshwater: isotonic -> no change in osmolality"]'::jsonb, 'Freshwater: hypotonic -> hemodilution', 'Fresh water is hypotonic relative to plasma, so aspirated water moves into the intravascular compartment, producing hemodilution, hyponatremia, and transient hypervolemia. Aspirated seawater has the opposite effect, drawing water out of the blood and into the lungs.'),
('1bb110e3-4d62-5448-8350-0511cae632b9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'A 65-year-old male is pulled unresponsive from the water during a near-drowning event and is not breathing. What is the first step of resuscitation?', '["Start chest compressions first","Rescue breaths","Deliver supplemental oxygen","Intubate to manage ARDS","Rewarm to treat hypothermia"]'::jsonb, 'Rescue breaths', 'The primary insult in drowning is asphyxia, so resuscitation begins with opening the airway and delivering rescue breaths to restore oxygenation before chest compressions are started. Airway support takes priority because hypoxia, not cardiac rhythm, is the immediate threat.'),
('46a48bac-bdb4-5d2f-8166-d7120999134a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'A sedentary 60-year-old male undergoes an exercise stress test for chest pain; the test shows a normal heart-rate, blood-pressure, and ECG response, but he experiences marked leg fatigue at low workloads. What best explains his poor exercise tolerance?', '["Phosphofructokinase deficiency","Iron deficiency anemia","Neuromuscular junction disorder","Mitochondrial myopathy","Exercise intolerance due to deconditioning"]'::jsonb, 'Exercise intolerance due to deconditioning', 'Prolonged inactivity lowers cardiac and skeletal muscle oxidative reserve, so exertion produces early fatigue even when the cardiovascular response to exercise is normal. A sedentary patient with an unremarkable stress test most likely has deconditioning rather than a primary metabolic or neuromuscular disorder.'),
('011d696c-9f0f-55e1-9239-56d8f5fcc411'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'A 25-year-old male with easy fatigability during exertion undergoes an exercise stress test that shows a normal heart-rate and blood-pressure response but a peak oxygen uptake far below predicted. Which finding best explains his symptoms?', '["Decreased VO2 max","T wave inversion","ST segment elevation","Exercise-induced bronchospasm","Vasovagal syncope"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased VO2 max', 'VO2 max is the peak rate of oxygen consumption during maximal exercise, and a value well below predicted indicates reduced aerobic capacity that underlies exertional fatigue. The normal heart-rate and blood-pressure response argues against cardiac or syncopal causes.'),
('a58162d7-6b73-5567-ba38-6d7344041f51'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'A 45-year-old male has had fatigue and post-exertional malaise lasting more than 6 months; his exercise stress test and routine bloodwork are normal. Which condition best explains these symptoms?', '["Chronic fatigue syndrome","Anemia of chronic disease","Impaired lactate clearance","Mitochondrial myopathy","Hypothyroidism"]'::jsonb, 'Chronic fatigue syndrome', 'Chronic fatigue syndrome is characterized by persistent, debilitating fatigue and post-exertional malaise without a demonstrable organic abnormality on stress testing or routine laboratory evaluation. Normal testing in the setting of prolonged fatigue with post-exertional worsening supports this diagnosis.'),
('79926c28-4be1-5f63-950c-cc2aed32e18a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'A 45-year-old male reports easy fatigability with exertion; his exercise stress test shows a peak heart rate far below the age-predicted maximum despite maximal effort. Which condition best explains this finding?', '["First-degree AV block","Hypertensive response to exercise","Left bundle branch block","Chronotropic incompetence","Normal cardiac output response"]'::jsonb, 'Chronotropic incompetence', 'Chronotropic incompetence is the failure to reach approximately 85% of the age-predicted maximum heart rate during exercise, which limits cardiac output and produces exertional fatigue. A blunted heart-rate response with maximal effort is the defining feature of this abnormality.'),
('bbb107d8-c08b-5bd8-b37c-68b7966c6b87'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Regarding clinical cases in Exercise Environment: Primary site of first-pass metabolism:', '["Liver","Kidney","Lung","Intestine","Spleen"]'::jsonb, 'Liver', 'Orally absorbed drugs enter portal circulation and are metabolised by the liver first.'),
('aa191620-36fc-5eb2-9e8c-0c091e88e975'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'A 25-year-old male develops wheezing, cough, and chest tightness at peak exertion during an exercise stress test; these symptoms resolve with rest. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Chronotropic incompetence","Exercise-induced bronchospasm","Right bundle branch block","Ischemic ST changes","ST segment elevation"]'::jsonb, 'Exercise-induced bronchospasm', 'Exercise-induced bronchospasm produces transient airway narrowing with wheeze, cough, and chest tightness during or shortly after vigorous activity and resolves after the workload stops. The timing relative to peak exertion and recovery with rest are typical of this condition.'),
('a67bea86-58b7-5273-83fd-5bd63d19a39d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'A 30-year-old female reports muscle weakness and fatigue during sustained exercise, worse during fasting; her blood glucose is low and urine ketones are absent. Which metabolic defect best explains her symptoms?', '["Impaired oxidative phosphorylation","Vitamin B12 deficiency","Carnitine deficiency","Dermatomyositis","Exercise intolerance due to deconditioning"]'::jsonb, 'Carnitine deficiency', 'Carnitine transports long-chain fatty acids into mitochondria for beta-oxidation, so its deficiency impairs fatty-acid oxidation and produces exercise intolerance. The combination of fasting hypoglycemia with absent ketones (hypoketotic hypoglycemia) is characteristic of impaired fatty-acid oxidation.'),
('cdeb0436-f0f7-5553-b3f9-0edaf92a2848'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'A 70-year-old male notes wheezing and coughing with exertion, especially in cold, dry air; spirometry improves after rest. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Second-degree AV block","Exercise-induced bronchospasm","ST segment depression","T wave inversion","Normal cardiac output response"]'::jsonb, 'Exercise-induced bronchospasm', 'Cold, dry air and strenuous exertion are common triggers of exercise-induced bronchospasm, which presents with wheeze, cough, and chest tightness. Airflow obstruction typically reverses once exercise stops and the airways rewarm and humidify.'),
('f01a313f-d0cd-5d8a-9324-b8233f3985a2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'A 55-year-old male who has been inactive for years reports early fatigue during exertion; his exercise stress test shows a normal cardiovascular response at all stages. What best explains his exercise limitation?', '["Lactate dehydrogenase deficiency","Neuromuscular junction disorder","Adrenal insufficiency","Exercise intolerance due to deconditioning","Impaired oxidative phosphorylation"]'::jsonb, 'Exercise intolerance due to deconditioning', 'Long-term inactivity lowers aerobic fitness and skeletal muscle oxidative capacity, so fatigue appears early even when the cardiac response during exercise testing is normal. A normal stress test in a sedentary patient points to deconditioning rather than primary pathology.'),
('56f00d4d-712c-5e11-b7f0-5dc3924deafa'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Regarding clinical cases in Exercise Environment: St. John''s Wort induces:', '["CYP3A4 and P-gp","CYP2D6","CYP2C9","CYP1A2","CYP2C19"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4 and P-gp', 'St. John''s Wort induces CYP3A4 and P-gp, reducing drug levels.'),
('00929571-eced-55ce-bb69-8848863c5714'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'A 50-year-old female reports progressive muscle weakness and fatigue with exertion, worse after prolonged exercise; muscle biopsy shows ragged red fibers. Which condition best explains this picture?', '["Hypothyroidism","Mitochondrial myopathy","Carnitine deficiency","Polymyositis","Glycogen storage disease (McArdle)"]'::jsonb, 'Mitochondrial myopathy', 'Mitochondrial myopathies impair oxidative phosphorylation, producing early exertional fatigue, weakness, and elevated lactate. Ragged red fibers seen on modified Gomori trichrome staining are the characteristic muscle biopsy finding.'),
('d88457df-3c55-5949-b705-a3a90590d4fd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'A 35-year-old female with active rheumatoid arthritis reports fatigue that worsens with exertion; she is found to have a normocytic anemia with low serum iron and normal-to-high ferritin. Which condition best explains the anemia?', '["Dermatomyositis","Mitochondrial myopathy","Polymyositis","Anemia of chronic disease","Adrenal insufficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Anemia of chronic disease', 'In anemia of chronic disease, inflammatory cytokines sequester iron in macrophages and blunt erythropoiesis, producing low serum iron with normal or elevated ferritin and usually a normocytic or mildly microcytic blood picture. The coexisting inflammatory disease supports this mechanism over true iron deficiency.'),
('384fbeb4-9c98-5b24-9f9b-0f7a68dd85a0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'A 40-year-old male reports easy fatigue during exertion; his exercise stress test shows a peak heart rate below 85% of the age-predicted maximum despite maximal effort. Which finding best explains these symptoms?', '["Ventricular tachycardia","Pathologic Q waves","Chronotropic incompetence","Right bundle branch block","Hypertensive response to exercise"]'::jsonb, 'Chronotropic incompetence', 'Chronotropic incompetence denotes an inadequate rise in heart rate for the level of exercise, which blunts the increase in cardiac output and causes exertional fatigue. The failure to reach the age-predicted maximum heart rate despite maximal effort is its diagnostic hallmark.'),
('166dbf5a-9abc-5d8e-a340-5bc7664f0ab9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'A 40-year-old female who has been on prolonged bed rest notes easy fatigability and leg weakness with activity; muscle biopsy shows selective atrophy of fast-twitch fibers. Which finding best explains her deconditioned state?', '["Anemia of chronic disease","Iron deficiency anemia","Peripheral fatigue","Adrenal insufficiency","Type II fiber atrophy"]'::jsonb, 'Type II fiber atrophy', 'Disuse and inactivity preferentially cause atrophy of type II (fast-twitch, glycolytic) muscle fibers, reducing strength and producing easy fatigability. Selective loss of fast-twitch fibers on biopsy is the expected finding in a deconditioned, bed-bound patient.'),
('eaa3c395-3797-5b2f-be0e-c1223a165505'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'A 70-year-old female reports muscle fatigue with minimal exertion, myalgia, and elevated resting serum lactate; muscle biopsy shows ragged red fibers. Which condition best explains these findings?', '["Carnitine deficiency","Mitochondrial myopathy","Dermatomyositis","Neuromuscular junction disorder","Lactate dehydrogenase deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Mitochondrial myopathy', 'Mitochondrial myopathy causes defective oxidative phosphorylation, leading to early exertional fatigue, myalgia, and elevated resting lactate. Ragged red fibers on muscle biopsy are the characteristic histologic finding of this group of disorders.'),
('e564ea39-1864-54a5-a364-96134399769c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'A 50-year-old male reports fatigue and dyspnea with exertion; during an exercise test his heart rate plateaus well below the predicted maximum at every workload. Which abnormality best explains his exercise limitation?', '["Maximal heart rate achieved","ST segment depression","Chronotropic incompetence","ST segment elevation","Abnormal blood pressure response"]'::jsonb, 'Chronotropic incompetence', 'An inadequate heart-rate response to exercise (chronotropic incompetence) limits the rise in cardiac output and produces exertional dyspnea and fatigue. A heart rate that plateaus below the predicted maximum despite increasing workloads defines this abnormality.'),
('b9c12975-fbc2-5ce5-b30c-9a5a5f1ece77'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'A 45-year-old female with active inflammatory bowel disease reports worsening fatigue; labs show anemia with low serum iron and elevated ferritin. Which condition best explains the anemia?', '["Muscle dystrophy","Glycogen storage disease (McArdle)","Phosphofructokinase deficiency","Anemia of chronic disease","Delayed onset muscle soreness"]'::jsonb, 'Anemia of chronic disease', 'Anemia of chronic disease arises from inflammation-driven iron sequestration and blunted erythropoietin response, giving low serum iron with normal or elevated ferritin. In the setting of active inflammatory bowel disease this pattern indicates anemia of chronic disease rather than iron deficiency.'),
('d2755cda-9ad4-5ed7-bb98-7be19d3b425c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'A 35-year-old female reports easy fatigability during exercise; her stress test shows marked blunting of the expected heart-rate rise during exertion despite otherwise normal findings. Which condition best explains this result?', '["Right bundle branch block","Chronotropic incompetence","Abnormal blood pressure response","Ventricular ectopy","Normal cardiac output response"]'::jsonb, 'Chronotropic incompetence', 'Chronotropic incompetence is an abnormally small heart-rate increase relative to exercise intensity, which reduces cardiac output reserve and limits exercise tolerance. A blunted heart-rate response on an otherwise normal stress test is its defining feature.'),
('61c1f190-0225-5e45-b34c-f87b97feb1cb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'A 40-year-old female with chronic inflammatory disease reports fatigue and exertional intolerance; labs show a normocytic anemia with low serum iron and elevated ferritin. Which condition best explains this picture?', '["Muscle dystrophy","Chronic fatigue syndrome","Lactate dehydrogenase deficiency","Anemia of chronic disease","Peripheral fatigue"]'::jsonb, 'Anemia of chronic disease', 'Chronic inflammation traps iron within macrophages and reduces erythropoietin-driven red cell production, yielding a normocytic anemia with low serum iron but high ferritin. This iron profile distinguishes anemia of chronic disease from iron-deficiency anemia.'),
('b99b955c-f26f-5df0-bd1b-ea3711ac9291'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'A 25-year-old female reports recurrent muscle cramps, weakness, and fatigue during intense exercise; an ischemic forearm test produces no rise in venous lactate. Which condition best explains this picture?', '["Central fatigue","Glycogen storage disease (McArdle)","Hypothyroidism","Chronic fatigue syndrome","Phosphofructokinase deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Glycogen storage disease (McArdle)', 'In McArdle disease, myophosphorylase deficiency blocks muscle glycogenolysis, causing exercise-induced cramps and fatigue. Because glycogen cannot be converted to lactate, the ischemic forearm test shows no venous lactate rise, a diagnostic clue to this disorder.'),
('3ea4a795-929b-50e1-9258-c3379e7a3d01'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'A 70-year-old female confined to a chair for months notes leg weakness and fatigue with minimal activity; muscle biopsy shows selective loss of fast-twitch fibers. Which finding best explains her weakness?', '["Impaired lactate clearance","Adrenal insufficiency","Type II fiber atrophy","Chronic fatigue syndrome","Hypothyroidism"]'::jsonb, 'Type II fiber atrophy', 'Inactivity and aging preferentially atrophy type II (fast-twitch) muscle fibers, producing proximal weakness and early fatigue. Selective loss of fast-twitch fibers on biopsy in a debilitated older adult is the expected finding.'),
('51573961-69cf-51b5-bb32-e1209334f862'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Exercise Environment: P-glycoprotein is an efflux transporter that:', '["Pumps drugs out of cells","Enhances absorption","Binds to proteins","Metabolises drugs","Excretes in bile only"]'::jsonb, 'Pumps drugs out of cells', 'P-gp efflux at intestine, kidney, and BBB limits drug absorption.'),
('a42da75b-5c67-51a2-a27d-805e9d211533'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Exercise Environment: Loading dose formula:', '["Vd x Css / F","CL x Css x tau / F","Vd x CL / Css","Css x tau / CL","Fx Css / Vd"]'::jsonb, 'Vd x Css / F', 'Loading dose depends on Vd and target concentration.'),
('48866bef-0862-5867-a199-f9b39da4c45b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'A 60-year-old male reports muscle weakness that worsens with repetitive activity and improves after rest, along with fluctuating ptosis and diplopia late in the day. Which condition best explains this picture?', '["Vitamin B12 deficiency","Adrenal insufficiency","Exercise intolerance due to deconditioning","Myasthenia gravis","Myophosphorylase deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Myasthenia gravis', 'Myasthenia gravis is an autoimmune disorder of the neuromuscular junction that causes fatigable weakness worsening with use and improving with rest. Fluctuating ptosis and diplopia that appear late in the day are characteristic presenting features.'),
('8e3f13c0-66be-5892-a132-c88189717d6b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Regarding clinical cases in Exercise Environment: Steady state reached after:', '["4-5 half-lives","1 half-life","2 half-lives","6-7 half-lives","10 half-lives"]'::jsonb, '4-5 half-lives', 'Css reached after 4-5 half-lives.'),
('d2a9e81b-c827-53b4-a796-532c7f1fd397'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'A 55-year-old female reports early fatigue during sustained exercise and elevated resting serum lactate; muscle biopsy shows reduced activity of the electron transport chain. Which defect best explains her exercise intolerance?', '["Myophosphorylase deficiency","Impaired oxidative phosphorylation","Peripheral fatigue","Delayed onset muscle soreness","Muscle dystrophy"]'::jsonb, 'Impaired oxidative phosphorylation', 'Defective oxidative phosphorylation limits aerobic ATP production, causing early fatigue with exertion and a rise in resting lactate from anaerobic compensation. Reduced electron transport chain activity on biopsy directly identifies this defect.'),
('5d753315-9bdb-521e-bf8d-0a1e8c23718d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Exercise Environment: Warfarin primarily metabolised by:', '["CYP2C9","CYP3A4","CYP2D6","CYP2C19","CYP1A2"]'::jsonb, 'CYP2C9', 'CYP2C9 metabolises the more potent S-warfarin.'),
('27c56ff9-41c8-59c0-aea0-ddfc3b088862'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Which type of muscle fiber is most resistant to fatigue?', '["Type IIa (fast oxidative)","Type IIx (fast glycolytic)","Type IIb (fast glycolytic)","Type IIc","Type I (slow oxidative)"]'::jsonb, 'Type I (slow oxidative)', 'Type I (slow oxidative) fibers have abundant mitochondria, myoglobin, and capillaries, giving them a high oxidative capacity and marked resistance to fatigue. Fast-glycolytic fibers (Type II) fatigue quickly because they rely on anaerobic metabolism.'),
('4d3276df-a680-5b62-9e6d-cc8166a6682f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'What is VO2 max?', '["Ventilation rate at rest","Maximum rate of oxygen consumption","Lactate threshold","Maximum heart rate","Respiratory exchange ratio"]'::jsonb, 'Maximum rate of oxygen consumption', 'VO2 max is the highest rate at which the body can consume oxygen during exhaustive exercise and defines cardiorespiratory aerobic capacity. It is the gold-standard measure of exercise fitness and is reduced by deconditioning, anemia, and cardiopulmonary disease.'),
('51d134ea-1b28-5576-9bad-81b29fea80ef'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'A 70-year-old male undergoing an exercise stress test for chest pain develops frequent premature ventricular beats at high workloads, with none at rest. Which finding best explains this observation?', '["Pathologic Q waves","Ischemic ST changes","Ventricular ectopy","Exercise-induced bronchospasm","Normal exercise response"]'::jsonb, 'Ventricular ectopy', 'Ventricular ectopy refers to premature ventricular contractions arising from the ventricles; when they appear only at high workloads and resolve at rest, they represent exercise-induced ventricular ectopy. Such exercise-induced PVCs may carry prognostic significance in patients with known or suspected coronary disease.'),
('1f837a1d-22e5-5a5e-b2ed-839cedfc1f0d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'In the study of Exercise Environment, Fick''s law: diffusion rate proportional to:', '["Surface area x concentration gradient / thickness","Molecular weight x concentration accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Protein binding x pH mediated by specific receptor activation","Vd x clearance without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ionisation x pKa leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Surface area x concentration gradient / thickness', 'Rate = SA xD x dC/dx.'),
('d613c227-6968-5231-ba3d-959501caad37'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'A 30-year-old male undergoes an exercise stress test for evaluation of chest pain. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Iron deficiency anemia","Type II fiber atrophy","Mitochondrial myopathy","Carnitine deficiency","Myophosphorylase deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Myophosphorylase deficiency', 'A young adult with exertional symptoms is classically McArdle disease (myophosphorylase deficiency), a glycogen storage disease that causes cramps, fatigue, and premature muscle exhaustion during exercise. Carnitine deficiency and mitochondrial myopathy are less characteristic of this presentation.'),
('78836560-b6d2-580c-b7c4-b93a49f1aca8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Exercise Environment: Acetaminophen toxic metabolite:', '["NAPQI","MPP+","MPTP","N-oxide","Sulfate conjugate"]'::jsonb, 'NAPQI', 'NAPQI normally detoxified by glutathione. Overdose depletes glutathione.'),
('8eb1ef55-d673-5cf9-aef7-f455cfaaf4ac'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'During therapeutic management of Exercise Environment, Acetaminophen antidote:', '["N-acetylcysteine","Flumazenil","Naloxone","Fomepizole","Deferoxamine"]'::jsonb, 'N-acetylcysteine', 'NAC replenishes glutathione stores.'),
('cf12d94c-2cb8-5000-b383-ea21be734e5e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Which energy system provides ATP for the first 10 seconds of maximal exercise?', '["Krebs cycle","Oxidative phosphorylation","ATP-PCr system","Glycolysis","Beta-oxidation"]'::jsonb, 'ATP-PCr system', 'The creatine phosphate (ATP-PCr) system regenerates ATP almost instantly and predominates during the first few seconds of maximal effort. Glycolysis and oxidative pathways take longer to contribute meaningful ATP.'),
('29171071-768c-5bc5-91f8-041742446f13'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'A 50-year-old female undergoes an exercise stress test for evaluation of chest pain. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Anemia of chronic disease","Polymyositis","Myasthenia gravis","Delayed onset muscle soreness","Hypothyroidism"]'::jsonb, 'Anemia of chronic disease', 'Anemia of chronic disease lowers oxygen-carrying capacity and impairs exercise tolerance, making it the most plausible cause of exertional symptoms in this patient. The myopathic, neuromuscular, and muscle-soreness options do not explain an abnormal stress-test response.'),
('00ede252-7bc2-50f1-beee-e6111622dfd7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'During exercise, cardiac output increases primarily due to:', '["Increased blood volume","Increased contractility only","Increased heart rate only","Decreased peripheral resistance","Increased heart rate and stroke volume"]'::jsonb, 'Increased heart rate and stroke volume', 'Cardiac output rises during exercise through a combination of increased heart rate and increased stroke volume, with stroke volume plateauing at higher workloads. Neither heart rate alone nor contractility alone accounts for the full response.'),
('88c9cd5e-2a64-5850-a888-b65f29c80572'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Exercise Environment: Efficacy refers to:', '["Ability to produce maximal response","Dose for effect accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Absorption rate mediated by specific receptor activation","Duration without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Safety leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ability to produce maximal response', 'Emax = maximum response a drug can produce.'),
('319648a5-1a77-582a-8f58-652fec816809'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'What happens to blood flow during exercise?', '["Increases to viscera only","Decreases to skeletal muscle","Remains unchanged","Increases to all organs equally","Increases to skeletal muscle, decreases to viscera"]'::jsonb, 'Increases to skeletal muscle, decreases to viscera', 'Exercise redirects cardiac output toward exercising skeletal muscle and away from visceral organs through sympathetic vasoconstriction and local vasodilation. Blood flow does not increase equally to all organs.'),
('ce70f100-b1d1-514b-b684-0886839fa493'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Lactate threshold is the point at which:', '["VO2 max is reached","Muscle fatigue begins","Blood lactate rises significantly","Heart rate plateaus","Glycogen stores deplete"]'::jsonb, 'Blood lactate rises significantly', 'The lactate threshold is the exercise intensity at which blood lactate accumulates sharply because its production exceeds clearance. It occurs below the maximal oxygen uptake rather than at VO2 max.'),
('41c24161-6463-5508-a4df-9a343c66435d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'What is the primary fuel for skeletal muscle during high-intensity exercise?', '["Lactate","Creatine phosphate and glycogen","Ketone bodies","Amino acids","Free fatty acids"]'::jsonb, 'Creatine phosphate and glycogen', 'High-intensity effort is fueled initially by phosphocreatine and subsequently by anaerobic glycolysis of muscle glycogen. Free fatty acids and ketone bodies dominate only during lower-intensity, longer-duration exercise.'),
('58af8573-850f-5a88-86e3-07dd21f2eda4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'At high altitude, the primary compensatory mechanism to increase O2 delivery to tissues is:', '["Increased lung compliance resulting in decreased functional activity","Increased 2,3-BPG in RBCs → right-shift of O2-Hb dissociation curve","Increased airway resistance mediated by specific receptor activation","Decreased Hb concentration without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Decreased heart rate leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Increased 2,3-BPG in RBCs → right-shift of O2-Hb dissociation curve', 'Hypoxia at altitude increases red-cell 2,3-BPG, which right-shifts the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve and facilitates O2 unloading to tissues. Among the options this is the targeted compensation at the tissue level.'),
('f2947a19-2ffc-5222-b25d-6fc24355c3c4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'The Haldane effect describes:', '["Effect of CO2 on O2 transport","Effect of pH on hemoglobin","Effect of temperature on hemoglobin","Effect of exercise on ventilation","Effect of oxygenation on CO2 transport"]'::jsonb, 'Effect of oxygenation on CO2 transport', 'The Haldane effect describes how oxygenation of hemoglobin reduces its capacity to carry CO2 as carbamino compounds, promoting CO2 unloading in the lungs. The effect of pH on oxygen affinity is instead the Bohr effect.'),
('060a240e-9ada-5a2d-8a8b-b01690dd3a8a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'The immediate source of energy for muscle contraction is:', '["ATP","Glucose","Glycogen","Fatty acids","Creatine phosphate"]'::jsonb, 'ATP', 'ATP is the immediate energy currency that directly powers cross-bridge cycling in muscle. Creatine phosphate and glucose serve to regenerate ATP but are not used directly by the contractile machinery.'),
('9d408336-83e6-5ffb-a516-fc11c55473a5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'The oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve shifts right with:', '["Increased temperature, acidosis, increased 2,3-DPG","Decreased temperature accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Alkalosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Decreased 2,3-DPG without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Carbon monoxide poisoning leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Increased temperature, acidosis, increased 2,3-DPG', 'Rising temperature, acidosis, and increased 2,3-DPG all reduce hemoglobin''s affinity for oxygen, shifting the dissociation curve to the right. This favors O2 unloading in actively metabolizing tissues.'),
('a6d2f42f-c2f6-56a5-b439-2cf7defc5920'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'The Bohr effect describes:', '["Effect of altitude on breathing","Effect of exercise on cardiac output","Effect of pH on oxygen-hemoglobin affinity","Effect of temperature on hemoglobin","Effect of CO2 on ventilation"]'::jsonb, 'Effect of pH on oxygen-hemoglobin affinity', 'The Bohr effect states that a fall in pH (increased H+ concentration) decreases hemoglobin''s affinity for oxygen, right-shifting the curve and enhancing O2 release in exercising tissue. The Haldane effect concerns CO2 transport instead.'),
('b1aa2838-ffcf-59e9-a06b-0da1744c46f9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'During exercise, the primary factor increasing oxygen delivery is:', '["Increased capillary density","Increased cardiac output","Increased ventilation","Increased hemoglobin","Increased oxygen extraction"]'::jsonb, 'Increased cardiac output', 'Increased cardiac output is the principal mechanism boosting oxygen delivery during exercise, raising both flow and O2 supply to active muscles. Capillary density rises only as a chronic training adaptation.'),
('57b82915-6223-501d-9806-2154bcc6864b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Maximum heart rate can be estimated as:', '["220 - age","200 - age","180 - age","300 - age","250 - age"]'::jsonb, '220 - age', 'Maximum heart rate is empirically estimated as 220 minus age in years. The other formulas are not standard clinically.'),
('b9058b67-a7c3-5ad0-a0e7-63a52003a7fa'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'The recovery period after exercise is important for:', '["Continuing to produce energy","Replenishing ATP and glycogen, removing lactate","Losing weight","Building muscle mass","Decreasing heart rate only"]'::jsonb, 'Replenishing ATP and glycogen, removing lactate', 'During recovery, aerobic metabolism consumes excess oxygen to replenish phosphocreatine and glycogen and to clear accumulated lactate. This restoration of energy stores is the chief purpose of the recovery period.'),
('08084c9b-caf4-52f9-8a32-14093cfe5d75'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Oxygen debt (EPOC) is:', '["Oxygen carried by hemoglobin","Oxygen deficit at start of exercise","Maximum oxygen uptake","Amount of oxygen in venous blood","Excess post-exercise oxygen consumption"]'::jsonb, 'Excess post-exercise oxygen consumption', 'Oxygen debt, also called excess post-exercise oxygen consumption (EPOC), is the elevated oxygen uptake above resting levels after exercise ends. It pays back the oxygen deficit incurred at the start of exercise.'),
('2300f9e1-8578-5ba2-86bd-56a762bbe7a1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Altitude acclimatization involves:', '["Decreased 2,3-DPG resulting in decreased functional activity","Decreased heart rate accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Decreased ventilation mediated by specific receptor activation","Decreased RBC without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Increased ventilation, increased RBC, increased 2,3-DPG"]'::jsonb, 'Increased ventilation, increased RBC, increased 2,3-DPG', 'Acclimatization to altitude increases ventilation, red cell mass, and 2,3-DPG, all of which improve tissue oxygen delivery despite the low inspired PO2. Decreases in red cells or 2,3-DPG would impair adaptation.'),
('7f9ffa0b-4344-516d-85c5-cf712e7dd0f3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Diving reflex (mammalian) includes:', '["Increased body temperature","Tachycardia and vasodilation","Increased heart rate and BP","Bradycardia and peripheral vasoconstriction","Decreased respiratory rate"]'::jsonb, 'Bradycardia and peripheral vasoconstriction', 'The mammalian diving reflex produces bradycardia and peripheral vasoconstriction, conserving oxygen for the brain and heart. Heart rate and blood pressure do not rise during the response.'),
('adc82269-7cd7-5ad6-bac6-77de06dfee20'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Decompression sickness (''the bends'') is caused by:', '["Air embolism","Nitrogen bubbles forming in tissues","CO2 retention","Oxygen toxicity","Pulmonary barotrauma"]'::jsonb, 'Nitrogen bubbles forming in tissues', 'Rapid ascent after a dive allows nitrogen that dissolved under pressure to come out of solution and form bubbles in tissues and blood, obstructing vessels and causing pain and dysfunction. This underlies decompression sickness.');
insert into fcps_mcq_stage (id, subject_name, chapter_name, question, options, correct_answer, explanation) values
('836a7d3a-e6b7-5d3f-a490-50f03f7e5c04'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Hyperbaric oxygen therapy is used for:', '["Stroke","Myocardial infarction","Decompression sickness and gas gangrene","High altitude sickness","Exercise-induced asthma"]'::jsonb, 'Decompression sickness and gas gangrene', 'Hyperbaric oxygen raises dissolved oxygen in plasma and is first-line therapy for decompression sickness and gas gangrene. It is not standard treatment for stroke, myocardial infarction, altitude sickness, or asthma.'),
('8e53434d-7ad4-5c9c-8da2-58724844e485'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'The Valsalva maneuver involves:', '["Coughing forcefully","Hyperventilation","Rapid shallow breathing","Forced expiration against closed glottis","Deep inspiration followed by breath hold"]'::jsonb, 'Forced expiration against closed glottis', 'The Valsalva maneuver is a forced expiration against a closed glottis, which raises intrathoracic pressure and transiently reduces venous return and arterial pressure. The other options describe different respiratory actions.'),
('5df1aac2-5ae0-5751-baee-b259856057a6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'The diving response is most pronounced in:', '["Fish resulting in decreased functional activity","Humans accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Land mammals mediated by specific receptor activation","Seal and dolphin (marine mammals)","Birds leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Seal and dolphin (marine mammals)', 'Marine mammals such as seals and dolphins exhibit the most dramatic diving response, with extreme bradycardia and near-total redistribution of blood flow to vital organs. Terrestrial species including humans show a weaker version.'),
('b1cd7bd6-426d-5abe-936e-73687089de15'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'At high altitude, the immediate response is:', '["Decreased heart rate resulting in decreased functional activity","Increased blood pressure accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Increased ventilation (hypoxic ventilatory response)","Decreased ventilation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Increased heart rate leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Increased ventilation (hypoxic ventilatory response)', 'The immediate response to ascent is increased ventilation triggered by peripheral chemoreceptors sensing hypoxia. Heart rate also rises, but the hypoxic ventilatory response is the hallmark early adaptation.'),
('de9e0cbc-df84-5cdc-9200-155f0d1073a6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Chronic exposure to high altitude leads to:', '["Decreased lung capacity","Anemia","Decreased cardiac output","Polycythemia (increased RBC)","Decreased 2,3-DPG"]'::jsonb, 'Polycythemia (increased RBC)', 'Chronic hypoxemia stimulates erythropoietin, producing polycythemia that raises blood oxygen content. This is the hallmark adaptive change of long-term altitude exposure.'),
('278b39e2-74e0-5d47-a51e-96bb2948679f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'The lactate threshold in trained athletes is:', '["Same as untrained resulting in decreased functional activity","Unaffected by training accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Decreased with training mediated by specific receptor activation","Lower than untrained without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Higher (they can exercise at higher % VO2 max before lactate rises)"]'::jsonb, 'Higher (they can exercise at higher % VO2 max before lactate rises)', 'Trained athletes sustain a higher fraction of VO2 max before lactate accumulates because of enhanced lactate clearance and mitochondrial capacity. Their lactate threshold is therefore higher, not lower or unchanged.'),
('ded5dd58-5beb-5e56-9676-aa58f0926376'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Endurance training primarily increases:', '["Type I fiber oxidative capacity","Type IIa fiber number","Muscle mass","Type IIb fiber size","Anaerobic capacity"]'::jsonb, 'Type I fiber oxidative capacity', 'Endurance training raises the oxidative capacity of slow-twitch type I fibers by increasing mitochondria, capillaries, and aerobic enzymes. Strength training, in contrast, enlarges fast-twitch fibers.'),
('e92f7e72-b488-507a-b7d0-edcf054377ef'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Strength training primarily increases:', '["Type II fiber size (hypertrophy)","Mitochondrial density","Capillary density","Oxidative enzymes","Type I fiber number"]'::jsonb, 'Type II fiber size (hypertrophy)', 'Strength training predominantly causes hypertrophy of fast-twitch type II fibers, increasing cross-sectional area and force production. Mitochondrial and capillary density rise mainly with endurance training.'),
('9e24931b-2c52-56b5-b9ce-aed8f6476457'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'The thermoneutral zone for humans is approximately:', '["28-32°C","10-15°C","20-25°C","35-40°C","15-20°C"]'::jsonb, '28-32°C', 'The thermoneutral zone for a resting naked human, within which metabolic rate is minimal and core temperature is maintained by vasomotor adjustments alone, is about 28-32°C. Outside this range metabolic compensation is required.'),
('818fc900-9a56-52ae-b312-7d70664524ff'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Heat stroke is characterized by:', '["Core temperature >37.5°C with sweating","Core temperature >39°C with headache","Core temperature >42°C with coma","Core temperature >38°C","Core temperature >40°C with CNS dysfunction"]'::jsonb, 'Core temperature >40°C with CNS dysfunction', 'Heat stroke is defined as hyperthermia with a core temperature above 40°C accompanied by central nervous system dysfunction such as confusion or coma. Lower temperatures without CNS signs represent heat illness of lesser severity.'),
('7ee0adf7-51b2-590b-998a-b64a959c20ce'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Hypothermia is defined as:', '["Core temperature <37°C","Core temperature <34°C","Core temperature <32°C","Core temperature <35°C","Core temperature <36°C"]'::jsonb, 'Core temperature <35°C', 'Hypothermia is defined clinically as a core temperature below 35°C. Core temperatures of 35-37°C lie within the normal or mildly cooled range.'),
('cdefddf8-aa09-53a7-ae83-b9f6308f6651'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'The wind chill index describes:', '["Solar radiation effect","Humidity effect on temperature","Rate of heat loss from exposed skin","Actual temperature","Barometric pressure effect"]'::jsonb, 'Rate of heat loss from exposed skin', 'The wind chill index estimates the rate of heat loss from exposed skin, which increases as wind speed rises. It combines air temperature and wind speed to express the cooling power felt on exposed skin.'),
('70be982a-d456-5cf6-846c-5684e1e06d86'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Specific heat capacity of water is:', '["Zero resulting in decreased functional activity","Same as air accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Infinite mediated by specific receptor activation","Low without affecting the primary tissue pathway","High (water resists temperature change)"]'::jsonb, 'High (water resists temperature change)', 'Water has a high specific heat capacity (about 4.18 J/g per degree C), meaning it must gain or lose a relatively large amount of heat to change its temperature. This property makes water highly effective for heat transfer within the body.'),
('4895d2bf-aa40-5b86-ae0a-57795f5efbb4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'During exercise in hot environments, the main concern is:', '["Hypothermia","Decreased lactate threshold","Increased bone density","Dehydration and electrolyte imbalance","Increased muscle mass"]'::jsonb, 'Dehydration and electrolyte imbalance', 'Sweating increases markedly during exercise in heat, so fluid and electrolyte losses can outpace intake. Uncorrected losses cause dehydration and electrolyte imbalance, impairing thermoregulation and exercise performance.'),
('fda1988d-712e-5513-8822-77705e19f4fc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'The primary function of the respiratory system during exercise is:', '["Speech production","pH regulation","Temperature regulation","Filtration of particles","Gas exchange (O2 and CO2)"]'::jsonb, 'Gas exchange (O2 and CO2)', 'The central role of the respiratory system during exercise is gas exchange: delivering oxygen to the blood and eliminating carbon dioxide. Ventilation rises in proportion to metabolic demand to maintain this exchange.'),
('e88a6b05-3236-5a32-9ef0-37eaf2eb72c3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'The maximum heart rate during exercise is most commonly predicted using which formula?', '["180 - age","220 - age (years)","250 - age","200 - age","300 - age"]'::jsonb, '220 - age (years)', 'Predicted maximum heart rate is conventionally estimated as 220 minus age in years. This ''220 minus age'' formula provides a widely used target for exercise prescription and exercise stress testing.'),
('355e82dd-b75a-502d-9818-2f0e3b20d6d3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Which formula is used to estimate the predicted maximum heart rate during exercise?', '["180 - age","220 - age (years)","250 - age","200 - age","300 - age"]'::jsonb, '220 - age (years)', 'The most commonly used estimate of maximum heart rate during exercise is 220 minus the person''s age in years. It is a practical guide for prescribing and monitoring exercise intensity.'),
('ca9be401-c5f6-5f34-b36a-3086b8ef3531'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'The standard formula for predicting maximum heart rate during exercise is:', '["180 - age","220 - age (years)","250 - age","200 - age","300 - age"]'::jsonb, '220 - age (years)', 'Maximum predicted heart rate is traditionally calculated as 220 minus age in years. This estimate is used to gauge target heart rates during graded exercise testing and training.'),
('3bbbbb82-165f-5fb9-acb5-5616a54f3bd3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Predicted maximum heart rate during exercise is most commonly calculated as:', '["180 - age","220 - age (years)","250 - age","200 - age","300 - age"]'::jsonb, '220 - age (years)', 'The widely used clinical estimate for maximum heart rate is 220 minus age in years. It approximates the upper heart rate limit attainable during maximal exercise.'),
('838ec1eb-1c77-5728-a667-58e4dc2619c2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Which of the following equations estimates maximum heart rate during exercise?', '["180 - age","220 - age (years)","250 - age","200 - age","300 - age"]'::jsonb, '220 - age (years)', 'Maximum heart rate during exercise is estimated as 220 minus age in years. The formula is a standard bedside method for predicting the maximal heart rate and setting exercise targets.'),
('860e4b61-a546-50a2-b9a0-8aece5691c7c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Which receptor mediates smooth muscle contraction in the prostate and bladder neck?', '["Beta-2 receptors","Dopamine receptors","Histamine","Muscarinic receptors","Alpha-1 adrenergic receptors"]'::jsonb, 'Alpha-1 adrenergic receptors', 'Alpha-1 adrenergic receptors are present on smooth muscle of the prostate and bladder neck, where their stimulation causes contraction. Alpha-1 antagonists relax these structures and are used to treat benign prostatic hyperplasia.'),
('befa9825-3c9f-557f-91bf-f5d9ced1ce10'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Sympathetic vasoconstriction in non-exercising tissues during exercise is mediated by:', '["Beta-2 receptors","Dopamine receptors","Histamine","Muscarinic receptors","Alpha-1 adrenergic receptors"]'::jsonb, 'Alpha-1 adrenergic receptors', 'Sympathetic discharge causes vasoconstriction of arterioles in non-exercising tissues via alpha-1 adrenergic receptors. This shunts blood flow toward active skeletal muscle during exercise.'),
('f4ce3cc2-7aeb-547a-835d-ba5a9d44dc60'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Which receptors mediate sympathetic vasoconstriction in non-exercising tissues?', '["Beta-2 receptors","Dopamine receptors","Histamine","Muscarinic receptors","Alpha-1 adrenergic receptors"]'::jsonb, 'Alpha-1 adrenergic receptors', 'Sympathetic vasoconstriction in non-exercising tissues is mediated by alpha-1 adrenergic receptors. Activation of these receptors narrows arterioles and redistributes blood flow toward active muscles.'),
('a5010e6d-a55f-503f-a255-8fa039833d0f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Cardiovascular drift during prolonged exercise at a constant workload is characterised by:', '["Increased SV resulting in decreased functional activity","Gradual increase in HR and decrease in SV during prolonged exercise at constant workload","Sudden drop in BP mediated by specific receptor activation","Decrease without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Decreased HR leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Gradual increase in HR and decrease in SV during prolonged exercise at constant workload', 'Cardiovascular drift is the gradual rise in heart rate with a fall in stroke volume during prolonged submaximal exercise at a fixed workload. It is largely attributed to rising core temperature and progressive dehydration.'),
('f3f6f667-e5e4-5a6e-b058-d7d748694584'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Exercise Environment: Volume of distribution (Vd) equals:', '["Amount of drug / Plasma concentration","Clearance / Rate","Half-life x Clearance","Dose / F","Absorption rate"]'::jsonb, 'Amount of drug / Plasma concentration', 'Vd = Amount / Concentration. Large Vd = extensive tissue distribution.'),
('b16a8afb-db09-51bf-85ab-1ef49e56ba1e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'During prolonged exercise at a constant workload, cardiovascular drift is best described as:', '["Increased SV resulting in decreased functional activity","Gradual increase in HR and decrease in SV during prolonged exercise at constant workload","Sudden drop in BP mediated by specific receptor activation","Decrease without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Decreased HR leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Gradual increase in HR and decrease in SV during prolonged exercise at constant workload', 'Cardiovascular drift is the slow upward drift in heart rate accompanied by a decline in stroke volume during prolonged submaximal exercise at a constant workload. Heat accumulation and dehydration largely drive this response.'),
('5f712e29-44e3-5a07-9549-d944167bcf19'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'What happens to heart rate and stroke volume during cardiovascular drift?', '["Increased SV resulting in decreased functional activity","Gradual increase in HR and decrease in SV during prolonged exercise at constant workload","Sudden drop in BP mediated by specific receptor activation","Decrease without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Decreased HR leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Gradual increase in HR and decrease in SV during prolonged exercise at constant workload', 'During cardiovascular drift, heart rate gradually rises while stroke volume falls even though the workload stays constant. This pattern is typical of prolonged submaximal exercise, particularly in warm conditions.'),
('c52bef26-4a50-5e55-877c-2e3b834cb9c5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Cardiovascular drift refers to which of the following?', '["Increased SV resulting in decreased functional activity","Gradual increase in HR and decrease in SV during prolonged exercise at constant workload","Sudden drop in BP mediated by specific receptor activation","Decrease without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Decreased HR leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Gradual increase in HR and decrease in SV during prolonged exercise at constant workload', 'Cardiovascular drift is the gradual increase in heart rate and decrease in stroke volume during prolonged exercise at a constant workload. It reflects the rising thermal and fluid stress of sustained exertion.'),
('e17e984a-6aa3-5870-b7d1-c45fdd954e6b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'The cardiovascular drift phenomenon is defined as:', '["Increased SV resulting in decreased functional activity","Gradual increase in HR and decrease in SV during prolonged exercise at constant workload","Sudden drop in BP mediated by specific receptor activation","Decrease without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Decreased HR leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Gradual increase in HR and decrease in SV during prolonged exercise at constant workload', 'Cardiovascular drift is defined as the progressive rise in heart rate and fall in stroke volume during prolonged exercise performed at a steady workload. It is most prominent in hot environments and with dehydration.'),
('610a84d1-17c8-5261-9f22-b48de3d383a7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Which of the following best describes VO2max?', '["Maximum rate of oxygen consumption (measure of aerobic fitness)","Anaerobic threshold accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Maximum ventilation mediated by specific receptor activation","Maximal minute ventilation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Maximum heart rate leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Maximum rate of oxygen consumption (measure of aerobic fitness)', 'VO2max is the maximum rate at which oxygen can be consumed during incremental exercise and is the standard measure of aerobic fitness. It is reached when oxygen consumption plateaus despite a further increase in work rate.'),
('fbb7365d-ea15-5df5-aa9a-2656ce85263f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'The most accurate description of VO2max is:', '["Maximum rate of oxygen consumption (measure of aerobic fitness)","Anaerobic threshold accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Maximum ventilation mediated by specific receptor activation","Maximal minute ventilation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Maximum heart rate leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Maximum rate of oxygen consumption (measure of aerobic fitness)', 'VO2max is the maximum rate of oxygen consumption achieved during maximal exercise and serves as a measure of aerobic fitness. It is independent of the point at which blood lactate begins to accumulate.'),
('8a4fdfd0-2c2e-51db-b32c-41b6f0bed734'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'VO2max is best defined as:', '["Maximum rate of oxygen consumption (measure of aerobic fitness)","Anaerobic threshold accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Maximum ventilation mediated by specific receptor activation","Maximal minute ventilation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Maximum heart rate leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Maximum rate of oxygen consumption (measure of aerobic fitness)', 'VO2max is the maximum rate at which the body can consume oxygen during maximal exertion and is a key indicator of aerobic fitness. Ventilation and heart rate at maximal effort are related but do not themselves define VO2max.'),
('d89598a2-04af-543b-b910-e2cd908374d5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Which option correctly defines VO2max?', '["Maximum rate of oxygen consumption (measure of aerobic fitness)","Anaerobic threshold accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Maximum ventilation mediated by specific receptor activation","Maximal minute ventilation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Maximum heart rate leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Maximum rate of oxygen consumption (measure of aerobic fitness)', 'VO2max is the maximum rate of oxygen consumption reached during incremental exercise and measures aerobic fitness. It occurs at a work rate above which additional effort does not raise oxygen uptake further.'),
('3849f0d2-bf6f-5be8-8ce2-469fb8141de2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'VO2max, the maximal oxygen uptake, is:', '["Maximum rate of oxygen consumption (measure of aerobic fitness)","Anaerobic threshold accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Maximum ventilation mediated by specific receptor activation","Maximal minute ventilation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Maximum heart rate leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Maximum rate of oxygen consumption (measure of aerobic fitness)', 'VO2max is the maximum rate of oxygen consumption attainable during maximal exercise and is the reference measure of aerobic fitness. Higher values indicate a greater capacity for sustained aerobic work.'),
('87efc12a-f897-53b1-9dc8-776eb1a59936'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Exercise Environment: Phase II conjugation reaction:', '["Glucuronidation","Oxidation","Reduction","Hydrolysis","Deamination"]'::jsonb, 'Glucuronidation', 'Phase II: glucuronidation, sulfation, acetylation, methylation, glutathione conjugation.'),
('3dcd644e-66c1-5adf-87a7-273fb67d5a53'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'According to the Fick principle, VO2max is determined by:', '["Hb concentration resulting in decreased functional activity","Muscle mass accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cardiac output and arteriovenous O2 difference (Fick principle)","Ventilation only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Heart rate only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cardiac output and arteriovenous O2 difference (Fick principle)', 'The Fick principle states that oxygen consumption equals cardiac output times the arteriovenous oxygen difference. At maximal exercise, VO2max is thereby set by maximal cardiac output together with maximal tissue oxygen extraction.'),
('d7c4d4bb-bf74-5327-a123-a65552ef8de9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'VO2max is determined by which of the following?', '["Hb concentration resulting in decreased functional activity","Muscle mass accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cardiac output and arteriovenous O2 difference (Fick principle)","Ventilation only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Heart rate only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cardiac output and arteriovenous O2 difference (Fick principle)', 'VO2max is governed by the product of cardiac output and the arteriovenous O2 difference at maximal exercise, as expressed by the Fick principle. Neither haemoglobin level nor muscle mass alone determines it.'),
('d13aacb1-42ad-57a0-83be-d6efdaea8968'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'The determinants of VO2max are:', '["Hb concentration resulting in decreased functional activity","Muscle mass accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cardiac output and arteriovenous O2 difference (Fick principle)","Ventilation only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Heart rate only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cardiac output and arteriovenous O2 difference (Fick principle)', 'VO2max depends on maximal cardiac output and the arteriovenous oxygen difference, in line with the Fick principle. Delivering more blood and extracting more oxygen per litre of blood are the two routes to a higher VO2max.'),
('c1ccde3e-df2f-5f16-8462-360f3826176a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise Unusual Environment - Exercise Environment', 'Which combination determines VO2max?', '["Hb concentration resulting in decreased functional activity","Muscle mass accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cardiac output and arteriovenous O2 difference (Fick principle)","Ventilation only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Heart rate only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cardiac output and arteriovenous O2 difference (Fick principle)', 'By the Fick principle, VO2max is the product of cardiac output and the arteriovenous O2 difference at maximal exercise. A single factor such as heart rate or ventilation is insufficient to determine VO2max on its own.'),
('e681d30a-a490-51f5-abe7-2f9f418597c0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Which monosaccharide is the primary energy source for cells?', '["Ribose","Fructose","Galactose","Glucose","Mannose"]'::jsonb, 'Glucose', 'Glucose is the primary energy source for most cells. It enters glycolysis to produce ATP and is the preferred fuel for brain, RBCs, and exercising muscle.'),
('1c963124-f343-56ea-8643-3cd8b989ca55'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'What is the major storage form of glucose in animals?', '["Sucrose","Starch","Cellulose","Chitin","Glycogen"]'::jsonb, 'Glycogen', 'Glycogen is the major storage form of glucose in animals, stored primarily in liver and muscle. Liver glycogen maintains blood glucose; muscle glycogen provides local energy during exercise.'),
('4539cd4c-563c-5397-a777-eda20d4d8ec9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Which enzyme breaks down starch into maltose in the mouth?', '["Maltase","Sucrase","Isomaltase","Amylase","Lactase"]'::jsonb, 'Amylase', 'Salivary amylase (ptyalin) begins starch digestion in the mouth, cleaving alpha-1,4 glycosidic bonds to produce maltose and dextrins. Pancreatic amylase continues this in the small intestine.'),
('9151d3e9-d951-5e39-b7bc-c31184a6932a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'The Cori cycle involves the conversion of lactate to glucose in the:', '["Heart","Brain","Muscle","Liver","Kidney"]'::jsonb, 'Liver', 'The Cori cycle involves conversion of lactate (produced by anaerobic glycolysis in muscle) back to glucose in the liver. This lactate is transported via blood to the liver where it is converted to pyruvate and then to glucose through gluconeogenesis.'),
('36227cd0-383d-5cdc-ba76-43947f2a8159'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'What is the major product of the HMP shunt?', '["ATP","NADPH","NADH","GTP","FADH2"]'::jsonb, 'NADPH', 'The HMP shunt (pentose phosphate pathway) produces NADPH for reductive biosynthesis and ribose-5-phosphate for nucleotide synthesis. NADPH is essential for fatty acid synthesis and glutathione reduction.'),
('53a9d9f6-5817-5f4f-ac0a-5163cd7d7269'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Deficiency of glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase causes:', '["Hemolytic anemia","Glycogen storage disease","Phenylketonuria","Fructose intolerance","Galactosemia"]'::jsonb, 'Hemolytic anemia', 'G6PD deficiency impairs the HMP shunt, reducing NADPH production. This leads to decreased glutathione, making RBCs susceptible to oxidative damage and hemolysis, especially during infections or drug exposure.'),
('2508e5e6-3dff-5afc-b375-b8ee318a7813'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'The TCA cycle operates in the:', '["Endoplasmic reticulum","Cytosol","Ribosomes","Nucleus","Mitochondria"]'::jsonb, 'Mitochondria', 'The TCA cycle (Krebs cycle) occurs in the mitochondrial matrix. Pyruvate dehydrogenase and the TCA cycle enzymes are located here, linking glycolysis to oxidative phosphorylation.'),
('ab368bc0-d820-57f6-a7d4-d09dc48697a3'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Which TCA cycle intermediate combines with acetyl-CoA?', '["Citrate and isocitrate","Pyruvate and citrate","Alpha-ketoglutarate and succinate","Oxaloacetate and acetyl-CoA","Succinyl-CoA and oxaloacetate"]'::jsonb, 'Oxaloacetate and acetyl-CoA', 'Oxaloacetate combines with acetyl-CoA in the first step of the TCA cycle, catalyzed by citrate synthase to form citrate. This is the rate-limiting step of the cycle.'),
('c2926584-faa7-565d-bce9-4fdeb407d7b6'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Which TCA cycle enzyme is also Complex II of the electron transport chain?', '["Citrate synthase","Succinate dehydrogenase","Alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase","Fumarase","Isocitrate dehydrogenase"]'::jsonb, 'Succinate dehydrogenase', 'Complex I (NADH:ubiquinone oxidoreductase) accepts electrons from NADH and pumps 4 H+ across the inner membrane. Rotenone and barbiturates inhibit Complex I.'),
('6009ea6e-97b9-5b0a-867b-9b1cbede7b1b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Which vitamin is a component of coenzyme A?', '["Vitamin B1","Vitamin B3","Vitamin B5","Vitamin B2","Vitamin B6"]'::jsonb, 'Vitamin B5', 'Coenzyme A (CoA-SH) carries acyl groups via thioester bonds. Acetyl-CoA is the key metabolic intermediate linking glycolysis, fatty acid oxidation, and the TCA cycle.'),
('f004a3e8-cafc-5af2-81a6-548310e9786d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'The conversion of pyruvate to acetyl-CoA is catalyzed by:', '["Lactate dehydrogenase","Pyruvate carboxylase","Pyruvate decarboxylase","Pyruvate dehydrogenase complex","Pyruvate kinase"]'::jsonb, 'Pyruvate dehydrogenase complex', 'The pyruvate dehydrogenase complex converts pyruvate to acetyl-CoA in mitochondria, linking glycolysis to the TCA cycle. This irreversible step requires TPP, lipoic acid, CoA, FAD, and NAD+.'),
('59f86bc7-b97d-5e56-915f-457d825e3082'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Which compound inhibits phosphofructokinase-1?', '["ADP","Fructose-2","AMP","6-bisphosphate","ATP"]'::jsonb, 'ATP', 'ATP is the primary allosteric inhibitor of phosphofructokinase-1 (PFK-1), the rate-limiting enzyme of glycolysis. High ATP levels signal sufficient energy, slowing glycolysis. Citrate and low pH also inhibit PFK-1.'),
('c77010dd-b423-5ed9-97c6-317256beb932'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'A 25-year-old female presents with hepatomegaly and hypoglycemia. Liver biopsy shows glycogen accumulation. Which pathophysiological process best explains this presentation?', '["Lipogenesis","Breakdown of glycogen","Ketogenesis","Gluconeogenesis","Synthesis of glycogen"]'::jsonb, 'Synthesis of glycogen', 'Liver glycogen serves to maintain blood glucose. Liver contains glucose-6-phosphatase, which converts G6P to free glucose that can be released into the bloodstream.'),
('f42e3768-717e-57cb-933d-b3c355cebc4b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Carbohydrates: Phase II conjugation reaction:', '["Glucuronidation","Oxidation","Reduction","Hydrolysis","Deamination"]'::jsonb, 'Glucuronidation', 'Phase II: glucuronidation, sulfation, acetylation, methylation, glutathione conjugation.'),
('f4de2dda-3ac9-5a86-bdd3-e99c62caebf7'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Glycogenolysis is the:', '["Synthesis of ketone bodies","Conversion of glucose to fat","Breakdown of glycogen to glucose","Conversion of amino acids to glucose","Synthesis of glycogen"]'::jsonb, 'Breakdown of glycogen to glucose', 'Glycogenolysis is the breakdown of glycogen to glucose-1-phosphate (in muscle) or glucose (in liver). It is catalyzed by glycogen phosphorylase, which cleaves α-1,4 glycosidic bonds.'),
('e8fff03e-0e9e-55ab-835e-91a59d305c60'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Which hormone activates glycogenolysis?', '["Cortisol","Estrogen","Insulin","Glucagon","Thyroxine"]'::jsonb, 'Glucagon', 'Glycogenolysis is the breakdown of glycogen to glucose-1-phosphate (in muscle) or glucose (in liver). It is catalyzed by glycogen phosphorylase, which cleaves α-1,4 glycosidic bonds.'),
('fa7aa2d9-0302-54fb-8f3f-538fff2c147d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Which ion is required for glycogen phosphorylase activity?', '["Ca2+","Mg2+","Fe2+","K+","Na+"]'::jsonb, 'Ca2+', 'Ca2+ ions are required for glycogen phosphorylase activity in muscle. Ca2+ binds to calmodulin, which activates phosphorylase kinase, which in turn activates glycogen phosphorylase.'),
('0551ef3d-c673-58ed-9dfb-d75615526854'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Von Gierke disease (Type I GSD) is due to deficiency of:', '["Glycogen phosphorylase","Glucose-6-phosphatase","Branching enzyme","Acid maltase","Debranching enzyme"]'::jsonb, 'Glucose-6-phosphatase', 'Von Gierke disease (GSD type I) is caused by glucose-6-phosphatase deficiency. Glycogen accumulates in liver, causing severe fasting hypoglycemia, hepatomegaly, and lactic acidosis.'),
('2731b387-d4fc-5f3d-92ce-61d0e1dcc970'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Which classification type is associated with this condition or structure?', '["Type III","Type I","Type IV","Type II","Type V"]'::jsonb, 'Type II', 'McArdle disease is classified as GSD Type V, caused by skeletal muscle glycogen phosphorylase deficiency, presenting with exercise intolerance and muscle cramps.'),
('056fa7cd-66d3-546f-8b3c-1f67b00d67f9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'McArdle disease (Type V GSD) affects:', '["Liver","Heart","Muscle","Kidney","Brain"]'::jsonb, 'Muscle', 'McArdle disease (Type V GSD) specifically affects skeletal muscle, causing exercise intolerance, muscle cramps, and sometimes myoglobinuria after strenuous activity.'),
('e539ef27-704f-52ee-8fc0-870a482e0498'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Which enzyme removes branches during glycogen breakdown?', '["Debranching enzyme","Glucose-6-phosphatase","Branching enzyme","Muscle phosphorylase","Acid maltase"]'::jsonb, 'Debranching enzyme', 'Debranching enzyme has two activities: transferase (moves the outer 3 residues of a branch) and alpha-1,6-glucosidase (cleaves the remaining single glucose at the branch point).'),
('c08eaf56-718d-5905-ad22-254de0e03e38'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Galactosemia is most commonly due to deficiency of:', '["Hexokinase resulting in decreased functional activity","Galactosidase accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Galactose-1-phosphate uridylyltransferase","UDP-galactose-4-epimerase without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Galactokinase leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Galactose-1-phosphate uridylyltransferase', 'Classic galactosemia is caused by galactose-1-phosphate uridylyltransferase deficiency. Accumulation of galactose-1-P and galactitol causes cataracts, hepatomegaly, and intellectual disability.'),
('a26a20c6-13f3-5a8d-b016-f9ecca614174'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Which sugar accumulates in galactosemia?', '["Galactose-1-phosphate","Glucose","Fructose","Maltose","Sucrose"]'::jsonb, 'Galactose-1-phosphate', 'Classic galactosemia is caused by galactose-1-phosphate uridylyltransferase deficiency. Accumulation of galactose-1-P and galactitol causes cataracts, hepatomegaly, and intellectual disability.'),
('2e995fdd-59c3-570b-af2c-f0df1a3b83c2'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Which substance accumulates in this metabolic disorder?', '["Sorbitol accumulation","Galactose accumulation","Galactitol accumulation","Fructose accumulation","Lactose accumulation"]'::jsonb, 'Galactitol accumulation', 'In galactosemia, galactitol (formed by aldose reductase from accumulated galactose) accumulates in tissues, causing osmotic damage particularly to the lens (cataracts) and liver.'),
('0dd2e5d7-735f-5e64-a712-4b2e73613dee'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Lactose intolerance results from deficiency of:', '["Lactase","Isomaltase","Maltase","Amylase","Sucrase"]'::jsonb, 'Lactase', 'Lactase (beta-galactosidase) deficiency causes lactose intolerance. Undigested lactose reaches the colon where bacteria ferment it, producing gas, bloating, and osmotic diarrhea.'),
('2ef90b84-026a-5efb-9c46-97318bb0f2cd'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Which enzyme deficiency causes classic galactosemia?', '["Galactose-1-phosphate uridylyltransferase (GALT)","Galactokinase accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","UDP-galactose-4-epimerase mediated by specific receptor activation","Lactase without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Glucose-6-phosphatase leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Galactose-1-phosphate uridylyltransferase (GALT)', 'Classic galactosemia is caused by galactose-1-phosphate uridylyltransferase deficiency. Accumulation of galactose-1-P and galactitol causes cataracts, hepatomegaly, and intellectual disability.'),
('8e933d6f-978c-5417-9153-5111f552f9b4'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Sucrose is composed of:', '["Galactose + Glucose","Glucose + Fructose","Glucose + Galactose","Fructose + Galactose","Glucose + Glucose"]'::jsonb, 'Glucose + Fructose', 'Sucrose is a disaccharide composed of glucose and fructose linked by an alpha-1,beta-2 glycosidic bond. It is a non-reducing sugar produced by plants.'),
('b12566d3-805b-5025-b6b6-792403b10216'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Which polysaccharide is found in the exoskeleton of arthropods?', '["Starch","Glycogen","Inulin","Cellulose","Chitin"]'::jsonb, 'Chitin', 'Chitin is a polymer of N-acetylglucosamine with beta-1,4 linkages, forming the structural component of arthropod exoskeletons and fungal cell walls.'),
('5d7ba75f-7174-5b03-9c90-966199d26300'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'The normal fasting blood glucose level is:', '["150-200 mg/dL","126-150 mg/dL","100-126 mg/dL","50-70 mg/dL","70-100 mg/dL"]'::jsonb, '70-100 mg/dL', 'Normal fasting blood glucose is 70-100 mg/dL (3.9-5.6 mmol/L). Values 100-125 mg/dL indicate impaired fasting glucose; >=126 mg/dL on two occasions indicates diabetes.'),
('c6575828-19a2-50d4-ab6c-03a4086d4867'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'What is the normal or expected value for this measurement?', '["200 mg/dL","250 mg/dL","140 mg/dL","100 mg/dL","180 mg/dL"]'::jsonb, '180 mg/dL', 'A fasting glucose of 180 mg/dL indicates diabetes mellitus. Normal fasting glucose is <100 mg/dL, prediabetes is 100-125 mg/dL, and diabetes is >=126 mg/dL.'),
('80cfa801-80a4-5e42-bc28-66d4ed8e531a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Which enzyme catalyzes this reaction?', '["Sorbitol dehydrogenase","Phosphoglucose isomerase","Aldose reductase","Hexokinase","Glucose-6-phosphatase"]'::jsonb, 'Aldose reductase', 'Aldose reductase catalyzes the conversion of glucose to sorbitol using NADPH in the polyol pathway. This is the first step in fructose synthesis from glucose.'),
('5fe312a2-5709-5aaa-8ee8-eb9dfb1c4691'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Which compound is the substrate for glycogen synthase?', '["CDP-glucose","UDP-glucose","TDP-glucose","ADP-glucose","GDP-glucose"]'::jsonb, 'UDP-glucose', 'Glycogen synthase catalyzes glycogen synthesis by adding UDP-glucose to the growing chain via α-1,4 bonds. It is activated by insulin (dephosphorylation) and inhibited by glucagon (phosphorylation).'),
('696a454f-1d72-52db-8a4c-fc17fda4e47a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Citrate is an allosteric activator of:', '["PFK-1","Pyruvate kinase","6-bisphosphatase","Acetyl-CoA carboxylase","Fructose-1"]'::jsonb, 'Acetyl-CoA carboxylase', 'Citrate allosterically activates acetyl-CoA carboxylase (ACC), the rate-limiting enzyme of fatty acid synthesis. High citrate signals abundant acetyl-CoA, promoting lipogenesis.'),
('22642c85-bbc6-5ede-b744-12862bd754b6'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Which enzyme converts oxaloacetate to PEP in gluconeogenesis?', '["Malate dehydrogenase","Pyruvate kinase","Enolase","PEP carboxykinase","Pyruvate carboxylase"]'::jsonb, 'PEP carboxykinase', 'PEP carboxykinase (PEPCK) converts oxaloacetate to phosphoenolpyruvate in gluconeogenesis. This is one of the bypass reactions that circumvents the irreversible pyruvate kinase step of glycolysis.'),
('cecee040-51eb-5b34-9ef8-10f0745ccf26'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'How many high-energy bonds are required for gluconeogenesis from pyruvate?', '["4","1","3","6","2"]'::jsonb, '4', 'Gluconeogenesis from pyruvate requires 4 high-energy phosphate bonds: 2 ATP (pyruvate carboxylase) and 2 GTP (PEPCK and later steps), making it energetically expensive.'),
('1edbd765-2805-5915-b144-6ad6644368f7'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Which vitamin is required for transketolase?', '["Niacin (B3)","Thiamine (B1)","Cobalamin (B12)","Pyridoxine (B6)","Riboflavin (B2)"]'::jsonb, 'Thiamine (B1)', 'Transketolase is a HMP shunt enzyme requiring TPP. It transfers 2-carbon units between sugar phosphates. Its activity is used to assess thiamine deficiency.'),
('f1b75205-1909-512d-98cb-cee3fa7deb7a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'The HMP shunt is most active in:', '["Liver and adipose tissue","Heart and spleen","Kidney and lung","RBCs and testes","Muscle and brain"]'::jsonb, 'Liver and adipose tissue', 'The HMP shunt (pentose phosphate pathway) produces NADPH for reductive biosynthesis and ribose-5-phosphate for nucleotide synthesis. NADPH is essential for fatty acid synthesis and glutathione reduction.'),
('04d2bbfc-2140-53cd-a154-5e8690fc6937'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'NADPH produced by the HMP shunt is used for:', '["Detoxification reactions","Steroid synthesis","All of the above","Reduction of glutathione","Fatty acid synthesis"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'The HMP shunt (pentose phosphate pathway) produces NADPH for reductive biosynthesis and ribose-5-phosphate for nucleotide synthesis. NADPH is essential for fatty acid synthesis and glutathione reduction.'),
('fdb5771d-1ca2-5fd6-8f64-de120107a3a7'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Ribose-5-phosphate is essential for:', '["ATP synthesis","NAD synthesis","All of the above","DNA synthesis","RNA synthesis"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Ribose-5-phosphate is essential for synthesis of nucleotides (ATP, DNA, RNA, NAD, FAD, CoA). The HMP shunt provides both ribose-5-phosphate and NADPH.'),
('9bed95a5-9259-5de4-b500-4c9c5fa0617f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'The overall equation of the TCA cycle yields per turn:', '["3 NADH + 1 FADH2 + 1 GTP","3 NADH + 2 FADH2 + 1 GTP","2 NADH + 1 FADH2 + 2 GTP","4 NADH + 1 FADH2 + 1 ATP","2 NADH + 2 FADH2 + 1 ATP"]'::jsonb, '3 NADH + 1 FADH2 + 1 GTP', 'The TCA cycle yields 3 NADH, 1 FADH2, and 1 GTP (or ATP) per turn. These reduced coenzymes generate additional ATP via the electron transport chain.'),
('137a4f27-aed4-5363-a6f8-9f1851974741'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'During therapeutic management of Carbohydrates, CYP enzyme metabolising most drugs:', '["CYP3A4","CYP2D6","CYP2C9","CYP2C19","CYP1A2"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4', 'CYP3A4 metabolises 50% of clinically used drugs.'),
('8aeafc95-bff8-52c2-923f-39ebf05cf5bc'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'A newborn presents with vomiting, lethargy, and developmental delay. Newborn screening shows elevated amino acids. Alpha-ketoglutarate is a precursor for the amino acid:', '["Cysteine","Serine","Aspartate","Alanine","Glutamate"]'::jsonb, 'Glutamate', 'Glutamate is derived from alpha-ketoglutarate (a TCA cycle intermediate) via transamination or glutamate dehydrogenase. It is a precursor for GABA, glutamine, and proline.'),
('6648bd07-2840-5599-8da6-fe2c08863ede'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'A newborn presents with vomiting, lethargy, and developmental delay. Newborn screening shows elevated amino acids. Oxaloacetate is a precursor for the amino acid:', '["Aspartate","Leucine","Glutamate","Valine","Alanine"]'::jsonb, 'Aspartate', 'Aspartate is derived from oxaloacetate (a TCA cycle intermediate) via transamination with AST. It is a precursor for asparagine, purines, and pyrimidines.'),
('77443ede-fa11-5a6f-89b3-d99a60228ae9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Which TCA cycle enzyme is inhibited by fluoroacetate?', '["Alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase","Isocitrate dehydrogenase","Aconitase","Succinate dehydrogenase","Citrate synthase"]'::jsonb, 'Aconitase', 'Fluoroacetate is converted to fluorocitrate, which inhibits aconitase, blocking the TCA cycle. This is an example of lethal synthesis where a compound is metabolized to a toxic inhibitor.'),
('7e9363a5-5b64-5c24-ab8a-d3baae02f12b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Arsenite inhibits which enzyme of the TCA cycle?', '["Isocitrate dehydrogenase","Malate dehydrogenase","Alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase","Citrate synthase","Succinate dehydrogenase"]'::jsonb, 'Alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase', 'Arsenite inhibits alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase (and pyruvate dehydrogenase) by binding to the lipoic acid cofactor, blocking the TCA cycle and pyruvate oxidation.'),
('10e92777-95af-54e1-8ea9-57dd80faefcf'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Malonate is a competitive inhibitor of:', '["Fumarase","Malate dehydrogenase","Isocitrate dehydrogenase","Succinate dehydrogenase","Citrate synthase"]'::jsonb, 'Succinate dehydrogenase', 'Malonate is a competitive inhibitor of succinate dehydrogenase (Complex II), structurally mimicking succinate but lacking the second carboxyl group needed for oxidation.'),
('0ed72565-4794-50e4-8177-d025bb136d75'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'A 65-year-old female presents with bleeding tendency. Dietary history suggests nutritional deficiency. Which anatomical region is most commonly implicated?', '["Niacin (B3)","Pantothenic acid (B5)","Riboflavin (B2)","Thiamine (B1)","Thiamine only"]'::jsonb, 'Thiamine (B1)', 'Thiamine (B1) deficiency causes wet beriberi with bleeding tendency due to impaired pyruvate dehydrogenase and transketolase function, affecting energy metabolism and platelet function.'),
('f9bf2551-2027-5645-b33a-4d78f1cf8b2f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'The conversion of succinyl-CoA to succinate is catalyzed by:', '["Succinyl-CoA hydrolase","Succinate dehydrogenase","Succinyl-CoA synthetase","Succinyl-CoA transferase","Fumarase"]'::jsonb, 'Succinyl-CoA synthetase', 'Succinyl-CoA synthetase (succinate thiokinase) catalyzes substrate-level phosphorylation, converting succinyl-CoA to succinate while generating GTP (or ATP) from GDP.'),
('f8041a0e-a9b1-5df9-a0d3-6c33dc5224ea'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Which of the following is not a substrate for gluconeogenesis?', '["Fatty acids (even-chain)","Acetyl-CoA","Glycerol","Alanine","Lactate"]'::jsonb, 'Acetyl-CoA', 'Acetyl-CoA cannot be used for net glucose synthesis in animals because pyruvate dehydrogenase is irreversible. However, even-chain fatty acids produce only acetyl-CoA, not glucose.'),
('1e753a85-16fd-5008-b88d-3a7c3fa198d9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Carbohydrates: Phase II conjugation reaction:', '["Glucuronidation","Oxidation","Reduction","Hydrolysis","Deamination"]'::jsonb, 'Glucuronidation', 'Phase II: glucuronidation, sulfation, acetylation, methylation, glutathione conjugation.'),
('d3120857-1205-5861-ab90-9b37b55b0439'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'UDP-glucose is used in:', '["Glycogenesis","Glycolysis","Gluconeogenesis","HMP shunt","Glycogenolysis"]'::jsonb, 'Glycogenesis', 'UDP-glucose is the activated form of glucose used in glycogenesis (glycogen synthesis). Glycogen synthase transfers glucose from UDP-glucose to the growing glycogen chain.'),
('90f3738f-ab29-568b-a0eb-78bd1b4893f2'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'A 50-year-old female has a fasting glucose of 280 mg/dL. Which anatomical structure is most likely involved?', '["Heart and spleen","All tissues","RBCs","Muscle and brain","Liver and kidney"]'::jsonb, 'Liver and kidney', 'A fasting glucose of 280 mg/dL indicates diabetes mellitus. The liver and kidney are primarily involved in glucose homeostasis, with the liver performing gluconeogenesis and glycogenolysis.'),
('cbaaf083-d4a7-5be1-aa94-b8abd17073fe'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Which organ is the primary site of gluconeogenesis?', '["Adipose tissue","Kidney","Muscle","Brain","Liver"]'::jsonb, 'Liver', 'The liver is the primary site of gluconeogenesis, maintaining blood glucose during fasting. The kidney also performs gluconeogenesis, contributing up to 40% during prolonged fasting.'),
('dfec3234-affe-5024-8507-02837702d9be'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'The Cori cycle involves the conversion of:', '["Glucose to lactate in muscle and lactate to glucose in liver","Fatty acids to glucose in liver","Amino acids to glucose in muscle","Glucose to fat in adipose","Glucose to glycogen in liver"]'::jsonb, 'Glucose to lactate in muscle and lactate to glucose in liver', 'The Cori cycle involves conversion of lactate (produced by anaerobic glycolysis in muscle) back to glucose in the liver. This lactate is transported via blood to the liver where it is converted to pyruvate and then to glucose through gluconeogenesis.'),
('f2f2c604-4ba5-5492-82b3-d2ad5a157534'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Which regulatory mechanism is being described?', '["Decrease in ATP under anaerobic conditions","Increase in lactate under aerobic conditions","Stimulation of glycolysis by oxygen","None of the above","Inhibition of glycolysis by oxygen"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibition of glycolysis by oxygen', 'The Pasteur effect describes inhibition of glycolysis by oxygen. Under aerobic conditions, oxidative phosphorylation produces more ATP, reducing the need for glycolysis.'),
('c394cb12-9dd6-5cd5-a937-cf39b5cc9f60'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Lab results for a 30-year-old female show abnormal levels of a key metabolite. What is the underlying mechanism of this condition?', '["RBCs","Normal cells","Cancer cells","Muscle cells","Brain cells"]'::jsonb, 'Cancer cells', 'The Warburg effect describes how cancer cells preferentially use aerobic glycolysis (glycolysis even in the presence of oxygen), producing lactate rather than fully oxidizing glucose.'),
('10553e7b-5a56-581e-b1cf-2406aad8bc87'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Which enzyme converts galactose to galactose-1-phosphate?', '["Galactokinase","GALT","UDP-galactose-4-epimerase","Lactase","Glucokinase"]'::jsonb, 'Galactokinase', 'Galactokinase catalyzes the first step of galactose metabolism, phosphorylating galactose to galactose-1-phosphate. Its deficiency causes milder galactosemia than classic GALT deficiency.'),
('9b4b85c0-84f9-5597-9cf4-566be7048a95'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'The main treatment for galactosemia is:', '["Supplements of galactose","Insulin therapy","Avoidance of fructose","Avoidance of lactose","Low protein diet"]'::jsonb, 'Avoidance of lactose', 'Classic galactosemia is caused by galactose-1-phosphate uridylyltransferase deficiency. Accumulation of galactose-1-P and galactitol causes cataracts, hepatomegaly, and intellectual disability.'),
('99ea33b1-1694-5780-a80a-18d3a8c58488'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Hereditary fructose intolerance is due to deficiency of:', '["Hexokinase","Aldolase B","Pyruvate kinase","Phosphofructokinase","Fructokinase"]'::jsonb, 'Aldolase B', 'Hereditary fructose intolerance is caused by aldolase B deficiency. Fructose-1-phosphate accumulates, trapping phosphate and inhibiting glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis, causing hypoglycemia.'),
('7c76da1f-5d30-52f5-aa11-0fee4b41c2f6'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Which carbohydrate is a reducing sugar?', '["Cellulose","Starch","Lactose","Trehalose","Sucrose"]'::jsonb, 'Lactose', 'Lactose is a reducing sugar because it has a free anomeric carbon on the glucose unit that can open to form an aldehyde group, which reduces Benedict''s or Fehling''s reagent.'),
('3eec6d67-b212-5429-ab3e-56545a79b956'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'What is the difference between alpha and beta anomers of glucose?', '["Configuration at C5","Configuration at C4","Configuration at C2","Configuration at C1","Configuration at C3"]'::jsonb, 'Configuration at C1', 'Alpha and beta anomers differ in the configuration at C1 (the anomeric carbon). In alpha-D-glucose the OH at C1 is axial (down); in beta it is equatorial (up).'),
('fdb56e4d-4fc8-5a61-bde6-dcbe33f7569b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Mutarotation refers to:', '["Formation of glycosidic bonds","Conversion of glucose to fructose","Denaturation of proteins","Racemization of amino acids","Change in optical rotation of a sugar solution"]'::jsonb, 'Change in optical rotation of a sugar solution', 'Mutarotation is the change in optical rotation of a sugar solution as alpha and beta anomers interconvert through the open-chain form until equilibrium is reached.'),
('2adeb724-eba1-5511-bce3-361bace65276'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Oligosaccharides contain how many monosaccharide units?', '["More than 50","10-20","2-10","1-2","20-50"]'::jsonb, '2-10', 'Oligosaccharides contain 2-10 monosaccharide units linked by glycosidic bonds. Disaccharides (2 units) like sucrose and lactose are the simplest oligosaccharides.'),
('616288a6-baf3-5802-881f-79682cb44535'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Lab results for a 50-year-old female show abnormal levels of a key metabolite. Which anatomical structure is most likely involved?', '["Glucose","Galactose","Fructose","None of the above","Never"]'::jsonb, 'Galactose', 'Galactose is involved in this metabolic pathway. In galactosemia, galactose-1-phosphate accumulates due to GALT deficiency, causing liver damage, cataracts, and intellectual disability.'),
('b54924f1-0d1a-59b4-a7dc-35294020e2f5'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Stachyose is a tetrasaccharide found in:', '["Legumes","Fruits","Milk","Roots","Grains"]'::jsonb, 'Legumes', 'Stachyose is a tetrasaccharide (galactose-galactose-glucose-fructose) found in legumes. Humans lack alpha-galactosidase, causing gas and bloating from bacterial fermentation.'),
('2d21fbe6-3e8e-5880-9adc-1f5c6e459ad5'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Amylose is a linear polymer of glucose with what type of linkage?', '["Alpha-1","Beta-1","4","None of the above","Never"]'::jsonb, 'Alpha-1', 'Amylose is a linear polymer of glucose with alpha-1,4 glycosidic linkages. It is a component of starch and forms a helical structure that traps iodine, producing a blue color.'),
('62ca7d41-eb95-5625-8cf8-16f157b1a68c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Amylopectin is a branched polymer with what type of branch linkage?', '["Beta-1","6","4","Alpha-1","None of the above"]'::jsonb, '4', 'Amylopectin has alpha-1,6 branch points every 24-30 glucose residues. These branches increase the number of non-reducing ends, allowing rapid glycogen phosphorylase action.'),
('4f8e3050-bcec-503b-8236-a2e1407ff273'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'The difference between amylopectin and glycogen is:', '["Amylopectin is more highly branched","They are identical","Amylopectin has beta linkages","Glycogen is more highly branched","Glycogen has beta linkages"]'::jsonb, 'Glycogen is more highly branched', 'Glycogen is more highly branched than amylopectin, with alpha-1,6 branches every 8-12 residues (vs 24-30 in amylopectin). This allows faster glucose mobilization.'),
('25e4ff8f-04b7-5b6c-a591-959b1e28ade4'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Which polysaccharide is a component of agar?', '["Chitin","Agarose","Starch","Pectin","Cellulose"]'::jsonb, 'Agarose', 'Agarose is a polysaccharide extracted from red algae that forms gels used in electrophoresis and bacterial culture media. It is a linear polymer of alternating galactose units.'),
('26afdfea-6542-5130-83ee-c8379451e8ae'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Dextrans are polymers of:', '["Galactose","Glucose","Ribose","Mannose","Fructose"]'::jsonb, 'Glucose', 'Dextrans are branched polymers of glucose with predominantly alpha-1,6 linkages (with some alpha-1,3 branches). They are produced by bacteria and used clinically as plasma expanders.'),
('9d52b5cf-6af7-51b3-88ed-e31e568705cb'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Lab results for a 70-year-old male show abnormal levels of a key metabolite. What is the most likely site of pathology?', '["Amino acids only","Repeating disaccharide units","Fatty acids only","Nucleotides only","Steroids only"]'::jsonb, 'Repeating disaccharide units', 'Repeating disaccharide units are characteristic of glycosaminoglycans (GAGs), which are long unbranched polysaccharides consisting of repeating disaccharide units of amino sugars and uronic acids.'),
('1840ef6b-73ee-547c-8676-b5c927493a6d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Hyaluronic acid is a glycosaminoglycan found in:', '["Blood","Bone","Cartilage","Muscle","Connective tissues"]'::jsonb, 'Connective tissues', 'Hyaluronic acid is found in connective tissues, synovial fluid, and the vitreous humor of the eye. It provides lubrication and shock absorption due to its high water-binding capacity.'),
('c97e603d-e29d-5c80-9220-881ba377915e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Chondroitin sulfate is a component of:', '["Bone","Cartilage","Tendons","Skin","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Chondroitin sulfate is a major component of cartilage, bone, tendons, and skin. It provides structural support and resilience to these tissues.'),
('ed031266-8022-5046-9b4e-92f5929e5b36'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Lab results for a 30-year-old male show abnormal levels of a key metabolite. What is the most likely site of pathology?', '["Blood and lymph","Cornea and cartilage","Bone and teeth","Brain and nerves","Liver and kidney"]'::jsonb, 'Cornea and cartilage', 'Mucopolysaccharidoses involve accumulation of GAGs in the cornea and cartilage, causing corneal clouding and skeletal abnormalities. Keratan sulfate accumulates in Morquio syndrome.'),
('f6ba7b36-5763-5a49-8813-0f4f88ae98a9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Which GAG is found in skin and heart valves?', '["Keratan sulfate","Dermatan sulfate","Hyaluronic acid","Heparin","Chondroitin sulfate"]'::jsonb, 'Dermatan sulfate', 'Dermatan sulfate is found in skin, heart valves, and blood vessels. It contributes to structural integrity and is deficient in certain mucopolysaccharidoses.'),
('cb5016a4-b98c-5ebc-9d22-04936d3bda47'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Which lipid or metabolite is involved in this pathway?', '["Proteins only resulting in decreased functional activity","Lipids accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Oligosaccharides attached to proteins/lipids","Fatty acids without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Nucleotides leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Oligosaccharides attached to proteins/lipids', 'Oligosaccharides attached to proteins and lipids are involved in cell recognition, signaling, and immune responses. They form the glycocalyx and determine blood group types.'),
('58653224-7b64-51d2-903b-11113f1ba608'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Which metabolite is involved in this carbohydrate pathway?', '["Glucose","Galactose","N-acetylglucosamine","Fucose","N-acetylgalactosamine"]'::jsonb, 'Fucose', 'Fucose is a deoxyhexose sugar involved in glycoprotein and glycolipid synthesis. It is a component of ABO blood group antigens and cell adhesion molecules.'),
('321a079a-5394-555d-83c8-2f827c921161'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'In the A blood group', '["N-acetylgalactosamine","Fucose","The terminal sugar added to H antigen is:","N-acetylglucosamine","Galactose"]'::jsonb, 'Galactose', 'In the A blood group, N-acetylgalactosamine is the terminal sugar added to the H antigen by A transferase, creating the A antigen on red blood cell surfaces.'),
('0bf7be48-292f-5019-a149-3e6eb5a4ddaf'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Sialic acid (N-acetylneuraminic acid) is a:', '["5-carbon sugar","9-carbon acidic monosaccharide","6-carbon sugar","Fatty acid","Amino acid"]'::jsonb, '9-carbon acidic monosaccharide', 'Sialic acid (N-acetylneuraminic acid) is a 9-carbon acidic monosaccharide found at the terminal positions of glycoproteins and glycolipids, important for cell recognition and signaling.'),
('12e59346-4444-55a2-b179-a255de3aef9e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Carbohydrates: St. John''s Wort induces:', '["CYP3A4 and P-gp","CYP2D6","CYP2C9","CYP1A2","CYP2C19"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4 and P-gp', 'St. John''s Wort induces CYP3A4 and P-gp, reducing drug levels.'),
('8069c75c-e09e-5acb-946f-1c64b9106da1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'A 50-year-old female presents with generalized edema. Where is the most probable location of the lesion?', '["RBCs","WBCs","Endothelial cells","Platelets","Neurons"]'::jsonb, 'RBCs', 'RBCs are the most likely site involved in this condition, as they are directly affected by hemoglobin disorders and rely entirely on glucose metabolism for energy.'),
('060438bc-9a3b-524b-b68a-07ddbd956b79'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'The Edman degradation is used for:', '["DNA sequencing","Carbohydrate analysis","Protein sequencing","Lipid analysis","RNA analysis"]'::jsonb, 'Protein sequencing', 'Edman degradation sequences proteins from the N-terminus by selectively cleaving one amino acid at a time using phenylisothiocyanate (PITC), identifying each residue sequentially.'),
('fe6890aa-f404-51be-ab5e-71e0e9f9999e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'The uronic acid pathway converts glucose to:', '["Glucuronic acid","Pentoses","UDP-glucuronic acid","All of the above","Ascorbic acid"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'The uronic acid pathway converts glucose to glucuronic acid, which is used for conjugation reactions (detoxification), glycosaminoglycan synthesis, and vitamin C synthesis in some species.'),
('8021da60-022a-51c5-9e89-9a76811540f5'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'UDP-glucuronic acid is used in:', '["DNA synthesis","Conjugation reactions (detoxification)","Fatty acid synthesis","Glycogen synthesis","Protein synthesis"]'::jsonb, 'Conjugation reactions (detoxification)', 'UDP-glucuronic acid is used in conjugation reactions for detoxification (e.g., bilirubin, drugs, steroids) via glucuronidation, making them more water-soluble for excretion.'),
('065da41f-c429-5ecf-a219-cdd7c97a061b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'The Maillard reaction involves:', '["Proteins and nucleic acids","Metals and proteins","Reducing sugars and amino acids","Lipids and proteins","Carbohydrates and lipids"]'::jsonb, 'Reducing sugars and amino acids', 'The Maillard reaction is a non-enzymatic reaction between reducing sugars and amino groups of proteins, producing brown pigments (melanoidins). It occurs during food cooking and contributes to AGEs in diabetes.'),
('a1883d93-372f-57a4-a242-dd7da1153d6c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Glycation of hemoglobin produces:', '["HbA2","HbS","HbF","HbH","HbA1c"]'::jsonb, 'HbA1c', 'Glycation of hemoglobin produces HbA1c (glycated hemoglobin). HbA1c levels reflect average blood glucose over the preceding 2-3 months and are used to monitor diabetes control.'),
('ae1caca4-1e00-5bf5-96c0-6bc5a6794537'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Regarding clinical cases in Carbohydrates: Highest capacity Phase II reaction:', '["Glucuronidation","Sulfation","Acetylation","Methylation","Glutathione conjugation"]'::jsonb, 'Glucuronidation', 'Due to abundant UDP-glucuronic acid cofactor.'),
('7bad2fbf-3a1a-5a81-8bc5-697a2067e0f9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Mannose is an epimer of glucose at:', '["C2","C3","C5","C1","C4"]'::jsonb, 'C2', 'Mannose is a C2 epimer of glucose. The difference is in the configuration at carbon 2 - in mannose the OH is on the left (in Fischer projection) while in glucose it is on the right.'),
('1ea3cf78-fb43-5388-85be-b04d1f1cde39'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Thiazolidinediones (TZDs) act as agonists of:', '["PPAR-alpha","PPAR-gamma","RXR","PPAR-delta","LXR"]'::jsonb, 'PPAR-gamma', 'Thiazolidinediones (rosiglitazone, pioglitazone) are PPAR-gamma agonists that improve insulin sensitivity in adipose tissue, muscle, and liver, used in type 2 diabetes treatment.'),
('28f20f77-be29-5885-b3e1-fd9050a987cd'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'SGLT2 inhibitors act on which organ?', '["Liver","Muscle","Intestine","Kidney","Pancreas"]'::jsonb, 'Kidney', 'SGLT transporters use sodium gradient for active glucose transport. SGLT1 (intestine) absorbs dietary glucose; SGLT2 (proximal tubule) reabsorbs 90% of filtered glucose. SGLT2 inhibitors treat diabetes.'),
('8393ccb3-f201-54ce-911b-9b1f7eee22c4'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'The normal HbA1c level for a non-diabetic person is:', '["<7.0%","<6.5%","<4%","<8.0%","<5.7%"]'::jsonb, '<5.7%', 'Normal HbA1c is <5.7%. Values 5.7-6.4% indicate prediabetes; >=6.5% on two occasions indicates diabetes. Target for diabetic patients is usually <7%.'),
('b6119825-e0f6-5d53-97df-9031cf58c5d8'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Impaired fasting glucose (IFG) is defined as:', '["FG 70-100 mg/dL","FG 126-140 mg/dL","FG <70 mg/dL","FG 100-125 mg/dL","FG >140 mg/dL"]'::jsonb, 'FG 100-125 mg/dL', 'Impaired fasting glucose (IFG) is defined as fasting glucose of 100-125 mg/dL (5.6-6.9 mmol/L). It represents prediabetes and increased risk for progression to type 2 diabetes.'),
('68b2b38b-4ba8-5998-ba0b-528903cc527f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'In the study of Carbohydrates, Loading dose formula:', '["Vd x Css / F","CL x Css x tau / F","Vd x CL / Css","Css x tau / CL","Fx Css / Vd"]'::jsonb, 'Vd x Css / F', 'Loading dose depends on Vd and target concentration.'),
('0913e86e-50ef-5cc1-ae1e-8d760174d818'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Carbohydrates: Maintenance dose formula:', '["CL x Css x tau / F","Vd x Css / F","Vd x Css x tau","CL x Vd / Css","Css x tau / Vd"]'::jsonb, 'CL x Css x tau / F', 'Maintenance dose depends on clearance and target concentration.'),
('c83db157-7520-59bb-83d0-e76b6cea2018'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Carbohydrates: Steady state reached after:', '["4-5 half-lives","1 half-life","2 half-lives","6-7 half-lives","10 half-lives"]'::jsonb, '4-5 half-lives', 'Css reached after 4-5 half-lives.'),
('f4e831e3-61e3-5b84-aa0b-f1918659c73e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Gestational diabetes is diagnosed with:', '["Fasting glucose only","OGTT using 75g glucose","HbA1c only","OGTT using 100g glucose","Random glucose only"]'::jsonb, 'OGTT using 100g glucose', 'Gestational diabetes is diagnosed using a 100g oral glucose tolerance test (OGTT), with two or more abnormal values: fasting >=95 mg/dL, 1h >=180 mg/dL, 2h >=155 mg/dL, 3h >=140 mg/dL.'),
('1870de66-5e86-5a01-b17d-57d3950f6a22'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Regarding clinical cases in Carbohydrates: Warfarin primarily metabolised by:', '["CYP2C9","CYP3A4","CYP2D6","CYP2C19","CYP1A2"]'::jsonb, 'CYP2C9', 'CYP2C9 metabolises the more potent S-warfarin.'),
('57c95694-b570-507e-9fd0-d3396255f407'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'In the study of Carbohydrates, Large Vd means drug is:', '["Distributed into tissues","Confined to plasma","Rapidly excreted","Bound to albumin","Hepatically metabolised"]'::jsonb, 'Distributed into tissues', 'Large Vd >1 L/kg indicates extensive tissue distribution.'),
('e722c24b-7b31-55a3-97ce-825c8108b8d7'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'A 50-year-old female presents with hepatomegaly and hypoglycemia. Liver biopsy shows glycogen accumulation. Which pathophysiological process best explains this presentation?', '["Cleaves glucose units","Terminates glycogen synthesis","Initiates glycogen synthesis","Activates branching","Provides glucose units for elongation"]'::jsonb, 'Provides glucose units for elongation', 'Liver glycogen serves to maintain blood glucose. Liver contains glucose-6-phosphatase, which converts G6P to free glucose that can be released into the bloodstream.'),
('eeb1be5c-198d-5c4f-9117-4d583c1ccb06'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Glycogenin is the protein that:', '["Initiates glycogen synthesis","Branches glycogen","Initiates glycogenolysis","Transports glucose","Cleaves glycogen"]'::jsonb, 'Initiates glycogen synthesis', 'Glycogenin is a protein primer that initiates glycogen synthesis by autoglucosylating itself, creating a short chain of glucose residues (about 8) that glycogen synthase can then extend.'),
('346c1f8c-5d2e-5c00-a1fb-c94e95282942'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Glycogen synthase is activated by:', '["Dephosphorylation","Ca2+","CAMP","Phosphorylation","AMP"]'::jsonb, 'Dephosphorylation', 'Glycogen synthase catalyzes glycogen synthesis by adding UDP-glucose to the growing chain via α-1,4 bonds. It is activated by insulin (dephosphorylation) and inhibited by glucagon (phosphorylation).'),
('9c589cba-43a7-5bb1-b3b9-555208a31386'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Regarding clinical cases in Carbohydrates: Orally absorbed drugs first enter:', '["Portal vein","Systemic circulation","Lymphatics","IVC","Thoracic duct"]'::jsonb, 'Portal vein', 'Portal vein -> liver (first-pass) -> systemic circulation.'),
('48a4fa4a-05e9-5fcd-98d9-34b3da0dc93b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'In the study of Carbohydrates, Acetaminophen toxic metabolite:', '["NAPQI","MPP+","MPTP","N-oxide","Sulfate conjugate"]'::jsonb, 'NAPQI', 'NAPQI normally detoxified by glutathione. Overdose depletes glutathione.'),
('2811fd08-4ba1-5249-b1cb-077fffecf5ab'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Alanine transaminase (ALT) converts alanine to:', '["Oxaloacetate","Acetyl-CoA","Pyruvate","Lactate","Alpha-ketoglutarate"]'::jsonb, 'Pyruvate', 'ALT (alanine aminotransferase) converts alanine to pyruvate and alpha-ketoglutarate to glutamate. This reaction links amino acid metabolism to gluconeogenesis via the glucose-alanine cycle.'),
('44beaa57-8e9f-555f-8de9-6e7d0df7f28d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Which of the following is an anaplerotic reaction for the TCA cycle?', '["Fumarate to malate","Malate to oxaloacetate","Citrate to isocitrate","Succinyl-CoA to succinate","Pyruvate to oxaloacetate"]'::jsonb, 'Pyruvate to oxaloacetate', 'Anaplerotic reactions replenish TCA cycle intermediates. Pyruvate carboxylase converts pyruvate to oxaloacetate, restoring cycle intermediates when they are depleted for biosynthesis.'),
('ad951fb8-8e12-5d8b-9d47-30c0d9acc246'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'The glyoxylate cycle is found in:', '["Human cells","Fungi only","Virus","Animals only","Plants and bacteria"]'::jsonb, 'Plants and bacteria', 'The glyoxylate cycle is found in plants and bacteria, allowing conversion of acetyl-CoA (from fatty acids) to succinate for gluconeogenesis. It bypasses the CO2-losing steps of the TCA cycle.'),
('71a37328-efa5-5a34-8c61-6cac7f5ddd44'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Which TCA cycle intermediate is oxidized to regenerate oxaloacetate?', '["Malate from glyoxylate and acetyl-CoA","Malate from fumarate","Pyruvate from malate","Citrate from oxaloacetate","Oxaloacetate from malate"]'::jsonb, 'Malate from glyoxylate and acetyl-CoA', 'In the glyoxylate cycle, malate is formed from glyoxylate and acetyl-CoA (by malate synthase) and then oxidized to oxaloacetate by malate dehydrogenase, regenerating the cycle acceptor.'),
('49bd47b2-32c8-5411-bb9b-f48b5270e500'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Which disease is associated with a deficiency of branched-chain alpha-keto acid dehydrogenase?', '["Alkaptonuria","Homocystinuria","Tyrosinemia","Maple syrup urine disease","Phenylketonuria"]'::jsonb, 'Maple syrup urine disease', 'Maple syrup urine disease is caused by branched-chain alpha-keto acid dehydrogenase deficiency, leading to accumulation of leucine, isoleucine, and valine, causing neurological damage.'),
('c0b7118d-9a23-5c25-b8d2-f44f9ff9b484'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Which amino acids accumulate in maple syrup urine disease?', '["Leucine","Valine","Isoleucine","Glycine","Alanine"]'::jsonb, 'Leucine', 'Maple syrup urine disease is caused by branched-chain alpha-ketoacid dehydrogenase deficiency. Leucine, isoleucine, and valine (branched-chain amino acids) accumulate, causing neurological damage and sweet-smelling (maple syrup) urine.'),
('9c2a8291-ddac-52a8-8887-c4f6214e23fe'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Which metabolic disorder causes sweet-smelling urine?', '["Maple syrup","Mousy","Cabbage","Rancid butter","Sweet"]'::jsonb, 'Maple syrup', 'Maple syrup urine disease causes sweet-smelling urine due to accumulation of branched-chain amino acids and their keto acids, particularly sotolone which gives the characteristic odor.'),
('a5c2cf2e-488b-5800-acc9-3d42c6adfea5'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Which disease or disorder results from this deficiency or abnormality?', '["Lactic acidosis","Homocystinuria","Phenylketonuria","Alkaptonuria","Maple syrup urine disease"]'::jsonb, 'Lactic acidosis', 'Lactic acidosis results from conditions that impair oxidative phosphorylation or increase glycolysis, causing lactate accumulation. Types A (hypoxia) and B (metabolic causes) exist.'),
('0c4c2b94-e747-5bf7-a9d0-d330940d463f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'The biotin-containing enzyme in gluconeogenesis is:', '["Fructose-1","PEP carboxykinase","Pyruvate carboxylase","6-bisphosphatase","Glucose-6-phosphatase"]'::jsonb, 'Pyruvate carboxylase', 'Biotin (vitamin B7) is a cofactor for carboxylases: pyruvate carboxylase, acetyl-CoA carboxylase, and propionyl-CoA carboxylase. Raw egg whites contain avidin that binds biotin.'),
('074bf7b1-d1d1-55cf-9415-90b015a52ea8'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Which of the following can be synthesized from glucose via glucuronic acid?', '["Vitamin D resulting in decreased functional activity","Vitamin A accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Vitamin K mediated by specific receptor activation","Vitamin C (in some species)","Vitamin E leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Vitamin C (in some species)', 'Vitamin C (ascorbic acid) can be synthesized from glucose via glucuronic acid pathway in most mammals (but not humans, guinea pigs, or primates who lack L-gulonolactone oxidase).'),
('07407f69-ad62-51f5-b8b7-0d2a81ef3e2e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'The polyol pathway converts glucose to:', '["Galactitol","Xylitol","Sorbitol","Fructose","Ribitol"]'::jsonb, 'Sorbitol', 'The polyol pathway converts glucose to sorbitol (via aldose reductase) and then to fructose (via sorbitol dehydrogenase). Sorbitol accumulation causes osmotic damage in diabetic complications.'),
('3a6393ac-5bc5-5606-aabb-25280e8f1b06'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'The molecular basis of diabetic neuropathy involves:', '["Increased insulin sensitivity","Increased sorbitol and decreased myo-inositol","Decreased sorbitol","Increased myo-inositol","Increased glucose uptake"]'::jsonb, 'Increased sorbitol and decreased myo-inositol', 'Diabetic neuropathy involves increased sorbitol (from the polyol pathway) causing osmotic damage, and decreased myo-inositol affecting nerve cell membrane integrity and Na+/K+-ATPase function.'),
('770002a0-f96b-5fe5-b3c9-acb2634a0809'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Advanced glycation end products (AGEs) contribute to:', '["Diabetic complications","All of the above","Aging","None of the above","Atherosclerosis"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'AGEs contribute to diabetic complications (retinopathy, nephropathy, neuropathy), atherosclerosis, and aging by modifying proteins, causing cross-linking, and activating inflammatory pathways.'),
('9d0404d3-f496-5a2d-86a9-17eeb2993886'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Which of the following is a sugar alcohol (polyol)?', '["Sorbitol","Galactose","Glucuronic acid","Glucose","Glucosamine"]'::jsonb, 'Sorbitol', 'Sorbitol (glucitol) is a sugar alcohol produced from glucose by aldose reductase. It accumulates in diabetic complications (cataracts, neuropathy) and is also found in fruits.'),
('8bdf3134-6115-5f12-be53-f03e45d08c30'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Xylitol is a sugar alcohol used as a sweetener derived from:', '["Glucose","Xylose","Galactose","Fructose","Ribose"]'::jsonb, 'Xylose', 'Xylitol is derived from xylose (a 5-carbon sugar) by reduction. It is used as a sugar-free sweetener that does not promote dental caries and may reduce Streptococcus mutans.'),
('ed62919b-b2a5-5704-8718-ff47fc766b72'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Carbohydrates: Zero-order kinetics means:', '["Constant amount eliminated/time","Constant fraction/time","Dose-dependent elimination","First-order at high doses","Saturable absorption"]'::jsonb, 'Constant amount eliminated/time', 'Zero-order: constant amount eliminated regardless of concentration.'),
('6b69b89e-cc89-54c2-9e8c-ab774e5ed5ce'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Trehalose is a non-reducing disaccharide composed of:', '["1 linkage","Two glucose units with alpha-1","Two fructose units","Glucose and fructose","Glucose and galactose"]'::jsonb, 'Two glucose units with alpha-1', 'Trehalose consists of two glucose units linked by an alpha-1,1 glycosidic bond. The anomeric carbons of both glucose units are involved in the bond, making it non-reducing.'),
('b3b46ba4-ade3-5100-a535-93e011ee1817'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Which enzyme digests trehalose?', '["Maltase","Trehalase","Sucrase","Lactase","Amylase"]'::jsonb, 'Trehalase', 'Trehalase is the enzyme that digests trehalose, found in the brush border of intestinal epithelial cells. It cleaves the alpha-1,1 bond to release two glucose molecules.'),
('064067b0-33b0-5d51-95b1-b618a5c6b2d5'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'The glycemic load considers:', '["Glycemic index and carbohydrate content per serving","Fiber content only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Carbohydrate content only mediated by specific receptor activation","Protein content only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Glycemic index only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Glycemic index and carbohydrate content per serving', 'Glycemic load = (glycemic index x carbohydrate content per serving) / 100. It accounts for both the quality (GI) and quantity of carbohydrates, providing a more practical measure.'),
('00841474-6ed4-5ace-b883-cc58ae7fa0e0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Which function is NOT associated with carbohydrates?', '["Structural component","Cellulose in cell walls","Cell signaling","Catalytic activity","Energy storage"]'::jsonb, 'Catalytic activity', 'Catalytic activity is not a function of carbohydrates. Carbohydrates serve as energy sources, structural components (cellulose, chitin), cell recognition molecules, and signaling molecules, but enzymes (catalysts) are proteins.'),
('13298515-d3ed-533d-8ac4-d22988da646b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Lipopolysaccharides (LPS) are components of:', '["Gram-positive bacterial membrane","Fungal cell wall","Mammalian plasma membrane","Gram-negative bacterial outer membrane","Viral envelope"]'::jsonb, 'Gram-negative bacterial outer membrane', 'LPS is a component of the outer membrane of Gram-negative bacteria. It consists of lipid A (endotoxin), core polysaccharide, and O-antigen, triggering strong immune responses.'),
('e4a4db86-6896-511c-9ff5-a7ee5e7554e8'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'The sugar component of LPS is:', '["O-antigen","Core polysaccharide","Teichoic acid","Lipid A","Peptidoglycan"]'::jsonb, 'O-antigen', 'The O-antigen is the outermost sugar component of LPS, consisting of repeating oligosaccharide units. It is the most variable part and determines the bacterial serotype.'),
('87aebc8a-6f9f-5a4c-adf3-c99e589f7829'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Peptidoglycan contains:', '["Glucose and fructose","Ribose and deoxyribose","N-acetylglucosamine and N-acetylmuramic acid","Chitin and cellulose","Galactose and mannose"]'::jsonb, 'N-acetylglucosamine and N-acetylmuramic acid', 'Peptidoglycan contains alternating N-acetylglucosamine (NAG) and N-acetylmuramic acid (NAM) units linked by beta-1,4 glycosidic bonds, forming the rigid bacterial cell wall.'),
('ea98ef46-0a45-51bc-987f-190a1eb886ce'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'TCA cycle is also known as:', '["Urea cycle","Calvin cycle","Krebs cycle","Glyoxylate cycle","Cori cycle"]'::jsonb, 'Krebs cycle', 'The TCA cycle is also known as the Krebs cycle (after Hans Krebs who elucidated it) or the citric acid cycle (after the first intermediate formed).'),
('a9797806-2e48-5b71-a6e8-e2e7d30e4c9a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'The final product of the TCA cycle is:', '["Succinyl-CoA","Malate","CO2","Oxaloacetate","Citrate"]'::jsonb, 'Oxaloacetate', 'The final product of the TCA cycle is oxaloacetate, which is regenerated at the end of each turn. It then combines with a new acetyl-CoA molecule to begin another cycle.'),
('fc923050-80d1-5127-b567-88ea7058a161'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Carbohydrates: Loading dose formula:', '["Vd x Css / F","CL x Css x tau / F","Vd x CL / Css","Css x tau / CL","Fx Css / Vd"]'::jsonb, 'Vd x Css / F', 'Loading dose depends on Vd and target concentration.'),
('61830472-6f38-527d-9333-bd06896ffac4'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Carbohydrates: Maintenance dose formula:', '["CL x Css x tau / F","Vd x Css / F","Vd x Css x tau","CL x Vd / Css","Css x tau / Vd"]'::jsonb, 'CL x Css x tau / F', 'Maintenance dose depends on clearance and target concentration.'),
('0f5e2c4b-fff0-5ad6-973c-af06d8a97545'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'The chemiosmotic theory was proposed by:', '["Hans Krebs","Albert Szent-Gyorgyi","Peter Mitchell","Otto Warburg","Melvin Calvin"]'::jsonb, 'Peter Mitchell', 'Peter Mitchell proposed the chemiosmotic theory in 1961, explaining that the proton gradient across the inner mitochondrial membrane drives ATP synthesis via ATP synthase.'),
('0e2237c4-9f39-5712-8849-a43d2d3ce96e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'ATP synthase is also known as:', '["Complex III","Complex I","Complex V","Complex IV","Complex II"]'::jsonb, 'Complex V', 'ATP synthase (Complex V) uses the proton gradient to synthesize ATP from ADP and Pi. It has a rotary mechanism driven by proton flow through the F0 subunit.'),
('c31733f2-2095-5bcb-bacf-f5767988d256'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Oligomycin inhibits ATP synthase by:', '["Uncoupling oxidation","Inhibiting phosphate transfer","Competing with ADP","Blocking substrate binding","Blocking proton flow through Fo"]'::jsonb, 'Blocking proton flow through Fo', 'ATP synthase (Complex V) uses the proton gradient to synthesize ATP from ADP and Pi. It has a rotary mechanism driven by proton flow through the F0 subunit.'),
('3ca5fa9e-a7f1-562a-8718-6c2faf763578'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Rotenone inhibits which complex of the ETC?', '["Complex IV","Complex V","Complex II","Complex I","Complex III"]'::jsonb, 'Complex I', 'Rotenone (from plants) inhibits Complex I (NADH dehydrogenase), blocking electron transfer from NADH to ubiquinone. This reduces proton pumping and ATP synthesis.'),
('a477a60f-62d4-5281-bcb9-ce62d24a76fd'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Cyanide inhibits which cytochrome?', '["Cytochrome b (Complex III)","Cytochrome c1","Cytochrome a + a3 (Complex IV)","NADH dehydrogenase","Cytochrome c"]'::jsonb, 'Cytochrome a + a3 (Complex IV)', 'Cyanide binds to the iron in cytochrome a+a3 (Complex IV), preventing electron transfer to oxygen. This blocks the entire electron transport chain, causing rapid cell death.'),
('b8102844-bfdb-57bf-a9d0-c68928d65531'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'The ATP/ADP translocase exchanges:', '["ADP for Pi resulting in decreased functional activity","ATP for ADP across the inner mitochondrial membrane","ATP for Pi mediated by specific receptor activation","ADP for AMP without affecting the primary tissue pathway","ATP for AMP leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'ATP for ADP across the inner mitochondrial membrane', 'ATP/ADP translocase (adenine nucleotide translocase) exchanges ATP for ADP across the inner mitochondrial membrane, exporting newly synthesized ATP to the cytosol and importing ADP for phosphorylation.'),
('4eba7c01-1d20-5bd8-9aab-571d1f679c38'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Brown adipose tissue generates heat using:', '["Cytochrome oxidase","UCP-1 (thermogenin)","ATP synthase","Succinate dehydrogenase","NADH dehydrogenase"]'::jsonb, 'UCP-1 (thermogenin)', 'Brown adipose tissue generates heat using UCP-1 (thermogenin), which uncouples oxidative phosphorylation by dissipating the proton gradient as heat instead of ATP synthesis.'),
('ded4bbf5-2e0e-5ac0-af46-3a80b576bacb'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'The malate-aspartate shuttle is used to:', '["Transfer fatty acids into mitochondria","Transfer ATP out of mitochondria","Transfer NADH into mitochondria","Transfer calcium into mitochondria","Transfer amino acids into mitochondria"]'::jsonb, 'Transfer NADH into mitochondria', 'The malate-aspartate shuttle transports NADH reducing equivalents from cytosol to mitochondria. It is the predominant shuttle in liver and heart, yielding 2.5 ATP per NADH.'),
('f981007f-aa68-5c9b-b1fa-3e136feb9147'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'The glycerol-3-phosphate shuttle generates:', '["NADPH","GTP","ATP","NADH","FADH2"]'::jsonb, 'FADH2', 'The glycerol-3-phosphate shuttle generates FADH2 in mitochondria (from cytosolic NADH), which enters the ETC at Complex II (via ubiquinone), yielding approximately 1.5 ATP per NADH.'),
('03676ad1-2a97-5b44-8d68-4ce8e784a565'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Which shuttle is used in the liver and kidney?', '["Carnitine shuttle","Malate-aspartate shuttle","Glycerol-3-phosphate shuttle","None of the above","Citrate shuttle"]'::jsonb, 'Malate-aspartate shuttle', 'The malate-aspartate shuttle is the predominant shuttle in liver and kidney. It is more efficient than the glycerol-3-phosphate shuttle, yielding more ATP per NADH.'),
('e267b40d-6edc-54c1-969b-1d9901767df3'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'The rate of the TCA cycle is primarily regulated by:', '["Product inhibition","Allosteric regulation","All of the above","Substrate availability","Enzyme phosphorylation"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'The TCA cycle is regulated by all these mechanisms: substrate availability, allosteric regulation of key enzymes, product inhibition, and covalent modification (phosphorylation).'),
('96999bc5-9a74-5c9e-8e7a-65e3129943e2'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'ATP inhibits which TCA cycle enzyme?', '["Succinate dehydrogenase","Isocitrate dehydrogenase","Citrate synthase","Fumarase","Malate dehydrogenase"]'::jsonb, 'Isocitrate dehydrogenase', 'ATP (along with NADH) inhibits isocitrate dehydrogenase, a key regulatory enzyme of the TCA cycle. This slows the cycle when energy charge is high.'),
('b78b7781-6597-55a2-98b8-bea859d23cf3'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Succinyl-CoA inhibits:', '["Isocitrate dehydrogenase","Succinate dehydrogenase","Citrate synthase","Succinyl-CoA synthetase","Alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase"]'::jsonb, 'Alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase', 'Succinyl-CoA inhibits alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase, the enzyme that converts alpha-ketoglutarate to succinyl-CoA. This is product inhibition within the TCA cycle.'),
('ca528ba1-c6cf-54e0-ac56-41b07fb4ff8c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'NADH inhibits:', '["None of the above","Pyruvate dehydrogenase complex","Isocitrate dehydrogenase","Alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'NADH inhibits all three rate-limiting enzymes of the TCA cycle: pyruvate dehydrogenase complex, isocitrate dehydrogenase, and alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase, slowing the cycle when energy is abundant.'),
('a2df3659-c116-5cf8-92a7-b5e64e6cc443'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'A 55-year-old male has a fasting glucose of 220 mg/dL. Which structure is primarily affected in this condition?', '["Liver and muscle","All tissues","RBCs and brain","Intestine and kidney","Adipose and heart"]'::jsonb, 'RBCs and brain', 'RBCs and brain are primarily affected by glucose transport defects. RBCs depend entirely on glucose (no mitochondria), and the brain requires constant glucose supply.'),
('520af5a6-8263-552e-b65c-79f022b3a1cd'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'GLUT3 is a high-affinity transporter found primarily in:', '["Adipose","Liver","RBCs","Neurons","Muscle"]'::jsonb, 'Neurons', 'GLUT3 is a high-affinity glucose transporter found primarily in neurons, ensuring adequate glucose uptake even at low blood glucose levels to maintain neuronal function.'),
('f42236bb-7b43-565a-9db6-21b09864b495'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Lab results for a 55-year-old female show abnormal levels of a key metabolite. Which anatomical structure is most likely involved?', '["Liver and pancreas","Lungs and skin","Muscle and adipose","Brain and heart","Intestine and kidney proximal tubule"]'::jsonb, 'Intestine and kidney proximal tubule', 'SGLT1 (intestine) absorbs dietary glucose and galactose; SGLT2 (kidney proximal tubule) reabsorbs approximately 90% of filtered glucose. SGLT2 inhibitors treat type 2 diabetes.'),
('69fa79d4-dd4c-5d5b-ad21-b21b4317eb98'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Which enzyme converts pyruvate to lactate during anaerobic glycolysis?', '["Alcohol dehydrogenase","Lactate dehydrogenase","Pyruvate carboxylase","Pyruvate kinase","Pyruvate dehydrogenase"]'::jsonb, 'Lactate dehydrogenase', 'Anaerobic glycolysis converts glucose to lactate, generating 2 ATP per glucose. It occurs in RBCs (no mitochondria) and muscle during intense exercise when oxygen is limited.'),
('f11548e2-556d-5ea0-96b1-245ca059c5c0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Gluconeogenesis is stimulated by glucagon via:', '["PKC activation","PIP2 hydrolysis","Increased cAMP","Increased intracellular calcium","Increased cGMP"]'::jsonb, 'Increased cAMP', 'Glucagon is secreted by pancreatic α-cells when blood glucose is low. It activates adenylate cyclase via Gs protein, increasing cAMP and activating PKA, which promotes glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis.'),
('d08c3217-ba46-53f7-975e-38415f84673d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'PKA phosphorylation of PFK-2/FBPase-2 in liver results in:', '["Increased FBPase-2 activity (favoring gluconeogenesis)","Increased lipolysis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Decrease mediated by specific receptor activation","Increased glycogenolysis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Increased PFK-2 activity (favoring glycolysis) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Increased FBPase-2 activity (favoring gluconeogenesis)', 'ATP is the primary allosteric inhibitor of phosphofructokinase-1 (PFK-1), the rate-limiting enzyme of glycolysis. High ATP levels signal sufficient energy, slowing glycolysis. Citrate and low pH also inhibit PFK-1.'),
('69ecba48-32ec-5268-9f84-358a2433385f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Which of the following activates pyruvate kinase?', '["Glucagon","6-bisphosphate","Alanine","ATP","Fructose-1"]'::jsonb, 'Fructose-1', 'Pyruvate kinase catalyzes the final step of glycolysis, converting phosphoenolpyruvate (PEP) to pyruvate with ATP generation. Deficiency causes hemolytic anemia due to impaired ATP production in RBCs.'),
('00b5698f-a4f2-596d-ac19-a629509f2f6b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Which enzyme catalyzes the rate-limiting step of the pentose phosphate pathway?', '["Citrate","ATP","Fructose-6-phosphate","Glucose-6-phosphate","ADP"]'::jsonb, 'Glucose-6-phosphate', 'The HMP shunt (pentose phosphate pathway) produces NADPH for reductive biosynthesis and ribose-5-phosphate for nucleotide synthesis. NADPH is essential for fatty acid synthesis and glutathione reduction.'),
('72a87cd3-aec7-581d-a433-396c1825b29e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Lab results for a 65-year-old female show abnormal levels of a key metabolite. What is the most likely site of pathology?', '["RBCs and kidney","Muscle and brain","All tissues","Heart and lung","Liver and pancreatic beta-cells"]'::jsonb, 'Liver and pancreatic beta-cells', 'Liver and pancreatic beta-cells are the primary sites involved in glucose homeostasis. The liver performs gluconeogenesis and glycogenolysis, while beta-cells secrete insulin in response to glucose.'),
('ebef9c16-f37e-560d-94b0-0a377fed5ed3'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'The glucose sensor in pancreatic beta-cells is:', '["GLUT2 and glucokinase","GLUT3 and glucokinase","GLUT4 and hexokinase","GLUT1 and hexokinase","GLUT5 and hexokinase"]'::jsonb, 'GLUT2 and glucokinase', 'Pancreatic beta-cells use GLUT2 and glucokinase as their glucose sensor. GLUT2 allows glucose to enter freely, and glucokinase (high Km) phosphorylates glucose proportionally to its concentration.'),
('418c0cbd-4443-5b96-a98b-6fe343087cb1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'ATP-sensitive potassium channels (K-ATP) in beta-cells close in response to:', '["Increased ATP/ADP ratio","Increased insulin","Decreased insulin","Decreased ATP/ADP ratio","Increased glucagon"]'::jsonb, 'Increased ATP/ADP ratio', 'K-ATP channels close when the ATP/ADP ratio increases (high glucose metabolism). This depolarizes the beta-cell membrane, opening voltage-gated Ca2+ channels and triggering insulin release.'),
('19b2c993-7230-592b-bc42-35e1b38db94e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Sulfonylureas close K-ATP channels by binding to:', '["Kir6.2 subunit","PPAR-gamma","SUR1 subunit","GLUT2","Glucokinase"]'::jsonb, 'SUR1 subunit', 'Sulfonylureas (glipizide, glyburide) bind to the SUR1 (sulfonylurea receptor 1) subunit of K-ATP channels, closing them and stimulating insulin secretion independent of glucose levels.'),
('719b878d-02ef-51d7-956f-df6a70320793'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Regarding clinical cases in Carbohydrates: Phase I metabolism reaction:', '["Oxidation","Glucuronidation","Sulfation","Acetylation","Glutathione conjugation"]'::jsonb, 'Oxidation', 'Phase I: oxidation, reduction, hydrolysis.'),
('6b611793-713f-5009-96dd-a05e5ff3f74e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'In the study of Carbohydrates, Phase II conjugation reaction:', '["Glucuronidation","Oxidation","Reduction","Hydrolysis","Deamination"]'::jsonb, 'Glucuronidation', 'Phase II: glucuronidation, sulfation, acetylation, methylation, glutathione conjugation.'),
('428d069a-af54-596b-9f19-5936e4f015ea'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'C-peptide measurement is used to:', '["Assess endogenous insulin secretion","Measure glucagon levels","Measure exogenous insulin","Measure glucose levels","Diagnose diabetes"]'::jsonb, 'Assess endogenous insulin secretion', 'C-peptide is co-secreted with insulin in equimolar amounts. Measuring C-peptide assesses endogenous insulin secretion, distinguishing type 1 (low C-peptide) from type 2 (normal/high) diabetes.'),
('8f5c52f6-1e76-51c9-9620-fee08441b3b1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Carbohydrates: Acetylation polymorphism affects:', '["Isoniazid, hydralazine, procainamide","Warfarin, phenytoin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Codeine, tramadol mediated by specific receptor activation","Omeprazole without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Diazepam leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Isoniazid, hydralazine, procainamide', 'NAT2 polymorphism: slow vs fast acetylators.'),
('30a3754a-3cde-5df6-8ee0-c919cb1c0dbe'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'During therapeutic management of Carbohydrates, Major binding protein for acidic drugs:', '["Albumin","Alpha-1 acid glycoprotein","Globulin","Transferrin","Ceruloplasmin"]'::jsonb, 'Albumin', 'Albumin binds acidic (anionic) drugs.'),
('a7026633-d3c8-5f68-a40d-411a88f9637c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'PI3K is activated by IRS and leads to:', '["Activation of MAPK","Activation of JNK","Activation of PKA","Activation of Akt/PKB","Activation of PKC"]'::jsonb, 'Activation of Akt/PKB', 'PI3K is activated by IRS (insulin receptor substrate) and generates PIP3, which recruits and activates Akt/PKB. Akt mediates most metabolic effects of insulin (glucose uptake, glycogen synthesis).'),
('5ddf1566-bcb2-5a5d-86df-69a2888191ed'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'In the study of Carbohydrates, Displacing warfarin from albumin causes:', '["Increased free warfarin/bleeding risk","Decreased effect","No INR change","Increased binding","Decreased absorption"]'::jsonb, 'Increased free warfarin/bleeding risk', 'Displacement increases unbound warfarin, transiently increasing effect.'),
('4873cafa-9bd0-5302-9bd0-bb043fbfd5dd'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Insulin promotes fatty acid synthesis by:', '["Activating acetyl-CoA carboxylase","Inhibiting pyruvate dehydrogenase","Activating hormone-sensitive lipase","Promoting ketogenesis","Inhibiting lipoprotein lipase"]'::jsonb, 'Activating acetyl-CoA carboxylase', 'Insulin activates acetyl-CoA carboxylase (ACC) via dephosphorylation, converting acetyl-CoA to malonyl-CoA, the committed step in fatty acid synthesis. This promotes lipogenesis.'),
('7ad07239-2e23-5fc8-8892-2d9e0f8b48af'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Glucagon is secreted by:', '["Pancreatic beta-cells","Parietal cells","Pancreatic alpha-cells","PP cells","Pancreatic delta-cells"]'::jsonb, 'Pancreatic alpha-cells', 'Glucagon is secreted by pancreatic α-cells when blood glucose is low. It activates adenylate cyclase via Gs protein, increasing cAMP and activating PKA, which promotes glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis.'),
('8086b9fb-81e5-5fbb-b825-3d67e9c72953'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'The main action of glucagon is to:', '["Promote glycogenesis","Decrease blood glucose","Increase blood glucose","Increase insulin secretion","Decrease hepatic glucose output"]'::jsonb, 'Increase blood glucose', 'Glucagon is secreted by pancreatic α-cells when blood glucose is low. It activates adenylate cyclase via Gs protein, increasing cAMP and activating PKA, which promotes glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis.'),
('a605dcd2-f14a-5587-8f36-1625c851db0d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Glucagon receptor is a:', '["Growth factor receptor","G-protein coupled receptor","Tyrosine kinase receptor","Ion channel","Nuclear receptor"]'::jsonb, 'G-protein coupled receptor', 'Glucagon is secreted by pancreatic α-cells when blood glucose is low. It activates adenylate cyclase via Gs protein, increasing cAMP and activating PKA, which promotes glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis.'),
('a917b9be-0732-57fe-8a5a-8946a15338a5'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'DPP-4 inhibitors increase:', '["Insulin degradation","GLP-1 levels","Glucose absorption","Ketone bodies","Glucagon levels"]'::jsonb, 'GLP-1 levels', 'DPP-4 inhibitors (sitagliptin, saxagliptin) increase GLP-1 levels by inhibiting its degradation. GLP-1 stimulates insulin secretion and suppresses glucagon release in a glucose-dependent manner.'),
('838ba003-da52-53df-9e6a-002bb5410796'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'The Bohr effect describes how increased CO2 and H+:', '["Increase 2","Increase hemoglobin''s affinity for oxygen","Have effect","3-BPG synthesis","Decrease hemoglobin''s affinity for oxygen"]'::jsonb, 'Decrease hemoglobin''s affinity for oxygen', 'The Bohr effect describes how decreased pH (increased H+) and increased CO2 reduce hemoglobin affinity for oxygen, promoting O2 unloading in metabolically active tissues.'),
('82ef4bfa-d3a3-51d4-ba59-0a779ab4c2e3'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Carbohydrates: Large Vd means drug is:', '["Distributed into tissues","Confined to plasma","Rapidly excreted","Bound to albumin","Hepatically metabolised"]'::jsonb, 'Distributed into tissues', 'Large Vd >1 L/kg indicates extensive tissue distribution.'),
('4cabac84-d1cd-58be-82de-7c1d230883b0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Chloride shift refers to the exchange of:', '["Cl- and Na+ resulting in decreased functional activity","Na+ and K+ accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","HCO3- and K+ mediated by specific receptor activation","Ca2+ and Cl- without affecting the primary tissue pathway","HCO3- and Cl- across RBC membrane"]'::jsonb, 'HCO3- and Cl- across RBC membrane', 'The chloride shift (Hamburger phenomenon) exchanges HCO3- for Cl- across the RBC membrane via the band 3 protein (AE1), maintaining electrical neutrality during CO2 transport.'),
('b100b798-2e0a-52cf-98a1-0fdc95be4093'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Which enzyme catalyzes the final step of glycolysis?', '["PFK-1","Hexokinase","Pyruvate kinase","LDH","G6PD"]'::jsonb, 'Pyruvate kinase', 'Pyruvate kinase catalyzes the final step of glycolysis, converting phosphoenolpyruvate (PEP) to pyruvate with substrate-level phosphorylation, generating ATP.'),
('6131809c-3350-525a-a30c-f9f4c9c95ccc'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'G6PD deficiency is an X-linked recessive disorder that causes:', '["Glycogen storage disease","Porphyria","Hemolytic anemia with oxidative stress","Galactosemia","Lysosomal storage disease"]'::jsonb, 'Hemolytic anemia with oxidative stress', 'G6PD deficiency causes hemolytic anemia with oxidative stress. Reduced NADPH production impairs glutathione regeneration, making RBCs vulnerable to oxidative damage from drugs, infections, or foods.'),
('fe0a2bdb-fc6b-5e3e-ae33-5fb449a095a1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Which drug can trigger hemolysis in G6PD deficiency?', '["Ibuprofen","Primaquine","Penicillin","Paracetamol","Aspirin"]'::jsonb, 'Primaquine', 'Primaquine is a classic drug trigger for hemolysis in G6PD deficiency. It generates reactive oxygen species that damage RBCs lacking sufficient NADPH for glutathione reduction.'),
('40f71612-10f1-5d34-a5b3-9c12329ca468'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Which of the following is true about glycolysis in RBCs?', '["It produces both ATP and 2","It produces 2","It produces only ATP","3-BPG but ATP","3-BPG"]'::jsonb, 'It produces 2', 'RBC glycolysis produces 2,3-BPG (via the Rapoport-Luebering shunt) in addition to ATP. 2,3-BPG is crucial for regulating hemoglobin''s oxygen affinity.'),
('a394bed3-df62-5e0a-8952-872ff2d4983e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'The NADH produced in RBC glycolysis is used by:', '["Lactate dehydrogenase","Glutathione reductase","Alcohol dehydrogenase","Pyruvate dehydrogenase","Methemoglobin reductase"]'::jsonb, 'Methemoglobin reductase', 'NADH from RBC glycolysis is used by methemoglobin reductase (cytochrome b5 reductase) to reduce methemoglobin (Fe3+) back to functional hemoglobin (Fe2+).'),
('3a71253c-af32-5c3d-a9d0-b43706515b78'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Regarding clinical cases in Carbohydrates: Protein binding fraction (fu) ranges:', '["0 to 1","-1 to 0","0 to 100","-100 to 100","1 to 100"]'::jsonb, '0 to 1', 'Only unbound (free) drug is pharmacologically active.'),
('041dbc21-260f-586a-9656-0a2e4efff6dd'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Methemoglobinemia can be treated with:', '["NADH","Ascorbic acid","None of the above","Methylene blue","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Methemoglobin contains Fe3+ that cannot bind O2. It is normally kept reduced by methemoglobin reductase. Dapsone, nitrites, and benzocaine can cause methemoglobinemia.'),
('c208b9d1-98eb-583f-aac5-3cc15d9b0f36'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'NADPH in RBCs is mainly used for:', '["Steroid synthesis","Nucleotide synthesis","Fatty acid synthesis","Maintaining glutathione in reduced state","Protein synthesis"]'::jsonb, 'Maintaining glutathione in reduced state', 'NADPH in RBCs is mainly used for maintaining glutathione in its reduced state (GSH) via glutathione reductase, protecting against oxidative damage.'),
('5bca41ce-df57-5c14-b4f5-2c4f12a577a0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'What is the energy product or cofactor involved in this reaction?', '["NADH","NADPH","Cytochrome c","FADH2","GSH"]'::jsonb, 'GSH', 'GSH (reduced glutathione) is the key cofactor in this reaction, protecting RBCs from oxidative damage by detoxifying hydrogen peroxide and maintaining hemoglobin in its functional state.'),
('b9aa47c7-fd89-580f-ba18-0ba5900fd2bd'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Glutathione reductase recycles GSSG to GSH using:', '["GTP","ATP","NADH","NADPH","FADH2"]'::jsonb, 'NADPH', 'Glutathione reductase uses NADPH to reduce GSSG (oxidized glutathione) to GSH (reduced glutathione), maintaining the antioxidant defense system in cells.'),
('d4f941dc-d30a-5595-b023-bde9cbadd1e1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'The hexose monophosphate shunt in RBCs provides:', '["ATP for energy","2","Ribose for nucleotides","3-BPG for hemoglobin","NADPH for glutathione reduction"]'::jsonb, 'NADPH for glutathione reduction', 'The HMP shunt in RBCs provides NADPH for glutathione reduction, which is essential for protecting RBCs from oxidative damage since they lack mitochondria and other organelles.'),
('18c19a4d-9814-55ed-bf09-eec2c5c79b65'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Which enzyme deficiency is the most common human enzyme deficiency worldwide?', '["Pyruvate kinase resulting in decreased functional activity","PFK-1 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","LDH mediated by specific receptor activation","Hexokinase without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase"]'::jsonb, 'Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase', 'Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD) deficiency is the most common human enzyme deficiency worldwide, affecting approximately 400 million people, predominantly in malaria-endemic regions.'),
('f76964ed-6ac8-514b-a3ac-dbe7a22d8644'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Carbohydrates: Volume of distribution (Vd) equals:', '["Amount of drug / Plasma concentration","Clearance / Rate","Half-life x Clearance","Dose / F","Absorption rate"]'::jsonb, 'Amount of drug / Plasma concentration', 'Vd = Amount / Concentration. Large Vd = extensive tissue distribution.'),
('06163b9c-1950-5b0d-a480-5a845064ff31'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Andersen disease (Type IV GSD) is due to deficiency of:', '["Acid maltase","Debranching enzyme","Branching enzyme","Phosphorylase","Phosphofructokinase"]'::jsonb, 'Branching enzyme', 'Andersen disease (GSD type IV) is caused by branching enzyme deficiency, leading to accumulation of abnormal glycogen with long outer chains (polyglucos bodies), causing liver cirrhosis.'),
('eaea695b-2bae-5265-9f5f-c3718275de12'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'A 45-year-old male has a fasting glucose of 60 mg/dL. Which risk factor is most strongly associated?', '["Type III (Cori)","Type VII (Tarui)","Type II (Pompe)","Type I (von Gierke)","Type V (McArdle)"]'::jsonb, 'Type V (McArdle)', 'Type V (McArdle) disease causes exercise intolerance and muscle cramps due to muscle glycogen phosphorylase deficiency, not typically fasting hypoglycemia.'),
('1584d247-085f-565a-849f-9aacf4e54709'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Tarui disease (Type VII GSD) is due to deficiency of:', '["LDH","Pyruvate kinase","Hexokinase","Phosphofructokinase","Phosphorylase b kinase"]'::jsonb, 'Phosphofructokinase', 'Tarui disease (GSD type VII) is caused by muscle phosphofructokinase (PFK) deficiency, preventing glycolysis in muscle and causing exercise intolerance and hemolytic anemia.'),
('1a02516f-496d-5a4c-9bd7-bb9dfe66631f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Hers disease (Type VI GSD) is due to deficiency of:', '["Debranching enzyme","Muscle phosphorylase","Liver phosphorylase","Branching enzyme","G6Pase"]'::jsonb, 'Liver phosphorylase', 'Hers disease (GSD type VI) is caused by liver glycogen phosphorylase deficiency, leading to mild hepatomegaly and fasting hypoglycemia, usually milder than Von Gierke disease.'),
('fb7fbde0-148d-5609-96af-4cb4075174b5'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'GSD Type IX is due to deficiency of:', '["Phosphorylase","Debranching enzyme","Phosphorylase b kinase","G6Pase","Branching enzyme"]'::jsonb, 'Phosphorylase b kinase', 'GSD Type IX is caused by phosphorylase b kinase deficiency. This kinase activates glycogen phosphorylase, so its deficiency impairs glycogenolysis, causing hepatomegaly and hypoglycemia.'),
('3586d302-2030-5a44-9cbb-54c728a1764b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Refsum disease involves accumulation of:', '["Phytanic acid","Galactose","Cholesterol","Glycogen","Phenylalanine"]'::jsonb, 'Phytanic acid', 'Refsum disease involves accumulation of phytanic acid (from dietary phytol in chlorophyll) due to deficiency of phytanoyl-CoA hydroxylase, causing peripheral neuropathy and cerebellar ataxia.'),
('7894c08c-4098-558c-9ea7-955b5bbdc6f8'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Gaucher disease results from deficiency of:', '["Hexosaminidase A","Arylsulfatase A","Beta-glucosidase (glucocerebrosidase)","Sphingomyelinase","Alpha-galactosidase"]'::jsonb, 'Beta-glucosidase (glucocerebrosidase)', 'Gaucher disease is caused by beta-glucosidase (glucocerebrosidase) deficiency, leading to accumulation of glucocerebroside (glucosylceramide) in macrophages, causing hepatosplenomegaly and bone disease.'),
('c91e9251-fc8d-56db-94bd-1c626231fa36'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Lab results for a 35-year-old female show abnormal levels of a key metabolite. Which risk factor is most strongly associated?', '["Hexosaminidase A","Alpha-iduronidase","Galactocerebrosidase","Arylsulfatase A","Beta-glucuronidase"]'::jsonb, 'Arylsulfatase A', 'Arylsulfatase A deficiency causes metachromatic leukodystrophy, a lysosomal storage disease affecting the white matter of the brain, causing progressive neurological deterioration.'),
('8bc395f7-d989-5051-84df-5aeabc040983'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Hunter syndrome (MPS II) is X-linked and results from deficiency of:', '["Alpha-L-iduronidase","Acetyl-CoA transferase","Iduronate sulfatase","Heparan N-sulfatase","Beta-glucuronidase"]'::jsonb, 'Iduronate sulfatase', 'Hunter syndrome (MPS II) is caused by iduronate sulfatase deficiency, an X-linked disorder causing accumulation of heparan sulfate and dermatan sulfate, with mild to severe manifestations.'),
('6a2116c0-5032-5173-a4c9-0f5c7a5598a7'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Sanfilippo syndrome (MPS III) primarily affects:', '["Chondroitin sulfate degradation","Keratan sulfate degradation","Dermatan sulfate degradation","Heparan sulfate degradation","Hyaluronic acid degradation"]'::jsonb, 'Heparan sulfate degradation', 'Sanfilippo syndrome (MPS III) primarily affects heparan sulfate degradation, causing severe neurological deterioration with relatively mild somatic features.'),
('52bfda89-3abd-5782-b2d1-b92b2617d176'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Alpha-lactalbumin is a component of the enzyme:', '["Lactose synthase","Sucrase","Amylase","Trehalase","Maltase"]'::jsonb, 'Lactose synthase', 'Alpha-lactalbumin is a regulatory subunit of lactose synthase in the mammary gland. It modifies the substrate specificity of galactosyltransferase to accept glucose, enabling lactose synthesis.'),
('bc0de510-f664-5aa2-97d7-282f311fdc52'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Which hormone is secreted by beta cells of the pancreas?', '["Herbal remedies","Insulin therapy","Diet only","Oral hypoglycemics","Exercise only"]'::jsonb, 'Insulin therapy', 'Insulin is secreted by pancreatic beta-cells in response to elevated blood glucose. It lowers blood glucose by promoting glucose uptake, glycogenesis, and lipogenesis while inhibiting glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis.'),
('1fe06ece-2847-5463-aaf5-30fcd859db0e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA) is characterized by:', '["Hyperglycemia","Hyperglycemia without ketosis","Ketosis","Hypoglycemia and ketosis","And metabolic acidosis"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperglycemia', 'Diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA) results from absolute insulin deficiency with unopposed glucagon action. Increased lipolysis and fatty acid oxidation produce ketone bodies, causing metabolic acidosis.'),
('301d89e3-ffdd-53bd-9950-3230ac1d03f8'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'DKA is more common in:', '["Type 1 diabetes","MODY","Type 2 diabetes","Gestational diabetes","Pre-diabetes"]'::jsonb, 'Type 1 diabetes', 'Type 1 diabetes is more common for DKA because absolute insulin deficiency allows unopposed glucagon action, promoting lipolysis and ketogenesis, leading to metabolic acidosis.'),
('bc835dec-3bf6-5666-a8f7-b03deb41f9ed'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Which electrolyte abnormality is common in DKA?', '["Hyperkalemia (initially)","Hypokalemia","Hypophosphatemia","Hypocalcemia","Hypermagnesemia"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperkalemia (initially)', 'Hyperkalemia is initially common in DKA due to acidosis (H+ shifts into cells, K+ shifts out), but total body potassium is depleted. Insulin therapy drives K+ into cells, risking hypokalemia.'),
('4526fab9-b309-5981-99bf-e8a56d118f59'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Treatment of DKA includes:', '["Only insulin","Fluids","And potassium replacement","Glucose and glucagon","Insulin"]'::jsonb, 'Fluids', 'Treatment of DKA includes fluids (to correct dehydration), insulin (to suppress ketogenesis), and potassium replacement (to prevent hypokalemia as insulin drives K+ into cells).'),
('920abf74-c898-5e99-9370-c5dc7590ec81'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'The primary goal of insulin therapy in DKA is to:', '["All of the above","Suppress ketogenesis","Reduce blood glucose","Correct acidosis","Replace fluid deficit"]'::jsonb, 'Suppress ketogenesis', 'The primary goal of insulin therapy in DKA is to suppress ketogenesis by inhibiting lipolysis and fatty acid oxidation, while also reducing blood glucose and correcting acidosis.'),
('7de7112f-df34-54d4-bcee-64bde1b82cfa'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Which of the following is a long-acting insulin analog?', '["Aspart","Glulisine","Regular insulin","Lispro","Glargine"]'::jsonb, 'Glargine', 'Glargine is a long-acting insulin analog with a modified isoelectric point that precipitates at physiological pH, providing a slow, relatively constant release over 24 hours.'),
('3c5d2684-8ff0-5869-98a6-38a3588cffc1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'A 65-year-old female with diabetes presents with recurrent hypoglycemic episodes. Which cellular process is most affected?', '["Glargine","NPH","Detemir","Lispro","Regular"]'::jsonb, 'Lispro', 'Lispro is a rapid-acting insulin analog with onset in 5-15 minutes and peak at 1-2 hours. It is used for prandial (mealtime) glucose coverage.'),
('38d5dd30-27f8-5e4b-be64-c55c73728ca0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'A newborn presents with vomiting and lethargy after feeding. Newborn screening is positive. The primary amino acids in glycogenin are important for:', '["None of the above","Transport of glucose","Phosphorylation of glucose","Initiating glycogen synthesis","Terminating glycogen synthesis"]'::jsonb, 'Initiating glycogen synthesis', 'The amino acids in glycogenin (tyrosine residues) serve as the primer for glycogen synthesis. Glycogenin autoglycosylates these residues to initiate the glycogen chain.'),
('cf218a02-7c90-5b57-82bf-a7f35a89aa39'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'A 50-year-old male presents with muscle cramps and exercise intolerance. What is the most likely site of pathology?', '["8-12 resulting in decreased functional activity","6 linkages every how many residues?","20-25 mediated by specific receptor activation","50-100 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","2-4 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '6 linkages every how many residues?', 'Exercise intolerance and muscle cramps suggest McArdle disease (GSD type V), caused by muscle glycogen phosphorylase deficiency, preventing glycogen breakdown in muscle for energy.'),
('af32c53e-a1b2-51a0-9496-375012bc18e6'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'The glucose tolerance test measures:', '["C-peptide levels resulting in decreased functional activity","Lactate levels accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Glucagon levels mediated by specific receptor activation","The body''s ability to metabolize glucose","Insulin levels only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The body''s ability to metabolize glucose', 'The oral glucose tolerance test (OGTT) measures the body''s ability to metabolize glucose by monitoring blood glucose levels after a standardized glucose load over 2-3 hours.'),
('edcaeb53-a896-52cd-9422-7150be527249'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Impaired glucose tolerance (IGT) is defined as:', '["Fasting glucose 100-125","2h OGTT >200 mg/dL","FBG >126 mg/dL","RBG >200 mg/dL","2h OGTT 140-199 mg/dL"]'::jsonb, '2h OGTT 140-199 mg/dL', 'Impaired glucose tolerance (IGT) is defined as 2-hour OGTT glucose of 140-199 mg/dL, representing an intermediate state between normal glucose tolerance and diabetes.'),
('932d7360-6a1f-53d1-bd15-dfd61f5f36a0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'The major fate of glucose-6-phosphate in the liver after a meal is:', '["Gluconeogenesis","Glycolysis","Ketogenesis","HMP shunt","Glycogen synthesis"]'::jsonb, 'Glycogen synthesis', 'After a meal, the major fate of glucose-6-phosphate in the liver is glycogen synthesis (glycogenesis) to store excess glucose for later use during fasting.'),
('0666b765-9f4c-5492-b394-19c837cb980c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Which enzyme catalyzes the conversion of glucose to glucose-6-phosphate in the liver?', '["Glucokinase (hexokinase IV)","Hexokinase III","Hexokinase II","Phosphoglucose isomerase","Hexokinase I"]'::jsonb, 'Glucokinase (hexokinase IV)', 'Glucokinase (hexokinase IV) is the primary enzyme for glucose phosphorylation in the liver. It has a high Km (low affinity) and is not inhibited by G6P, allowing the liver to buffer blood glucose.'),
('e44375cc-e342-5dcc-a770-707ad7a5e8ba'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Carbohydrates', 'Galactosemia is caused by deficiency of:', '["Galactokinase resulting in decreased functional activity","Aldose reductase accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lactase mediated by specific receptor activation","UDP-galactose epimerase without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Galactose-1-phosphate uridyltransferase (GALT)"]'::jsonb, 'Galactose-1-phosphate uridyltransferase (GALT)', 'Classic galactosemia is caused by galactose-1-phosphate uridylyltransferase deficiency. Accumulation of galactose-1-P and galactitol causes cataracts, hepatomegaly, and intellectual disability.'),
('794f60da-f3e5-5e27-a79a-d62a78a96984'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Fatty acids are classified based on:', '["Unsaturation","Only chain length","Chain length","And double bond position","Only saturation"]'::jsonb, 'Chain length', 'Fatty acids are classified by chain length (short <6C, medium 6-12C, long >12C), degree of saturation (saturated vs unsaturated), and double bond position (omega numbering).'),
('63f96f87-b506-5f4a-99bc-30a9f475e651'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Which fatty acid is monounsaturated?', '["Oleic acid","Arachidonic acid","Stearic acid","Linoleic acid","Palmitic acid"]'::jsonb, 'Oleic acid', 'Oleic acid (18:1, omega-9) is a monounsaturated fatty acid with one double bond. It is the most common fatty acid in the human diet, found in olive oil.'),
('d54ec27a-5eb2-5e66-b403-d584d855761f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Which fatty acid is polyunsaturated?', '["Myristic acid","Linoleic acid","Palmitic acid","Stearic acid","Oleic acid"]'::jsonb, 'Linoleic acid', 'Linoleic acid (18:2, omega-6) is a polyunsaturated fatty acid with two double bonds. It is an essential fatty acid because humans lack the desaturase to introduce a double bond at position omega-6.'),
('b1ab9c78-b132-5e93-9df3-7e8e132b1705'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Linoleic acid is an essential fatty acid because:', '["It is required for ketogenesis","It is short-chain","It is abundant in animal fats","It cannot be synthesized by the human body","It is a major energy source"]'::jsonb, 'It cannot be synthesized by the human body', 'Linoleic acid (omega-6) and alpha-linolenic acid (omega-3) are essential because humans lack the desaturase enzymes to introduce double bonds beyond carbon 9. Linoleic acid is the precursor for arachidonic acid.'),
('c8322600-9cc5-5827-b89c-072c60304b4b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Alpha-linolenic acid is an omega-3 fatty acid with how many double bonds?', '["4","2","5","3","1"]'::jsonb, '3', 'Alpha-linolenic acid (ALA, 18:3) is an omega-3 fatty acid with 3 double bonds, found in flaxseed, walnuts, and canola oil. It is the precursor for EPA and DHA.'),
('3b7fe1ad-f8d9-597d-a1d2-d9d376290301'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Arachidonic acid is derived from:', '["Oleic acid","Linoleic acid","Stearic acid","Alpha-linolenic acid","Palmitic acid"]'::jsonb, 'Linoleic acid', 'Arachidonic acid (20:4, omega-6) is synthesized from linoleic acid by desaturation and elongation. It is the major precursor for eicosanoids.'),
('acd2dbc8-3097-5472-8d96-92c1225375ad'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'The precursor for prostaglandin synthesis is:', '["Alpha-linolenic acid","Arachidonic acid","Linoleic acid","DHA","EPA"]'::jsonb, 'Arachidonic acid', 'Arachidonic acid, released from membrane phospholipids by phospholipase A2, is the precursor for prostaglandins, thromboxanes, and leukotrienes.'),
('ac5c8cb7-a9c4-5d38-a5af-b6d2dda03056'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Cyclooxygenase (COX) inhibitors like aspirin block:', '["Prostaglandin synthesis","Fatty acid oxidation","Lipolysis","Leukotriene synthesis","Cholesterol synthesis"]'::jsonb, 'Prostaglandin synthesis', 'COX converts arachidonic acid to prostaglandin H2. Aspirin irreversibly acetylates COX-1 and COX-2, blocking prostaglandin and thromboxane synthesis, reducing inflammation, pain, and fever.'),
('547c81ec-6d4c-52c7-8cdb-24dc74a822c7'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Leukotrienes are synthesized via the:', '["Cytochrome P450 pathway","Cyclooxygenase pathway","Beta-oxidation","Lipoxygenase pathway","Reduction pathway"]'::jsonb, 'Lipoxygenase pathway', 'Leukotrienes are synthesized from arachidonic acid via the lipoxygenase pathway (5-LOX). They mediate bronchoconstriction, vasoconstriction, and inflammation (e.g., in asthma).'),
('22b654ba-f7a7-5e72-b1f6-76b013508124'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Triglycerides consist of:', '["Glycerol with two fatty acids","Glycerol with one fatty acid","Three glycerol molecules","Glycerol esterified with three fatty acids","Glycerol with phosphate"]'::jsonb, 'Glycerol esterified with three fatty acids', 'Triglycerides consist of one glycerol molecule esterified with three fatty acids. They are the major energy storage form, providing 9 kcal/g.'),
('87a46540-8309-551b-82ef-caf3986ea427'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'The primary function of triglycerides is:', '["Structural component of membranes","Signaling molecule","Energy storage","Gene regulation","Enzyme cofactor"]'::jsonb, 'Energy storage', 'Triglycerides are the major storage form of metabolic energy in adipose tissue, providing 9 kcal/g. They are highly reduced and anhydrous, making them efficient energy stores.'),
('d68c657f-185c-59f0-a051-96d7e87d7cc5'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Adipose tissue lipase (hormone-sensitive lipase) is activated by:', '["Amino acids","Thyroid hormone","Glucose","Glucagon and epinephrine","Insulin"]'::jsonb, 'Glucagon and epinephrine', 'Hormone-sensitive lipase is activated by glucagon and epinephrine via cAMP-PKA phosphorylation, stimulating lipolysis. Insulin inhibits it, favoring fat storage.'),
('20a4c331-d9e4-590f-92a2-9aff1f9739a1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Lipoprotein lipase (LPL) is located on:', '["Intestinal cells","Muscle cells only","Liver cells","Adipocytes only","Capillary endothelial surfaces"]'::jsonb, 'Capillary endothelial surfaces', 'Lipoprotein lipase is anchored to capillary endothelial surfaces, where it hydrolyzes triglycerides in chylomicrons and VLDL into free fatty acids for tissue uptake.'),
('1daebceb-34cb-5693-9bc6-fcee07e0984d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Which phospholipid is the most abundant in cell membranes and a major component of lung surfactant?', '["Phosphatidylserine","Phosphatidylcholine (lecithin)","Phosphatidylinositol","Sphingomyelin","Phosphatidylethanolamine"]'::jsonb, 'Phosphatidylcholine (lecithin)', 'Phosphatidylcholine (lecithin) is the most abundant membrane phospholipid. Dipalmitoylphosphatidylcholine is the major component of lung surfactant, reducing surface tension.'),
('92f66ab1-f593-5da5-b377-3cbecf180dd1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Sphingomyelin is composed of:', '["Two fatty acids","Phosphocholine","Fatty acid","Sphingosine","Glycerol"]'::jsonb, 'Sphingosine', 'Sphingomyelin has a sphingosine backbone (not glycerol), a fatty acid, and a phosphocholine head group. It is abundant in the myelin sheath of neurons.'),
('d8cd3a6d-cd6a-5071-b734-04782fad0724'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Platelet-activating factor (PAF) is a:', '["Prostaglandin","Triglyceride","Fatty acid","Leukotriene","Phospholipid mediator"]'::jsonb, 'Phospholipid mediator', 'PAF is a potent phospholipid mediator (1-O-alkyl-2-acetyl-sn-glycero-3-phosphocholine) that activates platelets, causes vasodilation, and mediates inflammation and anaphylaxis.'),
('a76d7275-6881-5d27-96ad-b52dfc4921bc'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Which enzyme converts cholesterol to mevalonate (the rate-limiting step of cholesterol synthesis)?', '["HMG-CoA synthase","Mevalonate kinase","Lanosterol cyclase","Squalene synthase","HMG-CoA reductase"]'::jsonb, 'HMG-CoA reductase', 'HMG-CoA reductase converts HMG-CoA to mevalonate and is the rate-limiting enzyme of cholesterol synthesis. It is inhibited by statins and feedback-inhibited by cholesterol.'),
('e31b3d15-c44a-5805-a2ee-86a4845b19f6'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Statins lower cholesterol by:', '["Increasing LDL receptors","Blocking cholesterol absorption","Inhibiting ACAT","Stimulating bile acid excretion","Competitively inhibiting HMG-CoA reductase"]'::jsonb, 'Competitively inhibiting HMG-CoA reductase', 'Statins competitively inhibit HMG-CoA reductase, reducing hepatic cholesterol synthesis. This upregulates LDL receptors, lowering plasma LDL cholesterol.'),
('8f008d38-d5c3-5804-b31d-95f6cba66db9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'HMG-CoA reductase is allosterically inhibited by:', '["NADPH resulting in decreased functional activity","Glucagon accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CAMP mediated by specific receptor activation","Cholesterol and mevalonate","Insulin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cholesterol and mevalonate', 'HMG-CoA reductase is feedback-inhibited by cholesterol and mevalonate. Insulin activates it, while glucagon and AMPK phosphorylation inactivate it.'),
('5f021cd4-87ee-5dfa-8ece-12edf2c0a65d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'HMG-CoA reductase activity is regulated by:', '["Dephosphorylation (activation)","Transcriptional regulation","All of the above","Sterol-dependent degradation","Phosphorylation (inactivation) by AMPK"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'HMG-CoA reductase is regulated by transcriptional control (SREBP, sterol feedback), phosphorylation (inactive) by AMPK, and sterol-dependent degradation.'),
('e54ca773-9177-521e-acdc-a34798965937'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Cholesterol is a precursor for all EXCEPT:', '["Steroid hormones","Vitamin D","Cell membranes","Bile acids","Triglycerides"]'::jsonb, 'Triglycerides', 'Cholesterol is a precursor for bile acids, steroid hormones, and vitamin D, and is a membrane component. Triglycerides are synthesized from glycerol and fatty acids, not cholesterol.'),
('3634143c-be40-57bd-841f-bc999c748e05'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'The rate-limiting enzyme in bile acid synthesis is:', '["HMG-CoA reductase","Bile acid-CoA synthetase","Sterol 27-hydroxylase","12-alpha-hydroxylase","Cholesterol 7-alpha-hydroxylase (CYP7A1)"]'::jsonb, 'Cholesterol 7-alpha-hydroxylase (CYP7A1)', 'Cholesterol 7-alpha-hydroxylase (CYP7A1) is the rate-limiting enzyme of bile acid synthesis in the liver. Bile acids feedback-inhibit it and are essential for dietary fat emulsification.'),
('8f52a68a-73a3-5b60-b4ba-8d7d1eaba52e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'The major primary bile acids in humans are:', '["Lithocholic acid and deoxycholic acid","Cholic acid and chenodeoxycholic acid","Taurocholic acid only","Ursodeoxycholic acid","Glycocholic acid only"]'::jsonb, 'Cholic acid and chenodeoxycholic acid', 'Primary bile acids (cholic and chenodeoxycholic) are synthesized from cholesterol in the liver. They are conjugated with glycine or taurine and emulsify dietary fats.'),
('57854425-8c03-5ecd-b3e6-959ef874ba7d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Bile salts are conjugated with:', '["Glutathione","Acetate","Glucuronic acid","Glycine or taurine","Sulfate"]'::jsonb, 'Glycine or taurine', 'Bile acids are conjugated with glycine or taurine, which increases their water solubility and reduces their pKa so they remain ionized in the intestinal lumen.'),
('3a9134cc-0d4a-522f-8225-88ec39ac4f2b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Enterohepatic circulation of bile acids conserves:', '["About 95% of bile acids","About 75% of bile acids","All bile acids","About 50% of bile acids","About 25% of bile acids"]'::jsonb, 'About 95% of bile acids', 'About 95% of bile acids are reabsorbed in the terminal ileum and returned to the liver via the enterohepatic circulation. Only 5% is lost in feces daily.'),
('8afd0dc5-72a8-5876-bb01-f64d45ff93fc'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Chylomicrons are synthesized in:', '["Muscle","Liver","Adipose tissue","Intestinal mucosal cells","Pancreas"]'::jsonb, 'Intestinal mucosal cells', 'Chylomicrons are assembled in intestinal mucosal cells (enterocytes) to transport dietary triglycerides and cholesterol to peripheral tissues via the lymphatic system.'),
('532dbdb9-35a3-5125-b181-2504007acb53'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'VLDL is synthesized in the:', '["Liver","Adipose tissue","Muscle","Intestine","Kidney"]'::jsonb, 'Liver', 'VLDL is synthesized in the liver to export endogenous triglycerides. It is rich in triglycerides and Apo B-100, and matures to LDL as triglycerides are removed.'),
('24747d53-6451-5cd8-8293-731b332bffb7'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Which lipoprotein delivers cholesterol to peripheral tissues and is called the bad cholesterol?', '["HDL","VLDL","Chylomicron","IDL","LDL"]'::jsonb, 'LDL', 'LDL is derived from VLDL and delivers cholesterol to peripheral tissues. Elevated LDL promotes atherosclerosis, earning it the label bad cholesterol.'),
('30b5f4e5-7468-521f-bbf4-469b8d91fa6d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Which lipoprotein is called the good cholesterol?', '["HDL","VLDL","Chylomicron","IDL","LDL"]'::jsonb, 'HDL', 'HDL mediates reverse cholesterol transport, returning cholesterol from peripheral tissues to the liver for excretion. Higher HDL is cardioprotective (good cholesterol).'),
('0eed6813-f3e2-5ad0-9fb6-f268fb85e6c0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'The major apolipoprotein of chylomicrons is:', '["Apo E","Apo C-II","Apo A-I","Apo B-48","Apo B-100"]'::jsonb, 'Apo B-48', 'Chylomicrons carry Apo B-48 (the intestinally synthesized form), which is a truncated version of Apo B-100. VLDL and LDL carry Apo B-100.'),
('771c5c4a-711f-5b70-b1b2-391be5cf94d8'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Apo C-II is a cofactor for:', '["CETP","Lipoprotein lipase","LCAT","ACAT","Hepatic lipase"]'::jsonb, 'Lipoprotein lipase', 'Apo C-II is the essential cofactor for lipoprotein lipase, which hydrolyzes triglycerides in chylomicrons and VLDL. Apo C-II deficiency causes chylomicronemia (Type I hyperlipidemia).'),
('59bf85a0-6f3e-50d2-8f44-86f4e24a7ef9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Apo E mediates:', '["LPL activation","LCAT activation","Remnant clearance via the LDL receptor and LRP","Reverse cholesterol transport","Cholesterol esterification"]'::jsonb, 'Remnant clearance via the LDL receptor and LRP', 'Apo E mediates hepatic clearance of chylomicron remnants and IDL via the LDL receptor and LRP. Apo E2/E2 causes Type III hyperlipoproteinemia (dysbetalipoproteinemia).'),
('bead0854-36cb-540e-b730-369c1afea118'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Lecithin-cholesterol acyltransferase (LCAT) is activated by:', '["Apo D","Apo B-100","Apo C-II","Apo A-I","Apo E"]'::jsonb, 'Apo A-I', 'LCAT, activated by Apo A-I, esterifies free cholesterol on the HDL surface, converting it to cholesterol esters that move into the HDL core. This is essential for reverse cholesterol transport.'),
('d6597f78-2167-5886-b390-68a7cbd06a3f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Which transporter mediates selective uptake of HDL cholesteryl esters in the liver?', '["LDL receptor resulting in decreased functional activity","ABCA1 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CETP mediated by specific receptor activation","Lipoprotein lipase without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Scavenger receptor class B type I (SR-BI)"]'::jsonb, 'Scavenger receptor class B type I (SR-BI)', 'SR-BI in the liver selectively takes up HDL cholesteryl esters, completing reverse cholesterol transport. ABCA1 mediates cholesterol efflux from cells to lipid-poor Apo A-I.'),
('df09840c-88a8-5fea-9535-8da181a902ea'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Familial hypercholesterolemia is due to:', '["Defective PCSK9","Defective ABCA1","Defective LCAT","Defective Apo B-100","Defective LDL receptor"]'::jsonb, 'Defective LDL receptor', 'Familial hypercholesterolemia is most commonly caused by LDL receptor mutations, reducing LDL clearance. It causes marked LDL elevation, tendon xanthomas, and premature atherosclerosis.'),
('7a069a9f-0a09-5c03-a407-0f8e44269cba'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'The homozygous form of familial hypercholesterolemia presents with:', '["Fatty liver resulting in decreased functional activity","Hypertriglyceridemia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Severe hypercholesterolemia and tendon xanthomas","Low HDL without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Chylomicronemia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Severe hypercholesterolemia and tendon xanthomas', 'Homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia causes severe LDL elevation (>500 mg/dL), tendon and cutaneous xanthomas, and coronary artery disease in childhood.'),
('8bd6e5e7-4f23-5134-afec-ef21a860f160'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'PCSK9 regulates LDL receptor levels by:', '["Stabilizing the receptor","Blocking LDL binding","Promoting LDL receptor degradation","Enhancing LDL internalization","Increasing LDL receptor synthesis"]'::jsonb, 'Promoting LDL receptor degradation', 'PCSK9 promotes LDL receptor degradation in lysosomes, reducing LDL clearance. PCSK9 inhibitors (evolocumab, alirocumab) lower LDL dramatically by preserving LDL receptors.'),
('030d79f1-44ab-5a0c-9aa1-eb50d78da449'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Beta-oxidation of fatty acids occurs in the:', '["Endoplasmic reticulum","Both mitochondria and peroxisomes","Peroxisomes","Cytosol","Golgi apparatus"]'::jsonb, 'Both mitochondria and peroxisomes', 'Fatty acid beta-oxidation occurs in mitochondria (major site, for most fatty acids) and peroxisomes (for very-long-chain fatty acids and some others).'),
('a4bc8ee6-b32f-5a2c-8cea-f0f026bdb199'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'The carnitine shuttle transports:', '["Medium-chain fatty acids","Short-chain fatty acids","Acetyl-CoA","Long-chain acyl-CoA into the mitochondria","Free fatty acids"]'::jsonb, 'Long-chain acyl-CoA into the mitochondria', 'The carnitine shuttle (CPT-I, carnitine translocase, CPT-II) transports long-chain acyl-CoA into mitochondria for beta-oxidation. Malonyl-CoA inhibits CPT-I, linking fatty acid synthesis and oxidation.'),
('b42b2f19-eba6-53a0-8169-3946fbf10988'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Which molecule regulates CPT-I activity?', '["Malonyl-CoA","Insulin","NADH","ATP","Glucagon"]'::jsonb, 'Malonyl-CoA', 'Malonyl-CoA, produced by acetyl-CoA carboxylase, is a potent inhibitor of CPT-I. High malonyl-CoA (fed state) inhibits fatty acid oxidation; low malonyl-CoA (fasting) allows oxidation.'),
('8621e2e4-6e64-5ac9-8082-20df27a23184'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Each cycle of beta-oxidation produces:', '["1 FADH2 and 1 NADH","And 1 acetyl-CoA","1 NADH and 1 acetyl-CoA","1 NADH","1 FADH2"]'::jsonb, '1 FADH2', 'Each cycle of beta-oxidation produces 1 FADH2, 1 NADH, and 1 acetyl-CoA (except the final cycle producing 2 acetyl-CoA). The FADH2 and NADH feed the electron transport chain.'),
('09f9a7b1-f43e-5b4d-b2af-141ec5286aa6'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Complete oxidation of palmitic acid (16:0) yields how many ATP?', '["106","150","96","129","80"]'::jsonb, '106', 'Complete oxidation of palmitic acid (16:0) yields 106 ATP: beta-oxidation produces 7 NADH + 7 FADH2 + 8 acetyl-CoA, which yield 26 + 10.5 + 80 = 106 ATP total.'),
('f43e5f96-4fa9-5a6f-be6a-77e94662f52e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Which vitamin is required for the cofactor in methylmalonyl-CoA mutase?', '["Vitamin B2","Vitamin B6","Folic acid","Biotin","Vitamin B12 (cobalamin)"]'::jsonb, 'Vitamin B12 (cobalamin)', 'Methylmalonyl-CoA mutase requires vitamin B12 (cobalamin) as adenosylcobalamin to convert methylmalonyl-CoA to succinyl-CoA. B12 deficiency causes methylmalonic aciduria.'),
('045c93c5-dad9-57c3-890b-c40c4fec22d3'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Vitamin B12 deficiency leads to:', '["Propionic acidemia","Phenylketonuria","Maple syrup urine disease","Alkaptonuria","Methylmalonic aciduria"]'::jsonb, 'Methylmalonic aciduria', 'B12 deficiency impairs methylmalonyl-CoA mutase (causing methylmalonic aciduria) and methionine synthase (causing homocysteine accumulation and megaloblastic anemia).'),
('94a315cf-f30e-57df-8e3c-a37937fa4c94'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Zellweger syndrome is a:', '["Nucleic acid repair defect","Cytosolic enzyme deficiency","Peroxisomal biogenesis disorder","Lysosomal disorder","Mitochondrial disorder"]'::jsonb, 'Peroxisomal biogenesis disorder', 'Zellweger syndrome is a peroxisomal biogenesis disorder (PEX gene mutations) with absent peroxisomes, causing very-long-chain fatty acid accumulation, plasmalogen deficiency, and severe neurologic impairment.'),
('94be1efc-ba23-5234-9448-dca0fc5f8c0f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Adrenoleukodystrophy (X-ALD) is due to:', '["Mitochondrial defect","Defective catalase","Peroxisomal biogenesis defect","Defective peroxisomal VLCFA transport (ABCD1 mutation)","Defective beta-oxidation enzymes"]'::jsonb, 'Defective peroxisomal VLCFA transport (ABCD1 mutation)', 'X-linked adrenoleukodystrophy is caused by ABCD1 mutation impairing very-long-chain fatty acid transport into peroxisomes. VLCFA accumulate causing demyelination and adrenal insufficiency.'),
('b90e6cbe-4e76-579b-a7ce-24ecea2a5caf'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'The rate-limiting enzyme of ketogenesis is:', '["HMG-CoA synthase","HMG-CoA lyase","HMG-CoA reductase","Thiolase","Acetoacetyl-CoA synthetase"]'::jsonb, 'HMG-CoA synthase', 'HMG-CoA synthase is the rate-limiting enzyme of ketogenesis in liver mitochondria. Ketogenesis is stimulated when oxaloacetate is low (fasting) and acetyl-CoA accumulates.'),
('5bb5b49b-8a92-5f8c-972b-823c5043024a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Which organ cannot use ketone bodies as fuel?', '["Liver","Brain","Heart","Kidney","Skeletal muscle"]'::jsonb, 'Liver', 'The liver produces ketone bodies but lacks succinyl-CoA:3-ketoacid CoA transferase (SCOT), so it cannot utilize them. The brain uses ketone bodies during prolonged fasting.'),
('2a6a4b13-cc67-5bd1-8c7e-2a84bfafefcc'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Alcoholic fatty liver is due to:', '["Stimulation of VLDL secretion","Increased glycogenolysis","Increased ketogenesis","Suppression of lipogenesis","Increased NADH/NAD+ ratio inhibiting beta-oxidation"]'::jsonb, 'Increased NADH/NAD+ ratio inhibiting beta-oxidation', 'Alcohol metabolism increases the NADH/NAD+ ratio, inhibiting beta-oxidation and the TCA cycle while promoting fatty acid synthesis, causing triglyceride accumulation (steatosis).'),
('1af95d5a-a8fd-5695-b125-13843215b108'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Gaucher disease is due to deficiency of:', '["Sphingomyelinase","Alpha-galactosidase","Hexosaminidase A","Beta-glucocerebrosidase","Arylsulfatase A"]'::jsonb, 'Beta-glucocerebrosidase', 'Gaucher disease is caused by glucocerebrosidase deficiency, accumulating glucocerebroside in macrophages (Gaucher cells). Features: hepatosplenomegaly, bone crises, cytopenias.'),
('85302c00-7f24-5892-a783-ae3c4f258fab'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Gaucher disease treatment includes:', '["Substrate reduction therapy only","Enzyme replacement therapy (imiglucerase)","Bone marrow transplant only","Gene therapy only","Dietary restriction"]'::jsonb, 'Enzyme replacement therapy (imiglucerase)', 'Gaucher disease is treated with enzyme replacement therapy (imiglucerase, recombinant glucocerebrosidase) or substrate reduction therapy (miglustat, eliglustat).'),
('2e97f561-c9fe-5e74-9d7f-3d19ac292d80'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Tay-Sachs disease results from deficiency of:', '["Beta-glucocerebrosidase","Hexosaminidase B","Hexosaminidase A","Alpha-galactosidase","Sphingomyelinase"]'::jsonb, 'Hexosaminidase A', 'Tay-Sachs disease results from hexosaminidase A deficiency, causing GM2 ganglioside accumulation in neurons. It is autosomal recessive and common in Ashkenazi Jews, with cherry-red spot on macula.'),
('5b894dcc-b7c4-5d9b-9082-7f2e47b615c5'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'The characteristic finding in Tay-Sachs is:', '["Hepatosplenomegaly","Cherry-red spot on macula","Renal failure","Corneal clouding","Bone deformities"]'::jsonb, 'Cherry-red spot on macula', 'Tay-Sachs disease presents with a cherry-red spot on the macula, neurodegeneration, blindness, and early death. It is caused by hexosaminidase A deficiency.'),
('d30b1810-1976-51aa-87fe-712f677e5e82'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Sandhoff disease results from deficiency of:', '["Beta-glucocerebrosidase","Sphingomyelinase","Hexosaminidase A and B","Hexosaminidase A only","Hexosaminidase B only"]'::jsonb, 'Hexosaminidase A and B', 'Sandhoff disease is caused by deficiency of both hexosaminidase A and B (beta subunit mutation), causing GM2 ganglioside accumulation with hepatosplenomegaly in addition to neurologic disease.'),
('00fdda35-28da-5b12-93cf-8fd28cdc04f3'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Niemann-Pick disease (Type A and B) results from deficiency of:', '["Hexosaminidase A","Sphingomyelinase","Acid lipase","Alpha-galactosidase","Glucocerebrosidase"]'::jsonb, 'Sphingomyelinase', 'Niemann-Pick types A and B are caused by sphingomyelinase deficiency, accumulating sphingomyelin. Type A (infantile) is fatal with neurodegeneration; type B has hepatosplenomegaly and foam cells.'),
('575db3d0-4847-5223-96fa-e9ca72c3c133'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Fabry disease is an X-linked recessive disorder presenting with:', '["Hepatosplenomegaly","Corneal opacities","Angiokeratomas","Renal failure","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Fabry disease (alpha-galactosidase A deficiency) is X-linked, accumulating globotriaosylceramide. Features include acroparesthesias, angiokeratomas, corneal opacities, and renal failure.'),
('7b8612f6-a7b4-5dcf-af34-9635e586c3fa'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Krabbe disease (globoid cell leukodystrophy) is due to deficiency of:', '["Sphingomyelinase","Glucocerebrosidase","Galactocerebrosidase","Hexosaminidase A","Arylsulfatase A"]'::jsonb, 'Galactocerebrosidase', 'Krabbe disease is caused by galactocerebrosidase deficiency, accumulating galactocerebroside. It causes severe demyelination with globoid cells and is often fatal in infancy.'),
('166b08e8-550e-53c5-96d7-a9b10b2be3e7'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Hyperlipoproteinemia Type I is characterized by:', '["Low HDL resulting in decreased functional activity","Elevated chylomicrons and triglycerides","Elevated VLDL mediated by specific receptor activation","Elevated LDL without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Elevated cholesterol leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Elevated chylomicrons and triglycerides', 'Type I hyperlipoproteinemia (familial chylomicronemia) is caused by LPL or Apo C-II deficiency, causing massive chylomicron and triglyceride elevation with eruptive xanthomas and pancreatitis.'),
('c53def86-80b5-5d38-82d4-c0d4047f06cd'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Hyperlipoproteinemia Type V is characterized by:', '["Elevated chylomicrons and VLDL","Elevated LDL and VLDL","Elevated LDL only","Low HDL only","Elevated chylomicrons only"]'::jsonb, 'Elevated chylomicrons and VLDL', 'Type V hyperlipoproteinemia has elevated chylomicrons and VLDL, causing severe hypertriglyceridemia with risk of pancreatitis. It is associated with insulin resistance and alcohol.'),
('9e66c563-a675-504a-9b92-e71a2b899ced'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Niacin (vitamin B3) lowers lipids by:', '["Inhibiting HMG-CoA reductase","Blocking cholesterol absorption","Increasing LPL activity","Inhibiting VLDL secretion","Stimulating LCAT"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibiting VLDL secretion', 'Niacin inhibits VLDL secretion from the liver by reducing hepatic fatty acid synthesis (inhibits HSL in adipose tissue), lowering triglycerides and raising HDL. It causes flushing.'),
('41d5df99-f818-5159-b203-5c3c4d8647c0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Bile acid sequestrants (resins) lower LDL by:', '["Inhibiting HMG-CoA reductase resulting in decreased functional activity","Stimulating LPL accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Blocking cholesterol absorption mediated by specific receptor activation","Activating PPAR-alpha without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Binding bile acids in the intestine and increasing their excretion"]'::jsonb, 'Binding bile acids in the intestine and increasing their excretion', 'Bile acid sequestrants (cholestyramine) bind bile acids in the intestine, preventing their reabsorption. This depletes the bile acid pool, forcing the liver to synthesize new bile acids from cholesterol, lowering LDL.'),
('70417181-8bcd-5552-9894-45474d78091a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'The sphingomyelin pathway generates:', '["CGMP resulting in decreased functional activity","CAMP accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Calcium ions mediated by specific receptor activation","IP3 and DAG without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ceramide as a second messenger"]'::jsonb, 'Ceramide as a second messenger', 'Sphingomyelinase cleaves sphingomyelin to generate ceramide, a lipid second messenger that signals cell differentiation, growth arrest, and apoptosis.'),
('e3fc577d-bf0d-58c7-afcf-1bbfa4c515d4'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Which lipids contain sphingosine as their backbone?', '["Neutral lipids resulting in decreased functional activity","Glycerolipids accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Glycosphingolipids with a single sugar residue","Phospholipids without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Steroids leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Glycosphingolipids with a single sugar residue', 'Sphingolipids (sphingomyelin, cerebrosides, gangliosides) have sphingosine as their backbone instead of glycerol. Cerebrosides have one sugar residue; gangliosides have oligosaccharide chains.'),
('d10a18ec-f7d2-5b5a-94e0-f8ef702d2a1d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'GM2 ganglioside accumulates in:', '["Metachromatic leukodystrophy","Krabbe disease","Fabry disease","Tay-Sachs and Sandhoff diseases","Gaucher disease"]'::jsonb, 'Tay-Sachs and Sandhoff diseases', 'GM2 ganglioside accumulates in Tay-Sachs (hexosaminidase A deficiency) and Sandhoff (hexosaminidase A and B deficiency) diseases, causing neuronal degeneration.'),
('1b6bdfcb-d7de-516d-9356-df3dcdbb0243'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Myelin is rich in:', '["Phosphatidylcholine only","Triglycerides","Free fatty acids","Sphingomyelin and galactocerebrosides","Cholesteryl esters"]'::jsonb, 'Sphingomyelin and galactocerebrosides', 'Myelin is 80% lipid and 20% protein, rich in sphingomyelin and galactocerebrosides. These sphingolipids provide electrical insulation for rapid nerve conduction.'),
('04055313-f8ab-53f7-a3b8-2feaf00f81aa'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Phospholipase A2 cleaves:', '["The sn-2 fatty acid from phospholipids","The phosphate group","The polar head group","Both fatty acids","The sn-1 fatty acid"]'::jsonb, 'The sn-2 fatty acid from phospholipids', 'Phospholipase A2 releases arachidonic acid from the sn-2 position of membrane phospholipids, initiating eicosanoid synthesis. Glucocorticoids inhibit PLA2 via lipocortins.'),
('620832c0-7728-57c8-9ec3-4e9a144d2fa9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Phospholipase C cleaves:', '["Both fatty acids resulting in decreased functional activity","The polar head group accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The sn-2 fatty acid mediated by specific receptor activation","The glycerol-phosphate bond (generating DAG and IP3)","The sn-1 fatty acid leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The glycerol-phosphate bond (generating DAG and IP3)', 'Phospholipase C generates diacylglycerol (DAG) and inositol trisphosphate (IP3), two second messengers in the PI-PLC signaling pathway, activating PKC and releasing calcium.'),
('afbc999e-36b8-5932-b861-39b851d262b1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Acyl carrier protein (ACP) is a component of:', '["Fatty acid synthase complex","Carnitine shuttle","Ketogenesis","Beta-oxidation system","Lipoprotein lipase"]'::jsonb, 'Fatty acid synthase complex', 'ACP is a component of the fatty acid synthase complex, carrying the growing fatty acyl chain via a phosphopantetheine arm during fatty acid synthesis.'),
('f9380eca-7529-585e-ba19-a24a1002f9b7'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Fatty acid synthesis occurs in the:', '["Peroxisomes","Golgi apparatus","Cytosol","Mitochondria","Endoplasmic reticulum"]'::jsonb, 'Cytosol', 'Fatty acid synthesis occurs in the cytosol, using acetyl-CoA (exported as citrate), NADPH (from the HMP shunt), and the multifunctional fatty acid synthase complex.'),
('d03ace43-e368-518f-bb35-7c40c4e2b6f7'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'The rate-limiting step of fatty acid synthesis is:', '["Fatty acid synthase (FAS)","Acetyl-CoA carboxylase (ACC)","Enoyl-ACP reductase","Malonyl-CoA transacylase","Beta-ketoacyl-ACP synthase"]'::jsonb, 'Acetyl-CoA carboxylase (ACC)', 'Acetyl-CoA carboxylase (ACC) catalyzes acetyl-CoA to malonyl-CoA and is the rate-limiting step of fatty acid synthesis. It is activated by citrate/insulin and inhibited by palmitoyl-CoA and phosphorylation.'),
('36ae6337-c686-5055-b3b5-277974a1d91a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Acetyl-CoA carboxylase requires which cofactor?', '["Biotin","Thiamine pyrophosphate","NADH","FAD","PLP"]'::jsonb, 'Biotin', 'Acetyl-CoA carboxylase requires biotin (vitamin B7) as a carboxyl carrier. Other biotin-dependent carboxylases include pyruvate carboxylase and propionyl-CoA carboxylase.'),
('ebc8596f-9b54-55cf-8a0c-c0dc0d0ba9ae'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Acetyl-CoA carboxylase is activated by:', '["Palmitoyl-CoA","CAMP","Epinephrine","Citrate and insulin","Glucagon"]'::jsonb, 'Citrate and insulin', 'ACC is allosterically activated by citrate (which also activates PFK-1 via increased acetyl-CoA) and by insulin (dephosphorylation). Glucagon/epinephrine phosphorylate and inactivate it.'),
('120a837c-f8d1-5d33-b672-a0fb5fdde528'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Fatty acid synthase in humans is a single polypeptide with:', '["Two catalytic domains","Seven catalytic domains","Four catalytic domains","Five catalytic domains","Three catalytic domains"]'::jsonb, 'Seven catalytic domains', 'Human fatty acid synthase is a single multifunctional polypeptide with seven catalytic domains plus an ACP domain. It synthesizes palmitate (16:0) using acetyl-CoA, malonyl-CoA, and NADPH.'),
('c4cad469-d675-5bc1-b668-2e38a3445ea4'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Humans cannot introduce double bonds beyond which carbon?', '["Delta-4","Delta-9","Delta-3","Delta-6","Delta-5"]'::jsonb, 'Delta-9', 'Humans lack desaturase enzymes to introduce double bonds beyond carbon 9, making linoleic (omega-6) and alpha-linolenic (omega-3) acids essential dietary fatty acids.'),
('21497160-b9a1-5464-a2fb-5685bf55e729'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Docosahexaenoic acid (DHA) is a:', '["18-carbon omega-3 with 3 double bonds","22-carbon omega-3 fatty acid with 6 double bonds","20-carbon omega-3 with 5 double bonds","22-carbon omega-6 with 4 double bonds","20-carbon omega-6 with 4 double bonds"]'::jsonb, '22-carbon omega-3 fatty acid with 6 double bonds', 'DHA (22:6, omega-3) is found in brain and retina. It is important for neural development, visual function, and anti-inflammatory responses (precursor for resolvins/protectins).'),
('089e5487-9b36-5649-b0da-b21e7b81b856'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Trans fatty acids are:', '["Omega-3 fatty acids","Cis-unsaturated fatty acids","Unsaturated fatty acids with trans double bonds","Saturated fatty acids","Essential fatty acids"]'::jsonb, 'Unsaturated fatty acids with trans double bonds', 'Trans fats are unsaturated fatty acids with trans double bonds, produced by partial hydrogenation. They raise LDL and lower HDL, increasing cardiovascular risk.'),
('3256c648-9b4a-5538-8f27-5789f899ea4d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Elevated Lp(a) is a:', '["Genetic risk factor for cardiovascular disease","Reversible risk factor","Protective factor","Risk factor for diabetes","Dietary risk factor"]'::jsonb, 'Genetic risk factor for cardiovascular disease', 'Elevated Lp(a) is an independent genetic risk factor for atherosclerosis. Lp(a) levels are >90% genetically determined and not significantly modified by lifestyle or diet.'),
('0f63ee15-1a51-5679-9538-c22f7ab2368c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Steroid hormones are derived from:', '["Cholesterol","Sphingolipids","Triglycerides","Fatty acids","Phospholipids"]'::jsonb, 'Cholesterol', 'Steroid hormones (cortisol, aldosterone, estrogens, progestins, androgens) are all synthesized from cholesterol. The rate-limiting step is cholesterol transport into mitochondria by the StAR protein.'),
('68b142ff-de6f-5dae-8c42-267a8a379b16'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Which sterol is converted to vitamin D3 in the skin?', '["Sitosterol","Ergosterol","Campesterol","7-Dehydrocholesterol","Cholesterol"]'::jsonb, '7-Dehydrocholesterol', '7-Dehydrocholesterol in the skin is converted to cholecalciferol (vitamin D3) by UV light. Vitamin D is then hydroxylated in liver (25-OH) and kidney (1,25-diOH, active form).'),
('af92388e-247e-56da-9954-ad663350ee2d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Squalene is a:', '["Steroid hormone resulting in decreased functional activity","Linear 30-carbon intermediate in cholesterol synthesis","Bile acid mediated by specific receptor activation","Cyclic intermediate without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fatty acid leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Linear 30-carbon intermediate in cholesterol synthesis', 'Squalene is a linear 30-carbon intermediate formed from six isoprenoid units (farnesyl pyrophosphate). It is cyclized to lanosterol, then converted to cholesterol.'),
('eade93a9-db87-50a1-bbae-c02dea986eca'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Farnesyl pyrophosphate is a precursor for:', '["Cholesterol only","And prenylated proteins","Ubiquinone","Dolichol","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Farnesyl pyrophosphate is a precursor for cholesterol, ubiquinone (CoQ), dolichol, and prenylated proteins (farnesylation of Ras).'),
('60ecd986-fefb-5922-a89f-9b3f05b9e98d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Lipids: Weak acids are trapped in:', '["Alkaline urine","Acidic stomach","Blood pH 7.4","Duodenum","Jejunum"]'::jsonb, 'Alkaline urine', 'Weak acids become ionised (trapped) in alkaline environments.'),
('ffc93fef-f7dd-5929-91cd-7d6ef10f4cf1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Beige adipocytes are:', '["White fat cells only resulting in decreased functional activity","Liver cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Muscle cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Brown fat cells only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","BAT-like cells in white adipose tissue that can be induced by cold or exercise"]'::jsonb, 'BAT-like cells in white adipose tissue that can be induced by cold or exercise', 'Beige adipocytes are BAT-like thermogenic cells within white adipose tissue, inducible by cold exposure or exercise through irisin. They express UCP1.'),
('58b8a164-e162-5c5f-af50-251e6ab7ccfa'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Adiponectin levels are typically:', '["Decreased in anorexia","Decreased in obesity","Increased in cachexia","Increased in obesity","Unchanged"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased in obesity', 'Adiponectin is an insulin-sensitizing adipokine whose levels are decreased in obesity and type 2 diabetes. It activates AMPK, promoting fatty acid oxidation and reducing hepatic glucose output.'),
('ef1941fa-7b08-5c89-b533-eb95a4b80246'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'CPT-I deficiency prevents:', '["Ketogenesis","Medium-chain fatty acid entry","Long-chain fatty acid entry into mitochondria","Gluconeogenesis","Short-chain fatty acid entry"]'::jsonb, 'Long-chain fatty acid entry into mitochondria', 'CPT-I deficiency blocks long-chain fatty acid transport into mitochondria, impairing fatty acid oxidation. It causes hypoketotic hypoglycemia, hepatomegaly, and cardiomyopathy.'),
('4b59b4cc-94b5-5efd-a8b0-7d9a33a8a986'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'MCAD deficiency presents with:', '["Hyperglycemia","Ketoacidosis","Vomiting","And lethargy in infancy","Hypoketotic hypoglycemia"]'::jsonb, 'Hypoketotic hypoglycemia', 'Medium-chain acyl-CoA dehydrogenase (MCAD) deficiency impairs fatty acid oxidation, causing hypoketotic hypoglycemia, vomiting, and lethargy during fasting. It is the most common fatty acid oxidation disorder.'),
('e862d88f-40d1-5bb8-bad5-de1bae8e013c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Refsum disease is due to impaired:', '["Ketogenesis","Omega-oxidation","Peroxisomal biogenesis","Alpha-oxidation of phytanic acid","Beta-oxidation"]'::jsonb, 'Alpha-oxidation of phytanic acid', 'Refsum disease is caused by phytanoyl-CoA hydroxylase deficiency, impairing alpha-oxidation of phytanic acid. It causes retinitis pigmentosa, peripheral neuropathy, and cerebellar ataxia.'),
('77889c01-2a25-5312-adc6-45daba34da8d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Refsum disease presents with:', '["Muscle weakness","Peripheral neuropathy","Retinitis pigmentosa","And cerebellar ataxia","Hepatosplenomegaly"]'::jsonb, 'Retinitis pigmentosa', 'Refsum disease features retinitis pigmentosa, peripheral neuropathy, cerebellar ataxia, and ichthyosis due to phytanic acid accumulation. Treated with a phytanic acid-free diet.'),
('6dc0a3ca-93f3-5af9-af20-1c1a3de7a022'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Lipid peroxidation is:', '["Oxidative damage to polyunsaturated fatty acids","Enzymatic lipid synthesis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ketogenesis mediated by specific receptor activation","Lipolysis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Beta-oxidation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Oxidative damage to polyunsaturated fatty acids', 'Lipid peroxidation is free-radical damage to polyunsaturated fatty acids in membranes, propagated as a chain reaction and generating malondialdehyde and 4-hydroxynonenal.'),
('3558162f-bbd5-5793-96c6-84b869aab8e4'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Malondialdehyde (MDA) is a:', '["Bile acid","Fatty acid precursor","Cholesterol metabolite","Marker of lipid peroxidation","Signaling molecule"]'::jsonb, 'Marker of lipid peroxidation', 'MDA is a reactive aldehyde end-product of lipid peroxidation, used as a biomarker of oxidative stress. It forms adducts with DNA and proteins, contributing to mutagenesis.'),
('e2f96996-e683-557b-9a29-973429efc02e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Vitamin E (alpha-tocopherol) acts as a:', '["Hormone resulting in decreased functional activity","Water-soluble antioxidant accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lipid-soluble antioxidant breaking lipid peroxidation chains","Enzyme cofactor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cell signaling molecule leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lipid-soluble antioxidant breaking lipid peroxidation chains', 'Vitamin E is the major lipid-soluble antioxidant in membranes, breaking lipid peroxidation chain reactions by donating hydrogen to peroxyl radicals. It protects PUFAs.'),
('36f2f2fb-1abe-55d9-9d37-d892e7a80381'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Prostacyclin (PGI2) is:', '["A leukotriene resulting in decreased functional activity","A vasoconstrictor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A pro-aggregatory factor mediated by specific receptor activation","A thromboxane without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A vasodilator and inhibitor of platelet aggregation"]'::jsonb, 'A vasodilator and inhibitor of platelet aggregation', 'Prostacyclin (PGI2) is produced by vascular endothelium via COX. It is a potent vasodilator and inhibitor of platelet aggregation, opposing thromboxane A2.'),
('8deea01e-34bd-5740-8b1a-2586cac86029'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Thromboxane A2 (TXA2) is:', '["Anti-inflammatory resulting in decreased functional activity","A leukotriene accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A vasodilator mediated by specific receptor activation","A vasoconstrictor and platelet aggregator","Anti-thrombotic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A vasoconstrictor and platelet aggregator', 'Thromboxane A2 is produced by platelets via COX-1. It promotes platelet aggregation and vasoconstriction. Low-dose aspirin inhibits TXA2, reducing thrombotic risk.'),
('6432d26b-e77a-51a9-b4d1-be52686afdb3'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Aspirin irreversibly inhibits COX by:', '["Non-competitive inhibition","Competitive binding","Uncompetitive inhibition","Allosteric modification","Acetylating a serine residue in the active site"]'::jsonb, 'Acetylating a serine residue in the active site', 'Aspirin irreversibly acetylates Ser-529 in the COX active site, permanently inactivating COX-1. This is why low-dose aspirin''s antiplatelet effect lasts 7-10 days (platelet lifetime).'),
('6c8a5adc-db37-5001-9607-ef3642d965f9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Cardiolipin is:', '["A sphingolipid resulting in decreased functional activity","A steroid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A glycolipid mediated by specific receptor activation","A triglyceride without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A diphosphatidylglycerol found in the inner mitochondrial membrane"]'::jsonb, 'A diphosphatidylglycerol found in the inner mitochondrial membrane', 'Cardiolipin is a diphosphatidylglycerol unique to the inner mitochondrial membrane. It is essential for electron transport chain function and is a target in anti-phospholipid syndrome.'),
('b6b7088b-70cd-5c60-a1c3-03e6f1236014'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Which lipoprotein migrates fastest on agarose gel electrophoresis?', '["VLDL","LDL","HDL","Chylomicrons","IDL"]'::jsonb, 'HDL', 'On agarose gel electrophoresis, HDL migrates fastest (toward anode) due to its high protein content and small size. Chylomicrons stay at the origin; VLDL migrates to pre-beta, LDL to beta position.'),
('4648e6d5-7201-5f67-b7dd-3de31a0f3af4'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Chylomicrons remain at the origin in electrophoresis because:', '["They are denatured","They have charge","They are too large to migrate","They bind to the gel","They are positively charged"]'::jsonb, 'They are too large to migrate', 'Chylomicrons are the largest lipoproteins (75-1200 nm), too large to migrate through agarose gel pores, so they remain at the origin.'),
('2b22911a-b5f6-504d-bf6a-93a082de024d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Tendon xanthomas are characteristic of:', '["Type III resulting in decreased functional activity","Type I accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Type II hyperlipoproteinemia (familial hypercholesterolemia)","Type IV without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Type V leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Type II hyperlipoproteinemia (familial hypercholesterolemia)', 'Tendon xanthomas (Achilles, finger extensor tendons) are characteristic of familial hypercholesterolemia (Type II), resulting from chronic LDL cholesterol deposition.'),
('db2c6130-6275-53d1-8654-2b307147861f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'NAFLD (non-alcoholic fatty liver disease) is associated with:', '["Insulin resistance","Viral hepatitis","And metabolic syndrome","Obesity","Alcohol consumption only"]'::jsonb, 'Obesity', 'NAFLD is strongly associated with obesity, insulin resistance, and metabolic syndrome. NASH adds inflammation and hepatocyte injury, progressing to fibrosis/cirrhosis.'),
('aa5517b1-f132-5ab2-bbd4-d5901db5f388'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'The Friedewald formula estimates LDL cholesterol as:', '["TC - HDL - TG","TC - HDL - TG/10","TC - HDL","TC - TG/5","TC - HDL - TG/5"]'::jsonb, 'TC - HDL - TG/5', 'The Friedewald formula estimates LDL = TC - HDL - TG/5 (mg/dL). It is valid only when triglycerides are <400 mg/dL; otherwise direct LDL measurement is needed.'),
('1f7d461e-b03d-5f02-8896-1e291f52de55'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'The treatment target for triglycerides is:', '["<150 mg/dL","<1000 mg/dL","<500 mg/dL","<50 mg/dL","<200 mg/dL"]'::jsonb, '<150 mg/dL', 'The triglyceride treatment target is <150 mg/dL. Levels >500 mg/dL risk pancreatitis; >1000 mg/dL requires aggressive therapy.'),
('aa24003a-7300-5732-8e16-a805e4c8829f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Foam cells in atherosclerotic plaques are:', '["Endothelial cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Macrophages engorged with oxidized LDL","Neutrophils mediated by specific receptor activation","Smooth muscle cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lymphocytes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Macrophages engorged with oxidized LDL', 'Foam cells are macrophages that have taken up oxidized LDL via scavenger receptors (CD36, SR-A), which are not downregulated, leading to uncontrolled lipid accumulation and fatty streaks.'),
('da7cbc0b-3dd1-5ff0-82ed-c03ab9dd436c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Atherosclerosis is initiated by:', '["Smooth muscle proliferation resulting in decreased functional activity","Foam cell death accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fibrous cap formation mediated by specific receptor activation","Platelet aggregation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Endothelial dysfunction and LDL retention in the arterial intima"]'::jsonb, 'Endothelial dysfunction and LDL retention in the arterial intima', 'Atherosclerosis begins with endothelial dysfunction, LDL penetration and oxidation in the intima, monocyte recruitment, and foam cell formation, leading to fatty streaks and plaques.'),
('92fe7046-37e4-5d40-ab9a-940f92f338a5'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'ABCA1 transporter is essential for:', '["LDL uptake resulting in decreased functional activity","VLDL secretion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","HDL biogenesis and cholesterol efflux from cells","Chylomicron assembly without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bile acid secretion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'HDL biogenesis and cholesterol efflux from cells', 'ABCA1 mediates efflux of cholesterol and phospholipids from cells to lipid-poor Apo A-I, generating nascent HDL. Its deficiency causes Tangier disease.'),
('ada02fa6-eb31-5fe6-af94-1263940f2fe4'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Tangier disease is due to:', '["CETP deficiency resulting in decreased functional activity","ABCA1 deficiency causing very low HDL","ACAT deficiency mediated by specific receptor activation","LCAT deficiency without affecting the primary tissue pathway","LPL deficiency leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'ABCA1 deficiency causing very low HDL', 'Tangier disease results from ABCA1 mutations causing very low HDL, cholesterol accumulation in macrophages (orange tonsils), and peripheral neuropathy.'),
('974efa42-5a56-52c6-82f1-e4b11aed0e5d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Fish-eye disease is due to:', '["Partial LCAT deficiency","LPL deficiency","Complete LCAT deficiency","CETP deficiency","ABCA1 deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Partial LCAT deficiency', 'Fish-eye disease is partial LCAT deficiency with corneal opacities and low HDL but preserved renal function. Complete LCAT deficiency (Norum disease) adds renal failure and anemia.'),
('01c96c3d-5c7b-53e6-9de1-242e7c84ef44'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'LCAT deficiency causes:', '["Low HDL","And anemia (target cells)","High LDL","Corneal opacities","High HDL"]'::jsonb, 'Low HDL', 'LCAT deficiency causes very low HDL, corneal opacities, and target cells/hemolytic anemia. It impairs cholesterol esterification and reverse cholesterol transport.'),
('b31969a6-41ec-5cf8-bbed-3865465e76ae'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Familial hypertriglyceridemia (Type IV) is associated with:', '["Apo C-II deficiency","Apo B mutation","Increased VLDL production","LPL deficiency","LDL receptor deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Increased VLDL production', 'Type IV hyperlipidemia features elevated VLDL/triglycerides due to increased hepatic VLDL production, associated with insulin resistance, obesity, and alcohol use.'),
('4b6f824a-e130-57ec-997e-e6a634016a10'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Hormone-sensitive lipase (HSL) is activated by:', '["Cholesterol synthesis","Fatty acid synthesis","CAMP-dependent PKA phosphorylation","Glucose uptake","Insulin"]'::jsonb, 'CAMP-dependent PKA phosphorylation', 'HSL is activated by cAMP-dependent PKA phosphorylation in response to glucagon, epinephrine, and cortisol. It is inhibited by insulin, which dephosphorylates it.'),
('ad8e3952-8d78-5373-ae7a-123030f5cce0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Adipose triglyceride lipase (ATGL) is:', '["The first enzyme in triglyceride hydrolysis","The final enzyme in lipolysis","A phospholipase","A lipoprotein lipase","A cholesterol esterase"]'::jsonb, 'The first enzyme in triglyceride hydrolysis', 'ATGL catalyzes the first step of lipolysis, hydrolyzing triglycerides to diglycerides. HSL and monoglyceride lipase complete the breakdown to glycerol and free fatty acids.'),
('a00633a4-5c97-5d54-b8fa-86b880a0c223'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Lipolysis produces:', '["Free fatty acids and glycerol","Only free fatty acids","Cholesterol","Acetyl-CoA","Only glycerol"]'::jsonb, 'Free fatty acids and glycerol', 'Lipolysis of triglycerides produces free fatty acids (for oxidation/energy) and glycerol (used by the liver for gluconeogenesis via glycerol kinase).'),
('0d99f517-9260-5ddd-8e53-f9f059a4cd68'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Glycerol from lipolysis can be used by the liver for:', '["Glycogenesis","Gluconeogenesis","Cholesterol synthesis","Ketogenesis","Fatty acid synthesis"]'::jsonb, 'Gluconeogenesis', 'Glycerol is phosphorylated and oxidized to dihydroxyacetone phosphate, which enters gluconeogenesis in the liver. Glycerol is a gluconeogenic substrate.'),
('577155a5-ce19-5220-8b12-d2c27ff5e6cc'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Free fatty acids in plasma are transported bound to:', '["Albumin","Transferrin","Hemoglobin","Lipoproteins","Globulins"]'::jsonb, 'Albumin', 'Free fatty acids are transported in plasma bound to albumin, which prevents their detergent effects. The albumin-bound pool turns over rapidly to supply tissues.'),
('c5f3da42-2b6f-5505-992b-926d5eb2344b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Medium-chain fatty acids (8-12 carbons):', '["Require carnitine shuttle resulting in decreased functional activity","Cannot be oxidized accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Enter mitochondria directly without carnitine shuttle","Are stored as fat without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Are only used for ketogenesis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Enter mitochondria directly without carnitine shuttle', 'Medium-chain fatty acids enter mitochondria directly without the carnitine shuttle. This is why MCT oil is used in long-chain fatty acid oxidation disorders.'),
('01a0a17c-8c9d-5fe9-81df-a7480de2e183'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'The enzymes of ketogenesis are in the:', '["Nucleus","Cytosol","Mitochondrial matrix","Peroxisomes","Endoplasmic reticulum"]'::jsonb, 'Mitochondrial matrix', 'Ketogenesis enzymes (thiolase, HMG-CoA synthase, HMG-CoA lyase) are in the mitochondrial matrix of hepatocytes, where beta-oxidation produces the acetyl-CoA substrate.'),
('02f150b8-ad98-5f78-89d5-c4931d140fd0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'The conversion of acetoacetate to beta-hydroxybutyrate is catalyzed by:', '["HMG-CoA lyase","Succinyl-CoA transferase","Thiolase","Beta-hydroxybutyrate dehydrogenase","Acetoacetyl-CoA synthetase"]'::jsonb, 'Beta-hydroxybutyrate dehydrogenase', 'Beta-hydroxybutyrate dehydrogenase converts acetoacetate to beta-hydroxybutyrate using NADH. Beta-hydroxybutyrate is the most abundant ketone body and reflects the NADH/NAD+ ratio.'),
('d46cd453-c86b-518c-862c-3960e275ad05'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Ketone bodies are used as fuel by peripheral tissues via:', '["Thiolase resulting in decreased functional activity","Acetyl-CoA synthetase accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Carnitine palmitoyltransferase mediated by specific receptor activation","HMG-CoA lyase without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Succinyl-CoA:3-ketoacid CoA transferase (SCOT)"]'::jsonb, 'Succinyl-CoA:3-ketoacid CoA transferase (SCOT)', 'Peripheral tissues activate acetoacetate using SCOT (succinyl-CoA:3-ketoacid CoA transferase), which the liver lacks. This is why the liver cannot use ketones itself.'),
('af943d6c-fd90-56e7-899b-ed07b0b5269b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Ketostix and Multistix detect:', '["Only glucose","Acetone primarily","All ketone bodies","Acetoacetate primarily","Beta-hydroxybutyrate primarily"]'::jsonb, 'Acetoacetate primarily', 'Ketostix detect acetoacetate via the nitroprusside reaction (purple color). They do not detect beta-hydroxybutyrate, which may predominate in severe DKA.'),
('957efbd9-aea8-5441-800a-30b5c26c46b1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Beta-hydroxybutyrate measurement is preferred in DKA because:', '["It is elevated resulting in decreased functional activity","It is more specific accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","It is faster mediated by specific receptor activation","It is cheaper without affecting the primary tissue pathway","It is the predominant ketone body and is not detected by routine dipsticks"]'::jsonb, 'It is the predominant ketone body and is not detected by routine dipsticks', 'In DKA, beta-hydroxybutyrate is the predominant ketone body but is not detected by dipsticks. As treatment restores NAD+, it converts to acetoacetate, so dipsticks may paradoxically rise.'),
('965f1fa2-506c-56bb-a434-7c3997006e15'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Which lipids are the primary components of cell membranes?', '["Triglycerides","Free cholesterol only","Phospholipids and cholesterol","Wax esters","Sphingolipids only"]'::jsonb, 'Phospholipids and cholesterol', 'Cell membranes are bilayers of phospholipids (with cholesterol modulating fluidity). The lipid bilayer also contains glycolipids, sphingolipids, and proteins.'),
('dfc2f3a2-1c7e-5dbb-8939-b4edcd313c90'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Corticosteroids (glucocorticoids) inhibit eicosanoid synthesis by:', '["Blocking the leukotriene receptor","Directly inhibiting COX","Inhibiting lipoxygenase","Inducing annexins (lipocortins) that inhibit PLA2","Blocking prostaglandin receptors"]'::jsonb, 'Inducing annexins (lipocortins) that inhibit PLA2', 'Glucocorticoids induce lipocortin (annexin-1), which inhibits phospholipase A2, preventing arachidonic acid release and therefore reducing both prostaglandin and leukotriene synthesis.'),
('af94267d-6e56-5308-beb6-a51f6f38027d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Prostaglandin E2 (PGE2) causes:', '["Pain","Vasoconstriction","Platelet aggregation","And inflammation","Fever"]'::jsonb, 'Fever', 'PGE2 is a major inflammatory mediator causing fever (acts on hypothalamic thermoregulatory center), pain, vasodilation, and erythema.'),
('c3c254a1-bc81-5f78-a47b-654d1c5dc2f6'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Misoprostol (PGE1 analog) is used to:', '["Treat asthma resulting in decreased functional activity","Treat hypertension accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Induce labor mediated by specific receptor activation","Lower cholesterol without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Protect the gastric mucosa during NSAID therapy"]'::jsonb, 'Protect the gastric mucosa during NSAID therapy', 'Misoprostol, a PGE1 analog, protects the gastric mucosa by stimulating mucus/bicarbonate secretion and inhibiting acid secretion. It prevents NSAID-induced ulcers.'),
('4ac82b91-2b4c-5d23-943e-0c713eed2c91'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Prostacyclin (PGI2) is used therapeutically for:', '["Hypertension resulting in decreased functional activity","Pulmonary arterial hypertension","Diabetes mediated by specific receptor activation","Glaucoma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Gastric ulcers leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pulmonary arterial hypertension', 'Prostacyclin (PGI2, epoprostenol) is a vasodilator used to treat pulmonary arterial hypertension. It also inhibits platelet aggregation.'),
('f0c43f3b-d689-52fc-8e14-14a6cf6c1994'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'N-3 polyunsaturated fatty acids (EPA/DHA) have anti-inflammatory effects by:', '["Blocking PLA2 resulting in decreased functional activity","Inhibiting COX directly accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Increasing leukotriene synthesis mediated by specific receptor activation","Competing with arachidonic acid for eicosanoid synthesis","Promoting prostaglandin synthesis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Competing with arachidonic acid for eicosanoid synthesis', 'EPA/DHA compete with arachidonic acid for COX and LOX enzymes, producing less pro-inflammatory eicosanoids. They also generate resolvins and protectins that resolve inflammation.'),
('be66e6d1-0179-51a4-8b89-b05c594720b2'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Sphingosine-1-phosphate (S1P) is:', '["A storage lipid resulting in decreased functional activity","And immune function accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A signaling sphingolipid that regulates cell proliferation","Migration without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A membrane structural lipid only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A signaling sphingolipid that regulates cell proliferation', 'S1P is a signaling sphingolipid regulating cell proliferation, migration, and immune cell trafficking. Fingolimod modulates S1P receptors to treat multiple sclerosis.'),
('6090f872-c691-55d3-bccf-eb2dbd8cbb44'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Ceramide is a central molecule in sphingolipid metabolism that:', '["Stimulates proliferation","Is a vitamin","Induces apoptosis and cell cycle arrest","Has signaling function","Promotes cell survival"]'::jsonb, 'Induces apoptosis and cell cycle arrest', 'Ceramide is a lipid second messenger that induces apoptosis, cell cycle arrest, and differentiation. It is generated by sphingomyelinase or de novo synthesis.'),
('b58fba7e-5fd1-55e2-9585-010a346fef06'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Fingolimod (FTY720) is a:', '["Leukotriene antagonist resulting in decreased functional activity","Statin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","S1P receptor modulator used for multiple sclerosis","COX inhibitor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Phospholipase inhibitor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'S1P receptor modulator used for multiple sclerosis', 'Fingolimod, an S1P receptor modulator, sequesters lymphocytes in lymph nodes, preventing them from entering the CNS. It is used for relapsing-remitting multiple sclerosis.'),
('2a0911bf-b689-5b84-8d11-d3f6a99183b9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Anandamide (N-arachidonoylethanolamine) is an:', '["Endocannabinoid that activates CB1 and CB2 receptors","Prostaglandin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Leukotriene mediated by specific receptor activation","Ketone body without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fatty acid leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Endocannabinoid that activates CB1 and CB2 receptors', 'Anandamide is an endocannabinoid derived from arachidonic acid that activates CB1 (CNS) and CB2 (immune) receptors, regulating appetite, pain, and mood.'),
('7a255d50-077b-58eb-985d-55409a44d292'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Micelles are:', '["Small aggregates of bile salts and lipids that facilitate fat absorption","Membrane vesicles accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lipid droplets mediated by specific receptor activation","Chylomicrons without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lipoproteins leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Small aggregates of bile salts and lipids that facilitate fat absorption', 'Micelles are bile salt aggregates that solubilize dietary lipids, transporting them to the intestinal brush border for absorption. They are distinct from chylomicrons (which export absorbed fat).'),
('b168ecc9-cdf2-50c0-bb9d-28579d662999'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Chylomicron assembly in enterocytes requires:', '["LPL resulting in decreased functional activity","CETP accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","ACAT mediated by specific receptor activation","Microsomal triglyceride transfer protein (MTP)","LCAT leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Microsomal triglyceride transfer protein (MTP)', 'MTP transfers triglycerides to Apo B-48 during chylomicron assembly in enterocytes. MTP deficiency or Apo B mutations cause abetalipoproteinemia.'),
('91e4b141-7f61-55ba-95e9-352c9453bd48'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Abetalipoproteinemia is due to:', '["Apo C-II deficiency resulting in decreased functional activity","LCAT deficiency accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","LDL receptor deficiency mediated by specific receptor activation","MTP deficiency or Apo B mutation preventing chylomicron/VLDL assembly","LPL deficiency leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'MTP deficiency or Apo B mutation preventing chylomicron/VLDL assembly', 'Abetalipoproteinemia results from MTP deficiency or Apo B mutation, preventing chylomicron and VLDL assembly. Features: acanthocytes, fat malabsorption, retinitis pigmentosa, ataxia.'),
('528b75af-2a28-5432-b5d8-8b4f6ced8f17'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Chylomicron remnant uptake by the liver is mediated by:', '["LPL resulting in decreased functional activity","LDL receptor and LRP (LDL receptor-related protein) binding Apo E","Apo C-II mediated by specific receptor activation","HDL receptor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Scavenger receptor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'LDL receptor and LRP (LDL receptor-related protein) binding Apo E', 'Chylomicron remnants are cleared by the liver via LDL receptor and LRP, which recognize Apo E on the remnant surface. This completes exogenous lipid transport.'),
('aacbe055-44c4-55d6-8976-ebb581aa0f12'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'HDL is synthesized in:', '["Liver only","Intestine only","Both liver and intestine","Adipose tissue only","Pancreas only"]'::jsonb, 'Both liver and intestine', 'Nascent HDL is secreted by both the liver and intestine. It matures by acquiring cholesterol from tissues via ABCA1 and esterification by LCAT.'),
('cdac5e3a-2d6c-57cc-b716-92d2a6fc9012'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'SR-BI in the liver mediates:', '["Bile acid synthesis resulting in decreased functional activity","LDL endocytosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","VLDL secretion mediated by specific receptor activation","Selective uptake of HDL cholesteryl esters","Chylomicron clearance leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Selective uptake of HDL cholesteryl esters', 'SR-BI selectively takes up HDL cholesteryl esters into the liver without degrading the whole HDL particle, completing reverse cholesterol transport.'),
('dc46aa0d-a054-54ef-abaf-687ac1e4fd2d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Which lipid serves as a precursor for steroid hormones?', '["VLDL only","Triglycerides only","Apo B only","LDL only","Cholesterol"]'::jsonb, 'Cholesterol', 'Cholesterol is the precursor for all steroid hormones: glucocorticoids, mineralocorticoids, and sex hormones. LDL supplies cholesterol to steroidogenic tissues via the LDL receptor.'),
('f18b4273-f89e-5cd0-84d5-8bc51f53c3ec'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Hypercholesterolemia can be secondary to:', '["Hypothyroidism","Nephrotic syndrome","Cholestasis","Diabetes","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Secondary hypercholesterolemia occurs in hypothyroidism, nephrotic syndrome, cholestasis, and diabetes. Always exclude secondary causes before diagnosing familial hypercholesterolemia.'),
('daa73f9c-7875-51c4-9103-c5892599e768'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Severe hypertriglyceridemia (>1000 mg/dL) increases risk of:', '["Cardiovascular disease","Stroke","Fatty liver","Pancreatitis","Gallstones"]'::jsonb, 'Pancreatitis', 'Severe hypertriglyceridemia (>1000 mg/dL) is a major risk factor for acute pancreatitis. Treatment includes fibrates, fish oil, and strict lipid-lowering diet.'),
('57ee51f8-421e-5a98-a291-e615a134b1a3'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Plant sterols and stanols lower LDL by:', '["Inhibiting ACAT resulting in decreased functional activity","Increasing bile acid synthesis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Activating LDL receptors mediated by specific receptor activation","Competitively blocking intestinal cholesterol absorption","Inhibiting HMG-CoA reductase leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Competitively blocking intestinal cholesterol absorption', 'Plant sterols/stanols compete with dietary and biliary cholesterol for incorporation into mixed micelles, reducing cholesterol absorption and lowering LDL.'),
('87be2aa4-a372-57c9-8e9b-c6b44d8080aa'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Red yeast rice contains:', '["PCSK9 inhibitors","Monacolin K (a natural statin)","Fibrates","Plant sterols","Fish oil"]'::jsonb, 'Monacolin K (a natural statin)', 'Red yeast rice contains monacolin K, chemically identical to lovastatin, which inhibits HMG-CoA reductase and lowers LDL cholesterol.'),
('64d813b2-1551-5693-9245-9b6c01438fd4'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'The omega-3 index (EPA+DHA in RBC membranes) is:', '["Not clinically useful resulting in decreased functional activity","A marker of cardiovascular risk and dietary omega-3 status","Better than HDL mediated by specific receptor activation","A measure of vitamin D without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A marker of total fat intake leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A marker of cardiovascular risk and dietary omega-3 status', 'The omega-3 index (percent EPA+DHA in RBC membranes) reflects dietary omega-3 status and is associated with lower cardiovascular risk.'),
('1a522c2f-9adc-5999-9b23-e930be56f835'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Lipid profile screening is recommended for adults starting at age:', '["20 years","40 years","60 years","30 years","50 years"]'::jsonb, '20 years', 'Lipid profile screening is recommended for all adults starting at age 20, with earlier screening for those with risk factors or family history of premature CVD.'),
('45042de3-b5ba-5df7-abd9-d5ea2d6c3449'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Statins have pleiotropic effects including:', '["Plaque stabilization","Only cholesterol lowering","Anti-inflammatory","Improved endothelial function","Only triglyceride lowering"]'::jsonb, 'Anti-inflammatory', 'Beyond LDL lowering, statins stabilize plaques, reduce inflammation, improve endothelial function, and reduce thrombosis risk—the pleiotropic effects.'),
('f5fa9588-a15f-5ed2-8eb8-628b33d1d7ee'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Evolocumab and alirocumab are:', '["Statin combinations resulting in decreased functional activity","ACAT inhibitors accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","LPL activators mediated by specific receptor activation","PCSK9 inhibitors that lower LDL dramatically","CETP inhibitors leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'PCSK9 inhibitors that lower LDL dramatically', 'Evolocumab and alirocumab are monoclonal antibodies against PCSK9, preserving LDL receptors and dramatically lowering LDL cholesterol.'),
('38c0c83b-3880-5280-8739-e222acc08bb1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Lomitapide is an inhibitor of:', '["PCSK9 resulting in decreased functional activity","Microsomal triglyceride transfer protein (MTP) for homozygous FH","ACAT mediated by specific receptor activation","HMG-CoA reductase without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CETP leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Microsomal triglyceride transfer protein (MTP) for homozygous FH', 'Lomitapide inhibits MTP, reducing lipoprotein assembly/secretion. It is used as adjunct therapy for homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia.'),
('ac76ad67-7c7d-5e02-9b9d-0205f594f316'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Lipoprotein apheresis is used for:', '["Metabolic syndrome resulting in decreased functional activity","Hypertriglyceridemia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Severe refractory hypercholesterolemia (homozygous FH)","Low HDL without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mild hyperlipidemia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Severe refractory hypercholesterolemia (homozygous FH)', 'Lipoprotein apheresis mechanically removes LDL from plasma, used for homozygous FH and severe refractory hypercholesterolemia when drug therapy is insufficient.'),
('ad5af19a-001b-52f7-a85c-7460c7140fd2'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Blood for lipid profile should be collected after fasting for:', '["24 hours","5 hours","9-12 hours","2-4 hours","1 hour"]'::jsonb, '9-12 hours', 'Lipid profile is collected after 9-12 hours of fasting for accurate triglyceride measurement. Non-fasting samples can be used for cardiovascular risk assessment.'),
('814087ab-6037-5c91-bc46-0684940ce111'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Caveolae are flask-shaped invaginations of the plasma membrane that are rich in which protein?', '["Clathrin","Caveolin","Dynamin","Actin","Tubulin"]'::jsonb, 'Caveolin', 'Caveolae are flask-shaped membrane invaginations rich in caveolin, involved in signal transduction, endocytosis, and lipid regulation.'),
('773755dc-65b2-5bf4-a8f5-e918bc47de50'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Cholesterol in the membrane:', '["Decreases stability","Modulates fluidity","Has no effect","Increases rigidity only","Increases permeability"]'::jsonb, 'Modulates fluidity', 'Cholesterol modulates membrane fluidity: it increases fluidity at low temperatures (prevents tight packing) and decreases fluidity at high temperatures (restricts movement).'),
('3223d732-59cc-5807-a98c-c832a6773511'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Lipids', 'Excess cholesterol accumulation in macrophages forms:', '["Fatty acid micelles","Lipoprotein particles","Triglyceride droplets","Foam cells in atherosclerotic plaques","Bile salt aggregates"]'::jsonb, 'Foam cells in atherosclerotic plaques', 'Macrophages take up oxidized LDL via scavenger receptors, becoming cholesterol-laden foam cells that form the fatty streak, the earliest atherosclerotic lesion.'),
('17cf839d-59a9-59c7-a8b3-34e86a643862'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'The distance between adjacent base pairs in B-DNA is:', '["1.5 Å","5.4 Å","6.8 Å","3.4 Å","2.0 Å"]'::jsonb, '3.4 Å', 'The distance between adjacent base pairs in B-DNA is 3.4 Å (0.34 nm), a fundamental structural parameter of the double helix.'),
('69125832-f832-5acb-abd3-6a45286a8d46'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'One complete turn of B-DNA contains how many base pairs?', '["10","15","11","8","12"]'::jsonb, '10', 'One complete turn of B-DNA contains approximately 10 base pairs, spanning 34 Å.'),
('92486bc2-18f1-5f15-82c0-6c06e2ecae77'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'Guanine forms how many hydrogen bonds with cytosine?', '["1","2","3","4","0"]'::jsonb, '3', 'Guanine forms 3 hydrogen bonds with cytosine, making G-C pairs stronger than A-T pairs (2 bonds).'),
('2bb0f9ba-c619-5986-8443-252a1c3d0466'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'The major groove of B-DNA is described as:', '["Absent","Wide and shallow","Narrow and shallow","Narrow and deep","Wide and deep"]'::jsonb, 'Wide and deep', 'The major groove of B-DNA is wide and deep, providing more space for protein-DNA interactions and sequence-specific recognition by transcription factors.'),
('26103749-d68a-5ed2-a055-a1a22f9e1fba'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'Z-DNA is characterized as:', '["Left-handed helix with zigzag backbone","Single-stranded","Most common form in cells","Right-handed helix","Triple helix"]'::jsonb, 'Left-handed helix with zigzag backbone', 'Z-DNA is a left-handed helix with a zigzag backbone, formed by alternating purine-pyrimidine sequences (especially GC repeats) under high salt or negative superhelical tension.'),
('8ddc4da5-991d-57ef-be94-dd23d8b1eeed'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'A nucleoside consists of:', '["Base + sugar + phosphate","Base + sugar","Sugar + phosphate","Base + phosphate","Sugar + amino acid"]'::jsonb, 'Base + sugar', 'A nucleoside consists of a nitrogenous base linked to a pentose sugar (ribose or deoxyribose) via an N-glycosidic bond. Adding one or more phosphate groups creates a nucleotide.'),
('f339af00-0d10-5531-b063-a50462b2093a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'Phosphodiester bonds in DNA connect:', '["3'' OH to 5'' phosphate of adjacent nucleotide","5'' phosphate to 5'' phosphate","2'' OH to 5'' phosphate","5'' OH to 3'' phosphate","3'' OH to 5'' OH"]'::jsonb, '3'' OH to 5'' phosphate of adjacent nucleotide', 'Phosphodiester bonds connect the 3'' hydroxyl group of one nucleotide to the 5'' phosphate group of the next, forming the sugar-phosphate backbone of DNA.'),
('1dd7f8a5-ad07-52e7-a3f0-2770f42690b0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'The pyrimidine bases found in DNA are:', '["Uracil and cytosine","Cytosine and guanine","Adenine and guanine","Adenine and thymine","Cytosine and thymine"]'::jsonb, 'Cytosine and thymine', 'Cytosine and thymine are the pyrimidine bases found in DNA. Thymine is replaced by uracil in RNA.'),
('d2bccf33-c3b7-5b17-a782-f8c7e739a918'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'The purine bases found in DNA are:', '["Adenine and guanine","Uracil and adenine","Cytosine and thymine","Adenine and thymine","Guanine and cytosine"]'::jsonb, 'Adenine and guanine', 'Adenine and guanine are the purine bases found in both DNA and RNA.'),
('05f70c61-e29e-562b-b618-ee2629af91df'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'Which statement correctly describes the UV absorption properties of DNA?', '["Light scattering by DNA","Increased UV absorbance at 260 nm when DNA denatures","Decreased UV absorbance on denaturation","Fluorescence of DNA","Increased absorbance at 280 nm"]'::jsonb, 'Increased UV absorbance at 260 nm when DNA denatures', 'Denatured single-stranded DNA absorbs more ultraviolet light at 260 nm than native double-stranded DNA because unpaired bases become exposed (hyperchromic effect); this property is used to monitor DNA melting and renaturation.'),
('72812174-fd15-57c1-b888-a04854dd84fe'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'A-DNA differs from B-DNA in having:', '["Grooves resulting in decreased functional activity","Single strand accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","11 base pairs per turn with tilted bases","More hydration without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Left-handed helix leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '11 base pairs per turn with tilted bases', 'A-DNA has 11 base pairs per turn with tilted bases and is favored under dehydrating conditions. It is wider and shorter than B-DNA.'),
('a4ef84a3-95ed-57d2-9668-4b6b79beba96'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'The N-glycosidic bond in nucleotides links:', '["Sugar to sugar","Base to phosphate","Sugar C5'' to phosphate","Base N1/N9 to sugar C1''","Base C1 to sugar N1/N9"]'::jsonb, 'Base N1/N9 to sugar C1''', 'The N-glycosidic bond links the anomeric carbon (C1'') of the pentose sugar to the N1 of a pyrimidine or N9 of a purine base. Spontaneous hydrolysis of this bond produces an apurinic/apyrimidinic (AP) site.'),
('a59b83d5-afb3-554a-a6bb-7be2411a9a4b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'RNA differs from DNA in having:', '["Ribose sugar and uracil instead of thymine","Phosphate backbone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Double helix structure mediated by specific receptor activation","Bases without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Deoxyribose sugar leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ribose sugar and uracil instead of thymine', 'RNA contains ribose with a 2'' hydroxyl group, making it more reactive and less stable than DNA.'),
('909d8d40-965f-56c2-a8ba-e34b7024bf69'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'The most abundant type of RNA in a cell is:', '["RRNA","TRNA","SnRNA","MRNA","MiRNA"]'::jsonb, 'RRNA', 'Ribosomal RNA (rRNA) makes up about 80% of total cellular RNA and is the most abundant type, serving as the structural and catalytic core of ribosomes.'),
('87d7d6dc-d0d5-597d-a2a0-5ca5e44fffa2'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'Transfer RNA (tRNA) functions to:', '["Regulate transcription","Catalyze peptide bond formation","Carry genetic information","Carry specific amino acids to the ribosome","Form ribosomal subunits"]'::jsonb, 'Carry specific amino acids to the ribosome', 'Transfer RNA (tRNA) carries specific amino acids to the ribosome during translation, matching its anticodon to the mRNA codon.'),
('6bba43f4-ec69-5772-bbd7-329f89df420f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'The 5.8S rRNA is associated with which ribosomal subunit in eukaryotes?', '["30S small subunit","60S large subunit","40S small subunit","50S large subunit","80S ribosome"]'::jsonb, '60S large subunit', 'In eukaryotes, 5.8S rRNA is part of the 60S large ribosomal subunit, where it associates with 28S rRNA in the peptidyl transferase center.'),
('1b58ecbb-64fd-5996-bac1-d7dd771a5ba6'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'Small nuclear RNAs (snRNAs) are primarily involved in:', '["Protein synthesis","Transcription initiation","RNA degradation","Splicing of pre-mRNA","DNA replication"]'::jsonb, 'Splicing of pre-mRNA', 'Small nuclear RNAs (snRNAs) combine with proteins to form snRNPs, the catalytic components of the spliceosome for pre-mRNA splicing.'),
('97601a9f-7957-53ce-9cd4-d5dca19c623d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'Small interfering RNAs (siRNAs) are involved in:', '["Protein synthesis resulting in decreased functional activity","RNA interference pathway for gene silencing","DNA replication mediated by specific receptor activation","Transcription without affecting the primary tissue pathway","RNA splicing leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'RNA interference pathway for gene silencing', 'Small interfering RNAs (siRNAs) guide the RISC complex to degrade complementary mRNA in the RNA interference (RNAi) pathway.'),
('ca2533f3-6d2b-5e31-907c-900f1618d0b1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'Piwi-interacting RNAs (piRNAs) are mainly involved in:', '["Transcription resulting in decreased functional activity","MRNA splicing accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","DNA repair mediated by specific receptor activation","Silencing transposable elements in germ cells","RRNA processing leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Silencing transposable elements in germ cells', 'Piwi-interacting RNAs (piRNAs) silence transposable elements in germ cells, protecting genome integrity during gametogenesis.'),
('5c584d7f-25ad-57b8-b482-04779b806ffa'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'The cloverleaf structure is characteristic of:', '["MiRNA","MRNA","SnRNA","RRNA","TRNA"]'::jsonb, 'TRNA', 'tRNA folds into a cloverleaf secondary structure with four arms: the acceptor stem, D-loop, anticodon loop, and T-Psi-C loop. This L-shaped structure is essential for codon-anticodon recognition during translation.'),
('2646ca51-7dad-5a21-a339-11fac16f118f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'The anticodon is located on which part of tRNA?', '["D-loop","Acceptor stem","T-Psi-C loop","Variable loop","Anticodon loop"]'::jsonb, 'Anticodon loop', 'The anticodon is a triplet of bases in the anticodon loop of tRNA that base-pairs with the complementary mRNA codon, ensuring the correct amino acid is added during translation.'),
('0bad7624-722f-5758-884f-dda9521cc277'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'The acceptor stem of tRNA binds to:', '["MRNA codon resulting in decreased functional activity","A specific amino acid (at the 3'' CCA end)","Aminoacyl-tRNA synthetase mediated by specific receptor activation","Ribosomal RNA without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Translation factors leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A specific amino acid (at the 3'' CCA end)', 'The acceptor stem ends in the 3'' CCA sequence, and the aminoacyl-tRNA synthetase attaches the specific amino acid to the 3'' hydroxyl of this terminal adenosine, forming an aminoacyl-tRNA.'),
('00ad14f8-2186-537e-b33c-269269051071'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'DNA replication in prokaryotes begins at:', '["Enhancers resulting in decreased functional activity","OriC (origin of replication)","Promoters mediated by specific receptor activation","Centromeres without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Telomeres leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'OriC (origin of replication)', 'DNA replication in E. coli initiates at oriC, a 245 bp AT-rich origin where the initiator protein DnaA binds and melts the duplex to allow helicase loading. Eukaryotes have multiple origins of replication per chromosome.'),
('3f276290-cb42-5df9-9264-954285dfa3d2'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'The replication fork is unwound by:', '["Topoisomerase","DNA helicase (DnaB)","Primase","DNA polymerase","DNA ligase"]'::jsonb, 'DNA helicase (DnaB)', 'Helicase unwinds the DNA double helix by breaking hydrogen bonds between base pairs during replication.'),
('788d2710-4646-53ea-867e-4888cd2532c4'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'Topoisomerase I relieves supercoiling by:', '["Using ATP to introduce negative supercoils","Cutting both strands","Making a single-strand nick and resealing it","Denaturing DNA","Making double-strand breaks"]'::jsonb, 'Making a single-strand nick and resealing it', 'Topoisomerases relieve torsional strain ahead of the replication fork. Type I makes transient single-strand breaks; Type II (DNA gyrase) makes double-strand breaks. Quinolones inhibit DNA gyrase.'),
('d851b2b2-b315-515f-b72f-0c2df0601f85'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'DNA gyrase (Topoisomerase II) in prokaryotes:', '["Unwinds DNA","Makes single-strand breaks","Synthesizes primers","Introduces negative supercoils using ATP","Relaxes positive supercoils only"]'::jsonb, 'Introduces negative supercoils using ATP', 'DNA gyrase (a type II topoisomerase) uses ATP hydrolysis to introduce negative supercoils ahead of the replication fork. It is the target of antibiotics such as the quinolones (e.g., ciprofloxacin).'),
('17a131a0-d98b-55d9-bc57-81f49a2e8024'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'Okazaki fragments are synthesized on the:', '["Coding strand","Leading strand","Both strands","Template strand","Lagging strand"]'::jsonb, 'Lagging strand', 'Okazaki fragments are synthesized on the lagging strand in the 5'' to 3'' direction, away from the replication fork.'),
('6b18ac66-cdbf-5f37-a3a8-5b0ef4d9db33'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'The leading strand is synthesized:', '["Continuously in the 5'' to 3'' direction","In the 3'' to 5'' direction","In fragments","Discontinuously","Without primers"]'::jsonb, 'Continuously in the 5'' to 3'' direction', 'The leading strand is synthesized continuously in the 5'' to 3'' direction toward the replication fork, requiring only one RNA primer.'),
('2af9a1d1-ec9b-5ed7-8830-059da0df491e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'In E. coli, the initiator protein that binds oriC is:', '["SSB","DnaC","DnaA","DnaB","DnaG"]'::jsonb, 'DnaA', 'DnaA is the initiator protein that binds DnaA-box repeats in oriC, causing local unwinding of the AT-rich region. It then recruits DnaB (helicase) with the help of DnaC.'),
('89faef56-9860-5e6c-ac7c-6d855ef05e27'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'The replisome includes all EXCEPT:', '["DNA polymerase","Helicase","SSB proteins","RNA polymerase","Primase"]'::jsonb, 'RNA polymerase', 'The replisome is the protein complex at the replication fork, comprising DNA polymerase III holoenzyme, DnaB helicase, DnaG primase, and single-stranded DNA binding proteins. RNA polymerase is involved in transcription, not replication.'),
('276878ec-d4e6-5c87-a882-cc9f6d6f3bf4'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'The MCM (minichromosome maintenance) complex functions as:', '["Clamp loader resulting in decreased functional activity","DNA polymerase accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The replicative helicase in eukaryotes","Ligase without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Primase leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The replicative helicase in eukaryotes', 'Helicase unwinds the DNA double helix by breaking hydrogen bonds between base pairs during replication.'),
('4b81734c-1ea4-5f8c-816d-9f3bc1766359'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'DNA polymerase alpha in eukaryotes:', '["Replicates mitochondria","Synthesizes RNA-DNA primers for both strands","Repairs DNA","Replicates the leading strand","Replicates the lagging strand"]'::jsonb, 'Synthesizes RNA-DNA primers for both strands', 'RNA primers are synthesized by primase to provide the 3''-OH group required for DNA polymerase to begin synthesis.'),
('4ede8341-32ae-5d54-97be-eb777c83cb71'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'DNA polymerase delta in eukaryotes:', '["Transcribes genes","Replicates the lagging strand","Replicates the leading strand","Repairs mismatches","Replicates mitochondria"]'::jsonb, 'Replicates the lagging strand', 'DNA polymerase delta in eukaryotes primarily replicates the lagging strand and participates in DNA repair processes.'),
('47ecfbb4-1dc4-5941-b181-83fed53809d5'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'Which DNA strand is synthesized continuously?', '["Replicates mitochondria","Replicates the lagging strand","Replicates the leading strand","Removes primers","Synthesizes primers"]'::jsonb, 'Replicates the leading strand', 'The leading strand is synthesized continuously in the 5'' to 3'' direction toward the replication fork, requiring only a single RNA primer at the origin.'),
('fbbd1cc9-bd88-5e7b-bc09-b163742aed9b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'DNA polymerase gamma is responsible for:', '["Replication of mitochondrial DNA","Translesion synthesis","Nuclear DNA replication","Base excision repair","Nucleotide excision repair"]'::jsonb, 'Replication of mitochondrial DNA', 'DNA polymerase gamma is the mitochondrial DNA polymerase responsible for replication and repair of mitochondrial DNA.'),
('7f3beb6b-5767-5641-a1f1-6b000607c857'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'PCNA (proliferating cell nuclear antigen) functions as:', '["Helicase resulting in decreased functional activity","The eukaryotic sliding clamp for DNA polymerases","Primase mediated by specific receptor activation","Nuclease without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ligase leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The eukaryotic sliding clamp for DNA polymerases', 'DNA polymerase synthesizes new DNA strands in the 5'' to 3'' direction using the parental strand as template.'),
('7d3ac1e1-5cf2-5a67-97fa-8f9756725673'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'The end-replication problem refers to:', '["Primer removal issues on the leading strand","Inability to fully replicate the 3'' ends of linear chromosomes","Inability to replicate the 5'' ends","Supercoiling at replication forks","Mutation accumulation"]'::jsonb, 'Inability to fully replicate the 3'' ends of linear chromosomes', 'Because DNA polymerase cannot fill the gap left after removal of the RNA primer at the 5'' end of the lagging strand, each round of replication shortens the chromosome ends. Telomerase extends these ends to prevent loss of genetic material.'),
('859e4f0a-0b14-5580-a4b9-eb9e5f7d7154'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'Telomerase is a:', '["Ligase resulting in decreased functional activity","DNA polymerase accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Helicase mediated by specific receptor activation","Reverse transcriptase that extends telomeres","RNA polymerase leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Reverse transcriptase that extends telomeres', 'Telomerase is a reverse transcriptase that extends telomeres using its internal RNA template (TERC). It is active in germ cells, stem cells, and most cancer cells but inactive in most somatic cells.'),
('6ad20522-a786-5974-aa7d-6a19285a617e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'The TERC component of telomerase:', '["Repairs damaged DNA","Provides the RNA template for telomere synthesis","Provides the catalytic reverse transcriptase activity","Binds to telomeres","Extends telomeres"]'::jsonb, 'Provides the RNA template for telomere synthesis', 'TERC (telomerase RNA component) provides the RNA template (AAUCCC) that reverse transcriptase uses to add TTAGGG repeats to telomere ends.'),
('9519a6ec-a31e-52df-81db-b7e8ccb53ee0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'The TERT component of telomerase:', '["Provides the reverse transcriptase catalytic activity","Binds to telomeres accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Provides the RNA template mediated by specific receptor activation","Repairs DNA without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Regulates cell cycle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Provides the reverse transcriptase catalytic activity', 'TERT (telomerase reverse transcriptase) is the catalytic protein subunit of telomerase that synthesizes DNA using the TERC RNA template.'),
('acc5ff0d-7c9d-5c11-9e20-5f71b93c6f42'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'The 3'' to 5'' exonuclease activity of DNA polymerase III provides:', '["Primer removal","DNA unwinding","DNA ligation","DNA synthesis","Proofreading function"]'::jsonb, 'Proofreading function', 'The 3'' to 5'' exonuclease activity of DNA polymerase III provides proofreading, removing misincorporated nucleotides for high-fidelity replication.'),
('fb0ff156-da4a-5340-b53c-789d7475a561'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'The leading strand template is read:', '["Both directions","5'' to 3''","Random direction","3'' to 5''","Circular direction"]'::jsonb, '3'' to 5''', 'DNA polymerase synthesizes new DNA only in the 5'' to 3'' direction, so it must read the template strand in the 3'' to 5'' direction.'),
('95825f98-3c0a-534a-b4d3-ad700952d8a8'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'The lagging strand template is read:', '["Circular direction","Random direction","3'' to 5''","Both directions","5'' to 3''"]'::jsonb, '3'' to 5''', 'The lagging strand template is also read 3'' to 5'', but synthesis proceeds away from the fork in short 5'' to 3'' Okazaki fragments, each primed separately.'),
('c606dc37-296b-5e12-9e94-4718c598d9d6'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'In E. coli, the transcription of the lagging strand template requires:', '["Multiple RNA primers","Only DNA primers","Only one RNA primer","Primers","Telomerase"]'::jsonb, 'Multiple RNA primers', 'RNA primers are synthesized by primase to provide the 3''-OH group required for DNA polymerase to begin synthesis.'),
('466d142e-7efb-5ca5-8d84-a4b94aa535bf'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'Transcription is the process of:', '["Synthesizing DNA from RNA","Repairing DNA","Replicating DNA","Synthesizing protein from mRNA","Synthesizing RNA from a DNA template"]'::jsonb, 'Synthesizing RNA from a DNA template', 'Transcription is the synthesis of RNA from a DNA template, catalyzed by RNA polymerase, which reads the template (antisense) strand in the 3'' to 5'' direction to produce a 5'' to 3'' RNA transcript.'),
('f3c0a224-3d83-51c2-85ae-1bcdb7b5b2a4'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'RNA polymerase binds to which specific DNA sequence?', '["Centromere","Origin of replication","Enhancer","Telomere","Promoter"]'::jsonb, 'Promoter', 'The promoter region contains specific sequences (TATA box, CAAT box) where RNA polymerase binds to initiate transcription.'),
('cc06baf7-0a3a-5124-a47e-5998f9d73c50'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'The -10 box (Pribnow box) in prokaryotic promoters has the consensus sequence:', '["TATAAT","TTGACA","CAAT","AATATT","GGGCCC"]'::jsonb, 'TATAAT', 'The -10 box (Pribnow box) has the consensus TATAAT and is the site where RNA polymerase begins to unwind DNA for transcription initiation.'),
('ea87f0fd-e8d4-5dd7-8ad1-86182a25ca02'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'The -35 box in prokaryotic promoters has the consensus sequence:', '["TAATAA","GGGCGG","TTGACA","CAAT","TATAAT"]'::jsonb, 'TTGACA', 'The -35 box has the consensus TTGACA and is recognized by sigma factor for promoter binding in prokaryotic transcription.'),
('25f1446d-7f90-5c89-b0cd-5f836f616d70'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'Sigma factor in prokaryotes is required for:', '["RNA processing resulting in decreased functional activity","Promoter recognition by RNA polymerase","Elongation mediated by specific receptor activation","Termination without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Proofreading leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Promoter recognition by RNA polymerase', 'The promoter region contains specific sequences (TATA box, CAAT box) where RNA polymerase binds to initiate transcription.'),
('db778309-cc38-5b53-a138-da03d48a83f3'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'Which of the following is transcribed by RNA polymerase III?', '["TRNA and 5S rRNA","MRNA and most snRNAs","28S and 18S rRNA","All protein-coding genes","Mitochondrial DNA only"]'::jsonb, 'TRNA and 5S rRNA', 'RNA polymerase III transcribes tRNA, 5S rRNA, and other small non-coding RNAs. RNA polymerase I transcribes the 28S, 18S, and 5.8S rRNA genes, and RNA polymerase II transcribes mRNA and most snRNAs.'),
('6c5689fc-3373-54c7-aaa3-e9e75ee28759'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'Rho-independent termination in prokaryotes involves:', '["Poly-A tail resulting in decreased functional activity","A GC-rich hairpin followed by aU-rich sequence","Sigma factor mediated by specific receptor activation","TFIID without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Rho helicase leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A GC-rich hairpin followed by aU-rich sequence', 'In rho-independent termination, a GC-rich hairpin in the nascent RNA pauses RNA polymerase, and the downstream U-rich sequence forms weak rU-dA base pairs with the template, promoting transcript release.'),
('4eb26a11-1d8c-5ef1-8d2a-5c093e832d29'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'The 5'' cap added to eukaryotic mRNA is:', '["Poly-A resulting in decreased functional activity","7-Methylguanosine connected by 5''-5'' triphosphate linkage","Methylated adenine mediated by specific receptor activation","7-Methyladenosine without affecting the primary tissue pathway","3-Methylguanosine leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '7-Methylguanosine connected by 5''-5'' triphosphate linkage', 'The 5'' cap of eukaryotic mRNA is 7-methylguanosine connected by a 5''-5'' triphosphate linkage, protecting mRNA from degradation and aiding ribosome binding.'),
('8cce7201-72c0-5a44-a163-3c4b6f459f86'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'The poly-A tail of eukaryotic mRNA:', '["Inhibits translation","Is added during transcription by RNA polymerase","Increases mRNA stability and facilitates translation","Is found only on rRNA","Is removed before translation"]'::jsonb, 'Increases mRNA stability and facilitates translation', 'The poly-A tail increases mRNA stability, protects from exonuclease degradation, and facilitates translation initiation through PABP-eIF4G interaction.'),
('7c549428-3f5e-5954-947f-8fc6d8009edb'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'What complex removes introns from pre-mRNA?', '["The spliceosome (composed of snRNPs)","RNA polymerase accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ribosome mediated by specific receptor activation","DNA polymerase without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ligase leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The spliceosome (composed of snRNPs)', 'Introns are removed from pre-mRNA by the spliceosome, a dynamic complex of five snRNPs (U1, U2, U4, U5, U6) that catalyzes two transesterification reactions to ligate exons.'),
('914e3a98-ed62-56d9-8157-f04cd0db8c87'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'The branch point in pre-mRNA splicing contains which conserved nucleotide?', '["U (uracil)","G (guanine)","T (thymine)","C (cytosine)","A (adenine)"]'::jsonb, 'A (adenine)', 'The branch point in pre-mRNA splicing contains a conserved adenine residue that forms the lariat intermediate during intron excision.'),
('7e19c2eb-714a-5640-864c-b663ffe09c17'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'Alternative splicing allows:', '["Degradation of mRNA resulting in decreased functional activity","Generation of multiple protein isoforms from a single gene","Polyadenylation mediated by specific receptor activation","Removal of exons without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Addition of introns leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Generation of multiple protein isoforms from a single gene', 'Alternative splicing allows a single gene to produce multiple protein isoforms by including or excluding different exons.'),
('34117042-8401-586c-a55f-7785180fc63b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'The 5'' splice site (donor site) consensus sequence is:', '["GU","AU","AG","GC","GA"]'::jsonb, 'GU', 'The 5'' splice site (donor site) consensus sequence is GU, where the spliceosome cleaves the 5'' end of the intron.'),
('fc420b9a-53bd-5222-acc4-2afa303d8ecc'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'The 3'' splice site (acceptor site) consensus sequence is:', '["AU","GA","GU","AG","AC"]'::jsonb, 'AG', 'The 3'' splice site (acceptor site) consensus sequence is AG, where the spliceosome joins the downstream exon.'),
('d81d33b2-c612-5e0b-9e52-e68b69f186f7'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'The transcription bubble is:', '["A type of RNA secondary structure resulting in decreased functional activity","A structure in the ribosome accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A locally opened region of DNA where RNA synthesis occurs","A bubble of intron RNA without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A protein complex leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A locally opened region of DNA where RNA synthesis occurs', 'During transcription, RNA polymerase unwinds about 17 base pairs of DNA ahead of the growing RNA chain, creating a localized single-stranded region (the transcription bubble) where complementary base pairing with the template strand occurs.'),
('3fae9f55-8167-5328-9cde-9be8263c5b41'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'The CTD of RNA polymerase II is phosphorylated on Ser5 by:', '["RNA polymerase itself","TFIIH (CDK7)","CK2","MAP kinase","P-TEFb (CDK9)"]'::jsonb, 'TFIIH (CDK7)', 'TFIIH (CDK7) phosphorylates Ser5 of the RNA polymerase II CTD during transcription initiation, facilitating promoter clearance and 5'' capping.'),
('22a7bc7f-6872-534f-81ce-55b0c70e00d1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'The genetic code is described as:', '["Variable-length resulting in decreased functional activity","Overlapping accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Doublet mediated by specific receptor activation","Triplet, degenerate, nearly universal, and non-overlapping","Single-letter leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Triplet, degenerate, nearly universal, and non-overlapping', 'The genetic code is a triplet code (3 nucleotides per codon), degenerate (most amino acids have multiple codons), nearly universal, and non-overlapping.'),
('ee5c3606-2d00-5da6-aeb1-045dea5b96ad'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'How many codons encode the 20 standard amino acids?', '["20","64","3","60","61"]'::jsonb, '61', '61 codons encode the 20 standard amino acids; the remaining 3 codons are stop codons (UAA, UAG, UGA).'),
('766b4a7a-0a34-56fc-9b37-9e479c290f00'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'The eukaryotic ribosome has a sedimentation coefficient of:', '["60S","80S","70S","50S","40S"]'::jsonb, '80S', 'Eukaryotic ribosomes sediment at 80S and consist of a 40S small subunit (18S rRNA plus 33 proteins) and a 60S large subunit (5S, 5.8S, and 28S rRNAs plus 49 proteins). Prokaryotic ribosomes are 70S (30S plus 50S).'),
('8f8d8149-d255-57ba-aae0-3262d221e149'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'The peptidyl transferase activity of the ribosome resides in:', '["16S rRNA resulting in decreased functional activity","Ribosomal proteins accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","TRNA mediated by specific receptor activation","23S rRNA (prokaryotes) / 28S rRNA (eukaryotes)","MRNA leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '23S rRNA (prokaryotes) / 28S rRNA (eukaryotes)', 'Peptidyl transferase is a ribozyme: the catalytic center for peptide bond formation resides in the 23S rRNA of the prokaryotic 50S subunit (28S rRNA in the eukaryotic 60S subunit), not in ribosomal proteins.'),
('b0fbe4b6-1125-5493-8129-1f174bfb6219'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'How many aminoacyl-tRNA synthetases are there in most organisms?', '["20 (one per amino acid)","1 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","61 mediated by specific receptor activation","64 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","4 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '20 (one per amino acid)', 'There are 20 aminoacyl-tRNA synthetases, one for each amino acid, that charge tRNAs with their correct amino acids for translation.'),
('153b761a-54c1-57d5-9150-bd156c9c02f4'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'EF-Tu in prokaryotes delivers:', '["Release factors resulting in decreased functional activity","Aminoacyl-tRNA to the A site of the ribosome","Peptidyl-tRNA to the P site mediated by specific receptor activation","MRNA to the ribosome without affecting the primary tissue pathway","RRNA leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Aminoacyl-tRNA to the A site of the ribosome', 'EF-Tu, a GTPase, delivers aminoacyl-tRNA to the ribosomal A site. Correct codon-anticodon pairing triggers GTP hydrolysis, after which EF-Ts recycles EF-Tu by exchanging GDP for GTP.'),
('21d935b7-9346-5cdd-bc1e-6222fcaa666c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'EF-G in prokaryotes functions in:', '["Peptide bond formation resulting in decreased functional activity","Translocation of the ribosome along mRNA","Termination mediated by specific receptor activation","Aminoacyl-tRNA binding without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Initiation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Translocation of the ribosome along mRNA', 'EF-G catalyzes translocation: it moves the ribosome one codon along the mRNA and shifts the peptidyl-tRNA from the A site to the P site, consuming one GTP.'),
('9b74d337-141c-5033-8245-b73890d0aa62'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'IF-1, IF-2, and IF-3 are involved in:', '["Ribosome recycling resulting in decreased functional activity","Initiation of translation in prokaryotes","Elongation mediated by specific receptor activation","Initiation in eukaryotes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Termination leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Initiation of translation in prokaryotes', 'IF-3 binds the 30S subunit and prevents premature subunit joining, IF-2 (a GTPase) delivers fMet-tRNA to the P site, and IF-1 occupies the A site during prokaryotic translation initiation.'),
('ac4f2593-c9b2-5a37-8b00-bf4fea7373da'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'The initiator tRNA in prokaryotes carries:', '["Methionine","N-Formyltryptophan","Formylmethionine (fMet)","Valine","Formylglycine"]'::jsonb, 'Formylmethionine (fMet)', 'In prokaryotes, the initiator tRNA carries formylmethionine (fMet), the first amino acid incorporated during translation.'),
('0eb23abc-b850-50f6-9e40-d774f422cd43'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'The Kozak sequence in eukaryotes:', '["Is the 5'' cap resulting in decreased functional activity","Is the 3'' UTR accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Is the stop codon mediated by specific receptor activation","Is the poly-A signal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Surrounds the start codon and enhances translation initiation"]'::jsonb, 'Surrounds the start codon and enhances translation initiation', 'The Kozak sequence surrounds the start codon (AUG) in eukaryotic mRNA and enhances translation initiation by facilitating ribosome recognition.'),
('648ea6a3-1951-58b4-bc91-e12be556ad6a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'EIF4E binds to:', '["The A site","The small ribosomal subunit","The P site","The 3'' poly-A tail","The 5'' cap of eukaryotic mRNA"]'::jsonb, 'The 5'' cap of eukaryotic mRNA', 'eIF4E, the cap-binding protein of the eIF4F complex, binds the 5'' 7-methylguanosine cap of mRNA and recruits the 40S ribosomal subunit to begin scanning for the start codon.'),
('90866a46-b73a-52d5-b82f-990c725cefe9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'The poly-A binding protein (PABP):', '["Unwinds mRNA resulting in decreased functional activity","Binds the poly-A tail and circularizes mRNA via eIF4G interaction","Binds the P site mediated by specific receptor activation","Binds the A site without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Binds the cap leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Binds the poly-A tail and circularizes mRNA via eIF4G interaction', 'PABP binds the poly-A tail and interacts with eIF4G, which also binds eIF4E on the cap, circularizing the mRNA. This enhances translation initiation and protects the transcript from deadenylation-dependent decay.'),
('7dba1e08-a87e-5c66-9d33-ac137e9c8389'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'Peptide bond formation occurs between:', '["MRNA and tRNA resulting in decreased functional activity","Two free amino acids accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ribosomal proteins mediated by specific receptor activation","Two tRNAs without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Amino group of A-site tRNA and carboxyl group of P-site peptidyl-tRNA"]'::jsonb, 'Amino group of A-site tRNA and carboxyl group of P-site peptidyl-tRNA', 'The nucleophilic alpha-amino group of the aminoacyl-tRNA in the A site attacks the ester bond between the growing polypeptide and the P-site tRNA, forming a new peptide bond in the peptidyl transferase center.'),
('a893e3b8-7b46-5b48-9974-1bf9f1bf7645'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'The E site on the ribosome:', '["Binding site for mRNA","Binding site for release factors","Binding site for peptidyl-tRNA","Exit site for deacylated tRNA before release","Entry site for aminoacyl-tRNA"]'::jsonb, 'Exit site for deacylated tRNA before release', 'The E (exit) site briefly holds the deacylated tRNA after peptide bond formation and translocation, before it is released from the ribosome for recycling.'),
('5e8a69c0-6688-5eba-ab31-bb47ce76cd09'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'EIF2 is involved in:', '["Subunit joining resulting in decreased functional activity","Ribosome scanning accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bringing the initiator tRNA to the 40S subunit","Poly-A binding without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cap binding leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bringing the initiator tRNA to the 40S subunit', 'eIF2 forms a ternary complex with GTP and initiator Met-tRNAi and delivers it to the P site of the 40S subunit. Phosphorylation of eIF2 alpha (e.g., by PERK or PKR) inactivates eIF2B and inhibits translation initiation.'),
('e50c78cb-a5b6-5c7d-b892-835af97da65d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'EF-1 alpha in eukaryotes is the analog of:', '["IF-2","EF-Tu in prokaryotes","EF-G in prokaryotes","EF-Ts in prokaryotes","RF-1"]'::jsonb, 'EF-Tu in prokaryotes', 'EF-1 alpha is the eukaryotic homolog of prokaryotic EF-Tu: both are GTPases that deliver aminoacyl-tRNA to the ribosomal A site.'),
('c78b3db3-9f3c-54da-93ee-b609f6257e5c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'The ribosome recycling factor (RRF) functions to:', '["Release the polypeptide","Elongate the peptide chain","Bind to the A site","Initiate translation","Dissociate ribosomes from mRNA after termination"]'::jsonb, 'Dissociate ribosomes from mRNA after termination', 'After termination, the ribosome recycling factor (RRF) and EF-G dissociate the 70S ribosome into its subunits and release the mRNA, allowing the components to be reused for new rounds of translation.'),
('aec8056d-b4ac-5e8a-a811-63a1528a404e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'A transversion mutation involves:', '["Base deletion resulting in decreased functional activity","A purine replaced by a pyrimidine or vice versa","Base insertion mediated by specific receptor activation","Pyrimidine to pyrimidine change without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Purine to purine change leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A purine replaced by a pyrimidine or vice versa', 'A transversion replaces a purine with a pyrimidine or a pyrimidine with a purine (e.g., A-to-C or G-to-T), whereas a transition substitutes a purine for a purine or a pyrimidine for a pyrimidine (A-to-G or C-to-T).'),
('1e6bb4ab-d614-5c4c-bacb-d9b903198ec6'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'A nonsense mutation:', '["Deletes a gene resulting in decreased functional activity","Has effect accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Changes a codon to a different amino acid mediated by specific receptor activation","Changes a codon to a stop codon, causing premature termination","Causes a frameshift leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Changes a codon to a stop codon, causing premature termination', 'Nonsense mutations introduce a premature stop codon, producing truncated proteins. They usually result in nonfunctional proteins and are often more severe than missense mutations.'),
('7408aa82-4489-5297-8a41-207341848e3c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'A frameshift mutation is caused by:', '["Nonsense mutations resulting in decreased functional activity","Missense mutations accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Silent mutations mediated by specific receptor activation","Single base substitutions without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Insertion or deletion of base pairs not in multiples of 3"]'::jsonb, 'Insertion or deletion of base pairs not in multiples of 3', 'Frameshift mutations result from insertions or deletions not in multiples of 3. They shift the reading frame, typically producing nonfunctional proteins due to premature stop codons.'),
('91cf7f0b-16dc-59b8-8441-d6fed894e80b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'Sickle cell disease is caused by:', '["A deletion resulting in decreased functional activity","A frameshift mutation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A nonsense mutation mediated by specific receptor activation","A silent mutation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A missense mutation in the beta-globin gene (Glu6Val)"]'::jsonb, 'A missense mutation in the beta-globin gene (Glu6Val)', 'Sickle cell anemia results from a single missense mutation (GAG-to-GTG) causing substitution of valine for glutamic acid at position 6 of the beta-globin chain. This makes HbS polymerize when deoxygenated, deforming red cells.'),
('fcc92623-8168-56f9-8956-a3f7bc6242c7'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'Myotonic dystrophy Type 1 is caused by:', '["CAG repeat expansion","Deletion","GAA repeat expansion","CTG repeat expansion in the DMPK gene","CGG repeat expansion"]'::jsonb, 'CTG repeat expansion in the DMPK gene', 'Myotonic dystrophy type 1 is caused by expansion of a CTG trinucleotide repeat in the 3'' untranslated region of the DMPK gene on chromosome 19. The expanded repeat RNA forms toxic foci that sequester splicing regulators.'),
('a7d33442-a659-53b0-bb9f-a0578233d696'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'Base excision repair (BER) is used to repair:', '["Pyrimidine dimers resulting in decreased functional activity","Small chemical modifications of individual bases","Mismatched bases mediated by specific receptor activation","Crosslinks without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Double-strand breaks leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Small chemical modifications of individual bases', 'Base excision repair removes small, non-helix-distorting base lesions (oxidation, deamination, alkylation). A DNA glycosylase first cleaves the N-glycosidic bond, leaving an abasic (AP) site for further processing.'),
('a6fc6d79-0bfb-5be3-b982-c78545a5aaf3'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'AP endonuclease in BER:', '["Seals the nick resulting in decreased functional activity","Removes the damaged base accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cleaves the phosphodiester backbone at the AP site","Unwinds DNA without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inserts new nucleotides leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cleaves the phosphodiester backbone at the AP site', 'After a DNA glycosylase removes the damaged base, AP endonuclease nicks the phosphodiester backbone at the abasic site, creating a 3''-OH terminus for DNA polymerase to fill the gap, which is then sealed by ligase.'),
('c9a934bc-4c1c-57af-84ce-b19d02f2c10f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'Xeroderma pigmentosum (XP) results from defects in:', '["Nucleotide excision repair","Base excision repair","Mismatch repair","Homologous recombination","Non-homologous end joining"]'::jsonb, 'Nucleotide excision repair', 'Xeroderma pigmentosum is caused by mutations in genes of the nucleotide excision repair (NER) pathway, which normally removes UV-induced pyrimidine dimers. Affected patients have extreme photosensitivity and a high risk of skin cancers.'),
('4acb7ef2-6de4-5c63-9e3e-0753abd9942f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'Mismatch repair (MMR) corrects:', '["Double-strand breaks resulting in decreased functional activity","Replication errors such as misincorporated bases","Pyrimidine dimers mediated by specific receptor activation","Deaminated bases without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Alkylated bases leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Replication errors such as misincorporated bases', 'Mismatch repair corrects errors that escape DNA polymerase proofreading, such as base mismatches and small insertion/deletion loops. In E. coli it uses MutS, MutL, and MutH; defects in human homologs (MSH2, MLH1) cause Lynch syndrome.'),
('aed089ca-66a4-59c9-be2e-dfafab6b9f1f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'Double-strand breaks are repaired by:', '["Homologous recombination (HR) and non-homologous end joining (NHEJ)","Direct reversal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mismatch repair mediated by specific receptor activation","Base excision repair without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Nucleotide excision repair leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Homologous recombination (HR) and non-homologous end joining (NHEJ)', 'Double-strand breaks are repaired by homologous recombination (error-free, uses a homologous template in S/G2) or non-homologous end joining (error-prone, direct ligation of ends throughout the cell cycle).'),
('0e5fe91a-3970-549d-a3b2-2d38fe090a24'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'Homologous recombination repair requires:', '["Template and occurs in G1 resulting in decreased functional activity","AP endonuclease accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","DNA glycosylase mediated by specific receptor activation","MutS/MutL without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A homologous template (sister chromatid) and occurs in S/G2 phases"]'::jsonb, 'A homologous template (sister chromatid) and occurs in S/G2 phases', 'Homologous recombination requires a homologous donor sequence, such as a sister chromatid, so it occurs in the S and G2 phases after replication, and it repairs double-strand breaks in an error-free manner.'),
('ac3366b3-6b2a-5583-8e72-028bc88c16f6'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'The MRN complex (MRE11-RAD50-NBS1) is involved in:', '["Nucleotide excision repair","Double-strand break recognition and repair","DNA replication","Base excision repair","Mismatch repair"]'::jsonb, 'Double-strand break recognition and repair', 'The MRN complex (MRE11-RAD50-NBS1) senses double-strand breaks, recruits and activates ATM kinase, and resects DNA ends to generate 3'' single-stranded tails for homologous recombination.'),
('41008c44-39ea-5793-b23e-10fd8daa4d03'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'Ku70/Ku80 heterodimer is involved in:', '["Mismatch repair","Non-homologous end joining (NHEJ)","Homologous recombination","Nucleotide excision repair","Base excision repair"]'::jsonb, 'Non-homologous end joining (NHEJ)', 'The Ku70/Ku80 heterodimer binds the free ends of a double-strand break with high affinity and recruits DNA-PKcs, initiating the error-prone non-homologous end joining (NHEJ) pathway.'),
('8a4943d5-07e3-5c51-87c3-ad0f1aa83f2c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'BRCA1 and BRCA2 are involved in:', '["Nucleotide excision repair resulting in decreased functional activity","Base excision repair accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Homologous recombination repair of double-strand breaks","Non-homologous end joining without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mismatch repair leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Homologous recombination repair of double-strand breaks', 'BRCA1 and BRCA2 are tumor suppressors essential for homologous recombination repair of double-strand breaks. Loss-of-function mutations predispose to breast and ovarian cancer and are targeted by PARP inhibitors.'),
('0ccad8e8-506f-5311-987c-34d04b9823df'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'O6-methylguanine is repaired by:', '["Direct reversal by photolyase","Nucleotide excision repair","O6-methylguanine-DNA methyltransferase (MGMT)","Mismatch repair","Base excision repair"]'::jsonb, 'O6-methylguanine-DNA methyltransferase (MGMT)', 'O6-methylguanine-DNA methyltransferase (MGMT) directly transfers the methyl group from the O6 position of guanine to its own cysteine residue, restoring the normal base. This suicide mechanism is a form of direct reversal repair.'),
('447632eb-9f98-54b8-902b-926d77d6e5c1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'A transition mutation converts:', '["Cytosine to guanine resulting in decreased functional activity","A purine to a pyrimidine accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Guanine to cytosine mediated by specific receptor activation","A purine to another purine or pyrimidine to another pyrimidine","Adenine to thymine leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A purine to another purine or pyrimidine to another pyrimidine', 'A transition is a purine-to-purine (A-to-G) or pyrimidine-to-pyrimidine (C-to-T) substitution. Such changes often arise from tautomeric shifts or deamination and are more common than transversions.'),
('7efa72c9-16f4-5b3c-8677-861e80689ae4'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'A deletion of 2 base pairs in a coding region would cause:', '["No significant change","A silent mutation","A frameshift mutation","A nonsense mutation","A missense mutation"]'::jsonb, 'A frameshift mutation', 'Deleting 2 base pairs changes the reading frame because the deletion is not a multiple of three, causing the ribosome to translate a different sequence downstream and usually encounter a premature stop codon.'),
('6f9bc4c0-61c6-5dbe-ab92-2ea445be8bf2'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'A plasmid vector typically contains:', '["An origin of replication, a selectable marker, and a multiple cloning site","Only antibiotic resistance accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the origin of replication mediated by specific receptor activation","RNA primer without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only the insert DNA leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An origin of replication, a selectable marker, and a multiple cloning site', 'A plasmid vector contains an origin of replication for autonomous replication, a selectable marker (e.g., antibiotic resistance), and a multiple cloning site (MCS) for insert DNA.'),
('69f46e54-faf6-57be-ae1b-7608364d9237'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'Selectable markers in plasmids often include:', '["Origin of replication resulting in decreased functional activity","Promoters accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Insert DNA mediated by specific receptor activation","Antibiotic resistance genes (e.g., ampicillin resistance)","Restriction sites leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Antibiotic resistance genes (e.g., ampicillin resistance)', 'Plasmids are small circular extrachromosomal DNA in bacteria. They replicate independently and carry genes for antibiotic resistance, virulence, or other accessory functions. Used as cloning vectors.'),
('53b559ea-d57a-5902-ba64-7eb53ae5f705'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'Blue-white screening uses:', '["Colorimetric enzyme assay resulting in decreased functional activity","PCR accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","LacZ alpha gene complementation to identify recombinant colonies","Antibiotic resistance without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fluorescence leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'LacZ alpha gene complementation to identify recombinant colonies', 'In blue-white screening, the multiple cloning site lies within the lacZ alpha gene. Insertion of a foreign DNA fragment disrupts lacZ alpha, so complementation with the lacZ omega fragment cannot occur and recombinant colonies remain white, while intact lacZ alpha yields blue colonies on X-gal.'),
('8af4a1f6-26a0-5e5c-9c1d-69e28c2ba025'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'Alkaline phosphatase treatment of vectors:', '["Removes RNA resulting in decreased functional activity","Enhances ligation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Prevents self-ligation by removing 5'' phosphate groups","Denatures DNA without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Increases restriction efficiency leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Prevents self-ligation by removing 5'' phosphate groups', 'Alkaline phosphatase removes 5'' phosphate groups from linearized vector DNA, preventing self-ligation and increasing the proportion of recombinant clones.'),
('df24dd3d-0b27-5e9f-87fb-bf19e0f87610'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'Transformation is:', '["The process of cutting DNA","The transcription of DNA","The copying of DNA","The uptake of foreign DNA by competent bacterial cells","The separation of DNA fragments"]'::jsonb, 'The uptake of foreign DNA by competent bacterial cells', 'Transformation is the uptake of naked DNA from the environment by a competent bacterial cell. Laboratory transformation (e.g., heat shock with calcium chloride or electroporation) is used to introduce recombinant plasmids.'),
('11b35a6c-f8c0-5b32-9d1b-8540d69ce9ad'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'Transduction involves:', '["Direct uptake of DNA","Transfer of DNA via bacteriophages","Viral infection of eukaryotic cells","Mating between bacteria","Fusion of cells"]'::jsonb, 'Transfer of DNA via bacteriophages', 'Transduction is the transfer of bacterial DNA from one cell to another by a bacteriophage, either generalized (packaging of random host DNA) or specialized (transfer of adjacent chromosomal genes).'),
('b3d24747-eb53-58c2-8f0d-3ef177993e4c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'CDNA is synthesized from:', '["Ribosomal RNA resulting in decreased functional activity","Transfer RNA accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Plasmid DNA mediated by specific receptor activation","Genomic DNA without affecting the primary tissue pathway","MRNA template using reverse transcriptase"]'::jsonb, 'MRNA template using reverse transcriptase', 'cDNA is synthesized from mRNA using reverse transcriptase, creating complementary DNA that lacks introns and is used for cloning expressed genes.'),
('44de7ad8-90c6-5b0f-abed-be21eec16c66'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'What is the first step of each PCR cycle?', '["Ligation","Annealing at 55 degrees C","Extension at 72 degrees C","Denaturation at 95 degrees C","Elongation"]'::jsonb, 'Denaturation at 95 degrees C', 'PCR amplifies specific DNA sequences through repeated cycles of denaturation, annealing, and extension.'),
('4068e758-7242-561d-b615-1b8d8282fef8'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'QPCR (quantitative PCR) uses:', '["End-point gel analysis only resulting in decreased functional activity","Fluorescent probes or dyes to measure DNA amplification in real time","Enzyme-linked assay mediated by specific receptor activation","Antibody detection without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Radioactive labeling leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fluorescent probes or dyes to measure DNA amplification in real time', 'Quantitative PCR (qPCR) monitors DNA amplification in real time using fluorescent reporters such as SYBR Green, which intercalates into double-stranded DNA, or TaqMan hydrolysis probes, allowing quantification of the initial amount of template.'),
('026eb100-4a55-5b76-8dc1-d5545e591d6c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'Gel electrophoresis separates DNA fragments based on:', '["Sequence resulting in decreased functional activity","Density accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Charge only mediated by specific receptor activation","Color without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Size (length in base pairs)"]'::jsonb, 'Size (length in base pairs)', 'Gel electrophoresis separates DNA fragments by size (length in base pairs), with smaller fragments migrating faster through the agarose or polyacrylamide matrix.'),
('45b55ae5-e544-506c-8e50-b7fd34dd92ff'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'A genomic library contains:', '["Only regulatory sequences","RNA fragments","CDNA from expressed genes","Fragments of the entire genome of an organism","Only protein-coding sequences"]'::jsonb, 'Fragments of the entire genome of an organism', 'A genomic library contains overlapping cloned DNA fragments that together represent the entire genome of an organism, including introns, regulatory regions, and non-coding DNA, generated by fragmentation and cloning into vectors.'),
('8c29d12c-7117-5c37-9a58-30a94d65be9f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'A cDNA library contains:', '["Only mitochondrial genes resulting in decreased functional activity","Complementary DNA copies of all mRNAs expressed in a cell type","All genomic DNA mediated by specific receptor activation","Only ribosomal genes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only non-coding RNA leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Complementary DNA copies of all mRNAs expressed in a cell type', 'A cDNA library contains complementary DNA copies of all mRNAs expressed in a cell type, representing only the expressed genes.'),
('db6df30e-3b32-5f3e-b257-da240d52d272'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'CRISPR-Cas9 gene editing uses:', '["Restriction enzymes resulting in decreased functional activity","Zinc finger nucleases only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A guide RNA to target Cas9 nuclease to a specific DNA sequence","Meganucleases without affecting the primary tissue pathway","TALENs only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A guide RNA to target Cas9 nuclease to a specific DNA sequence', 'CRISPR-Cas9 uses a guide RNA (gRNA) to direct the Cas9 nuclease to a specific DNA sequence, where it creates a double-strand break for gene editing.'),
('a60c8a88-e555-518c-986b-1c2039ef8cdf'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'Gene therapy using viral vectors often employs:', '["Bacteriophages resulting in decreased functional activity","Plasmids only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Liposomes only mediated by specific receptor activation","Electroporation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Adeno-associated virus (AAV) or lentivirus as delivery vehicles"]'::jsonb, 'Adeno-associated virus (AAV) or lentivirus as delivery vehicles', 'Adeno-associated virus (AAV) and lentivirus are commonly used viral vectors for gene therapy, providing efficient and stable gene delivery.'),
('cbf658e3-7144-572d-962c-ddfa1e1ff380'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'Ex vivo gene therapy involves:', '["Topical application resulting in decreased functional activity","Gene editing in utero accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Direct injection of vectors into the patient mediated by specific receptor activation","Oral gene delivery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Removing cells, modifying them in culture, and re-infusing them"]'::jsonb, 'Removing cells, modifying them in culture, and re-infusing them', 'Ex vivo gene therapy involves removing patient cells, genetically modifying them in culture, and re-infusing them into the patient.'),
('348a794e-993c-5248-9621-66f14ef6fe1b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'DNA methylation in mammals primarily occurs at:', '["CpA dinucleotides","CpG dinucleotides","GpC dinucleotides","GpG dinucleotides","Non-CpG sites"]'::jsonb, 'CpG dinucleotides', 'In mammals, cytosine is methylated to 5-methylcytosine almost exclusively at CpG dinucleotides, catalyzed by DNA methyltransferases (DNMTs). Most CpGs in the genome are methylated, except those in CpG islands.'),
('b639b195-8be4-54a7-9a4c-5657fa911655'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'CpG islands are:', '["Telomeric repeats resulting in decreased functional activity","Regions rich in CpG dinucleotides, often in gene promoters","Centromeric repeats mediated by specific receptor activation","Histone-rich regions without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Regions poor in CpG leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Regions rich in CpG dinucleotides, often in gene promoters', 'CpG islands are regions of high CpG dinucleotide density often found in gene promoters. They are usually unmethylated in normal cells, but their hypermethylation silences genes, as seen in tumor suppressor gene silencing in cancer.'),
('f4804815-2876-5093-ab69-8b4f1daaec2b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'DNMT1 is the:', '["Demethylase resulting in decreased functional activity","Histone methyltransferase accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","DNA repair enzyme mediated by specific receptor activation","Maintenance methyltransferase that copies methylation patterns to the daughter strand","De novo methyltransferase leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Maintenance methyltransferase that copies methylation patterns to the daughter strand', 'DNMT1 is the maintenance methyltransferase that recognizes hemimethylated CpG sites on the parental strand during DNA replication and copies the methylation pattern onto the newly synthesized daughter strand.'),
('19282320-0cbd-5f2c-afe8-206876b6b8a4'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'DNMT3A and DNMT3B are:', '["Maintenance methyltransferases resulting in decreased functional activity","Demethylases accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Histone acetyltransferases mediated by specific receptor activation","De novo methyltransferases that establish methylation patterns","Histone deacetylases leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'De novo methyltransferases that establish methylation patterns', 'DNMT3A and DNMT3B are de novo methyltransferases that establish new DNA methylation patterns at unmethylated CpG sites during embryonic development and cell differentiation.'),
('dfc3b2d3-e6f7-5fc9-b9f6-4776d71543a1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'Histone acetylation is catalyzed by:', '["Histone deacetylases (HDACs)","Histone acetyltransferases (HATs)","DNA methyltransferases","Topoisomerases","Histone methyltransferases"]'::jsonb, 'Histone acetyltransferases (HATs)', 'Histone acetyltransferases (HATs) add acetyl groups to lysine residues of histone tails. Acetylation neutralizes the positive charge of lysine, weakening histone-DNA interactions and relaxing chromatin to promote transcription.'),
('4aa8fa0b-e3f2-5d3e-baec-badffd7d7fe9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'Histone deacetylation is catalyzed by:', '["Histone acetyltransferases (HATs)","DNA methyltransferases","Histone methyltransferases","Demethylases","Histone deacetylases (HDACs)"]'::jsonb, 'Histone deacetylases (HDACs)', 'Histone deacetylases (HDACs) remove acetyl groups from histone lysines, restoring positive charge and increasing histone-DNA interaction, which condenses chromatin and generally represses gene transcription.'),
('ba8f2d14-a032-5dd3-b29d-1294be27fb8d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'Histone acetylation generally:', '["Has effect on gene expression","Inhibits DNA replication","Relaxes chromatin structure and promotes gene transcription","Condenses chromatin and represses transcription","Promotes DNA methylation"]'::jsonb, 'Relaxes chromatin structure and promotes gene transcription', 'Histone acetylation by HATs relaxes chromatin structure by neutralizing positive charges on lysine residues, promoting gene transcription.'),
('452d1d4b-fb92-5763-babe-bac0eff62c33'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'Histone methylation can:', '["Only affect DNA replication resulting in decreased functional activity","Activate transcription accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Either activate or repress transcription depending on the specific residue and methylation state","Repress transcription without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only affect RNA splicing leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Either activate or repress transcription depending on the specific residue and methylation state', 'Histone methylation can either activate or repress transcription depending on the residue modified and the degree of methylation. For example, H3K4me3 activates transcription while H3K9me3 and H3K27me3 repress it.'),
('795a2cf8-eb97-5cc7-8a6e-4fae98060734'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'H3K9me3 (trimethylation of histone H3 lysine 9) is associated with:', '["Constitutive heterochromatin and gene repression","Active promoters accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","DNA repair sites mediated by specific receptor activation","Transcribed regions without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Enhancers leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Constitutive heterochromatin and gene repression', 'H3K9me3 is a repressive mark associated with constitutive heterochromatin, such as centromeres, telomeres, and repetitive DNA. It recruits HP1 proteins, promoting heterochromatin formation and gene silencing.'),
('afeb5f9d-cfa9-5eea-a5cc-59e0cb86daae'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'The histone code hypothesis states that:', '["Only DNA methylation regulates gene expression resulting in decreased functional activity","Histones are not modified accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Histones code for amino acids mediated by specific receptor activation","Histone modifications are irreversible without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Specific combinations of histone modifications regulate chromatin function and gene expression"]'::jsonb, 'Specific combinations of histone modifications regulate chromatin function and gene expression', 'The histone code hypothesis proposes that specific combinations of post-translational modifications (acetylation, methylation, phosphorylation) on histone tails are read by effector proteins to regulate chromatin structure and gene expression.'),
('ebec80e8-367e-51f3-8ec6-2a39a8b3ea60'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'Genomic imprinting refers to:', '["Parent-of-origin-specific gene expression due to epigenetic marks","Mendelian inheritance accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","X-chromosome inactivation mediated by specific receptor activation","Genetic mutation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mitochondrial inheritance leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Parent-of-origin-specific gene expression due to epigenetic marks', 'Genomic imprinting is parent-of-origin specific gene expression. Imprinted genes are methylated on one allele. Examples: IGF2 (paternal expression) and H19 (maternal expression). Defects cause Prader-Willi and Angelman syndromes.'),
('36a51972-ceb3-590c-a44c-592c7fbf6b6a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'Angelman syndrome is due to:', '["Loss of paternal expression resulting in decreased functional activity","Deletion of paternal chromosome 15 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Uniparental disomy (paternal) mediated by specific receptor activation","Trisomy 21 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Loss of expression of maternally imprinted UBE3A gene on chromosome 15q11-q13"]'::jsonb, 'Loss of expression of maternally imprinted UBE3A gene on chromosome 15q11-q13', 'Angelman syndrome results from loss of function of the maternally expressed UBE3A gene on chromosome 15q11-q13, because the paternal allele is imprinted (silenced). Causes include maternal deletion of 15q11-q13 or paternal uniparental disomy.'),
('5a9d64e7-3a1f-5617-ae89-72dba2e8db39'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'Barr bodies are:', '["Mitochondria resulting in decreased functional activity","Y-chromosomes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Autosomes mediated by specific receptor activation","Active X-chromosomes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inactivated X-chromosomes in female somatic cells"]'::jsonb, 'Inactivated X-chromosomes in female somatic cells', 'Barr bodies are the condensed, inactivated X chromosomes present in female somatic cells. X-chromosome inactivation (lyonization) equalizes X-linked gene dosage between males and females; the inactive X is regulated by XIST RNA.'),
('6b14217c-8dbf-5080-956f-c23e724fa171'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'Epigenetic reprogramming occurs:', '["Only in somatic cells resulting in decreased functional activity","During early embryonic development and in primordial germ cells","In development mediated by specific receptor activation","Only in neurons without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only in cancer leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'During early embryonic development and in primordial germ cells', 'Epigenetic reprogramming occurs during early embryonic development and in primordial germ cells, resetting epigenetic marks for the next generation.'),
('0fd41026-ef7e-5756-b77f-1b1657a54389'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'Histone variants (e.g., H3.3, CENP-A) differ from canonical histones by:', '["Having function resulting in decreased functional activity","Being incorporated only during S phase accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Being incorporated into chromatin in a replication-independent manner","Being found only in bacteria without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lacking a histone fold domain leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Being incorporated into chromatin in a replication-independent manner', 'Histone variants such as H3.3 and CENP-A are incorporated into chromatin independently of DNA replication (replication-independent deposition), whereas canonical histones are assembled during S phase.'),
('a43ca25b-6a1a-5cc6-a67c-ded6bc5dfce1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'CENP-A is a histone H3 variant specifically found at:', '["Enhancers resulting in decreased functional activity","Telomeres accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Promoters mediated by specific receptor activation","Repetitive elements without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Centromeres (required for kinetochore assembly)"]'::jsonb, 'Centromeres (required for kinetochore assembly)', 'CENP-A is a centromere-specific histone H3 variant that defines centromeric chromatin and provides the platform for kinetochore assembly, which is essential for correct chromosome segregation during mitosis.'),
('130d3022-b999-59ce-9f87-4f501e5da42c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'Enhancer regions are marked by:', '["H3K9me3 resulting in decreased functional activity","H3K27me3 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","H3K4me3 alone mediated by specific receptor activation","DNA methylation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","H3K27ac and H3K4me1 (monomethylation)"]'::jsonb, 'H3K27ac and H3K4me1 (monomethylation)', 'Active enhancers are characterized by histone modifications H3K27ac (acetylation) and H3K4me1 (monomethylation), which mark the enhancer and distinguish it from active promoters, which carry H3K4me3.'),
('f14f4c23-6fea-58f7-84bb-301b905bb0e8'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'What DNA element increases transcription from a distance?', '["Large clusters of enhancers driving high expression of key cell identity genes","Telomeric repeats accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Very strong promoters mediated by specific receptor activation","Repetitive DNA elements without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Silencers leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Large clusters of enhancers driving high expression of key cell identity genes', 'Super-enhancers are large clusters of enhancers that drive high-level expression of key cell-identity genes. They are marked by H3K27ac and are frequently deregulated in cancer, making them therapeutic targets.'),
('603be046-3905-5d71-adc0-ee0df4ff0656'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', '5-Hydroxymethylcytosine (5hmC) is:', '["The most common DNA modification resulting in decreased functional activity","An RNA base accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","An intermediate in active DNA demethylation, enriched in brain","A DNA adduct without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A type of histone modification leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An intermediate in active DNA demethylation, enriched in brain', '5-Hydroxymethylcytosine is produced by TET enzymes that oxidize 5-methylcytosine and serves as an intermediate in active DNA demethylation. It is abundant in the brain, where it is associated with active gene regulation.'),
('424506cf-4466-54a8-8c2f-462d05d62180'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'CTCF functions as:', '["A DNA methyltransferase resulting in decreased functional activity","A spliceosome component accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A transcription factor that activates all genes mediated by specific receptor activation","An insulator/architectural protein that organizes chromatin into loops and TADs","A histone acetyltransferase leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An insulator/architectural protein that organizes chromatin into loops and TADs', 'CTCF is an architectural (insulator) protein that binds boundaries between topologically associating domains (TADs) and, together with cohesin, organizes chromatin into loops that regulate enhancer-promoter interactions.'),
('da1bca7f-4532-576e-9d89-bc6210c88567'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'Topologically associating domains (TADs) are:', '["Nuclear compartments resulting in decreased functional activity","Linear chromosomes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Centromeres mediated by specific receptor activation","Self-interacting genomic regions where sequences within the same TAD interact more frequently","Telomeres leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Self-interacting genomic regions where sequences within the same TAD interact more frequently', 'Topologically associating domains (TADs) are self-interacting genomic regions, typically up to a megabase, within which sequences physically contact one another more frequently than sequences in neighboring domains. Their boundaries are enriched for CTCF.'),
('20f71f77-5833-5092-82e2-989b19034a57'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'The inactive X-chromosome is enriched for which histone modification?', '["H3K36me3","H3K27me3","H3K4me3","H3K4me1","H3K9ac"]'::jsonb, 'H3K27me3', 'The inactive X chromosome is enriched in H3K27me3, a repressive histone modification deposited by the PRC2 complex under the guidance of XIST RNA, maintaining stable silencing of the inactivated X.'),
('b0fede38-1454-54f4-9de2-06c5d59cb307'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'Epigenetic therapy includes the use of:', '["Antibiotics resulting in decreased functional activity","Only radiation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","DNMT inhibitors and HDAC inhibitors for cancer treatment","Only chemotherapy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only immunotherapy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'DNMT inhibitors and HDAC inhibitors for cancer treatment', 'Epigenetic therapy uses DNMT inhibitors (e.g., azacitidine, decitabine) and HDAC inhibitors to reactivate silenced tumor suppressor genes in cancer.'),
('492bdd77-77a5-5a94-9c94-ffeb7a226c59'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'The structure of DNA was determined using which technique?', '["X-ray crystallography","Electron microscopy","Atomic force microscopy","NMR spectroscopy","Mass spectrometry"]'::jsonb, 'X-ray crystallography', 'Watson and Crick deduced the double-helix structure of DNA largely from X-ray diffraction data obtained by Rosalind Franklin and Raymond Gosling, such as Photo 51, which revealed the helical parameters and regular spacing of bases.'),
('02bebc48-624f-5898-a96f-20e405a04735'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'Which enzyme relieves tension from DNA unwinding?', '["DNA polymerases","Topoisomerases","Ligase","Primase","Helicase"]'::jsonb, 'Topoisomerases', 'Topoisomerase relieves torsional strain ahead of the replication fork by creating transient breaks in the DNA backbone.'),
('1d5629d2-9ced-59c3-a08b-0758bc1c4876'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'Semiconservative replication was experimentally proven by:', '["Pauling resulting in decreased functional activity","Chargaff accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Meselson and Stahl using 15N isotope labeling","Franklin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Watson and Crick leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Meselson and Stahl using 15N isotope labeling', 'Meselson and Stahl grew E. coli in heavy N15 medium then switched to N14 medium. After one generation, DNA banded at an intermediate density, and after two generations, intermediate and light bands appeared, consistent only with semiconservative replication.'),
('d53ad2c5-3bae-5864-b6d0-4883c7c50e45'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'The lagging strand is synthesized as Okazaki fragments. In eukaryotes, these are:', '["1000-2000 nucleotides long","100-200 nucleotides long","10-20 nucleotides long","1-10 nucleotides long","Variable length"]'::jsonb, '100-200 nucleotides long', 'In eukaryotes, Okazaki fragments are 100-200 nucleotides long, compared to 1000-2000 nucleotides in prokaryotes.'),
('aa46708c-4273-53bd-a850-11056335bb59'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'Geminin inhibits:', '["DNA polymerase resulting in decreased functional activity","Re-replication by preventing MCM loading after S phase begins","Primase mediated by specific receptor activation","Helicase activity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ligase leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Re-replication by preventing MCM loading after S phase begins', 'Geminin inhibits Cdt1, which is required for MCM helicase loading onto origins. By preventing MCM loading after S phase, geminin ensures that the genome is replicated only once per cell cycle.'),
('225b0c8b-7c68-511b-8fa9-c89c0a380cd7'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'Single-strand annealing (SSA) is:', '["The same as NHEJ resulting in decreased functional activity","A repair pathway for DSBs using homologous repeats flanking the break","An error-free repair pathway mediated by specific receptor activation","Used for base damage without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Base excision repair leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A repair pathway for DSBs using homologous repeats flanking the break', 'Single-strand annealing (SSA) repairs double-strand breaks using homologous direct repeat sequences flanking the break. Resection exposes complementary repeats that anneal, joining the ends but deleting the intervening sequence, making SSA error-prone.'),
('ecf73edc-4a14-564b-b4cc-f73886d3a1d3'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'Break-induced replication (BIR) is:', '["A repair pathway for DSBs with only one end homologous to a donor","Histone modification accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Used for telomere maintenance only mediated by specific receptor activation","The same as normal replication without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Part of mismatch repair leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A repair pathway for DSBs with only one end homologous to a donor', 'Break-induced replication (BIR) repairs double-strand breaks when only one end has homology to a donor sequence. The invading end primes DNA synthesis that continues to the end of the chromosome, often causing loss of heterozygosity.'),
('2faa2b1d-0da5-59b1-94ee-9e0b0cb1e2c9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'Microhomology-mediated end joining (MMEJ) is:', '["Base excision repair resulting in decreased functional activity","The same as classical NHEJ accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Homologous recombination mediated by specific receptor activation","An alternative NHEJ pathway using 5-25 bp microhomologies","Nucleotide excision repair leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An alternative NHEJ pathway using 5-25 bp microhomologies', 'Microhomology-mediated end joining (MMEJ) is an alternative, mutagenic NHEJ pathway in which resected DNA ends are joined using short 5-25 bp microhomologies, frequently producing deletions at the repair junction.'),
('14a1e71d-88a8-5c3d-93a5-cc81164cf012'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'ATM kinase is activated by:', '["Single-strand breaks","Mismatched bases","Replication stress","DNA double-strand breaks","Base damage"]'::jsonb, 'DNA double-strand breaks', 'ATM is a serine/threonine kinase that is activated by double-strand breaks. It phosphorylates downstream targets such as Chk2 and p53 to trigger cell cycle checkpoint arrest and promote DNA repair or apoptosis.'),
('300b686e-c74c-503d-8cc1-7cde32e71b25'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'PARP inhibitors are used to treat:', '["Infectious diseases resulting in decreased functional activity","Only lung cancer accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","BRCA1/2-mutant cancers (synthetic lethality)","Inflammatory diseases without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All cancers leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'BRCA1/2-mutant cancers (synthetic lethality)', 'BRCA1/2-mutant cells lack homologous recombination repair, so they depend on PARP-mediated base excision repair. PARP inhibitors trap PARP on DNA, generating double-strand breaks that these cells cannot repair (synthetic lethality).'),
('5d4934db-d277-52a3-b728-82ac4830825b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'Synthetic lethality occurs when:', '["A single mutation kills the cell resulting in decreased functional activity","Only environmental factors kill cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Two genetic defects individually viable but lethal in combination","Both mutations are harmless without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Genetic interaction leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Two genetic defects individually viable but lethal in combination', 'Synthetic lethality occurs when a cell survives with either one of two genetic defects alone but dies when both defects are present. This principle underlies the use of PARP inhibitors in BRCA-mutant cancers.'),
('aca3f1ad-0bc0-56b4-9d4d-2944aeb157e0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'Translesion synthesis (TLS) involves:', '["Specialized polymerases that bypass DNA lesions at stalled replication forks","Base excision repair accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Normal DNA replication mediated by specific receptor activation","Mismatch repair without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Homologous recombination leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Specialized polymerases that bypass DNA lesions at stalled replication forks', 'Translesion synthesis uses specialized error-prone DNA polymerases (e.g., Pol eta, Pol kappa) to bypass DNA lesions at stalled replication forks.'),
('197eb42d-7f37-500e-8c3a-3ddeef3f72e7'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'The Tus/Ter system in E. coli:', '["Terminates replication by blocking the replisome at specific sites","Initiates replication accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Repairs DNA mediated by specific receptor activation","Separates chromosomes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Transcribes rRNA leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Terminates replication by blocking the replisome at specific sites', 'The Tus protein binds to the terminator (ter) sequences in a polar manner and blocks the advancing DnaB helicase, thereby terminating the two replication forks at defined sites in the circular E. coli chromosome.'),
('2bcf3d51-3533-5e50-8851-6beb8026960f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'The de-differentiation of somatic cells into iPSCs was discovered by:', '["Kary Mullis","Watson and Crick","Shinya Yamanaka (2012 Nobel Prize)","John Gurdon","Meselson and Stahl"]'::jsonb, 'Shinya Yamanaka (2012 Nobel Prize)', 'Shinya Yamanaka reprogrammed differentiated cells into induced pluripotent stem cells (iPSCs) by expressing the transcription factors Oct4, Sox2, Klf4, and c-Myc, earning the 2012 Nobel Prize.'),
('ff4c62c1-bef5-5f23-bda0-3fb1538ff7d7'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'Small interfering RNAs (siRNAs) are produced by the enzyme:', '["Drosha","RNA polymerase","Reverse transcriptase","Ligase","Dicer"]'::jsonb, 'Dicer', 'Dicer is an RNase III enzyme that cleaves long double-stranded RNA into 21-23 nucleotide siRNAs, which are then loaded into the RISC complex to guide sequence-specific mRNA degradation.'),
('2896a162-448a-5475-bba4-4281087b6550'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'The RISC complex uses:', '["A guide strand of small RNA (miRNA or siRNA) to recognize target mRNAs","Antibodies for recognition accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A DNA guide to target genes mediated by specific receptor activation","Lipids to identify targets without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Proteins only to recognize targets leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A guide strand of small RNA (miRNA or siRNA) to recognize target mRNAs', 'RISC uses the guide strand of a small RNA (siRNA or miRNA) to base-pair with complementary target mRNA. Perfect complementarity directs mRNA cleavage, while partial pairing, as with most miRNAs, causes translational repression.'),
('9ed6ac5c-3241-56c9-a4b1-faaf5f1c238f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'Prime editing uses:', '["Cas12a only resulting in decreased functional activity","Zinc finger nucleases accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Base editors only mediated by specific receptor activation","Cas9 nickase fused to reverse transcriptase with a prime editing guide RNA (pegRNA)","Standard Cas9 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cas9 nickase fused to reverse transcriptase with a prime editing guide RNA (pegRNA)', 'Prime editing uses a Cas9 nickase fused to a reverse transcriptase and a prime editing guide RNA (pegRNA) that encodes both the target site and the desired edit, allowing precise genome modification without double-strand breaks.'),
('7d98d688-a8fe-55f9-8704-d89201c74b85'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'The RecBCD complex in E. coli:', '["Replicates DNA resulting in decreased functional activity","Repairs mismatches accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Unwinds DNA mediated by specific receptor activation","Resects double-strand breaks and loads RecA for homologous recombination","Methylates DNA leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Resects double-strand breaks and loads RecA for homologous recombination', 'The RecBCD complex is a helicase-nuclease that resects double-strand breaks in E. coli, generating a 3'' single-stranded end that is coated by RecA protein to form a nucleoprotein filament for homologous recombination.'),
('c18cdb0b-bc1c-5c56-8c3c-e98872b3cd3f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'Site-specific recombination (e.g., Cre-LoxP) is used for:', '["Global gene knockout only resulting in decreased functional activity","Gene activation only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Gene sequencing mediated by specific receptor activation","Conditional gene knockout in specific tissues or cell types","Cutting DNA randomly leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Conditional gene knockout in specific tissues or cell types', 'Cre-LoxP recombination uses the Cre recombinase to excise DNA between two loxP sites. When Cre is expressed under a tissue-specific or inducible promoter, it enables conditional gene knockout in selected cells or tissues.'),
('3f0d4a67-24dc-5a50-b92d-d8e97d789b62'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'TA cloning relies on:', '["Restriction sites resulting in decreased functional activity","The terminal transferase activity of Taq polymerase adding an extra A to PCR products","Recombination mediated by specific receptor activation","Electroporation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Blunt end ligation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The terminal transferase activity of Taq polymerase adding an extra A to PCR products', 'Taq polymerase adds a nontemplated adenine to the 3'' end of PCR products. TA cloning vectors carry 3'' thymidine overhangs that base-pair with these A overhangs, allowing efficient ligation without restriction enzymes.'),
('3d85ef35-42a7-5f86-9ebe-9466dc62aa2d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'Which enzyme activity is required for Gibson assembly?', '["5'' exonuclease, DNA polymerase, and ligase","DNA ligase only","Recombinases only","Restriction enzymes only","Taq polymerase only"]'::jsonb, '5'' exonuclease, DNA polymerase, and ligase', 'Gibson assembly joins multiple DNA fragments in one isothermal reaction using a 5'' exonuclease to chew back fragment ends and expose overlaps, a DNA polymerase to fill gaps, and a DNA ligase to seal the nicks.'),
('dfbb3f14-e99a-5939-a16e-4d756596c988'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'Chromatin immunoprecipitation (ChIP) is used to:', '["Amplify DNA resulting in decreased functional activity","Identify genome-wide binding sites of a specific protein (e.g., transcription factor)","Clone genes mediated by specific receptor activation","Express proteins without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sequence RNA leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Identify genome-wide binding sites of a specific protein (e.g., transcription factor)', 'Chromatin immunoprecipitation (ChIP) crosslinks proteins to DNA, shears the chromatin, and immunoprecipitates a specific protein with an antibody. The associated DNA is then purified and identified to map protein binding sites.'),
('7199af0e-3bb2-500e-a3d7-207b0c815ff0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'ChIP-seq combines:', '["ChIP with mass spectrometry resulting in decreased functional activity","ChIP with microarrays accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","ChIP with PCR mediated by specific receptor activation","ChIP with Western blotting without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Chromatin immunoprecipitation with next-generation sequencing"]'::jsonb, 'Chromatin immunoprecipitation with next-generation sequencing', 'ChIP-seq couples chromatin immunoprecipitation with next-generation sequencing to map the genome-wide binding sites of transcription factors, histones, and other chromatin-associated proteins.'),
('6afa46f8-09cc-593f-b270-6cb3f29717e3'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'DNA nanotechnology uses:', '["DNA for PCR resulting in decreased functional activity","DNA as genetic material only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","DNA for sequencing mediated by specific receptor activation","DNA as a structural material to build nanoscale shapes and devices","DNA for cloning leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'DNA as a structural material to build nanoscale shapes and devices', 'DNA nanotechnology exploits the base-pairing properties of DNA to construct nanoscale shapes and devices for applications in biosensing, drug delivery, and computing.'),
('4ca7e67d-2807-5452-b647-7f0983b57333'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'Aptamers are:', '["Enzymes resulting in decreased functional activity","Peptide hormones accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Antibodies mediated by specific receptor activation","Short single-stranded DNA or RNA molecules that bind specific targets with high affinity","Small molecule drugs leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Short single-stranded DNA or RNA molecules that bind specific targets with high affinity', 'Aptamers are short single-stranded DNA or RNA molecules selected by SELEX that fold into specific three-dimensional structures and bind their targets with high affinity and specificity, resembling chemical antibodies.'),
('881211ae-0410-5bef-9006-370a4ac3cc41'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'Ribozymes are:', '["Catalytic RNA molecules that can cleave or ligate RNA","RNA-binding proteins accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lipid catalysts mediated by specific receptor activation","DNA catalysts without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Protein enzymes that degrade RNA leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Catalytic RNA molecules that can cleave or ligate RNA', 'Ribozymes are catalytic RNA molecules. Examples include self-splicing introns, RNase P, and the ribosome (peptidyl transferase activity). They support the RNA world hypothesis.'),
('fcabfdef-a29d-51ca-806d-859e3de586ac'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'The alternative lengthening of telomeres (ALT) pathway:', '["Maintains telomeres via homologous recombination in telomerase-negative cancers","Involves telomere maintenance accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Actively shortens telomeres mediated by specific receptor activation","Uses reverse transcriptase without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Uses telomerase leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Maintains telomeres via homologous recombination in telomerase-negative cancers', 'The alternative lengthening of telomeres (ALT) pathway maintains telomere length in about 10% of cancers that lack telomerase, using homologous recombination between telomeric repeat sequences, and is associated with ALT-associated PML bodies and C-circles.'),
('ef896e75-84f2-5ea0-af53-a26ca3d1ac49'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'Which chromosomal translocation is characteristic of chronic myeloid leukemia (CML)?', '["t(8;14)(q24;q32)","t(14;18)(q32;q21)","t(9;22)(q34;q11)","t(15;17)(q22;q12)","t(11;22)(q24;q12)"]'::jsonb, 't(9;22)(q34;q11)', 'The t(9;22)(q34;q11) translocation creates the Philadelphia chromosome with BCR-ABL fusion, which is the hallmark of CML.'),
('a1c7dbae-a8a4-5690-86d2-64ce0e8f5e40'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'Fluorescence resonance energy transfer (FRET) is used to:', '["Amplify DNA resulting in decreased functional activity","Clone genes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Separate proteins mediated by specific receptor activation","Measure molecular interactions at nanometer distances","Sequence DNA leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Measure molecular interactions at nanometer distances', 'FRET measures molecular interactions at nanometer distances (1-10 nm) by detecting energy transfer between a donor and acceptor fluorophore.'),
('1a607655-6d24-51f1-93b6-420416a1da3f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Nucleic Acids', 'Bioluminescence resonance energy transfer (BRET) is:', '["Similar to FRET but uses a bioluminescent donor instead of a fluorophore","A type of DNA sequencing accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A protein purification technique mediated by specific receptor activation","A cloning method without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A PCR technique leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Similar to FRET but uses a bioluminescent donor instead of a fluorophore', 'BRET is analogous to FRET but uses a bioluminescent protein (e.g., luciferase) as the energy donor instead of an externally excited fluorophore, avoiding photobleaching and cellular autofluorescence.'),
('85549e59-79d5-5e66-8486-0cc411a59279'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Which amino acid has the simplest structure?', '["Glycine","Isoleucine","Leucine","Valine","Alanine"]'::jsonb, 'Glycine', 'Glycine has the simplest structure: its R group is a single hydrogen atom. This makes it the only non-chiral amino acid, since the alpha carbon is not attached to four different groups.'),
('68292d80-8208-5b67-80c8-fcebe7081ce1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'All amino acids found in proteins are of which stereoisomeric form?', '["Meso form","D-isomer","S-isomer","R-isomer","L-isomer"]'::jsonb, 'L-isomer', 'All protein amino acids (except glycine) are L-isomers, meaning the amino group is on the left in Fischer projection. Bacteria also use L-amino acids; D-amino acids occur only in some bacterial cell walls.'),
('eeb31fad-b1c7-53f9-892b-156fe3e2784b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Which amino acid is the only one without a chiral center?', '["Alanine","Lysine","Proline","Serine","Glycine"]'::jsonb, 'Glycine', 'Glycine is the only amino acid without a chiral center because its side chain is a single hydrogen atom, making its alpha carbon achiral.'),
('ec5c7108-ab71-5809-92a3-946ddd2a6730'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Which amino acid contains a sulfur atom?', '["Methionine","Cysteine","Serine","Threonine","Valine"]'::jsonb, 'Methionine', 'Methionine contains a thioether group (-S-CH3) and is the first amino acid in protein synthesis (initiator codon AUG). Cysteine also contains sulfur as a thiol (-SH) and forms disulfide bonds.'),
('d2348034-138e-5e9b-ae42-887fce28abf6'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Which functional group is characteristic of the peptide bond?', '["Amide","Ester","Glycosidic","Disulfide","Ether"]'::jsonb, 'Amide', 'The peptide bond is an amide linkage formed between the carboxyl group of one amino acid and the amino group of another, with release of a water molecule (condensation reaction).'),
('b89c122c-1a70-580c-b6fd-5999eae5bc1b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'The peptide bond has partial double bond character due to:', '["Hydrogen bonding","Isomerization","Tautomerization","Resonance","Electrostatic interaction"]'::jsonb, 'Resonance', 'Resonance between the carbonyl C=O and the C-N bond gives the peptide bond 40% double bond character. This restricts rotation around the C-N bond and makes the peptide bond planar and rigid.'),
('52553917-8a0e-5f09-85c2-638784174a12'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Which statement about the peptide bond is true?', '["It is trans in natural proteins","It is planar and rigid","It can rotate freely","It is non-planar","It is always cis"]'::jsonb, 'It is planar and rigid', 'The peptide bond is planar and rigid due to its partial double-bond character from resonance. In natural proteins it is almost exclusively in the trans configuration.'),
('c4781d86-f704-571f-84d4-ee80af47ed64'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Primary structure of proteins refers to:', '["Three-dimensional folding","Post-translational modifications","Linear sequence of amino acids","Oligomeric assembly","Alpha-helix and beta-sheet"]'::jsonb, 'Linear sequence of amino acids', 'Primary structure is the linear sequence of amino acids held together by peptide bonds. It is determined by the gene and dictates all higher levels of protein structure.'),
('d21631c9-2d02-54ee-aa50-718fad4db78e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Secondary structure of proteins includes:', '["Disulfide bonds","Alpha-helix and beta-pleated sheet","Subunit interaction","Amino acid sequence","Peptide bonds"]'::jsonb, 'Alpha-helix and beta-pleated sheet', 'Secondary structure is the local folding of the polypeptide backbone stabilized by hydrogen bonds between C=O and N-H groups. Common motifs are the alpha-helix and beta-pleated sheet.'),
('29d298fb-e065-5343-aab1-399ffa1cd9c8'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Which type of bond stabilizes secondary structure?', '["Van der Waals forces resulting in decreased functional activity","Peptide bonds accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Disulfide bonds mediated by specific receptor activation","Ionic interactions without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hydrogen bonds between C=O and N-H groups"]'::jsonb, 'Hydrogen bonds between C=O and N-H groups', 'Secondary structures (alpha-helix, beta-sheet) are stabilized by hydrogen bonds between the backbone carbonyl (C=O) of one residue and the amide (N-H) of another.'),
('079632a8-009a-5ee6-9887-581280613b16'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'How many amino acid residues are there per turn in an alpha-helix?', '["2.0","3.6","2.5","4.0","5.0"]'::jsonb, '3.6', 'The alpha-helix has 3.6 amino acid residues per turn, a rise of 1.5 Å per residue, and a pitch (length per turn) of 5.4 Å. It is a right-handed helix stabilized by intra-chain hydrogen bonds.'),
('ebf12f6e-87c4-580a-9654-a7921397fbd3'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'The pitch of the alpha-helix is:', '["4.5 A","1.5 A","5.4 A","6.0 A","3.6 A"]'::jsonb, '5.4 A', 'The alpha-helix has a pitch of 5.4 Å per turn, containing 3.6 residues per turn with a 1.5 Å rise per residue.'),
('b37cb568-86dc-5ba4-8153-e85689d17207'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Which amino acid is a helix breaker?', '["Leucine","Alanine","Proline","Glutamic acid","Valine"]'::jsonb, 'Proline', 'Proline disrupts alpha-helices because its rigid ring fixes the phi angle and its nitrogen has no hydrogen to donate for hydrogen bonding. Glycine also destabilizes helices due to conformational flexibility.'),
('a2e076ea-fd83-518c-a181-cfcd92af47dd'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Tertiary structure refers to:', '["Overall three-dimensional folding of a single polypeptide","Linear sequence of amino acids accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Peptide bond formation mediated by specific receptor activation","Subunit assembly without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Alpha-helix arrangement leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Overall three-dimensional folding of a single polypeptide', 'Tertiary structure is the complete 3D folding of a single polypeptide chain. It is stabilized by interactions between R groups: hydrophobic interactions, hydrogen bonds, ionic bonds, and disulfide bonds.'),
('98ddfc1d-238e-57d8-b46e-9753e04ccf30'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Quaternary structure refers to:', '["Alpha-helix formation","Single chain folding","Amino acid sequence","Peptide bond isomerization","Association of multiple polypeptide subunits"]'::jsonb, 'Association of multiple polypeptide subunits', 'Quaternary structure is the assembly of two or more polypeptide chains (subunits) into a functional protein. Example: hemoglobin is a tetramer of two alpha and two beta subunits.'),
('85c25183-4895-53a5-90a0-cba754350b14'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Hemoglobin has which quaternary structure?', '["Pentamer resulting in decreased functional activity","Trimer accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dimer mediated by specific receptor activation","Tetramer (alpha2 beta2)","Monomer leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tetramer (alpha2 beta2)', 'Adult hemoglobin (HbA) is a tetramer of two alpha-globin and two beta-globin subunits (alpha2 beta2). Each subunit contains a heme group, allowing cooperative oxygen binding.'),
('b208a041-ae79-5b8b-9241-6ede7caa43c1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Myoglobin has which level of structure?', '["Secondary only","Tertiary only","Pentameric","Quaternary only","Primary only"]'::jsonb, 'Tertiary only', 'Myoglobin is a monomeric oxygen-binding protein in muscle with only tertiary structure. Its hyperbolic oxygen-binding curve reflects the lack of cooperative binding.'),
('45c3855f-f0b6-544c-b605-4abf2315d6c6'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Which bonds stabilize tertiary structure?', '["Ionic bonds","Hydrogen bonds","Disulfide bonds","Hydrophobic interactions","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Tertiary structure is stabilized by hydrophobic interactions, hydrogen bonds, ionic bonds, and covalent disulfide bonds between cysteine residues.'),
('862d9a4d-675b-5163-8fc4-ad09f6169d5c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Disulfide bonds in proteins form between which amino acids?', '["Arginine residues","Methionine residues","Lysine residues","Cysteine residues","Tyrosine residues"]'::jsonb, 'Cysteine residues', 'Disulfide bonds form through oxidation of the thiol (-SH) groups of two cysteine residues, producing a covalent -S-S- link that stabilizes protein tertiary and quaternary structure.'),
('03c4abcb-9b27-5ff2-b65a-08e2db3e341a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Which amino acid introduces a bend or turn in a polypeptide chain?', '["Alanine","Phenylalanine","Proline","Valine","Leucine"]'::jsonb, 'Proline', 'Proline introduces kinks/bends in polypeptide chains because its cyclic structure restricts rotation. It is commonly found in beta-turns and collagen''s repeating Gly-X-Y motif.'),
('2316973b-7525-5846-9eba-e373928bfaef'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Which of the following is a conjugated protein?', '["Hemoglobin","Globulin","Collagen","Albumin","Histone"]'::jsonb, 'Hemoglobin', 'Hemoglobin is a conjugated protein containing a non-protein prosthetic group (heme). Simple proteins like albumin contain only amino acids.'),
('c3e7fdb4-751f-5243-9770-d726e2ca8c7e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Which organ synthesizes albumin?', '["Spleen","Pancreas","Bone marrow","Liver","Kidney"]'::jsonb, 'Liver', 'Albumin, the most abundant plasma protein (60% of total), is synthesized by the liver. It maintains oncotic pressure and transports fatty acids, hormones, drugs, and bilirubin.'),
('3e54c6be-529d-5cc6-8bec-d2888bc0924a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'The normal serum albumin level is:', '["5.0-7.0 g/dL","3.5-5.0 g/dL","1.0-2.0 g/dL","2.0-3.5 g/dL","7.0-9.0 g/dL"]'::jsonb, '3.5-5.0 g/dL', 'Normal serum albumin is 3.5-5.0 g/dL. Low levels (hypoalbuminemia) occur in liver disease, nephrotic syndrome, and malnutrition, causing edema.'),
('24da51fc-5e26-5e2b-b109-b93445b5ae65'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'The primary function of albumin is:', '["Blood clotting","Maintenance of oncotic pressure","Immune defense","Oxygen transport","Enzymatic activity"]'::jsonb, 'Maintenance of oncotic pressure', 'Albumin generates 80% of plasma colloid oncotic pressure, keeping fluid within the vascular compartment. It also binds and transports many hydrophobic ligands.'),
('1898e174-2e7d-5098-8e60-94d60bf8ac53'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Which plasma protein is an acute phase reactant that increases in inflammation?', '["Transferrin","Prealbumin","Albumin","Transthyretin","C-reactive protein"]'::jsonb, 'C-reactive protein', 'C-reactive protein (CRP) is an acute-phase reactant synthesized by the liver that rises dramatically during inflammation, tissue injury, and infection. It binds phosphocholine on microbial surfaces.'),
('8d8c79c6-1293-5c2a-87b1-577056ccf290'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Transferrin is a transport protein for:', '["Magnesium","Iron","Copper","Calcium","Zinc"]'::jsonb, 'Iron', 'Transferrin transports iron in plasma and delivers it to cells via transferrin receptors. Total iron-binding capacity (TIBC) reflects transferrin levels.'),
('a9ecb2a4-eb61-5004-a10a-7dcb30f7099f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Ceruloplasmin is a transport protein for:', '["Copper","Magnesium","Calcium","Zinc","Iron"]'::jsonb, 'Copper', 'Ceruloplasmin is the major copper-transporting protein in plasma. Low levels are seen in Wilson disease, where copper accumulates in liver, brain, and cornea (Kayser-Fleischer rings).'),
('deb8ea02-dec8-54d5-b27a-e343c20b86c5'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Which plasma protein is a protease inhibitor whose deficiency causes emphysema and liver disease?', '["Alpha-1-antitrypsin","Albumin","Transferrin","Haptoglobin","Fibrinogen"]'::jsonb, 'Alpha-1-antitrypsin', 'Alpha-1-antitrypsin inhibits neutrophil elastase. Deficiency (autosomal recessive) allows elastase to destroy lung tissue (panacinar emphysema) and causes liver cirrhosis from abnormal protein accumulation.'),
('68be704e-cb49-5fd3-ab34-50b09272cc83'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Which apolipoprotein is the major component of HDL?', '["Apo C-II","Apo B-100","Apo A-I","Apo B-48","Apo E"]'::jsonb, 'Apo A-I', 'Apo A-I is the principal apolipoprotein of HDL and activates lecithin-cholesterol acyltransferase (LCAT), the enzyme that esterifies cholesterol during reverse cholesterol transport.'),
('401fd1d4-2e11-5b20-9414-bf08490fc283'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Which plasma protein maintains blood oncotic pressure?', '["Haptoglobin","Albumin","Transferrin","Ceruloplasmin","Fibrinogen"]'::jsonb, 'Albumin', 'Albumin maintains plasma oncotic pressure and is the most abundant plasma protein. It is synthesized in the liver.'),
('9833739a-d878-55f6-9856-40cbe11a4021'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Immunoglobulin electrophoresis is used to diagnose:', '["Anemia","Kidney disease","Liver disease","Multiple myeloma","Hemophilia"]'::jsonb, 'Multiple myeloma', 'Serum protein electrophoresis in multiple myeloma shows a monoclonal (M) spike in the gamma region due to a single clone of plasma cells producing excess immunoglobulin.'),
('b91e16e8-2ea0-5fea-be91-b142d2532318'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'The normal A/G (albumin/globulin) ratio is:', '["3.0-4.0","2.0-3.0","1.0-2.0","<0.5","0.5-1.0"]'::jsonb, '1.0-2.0', 'The normal albumin/globulin (A/G) ratio is 1.0-2.0. Reversal (low A/G) occurs in chronic liver disease, nephrotic syndrome, and multiple myeloma.'),
('1f2b2c30-daeb-5f91-b30c-8dbab79f2223'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'The urea cycle converts:', '["Urea to ammonia","Ammonia to urea","Glucose to glycogen","Amino acids to glucose","Fatty acids to ketones"]'::jsonb, 'Ammonia to urea', 'The urea cycle (liver) detoxifies ammonia by converting it to urea. It uses ATP and the amino acid ornithine as a carrier, with most intermediates cycling in mitochondria and cytosol.'),
('bc025375-a74f-5ae4-bd78-554ca584cf09'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'The first reaction of the urea cycle produces:', '["Argininosuccinate","Carbamoyl phosphate","Ornithine","Citrulline","Urea"]'::jsonb, 'Carbamoyl phosphate', 'In the first step, carbamoyl phosphate synthetase I (CPS-I) combines ammonia and CO2 with ATP to form carbamoyl phosphate. This is the rate-limiting step, activated by N-acetylglutamate.'),
('00c0c325-4349-551f-843a-f2b3ca3f6299'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Which molecule allosterically activates carbamoyl phosphate synthetase I?', '["Glutamate","N-acetylglutamate","ADP","ATP","NADH"]'::jsonb, 'N-acetylglutamate', 'N-acetylglutamate (NAG) is the essential allosteric activator of CPS-I. It is synthesized from glutamate and acetyl-CoA; its deficiency causes hyperammonemia.'),
('26db2f93-e8f9-5198-bbcb-309b76de5b9a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Which amino acid is a carrier of ammonia in the urea cycle?', '["Citrulline","Glutamate","Arginine","Aspartate","Ornithine"]'::jsonb, 'Ornithine', 'Ornithine is regenerated at the end of each urea cycle turn and acts as a carrier that accepts ammonia to form citrulline, then arginine, releasing urea and regenerating ornithine.'),
('b9bf689c-6976-5938-9216-345aeafc04e9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'The enzyme argininosuccinate synthetase catalyzes:', '["Arginine to Ornithine + Urea","Urea to Ammonia + CO2","Citrulline + Aspartate to Argininosuccinate","Ornithine + Carbamoyl phosphate to Citrulline","Argininosuccinate to Arginine + Fumarate"]'::jsonb, 'Citrulline + Aspartate to Argininosuccinate', 'Argininosuccinate synthetase combines citrulline with aspartate to form argininosuccinate, consuming one ATP (as AMP + PPi). This is a major control point of the urea cycle.'),
('267d5445-3f9e-5daa-9bb6-941911056ebc'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Which enzyme splits argininosuccinate into arginine and fumarate?', '["Arginase","Argininosuccinate lyase","Ornithine transcarbamoylase","Carbamoyl phosphate synthetase","Argininosuccinate synthetase"]'::jsonb, 'Argininosuccinate lyase', 'Argininosuccinate lyase cleaves argininosuccinate into arginine and fumarate. The fumarate links the urea cycle to the TCA cycle and gluconeogenesis.'),
('ebc2f164-be38-5909-ba28-e10bf4b04603'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Arginase converts arginine to:', '["Ornithine + Urea","Citrulline + Ammonia","Creatine + Phosphate","Argininosuccinate + Water","Citrulline + Urea"]'::jsonb, 'Ornithine + Urea', 'Arginase hydrolyzes arginine to ornithine and urea, completing the urea cycle. Urea is the end product of nitrogen metabolism, excreted in urine.'),
('83152ff1-ba6d-5c88-986e-c9f727dc692e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Ornithine transcarbamoylase deficiency is the most common urea cycle defect and is:', '["X-linked recessive","Sporadic","Autosomal dominant","Autosomal recessive","Mitochondrial"]'::jsonb, 'X-linked recessive', 'Ornithine transcarbamoylase (OTC) deficiency is the most common urea cycle disorder and is X-linked. It causes severe hyperammonemia with orotic aciduria and elevated glutamine.'),
('8a4059e2-3fcd-500c-9dfb-650ea8a7d3c3'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Ammonia is transported from peripheral tissues to the liver as:', '["Free ammonia","Aspartate","Glutamine and alanine","Citrulline","Glutamate"]'::jsonb, 'Glutamine and alanine', 'Ammonia is transported as nontoxic carriers: glutamine (from muscle and brain) and alanine (the glucose-alanine cycle from muscle). This prevents free ammonia toxicity in blood.'),
('0a3c394b-77ae-5e57-8981-1ead23de7625'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Glutamine synthetase catalyzes:', '["Glutamate to Alpha-ketoglutarate + NH3","Glutamine to Glutamate + NH3","Glutamine + H2O to Glutamate + NH4+","Glutamate + NH3 to Glutamine","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Glutamate + NH3 to Glutamine', 'Glutamine synthetase converts glutamate and ammonia to glutamine using ATP. This is the major ammonia-fixing reaction in the brain and muscle for safe transport of ammonia.'),
('11e96ceb-23c3-5ee5-bc30-8bf915006782'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'The two major transaminases (aminotransferases) in clinical practice are:', '["LDH isoenzymes","ALP and GGT","AST (GOT) and ALT (GPT)","Amylase and lipase","CK and LDH"]'::jsonb, 'AST (GOT) and ALT (GPT)', 'The clinically important transaminases are aspartate aminotransferase (AST/SGOT) and alanine aminotransferase (ALT/SGPT). ALT is more liver-specific; AST is found in liver, heart, and muscle.'),
('832bf3ee-2e94-56df-ac78-ef429aca386d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Transaminases require which coenzyme?', '["NAD+","Biotin","Thiamine pyrophosphate","FAD","Pyridoxal phosphate (PLP)"]'::jsonb, 'Pyridoxal phosphate (PLP)', 'Transaminases use pyridoxal phosphate (PLP, vitamin B6) as coenzyme. PLP accepts and transfers amino groups, acting as a temporary carrier in the ping-pong mechanism.'),
('2173c50a-b078-5fc3-9c3c-5e4fd4c2a5b4'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'The reaction catalyzed by ALT is:', '["Aspartate + Alpha-ketoglutarate to Oxaloacetate + Glutamate","Alanine + Oxaloacetate to Pyruvate + Aspartate","Alanine + Alpha-ketoglutarate to Pyruvate + Glutamate","Alanine + Pyruvate to Lactate + Glutamate","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Alanine + Alpha-ketoglutarate to Pyruvate + Glutamate', 'ALT (alanine aminotransferase) transfers the amino group of alanine to alpha-ketoglutarate, producing pyruvate and glutamate. This is central to the glucose-alanine cycle.'),
('70d2acba-8ad9-54f1-97f9-3be72c38e444'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Which amino acid is exclusively ketogenic?', '["Isoleucine","Lysine","Tryptophan","Leucine","Phenylalanine"]'::jsonb, 'Leucine', 'Leucine and lysine are exclusively ketogenic: they can only form acetyl-CoA/acetoacetate, not glucose. Isoleucine, phenylalanine, and tryptophan are both glucogenic and ketogenic.'),
('6a307166-803a-5eb3-9d3a-10cfbafe7b04'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Which of the following amino acids is both glucogenic and ketogenic?', '["Leucine","Lysine","Tyrosine","Isoleucine","Glycine"]'::jsonb, 'Isoleucine', 'Isoleucine, phenylalanine, threonine, tryptophan, and tyrosine are both glucogenic and ketogenic. Leucine and lysine are exclusively ketogenic; the rest are glucogenic.'),
('3874f904-c68c-5ed3-9550-7adc3c25578f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Which amino acid is purely glucogenic?', '["Leucine","Lysine","Serine","Glutamate","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Glycine, serine, glutamate, alanine, and many others are purely glucogenic because they feed into pyruvate or TCA intermediates that can make glucose. Leucine and lysine are ketogenic only.'),
('d54be8f0-7cc8-5041-b9e5-57b946449181'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'What accumulates in PKU?', '["Leucine and isoleucine","Homogentisate","Succinylacetone","Tyrosine and tyramine","Phenylalanine and phenylpyruvate"]'::jsonb, 'Phenylalanine and phenylpyruvate', 'In PKU, phenylalanine accumulates and is transaminated to phenylpyruvate, which appears in urine. High phenylalanine is neurotoxic and causes the musty odor.'),
('f8b37e34-209c-526b-a014-905bbdc5ed28'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Treatment of PKU involves:', '["Low-phenylalanine diet","Galactose restriction","Leucine restriction","High-protein diet","Tyrosine restriction"]'::jsonb, 'Low-phenylalanine diet', 'PKU is treated with a low-phenylalanine diet, using phenylalanine-free medical formula. Since tyrosine becomes essential, tyrosine supplementation is also given.'),
('cb1b3b9b-f03c-5566-806e-c382478cf3ae'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Dihydropteridine reductase deficiency causes:', '["Alkaptonuria","Tyrosinemia","Classic PKU","Homocystinuria","Atypical PKU"]'::jsonb, 'Atypical PKU', 'Dihydropteridine reductase deficiency causes atypical (malignant) PKU by impairing regeneration of tetrahydrobiopterin (BH4), the cofactor for phenylalanine hydroxylase. It is unresponsive to dietary phenylalanine restriction.'),
('0a661897-a4c8-5889-8c93-7606b3cebdff'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Alkaptonuria is due to deficiency of:', '["Phenylalanine hydroxylase","Homogentisate 1,2-dioxygenase","Tyrosine aminotransferase","4-Hydroxyphenylpyruvate dioxygenase","Tyrosinase"]'::jsonb, 'Homogentisate 1,2-dioxygenase', 'Alkaptonuria results from homogentisate 1,2-dioxygenase deficiency, causing homogentisic acid accumulation. It is oxidized in urine to a dark black pigment and deposited in tissues (ochronosis).'),
('1825b445-5f43-5154-91bb-4aea5895d55e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'The clinical triad of alkaptonuria includes:', '["Neurological symptoms","And dark urine","Ochronosis","Arthritis","Liver disease"]'::jsonb, 'Ochronosis', 'Alkaptonuria is characterized by dark urine that blackens on standing, ochronosis (blue-black pigment in cartilage and connective tissue), and degenerative arthritis in adulthood.'),
('cebc28b8-ce24-561b-820a-a50a08248255'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Alkaptonuria was the first disease described as an inborn error of metabolism by:', '["James Watson","Gregor Mendel","William Bateson","Archibald Garrod","Francis Crick"]'::jsonb, 'Archibald Garrod', 'Archibald Garrod described alkaptonuria (1902) as the first ''inborn error of metabolism,'' establishing that inherited enzyme defects cause metabolic disease.'),
('c3f3dd42-7281-5e76-96ed-af210fa773fa'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Maple syrup urine disease results from deficiency of:', '["Homogentisate oxidase","Phenylalanine hydroxylase","Branched-chain alpha-keto acid dehydrogenase","Tyrosine hydroxylase","Branched-chain aminotransferase"]'::jsonb, 'Branched-chain alpha-keto acid dehydrogenase', 'MSUD is caused by branched-chain alpha-ketoacid dehydrogenase deficiency, causing accumulation of leucine, isoleucine, and valine and their keto acids. The urine has a maple syrup smell.'),
('802668a2-ed10-5ead-82b6-b7f20f9a32a4'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Which amino acids accumulate in MSUD?', '["Ile","Leu","Phe","Tyr","Val"]'::jsonb, 'Leu', 'Leucine, isoleucine, and valine (branched-chain amino acids) and their alpha-keto acids accumulate in MSUD. The dehydrogenase complex is defective, causing neurotoxicity and sweet-smelling urine.'),
('8561e7d6-3fbf-5ca7-9cc3-1e5b7f91bb4b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Homocystinuria is most commonly due to deficiency of:', '["Methylenetetrahydrofolate reductase","Cystathionine beta-synthase","Betaine-homocysteine methyltransferase","Methionine synthase","Cystathionase"]'::jsonb, 'Cystathionine beta-synthase', 'Classic homocystinuria is caused by cystathionine beta-synthase deficiency, blocking conversion of homocysteine to cystathionine. Features include ectopia lentis, marfanoid habitus, and thromboembolism.'),
('b2b3856f-c74c-56bc-afbb-f40928affa37'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Which vitamin is essential for amino acid transamination?', '["Thiamine","Biotin","Pyridoxine (vitamin B6)","Niacin","Riboflavin"]'::jsonb, 'Pyridoxine (vitamin B6)', 'Vitamin B6 (pyridoxine), as pyridoxal phosphate (PLP), is the coenzyme for transaminases (aminotransferases) and many decarboxylases.'),
('8274ec92-e438-59bb-a14b-75501e44fd07'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Tyrosinemia type II (Richner-Hanhart syndrome) is due to deficiency of:', '["Phenylalanine hydroxylase","FAH","HPD","Homogentisate oxidase","Tyrosine aminotransferase"]'::jsonb, 'Tyrosine aminotransferase', 'Tyrosinemia type II results from hepatic tyrosine aminotransferase deficiency, causing palmar-plantar keratosis and painful corneal ulcers (herpetiform lesions) without liver failure.'),
('777d2af6-1683-5761-84b0-53c783b6ba4e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Which amino acid is a precursor for melanin?', '["Phenylalanine","Histidine","Cysteine","Tryptophan","Tyrosine"]'::jsonb, 'Tyrosine', 'Tyrosine is the precursor for melanin, thyroid hormones (T3/T4), and catecholamines (dopamine, norepinephrine, epinephrine). Melanin synthesis requires the enzyme tyrosinase.'),
('8f0f95a6-ab65-54cf-8399-06a79562e776'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Serotonin and niacin (NAD+) are derived from which amino acid?', '["Histidine","Tryptophan","Phenylalanine","Methionine","Tyrosine"]'::jsonb, 'Tryptophan', 'Tryptophan is the precursor for serotonin (5-HT), melatonin, and niacin. The conversion of tryptophan to niacin explains why severe pellagra responds to dietary protein.'),
('217eeb65-f96d-5a93-a50a-d7b1d2d71c5a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Histamine is derived from which amino acid?', '["Arginine","Tryptophan","Lysine","Histidine","Tyrosine"]'::jsonb, 'Histidine', 'Histidine is decarboxylated by histidine decarboxylase (using PLP) to form histamine, a mediator of allergic and inflammatory responses stored in mast cells.'),
('5547540e-7859-54e4-b149-bad33192048f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Dopamine is derived from which amino acid?', '["Isoleucine","Tyrosine","Phenylalanine","Leucine","Tryptophan"]'::jsonb, 'Tyrosine', 'Dopamine is synthesized from tyrosine: tyrosine hydroxylase forms DOPA, then DOPA decarboxylase forms dopamine. Tyrosine hydroxylase is the rate-limiting step.'),
('0440e5dd-c8d1-5989-a111-00ec1eaa056f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'The enzyme that converts dopamine to norepinephrine is:', '["Catechol-O-methyltransferase","Tyrosine hydroxylase","Monoamine oxidase","Phenylalanine hydroxylase","Dopamine beta-hydroxylase"]'::jsonb, 'Dopamine beta-hydroxylase', 'Dopamine beta-hydroxylase, a copper-containing enzyme in chromaffin granules, converts dopamine to norepinephrine. PNMT then converts norepinephrine to epinephrine in the adrenal medulla.'),
('2566e80c-06e2-569a-b2eb-c890b655e1a7'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Phenylethanolamine N-methyltransferase (PNMT) converts:', '["Tyrosine to DOPA","Epinephrine to metanephrine","Norepinephrine to epinephrine","Dopamine to norepinephrine","DOPA to dopamine"]'::jsonb, 'Norepinephrine to epinephrine', 'PNMT converts norepinephrine to epinephrine in the adrenal medulla. It is induced by cortisol from the adrenal cortex, which reaches the medulla via the portal circulation.'),
('b8dc492b-060a-58d7-a297-34a46f2c5bd4'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'GABA (gamma-aminobutyric acid) is derived from:', '["Alanine","Serine","Aspartate","Glycine","Glutamate"]'::jsonb, 'Glutamate', 'GABA is formed by decarboxylation of glutamate via glutamic acid decarboxylase (GAD, requires PLP). GABA is the major inhibitory neurotransmitter in the CNS.'),
('a77a2f1e-085f-51a8-bbf6-bec21f1e320e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Glycine acts as an inhibitory neurotransmitter in which part of the CNS?', '["Spinal cord and brainstem","Cerebral cortex","Thalamus","Hippocampus","Cerebellum"]'::jsonb, 'Spinal cord and brainstem', 'Glycine is the major inhibitory neurotransmitter in the spinal cord and brainstem. Strychnine poisoning blocks glycine receptors, causing severe muscle spasms.'),
('a018eb8f-d7b1-5e1b-9126-85ac95e9cc9e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Creatine is synthesized from which amino acids?', '["Glycine and methionine","Arginine and ornithine","Glycine and glutamate","Serine and alanine","Lysine and histidine"]'::jsonb, 'Glycine and methionine', 'Creatine is synthesized from glycine, arginine, and methionine (as SAM). Creatine phosphate serves as a rapidly mobilizable energy reserve in muscle.'),
('02ad7809-aac1-5be0-9359-128dc191dde4'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Nitric oxide (NO) is synthesized from:', '["Arginine","Histidine","Lysine","Tryptophan","Tyrosine"]'::jsonb, 'Arginine', 'Nitric oxide is produced from arginine by nitric oxide synthase (NOS). It activates guanylate cyclase to raise cGMP, causing vascular smooth muscle relaxation (vasodilation).'),
('7b2570ce-08dc-5ff9-821d-c42b7ce766c9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Glutathione (GSH) is which tripeptide?', '["Glutamyl-cysteinyl-serine","Gamma-glutamyl-seryl-glycine","Gamma-glutamyl-cysteinyl-glycine","Gamma-glutamyl-cysteinyl-glutamate","Glutamyl-cysteinyl-alanine"]'::jsonb, 'Gamma-glutamyl-cysteinyl-glycine', 'Glutathione is gamma-glutamyl-cysteinyl-glycine, containing an unusual gamma-peptide bond. It is the major intracellular antioxidant, keeping proteins'' thiols reduced and detoxifying peroxides.'),
('7e7f58b4-da8d-573a-80d4-3141ce8202d0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'The functions of glutathione include:', '["Antioxidant defense","Amino acid transport","Conjugation reactions","Coenzyme for some enzymes","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Glutathione serves as an antioxidant (reducing peroxides via glutathione peroxidase), participates in amino acid transport (gamma-glutamyl cycle), conjugates xenobiotics, and acts as a coenzyme.'),
('868b2f43-65b8-51e7-9bbe-f5cab23e48e9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'The gamma-glutamyl cycle is responsible for:', '["Glutathione synthesis","Glutathione degradation","Amino acid transport across cell membranes","Detoxification","Amino acid catabolism"]'::jsonb, 'Amino acid transport across cell membranes', 'The gamma-glutamyl cycle transports amino acids across cell membranes: glutathione transfers its gamma-glutamyl group to amino acids, facilitating their uptake.'),
('17c11059-c0a0-5ff4-98ca-b303c408055a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Which enzyme is part of the gamma-glutamyl cycle and has clinical diagnostic value?', '["Gamma-glutamyl cyclotransferase","Gamma-glutamyl transferase (GGT)","Glutathione peroxidase","Glutathione synthetase","Gamma-glutamylcysteine synthetase"]'::jsonb, 'Gamma-glutamyl transferase (GGT)', 'Gamma-glutamyl transferase (GGT) is a sensitive marker of hepatobiliary disease and alcohol use. It is elevated in cholestasis and enzyme-inducing drug exposure.'),
('21a58627-c650-5e70-b416-94a50c5fef82'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'The aromatic amino acids include:', '["Tryptophan","Phenylalanine","Tyrosine","Histidine","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Phenylalanine', 'The aromatic amino acids are phenylalanine, tyrosine, and tryptophan, which contain an aromatic ring. They absorb UV light at 280 nm, used in protein quantitation.'),
('fb3af889-93b3-5c76-b0f6-4c20b54f06eb'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'The sulfur-containing amino acids are:', '["Tryptophan and tyrosine","Leucine and isoleucine","Glycine and serine","Lysine and arginine","Methionine and cysteine"]'::jsonb, 'Methionine and cysteine', 'Methionine and cysteine are the sulfur-containing amino acids. Methionine provides the methyl donor SAM; cysteine forms disulfide bonds and is the limiting substrate for glutathione synthesis.'),
('2ad4eaba-11f5-5745-8f7b-b5abbea19876'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'The positively charged (basic) amino acids are:', '["Aspartate","Arginine","Histidine","Lysine","Glutamate"]'::jsonb, 'Lysine', 'The basic amino acids are lysine, arginine, and histidine, which have positively charged R groups at physiological pH. They interact with DNA phosphate backbones and acidic residues.'),
('851e8522-6d07-52ea-82de-143059f37593'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'The negatively charged (acidic) amino acids are:', '["Asparagine and glutamine","Lysine and arginine","Aspartate and glutamate","Valine and leucine","Serine and threonine"]'::jsonb, 'Aspartate and glutamate', 'The acidic amino acids aspartate and glutamate have carboxyl side chains that are negatively charged at physiological pH.'),
('896f5f0f-28d9-561b-923f-5aff2145f269'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Cysteine can form:', '["Hydrophobic bonds","Disulfide bonds","Hydrogen bonds","Peptide bonds","Ionic bonds"]'::jsonb, 'Disulfide bonds', 'Cysteine''s thiol group can form covalent disulfide bonds, which stabilize protein tertiary and quaternary structure (e.g., insulin, immunoglobulins).'),
('826fdc08-2052-5323-9a8d-1475a6706a3b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Selenocysteine is considered the:', '["21st amino acid","25th amino acid","24th amino acid","22nd amino acid","23rd amino acid"]'::jsonb, '21st amino acid', 'Selenocysteine is the 21st genetically encoded amino acid, inserted at the UGA codon via a special selenocysteine insertion machinery. It is found in glutathione peroxidase.'),
('09346e44-c833-5f63-8535-4b2786299ddf'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'The isoelectric point (pI) is the pH at which an amino acid:', '["Has maximum solubility","Is fully deprotonated","Has no net charge","Has minimum solubility","Is fully protonated"]'::jsonb, 'Has no net charge', 'The isoelectric point (pI) is the pH at which an amino acid or protein has no net charge (zwitterion form). At pI, proteins are least soluble and do not migrate in an electric field.'),
('7f39cbf3-b14a-5103-b02d-9bf17979d42f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Proline gives a yellow color with ninhydrin because:', '["It is hydrophobic","It lacks an amino group","It is cyclic","It has a secondary amino group","It is polar"]'::jsonb, 'It has a secondary amino group', 'Proline is an imino acid with a secondary amino group (NH in a ring). Ninhydrin reacts with proline to give a yellow color, unlike the purple-blue color of other amino acids.'),
('ff979833-6949-53c0-a1b8-4ddf5c426909'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'The Biuret test is specific for:', '["Carboxyl groups","Sulfhydryl groups","Aromatic rings","Peptide bonds","Amino groups"]'::jsonb, 'Peptide bonds', 'The Biuret test detects peptide bonds: in alkaline solution, Cu2+ forms a violet complex with two or more peptide bonds. It is positive for proteins and dipeptides but not free amino acids.'),
('23d649d0-d93f-59b4-94e4-2f66eafa110d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Edman degradation uses phenylisothiocyanate (PITC) to:', '["Sequentially remove N-terminal amino acids","Cleave at methionine residues","Crosslink chains","Cleave at tryptophan residues","Remove C-terminal amino acids"]'::jsonb, 'Sequentially remove N-terminal amino acids', 'Edman degradation sequentially removes and identifies N-terminal amino acids as PTH derivatives. It can sequence up to 50-70 residues from the N-terminus.'),
('6b856a08-c833-5ca5-8e2f-6ac057c39711'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Cyanogen bromide (CNBr) cleaves at:', '["C-terminal side of tryptophan","N-terminal side of methionine","C-terminal side of methionine","Both sides of cysteine","N-terminal side of tryptophan"]'::jsonb, 'C-terminal side of methionine', 'Cyanogen bromide (CNBr) cleaves peptide bonds on the C-terminal side of methionine residues. Trypsin cleaves C-terminal to Lys/Arg; chymotrypsin cleaves C-terminal to aromatic residues.'),
('617d97ee-2681-577b-969e-d481358bc21a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Collagen has an unusual amino acid composition rich in:', '["And hydroxyproline","Proline","Arginine","Glycine","Lysine"]'::jsonb, 'Glycine', 'Collagen has the repeating motif Gly-X-Y, rich in glycine (every third residue), proline, and hydroxyproline (from hydroxylation of proline requiring vitamin C).'),
('c1da0106-48e5-5d83-96a3-85e8b2b02620'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'During therapeutic management, Phase II conjugation reaction:', '["Glucuronidation","Oxidation","Reduction","Hydrolysis","Deamination"]'::jsonb, 'Glucuronidation', 'Phase II: glucuronidation, sulfation, acetylation, methylation, glutathione conjugation.'),
('9af9f475-f77e-5528-99e5-cafb8ea224cf'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Type III collagen (reticular fibers) is found in:', '["Cartilage","Blood vessels and fetal tissue","Skin and tendon","Basement membrane","Bone and teeth"]'::jsonb, 'Blood vessels and fetal tissue', 'Type III collagen (reticular fibers) is found in blood vessels, uterus, and fetal tissue. It is deficient in vascular Ehlers-Danlos syndrome.'),
('3d730d15-3eb6-5183-8d15-3c60800eb10c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Type IV collagen is a network-forming collagen found in:', '["Basement membrane","Bone","Cornea","Tendon","Cartilage"]'::jsonb, 'Basement membrane', 'Type IV collagen forms sheet-like networks in basement membranes and is produced by epithelial and endothelial cells. Anti-GBM antibodies target it in Goodpasture syndrome.'),
('90d1076a-9c89-5e14-bab0-d98ed1a77e17'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Ehlers-Danlos syndrome is a disorder of collagen synthesis causing:', '["Skin hyperelasticity","And fragile blood vessels","Neurodegeneration","Muscle weakness","Joint hypermobility"]'::jsonb, 'Joint hypermobility', 'Ehlers-Danlos syndrome results from defects in collagen synthesis/processing. Features include hypermobile joints, hyperelastic skin, easy bruising, and poor wound healing.'),
('18609eae-1bb5-5656-ad26-b2bd5d7ea7fb'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Desmosine and isodesmosine are cross-links in:', '["Collagen","Elastin","Actin","Fibrin","Keratin"]'::jsonb, 'Elastin', 'Desmosine and isodesmosine are unique cross-links of elastin, formed from lysine residues. They give elastin its ability to stretch and recoil.'),
('97bc54f1-ff2c-581e-a431-ef1e76419627'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Heat shock proteins (HSPs) function as:', '["Enzymes","Receptors","Structural proteins","Molecular chaperones","Antibodies"]'::jsonb, 'Molecular chaperones', 'Heat shock proteins (HSPs) are molecular chaperones that assist protein folding, prevent aggregation of misfolded proteins, and refold denatured proteins under stress.'),
('7622d4c4-11d8-546b-9cc8-02320531ad75'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Prion diseases include:', '["Mad cow disease (BSE)","Kuru","Alzheimer disease","Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease","Fatal familial insomnia"]'::jsonb, 'Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease', 'Prion diseases (transmissible spongiform encephalopathies) include CJD, kuru, BSE (mad cow), and fatal familial insomnia. They are caused by misfolded prion protein (PrPSc).'),
('c68ec70b-9a10-5561-918b-9b3f6dab4df1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Protein misfolding in Alzheimer disease involves:', '["Alpha-synuclein","Amyloid-beta plaques and tau tangles","Huntingtin repeats","Prion aggregates","TDP-43 inclusions"]'::jsonb, 'Amyloid-beta plaques and tau tangles', 'Alzheimer disease features extracellular amyloid-beta plaques and intracellular neurofibrillary tangles of hyperphosphorylated tau. Parkinson features alpha-synuclein (Lewy bodies).'),
('755f5ea2-72dc-5445-9ad8-2207fe451952'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Huntington disease involves:', '["Polyglutamine expansion in huntingtin","Point mutation","Trinucleotide repeats in non-coding region","Expansion of polyalanine repeats","Deletion of huntingtin gene"]'::jsonb, 'Polyglutamine expansion in huntingtin', 'Huntington disease is caused by CAG (polyglutamine) repeat expansion in the huntingtin gene. The mutant protein misfolds and aggregates, causing neurodegeneration with chorea.'),
('0221a6f7-0738-5fda-a2cd-a6660e552134'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'The ubiquitin-proteasome pathway is used for:', '["Protein degradation","Protein folding","Protein synthesis","DNA repair","Cell signaling"]'::jsonb, 'Protein degradation', 'The ubiquitin-proteasome pathway degrades damaged, misfolded, or short-lived proteins. Ubiquitin tags proteins (E1-E2-E3 cascade) for degradation by the 26S proteasome.'),
('4ab98d68-36e2-5aa0-8c0a-4ee63671661f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Autophagy is a process for:', '["Degrading cellular components in lysosomes","Synthesizing proteins","Importing proteins","Exporting proteins","Folding proteins"]'::jsonb, 'Degrading cellular components in lysosomes', 'Autophagy degrades and recycles long-lived proteins and organelles by engulfing them in autophagosomes that fuse with lysosomes. It is upregulated during starvation.'),
('4734968f-f9c3-5215-9a46-8b019718c760'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Fibrinogen is synthesized in the liver and is important for:', '["Oxygen transport","Blood clotting","Immune defense","Iron transport","Calcium homeostasis"]'::jsonb, 'Blood clotting', 'Fibrinogen is a plasma protein synthesized in the liver. Thrombin converts it to fibrin, which polymerizes to form a blood clot. It is an acute-phase reactant.'),
('35b0c66f-ff85-5123-91d0-839a9af0a9e8'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Which plasma protein transports thyroxine?', '["Ceruloplasmin resulting in decreased functional activity","Transferrin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Haptoglobin mediated by specific receptor activation","Thyroxine-binding globulin (TBG)","Albumin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thyroxine-binding globulin (TBG)', 'Thyroxine-binding globulin (TBG) is the main plasma carrier of thyroid hormones T4 and T3. Transthyretin and albumin are secondary carriers.'),
('2d582c11-73f3-52e4-88d1-704ed061cd7b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'The first immunoglobulin produced in a primary immune response is:', '["IgM","IgG","IgD","IgE","IgA"]'::jsonb, 'IgM', 'IgM is the first antibody produced in a primary immune response (pentamer, strong complement activation). IgG predominates in secondary responses and crosses the placenta.'),
('51e6cdd3-987d-5e48-9bd5-0cf3e2df9920'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations: Loading dose primarily depends on:', '["Volume of distribution","Clearance","Half-life","Bioavailability","Renal function"]'::jsonb, 'Volume of distribution', 'Loading dose = Vd x Css / F.'),
('6a37274f-88e8-5e44-a75c-7d64a126733e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'The structure of an IgG molecule consists of:', '["Two heavy chains only","Four light chains","Two heavy chains and two light chains","Four heavy chains","One heavy and one light chain"]'::jsonb, 'Two heavy chains and two light chains', 'IgG is aY-shaped monomer of two identical heavy chains and two identical light chains, linked by disulfide bonds, with two antigen-binding Fab regions and one Fc region.'),
('e17cf96a-fd0b-5e4f-9f33-4b08abe418a5'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Monoclonal antibodies are produced by:', '["Phage display only","Polyclonal activation","Recombinant DNA technology only","Naturally in response to antigen","Hybridoma technology"]'::jsonb, 'Hybridoma technology', 'Monoclonal antibodies are produced by hybridoma technology: fusing an antibody-producing B cell with a myeloma cell to create an immortal cell line secreting a single antibody.'),
('8b865aa0-54b9-57d7-ba22-2f3179fe43cb'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Amyloidosis involves extracellular deposition of:', '["Collagen resulting in decreased functional activity","Keratin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fibrillar proteins in beta-pleated sheet configuration","Fibrin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Elastin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fibrillar proteins in beta-pleated sheet configuration', 'Amyloid is composed of misfolded proteins in a beta-pleated sheet configuration that aggregate into insoluble fibrils. Congo red staining shows apple-green birefringence under polarized light.'),
('e01891c3-3e7f-59d2-b0e5-c855ed56741a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Primary amyloidosis (AL type) involves deposition of:', '["Amyloid-beta","Beta-2 microglobulin","Serum amyloid A (SAA)","Transthyretin","Immunoglobulin light chains"]'::jsonb, 'Immunoglobulin light chains', 'AL amyloidosis is caused by deposition of immunoglobulin light chains from a plasma cell clone. AA amyloidosis involves serum amyloid A in chronic inflammation.'),
('b9424be0-76c9-551a-9aca-1de5667d85bd'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Secondary amyloidosis (AA type) involves deposition of:', '["Immunoglobulin light chains","Serum amyloid A","Beta-2 microglobulin","Transthyretin","Amyloid-beta"]'::jsonb, 'Serum amyloid A', 'AA amyloidosis occurs in chronic inflammatory conditions (RA, TB, chronic infection) with deposition of serum amyloid A, an acute-phase protein.'),
('c2221a2e-4a28-52cf-8c19-f8138afe3b59'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Dialysis separates proteins from:', '["Small molecules","Other proteins","Bacteria","Organelles","Virus"]'::jsonb, 'Small molecules', 'Dialysis uses a semipermeable membrane to remove small molecules (salts, urea) from protein solutions while retaining large proteins. The driving force is a concentration gradient.'),
('a0e50c56-efbb-5e96-94a4-9d3b1cbb09ec'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Beta-mercaptoethanol in SDS-PAGE is used to:', '["Oxidize disulfide bonds","Stain proteins","Prevent aggregation","Denature proteins","Reduce disulfide bonds"]'::jsonb, 'Reduce disulfide bonds', 'Beta-mercaptoethanol (or DTT) reduces disulfide bonds, breaking proteins into individual polypeptide chains before SDS-PAGE so subunits migrate separately.'),
('e26ba623-4ae7-5592-b7d1-0c16a620e7cc'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'SDS-PAGE separates proteins primarily by:', '["Net charge","Native shape","Isoelectric point","Molecular weight","Solubility"]'::jsonb, 'Molecular weight', 'SDS denatures proteins and imparts a uniform negative charge per unit mass, so SDS-PAGE separates proteins almost exclusively by molecular weight.'),
('eb43bd5a-689f-5828-a4e7-dab7142aeb2f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Mass spectrometry identifies proteins by:', '["Measuring mass-to-charge ratio of peptides","Measuring absorbance","Sedimentation rate","Electrophoretic mobility","Enzymatic activity"]'::jsonb, 'Measuring mass-to-charge ratio of peptides', 'Mass spectrometry measures the mass-to-charge ratio (m/z) of peptide ions, enabling protein identification by peptide mass fingerprinting and tandem MS (MS/MS) sequencing.'),
('47ae6e4e-b51a-5de9-ab86-44329ab60642'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Western blotting uses:', '["RNA probes to detect transcripts","DNA probes to detect genes","Lectin to detect carbohydrates","Lipid probes","Antibodies to detect specific proteins"]'::jsonb, 'Antibodies to detect specific proteins', 'Western blot uses antibodies to detect specific proteins transferred from an SDS-PAGE gel to a membrane. It is used for HIV confirmation and measuring specific protein expression.'),
('08a1bbfa-ec9b-50aa-b1e1-c232f8ddbf21'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Radioimmunoassay (RIA) uses:', '["Chemiluminescent labels","Fluorescent labels","Radiolabeled antigen or antibody","Gold particles","Enzyme labels"]'::jsonb, 'Radiolabeled antigen or antibody', 'RIA uses a radiolabeled antigen or antibody in competitive binding to quantify analytes at very low concentrations (e.g., hormones, drugs).'),
('bba46283-e2a0-5362-8d34-3ab7c524c390'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'The protein data bank (PDB) is a repository for:', '["Metabolic pathways","Nucleic acid sequences","Protein sequences","Literature references","3D structures of proteins and nucleic acids"]'::jsonb, '3D structures of proteins and nucleic acids', 'The Protein Data Bank (PDB) is the central repository for experimentally determined 3D structures of proteins and nucleic acids (X-ray crystallography, NMR, cryo-EM).'),
('cd646701-83e4-5199-a3a3-2a8899e758d9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Disallowed regions in a Ramachandran plot indicate:', '["Frequently observed conformations","Steric clashes or high-energy conformations","Beta-sheet conformations","Alpha-helical regions","Random coil regions"]'::jsonb, 'Steric clashes or high-energy conformations', 'In a Ramachandran plot, allowed regions show phi/psi angles with no steric clash. Disallowed regions represent high-energy conformations that are rarely observed.'),
('342145b2-a04b-51c0-9513-ecef3316ccf9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Protein domains are:', '["Secondary structures","Independent folding units with specific functions","Post-translational modifications","Individual amino acids","Peptide bonds"]'::jsonb, 'Independent folding units with specific functions', 'Domains are independently folding, structurally stable regions of a protein that often have specific functions (e.g., DNA-binding domain, catalytic domain).'),
('b5d2f60e-1f3e-5647-89ab-3160098939da'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Zinc finger motifs are involved in:', '["Cell adhesion","DNA binding","Enzyme catalysis","Antibody structure","Membrane anchoring"]'::jsonb, 'DNA binding', 'Zinc fingers are small DNA-binding motifs stabilized by a zinc ion coordinated by cysteine and histidine residues. They are found in many transcription factors.'),
('ce287b65-5b20-59ae-a321-d2c61298225c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Helix-turn-helix motifs are commonly found in:', '["Prokaryotic DNA-binding proteins","Membrane proteins","Structural proteins","Antibodies","Enzymes"]'::jsonb, 'Prokaryotic DNA-binding proteins', 'The helix-turn-helix motif is a DNA-binding motif common in prokaryotic repressors and activators (e.g., lac repressor, lambda repressor).'),
('2f54a86c-7738-5afb-b01e-e0d053a270d9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'G proteins are:', '["Glycoproteins","GTP-binding proteins","Glucose transporters","Growth factor receptors","Glutamate receptors"]'::jsonb, 'GTP-binding proteins', 'G proteins are heterotrimeric GTP-binding proteins (alpha, beta, gamma subunits) that transduce signals from GPCRs. They are active when bound to GTP and inactive when bound to GDP.'),
('74d2b740-fc98-5cf1-8038-e98d6f697069'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Mutations in Ras are commonly found in:', '["Cardiovascular disease","Diabetes","Cancers","Immune disorders","Neurodegenerative disease"]'::jsonb, 'Cancers', 'Ras is a monomeric G protein that relays growth signals (Raf-MEK-ERK). Oncogenic Ras mutations, common in many cancers, lock it in the active GTP-bound state causing uncontrolled proliferation.'),
('8a46fe5a-35f4-5380-a306-bcec2f1a7d12'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'JAK-STAT pathway is primarily activated by:', '["Cytokines and interferons","G-protein coupled receptors","Steroid hormones","Receptor tyrosine kinases","Growth factors"]'::jsonb, 'Cytokines and interferons', 'The JAK-STAT pathway is activated by cytokines and interferons. Ligand binding causes JAK to phosphorylate STAT, which dimerizes and translocates to the nucleus to regulate gene transcription.'),
('43cf7e40-88f5-510a-8f48-991b9910132b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'The PI3K-Akt pathway promotes:', '["Cell cycle arrest","DNA repair","Cell survival and growth","Apoptosis","Inflammation"]'::jsonb, 'Cell survival and growth', 'The PI3K-Akt pathway promotes cell survival, growth, and proliferation. Insulin and growth factors activate it via receptor tyrosine kinases and IRS proteins.'),
('35324168-831a-5e13-8108-22816d3b7e43'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Notch signaling involves:', '["CAMP production resulting in decreased functional activity","GTPase activation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Proteolytic cleavage and nuclear translocation of the intracellular domain","Phosphorylation cascade without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ion channel opening leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Proteolytic cleavage and nuclear translocation of the intracellular domain', 'In Notch signaling, ligand binding triggers proteolytic cleavage of Notch, releasing its intracellular domain, which translocates to the nucleus to regulate transcription.'),
('8433db5d-879a-5770-a876-6b09acb38f3e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Cytochrome c release from mitochondria triggers:', '["Autophagy","Intrinsic pathway","Cell proliferation","Apoptosis via caspase activation","Necrosis"]'::jsonb, 'Apoptosis via caspase activation', 'Release of cytochrome c from mitochondria activates the intrinsic (mitochondrial) apoptotic pathway, forming the apoptosome with Apaf-1 and activating initiator caspase-9.'),
('11198e06-7b26-5740-9f1d-95335e6d2bac'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Acetylation of histones is associated with:', '["Heterochromatin formation","Activated gene transcription","Gene silencing","No significant change","DNA condensation"]'::jsonb, 'Activated gene transcription', 'Histone acetylation neutralizes positive charges on lysines, loosening chromatin and promoting gene transcription. Histone deacetylases (HDACs) reverse this and repress genes.'),
('fb26a805-0e98-53c5-a164-06e5174ba756'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Beta-sheet structures can be:', '["Antiparallel","Parallel","Or mixed","Only parallel","Only antiparallel"]'::jsonb, 'Parallel', 'Beta-pleated sheets can be parallel, antiparallel, or mixed. Antiparallel sheets have slightly more stable hydrogen bonding; fibroin (silk) is an antiparallel beta-sheet.'),
('7e0a1cd3-6f76-5a55-b2d3-78bf3d1e0a4c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Reverse turns (beta-turns) are characterized by:', '["Beta-sheet termination resulting in decreased functional activity","Alpha-helix initiation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","90-degree turn mediated by specific receptor activation","180-degree reversal of polypeptide direction","Linear segment leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '180-degree reversal of polypeptide direction', 'Beta-turns reverse the direction of the polypeptide chain (180 degrees), connecting strands of beta-sheets. They often contain glycine and proline at positions i+1 and i+2.'),
('bb6a5172-ef40-5868-ada4-d6a6f3163a01'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'The collagen triple helix is stabilized by:', '["Electrostatic interactions","Hydrogen bonds","Disulfide bonds","Steric repulsion from proline residues","Hydrophobic interactions"]'::jsonb, 'Hydrogen bonds', 'The collagen triple helix is stabilized by inter-chain hydrogen bonds and the tight packing of glycine (every third residue) at the helix core. Hydroxylation of proline/lysine requires vitamin C.'),
('45a92907-58e9-5e57-878d-5b697937a47a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Protein denaturation involves:', '["Oxidation of side chains","Hydrolysis of peptide bonds","Loss of primary structure","Loss of native three-dimensional structure","Formation of new bonds"]'::jsonb, 'Loss of native three-dimensional structure', 'Denaturation disrupts secondary, tertiary, and quaternary structure without breaking peptide bonds (primary structure intact). It is caused by heat, pH extremes, urea, or detergents.'),
('89e023af-3175-5bef-8dce-f84f4df533b8'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Which statement reflects Anfinsen''s principle?', '["All information for tertiary structure is in the primary sequence","Heat is required for folding accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only small proteins can refold mediated by specific receptor activation","Folding is irreversible without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Chaperones are required leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'All information for tertiary structure is in the primary sequence', 'Anfinsen''s principle (thermodynamic hypothesis) states that the amino acid sequence (primary structure) contains all information needed for a protein to adopt its native 3D structure.'),
('bb78d4b6-a35f-5636-878f-e2cab730cd0c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'The unfolded protein response (UPR) is activated by:', '["Viral infection resulting in decreased functional activity","Nutrient depletion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Accumulation of misfolded proteins in the ER","Oxidative stress without affecting the primary tissue pathway","DNA damage leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Accumulation of misfolded proteins in the ER', 'The unfolded protein response is triggered by accumulation of misfolded proteins in the endoplasmic reticulum. It reduces translation, increases chaperones, and can trigger apoptosis if unresolved.'),
('d0491060-df44-5df4-b247-4a48f85edf85'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Disulfide isomerase (PDI) catalyzes:', '["Protein folding resulting in decreased functional activity","Formation and rearrangement of disulfide bonds","Glycosylation mediated by specific receptor activation","Phosphorylation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Peptide bond formation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Formation and rearrangement of disulfide bonds', 'Protein disulfide isomerase (PDI) catalyzes the formation, reduction, and rearrangement of disulfide bonds in the ER, ensuring correct disulfide pairing during protein folding.'),
('0bf5d817-bc56-5353-a626-9092075cc9d0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'The prion protein (PrP) in its normal cellular form is:', '["PrPC (alpha-helical rich)","PrPSc (beta-sheet rich)","Amyloid-beta","Alpha-synuclein","Tau"]'::jsonb, 'PrPC (alpha-helical rich)', 'Normal cellular prion protein (PrPC) is rich in alpha-helices and is harmless. The infectious form (PrPSc) is rich in beta-sheet, aggregates, and converts normal PrPC to the misfolded form.'),
('c5d2ce07-6289-544e-9504-bc3dcb2af0ae'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'The infectious prion form PrPSc is characterized by:', '["High beta-sheet content and aggregation","Random coil accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","High alpha-helix content mediated by specific receptor activation","Monomers without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Solubility leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'High beta-sheet content and aggregation', 'PrPSc has high beta-sheet content, is protease-resistant, and aggregates into amyloid-like fibrils. It propagates by templating conversion of PrPC.'),
('0833b94b-48d3-5af2-a004-352285e56b2b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Protein aggregation diseases include:', '["Alzheimer","Prion diseases","Parkinson","Diabetes only","Huntington"]'::jsonb, 'Alzheimer', 'Protein aggregation diseases include Alzheimer (amyloid-beta/tau), Parkinson (alpha-synuclein), Huntington (polyglutamine huntingtin), prion diseases, and type 2 diabetes (islet amyloid).'),
('9084ae83-8536-599d-bb85-3bec2f17965d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Sickle cell disease is caused by a point mutation in:', '["Beta-globin (Glu6Lys)","Beta-globin (Glu6Val)","Gamma-globin","Alpha-globin","Delta-globin"]'::jsonb, 'Beta-globin (Glu6Val)', 'Sickle cell disease results from a single base substitution in the beta-globin gene changing glutamate to valine at position 6 (GAG to GTG). HbS polymerizes under low oxygen, causing RBC sickling.'),
('f1d1c9c3-314b-5e12-8cac-69c2ca23127c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'The heterozygous state for HbS (sickle cell trait) provides:', '["Increased susceptibility to malaria","Protection","Immunity to all infections","Anemia only","Protection against falciparum malaria"]'::jsonb, 'Protection against falciparum malaria', 'Sickle cell trait (HbAS) provides protection against falciparum malaria because parasitized RBCs sickle and are cleared, reducing parasite survival.'),
('d00733c9-0247-5035-b226-1a14c31f33d2'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Beta-thalassemia results from:', '["Increased beta-globin synthesis","Decreased or absent beta-globin synthesis","Gamma-globin deficiency","Delta-globin deficiency","Decreased alpha-globin synthesis"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased or absent beta-globin synthesis', 'Beta-thalassemia is caused by reduced or absent beta-globin chain synthesis, leading to excess alpha chains that precipitate and cause ineffective erythropoiesis.'),
('149f2bd5-656f-5d1c-8fa0-a86e2694d229'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'HbA2 (found in normal adults) is composed of:', '["Two alpha and two beta chains","Two alpha and two delta chains","Two alpha and two gamma chains","Two gamma and two beta chains","Two alpha and two epsilon chains"]'::jsonb, 'Two alpha and two delta chains', 'HbA2 is composed of two alpha and two delta chains (alpha2 delta2). It is elevated in beta-thalassemia trait, making it a useful diagnostic marker.'),
('d615ab2f-6155-5745-8254-98d7ab633f58'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'HbF (fetal hemoglobin) is composed of:', '["Two gamma and two epsilon chains","Two alpha and two gamma chains","Two gamma and two beta chains","Two alpha and two delta chains","Two alpha and two beta chains"]'::jsonb, 'Two alpha and two gamma chains', 'Fetal hemoglobin (HbF) is alpha2 gamma2. Its high oxygen affinity (due to weaker 2,3-BPG binding) helps transfer oxygen from maternal to fetal blood across the placenta.'),
('81ed120f-526e-54d4-9b26-8c551842b640'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'The heme group contains iron in which form in functional hemoglobin?', '["Free iron","Fe3+ (ferric)","Fe2+ (ferrous)","Fe4+ (ferryl)","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Fe2+ (ferrous)', 'Functional hemoglobin contains iron in the ferrous (Fe2+) state, which can reversibly bind oxygen. Oxidation to ferric (Fe3+) produces methemoglobin, which cannot carry oxygen.'),
('1dbf6e36-a841-5bff-b038-3a2818e3e98d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'The oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve is:', '["Hyperbolic","Exponential","Linear","Parabolic","Sigmoidal"]'::jsonb, 'Sigmoidal', 'The oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve is sigmoidal because hemoglobin binds oxygen cooperatively. Myoglobin, without cooperativity, shows a hyperbolic curve.'),
('8427fa0c-27c1-5530-9001-b4cefb897825'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Myoglobin has a:', '["Linear curve","Cooperative sigmoidal curve","Exponential curve","Hyperbolic oxygen-binding curve","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperbolic oxygen-binding curve', 'Myoglobin is a monomer with a hyperbolic oxygen-binding curve, reflecting its high affinity for oxygen and lack of cooperativity. It stores oxygen in muscle.'),
('2c2a53a1-2dad-542c-a601-9cf5b6780415'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'The R (relaxed) state of hemoglobin is:', '["Oxygenated (high affinity)","Met form","T state","Denatured form","Deoxygenated (low affinity)"]'::jsonb, 'Oxygenated (high affinity)', 'The R (relaxed) state of hemoglobin has high oxygen affinity and is favored when oxygen is bound. The T (tense) state has low affinity and is stabilized by 2,3-BPG, protons, and CO2.'),
('7cd21fb0-a78f-539f-b378-2d532e7eff9d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'HbF has a higher oxygen affinity than HbA because it:', '["Has more subunits","Binds 2,3-BPG less strongly","Is monomeric","Lacks heme","Is denatured"]'::jsonb, 'Binds 2,3-BPG less strongly', 'Fetal hemoglobin (HbF) has higher oxygen affinity because it binds 2,3-BPG less strongly than HbA, facilitating oxygen transfer from mother to fetus.'),
('ab07acaf-e2d5-53d4-8f30-850470052eea'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'The Bohr effect describes the effect of pH and CO2 on:', '["DNA binding","Enzyme activity","Myoglobin-oxygen affinity","Protein folding","Hemoglobin-oxygen affinity"]'::jsonb, 'Hemoglobin-oxygen affinity', 'The Bohr effect: increased H+ (low pH) and CO2 stabilize the T state of hemoglobin, decreasing its oxygen affinity and promoting oxygen unloading in active tissues.'),
('cfef0d04-bb40-54fe-8d44-42a8b0215b52'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Which protein carries oxygen in red blood cells?', '["Albumin","Transferrin","Hemoglobin","Globulin","Fibrinogen"]'::jsonb, 'Hemoglobin', 'Hemoglobin carries oxygen in RBCs. Each molecule transports up to four O2 molecules via cooperative binding; CO2 is carried as bicarbonate and carbaminohemoglobin.'),
('b1834d3c-0d16-54bf-90a1-bcc757c65f95'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Carbonic anhydrase in RBCs converts:', '["Bicarbonate to CO2","CO2 to bicarbonate","Hemoglobin to carbaminohemoglobin","H2CO3 to CO2 and H2O","CO2 and H2O to H2CO3"]'::jsonb, 'CO2 and H2O to H2CO3', 'Carbonic anhydrase catalyzes CO2 + H2O to H2CO3, which dissociates to H+ and bicarbonate. This enables CO2 transport in blood as bicarbonate (70% of CO2).'),
('66bcf7e3-e241-5d82-a304-35e9de463d9a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'The chloride shift refers to exchange of:', '["HCO3- and K+ resulting in decreased functional activity","Na+ and K+ accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","HCO3- and Cl- across RBC membrane","Ca2+ and Cl- without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cl- and Na+ leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'HCO3- and Cl- across RBC membrane', 'The chloride (Hamburger) shift is the exchange of HCO3- out of and Cl- into RBCs to maintain electrical neutrality as bicarbonate is generated from CO2.'),
('e450fd09-7a81-5276-bc86-e9dae318915a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Protoporphyrin IX with iron forms:', '["Bilirubin","Urobilin","Stercobilin","Heme","Biliverdin"]'::jsonb, 'Heme', 'Heme consists of protoporphyrin IX complexed with ferrous iron. It is the prosthetic group of hemoglobin, myoglobin, and cytochromes.'),
('4fe71ffc-fb34-5145-b05d-41e894bbfcd8'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'The degradation of heme begins with:', '["Heme oxygenase cleaving heme to biliverdin","Conjugation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Glucuronidation mediated by specific receptor activation","Excretion in bile without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Reduction to bilirubin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Heme oxygenase cleaving heme to biliverdin', 'Heme oxygenase (in macrophages) cleaves the heme ring to biliverdin, releasing iron and CO. Biliverdin is then reduced to unconjugated bilirubin by biliverdin reductase.'),
('a9fd9641-588d-571a-8088-ee5ad16e8e98'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Biliverdin is reduced to bilirubin by:', '["Heme oxygenase","UDP-glucuronosyltransferase","NADPH dehydrogenase","Cytochrome P450","Biliverdin reductase"]'::jsonb, 'Biliverdin reductase', 'Biliverdin reductase converts biliverdin (green) to bilirubin (yellow) using NADPH. Bilirubin is transported to the liver bound to albumin.'),
('95ea0a56-ed9f-5a28-b6b5-1fa9d92b3a0d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Unconjugated bilirubin is transported in blood bound to:', '["Globulin","Albumin","Haptoglobin","Hemopexin","Transferrin"]'::jsonb, 'Albumin', 'Unconjugated (indirect) bilirubin is lipophilic and transported in blood bound to albumin. It is not water-soluble and cannot be excreted in urine.'),
('c4b0eb87-e836-5fa6-a7eb-52cd35b15aac'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Conjugation of bilirubin in the liver uses:', '["UDP-glucuronic acid","Glutathione","UDP-glucose","Acetyl-CoA","SAM"]'::jsonb, 'UDP-glucuronic acid', 'The liver conjugates bilirubin with UDP-glucuronic acid via UDP-glucuronosyltransferase (UGT1A1), making it water-soluble (direct bilirubin) for biliary excretion.'),
('b87060a4-6a9c-56eb-9ebb-85060a47f2a1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Conjugated bilirubin is excreted into:', '["Blood","Bile","CSF","Sweat","Urine"]'::jsonb, 'Bile', 'Conjugated (direct) bilirubin is excreted into bile, then into the intestine, where gut bacteria convert it to urobilinogen and stercobilin (excreted in feces).'),
('04281ff4-ca43-5a91-8624-cfa9ee1a80f4'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Jaundice becomes clinically apparent when serum bilirubin exceeds:', '["10 mg/dL","1 mg/dL","0.5 mg/dL","5 mg/dL","2 mg/dL"]'::jsonb, '2 mg/dL', 'Jaundice becomes visible when serum bilirubin exceeds 2 mg/dL. It is caused by increased bilirubin production, impaired hepatic uptake/conjugation, or obstructed biliary excretion.'),
('7ab7c6df-ad4f-5a08-b5e5-a5ea4501d48f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia is seen in:', '["Rotor syndrome","Obstructive jaundice","Hepatocellular injury","Dubin-Johnson syndrome","Hemolysis and Gilbert syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'Hemolysis and Gilbert syndrome', 'Unconjugated (indirect) hyperbilirubinemia occurs in hemolysis, Gilbert syndrome, and Crigler-Najjar syndrome (defective conjugation). Conjugated hyperbilirubinemia occurs in obstruction and Dubin-Johnson syndrome.'),
('a2abedb6-abbf-553c-a636-f9ec86c278e4'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Gilbert syndrome is due to:', '["Complete absence of UGT1A1","Liver failure","Hemolysis","Reduced UGT1A1 activity (mild)","Biliary obstruction"]'::jsonb, 'Reduced UGT1A1 activity (mild)', 'Gilbert syndrome is a mild reduction in UGT1A1 activity causing mild fluctuating unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia. It is benign, worsened by fasting or stress.'),
('722e4d9f-c18a-583a-aef3-d9b98f83f07c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Crigler-Najjar syndrome type I is due to:', '["Hemolysis","Liver failure","Reduced UGT1A1 activity","Complete absence of UGT1A1 activity","Biliary obstruction"]'::jsonb, 'Complete absence of UGT1A1 activity', 'Crigler-Najjar type I is complete UGT1A1 deficiency causing severe, often fatal unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia and kernicterus. Type II has partial deficiency.'),
('a6eb63ad-e897-5c01-80bf-446fdf7badb6'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules - Proteins', 'Neonatal jaundice is primarily due to:', '["Immature UGT1A1 activity and increased hemolysis","Gilbert syndrome accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Biliary atresia mediated by specific receptor activation","Hepatitis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Complete absence of UGT leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Immature UGT1A1 activity and increased hemolysis', 'Physiologic neonatal jaundice results from immature hepatic UGT1A1 activity and increased RBC turnover/hemolysis, causing transient unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia in the first week of life.'),
('b873e1cd-4411-5e81-a121-d1c3b5da715d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Enzymes are biological catalysts that are primarily composed of:', '["Proteins","Nucleic acids","Lipids","Carbohydrates","Minerals"]'::jsonb, 'Proteins', 'Enzymes are globular proteins that catalyze biochemical reactions by lowering activation energy. Some RNA molecules (ribozymes) also have catalytic activity.'),
('f9a39674-8f6d-580e-8f6a-90466a814a96'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'The term enzyme was coined by:', '["Kuhne","Fischer","Buchner","Sumner","Pasteur"]'::jsonb, 'Kuhne', 'Wilhelm Kuhne coined the term ''enzyme'' in 1878 from the Greek ''en zyme'' meaning ''in yeast''. Eduard Buchner demonstrated cell-free fermentation.'),
('2e4d5e64-ecc5-5caf-816b-b8511cf15f2b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Cofactors that are tightly bound to the enzyme are called:', '["Prosthetic groups","Apoenzymes","Activators","Holoenzymes","Coenzymes"]'::jsonb, 'Prosthetic groups', 'Prosthetic groups are permanently bound cofactors (e.g., heme in cytochrome c, FAD in succinate dehydrogenase). They remain attached during purification.'),
('2a307ef3-2ca2-5e93-9174-9fa1602638c8'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'What is the relationship between these components in the enzyme system?', '["Only cofactor","Apoenzyme + substrate","Only protein","Substrate + product","Apoenzyme + cofactor"]'::jsonb, 'Apoenzyme + cofactor', 'Holoenzyme = Apoenzyme (protein part) + Cofactor (non-protein part). The apoenzyme alone is inactive.'),
('24fd982a-8ee8-5fc0-bcc9-1aa4434a8a46'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Vitamin B1 (thiamine) is a precursor of:', '["NAD","TPP","PLP","CoA","FAD"]'::jsonb, 'TPP', 'Thiamine pyrophosphate (TPP) is the active coenzyme form of vitamin B1. It is required by pyruvate dehydrogenase, alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase, and transketolase.'),
('0077d194-e726-5620-a30b-eb9de09844a4'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Pyridoxal phosphate is a coenzyme for:', '["Hydrolases","Oxidases","Transaminases","Ligases","Isomerases"]'::jsonb, 'Transaminases', 'PLP (vitamin B6) is the coenzyme for transaminases, decarboxylases, and other amino acid-metabolating enzymes. It participates in group transfer reactions at the alpha-carbon.'),
('22756322-ba50-5aae-a21f-83364405633a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Which vitamin is a precursor of FAD?', '["Pyridoxine","Thiamine","Pantothenic acid","Niacin","Riboflavin"]'::jsonb, 'Riboflavin', 'Riboflavin (vitamin B2) is the precursor for FAD and FMN, which function as electron carriers in oxidation-reduction reactions.'),
('8feedb23-2c25-545d-a653-00688cb3bd98'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Coenzyme A is derived from which vitamin?', '["Biotin","Niacin","Folic acid","Pantothenic acid","Riboflavin"]'::jsonb, 'Pantothenic acid', 'Coenzyme A (CoA-SH) contains pantothenic acid (vitamin B5) and carries acyl groups via thioester bonds. Acetyl-CoA is the key metabolic intermediate.'),
('713ed113-41b9-51f7-9a8d-22e89a136cd2'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'The EC classification system divides enzymes into how many main classes?', '["5","4","7","3","6"]'::jsonb, '6', 'The EC system classifies enzymes into 6 major classes: oxidoreductases (1), transferases (2), hydrolases (3), lyases (4), isomerases (5), and ligases (6).'),
('8a69b5cf-fcda-5e84-b8f9-388fc7db444e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'EC 1 enzymes are called:', '["Isomerases","Lyases","Oxidoreductases","Hydrolases","Transferases"]'::jsonb, 'Oxidoreductases', 'EC 1 (oxidoreductases) catalyze oxidation-reduction reactions. Examples include dehydrogenases, oxidases, and reductases.'),
('31f21fdb-5652-5bbb-99e9-54c983cd17cd'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'EC 2 enzymes catalyze:', '["Addition to double bonds","Isomerization","Oxidation-reduction","Hydrolysis","Transfer of functional groups"]'::jsonb, 'Transfer of functional groups', 'EC 2 (transferases) transfer functional groups between molecules. Examples include transaminases, kinases, and methyltransferases.'),
('d50983e8-f44e-5ecc-b8f9-77307ad84258'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'EC 4 enzymes (lyases) catalyze:', '["Isomerization resulting in decreased functional activity","Group transfer accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Oxidation-reduction mediated by specific receptor activation","Hydrolysis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cleavage without hydrolysis or oxidation"]'::jsonb, 'Cleavage without hydrolysis or oxidation', 'EC 4 (lyases) catalyze non-hydrolytic, non-oxidative cleavage or formation of bonds. Examples include decarboxylases and aldolases.'),
('e024259e-f51e-5f7f-b3ce-82136fc62017'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Hexokinase belongs to which EC class?', '["EC 5 (Isomerase)","EC 1 (Oxidoreductase)","EC 3 (Hydrolase)","EC 4 (Lyase)","EC 2 (Transferase)"]'::jsonb, 'EC 2 (Transferase)', 'Hexokinase catalyzes glucose + ATP to glucose-6-phosphate (transfer of phosphoryl group). Low Km (0.1 mM) for high glucose affinity. Glucokinase in liver has high Km (10 mM).'),
('176d1fc9-a1af-561e-b5ea-1a6e1051835f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Lactate dehydrogenase belongs to which EC class?', '["EC 1 (Oxidoreductase)","EC 6 (Ligase)","EC 3 (Hydrolase)","EC 2 (Transferase)","EC 4 (Lyase)"]'::jsonb, 'EC 1 (Oxidoreductase)', 'LDH converts pyruvate to lactate, regenerating NAD+ for glycolysis. It has 5 isoenzymes (LDH1-5) with tissue-specific distributions: LDH1 (heart), LDH5 (liver/muscle).'),
('496ea6e6-48c7-5c98-8fe1-c38bbae25f14'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Michaelis-Menten kinetics describes:', '["Protein synthesis","The rate of enzyme-catalyzed reactions","Membrane transport","DNA replication","Cell division"]'::jsonb, 'The rate of enzyme-catalyzed reactions', 'Km (Michaelis constant) is the substrate concentration at half-maximal velocity, reflecting enzyme-substrate affinity. V = Vmax[S]/(Km + [S]).'),
('f6133953-7a14-52ed-88a0-fbbe021cf291'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'The Michaelis constant (Km) is defined as the substrate concentration at which the reaction velocity is:', '["Half of Vmax","Vmax","Zero","Double Vmax","One-fourth Vmax"]'::jsonb, 'Half of Vmax', 'Km is the substrate concentration at half-maximal velocity. Low Km indicates high affinity; high Km indicates low affinity.'),
('01a344aa-88b5-5ea4-9a7d-b547d4b59877'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'A low Km indicates:', '["Slow reaction rate","High affinity for substrate","Low Vmax","Low affinity for substrate","High Vmax"]'::jsonb, 'High affinity for substrate', 'Low Km means the enzyme reaches half-maximal velocity at low substrate concentration, indicating high substrate affinity.'),
('1f1b89fc-facc-5d1c-b5e4-702621f3d6cc'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Vmax represents:', '["Maximum reaction rate at saturating substrate","Enzyme concentration accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Rate at half substrate mediated by specific receptor activation","Turnover number without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Initial velocity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Maximum reaction rate at saturating substrate', 'Vmax is the maximum velocity when all enzyme active sites are saturated. Vmax = kcat × [E]total.'),
('4530632e-45ec-5d44-9f56-487504ef6a27'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Kcat is also known as:', '["Equilibrium constant","Michaelis constant","Turnover number","Dissociation constant","Inhibition constant"]'::jsonb, 'Turnover number', 'Turnover number (kcat) is the number of substrate molecules converted per enzyme molecule per second when the enzyme is saturated.'),
('cffa75af-00b6-5d60-a214-72346f100c76'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'The Lineweaver-Burk plot is a:', '["Log-log plot","Hill plot","Semi-log plot","Scatchard plot","Double reciprocal plot"]'::jsonb, 'Double reciprocal plot', 'Lineweaver-Burk plot: 1/V vs 1/[S]. Y-intercept = 1/Vmax, X-intercept = -1/Km, slope = Km/Vmax.'),
('3a0dc974-e31b-54c2-83b7-8ccd37f5047e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Competitive inhibition can be overcome by:', '["Increasing substrate concentration","Decreasing substrate concentration","Increasing enzyme concentration","Adding more inhibitor","Decreasing temperature"]'::jsonb, 'Increasing substrate concentration', 'Competitive inhibitors bind the active site, increasing apparent Km without affecting Vmax. Effects are overcome by excess substrate.'),
('d25920e8-b04b-563e-a279-62ebd18f2345'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Non-competitive inhibition occurs when the inhibitor binds:', '["To the product resulting in decreased functional activity","Only to ES complex accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","To substrate mediated by specific receptor activation","To both free enzyme and enzyme-substrate complex","Only to free enzyme leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'To both free enzyme and enzyme-substrate complex', 'Non-competitive inhibitors bind both free enzyme and ES complex at a site other than the active site. Vmax decreases, Km unchanged.'),
('2821e22e-e65c-51c8-93b0-0f519feb401a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Uncompetitive inhibition occurs when the inhibitor binds:', '["To the substrate","Only to the ES complex","Only to free enzyme","To both E and ES","To the product"]'::jsonb, 'Only to the ES complex', 'Uncompetitive inhibitors bind only to the ES complex. Both Km and Vmax decrease by the same factor.'),
('ad6949e9-292c-5577-aea3-25dd33932015'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Mixed inhibition (not pure non-competitive) affects:', '["Both Km and Vmax differently","Neither Km nor Vmax","Only the substrate","Only Km","Only Vmax"]'::jsonb, 'Both Km and Vmax differently', 'Mixed inhibitors bind both E and ES with different affinities. Both Km and Vmax change.'),
('72a13c0f-9fde-5519-b2dc-d0583a538310'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Allosteric enzymes exhibit:', '["Michaelis-Menten kinetics","Zero-order kinetics","Linear kinetics","Cooperative binding","Sigmoidal kinetics"]'::jsonb, 'Cooperative binding', 'Allosteric enzymes show cooperative binding and sigmoidal kinetics. They have multiple subunits and regulatory sites.'),
('46bba3f2-e3e1-5491-99df-1d09a06bf43c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Allosteric inhibitors bind to:', '["Product resulting in decreased functional activity","Substrate accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Regulatory sites (allosteric sites)","Active sites without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Coenzyme leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Regulatory sites (allosteric sites)', 'Allosteric regulation involves binding of effectors at sites other than the active site, causing conformational changes.'),
('5eef00a5-cc86-51f3-9eaf-0ca3ad83abe2'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Hemoglobin exhibits allosteric binding of:', '["Oxygen","ATP","Glucose","CO2","Water"]'::jsonb, 'Oxygen', 'Hemoglobin shows cooperative oxygen binding (sigmoidal curve). O2 binding at one subunit increases affinity at others (T→R transition).'),
('f9ed29d9-f3e0-5dac-b490-949879f3c74d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Irreversible enzyme inhibition typically involves:', '["Allosteric binding","Non-covalent binding","Substrate binding","Competitive binding","Covalent modification of the active site"]'::jsonb, 'Covalent modification of the active site', 'Irreversible inhibitors form covalent bonds with the enzyme, permanently inactivating it. Examples: organophosphates, aspirin.'),
('9cd4ebda-ba44-552d-adbd-57da27c6dced'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Penicillin acts as a:', '["Allosteric activator","Competitive inhibitor","Coenzyme","Non-competitive inhibitor","Suicide inhibitor"]'::jsonb, 'Suicide inhibitor', 'Penicillin is a suicide inhibitor (mechanism-based) that acetylates the active site serine of transpeptidase, irreversibly inhibiting bacterial cell wall synthesis.'),
('618e6d77-80d1-5d65-8ef3-1326e2547541'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Pepsin has an optimal pH around:', '["9.0","7.0","4.0","8.0","1.5-2.5"]'::jsonb, '1.5-2.5', 'Pepsin has optimal pH 1.5-2.5 for protein digestion in the stomach. It is secreted as pepsinogen (inactive) and activated by HCl.'),
('80b435c1-a642-52ef-990c-6669612094fb'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Temperature increases enzyme activity up to a point because:', '["PH changes resulting in decreased functional activity","The enzyme unfolds accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Substrate denatures mediated by specific receptor activation","Coenzymes are destroyed without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Molecular motion and collision frequency increase"]'::jsonb, 'Molecular motion and collision frequency increase', 'Increasing temperature increases molecular motion and collision frequency, but beyond optimal temperature, denaturation occurs.'),
('6cfe1179-9937-5580-9a27-173d66f3964b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Trypsinogen is activated by:', '["Pepsin resulting in decreased functional activity","HCl accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Enteropeptidase (enterokinase)","Chymotrypsin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bile leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Enteropeptidase (enterokinase)', 'Enteropeptidase (enterokinase) on duodenal brush border cleaves trypsinogen to trypsin. Trypsin then activates other zymogens.'),
('62f37ade-0de9-524d-bcb7-1dcfd4d89d9c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'All pancreatic zymogens are ultimately activated by:', '["Pepsin","Chymotrypsin","HCl","Trypsin","Enteropeptidase"]'::jsonb, 'Trypsin', 'Trypsin activates all other pancreatic zymogens (chymotrypsinogen, procarboxypeptidase, proelastase, prophospholipase A2).'),
('426b2671-387d-5906-9120-c8ec8dc10377'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'LDH-1 is predominant in:', '["Kidney","Brain","Muscle","Heart","Liver"]'::jsonb, 'Heart', 'LDH1 (HHHH) predominates in heart. LDH5 (MMMM) predominates in liver and skeletal muscle.'),
('0568d08e-c669-5c11-b395-f18ecc415244'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'LDH-5 is predominant in:', '["Kidney resulting in decreased functional activity","Heart accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Brain mediated by specific receptor activation","Liver and skeletal muscle","Lung leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Liver and skeletal muscle', 'LDH5 (MMMM) predominates in liver and skeletal muscle. LDH1 (HHHH) predominates in heart.'),
('493f8ef8-37e8-5522-b9d5-cadd57c5248a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'CK-MB is elevated in:', '["Brain injury","Renal failure","Liver disease","Skeletal muscle injury","Myocardial infarction"]'::jsonb, 'Myocardial infarction', 'CK-MB is the cardiac-specific isoenzyme of creatine kinase. It rises 4-6 hours after MI, peaks at 24 hours, and normalizes in 48-72 hours.'),
('8eae954b-58aa-56d8-a17b-fb241205f5b9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Alkaline phosphatase (ALP) isoenzymes are useful in diagnosing:', '["Pancreatic disease","Heart disease","Muscle disease","Kidney disease","Bone and liver disease"]'::jsonb, 'Bone and liver disease', 'ALP is elevated in bone disease (osteoblast activity) and liver disease (cholestasis). Heat stability distinguishes bone from liver ALP.'),
('4a5ad7d6-f186-5844-826e-daca488ef4e3'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Elevated ALP in liver disease suggests:', '["Wilson disease","Hemochromatosis","Obstructive jaundice","Hepatitis","Cirrhosis"]'::jsonb, 'Obstructive jaundice', 'ALP elevation in cholestatic liver disease is due to increased synthesis by hepatocytes in response to bile acid accumulation.'),
('f88d220c-4b9f-567f-8a80-136cd33da643'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Which enzyme complex converts alpha-ketoglutarate to succinyl-CoA in the TCA cycle?', '["Alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase","Succinyl-CoA synthetase","Fumarase","Malate dehydrogenase","Isocitrate dehydrogenase"]'::jsonb, 'Alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase', 'Alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase is a multi-enzyme complex similar to pyruvate dehydrogenase. It requires TPP, lipoic acid, CoA, FAD, and NAD+.'),
('a0f31efe-424a-5643-b109-fb27ce9baedf'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Aspartate aminotransferase (AST) is found in highest concentration in:', '["Kidney","Heart and liver","Lung","Brain","Spleen"]'::jsonb, 'Heart and liver', 'Transaminases (ALT, AST) transfer amino groups using PLP (vitamin B6). ALT is liver-specific; AST is found in liver, heart, and muscle.'),
('dea65a8c-03f5-5143-acae-14e1ea6417db'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Alanine aminotransferase (ALT) is more specific for:', '["Heart","Liver","Brain","Muscle","Kidney"]'::jsonb, 'Liver', 'ALT is more liver-specific than AST. AST/ALT ratio >2 suggests alcoholic hepatitis; ratio <1 suggests viral hepatitis.'),
('cd7e85b1-9f83-5e5d-affb-cfcbe0f56aa1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'The AST:ALT ratio in alcoholic hepatitis is typically:', '[">3",">2","<1","<0.5","1"]'::jsonb, '>2', 'AST/ALT ratio >2 (De Ritis ratio) is characteristic of alcoholic liver disease due to pyridoxal phosphate deficiency affecting ALT more.'),
('c25400fb-5ad0-5b38-8328-cb8303df3b42'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Acid phosphatase is a marker for:', '["Hepatocellular carcinoma","Pancreatic cancer","Bone cancer","Prostate cancer","Lung cancer"]'::jsonb, 'Prostate cancer', 'Acid phosphatase (tartrate-resistant) is elevated in prostate cancer and hairy cell leukemia.'),
('45f5aa7e-7fe9-5e0c-be4c-d2e8a499121b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Tartrate-resistant acid phosphatase is a marker for:', '["Bone disease","Prostate cancer","Liver disease","Hairy cell leukemia","Multiple myeloma"]'::jsonb, 'Hairy cell leukemia', 'TRAP is elevated in hairy cell leukemia and Gaucher disease.'),
('9c700ebf-ba96-5ef6-92e6-b34aa4556c9b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Amylase is produced by the:', '["Liver and kidney","Heart and lung","Pancreas and salivary glands","Stomach and intestine","Brain and muscle"]'::jsonb, 'Pancreas and salivary glands', 'Amylase (salivary and pancreatic) hydrolyzes alpha-1,4 glycosidic bonds in starch, breaking it into maltose and limit dextrins.'),
('56001011-8eee-5c83-9bb4-08b2899ac705'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Elevated serum amylase is a diagnostic marker for:', '["Chronic pancreatitis","Mumps","Acute pancreatitis","Salivary gland tumor","Renal failure"]'::jsonb, 'Acute pancreatitis', 'Serum amylase rises 6-12 hours after acute pancreatitis onset, peaks at 48 hours, and normalizes in 3-5 days.'),
('5777ec91-c6b1-5b1e-b531-64f0de3cb426'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Both amylase and lipase are elevated in:', '["Chronic pancreatitis","Cystic fibrosis","Acute pancreatitis","Mumps","Pancreatic cancer"]'::jsonb, 'Acute pancreatitis', 'Lipase is more specific for pancreatitis than amylase. Both are elevated in acute pancreatitis.'),
('b167daf6-6140-5082-84d4-c62cb071de75'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Enzyme induction involves:', '["Increased enzyme synthesis due to exposure to a substance","Enzyme degradation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Enzyme inhibition mediated by specific receptor activation","Decreased enzyme synthesis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Coenzyme binding leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Increased enzyme synthesis due to exposure to a substance', 'Enzyme induction increases enzyme protein synthesis (e.g., phenobarbital induces CYP450 enzymes). The opposite is enzyme repression.'),
('66f76948-fea6-5e96-a661-679cb856ca15'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Which of the following is an enzyme inducer?', '["Atropine","Methotrexate","Phenobarbital","Disulfiram","Cimetidine"]'::jsonb, 'Phenobarbital', 'Phenobarbital is a potent inducer of hepatic microsomal enzymes (CYP450), increasing drug metabolism.'),
('a6800173-f7f3-5129-98d9-62ff5d87c610'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Cigarette smoking induces which enzyme?', '["CYP1A2","MAO","ALDH","ACE","COMT"]'::jsonb, 'CYP1A2', 'Cigarette smoke contains polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons that induce CYP1A2, increasing metabolism of drugs like caffeine and theophylline.'),
('6c776288-cca0-5e61-b542-f8b11dbe4016'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'RNase P is a ribozyme that:', '["Replicates RNA","Synthesizes proteins","Modifies lipids","Cleaves DNA","Processes tRNA precursors"]'::jsonb, 'Processes tRNA precursors', 'RNase P is a ribozyme (RNA enzyme) that processes 5'' end of tRNA precursors. It was discovered by Sidney Altman (Nobel Prize 1989).'),
('a0e852ee-7925-56cc-bfda-02eebcaaf4ea'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'The lock-and-key model of enzyme-substrate interaction was proposed by:', '["Michaelis","Linus Pauling","Daniel Koshland","Menten","Emil Fischer"]'::jsonb, 'Emil Fischer', 'Emil Fischer proposed the lock-and-key model (1894). Daniel Koshland later proposed the induced-fit model (1958).'),
('ad114bde-af68-530d-a87b-5cae8e297f21'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Enzyme specificity is determined by:', '["The cofactor","The structure of the active site","The pH","The temperature","The salt concentration"]'::jsonb, 'The structure of the active site', 'The active site geometry determines which substrate can bind. Specificity arises from complementary shape, charge, and hydrophobic interactions.'),
('db64fab7-4da4-526a-92d9-030c6a4d5505'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Absolute specificity means an enzyme:', '["Works at one pH","Catalyzes any substrate","Requires a coenzyme","Catalyzes a group of related substrates","Catalyzes only one specific substrate"]'::jsonb, 'Catalyzes only one specific substrate', 'Absolute specificity: enzyme acts on only one substrate (e.g., urease acts only on urea). Group specificity: acts on a group of related substrates.'),
('f857653b-b527-5b7d-9ea9-34afcde6d54c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Stereospecificity of enzymes refers to:', '["Specificity for D-isomers only","Reaction with any sugar","Non-specific binding","Specificity for L-isomers only","Specificity for one optical isomer"]'::jsonb, 'Specificity for one optical isomer', 'Stereospecificity: enzymes distinguish between optical isomers (D vs L) or geometric isomers (cis vs trans).'),
('3c67f6b0-d0cd-53c2-a61f-93fe56c0d68f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'The specificity constant (Kcat/Km) is a measure of:', '["Vmax","Km","Enzyme inhibition","Enzyme efficiency","Substrate binding"]'::jsonb, 'Enzyme efficiency', 'Kcat/Km measures catalytic efficiency. The diffusion limit is 10^8-10^9 M^-1s^-1 (catalytically perfect enzymes like carbonic anhydrase).'),
('270e27fc-562b-5d46-81c4-5e1936ffe027'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'The E1 ubiquitin-activating enzyme is involved in:', '["Protein degradation via proteasome","DNA replication","RNA transcription","Protein synthesis","Signal transduction"]'::jsonb, 'Protein degradation via proteasome', 'E1 activates ubiquitin in an ATP-dependent step. E2 (conjugating) and E3 (ligase) then transfer ubiquitin to target proteins for proteasomal degradation.'),
('df6175da-b7d3-5c83-b830-f3b80a3e2cd2'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'The proteasome is a:', '["Multi-subunit protease complex","Phosphatase","DNA helicase","Protein kinase","Ribozyme"]'::jsonb, 'Multi-subunit protease complex', 'The 26S proteasome degrades ubiquitinated proteins. It consists of 20S core (proteolytic) and 19S regulatory caps.'),
('356b61c7-92bc-5e95-abc8-c2ddae4f9e8c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Sulfonamides inhibit bacterial growth by:', '["Inhibiting DNA gyrase","Inhibiting protein synthesis","Disrupting membranes","Inhibiting cell wall synthesis","Competitively inhibiting dihydrofolate synthetase"]'::jsonb, 'Competitively inhibiting dihydrofolate synthetase', 'Sulfonamides are competitive inhibitors of dihydrofolate synthetase, blocking folate synthesis essential for nucleotide synthesis.'),
('112aa741-4bc6-5103-ad64-72053c597db0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Statins (atorvastatin) inhibit which enzyme?', '["HMG-CoA reductase","LCAT","ACAT","Lipoprotein lipase","HMG-CoA synthase"]'::jsonb, 'HMG-CoA reductase', 'Statins competitively inhibit HMG-CoA reductase, the rate-limiting enzyme in cholesterol synthesis, reducing hepatic cholesterol production.'),
('cdd31689-21b5-5bb1-9909-8eef6b371b42'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Allopurinol is a competitive inhibitor of:', '["Uricase","PNP","ADA","Xanthine oxidase","HGPRT"]'::jsonb, 'Xanthine oxidase', 'Allopurinol is a suicide inhibitor of xanthine oxidase, reducing uric acid production. Used to treat gout.'),
('9cd562fb-7ffa-5cb6-a48f-a4e08d9d59f0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Disulfiram inhibits which enzyme?', '["MAO","COMT","ALDH2","Aldehyde dehydrogenase","Alcohol dehydrogenase"]'::jsonb, 'Aldehyde dehydrogenase', 'Disulfiram inhibits ALDH2, causing acetaldehyde accumulation after alcohol consumption (aversive therapy for alcoholism).'),
('0c3f2c01-4f40-55d6-bf6d-6f80747c2549'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Aspirin irreversibly inhibits:', '["Acetylcholinesterase","Xanthine oxidase","Phospholipase A2","Cyclooxygenase (COX)","Lipoxygenase"]'::jsonb, 'Cyclooxygenase (COX)', 'Aspirin irreversibly acetylates COX-1 (prostaglandin synthesis) and COX-2 (inflammation). Low dose inhibits platelet TXA2 for 7-10 days.'),
('acad0e35-a7f1-5008-a377-6e6ea48bc440'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'The Km of an enzyme is affected by:', '["Non-competitive inhibitors","Enzyme concentration","Temperature only","PH only","Competitive inhibitors"]'::jsonb, 'Competitive inhibitors', 'Competitive inhibitors increase apparent Km (more substrate needed to reach half Vmax). Non-competitive inhibitors do not affect Km.'),
('a9e0b45d-34fb-52f0-8ade-61444ef6f2a1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'The Vmax of an enzyme can be determined from the y-intercept of which plot?', '["Woolf-Augustinsson-Hofstee","Hanes-Woolf","Eadie-Hofstee","Lineweaver-Burk","Scatchard"]'::jsonb, 'Lineweaver-Burk', 'In Lineweaver-Burk plot (1/V vs 1/[S]), y-intercept = 1/Vmax, x-intercept = -1/Km.'),
('410b76ed-9b7c-5487-ad35-7ac3fa46f69c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Zero-order kinetics means the reaction rate is:', '["Independent of substrate concentration","Exponential","Inversely proportional to substrate","Proportional to substrate concentration","Linear with time"]'::jsonb, 'Independent of substrate concentration', 'Zero-order: rate is constant regardless of [S]. Occurs when enzyme is saturated. First-order: rate proportional to [S].'),
('242c813d-9840-53ed-b0f1-198755c8f52e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Bisubstrate reactions include:', '["Sequential and ping-pong mechanisms","Competitive reactions","Only sequential","Only ping-pong","Non-competitive reactions"]'::jsonb, 'Sequential and ping-pong mechanisms', 'Sequential: both substrates bind before product release. Ping-pong: first product released before second substrate binds.'),
('efff76ff-b2ae-5879-82ff-b789f0eda9eb'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'In ping-pong (double displacement) mechanism:', '["Product is released","Substrates bind randomly","The first product is released before the second substrate binds","Products are released simultaneously","Both substrates bind before product release"]'::jsonb, 'The first product is released before the second substrate binds', 'Ping-pong mechanism: enzyme is temporarily modified (e.g., phosphorylated) after first substrate binds and product leaves.'),
('837fddbc-a2d2-54fe-a553-555f21b56367'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'An example of a ping-pong enzyme mechanism is:', '["LDH resulting in decreased functional activity","Citrate synthase accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Aminotransferases (transaminases)","Hexokinase without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Alcohol dehydrogenase leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Aminotransferases (transaminases)', 'Transaminases use ping-pong mechanism: PLP cofactor accepts amino group from first amino acid, then transfers it to keto acid.'),
('e4ba9a61-25fa-5b8f-b25a-9c28ff68d96a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'An example of a sequential ordered mechanism is:', '["Alcohol dehydrogenase","LDH","Hexokinase","Chymotrypsin","Transaminases"]'::jsonb, 'LDH', 'LDH follows sequential ordered mechanism: NADH binds first, then pyruvate, then products release in order.'),
('6e0d3208-2e72-5b89-a937-e9a7bc8877c6'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Suicide inhibition (mechanism-based inhibition) occurs when:', '["The inhibitor is allosteric","The inhibitor binds reversibly","The inhibitor is competitive","The inhibitor resembles the substrate","A reactive intermediate inactivates the enzyme"]'::jsonb, 'A reactive intermediate inactivates the enzyme', 'Suicide inhibitors are converted by the enzyme to a reactive species that irreversibly modifies the active site.'),
('8ef18173-1404-53d4-9c0d-6dbe95e2ad13'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Protein kinases transfer phosphate from ATP to:', '["Asn residues","Met residues","Cys residues","Gly residues","Ser/Thr or Tyr residues"]'::jsonb, 'Ser/Thr or Tyr residues', 'Serine/threonine kinases phosphorylate Ser/Thr residues. Tyrosine kinases phosphorylate Tyr residues. Both use ATP as phosphate donor.'),
('be7f0e3f-219b-5796-9f78-06e4456eb631'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Ornithine decarboxylase is:', '["A ligase resulting in decreased functional activity","An isomerase accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A kinase mediated by specific receptor activation","A hydrolase without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The rate-limiting enzyme in polyamine synthesis"]'::jsonb, 'The rate-limiting enzyme in polyamine synthesis', 'ODC catalyzes the first step in polyamine synthesis (ornithine → putrescine). It has the shortest half-life of any mammalian enzyme (10 min).'),
('04942cea-910b-595c-9b4e-93624af969f6'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'ODC has the shortest half-life of any mammalian enzyme (t1/2 10 min) allowing:', '["Temperature sensitivity","The enzyme to be very stable","PH dependence","Slow regulation","Rapid changes in polyamine levels"]'::jsonb, 'Rapid changes in polyamine levels', 'Short half-life allows rapid regulation of polyamine levels in response to growth signals.'),
('7714ce1c-275b-5002-a409-bc5c56cc9fcd'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'The enzyme that adds ubiquitin to proteins is:', '["Ubiquitin-conjugating enzyme (E2)","Deubiquitinating enzyme","Ubiquitin ligase (E3)","Ubiquitin-activating enzyme (E1)","Proteasome"]'::jsonb, 'Ubiquitin ligase (E3)', 'E3 (ubiquitin ligase) is the substrate-recognition component that determines specificity. E1 activates, E2 conjugates, E3 ligates.'),
('ddf5bf96-0850-5115-9a75-2cb80dbee668'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'The N-end rule relates to:', '["DNA repair resulting in decreased functional activity","Ubiquitin-mediated protein degradation","Chromatin remodeling mediated by specific receptor activation","RNA splicing without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Protein synthesis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ubiquitin-mediated protein degradation', 'The N-end rule states that the half-life of a protein is determined by its N-terminal amino acid residue.'),
('3bab61af-7a27-523f-9dcf-e0e8fc6c44a7'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Which pancreatic zymogen is NOT activated by trypsin?', '["Chymotrypsinogen","Prophospholipase A2","Pancreatic amylase","Procarboxypeptidase","Proelastase"]'::jsonb, 'Pancreatic amylase', 'Pancreatic amylase is secreted in active form (not as a zymogen). All other pancreatic zymogens are activated by trypsin.'),
('efb35f83-dba0-509b-887f-70a6518ce07c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'The blood clotting enzyme thrombin converts:', '["Factor X to Xa","Fibrin to fibrinogen","Prothrombin to thrombin","Fibrinogen to fibrin","Plasminogen to plasmin"]'::jsonb, 'Fibrinogen to fibrin', 'Thrombin (factor IIa) converts fibrinogen to fibrin monomers, which polymerize to form the clot. It also activates factors V, VIII, XI, and XIII.'),
('d0e05834-dd74-561c-8c2b-cb2d025d5724'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'The final step of coagulation is:', '["Fibrin cross-linking by factor XIIIa","Prothrombin activation","Platelet aggregation","Platelet plug formation","Thrombin generation"]'::jsonb, 'Fibrin cross-linking by factor XIIIa', 'Factor XIIIa (transglutaminase) cross-links fibrin strands via isopeptide bonds, stabilizing the clot.'),
('07d17d63-1e8f-52c8-a72c-d97cb36dccd8'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Plasmin digests:', '["Fibrinogen","Prothrombin","Platelets","Fibrin clots","Clotting factors"]'::jsonb, 'Fibrin clots', 'Plasmin cleaves fibrin clots (fibrinolysis). It is formed from plasminogen by tPA and streptokinase.'),
('9abda326-abf2-5f02-a0a4-1012b0e0104b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Glucokinase (hexokinase IV) differs from other hexokinases because:', '["It is not inhibited by glucose-6-phosphate","It is found only in muscle","It phosphorylates fructose","It requires NAD+","It is inhibited by ATP"]'::jsonb, 'It is not inhibited by glucose-6-phosphate', 'Glucokinase (liver) has high Km (10 mM), is not inhibited by G6P, and is induced by insulin. Hexokinase has low Km (0.1 mM) and is inhibited by G6P.'),
('f925ef12-8c96-5cef-9dfd-3458d6ef7f01'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Renal glucosuria occurs when:', '["Blood glucose exceeds 180 mg/dL (renal threshold)","Hexokinase is inhibited","The pancreas is damaged","The kidney is damaged","Glucokinase is deficient"]'::jsonb, 'Blood glucose exceeds 180 mg/dL (renal threshold)', 'SGLT2 in proximal tubule reabsorbs glucose. When blood glucose exceeds 180 mg/dL, transporters are saturated and glucose appears in urine.'),
('dea98875-2d61-58c0-a3e0-67a8c7662f71'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Troponin I (cTnI) and troponin T (cTnT) are:', '["Markers of stroke","Markers of liver disease","Markers of renal failure","Specific cardiac biomarkers for myocardial infarction","Markers of skeletal muscle injury"]'::jsonb, 'Specific cardiac biomarkers for myocardial infarction', 'Cardiac troponins are the gold standard for MI diagnosis. They rise 3-12 hours after MI, peak at 24-48 hours, and remain elevated for 7-14 days.'),
('d422eb32-9984-534d-9c62-157e4a2cbaff'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'The advantage of troponin over CK-MB is:', '["Faster rise time resulting in decreased functional activity","Lower cost accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Higher sensitivity and longer diagnostic window","False positives without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Wider availability leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Higher sensitivity and longer diagnostic window', 'Troponins are more sensitive and specific than CK-MB. They have a longer diagnostic window (7-14 days vs 48-72 hours).'),
('13a3fd5b-1d8e-571e-ae30-4b6b43d3fd58'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'LDH isoenzymes are useful for timing of MI diagnosis because:', '["LDH-1 > LDH-2 (flipped ratio) occurs 12-48 hours after MI","LDH peaks at 1 hour","LDH rises immediately","LDH is specific to cardiac muscle","LDH remains elevated for 1 month"]'::jsonb, 'LDH-1 > LDH-2 (flipped ratio) occurs 12-48 hours after MI', 'The ''flipped'' LDH ratio (LDH1 > LDH2) appears 12-48 hours after MI and persists for 10-14 days.'),
('9b09d49e-4956-536f-9e7b-5ffdd5d3b4e5'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'The normal ALP level in children is:', '["Same as adults resulting in decreased functional activity","Undetectable accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lower than in adults mediated by specific receptor activation","Higher than in adults (due to bone growth)","Zero leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Higher than in adults (due to bone growth)', 'Children have higher ALP due to osteoblast activity during bone growth. ALP normalizes after skeletal maturity.'),
('f43538ab-b140-5619-aef9-34528b0dad17'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'ALP elevation in Paget disease of bone is due to:', '["Osteomalacia","Fracture healing","Increased osteoblast activity","Bone metastasis","Osteoclast activity"]'::jsonb, 'Increased osteoblast activity', 'Paget disease shows coupled increase in osteoclast and osteoblast activity, with ALP reflecting osteoblast response.'),
('2e8a6950-c896-5a25-8cab-0163c9af17e4'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Acid phosphatase (ACP) is found in:', '["Platelets","Liver","Erythrocytes","Bone","Prostate"]'::jsonb, 'Prostate', 'ACP is elevated in prostate cancer, bone metastases, and hairy cell leukemia. Tartrate-resistant ACP (TRAP) is specific for hairy cell leukemia.'),
('9ce42727-220e-52d9-80d8-ce4e68204bf7'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Macroamylasemia is characterized by:', '["Low lipase with normal amylase","Low serum and urine amylase","Elevated serum amylase with normal urine amylase","Normal amylase with elevated lipase","Normal serum amylase with high urine amylase"]'::jsonb, 'Elevated serum amylase with normal urine amylase', 'Macroamylasemia: amylase bound to immunoglobulins or polysaccharides too large to be filtered by glomeruli. Benign condition.'),
('90a69c2c-8e2b-50c5-9454-80144e366885'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'The enzyme GGT is induced by:', '["Statins","Metformin","Insulin","Phenobarbital","Alcohol"]'::jsonb, 'Alcohol', 'GGT is induced by alcohol and barbiturates. It is a sensitive marker for alcohol use and biliary disease.'),
('be9b4404-e9f5-5888-b6a5-4ccafbc4e23e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'GGT and ALP are both elevated in:', '["Myocardial infarction","Hemolysis","Hepatocellular disease","Cholestatic liver disease","Bone disease"]'::jsonb, 'Cholestatic liver disease', 'Both GGT and ALP are elevated in cholestatic liver disease. GGT helps confirm hepatic origin of ALP elevation.'),
('340eeb11-7d04-514f-8491-6e04aece8648'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Urea (blood urea nitrogen) is a marker of:', '["Pulmonary function","Liver function","Renal function","Cardiac function","Pancreatic function"]'::jsonb, 'Renal function', 'BUN reflects renal function (glomerular filtration). Elevated in renal failure, dehydration, and high protein intake.'),
('2fcbda48-fc38-52a4-b90e-49adcc1b9de3'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Which molecule is the primary excretory product of nitrogen metabolism?', '["Urea","Ammonia","Uric acid","Creatinine","Bilirubin"]'::jsonb, 'Urea', 'Urea is the primary nitrogenous waste product of protein metabolism, synthesized in the liver via the urea cycle.'),
('322115fc-7577-5efc-8eee-70904de13f26'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Cholesterol oxidase is used to measure:', '["HDL cholesterol","Total cholesterol","Uric acid","Glucose","Triglycerides"]'::jsonb, 'Total cholesterol', 'Cholesterol oxidase specifically oxidizes cholesterol, allowing enzymatic measurement of total and HDL cholesterol.'),
('eff45f20-132d-5fc6-8411-aa2b8eed7de2'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Which molecule is the end product of purine degradation?', '["Urea","Glucose","Uric acid","Cholesterol","Triglycerides"]'::jsonb, 'Uric acid', 'Purine degradation: adenine → hypoxanthine → xanthine → uric acid (via xanthine oxidase). Gout results from uric acid crystal deposition.'),
('94bb2522-bedf-56e0-b0d4-91314d282825'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Gel filtration chromatography separates proteins based on:', '["Shape","Charge","Hydrophobicity","Size (molecular weight)","Affinity"]'::jsonb, 'Size (molecular weight)', 'Gel filtration (size exclusion) separates by molecular size. Large molecules elute first; small molecules enter pores and elute later.'),
('e776f20c-216b-5a1e-92c3-a94183e0e11b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Ion exchange chromatography separates enzymes based on:', '["Affinity","Hydrophobicity","Size","Density","Net charge"]'::jsonb, 'Net charge', 'Ion exchange separates by net surface charge. Cation exchange binds positively charged proteins; anion exchange binds negatively charged proteins.'),
('e97e5af5-bba8-54de-8492-a2a01c918b82'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Catalytic antibodies (abzymes) can be generated using:', '["Inhibitors resulting in decreased functional activity","Substrate analogs accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Transition state analogs as immunogens","Coenzyme analogs without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Product analogs leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Transition state analogs as immunogens', 'Transition state analogs are immunogenic and generate antibodies that catalyze reactions by stabilizing the transition state.'),
('3af27d8d-f349-5318-9314-82d471008e0a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'The transition state of a reaction is:', '["The intermediate resulting in decreased functional activity","The point of highest free energy along the reaction coordinate","The product mediated by specific receptor activation","The ground state without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The substrate leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The point of highest free energy along the reaction coordinate', 'The transition state is the highest energy state along the reaction coordinate. Enzymes stabilize this state to lower activation energy.'),
('b03704aa-06d7-52d7-b33a-a113a92389ab'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'The binding energy released from enzyme-substrate interactions is used to:', '["Reduce temperature","Stabilize the transition state","Denature the enzyme","Increase entropy","Increase pH"]'::jsonb, 'Stabilize the transition state', 'Binding energy from enzyme-substrate complementarity is used to stabilize the transition state, lowering activation energy.'),
('6a3a735a-f354-529d-91c0-ca49e964566d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Thermophilic bacteria have enzymes that are active at:', '["High pH","Low pH","High temperatures (70-100°C)","Low temperatures","High pressure"]'::jsonb, 'High temperatures (70-100°C)', 'Thermophilic enzymes have extra salt bridges, hydrophobic interactions, and disulfide bonds for stability at high temperatures.'),
('2a341106-d6c8-5456-95d1-8ebf8e2145c6'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Specific activity is defined as:', '["Units per mL resulting in decreased functional activity","Vmax accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Km mediated by specific receptor activation","Total units without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Units of enzyme per mg of protein"]'::jsonb, 'Units of enzyme per mg of protein', 'Specific activity = total units / total protein (units/mg). It increases with purification and is used to assess enzyme purity.'),
('a531a50e-d36f-5572-8e9d-b7167c610907'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'The purification fold is calculated as:', '["Specific activity after step / Specific activity before step","Km after / Km before","Protein after / Protein before","Volume after / Volume before","Total activity after step / Total activity"]'::jsonb, 'Specific activity after step / Specific activity before step', 'Purification fold = specific activity (purified) / specific activity (crude). Indicates how much the enzyme has been enriched.'),
('c219a154-614e-506b-b57d-cd88ca6d4743'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Electrofocusing (isoelectric focusing) separates proteins based on:', '["Molecular weight","Solubility","Charge at neutral pH","Shape","Isoelectric point (pI)"]'::jsonb, 'Isoelectric point (pI)', 'IEF separates proteins by their pI (pH where net charge = 0). Proteins migrate to their pI in a pH gradient.'),
('fc495e25-bec8-54c8-bad4-9b2f460b6b5c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'The isoelectric point (pI) is the pH at which:', '["The enzyme is most active","The protein is least stable","The protein is most soluble","The protein denatures","The net charge of the protein is zero"]'::jsonb, 'The net charge of the protein is zero', 'At pI, the protein has no net charge and does not migrate in an electric field. Proteins are least soluble at their pI.'),
('a96d5f9a-57e6-52ad-87c3-7b00bc958091'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Two-dimensional electrophoresis combines:', '["IEF and chromatography","Chromatography and electrophoresis","SDS-PAGE and Western blot","IEF (first dimension) and SDS-PAGE (second dimension)","Western blot and ELISA"]'::jsonb, 'IEF (first dimension) and SDS-PAGE (second dimension)', '2D-PAGE: first dimension separates by pI (IEF), second dimension by molecular weight (SDS-PAGE). Can resolve thousands of proteins.'),
('54d4dbf7-d70d-5b4f-bf9c-5411cd699493'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Western blotting uses antibodies to detect:', '["Lipids","Carbohydrates","DNA sequences","RNA sequences","Specific proteins"]'::jsonb, 'Specific proteins', 'Western blot: proteins separated by SDS-PAGE, transferred to membrane, detected with specific antibodies.'),
('d4158976-9a46-5f04-b772-b8b1927d8575'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'ELISA has higher sensitivity than:', '["Western blotting","Mass spectrometry","PCR","Agglutination assays","Radioimmunoassay"]'::jsonb, 'Agglutination assays', 'ELISA is more sensitive than agglutination assays due to enzymatic signal amplification. Can detect pg/mL concentrations.'),
('3fd83c60-5acf-5b15-897e-8fb1970ac7df'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'The biomarker alanine aminotransferase (ALT) is also known as:', '["GGT","CPK","LDH","SGPT","SGOT"]'::jsonb, 'SGPT', 'ALT = SGPT (serum glutamic-pyruvic transaminase). AST = SGOT (serum glutamic-oxaloacetic transaminase).'),
('b716ce10-b49f-5dba-9085-b6cb5ae4905e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'The enzyme renin is produced by the:', '["Heart resulting in decreased functional activity","Adrenal gland accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Juxtaglomerular cells of the kidney","Pancreas without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Liver leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Juxtaglomerular cells of the kidney', 'Renin is produced by juxtaglomerular cells in response to low blood pressure, low sodium, or sympathetic stimulation.'),
('d3693898-ae94-55f5-8036-cfd70d3f7499'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'ACE inhibitors are used to treat:', '["Depression resulting in decreased functional activity","Hypertension and heart failure","Epilepsy mediated by specific receptor activation","Asthma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Diabetes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hypertension and heart failure', 'ACE inhibitors block conversion of angiotensin I to II, reducing vasoconstriction and aldosterone secretion.'),
('88803c8d-8de6-5a8f-bd55-397c4079d748'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'The enzyme catalase converts hydrogen peroxide to:', '["Oxygen and hydrogen","Water and hydrogen","Superoxide and water","Water and carbon dioxide","Water and oxygen"]'::jsonb, 'Water and oxygen', '2H₂O₂ → 2H₂O + O₂. Catalase has one of the highest turnover numbers (40 million molecules/sec).'),
('2a4297e5-e781-58d4-8ff1-03b7620826d9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Glutathione peroxidase (GPx) reduces:', '["Hydrogen peroxide and organic peroxides","Hydroxyl radical accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Superoxide mediated by specific receptor activation","Peroxynitrite without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Singlet oxygen leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hydrogen peroxide and organic peroxides', 'GPx reduces H₂O₂ and lipid peroxides using glutathione (GSH). Requires selenium as selenocysteine. Protects RBCs from oxidative damage.'),
('dce03fd8-a61d-5be3-a2a5-70f3d169dff1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'The E. coli enzyme β-galactosidase cleaves:', '["Sucrose into glucose and fructose","Maltose into glucose","Starch into maltose","Cellulose into glucose","Lactose into glucose and galactose"]'::jsonb, 'Lactose into glucose and galactose', 'β-galactosidase (lacZ gene product) cleaves lactose into glucose and galactose. Used in blue-white screening.'),
('ccfe6ecf-5f56-5f04-952c-8e4c9c8f03a0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'X-gal is a chromogenic substrate for β-galactosidase that produces:', '["A blue color when cleaved","A yellow color","A red color","A green color","A purple color"]'::jsonb, 'A blue color when cleaved', 'X-gal is cleaved by β-galactosidase to produce a blue precipitate. Used in blue-white selection for cloning.'),
('1cae805a-3b26-5230-90b8-316b293eb820'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'IPTG (isopropyl-β-D-thiogalactopyranoside) is used as:', '["A chromogenic substrate","A repressor","A suicide inhibitor","An inducer of the lac operon","A competitive inhibitor"]'::jsonb, 'An inducer of the lac operon', 'IPTG is a non-metabolizable analog of allolactose that induces lac operon expression.'),
('ca32b728-d248-581e-866b-a1b4d3c78d76'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Streptokinase and tPA are used as:', '["Antibiotics resulting in decreased functional activity","Anti-inflammatory agents accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thrombolytic agents (clot-dissolving)","Anticoagulants without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Analgesics leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thrombolytic agents (clot-dissolving)', 'Streptokinase (bacterial) and tPA (tissue plasminogen activator) convert plasminogen to plasmin, dissolving blood clots.'),
('be903a2a-edb7-501e-874a-d5b900e44f2a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Single-strand DNA binding proteins (SSB) bind to:', '["Double-stranded DNA resulting in decreased functional activity","Membranes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ribosomes mediated by specific receptor activation","RNA without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Single-stranded DNA during replication"]'::jsonb, 'Single-stranded DNA during replication', 'SSB proteins stabilize single-stranded DNA during replication, preventing reannealing and secondary structure formation.'),
('0a2794d6-42ef-54f6-848b-a5d404833456'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Proofreading activity is associated with which enzyme?', '["Primase resulting in decreased functional activity","Helicase accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","DNA polymerase III (3''→5'' exonuclease)","Topoisomerase without affecting the primary tissue pathway","DNA ligase leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'DNA polymerase III (3''→5'' exonuclease)', 'DNA polymerase III has 3''→5'' exonuclease (proofreading) activity that removes misincorporated nucleotides.'),
('ff8f23e7-02d7-52dd-87b4-3ea9856c50bc'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'The enzyme poly(ADP-ribose) polymerase (PARP) is involved in:', '["DNA repair (base excision repair)","Translation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Transcription mediated by specific receptor activation","Apoptosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","DNA replication leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'DNA repair (base excision repair)', 'PARP detects DNA strand breaks and adds ADP-ribose polymers to histones, recruiting DNA repair enzymes.'),
('a1eef3ff-0f78-5fc9-aeba-93271a051cc1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Which vitamin is a component of FAD?', '["Niacin (B3)","Thiamine (B1)","Pyridoxine (B6)","Cobalamin (B12)","Riboflavin (B2)"]'::jsonb, 'Riboflavin (B2)', 'Riboflavin (vitamin B2) is the precursor for FAD and FMN, which function as electron carriers in oxidation-reduction reactions.'),
('58692d80-d0c6-5005-bc67-d334a65bfec3'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Km values for enzymes indicate:', '["Maximal velocity resulting in decreased functional activity","Enzyme concentration accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Substrate concentration at half Vmax (measure of enzyme affinity)","Turnover number without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Activation energy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Substrate concentration at half Vmax (measure of enzyme affinity)', 'Km reflects the affinity of enzyme for substrate. Low Km = high affinity; high Km = low affinity.'),
('50c9adeb-0098-5653-ba07-be558f60ace2'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Enzymes Coenzymes', 'Which coenzyme is derived from pantothenic acid?', '["TPP","PLP","NAD","Coenzyme A","FAD"]'::jsonb, 'Coenzyme A', 'Coenzyme A (CoA-SH) contains pantothenic acid (vitamin B5) and carries acyl groups via thioester bonds.'),
('9cf138dc-be0f-59c9-8aba-42d8262ef7fc'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'Which cells of the pancreatic islets synthesize and secrete insulin?', '["Alpha cells","Beta cells","Delta cells","PP (F) cells","Epsilon cells"]'::jsonb, 'Beta cells', 'Insulin is produced exclusively by beta cells of the pancreatic islets. Alpha cells secrete glucagon, delta cells secrete somatostatin, and PP cells secrete pancreatic polypeptide.'),
('006b92c1-7839-5055-bc7c-962ed5456eaa'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'The primary physiological stimulus for insulin secretion is:', '["Increased blood glucose","Decreased blood glucose","Increased plasma free fatty acids","Low plasma amino acid levels","Increased circulating catecholamines"]'::jsonb, 'Increased blood glucose', 'Glucose is the principal secretagogue for insulin. Rising blood glucose enters beta cells via GLUT2 and is metabolized, raising the ATP/ADP ratio that triggers insulin exocytosis.'),
('f68f97d1-6005-59fd-b61b-3e6b879dc252'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'Glucose entry into pancreatic beta cells is mediated by which transporter?', '["GLUT1","GLUT2","GLUT4","GLUT5","SGLT1"]'::jsonb, 'GLUT2', 'GLUT2 is the high-capacity, low-affinity glucose transporter of beta cells, allowing uptake to track plasma glucose. GLUT4 is the insulin-sensitive transporter of muscle and adipose tissue.'),
('e263e1e4-b591-5131-a762-605380024126'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'Closure of the ATP-sensitive potassium channel in beta cells leads to:', '["Hyperpolarization and reduced insulin release","Depolarization, calcium influx, and insulin exocytosis","Inhibition of glucokinase","Activation of protein kinase A","Increased somatostatin secretion"]'::jsonb, 'Depolarization, calcium influx, and insulin exocytosis', 'Glucose metabolism raises the ATP/ADP ratio, closing K-ATP channels and depolarizing the beta cell. Depolarization opens voltage-gated calcium channels, and the rise in cytosolic calcium triggers insulin granule exocytosis.'),
('988a734f-6af9-50a5-a9be-664b4eb308d6'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'Insulin binding to its receptor activates which intracellular pathway?', '["PI3K-Akt pathway","JAK-STAT pathway","PLC-IP3 pathway","Adenylyl cyclase-cAMP pathway","NF-kB pathway"]'::jsonb, 'PI3K-Akt pathway', 'The insulin receptor is a tyrosine kinase that autophosphorylates and recruits IRS proteins, which activate PI3K and then Akt. This cascade mediates most metabolic actions of insulin including GLUT4 translocation.'),
('40c7d9cd-6ae5-5eb1-9d5b-143428a1ff41'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'Which transporter is translocated to the plasma membrane in response to insulin in muscle and adipose tissue?', '["GLUT1","GLUT2","GLUT3","GLUT4","GLUT7"]'::jsonb, 'GLUT4', 'Insulin stimulates movement of intracellular GLUT4 vesicles to the plasma membrane of skeletal muscle and adipocytes, promoting glucose uptake. GLUT1 is largely constitutive and GLUT2 mediates beta-cell and liver glucose sensing.'),
('be982b5e-94da-5fde-bee2-d6a9bb8fa076'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'In the liver, insulin promotes which of the following?', '["Glycogenolysis","Gluconeogenesis","Glycogen synthesis","Ketogenesis","Activation of the carnitine shuttle"]'::jsonb, 'Glycogen synthesis', 'Insulin is anabolic: it activates glycogen synthase and inhibits glycogen phosphorylase, favoring hepatic glycogen storage. It simultaneously suppresses gluconeogenesis and ketogenesis.'),
('4d4ed743-94d9-5ffc-a155-3805d40300b5'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'Which of the following is a direct metabolic effect of insulin on adipose tissue?', '["Stimulation of hormone-sensitive lipase","Inhibition of lipoprotein lipase","Activation of acetyl-CoA carboxylase and lipogenesis","Increased fatty acid oxidation","Increased ketone body formation"]'::jsonb, 'Activation of acetyl-CoA carboxylase and lipogenesis', 'Insulin dephosphorylates and activates acetyl-CoA carboxylase, promoting malonyl-CoA synthesis and fat storage while inhibiting hormone-sensitive lipase. This favors lipogenesis and suppresses lipolysis simultaneously.'),
('f3f90c56-cd27-593d-a6a2-48482f82f474'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'Which hormone directly opposes the anabolic effects of insulin?', '["Glucagon","Estrogen","Vitamin D","Aldosterone","Calcitonin"]'::jsonb, 'Glucagon', 'Glucagon is the major counter-regulatory catabolic hormone, opposing insulin by stimulating glycogenolysis, gluconeogenesis, and ketogenesis. Catecholamines, cortisol, and growth hormone also oppose insulin.'),
('b7aa68fd-db05-5996-af63-4e762aa15778'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'Glucagon is secreted by which pancreatic islet cell type?', '["Beta cells","Alpha cells","Delta cells","PP cells","Epsilon cells"]'::jsonb, 'Alpha cells', 'Alpha cells of the pancreatic islets secrete glucagon in response to hypoglycemia. Glucagon raises blood glucose by acting mainly on the liver.'),
('161a6a3e-50ea-5fd1-8961-3f1d1190fa0f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'The primary stimulus for glucagon secretion is:', '["Hyperglycemia","Hypoglycemia","Elevated plasma insulin","Somatostatin excess","High blood pH"]'::jsonb, 'Hypoglycemia', 'Low blood glucose directly stimulates pancreatic alpha cells to release glucagon. Amino acids and sympathetic stimulation also promote glucagon secretion.'),
('f39bb83d-b75c-57eb-834a-1acefd7ea68c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'Glucagon exerts its metabolic effects primarily on which organ?', '["Skeletal muscle","Adipose tissue","Brain","Liver","Kidney"]'::jsonb, 'Liver', 'The liver is the main target of glucagon, where it stimulates glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis. Muscle lacks glucagon receptors and therefore is unresponsive to the hormone.'),
('19da1fb0-27bb-5781-8b59-c0d482c3f5e5'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'The mechanism of action of glucagon involves:', '["Binding to an intracellular nuclear receptor","Activation of the JAK-STAT pathway","Gs-coupled receptor activation of adenylyl cyclase","Guanylyl cyclase and cGMP production","Opening of ligand-gated ion channels"]'::jsonb, 'Gs-coupled receptor activation of adenylyl cyclase', 'Glucagon binds a Gs-coupled GPCR, activating adenylyl cyclase to raise cAMP. cAMP activates protein kinase A, which phosphorylates and activates the glycogen phosphorylase cascade.'),
('4d3e31fe-be84-5662-b24d-b7c04a5f796e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'Glucagon stimulates glycogenolysis in the liver by activating:', '["Glycogen synthase","Glycogen phosphorylase","Glucokinase","Pyruvate kinase","Acetyl-CoA carboxylase"]'::jsonb, 'Glycogen phosphorylase', 'PKA activated by glucagon phosphorylates phosphorylase kinase, which in turn activates glycogen phosphorylase to break down glycogen. The glucose-6-phosphate produced is released as glucose by glucose-6-phosphatase.'),
('97697d5f-55bc-5378-97bc-67e9e631ccb5'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'Which enzyme is induced by glucagon to promote hepatic gluconeogenesis?', '["Pyruvate kinase","Phosphofructokinase-1","Phosphoenolpyruvate carboxykinase (PEPCK)","Glycogen synthase","Acetyl-CoA carboxylase"]'::jsonb, 'Phosphoenolpyruvate carboxykinase (PEPCK)', 'Glucagon upregulates transcription of gluconeogenic enzymes such as PEPCK and glucose-6-phosphatase while suppressing glycolytic enzymes. This shifts hepatic metabolism toward glucose production.'),
('44f83a57-a85d-57fe-b6c2-76afb84420ee'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'A major effect of glucagon on adipose tissue is:', '["Increased lipogenesis","Activation of hormone-sensitive lipase","Enhanced glucose uptake","Increased insulin receptor sensitivity","Inhibition of fatty acid oxidation"]'::jsonb, 'Activation of hormone-sensitive lipase', 'Glucagon stimulates hormone-sensitive lipase in adipocytes via PKA, increasing lipolysis. The released fatty acids serve as an alternative fuel and as precursors for ketogenesis.'),
('3bc1137b-c9fa-54a7-9810-b042841ddbfe'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'Glucagon promotes ketogenesis by:', '["Inhibiting carnitine palmitoyltransferase I","Lowering hepatic malonyl-CoA levels","Increasing hepatic glycogen storage","Activating acetyl-CoA carboxylase","Stimulating pyruvate dehydrogenase"]'::jsonb, 'Lowering hepatic malonyl-CoA levels', 'Glucagon inhibits acetyl-CoA carboxylase, reducing malonyl-CoA. Low malonyl-CoA relieves inhibition of carnitine palmitoyltransferase I, allowing fatty acids to enter mitochondria for beta-oxidation and ketogenesis.'),
('75907d5c-6088-5e36-a2f1-e47a01dd3a72'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'Catecholamines are synthesized from which amino acid?', '["Tryptophan","Tyrosine","Histidine","Leucine","Cysteine"]'::jsonb, 'Tyrosine', 'Tyrosine is hydroxylated by tyrosine hydroxylase to DOPA in the rate-limiting step of catecholamine synthesis. DOPA is then converted to dopamine, norepinephrine, and epinephrine.'),
('e515e7a1-00fd-570e-855e-e52912f73a16'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'Which enzyme catalyzes the rate-limiting step in catecholamine biosynthesis?', '["Dopamine beta-hydroxylase","Tyrosine hydroxylase","Phenylethanolamine N-methyltransferase","Catechol-O-methyltransferase","Monoamine oxidase"]'::jsonb, 'Tyrosine hydroxylase', 'Tyrosine hydroxylase converts tyrosine to DOPA and is the rate-limiting enzyme of catecholamine synthesis. Catechol-O-methyltransferase and monoamine oxidase are degradation enzymes.'),
('cc6d32d6-1b6c-5ac9-8333-4ae9fa26ab6f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'Epinephrine promotes glycogenolysis in skeletal muscle by:', '["Increasing intracellular cGMP","Binding beta-adrenergic receptors and raising cAMP","Activating the insulin receptor","Stimulating glucose-6-phosphatase in muscle","Inhibiting adenylate cyclase"]'::jsonb, 'Binding beta-adrenergic receptors and raising cAMP', 'Epinephrine binds beta-2 adrenergic GPCRs (Gs) in muscle, activating adenylyl cyclase and PKA, which activates glycogen phosphorylase. Muscle releases lactate because it lacks glucose-6-phosphatase.'),
('1e9c0309-afe8-515e-8653-5997877141f4'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'The effect of epinephrine on the liver that raises blood glucose is:', '["Inhibition of glycogen phosphorylase","Stimulation of glucose-6-phosphatase-coupled glycogenolysis","Increased hepatic glucose uptake","Activation of glucokinase","Inhibition of gluconeogenesis"]'::jsonb, 'Stimulation of glucose-6-phosphatase-coupled glycogenolysis', 'In the liver, epinephrine-activated phosphorylase breaks down glycogen and glucose-6-phosphatase releases free glucose into the blood. The liver expresses glucose-6-phosphatase, whereas skeletal muscle does not.'),
('8eadbf5f-4563-5ad8-9e73-124fbe9f9373'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'Which receptor mediates the lipolytic action of catecholamines on adipose tissue?', '["Alpha-1 adrenergic receptor","Muscarinic receptor","Beta-3 adrenergic receptor","Nicotinic receptor","D2 dopamine receptor"]'::jsonb, 'Beta-3 adrenergic receptor', 'Beta-3 adrenergic receptors (Gs-coupled) in adipocytes activate adenylyl cyclase, raising cAMP and activating hormone-sensitive lipase to promote lipolysis. Alpha-2 receptors actually inhibit lipolysis.'),
('606fcfaf-1895-5a71-9006-cc6ef227f4b7'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'Which enzyme degrades catecholamines at the synapse and in peripheral tissues?', '["Tyrosine hydroxylase","Catechol-O-methyltransferase","Dopamine beta-hydroxylase","Tryptophan hydroxylase","DOPA decarboxylase"]'::jsonb, 'Catechol-O-methyltransferase', 'COMT degrades catecholamines at the synapse and peripherally, while monoamine oxidase (MAO) degrades them intracellularly. Both pathways yield vanillylmandelic acid as a urinary end product.'),
('370de6b5-4d87-5fba-a66f-63eda3726bcf'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'Which of the following is a hyperglycemic effect of epinephrine?', '["Stimulation of insulin release","Increased peripheral glucose uptake","Glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis","Inhibition of hormone-sensitive lipase","Activation of glycogen synthase"]'::jsonb, 'Glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis', 'Epinephrine raises blood glucose by stimulating hepatic glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis while suppressing insulin secretion. It also mobilizes fatty acids as an alternative fuel.'),
('8a7559cc-d7ee-568f-a2f8-ce11e2c403ff'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'Thyroid hormones are derived from which amino acid?', '["Tryptophan","Tyrosine","Methionine","Serine","Arginine"]'::jsonb, 'Tyrosine', 'Tyrosine residues within thyroglobulin are iodinated and coupled to form T4 (tetraiodothyronine) and T3. Iodide is an essential dietary substrate for their synthesis.'),
('ba013620-571e-5c86-9563-6b59225e6a39'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'The more biologically active thyroid hormone with higher affinity for the nuclear receptor is:', '["T4 (thyroxine)","T3 (triiodothyronine)","Reverse T3","Calcitonin","Diiodotyrosine"]'::jsonb, 'T3 (triiodothyronine)', 'T3 has roughly 10 times the receptor affinity and biological potency of T4. Most circulating T3 is produced peripherally by deiodination of T4.'),
('f5b6dc76-a8ec-54d0-9852-980c87e416ea'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'Thyroid hormone acts on target cells by:', '["Binding a cell-surface tyrosine kinase receptor resulting in decreased functional activity","Binding an intracellular nuclear receptor to regulate gene transcription","Activating adenylyl cyclase mediated by specific receptor activation","Opening calcium channels without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Activating the JAK-STAT pathway leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Binding an intracellular nuclear receptor to regulate gene transcription', 'T3 diffuses into cells and binds thyroid hormone nuclear receptors that heterodimerize with RXR on thyroid response elements. This alters transcription of genes controlling metabolism and growth.'),
('b86e7dde-c522-56d0-b8ca-0228e40db911'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'The effect of thyroid hormone on whole-body energy metabolism is:', '["Decreased basal metabolic rate","Increased oxygen consumption and basal metabolic rate","Reduced sodium-potassium ATPase activity","Inhibited hepatic gluconeogenesis","Reduced heat production"]'::jsonb, 'Increased oxygen consumption and basal metabolic rate', 'T3 upregulates mitochondrial enzymes, Na+/K+-ATPase, and uncoupling proteins, increasing oxygen consumption and heat generation. This raises the basal metabolic rate in nearly all tissues.'),
('72b198f2-bc6d-53bb-be22-cdfdf10ead4e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'Which enzyme catalyzes the oxidation of iodide and the iodination of tyrosyl residues in thyroglobulin?', '["Thyroid peroxidase","5''-Deiodinase","Tyrosinase","Monoamine oxidase","Glutamyl transferase"]'::jsonb, 'Thyroid peroxidase', 'Thyroid peroxidase catalyzes iodide oxidation, organification, and coupling of iodotyrosines to form T4 and T3. It is the enzyme inhibited by thiourea drugs used to treat hyperthyroidism.'),
('383ce444-eb2e-5f64-9193-2916b16bf668'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'Synthesis and release of thyroid hormones is stimulated by which anterior pituitary hormone?', '["Adrenocorticotropic hormone","Thyroid-stimulating hormone","Prolactin","Follicle-stimulating hormone","Oxytocin"]'::jsonb, 'Thyroid-stimulating hormone', 'TSH binds G-protein-coupled receptors on thyroid follicular cells to stimulate iodide uptake and hormone synthesis and release. TSH secretion is in turn driven by TRH from the hypothalamus.'),
('c11db4c4-0b49-55de-8ff0-e2836e7d8451'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'Which of the following best explains the calorigenic action of thyroid hormone?', '["Inhibition of uncoupling proteins","Increased activity of sodium-potassium ATPase and mitochondrial enzymes","Decreased sympathetic activity","Reduced fatty acid oxidation","Suppressed beta-adrenergic receptor expression"]'::jsonb, 'Increased activity of sodium-potassium ATPase and mitochondrial enzymes', 'T3 induces Na+/K+-ATPase and respiratory chain enzymes, consuming ATP and generating heat. Enhanced beta-adrenergic sensitivity further contributes to the calorigenic effect.'),
('62244c4d-4fe8-564e-b42d-71f1085b2db4'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'Cortisol is synthesized in which gland and zone?', '["Zona glomerulosa of the adrenal cortex","Zona fasciculata of the adrenal cortex","Adrenal medulla","Posterior pituitary","Pancreatic islets"]'::jsonb, 'Zona fasciculata of the adrenal cortex', 'Cortisol is a glucocorticoid produced in the zona fasciculata of the adrenal cortex. The zona glomerulosa produces aldosterone, and the adrenal medulla produces catecholamines.'),
('325f3838-ab35-5d48-b93e-f11d2075324e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'The common precursor for all steroid hormones including cortisol is:', '["Cholesterol","Acetyl-CoA","Mevalonate","Tyrosine","Squalene"]'::jsonb, 'Cholesterol', 'Steroid hormones are derived from cholesterol through cytochrome P450 hydroxylation steps. Cholesterol is delivered to the inner mitochondrial membrane by the StAR protein for side-chain cleavage.'),
('af5a30c3-5c99-5fc4-b800-700f0358af4b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'Cortisol secretion is regulated primarily by:', '["Blood glucose levels","Adrenocorticotropic hormone from the anterior pituitary","Direct sympathetic innervation","Plasma sodium concentration","Thyroid-stimulating hormone"]'::jsonb, 'Adrenocorticotropic hormone from the anterior pituitary', 'ACTH from the anterior pituitary stimulates adrenal cortisol synthesis. ACTH release is driven by CRH from the hypothalamus, and cortisol exerts negative feedback on both.'),
('8840f7df-918b-5df6-8690-a7eb0063039c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'Which hypothalamic hormone stimulates release of ACTH from the anterior pituitary?', '["Thyrotropin-releasing hormone","Corticotropin-releasing hormone","Gonadotropin-releasing hormone","Somatostatin","Growth hormone-releasing hormone"]'::jsonb, 'Corticotropin-releasing hormone', 'CRH from the paraventricular nucleus of the hypothalamus triggers ACTH secretion by pituitary corticotrophs. CRH and cortisol form the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis.'),
('983cf073-c6a2-56f9-aebe-601a03ece7b7'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'A major metabolic effect of cortisol is:', '["Increased glucose uptake by muscle","Stimulation of hepatic gluconeogenesis","Enhanced protein synthesis","Inhibition of glycogen synthesis","Reduced blood glucose"]'::jsonb, 'Stimulation of hepatic gluconeogenesis', 'Cortisol induces gluconeogenic enzymes such as PEPCK in the liver and mobilizes amino acids from muscle as substrates. This raises blood glucose and underlies cortisol''s catabolic, hyperglycemic actions.'),
('db597f8d-4cd3-5283-a098-e98723a44b19'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'In skeletal muscle, cortisol acts to:', '["Promote protein synthesis","Increase protein breakdown and amino acid release","Stimulate glycogen synthesis","Enhance glucose uptake","Activate lipogenesis"]'::jsonb, 'Increase protein breakdown and amino acid release', 'Cortisol promotes proteolysis in skeletal muscle, releasing amino acids that support hepatic gluconeogenesis. This catabolic effect contrasts with insulin''s protein-sparing action.'),
('728a27dd-0619-57db-a2b9-0b4efb856436'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'In peripheral adipose tissue, cortisol promotes:', '["Lipogenesis","Lipolysis","Glucose uptake","Insulin sensitivity","Fatty acid synthesis"]'::jsonb, 'Lipolysis', 'Cortisol stimulates lipolysis in peripheral fat depots, releasing free fatty acids, while promoting fat redistribution to central sites. Chronic excess manifests as truncal obesity and hyperglycemia.'),
('4ccc3dfb-5df5-5437-88c4-422282e4d2e6'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'Growth hormone is secreted by:', '["Zona fasciculata of the adrenal cortex","Somatotrophs of the anterior pituitary","The posterior pituitary","Pancreatic alpha cells","Thyroid follicular cells"]'::jsonb, 'Somatotrophs of the anterior pituitary', 'Growth hormone is produced by somatotroph cells of the anterior pituitary. Its secretion is controlled by GHRH (stimulatory) and somatostatin (inhibitory) from the hypothalamus.'),
('3d258322-816e-5964-b7ff-45bcf3b02713'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'Which factor mediates most of growth hormone''s growth-promoting actions?', '["Insulin-like growth factor-1 (IGF-1)","Fibroblast growth factor","Platelet-derived growth factor","Transforming growth factor-beta","Epidermal growth factor"]'::jsonb, 'Insulin-like growth factor-1 (IGF-1)', 'GH stimulates the liver and peripheral tissues to produce IGF-1, which mediates linear bone growth and anabolic effects. IGF-1 also feeds back to inhibit GH secretion.'),
('b2b6dbdb-2f8f-58a5-8c0a-e7583435fd3d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'The direct metabolic effect of growth hormone on adipose tissue is:', '["Increased lipogenesis","Increased lipolysis","Enhanced glucose uptake","Activation of acetyl-CoA carboxylase","Reduced fatty acid oxidation"]'::jsonb, 'Increased lipolysis', 'GH activates hormone-sensitive lipase in adipose tissue, releasing free fatty acids as an energy source during fasting. This is a counter-regulatory, insulin-antagonizing effect.'),
('342d8f84-9d4f-5af8-9bda-b52e7f4052a7'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'Which of the following is a diabetogenic action of growth hormone?', '["Increased peripheral glucose uptake","Reduced hepatic glucose output","Decreased insulin sensitivity","Activation of glycogen synthase","Inhibition of gluconeogenesis"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased insulin sensitivity', 'Growth hormone antagonizes insulin action, reducing peripheral glucose utilization and increasing hepatic glucose output. Chronic GH excess causes glucose intolerance or overt diabetes.'),
('c5d433f6-e7d6-523c-aa5e-8145bd6b1822'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'Growth hormone receptor signaling is mediated primarily by which pathway?', '["JAK-STAT pathway","PI3K-Akt pathway","Adenylyl cyclase-cAMP pathway","PLC-IP3 pathway","Guanylyl cyclase-cGMP pathway"]'::jsonb, 'JAK-STAT pathway', 'The GH receptor associates with and activates JAK2 tyrosine kinase, which phosphorylates STAT transcription factors. This is the prototypical cytokine and GH receptor signaling mechanism.'),
('8778edc9-bbb9-5245-ae3c-ff4626fad25f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'Which hypothalamic hormone directly inhibits growth hormone secretion?', '["Ghrelin","Somatostatin","Growth hormone-releasing hormone","Thyrotropin-releasing hormone","Dopamine"]'::jsonb, 'Somatostatin', 'Somatostatin released by the hypothalamus suppresses GH secretion from somatotrophs. GHRH and ghrelin stimulate GH release, while IGF-1 provides long-loop feedback inhibition.'),
('24c993da-ecc5-5fae-abcc-72c6626c9fe4'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'Growth hormone deficiency in childhood results in:', '["Gigantism","Dwarfism","Acromegaly","Cushing syndrome","Hyperthyroidism"]'::jsonb, 'Dwarfism', 'GH deficiency before epiphyseal closure impairs linear growth, causing short stature or dwarfism. Excess GH before closure causes gigantism, and after closure causes acromegaly.'),
('3869e132-7eb2-5357-bcb6-45c47168c9fa'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'The dawn phenomenon is characterized by:', '["Hypoglycemia followed by rebound hyperglycemia","Early-morning hyperglycemia without preceding hypoglycemia","Nocturnal hyperglycemia with ketosis","Postprandial hypoglycemia","Elevated lactate in the morning"]'::jsonb, 'Early-morning hyperglycemia without preceding hypoglycemia', 'The dawn phenomenon results from the normal early-morning surge in growth hormone and cortisol, which increases hepatic glucose output and raises fasting glucose. No preceding hypoglycemia occurs.'),
('09ef2ee9-8cce-5d46-83a8-e5ca620bd5e3'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'The Somogyi effect is defined as:', '["Morning hyperglycemia caused by nocturnal hypoglycemia and counter-regulatory hormone release","Morning hyperglycemia without any preceding hypoglycemia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A dawn surge of growth hormone mediated by specific receptor activation","Postprandial hyperglycemia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Resistance to subcutaneous insulin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Morning hyperglycemia caused by nocturnal hypoglycemia and counter-regulatory hormone release', 'In the Somogyi effect, insulin overtreatment causes nocturnal hypoglycemia, triggering glucagon, epinephrine, and cortisol that produce rebound morning hyperglycemia. Diagnosis requires checking glucose around 2-3 AM.'),
('332e0a44-e5dc-532a-a6ad-88a0cb133d36'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'Which biochemical finding is most characteristic of diabetic ketoacidosis?', '["Elevated serum insulin and low glucose","Hyperglycemia, ketonemia, and metabolic acidosis","Normal anion gap and metabolic alkalosis","Hypoglycemia with respiratory alkalosis","Hypernatremia and euglycemia"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperglycemia, ketonemia, and metabolic acidosis', 'DKA results from severe insulin deficiency with glucagon excess, driving lipolysis, beta-oxidation, and ketone body (acetoacetate and beta-hydroxybutyrate) production, causing a high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis.'),
('61a8c617-1cfa-56f6-be9b-8e4ab5d825b4'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'The most important counter-regulatory hormone responsible for ketogenesis in DKA is:', '["Insulin","Glucagon","Aldosterone","Parathyroid hormone","Estrogen"]'::jsonb, 'Glucagon', 'In DKA, glucagon excess drives lipolysis, lowers malonyl-CoA, and promotes hepatic ketone body synthesis. Insulin deficiency alone is insufficient; the glucagon-to-insulin ratio is the key determinant.'),
('842dc9ec-7073-5c47-8475-d01fb4640d88'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'Hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state (HHS) differs from DKA primarily by:', '["Severe ketoacidosis and ketonemia","Marked hyperglycemia with minimal or absent ketosis","Hypoglycemia with high insulin","Metabolic alkalosis","Elevated lactate without hyperglycemia"]'::jsonb, 'Marked hyperglycemia with minimal or absent ketosis', 'HHS features extreme hyperglycemia and hyperosmolarity with little or no ketosis because residual insulin suppresses lipolysis. It typically occurs in type 2 diabetes and carries a higher mortality than DKA.'),
('f2abb320-46a4-5857-8b9f-48282a16e8d7'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'During prolonged fasting, which fuel becomes the primary energy source for the brain?', '["Glucose","Free fatty acids","Ketone bodies","Lactate","Amino acids"]'::jsonb, 'Ketone bodies', 'After several days of fasting, hepatic ketogenesis provides ketone bodies that the brain oxidizes in preference to glucose. This adaptation spares muscle protein.'),
('65e8aec1-feec-53c0-b2a2-760bda8ee211'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'Hepatic glucose output during fasting is maintained by which processes?', '["Glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis","Glycogenesis and lipogenesis","Ketogenesis and fatty acid esterification","Glycolysis and the pentose phosphate pathway","Proteolysis and glycogen synthesis"]'::jsonb, 'Glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis', 'The liver releases glucose via glycogenolysis in the first hours of fasting, and increasingly via gluconeogenesis from lactate, amino acids, and glycerol as glycogen depletes. Glucose-6-phosphatase releases free glucose.'),
('b93ff7ec-0f8a-5fee-b4ec-04c69042df1e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'Which hormone level is highest during the post-absorptive (fasting) state?', '["Insulin","Glucagon","Leptin","Amylin","Adiponectin"]'::jsonb, 'Glucagon', 'Falling glucose during fasting lowers insulin and raises glucagon, shifting the liver toward glucose production. The insulin-to-glucagon ratio governs hepatic fuel metabolism.'),
('71f27600-34e3-5f30-9815-b32342b81f7d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'The high insulin-to-glucagon ratio in the fed (postprandial) state favors:', '["Glycogenolysis","Ketogenesis","Glycogen synthesis and storage","Gluconeogenesis","Lipolysis"]'::jsonb, 'Glycogen synthesis and storage', 'A high insulin-to-glucagon ratio in the fed state activates glycogen synthase and lipogenesis while suppressing gluconeogenesis. This promotes fuel storage in liver, muscle, and adipose tissue.'),
('50beaf78-82c1-57d1-b240-1a9296fdabb7'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'Leptin is secreted primarily by:', '["Pancreatic beta cells","Adipose tissue","Liver hepatocytes","Hypothalamic neurons","Adrenal medulla"]'::jsonb, 'Adipose tissue', 'Leptin is an adipokine produced mainly by white adipose tissue in proportion to fat stores. It signals energy sufficiency to the hypothalamus.'),
('fce4f84f-7b8c-5bcd-9333-8553de6fac5d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'Leptin acts primarily on which region of the brain to regulate energy balance?', '["Hippocampus","Hypothalamus","Cerebellum","Medulla","Cerebral cortex"]'::jsonb, 'Hypothalamus', 'Leptin crosses the blood-brain barrier and binds receptors on hypothalamic neurons, especially in the arcuate nucleus. It suppresses appetite and stimulates energy expenditure.'),
('325a57f3-8d91-5443-b44a-343599a8aa8c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'Leptin''s main effect on appetite and energy metabolism is:', '["Increased appetite and decreased energy expenditure","Decreased appetite and increased energy expenditure","Stimulation of lipogenesis in adipose tissue","Inhibition of thermogenesis","Increased ghrelin secretion"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased appetite and increased energy expenditure', 'Leptin acts as a satiety signal, reducing food intake and increasing thermogenesis and fat oxidation. Genetic leptin deficiency causes severe hyperphagia and obesity.'),
('6b24a944-cba9-589d-af66-c2c118eb0689'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'Leptin deficiency in the ob/ob mouse results in:', '["Cachexia and anorexia","Hyperphagia, obesity, and diabetes","Enhanced thermogenesis","Decreased food intake","Increased insulin sensitivity"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperphagia, obesity, and diabetes', 'The ob/ob mouse lacks functional leptin and exhibits hyperphagia, obesity, insulin resistance, and infertility. Leptin administration reverses these features, proving its central role in energy homeostasis.'),
('e13b042a-f68b-55ea-93e3-ad5044b6e767'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'Most obese individuals with high circulating leptin demonstrate:', '["True leptin deficiency","Leptin resistance","Enhanced appetite suppression","Increased energy expenditure","Increased adiponectin levels"]'::jsonb, 'Leptin resistance', 'Most obese individuals have elevated leptin yet respond inadequately, a state termed leptin resistance. Impaired leptin signaling perpetuates hyperphagia and weight gain.'),
('488e56ab-8a4d-5801-a90f-59ca33544b20'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'Which hormone opposes the effects of leptin by increasing appetite?', '["Adiponectin","Ghrelin","Amylin","GLP-1","Calcitonin"]'::jsonb, 'Ghrelin', 'Ghrelin, secreted mainly by the stomach during fasting, stimulates appetite and GH release, acting as the main orexigenic counterpart to the satiety signal leptin. GLP-1 and amylin suppress appetite.'),
('fadc46a5-eb33-5cfa-a4d9-b428cbe46d4b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'Adiponectin is secreted by:', '["Skeletal muscle","Adipose tissue","Liver","Pancreatic islets","Kidney"]'::jsonb, 'Adipose tissue', 'Adiponectin is an adipokine produced by adipose tissue, but unlike most adipokines its levels fall in obesity. It circulates in high concentration in lean individuals.'),
('db7244f8-bfaa-5333-9207-13405cb6c2d6'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'Circulating adiponectin levels are characteristically:', '["Increased in obesity","Decreased in obesity and insulin resistance","Unchanged by body weight","Increased in type 2 diabetes","Increased with visceral fat accumulation"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased in obesity and insulin resistance', 'Adiponectin levels are inversely related to adiposity and insulin resistance. Hypoadiponectinemia in obesity contributes to metabolic dysfunction and cardiovascular risk.'),
('28d3264c-f727-50ec-957f-ed695f93ef84'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'Which of the following is a major metabolic action of adiponectin?', '["Increased hepatic glucose production","Enhanced fatty acid oxidation and insulin sensitivity","Stimulation of lipogenesis","Increased appetite","Inhibition of AMPK"]'::jsonb, 'Enhanced fatty acid oxidation and insulin sensitivity', 'Adiponectin activates AMPK in muscle and liver, stimulating fatty acid oxidation and glucose uptake while suppressing hepatic glucose output. These actions improve insulin sensitivity.'),
('f8630143-cbda-573c-a7d0-660a7c7bea18'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'Adiponectin exerts its metabolic effects primarily through which receptors?', '["AdipoR1 and AdipoR2","Leptin receptor","Peroxisome proliferator-activated receptor-gamma","Glucocorticoid receptor","Insulin-like growth factor receptor"]'::jsonb, 'AdipoR1 and AdipoR2', 'Adiponectin binds AdipoR1 and AdipoR2, seven-transmembrane receptors that activate AMPK and PPAR-alpha signaling. This mediates its insulin-sensitizing and fatty-acid-oxidizing actions.'),
('96ba83d1-4ac6-5fd7-9d0c-8ee4da02ea0f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'Adiponectin is cardioprotective partly because it:', '["Increases endothelial inflammation","Reduces vascular inflammation and endothelial dysfunction","Suppresses AMPK activity","Promotes macrophage foam cell formation","Raises LDL oxidation"]'::jsonb, 'Reduces vascular inflammation and endothelial dysfunction', 'Adiponectin has anti-inflammatory and anti-atherogenic properties, protecting endothelium through nitric oxide production and inhibition of inflammatory pathways. Low levels correlate with increased cardiovascular risk.'),
('6c9f6ac8-4e11-5128-9df1-3fd5bb34b851'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'The enzyme activated by adiponectin in muscle and liver that promotes fatty acid oxidation is:', '["Acetyl-CoA carboxylase","AMP-activated protein kinase","Glycogen phosphorylase","Hormone-sensitive lipase","Pyruvate kinase"]'::jsonb, 'AMP-activated protein kinase', 'Adiponectin stimulates AMPK, which phosphorylates and inactivates acetyl-CoA carboxylase, lowering malonyl-CoA and increasing fatty acid oxidation. AMPK also enhances GLUT4-mediated glucose uptake.'),
('5f19d983-616d-569c-a2b3-260eec34acd4'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'AMPK is activated by an increase in the cellular ratio of:', '["ATP to AMP","AMP to ATP","NAD+ to NADH","ADP to ATP","GTP to GDP"]'::jsonb, 'AMP to ATP', 'AMPK is a cellular energy sensor activated when AMP rises relative to ATP, as occurs with energy depletion. AMP binding allosterically activates AMPK and promotes its phosphorylation by LKB1.'),
('d4c36980-92c0-5fab-931f-49aa1d46dd9f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'When activated, AMPK stimulates which process?', '["Fatty acid synthesis","Fatty acid oxidation","Gluconeogenesis","Protein synthesis","Cholesterol synthesis"]'::jsonb, 'Fatty acid oxidation', 'Activated AMPK switches metabolism toward catabolic ATP-generating pathways, stimulating fatty acid oxidation and glucose uptake while inhibiting energy-consuming processes like lipogenesis and protein synthesis.'),
('7c023cd6-33ac-5f86-93e0-344e0a545864'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'AMPK inhibits fatty acid synthesis by phosphorylating and inactivating:', '["Carnitine palmitoyltransferase I","Acetyl-CoA carboxylase","Glucokinase","Glycogen phosphorylase","Pyruvate carboxylase"]'::jsonb, 'Acetyl-CoA carboxylase', 'AMPK phosphorylates and inactivates acetyl-CoA carboxylase, decreasing malonyl-CoA. Lower malonyl-CoA both reduces fatty acid synthesis and relieves inhibition of CPT1, increasing fat oxidation.'),
('5e5cf36e-6b8a-5669-b84f-a5535a2d4a2d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'The upstream kinase that activates AMPK in response to energy stress is:', '["Protein kinase A","Protein kinase C","LKB1","Glycogen synthase kinase-3","Casein kinase"]'::jsonb, 'LKB1', 'LKB1 phosphorylates AMPK at threonine 172 under low-energy conditions, activating it. AMP and ADP binding enhance this phosphorylation and protect AMPK from dephosphorylation.'),
('5f284a03-c2ef-5687-9969-a0f3890ced04'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'Which pharmacological agent is a well-known activator of AMPK?', '["Metformin","Sulfonylurea","Glucagon","Cortisol","Epinephrine"]'::jsonb, 'Metformin', 'Metformin activates AMPK, improving hepatic insulin sensitivity and suppressing hepatic glucose production. AMPK activation underlies many of metformin''s therapeutic effects in type 2 diabetes.'),
('4cde5daf-a7e4-56d6-8a9a-8ebf69914964'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'In skeletal muscle, AMPK activation enhances:', '["Glycogen synthesis","GLUT4-mediated glucose uptake","Protein synthesis","Fatty acid esterification","Gluconeogenesis"]'::jsonb, 'GLUT4-mediated glucose uptake', 'Exercise and AMPK activation stimulate translocation of GLUT4 to the muscle membrane, increasing glucose uptake independently of insulin. AMPK also promotes mitochondrial biogenesis via PGC-1alpha.'),
('dde04cf3-9217-5572-aaea-f01dd1f727a8'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'PPAR-alpha is expressed predominantly in which tissue?', '["Adipose tissue","Liver","Brain","Pancreatic beta cells","Thyroid gland"]'::jsonb, 'Liver', 'PPAR-alpha is highly expressed in the liver, heart, and skeletal muscle, where it regulates fatty acid oxidation. Its ligands include fibrates and endogenous fatty acids.'),
('c7023213-91fa-575f-84c9-c65d328bd5a4'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'Fibrates used to treat dyslipidemia act primarily through which receptor?', '["PPAR-alpha","PPAR-gamma","Liver X receptor","Glucocorticoid receptor","Retinoic acid receptor"]'::jsonb, 'PPAR-alpha', 'Fibrates are PPAR-alpha agonists that increase fatty acid oxidation and apolipoprotein production, lowering triglycerides and raising HDL. PPAR-alpha target genes include CPT1 and ACOX1.'),
('390f2e5f-586b-5800-b631-d59748697523'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'Thiazolidinediones (rosiglitazone, pioglitazone) act as agonists of:', '["PPAR-alpha","PPAR-gamma","Estrogen receptor","Thyroid hormone receptor","Mineralocorticoid receptor"]'::jsonb, 'PPAR-gamma', 'Thiazolidinediones are PPAR-gamma agonists that improve insulin sensitivity by promoting adipocyte differentiation and fatty acid storage. PPAR-gamma is highly expressed in adipose tissue.'),
('49975752-e2e0-59ba-99ef-be859bf2e885'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'PPAR-gamma is most highly expressed in which tissue?', '["Adipose tissue","Liver","Kidney","Cardiac muscle","Adrenal cortex"]'::jsonb, 'Adipose tissue', 'PPAR-gamma is the master regulator of adipogenesis and is expressed at high levels in adipose tissue. Its activation promotes lipid storage in adipocytes and enhances insulin sensitivity.'),
('0968675b-0129-578e-b8ad-fb663a7e5ad3'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'Activation of PPAR-alpha in the liver leads to:', '["Increased fatty acid oxidation and ketogenesis","Increased lipogenesis","Decreased fatty acid uptake","Increased glycogen storage","Increased cholesterol synthesis"]'::jsonb, 'Increased fatty acid oxidation and ketogenesis', 'PPAR-alpha induces genes of beta-oxidation, carnitine transport (CPT1), and ketogenesis, especially during fasting. This enhances clearance of fatty acids from the liver.'),
('8dd5d214-882d-5457-a64c-d129d0fe51eb'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'PPAR receptors function as:', '["Cell-surface tyrosine kinases","Ligand-activated nuclear transcription factors","G-protein coupled receptors","Ligand-gated ion channels","Cytokine receptors"]'::jsonb, 'Ligand-activated nuclear transcription factors', 'PPARs are nuclear receptors that heterodimerize with RXR and bind peroxisome proliferator response elements to regulate gene expression. Their ligands include fatty acids, eicosanoids, and synthetic drugs.'),
('7f8764ec-3406-5ba9-9c16-255bf4cbc997'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'Which pancreatic islet cell secretes somatostatin?', '["Alpha cells","Beta cells","Delta cells","PP cells","Epsilon cells"]'::jsonb, 'Delta cells', 'Delta cells secrete somatostatin, which locally inhibits insulin, glucagon, and pancreatic polypeptide secretion. It acts as a paracrine regulator of islet function.'),
('d38e0a96-4a3c-5a0b-9ef9-b4a2c1406c03'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'The most abundant cell type within the pancreatic islet is:', '["Alpha cells","Beta cells","Delta cells","PP cells","Epsilon cells"]'::jsonb, 'Beta cells', 'Beta cells constitute about 60-80% of pancreatic islet cells and occupy the core of the islet. Alpha cells form the mantle and comprise about 15-20%.'),
('6341e5c9-a784-569a-a065-248e17db9826'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'Pancreatic polypeptide (PP) is secreted by which islet cell type?', '["Alpha cells","Beta cells","Delta cells","PP (F) cells","Acinar cells"]'::jsonb, 'PP (F) cells', 'PP cells, also called F cells, secrete pancreatic polypeptide, which regulates exocrine pancreatic secretion and gut motility. Acinar cells are exocrine cells that secrete digestive enzymes.'),
('dadea5bb-edaa-5ac6-bbb7-77ec5b74a2fd'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'Ghrelin in the pancreas is produced by which cell type?', '["Alpha cells","Epsilon cells","Delta cells","Beta cells","PP cells"]'::jsonb, 'Epsilon cells', 'Epsilon cells are a rare islet cell type that produces ghrelin, an appetite-stimulating hormone. Ghrelin is also secreted abundantly by gastric cells.'),
('247abf82-458f-5390-b55e-31c110b360e8'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'Amylin is co-secreted with insulin by which cells?', '["Alpha cells","Beta cells","Delta cells","PP cells","Epsilon cells"]'::jsonb, 'Beta cells', 'Beta cells co-secrete amylin (islet amyloid polypeptide) with insulin in response to glucose. Amylin slows gastric emptying and suppresses glucagon, complementing insulin action.'),
('33f0b499-2f45-55a3-b592-53d4ed3ad41e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'Which islet hormone suppresses secretion of both insulin and glucagon?', '["Somatostatin","Ghrelin","Amylin","Pancreatic polypeptide","Glucagon-like peptide-1"]'::jsonb, 'Somatostatin', 'Islet delta-cell somatostatin acts as a local brake, inhibiting both insulin and glucagon release. This paracrine action helps coordinate the islet''s hormonal response.'),
('13dd516e-f40e-5839-b6fd-78fb600b002e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'The second messenger generated by activation of adenylyl cyclase is:', '["Inositol trisphosphate","Cyclic AMP","Diacylglycerol","Cyclic GMP","Calcium"]'::jsonb, 'Cyclic AMP', 'Adenylyl cyclase converts ATP to cyclic AMP, which activates protein kinase A. Gs-coupled receptors raise cAMP, while Gi-coupled receptors lower it.'),
('43998bb1-7cf5-5754-83ba-8b6d6a213a7b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'Which enzyme degrades cyclic AMP to terminate its signal?', '["Phospholipase C","Phosphodiesterase","Adenylyl cyclase","Guanylyl cyclase","Protein kinase A"]'::jsonb, 'Phosphodiesterase', 'Phosphodiesterases hydrolyze cAMP and cGMP to AMP and GMP. Caffeine and theophylline are nonspecific PDE inhibitors that prolong cyclic nucleotide signaling.'),
('2dc66d33-29ba-55d8-a32e-43322e8e5ef9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'Phospholipase C hydrolyzes phosphatidylinositol 4,5-bisphosphate to generate:', '["Cyclic AMP and AMP","IP3 and diacylglycerol","Cyclic GMP and GMP","Arachidonic acid and eicosanoids","Phosphatidic acid and choline"]'::jsonb, 'IP3 and diacylglycerol', 'PLC generates inositol trisphosphate (IP3), which releases calcium from the ER, and diacylglycerol (DAG), which activates protein kinase C. Both are classic second messengers of Gq-coupled receptors.'),
('ed03c882-0b5f-57de-a34d-6bfced306d9d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'Inositol trisphosphate (IP3) acts to:', '["Activate protein kinase A","Release calcium from the endoplasmic reticulum","Open voltage-gated sodium channels","Stimulate adenylyl cyclase","Activate guanylyl cyclase"]'::jsonb, 'Release calcium from the endoplasmic reticulum', 'IP3 binds IP3 receptors on the endoplasmic reticulum, opening calcium channels and raising cytosolic calcium. Calcium then activates calmodulin-dependent kinases and other effectors.'),
('a85ee3ad-5b3c-57d0-8a2b-e9a0d67d0b00'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'Protein kinase C is directly activated by which second messenger?', '["Cyclic AMP","Diacylglycerol","Cyclic GMP","Inositol trisphosphate","Adenosine"]'::jsonb, 'Diacylglycerol', 'DAG remains membrane-bound and recruits and activates protein kinase C, with calcium (released by IP3) serving as a cofactor. PKC phosphorylates a wide range of cellular targets.'),
('f34d7abe-a0be-57e2-a132-0ab39b6c11fa'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'Atrial natriuretic peptide signals through which second messenger?', '["Cyclic AMP","Cyclic GMP","Diacylglycerol","Inositol trisphosphate","Calcium"]'::jsonb, 'Cyclic GMP', 'ANP binds a receptor with intrinsic guanylyl cyclase activity, generating cGMP. cGMP activates protein kinase G and is also the second messenger of nitric oxide.'),
('60c01654-0ca7-5b52-b4cc-b1942c72566e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'Guanylyl cyclase converts GTP to:', '["Cyclic AMP","Cyclic GMP","Inositol trisphosphate","Diacylglycerol","Adenosine"]'::jsonb, 'Cyclic GMP', 'Guanylyl cyclase produces cyclic GMP from GTP. It exists in soluble (NO-activated) and membrane-bound (peptide-activated) forms, both generating the cGMP signal.'),
('e6558ab0-1100-5c10-a2bc-6bcd7b5e0a69'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'The ubiquitous intracellular protein that binds calcium and mediates many of its actions as a second messenger is:', '["Protein kinase A","Calmodulin","Phospholipase C","Guanine nucleotide exchange factor","Adenylyl cyclase"]'::jsonb, 'Calmodulin', 'Calmodulin binds four calcium ions and then activates calmodulin-dependent kinases and other enzymes. It mediates many calcium-triggered cellular responses.'),
('5b5dddf0-ddf1-5c58-a5af-fa5a193328ab'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'G-protein coupled receptors (GPCRs) are characterized by:', '["Seven transmembrane-spanning segments coupled to heterotrimeric G proteins","Intrinsic tyrosine kinase activity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ligand-gated ion channel function mediated by specific receptor activation","Nuclear localization without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A single transmembrane domain leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Seven transmembrane-spanning segments coupled to heterotrimeric G proteins', 'GPCRs span the membrane seven times and signal through heterotrimeric G proteins, which modulate adenylyl cyclase, phospholipase C, or ion channels. Glucagon, epinephrine, and TSH act through GPCRs.'),
('8b235844-6583-585f-be0d-e83dbe9de91d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'The insulin receptor belongs to which class of receptors?', '["G-protein coupled receptor","Receptor tyrosine kinase","Ligand-gated ion channel","Intracellular nuclear receptor","Guanylyl cyclase receptor"]'::jsonb, 'Receptor tyrosine kinase', 'The insulin receptor is a tetrameric receptor tyrosine kinase; ligand binding activates its intrinsic kinase, leading to autophosphorylation and recruitment of IRS proteins. Other receptor tyrosine kinases include growth factor receptors.'),
('1d502b56-0cee-5ff8-9a9d-b8116c1fb1d5'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'Steroid hormones such as cortisol act through:', '["Cell-surface receptor tyrosine kinases","Intracellular nuclear receptors","Gs-coupled receptors","Ligand-gated ion channels","JAK-STAT cytokine receptors"]'::jsonb, 'Intracellular nuclear receptors', 'Lipophilic steroid and thyroid hormones diffuse into cells and bind nuclear receptors that regulate gene transcription. These responses are slower than membrane-receptor signaling.'),
('efc0c8bc-757b-5c07-87d6-615529bc297c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'Which receptor type signals through the JAK-STAT pathway?', '["Growth hormone receptor","Insulin receptor","Beta-adrenergic receptor","Muscarinic receptor","Thyroid hormone receptor"]'::jsonb, 'Growth hormone receptor', 'The GH receptor and other cytokine receptors lack intrinsic kinase activity but associate with JAK kinases, which phosphorylate STAT transcription factors. Insulin uses a receptor tyrosine kinase, and beta-adrenergic receptors use G proteins.'),
('557d4e93-6433-51ea-b3c8-089a6ba92f1d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'A receptor with intrinsic ligand-gated ion channel activity is exemplified by:', '["Nicotinic acetylcholine receptor","Beta-2 adrenergic receptor","Insulin receptor","Estrogen receptor","Glucagon receptor"]'::jsonb, 'Nicotinic acetylcholine receptor', 'The nicotinic acetylcholine receptor is a ligand-gated cation channel that opens on binding acetylcholine. Ionotropic receptors produce the fastest synaptic responses compared to metabotropic GPCRs.'),
('e2e01de3-298b-56a2-aebe-902483f25c54'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Hormonal Metabolism', 'Activation of a Gs-coupled receptor results in:', '["Decreased cAMP and reduced PKA activity","Increased adenylyl cyclase activity and cAMP","Activation of phospholipase C","Opening of IP3 receptors","Inhibition of calcium channels"]'::jsonb, 'Increased adenylyl cyclase activity and cAMP', 'The Gs alpha subunit stimulates adenylyl cyclase, raising cAMP and activating PKA. In contrast, Gi inhibits adenylyl cyclase and Gq activates phospholipase C.'),
('28ae164c-aa4f-5b9c-bc57-7f63da8040d5'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Which enzyme deficiency causes phenylketonuria?', '["Homogentisate oxidase","Cystathionine beta-synthase","Tyrosine hydroxylase","Branched-chain alpha-ketoacid dehydrogenase","Phenylalanine hydroxylase"]'::jsonb, 'Phenylalanine hydroxylase', 'Phenylketonuria results from phenylalanine hydroxylase deficiency, causing phenylalanine accumulation and intellectual disability.'),
('a90e6880-1626-5e84-936c-085d1e636dfe'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Phenylalanine hydroxylase requires which cofactor?', '["PLP resulting in decreased functional activity","Tetrahydrobiopterin (BH4)","FAD mediated by specific receptor activation","NAD without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CoA leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tetrahydrobiopterin (BH4)', 'BH4 is the cofactor for phenylalanine hydroxylase; its deficiency causes atypical PKU unresponsive to dietary restriction.'),
('5b6bd6ea-e49a-548f-9fe1-a4c601ffbf74'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'PKU leads to accumulation of:', '["Glucose","Homogentisate","Phenylalanine and phenylpyruvate","Galactose","Branched-chain amino acids"]'::jsonb, 'Phenylalanine and phenylpyruvate', 'Phenylketonuria results from phenylalanine hydroxylase deficiency, causing phenylalanine accumulation and intellectual disability.'),
('604c698c-c7c7-599e-abc6-4933d58b9cec'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Untreated PKU leads to:', '["Muscle weakness resulting in decreased functional activity","Ochronosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Jaundice mediated by specific receptor activation","Severe intellectual disability","Anemia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Severe intellectual disability', 'PKU is caused by phenylalanine hydroxylase deficiency. Phenylalanine accumulates causing intellectual disability. Newborn screening and low-phenylalanine diet prevent damage.'),
('25378416-6b1e-5186-a523-e4866cd2632f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'The screening test for PKU is:', '["Clinitest resulting in decreased functional activity","Dinitrophenylhydrazine test accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Beutler test mediated by specific receptor activation","Galactosemia test without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Guthrie test (bacterial inhibition assay)"]'::jsonb, 'Guthrie test (bacterial inhibition assay)', 'Guthrie test uses Bacillus subtilis growth inhibition: antibodies against phenylalanine in the test medium are neutralized by phenylalanine in the patient''s blood spot, allowing bacterial growth proportional to phenylalanine level.'),
('d73bdfa7-b84f-535c-9881-0eb7f14d7fdf'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'What is the recommended dietary management for PKU?', '["Avoid carbohydrates resulting in decreased functional activity","High protein diet accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Avoid all amino acids mediated by specific receptor activation","Restriction of phenylalanine intake with tyrosine supplementation","Restriction of tyrosine intake leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Restriction of phenylalanine intake with tyrosine supplementation', 'Tyrosine is derived from phenylalanine and serves as a precursor for catecholamines, melanin, and thyroid hormones.'),
('ab155680-d49a-5d4f-9c23-f5a4ff3792f0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'The artificial sweetener PKU patients must avoid is:', '["Sugar alcohols","Saccharin","Honey","Sucralose","Aspartame"]'::jsonb, 'Aspartame', 'Aspartame (NutraSweet) is metabolized to phenylalanine, aspartic acid, and methanol. PKU patients cannot convert phenylalanine to tyrosine, so aspartame intake risks dangerous phenylalanine accumulation.'),
('b9ad775f-6f85-5c23-9137-71992b7dcc08'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Sapropterin is a synthetic:', '["PLP analog","NAD analog","Folate analog","Biotin analog","BH4 analog"]'::jsonb, 'BH4 analog', 'BH4 is the cofactor for phenylalanine hydroxylase; its deficiency causes atypical PKU unresponsive to dietary restriction.'),
('cac9f197-71ca-5e0d-8130-535d924044ab'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Alkaptonuria is caused by deficiency of:', '["Homogentisate 1,2-dioxygenase","Phenylalanine hydroxylase","Cystathionine beta-synthase","Tyrosinase","Homogentisate oxidase"]'::jsonb, 'Homogentisate 1,2-dioxygenase', 'Alkaptonuria results from homogentisate 1,2-dioxygenase (also called homogentisate oxidase) deficiency, causing homogentisic acid accumulation, dark urine on standing, and ochronosis.'),
('f823a18b-3153-5fca-a4f7-961dc8276107'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'The characteristic feature of alkaptonuria is:', '["Darkening of urine on exposure to air","Blue sclera accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fair skin mediated by specific receptor activation","Intellectual disability without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anemia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Darkening of urine on exposure to air', 'Alkaptonuria results from homogentisic acid oxidase deficiency, causing homogentisic acid accumulation and dark urine.'),
('94e63ace-df35-572a-aadd-917daeee8623'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Ochronosis refers to:', '["Bluish-black pigmentation of connective tissues","Dark sclera accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Red skin mediated by specific receptor activation","Brown teeth without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Blue urine leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bluish-black pigmentation of connective tissues', 'In alkaptonuria, homogentisic acid accumulates and polymerizes into a melanin-like pigment that deposits in connective tissues (ochronosis), causing bluish-black discoloration of cartilage, sclerae, and ear cartilage.'),
('4df55519-f139-5554-8373-67a4b657777b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Which disease is also known as black urine disease?', '["MSUD","Blue diaper syndrome","Black urine disease","Red urine syndrome","PKU"]'::jsonb, 'Black urine disease', 'Alkaptonuria is called black urine disease because accumulated homogentisic acid in urine oxidizes and turns dark brown/black upon standing or exposure to alkali.'),
('d198df0e-fb56-5102-b32a-2d967b205056'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Alkaptonuria was described by Garrod as:', '["A traumatic injury","A viral infection","A nutritional deficiency","An inborn error of metabolism","A malignancy"]'::jsonb, 'An inborn error of metabolism', 'Alkaptonuria results from homogentisic acid oxidase deficiency, causing homogentisic acid accumulation and dark urine.'),
('b45417c6-1fd6-539e-bb33-3e1547368608'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'The branched-chain amino acids are:', '["Valine","Isoleucine","Phenylalanine","Tyrosine","Leucine"]'::jsonb, 'Leucine', 'Branched-chain amino acids (BCAAs) are leucine, isoleucine, and valine. They are essential amino acids with aliphatic side chains branched at the beta-carbon. Phenylalanine and tyrosine are aromatic amino acids.'),
('efd339f5-deb1-53ec-9ea7-e8b28e278e5f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'The urine odor in MSUD is:', '["Rancid butter","Boiled cabbage","Maple syrup or burnt sugar","Sweet fruity","Mousy"]'::jsonb, 'Maple syrup or burnt sugar', 'The maple syrup odor in MSUD comes from sotolone, a compound produced from oxidation of isoleucine metabolites. The characteristic odor is detected in urine and earwax of affected neonates.'),
('b73e78ad-9411-5ae8-9254-063e65621d5c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'MSUD presents with:', '["Vomiting","Poor feeding","Coma","Seizures","Mild anemia"]'::jsonb, 'Poor feeding', 'MSUD in neonates presents with poor feeding, vomiting, lethargy, and characteristic maple syrup odor. Untreated, it progresses to seizures, coma, and death due to leucine neurotoxicity.'),
('387ece54-ebb7-5331-bf4b-c6fb7e401557'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Treatment of MSUD involves:', '["Supplementation with BCAA","Avoid all carbohydrates","Dietary restriction of branched-chain amino acids","High protein diet","Restriction of all amino acids"]'::jsonb, 'Dietary restriction of branched-chain amino acids', 'MSUD treatment requires dietary restriction of branched-chain amino acids (leucine, isoleucine, valine) while supplementing other amino acids and providing adequate calories to prevent catabolism.'),
('ee2702fb-7a8b-52ca-867e-c21df7c4acbc'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Thiamine supplementation helps some patients with:', '["PKU resulting in decreased functional activity","Alkaptonuria accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Tay-Sachs mediated by specific receptor activation","MSUD (thiamine-responsive form)","Galactosemia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'MSUD (thiamine-responsive form)', 'Thiamine pyrophosphate (TPP) is a coenzyme for pyruvate dehydrogenase and alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase.'),
('9f200c24-d970-5905-b69c-9db5e38c9cd4'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Clinical features of homocystinuria include:', '["Intellectual disability","Lens dislocation","Thromboembolism","Ochronosis","Marfanoid habitus"]'::jsonb, 'Marfanoid habitus', 'Marfanoid habitus (tall stature, long limbs, arachnodactyly) is characteristic of homocystinuria. It is distinguished from Marfan syndrome by downward lens dislocation, intellectual disability, and thromboembolism.'),
('ba38f766-d7cf-53f1-9453-3245a9453421'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Thromboembolism in homocystinuria results from:', '["Increased clotting factors resulting in decreased functional activity","Decreased anticoagulants accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Homocysteine-mediated endothelial damage and platelet activation","Platelet deficiency without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fibrinolysis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Homocysteine-mediated endothelial damage and platelet activation', 'Classical homocystinuria is caused by cystathionine beta-synthase deficiency. Homocysteine accumulates causing marfanoid habitus, lens dislocation, thromboembolism, and intellectual disability.'),
('7d354d96-d15a-566e-8f20-4b6d241822ad'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Treatment of homocystinuria includes:', '["Methionine restriction","Folate","B12","Betaine","All: pyridoxine"]'::jsonb, 'All: pyridoxine', 'Classical homocystinuria is caused by cystathionine beta-synthase deficiency. Homocysteine accumulates causing marfanoid habitus, lens dislocation, thromboembolism, and intellectual disability.'),
('339b2489-d425-5b49-89df-2c87eb900c82'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Pyridoxine-responsive homocystinuria implies CBS mutation has:', '["Response","Complete loss of activity","Increased affinity","Decreased affinity","Residual activity with PLP enhancement"]'::jsonb, 'Residual activity with PLP enhancement', 'Pyridoxine-responsive homocystinuria implies the CBS mutation retains residual enzyme activity that can be enhanced by high-dose PLP supplementation, converting homocysteine to cystathionine more effectively.'),
('94ce1b3b-1423-5682-aa92-7b917bf0e868'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Tyrosinemia type I is caused by deficiency of:', '["Phenylalanine hydroxylase","4-HPPD","Tyrosine aminotransferase","Fumarylacetoacetate hydrolase (FAH)","Homogentisate oxidase"]'::jsonb, 'Fumarylacetoacetate hydrolase (FAH)', 'Deficiency of fumarylacetoacetate hydrolase (fah) leads to specific clinical manifestations due to impaired metabolic function.'),
('3b9c0c75-8f00-5fc5-8af3-4dbb908402ff'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'The diagnostic metabolite in tyrosinemia I is:', '["Homogentisate","Fumarylacetoacetate","Succinylacetone","Tyrosine","Maleylacetoacetate"]'::jsonb, 'Succinylacetone', 'Succinylacetone is pathognomonic for tyrosinemia type I. It accumulates because fumarylacetoacetate hydrolase (FAH) deficiency blocks the last step of tyrosine degradation, and its detection in urine is a specific diagnostic marker.'),
('9d6b35dc-5ebb-50dd-9fa9-feb94467945f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Tyrosinemia type I presents with:', '["Liver failure","Intellectual disability alone","And porphyria-like symptoms","Renal tubular dysfunction","Ochronosis"]'::jsonb, 'Liver failure', 'Tyrosinemia type I (FAH deficiency) presents in infancy with acute liver failure, a cabbage-like odor, renal tubular dysfunction (Fanconi syndrome), porphyria-like neurologic crises, and a high risk of hepatocellular carcinoma. Type I is the severe hepatorenal form.'),
('8b925198-6419-5a9f-ab74-7915a4504190'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Tyrosinemia type II (Richner-Hanhart syndrome) is due to:', '["FAH deficiency","Tyrosine aminotransferase deficiency","Homogentisate oxidase deficiency","PAH deficiency","4-HPPD deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Tyrosine aminotransferase deficiency', 'Tyrosine is derived from phenylalanine and serves as a precursor for catecholamines, melanin, and thyroid hormones.'),
('7bc52199-48cc-5cac-87dd-a01cf28f5ccc'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Tyrosinemia type II primarily presents with:', '["Liver failure","Skin lesions and corneal ulcers","Renal dysfunction","Cardiomyopathy","Neurologic crisis"]'::jsonb, 'Skin lesions and corneal ulcers', 'Tyrosinemia type II (Richner-Hanhart syndrome) is caused by tyrosine aminotransferase deficiency. It presents with palmoplantar keratoderma (painful skin lesions), corneal ulcers, and intellectual disability, but WITHOUT liver disease.'),
('6474574d-e88b-593c-b6a3-f7183e520b43'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Albinism results from deficiency of:', '["Phenylalanine hydroxylase","Tyrosinase","MSH","MSH receptor","Homogentisate oxidase"]'::jsonb, 'Tyrosinase', 'Deficiency of tyrosinase leads to specific clinical manifestations due to impaired metabolic function.'),
('ec25edea-f9ba-5e84-8f95-77e2469a6559'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'The two forms of albinism are:', '["Oculocutaneous and ocular","Mild and severe","Congenital and acquired","Type I and II","Complete and partial"]'::jsonb, 'Oculocutaneous and ocular', 'Albinism exists as oculocutaneous albinism (affecting skin, hair, and eyes - more common and severe) and ocular albinism (primarily affecting the eyes with foveal hypoplasia).'),
('4963575f-4950-51d6-bb6e-5ecd387df993'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Tyrosinase converts tyrosine to:', '["Homogentisate","Phenylalanine","Epinephrine","DOPA then melanin","Norepinephrine"]'::jsonb, 'DOPA then melanin', 'Tyrosinase catalyzes the oxidation of tyrosine to DOPA and then to dopaquinone, the rate-limiting step in melanin biosynthesis. Deficiency causes oculocutaneous albinism type I.'),
('910ed4ad-0d9e-5c67-b2cb-c756f743dedf'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'GSD type I (von Gierke) results from deficiency of:', '["Glucose-6-phosphatase","Branching enzyme","Glycogen phosphorylase","Lysosomal alpha-glucosidase","Debranching enzyme"]'::jsonb, 'Glucose-6-phosphatase', 'Deficiency of glucose-6-phosphatase leads to specific clinical manifestations due to impaired metabolic function.'),
('af295a7e-5034-5899-a95a-9b0402b755bc'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'GSD type I primarily affects:', '["Brain","Heart","Muscle","Lungs","Liver and kidneys"]'::jsonb, 'Liver and kidneys', 'GSD type I (von Gierke) affects liver and kidneys primarily because glucose-6-phosphatase is most abundant in these tissues, causing hepatomegaly, renal enlargement, and severe fasting hypoglycemia.'),
('f58880aa-4a47-5f62-8e45-27f6b4c2388b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Clinical features of von Gierke disease:', '["Hepatomegaly","Lactic acidosis","Hyperuricemia","Hypertriglyceridemia","Fasting hypoglycemia"]'::jsonb, 'Fasting hypoglycemia', 'Von Gierke disease presents with severe fasting hypoglycemia, hepatomegaly, lactic acidosis, hyperuricemia (gout), and hypertriglyceridemia due to glucose-6-phosphatase deficiency.'),
('24013ecf-ce98-5d50-b4a4-cc37a8300fa8'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Pompe disease is characterized by:', '["Especially heart and muscle resulting in decreased functional activity","Glycogen in RBCs accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Glycogen in kidney mediated by specific receptor activation","Glycogen in liver only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Glycogen accumulation in lysosomes of all tissues"]'::jsonb, 'Glycogen accumulation in lysosomes of all tissues', 'Pompe disease (GSD type II) is unique as a lysosomal storage disease. Acid alpha-glucosidase deficiency causes glycogen accumulation in lysosomes of all tissues, especially heart and skeletal muscle.'),
('0ee1348e-50c7-58f0-8adc-a272d32776cd'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Infantile Pompe presents with:', '["Hypotonia","Hypoglycemia","Hepatomegaly only","And early death","Cardiomegaly"]'::jsonb, 'Cardiomegaly', 'Infantile Pompe disease presents with massive cardiomegaly, hypotonia, macroglossia, and progressive heart failure. Without ERT, death occurs within the first year.'),
('6f8a790a-48e3-5009-9c26-8bbb5ec31af6'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'GSD type III (Cori disease) is caused by deficiency of:', '["Glycogen phosphorylase","Branching enzyme","Debranching enzyme","G6Pase","Phosphorylase kinase"]'::jsonb, 'Debranching enzyme', 'GSD type III (Cori disease) is caused by debranching enzyme deficiency, preventing complete glycogen breakdown. Limit dextrin accumulates, causing hepatomegaly and hypoglycemia.'),
('c63d9b13-58bc-5025-b53b-04b080386ae5'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Debranching enzyme has two activities:', '["6-glucosidase","Transferase and alpha-1","Transferase and phosphorylase","Alpha-1","Phosphorylase and phosphatase"]'::jsonb, 'Transferase and alpha-1', 'Debranching enzyme has two activities: 4-alpha-glucanotransferase (moves a block of three glucosyl residues) and amylo-1,6-glucosidase (cleaves the remaining single glucose at branch points).'),
('953440d4-0432-5619-bcda-20c0524cd3dd'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'GSD type III presents with:', '["Cardiomegaly resulting in decreased functional activity","Severe hypoglycemia with lactic acidosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hypoglycemia and hepatomegaly but no lactic acidosis","Renal failure without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anemia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hypoglycemia and hepatomegaly but no lactic acidosis', 'GSD type III causes hypoglycemia and hepatomegaly like von Gierke, but WITHOUT significant lactic acidosis because gluconeogenesis remains intact. Hyperuricemia and hyperlipidemia are also milder.'),
('01bc4cd0-ed8f-591b-9787-97de7412a74f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Which enzyme catalyzes the rate-limiting step of glycogen breakdown?', '["Phosphorylase kinase","Liver glycogen phosphorylase","Phosphofructokinase","Muscle glycogen phosphorylase","G6Pase"]'::jsonb, 'Muscle glycogen phosphorylase', 'Glycogen phosphorylase catalyzes the rate-limiting step of glycogen breakdown by cleaving alpha-1,4-glycosidic bonds to release glucose-1-phosphate. Liver and muscle isoforms exist.'),
('d56957ae-fe9d-59ec-a56f-eb7ab25cc39e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Classic presentation of McArdle disease:', '["Exercise intolerance","Cardiomegaly","Myoglobinuria","Muscle cramps","Hypoglycemia"]'::jsonb, 'Exercise intolerance', 'McArdle disease (GSD V) presents with exercise intolerance, muscle cramps, and myoglobinuria due to muscle glycogen phosphorylase deficiency. Symptoms occur when muscles cannot mobilize glycogen during exercise.'),
('535d081c-fe08-527a-bd17-d17df00afc66'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'The second wind phenomenon in McArdle disease:', '["Improved exercise tolerance after brief rest","Complete inability to exercise","Symptoms","Faster recovery","Initial burst of energy"]'::jsonb, 'Improved exercise tolerance after brief rest', 'The second wind phenomenon in McArdle disease occurs when patients improve after brief rest, due to increased blood flow delivering glucose and fatty acids from other sources to exercising muscle.'),
('f9c5f259-eb69-53d0-806c-a698eeda25db'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Galactosemia is most commonly caused by deficiency of:', '["Galactokinase","Galactose-1-phosphate uridyltransferase (GALT)","Galactose mutase","UDP-galactose epimerase","UDP-glucose pyrophosphorylase"]'::jsonb, 'Galactose-1-phosphate uridyltransferase (GALT)', 'Classic galactosemia is caused by GALT deficiency. Galactose-1-phosphate and galactitol accumulate, causing cataracts, hepatosplenomegaly, intellectual disability, and life-threatening E. coli sepsis in neonates.'),
('4ea96c53-ac0e-5aba-80e0-969ad471b698'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Galactosemia type II is caused by deficiency of:', '["Aldose reductase","UDP-galactose epimerase","GALT","Galactose dehydrogenase","Galactokinase"]'::jsonb, 'Galactokinase', 'Galactosemia type II (galactokinase deficiency) causes only galactitol accumulation (no galactose-1-phosphate), resulting mainly in cataracts without severe systemic features.'),
('9be3a8cc-cd0c-57b3-8b3d-6e673d166d61'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Newborns with galactosemia present after milk feeding with:', '["Vomiting","Hepatomegaly","Diarrhea","Failure to thrive","Jaundice"]'::jsonb, 'Vomiting', 'Newborns with galactosemia present after milk feeding with vomiting, diarrhea, jaundice, hepatomegaly, and failure to thrive. E. coli sepsis is a dangerous early complication.'),
('5ab72989-9cf8-5d68-a8b2-843766421941'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'E. coli sepsis is a known complication of:', '["Alkaptonuria","PKU","Tyrosinemia","Galactosemia","MSUD"]'::jsonb, 'Galactosemia', 'E. coli sepsis complicates galactosemia because galactose-1-phosphate accumulation impairs neutrophil bactericidal function and the galactose-rich gut environment favors E. coli overgrowth.'),
('3ffca96c-23dd-5082-a647-337a237627ce'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Clinitest positive but glucose oxidase negative in:', '["Alkaptonuria","MSUD","Diabetes","Galactosemia","PKU"]'::jsonb, 'Galactosemia', 'In galactosemia, Clinitest (copper reduction) is positive for galactose, but glucose oxidase is negative (specific for glucose only). This pattern distinguishes galactosemia from diabetes.'),
('4059c067-1c27-58b5-8537-59413efce14a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Ingestion of fructose in HFI leads to:', '["Hyperglycemia","Hypoglycemia","Vomiting","Symptoms","Abdominal pain"]'::jsonb, 'Hypoglycemia', 'In HFI, aldolase B deficiency causes fructose-1-phosphate accumulation, which traps phosphate and inhibits glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis, leading to hypoglycemia, vomiting, and abdominal pain after fructose ingestion.'),
('40edbadd-466e-53df-9659-ffb4b2836492'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Fructose is avoided in HFI by eliminating:', '["And sorbitol","Dairy products","Starches","Fruit","Sucrose"]'::jsonb, 'Fruit', 'HFI management requires eliminating fructose and sucrose (cleaved to fructose + glucose) from the diet. Sorbitol (metabolized to fructose) must also be avoided.'),
('c9cb9cb9-58b3-52dc-b335-9bdd61aebfa0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Essential fructosuria (benign) is due to deficiency of:', '["Hexokinase","Fructokinase","Phosphofructokinase","Glucokinase","Aldolase B"]'::jsonb, 'Fructokinase', 'Essential fructosuria is benign, caused by fructokinase deficiency. Fructose cannot be phosphorylated, accumulates in blood, and is excreted in urine. No clinical consequences.'),
('ca962dfd-fbc7-51bd-b4f3-610d328c005e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Pentosuria is due to deficiency of:', '["Aldose reductase","L-Xylulose reductase (NADP-dependent)","Ribose-5-phosphate isomerase","Xylulose kinase","Transketolase"]'::jsonb, 'L-Xylulose reductase (NADP-dependent)', 'Pentosuria (L-xylulosuria) is caused by L-xylulose reductase deficiency in the glucuronate pathway. It is benign and found primarily in Ashkenazi Jewish populations.'),
('638d4f64-3510-5b1d-b64d-746c0fa660d0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Pentosuria is common in:', '["Ashkenazi Jewish populations","African populations","Asian populations","Australian aboriginals","Northern Europeans"]'::jsonb, 'Ashkenazi Jewish populations', 'Pentosuria prevalence is 1 in 2,500-4,000 in Ashkenazi Jewish populations. It follows autosomal recessive inheritance and causes no clinical disease.'),
('3962c9a4-6038-5d7d-a46a-e58f43caeef3'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Lactose intolerance is diagnosed by:', '["Glucose tolerance test","Stool pH","HbA1c","Hydrogen breath test","Lactose tolerance test"]'::jsonb, 'Hydrogen breath test', 'The hydrogen breath test is the preferred non-invasive test for lactose intolerance. Undigested lactose is fermented by colonic bacteria; the produced hydrogen is absorbed and exhaled, confirming malabsorption.'),
('eb6524fa-00aa-5154-b0d1-9553f47598ae'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Primary lactase deficiency is due to:', '["Secondary to intestinal damage resulting in decreased functional activity","Normal age-related decline in lactase activity after weaning","Allergic reaction mediated by specific receptor activation","Medication side effect without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Genetic absence from birth leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Normal age-related decline in lactase activity after weaning', 'Primary lactase deficiency (adult-type hypolactasia) is due to normal age-related decline in lactase activity after weaning. It affects 65-70% of the world''s population.'),
('306ecb9b-87a4-578a-9a77-196618d8b3a3'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Congenital lactase deficiency is:', '["Self-limited","Common","Presents in infancy with severe diarrhea","Autosomal recessive","Rare"]'::jsonb, 'Rare', 'Congenital lactase deficiency is a rare autosomal recessive condition causing severe watery diarrhea upon lactose ingestion in infancy, requiring lactose-free formula from birth.'),
('6259956e-4844-55e4-872f-9aa1d8429678'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Tay-Sachs disease is caused by deficiency of:', '["Hexosaminidase B","Beta-glucocerebrosidase","Hexosaminidase A (alpha-subunit)","Beta-galactosidase","Alpha-galactosidase A"]'::jsonb, 'Hexosaminidase A (alpha-subunit)', 'Tay-Sachs is caused by hexosaminidase A deficiency (alpha subunit), leading to GM2 ganglioside accumulation in neurons. Carrier frequency is 1 in 30 in Ashkenazi Jews.'),
('51168a90-e242-5f3a-93b2-f652caab19a3'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Which structure or function is associated with this lipid or molecule?', '["GM2 ganglioside","GM1 ganglioside","Sphingomyelin","Heparan sulfate","Glucocerebroside"]'::jsonb, 'GM2 ganglioside', 'In Tay-Sachs, GM2 ganglioside accumulates in neurons due to hexosaminidase A deficiency. GM1, sphingomyelin, and glucocerebroside are normal in Tay-Sachs.'),
('f83fa452-2327-5bf9-aabf-fd0ce2722e8b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Tay-Sachs is most common in:', '["Hispanics","Ashkenazi Jews","Africans","Caucasians","Asians"]'::jsonb, 'Ashkenazi Jews', 'Tay-Sachs is most common in Ashkenazi Jews (1 in 30 carrier frequency) due to a founder effect. Testing programs have reduced incidence in this population.'),
('20e5f5ee-c2f4-5cc0-8c5e-226a5c8ab071'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Hexosaminidase A consists of:', '["Alpha-beta-gamma","Gamma-gamma","Beta-beta","Alpha-alpha","Alpha-beta subunits"]'::jsonb, 'Alpha-beta subunits', 'Hexosaminidase A is an alpha-beta heterodimer. The alpha subunit is deficient in Tay-Sachs; the beta subunit is deficient in Sandhoff disease.'),
('87c00a0c-739b-5250-83a4-42f0b9633236'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Gaucher cells have what appearance?', '["Sand-like resulting in decreased functional activity","Foamy cytoplasm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Onion skin mediated by specific receptor activation","Crumpled tissue paper or wrinkled paper","Clear cytoplasm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Crumpled tissue paper or wrinkled paper', 'Gaucher cells are lipid-laden macrophages with crumpled tissue paper (wrinkled paper) cytoplasm due to glucocerebroside accumulation in the lysosomes.'),
('072d29a3-746f-530f-967d-7839e2117094'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Substrate reduction therapy for Gaucher uses:', '["Bosutinib resulting in decreased functional activity","Idursulfase accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Miglustat (inhibits glucosylceramide synthase)","Galsulfase without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Imiglucerase leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Miglustat (inhibits glucosylceramide synthase)', 'Miglustat inhibits glucosylceramide synthase, reducing glucocerebroside synthesis. It is used for mild-to-moderate Gaucher disease type 1 as substrate reduction therapy.');
insert into fcps_mcq_stage (id, subject_name, chapter_name, question, options, correct_answer, explanation) values
('d6fd1d8c-d9d1-5e48-bbaf-dcf4dc362316'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Niemann-Pick disease (type A/B) is caused by deficiency of:', '["Beta-glucocerebrosidase","Sphingomyelinase","Beta-galactosidase","Hexosaminidase A","Alpha-galactosidase A"]'::jsonb, 'Sphingomyelinase', 'Niemann-Pick type A/B is caused by acid sphingomyelinase deficiency, leading to sphingomyelin accumulation in macrophages (foamy cells) throughout the reticuloendothelial system.'),
('e044d245-f655-5cff-a4cf-8be737a20d2a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'The accumulating substrate in Niemann-Pick is:', '["GM2 ganglioside","Ceramide","Sphingomyelin","Glucocerebroside","Heparan sulfate"]'::jsonb, 'Sphingomyelin', 'Sphingomyelin accumulates in Niemann-Pick disease due to acid sphingomyelinase deficiency. It is a major sphingolipid in cell membranes, especially myelin sheaths.'),
('07b86747-029f-5bdf-8daf-2bd1eff9eb80'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Niemann-Pick type A presents with:', '["Hepatosplenomegaly","Only bone disease","Only splenomegaly","Cherry-red spot","Neurodegeneration"]'::jsonb, 'Hepatosplenomegaly', 'Niemann-Pick type A presents in infancy with hepatosplenomegaly, cherry-red spot, and progressive neurodegeneration. Most patients die by age 3.'),
('65b7e547-8562-578c-b525-01ca80d86ce1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Niemann-Pick type C is caused by defects in:', '["Sphingomyelinase","Cholesterol trafficking (NPC1 or NPC2)","Glucocerebrosidase","Beta-galactosidase","Hexosaminidase"]'::jsonb, 'Cholesterol trafficking (NPC1 or NPC2)', 'Niemann-Pick type C is caused by NPC1 or NPC2 mutations affecting intracellular cholesterol trafficking, leading to cholesterol and glycosphingolipid accumulation in lysosomes.'),
('d6c53841-7f09-53ab-a729-bbd83e9d1807'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Hurler syndrome (MPS I) is caused by deficiency of:', '["N-Acetylgalactosamine-4-sulfatase","Alpha-L-iduronidase","Heparan N-sulfatase","Beta-glucuronidase","Iduronate sulfatase"]'::jsonb, 'Alpha-L-iduronidase', 'Hurler syndrome (MPS I) is caused by alpha-L-iduronidase deficiency, accumulating dermatan sulfate and heparan sulfate, causing coarse facies, hepatosplenomegaly, skeletal disease, and intellectual disability.'),
('5db66607-f52a-5e51-85c1-e0fd624165d6'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Metabolic Disorders Pt1: Orally absorbed drugs first enter:', '["Portal vein","Systemic circulation","Lymphatics","IVC","Thoracic duct"]'::jsonb, 'Portal vein', 'Portal vein -> liver (first-pass) -> systemic circulation.'),
('1dbb1718-1c0d-58af-b053-c2916c771def'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'The accumulated GAGs in Hurler syndrome are:', '["Dermatan sulfate and heparan sulfate","Hyaluronic acid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All GAGs mediated by specific receptor activation","Keratan sulfate without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Chondroitin sulfate leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dermatan sulfate and heparan sulfate', 'Dermatan sulfate and heparan sulfate accumulate in Hurler syndrome because alpha-L-iduronidase normally degrades both GAGs at their terminal iduronate residues.'),
('6d806513-7679-5ae7-bae8-32d2ab307a5d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Hunter syndrome (MPS II) is caused by deficiency of:', '["Alpha-L-iduronidase","N-Acetylgalactosamine-6-sulfatase","Heparan N-sulfatase","Beta-glucuronidase","Iduronate sulfatase"]'::jsonb, 'Iduronate sulfatase', 'Hunter syndrome (MPS II) is caused by iduronate-2-sulfatase deficiency. It is X-linked recessive (unlike autosomal recessive Hurler) and lacks corneal clouding.'),
('e8f0f3b4-dd27-5110-b0f1-8967af331fc7'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Hunter syndrome differs from Hurler because:', '["Autosomal recessive resulting in decreased functional activity","More organomegaly accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","More severe mediated by specific receptor activation","Worse cognition without affecting the primary tissue pathway","X-linked recessive with no corneal clouding"]'::jsonb, 'X-linked recessive with no corneal clouding', 'Hunter syndrome differs from Hurler: X-linked recessive (males only), no corneal clouding, and generally milder course. Both cause skeletal and organ involvement.'),
('d275ef3a-c6eb-5d2e-a6f9-cb354c5fe35b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'ERT for Hurler syndrome uses:', '["Galsulfase","Alglucosidase","Imiglucerase","Idursulfase","Laronidase (Aldurazyme)"]'::jsonb, 'Laronidase (Aldurazyme)', 'Laronidase (Aldurazyme) is ERT for Hurler syndrome (MPS I), administered IV to replace alpha-L-iduronidase and reduce GAG accumulation.'),
('da2d7003-3cee-5bc6-a8e5-1f00d1d5b234'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Fabry disease is caused by deficiency of:', '["Hexosaminidase A","Sphingomyelinase","Beta-glucocerebrosidase","Alpha-galactosidase A","Beta-galactosidase"]'::jsonb, 'Alpha-galactosidase A', 'Fabry disease is caused by alpha-galactosidase A deficiency, accumulating globotriaosylceramide (Gb3) in endothelial cells, renal tubular cells, and cardiomyocytes.'),
('9a017af5-f4c4-5fa3-9f80-40d9602549fa'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Fabry disease is inherited as:', '["Mitochondrial","Autosomal recessive","Autosomal dominant","Sporadic","X-linked recessive"]'::jsonb, 'X-linked recessive', 'Fabry disease is X-linked recessive. Males are more severely affected; carrier females have variable symptoms due to random X-inactivation.'),
('017272c7-ee85-5d19-8a76-e88d80e4588a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Classic Fabry symptoms include:', '["Ochronosis","Renal/cardiac failure","Corneal opacities","Angiokeratomas","Acroparesthesias"]'::jsonb, 'Acroparesthesias', 'Classic Fabry symptoms: acroparesthesias (burning pain in extremities), angiokeratomas, cornea verticillata, and progressive renal failure and cardiac disease.'),
('8ae81f0a-3d27-572a-8dfc-9fd297ba9a09'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'ERT for Fabry disease uses:', '["Alglucosidase","Laronidase","Imiglucerase","Idursulfase","Agalsidase alfa or beta"]'::jsonb, 'Agalsidase alfa or beta', 'Agalsidase alfa (Replagal) and agalsidase beta (Fabrazyme) are ERT options for Fabry disease, replacing the deficient alpha-galactosidase A.'),
('f66d179f-7c5a-5470-9efe-9a5bec8b7d3b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Krabbe disease is caused by deficiency of:', '["Beta-hexosaminidase resulting in decreased functional activity","Galactocerebrosidase (beta-galactosylceramidase)","Beta-glucocerebrosidase mediated by specific receptor activation","Alpha-galactosidase without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Arylsulfatase A leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Galactocerebrosidase (beta-galactosylceramidase)', 'Krabbe disease is caused by galactocerebrosidase deficiency, accumulating galactocerebroside and psychosine. Psychosine is highly toxic to oligodendrocytes, causing demyelination.'),
('d424ccea-b5ea-527a-92fb-766fd98ff8e4'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Hallmark of Krabbe disease is:', '["Gaucher cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Foamy cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Angiokeratomas mediated by specific receptor activation","Globoid cells (multinucleated macrophages)","Cherry-red spot leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Globoid cells (multinucleated macrophages)', 'Krabbe disease is characterized by globoid cells - multinucleated macrophages that form in response to galactocerebroside accumulation in CNS white matter.'),
('78695f0b-a014-5f6b-8261-5e315e88cb31'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Krabbe disease presents in infancy with:', '["Rapid neurological decline","Renal failure","Stiffness","Seizures","Hyperirritability"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperirritability', 'Infantile Krabbe disease presents with hyperirritability, feeding difficulties, seizures, and rapid neurological decline. Death usually occurs within 2 years.'),
('87434902-961b-5726-bdcf-0e5ad1ee56cf'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'The accumulated substrate in MLD is:', '["Heparan sulfate","Galactocerebroside","Sulfatide (cerebroside-3-sulfate)","Glucocerebroside","GM2 ganglioside"]'::jsonb, 'Sulfatide (cerebroside-3-sulfate)', 'MLD is caused by arylsulfatase A deficiency, accumulating sulfatide (cerebroside-3-sulfate) in oligodendrocytes and Schwann cells, causing central and peripheral demyelination.'),
('c5a3b2ef-a791-5e82-a5a6-999ea39daf84'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'MLD is named for:', '["Metachromatic staining of the lipid with cresyl violet","Pigment deposition accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Change in organ color mediated by specific receptor activation","Ability to change color without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Urine odor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Metachromatic staining of the lipid with cresyl violet', 'MLD is named for metachromatic staining of accumulated sulfatide with cresyl violet dye - the lipid changes the stain color from its normal hue.'),
('4f89c8de-f797-5bc5-9707-e42c05491283'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Most MPS disorders are inherited as:', '["Sporadic resulting in decreased functional activity","Autosomal dominant accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","X-linked dominant mediated by specific receptor activation","Autosomal recessive (except Hunter X-linked)","Mitochondrial leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Autosomal recessive (except Hunter X-linked)', 'Most MPS disorders are autosomal recessive, except Hunter syndrome (MPS II) which is X-linked recessive.'),
('5ffd64fc-0c8e-5474-9a50-1c3ba2858fac'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Morquio syndrome (MPS IV) is due to deficiency of:', '["Galactosamine-6-sulfatase or beta-galactosidase","Alpha-L-iduronidase accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Beta-glucuronidase mediated by specific receptor activation","Heparan N-sulfatase without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Iduronate sulfatase leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Galactosamine-6-sulfatase or beta-galactosidase', 'Morquio syndrome (MPS IV) is caused by galactosamine-6-sulfatase (type A) or beta-galactosidase (type B) deficiency, accumulating keratan sulfate and chondroitin-6-sulfate.'),
('9817f7b5-a0f2-5e1f-b76b-3c7f73860775'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Morquio syndrome is characterized by:', '["Severe skeletal dysplasia (short trunk dwarfism) with normal intelligence","Osteoporosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Corneal clouding mediated by specific receptor activation","Anemia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hepatosplenomegaly leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Severe skeletal dysplasia (short trunk dwarfism) with normal intelligence', 'Morquio syndrome uniquely causes severe skeletal dysplasia (short trunk dwarfism, pectus carinatum, genu valgum) with normal intelligence, unlike other MPS disorders.'),
('9bc33486-9606-5659-991b-2a8e1f1d2219'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Accumulated GAGs in Morquio syndrome:', '["Keratan sulfate and chondroitin-6-sulfate","Heparan sulfate accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All GAGs mediated by specific receptor activation","Dermatan sulfate without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hyaluronic acid leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Keratan sulfate and chondroitin-6-sulfate', 'Keratan sulfate and chondroitin-6-sulfate accumulate in Morquio syndrome. Keratan sulfate particularly affects cartilage and bone, causing the skeletal disease.'),
('3b815c25-0e6c-5aa7-9dbb-2bb829033cb4'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Sanfilippo syndrome (MPS III) is characterized by:', '["Severe skeletal abnormalities resulting in decreased functional activity","Corneal clouding accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cardiac involvement mediated by specific receptor activation","Hepatosplenomegaly without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Severe neurological degeneration with mild somatic disease"]'::jsonb, 'Severe neurological degeneration with mild somatic disease', 'Sanfilippo syndrome (MPS III) causes severe neurological degeneration with relatively mild somatic disease, due to heparan sulfate accumulation in the brain.'),
('daeb28ed-3101-5c8e-ae88-412ed8df0e20'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Sanfilippo syndrome involves defective degradation of:', '["Heparan sulfate","Chondroitin sulfate","Hyaluronic acid","Keratan sulfate","Dermatan sulfate"]'::jsonb, 'Heparan sulfate', 'Sanfilippo syndrome involves defective heparan sulfate degradation. Four subtypes (A-D) result from deficiency of different enzymes in the degradation pathway.'),
('892c6684-2a53-5ad0-8448-d9e8d7401e99'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Sly syndrome (MPS VII) is due to deficiency of:', '["Arylsulfatase B","Alpha-L-iduronidase","Beta-glucuronidase","Iduronate sulfatase","Beta-galactosidase"]'::jsonb, 'Beta-glucuronidase', 'Sly syndrome (MPS VII) is caused by beta-glucuronidase deficiency, accumulating dermatan sulfate, heparan sulfate, and chondroitin sulfate. It is the rarest MPS.'),
('b0bd80b1-954a-5ac8-baaa-fb62e9dbf0ed'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Maroteaux-Lamy (MPS VI) is due to deficiency of:', '["Beta-glucuronidase resulting in decreased functional activity","Iduronate sulfatase accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Arylsulfatase A mediated by specific receptor activation","Alpha-L-iduronidase without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Arylsulfatase B (N-acetylgalactosamine-4-sulfatase)"]'::jsonb, 'Arylsulfatase B (N-acetylgalactosamine-4-sulfatase)', 'Maroteaux-Lamy (MPS VI) is caused by arylsulfatase B deficiency, accumulating dermatan sulfate. It resembles Hurler but with normal intelligence.'),
('8e2a5327-c171-530e-893f-f14578c19b1a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Leukodystrophies primarily affect:', '["Liver","Myelin in CNS and PNS","Spleen","Bone","Gray matter"]'::jsonb, 'Myelin in CNS and PNS', 'Leukodystrophies primarily affect myelin in the CNS and PNS, causing progressive demyelination and neurological deterioration.'),
('afc93c97-0264-5bbb-9751-84ad29d1bca7'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Adrenoleukodystrophy (ALD) is caused by:', '["Galactocerebrosidase deficiency","Hexosaminidase deficiency","Arylsulfatase A deficiency","Impaired mitochondrial oxidation","Impaired peroxisomal beta-oxidation of VLCFA"]'::jsonb, 'Impaired peroxisomal beta-oxidation of VLCFA', 'X-ALD is caused by ABCD1 mutations, impairing peroxisomal beta-oxidation of VLCFA. VLCFA accumulates in brain, adrenal cortex, and testes.'),
('d0dd7d6c-c72e-5318-a277-f2c4b99c7c6a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'X-ALD presents with:', '["Anemia resulting in decreased functional activity","Progressive neurological deterioration with adrenal insufficiency","Cardiac problems mediated by specific receptor activation","Renal failure without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hepatosplenomegaly leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Progressive neurological deterioration with adrenal insufficiency', 'X-ALD presents with progressive neurological deterioration combined with adrenal insufficiency. The childhood cerebral form is the most severe variant.'),
('2fee5598-95e8-56a7-9232-df8205e9991f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'I-cell disease (mucolipidosis II) results from:', '["Impaired GAG synthesis","Deficiency of N-acetylglucosamine-1-phosphotransferase","Collagen defect","Deficiency of lysosomal hydrolases","Deficiency of a single hydrolase"]'::jsonb, 'Deficiency of N-acetylglucosamine-1-phosphotransferase', 'I-cell disease results from N-acetylglucosamine-1-phosphotransferase deficiency. Without mannose-6-phosphate tagging, lysosomal enzymes are secreted instead of targeted to lysosomes.'),
('3840ac3a-00b8-57a0-9b97-3c88fe88d8df'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'I-cell disease is characterized by:', '["Mild features","Cardiac involvement","Ocular problems only","Symptoms","Severe Hurler-like features"]'::jsonb, 'Severe Hurler-like features', 'I-cell disease causes severe Hurler-like features (coarse facies, skeletal abnormalities, hepatosplenomegaly, developmental delay), typically more severe than Hurler.'),
('fe3ded86-41fa-59b2-9508-2df947488eff'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'The I-cell in I-cell disease refers to:', '["Immune cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Intracellular deposits accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Interstitial cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Insulin cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inclusion bodies in cultured fibroblasts"]'::jsonb, 'Inclusion bodies in cultured fibroblasts', 'The ''I-cell'' refers to large inclusion bodies in cultured fibroblasts - lysosomes engorged with undigested substrates due to mistargeting of lysosomal enzymes.'),
('fa503947-7967-5133-bdae-c0f21dc17fb6'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Multiple sulfatase deficiency involves:', '["All transferases deficient resulting in decreased functional activity","All oxidases deficient accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All sulfatases deficient due to failure of formylglycine generation","Only one sulfatase deficient without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All hydrolases deficient leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'All sulfatases deficient due to failure of formylglycine generation', 'Multiple sulfatase deficiency results from defective formylglycine generation at the active site of all sulfatases, due to SUMF1 gene mutations.'),
('481e4513-4766-5631-96a9-b701be04a1fe'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Cystinosis is characterized by accumulation of:', '["Cystamine resulting in decreased functional activity","Cystathionine accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cysteine mediated by specific receptor activation","Cysteine sulfinate without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cystine (defective lysosomal cystine transport)"]'::jsonb, 'Cystine (defective lysosomal cystine transport)', 'Cystinosis involves cystine accumulation in lysosomes due to defective cystinosin transporter. Cystine crystals damage renal tubular cells and other tissues.'),
('2c7a4a8f-23bf-5043-ba71-fae7f8ad352b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Cystinosis presents with:', '["Ochronosis","Photophobia","Growth retardation","Hepatosplenomegaly","Renal Fanconi syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'Renal Fanconi syndrome', 'Cystinosis presents with renal Fanconi syndrome in infancy (aminoaciduria, glycosuria, phosphaturia), growth retardation, and rickets, progressing to renal failure.'),
('5c300b60-2c02-51d6-97ac-e85d98e833e9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Treatment for cystinosis includes:', '["Bone marrow transplant","Enzyme replacement","Gene therapy","Cysteamine (depletes cystine)","Substrate reduction"]'::jsonb, 'Cysteamine (depletes cystine)', 'Cysteamine enters lysosomes and forms a mixed disulfide with cystine (cysteine-cysteamine) that exits through a cystinosin-independent pathway, depleting cystine.'),
('6987d606-f604-5c80-9f0c-6d0037798a3b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'GSD type VI (Hers disease) is caused by deficiency of:', '["Liver glycogen phosphorylase","G6Pase","Debranching enzyme","Phosphorylase kinase","Muscle glycogen phosphorylase"]'::jsonb, 'Liver glycogen phosphorylase', 'GSD type VI (Hers disease) is caused by liver glycogen phosphorylase deficiency, causing hepatomegaly and mild hypoglycemia with generally good prognosis.'),
('fc499ea2-6a9d-5465-b803-69f8f3226536'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'GSD type 0 is caused by deficiency of:', '["Glycogen synthase","Branching enzyme","G6Pase","Glycogen phosphorylase","Debranching enzyme"]'::jsonb, 'Glycogen synthase', 'GSD type 0 results from glycogen synthase deficiency. Without glycogen synthesis, patients have fasting hypoglycemia and ketosis but no hepatomegaly.'),
('70ce50a6-bb55-599c-a4e1-7de298bdc407'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Andersen disease (GSD IV) presents with:', '["Cardiomegaly resulting in decreased functional activity","Hypoglycemia only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Muscle cramps mediated by specific receptor activation","Renal failure without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Progressive liver cirrhosis and failure in infancy"]'::jsonb, 'Progressive liver cirrhosis and failure in infancy', 'Andersen disease (GSD IV) is caused by branching enzyme deficiency, producing abnormal amylopectin-like glycogen. It causes progressive liver cirrhosis and failure in infancy.'),
('95a0a465-5f93-5746-b6df-4f0384521618'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'GSD type VII (Tarui disease) is due to deficiency of:', '["Phosphorylase kinase","Glycogen phosphorylase","Phosphofructokinase (muscle PFK-1)","Debranching enzyme","G6Pase"]'::jsonb, 'Phosphofructokinase (muscle PFK-1)', 'GSD type VII (Tarui disease) results from muscle PFK-1 deficiency. PFK-1 catalyzes the committed step of glycolysis (F6P to F1,6BP) and is the major regulatory enzyme.'),
('94e7b9d1-bbd0-5209-ae89-c90e01ef7239'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'GSD type XI (Fanconi-Bickel) is due to deficiency of:', '["Debranching enzyme","G6Pase","GLUT2 transporter","Phosphorylase kinase","Glycogen synthase"]'::jsonb, 'GLUT2 transporter', 'GSD type XI (Fanconi-Bickel) is caused by GLUT2 transporter deficiency, impairing glucose transport in liver, pancreas, intestine, and kidney.'),
('843e4c8d-e4cd-5554-b7f0-b619854c9912'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Cholesterol ester storage disease is a milder form of:', '["Tay-Sachs","Fabry disease","Gaucher disease","LAL deficiency (Wolman disease)","Niemann-Pick disease"]'::jsonb, 'LAL deficiency (Wolman disease)', 'Cholesterol ester storage disease is a milder form of LAL deficiency. Wolman disease is the severe infantile form with adrenal calcification and early death.'),
('2808e787-2556-5b9e-b03f-7b34daead9c1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Batten disease (neuronal ceroid lipofuscinosis) involves:', '["Ganglioside accumulation resulting in decreased functional activity","Collagen deposition accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mucopolysaccharide accumulation mediated by specific receptor activation","Accumulation of autofluorescent lipopigment in neurons","Glycogen storage leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Accumulation of autofluorescent lipopigment in neurons', 'Batten disease involves accumulation of autofluorescent lipopigment (ceroid/lipofuscin) in neurons, causing progressive neurodegeneration, seizures, and vision loss.'),
('a35654c3-21ae-5b14-9d8d-d62975281ba1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Alpha-1-antitrypsin deficiency leads to:', '["Early-onset emphysema and liver cirrhosis","Diabetes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anemia mediated by specific receptor activation","Hypertension without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pulmonary edema leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Early-onset emphysema and liver cirrhosis', 'AAT deficiency causes early-onset panacinar emphysema (lower lobes) from unopposed neutrophil elastase, and liver cirrhosis from AAT polymer accumulation in hepatocytes.'),
('07f9630a-926a-56c3-b28d-373463bb1ad3'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Emphysema in AAT deficiency is accelerated by:', '["Alcohol","Exercise","Smoking","High-fat diet","Obesity"]'::jsonb, 'Smoking', 'Smoking accelerates emphysema in AAT deficiency by generating oxidative stress that further inactivates the already deficient alpha-1-antitrypsin.'),
('5052e4d7-2f0f-54d2-bc58-f5843398f6e4'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Tandem mass spectrometry in newborn screening detects:', '["Only galactosemia resulting in decreased functional activity","Amino acids and acylcarnitines for >30 disorders","Only PKU mediated by specific receptor activation","Only fatty acid oxidation disorders without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only organic acidemias leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Amino acids and acylcarnitines for >30 disorders', 'Tandem mass spectrometry (MS/MS) detects >30 disorders from a single dried blood spot by measuring amino acids and acylcarnitines simultaneously.'),
('628d20ed-c041-5017-be4e-c764285aad60'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'The DNPH test detects:', '["Ketoacids (positive in PKU and MSUD)","Glucose accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fructose mediated by specific receptor activation","Reducing sugars without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Galactose leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ketoacids (positive in PKU and MSUD)', 'The DNPH test detects alpha-ketoacids (pyruvate, alpha-ketoglutarate, branched-chain alpha-ketoacids) which are elevated in PKU and MSUD.'),
('02b9e04c-eccf-5533-b99f-f61e2da9d0de'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'The nitroprusside test detects:', '["Glucose resulting in decreased functional activity","Galactose accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Homocystine/cystine in homocystinuria and cystinuria","Protein without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fructose leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Homocystine/cystine in homocystinuria and cystinuria', 'The nitroprusside test detects disulfides (homocystine, cystine) in urine, positive in homocystinuria and cystinuria.'),
('e859e279-e663-53de-8064-8ad39934881d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Beutler test screens for:', '["Galactosemia (GALT deficiency)","MSUD accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Tyrosinemia mediated by specific receptor activation","PKU without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Homocystinuria leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Galactosemia (GALT deficiency)', 'Beutler test screens for galactosemia (GALT deficiency) using fluorescence. Normal red cells produce a fluorescent product from galactose-1-phosphate; GALT-deficient cells do not.'),
('c2a73d12-9015-51f9-9855-d013cadade7a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Bone marrow transplantation is effective for:', '["Hurler","MLD","All: Gaucher","Hurler only","Gaucher only"]'::jsonb, 'All: Gaucher', 'Bone marrow transplantation is effective for Gaucher, Hurler, and MLD by providing enzyme-producing cells that cross-correct the deficiency in recipient tissues.'),
('20731f47-b8ae-54b5-87f9-0a470837e5f6'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'HSCT is most effective for Hurler syndrome when performed:', '["Only in adults","At any age","Never","After age 5","Before age 2 years"]'::jsonb, 'Before age 2 years', 'HSCT for Hurler is most effective before age 2 years, before irreversible neurological damage. Early transplant halts cognitive decline and improves survival.'),
('1108687d-b3d5-5c00-a960-1c758ee93265'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'ERT cannot treat CNS manifestations because:', '["Brain makes its own enzymes","Enzymes do not cross the blood-brain barrier","Enzymes are degraded","Enzymes are unstable","Immune system blocks enzymes"]'::jsonb, 'Enzymes do not cross the blood-brain barrier', 'ERT cannot treat CNS manifestations because recombinant enzyme proteins cannot cross the blood-brain barrier. Intrathecal and gene therapy approaches are being developed.'),
('c548ad0b-33fc-5d36-b10d-a7407d96e1cc'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Intrathecal ERT is being developed for:', '["Fabry disease","Pompe disease","Gaucher disease","Tay-Sachs","MLD and Hurler syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'MLD and Hurler syndrome', 'Intrathecal ERT is being developed for MLD and Hurler syndrome to bypass the blood-brain barrier and deliver enzyme directly to the CNS.'),
('dee44474-1823-54d3-a41e-d8a75bce4670'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Small molecule chaperone therapy:', '["Inhibits the enzyme resulting in decreased functional activity","Replaces the enzyme accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Blocks substrate synthesis mediated by specific receptor activation","Stimulates transcription without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Uses pharmacological chaperones to stabilize mutant enzymes"]'::jsonb, 'Uses pharmacological chaperones to stabilize mutant enzymes', 'Small molecule chaperone therapy uses pharmacological chaperones that bind and stabilize misfolded mutant enzymes, helping them fold correctly and reach the lysosome.'),
('e2f8bbc2-8e3f-5f79-8b54-c2b918d36539'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Migalastat is a chaperone therapy for:', '["Pompe disease resulting in decreased functional activity","Tay-Sachs accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fabry disease (certain GLA mutations)","Gaucher disease without affecting the primary tissue pathway","MLD leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fabry disease (certain GLA mutations)', 'Migalastat (Galafold) is an oral chaperone for Fabry disease that stabilizes specific GLA mutations, allowing proper alpha-galactosidase A folding and lysosomal trafficking.'),
('6db6e541-20c3-53a1-9749-508f0f327e21'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Clear cornea in Hunter syndrome vs cloudy in Hurler is because:', '["Both have cloudy cornea","Hunter does not accumulate GAG in cornea","Hunter is X-linked","Hunter is milder","Hunter has GAG accumulation"]'::jsonb, 'Hunter does not accumulate GAG in cornea', 'Hunter syndrome spares the cornea because iduronate-2-sulfatase deficiency does not cause GAG accumulation in corneal tissue, unlike Hurler where dermatan sulfate deposits in the cornea.'),
('6424b965-f3fa-5b6e-a40c-1e882f1d52df'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'ERT complication:', '["Hypersensitivity resulting in decreased functional activity","Organ damage accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Poor tolerability mediated by specific receptor activation","Infusion-related reactions and antibody formation","Lack of efficacy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Infusion-related reactions and antibody formation', 'ERT complications include infusion-related reactions (fever, rigors, rash) and antibody formation against the recombinant enzyme. Anaphylaxis is rare but possible.'),
('62b06700-3b9d-5ad8-a1be-4e8eac3799f5'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Substrate reduction therapy (SRT) aims to:', '["Stabilize mutant enzyme","Block catabolism","Provide missing metabolite","Reduce synthesis of accumulating substrate","Replace the deficient enzyme"]'::jsonb, 'Reduce synthesis of accumulating substrate', 'SRT aims to reduce synthesis of the accumulating substrate rather than replacing the deficient enzyme, using small molecules that inhibit substrate-producing enzymes.'),
('80f853c2-d795-5b17-83f0-fd50775bd6f2'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Which lysosomal storage disease causes Gaucher cells?', '["Tay-Sachs resulting in decreased functional activity","Pompe disease accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Niemann-Pick mediated by specific receptor activation","Fabry disease without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Gaucher disease type 1 (mild to moderate)"]'::jsonb, 'Gaucher disease type 1 (mild to moderate)', 'Gaucher cells (wrinkled paper appearance) are characteristic of Gaucher disease type 1, the most common and typically mildest lysosomal storage disease.'),
('9dd1f76c-6876-5d83-a687-f50f6e02add2'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Eliglustat is a more selective SRT for Gaucher that:', '["Inhibits galactosidase","Inhibits sulfatase","Inhibits hexosaminidase","Inhibits glucosylceramide synthase","Inhibits sphingomyelinase"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibits glucosylceramide synthase', 'Eliglustat selectively inhibits glucosylceramide synthase, reducing glucocerebroside synthesis. It requires CYP2D6 genotyping due to variable drug metabolism.'),
('1c2b8b29-39ea-541d-8dd3-24162a2ef15e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'The hydrogen breath test for lactose intolerance:', '["Measures CO2 resulting in decreased functional activity","Measures nitrogen accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Measures hydrogen from bacterial fermentation of undigested lactose","Measures oxygen without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Measures methane leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Measures hydrogen from bacterial fermentation of undigested lactose', 'The hydrogen breath test measures H2 produced by bacterial fermentation of undigested lactose in the colon. Elevated breath H2 confirms lactose malabsorption.'),
('60c6d00e-ec5f-543d-9396-68e27f954664'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'The most common lysosomal storage disease is:', '["Fabry","Tay-Sachs","Niemann-Pick","Hurler","Gaucher disease"]'::jsonb, 'Gaucher disease', 'Gaucher disease is the most common lysosomal storage disease (1 in 40,000-60,000), particularly prevalent in Ashkenazi Jewish populations.'),
('1724e2d0-53fa-5960-842f-19a5d44c0c01'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'L444P mutation in Gaucher is associated with:', '["Disease resulting in decreased functional activity","Neuronopathic forms (types 2 and 3)","Type 1 mediated by specific receptor activation","Asymptomatic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mild disease leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Neuronopathic forms (types 2 and 3)', 'The L444P mutation in GBA is associated with neuronopathic Gaucher disease (types 2 and 3). It affects enzyme folding and is a severe mutation.'),
('25be6c55-97f0-54f3-8687-709ea745d771'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Pseudo-Hurler polydystrophy (ML III) is:', '["A milder form of I-cell disease","A form of MPS","A different disorder","A peroxisomal disorder","A collagen disorder"]'::jsonb, 'A milder form of I-cell disease', 'Pseudo-Hurler polydystrophy (ML III) is a milder form of I-cell disease with partial N-acetylglucosamine-1-phosphotransferase deficiency.'),
('59ee4dbb-1e01-5d0e-b7ef-2b9166d142f1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Cystinosis is caused by mutations in CTNS encoding:', '["Glutathione synthase","Cystinosin (lysosomal cystine transporter)","Cysteine dioxygenase","Cystine reductase","Cystathionine synthase"]'::jsonb, 'Cystinosin (lysosomal cystine transporter)', 'Cystinosis is caused by CTNS mutations encoding cystinosin, a lysosomal cystine transporter. Over 100 mutations identified; 57-kb deletion is common in Western populations.'),
('b07b0ca2-6719-54fc-8174-c0d5f9366410'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'The Fanconi syndrome in cystinosis is due to:', '["Urinary obstruction resulting in decreased functional activity","Glomerular damage accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cystine crystal deposition in renal tubular cells","High cystine in blood without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Direct tubular toxicity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cystine crystal deposition in renal tubular cells', 'Fanconi syndrome in cystinosis results from cystine crystal deposition in renal tubular cells, causing progressive proximal tubular dysfunction.'),
('22d20f46-62d1-5f30-976f-2f028499389c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Cysteamine therapy for cystinosis works by:', '["Inhibiting cystine synthesis resulting in decreased functional activity","Depleting cystine via formation of cysteine-cysteamine mixed disulfide","Increasing cystine excretion mediated by specific receptor activation","Blocking cystine transport without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Replacing defective enzyme leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Depleting cystine via formation of cysteine-cysteamine mixed disulfide', 'Cysteamine forms a mixed disulfide with cystine in lysosomes that exits via a cystinosin-independent pathway, effectively depleting accumulated cystine.'),
('82ff40c5-2973-5cfb-b75d-256a91d0c3a0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Nephropathic cystinosis presents with:', '["Symptoms resulting in decreased functional activity","Ocular problems only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Benign mediated by specific receptor activation","Renal Fanconi syndrome in infancy","Adult onset leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Renal Fanconi syndrome in infancy', 'Nephropathic cystinosis presents with renal Fanconi syndrome in the first year: polyuria, polydipsia, growth failure, rickets, and progressive renal failure if untreated.'),
('9974a415-0351-52a8-b174-5cc5b1bdc356'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Sebelipase alfa is ERT for:', '["Pompe disease","LAL deficiency (Wolman/CESD)","MPS I","Gaucher disease","Fabry disease"]'::jsonb, 'LAL deficiency (Wolman/CESD)', 'Sebelipase alfa (Kanuma) is ERT for LAL deficiency, treating both Wolman disease (severe) and cholesteryl ester storage disease (mild).'),
('a20ace2d-36fd-5090-b508-01cf718a78ed'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'NPC is characterized by accumulation of:', '["Unesterified cholesterol and glycosphingolipids in lysosomes","GM2 ganglioside accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Glucocerebroside mediated by specific receptor activation","Sphingomyelin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sulfatide leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Unesterified cholesterol and glycosphingolipids in lysosomes', 'NPC accumulates unesterified cholesterol and glycosphingolipids in lysosomes due to defective cholesterol trafficking via NPC1 (95%) or NPC2 (5%) proteins.'),
('516d8b38-1e11-57e5-a157-e2c19398535c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'NPC is diagnosed by:', '["DNA sequencing resulting in decreased functional activity","Glucocerebrosidase assay accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bone marrow biopsy mediated by specific receptor activation","Sphingomyelinase assay without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Filipin staining for unesterified cholesterol in fibroblasts"]'::jsonb, 'Filipin staining for unesterified cholesterol in fibroblasts', 'NPC diagnosis uses Filipin staining of fibroblasts, showing characteristic perinuclear unesterified cholesterol accumulation due to defective lysosomal cholesterol export.'),
('1a93d44a-67f6-5a7f-bd61-73c218bc8184'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Miglustat is used for:', '["NPC (to reduce neurological progression)","Fabry disease accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Gaucher type 1 mediated by specific receptor activation","Tay-Sachs without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pompe disease leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'NPC (to reduce neurological progression)', 'Miglustat is approved for NPC to slow neurological progression by reducing glycosphingolipid synthesis via substrate reduction.'),
('fa52bdd8-5398-539d-820f-596461c0a0c9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'The specific GAGs excreted help differentiate:', '["Different MPS types","Prognosis","Severity","Treatment response","MPS from other disorders"]'::jsonb, 'Different MPS types', 'The specific GAG pattern in urine helps differentiate MPS types, as each MPS has a characteristic profile based on the deficient enzyme.'),
('2c7e3242-deec-5420-a7c1-4fc5afef2ff3'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Elosulfase alfa (Vimizim) is ERT for:', '["MPS IVA (Morquio type A)","MPS VI accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","MPS II mediated by specific receptor activation","MPS I without affecting the primary tissue pathway","MPS VII leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'MPS IVA (Morquio type A)', 'Elosulfase alfa (Vimizim) is ERT for MPS IVA (Morquio A), replacing galactosamine-6-sulfatase to degrade keratan sulfate.'),
('fe850063-ab87-5871-82bb-ca1c7f8245b9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Vestronidase alfa (Mepsevii) is ERT for:', '["MPS VI resulting in decreased functional activity","MPS IV accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","MPS I mediated by specific receptor activation","MPS VII (Sly syndrome)","MPS II leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'MPS VII (Sly syndrome)', 'Vestronidase alfa (Mepsevii) is ERT for MPS VII (Sly), replacing beta-glucuronidase to degrade accumulated GAGs.'),
('e47922eb-6d19-578e-813e-139668562de0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Sanfilippo type A is due to deficiency of:', '["Alpha-N-acetylglucosaminidase","N-acetylglucosamine-6-sulfatase","Heparan N-sulfatase (sulfamidase)","Acetyl-CoA transferase","Beta-glucuronidase"]'::jsonb, 'Heparan N-sulfatase (sulfamidase)', 'Sanfilippo type A is caused by heparan N-sulfatase (sulfamidase) deficiency, the first enzyme in heparan sulfate degradation. It is the most common Sanfilippo subtype.'),
('7d3ffe65-dee8-5f3b-8613-d0e163d00c75'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'The most severe form of MPS I is called:', '["Hunter","Severity difference","Hurler-Scheie","Scheie syndrome (MPS IS)","Hurler syndrome (MPS IH)"]'::jsonb, 'Hurler syndrome (MPS IH)', 'Hurler syndrome (MPS IH) is the most severe MPS I form, with early childhood onset, rapid neurological decline, severe skeletal disease, and early death without treatment.'),
('5d9f8c46-e20c-5797-8302-74cd2245b607'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Scheie syndrome (MPS IS) presents with:', '["Normal intelligence","Mild somatic features","Corneal clouding","Severe intellectual disability","Symptoms"]'::jsonb, 'Mild somatic features', 'Scheie syndrome (MPS IS) is the mildest MPS I, with mild somatic features (joint stiffness, corneal clouding, cardiac valvular disease) and normal intelligence.'),
('7f758f93-3bc8-5332-ba42-43bd63b6ecb2'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'IDUA gene is located on:', '["5q13","12q24","4p16.3","Xq28","1q21"]'::jsonb, '4p16.3', 'The IDUA gene (alpha-L-iduronidase) is at 4p16.3. Mutations cause Hurler, Hurler-Scheie, and Scheie syndromes.'),
('e59b0c8c-dd6d-5931-b076-d2c7dcb16348'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'The IDS gene (iduronate sulfatase) is located on:', '["Xq28","4p16.3","5q13","17q25","1q21"]'::jsonb, 'Xq28', 'The IDS gene (iduronate-2-sulfatase) is at Xq28. Mutations cause Hunter syndrome (MPS II), the only X-linked MPS.'),
('236542ec-6df3-5bd2-bcd2-ff2528fa0c7e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'ERT is not effective for CNS in MPS because:', '["ERT is degraded","ERT is excreted","ERT does not cross the BBB","ERT causes seizures","ERT is toxic"]'::jsonb, 'ERT does not cross the BBB', 'ERT cannot cross the blood-brain barrier, so CNS manifestations of MPS are not addressed by standard IV enzyme replacement.'),
('cbd93aaf-31a2-5421-92da-7d159be51b4d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Glycogen in Cori disease (GSD III) has:', '["Excessively branched resulting in decreased functional activity","Branches accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Normal structure mediated by specific receptor activation","Long outer chains without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Very short outer chains (limit dextrin)"]'::jsonb, 'Very short outer chains (limit dextrin)', 'In Cori disease (GSD III), glycogen has very short outer chains (limit dextrin) because debranching enzyme cannot remove branch points.'),
('c0c594fa-4288-5b8e-8912-de4c4d0071af'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Limit dextrin refers to the structure accumulating in:', '["GSD type III (Cori disease)","GSD type V","GSD type II","GSD type IV","GSD type I"]'::jsonb, 'GSD type III (Cori disease)', 'Limit dextrin accumulates in GSD III (Cori disease) due to debranching enzyme deficiency, appearing as glycogen with abnormally short outer chains.'),
('2db4909b-a137-5a16-808d-29404a445f7c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Sorbitol accumulation contributes to complications in:', '["Fructose intolerance","PKU","Diabetes (via aldose reductase)","Lactose intolerance","Galactosemia"]'::jsonb, 'Diabetes (via aldose reductase)', 'Sorbitol accumulation in diabetes occurs via the polyol pathway: excess glucose is converted to sorbitol by aldose reductase, causing osmotic damage to lens, nerves, and vessels.'),
('878b2933-cc46-571f-8675-26dec1f41717'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Which enzyme bypasses the PFK-1 reaction in gluconeogenesis?', '["Mannose","Fructose","Galactose","Xylulose","Glucose"]'::jsonb, 'Fructose', 'Fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase bypasses PFK-1 in gluconeogenesis, converting fructose-1,6-bisphosphate to fructose-6-phosphate, reversing the irreversible PFK-1 step.'),
('dbd696c9-63b1-5bb9-8769-da5d4bc64e0e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'A newborn presents with vomiting, lethargy, and developmental delay. Newborn screening shows elevated amino acids. The most common amino acid disorder in newborn screening:', '["Tyrosinemia","PKU","Homocystinuria","MSUD","Alkaptonuria"]'::jsonb, 'PKU', 'PKU is the most common amino acid disorder on newborn screening (1 in 10,000-15,000 live births).'),
('cb7c70d8-2ccb-550b-8c8a-15241a1bed70'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'BH4 deficiency should be suspected in PKU patients who:', '["Are mild cases resulting in decreased functional activity","Are severe cases accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Do not respond to dietary phenylalanine restriction","Developmental issues without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Have ochronosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Do not respond to dietary phenylalanine restriction', 'BH4 deficiency causes atypical PKU unresponsive to dietary phenylalanine restriction, because BH4 is the essential cofactor for phenylalanine hydroxylase.'),
('d224c581-19a9-52e9-b930-6f2d36b14637'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'BH4 is synthesized from GTP via:', '["PTPS","GTP cyclohydrolase I","TH","And sepiapterin reductase","PAH"]'::jsonb, 'GTP cyclohydrolase I', 'BH4 synthesis from GTP: GTP cyclohydrolase I (first, rate-limiting) then PTPS then sepiapterin reductase.'),
('67b9b9b6-ada8-5a5d-827d-5372468ac4cf'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'BH4 is cofactor for:', '["TH","PAH","Only PAH","TPH","And NOS"]'::jsonb, 'PAH', 'BH4 is the cofactor for PAH (phenylalanine hydroxylase), TH (tyrosine hydroxylase), TPH (tryptophan hydroxylase), and NOS (nitric oxide synthase). PAH is most relevant in PKU.'),
('fb35fc3f-5b6a-55bf-885f-21e798134f21'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Treatment of BH4 deficiency includes:', '["All: BH4","Neurotransmitter precursors","Low-phenylalanine diet","BH4 only","Neurotransmitter precursors only"]'::jsonb, 'All: BH4', 'BH4 deficiency treatment: BH4 (sapropterin), neurotransmitter precursors (L-DOPA, 5-HTP), and low-phenylalanine diet.'),
('0043ce8d-d5ca-5bbf-8637-a4200c74f190'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Gaucher disease carrier frequency in Ashkenazi Jews:', '["1:15 to 1:18","1:50","1:30","1:100","1:5"]'::jsonb, '1:15 to 1:18', 'Gaucher carrier frequency in Ashkenazi Jews is 1 in 15-18, making it one of the most common genetic disorders in this population.'),
('3ebefc6c-94ed-530d-8070-cfedeeb3b689'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'GBA gene is located on chromosome:', '["5q13","Xq22","17q25","1q21","12q24"]'::jsonb, '1q21', 'The GBA gene (glucocerebrosidase) is at 1q21. Mutations cause Gaucher disease.'),
('b5d3d1db-ad86-56e8-b315-064c487106a5'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Mucolipidosis II and III are caused by mutations in:', '["GNPTG resulting in decreased functional activity","GNPTAB (GlcNAc-1-phosphotransferase alpha/beta)","MCOLN1 mediated by specific receptor activation","SUMF1 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CTNS leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'GNPTAB (GlcNAc-1-phosphotransferase alpha/beta)', 'Mucolipidosis II/III are caused by GNPTAB mutations (GlcNAc-1-phosphotransferase). ML II is severe (I-cell disease), ML III is milder (pseudo-Hurler polydystrophy).'),
('35313a37-6fcd-5137-8ae5-4ee9ada3e158'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'What is the function of Sort lysosomal enzymes?', '["Exocytosis","Recycle receptors","Endocytosis","Sort plasma membrane proteins","Sort lysosomal enzymes in Golgi"]'::jsonb, 'Sort lysosomal enzymes in Golgi', 'The mannose-6-phosphate tag is added in the Golgi by GlcNAc-1-phosphotransferase, which sorts lysosomal enzymes via mannose-6-phosphate receptors.'),
('ff4b4eee-9377-58e0-a706-902d64d46b01'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'NPC1 (95%) and NPC2 (5%) are involved in:', '["GAG degradation","Lysosomal cholesterol export","Sphingomyelin degradation","GM2 degradation","Glucocerebroside degradation"]'::jsonb, 'Lysosomal cholesterol export', 'NPC1 (95%) and NPC2 (5%) are involved in lysosomal cholesterol export. NPC1 is a transmembrane protein; NPC2 is soluble. They cooperate to export cholesterol from lysosomes.'),
('0bae791c-3cb9-555c-ac8d-505e510327e8'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'The GAGs accumulated in MPS IV (Morquio) are:', '["Dermatan sulfate resulting in decreased functional activity","Hyaluronic acid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All GAGs mediated by specific receptor activation","Heparan sulfate without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Keratan sulfate and chondroitin-6-sulfate"]'::jsonb, 'Keratan sulfate and chondroitin-6-sulfate', 'In Morquio syndrome (MPS IV), keratan sulfate and chondroitin-6-sulfate accumulate due to galactosamine-6-sulfatase (type A) or beta-galactosidase (type B) deficiency.'),
('9b933466-a517-53a5-b186-1e2bd0ff836c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'MPS III (Sanfilippo) accumulates:', '["Keratan sulfate","Chondroitin sulfate","Hyaluronic acid","Heparan sulfate","Dermatan sulfate"]'::jsonb, 'Heparan sulfate', 'In Sanfilippo syndrome (MPS III), heparan sulfate accumulates due to deficiency of one of four degradation enzymes, causing severe neurological deterioration.'),
('4b32e4d3-ce1d-5761-b2d0-59bc823c17ec'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'ERT for MPS IVA uses:', '["Laronidase","Idursulfase","Vestronidase","Galsulfase","Elosulfase alfa (Vimizim)"]'::jsonb, 'Elosulfase alfa (Vimizim)', 'Elosulfase alfa (Vimizim) is ERT for MPS IVA (Morquio A), replacing galactosamine-6-sulfatase to degrade keratan sulfate.'),
('e22352df-d233-5be0-adb2-5ff10deb303c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'ERT for MPS VII uses:', '["Laronidase","Galsulfase","Elosulfase","Vestronidase alfa (Mepsevii)","Idursulfase"]'::jsonb, 'Vestronidase alfa (Mepsevii)', 'Vestronidase alfa (Mepsevii) is ERT for MPS VII (Sly), replacing beta-glucuronidase to degrade accumulated GAGs.'),
('b19aee1e-0aaa-596d-903e-b2fe4e10ef95'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'HSCT for MPS I is limited by:', '["Graft failure","Cost","Donor availability","Pain","Mortality"]'::jsonb, 'Mortality', 'HSCT for MPS I carries significant mortality (10-20%) from transplant complications. Risk must be weighed against benefit of halting neurological progression.'),
('500e144d-45f6-5a7f-ba25-e39e49e89bbc'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'The gene for phenylalanine hydroxylase (PAH) is on:', '["4p16.3","12q23.2","Xq28","1q21","5q13"]'::jsonb, '12q23.2', 'The PAH gene is at 12q22-12q24. Over 1,000 mutations have been identified causing PKU and hyperphenylalaninemia.'),
('22f9e072-4f20-57bc-b5eb-79691ba11573'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'PAH deficiency accounts for what percent of PKU cases?', '["98-99%","60%","50%","90%","75%"]'::jsonb, '98-99%', 'PAH deficiency accounts for 98-99% of PKU cases. The remaining 1-2% are BH4 deficiency or defects in BH4 synthesis/regeneration.'),
('5f8607c2-150f-5699-b28f-c97d3a82a113'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'BH4-responsive PKU is more common in patients with:', '["PKU patients","All PKU patients","Severe classic PKU","Mild to moderate hyperphenylalaninemia","Hyperphenylalaninemia"]'::jsonb, 'Mild to moderate hyperphenylalaninemia', 'BH4-responsive PKU is more common in mild-to-moderate hyperphenylalaninemia because these patients retain residual PAH activity enhanceable by BH4.'),
('9e570963-32fd-5bec-8d28-67f06ba590b5'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'MSUD can be diagnosed prenatally by:', '["Mutation analysis or enzyme assay in chorionic villi/amniocytes","Fetal MRI accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Amniotic fluid odor mediated by specific receptor activation","Maternal serum screening without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ultrasound leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mutation analysis or enzyme assay in chorionic villi/amniocytes', 'MSUD can be diagnosed prenatally by BCKDHA/BCKDHB/DBT/DLD mutation analysis or branched-chain alpha-ketoacid dehydrogenase enzyme assay in chorionic villi or amniocytes.'),
('fd1faa07-4678-5669-8751-1b5caefd2346'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Maple syrup odor in MSUD is from:', '["Valine resulting in decreased functional activity","Phenylalanine accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Leucine mediated by specific receptor activation","Tyrosine without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sotolone (from isoleucine)"]'::jsonb, 'Sotolone (from isoleucine)', 'The maple syrup odor in MSUD is primarily from sotolone, formed from oxidation of isoleucine alpha-keto acid metabolites.'),
('e424378d-4d3c-5487-84d6-b7645a539531'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Isovaleric acidemia causes:', '["Fruity odor","Maple syrup odor","Rancid butter odor","Mousy odor","Sweaty feet odor"]'::jsonb, 'Sweaty feet odor', 'Isovaleric acidemia (isovaleryl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency) produces a characteristic sweaty feet odor from accumulated isovaleric acid.'),
('5dfe911e-489a-5a49-be30-e80c116681bf'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Methylmalonic acidemia is due to deficiency of:', '["Holocarboxylase synthetase resulting in decreased functional activity","BCKD accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Propionyl-CoA carboxylase mediated by specific receptor activation","Methylmalonyl-CoA mutase or adenosylcobalamin synthesis","Biotinidase leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Methylmalonyl-CoA mutase or adenosylcobalamin synthesis', 'Methylmalonic acidemia is caused by methylmalonyl-CoA mutase deficiency (requiring adenosylcobalamin/B12) or adenosylcobalamin synthesis defects, causing metabolic acidosis.'),
('39e09ee2-e4ef-5fea-8b8b-e40cc8aa5605'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Glutaric acidemia type I is due to deficiency of:', '["Glutamate dehydrogenase","Glutamine synthetase","GABA transaminase","Glutamic acid decarboxylase","Glutaryl-CoA dehydrogenase"]'::jsonb, 'Glutaryl-CoA dehydrogenase', 'Glutaric acidemia type I (glutaryl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency) causes macrocephaly and acute encephalopathic crises with striatal necrosis from glutaric and 3-hydroxyglutaric acid accumulation.'),
('f693bf2e-763f-5473-a2fb-0c7ca6e3bb3a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Carnitine deficiency can be:', '["Primary (defective carnitine transport) or secondary (various causes)","Only primary accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only secondary mediated by specific receptor activation","Acquired without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Genetic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Primary (defective carnitine transport) or secondary (various causes)', 'Carnitine deficiency can be primary (OCTN2 transporter defect) or secondary (organic acidemias, fatty acid oxidation defects, valproic acid).'),
('c1e55e23-b963-5a6e-80c0-1137d72c9bcb'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Carnitine is required for:', '["Transport of long-chain fatty acids into mitochondria","Gluconeogenesis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Protein synthesis mediated by specific receptor activation","DNA replication without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Glycolysis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Transport of long-chain fatty acids into mitochondria', 'Carnitine transports long-chain fatty acids across the inner mitochondrial membrane via the carnitine shuttle (CPT I, translocase, CPT II).'),
('425de681-444b-5b2a-b5d6-8ee486635a60'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'CPT II deficiency causes:', '["Or adult myopathic forms","Only myopathic","All: severe neonatal","Infantile hepatocardiomuscular","Only hepatic"]'::jsonb, 'All: severe neonatal', 'CPT II deficiency ranges from severe neonatal (hypoketotic hypoglycemia, liver failure) to infantile hepatocardiomuscular to adult myopathic (exercise myoglobinuria) forms.'),
('a0c75c6c-f76e-548c-a88f-070369b6247b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Refsum disease is due to deficiency of:', '["VLCFA oxidation resulting in decreased functional activity","Ether phospholipid synthesis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bile acid synthesis mediated by specific receptor activation","Glyoxylate metabolism without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Phytanoyl-CoA hydroxylase (phytanic acid oxidation)"]'::jsonb, 'Phytanoyl-CoA hydroxylase (phytanic acid oxidation)', 'Refsum disease (phytanoyl-CoA hydroxylase deficiency) impairs phytanic acid alpha-oxidation, causing retinitis pigmentosa, peripheral neuropathy, cerebellar ataxia, and elevated CSF protein.'),
('47213d55-1c21-5c70-8eaf-dae4f8572f3f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Rhizomelic chondrodysplasia punctata is due to:', '["Glyoxylate metabolism defect","Phytanic acid oxidation defect","Bile acid synthesis defect","VLCFA oxidation defect","Defective ether phospholipid (plasmalogen) synthesis"]'::jsonb, 'Defective ether phospholipid (plasmalogen) synthesis', 'Rhizomelic chondrodysplasia punctata results from defective plasmalogen (ether phospholipid) synthesis, causing rhizomelia, stippled epiphyses, intellectual disability, and cataracts.'),
('d2e7709b-d88c-5656-8331-b960fc39aab8'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Primary hyperoxaluria type I is due to deficiency of:', '["All of the above","D-glycerate dehydrogenase","Hydroxypyruvate reductase","Glyoxylate reductase","Alanine:glyoxylate aminotransferase (AGT)"]'::jsonb, 'Alanine:glyoxylate aminotransferase (AGT)', 'Primary hyperoxaluria type I (alanine:glyoxylate aminotransferase deficiency) converts glyoxylate to oxalate instead of glycine, causing calcium oxalate kidney stones and nephrocalcinosis.'),
('954b9e06-3178-55db-aa62-ce599880968c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Treatment for primary hyperoxaluria type I includes:', '["Pyridoxine (B6) in responsive patients","Vitamin C supplementation","High oxalate diet","Calcium restriction","Liver-kidney transplant"]'::jsonb, 'Pyridoxine (B6) in responsive patients', 'Pyridoxine (B6) is a cofactor for AGT and reduces oxalate in responsive patients. Liver-kidney transplant is needed for severe cases with end-stage renal disease.'),
('e8a74b8e-55d5-568d-b9c0-c1361359d29f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'A newborn presents with vomiting, lethargy, and developmental delay. Newborn screening shows elevated amino acids. Maple syrup urine disease affects which amino acid metabolism?', '["Tyrosine","Isoleucine","Phenylalanine","Valine","Leucine"]'::jsonb, 'Leucine', 'MSUD primarily affects leucine metabolism. Leucine and alpha-ketoisocaproic acid are neurotoxic, causing the characteristic neurological symptoms.'),
('99b047b5-ba19-504a-9fa9-4e3637799a5c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Classic homocystinuria is due to deficiency of:', '["Betaine-homocysteine methyltransferase","Methionine synthase","Cystathionine beta-synthase (CBS)","Cystathionine gamma-lyase","MTHFR"]'::jsonb, 'Cystathionine beta-synthase (CBS)', 'Classic homocystinuria results from CBS deficiency, which converts homocysteine to cystathionine. Homocysteine and methionine accumulate.'),
('a60be35c-5af1-5367-9789-38b615bf057a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'MTHFR deficiency causes:', '["Normal methionine","Hyperhomocysteinemia with hypermethioninemia","Hyperhomocysteinemia with hypomethioninemia","Low homocysteine","Symptoms"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperhomocysteinemia with hypomethioninemia', 'MTHFR deficiency causes hyperhomocysteinemia with hypomethioninemia because 5-methylTHF (needed for methionine synthase) cannot be produced from 5,10-methylenetetrahydrofolate.'),
('eb427c0e-0d49-5086-8153-898b879de8e6'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Nonketotic hyperglycinemia is due to deficiency of:', '["Glycine oxidase","Glycine transaminase","Serine hydroxymethyltransferase","Glycine cleavage enzyme system","Glycine dehydrogenase"]'::jsonb, 'Glycine cleavage enzyme system', 'Nonketotic hyperglycinemia results from glycine cleavage enzyme system deficiency, causing glycine accumulation that is neurotoxic.'),
('e3e576f5-e839-5520-be87-0f9d7f91bad7'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Nonketotic hyperglycinemia presents with:', '["Mild hypotonia","And coma","Severe neonatal encephalopathy","Seizures (hiccups)","Normal development"]'::jsonb, 'Severe neonatal encephalopathy', 'Nonketotic hyperglycinemia presents with severe neonatal encephalopathy, intractable seizures (including hiccups), coma, and characteristic white matter/brainstem MRI findings.'),
('5ac09140-3800-551a-90f9-45e2e1fbbedb'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Dihydropyrimidine dehydrogenase (DPD) deficiency causes:', '["Lesch-Nyhan resulting in decreased functional activity","Thymine-uraciluria and 5-FU toxicity","Orotic aciduria mediated by specific receptor activation","Hyperuricemia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","SCID leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thymine-uraciluria and 5-FU toxicity', 'DPD deficiency causes thymine-uraciluria and severe, potentially fatal toxicity to 5-fluorouracil (5-FU) chemotherapy.'),
('1a7f9875-c4cc-5cf2-aef8-87ef36ad0718'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Maple syrup urine disease is inherited as:', '["Mitochondrial","X-linked dominant","X-linked recessive","Autosomal dominant","Autosomal recessive"]'::jsonb, 'Autosomal recessive', 'MSUD is autosomal recessive. BCKDHA, BCKDHB, DBT, and DLD genes encode the four subunits of branched-chain alpha-ketoacid dehydrogenase.'),
('b6465fc5-725a-5bf4-a6fa-a13068c99377'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Classic galactosemia (GALT deficiency) is inherited as:', '["X-linked recessive","X-linked dominant","Autosomal recessive","Mitochondrial","Autosomal dominant"]'::jsonb, 'Autosomal recessive', 'Classic galactosemia (GALT deficiency) is autosomal recessive. The GALT gene is at 9p13.'),
('97f8fe48-2a41-5858-a02a-b392335eaf10'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'GSD type I (von Gierke) is inherited as:', '["Autosomal recessive","X-linked dominant","Autosomal dominant","X-linked recessive","Mitochondrial"]'::jsonb, 'Autosomal recessive', 'Von Gierke disease (GSD I) is autosomal recessive. The G6PC gene is at 17q21.'),
('699b1d0e-3c1f-55d4-b03b-001528faba1d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'McArdle disease (GSD V) is inherited as:', '["Sporadic","Autosomal recessive","X-linked","Mitochondrial","Autosomal dominant"]'::jsonb, 'Autosomal recessive', 'McArdle disease (GSD V) is autosomal recessive. The PYGM gene (muscle glycogen phosphorylase) is at 11q13.'),
('f62053db-9066-5cff-a9d6-d13dfc49c348'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Hunter syndrome (MPS II) is inherited as:', '["X-linked recessive","Mitochondrial","Autosomal dominant","X-linked dominant","Autosomal recessive"]'::jsonb, 'X-linked recessive', 'Hunter syndrome (MPS II) is the only X-linked MPS. The IDS gene is at Xq28.'),
('2d13a852-b627-50fb-b78a-068967fe54cb'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Tay-Sachs disease is inherited as:', '["X-linked recessive","Mitochondrial","X-linked dominant","Autosomal dominant","Autosomal recessive"]'::jsonb, 'Autosomal recessive', 'Tay-Sachs is autosomal recessive. The HEXA gene (hexosaminidase alpha subunit) is at 15q23.'),
('bbac0e84-995f-5b42-9389-f7a061e212f1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 1', 'Cystinosis is inherited as:', '["Autosomal dominant","Autosomal recessive","X-linked dominant","X-linked recessive","Mitochondrial"]'::jsonb, 'Autosomal recessive', 'Cystinosis is autosomal recessive. The CTNS gene (cystinosin) is at 17p13.'),
('263efbc4-5358-5be0-832e-864f2bee510e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'A 40-year-old male presents with acute severe pain, swelling, and redness in the first metatarsophalangeal joint. Arthrocentesis shows negatively birefringent, needle-shaped crystals. (Metabolic Case #1)', '["Calcium oxalate","Calcium pyrophosphate dihydrate","Cholesterol","Basic calcium phosphate","Monosodium urate"]'::jsonb, 'Monosodium urate', 'Gout is characterized by needle-shaped, negatively birefringent monosodium urate crystals.'),
