('0cf40a6e-b9d0-52dc-b786-dfa458b946ec'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'Hydroxyurea inhibits:', '["Ribonucleotide reductase","Thymidylate synthase","IMP dehydrogenase","Xanthine oxidase","DHFR"]'::jsonb, 'Ribonucleotide reductase', 'Hydroxyurea inhibits ribonucleotide reductase, blocking ribonucleotide to deoxyribonucleotide conversion. Used in sickle cell (increases HbF) and myeloproliferative disorders.'),
('f057fa5c-956e-5287-9527-30b7e9b1a62c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'A 70-year-old female presents with progressive abdominal distension, bone pain, and fatigue. Physical examination reveals massive splenomegaly and hepatomegaly. CBC shows pancytopenia. Bone marrow biopsy reveals lipid-laden macrophages resembling crumpled tissue paper. What is the deficient enzyme?', '["Glucocerebrosidase (beta-glucosidase)","Sphingomyelinase","Hexosaminidase A","Galactocerebrosidase","Alpha-galactosidase A"]'::jsonb, 'Glucocerebrosidase (beta-glucosidase)', 'This patient presents with Gaucher''s disease, the most common lysosomal storage disease. It is caused by glucocerebrosidase deficiency, leading to accumulation of glucocerebroside in macrophages (Gaucher cells), which classically resemble crumpled tissue paper.'),
('93084643-7ee8-52e0-be6a-b06178fceb43'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'Myoadenylate deaminase deficiency causes:', '["Hyperuricemia resulting in decreased functional activity","Exercise-induced myopathy and easy fatigability","Gout mediated by specific receptor activation","Renal failure without affecting the primary tissue pathway","SCID leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Exercise-induced myopathy and easy fatigability', 'Myoadenylate deaminase (AMP deaminase 3) deficiency is the most common metabolic myopathy. Impairs purine nucleotide cycle, causing exercise-induced myopathy and easy fatigability.'),
('ef654518-91fb-5523-9356-4c026a1ae7f1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of Metabolic Disorders Pt2, Tartrate-resistant acid phosphatase is a marker for:', '["Bone disease","Prostate cancer","Liver disease","Hairy cell leukemia","Multiple myeloma"]'::jsonb, 'Hairy cell leukemia', 'TRAP is elevated in hairy cell leukemia and Gaucher disease.'),
('28700428-02ab-5e1d-8d7b-930014906079'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'Beta-ureidopropionase deficiency causes:', '["Beta-alanine and beta-aminoisobutyric acid accumulation","SCID accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Gout mediated by specific receptor activation","Hyperuricemia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Orotic aciduria leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Beta-alanine and beta-aminoisobutyric acid accumulation', 'Beta-ureidopropionase deficiency causes beta-alanine and beta-aminoisobutyric acid accumulation, potentially causing intellectual disability.'),
('a725e23f-2540-5f3a-ba64-cc3b41857c0a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'In the study of Metabolic Disorders Pt2, Uncompetitive inhibition occurs when the inhibitor binds:', '["To the substrate","Only to the ES complex","Only to free enzyme","To both E and ES","To the product"]'::jsonb, 'Only to the ES complex', 'Uncompetitive inhibitors bind only to the ES complex. Both Km and Vmax decrease by the same factor.'),
('2dbb33cd-419a-563f-bc60-9e45e2824048'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'Urinary alkalinization (K citrate) treats uric acid stones by:', '["Chelating urate","Increasing urine volume","Blocking urate absorption","Increasing urate solubility at higher pH","Decreasing urate production"]'::jsonb, 'Increasing urate solubility at higher pH', 'Potassium citrate alkalinizes urine to pH >6.5-7.0, where uric acid is ionized and 10x more soluble, preventing stone formation.'),
('343d4ac5-a43e-57ac-812e-1bc62bee319b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'A 30-year-old male presents with muscle cramps and fatigue during intense exercise. Urinalysis performed after exercise shows dark red-brown urine that is positive for blood, but no red blood cells are seen on microscopy. Blood tests show an elevated creatine kinase (CK) and no rise in blood lactate levels during an ischemic forearm exercise test. What is the deficient enzyme?', '["Phosphofructokinase resulting in decreased functional activity","Myophosphorylase (skeletal muscle glycogen phosphorylase)","Debranching enzyme mediated by specific receptor activation","Acid alpha-glucosidase without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Glucose-6-phosphatase leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Myophosphorylase (skeletal muscle glycogen phosphorylase)', 'McArdle''s disease (GSD Type V) is caused by a deficiency in skeletal muscle glycogen phosphorylase (myophosphorylase). Patients develop muscle cramping and myoglobinuria (causing red-brown urine) during exercise, with a characteristic failure of venous lactate to rise during ischemic exercise.'),
('82cc56b0-5744-5245-9980-d676bc9df090'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'Ehlers-Danlos syndrome is caused by defects in:', '["Fibrillin resulting in decreased functional activity","Laminin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Elastin mediated by specific receptor activation","Collagen synthesis or structure (various types)","Proteoglycans leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Collagen synthesis or structure (various types)', 'Collagen contains hydroxyproline and hydroxylysine, requiring vitamin C for hydroxylation.'),
('c83a153c-4ddc-5db0-a65d-1a12b50dbe19'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'The most common type of Ehlers-Danlos syndrome is:', '["Classic type (EDS I/II)","Kyphoscoliotic type","Vascular type (EDS IV)","Hypermobile type (EDS type III)","Dermatosparaxis"]'::jsonb, 'Hypermobile type (EDS type III)', 'Hypermobile EDS (type III) is the most common (90% of cases). Characterized by joint hypermobility, chronic pain, and recurrent dislocations. Genetic basis still unknown.'),
('399d58ef-2f39-5930-b4e4-389ec5a17f09'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'Vascular Ehlers-Danlos (type IV) is caused by deficiency of:', '["Type III collagen (COL3A1 mutations)","Type V collagen accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Type I collagen mediated by specific receptor activation","Lysyl hydroxylase without affecting the primary tissue pathway","ADAMTS2 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Type III collagen (COL3A1 mutations)', 'Collagen contains hydroxyproline and hydroxylysine, requiring vitamin C for hydroxylation.'),
('4dc491f9-4b08-51b5-bbb9-77507881b359'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Metabolic Disorders Pt2: Which factor mediates most of growth hormone''s growth-promoting actions?', '["Insulin-like growth factor-1 (IGF-1)","Fibroblast growth factor","Platelet-derived growth factor","Transforming growth factor-beta","Epidermal growth factor"]'::jsonb, 'Insulin-like growth factor-1 (IGF-1)', 'GH stimulates the liver and peripheral tissues to produce IGF-1, which mediates linear bone growth and anabolic effects. IGF-1 also feeds back to inhibit GH secretion.'),
('6eb55049-650d-58b3-9853-e24a08ece4b0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'A 9-month-old infant presents with hepatomegaly, lactic acidosis, hyperuricemia, and severe fasting hypoglycemia that does not respond to glucagon administration. Physical exam shows a doll-like facial appearance. Which enzyme is deficient in this patient?', '["Glucose-6-phosphatase","Acid alpha-glucosidase (acid maltase)","Debranching enzyme","Muscle glycogen phosphorylase","Galactose-1-phosphate uridyltransferase"]'::jsonb, 'Glucose-6-phosphatase', 'This clinical picture describes Von Gierke''s disease (Glycogen Storage Disease Type I), caused by a deficiency in glucose-6-phosphatase. This enzyme is crucial for both glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis, explaining the severe fasting hypoglycemia and lactic acidosis.'),
('a000d01d-02e4-5d9d-9512-45b52aae555b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of Metabolic Disorders Pt2, Stereospecificity of enzymes refers to:', '["Specificity for D-isomers only","Reaction with any sugar","Non-specific binding","Specificity for L-isomers only","Specificity for one optical isomer"]'::jsonb, 'Specificity for one optical isomer', 'Stereospecificity: enzymes distinguish between optical isomers (D vs L) or geometric isomers (cis vs trans).'),
('4ff0f0d9-903c-5950-b2d9-65464948b7ea'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'Osteogenesis imperfecta type I is the:', '["Most severe (perinatal lethal) resulting in decreased functional activity","Moderate severity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mildest form with blue sclera and fractures (autosomal dominant)","Silent ( symptoms) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Severe with progressive deformity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mildest form with blue sclera and fractures (autosomal dominant)', 'OI type I is the mildest form. Caused by COL1A1 nonsense mutations producing half-normal amounts of structurally normal type I collagen. Features blue sclera, mild fractures, and hearing loss.'),
('be6eb1ee-8f12-5811-ae41-fcb8fbdf4399'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'In the study of Metabolic Disorders Pt2, Allosteric inhibitors bind to:', '["Product resulting in decreased functional activity","Substrate accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Regulatory sites (allosteric sites)","Active sites without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Coenzyme leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Regulatory sites (allosteric sites)', 'Allosteric regulation involves binding of effectors at sites other than the active site, causing conformational changes.'),
('aab78f96-a8df-51d4-b0ad-e2b887c5d692'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Metabolic Disorders Pt2: Emphysema in AAT deficiency is accelerated by:', '["Alcohol","Exercise","Smoking","High-fat diet","Obesity"]'::jsonb, 'Smoking', 'Smoking accelerates emphysema in AAT deficiency by generating oxidative stress that further inactivates the already deficient alpha-1-antitrypsin.'),
('6adcebe1-d8d8-5fdb-b827-a80553348e33'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'Dentinogenesis imperfecta (brittle teeth) is associated with:', '["Paget disease","Osteogenesis imperfecta","Ehlers-Danlos","Marfan syndrome","Epidermolysis bullosa"]'::jsonb, 'Osteogenesis imperfecta', 'Dentinogenesis imperfecta (opalescent teeth that chip easily) is associated with OI types I, II, and III.'),
('8a65682e-f079-5338-8b4b-f5391d36b3d5'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'A 60-year-old female presents with acute, severe pain, swelling, and redness in the first metatarsophalangeal joint. Arthrocentesis of the joint reveals negatively birefringent, needle-shaped crystals under polarized light. What is the composition of these crystals?', '["Cholesterol","Basic calcium phosphate","Calcium pyrophosphate dihydrate","Calcium oxalate","Monosodium urate"]'::jsonb, 'Monosodium urate', 'Acute gout classically affects the 1st MTP joint (podagra). Synovial fluid analysis showing needle-shaped, negatively birefringent crystals confirms monosodium urate crystal deposition. Pseudogout crystals are weakly positively birefringent and rod/rhomboid-shaped (calcium pyrophosphate).'),
('e1547af9-af34-59f3-9eb7-2f7de3b33623'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'The leading cause of death in Marfan syndrome is:', '["Mitral valve prolapse","Infective endocarditis","Heart failure","Arrhythmia","Aortic root dilation and dissection"]'::jsonb, 'Aortic root dilation and dissection', 'Lead inhibits delta-aminolevulinic acid dehydrogenase and ferrochelatase causing sideroblastic anemia. Also causes basophilic stippling, abdominal colic, and neurotoxicity.'),
('d4cf5442-2610-5a00-b568-af8c3c966164'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'The FBN1 gene encodes:', '["Fibronectin resulting in decreased functional activity","Elastin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Collagen type I mediated by specific receptor activation","Collagen type III without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fibrillin-1 (glycoprotein component of microfibrils)"]'::jsonb, 'Fibrillin-1 (glycoprotein component of microfibrils)', 'FBN1 encodes fibrillin-1, a 350-kDa glycoprotein forming microfibrils in connective tissue. Also regulates TGF-beta signaling.'),
('d572fb50-6164-543f-a22e-8439e3187030'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'FBN1 is located on:', '["12q13","2q31","7q22","17q21","15q21.1"]'::jsonb, '15q21.1', 'FBN1 is located on chromosome 15q21.1. Over 3,000 mutations identified, mostly missense mutations affecting cysteine residues.'),
('069d2de3-443f-53ba-9872-b01995b88a65'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Metabolic Disorders Pt2: Glycogen in Cori disease (GSD III) has:', '["Excessively branched resulting in decreased functional activity","Branches accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Normal structure mediated by specific receptor activation","Long outer chains without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Very short outer chains (limit dextrin)"]'::jsonb, 'Very short outer chains (limit dextrin)', 'In Cori disease (GSD III), glycogen has very short outer chains (limit dextrin) because debranching enzyme cannot remove branch points.'),
('b58c9e6f-108e-599b-ba78-f63a7bf78d81'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'A 40-year-old female presents with acute, severe pain, swelling, and redness in the first metatarsophalangeal joint. Arthrocentesis of the joint reveals negatively birefringent, needle-shaped crystals under polarized light. What is the composition of these crystals?', '["Basic calcium phosphate","Calcium oxalate","Cholesterol","Monosodium urate","Calcium pyrophosphate dihydrate"]'::jsonb, 'Monosodium urate', 'Acute gout classically affects the 1st MTP joint (podagra). Synovial fluid analysis showing needle-shaped, negatively birefringent crystals confirms monosodium urate crystal deposition. Pseudogout crystals are weakly positively birefringent and rod/rhomboid-shaped (calcium pyrophosphate).'),
('814b1342-7a23-5e5d-8d59-b35adc0de20c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases: Zero-order kinetics means:', '["Constant amount eliminated/time","Constant fraction/time","Dose-dependent elimination","First-order at high doses","Saturable absorption"]'::jsonb, 'Constant amount eliminated/time', 'Zero-order: constant amount eliminated regardless of concentration.'),
('ecf3fb9e-446f-50db-9406-47c2f7de53e9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'Thiamine deficiency causes:', '["Rickets resulting in decreased functional activity","Beriberi (dry and wet) and Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome","Pellagra mediated by specific receptor activation","Scurvy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anemia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Beriberi (dry and wet) and Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome', 'Thiamine (B1) deficiency causes beriberi: dry (peripheral neuropathy), wet (high-output heart failure), and Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome.'),
('2bc61464-7dc6-5292-a801-7deedfd30862'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'Dry beriberi presents with:', '["Cardiomegaly","Edema","Muscle wasting","Peripheral neuropathy","Paresthesias"]'::jsonb, 'Peripheral neuropathy', 'Dry beriberi presents with symmetric peripheral neuropathy (stocking-glove), muscle wasting, and weakness. Can progress to foot/wrist drop.'),
('d433f0d6-e832-5274-ae44-94d9311c39f5'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Metabolic Disorders Pt2: Acid phosphatase (ACP) is found in:', '["Platelets","Liver","Erythrocytes","Bone","Prostate"]'::jsonb, 'Prostate', 'ACP is elevated in prostate cancer, bone metastases, and hairy cell leukemia. Tartrate-resistant ACP (TRAP) is specific for hairy cell leukemia.'),
('f8cc9775-cd47-5b58-a3ca-9307649a7e5a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'Wernicke encephalopathy is characterized by:', '["Confusion","Nystagmus/ophthalmoplegia","Ataxia","Heart failure only","Peripheral neuropathy only"]'::jsonb, 'Confusion', 'Wernicke encephalopathy triad: confusion, ophthalmoplegia/nystagmus, and gait ataxia. Due to thiamine deficiency affecting brain glucose metabolism. Medical emergency.'),
('2205e153-31d8-5c16-a802-873f286894b8'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'Korsakoff syndrome presents with:', '["Nystagmus alone resulting in decreased functional activity","Amnesia (retrograde and anterograde) and confabulation","Ataxia alone mediated by specific receptor activation","Peripheral neuropathy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Confusion alone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Amnesia (retrograde and anterograde) and confabulation', 'Korsakoff syndrome presents with anterograde/retrograde amnesia and confabulation. Often irreversible. Follows untreated Wernicke encephalopathy.'),
('81dff29a-584d-5aea-9222-03fe13115f93'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome is most commonly associated with:', '["Bariatric surgery","Malabsorption","Pregnancy","Starvation","Chronic alcoholism"]'::jsonb, 'Chronic alcoholism', 'WKS is most commonly associated with chronic alcoholism, which causes poor nutrition, impaired thiamine absorption, and reduced hepatic storage.'),
('94dbb5cb-7623-516d-8c83-aa8f7b6a1295'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Metabolic Disorders Pt2: Dihydropyrimidine dehydrogenase (DPD) deficiency causes:', '["Lesch-Nyhan resulting in decreased functional activity","Thymine-uraciluria and 5-FU toxicity","Orotic aciduria mediated by specific receptor activation","Hyperuricemia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","SCID leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thymine-uraciluria and 5-FU toxicity', 'DPD deficiency causes thymine-uraciluria and severe, potentially fatal toxicity to 5-fluorouracil (5-FU) chemotherapy.'),
('74ac438c-a0cf-5434-9c52-9d088d8ad620'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Metabolic Disorders Pt2: The high insulin-to-glucagon ratio in the fed (postprandial) state favors:', '["Glycogenolysis","Ketogenesis","Glycogen synthesis and storage","Gluconeogenesis","Lipolysis"]'::jsonb, 'Glycogen synthesis and storage', 'A high insulin-to-glucagon ratio in the fed state activates glycogen synthase and lipogenesis while suppressing gluconeogenesis. This promotes fuel storage in liver, muscle, and adipose tissue.'),
('e5a0af9a-9c98-5e6e-9e5f-a7aaa9deec53'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'A newborn presents with vomiting, lethargy, and developmental delay. Newborn screening shows elevated amino acids. Niacin can be synthesized from the amino acid:', '["Tyrosine","Tryptophan","Lysine","Phenylalanine","Methionine"]'::jsonb, 'Tryptophan', 'Niacin can be synthesized from tryptophan via the kynurenine pathway (requires B6, B2, iron). 60 mg tryptophan = 1 mg niacin.'),
('eb654036-4d48-5046-b6c3-94e8b6c4ee83'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'Pantothenic acid deficiency is rare but can cause:', '["Numbness","Burning feet syndrome","Paresthesias","Fatigue","Pellagra"]'::jsonb, 'Fatigue', 'Pantothenic acid (B5) is a component of CoA and ACP. Deficiency is rare but can cause burning feet syndrome.'),
('67c9321d-ccec-5242-93c6-5d381a7efba0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'In the study of Metabolic Disorders Pt2, Which molecule is the primary excretory product of nitrogen metabolism?', '["Urea","Ammonia","Uric acid","Creatinine","Bilirubin"]'::jsonb, 'Urea', 'Urea is the primary nitrogenous waste product of protein metabolism, synthesized in the liver via the urea cycle.'),
('5dfe225b-b5dd-51fc-8cb3-46fd6fe100fe'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'A 65-year-old female presents with muscle cramps and fatigue during intense exercise. Urinalysis performed after exercise shows dark red-brown urine that is positive for blood, but no red blood cells are seen on microscopy. Blood tests show an elevated creatine kinase (CK) and no rise in blood lactate levels during an ischemic forearm exercise test. What is the deficient enzyme?', '["Myophosphorylase (skeletal muscle glycogen phosphorylase)","Acid alpha-glucosidase accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Phosphofructokinase mediated by specific receptor activation","Debranching enzyme without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Glucose-6-phosphatase leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Myophosphorylase (skeletal muscle glycogen phosphorylase)', 'McArdle''s disease (GSD Type V) is caused by a deficiency in skeletal muscle glycogen phosphorylase (myophosphorylase). Patients develop muscle cramping and myoglobinuria (causing red-brown urine) during exercise, with a characteristic failure of venous lactate to rise during ischemic exercise.'),
('089bd50d-851d-54c4-bc55-5a7a55cfde99'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Metabolic Disorders Pt2: Amylase is produced by the:', '["Liver and kidney","Heart and lung","Pancreas and salivary glands","Stomach and intestine","Brain and muscle"]'::jsonb, 'Pancreas and salivary glands', 'Amylase (salivary and pancreatic) hydrolyzes alpha-1,4 glycosidic bonds in starch, breaking it into maltose and limit dextrins.'),
('fa0a80a3-14ea-5196-8ab4-948e5bbfee3b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'Consumption of raw egg whites can cause biotin deficiency because:', '["Egg whites block biotin synthesis","Preventing absorption","Egg whites destroy biotin","Avidin in egg white binds biotin","Egg whites increase biotin excretion"]'::jsonb, 'Avidin in egg white binds biotin', 'Biotin (B7/H) cofactor for carboxylases essential in metabolism.'),
('d6d8e5e4-5e82-53ad-9935-833726604405'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'Vitamin B12 (cobalamin) is a cofactor for:', '["Transaminases resulting in decreased functional activity","Oxidases accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Decarboxylases mediated by specific receptor activation","Carboxylases without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Methionine synthase and methylmalonyl-CoA mutase"]'::jsonb, 'Methionine synthase and methylmalonyl-CoA mutase', 'B12 is a cofactor for methionine synthase (homocysteine -> methionine) and methylmalonyl-CoA mutase (methylmalonyl-CoA -> succinyl-CoA).'),
('b0f08f3e-27ab-56d8-9e7e-46db8c89737e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'In the study of Metabolic Disorders Pt2, Disulfiram inhibits which enzyme?', '["MAO","COMT","ALDH2","Aldehyde dehydrogenase","Alcohol dehydrogenase"]'::jsonb, 'Aldehyde dehydrogenase', 'Disulfiram inhibits ALDH2, causing acetaldehyde accumulation after alcohol consumption (aversive therapy for alcoholism).'),
('8e7a5eb6-c5af-533a-951b-87555b135102'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'Subacute combined degeneration of the cord is due to deficiency of:', '["Folate","Vitamin E","Vitamin B12","Vitamin B1","Vitamin B6"]'::jsonb, 'Vitamin B12', 'Thiamine pyrophosphate (TPP) is a coenzyme for pyruvate dehydrogenase and alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase.'),
('662b4049-6f38-57db-8b7b-5589b40a9817'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Metabolic Disorders Pt2: Glutaric acidemia type I is due to deficiency of:', '["Glutamate dehydrogenase","Glutamine synthetase","GABA transaminase","Glutamic acid decarboxylase","Glutaryl-CoA dehydrogenase"]'::jsonb, 'Glutaryl-CoA dehydrogenase', 'Glutaric acidemia type I (glutaryl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency) causes macrocephaly and acute encephalopathic crises with striatal necrosis from glutaric and 3-hydroxyglutaric acid accumulation.'),
('acab3c9e-9160-5ce1-b5da-f0df94032613'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'Scurvy presents with:', '["Ecchymoses","Dermatitis","Poor wound healing","Perifollicular hemorrhages","Swollen bleeding gums"]'::jsonb, 'Swollen bleeding gums', 'Scurvy (vitamin C deficiency) presents with perifollicular hemorrhages, bleeding gums, poor wound healing, ecchymoses, and corkscrew hairs. Due to impaired collagen hydroxylation.'),
('7a00e636-0462-5f77-9933-7afdafcf2dee'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'Vitamin A (retinol) is essential for:', '["Immune function","Epithelial integrity","Vision (rhodopsin)","Bone growth","Blood clotting"]'::jsonb, 'Vision (rhodopsin)', 'Vitamin A is essential for vision (retinal in rhodopsin), epithelial differentiation, immune function, reproduction, and growth. Stored in liver.'),
('fccce14c-599b-5470-996b-fe2cb9b0759a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'Bitot spots are:', '["Ulcers resulting in decreased functional activity","Pigmented spots accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cysts mediated by specific receptor activation","Foamy white patches on the conjunctiva (vitamin A deficiency)","Hemorrhagic spots leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Foamy white patches on the conjunctiva (vitamin A deficiency)', 'Bitot spots are foamy white patches on the conjunctiva from keratinization in vitamin A deficiency. Not reversible with supplementation.'),
('98766917-75d2-5291-8890-597d8eac1468'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Metabolic Disorders Pt2: Krabbe disease presents in infancy with:', '["Rapid neurological decline","Renal failure","Stiffness","Seizures","Hyperirritability"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperirritability', 'Infantile Krabbe disease presents with hyperirritability, feeding difficulties, seizures, and rapid neurological decline. Death usually occurs within 2 years.'),
('e41b0f37-8eab-5afa-9279-325dc518d72c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Metabolic Disorders Pt2: Tyrosinemia type II (Richner-Hanhart syndrome) is due to:', '["FAH deficiency","Tyrosine aminotransferase deficiency","Homogentisate oxidase deficiency","PAH deficiency","4-HPPD deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Tyrosine aminotransferase deficiency', 'Tyrosine is derived from phenylalanine and serves as a precursor for catecholamines, melanin, and thyroid hormones.'),
('b646c9d1-1dc0-5382-9b03-ea37c3e410da'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'Vitamin D (cholecalciferol) is synthesized in:', '["Intestine resulting in decreased functional activity","Skin upon UVB exposure","Kidney mediated by specific receptor activation","Bone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Liver leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Skin upon UVB exposure', 'UV spectroscopy uses 200-400 nm wavelength range, where aromatic amino acids and nucleic acids absorb.'),
('e7694048-671e-55de-b7c5-3f0cc27dcf52'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Metabolic Disorders Pt2: Pancreatic polypeptide (PP) is secreted by which islet cell type?', '["Alpha cells","Beta cells","Delta cells","PP (F) cells","Acinar cells"]'::jsonb, 'PP (F) cells', 'PP cells, also called F cells, secrete pancreatic polypeptide, which regulates exocrine pancreatic secretion and gut motility. Acinar cells are exocrine cells that secrete digestive enzymes.'),
('590fbf73-3b9b-5d72-83db-ad7d4370d583'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'A 40-year-old male presents with acute, severe pain, swelling, and redness in the first metatarsophalangeal joint. Arthrocentesis of the joint reveals negatively birefringent, needle-shaped crystals under polarized light. What is the composition of these crystals?', '["Calcium pyrophosphate dihydrate","Calcium oxalate","Basic calcium phosphate","Monosodium urate","Cholesterol"]'::jsonb, 'Monosodium urate', 'Acute gout classically affects the 1st MTP joint (podagra). Synovial fluid analysis showing needle-shaped, negatively birefringent crystals confirms monosodium urate crystal deposition. Pseudogout crystals are weakly positively birefringent and rod/rhomboid-shaped (calcium pyrophosphate).'),
('9a73dd45-9cfb-5d6e-b03f-3cddc2ea069b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'The primary storage form of vitamin D in the body is:', '["25-Dihydroxycholecalciferol","7-Dehydrocholesterol","1","Cholecalciferol","25-Hydroxycholecalciferol (calcidiol)"]'::jsonb, '25-Hydroxycholecalciferol (calcidiol)', '25-Hydroxycholecalciferol (calcidiol) is the major circulating form. Produced in liver by 25-hydroxylase. Measured to assess vitamin D status.'),
('4db4d9e8-2828-5574-9b7b-acfbd937dcad'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'Vitamin E deficiency causes:', '["Ataxia","Peripheral neuropathy","Myopathy","Bleeding","Hemolytic anemia (neonates)"]'::jsonb, 'Hemolytic anemia (neonates)', 'Vitamin E deficiency causes hemolytic anemia (neonates), peripheral neuropathy, ataxia, and myopathy. Vitamin E protects cell membranes from lipid peroxidation.'),
('4b7de363-d5cb-5773-84ba-ac6687326227'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of Metabolic Disorders Pt2, The gene for phenylalanine hydroxylase (PAH) is on:', '["4p16.3","12q23.2","Xq28","1q21","5q13"]'::jsonb, '12q23.2', 'The PAH gene is at 12q22-12q24. Over 1,000 mutations have been identified causing PKU and hyperphenylalaninemia.'),
('f70f6c58-3cbd-538b-bcfa-0603e69c6031'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'Which vitamin is essential for blood clotting?', '["Antagonizing calcium resulting in decreased functional activity","Inhibiting vitamin K epoxide reductase (blocking vitamin K recycling)","Inhibiting clotting factors directly mediated by specific receptor activation","Enhancing vitamin K degradation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Blocking vitamin K absorption leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibiting vitamin K epoxide reductase (blocking vitamin K recycling)', 'Warfarin inhibits vitamin K epoxide reductase (VKORC1), blocking recycling of vitamin K epoxide back to its reduced active form. This prevents gamma-carboxylation of clotting factors.'),
('148bfe08-8401-58bb-9e68-f28c7cdc1fba'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'A 55-year-old female presents with muscle cramps and fatigue during intense exercise. Urinalysis performed after exercise shows dark red-brown urine that is positive for blood, but no red blood cells are seen on microscopy. Blood tests show an elevated creatine kinase (CK) and no rise in blood lactate levels during an ischemic forearm exercise test. What is the deficient enzyme?', '["Glucose-6-phosphatase resulting in decreased functional activity","Phosphofructokinase accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Myophosphorylase (skeletal muscle glycogen phosphorylase)","Acid alpha-glucosidase without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Debranching enzyme leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Myophosphorylase (skeletal muscle glycogen phosphorylase)', 'McArdle''s disease (GSD Type V) is caused by a deficiency in skeletal muscle glycogen phosphorylase (myophosphorylase). Patients develop muscle cramping and myoglobinuria (causing red-brown urine) during exercise, with a characteristic failure of venous lactate to rise during ischemic exercise.'),
('55020acf-15f9-51f8-b116-df7b849a3882'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'Vitamin K2 (menaquinone) is produced by:', '["Animals","Algae","Fungi","Gut bacteria","Green plants"]'::jsonb, 'Gut bacteria', 'Vitamin K2 (menaquinone) is produced by gut bacteria. MK-4 from animal products; MK-7 from fermented soybeans.'),
('a156005f-87a2-571a-a9de-3587dc312af7'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'Vitamin deficiencies in chronic alcoholism:', '["B6","All: B1","Folate","B12","Vitamin A"]'::jsonb, 'All: B1', 'Thiamine pyrophosphate (TPP) is a coenzyme for pyruvate dehydrogenase and alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase.'),
('7d904589-70aa-5c33-a9b2-2ce55a4271c1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of Metabolic Disorders Pt2, Atrial natriuretic peptide signals through which second messenger?', '["Cyclic AMP","Cyclic GMP","Diacylglycerol","Inositol trisphosphate","Calcium"]'::jsonb, 'Cyclic GMP', 'ANP binds a receptor with intrinsic guanylyl cyclase activity, generating cGMP. cGMP activates protein kinase G and is also the second messenger of nitric oxide.'),
('cf48aca5-3097-503a-a67d-bd582e85f7e0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'The most common nutritional deficiency worldwide is:', '["Calcium deficiency","Zinc deficiency","Iron deficiency","Iodine deficiency","Vitamin D deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Iron deficiency', 'Iron deficiency is the most common nutritional deficiency worldwide, affecting 2 billion people. Most common in women of reproductive age and developing countries.'),
('3fc3db47-b0b1-50a2-a51a-61c93c28d2e2'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'Iron absorption occurs primarily in:', '["Stomach resulting in decreased functional activity","Rectum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Colon mediated by specific receptor activation","Ileum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Duodenum and proximal jejunum"]'::jsonb, 'Duodenum and proximal jejunum', 'Iron absorption occurs in duodenum and proximal jejunum via DMT1 (non-heme) and separate heme pathway. Regulated by hepcidin (degrades ferroportin).'),
('fd32fe29-16fe-56a2-a99f-3d04d9a07153'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'In anemia of chronic disease:', '["Hepcidin is elevated","Transferrin saturation is high","Iron absorption is increased","Ferritin is low","Trapping iron in macrophages"]'::jsonb, 'Hepcidin is elevated', 'In anemia of chronic disease, elevated hepcidin (from IL-6 stimulation) causes ferroportin degradation, trapping iron in macrophages and enterocytes. Results in high ferritin, low TIBC, low transferrin saturation.'),
('2fa25224-6a7d-5684-9043-11c09a2738dd'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Metabolic Disorders Pt2: Cystinosis is inherited as:', '["Autosomal dominant","Autosomal recessive","X-linked dominant","X-linked recessive","Mitochondrial"]'::jsonb, 'Autosomal recessive', 'Cystinosis is autosomal recessive. The CTNS gene (cystinosin) is at 17p13.'),
('267ea536-c590-5b06-a902-b7d53e8db575'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'Zinc deficiency causes:', '["Acrodermatitis enteropathica","Growth retardation","Hair loss","Hypogonadism","Immune dysfunction"]'::jsonb, 'Growth retardation', 'Zinc deficiency causes growth retardation, hypogonadism, immune dysfunction, impaired wound healing, and dermatitis (acrodermatitis enteropathica = genetic SLC39A4 mutation).'),
('b84e6822-572d-581c-a409-79bdff7db9d2'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Metabolic Disorders Pt2: BH4 deficiency should be suspected in PKU patients who:', '["Are mild cases resulting in decreased functional activity","Are severe cases accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Do not respond to dietary phenylalanine restriction","Developmental issues without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Have ochronosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Do not respond to dietary phenylalanine restriction', 'BH4 deficiency causes atypical PKU unresponsive to dietary phenylalanine restriction, because BH4 is the essential cofactor for phenylalanine hydroxylase.'),
('3089eed9-c946-5817-a2ec-7e94989bfc86'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'Menkes disease presents with:', '["Neurological deterioration","Connective tissue defects","Kinky/hypopigmented hair","Goiter","Anemia"]'::jsonb, 'Kinky/hypopigmented hair', 'Menkes disease (X-linked, ATP7A mutation) causes copper malabsorption. Features: kinky/hypopigmented hair, neurological deterioration, connective tissue defects.'),
('eefa333b-aaf5-5661-ac9e-5065d7a4e44e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Metabolic Disorders Pt2: Tyrosinase converts tyrosine to:', '["Homogentisate","Phenylalanine","Epinephrine","DOPA then melanin","Norepinephrine"]'::jsonb, 'DOPA then melanin', 'Tyrosinase catalyzes the oxidation of tyrosine to DOPA and then to dopaquinone, the rate-limiting step in melanin biosynthesis. Deficiency causes oculocutaneous albinism type I.'),
('2d176af4-6d1b-5c38-9b87-5d76246953f7'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'Which disorder causes copper accumulation in the liver and brain?', '["Lead deposits resulting in decreased functional activity","Copper deposits in the cornea (Descemet membrane) seen in Wilson disease","Calcium deposits mediated by specific receptor activation","Iron deposits without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Zinc deposits leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Copper deposits in the cornea (Descemet membrane) seen in Wilson disease', 'Wilson disease causes copper accumulation in liver (cirrhosis), brain (parkinsonism, psychiatric symptoms), and cornea (Kayser-Fleischer rings in Descemet membrane).'),
('d23693da-7c63-5ba4-9228-c7857bf7e6e8'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'Treatment of Wilson disease includes:', '["All: penicillamine (chelator)","Trientine only","Penicillamine only","Trientine","Zinc acetate"]'::jsonb, 'All: penicillamine (chelator)', 'Wilson disease is caused by ATP7B mutation impairing copper excretion. Copper accumulates in liver, brain, and cornea (Kayser-Fleischer rings). Treated with penicillamine and zinc.'),
('2b18291e-b543-5963-b235-b0a5cd7836d6'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'Ceruloplasmin is:', '["A selenium-binding protein resulting in decreased functional activity","A zinc transporter accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","An iron-carrying protein mediated by specific receptor activation","A copper-carrying protein (ferroxidase) synthesized in the liver","A manganese enzyme leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A copper-carrying protein (ferroxidase) synthesized in the liver', 'Ceruloplasmin carries 70% of plasma copper and has ferroxidase activity (converts Fe2+ to Fe3+ for transferrin loading). Acute phase reactant.'),
('14800ddd-fd0f-520f-8402-55834cfbe436'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'Selenium is a component of:', '["Glutathione peroxidase and deiodinases","Catalase accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","SOD mediated by specific receptor activation","Cytochrome c without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hemoglobin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Glutathione peroxidase and deiodinases', 'Selenium is incorporated as selenocysteine into glutathione peroxidase (antioxidant), thioredoxin reductase, and deiodinases (T4->T3).'),
('a177fbe3-8873-5acf-92d4-20f39229f995'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Metabolic Disorders Pt2: Substrate reduction therapy (SRT) aims to:', '["Stabilize mutant enzyme","Block catabolism","Provide missing metabolite","Reduce synthesis of accumulating substrate","Replace the deficient enzyme"]'::jsonb, 'Reduce synthesis of accumulating substrate', 'SRT aims to reduce synthesis of the accumulating substrate rather than replacing the deficient enzyme, using small molecules that inhibit substrate-producing enzymes.'),
('a055fee7-b800-5ce8-b006-35137a7e6dfa'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Metabolic Disorders Pt2: Limit dextrin refers to the structure accumulating in:', '["GSD type III (Cori disease)","GSD type V","GSD type II","GSD type IV","GSD type I"]'::jsonb, 'GSD type III (Cori disease)', 'Limit dextrin accumulates in GSD III (Cori disease) due to debranching enzyme deficiency, appearing as glycogen with abnormally short outer chains.'),
('7c15ff15-79b9-5f21-a835-ea8d3a1e825d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'Fluoride is important for:', '["Immune function resulting in decreased functional activity","Thyroid function accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dental health (prevents dental caries and strengthens enamel)","Bone health only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Antioxidant defense leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dental health (prevents dental caries and strengthens enamel)', 'Fluoride strengthens dental enamel (fluorapatite, more acid-resistant). Prevents dental caries. Also strengthens bone.'),
('99e3e019-ab27-58d8-b9ad-4d90b8c74661'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'Fluoride deficiency increases risk of:', '["Osteoporosis","Anemia","Goiter","Rickets","Dental caries"]'::jsonb, 'Dental caries', 'Fluoride deficiency increases dental caries risk. Water fluoridation (0.7-1.2 ppm) reduces caries by 20-40%.'),
('5cad9a61-a0ba-5400-a4c1-602fc507ddd4'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'Excess fluoride causes:', '["Fluorosis (dental mottling and skeletal fluorosis)","Goiter accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Rickets mediated by specific receptor activation","Cardiomyopathy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anemia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fluorosis (dental mottling and skeletal fluorosis)', 'Excess fluoride causes fluorosis: dental (white/brown mottling of enamel) and skeletal (joint pain, ligament calcification, osteosclerosis).'),
('c9f93d24-c8ed-5ed5-a7ac-45fecded8ec7'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Metabolic Disorders Pt2: Michaelis-Menten kinetics describes:', '["Protein synthesis","The rate of enzyme-catalyzed reactions","Membrane transport","DNA replication","Cell division"]'::jsonb, 'The rate of enzyme-catalyzed reactions', 'Km (Michaelis constant) is the substrate concentration at half-maximal velocity, reflecting enzyme-substrate affinity. V = Vmax[S]/(Km + [S]).'),
('23412087-2820-5721-9545-d1d3fe25def2'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'Skeletal fluorosis presents with:', '["Osteosclerosis","Osteoporosis","And calcification of ligaments","Stiffness","Joint pain"]'::jsonb, 'Joint pain', 'Skeletal fluorosis presents with joint pain, stiffness, osteosclerosis, and ligament calcification. Endemic in areas with >4 ppm groundwater fluoride.'),
('e938e2b6-15a1-51a1-9d02-587b4463ba5b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of Metabolic Disorders Pt2, Maroteaux-Lamy (MPS VI) is due to deficiency of:', '["Beta-glucuronidase resulting in decreased functional activity","Iduronate sulfatase accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Arylsulfatase A mediated by specific receptor activation","Alpha-L-iduronidase without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Arylsulfatase B (N-acetylgalactosamine-4-sulfatase)"]'::jsonb, 'Arylsulfatase B (N-acetylgalactosamine-4-sulfatase)', 'Maroteaux-Lamy (MPS VI) is caused by arylsulfatase B deficiency, accumulating dermatan sulfate. It resembles Hurler but with normal intelligence.'),
('c66af596-7e83-5925-942d-6efaf9ef4113'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'Magnesium is absorbed in:', '["Duodenum only resulting in decreased functional activity","Small intestine (mainly ileum) and colon","Stomach mediated by specific receptor activation","Large intestine only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Jejunum only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Small intestine (mainly ileum) and colon', 'Mg2+ is absorbed primarily in the small intestine (jejunum/ileum) and colon via TRPM6/7 channels.'),
('4761c9f9-e409-5140-a775-09eda03d8934'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'Calcium homeostasis is regulated by:', '["Only calcitriol","All: PTH","Only PTH","Calcitonin","Calcitriol"]'::jsonb, 'All: PTH', 'Ca2+ homeostasis: PTH (increases Ca2+ via bone resorption, renal reabsorption, calcitriol synthesis), calcitonin (decreases Ca2+ via bone deposition), and calcitriol (intestinal absorption).'),
('7f1efe51-ac19-5047-836e-19a7fd46db25'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'Hypocalcemia causes:', '["Paresthesias","Trousseau sign","Tetany","Chvostek sign","Hypertension"]'::jsonb, 'Tetany', 'Hypocalcemia causes neuromuscular excitability: paresthesias, Chvostek sign (facial twitch), Trousseau sign (carpopedal spasm), tetany, seizures.'),
('36d74813-52be-555d-9619-c63b89b647b5'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Metabolic Disorders Pt2: Protein binding fraction (fu) ranges:', '["0 to 1","-1 to 0","0 to 100","-100 to 100","1 to 100"]'::jsonb, '0 to 1', 'Only unbound (free) drug is pharmacologically active.'),
('4fc63981-9f05-51a9-9f94-1cc8494b915c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'Primary hyperparathyroidism presents with:', '["Hypercalcemia","None of the above","Hypophosphatemia","High PTH","Hyperphosphatemia"]'::jsonb, 'Hypercalcemia', 'Primary hyperparathyroidism: hypercalcemia with elevated PTH, low phosphate. Often asymptomatic. Most commonly from parathyroid adenoma.'),
('604b207e-a2af-5fe4-9c65-9293ff850649'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Metabolic Disorders Pt2: The most important counter-regulatory hormone responsible for ketogenesis in DKA is:', '["Insulin","Glucagon","Aldosterone","Parathyroid hormone","Estrogen"]'::jsonb, 'Glucagon', 'In DKA, glucagon excess drives lipolysis, lowers malonyl-CoA, and promotes hepatic ketone body synthesis. Insulin deficiency alone is insufficient; the glucagon-to-insulin ratio is the key determinant.'),
('3936174b-5d87-592a-8377-1ab6840ce63f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'Sodium is the major:', '["Nuclear cation","Extracellular cation","Intracellular cation","Mitochondrial cation","Cytosolic cation"]'::jsonb, 'Extracellular cation', 'Na+ is the major extracellular cation (140 mEq/L). Responsible for ECF osmolality and water balance. Regulated by aldosterone and ADH.'),
('74066811-88d5-5a5c-bafd-231f386e7c85'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'Hyponatremia causes:', '["Tetany","Hyperreflexia","Confusion","Seizures","Coma (cerebral edema from water shift)"]'::jsonb, 'Confusion', 'Hyponatremia (<135 mEq/L) causes water shift into cells (cerebral edema). Symptoms: confusion, seizures, coma. Most common cause: SIADH.'),
('7871f958-42ed-5100-91c5-78e377e99c1b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'Manganese is a cofactor for:', '["And glutamine synthetase","Arginase","Superoxide dismutase (Mn-SOD)","Cytochrome oxidase","Catalase"]'::jsonb, 'Arginase', 'Mn2+ is a cofactor for arginase (urea cycle), Mn-SOD (mitochondrial antioxidant), and glutamine synthetase.'),
('6a7857cb-ddad-5188-8803-259e6c0651a1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of Metabolic Disorders Pt2, Treatment of homocystinuria includes:', '["Methionine restriction","Folate","B12","Betaine","All: pyridoxine"]'::jsonb, 'All: pyridoxine', 'Classical homocystinuria is caused by cystathionine beta-synthase deficiency. Homocysteine accumulates causing marfanoid habitus, lens dislocation, thromboembolism, and intellectual disability.'),
('ab06c12c-3425-5f57-ab2a-2fc5f377e6f8'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'Chromium (trivalent) enhances:', '["Iron absorption","Insulin action (glucose tolerance factor)","Thyroid function","Calcium absorption","Vitamin D activation"]'::jsonb, 'Insulin action (glucose tolerance factor)', 'Trivalent chromium enhances insulin action as part of the glucose tolerance factor (GTF), potentiating insulin receptor binding.'),
('5a37397b-6c84-5fab-a09f-c9657918447a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'In the study of Metabolic Disorders Pt2, Clinitest positive but glucose oxidase negative in:', '["Alkaptonuria","MSUD","Diabetes","Galactosemia","PKU"]'::jsonb, 'Galactosemia', 'In galactosemia, Clinitest (copper reduction) is positive for galactose, but glucose oxidase is negative (specific for glucose only). This pattern distinguishes galactosemia from diabetes.'),
('36f46e40-3fac-55ff-b82e-597d92f551cc'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Metabolic Disorders Pt2: The hydrogen breath test for lactose intolerance:', '["Measures CO2 resulting in decreased functional activity","Measures nitrogen accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Measures hydrogen from bacterial fermentation of undigested lactose","Measures oxygen without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Measures methane leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Measures hydrogen from bacterial fermentation of undigested lactose', 'The hydrogen breath test measures H2 produced by bacterial fermentation of undigested lactose in the colon. Elevated breath H2 confirms lactose malabsorption.'),
('7e7e9543-1738-50c9-8f02-58cff961e979'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Metabolic Disorders Pt2: Which coenzyme is derived from pantothenic acid?', '["TPP","PLP","NAD","Coenzyme A","FAD"]'::jsonb, 'Coenzyme A', 'Coenzyme A (CoA-SH) contains pantothenic acid (vitamin B5) and carries acyl groups via thioester bonds.'),
('1b8b431b-2f19-5878-b1cf-e19fe1e2dd2a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of Metabolic Disorders Pt2, Proofreading activity is associated with which enzyme?', '["Primase resulting in decreased functional activity","Helicase accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","DNA polymerase III (3''→5'' exonuclease)","Topoisomerase without affecting the primary tissue pathway","DNA ligase leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'DNA polymerase III (3''→5'' exonuclease)', 'DNA polymerase III has 3''→5'' exonuclease (proofreading) activity that removes misincorporated nucleotides.'),
('283f52cb-1cb4-5a7c-b069-a5ce7ad956d8'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'Iron deficiency anemia laboratory findings include:', '["Low transferrin saturation","Low ferritin","High TIBC","Low MCV","Low iron"]'::jsonb, 'Low ferritin', 'Iron deficiency anemia labs: low ferritin, low serum iron, low transferrin saturation (<20%), high TIBC, low MCV, low MCH, target cells.'),
('06381168-ebd6-5c01-b449-431f9d45f45b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'The first laboratory abnormality in iron deficiency is:', '["Low MCV resulting in decreased functional activity","High TIBC accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Low serum ferritin (depleted iron stores)","Low transferrin saturation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Low hemoglobin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Low serum ferritin (depleted iron stores)', 'The first abnormality is low serum ferritin (depleted stores). Serum iron and transferrin saturation drop next, then elevated TIBC. Anemia is late.'),
('3771a20a-61db-5be5-95c8-f728809bb221'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'Wernicke encephalopathy is most commonly caused by:', '["Niacin deficiency","Vitamin B6 deficiency","Folate deficiency","Vitamin B12 deficiency","Thiamine deficiency in alcoholism"]'::jsonb, 'Thiamine deficiency in alcoholism', 'Thiamine pyrophosphate (TPP) is a coenzyme for pyruvate dehydrogenase and alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase.'),
('e3c10989-754f-537e-99c9-a43cf33fd793'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Metabolic Disorders Pt2: Epinephrine promotes glycogenolysis in skeletal muscle by:', '["Increasing intracellular cGMP","Binding beta-adrenergic receptors and raising cAMP","Activating the insulin receptor","Stimulating glucose-6-phosphatase in muscle","Inhibiting adenylate cyclase"]'::jsonb, 'Binding beta-adrenergic receptors and raising cAMP', 'Epinephrine binds beta-2 adrenergic GPCRs (Gs) in muscle, activating adenylyl cyclase and PKA, which activates glycogen phosphorylase. Muscle releases lactate because it lacks glucose-6-phosphatase.'),
('a32c12e1-770b-562b-a5b7-737fa84b2873'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'Niacin deficiency can manifest in Hartnup disease because:', '["Niacin is not utilized resulting in decreased functional activity","Impaired tryptophan absorption in kidney/intestine reduces NAD synthesis","Vitamin B6 is deficient mediated by specific receptor activation","Niacin is not absorbed without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Folate is deficient leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Impaired tryptophan absorption in kidney/intestine reduces NAD synthesis', 'Hartnup disease: autosomal recessive defective neutral amino acid transporter (SLC6A9). Impaired tryptophan absorption reduces NAD synthesis, causing pellagra-like symptoms.'),
('6c3ab3a0-e7e0-582a-be9c-6a76a99613c6'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'A 65-year-old female presents with progressive abdominal distension, bone pain, and fatigue. Physical examination reveals massive splenomegaly and hepatomegaly. CBC shows pancytopenia. Bone marrow biopsy reveals lipid-laden macrophages resembling crumpled tissue paper. What is the deficient enzyme?', '["Alpha-galactosidase A","Hexosaminidase A","Glucocerebrosidase (beta-glucosidase)","Galactocerebrosidase","Sphingomyelinase"]'::jsonb, 'Glucocerebrosidase (beta-glucosidase)', 'This patient presents with Gaucher''s disease, the most common lysosomal storage disease. It is caused by glucocerebrosidase deficiency, leading to accumulation of glucocerebroside in macrophages (Gaucher cells), which classically resemble crumpled tissue paper.'),
('63c7bde1-0cc9-58b8-b764-f370c2a9e06e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'Biotin is involved in:', '["Carboxylation reactions (carries CO2)","Reduction accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Oxidation mediated by specific receptor activation","Decarboxylation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Transamination leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Carboxylation reactions (carries CO2)', 'Biotin carries CO2 in carboxylation reactions. Four biotin-dependent carboxylases in humans: pyruvate carboxylase, acetyl-CoA carboxylase, propionyl-CoA carboxylase, and MCC.'),
('dae80b82-6944-55a5-ba36-cfdc48f2ef42'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'Folate is especially abundant in:', '["Green leafy vegetables","Legumes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dairy mediated by specific receptor activation","Meat without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Liver leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Green leafy vegetables', 'Folate is abundant in green leafy vegetables, legumes, liver, and citrus fruits. Natural food folate is polyglutamated; folic acid (supplements) is monoglutamated and more bioavailable.'),
('eb4ac17e-94da-5f4c-9593-7c0f72a51f8e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Metabolic Disorders Pt2: The Km of an enzyme is affected by:', '["Non-competitive inhibitors","Enzyme concentration","Temperature only","PH only","Competitive inhibitors"]'::jsonb, 'Competitive inhibitors', 'Competitive inhibitors increase apparent Km (more substrate needed to reach half Vmax). Non-competitive inhibitors do not affect Km.'),
('7e5d1892-f39e-5cfd-8742-79fefcae4ab3'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'The treatment for pernicious anemia is:', '["Folate resulting in decreased functional activity","Oral B12 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dietary changes only mediated by specific receptor activation","Iron without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Parenteral (IM) vitamin B12 for life"]'::jsonb, 'Parenteral (IM) vitamin B12 for life', 'Pernicious anemia requires lifelong parenteral B12 replacement (IM injections) because the stomach cannot produce intrinsic factor needed for B12 absorption.'),
('68fa1ceb-188c-58f2-b68a-2b36d91f2990'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'Subacute combined degeneration of the cord affects:', '["Dorsal columns (vibration/proprioception loss) and corticospinal tracts (spasticity)","Spinothalamic tract accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cerebellum mediated by specific receptor activation","Basal ganglia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cranial nerves leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal columns (vibration/proprioception loss) and corticospinal tracts (spasticity)', 'SCD affects dorsal columns (vibration/proprioception loss -> sensory ataxia), lateral corticospinal tracts (spasticity), and peripheral nerves (neuropathy).'),
('ada7fa12-df72-53c3-91c9-aef582f37628'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'The richest dietary sources of vitamin C are:', '["Peppers","Kiwi","Tomatoes","Citrus fruits","Meat"]'::jsonb, 'Citrus fruits', 'Richest vitamin C sources: bell peppers, kiwi, citrus fruits, strawberries, broccoli, tomatoes. Heat-labile. Smokers need 35 mg/day more.'),
('72a9da63-f6e3-5bde-9bce-01ccb975f450'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Metabolic Disorders Pt2: The Michaelis constant (Km) is defined as the substrate concentration at which the reaction velocity is:', '["Half of Vmax","Vmax","Zero","Double Vmax","One-fourth Vmax"]'::jsonb, 'Half of Vmax', 'Km is the substrate concentration at half-maximal velocity. Low Km indicates high affinity; high Km indicates low affinity.'),
('5f800a29-6dc5-59d1-ab44-e78f5b89fc13'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'Vitamin A deficiency is the leading cause of:', '["Goiter resulting in decreased functional activity","Anemia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Neural tube defects mediated by specific receptor activation","Rickets without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Preventable childhood blindness"]'::jsonb, 'Preventable childhood blindness', 'Vitamin A deficiency is the leading cause of preventable childhood blindness. Causes xerophthalmia progression: night blindness -> Bitot spots -> keratomalacia -> blindness.'),
('05228989-e2c4-57a8-90a4-f174ee1ff795'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Regulation Disorders - Metabolic Disorders Part 2', 'Isotretinoin (13-cis-retinoic acid) is used for:', '["Wrinkles resulting in decreased functional activity","Acne (it is a vitamin A derivative)","Vitamin A deficiency mediated by specific receptor activation","Psoriasis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cancer leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Acne (it is a vitamin A derivative)', 'Isotretinoin (Accutane) is a vitamin A derivative for severe nodulocystic acne. Reduces sebaceous gland size. Highly teratogenic.'),
('2ce24e74-d8b6-5afe-8566-247eb776cf36'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Beer-Lambert law states that absorbance is:', '["Inversely proportional to concentration","Independent of path length","Exponentially related to concentration","Directly proportional to transmittance","Directly proportional to concentration and path length"]'::jsonb, 'Directly proportional to concentration and path length', 'Beer-Lambert law: A = εcl, where absorbance is directly proportional to concentration (c) and path length (l).'),
('8ad08bda-6515-5564-870c-e92f78ad0d0c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Ultraviolet-visible (UV-Vis) spectrophotometry is used to:', '["Separate compounds resulting in decreased functional activity","Sequence DNA accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Identify functional groups mediated by specific receptor activation","Quantify compounds that absorb UV or visible light","Determine molecular weight leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Quantify compounds that absorb UV or visible light', 'UV spectroscopy uses 200-400 nm wavelength range, where aromatic amino acids and nucleic acids absorb.'),
('3924dbed-7618-5ce2-ad39-fc6fd9b4984b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'In double-beam spectrophotometers:', '["Two light sources are used","Two monochromators are used","Two samples are measured","Two detectors are used","The light beam splits into sample and reference paths"]'::jsonb, 'The light beam splits into sample and reference paths', 'The light beam splits into sample and reference paths is a principle or component of spectrophotometric analysis for measuring light absorption.'),
('d39d8bfd-38b7-5299-9ce8-f6cc1ef5fd1c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Flame photometry is especially useful for:', '["Sodium","Iron","Calcium","Potassium","Lithium"]'::jsonb, 'Sodium', 'Flame photometry measures metal ions (Na+, K+, Ca2+, Li+) by their characteristic flame colors. Na+ emits yellow (589 nm), K+ emits violet (766 nm). Used for electrolyte measurement in clinical labs.'),
('69f51b95-54bd-54c3-82f2-e23a1b395c0b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Nephelometry measures:', '["Chemiluminescence resulting in decreased functional activity","Fluorescence emitted accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Light absorbed mediated by specific receptor activation","Light scattered by particles in solution at an angle","Light transmitted leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Light scattered by particles in solution at an angle', 'Nephelometry measures light scattered at an angle (usually 90 degrees) by immune complexes or particles in solution. Used for quantifying immunoglobulins, complement, and other analytes.'),
('bf352584-af4f-581e-b1de-dd506b6ed486'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Turbidimetry measures:', '["Fluorescence resulting in decreased functional activity","Decrease in transmitted light due to particle scattering","Refractive index mediated by specific receptor activation","Increase in scattered light without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Light absorption only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Decrease in transmitted light due to particle scattering', 'Turbidimetry measures the decrease in transmitted light due to particle scattering. Unlike nephelometry, it measures light passing through the sample rather than at an angle.'),
('eb1c5f64-1fc5-5568-906d-dd096b1811f6'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'An isosbestic point is:', '["The turbidity point resulting in decreased functional activity","The absorption maximum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The minimum absorption mediated by specific receptor activation","A wavelength where two species have equal absorbance","The fluorescence maximum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A wavelength where two species have equal absorbance', 'An isosbestic point is a wavelength where two interconverting species have equal molar absorptivity. Its presence indicates a clean two-species system without side reactions.'),
('7c5a37f3-ff05-5ac0-bb7c-14999c04e1da'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'The molar extinction coefficient (epsilon) has units of:', '["L/mol","M^-1 cm^-1","L mol^-1 cm^-1","M","Cm^-1"]'::jsonb, 'L mol^-1 cm^-1', 'Molar extinction coefficient (epsilon) has units of L per mol per cm (or M^-1 cm^-1). It quantifies how strongly a substance absorbs light at a given wavelength.'),
('f4b353aa-d1e7-5ee3-99bc-547a159d6bd4'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'The Trinder reaction uses:', '["Naphthol resulting in decreased functional activity","Bromocresol green accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","O-phenylenediamine mediated by specific receptor activation","Tetrazolium without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Phenol and 4-aminoantipyrine producing quinoneimine (red color)"]'::jsonb, 'Phenol and 4-aminoantipyrine producing quinoneimine (red color)', 'The Trinder reaction uses phenol (or a phenol derivative) and 4-aminoantipyrine in the presence of peroxidase (POD) and H2O2 to produce a red quinoneimine dye, measured at approximately 500 nm.'),
('f930cf62-c924-53ee-bef4-5214ca4ddf4a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Bromocresol green (BCG) method measures:', '["Cholesterol resulting in decreased functional activity","Glucose accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Uric acid mediated by specific receptor activation","Albumin (forms a green complex at pH 4.2)","Creatinine leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Albumin (forms a green complex at pH 4.2)', 'BCG binds to albumin at pH 4.2, forming a green-colored complex measured at approximately 630 nm. It is the most common dye-binding method for serum albumin.'),
('b633b7f3-6816-5be1-b04e-be74abd4fe74'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Jaffe reaction (alkaline picrate) is used for:', '["Protein measurement","Bilirubin measurement","Glucose measurement","Urea measurement","Creatinine measurement"]'::jsonb, 'Creatinine measurement', 'The Jaffe reaction involves creatinine reacting with alkaline picrate to form a red-orange complex measured at 510-520 nm. It is the classic method for creatinine measurement.'),
('7b993e47-d204-5a46-96c7-f07b137eb767'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'The Biuret method measures:', '["Creatinine resulting in decreased functional activity","Glucose accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Albumin mediated by specific receptor activation","Urea without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Total protein (peptide bonds form purple complex with Cu2+)"]'::jsonb, 'Total protein (peptide bonds form purple complex with Cu2+)', 'The Biuret method detects peptide bonds by their reaction with Cu2+ in alkaline solution, forming a purple complex measured at 540 nm. Used for total protein measurement.'),
('75a09d9b-39ce-567a-a646-604035f1c9cd'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Lowry method for protein is based on:', '["Kjeldahl method resulting in decreased functional activity","UV absorption at 280 nm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Biuret reaction followed by Folin-Ciocalteu reagent reduction","Only Biuret reaction without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bradford dye binding leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Biuret reaction followed by Folin-Ciocalteu reagent reduction', 'The Lowry method combines the Biuret reaction (Cu2+-peptide complex) with reduction of Folin-Ciocalteu reagent by aromatic amino acids and the Cu+ generated, producing a blue color measured at 750 nm.'),
('f282ebbe-ac7d-51c3-ba8f-584c26a0f279'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'The principle of a pH electrode is based on:', '["Conductivity measurement resulting in decreased functional activity","Spectrophotometry accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Coulometry mediated by specific receptor activation","Amperometric measurement without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Potentiometric measurement of H+ activity across a glass membrane"]'::jsonb, 'Potentiometric measurement of H+ activity across a glass membrane', 'The principle involves potentiometric measurement of h+ activity across a glass membrane, which is the basis for this technique or process.'),
('9311f431-a1d0-5b5c-98d9-f2afaf3f2860'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'The Clark oxygen electrode measures:', '["PH resulting in decreased functional activity","Ions accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","O2 by amperometric reduction at a platinum cathode","Glucose without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CO2 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'O2 by amperometric reduction at a platinum cathode', 'The Clark electrode measures pO2 by amperometric reduction of O2 at a platinum cathode, generating a current proportional to oxygen concentration.'),
('b8c95fa0-e850-5fb1-a7f6-5ccd49776846'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Enzyme multiplied immunoassay technique (EMIT) is:', '["A nephelometric assay resulting in decreased functional activity","A homogeneous immunoassay using enzyme-labeled drug and antibody","A heterogeneous immunoassay mediated by specific receptor activation","A chromatographic assay without affecting the primary tissue pathway","An electrophoretic assay leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A homogeneous immunoassay using enzyme-labeled drug and antibody', 'EMIT is a homogeneous enzyme immunoassay where enzyme-labeled drug competes with patient drug for antibody binding. Antibody binding inhibits enzyme activity, so signal is inversely proportional to drug concentration.'),
('24d4c234-5035-529e-ad57-81a3547af68b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'ELISA is an example of:', '["Heterogeneous enzyme immunoassay (bound/free separation required)","Turbidimetric assay accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Nephelometric assay mediated by specific receptor activation","Fluorescence assay without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Homogeneous immunoassay leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Heterogeneous enzyme immunoassay (bound/free separation required)', 'ELISA is a heterogeneous enzyme immunoassay requiring separation of bound and free fractions. Enzyme-labeled antibody produces a colorimetric signal proportional to analyte concentration.'),
('0f28619d-44db-5815-8eca-005810e23d9f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Direct ELISA uses:', '["An unlabeled primary and labeled secondary resulting in decreased functional activity","An enzyme-labeled primary antibody to detect antigen directly","A competitive format mediated by specific receptor activation","A capture antibody without affecting the primary tissue pathway","An enzyme-labeled antigen leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An enzyme-labeled primary antibody to detect antigen directly', 'Direct ELISA uses an enzyme-labeled primary antibody that binds directly to the antigen immobilized on the solid phase. No secondary antibody is needed.'),
('08112617-9bcf-509a-8560-d36378ea2d55'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Radioimmunoassay (RIA) was developed by:', '["Rosalyn Yalow and Solomon Berson","Kohler and Milstein","Porter and Edelman","Pauling","Sanger"]'::jsonb, 'Rosalyn Yalow and Solomon Berson', 'RIA was developed by Rosalyn Yalow and Solomon Berson in the 1950s, using radiolabeled antigens competing with unlabeled antigens for limited antibody binding sites.'),
('47e4ba36-2d70-5381-b6be-13be7f1364ad'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'RIA offers high:', '["Speed resulting in decreased functional activity","Simplicity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Specificity only mediated by specific receptor activation","Sensitivity (detecting pg/mL or lower concentrations)","Safety leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sensitivity (detecting pg/mL or lower concentrations)', 'RIA uses radiolabeled antigens competing with unlabeled antigens for antibody binding. Very sensitive but requires radioactive materials. Largely replaced by ELISA and mass spectrometry.'),
('27e09e1a-69aa-5251-a8ac-889d707f5317'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Disadvantages of RIA include:', '["Low specificity only","Regulatory burden","All: radioactive waste","Short shelf-life of isotopes","Low sensitivity only"]'::jsonb, 'All: radioactive waste', 'RIA uses radiolabeled antigens competing with unlabeled antigens for antibody binding. Very sensitive but requires radioactive materials. Largely replaced by ELISA and mass spectrometry.'),
('a590fd81-0470-52dc-83bd-db3aeee5ea1c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'IRMA (immunoradiometric assay) uses:', '["Excess radiolabeled antibody (not antigen)","Competitive format","Solid phase antigen","Excess radiolabeled antigen","Limited antibody"]'::jsonb, 'Excess radiolabeled antibody (not antigen)', 'IRMA uses excess radiolabeled antibodies (not antigen) in a non-competitive format. Two-site IRMA uses capture antibody on solid phase and labeled detection antibody, giving higher sensitivity than RIA.'),
('58357cbc-df3b-5fe4-b6d9-65514730556d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'TMB (tetramethylbenzidine) substrate for HRP produces:', '["A blue color (converted to yellow with stop solution)","A red color accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A colorless product mediated by specific receptor activation","A green color without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A purple color leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A blue color (converted to yellow with stop solution)', 'TMB is oxidized by HRP in the presence of H2O2 to produce a blue color (absorbance at 370/652 nm). Addition of sulfuric acid stops the reaction and turns it yellow (450 nm).'),
('433a57ed-3039-5ec9-9f31-4286342ea826'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'P-Nitrophenyl phosphate (pNPP) is a substrate for:', '["Peroxidase resulting in decreased functional activity","Glucose oxidase accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Alkaline phosphatase (produces yellow p-nitrophenol)","Beta-galactosidase without affecting the primary tissue pathway","HRP leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Alkaline phosphatase (produces yellow p-nitrophenol)', 'pNPP is hydrolyzed by alkaline phosphatase to produce p-nitrophenol, a yellow product measurable at 405 nm. Used as a chromogenic substrate in ALP-based ELISA systems.'),
('d969578e-e64c-5a09-8071-1ddce9657a1f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'O-phenylenediamine (OPD) is a substrate for:', '["Glucose oxidase","Beta-galactosidase","Alkaline phosphatase","Urease","HRP (produces orange-brown color)"]'::jsonb, 'HRP (produces orange-brown color)', 'OPD is oxidized by HRP/H2O2 to produce an orange-brown color measurable at 492 nm. It is one of the classic chromogenic substrates for HRP.'),
('cdea2d19-5e25-5db5-9b01-0431c44027b1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'ABTS is a chromogenic substrate for:', '["ALP","Glucose oxidase","HRP (produces green color)","Peroxidase","Beta-galactosidase"]'::jsonb, 'HRP (produces green color)', 'ABTS is oxidized by HRP/H2O2 to produce a green soluble product measurable at 405-415 nm. It is used as a chromogenic substrate in ELISA.'),
('e10dbede-d8e0-550b-89fd-c14a43bad9c2'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Indirect ELISA detects:', '["Cytokines","Haptens","Antigens in patient serum","Antibodies in patient serum","Hormones"]'::jsonb, 'Antibodies in patient serum', 'Indirect ELISA detects antibodies in patient serum by first coating the plate with antigen, then adding patient serum, followed by enzyme-labeled anti-human IgG. Signal is proportional to antibody concentration.'),
('468bd61a-d61a-5688-9bc0-f41ceb3ed687'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Western blotting combines:', '["PCR and electrophoresis resulting in decreased functional activity","Electrophoresis (SDS-PAGE) and immunoassay (antibody detection)","Chromatography and electrophoresis mediated by specific receptor activation","Southern blotting without affecting the primary tissue pathway","ELISA and PCR leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Electrophoresis (SDS-PAGE) and immunoassay (antibody detection)', 'Electrophoresis separates molecules based on size and charge by migration through a gel matrix under electric field.'),
('c4f81a8e-da80-56fa-aafd-09e5f2ef8b01'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'The advantage of CLIA over ELISA is:', '["Simplicity resulting in decreased functional activity","Equipment needed accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Higher sensitivity and wider dynamic range","Longer shelf-life without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lower cost leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Higher sensitivity and wider dynamic range', 'CLIA offers higher sensitivity (down to femtogram levels), wider dynamic range, and longer reagent shelf-life compared to ELISA. It uses chemical energy rather than enzymes to generate light.'),
('89026606-7db3-5648-80d9-fd3e85ddb7df'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Electrochemiluminescence (ECL) generates light:', '["In a gel resulting in decreased functional activity","On a membrane accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","In solution mediated by specific receptor activation","In a cuvette without affecting the primary tissue pathway","At an electrode surface (as in Roche Elecsys systems)"]'::jsonb, 'At an electrode surface (as in Roche Elecsys systems)', 'ECL generates light at an electrode surface where ruthenium tris(bipyridyl) is oxidized and reacts with tripropylamine, producing an excited state that emits light at 620 nm.'),
('bd96d4b2-c970-541b-b053-6663e2795e1b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Acridinium esters are used in:', '["Turbidimetry resulting in decreased functional activity","Chemiluminescence immunoassays","RIA mediated by specific receptor activation","Fluorescence assays without affecting the primary tissue pathway","ELISA leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Chemiluminescence immunoassays', 'Acridinium esters are used as labels in chemiluminescence immunoassays. They produce light when oxidized with H2O2 in alkaline solution, without need for an enzyme or external light source.'),
('5d234072-670e-595e-8e52-dbbc5b3a04be'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'The pregnancy test detects:', '["Estrogen resulting in decreased functional activity","LH accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Progesterone mediated by specific receptor activation","HCG (human chorionic gonadotropin) in urine","FSH leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'HCG (human chorionic gonadotropin) in urine', 'Pregnancy tests detect HCG in urine using immunochromatographic lateral flow assays with anti-HCG antibodies. HCG is produced by trophoblastic tissue after implantation.'),
('afc32dff-c420-5baf-b4b5-ab7998627aea'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Agglutination reactions are based on:', '["Color change resulting in decreased functional activity","Visible clumping when antibody cross-links particulate antigens","Light scattering mediated by specific receptor activation","Fluorescence without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Precipitation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Visible clumping when antibody cross-links particulate antigens', 'Agglutination reactions involve antibodies cross-linking particulate antigens (RBCs, latex particles) to form visible clumps. Used in blood typing, Widal test, and CRP measurement.'),
('fa5b9dde-f29f-57a1-8eab-3b6c358fa377'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Immunoelectrophoresis combines:', '["Electrophoresis followed by immunodiffusion for antigen identification","PCR accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Electrophoresis and chromatography mediated by specific receptor activation","Chromatography and ELISA without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Western blotting leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Electrophoresis followed by immunodiffusion for antigen identification', 'Electrophoresis separates molecules based on size and charge by migration through a gel matrix under electric field.'),
('f4171d16-562c-52d4-acfd-403d1015116f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Rocket immunoelectrophoresis (Laurell) is:', '["Immunofixation resulting in decreased functional activity","Radial immunodiffusion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Capillary electrophoresis mediated by specific receptor activation","Electrophoresis of antigen into antibody-containing gel forming rocket-shaped precipitin","Double immunodiffusion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Electrophoresis of antigen into antibody-containing gel forming rocket-shaped precipitin', 'Electrophoresis separates molecules based on size and charge by migration through a gel matrix under electric field.'),
('7628b7f6-5632-5de1-84c2-351585a3efd4'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Countercurrent immunoelectrophoresis uses:', '["Electric field to force antigen and antibody toward each other (faster than diffusion)","Centrifugation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Simple diffusion mediated by specific receptor activation","Radial diffusion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Capillary action leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Electric field to force antigen and antibody toward each other (faster than diffusion)', 'Countercurrent immunoelectrophoresis uses an electric field to drive antigen and antibody toward each other simultaneously from opposite wells, forming precipitin lines faster than double diffusion.'),
('657ae04c-5d45-576a-a26d-5256d1e95cca'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Ouchterlony double immunodiffusion detects:', '["Partial identity resulting in decreased functional activity","Antigen-antibody reactions with patterns showing identity","Quantitative antigen levels mediated by specific receptor activation","Or non-identity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Antibody titers leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Antigen-antibody reactions with patterns showing identity', 'Ouchterlony double diffusion detects antigen-antibody reactions. Patterns show identity (fused arcs), non-identity (crossing lines), or partial identity (spurs) between adjacent antigen wells.'),
('470597f1-b4de-5b60-92a8-51ec8eab1219'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'The Ouchterlony pattern of identity shows:', '["Parallel lines resulting in decreased functional activity","Lines accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Crossing of lines mediated by specific receptor activation","Fusion of precipitin lines (arcs) between adjacent wells","Spur formation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fusion of precipitin lines (arcs) between adjacent wells', 'In the pattern of identity, precipitin lines from adjacent wells fuse into smooth arcs, indicating the antigens in both wells are immunologically identical.'),
('6952fff8-4dcb-563d-bd41-ed58f2822dec'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'The Ouchterlony pattern of non-identity shows:', '["Spur formation","Crossing of precipitin lines","Parallel lines","Fusion of lines","Lines"]'::jsonb, 'Crossing of precipitin lines', 'In the pattern of non-identity, precipitin lines from adjacent wells cross each other, indicating the antigens are completely different and share no common epitopes.'),
('d12c4250-8e03-594f-8f71-d489b45a7102'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'The Ouchterlony pattern of partial identity shows:', '["Spur resulting in decreased functional activity","Crossing lines accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A spur (one line continues beyond the intersection)","Double lines without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fused lines leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A spur (one line continues beyond the intersection)', 'In the pattern of partial identity, a spur forms when one antigen has epitopes not shared by the other, indicating partial immunological similarity.'),
('576ffc48-01bf-527f-879d-c683123042b0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Immunonephelometry measures:', '["Light absorbed resulting in decreased functional activity","Fluorescence accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Light transmitted mediated by specific receptor activation","Light scattered at an angle by immune complexes","Chemiluminescence leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Light scattered at an angle by immune complexes', 'Immunonephelometry measures light scattered at an angle (typically 90 degrees) by immune complexes formed in solution. Used for quantifying immunoglobulins, complement, and specific proteins.'),
('85404d9d-e080-5bc9-9247-ca979add2f7c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Rate nephelometry measures:', '["End-point scatter resulting in decreased functional activity","Integrated scatter accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Peak rate of complex formation (change in scatter over time)","Equilibrium scatter without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Initial scatter leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Peak rate of complex formation (change in scatter over time)', 'Rate nephelometry measures the rate of increase in scattered light during antigen-antibody complex formation, providing results faster than end-point nephelometry.'),
('06078f68-2370-51f2-a4d6-0f9775688d9d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Antigen excess (prozone effect) causes:', '["No significant change resulting in decreased functional activity","Precipitation only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Falsely high results mediated by specific receptor activation","Accurate results without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Falsely low results in nephelometry/turbidimetry (soluble complexes)"]'::jsonb, 'Falsely low results in nephelometry/turbidimetry (soluble complexes)', 'In antigen excess (prozone), soluble immune complexes form instead of large precipitating complexes, causing falsely low or absent signal in nephelometry/turbidimetry.'),
('ee9f0f77-63dc-5009-958f-a73bc514d14d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'The hook effect can be avoided by:', '["Sample dilution","Two-step assay","Lowering temperature","Increasing incubation time","Or using excess antibodies"]'::jsonb, 'Sample dilution', 'The hook effect (prozone) can be avoided by sample dilution, which reduces antigen concentration into the optimal range for immune complex formation and signal generation.'),
('8d6076f7-2c90-53bf-b501-1b7f91a021b9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Surface plasmon resonance (SPR) measures:', '["Absorbance resulting in decreased functional activity","Radioactivity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Enzyme activity mediated by specific receptor activation","Real-time biomolecular interactions without labels","Fluorescence leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Real-time biomolecular interactions without labels', 'SPR measures real-time biomolecular interactions without labels by detecting changes in refractive index at a gold surface when molecules bind. Used for kinetic analysis of protein-protein and protein-DNA interactions.'),
('b06911a6-a6f4-5a02-8cab-bc7e59d1b633'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Biosensors combine:', '["Antibodies and fluorescence resulting in decreased functional activity","All of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Enzymes and electrodes mediated by specific receptor activation","A biological recognition element with a physicochemical transducer","DNA and electrochemistry leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A biological recognition element with a physicochemical transducer', 'Biosensors combine a biological recognition element (enzyme, antibody, nucleic acid, cell) with a physicochemical transducer (electrochemical, optical, piezoelectric) to detect analytes.'),
('fa7c26e7-3997-5231-bf49-6b36f3a39ccd'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Electrophoresis is the movement of charged particles in:', '["An electric field (toward opposite charge electrode)","A thermal gradient accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A pressure gradient mediated by specific receptor activation","A magnetic field without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A gravitational field leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An electric field (toward opposite charge electrode)', 'Electrophoresis is the movement of charged particles in an electric field toward the electrode of opposite charge. Migration rate depends on charge, size, and shape of the molecule.'),
('604dd147-32bf-5352-9b85-46f869b413ec'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'SDS-PAGE denatures proteins and gives them:', '["Fixed charge resulting in decreased functional activity","Variable charge accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Uniform positive charge mediated by specific receptor activation","Uniform negative charge proportional to mass","Charge leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Uniform negative charge proportional to mass', 'SDS denatures proteins and coats them with uniform negative charge proportional to their mass, eliminating charge and shape variables so separation depends only on molecular weight.'),
('6d8b2950-3fb2-57ac-a693-bd89f1e504c9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'The reducing agent used in SDS-PAGE to break disulfide bonds is:', '["Beta-mercaptoethanol (BME) or dithiothreitol (DTT)","Urea accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","TEMED mediated by specific receptor activation","APS without affecting the primary tissue pathway","SDS leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Beta-mercaptoethanol (BME) or dithiothreitol (DTT)', 'BME or DTT are reducing agents that break disulfide bonds in proteins during SDS-PAGE sample preparation, ensuring complete denaturation for accurate molecular weight determination.'),
('37eb0d97-21fd-5b1c-83f4-1f653a3c753d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Agarose gel electrophoresis is used for:', '["Protein separation (native)","DNA/RNA separation (nucleic acids)","Isoenzyme analysis","Lipoprotein separation","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'DNA/RNA separation (nucleic acids)', 'Agarose gel electrophoresis is primarily used for separating DNA and RNA fragments by size. Agarose forms a porous matrix through which nucleic acids migrate toward the positive electrode.'),
('8590c1af-4e8a-508f-9269-e470d1f465be'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Capillary electrophoresis (CE) uses:', '["Narrow capillary tubes with high voltage for rapid separation","Thin-layer plates accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Slab gels mediated by specific receptor activation","Wide tubes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Paper support leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Narrow capillary tubes with high voltage for rapid separation', 'Capillary electrophoresis uses narrow-bore capillaries (25-75 um ID) with high voltage (10-30 kV) for rapid, high-resolution separation with minimal sample and reagent consumption.'),
('6aa1758b-3d27-5ccc-bf17-d305c2b29ea2'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'The advantages of capillary electrophoresis include:', '["All: speed","High resolution","Speed only","Automation","Small sample volume"]'::jsonb, 'All: speed', 'CE advantages include rapid analysis (minutes), high resolution, small sample volume (nL), automation, and quantitative capability.'),
('c9724aba-9a76-5047-bea7-971bd82aed06'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'The pH gradient in IEF is established by:', '["Ampholytes (amphoteric molecules with various pI values)","Buffer accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Salt mediated by specific receptor activation","Acrylamide without affecting the primary tissue pathway","SDS leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ampholytes (amphoteric molecules with various pI values)', 'The pH gradient in IEF is established by carrier ampholytes -- small amphoteric molecules with various pI values that migrate to their isoelectric positions under electric field.'),
('4b5f91a8-8391-5a37-bcc3-de70a767f23e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', '2D electrophoresis can resolve:', '["Thousands of proteins from a complex sample","Only large proteins","Up to 100 proteins","Only basic proteins","Only acidic proteins"]'::jsonb, 'Thousands of proteins from a complex sample', 'Two-dimensional electrophoresis separates proteins by pI (first dimension) then by molecular weight (second dimension). Can resolve thousands of proteins simultaneously. Used in proteomics.'),
('cb57d190-ce63-5cc3-a0b4-9807537e919d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Western blot after 2D electrophoresis is called:', '["2D Western blot or immunoblot","Southwestern blot","Southern blot","Far Western blot","Northern blot"]'::jsonb, '2D Western blot or immunoblot', 'Western blotting detects specific proteins by SDS-PAGE, transfer to membrane, and antibody-based detection.'),
('ce316299-983a-5381-bb69-4d678a37aceb'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Serum protein electrophoresis (SPE) by agarose gel separates into:', '["Alpha-2","Gamma globulins","Alpha-1","Beta","Albumin"]'::jsonb, 'Albumin', 'Protein electrophoresis separates proteins by charge and size. Serum protein electrophoresis shows albumin, alpha-1, alpha-2, beta, and gamma globulin bands. Abnormal patterns indicate disease.'),
('0b34ee24-8d29-55a3-a336-3222f0bcf64b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'The normal serum albumin peak is:', '["Near the anode","Large","Broad","Small","Homogeneous"]'::jsonb, 'Large', 'The normal serum albumin peak is the largest and most prominent band on SPE, located near the anode (positive end), representing 3.5-5.0 g/dL.'),
('6fdfb1b9-82b4-5369-bc15-28672f85b327'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Monoclonal gammopathy (multiple myeloma) appears as:', '["Narrow band (M-spike) in the gamma region","A sharp","Broad increase in gamma globulins","Beta-gamma bridging","Albumin spike"]'::jsonb, 'A sharp', 'In multiple myeloma, monoclonal gammopathy appears as a narrow, sharp band (M-spike) in the gamma (or beta) region on SPE, representing a single clone of plasma cells producing identical immunoglobulin.'),
('203298fc-4523-5314-96c4-7c61f47c8f0c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Polyclonal hypergammaglobulinemia appears as:', '["Broad increase in the gamma region (diffuse)","Normal gamma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A sharp spike mediated by specific receptor activation","Beta-gamma fusion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hypogammaglobulinemia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Broad increase in the gamma region (diffuse)', 'Polyclonal hypergammaglobulinemia appears as a broad, diffuse increase in the gamma region on SPE, reflecting polyclonal B-cell activation producing diverse immunoglobulins (seen in chronic infections, liver disease, autoimmune conditions).'),
('e3bdf8e8-426c-5e46-ad48-39929a5438a9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Beta-gamma bridging on SPE suggests:', '["Multiple myeloma resulting in decreased functional activity","Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Acute inflammation mediated by specific receptor activation","Cirrhosis (IgA increase bridging beta and gamma)","Nephrotic syndrome leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cirrhosis (IgA increase bridging beta and gamma)', 'Beta-gamma bridging on SPE (fusion of beta and gamma bands) is characteristic of cirrhosis, caused by increased IgA and decreased beta-lipoproteins.'),
('8e0b9e20-e936-51b8-ba93-b42099ce3ef9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'HbA1c is measured by:', '["Capillary electrophoresis","HPLC only","All: HPLC","Immunoassay","Immunoassay only"]'::jsonb, 'All: HPLC', 'HbA1c can be measured by HPLC (most common reference method), capillary electrophoresis, immunoassay, or enzymatic methods. HPLC separates hemoglobin variants based on charge.'),
('b26e39b4-c55c-5032-98f4-b9816c62e512'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Lipoprotein (a) [Lp(a)] is:', '["A triglyceride-rich lipoprotein resulting in decreased functional activity","An LDL-like particle with an additional apo(a) protein","A form of HDL mediated by specific receptor activation","A chylomicron without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A fatty acid-binding protein leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An LDL-like particle with an additional apo(a) protein', 'Lp(a) is an LDL-like particle with an additional apo(a) protein covalently linked to apoB-100. Elevated Lp(a) is an independent risk factor for atherosclerosis and thrombosis.'),
('c04e397c-ec46-55e2-a2a8-309c766a101b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Alkaline phosphatase isoenzymes can be separated by:', '["Only immunoassay","Only electrophoresis","Only heat stability","Only chemical inhibition","Electrophoresis and heat stability"]'::jsonb, 'Electrophoresis and heat stability', 'Alkaline phosphatase isoenzymes (bone, liver, intestine, placental) can be separated by electrophoresis and distinguished by heat stability and chemical inhibition patterns.'),
('74fb23f3-2234-58e7-87f1-4bd125dd9cac'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Hemoglobin electrophoresis at alkaline pH (pH 8.6):', '["HbA2","HbC migrate differently","HbS","HbA","HbF"]'::jsonb, 'HbA', 'At alkaline pH (8.6), hemoglobin electrophoresis separates Hb variants by charge: HbA migrates fastest toward anode, followed by HbA2, HbF, HbS, and HbC.'),
('205c1513-86d6-5dff-9874-0dd089043582'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Hemoglobin electrophoresis in beta-thalassemia major shows:', '["Elevated HbA2","Elevated HbF only","Elevated HbF","Reduced or absent HbA","Elevated HbA2 only"]'::jsonb, 'Elevated HbF', 'In beta-thalassemia major, there is reduced or absent HbA (HbA <5%) and markedly elevated HbF (60-90%) due to failed beta-globin chain production.'),
('32f0ce8e-b002-55b4-a260-c01401c38e35'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'HbA2 is elevated (3.5-7%) in:', '["Normal individuals","Beta-thalassemia trait (minor)","HbC trait","Alpha-thalassemia","Sickle cell trait"]'::jsonb, 'Beta-thalassemia trait (minor)', 'HbA2 is elevated (3.5-7%) in beta-thalassemia trait, making it the key diagnostic marker. Normal HbA2 is 2-3.5%.'),
('3ffd5039-237c-5e1b-91f7-c68e4990fa8f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) electrophoresis shows:', '["Albumin spike resulting in decreased functional activity","Normal pattern accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Polyclonal increase mediated by specific receptor activation","M-spike without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Oligoclonal bands in multiple sclerosis"]'::jsonb, 'Oligoclonal bands in multiple sclerosis', 'CSF analysis assesses CNS infections, hemorrhage, and malignancy. Normal: clear, glucose 50-80 mg/dL, protein 15-45 mg/dL, <5 WBC/mm3. Bacterial meningitis shows high WBC, high protein, low glucose.'),
('9e49e02f-c51b-57e5-8306-4e8759c39c34'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Urine protein electrophoresis is used for:', '["Serum protein quantitation resulting in decreased functional activity","Enzyme analysis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bence Jones protein (free light chains) detection","Hemoglobin analysis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lipoprotein analysis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bence Jones protein (free light chains) detection', 'Urine protein electrophoresis is used to detect Bence Jones protein (free immunoglobulin light chains), which migrate as a narrow band in the beta-gamma region. Seen in multiple myeloma and light chain disease.'),
('b5c2fc25-9946-581c-9f3c-20229d79c345'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'The buffer in electrophoresis:', '["Maintains pH and conducts electric current","Precipitates proteins","Crosslinks proteins","Denatures proteins","Stains the gel"]'::jsonb, 'Maintains pH and conducts electric current', 'The buffer in electrophoresis serves two purposes: maintaining pH to preserve molecular charge and conducting electric current through the system.'),
('5cb8498d-1d7b-58ca-aa5c-31f85e273df4'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Pulsed-field gel electrophoresis (PFGE) separates:', '["Proteins resulting in decreased functional activity","RNA accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Small DNA fragments mediated by specific receptor activation","Lipids without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Very large DNA molecules (entire chromosomes/bacterial genomes)"]'::jsonb, 'Very large DNA molecules (entire chromosomes/bacterial genomes)', 'PFGE separates very large DNA molecules (10 kb to 10 Mb) by periodically changing the direction of the electric field, allowing resolution of entire chromosomes and bacterial genomes.'),
('acb291fe-257b-50d6-b771-c849aed01d77'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Denaturing gradient gel electrophoresis (DGGE) separates:', '["RNA resulting in decreased functional activity","Proteins accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lipids mediated by specific receptor activation","DNA fragments of same size but different sequence (melting behavior)","Carbohydrates leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'DNA fragments of same size but different sequence (melting behavior)', 'DGGE separates DNA fragments of the same size but different sequence based on their melting behavior in a chemical denaturant gradient. Sequence differences cause different melting positions.'),
('a97c1087-e99f-5db0-ae7f-a9673edf044b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Restriction fragment length polymorphism (RFLP) analysis uses:', '["DNA sequencing resulting in decreased functional activity","Restriction enzyme digestion followed by gel electrophoresis","PCR alone mediated by specific receptor activation","All of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Southern blotting leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Restriction enzyme digestion followed by gel electrophoresis', 'Type II restriction enzymes cut DNA at specific palindromic sequences, producing either sticky or blunt ends.'),
('24cd1699-b720-5441-ba70-d482b8a5fd64'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Which technique amplifies specific DNA sequences?', '["Microarray resulting in decreased functional activity","Southern blot accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","RFLP mediated by specific receptor activation","Sequencing without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Multiplex PCR of tetranucleotide repeats followed by capillary electrophoresis"]'::jsonb, 'Multiplex PCR of tetranucleotide repeats followed by capillary electrophoresis', 'STR (short tandem repeat) profiling uses multiplex PCR of tetranucleotide repeats followed by capillary electrophoresis for DNA fingerprinting and forensic identification.'),
('d9ba8c2b-6e09-51f4-a3c0-e21a5aeacd10'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Chromosome analysis (karyotyping) uses:', '["Condensed chromosomes","Light microscopy of stained","PCR","Sequencing","Electrophoresis"]'::jsonb, 'Light microscopy of stained', 'Chromosome analysis (karyotyping) uses light microscopy of stained metaphase chromosomes to detect numerical (aneuploidy) and structural (translocations, deletions) abnormalities. Resolution approximately 5-10 Mb.'),
('73d03138-67de-5ff0-8d03-8784db65742c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Regarding clinical cases in Diagnostic Techniques Pt1: Primary site of first-pass metabolism:', '["Liver","Kidney","Lung","Intestine","Spleen"]'::jsonb, 'Liver', 'Orally absorbed drugs enter portal circulation and are metabolised by the liver first.'),
('b32d601e-92f2-51fc-a43e-f3b4780503df'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Comparative genomic hybridization (CGH) detects:', '["Chromosomal translocations only","Gene expression levels","Protein levels","DNA copy number changes across the entire genome","Specific point mutations"]'::jsonb, 'DNA copy number changes across the entire genome', 'CGH detects DNA copy number changes (gains/losses) across the entire genome by competitive hybridization of test and reference DNA to metaphase chromosomes or DNA arrays.'),
('63cd1a10-2392-55b4-bec6-4c29bdb3201a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Array CGH uses:', '["Southern blot resulting in decreased functional activity","Metaphase chromosomes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","DNA microarrays to measure copy number with higher resolution","FISH without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Electrophoresis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'DNA microarrays to measure copy number with higher resolution', 'Array CGH uses DNA microarrays instead of metaphase chromosomes, achieving higher resolution (down to kb) for detecting copy number variations across the genome.'),
('ec94ac1a-4e51-5b82-b1b3-6ae25e009282'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'The resolution of array CGH is:', '["Lower than conventional CGH resulting in decreased functional activity","Lower than FISH accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Same as karyotyping mediated by specific receptor activation","Same as FISH without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Higher than conventional CGH (down to kb resolution)"]'::jsonb, 'Higher than conventional CGH (down to kb resolution)', 'Array CGH resolution can reach <100 kb depending on probe density, compared to 5-10 Mb for conventional CGH using metaphase chromosomes.'),
('c48e24ab-5174-5a4a-82d8-3ed410dc3f29'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Southern blotting detects:', '["Specific RNA sequences","Specific carbohydrates","Specific DNA sequences in a sample","Specific lipids","Specific proteins"]'::jsonb, 'Specific DNA sequences in a sample', 'Southern blotting detects specific DNA sequences by gel electrophoresis, transfer to membrane, and hybridization with a labeled probe. Used for gene rearrangements, RFLP analysis, and DNA methylation studies.'),
('1f468302-f97e-570e-9415-27cf0826a28f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Northern blotting detects:', '["Specific DNA sequences","Carbohydrates","Specific RNA sequences","Proteins","Lipids"]'::jsonb, 'Specific RNA sequences', 'Northern blotting detects specific RNA sequences by gel electrophoresis, transfer to membrane, and hybridization with a labeled probe. Used to study gene expression and RNA processing.'),
('155051de-08a5-58d1-bef0-7a50477b0514'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Northwestern blotting detects:', '["RNA-protein interactions","Protein-protein","Lipid-protein","DNA-protein interactions","Carbohydrate-protein"]'::jsonb, 'RNA-protein interactions', 'Northwestern blotting detects RNA-protein interactions by separating proteins by SDS-PAGE, transferring to membrane, then probing with labeled RNA to identify RNA-binding proteins.'),
('c40cfd56-d173-5db1-ae1b-f37c08997f56'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Southwestern blotting detects:', '["Carbohydrate-protein resulting in decreased functional activity","Protein-protein accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lipid-protein mediated by specific receptor activation","RNA-protein without affecting the primary tissue pathway","DNA-binding proteins using labeled DNA probes"]'::jsonb, 'DNA-binding proteins using labeled DNA probes', 'Southwestern blotting detects DNA-binding proteins by separating proteins by SDS-PAGE, transferring to membrane, then probing with labeled DNA to identify transcription factors and other DNA-binding proteins.'),
('aebffd4e-44f8-5a2b-ad1d-e4d6446ed592'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'DNA microarrays (gene chips) measure:', '["Gene expression levels (thousands of genes simultaneously)","Lipid levels accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Metabolite levels mediated by specific receptor activation","Protein levels without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Enzyme activity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Gene expression levels (thousands of genes simultaneously)', 'DNA microarrays measure gene expression levels of thousands of genes simultaneously by hybridizing labeled cDNA (from mRNA) to complementary probes arrayed on a solid support.'),
('709680e6-7b68-5704-86a8-b99e885bee54'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'The principle of DNA microarray is:', '["Antibody binding resulting in decreased functional activity","Charge binding accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Size separation mediated by specific receptor activation","Complementary base pairing (hybridization) of labeled cDNA to arrayed probes","Enzymatic reaction leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Complementary base pairing (hybridization) of labeled cDNA to arrayed probes', 'The principle of DNA microarray is complementary base pairing (hybridization) of labeled cDNA to arrayed oligonucleotide or cDNA probes, with signal intensity proportional to transcript abundance.'),
('ffee2dbb-2e64-5180-b228-1d8d42580441'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Comparative genomic hybridization (CGH) is used for:', '["Protein identification resulting in decreased functional activity","Detecting chromosomal copy number changes (gains/losses)","Metabolite profiling mediated by specific receptor activation","Gene expression without affecting the primary tissue pathway","DNA sequencing leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Detecting chromosomal copy number changes (gains/losses)', 'CGH is used for detecting chromosomal copy number changes (gains and losses) across the entire genome without requiring cell culture, useful for tumor genome profiling.'),
('51cab272-ffb0-539b-9314-bfa83f84f275'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'The resolution of conventional CGH is:', '["10 kb resulting in decreased functional activity","100 kb accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","1 Mb mediated by specific receptor activation","<1 kb without affecting the primary tissue pathway","5-10 Mb (limited by metaphase chromosome banding)"]'::jsonb, '5-10 Mb (limited by metaphase chromosome banding)', 'Conventional CGH resolution is limited to 5-10 Mb by the banding resolution of metaphase chromosomes, unable to detect small deletions or duplications.'),
('36f57ec3-183b-51ac-bb67-c4023de19896'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Array CGH resolution can reach:', '["5 Mb resulting in decreased functional activity","10 Mb accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","1 Mb mediated by specific receptor activation","1 mm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","<100 kb (depending on probe density)"]'::jsonb, '<100 kb (depending on probe density)', 'Array CGH resolution can reach <100 kb depending on probe density, a 50-100 fold improvement over conventional CGH.'),
('bf455d82-adbd-590c-82c9-b10e5fde545c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Bisulfite sequencing principle:', '["All cytosines are converted resulting in decreased functional activity","Methylated cytosines are digested accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Unmethylated cytosines are protected mediated by specific receptor activation","All cytosines remain without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Unmethylated cytosines convert to uracil (read as T); methylated cytosines unchanged"]'::jsonb, 'Unmethylated cytosines convert to uracil (read as T); methylated cytosines unchanged', 'Bisulfite treatment converts unmethylated cytosines to uracil (read as T after PCR), while methylated cytosines remain unchanged. Subsequent sequencing reveals methylation patterns at single-base resolution.'),
('8be7fa70-9757-5010-866f-34e09359c3e3'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'ChIP-seq is:', '["ChIP followed by next-generation sequencing of the precipitated DNA","ChIP followed by electrophoresis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","ChIP followed by Southern blot mediated by specific receptor activation","ChIP followed by PCR without affecting the primary tissue pathway","ChIP followed by microarray leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'ChIP followed by next-generation sequencing of the precipitated DNA', 'ChIP-seq combines chromatin immunoprecipitation (ChIP) with next-generation sequencing to identify genome-wide binding sites of proteins (histones, transcription factors) to DNA.'),
('0e255eaf-f259-5b5c-af6a-b7c17f204ca7'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Rf (retention factor) in TLC/paper chromatography is:', '["Distance traveled by compound / distance traveled by solvent front","Sample volume / plate area accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Compound height / plate height mediated by specific receptor activation","Compound mass / solvent mass without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Distance traveled by solvent / compound leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Distance traveled by compound / distance traveled by solvent front', 'Rf (retention factor) = distance traveled by compound / distance traveled by solvent front. It is characteristic for a given compound under specific solvent conditions.'),
('7184d4f8-0221-5ee7-b6ee-ac406786ec97'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Column chromatography uses:', '["A flat plate resulting in decreased functional activity","A membrane accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A paper sheet mediated by specific receptor activation","A tube packed with stationary phase; mobile phase flows through","A capillary tube leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A tube packed with stationary phase; mobile phase flows through', 'Column chromatography separates molecules based on charge, size, or affinity. HPLC uses high pressure for rapid, high-resolution separation. Used for drug analysis, metabolite quantification, and protein purification.'),
('e6928e6c-4cd9-5597-8541-3e9059d05280'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'In affinity chromatography:', '["Hydrophobicity separates resulting in decreased functional activity","Charge separates accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Size separates mediated by specific receptor activation","Solubility separates without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A specific ligand on the column binds target molecule; eluted with excess ligand or pH change"]'::jsonb, 'A specific ligand on the column binds target molecule; eluted with excess ligand or pH change', 'Affinity chromatography uses a specific ligand immobilized on the column to selectively bind the target molecule. Elution is achieved with excess free ligand, pH change, or ionic strength variation.'),
('ed7f1a5a-86fe-5630-8dd7-ea1525db5833'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Reverse-phase HPLC uses:', '["Ion exchange resin resulting in decreased functional activity","Non-polar stationary phase (C18) and polar mobile phase (water/organic)","Size exclusion mediated by specific receptor activation","Polar stationary phase without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Normal phase only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Non-polar stationary phase (C18) and polar mobile phase (water/organic)', 'High-performance liquid chromatography (HPLC) separates compounds by passing pressurized liquid through a column with stationary phase. Used for drug level monitoring, amino acid analysis, and metabolic profiling.'),
('74efa2ab-b27b-5283-8823-1132bae01faf'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Liquid chromatography-mass spectrometry (LC-MS) couples:', '["HPLC (or UPLC) to mass spectrometry for identification and quantitation","LC to UV accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CE to MS mediated by specific receptor activation","MS to MS without affecting the primary tissue pathway","GC to MS leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'HPLC (or UPLC) to mass spectrometry for identification and quantitation', 'LC-MS couples liquid chromatography (HPLC/UPLC) separation with mass spectrometry detection, providing both separation and molecular identification/quantification of analytes.'),
('e7cf5325-02ab-54a3-a04b-aa5880d50441'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Gas chromatography (GC) separates:', '["Carbohydrates resulting in decreased functional activity","Volatile compounds that can be vaporized without decomposition","Non-volatile compounds mediated by specific receptor activation","Nucleic acids without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Large proteins leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Volatile compounds that can be vaporized without decomposition', 'Gas chromatography separates volatile and thermally stable compounds by partitioning between a gaseous mobile phase (carrier gas) and a liquid/solid stationary phase in a capillary column.'),
('9fccc601-237c-5275-8f1b-b72f5ac5c7b4'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'GC-MS is the gold standard for:', '["Protein analysis","Toxicology","DNA analysis","Drug confirmation","And metabolic profiling"]'::jsonb, 'Drug confirmation', 'GC-MS is the gold standard for drug confirmation and toxicology screening, providing both separation (GC) and molecular identification (MS) with high sensitivity and specificity.'),
('826705e8-ccc7-5bff-938a-0ee23e3fcfaf'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'The stationary phase in GC is:', '["Acrylamide resulting in decreased functional activity","Silica beads accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Agarose gel mediated by specific receptor activation","A liquid (polymer coating) inside a capillary column","Paper leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A liquid (polymer coating) inside a capillary column', 'The stationary phase in GC is typically a liquid polymer coating (e.g., dimethylpolysiloxane) inside a fused silica capillary column, with film thickness of 0.1-5 um.'),
('e9e81c0f-0a07-52dc-8d82-cf7be10d4771'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Ultra-performance liquid chromatography (UPLC) uses:', '["Gravity flow resulting in decreased functional activity","Smaller particle columns (<2 micromolar) with very high pressure","Lower pressure mediated by specific receptor activation","Larger columns without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Manual injection leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Smaller particle columns (<2 micromolar) with very high pressure', 'UPLC uses columns packed with very small particles (<2 um) operated at very high pressures (>10,000 psi), providing faster analysis and higher resolution than conventional HPLC.'),
('aeec9584-e69e-5022-9955-1fa81d5263ba'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Immunohistochemistry (IHC) uses:', '["Chromatography resulting in decreased functional activity","Antibodies to detect specific antigens in tissue sections","Sequencing mediated by specific receptor activation","Electrophoresis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","PCR leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Antibodies to detect specific antigens in tissue sections', 'Immunohistochemistry uses labeled antibodies to detect specific proteins in tissue sections. Used for tumor classification (Ki-67 for proliferation, CK for epithelial origin) and diagnosis.'),
('091c14b2-2125-56b3-94a4-d00a69ddf554'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Flow cytometry measures:', '["Whole blood only resulting in decreased functional activity","Plasma only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Serum only mediated by specific receptor activation","Multiple physical and fluorescent characteristics of individual cells in suspension","Fixed tissue sections leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Multiple physical and fluorescent characteristics of individual cells in suspension', 'Flow cytometry measures multiple physical (forward scatter, side scatter) and fluorescent characteristics of individual cells in suspension as they pass through a laser beam.'),
('e9f0bc72-31c6-5e9e-a5d4-d8b0c19657d2'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Flow cytometry uses:', '["Forward scatter only","All: forward scatter (size)","Side scatter only","Fluorescence (labeled antibodies)","Side scatter (granularity)"]'::jsonb, 'All: forward scatter (size)', 'Flow cytometry uses forward scatter (proportional to cell size), side scatter (proportional to internal complexity/granularity), and fluorescence (from labeled antibodies or dyes) for cell characterization.'),
('0f30fe58-070c-57bd-aa94-bba02e01789b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'The most common clinical application of flow cytometry is:', '["DNA sequencing resulting in decreased functional activity","Hormone testing accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Microbiology mediated by specific receptor activation","Immunophenotyping of leukemias and lymphomas","Blood typing leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Immunophenotyping of leukemias and lymphomas', 'The most common clinical application of flow cytometry is immunophenotyping of leukemias and lymphomas, using panels of fluorescently labeled antibodies to identify cell surface and intracellular markers.'),
('8498178c-b797-534b-9ec8-818dbd2e2df0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'The resolution of current fluorescence microscopes is:', '["10 micromolar resulting in decreased functional activity","1 micromolar accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","200 nm laterally (Abbes diffraction limit)","10 nm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","1 nm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '200 nm laterally (Abbes diffraction limit)', 'The resolution of conventional fluorescence microscopes is limited by the Abbe diffraction limit to approximately 200 nm laterally and 500 nm axially.'),
('47f344ea-9ec4-570c-88d2-567133f273df'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Confocal microscopy uses:', '["Phase contrast","Dark field","Improving resolution and contrast","Fluorescence only","A pinhole to reject out-of-focus light"]'::jsonb, 'A pinhole to reject out-of-focus light', 'Confocal microscopy uses a pinhole aperture to reject out-of-focus light, enabling optical sectioning and improved resolution and contrast compared to conventional fluorescence microscopy.'),
('3b6559c5-976e-5c87-82cb-00d6cb88de16'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Fluorescence microscopy uses:', '["Fluorescent dyes/proteins excited by specific wavelength","Bright field illumination accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Emitting longer wavelength mediated by specific receptor activation","Phase contrast without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Dark field leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fluorescent dyes/proteins excited by specific wavelength', 'Fluorescence microscopy uses fluorescent dyes or proteins that are excited by specific wavelengths of light and emit at longer wavelengths, allowing specific visualization of cellular structures.'),
('3f82ff36-6877-5279-b3e2-874be0ae5beb'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Green fluorescent protein (GFP) is used as:', '["A genetically encoded fluorescent tag to study protein localization in living cells","A stain for fixed cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","An antibody label mediated by specific receptor activation","A lipid stain without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A DNA stain leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A genetically encoded fluorescent tag to study protein localization in living cells', 'GFP is a genetically encoded fluorescent tag from jellyfish Aequorea victoria. It can be fused to proteins of interest to study their localization and dynamics in living cells without fixation.'),
('60c2ddb2-5846-518c-b9f3-8ff3ae85bbad'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'DAPI is a fluorescent stain for:', '["DNA (blue fluorescence; binds A-T rich regions)","Carbohydrates accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Proteins mediated by specific receptor activation","RNA without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lipids leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'DNA (blue fluorescence; binds A-T rich regions)', 'DAPI (4'',6-diamidino-2-phenylindole) is a fluorescent stain that binds A-T rich regions of DNA, producing blue fluorescence when excited with UV light. Used for nuclear counterstaining.'),
('687b6f3c-9c52-542f-a674-4030dcf53e20'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Phalloidin stains:', '["Intermediate filaments resulting in decreased functional activity","F-actin (filamentous actin) for cytoskeleton visualization","Microtubules mediated by specific receptor activation","Mitochondria without affecting the primary tissue pathway","DNA leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'F-actin (filamentous actin) for cytoskeleton visualization', 'Phalloidin is a toxin from Amanita phalloides mushroom that specifically binds F-actin (filamentous actin). Fluorescently labeled phalloidin is used to visualize the actin cytoskeleton.'),
('df50b915-6115-56aa-8abd-e588c7a88a12'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'LysoTracker stains:', '["Golgi","Mitochondria","Lysosomes","Nuclei","ER"]'::jsonb, 'Lysosomes', 'LysoTracker are fluorescent dyes that accumulate in acidic organelles (lysosomes, pH approximately 4.5-5.0). Used for live-cell imaging of lysosomes and endosomes.'),
('9039c94e-5a10-50ae-91d8-c151aaa2bbdb'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) visualizes:', '["Internal structure resulting in decreased functional activity","Surface topography of specimens (3D appearance)","Proteins mediated by specific receptor activation","Cellular components without affecting the primary tissue pathway","DNA leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Surface topography of specimens (3D appearance)', 'SEM visualizes surface topography by scanning the specimen with a focused electron beam and detecting secondary electrons, producing detailed 3D-appearing images of surfaces.'),
('89708135-6b3a-511b-849e-e8cea13893eb'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Cryo-electron microscopy (cryo-EM) is used for:', '["Elemental analysis resulting in decreased functional activity","High-resolution structure determination of macromolecules in near-native state","Stained sections only mediated by specific receptor activation","Surface only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Live imaging leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'High-resolution structure determination of macromolecules in near-native state', 'Cryo-EM determines high-resolution structures of macromolecules in near-native state by flash-freezing specimens in vitreous ice, avoiding artifacts from chemical fixation or crystallization.'),
('f290d8d4-c827-5936-8d7e-7044b042893e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Cryo-EM advantages include:', '["High resolution","Cheaper","Native conformation","No crystallization needed","Faster than X-ray crystallography"]'::jsonb, 'No crystallization needed', 'Cryo-EM advantages include: no crystallization needed, near-native conformation, ability to study large complexes and heterogeneous samples, and recent resolution improvements approaching atomic level.'),
('9c6b15b0-89fd-5be9-9993-be0d5c55e0c0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'X-ray crystallography determines:', '["Molecular weight resulting in decreased functional activity","3D atomic structure of crystallized macromolecules","Elemental composition mediated by specific receptor activation","Conformation in solution without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sequence leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '3D atomic structure of crystallized macromolecules', 'X-ray crystallography determines 3D protein structure by analyzing diffraction patterns of X-rays through crystals. Provided first protein structures (myoglobin, hemoglobin). Resolution typically 1-3 Angstrom.'),
('cf40ac12-fb23-510b-95b6-ef6c1c18d9f4'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'NMR spectroscopy is best suited for determining the 3D structure of which type of molecules?', '["Large protein complexes","Small to medium proteins in solution","Membrane-bound proteins","Nucleic acid sequences","Lipid bilayers"]'::jsonb, 'Small to medium proteins in solution', 'NMR spectroscopy is ideal for determining 3D structures of small to medium proteins in solution, as it does not require crystallization.'),
('e107fb60-2a9f-5ce5-b841-0d355d6ec9a0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Mass spectrometry (MS) measures:', '["Absorbance resulting in decreased functional activity","Radioactivity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fluorescence mediated by specific receptor activation","Mass-to-charge ratio (m/z) of ions for identification and quantitation","Conductivity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mass-to-charge ratio (m/z) of ions for identification and quantitation', 'Mass spectrometry measures the mass-to-charge ratio (m/z) of ionized molecules, providing molecular weight and structural information for identification and quantification.'),
('8672559d-7145-55f8-a7bc-f15f342a1f05'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Quadrupole mass analyzer uses:', '["A time-of-flight tube resulting in decreased functional activity","A sector magnet accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A magnetic field mediated by specific receptor activation","Four parallel rods with oscillating electric fields to filter specific m/z","An ion trap leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Four parallel rods with oscillating electric fields to filter specific m/z', 'A quadrupole mass analyzer uses four parallel rods with oscillating DC and RF electric fields to filter ions by m/z. Only ions with a specific m/z have stable trajectories and reach the detector.'),
('478136b1-815a-5c8a-8e25-abb157cda0bb'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Ion trap mass analyzer:', '["Then selectively ejects them by m/z","Separates by flight time","Uses magnetic sector","Traps ions in 3D electric field","Transmits all ions"]'::jsonb, 'Traps ions in 3D electric field', 'An ion trap mass analyzer traps ions in a 3D electric field (Paul trap) and selectively ejects them by m/z for detection. Used in many benchtop mass spectrometers.'),
('681fb3b8-30b7-5de3-bf18-87d5d6c78fc1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'FT-ICR (Fourier-transform ion cyclotron resonance) MS:', '["Simplest MS resulting in decreased functional activity","Most portable MS accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Highest resolution mass spectrometry using magnetic trap and cyclotron frequency","Fastest MS without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cheapest MS leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Highest resolution mass spectrometry using magnetic trap and cyclotron frequency', 'FT-ICR MS achieves the highest resolution (>10^6) by trapping ions in a magnetic field and measuring their cyclotron frequency. Fourier transform converts the frequency domain signal to a mass spectrum.'),
('eea0c4fd-0beb-5f99-ba91-1fd8bda5758d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'The ELISA method with highest sensitivity is:', '["Indirect ELISA resulting in decreased functional activity","Direct ELISA accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dot-blot ELISA mediated by specific receptor activation","Sandwich ELISA (capture-detection antibody pair)","Competitive ELISA leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sandwich ELISA (capture-detection antibody pair)', 'Sandwich ELISA has the highest sensitivity because it uses two antibodies (capture and detection) specific to different epitopes on the analyte, reducing background and improving specificity.'),
('157c5510-aa4e-5637-abbc-2d998f8e1635'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Standard curves in ELISA are plotted as:', '["Concentration vs absorbance resulting in decreased functional activity","Absorbance vs concentration (often log-log or 4-parameter logistic)","Wavelength vs absorbance mediated by specific receptor activation","Time vs absorbance without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Temperature vs absorbance leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Absorbance vs concentration (often log-log or 4-parameter logistic)', 'Standard curves in ELISA are plotted as absorbance (or signal) vs concentration, typically using log-log or 4-parameter logistic curve fitting for accurate interpolation of unknown concentrations.'),
('a3816361-45cf-58b6-ac8f-4bfafdc33a00'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Point-of-care testing (POCT) devices are:', '["Near-patient diagnostic tests","Portable","Laboratory-based instruments","Rapid","High-throughput analyzers"]'::jsonb, 'Portable', 'POCT devices are near-patient diagnostic tests that are portable, rapid, and provide results at the point of care without sending samples to a central laboratory.'),
('a8699e96-6171-5223-97e0-9c4fcc0a678f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Diagnostic Techniques Pt1: Phase II conjugation reaction:', '["Glucuronidation","Oxidation","Reduction","Hydrolysis","Deamination"]'::jsonb, 'Glucuronidation', 'Phase II: glucuronidation, sulfation, acetylation, methylation, glutathione conjugation.'),
('0f8e2d08-8fd5-5153-a743-46b4bbe3618b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'The K+ ion-selective electrode uses:', '["Valinomycin (a potassium ionophore in a PVC membrane)","An antibody layer accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A solid crystal mediated by specific receptor activation","A glass membrane without affecting the primary tissue pathway","An enzyme layer leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Valinomycin (a potassium ionophore in a PVC membrane)', 'Ion-selective electrodes (ISE) measure specific ion concentrations (Na+, K+, Cl-, Ca2+) by generating voltage proportional to ion activity. Used in blood gas analyzers and electrolyte panels.'),
('af6f19af-3b81-5194-86ac-953b3deba014'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'The principle of a random-access analyzer is:', '["Sequential sample processing resulting in decreased functional activity","Manual operation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Batch processing mediated by specific receptor activation","Single test per run without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Samples and reagents can be processed in any order with individual testing"]'::jsonb, 'Samples and reagents can be processed in any order with individual testing', 'A random-access analyzer can process samples and reagents in any order, performing individual tests on each sample as needed, unlike batch analyzers.'),
('6db46948-6b55-5fa1-aadc-3de73b330f54'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'The Kodak Ektachem (Vitros) dry slide technology uses:', '["Wet chemistry resulting in decreased functional activity","And detection layers accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thin-film multilayer slides with spreading","Reagent without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Paper strips leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thin-film multilayer slides with spreading', 'Vitros (Kodak Ektachem) dry slide technology uses thin-film multilayer slides with spreading, separation, and detection layers for clinical chemistry analysis without liquid reagents.'),
('3edadc41-cb0c-52a6-84af-05e1d4a1dc46'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'The urine reagent strip (dipstick) tests for:', '["Blood resulting in decreased functional activity","Ketones accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Protein mediated by specific receptor activation","Multiple analytes: glucose","PH leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Multiple analytes: glucose', 'Urine reagent strips (dipsticks) test for multiple analytes simultaneously including glucose, protein, blood, ketones, bilirubin, leukocyte esterase, nitrite, pH, and specific gravity.'),
('a0082e40-02bd-51ae-8be9-d678d49104aa'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'The principle of the urine protein test pad is:', '["Bradford reaction resulting in decreased functional activity","Biuret reaction accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Protein error of indicators (tetrabromophenol blue changes color with protein)","Lowry reaction without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Silver stain leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Protein error of indicators (tetrabromophenol blue changes color with protein)', 'The urine protein test pad uses the protein error of indicators -- tetrabromophenol blue changes from yellow to blue-green in the presence of protein, with the color change proportional to protein concentration.'),
('d717aa71-e983-5319-9ce4-cb6183383934'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'The principle of the urine blood test pad is:', '["Immunoassay resulting in decreased functional activity","Pseudoperoxidase activity of hemoglobin catalyzing chromogen oxidation","Microscopic RBC detection mediated by specific receptor activation","Direct hemoglobin detection without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Enzymatic assay leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pseudoperoxidase activity of hemoglobin catalyzing chromogen oxidation', 'The urine blood test pad detects hemoglobin by its pseudoperoxidase activity, which catalyzes the oxidation of a chromogen by an organic peroxide, producing a color change.'),
('dbb6b961-28a6-552e-9292-7d90f1daf802'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'The principle of the urine bilirubin test pad is:', '["Nitroprusside reaction resulting in decreased functional activity","Diazo reaction (diazotized dichloroaniline coupling with bilirubin)","Biuret reaction mediated by specific receptor activation","Jaffe reaction without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Trinder reaction leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Diazo reaction (diazotized dichloroaniline coupling with bilirubin)', 'Bilirubin is the breakdown product of heme, conjugated in the liver and excreted in bile.'),
('c443bbda-8185-5580-ba62-9f69011cb9f2'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'The principle of the urine leukocyte esterase test pad is:', '["Leukocyte esterase cleaves indoxyl ester","Greiss reaction","Nitroprusside","Ehrlich reaction","Which reacts with diazonium salt forming purple"]'::jsonb, 'Leukocyte esterase cleaves indoxyl ester', 'The leukocyte esterase test pad detects the enzyme esterase released by leukocytes. It cleaves an indoxyl ester substrate, releasing indoxyl which is oxidized to indigo (blue-purple color).'),
('f3761b28-ce86-5b74-a4e6-c6b45e4b0b4a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Specific gravity in urine is estimated by:', '["Weight resulting in decreased functional activity","Osmometry accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Change in pKa of polyelectrolyte indicator with ionic concentration","Refractometry without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Density leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Change in pKa of polyelectrolyte indicator with ionic concentration', 'Urine specific gravity is estimated by the change in pKa of a polyelectrolyte indicator (bromothymol blue) with ionic concentration. As ionic concentration increases, the indicator releases H+, changing color.'),
('383c8c3b-88fc-5970-b3a3-2cf7c39e288d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'The principle of fecal occult blood (FOB) testing:', '["Enzymatic assay resulting in decreased functional activity","Pseudoperoxidase activity of heme catalyzing color development of guaiac/resin","Microscopic detection mediated by specific receptor activation","Immunoassay without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Culture leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pseudoperoxidase activity of heme catalyzing color development of guaiac/resin', 'FOB testing (guaiac-based) relies on the pseudoperoxidase activity of heme to catalyze oxidation of guaiac by H2O2, producing a blue color change.'),
('58aba1f3-4b38-5719-bcc1-7e4f450039b0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Immunochemical fecal occult blood test (iFOBT) uses:', '["Automated analyzer resulting in decreased functional activity","Chemical reaction accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Antibodies specific to human hemoglobin (more specific than guaiac)","Heme peroxidase without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Microscopy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Antibodies specific to human hemoglobin (more specific than guaiac)', 'Hemoglobin is a tetrameric protein with four subunits, each containing a heme group for oxygen binding.'),
('a72f6e7f-6262-50dc-8740-24a52f8ffa72'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'The charge-coupled device (CCD) camera is used in:', '["Chromatography","Blots","Digital imaging of gels","And chemiluminescence","Spectrophotometry"]'::jsonb, 'Digital imaging of gels', 'CCD cameras are used in digital imaging of gels, blots, and chemiluminescence, providing quantitative capture of fluorescent, chemiluminescent, and colorimetric signals.'),
('4efbede9-11ed-5ede-83ed-0d0a139e29c5'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'A spectrophotometer consists of:', '["Sample cuvette","Light source","Detector","Light source only","Monochromator"]'::jsonb, 'Light source', 'A spectrophotometer consists of a light source (tungsten lamp for visible, deuterium lamp for UV), monochromator (grating or prism), sample compartment (cuvette), and detector (photodiode or PMT).'),
('fd98360b-c4ed-5ebf-a2eb-54c2ae7b685e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'A cuvette for UV spectrophotometry must be made of:', '["Paper resulting in decreased functional activity","Quartz or silica (transmits UV light)","Glass (absorbs UV) mediated by specific receptor activation","Metal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Plastic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Quartz or silica (transmits UV light)', 'UV spectroscopy uses 200-400 nm wavelength range, where aromatic amino acids and nucleic acids absorb.'),
('1d9424b4-d286-5f38-bec6-a639eea6f908'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'The wavelength accuracy of a spectrophotometer is checked using:', '["Holmium oxide or didymium filters","Blank solution accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Water mediated by specific receptor activation","Sample without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Air leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Holmium oxide or didymium filters', 'Wavelength accuracy of a spectrophotometer is checked using holmium oxide or didymium glass filters, which have sharp, well-characterized absorption peaks at known wavelengths.'),
('c72f7089-ba4b-5c2c-97f0-0b1dcd53f9c0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Stray light in a spectrophotometer causes:', '["Better resolution resulting in decreased functional activity","Increased sensitivity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Faster measurement mediated by specific receptor activation","Deviation from Beer-Lambert law (nonlinearity at high absorbance)","Lower noise leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Deviation from Beer-Lambert law (nonlinearity at high absorbance)', 'Stray light (unwanted light reaching the detector) causes deviation from Beer-Lambert law, particularly at high absorbance values, leading to nonlinearity and inaccurate measurements.'),
('03b2a481-61ab-5a6b-9a29-95aeb15e768c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'The difference between a monochromator and a filter photometer is:', '["Both are the same resulting in decreased functional activity","Filter uses a grating accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Monochromator selects continuous wavelength band; filters pass discrete bands","Monochromator uses filters without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Filter is more precise leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Monochromator selects continuous wavelength band; filters pass discrete bands', 'A monochromator uses a grating or prism to select a continuous band of wavelengths from a polychromatic source, while filter photometers use interference or absorption filters to pass discrete wavelength bands.'),
('6b7b79c2-0ec2-58bf-9154-9f0d8432714b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Fluorescence is more sensitive than absorbance because:', '["Fluorescence uses stronger light resulting in decreased functional activity","Fluorescence is simpler accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fluorescence is cheaper mediated by specific receptor activation","Fluorescence measures emitted light against dark background; absorbance measures small changes in bright light","Fluorescence is faster leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fluorescence measures emitted light against dark background; absorbance measures small changes in bright light', 'Fluorescence is more sensitive because it measures emitted light against a dark background (no signal at zero concentration), whereas absorbance measures small changes in bright transmitted light.'),
('e5d4c89d-edec-5b1e-89e7-32a0f0e03709'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Luminometers measure:', '["Scatter resulting in decreased functional activity","Chemiluminescence (light from chemical reactions)","Fluorescence mediated by specific receptor activation","Absorbance without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Radioactivity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Chemiluminescence (light from chemical reactions)', 'Luminometers measure chemiluminescence -- the light produced by chemical reactions (e.g., luciferin-luciferase, acridinium ester oxidation). Used in immunoassays and ATP measurement.'),
('abc838b3-2b08-55c1-97b5-6a1befd7c5c4'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'The choice of method depends on:', '["All: analyte concentration","Sample matrix","Throughput","Required sensitivity","Cost"]'::jsonb, 'All: analyte concentration', 'The choice of analytical method depends on analyte concentration, sample matrix, required sensitivity and specificity, throughput needs, available equipment, and cost.'),
('0cfcbaae-b704-5c95-ba5d-dde6d5304fa5'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'The Levey-Jennings chart is used to:', '["Plot standard curves resulting in decreased functional activity","Monitor QC data over time for shifts and trends in test performance","Design experiments mediated by specific receptor activation","Record patient results without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Calculate reference ranges leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Monitor QC data over time for shifts and trends in test performance', 'A Levey-Jennings chart plots QC results over time to monitor for shifts (sudden changes) and trends (gradual changes) in test performance, enabling detection of systematic and random errors.'),
('10eb4965-e475-56e2-a4f8-60f69f108713'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Accuracy in laboratory measurement refers to:', '["Precision resulting in decreased functional activity","Reproducibility accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Closeness of measured value to the true value","Sensitivity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Specificity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Closeness of measured value to the true value', 'Accuracy refers to the closeness of a measured value to the true or accepted reference value. It is assessed by analyzing control materials with known values and comparing to target values.'),
('329d6c77-4080-58fe-80f5-4fc5d77851d5'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Specificity (diagnostic) is:', '["True negatives / (true negatives + false positives)","Positive predictive value","True positives / (true positives + false negatives)","Accuracy","Negative predictive value"]'::jsonb, 'True negatives / (true negatives + false positives)', 'Specificity = TN / (TN + FP). It measures the proportion of true negatives correctly identified, indicating how well the test avoids false positives.'),
('ac901dd8-7ee6-5942-972a-1bd2e4a9a602'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Positive predictive value (PPV) depends on:', '["Sensitivity only resulting in decreased functional activity","Prevalence of the disease (PPV = TP / (TP + FP))","Age of patient mediated by specific receptor activation","Specificity only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sample size leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Prevalence of the disease (PPV = TP / (TP + FP))', 'PPV depends on prevalence: PPV = TP / (TP + FP). At low prevalence, even a highly specific test will have a low PPV due to the large number of false positives relative to true positives.'),
('80413d58-6e83-563e-85f4-9fab87b94bf8'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Lipemia interferes by:', '["Altering ionic strength resulting in decreased functional activity","Changing pH accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Precipitating proteins mediated by specific receptor activation","Binding analytes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Scattering light (turbidity) in spectrophotometric assays"]'::jsonb, 'Scattering light (turbidity) in spectrophotometric assays', 'Lipemia (turbid lipemic serum) interferes with spectrophotometric assays by scattering light, causing falsely elevated absorbance readings. Can be addressed by ultracentrifugation or sample dilution.'),
('2486e293-7976-5053-8310-20307860dec3'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Which technique measures light absorption by molecules?', '["Atomic absorption spectrophotometry (AAS)","Spectrophotometry accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","ISE mediated by specific receptor activation","Flame photometry without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Titration leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Atomic absorption spectrophotometry (AAS)', 'Atomic absorption spectrophotometry (AAS) measures light absorption by free gaseous atoms. Used for trace metal analysis (lead, iron, zinc) with high specificity.'),
('dfd03104-7d6e-5537-9db2-03f9b0289961'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'The reference method for glucose is:', '["Glucose dehydrogenase resulting in decreased functional activity","Glucose oxidase/peroxidase accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","GOD-POD mediated by specific receptor activation","Hexokinase method (glucose + ATP -> G6P -> NADPH)","O-toluidine leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hexokinase method (glucose + ATP -> G6P -> NADPH)', 'Hexokinase method (glucose + ATP -> G6P -> NADPH) phosphorylates glucose to glucose-6-phosphate, trapping it inside cells and committing it to metabolism.'),
('9b92d0ad-5a46-5273-a626-e2fb57bc8947'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 1', 'Enzymatic creatinine assays (using creatininase) are:', '["More expensive but more specific resulting in decreased functional activity","More specific than Jaffe (less interference) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Less specific mediated by specific receptor activation","Same as Jaffe without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Both A (More expensive but more specific) and B (More specific than Jaffe (less interference))"]'::jsonb, 'Both A (More expensive but more specific) and B (More specific than Jaffe (less interference))', 'Enzymatic creatinine assays using creatininase are more specific than the Jaffe reaction (less interference from proteins, bilirubin, glucose) but more expensive. They are preferred in pediatric and neonatal settings.'),
