('53b13625-6b80-5c6c-bea9-2f2451ebe95d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'A 40-year-old male with suspected enteroviral meningitis undergoes lumbar puncture. The physician wants to detect enterovirus RNA in the CSF. (Diagnostic Case #48)', '["Western blot resulting in decreased functional activity","Southern blot accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","ELISA mediated by specific receptor activation","Flow cytometry without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Reverse transcription polymerase chain reaction (RT-PCR)"]'::jsonb, 'Reverse transcription polymerase chain reaction (RT-PCR)', 'RT-PCR is used to amplify and detect viral RNA.'),
('165b11c8-83ea-5c52-8566-a4c0790b4274'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'A 20-year-old male with suspected enteroviral meningitis undergoes lumbar puncture. The physician wants to detect enterovirus RNA in the CSF. (Diagnostic Case #49)', '["Western blot resulting in decreased functional activity","Southern blot accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","ELISA mediated by specific receptor activation","Flow cytometry without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Reverse transcription polymerase chain reaction (RT-PCR)"]'::jsonb, 'Reverse transcription polymerase chain reaction (RT-PCR)', 'RT-PCR is used to amplify and detect viral RNA.'),
('842893d5-06df-5737-8c24-0f821a754afb'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'The first step of Southern blotting is:', '["Electrophoresis resulting in decreased functional activity","Digestion of DNA with restriction enzymes","Hybridization mediated by specific receptor activation","Transfer without affecting the primary tissue pathway","PCR leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Digestion of DNA with restriction enzymes', 'Southern blotting begins with restriction enzyme digestion of genomic DNA, followed by gel electrophoresis, transfer to a membrane, and hybridization with a labeled probe.'),
('4df2dc29-451b-5775-888b-e3fd0c4b06f3'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'In the study of Diagnostic Techniques Pt2, In double-beam spectrophotometers:', '["Two light sources are used","Two monochromators are used","Two samples are measured","Two detectors are used","The light beam splits into sample and reference paths"]'::jsonb, 'The light beam splits into sample and reference paths', 'The light beam splits into sample and reference paths is a principle or component of spectrophotometric analysis for measuring light absorption.'),
('d24faa17-c4e3-558f-ba66-9882936f4ce1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Diagnostic Techniques Pt2: Comparative genomic hybridization (CGH) detects:', '["Chromosomal translocations only","Gene expression levels","Protein levels","DNA copy number changes across the entire genome","Specific point mutations"]'::jsonb, 'DNA copy number changes across the entire genome', 'CGH detects DNA copy number changes (gains/losses) across the entire genome by competitive hybridization of test and reference DNA to metaphase chromosomes or DNA arrays.'),
('28442a20-0b4c-5581-b32c-0c4f45b50bde'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'The main difference between Southern and Northern blotting is:', '["Northern uses different detection","Northern uses different probe","Northern uses RNA instead of DNA","Northern uses different transfer","Northern uses different membrane"]'::jsonb, 'Northern uses RNA instead of DNA', 'Southern blotting detects DNA while Northern blotting detects RNA; the two methods otherwise share the same electrophoretic separation and probe-hybridization principles.'),
('48ce13f9-7a69-522c-bc6d-145d481d640f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'A 25-year-old female with suspected acute viral meningitis undergoes a lumbar puncture. The physician wants to detect the presence of enterovirus RNA in the cerebrospinal fluid. Which molecular diagnostic method is most appropriate for this purpose?', '["Southern blot resulting in decreased functional activity","Reverse transcription polymerase chain reaction (RT-PCR)","Western blot mediated by specific receptor activation","ELISA without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Flow cytometry leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Reverse transcription polymerase chain reaction (RT-PCR)', 'Detection of viral RNA requires reverse transcription to synthesize complementary DNA (cDNA), followed by PCR amplification (RT-PCR). PCR is highly sensitive and specific for detecting nucleic acids in CSF, whereas Southern blotting is used for genomic DNA and Western/ELISA for proteins.'),
('5c3c714b-901d-5011-bf8e-d6dec16b76d4'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'The primary antibody in Western blotting:', '["Binds the membrane","Binds the secondary antibody","Binds all proteins","Specifically binds the target protein","Binds DNA"]'::jsonb, 'Specifically binds the target protein', 'The primary antibody specifically binds an epitope on the target protein, and a labeled secondary antibody that recognizes the primary antibody provides the detection signal.'),
('cf263cd0-6494-5585-a722-ee63cb5563ad'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'Alkaline phosphatase detection in Western blot uses:', '["TMB resulting in decreased functional activity","AEC accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","BCIP/NBT substrate (purple/blue precipitate)","DAB without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Luminol leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'BCIP/NBT substrate (purple/blue precipitate)', 'Alkaline phosphatase linked to the secondary antibody converts the substrates BCIP/NBT into an insoluble purple-blue precipitate at the site of the target protein.'),
('8aa3a46c-6c6e-5d77-a63b-d40efda0a5da'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Diagnostic Techniques Pt2: An isosbestic point is:', '["The turbidity point resulting in decreased functional activity","The absorption maximum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The minimum absorption mediated by specific receptor activation","A wavelength where two species have equal absorbance","The fluorescence maximum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A wavelength where two species have equal absorbance', 'An isosbestic point is a wavelength where two interconverting species have equal molar absorptivity. Its presence indicates a clean two-species system without side reactions.'),
('b1d6c935-769a-5076-9cb8-34fbfa7778aa'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'Agarose gel electrophoresis for DNA uses:', '["Ponceau S resulting in decreased functional activity","Coomassie blue accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Amido black mediated by specific receptor activation","Ethidium bromide (EtBr) or gel-red for visualization under UV","Silver stain leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ethidium bromide (EtBr) or gel-red for visualization under UV', 'DNA in agarose gels is visualized with intercalating dyes such as ethidium bromide (EtBr) or GelRed, which fluoresce when illuminated with UV or blue light.'),
('b57dc667-1191-523f-b0fd-4877cc662792'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'DNA size markers (ladder) are:', '["RNA resulting in decreased functional activity","Random fragments accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Proteins mediated by specific receptor activation","Known-size DNA fragments used to estimate sample fragment sizes","Unknown fragments leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Known-size DNA fragments used to estimate sample fragment sizes', 'DNA ladders are mixtures of DNA fragments of known size run alongside samples; fragment sizes are estimated by comparing migration distances.'),
('83b977c8-be46-5efe-8ee9-e1567b7ca8d2'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'Pulsed-field gel electrophoresis (PFGE) resolves:', '["Proteins resulting in decreased functional activity","Large DNA molecules (50 kb to 10 Mb) by alternating electric fields","Small DNA mediated by specific receptor activation","RNA without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lipids leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Large DNA molecules (50 kb to 10 Mb) by alternating electric fields', 'Pulsed-field gel electrophoresis alternates the direction of the electric field so that very large DNA molecules (50 kb to 10 Mb) can reorient and migrate through the gel.'),
('47ba2caf-024b-5e12-9c0a-ad6697f1f9fc'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'In the study of Diagnostic Techniques Pt2, The charge-coupled device (CCD) camera is used in:', '["Chromatography","Blots","Digital imaging of gels","And chemiluminescence","Spectrophotometry"]'::jsonb, 'Digital imaging of gels', 'CCD cameras are used in digital imaging of gels, blots, and chemiluminescence, providing quantitative capture of fluorescent, chemiluminescent, and colorimetric signals.'),
('a94b59e3-129e-5843-a90e-35828a9bf377'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Diagnostic Techniques Pt2: Green fluorescent protein (GFP) is used as:', '["A genetically encoded fluorescent tag to study protein localization in living cells","A stain for fixed cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","An antibody label mediated by specific receptor activation","A lipid stain without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A DNA stain leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A genetically encoded fluorescent tag to study protein localization in living cells', 'GFP is a genetically encoded fluorescent tag from jellyfish Aequorea victoria. It can be fused to proteins of interest to study their localization and dynamics in living cells without fixation.'),
('acaa9f9b-166e-5635-862d-a03fe96581e1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'The sequence is read by a:', '["Laser detector (fluorescence) at the end of the capillary","X-ray film accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mass detector mediated by specific receptor activation","Human eye without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Scintillation counter leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Laser detector (fluorescence) at the end of the capillary', 'In capillary Sanger sequencing, fluorescently labeled fragments pass a laser and a detector reads the color at each position, reconstructing the sequence.'),
('30cabade-8e02-5387-9804-ee9ca5f5ea08'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'Illumina sequencing uses:', '["Pyrosequencing resulting in decreased functional activity","Ion semiconductor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bridge amplification on a flow cell and reversible terminator sequencing","Single-molecule real-time without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Nanopore leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bridge amplification on a flow cell and reversible terminator sequencing', 'Illumina sequencing uses bridge amplification to create clusters of identical templates on a flow cell, then sequences by synthesis with fluorescent reversible terminators added one base at a time.'),
('a231c59b-1d9b-52c4-8c29-a00a568eca2a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Diagnostic Techniques Pt2: The principle of DNA microarray is:', '["Antibody binding resulting in decreased functional activity","Charge binding accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Size separation mediated by specific receptor activation","Complementary base pairing (hybridization) of labeled cDNA to arrayed probes","Enzymatic reaction leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Complementary base pairing (hybridization) of labeled cDNA to arrayed probes', 'The principle of DNA microarray is complementary base pairing (hybridization) of labeled cDNA to arrayed oligonucleotide or cDNA probes, with signal intensity proportional to transcript abundance.'),
('5a653a93-6322-5f2e-9013-8afaa4ce6802'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'PacBio (single molecule real-time) sequencing:', '["Images a single DNA polymerase molecule incorporating fluorescent phospholinked nucleotides","Measures current accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Measures pH change mediated by specific receptor activation","Uses reversible terminators without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sequences by synthesis in clusters leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Images a single DNA polymerase molecule incorporating fluorescent phospholinked nucleotides', 'PacBio single-molecule real-time sequencing images a single DNA polymerase molecule as it incorporates fluorescently labeled phospholinked nucleotides, giving long reads in real time.'),
('7ce0b425-d7ad-51ee-96ef-aca4d563a909'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Diagnostic Techniques Pt2: Standard curves in ELISA are plotted as:', '["Concentration vs absorbance resulting in decreased functional activity","Absorbance vs concentration (often log-log or 4-parameter logistic)","Wavelength vs absorbance mediated by specific receptor activation","Time vs absorbance without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Temperature vs absorbance leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Absorbance vs concentration (often log-log or 4-parameter logistic)', 'Standard curves in ELISA are plotted as absorbance (or signal) vs concentration, typically using log-log or 4-parameter logistic curve fitting for accurate interpolation of unknown concentrations.'),
('e1ee6e23-a68b-5ecf-a72d-a2aac1390a2a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'Nanopore sequencing (Oxford Nanopore) detects:', '["PH changes resulting in decreased functional activity","Light emission accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Changes in ionic current as DNA passes through a protein nanopore","Fluorescence without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Heat changes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Changes in ionic current as DNA passes through a protein nanopore', 'Nanopore sequencing threads a DNA strand through a protein pore and detects base-dependent changes in ionic current, enabling real-time long-read sequencing.'),
('acc86224-6df2-5db4-8263-8b9b20dbb685'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'Ion semiconductor (Ion Torrent) sequencing detects:', '["Light resulting in decreased functional activity","Heat accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","H+ ions released when nucleotides are incorporated (pH change)","Fluorescence without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Current leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'H+ ions released when nucleotides are incorporated (pH change)', 'Ion Torrent sequencing detects the H+ ions released when nucleotides are incorporated; the resulting local pH change is sensed by a semiconductor chip.'),
('1408dd35-a12f-50b4-bc95-91eedad8ae7c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'A 60-year-old male with suspected acute viral meningitis undergoes a lumbar puncture. The physician wants to detect the presence of enterovirus RNA in the cerebrospinal fluid. Which molecular diagnostic method is most appropriate for this purpose?', '["Flow cytometry resulting in decreased functional activity","Reverse transcription polymerase chain reaction (RT-PCR)","Southern blot mediated by specific receptor activation","Western blot without affecting the primary tissue pathway","ELISA leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Reverse transcription polymerase chain reaction (RT-PCR)', 'Detection of viral RNA requires reverse transcription to synthesize complementary DNA (cDNA), followed by PCR amplification (RT-PCR). PCR is highly sensitive and specific for detecting nucleic acids in CSF, whereas Southern blotting is used for genomic DNA and Western/ELISA for proteins.'),
('4ccb326c-be2b-563f-976e-548e566e07ff'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'A patient''s screening test for Lyme disease is positive. To confirm the diagnosis, the laboratory performs an assay that separates Borrelia burgdorferi proteins by gel electrophoresis, transfers them to a membrane, and incubates it with patient serum to detect specific antibodies. What is this technique?', '["Western blot","Northern blot","Flow cytometry","RT-PCR","Southern blot"]'::jsonb, 'Western blot', 'Western blotting is used to detect specific proteins in a sample or specific antibodies against proteins (as in confirmatory Lyme disease or historical HIV testing). It involves gel electrophoresis, transfer to a membrane, and antibody hybridization.'),
('15c0d667-684c-5a4a-b5af-75190c2cdb57'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Diagnostic Techniques Pt2: The Biuret method measures:', '["Creatinine resulting in decreased functional activity","Glucose accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Albumin mediated by specific receptor activation","Urea without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Total protein (peptide bonds form purple complex with Cu2+)"]'::jsonb, 'Total protein (peptide bonds form purple complex with Cu2+)', 'The Biuret method detects peptide bonds by their reaction with Cu2+ in alkaline solution, forming a purple complex measured at 540 nm. Used for total protein measurement.'),
('1c813307-048e-5b5d-8e78-210d4da65623'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'Sequencing depth (coverage) refers to:', '["Total bases resulting in decreased functional activity","Number of reads accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Read length mediated by specific receptor activation","Genome size without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The average number of times each base is sequenced"]'::jsonb, 'The average number of times each base is sequenced', 'Sequencing depth (coverage) is the average number of times each base is represented in the reads; higher depth improves the confidence of variant calls.'),
('bca91c7a-a78b-57a2-9248-1cb7bf5b00a9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of Diagnostic Techniques Pt2, Point-of-care testing (POCT) devices are:', '["Near-patient diagnostic tests","Portable","Laboratory-based instruments","Rapid","High-throughput analyzers"]'::jsonb, 'Portable', 'POCT devices are near-patient diagnostic tests that are portable, rapid, and provide results at the point of care without sending samples to a central laboratory.'),
('73416104-294a-540f-a2ae-b79a6bfa6d8d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Diagnostic Techniques Pt2: Positive predictive value (PPV) depends on:', '["Sensitivity only resulting in decreased functional activity","Prevalence of the disease (PPV = TP / (TP + FP))","Age of patient mediated by specific receptor activation","Specificity only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sample size leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Prevalence of the disease (PPV = TP / (TP + FP))', 'PPV depends on prevalence: PPV = TP / (TP + FP). At low prevalence, even a highly specific test will have a low PPV due to the large number of false positives relative to true positives.'),
('03f33d26-504e-5699-991e-ddafbd865b00'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'A 65-year-old male undergoes screening for HIV infection. The initial screening test utilizes patient serum to detect both HIV-1/2 antibodies and the gp24 antigen. Which immunodiagnostic technique is most commonly used for this combined screening?', '["Polymerase chain reaction (PCR)","Immunohistochemistry","Northern blot","Western blot","Enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA)"]'::jsonb, 'Enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA)', 'Combined antigen/antibody detection assays (fourth-generation tests) for HIV screening are performed using Enzyme-Linked Immunosorbent Assay (ELISA). ELISA is highly sensitive and automated, making it the preferred screening method before confirmatory testing.'),
('1507c826-8da7-5360-b329-521609322e60'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'The 1000 Genomes Project aimed to:', '["Catalog human genetic variation by sequencing many individuals","Study evolution accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Map all genes mediated by specific receptor activation","Sequence one genome without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Find disease genes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Catalog human genetic variation by sequencing many individuals', 'The 1000 Genomes Project sequenced thousands of individuals from diverse populations to build a public catalog of human genetic variation.'),
('04150b48-91ba-5850-9bbc-e4a86d538ae6'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'A 60-year-old female with suspected acute viral meningitis undergoes a lumbar puncture. The physician wants to detect the presence of enterovirus RNA in the cerebrospinal fluid. Which molecular diagnostic method is most appropriate for this purpose?', '["Reverse transcription polymerase chain reaction (RT-PCR)","Western blot accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Flow cytometry mediated by specific receptor activation","Southern blot without affecting the primary tissue pathway","ELISA leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Reverse transcription polymerase chain reaction (RT-PCR)', 'Detection of viral RNA requires reverse transcription to synthesize complementary DNA (cDNA), followed by PCR amplification (RT-PCR). PCR is highly sensitive and specific for detecting nucleic acids in CSF, whereas Southern blotting is used for genomic DNA and Western/ELISA for proteins.'),
('024cfc0f-c207-51bb-920e-b187e0460bef'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of Diagnostic Techniques Pt2, Jaffe reaction (alkaline picrate) is used for:', '["Protein measurement","Bilirubin measurement","Glucose measurement","Urea measurement","Creatinine measurement"]'::jsonb, 'Creatinine measurement', 'The Jaffe reaction involves creatinine reacting with alkaline picrate to form a red-orange complex measured at 510-520 nm. It is the classic method for creatinine measurement.'),
('ba07f4c4-0c9b-5198-b5e7-e83c2e019a29'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'The CRISPR-Cas9 system is derived from:', '["Archea resulting in decreased functional activity","Eukaryotic cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Viruses mediated by specific receptor activation","Fungi without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bacterial adaptive immune system"]'::jsonb, 'Bacterial adaptive immune system', 'CRISPR-Cas9 originates from the bacterial adaptive immune system, which stores short fragments of viral DNA to guide cleavage of invading phage genomes.'),
('03c2ef6a-e3f7-53f9-9e94-a97eb464000a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'In the study of Diagnostic Techniques Pt2, P-Nitrophenyl phosphate (pNPP) is a substrate for:', '["Peroxidase resulting in decreased functional activity","Glucose oxidase accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Alkaline phosphatase (produces yellow p-nitrophenol)","Beta-galactosidase without affecting the primary tissue pathway","HRP leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Alkaline phosphatase (produces yellow p-nitrophenol)', 'pNPP is hydrolyzed by alkaline phosphatase to produce p-nitrophenol, a yellow product measurable at 405 nm. Used as a chromogenic substrate in ALP-based ELISA systems.'),
('6dd832e8-8b67-5fb2-af3e-983e344ccdb6'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'Cas9 nuclease creates:', '["Base modifications resulting in decreased functional activity","Histone modifications accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","DNA methylation mediated by specific receptor activation","Single-strand nicks without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Double-strand breaks (DSBs) at the target DNA site"]'::jsonb, 'Double-strand breaks (DSBs) at the target DNA site', 'Cas9 nuclease creates a double-strand break a few bases upstream of the PAM site, which the cell repairs by NHEJ (mutagenic) or HDR (precise) pathways.'),
('deb0145c-a25c-55ac-9e67-2078ee559a2b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'The PAM (protospacer adjacent motif) sequence is:', '["The repair template resulting in decreased functional activity","The target sequence accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The guide RNA mediated by specific receptor activation","The promoter without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A short sequence (NGG for SpCas9) required adjacent to the target for Cas9 recognition"]'::jsonb, 'A short sequence (NGG for SpCas9) required adjacent to the target for Cas9 recognition', 'The PAM is a short sequence (NGG for SpCas9) immediately adjacent to the target site that is required for Cas9 to recognize and unwind the DNA.'),
('422cdc6d-9e66-5c47-887b-4ffd5c432666'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of Diagnostic Techniques Pt2, Mass spectrometry (MS) measures:', '["Absorbance resulting in decreased functional activity","Radioactivity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fluorescence mediated by specific receptor activation","Mass-to-charge ratio (m/z) of ions for identification and quantitation","Conductivity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mass-to-charge ratio (m/z) of ions for identification and quantitation', 'Mass spectrometry measures the mass-to-charge ratio (m/z) of ionized molecules, providing molecular weight and structural information for identification and quantification.'),
('ac6bff5a-b438-5187-b11c-377871843ed5'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'CRISPR-Cas9 can be used for:', '["Activation","Epigenetic modification","All: gene knockout","Knockin","Repression"]'::jsonb, 'All: gene knockout', 'CRISPR-Cas9 can knock out genes through NHEJ, knock in sequences through HDR, and, as dCas9 fusions, activate or repress gene transcription.'),
('cf625f91-c27a-5eea-ad5b-487150857119'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'Base editing uses:', '["Nuclease only resulting in decreased functional activity","Ligase only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Recombinase only mediated by specific receptor activation","PCR only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cas9 nickase fused to a deaminase enzyme to convert one base to another without DSB"]'::jsonb, 'Cas9 nickase fused to a deaminase enzyme to convert one base to another without DSB', 'Base editing couples a Cas9 nickase to a deaminase enzyme that converts one base to another (for example C to T) without creating a double-strand break.'),
('79688e24-6430-5c21-974a-594a166a435e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'Gene therapy involves:', '["Blotting only resulting in decreased functional activity","Cloning only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Introduction of genetic material into cells to treat or prevent disease","Sequencing only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","PCR only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Introduction of genetic material into cells to treat or prevent disease', 'Gene therapy delivers functional genetic material into a patient''s cells to correct a defective gene or to supply a therapeutic protein.'),
('8039ba7e-1d45-507c-9ed4-f52a8ea68751'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'Ex vivo gene therapy:', '["Direct injection into patient","And returned","In vivo modification","Genetically modified","Cells are removed from patient"]'::jsonb, 'Cells are removed from patient', 'Ex vivo gene therapy removes the patient''s cells, genetically modifies them in culture (usually with a viral vector), and returns them to the patient.'),
('9b187855-d203-5584-9e2f-aace907ac6af'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'Viral vectors for gene therapy include:', '["Adenovirus","Lentivirus","All: retrovirus","AAV","Only retrovirus"]'::jsonb, 'All: retrovirus', 'Viral vectors for gene therapy include retroviruses, lentiviruses, adenoviruses, and adeno-associated viruses, which differ in cargo capacity, immunogenicity, and integration behavior.'),
('f5643665-8f21-5cc8-94e9-9f7d84256bbd'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'AAV (adeno-associated virus) vectors are:', '["High immunogenicity","Integrating","Low immunogenicity","Used for in vivo gene therapy","Non-integrating"]'::jsonb, 'Non-integrating', 'AAV vectors are non-integrating and weakly immunogenic, making them attractive for in vivo gene therapy, though their 4.7 kb cargo limit restricts insert size.'),
('ded45aa2-8f15-5610-978f-a8dae62ae77f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'In the study of Diagnostic Techniques Pt2, Urine protein electrophoresis is used for:', '["Serum protein quantitation resulting in decreased functional activity","Enzyme analysis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bence Jones protein (free light chains) detection","Hemoglobin analysis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lipoprotein analysis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bence Jones protein (free light chains) detection', 'Urine protein electrophoresis is used to detect Bence Jones protein (free immunoglobulin light chains), which migrate as a narrow band in the beta-gamma region. Seen in multiple myeloma and light chain disease.'),
('6b31d23f-22fd-550f-a421-32464b50a911'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'Adenoviral vectors:', '["Large cargo","Low immunogenicity","High transduction efficiency","But highly immunogenic (not integrating)","Integrate into genome"]'::jsonb, 'High transduction efficiency', 'Adenoviral vectors transduce both dividing and non-dividing cells with high efficiency, but their strong immunogenicity limits long-term gene expression.'),
('18b5ed58-ffd9-5b79-9b8d-b640eebd85a5'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'The first approved gene therapy (1990) was for:', '["Cystic fibrosis resulting in decreased functional activity","Hemophilia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Leber congenital amaurosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Sickle cell without affecting the primary tissue pathway","ADA-SCID (adenosine deaminase deficiency)"]'::jsonb, 'ADA-SCID (adenosine deaminase deficiency)', 'The first approved gene therapy, begun in 1990, treated ADA-SCID by ex vivo retroviral transfer of the adenosine deaminase gene into the patient''sT cells.'),
('6b7d8231-3cff-5c12-b9dd-a7a01de5e35f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of Diagnostic Techniques Pt2, The Levey-Jennings chart is used to:', '["Plot standard curves resulting in decreased functional activity","Monitor QC data over time for shifts and trends in test performance","Design experiments mediated by specific receptor activation","Record patient results without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Calculate reference ranges leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Monitor QC data over time for shifts and trends in test performance', 'A Levey-Jennings chart plots QC results over time to monitor for shifts (sudden changes) and trends (gradual changes) in test performance, enabling detection of systematic and random errors.'),
('b7b19d05-076d-5a66-9b44-11a48f60340b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Diagnostic Techniques Pt2: Liquid chromatography-mass spectrometry (LC-MS) couples:', '["HPLC (or UPLC) to mass spectrometry for identification and quantitation","LC to UV accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CE to MS mediated by specific receptor activation","MS to MS without affecting the primary tissue pathway","GC to MS leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'HPLC (or UPLC) to mass spectrometry for identification and quantitation', 'LC-MS couples liquid chromatography (HPLC/UPLC) separation with mass spectrometry detection, providing both separation and molecular identification/quantification of analytes.'),
('5b2ef029-ac15-5abb-abcf-fe192fb6f0f9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'A 55-year-old female undergoes screening for HIV infection. The initial screening test utilizes patient serum to detect both HIV-1/2 antibodies and the gp24 antigen. Which immunodiagnostic technique is most commonly used for this combined screening?', '["Polymerase chain reaction (PCR)","Enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA)","Western blot","Immunohistochemistry","Northern blot"]'::jsonb, 'Enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA)', 'Combined antigen/antibody detection assays (fourth-generation tests) for HIV screening are performed using Enzyme-Linked Immunosorbent Assay (ELISA). ELISA is highly sensitive and automated, making it the preferred screening method before confirmatory testing.'),
('7dbea6e9-3f6f-5f8a-9a76-12bbad8026b9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'A 50-year-old female with suspected acute viral meningitis undergoes a lumbar puncture. The physician wants to detect the presence of enterovirus RNA in the cerebrospinal fluid. Which molecular diagnostic method is most appropriate for this purpose?', '["Southern blot resulting in decreased functional activity","Reverse transcription polymerase chain reaction (RT-PCR)","Flow cytometry mediated by specific receptor activation","ELISA without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Western blot leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Reverse transcription polymerase chain reaction (RT-PCR)', 'Detection of viral RNA requires reverse transcription to synthesize complementary DNA (cDNA), followed by PCR amplification (RT-PCR). PCR is highly sensitive and specific for detecting nucleic acids in CSF, whereas Southern blotting is used for genomic DNA and Western/ELISA for proteins.'),
('298bbda1-8d7b-5df5-9a4b-d6dac0535ed6'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'Conditional knockout uses:', '["RNAi resulting in decreased functional activity","CRISPR accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Random KO mediated by specific receptor activation","Global KO without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cre-loxP system to delete gene in specific tissues or times"]'::jsonb, 'Cre-loxP system to delete gene in specific tissues or times', 'Conditional knockouts use the Cre-loxP system: loxP-flanked gene segments are deleted only in tissues or at times when Cre recombinase is active.'),
('d911e881-ded2-5507-9f2d-815c8ff926a4'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of Diagnostic Techniques Pt2, In affinity chromatography:', '["Hydrophobicity separates resulting in decreased functional activity","Charge separates accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Size separates mediated by specific receptor activation","Solubility separates without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A specific ligand on the column binds target molecule; eluted with excess ligand or pH change"]'::jsonb, 'A specific ligand on the column binds target molecule; eluted with excess ligand or pH change', 'Affinity chromatography uses a specific ligand immobilized on the column to selectively bind the target molecule. Elution is achieved with excess free ligand, pH change, or ionic strength variation.'),
('3ba52200-77ef-5f48-b8bb-6ff2416f6b75'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'Humanized mice are:', '["Mice carrying human genes or human immune cells for biomedical research","Transgenic only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cloned humans mediated by specific receptor activation","CRISPR only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Knockout only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mice carrying human genes or human immune cells for biomedical research', 'Humanized mice carry human genes, tissues, or immune cells, allowing the study of human-specific diseases and therapies in an animal model.'),
('deeea6d5-28f8-5509-a3fe-d72b30b98d2f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'In the study of Diagnostic Techniques Pt2, Pulsed-field gel electrophoresis (PFGE) separates:', '["Proteins resulting in decreased functional activity","RNA accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Small DNA fragments mediated by specific receptor activation","Lipids without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Very large DNA molecules (entire chromosomes/bacterial genomes)"]'::jsonb, 'Very large DNA molecules (entire chromosomes/bacterial genomes)', 'PFGE separates very large DNA molecules (10 kb to 10 Mb) by periodically changing the direction of the electric field, allowing resolution of entire chromosomes and bacterial genomes.'),
('da114f6f-53c4-52bc-8c8f-efedee75e9c0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Diagnostic Techniques Pt2: Lowry method for protein is based on:', '["Kjeldahl method resulting in decreased functional activity","UV absorption at 280 nm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Biuret reaction followed by Folin-Ciocalteu reagent reduction","Only Biuret reaction without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bradford dye binding leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Biuret reaction followed by Folin-Ciocalteu reagent reduction', 'The Lowry method combines the Biuret reaction (Cu2+-peptide complex) with reduction of Folin-Ciocalteu reagent by aromatic amino acids and the Cu+ generated, producing a blue color measured at 750 nm.'),
('ceacd8af-02d3-5cfd-8d64-01530bfd3fa6'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Diagnostic Techniques Pt2: Radioimmunoassay (RIA) was developed by:', '["Rosalyn Yalow and Solomon Berson","Kohler and Milstein","Porter and Edelman","Pauling","Sanger"]'::jsonb, 'Rosalyn Yalow and Solomon Berson', 'RIA was developed by Rosalyn Yalow and Solomon Berson in the 1950s, using radiolabeled antigens competing with unlabeled antigens for limited antibody binding sites.'),
('b37b2148-a2df-56df-9770-087af10a8aa2'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'Meganucleases are:', '["Small restriction enzymes resulting in decreased functional activity","Homing endonucleases with long recognition sequences for genome editing","Cas9 mediated by specific receptor activation","Zinc finger proteins without affecting the primary tissue pathway","TALEs leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Homing endonucleases with long recognition sequences for genome editing', 'Meganucleases are homing endonucleases that recognize long DNA sequences (14-40 bp), allowing very specific genome editing.'),
('07e9ecfc-07ca-5585-a88d-59e6ce7137a3'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Diagnostic Techniques Pt2: Antigen excess (prozone effect) causes:', '["No significant change resulting in decreased functional activity","Precipitation only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Falsely high results mediated by specific receptor activation","Accurate results without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Falsely low results in nephelometry/turbidimetry (soluble complexes)"]'::jsonb, 'Falsely low results in nephelometry/turbidimetry (soluble complexes)', 'In antigen excess (prozone), soluble immune complexes form instead of large precipitating complexes, causing falsely low or absent signal in nephelometry/turbidimetry.'),
('4b32ebc6-151c-5371-a6ac-707ed15df718'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'Channelrhodopsin, used in optogenetics, is best described as which type of molecule?', '["A voltage-gated sodium channel","A light-gated cation channel from algae","AG-protein coupled receptor","A light-gated chloride pump","A neurotransmitter receptor"]'::jsonb, 'A light-gated cation channel from algae', 'Channelrhodopsin is a light-gated cation channel from algae that is used in optogenetics to control neuronal activity using light.'),
('a3900de8-6f8f-5d91-bc16-21a66f3993c3'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'Halorhodopsin is a:', '["Proton pump resulting in decreased functional activity","Light-activated chloride pump that hyperpolarizes neurons","Cation channel mediated by specific receptor activation","Sodium channel without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Potassium channel leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Light-activated chloride pump that hyperpolarizes neurons', 'Halorhodopsin is a light-activated chloride pump from archaea; by pumping chloride into neurons it hyperpolarizes them, allowing optogenetic silencing of activity.'),
('9c302b16-bd3b-5050-a52a-8c168174043a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'Bioluminescence imaging uses:', '["Luciferase enzymes that produce light upon substrate addition for in vivo imaging","Fluorescent proteins accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Radioactive tracers mediated by specific receptor activation","MRI contrast without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ultrasound leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Luciferase enzymes that produce light upon substrate addition for in vivo imaging', 'Bioluminescence imaging uses luciferase enzymes that emit light when they act on their substrate, allowing noninvasive tracking of cells and gene expression in living animals.'),
('fbf20f40-03be-55e3-a343-a36d51536792'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of Diagnostic Techniques Pt2, Alkaline phosphatase isoenzymes can be separated by:', '["Only immunoassay","Only electrophoresis","Only heat stability","Only chemical inhibition","Electrophoresis and heat stability"]'::jsonb, 'Electrophoresis and heat stability', 'Alkaline phosphatase isoenzymes (bone, liver, intestine, placental) can be separated by electrophoresis and distinguished by heat stability and chemical inhibition patterns.'),
('0094298d-809e-58a5-97e2-810344b87c66'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'Tissue-specific promoters allow:', '["Inducible expression resulting in decreased functional activity","Constitutive expression accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ubiquitous expression mediated by specific receptor activation","Expression of transgene in a particular cell type or tissue","Expression leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Expression of transgene in a particular cell type or tissue', 'Tissue-specific promoters restrict transgene expression to a particular cell type or tissue, avoiding unwanted expression in other tissues.'),
('a09218ce-6ad5-5721-b476-b6f4cc7a584b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'Somatic cell nuclear transfer (SCNT) creates:', '["Stem cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Cloned animals by transferring nucleus from somatic cell into enucleated oocyte","Transgenic animals mediated by specific receptor activation","Knockout animals without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Genetically identical animals leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cloned animals by transferring nucleus from somatic cell into enucleated oocyte', 'Somatic cell nuclear transfer creates cloned animals by transferring the nucleus of a somatic cell into an enucleated oocyte, which develops into an animal genetically identical to the nucleus donor.'),
('e3dfd6ee-ba6e-56b4-9883-b26e3b76ddce'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Diagnostic Techniques Pt2: Lipemia interferes by:', '["Altering ionic strength resulting in decreased functional activity","Changing pH accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Precipitating proteins mediated by specific receptor activation","Binding analytes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Scattering light (turbidity) in spectrophotometric assays"]'::jsonb, 'Scattering light (turbidity) in spectrophotometric assays', 'Lipemia (turbid lipemic serum) interferes with spectrophotometric assays by scattering light, causing falsely elevated absorbance readings. Can be addressed by ultracentrifugation or sample dilution.'),
('cf74b228-4954-59bc-90a3-9b146b927212'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'Fluorescence-activated cell sorting (FACS) separates:', '["DNAs resulting in decreased functional activity","Lipids accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cells based on fluorescent labeling and light scatter properties","RNAs without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Proteins leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cells based on fluorescent labeling and light scatter properties', 'FACS sorts cells by passing them in single file through a laser and using fluorescence and light-scatter signals to separate specific cell populations.'),
('20a55e49-9800-5e5b-874f-6df9e00f3572'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of Diagnostic Techniques Pt2, Western blot after 2D electrophoresis is called:', '["2D Western blot or immunoblot","Southwestern blot","Southern blot","Far Western blot","Northern blot"]'::jsonb, '2D Western blot or immunoblot', 'Western blotting detects specific proteins by SDS-PAGE, transfer to membrane, and antibody-based detection.'),
('ca2bd54f-3b5d-5bac-97d4-95391d50c90b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'Co-immunoprecipitation (co-IP) identifies:', '["Lipid interactions resulting in decreased functional activity","Protein-protein interactions (proteins bound to the target)","Carbohydrate interactions mediated by specific receptor activation","Protein-DNA interactions without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Protein-RNA interactions leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Protein-protein interactions (proteins bound to the target)', 'Co-immunoprecipitation identifies proteins that physically interact with the target by immunoprecipitating the target and detecting the coprecipitated partners by Western blot or mass spectrometry.'),
('af85cd32-a898-54f6-af97-cbcd48bb8345'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'Yeast two-hybrid (Y2H) system detects:', '["Lipid-protein resulting in decreased functional activity","DNA-protein accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","RNA-protein mediated by specific receptor activation","Protein-protein interactions in living yeast cells","Nucleic acid interactions leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Protein-protein interactions in living yeast cells', 'The yeast two-hybrid system detects protein-protein interactions by reconstituting a split transcription factor when bait and prey proteins bind each other inside living yeast cells.'),
('c111c36d-cf07-5ae9-89ac-72761129ebfa'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'SELEX (systematic evolution of ligands by exponential enrichment) selects:', '["Small molecules resulting in decreased functional activity","Peptides accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Proteins mediated by specific receptor activation","Antibodies without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Aptamers (nucleic acid ligands) that bind target molecules"]'::jsonb, 'Aptamers (nucleic acid ligands) that bind target molecules', 'SELEX iteratively selects aptamers by repeated rounds of binding, partitioning, and amplification until nucleic-acid ligands with high affinity for the target emerge.'),
('0abe0cc3-6daf-5e59-8a93-0a765fe8e541'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'The Polymerase Chain Reaction is used in:', '["Cloning","Genotyping","Forensics","Sequencing","All: diagnostics"]'::jsonb, 'All: diagnostics', 'PCR underpins cloning, genotyping, forensic identification, library preparation for sequencing, and molecular diagnostics, making it a core tool across these applications.'),
('efcb8f33-5427-5691-8f7d-16081d7aa1d6'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'Real-time PCR provides:', '["Semi-quantitative resulting in decreased functional activity","Quantitative data on starting template amount","Qualitative only mediated by specific receptor activation","End-point only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only presence/absence leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Quantitative data on starting template amount', 'Real-time PCR measures fluorescence during each cycle, and the cycle threshold (Ct) is inversely proportional to the starting amount of template, allowing quantitation.'),
('4ec90074-fabd-5d9e-9724-58cabd3dc4b9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'PCR product can be analyzed by:', '["Gel only","Sequencing","All: agarose gel electrophoresis","Melt curve","Capillary electrophoresis"]'::jsonb, 'All: agarose gel electrophoresis', 'PCR products can be verified and analyzed by agarose gel electrophoresis, sequencing, melting-curve analysis, or capillary electrophoresis.'),
('10e43b2c-9d41-5b2d-b7a6-d5d677b43e28'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'Melt curve analysis in qPCR:', '["Measures absorbance resulting in decreased functional activity","Measures extension accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Monitors DNA denaturation as temperature increases; identifies products by Tm","Measures annealing without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Measures fluorescence leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Monitors DNA denaturation as temperature increases; identifies products by Tm', 'Melting-curve analysis raises the temperature while measuring fluorescence; each product dissociates at a characteristic Tm, revealing product identity and purity.'),
('19ae9d07-0619-5c50-b82a-5c0d95caccdb'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'DUTP and uracil-N-glycosylase (UNG) prevent:', '["Non-specific amplification resulting in decreased functional activity","Carryover contamination in PCR (UNG degrades uracil-containing DNA)","Polymerase errors mediated by specific receptor activation","Primer-dimer without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mispriming leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Carryover contamination in PCR (UNG degrades uracil-containing DNA)', 'Incorporating dUTP into PCR products makes them sensitive to uracil-N-glycosylase, which destroys contaminating products from previous reactions before the new PCR begins.'),
('f8a983a8-eb33-5d9f-a398-106a13faf24a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'Sanger sequencing is considered the gold standard for:', '["Confirming variants identified by NGS and sequencing smaller regions","RNA sequencing accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Whole genome sequencing mediated by specific receptor activation","Exome sequencing without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Epigenetic sequencing leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Confirming variants identified by NGS and sequencing smaller regions', 'Sanger sequencing remains the gold standard for confirming variants detected by NGS and for sequencing smaller targeted regions because of its high per-base accuracy.'),
('0554a45f-5156-5a21-8f35-87e08fab1e3c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'The clinical sensitivity of NGS depends on:', '["All: depth of coverage","Bioinformatics","Variant allele frequency","Library quality","Depth only"]'::jsonb, 'All: depth of coverage', 'The clinical sensitivity of NGS depends on sequencing depth, bioinformatics pipelines, and library quality; low depth or poor coverage can lead to missed variants.'),
('c7fb2d11-bb07-5d73-85dd-d97708cf5e0d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'FASTQ format is:', '["Standard format for raw sequencing reads with quality scores","Database format accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Annotation format mediated by specific receptor activation","Alignment file format without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Variant file format leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Standard format for raw sequencing reads with quality scores', 'FASTQ is the standard file format for raw sequencing reads, containing the nucleotide sequence plus a Phred quality score for each base.'),
('7f94f4c9-ba4b-56b8-a4e7-da319286672f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'In the study of Diagnostic Techniques Pt2, Confocal microscopy uses:', '["Phase contrast","Dark field","Improving resolution and contrast","Fluorescence only","A pinhole to reject out-of-focus light"]'::jsonb, 'A pinhole to reject out-of-focus light', 'Confocal microscopy uses a pinhole aperture to reject out-of-focus light, enabling optical sectioning and improved resolution and contrast compared to conventional fluorescence microscopy.'),
('a8b02fa7-f094-562f-8cab-e0f8362f4557'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'The reference human genome (GRCh38) is:', '["One individual''s genome resulting in decreased functional activity","A composite genome assembly used as standard for comparison","A consensus of many individuals mediated by specific receptor activation","A theoretical sequence without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The Neanderthal genome leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A composite genome assembly used as standard for comparison', 'GRCh38 is a composite reference genome assembled from many individuals; it serves as the standard for aligning and interpreting human sequencing data.'),
('4776470f-e799-5ff4-bdf4-1f2a1520874c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Diagnostic Techniques Pt2: Comparative genomic hybridization (CGH) is used for:', '["Protein identification resulting in decreased functional activity","Detecting chromosomal copy number changes (gains/losses)","Metabolite profiling mediated by specific receptor activation","Gene expression without affecting the primary tissue pathway","DNA sequencing leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Detecting chromosomal copy number changes (gains/losses)', 'CGH is used for detecting chromosomal copy number changes (gains and losses) across the entire genome without requiring cell culture, useful for tumor genome profiling.'),
('d37c17db-153d-5dfb-ad77-eba349f477f7'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'HLA-B*5701 genotyping:', '["Predicts abacavir hypersensitivity (required before abacavir use)","Statins accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Warfarin dosing mediated by specific receptor activation","Clopidogrel response without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Codeine response leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Predicts abacavir hypersensitivity (required before abacavir use)', 'HLA-B*5701 genotyping identifies patients at risk of abacavir hypersensitivity syndrome, so testing is required before starting abacavir.'),
('a113d365-8b07-58e6-99ce-df290d8331fe'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Diagnostic Techniques Pt2: Phalloidin stains:', '["Intermediate filaments resulting in decreased functional activity","F-actin (filamentous actin) for cytoskeleton visualization","Microtubules mediated by specific receptor activation","Mitochondria without affecting the primary tissue pathway","DNA leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'F-actin (filamentous actin) for cytoskeleton visualization', 'Phalloidin is a toxin from Amanita phalloides mushroom that specifically binds F-actin (filamentous actin). Fluorescently labeled phalloidin is used to visualize the actin cytoskeleton.'),
('05dbfae7-2ea5-5ea2-a302-48644f6a3fdf'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'In the study of Diagnostic Techniques Pt2, The urine reagent strip (dipstick) tests for:', '["Blood resulting in decreased functional activity","Ketones accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Protein mediated by specific receptor activation","Multiple analytes: glucose","PH leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Multiple analytes: glucose', 'Urine reagent strips (dipsticks) test for multiple analytes simultaneously including glucose, protein, blood, ketones, bilirubin, leukocyte esterase, nitrite, pH, and specific gravity.'),
('0724939a-e53f-5ad4-a919-fec3141cb478'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Diagnostic Techniques Pt2: The Levey-Jennings chart is used to:', '["Plot standard curves resulting in decreased functional activity","Monitor QC data over time for shifts and trends in test performance","Design experiments mediated by specific receptor activation","Record patient results without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Calculate reference ranges leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Monitor QC data over time for shifts and trends in test performance', 'A Levey-Jennings chart plots QC results over time to monitor for shifts (sudden changes) and trends (gradual changes) in test performance, enabling detection of systematic and random errors.'),
('4d416290-c1a4-5bf9-8348-43acf9cffffb'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'Electroporation in gene therapy:', '["Microinjection resulting in decreased functional activity","Viral delivery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Chemical transfection mediated by specific receptor activation","Uses electric pulses to deliver DNA into cells (non-viral)","Biolistics leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Uses electric pulses to deliver DNA into cells (non-viral)', 'Electroporation is a non-viral method that uses electric pulses to create transient pores in the cell membrane, allowing DNA entry into cells.'),
('dab3e41b-64f0-5c1f-a9ff-b6e48375e557'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'Lipofection uses:', '["Calcium phosphate resulting in decreased functional activity","Viral capsids accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Microinjection mediated by specific receptor activation","Electric pulses without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cationic liposomes that complex with DNA and fuse with cell membrane"]'::jsonb, 'Cationic liposomes that complex with DNA and fuse with cell membrane', 'Lipofection uses cationic liposomes that complex with negatively charged DNA and fuse with the cell membrane to deliver the DNA into cells.'),
('9432bca6-d8e4-5ef0-8108-711d89194629'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Diagnostic Techniques Pt2: HbA2 is elevated (3.5-7%) in:', '["Normal individuals","Beta-thalassemia trait (minor)","HbC trait","Alpha-thalassemia","Sickle cell trait"]'::jsonb, 'Beta-thalassemia trait (minor)', 'HbA2 is elevated (3.5-7%) in beta-thalassemia trait, making it the key diagnostic marker. Normal HbA2 is 2-3.5%.'),
('4f143f68-a89c-54af-8f2c-bf81479bb2b0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'In the study of Diagnostic Techniques Pt2, Ouchterlony double immunodiffusion detects:', '["Partial identity resulting in decreased functional activity","Antigen-antibody reactions with patterns showing identity","Quantitative antigen levels mediated by specific receptor activation","Or non-identity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Antibody titers leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Antigen-antibody reactions with patterns showing identity', 'Ouchterlony double diffusion detects antigen-antibody reactions. Patterns show identity (fused arcs), non-identity (crossing lines), or partial identity (spurs) between adjacent antigen wells.'),
('d6cc9fe7-9b4c-5e81-aeb5-1d31db1d5272'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Diagnostic Techniques Pt2: The difference between a monochromator and a filter photometer is:', '["Both are the same resulting in decreased functional activity","Filter uses a grating accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Monochromator selects continuous wavelength band; filters pass discrete bands","Monochromator uses filters without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Filter is more precise leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Monochromator selects continuous wavelength band; filters pass discrete bands', 'A monochromator uses a grating or prism to select a continuous band of wavelengths from a polychromatic source, while filter photometers use interference or absorption filters to pass discrete wavelength bands.'),
('e3a01780-1203-56d6-ac8d-92620655c691'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'Whole genome sequencing has the advantage of:', '["Faster than exome resulting in decreased functional activity","Lower cost than exome accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Higher sequencing depth than exome mediated by specific receptor activation","Detecting only coding variants without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Detecting intronic variants and structural variants missed by exome sequencing"]'::jsonb, 'Detecting intronic variants and structural variants missed by exome sequencing', 'Whole genome sequencing covers non-coding regions and structural variants that exome sequencing misses, though at higher cost and greater data-processing demands.'),
('21888967-fe88-586e-9812-ad88af867e46'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'ACMG secondary findings:', '["Only cancer genes resulting in decreased functional activity","Genes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only cardiac genes mediated by specific receptor activation","All genes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","59 medically actionable genes recommended for return from clinical sequencing"]'::jsonb, '59 medically actionable genes recommended for return from clinical sequencing', 'The ACMG recommends returning pathogenic variants in a defined set of 59 medically actionable genes even when they are incidental to the original test indication.'),
('aa641c1f-c39b-5d0b-9d83-1e28f81fbfbd'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Diagnostic Techniques Pt2: ChIP-seq is:', '["ChIP followed by next-generation sequencing of the precipitated DNA","ChIP followed by electrophoresis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","ChIP followed by Southern blot mediated by specific receptor activation","ChIP followed by PCR without affecting the primary tissue pathway","ChIP followed by microarray leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'ChIP followed by next-generation sequencing of the precipitated DNA', 'ChIP-seq combines chromatin immunoprecipitation (ChIP) with next-generation sequencing to identify genome-wide binding sites of proteins (histones, transcription factors) to DNA.'),
('4eaadb75-21f5-5dbb-9fe8-01ca66a75e38'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'In the study of Diagnostic Techniques Pt2, Hemoglobin electrophoresis at alkaline pH (pH 8.6):', '["HbA2","HbC migrate differently","HbS","HbA","HbF"]'::jsonb, 'HbA', 'At alkaline pH (8.6), hemoglobin electrophoresis separates Hb variants by charge: HbA migrates fastest toward anode, followed by HbA2, HbF, HbS, and HbC.'),
('e6cc5b35-502d-5176-b77a-b4a982e8eba1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques - Diagnostic Techniques Part 2', 'Array CGH is the first-tier test for:', '["Karyotyping resulting in decreased functional activity","FISH accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Gene panels mediated by specific receptor activation","Chromosomal microarray analysis in developmental delay and congenital anomalies","Sequencing leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Chromosomal microarray analysis in developmental delay and congenital anomalies', 'Chromosomal microarray (array CGH) is the first-tier test for developmental delay and congenital anomalies because it detects copy-number changes at high resolution.'),
('5275584c-2797-5057-baef-86ff5817c75e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'General Pharmacology - General Pharmacology', 'Simple diffusion across cell membrane depends on:', '["Molecular weight only resulting in decreased functional activity","Lipid solubility and concentration gradient","Carrier proteins mediated by specific receptor activation","ATP energy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Receptor binding leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lipid solubility and concentration gradient', 'Passive diffusion depends on lipid solubility, concentration gradient, and molecular size.'),
('fd342d3e-20cd-54b9-976a-70a00e5853cd'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'General Pharmacology - General Pharmacology', 'Weak acids are best absorbed in:', '["Intestine (alkaline pH)","Stomach (acidic pH)","Colon","Rectum","Esophagus"]'::jsonb, 'Stomach (acidic pH)', 'Weak acids are unionized in acidic gastric pH and absorbed.'),
('86995bfd-00be-5796-8abd-65408c4258c6'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'General Pharmacology - General Pharmacology', 'Weak bases are best absorbed in:', '["Stomach resulting in decreased functional activity","Duodenum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Jejunum/Ileum (alkaline pH)","Colon without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Esophagus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Jejunum/Ileum (alkaline pH)', 'Weak bases are unionized in alkaline intestinal pH and absorbed.'),
('c6bb5d94-1e01-59cf-89e9-c228b9d15a58'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'General Pharmacology - General Pharmacology', 'Aspirin is classified as:', '["Weak base","Strong acid","Weak acid","Strong base","Neutral drug"]'::jsonb, 'Weak acid', 'Aspirin (acetylsalicylic acid) is a weak acid with pKa 3.5.'),
('91d924b2-7213-58b7-897a-be589bd2a0d3'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'General Pharmacology - General Pharmacology', 'Morphine is classified as:', '["Weak acid","Strong acid","Weak base","Strong base","Neutral drug"]'::jsonb, 'Weak base', 'Morphine is a weak base with pKa 8.0.'),
('1d9bb481-dff9-5dcb-9d9d-2a72a77b9f9f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'General Pharmacology - General Pharmacology', 'During therapeutic management of General Pharmacology, Rifampicin induces:', '["CYP3A4 and other CYPs","CYP2D6 only","CYP2C19 only","CYP1A2 only","CYP2C9 only"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4 and other CYPs', 'Rifampicin induces CYP3A4, CYP2C9, CYP2C19, and P-gp.'),
('37554658-93ee-5d81-ae7d-4ca1a5bcca0d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'General Pharmacology - General Pharmacology', 'First-pass metabolism occurs primarily in:', '["Kidneys","Lungs","Liver","Intestine","Spleen"]'::jsonb, 'Liver', 'Orally absorbed drugs enter portal circulation and are metabolized by liver first.'),
('6e738f32-9df9-58e3-9de9-ef709eeebc7f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'General Pharmacology - General Pharmacology', 'Volume of distribution (Vd) is:', '["Actual volume of body water resulting in decreased functional activity","Theoretical volume to contain total drug at plasma concentration","Rate of drug elimination mediated by specific receptor activation","Half-life of drug without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Clearance rate leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Theoretical volume to contain total drug at plasma concentration', 'Vd = Amount of drug in body / Plasma concentration.'),
('747d5fa8-e75a-534d-979b-b5152a7fb405'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'General Pharmacology - General Pharmacology', 'Large Vd indicates:', '["Drug confined to plasma","Extensive tissue distribution","Rapid elimination","High protein binding","Low bioavailability"]'::jsonb, 'Extensive tissue distribution', 'Large Vd (>1 L/kg) indicates drug distributes extensively into tissues.'),
('928c7098-570e-530b-9c55-91b3896df255'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'General Pharmacology - General Pharmacology', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in General Pharmacology: Weak acids are trapped in:', '["Alkaline urine","Acidic stomach","Blood pH 7.4","Duodenum","Jejunum"]'::jsonb, 'Alkaline urine', 'Weak acids become ionised (trapped) in alkaline environments.'),
('33a4fa8d-b8b7-5b9b-bb07-87d882e9278f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'General Pharmacology - General Pharmacology', 'During therapeutic management of General Pharmacology, Zero-order kinetics means:', '["Constant amount eliminated/time","Constant fraction/time","Dose-dependent elimination","First-order at high doses","Saturable absorption"]'::jsonb, 'Constant amount eliminated/time', 'Zero-order: constant amount eliminated regardless of concentration.'),
('435b9277-cb20-568f-96dc-cb979221cba4'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'General Pharmacology - General Pharmacology', 'Loading dose depends primarily on:', '["Clearance","Half-life","Volume of distribution","Bioavailability","Protein binding"]'::jsonb, 'Volume of distribution', 'Loading dose depends on Vd to fill the apparent volume quickly.'),
('13bab17e-1b1a-52b7-9395-a942e118974f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'General Pharmacology - General Pharmacology', 'Maintenance dose depends primarily on:', '["Volume of distribution","Loading dose","Clearance","Half-life","Protein binding"]'::jsonb, 'Clearance', 'Maintenance dose is determined by clearance to maintain steady-state.'),
('636bd384-206b-5408-a2a9-9dadb094867e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'General Pharmacology - General Pharmacology', 'Half-life is proportional to:', '["Clearance / Vd","Vd / Clearance","Vd × Clearance","Clearance × Bioavailability","Loading dose / Maintenance dose"]'::jsonb, 'Vd / Clearance', 't1/2 = 0.693 × Vd / Cl.'),
('02e6fa27-d045-5962-8afd-960a752e5651'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'General Pharmacology - General Pharmacology', 'Steady-state (95%) is reached after:', '["1 half-life","2 half-lives","3 half-lives","4-5 half-lives","7 half-lives"]'::jsonb, '4-5 half-lives', 'After 4-5 half-lives, 94-97% of steady-state is reached.'),
('53a1ce8a-6174-505a-a4f6-915c6c5d97f9'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'General Pharmacology - General Pharmacology', 'During therapeutic management of General Pharmacology, Acetylation polymorphism affects:', '["Isoniazid, hydralazine, procainamide","Warfarin, phenytoin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Codeine, tramadol mediated by specific receptor activation","Omeprazole without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Diazepam leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Isoniazid, hydralazine, procainamide', 'NAT2 polymorphism: slow vs fast acetylators.'),
('679c2541-d5c7-579d-9b39-a38b0122fb66'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'General Pharmacology - General Pharmacology', 'First-order kinetics means:', '["Constant amount eliminated/time","Constant fraction eliminated/time","Saturation kinetics","Non-linear kinetics","Dose-dependent"]'::jsonb, 'Constant fraction eliminated/time', 'First-order: constant percentage eliminated per unit time.'),
('f385caa5-644b-5ebb-937d-97fb75fae1a4'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'General Pharmacology - General Pharmacology', 'Phenytoin exhibits zero-order kinetics because:', '["Renal tubular secretion saturates","CYP enzymes become saturated","Protein binding saturates","Gut absorption saturates","Biliary excretion saturates"]'::jsonb, 'CYP enzymes become saturated', 'CYP2C9/CYP3A4 become saturated at therapeutic phenytoin concentrations.'),
('2b19146a-7bde-5a5e-b358-a47b9ba1d7f8'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'General Pharmacology - General Pharmacology', 'Ethanol exhibits zero-order kinetics because:', '["CYP2E1 is saturated","ADH is saturated","ALDH is saturated","Renal excretion saturates","Gastric absorption saturates"]'::jsonb, 'ADH is saturated', 'Alcohol dehydrogenase (ADH) becomes saturated at relatively low ethanol concentrations.'),
('bcd20fc1-8f83-516b-998d-b4498922a801'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'General Pharmacology - General Pharmacology', 'The most important CYP450 enzyme is:', '["CYP1A2","CYP2C9","CYP2C19","CYP2D6","CYP3A4"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4', 'CYP3A4 metabolizes approximately 50% of all drugs.'),
('790e5b0b-9d47-5679-95f1-350ff120dd5c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'General Pharmacology - General Pharmacology', 'CYP2D6 metabolizes approximately:', '["10% of drugs","20% of drugs","25-30% of drugs","50% of drugs","75% of drugs"]'::jsonb, '25-30% of drugs', 'CYP2D6 metabolizes about 25-30% of clinically used drugs.'),
('19b8e64f-682a-50b1-8a7c-a500054c7b8a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'General Pharmacology - General Pharmacology', 'CYP2C9 metabolizes:', '["Metoprolol","Omeprazole","Warfarin (S-enantiomer)","Codeine","Erythromycin"]'::jsonb, 'Warfarin (S-enantiomer)', 'CYP2C9 metabolizes the more potent S-warfarin.'),
('df60b453-20c5-54d9-a4b9-80bc5636e54f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'General Pharmacology - General Pharmacology', 'CYP2C19 metabolizes:', '["Metoprolol","Omeprazole","Warfarin","Codeine","Erythromycin"]'::jsonb, 'Omeprazole', 'CYP2C19 is the primary enzyme for omeprazole metabolism.'),
('a41f61bd-8ad6-5511-bbb1-3a40fa5cf478'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'General Pharmacology - General Pharmacology', 'Codeine is activated to morphine by:', '["CYP3A4","CYP2D6","CYP2C9","CYP2C19","CYP1A2"]'::jsonb, 'CYP2D6', 'CYP2D6 converts codeine to morphine (active metabolite).'),
('77ba37b1-c127-55f4-84d9-1ab983238386'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'General Pharmacology - General Pharmacology', 'CYP2D6 poor metabolizers taking codeine experience:', '["Enhanced analgesia","No analgesic effect","Respiratory depression","Increased metabolism","Toxicity"]'::jsonb, 'No analgesic effect', 'Poor metabolizers cannot convert codeine to morphine.'),
('0b5b576f-e2b5-5c28-b668-ac7a1536bfdc'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'General Pharmacology - General Pharmacology', 'CYP2D6 ultrarapid metabolizers taking codeine risk:', '["No effect","Toxicity from excessive morphine","Decreased analgesia","Drug resistance","Allergic reaction"]'::jsonb, 'Toxicity from excessive morphine', 'Ultrarapid metabolizers produce too much morphine.'),
('02a68c31-4c6e-5eea-8b0f-f783ca4e9fab'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'General Pharmacology - General Pharmacology', 'Rifampicin is a potent:', '["CYP450 inhibitor","CYP450 inducer","CYP450 substrate only","P-gp inhibitor","CYP2D6 inhibitor"]'::jsonb, 'CYP450 inducer', 'Rifampicin potently induces CYP3A4, CYP2C9, CYP2C19, and P-gp.'),
('f11eee94-34ab-58ef-926b-b8561a34ddae'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'General Pharmacology - General Pharmacology', 'Rifampicin induces all EXCEPT:', '["CYP3A4","CYP2C9","CYP2C19","CYP2D6","P-glycoprotein"]'::jsonb, 'CYP2D6', 'Rifampicin does NOT induce CYP2D6.'),
('04c4008b-eb52-5ef3-8db2-703b3c4635a1'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'General Pharmacology - General Pharmacology', 'Ketoconazole is a potent:', '["CYP3A4 inducer","CYP3A4 inhibitor","CYP2D6 inducer","CYP2C9 inhibitor","P-gp inducer"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4 inhibitor', 'Ketoconazole is a potent CYP3A4 inhibitor.'),
('4fca8821-73c8-5598-b475-d186afbc6df8'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'General Pharmacology - General Pharmacology', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in General Pharmacology: Orally absorbed drugs first enter:', '["Portal vein","Systemic circulation","Lymphatics","IVC","Thoracic duct"]'::jsonb, 'Portal vein', 'Portal vein -> liver (first-pass) -> systemic circulation.'),
('5a079a62-b7eb-55d4-a2b0-8f98b64a2063'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'General Pharmacology - General Pharmacology', 'During therapeutic management of General Pharmacology, Acetaminophen toxic metabolite:', '["NAPQI","MPP+","MPTP","N-oxide","Sulfate conjugate"]'::jsonb, 'NAPQI', 'NAPQI normally detoxified by glutathione. Overdose depletes glutathione.'),
('9c4f1efb-aa15-5e4c-bb78-844663e42722'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'General Pharmacology - General Pharmacology', 'Regarding clinical cases in General Pharmacology: Acetaminophen antidote:', '["N-acetylcysteine","Flumazenil","Naloxone","Fomepizole","Deferoxamine"]'::jsonb, 'N-acetylcysteine', 'NAC replenishes glutathione stores.'),
('eb29ec5a-6b69-5abb-b187-80b18ac759c1'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'General Pharmacology - General Pharmacology', 'Warfarin + rifampicin results in:', '["Increased INR resulting in decreased functional activity","Decreased INR (reduced warfarin effect)","No change mediated by specific receptor activation","Increased bleeding without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Warfarin toxicity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased INR (reduced warfarin effect)', 'Rifampicin induces CYP2C9, increasing warfarin metabolism.'),
('311df0f6-c9db-52a0-b53d-811b86356dd3'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'General Pharmacology - General Pharmacology', 'Warfarin + fluconazole results in:', '["Decreased INR","Increased INR (bleeding risk)","No change","Decreased warfarin metabolism","Both B and D"]'::jsonb, 'Both B and D', 'Fluconazole inhibits CYP2C9, decreasing warfarin metabolism.'),
('a81cce62-3c3e-5515-a17d-fff310d9f427'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'General Pharmacology - General Pharmacology', 'Therapeutic index (TI) is:', '["ED50 / TD50","TD50 / ED50","LD50 / ED50","ED50 / LD50","TD50 / LD50"]'::jsonb, 'TD50 / ED50', 'TI = TD50/ED50. Higher TI means safer drug.'),
('4ae01203-93db-5400-b3c1-ea38db1bc591'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'General Pharmacology - General Pharmacology', 'Narrow therapeutic index drugs require:', '["Less monitoring","Therapeutic drug monitoring","Higher doses","Less frequent dosing","No dose adjustment"]'::jsonb, 'Therapeutic drug monitoring', 'Narrow TI drugs (warfarin, digoxin, lithium) require monitoring.'),
('c39520c0-28ae-5643-8175-9db71363b719'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'General Pharmacology - General Pharmacology', 'Acidic drugs primarily bind to:', '["Alpha-1 acid glycoprotein","Albumin","Globulins","Transferrin","Ceruloplasmin"]'::jsonb, 'Albumin', 'Albumin binds acidic drugs (warfarin, phenytoin, NSAIDs).'),
('0e6b7b48-6b48-5f5d-bba0-242e0c70f02e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'General Pharmacology - General Pharmacology', 'Basic drugs primarily bind to:', '["Albumin","Alpha-1 acid glycoprotein","Transferrin","Ceruloplasmin","Haptoglobin"]'::jsonb, 'Alpha-1 acid glycoprotein', 'AAG binds basic drugs (lidocaine, propranolol).'),
('d25d0ab8-0d78-560a-9023-28401b01377f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'General Pharmacology - General Pharmacology', 'Displacement of warfarin from albumin increases:', '["Warfarin effect","Free warfarin and bleeding risk","Warfarin binding","INR","Both B and D"]'::jsonb, 'Both B and D', 'Displacement increases free warfarin, increasing effect and INR.'),
('90dccc4a-e13d-58d1-865b-ad6165ac8c2c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'General Pharmacology - General Pharmacology', 'Clearance is defined as:', '["Volume of plasma cleared of drug per unit time","Total amount of drug in body","Time to eliminate drug","Rate of absorption","Concentration at steady state"]'::jsonb, 'Volume of plasma cleared of drug per unit time', 'Clearance (Cl) = Rate of elimination / Plasma concentration.'),
('6e180369-e6d9-52f7-9f77-c0e2892dde9a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'General Pharmacology - General Pharmacology', 'High extraction ratio drugs clearance depends on:', '["Intrinsic clearance only","Hepatic blood flow only","Protein binding only","Enzyme activity only","Gut absorption"]'::jsonb, 'Hepatic blood flow only', 'High extraction drugs are flow-limited.'),
('d04011c9-574f-5945-8b4a-01883ef25bf0'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'General Pharmacology - General Pharmacology', 'Low extraction ratio drugs clearance depends on:', '["Hepatic blood flow resulting in decreased functional activity","Intrinsic clearance and protein binding","Gut motility mediated by specific receptor activation","Renal function without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lung function leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Intrinsic clearance and protein binding', 'Low extraction drugs are capacity-limited.'),
('3b4a0729-0304-59ff-a096-e1ae044abf8b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'General Pharmacology - General Pharmacology', 'Urinary alkalinization increases excretion of:', '["Weak bases","Weak acids","Strong acids","Strong bases","Neutral drugs"]'::jsonb, 'Weak acids', 'Alkaline urine ionizes weak acids, preventing reabsorption.'),
('eac31423-63af-569f-bddb-261828c7c4c1'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'General Pharmacology - General Pharmacology', 'Urinary acidification increases excretion of:', '["Weak acids","Weak bases","Strong acids","Neutral drugs","Lipid-soluble drugs"]'::jsonb, 'Weak bases', 'Acidic urine ionizes weak bases, preventing reabsorption.'),
('e11e6754-ab08-5521-8b05-e9bf80d98c4e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'General Pharmacology - General Pharmacology', 'Enterohepatic circulation increases drug:', '["Absorption","Half-life","Onset of action","Peak time","Loading dose"]'::jsonb, 'Half-life', 'Drugs excreted in bile reabsorbed from intestine, increasing half-life.'),
('a3d7cc46-667e-5348-bf15-68ab8660ca5b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'General Pharmacology - General Pharmacology', 'Phase I metabolism is also called:', '["Conjugation","Functionalization","Binding","Excretion","Distribution"]'::jsonb, 'Functionalization', 'Phase I (oxidation, reduction, hydrolysis) introduces functional groups.'),
('231375f5-3262-508e-bfb7-6d44ee902a58'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'General Pharmacology - General Pharmacology', 'Phase II metabolism is:', '["Oxidation only","Conjugation reactions","Reduction only","Hydrolysis only","Deamination only"]'::jsonb, 'Conjugation reactions', 'Phase II: glucuronidation, sulfation, acetylation, methylation.'),
('194df658-7623-5506-a731-1bdecc486c62'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'General Pharmacology - General Pharmacology', 'Glucuronidation is catalyzed by:', '["CYP450 enzymes","UDP-glucuronosyltransferase (UGT)","Sulfotransferase","Glutathione S-transferase","N-acetyltransferase"]'::jsonb, 'UDP-glucuronosyltransferase (UGT)', 'UGT catalyzes glucuronidation, the most common Phase II reaction.'),
('f58dd3d1-7127-5349-92c0-10099b72ccc8'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'General Pharmacology - General Pharmacology', 'N-acetylation polymorphism affects metabolism of:', '["Warfarin","Isoniazid","Phenytoin","Codeine","Omeprazole"]'::jsonb, 'Isoniazid', 'NAT2 polymorphism causes fast vs slow acetylators.'),
('f29ee9c3-2317-578c-8ad9-5e03401ab066'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'General Pharmacology - General Pharmacology', 'Slow acetylators on isoniazid risk:', '["Reduced drug effect","Peripheral neuropathy","Hepatotoxicity","Nephrotoxicity","Cardiotoxicity"]'::jsonb, 'Peripheral neuropathy', 'Slow acetylators accumulate INH, causing peripheral neuropathy.'),
('b9b3d879-6083-5392-b97d-11a450893743'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'General Pharmacology - General Pharmacology', 'Fick''s law states diffusion rate is proportional to:', '["Molecular weight × concentration resulting in decreased functional activity","Surface area × concentration gradient / membrane thickness","pH × pKa mediated by specific receptor activation","Protein binding × molecular weight without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Clearance × Vd leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Surface area × concentration gradient / membrane thickness', 'Fick''s law: Rate = (SA × D × ΔC) / thickness.'),
('b9e45c4e-dc2e-5152-aabb-dd5e8ed2ecb7'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'General Pharmacology - General Pharmacology', 'Most drugs cross GI membrane by:', '["Active transport","Facilitated diffusion","Passive diffusion","Pinocytosis","Endocytosis"]'::jsonb, 'Passive diffusion', 'Most drugs absorb by passive diffusion (lipid solubility dependent).'),
('fbef10af-8ea1-5b4d-a211-c03b02fe8426'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'General Pharmacology - General Pharmacology', 'Oral drugs first enter:', '["Systemic circulation","Portal vein to liver","Lymphatics","Inferior vena cava","Thoracic duct"]'::jsonb, 'Portal vein to liver', 'Oral drugs enter portal circulation, undergo first-pass metabolism.'),
('1a95de51-d78e-5e18-a3e7-517edc04439b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'General Pharmacology - General Pharmacology', 'IV administration bioavailability is:', '["0%","50%","75%","100%","Variable"]'::jsonb, '100%', 'IV route bypasses absorption; bioavailability is 100%.'),
('41dfceef-70ff-5e80-b263-1c38e93da16d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'General Pharmacology - General Pharmacology', 'P-glycoprotein (P-gp) is:', '["Drug metabolizing enzyme resulting in decreased functional activity","Efflux transporter pumping drugs out of cells","Drug binding protein mediated by specific receptor activation","Phase II enzyme without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Receptor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Efflux transporter pumping drugs out of cells', 'P-gp is an efflux pump limiting drug absorption and promoting excretion.'),
('461f3ebe-06a5-563e-a6f1-f2c09b0c3f98'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'General Pharmacology - General Pharmacology', 'Blood-brain barrier limits entry of:', '["Lipophilic drugs","Polar/large molecules","Small molecules","All drugs equally","Only toxic drugs"]'::jsonb, 'Polar/large molecules', 'BBB has tight junctions and P-gp efflux, limiting polar/large molecules.'),
('6ed7eb77-4775-5bae-b242-b208b6cbe1be'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'General Pharmacology - General Pharmacology', 'CNS penetration is enhanced by:', '["High molecular weight","High lipid solubility","High protein binding","High ionization","High water solubility"]'::jsonb, 'High lipid solubility', 'Lipophilic drugs cross BBB more easily.'),
('07549153-e4c3-596d-83c9-f8b1e0cd8ab5'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'General Pharmacology - General Pharmacology', 'GPCRs have how many transmembrane domains?', '["3","5","7","9","12"]'::jsonb, '7', 'G-protein coupled receptors have 7 transmembrane-spanning domains.'),
('4a04ac66-b64f-548c-84c2-f56abe6ed84c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'General Pharmacology - General Pharmacology', 'Full agonist produces:', '["No response","Partial response","Maximum possible response","Inverse response","Blockade of response"]'::jsonb, 'Maximum possible response', 'Full agonist has high efficacy and produces maximum response.'),
('a3e1a100-71b7-539a-b74d-22bc1e41b3bb'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'General Pharmacology - General Pharmacology', 'Partial agonist produces:', '["No response resulting in decreased functional activity","Response less than full agonist even at high doses","Maximum response mediated by specific receptor activation","Inverse response without affecting the primary tissue pathway","No binding leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Response less than full agonist even at high doses', 'Partial agonist has lower efficacy than full agonist.'),
('4c8443e6-7f0e-5131-ba8f-ef5ae23651c3'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'General Pharmacology - General Pharmacology', 'Antagonist produces:', '["Response by itself resulting in decreased functional activity","Blocks agonist response without intrinsic activity","Partial response mediated by specific receptor activation","Enhanced response without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inverse response leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Blocks agonist response without intrinsic activity', 'Antagonist has affinity but zero intrinsic activity.'),
('5a634597-b8d3-5ccb-ba53-f8c9608e687c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'General Pharmacology - General Pharmacology', 'Inverse agonist produces:', '["Same effect as agonist","Response opposite to agonist","No response","Enhanced response","Partial response"]'::jsonb, 'Response opposite to agonist', 'Inverse agonist reduces constitutive activity below baseline.'),
('ff5549bf-ef19-55db-b66b-b5ac30ba6480'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'General Pharmacology - General Pharmacology', 'Competitive antagonist:', '["Irreversibly binds receptor","Shifts dose-response curve rightward","Decreases Emax","Cannot be overcome by increasing agonist","Has no affinity"]'::jsonb, 'Shifts dose-response curve rightward', 'Competitive antagonists are surmountable; increase EC50 but not Emax.'),
('7bd62f4c-19e6-5981-a0ef-14dda3357eba'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'General Pharmacology - General Pharmacology', 'Non-competitive antagonist:', '["Shifts curve rightward only","Decreases Emax (cannot be overcome)","Has no effect on Emax","Increases potency","Is surmountable"]'::jsonb, 'Decreases Emax (cannot be overcome)', 'Non-competitive antagonists reduce maximum response.'),
