('49211469-c0f6-5e5a-965b-8376b8be0eff'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Apomorphine is used in advanced Parkinson''s disease for the treatment of:', '["Chronic Parkinson''s resulting in decreased functional activity","Acute off episodes in Parkinson''s","Hypertension mediated by specific receptor activation","Diabetes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Asthma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Acute off episodes in Parkinson''s', 'Apomorphine is for acute off episodes in advanced Parkinson''s.'),
('b432a6e1-3b8c-5404-8d7a-a06ab5b3500c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Compared with metoclopramide, which of the following is true of domperidone?', '["Crosses BBB readily","Has less CNS penetration","Is a DA agonist","Is used for Parkinson''s","Has no prokinetic effect"]'::jsonb, 'Has less CNS penetration', 'Domperidone poorly crosses the BBB.'),
('3b3500d0-fb4d-55bc-8299-82e184099742'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Ondansetron exerts its antiemetic effect by blocking which receptor?', '["Dopamine D2","5-HT3 (serotonin)","Histamine H1","Muscarinic M1","NK1"]'::jsonb, '5-HT3 (serotonin)', 'Ondansetron is a selective 5-HT3 antagonist.'),
('352d2d95-43ca-54c5-afa4-529989f0eb13'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Aprepitant produces its antiemetic effect by antagonizing which receptor?', '["5-HT3","Dopamine D2","NK1 (substance P)","Histamine H1","Muscarinic M1"]'::jsonb, 'NK1 (substance P)', 'Aprepitant is an NK1 receptor antagonist.'),
('6f482e8a-6bed-58ef-970f-a418e8446630'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'For the prevention of motion sickness, the transdermal scopolamine patch should be applied:', '["1-2 hours","4-8 hours (or night before)","24 hours","After symptoms start","1 week before"]'::jsonb, '4-8 hours (or night before)', 'Scopolamine patch is applied 4-8 hours before travel.'),
('eefc7fa1-49e8-5b2b-8a8d-a848884c007b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Besides histamine H1-receptor antagonism, promethazine is also notable for its:', '["Alpha-1 blocking","Anticholinergic and sedative properties","Beta-blocking","DA agonist activity","Nicotinic blocking"]'::jsonb, 'Anticholinergic and sedative properties', 'Promethazine has marked anticholinergic and sedative effects.'),
('6cc6c288-aad9-5d0d-9bac-00ac6452091b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Compared with atropine, hyoscine (scopolamine) characteristically produces greater:', '["Tachycardia","CNS depression and sedation","Mydriasis only","Bronchodilation","Urinary retention"]'::jsonb, 'CNS depression and sedation', 'Hyoscine crosses the BBB more readily, causing greater CNS effects.'),
('4debe710-aa7c-5f93-94c2-d521bf95b8f6'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'In Parkinson''s disease, benztropine is most useful for:', '["Reducing tremor and rigidity","Increasing DA levels","Inhibiting MAO-B","Blocking COMT","Stimulating DA receptors"]'::jsonb, 'Reducing tremor and rigidity', 'Benztropine reduces tremor and rigidity in Parkinson''s.'),
('5b02d243-87e0-5b42-8afa-b665af98b0c2'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Trihexyphenidyl belongs to which class of antiparkinsonian drugs?', '["DA agonist","Central anticholinergic","MAO-B inhibitor","COMT inhibitor","Amantadine-like drug"]'::jsonb, 'Central anticholinergic', 'Trihexyphenidyl is an anticholinergic for Parkinson''s and EPS.'),
('29d1bb42-964f-524e-a0f6-1c39cbf79b3f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'The principal difference between neostigmine and pyridostigmine lies in their:', '["Mechanism of action","Duration of action","Route of administration","Selectivity","Reversibility"]'::jsonb, 'Duration of action', 'Main difference is pyridostigmine''s longer duration.'),
('336f8f7b-368e-5e75-b133-4efe20de1a2d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'The ultrashort duration of action of edrophonium is explained by the fact that it:', '["Irreversibly bound","Binds reversibly and rapidly redistributes","Rapidly metabolized by MAO","Renally excreted immediately","Competitive antagonist"]'::jsonb, 'Binds reversibly and rapidly redistributes', 'Edrophonium binds reversibly with fast dissociation.'),
('5166cf4c-8594-5aa1-ab2e-3b40bb85da57'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'The Tensilon (edrophonium) test for myasthenia gravis is performed by:', '["Giving edrophonium and observing brief improvement","Giving neostigmine orally","Giving atropine IV","Giving succinylcholine","Giving pyridostigmine over days"]'::jsonb, 'Giving edrophonium and observing brief improvement', 'Edrophonium IV; temporary improvement confirms MG.'),
('5acbdfdd-b553-5efc-aeda-c5536ed84736'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Ambenonium is classified as which of the following?', '["Direct muscarinic agonist","Reversible AChE inhibitor","Irreversible AChE inhibitor","Nicotinic antagonist","Ganglionic blocker"]'::jsonb, 'Reversible AChE inhibitor', 'Ambenonium is a reversible AChE inhibitor for MG.'),
('09be27f2-fb40-50b7-90d6-6bf08186c312'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following best describes demecarium?', '["Long-acting AChE inhibitor for glaucoma","Short-acting muscarinic agonist","Nicotinic antagonist","Ganglionic blocker","Direct sympathomimetic"]'::jsonb, 'Long-acting AChE inhibitor for glaucoma', 'Demecarium is a long-acting AChE inhibitor for open-angle glaucoma.'),
('d9b1d6d9-324a-564f-b320-b52c69100edc'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following best describes echothiophate?', '["Short-acting AChE inhibitor resulting in decreased functional activity","Long-acting irreversible AChE inhibitor for glaucoma","Muscarinic antagonist mediated by specific receptor activation","Nicotinic agonist without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ganglionic stimulant leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Long-acting irreversible AChE inhibitor for glaucoma', 'Echothiophate is long-acting irreversible for glaucoma.'),
('ff9b2d40-e205-5b93-b979-9b2f7fe9e4c6'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which mnemonic classically describes the clinical features of atropine (antimuscarinic) poisoning?', '["SLUDGE resulting in decreased functional activity","Blind as a bat, dry as a bone, red as a beet, mad as a hatter, hot as a hare","AMPLE mediated by specific receptor activation","AMPLE history without affecting the primary tissue pathway","OPQRST leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Blind as a bat, dry as a bone, red as a beet, mad as a hatter, hot as a hare', 'Classic mnemonic: Blind, dry, red, mad, hot.'),
('7c9425db-d5e4-584b-8666-0b6053f51b02'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'In addition to inhibiting acetylcholinesterase, neostigmine also possesses which property at the neuromuscular junction?', '["Direct muscarinic agonism","Direct NMJ receptor stimulation","Ganglionic blockade","Alpha-receptor blockade","Beta-receptor blockade"]'::jsonb, 'Direct NMJ receptor stimulation', 'Neostigmine has direct agonist activity at NMJ nicotinic receptors.'),
('0211c42f-b131-5a42-a3b5-29aefd02698e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Plasma cholinesterase inhibition (e.g., by systemic absorption of echothiophate eye drops) prolongs the action of which drug?', '["Antibiotics","Succinylcholine","Diuretics","Antihistamines","Analgesics"]'::jsonb, 'Succinylcholine', 'Echothiophate inhibits plasma cholinesterase, prolonging succinylcholine.'),
('a97d9086-3f1b-5e4f-a2dc-f89ae7a72b8e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'AChE inhibitors such as neostigmine increase gastrointestinal motility because:', '["Block muscarinic receptors resulting in decreased functional activity","Increased ACh enhances parasympathetic stimulation of gut smooth muscle","Directly relax smooth muscle mediated by specific receptor activation","Block sympathetic tone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Stimulate beta-2 receptors leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Increased ACh enhances parasympathetic stimulation of gut smooth muscle', 'Increased ACh stimulates M3 on gut smooth muscle.'),
('625d8956-379b-5761-8531-0a042fb3d177'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Physostigmine is able to cross the blood-brain barrier because it is a:', '["Quaternary ammonium","Tertiary amine (lipophilic)","Large molecule","Protein-bound","Ionized at pH 7.4"]'::jsonb, 'Tertiary amine (lipophilic)', 'Physostigmine is a tertiary amine, lipophilic enough to cross BBB.'),
('a09348de-fd97-5903-8087-87affc710658'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'A positive Tensilon (edrophonium) test in myasthenia gravis characteristically produces:', '["Worsening of symptoms","Brief improvement then worsening","No change","Permanent improvement","Increased weakness lasting hours"]'::jsonb, 'Brief improvement then worsening', 'Positive: brief improvement in MG, then worsening.'),
('6d62ff32-c602-528d-98fe-1c8fb57e7556'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Cholinergic crisis in a myasthenic patient results from which of the following?', '["Too little AChE inhibitor","Too much AChE inhibitor (ACh excess at NMJ)","Autoimmune destruction","Excess curare-like substances","Viral infection of motor neurons"]'::jsonb, 'Too much AChE inhibitor (ACh excess at NMJ)', 'Cholinergic crisis results from excessive AChE inhibitor.');
insert into fcps_mcq_stage (id, subject_name, chapter_name, question, options, correct_answer, explanation) values
('24baa683-3da6-5343-8266-15a87e833e71'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which test is used to differentiate a myasthenic crisis from a cholinergic crisis?', '["Edrophonium (Tensilon) test","Anti-AChR antibodies","EMG studies","Muscle biopsy","Spinal tap"]'::jsonb, 'Edrophonium (Tensilon) test', 'Edrophonium test differentiates: improvement = MG crisis, worsening = cholinergic crisis.'),
('c236ca85-0ffb-55ea-bc6c-24c4cfbcfa51'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Amifostine is used to prevent which complication of radiation therapy?', '["Chemotherapy nephrotoxicity","Radiation-induced xerostomia","Hypertension","Diabetes","Asthma"]'::jsonb, 'Radiation-induced xerostomia', 'Amifostine stimulates salivary glands, reducing radiation-induced dry mouth.'),
('04470124-55ad-5cc0-b985-34847618e549'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Atropine is racemic DL-hyoscyamine; which is its pharmacologically more active isomer?', '["Scopolamine","L-hyoscyamine","Pilocarpine","Physostigmine","Muscarine"]'::jsonb, 'L-hyoscyamine', 'Atropine is DL-hyoscyamine; L-isomer is more potent.'),
('64409888-1bb4-55b9-9a56-38333c27ed05'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Compared with atropine, scopolamine (hyoscine):', '["More potent at peripheral receptors","Crosses BBB more readily","More alpha-blocking activity","Nicotinic agonist activity","Longer half-life"]'::jsonb, 'Crosses BBB more readily', 'Scopolamine is more lipophilic and crosses BBB more readily.'),
('a99d51e5-fbe6-54b2-8930-44b1808ee750'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'The specific medical antidote for organophosphate poisoning is:', '["Atropine and pralidoxime","Physostigmine and neostigmine","Epinephrine and dopamine","Propranolol and metoprolol","Succinylcholine and tubocurarine"]'::jsonb, 'Atropine and pralidoxime', 'OP poisoning: atropine + pralidoxime.'),
('803656e6-28c8-5c85-970e-275786e9523b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Pralidoxime is ineffective when given late in organophosphate poisoning because:', '["Short half-life","AChE ages and becomes resistant to reactivation","Only effective in first hour","Rapidly metabolized","Loses potency with time"]'::jsonb, 'AChE ages and becomes resistant to reactivation', 'AChE ages, making pralidoxime ineffective after aging.'),
('dfad4b3f-281f-5369-8837-47ee449635fc'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'In organophosphate poisoning, the ''aging'' of acetylcholinesterase refers to:', '["Patient becoming older resulting in decreased functional activity","Loss of alkyl group making reactivation impossible","Enzyme regeneration mediated by specific receptor activation","Increased enzyme activity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Spontaneous recovery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Loss of alkyl group making reactivation impossible', 'Aging: dealkylation of phosphorylated AChE.'),
('b52f9f4a-c8d9-5add-bd64-5d3324e2f6a3'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Compared with organophosphates, carbamate acetylcholinesterase inhibitors:', '["Cause irreversible inhibition","Cause reversible inhibition (no aging)","Are more toxic","Require pralidoxime","Do not affect AChE"]'::jsonb, 'Cause reversible inhibition (no aging)', 'Carbamates reversibly inhibit AChE without aging.'),
('3e877d57-ba06-53f2-9204-8d677a625ef3'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'At the end of surgery, neostigmine is used to reverse which type of neuromuscular blockade?', '["Depolarizing","Non-depolarizing","Both","Neither","Succinylcholine only"]'::jsonb, 'Non-depolarizing', 'Neostigmine reverses non-depolarizing NMJ blockade.'),
('fc48cf3b-b685-561f-9b9a-cce14284e450'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'When neostigmine is used to reverse neuromuscular blockade, atropine is co-administered to:', '["Enhance neostigmine effect","Prevent muscarinic side effects","Increase AChE inhibition","Block nicotinic receptors","Prevent hypertension"]'::jsonb, 'Prevent muscarinic side effects', 'Atropine prevents muscarinic effects of increased ACh.'),
('d116e21f-704b-5df9-ae91-33a2df739663'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'The Tensilon (edrophonium) test is now rarely used to diagnose myasthenia gravis because:', '["Too expensive resulting in decreased functional activity","Safer tests (anti-AChR antibodies, EMG) available","Not effective mediated by specific receptor activation","Too many side effects without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Not available leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Safer tests (anti-AChR antibodies, EMG) available', 'Anti-AChR testing and EMG are safer and more specific.'),
('28d14c8f-cde1-5a99-a5a3-b84e8cf1e3d4'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Corticosteroids improve myasthenia gravis because they:', '["Increase ACh release resulting in decreased functional activity","Suppress autoimmune response against ACh receptors","Block muscarinic receptors mediated by specific receptor activation","Inhibit AChE without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Stimulate NMJ receptors leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Suppress autoimmune response against ACh receptors', 'Corticosteroids reduce the autoimmune attack on NMJ receptors.'),
('a7b23892-6f3c-52f5-a605-2990171691e8'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'In myasthenia gravis, azathioprine is used as a:', '["AChE inhibitor","Steroid-sparing immunosuppressant","Direct NMJ stimulant","Muscarinic antagonist","Ganglionic blocker"]'::jsonb, 'Steroid-sparing immunosuppressant', 'Azathioprine is a steroid-sparing immunosuppressant.'),
('c8e498e4-0bc9-5ea9-9789-0e24a8ac8603'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Thymectomy is recommended for which group of patients with myasthenia gravis?', '["Any form resulting in decreased functional activity","Thymoma or generalized MG without thymoma","Only ocular MG mediated by specific receptor activation","Only elderly without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pregnant women only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thymoma or generalized MG without thymoma', 'Thymectomy recommended for thymoma or generalized MG.'),
('cac02a38-2fa3-5dfb-a80b-c8c48adc5dc8'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'In a patient with congenital pseudocholinesterase deficiency, administration of succinylcholine produces:', '["No effect","Prolonged paralysis (2-4+ hours)","Enhanced effect","Rapid metabolism","Bradycardia only"]'::jsonb, 'Prolonged paralysis (2-4+ hours)', 'Duration greatly prolonged due to inability to hydrolyze.'),
('379f79ad-1cca-5d61-8e24-c254a2fb9ce9'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'A characteristic ocular side effect of the AChE inhibitors used in glaucoma (owing to ciliary spasm) is:', '["Increased IOP resulting in decreased functional activity","Brow ache and myopia from ciliary spasm","Corneal opacity mediated by specific receptor activation","Retinal detachment without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lens dislocation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Brow ache and myopia from ciliary spasm', 'Ciliary spasm causes brow ache and temporary myopia.'),
('0fcb8174-4ab6-5c8e-8ce7-25fcb23d1924'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following drugs depletes catecholamines by blocking the vesicular monoamine transporter (VMAT)?', '["Reserpine","Cocaine","Propranolol","Atropine","Neostigmine"]'::jsonb, 'Reserpine', 'Reserpine blocks VMAT, preventing catecholamine storage.'),
('4119d962-5eb6-5811-a21f-0996c34ce613'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Subcutaneous infiltration of phentolamine is used to prevent ischemic necrosis following extravasation of:', '["Dopamine","Norepinephrine","Epinephrine","Both A and B","All sympathomimetics"]'::jsonb, 'Both A and B', 'Phentolamine infiltrated SC prevents ischemic necrosis from extravasated catecholamines.'),
('3274dbcc-d10a-58cf-93bc-5ccf815e903b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Why do beta-blockers have the potential to worsen acutely decompensated heart failure?', '["Blocks sympathetic drive needed to maintain CO","Causes vasodilation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lowers BP mediated by specific receptor activation","Blocks beta-2 receptors without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Increases HR leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Blocks sympathetic drive needed to maintain CO', 'Sympathetic drive compensates in HF; beta-blockade removes this.'),
('3960a26f-aacd-5bfd-a896-af9024ecee48'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Labetalol is a preferred antihypertensive in pregnancy, especially for the treatment of:', '["Pre-eclampsia/eclampsia","Gestational diabetes","Preterm labor","Hyperthyroidism","Asthma"]'::jsonb, 'Pre-eclampsia/eclampsia', 'Labetalol is for hypertension in pregnancy including pre-eclampsia.'),
('8dcb3611-ce4e-5765-ae41-7cb13f8a3ecb'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Compared with succinylcholine, rocuronium:', '["Is depolarizing","Has longer onset and duration","Has shorter duration","Is reversed by neostigmine","Cannot be reversed"]'::jsonb, 'Has longer onset and duration', 'Rocuronium is non-depolarizing with longer onset/duration.'),
('2e647103-abb4-5f19-9201-ffe824dac539'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Neostigmine may worsen paralysis if used to reverse which type of neuromuscular blockade?', '["Non-depolarizing resulting in decreased functional activity","Depolarizing (succinylcholine-type)","Competitive mediated by specific receptor activation","All types without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Depolarizing (succinylcholine-type)', 'Neostigmine worsens depolarizing blockade.'),
('240544ae-3918-52ee-aaf1-a1855806c48c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Malignant hyperthermia susceptibility is inherited as which pattern?', '["Autosomal dominant","Autosomal recessive","X-linked dominant","X-linked recessive","Mitochondrial"]'::jsonb, 'Autosomal dominant', 'MH susceptibility follows autosomal dominant inheritance.'),
('224bc9a4-2f79-5d03-8b89-f234416316e4'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Dantrolene treats malignant hyperthermia by binding to which receptor?', '["Nicotinic resulting in decreased functional activity","Ryanodine receptor (RyR1) on SR","Muscarinic mediated by specific receptor activation","Alpha without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Beta leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ryanodine receptor (RyR1) on SR', 'Dantrolene binds RyR1, blocking SR calcium release.'),
('2db85d04-1b09-5823-ae76-df892aebf6de'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Malignant hyperthermia is most commonly triggered by which of the following?', '["Succinylcholine alone resulting in decreased functional activity","Volatile anesthetics alone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Combination of succinylcholine and volatile anesthetics","Local anesthetics without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Antibiotics leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Combination of succinylcholine and volatile anesthetics', 'The combination is the most common trigger.'),
('03e2449a-e4ae-596d-b49f-90fb7ce11acb'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'After an intravenous injection, succinylcholine produces paralysis within approximately:', '["30-60 seconds","5-10 minutes","30-45 minutes","1-2 hours","24 hours"]'::jsonb, '30-60 seconds', 'Succinylcholine has the fastest onset (30-60 seconds IV).'),
('ecbb92cf-718f-51de-8fa8-c8d614b7500a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Administration of succinylcholine characteristically causes a transient rise in which serum electrolyte?', '["Sodium","Potassium","Calcium","Chloride","Magnesium"]'::jsonb, 'Potassium', 'Succinylcholine causes K+ efflux, raising serum K+ by 0.5-1.0 mEq/L.'),
('d31c79df-6484-5d8a-8148-addd5d1194a5'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'The plasma half-life of succinylcholine is approximately:', '["1-2 minutes","10-15 minutes","1-2 hours","6-8 hours","24 hours"]'::jsonb, '1-2 minutes', 'Succinylcholine has extremely short half-life (1-2 min).'),
('034029b4-f24f-5787-8c12-1ffa9ce95c49'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Atracurium is particularly useful in patients with hepatic or renal failure because it undergoes:', '["Cheapest resulting in decreased functional activity","Organ-independent Hofmann elimination","Most potent mediated by specific receptor activation","Shortest duration without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Crosses BBB leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Organ-independent Hofmann elimination', 'Hofmann elimination is organ-independent, safe in multiorgan failure.'),
('3966a510-1e3a-5e2e-aa7d-c3d556421c11'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which neuromuscular blocking agent has the longest duration of action (approximately 60-90 minutes)?', '["Rocuronium","Succinylcholine","Pancuronium","Vecuronium","Atracurium"]'::jsonb, 'Pancuronium', 'Pancuronium has longest duration (60-90 min).'),
('23fa1c7f-2045-5a25-b347-e161fcf2cc9d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Pancuronium characteristically produces tachycardia because it:', '["Has vagolytic activity","Stimulates beta-1 receptors","Blocks alpha receptors","Dilates blood vessels","Blocks cardiac muscarinic receptors"]'::jsonb, 'Has vagolytic activity', 'Pancuronium blocks vagal muscarinic receptors at SA node.'),
('469c5d38-16ef-58dc-a2f5-955d1de34804'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Neostigmine reverses non-depolarizing neuromuscular blockade by:', '["Direct muscle stimulation","Increasing ACh to compete with blocker","Blocking the antagonist","Releasing NE","Blocking presynaptic receptors"]'::jsonb, 'Increasing ACh to compete with blocker', 'Increased ACh competitively displaces non-depolarizing agents.'),
('d3c0ce6d-52dd-5971-b60c-78da5b13609d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Homatropine has a shorter cycloplegic effect than atropine in the eye because it is:', '["More rapidly metabolized locally","Lower M3 affinity","Rapidly absorbed systemically","Partial agonist","Less lipophilic"]'::jsonb, 'More rapidly metabolized locally', 'Homatropine is more rapidly metabolized in the eye.'),
('ef0c88b9-8408-5e51-bef2-12f9490de4c4'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which characteristic applies to quaternary ammonium antimuscarinic drugs such as glycopyrrolate?', '["Crosses BBB easily","Poor oral bioavailability","Potent CNS drug","Rapidly metabolized","Nicotinic activity"]'::jsonb, 'Poor oral bioavailability', 'Quaternary compounds are ionized, poorly absorbed orally.'),
('8e870195-d331-595c-814a-796adc537ba5'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Tiotropium produces prolonged bronchodilation due to:', '["Slow dissociation from M3 receptors","Slowly metabolized","Tissue accumulation","Renal clearance","High protein binding"]'::jsonb, 'Slow dissociation from M3 receptors', 'Tiotropium dissociates very slowly from M3 receptors.'),
('4337001a-910a-5c16-b838-46abdf6e94b5'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Apart from its anticholinergic activity, benztropine also acts as an:', '["Antihistamine","Local anesthetic","MAO inhibitor","Beta-blocker","Alpha agonist"]'::jsonb, 'Antihistamine', 'Benztropine has anticholinergic and antihistaminic properties.'),
('c32aba43-8a02-591e-8290-d8b74519b7c3'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'The extrapyramidal side effects of typical antipsychotics are attributed to:', '["Excess DA at receptors resulting in decreased functional activity","DA receptor blockade (D2) in nigrostriatal pathway","Serotonin excess mediated by specific receptor activation","ACh excess without affecting the primary tissue pathway","GABA excess leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'DA receptor blockade (D2) in nigrostriatal pathway', 'Antipsychotics block D2 receptors in the striatum.'),
('7813f3c7-35af-5328-a224-99d6fa27bce0'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following describes the principal antiparkinsonian mechanism of amantadine?', '["Is anticholinergic","Enhances DA release and blocks reuptake","Is COMT inhibitor","Is MAO-B inhibitor","Is D2 agonist"]'::jsonb, 'Enhances DA release and blocks reuptake', 'Amantadine enhances DA release, blocks reuptake.'),
('350de9d9-5b61-5bf6-922b-268a12eb574f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Levodopa is combined with carbidopa because carbidopa:', '["Enhances DA effects resulting in decreased functional activity","Inhibits peripheral DOPA decarboxylase, increasing CNS levodopa","Is MAO inhibitor mediated by specific receptor activation","Blocks DA reuptake without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Is COMT inhibitor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibits peripheral DOPA decarboxylase, increasing CNS levodopa', 'Carbidopa inhibits peripheral conversion of levodopa to dopamine.'),
('640c1c92-e975-5f1c-84b3-5c94ee32c5b4'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Entacapone, an adjunct in the treatment of Parkinson''s disease, is classified as a:', '["MAO-B inhibitor","COMT inhibitor","DA agonist","Anticholinergic","Beta-blocker"]'::jsonb, 'COMT inhibitor', 'Entacapone inhibits COMT peripherally.'),
('9ebeb022-71e5-5891-aa14-a96ff6067b3c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Selegiline, used early in the treatment of Parkinson''s disease, is a:', '["MAO-A inhibitor","Selective MAO-B inhibitor","Non-selective MAO inhibitor","COMT inhibitor","DA antagonist"]'::jsonb, 'Selective MAO-B inhibitor', 'Selegiline is a selective MAO-B inhibitor for early Parkinson''s.'),
('f5197f03-af9b-5292-85bd-06c8f6a5d079'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Compared with tolcapone, which of the following characteristics applies to entacapone?', '["Acts only peripherally","Acts centrally and peripherally","Is more potent","Is cheaper","Has longer duration"]'::jsonb, 'Acts only peripherally', 'Entacapone acts peripherally only.'),
('946f2414-881f-511d-8609-a4feda271ece'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Antipsychotic-induced oculogyric crisis (acute dystonia) is best treated with:', '["DA agonists resulting in decreased functional activity","Anticholinergics (benztropine, diphenhydramine)","MAO inhibitors mediated by specific receptor activation","COMT inhibitors without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Beta-blockers leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anticholinergics (benztropine, diphenhydramine)', 'Anticholinergics or diphenhydramine treat oculogyric crisis.'),
('92d066d6-fb35-5663-811b-495b4df7dda9'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following statements regarding piracetam is correct?', '["Has anticholinergic properties","May enhance neuromuscular transmission","Blocks nicotinic receptors","Inhibits AChE","Stimulates DA receptors"]'::jsonb, 'May enhance neuromuscular transmission', 'Piracetam may enhance cholinergic transmission.'),
('c772f204-084b-54c7-b904-a9bbea107e9f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Anti-AChR antibody testing and EMG have largely replaced which diagnostic method in myasthenia gravis?', '["Edrophonium test largely","CT scan accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","MRI mediated by specific receptor activation","Ultrasound without affecting the primary tissue pathway","X-ray leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Edrophonium test largely', 'Anti-AChR antibody testing is more specific and safer.'),
('4562ab58-6c4b-505e-893c-63ea20cc99ff'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'In neuromyelitis optica (NMO), the serum antibodies are directed against:', '["ACh receptor","Aquaporin-4","Myelin basic protein","VGCC","MuSK"]'::jsonb, 'Aquaporin-4', 'Anti-aquaporin-4 antibodies are found in NMO.'),
('c2725a1a-d0dc-5d11-b906-9a6c9e95a081'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'MuSK-antibody-positive myasthenia gravis characteristically responds best to:', '["Neostigmine","Rituximab and plasma exchange","Corticosteroids alone","Thymectomy","Azathioprine"]'::jsonb, 'Rituximab and plasma exchange', 'MuSK-MG responds poorly to AChE inhibitors but well to rituximab.'),
('0a4e295d-b483-58de-98d8-ad15b3970f9b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'In Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome, muscle strength characteristically improves following:', '["Repeated stimulation (facilitation)","Rest accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Corticosteroids mediated by specific receptor activation","AChE inhibitors without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anticholinergics leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Repeated stimulation (facilitation)', 'Repeated stimulation causes calcium accumulation and improved transmission.'),
('d23bddba-ac0e-593b-96c1-cf47973be63b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Botulinum toxin produces flaccid paralysis by:', '["Blocking ACh release from nerve terminals","Blocking nicotinic receptors","Blocking muscarinic receptors","Inhibiting AChE","Blocking sodium channels"]'::jsonb, 'Blocking ACh release from nerve terminals', 'Botulinum toxin cleaves SNARE proteins, preventing ACh release.'),
('12327d62-1a21-5761-9ae3-6f537aaaab46'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'RimabotulinumtoxinB differs from botulinum toxin type A in that it represents a:', '["More potent resulting in decreased functional activity","Different serotype (B vs A) and different SNARE target","Acts faster mediated by specific receptor activation","Lasts longer without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cheaper leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Different serotype (B vs A) and different SNARE target', 'Type B cleaves VAMP; Type A cleaves SNAP-25.'),
('8e65b211-5f4a-553c-8cab-4838111cac99'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Botulism antitoxin should preferably be administered:', '["Within 24-72 hours of symptom onset","After 1 week","Only in children","Only in elderly","Any time equally"]'::jsonb, 'Within 24-72 hours of symptom onset', 'Antitoxin binds circulating toxin before it binds nerve terminals.'),
('8748a110-006f-5507-b237-02caedf84672'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Infantile botulism is characteristically associated with which dietary exposure?', '["Honey consumption","Canned food","Improper home canning","All of the above","None"]'::jsonb, 'Honey consumption', 'Infantile botulism associated with honey consumption.'),
('4ed821c9-6705-5a4b-ab7f-7e215af9486d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'The intravenous form of dantrolene is specifically indicated for:', '["Chronic spasticity only","Malignant hyperthermia emergencies","Oral use only","Topical use","Inhalation"]'::jsonb, 'Malignant hyperthermia emergencies', 'IV dantrolene is for emergency MH treatment.'),
('1ffca450-ba26-551d-bafc-8248c8101365'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'The minimum initial dose of intravenous dantrolene for malignant hyperthermia in an adult requires approximately:', '["1 vial (60mg) resulting in decreased functional activity","5-10 vials (300-600mg minimum)","50 vials mediated by specific receptor activation","100 vials without affecting the primary tissue pathway","200 vials leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '5-10 vials (300-600mg minimum)', 'Typical adult dose: 2.5 mg/kg, requiring 5-10 vials.'),
('5bccd525-0196-59dd-92c9-0f72dda2e0a1'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Dantrolene acts mainly at which location within the skeletal muscle cell?', '["At the neuromuscular junction resulting in decreased functional activity","On the sarcoplasmic reticulum (intracellular)","On the motor nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","On the brain without affecting the primary tissue pathway","On the spinal cord leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'On the sarcoplasmic reticulum (intracellular)', 'Dantrolene acts on RyR1 in the SR.'),
('7c13e013-e45b-585c-bad7-b808b26735bc'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Oral dantrolene is useful in the treatment of spasticity due to which of the following?', '["Stroke only","MS only","Spinal cord injury only","All of the above","None"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Oral dantrolene for spasticity from stroke, MS, SCI, and cerebral palsy.'),
('66ee31e3-a113-5a02-bd95-f224cd5b33ed'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Hypertensive crisis due to a tyramine reaction in a patient taking an MAO inhibitor is treated with:', '["Beta-blockers resulting in decreased functional activity","Alpha-blockers (phentolamine) or nitrates","Diuretics mediated by specific receptor activation","CCB without affecting the primary tissue pathway","ACE inhibitors leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Alpha-blockers (phentolamine) or nitrates', 'Alpha-blockade or direct vasodilation for tyramine crisis.'),
('e7fc6d78-7909-55fe-a3d6-2c6d21448839'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following clinical features characterizes the serotonin syndrome?', '["Hypothermia, bradycardia, miosis","Hyperthermia, agitation, clonus, hyperreflexia","Hypotension, diarrhea, miosis","Bradycardia, constipation, mydriasis","None"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperthermia, agitation, clonus, hyperreflexia', 'Triad: mental changes, autonomic instability, neuromuscular hyperactivity.'),
('145dcc8c-2a0d-5d69-b7fd-fc591f150a9c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'The drug used in the treatment of serotonin syndrome is:', '["Cyproheptadine (5-HT2A antagonist)","Atropine accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Propranolol mediated by specific receptor activation","Naloxone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Flumazenil leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cyproheptadine (5-HT2A antagonist)', 'Cyproheptadine is a 5-HT2A antagonist for serotonin syndrome.'),
('9e63e77d-77f8-5915-b0d1-503e6a57d0c5'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'In a patient with a cholinergic crisis, administration of edrophonium results in:', '["Improvement","Further worsening","No effect","Bradycardia only","Mydriasis only"]'::jsonb, 'Further worsening', 'In cholinergic crisis, edrophonium worsens weakness.'),
('9136abd6-04ac-5728-b25f-e12abef250b8'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Succinylcholine produces its neuromuscular blocking effect through:', '["Non-depolarizing blockade","Initial depolarization then desensitization","Reversible AChE inhibition","Direct calcium release","Potassium channel blockade"]'::jsonb, 'Initial depolarization then desensitization', 'Succinylcholine initially depolarizes then desensitizes.'),
('b11565df-eff6-546c-9a12-3400bab18be6'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Of which plant is tubocurarine the active constituent?', '["Curare (Chondrodendron tomentosum)","Digitalis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Opium poppy mediated by specific receptor activation","Foxglove without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Belladonna leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Curare (Chondrodendron tomentosum)', 'Tubocurarine is the active constituent of curare.'),
('ecf45660-c512-5908-a8e3-752b42147d41'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which condition prolongs the paralysis produced by succinylcholine?', '["Pseudocholinesterase deficiency","Hypertension accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Diabetes mediated by specific receptor activation","Asthma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hypothyroidism leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pseudocholinesterase deficiency', 'Pseudocholinesterase deficiency prolongs paralysis.'),
('373a0ad4-76f3-5644-93b2-fabadebbca50'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'What ocular effect does atropine produce after topical application?', '["Miosis only","Mydriasis and cycloplegia","No effect","Increased IOP only","Conjunctival vasoconstriction"]'::jsonb, 'Mydriasis and cycloplegia', 'Atropine blocks M3 causing mydriasis and cycloplegia.'),
('bf52ed18-39b2-514c-af52-f3a4b5441391'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'What is the first-line drug for the treatment of anaphylaxis?', '["Corticosteroids","Antihistamines","Epinephrine IM","Diphenhydramine IV","Aminophylline"]'::jsonb, 'Epinephrine IM', 'Epinephrine is the first-line treatment for anaphylaxis.'),
('3d8246b5-ea2d-5465-8591-591c281a1b41'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'What concentration of epinephrine is used for IM injection in anaphylaxis?', '["1:1000 (1 mg/mL)","1:10,000","1:100,000","1:1,000,000","Any dilution"]'::jsonb, '1:1000 (1 mg/mL)', '1:1000 epinephrine (1mg/mL) for IM injection.'),
('1c9e7ac4-beb5-5fb4-bf54-1c17d135b624'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'What dose of epinephrine is given intravenously during cardiac arrest?', '["0.3-0.5 mg resulting in decreased functional activity","1 mg (1:10,000 dilution)","5 mg mediated by specific receptor activation","10 mg without affecting the primary tissue pathway","50 mg leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '1 mg (1:10,000 dilution)', 'IV epi in cardiac arrest: 1 mg (10mL of 1:10,000) every 3-5 min.'),
('7745ece2-44f9-53bd-9ab0-a9e35f4357b4'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'During CPR, how do the alpha-1 effects of epinephrine improve coronary and cerebral perfusion?', '["Direct myocardial stimulation only resulting in decreased functional activity","Coronary vasoconstriction and improved coronary perfusion","Peripheral vasodilation mediated by specific receptor activation","CNS stimulation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Beta-blockade leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Coronary vasoconstriction and improved coronary perfusion', 'Alpha-1 effects improve coronary and cerebral perfusion pressure.'),
('6d772e2e-6a11-5230-9ec5-b8b6102b562c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Compared with epinephrine, norepinephrine:', '["Has more beta-2 activity","Has predominantly alpha-1 activity","Is purely vasodilator","Has no BP effect","Is used for asthma"]'::jsonb, 'Has predominantly alpha-1 activity', 'NE has predominantly alpha-1 vasoconstrictor activity.'),
('3e890929-4279-5ad7-8414-d05f138695cd'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Positive inotropic agents worsen hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy because they:', '["Blocks alpha receptors resulting in decreased functional activity","Increases contractility, worsening the gradient","Blocks beta-1 receptors mediated by specific receptor activation","Lowers BP without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Causes bradycardia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Increases contractility, worsening the gradient', 'Increased contractility worsens the LVOT gradient.'),
('0957120e-0c20-52b6-baf9-45dbba903eda'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'The dose-dependent effects of dopamine (renal, inotropic, and vasopressor) include:', '["0.5-2 mcg/kg/min (renal)","2-10 mcg/kg/min (inotropic)","10-20 mcg/kg/min (vasopressor)","All of the above","None"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Dopamine has dose-dependent effects.'),
('a66c1503-b3e1-5ccd-8885-cfe6d22b494f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'What is the first-line vasopressor in septic shock?', '["Epinephrine","Norepinephrine","Dopamine","Phenylephrine","Dobutamine"]'::jsonb, 'Norepinephrine', 'Norepinephrine is first-line vasopressor in septic shock.'),
('213e24bb-1ba1-5e71-b197-037a8490d87c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'In which type of shock is phenylephrine particularly useful?', '["Septic shock resulting in decreased functional activity","Neurogenic shock (spinal anesthesia)","Cardiogenic shock mediated by specific receptor activation","Anaphylaxis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Heart failure leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Neurogenic shock (spinal anesthesia)', 'Phenylephrine useful in neurogenic shock.'),
