('82ed6060-74b2-5ab9-9713-2d323709d5d4'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Labetalol is contraindicated in which condition?', '["Severe asthma","Pheochromocytoma","Hypertension","Heart failure","Angina"]'::jsonb, 'Severe asthma', 'Labetalol''s beta-blockade can precipitate bronchospasm.'),
('9613281d-d018-53e2-973f-d7044234a314'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which drug reduces aqueous humor production by alpha-2 agonism in glaucoma?', '["Brimonidine","Prazosin","Phentolamine","Clonidine","Methyldopa"]'::jsonb, 'Brimonidine', 'Brimonidine reduces aqueous humor production via alpha-2 agonism.'),
('863cc753-8043-59a1-b198-2ad95e8521db'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'What is the mechanism of action of brimonidine in reducing intraocular pressure?', '["Blocking aqueous production via alpha-2 agonism","Increasing aqueous outflow","Blocking muscarinic receptors","Blocking beta receptors","Direct vasodilation"]'::jsonb, 'Blocking aqueous production via alpha-2 agonism', 'Brimonidine reduces aqueous production and increases uveoscleral outflow.'),
('1a994c4f-2fa1-5bf3-a2b9-98882d12c7fe'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'What is the clinical use of daprazole eye drops?', '["Produce mydriasis","Reverse mydriasis (miosis)","Treat glaucoma","Treat conjunctivitis","Treat corneal ulcer"]'::jsonb, 'Reverse mydriasis (miosis)', 'Daprazole is an alpha-1 blocker that reverses mydriasis.'),
('8c688c81-5872-538b-b4d5-6865f9ff2a26'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Cyclopentolate is used for:', '["Glaucoma","Cycloplegia for refraction","Conjunctivitis","Corneal ulcer","IOP elevation"]'::jsonb, 'Cycloplegia for refraction', 'Cyclopentolate is a short-acting cycloplegic for refraction.'),
('fc6efa03-274a-5b64-8293-9cd91a1f0a02'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Homatropine is most commonly used for cycloplegic refraction in which group of patients?', '["Children (pediatric refraction)","Adults mainly accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Elderly mediated by specific receptor activation","All ages equally without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Neonates leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Children (pediatric refraction)', 'Homatropine is commonly used for cycloplegic refraction in children.'),
('75236291-f9ee-5b06-b499-740d4cbeab84'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'The mydriasis produced by tropicamide lasts approximately:', '["15-30 minutes","2-4 hours","6-8 hours","24-48 hours","1-2 weeks"]'::jsonb, '2-4 hours', 'Tropicamide causes mydriasis lasting 2-4 hours.'),
('93849de8-5139-5083-962c-40f9cf2128fc'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following is the most potent and longest-acting mydriatic?', '["Tropicamide","Homatropine","Atropine","Cyclopentolate","Phenylephrine"]'::jsonb, 'Atropine', 'Atropine is the most potent and longest-acting mydriatic.'),
('7c0f67b3-0c0c-5fbf-9793-8e5bb1369caf'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'What is the ocular effect produced by topical phenylephrine?', '["Cycloplegia resulting in decreased functional activity","No cycloplegia (pure mydriasis via alpha-1)","Miosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Decreased IOP without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Increased lacrimation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'No cycloplegia (pure mydriasis via alpha-1)', 'Phenylephrine dilates pupil without cycloplegia.'),
('89d1c28c-26dc-5ae7-9352-cb886abae433'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'What systemic adverse effects can topical timolol produce?', '["Bradycardia and bronchospasm","Tachycardia","Hypertension","Hyperglycemia","Seizures"]'::jsonb, 'Bradycardia and bronchospasm', 'Topical timolol can cause systemic beta-blocker effects.'),
('88580578-ba6a-52d4-a4d6-70d23e8b54d7'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Bimatoprost belongs to which class of drugs used in glaucoma?', '["Prostamide analog","Beta-blocker","Alpha-agonist","Carbonic anhydrase inhibitor","Muscarinic antagonist"]'::jsonb, 'Prostamide analog', 'Bimatoprost is a prostamide analog for glaucoma and eyelash growth.'),
('fd8fa3d0-e166-540e-843b-988c2832efcf'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Travoprost is classified as a:', '["Prostaglandin analog","Beta-blocker","Alpha-agonist","Carbonic anhydrase inhibitor","Anticholinergic"]'::jsonb, 'Prostaglandin analog', 'Travoprost is a PGF2alpha analog for glaucoma.'),
('16e4dc37-59e0-5b70-b125-4f68305404e2'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Unoprostone is classified as a:', '["Prostaglandin analog (docosanoid)","Beta-blocker","Alpha-agonist","Carbonic anhydrase inhibitor","Muscarinic agonist"]'::jsonb, 'Prostaglandin analog (docosanoid)', 'Unoprostone is a docosanoid for glaucoma.'),
('b527a1fb-ecf5-5687-8e3d-7800bf7a08a0'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'What ocular adverse effect is associated with long-term use of prostaglandin analogs in glaucoma?', '["Corneal opacity","Iris pigmentation (darkening)","Retinal detachment","Cataract formation","Conjunctival blanching"]'::jsonb, 'Iris pigmentation (darkening)', 'Prostaglandin analogs can cause irreversible iris pigmentation.'),
('82fdd4f6-62cc-5fc8-b4f5-da7f25d41b22'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Deepening of the eyelid sulcus (periorbital fat atrophy) in users of prostaglandin analogs results from their ability to:', '["Inhibit lipogenesis resulting in decreased functional activity","Cause local tissue remodeling and fat redistribution","Block insulin receptors mediated by specific receptor activation","Increase cortisol without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Block growth factors leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cause local tissue remodeling and fat redistribution', 'Periorbital fat atrophy is a known side effect.'),
('efee88df-5543-5022-bde6-35f349a32191'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Apraclonidine eye drops are used in the diagnosis of which condition?', '["Horner syndrome","Glaucoma","Uveitis","Retinal detachment","Corneal ulcer"]'::jsonb, 'Horner syndrome', 'Apraclonidine reverses miosis of Horner syndrome.'),
('80caefef-175f-540e-a978-33be98d3e30a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'The classic triad of signs in Horner syndrome is:', '["Miosis, ptosis, anhidrosis","Mydriasis, ptosis, sweating","Miosis, mydriasis, sweating","Ptosis, mydriasis, anhidrosis","No abnormality"]'::jsonb, 'Miosis, ptosis, anhidrosis', 'Horner syndrome: miosis, ptosis, anhidrosis.'),
('c90f73f6-a5ad-57a7-95d4-107225c3d5e1'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Based on the level of interruption of the oculosympathetic pathway, Horner syndrome is classified as:', '["First, second, or third order","Anterior or posterior","Central or peripheral","Sympathetic or parasympathetic","Ocular or systemic"]'::jsonb, 'First, second, or third order', 'Classified by level of sympathetic pathway disruption.'),
('13bfd38d-347b-57cd-8db4-cbd24ee00c9e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'After instillation of cocaine eye drops in a patient with Horner syndrome, the expected response is:', '["Dilate normal pupil but not Horner (no NE release)","Dilate pupil in Horner accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Constrict pupil mediated by specific receptor activation","No effect without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bilateral mydriasis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dilate normal pupil but not Horner (no NE release)', 'Cocaine blocks NE reuptake; in Horner, no NE to block.'),
('51b8a3ec-4586-5137-94ce-cd3a90d0141d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Evaluation of denervation supersensitivity (e.g., with apraclonidine) in Horner syndrome is used to:', '["Confirm diagnosis and localize lesion","Treat the condition","Cause mydriasis","Lower IOP","Reduce inflammation"]'::jsonb, 'Confirm diagnosis and localize lesion', 'Denervation supersensitivity helps confirm and localize Horner.'),
('107e5634-1414-5ea7-b4fd-ca5440d8c634'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'In essential blepharospasm, botulinum toxin is injected into which muscle?', '["Levator palpebrae superioris","Orbicularis oculi (pretarsal)","Ciliary muscle","Dilator pupillae","Rectus muscles"]'::jsonb, 'Orbicularis oculi (pretarsal)', 'Botulinum toxin injected into orbicularis oculi.'),
('afc7575e-8584-5189-8a0b-c025ccff241a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'In cervical dystonia, botulinum toxin is injected into which muscles?', '["SCM and trapezius","Rectus abdominis","Quadriceps","Deltoid","Biceps"]'::jsonb, 'SCM and trapezius', 'Botulinum toxin injected into affected neck muscles.'),
('a92ec8d4-5803-55c9-a7df-40aa2c14d7c4'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'For palmar and plantar hyperhidrosis, botulinum toxin is administered by:', '["Palms and soles (intradermal injection)","Deep muscle injection","Intravenous injection","Oral administration","Inhalation"]'::jsonb, 'Palms and soles (intradermal injection)', 'Botulinum toxin injected intradermally into palmar/plantar skin.'),
('3208bfca-571c-51c9-8cd5-e116c305fece'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'For detrusor overactivity, botulinum toxin is injected into which structure?', '["Detrusor muscle (intravesical injection)","Urethral sphincter accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Kidney mediated by specific receptor activation","Bladder neck without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ureter leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Detrusor muscle (intravesical injection)', 'Botulinum toxin injected into the detrusor muscle.'),
('36c6b039-36fe-560e-ad35-d0b281668436'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Botulinum toxin type A exerts its effect by cleaving which protein?', '["SNAP-25","VAMP (synaptobrevin)","Syntaxin","None","All"]'::jsonb, 'SNAP-25', 'Type A specifically cleaves SNAP-25.'),
('ac3fb417-66c9-54b4-bdde-881d97f2de48'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'The full paralytic effect of botulinum toxin typically develops within:', '["Immediate","24-72 hours","1-2 weeks","1 month","6 months"]'::jsonb, '1-2 weeks', 'Botulinum toxin typically takes 1-2 weeks for full effect.'),
('04ed1e40-2d95-5069-8e80-fdbd715d9ced'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'The clinical effect of a single injection of botulinum toxin usually lasts approximately:', '["1-2 weeks","1-3 months","6-12 months","Permanent","2-3 years"]'::jsonb, '1-3 months', 'Effects last approximately 3-6 months.'),
('306ce724-4df4-531d-9d23-ddd051070a02'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Neostigmine is available in which formulations?', '["Oral and injectable","Oral only","Injectable only","Topical only","Inhalational"]'::jsonb, 'Oral and injectable', 'Neostigmine is available as oral and injectable.'),
('e7d6f72c-591b-50f2-a489-cfeb2cc3e42c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Echothiophate is used in the treatment of which type of glaucoma?', '["Angle-closure","Open-angle (chronic)","Narrow-angle","Congenital","Secondary only"]'::jsonb, 'Open-angle (chronic)', 'Echothiophate is for chronic open-angle glaucoma.'),
('70322a11-e8f7-5e86-abbd-6833877f5a83'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Pilocarpine causes spasm of accommodation, which clinically produces:', '["Blurred distance vision (myopic shift)","Blurred near vision accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","No accommodation effect mediated by specific receptor activation","Double vision without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ptosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Blurred distance vision (myopic shift)', 'Pilocarpine causes ciliary spasm, making distance blurry.'),
('e205bc3e-7d0c-5863-9ca4-8a609bcdd049'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Pirenzepine is a:', '["M1 blocker for PUD","M2 blocker for arrhythmia","M3 blocker for asthma","M4 blocker for CNS","M5 blocker for vascular"]'::jsonb, 'M1 blocker for PUD', 'Pirenzepine is an M1 blocker reducing gastric acid secretion.'),
('8199f382-7c32-596d-bc05-3be5f6c35b72'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Telenzepine is a:', '["Selective M1 blocker for PUD","M2 blocker","M3 blocker","Non-selective blocker","Nicotinic blocker"]'::jsonb, 'Selective M1 blocker for PUD', 'Telenzepine is a selective M1 blocker for PUD.'),
('61fd3792-8ce7-51d4-adeb-f5a2aa80f60b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following features are seen in autonomic failure?', '["Orthostatic hypotension","Neurogenic bladder","Erectile dysfunction","All of the above","None"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Autonomic failure involves multiple systems.'),
('255f9f24-424e-5a35-ac91-b7490c2d147b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Multiple system atrophy (MSA) involves degeneration of:', '["Autonomic nuclei in brainstem and spinal cord","Motor cortex only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sensory nerves mediated by specific receptor activation","Cerebellum only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Peripheral nerves only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Autonomic nuclei in brainstem and spinal cord', 'MSA involves degeneration of autonomic centers.'),
('015b2392-ee07-505d-9edb-b977cf410fac'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'In contrast to multiple system atrophy, pure autonomic failure:', '["Involves only peripheral autonomic NS","Also involves parkinsonism and cerebellar ataxia","Better prognosis","Always hereditary","No orthostatic hypotension"]'::jsonb, 'Involves only peripheral autonomic NS', 'Pure autonomic failure is confined to peripheral autonomic neurons.'),
('6e2015ad-f12f-57e3-a77c-9a575697845f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Familial dysautonomia (Riley-Day syndrome) results from a mutation in which gene?', '["Dopamine beta-hydroxylase gene","IKBKAP gene (ELP1)","Tyrosine hydroxylase gene","COMT gene","MAO gene"]'::jsonb, 'IKBKAP gene (ELP1)', 'Riley-Day: autosomal recessive, IKBKAP/ELP1 mutation.'),
('616b6471-d400-5541-8f43-beb8cf401d47'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'The characteristic clinical feature of dopamine beta-hydroxylase deficiency is:', '["Severe orthostatic hypotension (no NE/E synthesis)","Hypertension accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Tachycardia mediated by specific receptor activation","Asthma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Diabetes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Severe orthostatic hypotension (no NE/E synthesis)', 'DBH deficiency prevents dopamine to NE conversion.'),
('00c5ffa7-2331-58c8-a898-9c13c6440f4a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following are used in the treatment of orthostatic hypotension?', '["Fludrocortisone","Midodrine (alpha-1 agonist)","Droxidopa (NE precursor)","All of the above","None"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Fludrocortisone, midodrine, and droxidopa are all used.'),
('acd73c46-3d2c-5ec7-a062-38cdc31eb009'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'What is the mechanism of action of ivabradine?', '["Blocks beta receptors","Selectively inhibits If channels (slows HR)","Blocks calcium channels","Blocks potassium channels","Inhibits ACE"]'::jsonb, 'Selectively inhibits If channels (slows HR)', 'Ivabradine inhibits pacemaker If current, reducing HR.'),
('97ca3a01-af10-5780-8b0a-02f484b32e6d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Sacubitril is a:', '["Neprilysin inhibitor (increases natriuretic peptides)","ACE inhibitor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","ARB mediated by specific receptor activation","Beta-blocker without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Aldosterone antagonist leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Neprilysin inhibitor (increases natriuretic peptides)', 'Sacubitril inhibits neprilysin, increasing natriuretic peptides.'),
('97435f86-e148-5168-b5b0-18b29954d482'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Sacubitril/valsartan (Entresto) is indicated for the treatment of:', '["Hypertension only resulting in decreased functional activity","Heart failure with reduced EF","Diabetes mediated by specific receptor activation","Asthma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Glaucoma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Heart failure with reduced EF', 'Entresto is for heart failure with reduced EF.'),
('103f81cb-cda5-5c80-a4a3-fcb3a82c6a9f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'The combination of hydralazine and isosorbide dinitrate (A-HeFT trial) was shown to be particularly beneficial in:', '["All patients resulting in decreased functional activity","African American patients particularly","Elderly only mediated by specific receptor activation","Children without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pregnant women leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'African American patients particularly', 'A-HeFT trial showed benefit in African American HF patients.'),
('26c43d8d-7474-52ac-b41a-6b5b0b1287bd'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'What is the mechanism by which spironolactone reduces cardiac fibrosis and remodeling?', '["Increases K+ excretion resulting in decreased functional activity","Blocks aldosterone receptors, reducing cardiac remodeling","Direct vasodilator mediated by specific receptor activation","Inhibits ACE without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Blocks beta receptors leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Blocks aldosterone receptors, reducing cardiac remodeling', 'Spironolactone blocks aldosterone, reducing fibrosis and remodeling.'),
('5439e287-35cf-5ccd-9a74-f7c64de334b6'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Amiloride exerts its diuretic action by:', '["Blocking ENaC","Blocking aldosterone receptors","Inhibiting Na-K-2Cl cotransporter","Inhibiting NaCl cotransporter","Blocking ADH"]'::jsonb, 'Blocking ENaC', 'Amiloride blocks ENaC in the collecting duct.'),
('e0e4d41b-077d-5a30-beab-a6ce7115031a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'A potentially dangerous adverse effect of spironolactone therapy is:', '["Hypokalemia","Hyperkalemia","Hyponatremia","Hypernatremia","Hypocalcemia"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperkalemia', 'Spironolactone can cause dangerous hyperkalemia.'),
('cac61442-e142-5791-9d61-b81db8845bba'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which diuretic provides rapid diuresis and venodilation and is therefore useful in acute pulmonary edema?', '["Spironolactone","Furosemide (loop diuretic)","Hydrochlorothiazide","Amiloride","Mannitol"]'::jsonb, 'Furosemide (loop diuretic)', 'Furosemide provides rapid diuresis and venodilation.'),
('20c3a639-aa0e-54ab-b3df-7b2f4d7528f1'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Furosemide acts by inhibiting the Na-K-2Cl cotransporter in which part of the nephron?', '["Proximal tubule","Thick ascending limb of Henle (Na-K-2Cl)","Distal convoluted tubule","Collecting duct","Bowman''s capsule"]'::jsonb, 'Thick ascending limb of Henle (Na-K-2Cl)', 'Furosemide inhibits Na-K-2Cl cotransporter in thick ascending limb.'),
('2c23f64a-0de7-526f-8b51-c6ccfabc0c98'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Compared with furosemide, bumetanide is:', '["Less potent","More potent (1mg = 40mg furosemide)","Not a loop diuretic","Acts on distal tubule","Potassium-sparing"]'::jsonb, 'More potent (1mg = 40mg furosemide)', 'Bumetanide is 40x more potent than furosemide.'),
('1791e4a6-e13f-5874-b589-df51a6e1d457'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Compared with furosemide, torsemide is characterized by being:', '["More protein-bound, slower dissociation","Blocks different receptors","Different nephron segments","Metabolized more slowly","Renally excreted more slowly"]'::jsonb, 'More protein-bound, slower dissociation', 'Torsemide has higher protein binding and slower dissociation.'),
('a2310c90-29a1-5214-a929-9991794ce9a7'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which feature distinguishes ethacrynic acid from the other loop diuretics?', '["Less ototoxic resulting in decreased functional activity","NOT a sulfonamide (safe in sulfa allergy)","More potent mediated by specific receptor activation","Shorter duration without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Acts on proximal tubule leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'NOT a sulfonamide (safe in sulfa allergy)', 'Ethacrynic acid is the only non-sulfonamide loop diuretic.'),
('860e5598-36ad-52c1-9f99-4cbec0c8f490'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which diuretic is potassium-sparing?', '["Furosemide resulting in decreased functional activity","Hydrochlorothiazide accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Spironolactone (potassium-sparing)","Chlorthalidone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Metolazone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Spironolactone (potassium-sparing)', 'Spironolactone is potassium-sparing.'),
('e1af0cf3-a807-5936-8337-377d7b7e2054'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Methyclothiazide belongs to which class of diuretics?', '["Loop diuretic","Thiazide diuretic","Potassium-sparing diuretic","Osmotic diuretic","Carbonic anhydrase inhibitor"]'::jsonb, 'Thiazide diuretic', 'Methyclothiazide is a thiazide diuretic.'),
('076ef53f-272b-5794-be57-873632072ffc'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'What is the advantage of chlorthalidone over hydrochlorothiazide?', '["Cheaper resulting in decreased functional activity","Longer duration and stronger CV outcome evidence","Fewer side effects mediated by specific receptor activation","More potent diuretic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Does not cause hypokalemia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Longer duration and stronger CV outcome evidence', 'Chlorthalidone has longer duration and stronger outcome data.'),
('ccb2871e-9331-533f-9fbb-c04fafc534ef'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Sequential nephron blockade for refractory edema involves combining:', '["Two thiazides","A loop diuretic with a thiazide-like diuretic","Two loop diuretics","A thiazide with an aldosterone antagonist","A loop diuretic with amiloride"]'::jsonb, 'A loop diuretic with a thiazide-like diuretic', 'Sequential nephron blockade: loop + thiazide for refractory edema.'),
('32a1668c-8f86-57e2-9457-da567ae65636'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Combined therapy with a loop diuretic and a thiazide-like diuretic is indicated for:', '["Mild edema resulting in decreased functional activity","Refractory edema not responding to single diuretic","Hypertension only mediated by specific receptor activation","Glaucoma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Heart failure only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Refractory edema not responding to single diuretic', 'Combination is for refractory edema.'),
('b402777f-5e8c-53c4-96fb-a8240eeca55f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following is true regarding chlorothiazide?', '["Oral only resulting in decreased functional activity","Intravenous formulation available","Topical only mediated by specific receptor activation","Inhalational without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Rectal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Intravenous formulation available', 'Chlorothiazide has an IV formulation for patients who cannot take oral.'),
('563e0dea-cd82-557d-842f-1c3f763e82ba'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Indapamide is a:', '["Thiazide-like diuretic with additional vasodilatory properties","Loop diuretic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Potassium-sparing diuretic mediated by specific receptor activation","Osmotic diuretic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CA inhibitor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thiazide-like diuretic with additional vasodilatory properties', 'Indapamide has thiazide-like diuretic and vasodilatory properties.'),
('e1d4c23d-8694-58f9-afe7-84726c487799'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which combination of drugs, termed the ''triple whammy'', predisposes to acute kidney injury?', '["ACE inhibitor + NSAID + diuretic","ACE inhibitor + statin + aspirin","NSAID + antibiotic + PPI","Beta-blocker + CCB + diuretic","ARB + statin + diuretic"]'::jsonb, 'ACE inhibitor + NSAID + diuretic', 'Triple whammy: ACE inhibitor + NSAID + diuretic causes AKI.'),
('91e3412f-8d48-556f-9367-9df064317375'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which class of antihypertensive drugs is most commonly associated with postural hypotension?', '["Alpha-1 blockers","Beta-blockers","Calcium channel blockers","ACE inhibitors","All antihypertensives"]'::jsonb, 'Alpha-1 blockers', 'Alpha-1 blockers are most commonly associated with postural hypotension.'),
('696d85da-f242-582c-8b11-3a3a3e9c6aa7'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which class of antihypertensive drugs characteristically causes reflex tachycardia?', '["Direct vasodilators (hydralazine, minoxidil)","Beta-blockers accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Central alpha-2 agonists mediated by specific receptor activation","Alpha-1 blockers without affecting the primary tissue pathway","ACE inhibitors leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Direct vasodilators (hydralazine, minoxidil)', 'Direct vasodilators cause significant reflex tachycardia.'),
('d1359a34-a385-5727-b1e1-d1d59f11dd80'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'The dry cough associated with ACE inhibitors is caused by:', '["Increased angiotensin II","Increased bradykinin and substance P","Increased aldosterone","Increased histamine","Increased serotonin"]'::jsonb, 'Increased bradykinin and substance P', 'ACE inhibitor cough: increased bradykinin and substance P accumulation.'),
('447d6628-31f9-5024-a84e-d4a8a81fec02'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which structural feature of captopril binds to the zinc atom in the ACE active site?', '["Has a sulfhydryl group (binds ACE zinc)","Is a prodrug","Has the longest half-life","Is the most selective","Has no side effects"]'::jsonb, 'Has a sulfhydryl group (binds ACE zinc)', 'Captopril''s sulfhydryl group chelates zinc in ACE active site.'),
('3d00f38a-e8ad-52f9-96cf-f87585c4e733'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'In vivo, enalapril is hydrolyzed to which active metabolite?', '["Enalaprilat (active diacid form)","Captopril accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lisinopril mediated by specific receptor activation","Ramipril without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Benazepril leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Enalaprilat (active diacid form)', 'Enalapril is hydrolyzed to enalaprilat, the active form.'),
('b57f54ce-a154-542f-93f3-cedb9f541bac'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Regarding lisinopril, which statement is correct?', '["Is a prodrug","Is directly active (not a prodrug)","Has shorter half-life","Has more side effects","Is less potent"]'::jsonb, 'Is directly active (not a prodrug)', 'Lisinopril is directly active, not a prodrug.'),
('2338c409-c27b-5574-8d31-8211f8d66f39'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which trial demonstrated the cardiovascular protective effects of ramipril in high-risk patients?', '["HOPE trial","ALLHAT trial","LIFE trial","ASCOT trial","VALUE trial"]'::jsonb, 'HOPE trial', 'HOPE trial showed ramipril''s cardiovascular protective effects.'),
('bc5a47e5-0e54-59d4-89c5-698f02ba466c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Why do angiotensin receptor blockers (ARBs) not produce cough?', '["Inhibits ACE resulting in decreased functional activity","Blocks AT1 receptors (no bradykinin accumulation)","Is a prodrug mediated by specific receptor activation","Is more potent without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Causes more cough leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Blocks AT1 receptors (no bradykinin accumulation)', 'ARBs block AT1 receptors without affecting bradykinin metabolism.'),
('a7eea2bf-5a03-59e4-a4a1-bfc5cbf90ede'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Why does losartan lower serum uric acid levels?', '["It blocks AT1 receptors in the kidney","It inhibits uric acid reabsorption via URAT1 transporter","It inhibits xanthine oxidase","It increases uric acid production","It blocks adenosine receptors"]'::jsonb, 'It inhibits uric acid reabsorption via URAT1 transporter', 'Losartan blocks URAT1, increasing uric acid excretion.'),
('ae73df41-45dc-59dc-9021-65751a92dd46'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'What property of telmisartan accounts for its prolonged duration of action?', '["It is a prodrug","It has high lipophilicity and tissue binding","It is renally excreted slowly","It is metabolized slowly","It blocks multiple receptors"]'::jsonb, 'It has high lipophilicity and tissue binding', 'Telmisartan''s high lipophilicity gives it prolonged tissue binding.'),
('92176533-e586-53b0-a861-c8e9215ecb23'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which statement is true regarding irbesartan?', '["It is the cheapest ARB resulting in decreased functional activity","It has proven renoprotective effects in diabetic nephropathy trials","It is the most potent ARB mediated by specific receptor activation","It has no side effects without affecting the primary tissue pathway","It is available IV only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'It has proven renoprotective effects in diabetic nephropathy trials', 'Irbesartan has strong evidence for renoprotection in diabetic nephropathy.'),
('b3841c55-c688-5bf3-9507-8e7b40e8f576'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which statement is correct regarding candesartan?', '["It is a prodrug with high AT1 receptor affinity","It blocks multiple receptors","It inhibits ACE","It has a short half-life","It is not an ARB"]'::jsonb, 'It is a prodrug with high AT1 receptor affinity', 'Candesartan has very high AT1 receptor binding affinity.'),
('b42a6212-0a96-5615-a37d-cb8459773d4b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'In the RALES trial, spironolactone reduced mortality in severe heart failure by approximately:', '["10%","20%","30%","40%","50%"]'::jsonb, '30%', 'RALES trial: spironolactone reduced mortality by 30% in severe HF.'),
('886b9502-0484-59f6-9c98-5cb2253f1b5d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'What was the outcome of the EPHESUS trial with eplerenone in post-myocardial infarction heart failure patients?', '["No benefit","Significant mortality reduction","Increased mortality","No effect on mortality","Increased hospitalization"]'::jsonb, 'Significant mortality reduction', 'EPHESUS showed eplerenone reduced mortality post-MI with HF.'),
('d59fabc6-49af-5fdd-af07-05c2bbbefe86'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'The predominant hemodynamic effect of hydralazine is:', '["Venodilation predominantly resulting in decreased functional activity","Arteriolar dilation (increased CO, reflex tachycardia)","Both arterial and venous dilation mediated by specific receptor activation","No hemodynamic effects without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bradycardia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Arteriolar dilation (increased CO, reflex tachycardia)', 'Hydralazine causes arteriolar dilation, increasing CO and causing reflex tachycardia.'),
('00cf6d48-5f59-548a-90d1-96992b422a9e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'The hemodynamic effect of minoxidil is:', '["Selective venodilation","Arteriolar dilation (potent vasodilator)","Both arterial and venous dilation","No hemodynamic effects","Bradycardia"]'::jsonb, 'Arteriolar dilation (potent vasodilator)', 'Minoxidil is a potent arteriolar vasodilator.'),
('8be26fe8-094b-5f8f-ba2a-8332b7b6736b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Adverse effects of minoxidil include:', '["Coronary steal phenomenon","Hypertrichosis (excessive hair growth)","Both A and B","No side effects","Only hypotension"]'::jsonb, 'Both A and B', 'Minoxidil causes both coronary steal and hypertrichosis.'),
('54d4a463-f6fd-5bad-a942-7704b260de1a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Diazoxide is used clinically for:', '["Hypertensive emergencies and insulinoma","Only hypertension accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only insulinoma mediated by specific receptor activation","Diabetes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Asthma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hypertensive emergencies and insulinoma', 'Diazoxide is used for hypertensive emergencies and insulinoma (hyperinsulinism).'),
('48631c7c-8357-551f-ba86-97425166915a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Sodium nitroprusside produces vasodilation by releasing:', '["Nitric oxide and cyanide","Only nitric oxide","Only cyanide","Carbon monoxide","Sulfur dioxide"]'::jsonb, 'Nitric oxide and cyanide', 'Nitroprusside releases NO (vasodilation) and cyanide (toxicity).'),
('885b78fe-e254-51f2-bb55-909a1e947b45'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following are antidotes for cyanide poisoning?', '["Sodium thiosulfate and hydroxocobalamin","Naloxone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Flumazenil mediated by specific receptor activation","Atropine without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Physostigmine leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sodium thiosulfate and hydroxocobalamin', 'Sodium thiosulfate and hydroxocobalamin are cyanide antidotes.'),
('1a0dc365-1fa0-5002-ae05-62aefab9cd60'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Methylene blue is used in the treatment of:', '["Methemoglobinemia and cyanide poisoning (in combination)","Hypertension only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Diabetes only mediated by specific receptor activation","Asthma only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Heart failure only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Methemoglobinemia and cyanide poisoning (in combination)', 'Methylene blue treats methemoglobinemia and is adjunct in cyanide poisoning.'),
('3e137d6a-2bb8-5c99-966b-5597b23cbe7d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Dihydropyridine (DHP) calcium channel blockers (e.g., amlodipine) preferentially:', '["Preferentially dilates arterioles (reduces afterload)","Has strong cardiac depressant effects","Blocks AV conduction primarily","Is used for arrhythmias only","Is a beta-blocker"]'::jsonb, 'Preferentially dilates arterioles (reduces afterload)', 'DHP CCBs preferentially dilate arterioles.'),
('7e848f87-93d5-5fe7-9d43-04d0b9e8f1b3'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Short-acting nifedipine should be avoided in which clinical scenario?', '["Stable angina resulting in decreased functional activity","Hypertensive emergencies with reflex tachycardia risk","Hypertension mediated by specific receptor activation","Heart failure without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Diabetes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hypertensive emergencies with reflex tachycardia risk', 'Short-acting nifedipine can cause precipitous BP drop and reflex tachycardia.'),
('99323181-6479-5c26-81ab-e8f45608ffe3'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'In contrast to dihydropyridines, verapamil:', '["Preferentially dilates arterioles resulting in decreased functional activity","Has strong cardiac depressant effects (negative inotrope/chronotrope)","Is used for arrhythmias only mediated by specific receptor activation","Is a beta-blocker without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Causes reflex tachycardia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Has strong cardiac depressant effects (negative inotrope/chronotrope)', 'Verapamil has strong cardiac effects: negative inotrope and chronotrope.'),
('5879a239-3fee-5b90-9f5f-766cac760846'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Diltiazem differs from other calcium channel blockers by virtue of its:', '["Selectivity for vascular vs cardiac tissue","Potency accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Half-life mediated by specific receptor activation","Side effects without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Route of administration leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Selectivity for vascular vs cardiac tissue', 'Diltiazem has intermediate vascular/cardiac selectivity.'),
('1a285c8f-b876-54be-b949-d2143ef6b71f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Because diltiazem has less negative inotropic effect than verapamil, it is preferred for use in:', '["Patients with better preserved cardiac function","Patients with severe LV dysfunction","Patients with HF only","All heart failure patients","None"]'::jsonb, 'Patients with better preserved cardiac function', 'Diltiazem has less negative inotropic effect than verapamil.'),
('d8b36bac-e1fd-5a75-a442-e4e2570c8270'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Nicardipine given intravenously is commonly used for:', '["Hypertensive emergencies (IV)","Only chronic hypertension","Only angina","Heart failure","Diabetes"]'::jsonb, 'Hypertensive emergencies (IV)', 'Nicardipine IV is used for hypertensive emergencies.'),
('98d72384-2da3-5b5c-ab29-6af636b614f5'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Bepridil is unique among calcium channel blockers because it possesses:', '["Both DHP and non-DHP properties","Only DHP properties","Only non-DHP properties","No CCB activity","Only antiarrhythmic activity"]'::jsonb, 'Both DHP and non-DHP properties', 'Bepridil has properties of both DHP and non-DHP CCBs.'),
('9029aeb0-1845-5e6e-b6ef-8af74448cf6d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Common adverse effects of verapamil include:', '["Peripheral edema","Bradycardia and constipation","Headache","Hyperkalemia","None"]'::jsonb, 'Bradycardia and constipation', 'Verapamil causes bradycardia (cardiac effect) and constipation (smooth muscle relaxation).'),
('340ad40d-8179-57bb-a543-33f4c98dbacf'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Verapamil is relatively contraindicated in which condition?', '["Angina resulting in decreased functional activity","Hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy (verapamil)","Diabetes mediated by specific receptor activation","Asthma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Heart failure leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy (verapamil)', 'Verapamil is contraindicated in HOCM (can worsen obstruction).'),
('3596feaa-e07a-50f9-a9ca-93b73691df0f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Regarding the use of calcium channel blockers in heart failure, which statement is correct?', '["Contraindicated in all HF","Generally avoided in HFrEF, but amlodipine is safe","The treatment of choice for HF","Beneficial in all HF types","Not used in HF"]'::jsonb, 'Generally avoided in HFrEF, but amlodipine is safe', 'Amlodipine and felodipine are safe in HFrEF (PRAISE trial).'),
('200e4a8b-e9df-5d6f-a4a4-e81d78afd4e2'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'In which setting did the RALES trial demonstrate a mortality benefit?', '["Spironolactone in severe heart failure","Enalapril in heart failure","Carvedilol in heart failure","Losartan in heart failure","None"]'::jsonb, 'Spironolactone in severe heart failure', 'RALES: spironolactone reduced mortality in severe HF.'),
('72b1597e-fab1-5a43-9b05-174d2c8d6807'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which trial demonstrated that enalapril reduced mortality in patients with severe heart failure?', '["Spironolactone in HF","Enalapril in severe HF","Carvedilol in HF","Losartan in HF","None"]'::jsonb, 'Enalapril in severe HF', 'CONSENSUS: enalapril reduced mortality in severe HF.'),
('d457c486-7d2c-5367-a662-e5d444fee6bc'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'The ELITE-II trial compared which two drugs?', '["Losartan vs captopril in HF","Enalapril vs spironolactone in HF","Carvedilol vs metoprolol in HF","None","All"]'::jsonb, 'Losartan vs captopril in HF', 'ELITE-II: losartan was non-inferior to captopril in HF.'),
('b0ca1e44-62fb-5cac-8407-dd6f1d08c744'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'The VALUE trial compared which two drugs?', '["Amlodipine vs valsartan in hypertension","Losartan vs enalapril in hypertension","Carvedilol vs metoprolol in HF","None","All"]'::jsonb, 'Amlodipine vs valsartan in hypertension', 'VALUE: amlodipine vs valsartan in high-risk hypertension.'),
('b2e0ed77-1321-5bee-8747-51e1e469d6f6'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'The LIFE trial compared which two drugs?', '["Losartan vs atenolol in hypertensive LVH","Enalapril vs captopril in HF accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Carvedilol vs metoprolol in HF mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Losartan vs atenolol in hypertensive LVH', 'LIFE: losartan was superior to atenolol in hypertensive LVH.'),
('37a95893-6612-58db-9b93-1c3e050757f1'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'The ALLHAT trial compared which treatment regimens?', '["Chlorthalidone vs amlodipine vs lisinopril vs doxazosin","Losartan vs enalapril in HF accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Carvedilol vs metoprolol in HF mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Chlorthalidone vs amlodipine vs lisinopril vs doxazosin', 'ALLHAT: thiazide diuretic was superior or equivalent to other antihypertensives.'),
('fc8889c3-a18d-5b85-bcb9-bd82a123feeb'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'The HOPE trial evaluated the effect of which drug in high cardiovascular risk patients?', '["Ramipril in high cardiovascular risk patients","Losartan in HF accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Carvedilol in HF mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ramipril in high cardiovascular risk patients', 'HOPE: ramipril reduced CV events in high-risk patients.'),
('9664e5ad-3872-5efb-9b70-67d7889f1da1'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'The ASCOT trial compared:', '["Amlodipine +/- perindopril vs atenolol +/- thiazide","Losartan vs enalapril in HF accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Carvedilol vs metoprolol in HF mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Amlodipine +/- perindopril vs atenolol +/- thiazide', 'ASCOT-BPLA: amlodipine-based was superior to atenolol-based therapy.'),
('1389404e-1d29-5108-93b1-341d1cce18fb'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'The ACCOMPLISH trial compared:', '["Benazepril + amlodipine vs benazepril + HCTZ in high-risk patients","Losartan vs enalapril in HF accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Carvedilol vs metoprolol in HF mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Benazepril + amlodipine vs benazepril + HCTZ in high-risk patients', 'ACCOMPLISH: ACEI + CCB was superior to ACEI + thiazide in high-risk patients.'),
('a7cbbf52-9dad-5da5-8621-9769be16434d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'The IDNT trial evaluated which drug in diabetic nephropathy?', '["Irbesartan in diabetic nephropathy","Losartan in diabetic nephropathy","Valsartan in diabetic nephropathy","None","All"]'::jsonb, 'Irbesartan in diabetic nephropathy', 'IDNT: irbesartan slowed progression of diabetic nephropathy.'),
('a477dfe1-93d0-5d2f-9709-76c87b99deae'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'The RENAAL trial evaluated which drug in diabetic nephropathy?', '["Losartan in diabetic nephropathy","Irbesartan in diabetic nephropathy","Valsartan in diabetic nephropathy","None","All"]'::jsonb, 'Losartan in diabetic nephropathy', 'RENAAL: losartan slowed progression of diabetic nephropathy.'),
('d1cfceba-4e30-5025-9d37-2fd6425b9424'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'The DETAIL trial demonstrated that telmisartan was non-inferior to enalapril in diabetic nephropathy. Which comparison does the DETAIL trial refer to?', '["Telmisartan vs enalapril in diabetic nephropathy","Losartan vs captopril in HF accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Irbesartan vs enalapril mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Telmisartan vs enalapril in diabetic nephropathy', 'DETAIL: telmisartan was non-inferior to enalapril in diabetic nephropathy.'),
('94f095bd-79f3-5f47-ae84-52aa0b534c45'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'The ONTARGET trial compared which of the following regimens in patients at high cardiovascular risk?', '["Ramipril vs telmisartan vs combination in high CV risk","Losartan vs enalapril in HF accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Carvedilol vs metoprolol in HF mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ramipril vs telmisartan vs combination in high CV risk', 'ONTARGET: telmisartan was non-inferior to ramipril; combination increased side effects.'),
('600bf178-5f7c-582d-abb2-d8fe3a93b1a4'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'The TRANSCEND trial evaluated which of the following?', '["Telmisartan in patients intolerant to ACE inhibitors","Losartan in HF accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Carvedilol in HF mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Telmisartan in patients intolerant to ACE inhibitors', 'TRANSCEND: telmisartan had modest benefit in ACEI-intolerant patients.'),
('798b7fc4-3a23-5a35-8d11-801f4f787554'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'The 4S trial demonstrated that simvastatin reduced which of the following?', '["Cholesterol only resulting in decreased functional activity","Coronary events in post-MI patients","Blood pressure only mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Coronary events in post-MI patients', 'The 4S trial showed simvastatin reduced coronary events (not spironolactone).'),
('e373cff6-d5fe-5023-939d-39000086d65c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'The WOSCOPS trial evaluated which of the following?', '["Pravastatin in primary prevention of CAD","Atorvastatin in secondary prevention","Simvastatin in primary prevention","None","All"]'::jsonb, 'Pravastatin in primary prevention of CAD', 'WOSCOPS: pravastatin reduced CAD events in primary prevention.'),
('c510fdcd-4634-5eb3-ac8c-3e430cd34f0c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'The CARE trial evaluated which of the following?', '["Pravastatin in post-MI patients with average cholesterol","Atorvastatin in primary prevention accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Simvastatin in secondary prevention mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pravastatin in post-MI patients with average cholesterol', 'CARE: pravastatin reduced coronary events post-MI even with average cholesterol.'),
('88189f53-2c49-59d4-a4ec-402bd4b777b3'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'The LIPID trial evaluated which of the following?', '["Pravastatin in patients with prior MI or unstable angina","Atorvastatin in primary prevention accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Simvastatin in secondary prevention mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pravastatin in patients with prior MI or unstable angina', 'LIPID: pravastatin reduced mortality in CAD patients.'),
('2b4db16d-1274-597f-89ab-7dd30217c3ae'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'The HPS (Heart Protection Study) trial evaluated which of the following?', '["Simvastatin in high-risk patients","Atorvastatin in primary prevention","Pravastatin in primary prevention","None","All"]'::jsonb, 'Simvastatin in high-risk patients', 'HPS: simvastatin reduced CV events in a broad range of high-risk patients.'),
('bb8d0ced-14f9-5c0d-92ac-97943af359d5'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'The PROVE-IT trial compared which of the following?', '["Atorvastatin 80mg vs pravastatin 40mg post-ACS","Simvastatin vs pravastatin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Atorvastatin vs simvastatin mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Atorvastatin 80mg vs pravastatin 40mg post-ACS', 'PROVE-IT: intensive statin therapy (atorvastatin 80mg) was superior post-ACS.'),
('f1225788-21ed-5cfe-b17d-231261d3a7e7'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'The TNT trial compared which of the following?', '["Atorvastatin 80mg vs 10mg in stable CAD","Pravastatin in primary prevention","Simvastatin in secondary prevention","None","All"]'::jsonb, 'Atorvastatin 80mg vs 10mg in stable CAD', 'TNT: high-dose atorvastatin was superior to low-dose in stable CAD.'),
('bbce358d-540e-5ea8-9208-9cfa98d927af'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'The JUPITER trial evaluated which of the following?', '["Rosuvastatin in apparently healthy patients with elevated CRP","Atorvastatin in primary prevention accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pravastatin in secondary prevention mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Rosuvastatin in apparently healthy patients with elevated CRP', 'JUPITER: rosuvastatin reduced CV events in patients with elevated CRP.'),
('4b949508-5952-567b-9543-f019c7fd2888'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'The IDEAL trial compared which of the following?', '["Atorvastatin 80mg vs simvastatin 20-40mg in post-MI","Pravastatin vs simvastatin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Atorvastatin vs rosuvastatin mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Atorvastatin 80mg vs simvastatin 20-40mg in post-MI', 'IDEAL: high-dose atorvastatin was non-inferior to simvastatin in post-MI.'),
('dbacc3f8-e7ac-502e-bcbf-24386b67b499'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'The SHARP trial evaluated which of the following?', '["Simvastatin + ezetimibe in CKD patients","Atorvastatin in CKD accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pravastatin in CKD mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Simvastatin + ezetimibe in CKD patients', 'SHARP: simvastatin + ezetimibe reduced CV events in CKD.'),
('d1fd61e0-4f9c-5c6b-a1e6-a880a97882d6'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'The FOURIER trial evaluated which of the following?', '["Evolocumab (PCSK9 inhibitor) in stable atherosclerotic CV disease","Atorvastatin in primary prevention accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pravastatin in secondary prevention mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Evolocumab (PCSK9 inhibitor) in stable atherosclerotic CV disease', 'FOURIER: evolocumab reduced CV events on top of statin therapy.'),
('3e4bb8fc-eacf-531c-a680-971f7dbde3e2'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'The ODYSSEY OUTCOMES trial evaluated which of the following?', '["Alirocumab (PCSK9 inhibitor) after ACS","Evolocumab in primary prevention","Atorvastatin in secondary prevention","None","All"]'::jsonb, 'Alirocumab (PCSK9 inhibitor) after ACS', 'ODYSSEY: alirocumab reduced CV events post-ACS.'),
('30cf7a53-fbc7-5f7a-a91b-8b4297818178'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following best describes the mechanism of action of ezetimibe?', '["Inhibiting cholesterol absorption in the intestine (NPC1L1)","HMG-CoA reductase inhibition","Bile acid sequestration","Inhibiting cholesterol synthesis","Increasing LDL receptor expression"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibiting cholesterol absorption in the intestine (NPC1L1)', 'Ezetimibe inhibits NPC1L1 in the small intestine, reducing cholesterol absorption.'),
('a88ec3ec-3843-588e-ba0e-40cad1727015'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following best describes the mechanism of action of bile acid sequestrant resins?', '["Inhibiting HMG-CoA reductase resulting in decreased functional activity","Binding bile acids in the intestine, increasing hepatic LDL receptor expression","Inhibiting cholesterol absorption mediated by specific receptor activation","Increasing bile acid synthesis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Blocking cholesterol synthesis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Binding bile acids in the intestine, increasing hepatic LDL receptor expression', 'Resins bind bile acids, forcing liver to use cholesterol to make more bile acids.'),
('c0684202-b079-56b6-862d-67ac6a2b921b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Fibrates primarily lower which of the following?', '["LDL cholesterol resulting in decreased functional activity","Triglycerides (via PPAR-alpha activation)","HDL only mediated by specific receptor activation","Blood pressure without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Blood glucose leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Triglycerides (via PPAR-alpha activation)', 'Fibrates activate PPAR-alpha, reducing triglycerides and modestly increasing HDL.'),
('84cc7df4-fbce-50a0-9b07-bb67c3b9bdd4'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following best describes the mechanism of action of statins?', '["Inhibiting HMG-CoA reductase (rate-limiting step in cholesterol synthesis)","Inhibiting cholesterol absorption accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Binding bile acids mediated by specific receptor activation","Increasing bile acid synthesis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Blocking cholesterol synthesis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibiting HMG-CoA reductase (rate-limiting step in cholesterol synthesis)', 'Statins competitively inhibit HMG-CoA reductase, the rate-limiting enzyme in cholesterol synthesis.'),
('ced38720-0843-5500-b0d6-48d2747d1071'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following is the most potent statin for lowering LDL cholesterol?', '["Pravastatin","Simvastatin","Atorvastatin","Rosuvastatin","Fluvastatin"]'::jsonb, 'Rosuvastatin', 'Rosuvastatin is the most potent statin for LDL reduction.'),
('6358d7ff-5cc0-5bb4-8a73-c581bee0f51b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following is true regarding pravastatin?', '["Is the most potent resulting in decreased functional activity","Is hydrophilic (less tissue penetration, fewer muscle effects)","Is the cheapest mediated by specific receptor activation","Has the longest half-life without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Is the most selective leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Is hydrophilic (less tissue penetration, fewer muscle effects)', 'Pravastatin is hydrophilic, which may explain its lower myopathy risk.'),
('3c42e41f-5602-55b2-9e71-a918237d11a9'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Why are statins contraindicated in pregnancy?', '["They are teratogenic (cholesterol needed for fetal development)","They cause hypoglycemia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","They lower blood pressure too much mediated by specific receptor activation","They cause constipation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","They cause hyperkalemia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'They are teratogenic (cholesterol needed for fetal development)', 'Statins are teratogenic; cholesterol is essential for fetal CNS development.'),
('e7b3bd8b-914d-51ec-954f-efe4b9beec77'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following increases the risk of statin-induced myopathy?', '["Concurrent use of CYP3A4 inhibitors (erythromycin, azole antifungals, grapefruit juice)","Concurrent use of beta-blockers accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Concurrent use of diuretics mediated by specific receptor activation","Concurrent use of ACE inhibitors without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Concurrent use of aspirin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Concurrent use of CYP3A4 inhibitors (erythromycin, azole antifungals, grapefruit juice)', 'CYP3A4 inhibitors increase statin levels, raising myopathy risk.'),
('d612dfd8-ad4c-5859-9e84-0cac12e793e0'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following is true regarding the effect of statins on coenzyme Q10 (CoQ10)?', '["Statins inhibit CoQ10 synthesis (same pathway)","Statins increase CoQ10 levels","Statins have no effect on CoQ10","Statins increase CoQ10 excretion","None"]'::jsonb, 'Statins inhibit CoQ10 synthesis (same pathway)', 'Statins inhibit HMG-CoA reductase, which also produces CoQ10 as a byproduct.'),
('b1a2b2f5-6a95-5c4b-bede-fbfd05ec87b4'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following best describes icosapent ethyl?', '["Lowers triglycerides and reduces CV events","Only lowers triglycerides accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only reduces CV events mediated by specific receptor activation","Is a statin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Is a fibrate leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lowers triglycerides and reduces CV events', 'Icosapent ethyl reduces triglycerides and CV events (REDUCE-IT trial).'),
('9ac3d673-5807-5935-a34f-9b4e7695af68'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'The REDUCE-IT trial evaluated which of the following?', '["Icosapent ethyl in statin-treated patients with elevated triglycerides","Omega-3 fatty acids in primary prevention accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Simvastatin in secondary prevention mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Icosapent ethyl in statin-treated patients with elevated triglycerides', 'REDUCE-IT: icosapent ethyl reduced CV events in statin-treated patients with elevated TG.'),
('7b33de49-87f0-57a5-9bf4-5c1f7054ca57'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following best describes the mechanism of action of bempedoic acid?', '["Inhibiting HMG-CoA reductase (upstream of statins)","Inhibiting ATP citrate lyase (ACL), upstream of HMG-CoA reductase","Inhibiting cholesterol absorption","Binding bile acids","Increasing LDL receptor expression"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibiting ATP citrate lyase (ACL), upstream of HMG-CoA reductase', 'Bempedoic acid inhibits ACL, upstream of HMG-CoA reductase in the cholesterol synthesis pathway.'),
('eaa54ef5-53a5-5da7-851c-c1d7d2928d35'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Bempedoic acid is indicated in patients who:', '["Are statin-intolerant or need additional LDL lowering","Have triglyceride elevation only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Have low HDL only mediated by specific receptor activation","Have hypertension only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Have diabetes only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Are statin-intolerant or need additional LDL lowering', 'Bempedoic acid is for statin-intolerant patients or those needing additional LDL reduction.'),
('bd31c5b1-816f-5656-b531-9cfbfc2e7136'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following best describes the mechanism of action of inclisiran?', '["HMG-CoA reductase","PCSK9 mRNA (reducing PCSK9 production)","Cholesterol absorption","Bile acid synthesis","Triglyceride synthesis"]'::jsonb, 'PCSK9 mRNA (reducing PCSK9 production)', 'Inclisiran is a siRNA that silences PCSK9 mRNA, reducing PCSK9 protein production.'),
('4b21d5b7-6cb0-51b1-b8dc-f22a05d703ee'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Lomitapide is indicated for which of the following?', '["Heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia resulting in decreased functional activity","Homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia (MTP inhibitor)","Primary prevention of CAD mediated by specific receptor activation","Secondary prevention of CAD without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia (MTP inhibitor)', 'Lomitapide inhibits microsomal triglyceride transfer protein (MTP), reducing VLDL production.'),
('f547e034-b77b-5c00-893d-b4b7e944aa6a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following is the molecular target of evinacumab?', '["LDL receptor resulting in decreased functional activity","ANGPTL3 (reducing triglycerides and LDL)","PCSK9 mediated by specific receptor activation","HMG-CoA reductase without affecting the primary tissue pathway","NPC1L1 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'ANGPTL3 (reducing triglycerides and LDL)', 'Evinacumab inhibits ANGPTL3, reducing both triglycerides and LDL cholesterol.'),
('23eb880d-4071-5165-989a-d88df7f88521'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'PCSK9 inhibitors (evolocumab, alirocumab) are administered by which of the following routes?', '["Oral tablets","Subcutaneous injection every 2-4 weeks","IV infusion","Intramuscular injection","Topical application"]'::jsonb, 'Subcutaneous injection every 2-4 weeks', 'PCSK9 inhibitors are given as SC injection every 2-4 weeks.'),
('cf4aaae0-d0a2-5dda-859e-8f7920322ff9'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following is the most important side effect of statins?', '["Hepatotoxicity resulting in decreased functional activity","Myopathy (myalgia, myositis, rhabdomyolysis)","Hyperkalemia mediated by specific receptor activation","Hypoglycemia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Constipation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Myopathy (myalgia, myositis, rhabdomyolysis)', 'Myopathy is the most important statin side effect.'),
('ef1f4560-5f8e-5a74-9f48-3add75f29728'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following monitoring is recommended for patients receiving statins?', '["Checking LFTs at baseline and as needed","Checking renal function","Checking blood glucose","Checking thyroid function","None needed"]'::jsonb, 'Checking LFTs at baseline and as needed', 'LFTs should be checked at baseline and if symptoms of hepatotoxicity arise.'),
('ebdc9b8d-7d3f-561e-867b-740d1fa22acf'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Statins are typically stopped when which of the following occurs?', '["ALT >3x ULN with symptoms","ALT >3x ULN without symptoms","CK <3x ULN","LDL >100 mg/dL","None"]'::jsonb, 'ALT >3x ULN with symptoms', 'Statins are typically stopped if ALT >3x ULN with symptoms or persistent elevation.'),
('24200126-2db2-5dbd-a734-cf4b1ec304aa'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following best describes creatine kinase (CK) levels in statin-associated myopathy?', '["<3x ULN resulting in decreased functional activity","3-10x ULN (myalgia without CK elevation possible)","Always normal mediated by specific receptor activation","Only elevated in rhabdomyolysis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '3-10x ULN (myalgia without CK elevation possible)', 'Myalgia can occur without CK elevation; rhabdomyolysis has very high CK.'),
('7eb59ff7-a4e8-5369-9246-771af0d6c012'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following best describes rhabdomyolysis?', '["Muscle pain, weakness, and very high CK (>10x ULN)","Only muscle pain accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only high CK mediated by specific receptor activation","Only dark urine without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Muscle pain, weakness, and very high CK (>10x ULN)', 'Rhabdomyolysis: severe myopathy with very high CK, myoglobinuria, and risk of AKI.'),
('258a9add-4a5c-5191-b581-1ba1ff12ee9e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following drug interactions significantly increase the levels of simvastatin, lovastatin, and atorvastatin, raising myopathy risk?', '["CYP3A4 inhibitors (increase statin levels, raise myopathy risk)","CYP2D6 inhibitors accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CYP1A2 inhibitors mediated by specific receptor activation","CYP2C19 inhibitors without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CYP2E1 inhibitors leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4 inhibitors (increase statin levels, raise myopathy risk)', 'CYP3A4 inhibitors significantly increase simvastatin, lovastatin, and atorvastatin levels.'),
('0d32b186-d455-5af2-bbeb-a78b77ab4cf1'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Rosuvastatin is primarily metabolized by which of the following CYP enzymes?', '["CYP3A4","CYP2C9 and CYP2C19","CYP1A2","CYP2D6","Not metabolized by CYP enzymes"]'::jsonb, 'CYP2C9 and CYP2C19', 'Rosuvastatin is primarily metabolized by CYP2C9 and CYP2C19, not CYP3A4.'),
('e9642ee6-3bbb-5892-9ee9-121bf46b6d0a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Grapefruit juice increases the blood levels of which of the following statins?', '["Pravastatin and rosuvastatin resulting in decreased functional activity","Simvastatin, lovastatin, and atorvastatin (CYP3A4 substrates)","All statins equally mediated by specific receptor activation","No statins without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Simvastatin, lovastatin, and atorvastatin (CYP3A4 substrates)', 'Grapefruit juice inhibits intestinal CYP3A4, increasing levels of CYP3A4-metabolized statins.'),
('282ea38b-29c0-5ffb-95e3-b3d1932ddc1a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Why is fenofibrate preferred over gemfibrozil for combination with a statin?', '["It is cheaper resulting in decreased functional activity","It has lower risk of rhabdomyolysis when combined with statins","It is more potent mediated by specific receptor activation","It has fewer side effects without affecting the primary tissue pathway","It works faster leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'It has lower risk of rhabdomyolysis when combined with statins', 'Fenofibrate does not inhibit glucuronidation of statins, reducing rhabdomyolysis risk.'),
('8dd57c75-d61e-5c88-a4d2-88e3e14a5d71'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following are common side effects of niacin?', '["Flushing (prostaglandin-mediated), hyperglycemia, hyperuricemia","Bradycardia, bronchospasm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hypokalemia, hyponatremia mediated by specific receptor activation","Weight loss, hypoglycemia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Flushing (prostaglandin-mediated), hyperglycemia, hyperuricemia', 'Niacin causes flushing (can be reduced with aspirin), hyperglycemia, and hyperuricemia.'),
('f36fa503-5ead-5df0-9e75-52115eede864'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following can reduce niacin-induced flushing?', '["Aspirin (prostaglandin synthesis inhibition)","Antihistamines accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Beta-blockers mediated by specific receptor activation","Calcium channel blockers without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Aspirin (prostaglandin synthesis inhibition)', 'Aspirin reduces niacin-induced flushing by inhibiting prostaglandin synthesis.'),
('6d02651c-0c6b-5f68-9430-9845f1094b0f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Niacin should be used cautiously in patients with which of the following conditions?', '["Hyperlipidemia resulting in decreased functional activity","Diabetes and gout (can worsen hyperglycemia and hyperuricemia)","Hypertension mediated by specific receptor activation","Heart failure without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Diabetes and gout (can worsen hyperglycemia and hyperuricemia)', 'Niacin can worsen hyperglycemia in diabetics and hyperuricemia in gout patients.'),
('35d58474-8d50-53c2-b69d-f0a488b1d254'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following are common side effects of bile acid sequestrants?', '["Constipation, malabsorption of fat-soluble vitamins","Hyperkalemia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hypoglycemia mediated by specific receptor activation","Bradycardia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Constipation, malabsorption of fat-soluble vitamins', 'Bile acid sequestrants cause constipation and can bind fat-soluble vitamins and drugs.'),
('943f37d8-f196-515d-937e-f96b9713632d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following best describes how bile acid sequestrant resins act?', '["They are ion exchange resins that bind anions in the GI tract","They inhibit CYP enzymes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","They block absorption at the transporter level mediated by specific receptor activation","They are PPIs without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'They are ion exchange resins that bind anions in the GI tract', 'Resins non-selectively bind anions in the gut, including other drugs.'),
('0dd794ea-18ec-585d-8bdc-b0bcfb729994'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following best describes the side-effect profile of ezetimibe?', '["Severe resulting in decreased functional activity","Mild (GI symptoms, rarely elevated LFTs)","Cardiovascular mediated by specific receptor activation","Neurological without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Renal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mild (GI symptoms, rarely elevated LFTs)', 'Ezetimibe is generally well-tolerated with mild GI side effects.'),
('d0402dd0-c658-5e4a-9aa4-18bb87cb64df'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following best describes the side-effect profile of PCSK9 inhibitors?', '["Severe hepatotoxicity resulting in decreased functional activity","Injection site reactions, nasopharyngitis (generally well-tolerated)","Myopathy mediated by specific receptor activation","Hyperkalemia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hypoglycemia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Injection site reactions, nasopharyngitis (generally well-tolerated)', 'PCSK9 inhibitors are generally well-tolerated with injection site reactions.'),
('63d43dba-af36-5daf-9ea8-9ba50ba3aa09'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following best describes the side-effect profile of inclisiran?', '["Severe myopathy resulting in decreased functional activity","Injection site reactions (generally well-tolerated)","Hepatotoxicity mediated by specific receptor activation","Hyperkalemia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hypoglycemia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Injection site reactions (generally well-tolerated)', 'Inclisiran is well-tolerated with mainly injection site reactions.'),
('67957c8b-512c-5085-9542-8a4a649d486f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'According to guidelines, what is the LDL cholesterol target in secondary prevention?', '["LDL <100 mg/dL","LDL <70 mg/dL (or >=50% reduction)","LDL <130 mg/dL","LDL <160 mg/dL","No specific target"]'::jsonb, 'LDL <70 mg/dL (or >=50% reduction)', 'Guidelines recommend LDL <70 mg/dL or >=50% reduction in secondary prevention.'),
('27db8b3b-dbe1-5bd0-8324-58c5496614b9'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'According to ESC 2019 guidelines, what is the LDL cholesterol target in very high-risk ASCVD patients?', '["<100 mg/dL","<70 mg/dL","<55 mg/dL (ESC guidelines)","<130 mg/dL","No target"]'::jsonb, '<55 mg/dL (ESC guidelines)', 'ESC 2019 guidelines: LDL <55 mg/dL in very high-risk ASCVD.'),
('ce406e37-d5ac-5de5-be5b-96ab8213e6bc'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following is the recommended treatment for heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia (HeFH)?', '["Lifestyle modifications only resulting in decreased functional activity","High-intensity statins + ezetimibe +/- PCSK9 inhibitor","Fibrates only mediated by specific receptor activation","Niacin only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'High-intensity statins + ezetimibe +/- PCSK9 inhibitor', 'HeFH requires aggressive LDL lowering with combination therapy.'),
('6ff3f603-8105-59fb-afa2-ca82b02836d1'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following is the recommended treatment for homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia (HoFH)?', '["Statin monotherapy resulting in decreased functional activity","LDL apheresis + lomitapide + evinacumab","Fibrates only mediated by specific receptor activation","Niacin only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lifestyle only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'LDL apheresis + lomitapide + evinacumab', 'HoFH requires multiple therapies including apheresis and novel agents.'),
('e85487e0-b530-5e8e-ad6a-6ef46e39003d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following best describes LDL apheresis?', '["Directly removes LDL from the blood","Increases LDL production","Blocks LDL absorption","Increases bile acid synthesis","None"]'::jsonb, 'Directly removes LDL from the blood', 'LDL apheresis is an extracorporeal procedure that selectively removes LDL from plasma.'),
('25928718-a94f-5bc9-9a27-e802a6c6f983'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following best describes the mechanism by which the Mediterranean diet reduces cardiovascular risk?', '["Lowering LDL cholesterol resulting in decreased functional activity","Reducing inflammation and oxidative stress, lowering triglycerides","Raising HDL only mediated by specific receptor activation","Lowering blood pressure only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Reducing inflammation and oxidative stress, lowering triglycerides', 'Mediterranean diet: rich in omega-3s, antioxidants; reduces inflammation and CV events.'),
('db89eb5a-8fcc-5877-9309-67e54fb4e438'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following best describes the cardiovascular benefits of regular exercise?', '["Only lowering blood pressure resulting in decreased functional activity","Improving lipid profile (raising HDL, lowering triglycerides), improving insulin sensitivity, reducing inflammation","Only lowering heart rate mediated by specific receptor activation","Only raising HDL without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Improving lipid profile (raising HDL, lowering triglycerides), improving insulin sensitivity, reducing inflammation', 'Exercise has multiple CV benefits: lipid improvement, insulin sensitivity, anti-inflammation.'),
('632d8b62-0ae1-50d1-bbd1-32fb26454eb2'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following best describes the effect of modest weight loss on cardiovascular risk factors?', '["Have no effect on CV risk resulting in decreased functional activity","Significantly improve lipid profile, blood pressure, and glucose control","Worsen lipid profile mediated by specific receptor activation","Increase blood pressure without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Significantly improve lipid profile, blood pressure, and glucose control', 'Modest weight loss significantly improves multiple CV risk factors.'),
('ea6aebea-a3b6-50f8-87d8-b47ab3633992'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following best describes the cardiovascular benefit of smoking cessation?', '["Only reducing blood pressure resulting in decreased functional activity","Significantly reducing CV risk (benefit begins within weeks)","Only improving lipid profile mediated by specific receptor activation","No benefit without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Significantly reducing CV risk (benefit begins within weeks)', 'Smoking cessation rapidly reduces CV risk; benefit begins within weeks.'),
('bd65a98a-4b72-5eeb-b8a7-57ac288ee316'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Aspirin for primary prevention of cardiovascular disease is which of the following?', '["Recommended for all adults resulting in decreased functional activity","Recommended for high-risk patients (10-year ASCVD risk >10%) without increased bleeding risk","Contraindicated mediated by specific receptor activation","Only used in secondary prevention without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Recommended for high-risk patients (10-year ASCVD risk >10%) without increased bleeding risk', 'Aspirin for primary prevention: high-risk patients with low bleeding risk.'),
('5e0e55b7-6e75-5448-86e0-9e7b6f3ddf1c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Low-dose aspirin exerts its antiplatelet effect primarily by inhibiting which of the following?', '["COX-1 in platelets (preventing TXA2 synthesis)","COX-2 in endothelial cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Both COX-1 and COX-2 equally mediated by specific receptor activation","Neither without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'COX-1 in platelets (preventing TXA2 synthesis)', 'Low-dose aspirin irreversibly inhibits platelet COX-1, reducing TXA2 and platelet aggregation.'),
('97557b86-99cf-5ee7-8a7a-f277a270fcea'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following are causes of aspirin resistance?', '["Genetic polymorphisms in COX-1, drug interactions, non-compliance","Only non-compliance accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only genetic factors mediated by specific receptor activation","Only drug interactions without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Genetic polymorphisms in COX-1, drug interactions, non-compliance', 'Aspirin resistance: genetic COX-1 variants, drug interactions (ibuprofen), non-compliance.'),
('89005235-0203-5916-9e7f-15186c7fcdf6'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Clopidogrel belongs to which of the following classes of antiplatelet drugs?', '["COX inhibitor","P2Y12 (ADP) receptor antagonist (thienopyridine)","Glycoprotein IIb/IIIa inhibitor","Direct thrombin inhibitor","Factor Xa inhibitor"]'::jsonb, 'P2Y12 (ADP) receptor antagonist (thienopyridine)', 'Clopidogrel irreversibly blocks the P2Y12 ADP receptor on platelets.'),
('72cb8470-30de-54ec-85cc-c9cb31542280'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Clopidogrel is a prodrug that is activated primarily by which of the following CYP enzymes?', '["CYP2C19 activation","CYP3A4 activation","CYP2D6 activation","Direct activation","No activation"]'::jsonb, 'CYP2C19 activation', 'Clopidogrel is a prodrug requiring CYP2C19-mediated bioactivation.'),
('bfd7fafb-7f01-5c30-a38b-1f8551b4ed79'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'In CYP2C19 poor metabolizers, clopidogrel produces which of the following?', '["Enhanced response to clopidogrel resulting in decreased functional activity","Reduced response to clopidogrel (higher CV risk)","No change in response mediated by specific receptor activation","Increased bleeding risk without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Reduced response to clopidogrel (higher CV risk)', 'CYP2C19 poor metabolizers cannot convert clopidogrel to active form, reducing efficacy.'),
('d32ae585-cda4-5ed3-b60a-6fdaebbed8f5'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following best describes prasugrel compared with clopidogrel?', '["Requires CYP2C19 activation (like clopidogrel) resulting in decreased functional activity","Is more rapidly and consistently activated (less CYP2C19 dependence)","Is a reversible inhibitor mediated by specific receptor activation","Is a GP IIb/IIIa inhibitor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Is more rapidly and consistently activated (less CYP2C19 dependence)', 'Prasugrel is more reliably activated than clopidogrel, with less CYP2C19 influence.'),
('6c870d1e-58db-5440-b1ba-b99101ecb11b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Ticagrelor belongs to which of the following classes of antiplatelet drugs?', '["Thienopyridine (irreversible) resulting in decreased functional activity","Cyclopentyltriazolopyrimidine (reversible P2Y12 inhibitor)","COX inhibitor mediated by specific receptor activation","GP IIb/IIIa inhibitor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Direct thrombin inhibitor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cyclopentyltriazolopyrimidine (reversible P2Y12 inhibitor)', 'Ticagrelor is a reversible, direct-acting P2Y12 inhibitor (not a prodrug).'),
('556c8ca7-262e-5e80-9e4c-8ac38cf2d18b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following is a common side effect of ticagrelor?', '["Dyspnea (common side effect)","Constipation only","Bradycardia only","Hyperkalemia only","None"]'::jsonb, 'Dyspnea (common side effect)', 'Dyspnea is a common ticagrelor side effect (mechanism unclear).'),
('0b97039f-3be8-58bc-a64a-6fe4b18ff93a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following best describes cangrelor?', '["Oral P2Y12 inhibitor resulting in decreased functional activity","IV direct-acting reversible P2Y12 inhibitor","Thienopyridine mediated by specific receptor activation","COX inhibitor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","GP IIb/IIIa inhibitor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'IV direct-acting reversible P2Y12 inhibitor', 'Cangrelor is an IV P2Y12 inhibitor with very short half-life for acute settings.'),
('e6a76bd1-9443-54f1-8ae4-45a6ef79af8f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Vorapaxar is which of the following types of antiplatelet agent?', '["P2Y12 inhibitor resulting in decreased functional activity","Protease-activated receptor-1 (PAR-1) antagonist","COX inhibitor mediated by specific receptor activation","GP IIb/IIIa inhibitor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Direct thrombin inhibitor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Protease-activated receptor-1 (PAR-1) antagonist', 'Vorapaxar blocks thrombin''s platelet-activating effect via PAR-1.'),
('3e70de82-4113-57be-9bfe-c9518ccc398a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following are glycoprotein IIb/IIIa inhibitors?', '["Abciximab, eptifibatide, tirofiban","Clopidogrel, prasugrel, ticagrelor","Warfarin, heparin","Rivaroxaban, apixaban","None"]'::jsonb, 'Abciximab, eptifibatide, tirofiban', 'GP IIb/IIIa inhibitors: abciximab (monoclonal antibody), eptifibatide, tirofiban (small molecules).'),
('65e7854e-725a-5c50-ad52-367182247f7d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Glycoprotein IIb/IIIa inhibitors block which of the following?', '["P2Y12 receptors resulting in decreased functional activity","The final common pathway of platelet aggregation (fibrinogen binding to GP IIb/IIIa)","COX enzymes mediated by specific receptor activation","Thrombin receptors without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Factor Xa leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The final common pathway of platelet aggregation (fibrinogen binding to GP IIb/IIIa)', 'GP IIb/IIIa inhibitors block fibrinogen binding, preventing platelet aggregation regardless of stimulus.'),
('73bcf9af-f2e5-569d-821c-c06ea46f27a5'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following best describes abciximab?', '["Small molecule GP IIb/IIIa inhibitor resulting in decreased functional activity","Chimeric monoclonal antibody fragment (Fab) against GP IIb/IIIa","P2Y12 inhibitor mediated by specific receptor activation","COX inhibitor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Direct thrombin inhibitor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Chimeric monoclonal antibody fragment (Fab) against GP IIb/IIIa', 'Abciximab is a monoclonal antibody fragment that binds GP IIb/IIIa.'),
('56fcbede-c304-5900-901c-8fdd033b0774'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following best describes eptifibatide?', '["Monoclonal antibody","Cyclic heptapeptide GP IIb/IIIa inhibitor","P2Y12 inhibitor","COX inhibitor","Direct thrombin inhibitor"]'::jsonb, 'Cyclic heptapeptide GP IIb/IIIa inhibitor', 'Eptifibatide is a synthetic cyclic peptide that inhibits GP IIb/IIIa.'),
('bee517f8-c15c-5dcc-a258-703ae4709304'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'GP IIb/IIIa inhibitors are administered by which of the following routes?', '["Oral route resulting in decreased functional activity","IV infusion (abciximab, eptifibatide, tirofiban)","Subcutaneous injection mediated by specific receptor activation","Intramuscular injection without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'IV infusion (abciximab, eptifibatide, tirofiban)', 'All GP IIb/IIIa inhibitors are given by IV infusion.'),
('26a58fdc-4b85-589a-8ee4-0ed02847cde2'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following best describes the mechanism of action of heparin?', '["Directly inhibiting thrombin resulting in decreased functional activity","Enhancing antithrombin III activity (indirect thrombin/Xa inhibitor)","Inhibiting factor Xa directly mediated by specific receptor activation","Inhibiting platelet aggregation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Blocking vitamin K synthesis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Enhancing antithrombin III activity (indirect thrombin/Xa inhibitor)', 'Heparin binds antithrombin III, enhancing its inhibition of thrombin and factor Xa.'),
('62836506-cb83-5338-9ad6-9faa279fc4e2'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following is true regarding unfractionated heparin (UFH) compared with LMWH?', '["Is more predictable","Has shorter half-life, requires aPTT monitoring","Is given SC only","Has only anti-Xa activity","Is safer in renal failure"]'::jsonb, 'Has shorter half-life, requires aPTT monitoring', 'UFH has shorter half-life, requires aPTT monitoring, and has both anti-IIa and anti-Xa activity.'),
('efd5ca72-6a22-5559-9d12-0cf6a4af5e09'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Unfractionated heparin is preferred over LMWH in which of the following situations?', '["Rapid reversibility is needed (protamine reversal)","Chronic anticoagulation is needed","Renal failure is present","Outpatient treatment is desired","None"]'::jsonb, 'Rapid reversibility is needed (protamine reversal)', 'UFH is preferred when rapid reversibility with protamine is needed (e.g., surgery, bleeding).'),
('247910ad-ffef-5588-8ac5-9b6907028367'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Protamine completely reverses which of the following?', '["LMWH only resulting in decreased functional activity","UFH (but only partially reverses LMWH)","Both UFH and LMWH completely mediated by specific receptor activation","Neither without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'UFH (but only partially reverses LMWH)', 'Protamine completely reverses UFH but only partially reverses LMWH (anti-Xa effect persists).'),
('7a3b40f3-a7fe-5669-9749-46eb139c650f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (HIT) is caused by which of the following?', '["Direct platelet destruction resulting in decreased functional activity","Immune-mediated platelet activation via HIT antibodies against PF4-heparin complex","Bone marrow suppression mediated by specific receptor activation","Splenomegaly without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Immune-mediated platelet activation via HIT antibodies against PF4-heparin complex', 'HIT: IgG antibodies against PF4-heparin complex activate platelets, causing thrombocytopenia and thrombosis.'),
('67205e3d-27d7-55a8-a545-b9a265e62c42'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following is the recommended management of heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (HIT)?', '["Continuing heparin resulting in decreased functional activity","Stopping heparin and starting alternative anticoagulant (argatroban, bivalirudin)","Giving platelet transfusion mediated by specific receptor activation","Giving vitamin K without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Stopping heparin and starting alternative anticoagulant (argatroban, bivalirudin)', 'HIT: stop ALL heparin and start alternative anticoagulant.'),
('81a71716-ea89-5cad-a58a-13e6a1ca1211'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following best describes argatroban?', '["Direct thrombin inhibitor (DTI) used in HIT","Factor Xa inhibitor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Heparin analog mediated by specific receptor activation","Vitamin K antagonist without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Antiplatelet drug leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Direct thrombin inhibitor (DTI) used in HIT', 'Argatroban is a direct thrombin inhibitor used in HIT.'),
('631791e0-f379-5918-84b6-a15a0dfa6fbe'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following best describes bivalirudin?', '["Direct thrombin inhibitor (DTI) used in HIT and PCI","Factor Xa inhibitor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Heparin analog mediated by specific receptor activation","Vitamin K antagonist without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Antiplatelet drug leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Direct thrombin inhibitor (DTI) used in HIT and PCI', 'Bivalirudin is a DTI used in HIT and during PCI.'),
('79b8ae18-000d-50dd-8d0d-57a32f999883'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following best describes fondaparinux?', '["Heparin analog (synthetic pentasaccharide) with selective anti-Xa activity","Direct thrombin inhibitor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Factor Xa inhibitor (direct) mediated by specific receptor activation","Vitamin K antagonist without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Antiplatelet drug leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Heparin analog (synthetic pentasaccharide) with selective anti-Xa activity', 'Fondaparinux is a synthetic pentasaccharide that selectively inhibits factor Xa via antithrombin.'),
('b35679c1-babc-510b-a347-b4adcb5dc479'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Fondaparinux does not cause heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (HIT) because:', '["It is very small resulting in decreased functional activity","It does not bind to PF4 (no PF4-heparin complex formation)","It is rapidly metabolized mediated by specific receptor activation","It is not anticoagulant without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'It does not bind to PF4 (no PF4-heparin complex formation)', 'Fondaparinux does not bind PF4, so HIT antibodies cannot form.'),
('ff995681-b6ee-5261-a775-38c11b7a4f76'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Why does the dose of warfarin vary widely between individuals?', '["It is very potent resulting in decreased functional activity","Individual variation in dose requirement (genetic and environmental factors)","It has no side effects mediated by specific receptor activation","It is very safe without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Individual variation in dose requirement (genetic and environmental factors)', 'Warfarin dose varies widely due to genetic (CYP2C9, VKORC1) and environmental factors.'),
('15596473-f980-5177-8578-e88cacfb76ac'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'What is the target INR for warfarin for most indications such as atrial fibrillation and venous thromboembolism?', '["1.0-2.0","2.0-3.0","3.0-4.0","4.0-5.0","5.0-6.0"]'::jsonb, '2.0-3.0', 'Target INR for AF, VTE, and most indications: 2.0-3.0.'),
('4d0b7a78-067e-5e7e-8884-e30ac35a6ef5'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'What is the target INR for warfarin in patients with a mechanical mitral valve?', '["2.0-3.0","2.5-3.5","3.0-4.0","4.0-5.0","1.5-2.5"]'::jsonb, '2.5-3.5', 'Mechanical mitral valve: target INR 2.5-3.5 (higher than other indications).'),
('1e7ef33b-7ec5-56db-9fcb-1d357446a240'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following is correct regarding reversal of warfarin-induced over-anticoagulation or bleeding?', '["Vitamin K (phytonadione) for non-urgent reversal, FFP/PCC for urgent reversal","Protamine accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Heparin mediated by specific receptor activation","Aspirin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Vitamin K (phytonadione) for non-urgent reversal, FFP/PCC for urgent reversal', 'Vitamin K for non-urgent; FFP/PCC for urgent warfarin reversal.'),
('5cbad947-32a4-5e30-a4c4-a6b8c6f435be'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Vitamin K (phytonadione) is available by which of the following routes of administration?', '["Oral and IV","Oral only","IV only","IM only","SC only"]'::jsonb, 'Oral and IV', 'Phytonadione is available as oral tablets and IV injection.'),
('b5160608-da46-50e6-9756-dd1e37ab9445'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Why is warfarin avoided during the first trimester of pregnancy?', '["It is teratogenic (nasal hypoplasia, stippled epiphyses in first trimester)","It causes bleeding accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","It lowers blood pressure mediated by specific receptor activation","It causes hyperkalemia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'It is teratogenic (nasal hypoplasia, stippled epiphyses in first trimester)', 'Warfarin is teratogenic, especially in first trimester (nasal hypoplasia, stippled epiphyses).'),
('e2dd1e70-252e-554c-9c56-7b02ac0f537c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Heparin is preferred over warfarin in pregnancy because:', '["It crosses the placenta resulting in decreased functional activity","It does not cross the placenta (safe for fetus)","It is more potent mediated by specific receptor activation","It is cheaper without affecting the primary tissue pathway","It is oral leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'It does not cross the placenta (safe for fetus)', 'Heparin does not cross the placenta, making it safe in pregnancy.'),
('2d57f261-4550-5806-b2ab-f998bb21b462'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following best describes dabigatran?', '["Direct thrombin inhibitor (oral)","Factor Xa inhibitor (oral)","Vitamin K antagonist","Heparin analog","Antiplatelet drug"]'::jsonb, 'Direct thrombin inhibitor (oral)', 'Dabigatran is an oral direct thrombin inhibitor (reversible).'),
('af14388e-0eef-5fbc-8c26-fbdfd244fe37'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Dabigatran etexilate is a prodrug that is converted into which of the following?', '["Dabigatran (active form) by esterases in the GI tract","An inactive metabolite accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A more potent metabolite mediated by specific receptor activation","A prodrug without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dabigatran (active form) by esterases in the GI tract', 'Dabigatran etexilate is a prodrug hydrolyzed to active dabigatran by esterases.'),
('71ba46f9-c162-5c2a-afa9-98017f0bda91'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following is the specific reversal agent for dabigatran?', '["Vitamin K resulting in decreased functional activity","Idarucizumab (specific reversal agent)","Protamine mediated by specific receptor activation","FFP without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Idarucizumab (specific reversal agent)', 'Idarucizumab is a monoclonal antibody fragment that specifically reverses dabigatran.'),
('49d8717b-f2d3-5c8f-96d6-c24e77ec8061'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following best describes rivaroxaban?', '["Direct thrombin inhibitor","Factor Xa inhibitor (oral)","Vitamin K antagonist","Heparin analog","Antiplatelet drug"]'::jsonb, 'Factor Xa inhibitor (oral)', 'Rivaroxaban is an oral direct factor Xa inhibitor.'),
('e8bbfb49-15af-5496-8b1f-48856e7c962e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following is an advantage of DOACs over warfarin?', '["They are more potent","They do not require routine INR monitoring","They have no food interactions","They are cheaper","They are safer in renal failure"]'::jsonb, 'They do not require routine INR monitoring', 'DOACs have predictable pharmacokinetics, no routine monitoring needed.'),
('1e99ccc1-361c-54d8-805a-3adc9a8fe69e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Dabigatran requires dose adjustment in which of the following conditions?', '["Hepatic impairment resulting in decreased functional activity","Renal impairment (CrCl <50 mL/min)","Pulmonary disease mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Renal impairment (CrCl <50 mL/min)', 'Dabigatran requires dose adjustment/reduction in renal impairment.'),
('b7255735-0124-5233-9584-3ad7a1479c6e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Higher doses of rivaroxaban should be taken with which of the following to enhance absorption?', '["Any time","Food (to enhance absorption)","On an empty stomach","At bedtime only","None"]'::jsonb, 'Food (to enhance absorption)', 'Higher doses of rivaroxaban should be taken with food to enhance absorption.'),
('b0e67a99-1e8b-5a7d-b8d4-850d0b0ab026'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Compared with warfarin, apixaban is associated with a lower rate of which of the following?', '["GI bleeding resulting in decreased functional activity","Major bleeding (compared to warfarin and other DOACs)","Stroke mediated by specific receptor activation","VTE recurrence without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Major bleeding (compared to warfarin and other DOACs)', 'Apixaban has lower major bleeding risk than warfarin in trials.'),
('7e148d54-7ee7-53f9-8f25-aee6c34896df'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Edoxaban demonstrates reduced efficacy in which of the following patient groups?', '["CrCl >95 mL/min (reduced efficacy due to increased clearance)","CrCl <15 mL/min accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hepatic impairment mediated by specific receptor activation","Hypertension without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CrCl >95 mL/min (reduced efficacy due to increased clearance)', 'Edoxaban has reduced efficacy in patients with CrCl >95 mL/min due to increased renal clearance.'),
('51741bf9-560d-59ef-868e-1a8f51de271d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following is the main advantage of DOACs over warfarin in patients with atrial fibrillation?', '["Lower cost resulting in decreased functional activity","Lower rate of intracranial hemorrhage","More potent anticoagulation mediated by specific receptor activation","Better compliance without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lower rate of intracranial hemorrhage', 'DOACs have lower ICH risk compared to warfarin.'),
('ce31947b-9fd5-545e-a6f4-edac41ee1f1d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Triple antithrombotic therapy (oral anticoagulant plus dual antiplatelet therapy) is indicated in which of the following patients?', '["AF patients with ACS or PCI","All AF patients","All ACS patients","All PCI patients","None"]'::jsonb, 'AF patients with ACS or PCI', 'Triple therapy is for AF patients undergoing ACS or PCI, balancing ischemic and bleeding risk.'),
('ee710bfd-da1a-5b91-a89a-52d3ef647bb0'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which trial demonstrated that low-dose rivaroxaban plus aspirin reduced cardiovascular events in patients with stable CAD or ACS?', '["ATLAS ACS 2-TIMI 51 trial","RE-LY trial accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","ROCKET-AF trial mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'ATLAS ACS 2-TIMI 51 trial', 'ATLAS ACS 2-TIMI 51: low-dose rivaroxaban + aspirin reduced CV events in stable CAD/ACS.'),
('a773f9ae-7149-5046-b76f-d5f4a7452b5d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which trial demonstrated that rivaroxaban 2.5 mg twice daily plus aspirin reduced cardiovascular events in patients with stable CAD?', '["Rivaroxaban + aspirin vs aspirin alone in stable CAD","Dabigatran vs warfarin in AF accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Apixaban vs warfarin in AF mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Rivaroxaban + aspirin vs aspirin alone in stable CAD', 'COMPASS: rivaroxaban 2.5mg BID + aspirin reduced CV events in stable CAD.'),
('eed2c0f9-bde7-5c49-8137-1829792d8cbb'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which trial evaluated antithrombotic strategies after PCI in patients with atrial fibrillation?', '["Antithrombotic strategies after PCI in AF patients","Rivaroxaban vs warfarin in AF accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Apixaban vs warfarin in AF mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Antithrombotic strategies after PCI in AF patients', 'PIONEER: reduced-dose rivaroxaban regimens had less bleeding after PCI in AF.'),
('3a617fa4-38fa-5c6a-8f29-8d712d5ddc2a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which trial compared dabigatran-based dual therapy versus triple therapy after PCI in patients with atrial fibrillation?', '["Dabigatran-based dual vs triple therapy after PCI in AF","Rivaroxaban vs warfarin in AF accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Apixaban vs warfarin in AF mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dabigatran-based dual vs triple therapy after PCI in AF', 'RE-DUAL PCI: dabigatran-based dual therapy had less bleeding than triple therapy.'),
('9909f6cc-3bb4-5675-b8bf-d84a2d1123d9'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which trial evaluated apixaban-based antithrombotic strategies after PCI in patients with atrial fibrillation?', '["Apixaban-based strategies after PCI in AF","Rivaroxaban vs warfarin in AF accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dabigatran vs warfarin in AF mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Apixaban-based strategies after PCI in AF', 'AUGUSTUS: apixaban-based regimens had less bleeding after PCI in AF.'),
('fbd0508d-539b-5b4b-a4fb-798a1b485535'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which trial evaluated edoxaban-based antithrombotic strategies after PCI in patients with atrial fibrillation?', '["Edoxaban-based dual vs triple therapy after PCI in AF","Rivaroxaban vs warfarin in AF accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Apixaban vs warfarin in AF mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Edoxaban-based dual vs triple therapy after PCI in AF', 'ENTRUST-AF PCI: edoxaban-based dual therapy was non-inferior for bleeding.'),
('a12708c4-f099-5ec8-a724-bc55279a9abb'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'After elective PCI, dual antiplatelet therapy (DAPT) consists of which of the following?', '["Aspirin only resulting in decreased functional activity","Aspirin + clopidogrel (or prasugrel/ticagrelor) for 6-12 months","Triple therapy mediated by specific receptor activation","Anticoagulation only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Aspirin + clopidogrel (or prasugrel/ticagrelor) for 6-12 months', 'DAPT (aspirin + P2Y12 inhibitor) for 6-12 months post-elective PCI.'),
('5aa79aaf-b0e0-51dd-9239-f8e27dc5422b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'What is the recommended duration of dual antiplatelet therapy (DAPT) after an acute coronary syndrome?', '["1 month resulting in decreased functional activity","6-12 months (12 months for STEMI)","2 weeks only mediated by specific receptor activation","5 years without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lifelong leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '6-12 months (12 months for STEMI)', 'DAPT for 6-12 months post-ACS (12 months for STEMI).'),
('c31d98e9-5166-5efc-a7c5-b5cea029772f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following are options for de-escalation of antiplatelet therapy after ACS or PCI?', '["Switching from prasugrel/ticagrelor to clopidogrel after 3-6 months","Stopping aspirin and continuing P2Y12 inhibitor (aspirin-free strategy)","Both A and B","Stopping all antiplatelet therapy","None"]'::jsonb, 'Both A and B', 'De-escalation: switch to clopidogrel or aspirin-free strategies.'),
('1dc060ef-5ea5-55e7-863f-7f2ede65014f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which trial showed that 1-month DAPT followed by clopidogrel monotherapy was non-inferior after PCI?', '["1-month vs 12-month DAPT after PCI","Aspirin vs clopidogrel in AF","Rivaroxaban vs warfarin in AF","None","All"]'::jsonb, '1-month vs 12-month DAPT after PCI', 'STOPDAPT-2: 1-month DAPT followed by clopidogrel monotherapy was non-inferior.'),
('b8f34f65-f420-5b12-8dee-12dd6b2c863d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which trial showed that ticagrelor monotherapy after 3 months of DAPT reduced bleeding after PCI?', '["Ticagrelor monotherapy after 3 months of DAPT post-PCI","Aspirin vs clopidogrel accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Rivaroxaban vs warfarin mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ticagrelor monotherapy after 3 months of DAPT post-PCI', 'TWILIGHT: ticagrelor monotherapy after 3-month DAPT reduced bleeding.'),
('c0e510ab-7bec-5188-bf5a-3683df5aaf61'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Autonomic Nervous System - ANS Pharmacology', 'Which trial evaluated ticagrelor monotherapy after 1 month of dual antiplatelet therapy following PCI?', '["Ticagrelor monotherapy after 1-month DAPT post-PCI","Aspirin vs clopidogrel accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Rivaroxaban vs warfarin mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ticagrelor monotherapy after 1-month DAPT post-PCI', 'GLOBAL LEADERS: aspirin + ticagrelor for 1 month, then ticagrelor monotherapy.'),
('4e055111-64b8-5847-946c-83f2b91cca4b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following is a volatile liquid anesthetic?', '["Thiopental","Propofol","Sevoflurane","Ketamine","Fentanyl"]'::jsonb, 'Sevoflurane', 'Sevoflurane is a volatile liquid anesthetic (inhalational). Thiopental and Propofol are IV anesthetics. Ketamine is a dissociative anesthetic. Fentanyl is an opioid analgesic.'),
('1722fff9-4179-5c02-b62e-d63c942a109d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'The most commonly used intravenous induction agent is:', '["Thiopental","Propofol","Midazolam","Etomidate","Ketamine"]'::jsonb, 'Propofol', 'Propofol is the most commonly used IV induction agent due to its rapid onset, short duration, antiemetic properties, and smooth recovery profile.'),
('9c7ec74d-edee-59c8-a2eb-3746495c0fc6'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'The minimum alveolar concentration (MAC) is a measure of:', '["Analgesic potency","Sedative potency","Potency of inhalational anesthetics","Duration of anesthesia","Metabolism of anesthetics"]'::jsonb, 'Potency of inhalational anesthetics', 'MAC is the concentration of an inhaled anesthetic at 1 atmosphere that produces immobility in 50% of subjects exposed to a painful stimulus. It is a measure of potency.'),
('9f03dae0-43f9-5c4f-84b5-722a44ebb682'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which inhalational anesthetic has the highest MAC value (weakest potency)?', '["Sevoflurane","Desflurane","Isoflurane","Nitrous oxide","Halothane"]'::jsonb, 'Nitrous oxide', 'Nitrous oxide has the highest MAC (>100), meaning it is the weakest inhalational anesthetic. Higher MAC = lower potency.'),
('f58b4e92-6240-5b82-829a-35ef1c41962c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which inhalational anesthetic is a cardiac sensitizer to catecholamines?', '["Sevoflurane","Desflurane","Isoflurane","Halothane","Nitrous oxide"]'::jsonb, 'Halothane', 'Halothane sensitizes the myocardium to catecholamines, increasing the risk of cardiac arrhythmias. This is a well-known adverse effect of halothane.'),
('fc2d19cf-df28-565d-98eb-79fa33260bf8'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following anesthetics undergoes biotransformation to produce nephrotoxic compound A (Compound A)?', '["Desflurane","Isoflurane","Sevoflurane","Halothane","Nitrous oxide"]'::jsonb, 'Sevoflurane', 'Sevoflurane degrades in the presence of soda lime to form Compound A (fluoromethyl-2,2-difluoro-1-(trifluoromethyl)vinyl ether), which is nephrotoxic.'),
('47d6e222-eda9-5802-99c4-7ab97cf6bb80'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Ketamine produces which type of anesthesia?', '["General anesthesia","Regional anesthesia","Dissociative anesthesia","Local anesthesia","Conduction anesthesia"]'::jsonb, 'Dissociative anesthesia', 'Ketamine produces dissociative anesthesia characterized by analgesia, amnesia, and catalepsy while maintaining some respiratory and cardiovascular functions.'),
('bb448620-d567-592e-a05d-761582e5224c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following IV anesthetics increases intracranial pressure?', '["Propofol","Thiopental","Midazolam","Ketamine","Etomidate"]'::jsonb, 'Ketamine', 'Ketamine increases cerebral blood flow and intracranial pressure due to sympathomimetic effects. It is contraindicated in patients with raised ICP.'),
('7bee003b-77ad-5671-ac99-a973f589ce46'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which anesthetic agent is preferred in patients with bronchospasm?', '["Halothane","Thiopental","Propofol","Ketamine","Etomidate"]'::jsonb, 'Ketamine', 'Ketamine is a bronchodilator and is preferred in patients with bronchospasm or asthma due to its sympathomimetic and bronchodilatory effects.'),
('cea0c007-f251-5226-b88a-a11992922347'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Propofol infusion syndrome is characterized by:', '["Hyperkalemia, metabolic acidosis, rhabdomyolysis","Hypokalemia, metabolic alkalosis, rhabdomyolysis","Hyperkalemia, metabolic alkalosis, rhabdomyolysis","Hypokalemia, metabolic acidosis, rhabdomyolysis","Hypernatremia, metabolic acidosis"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperkalemia, metabolic acidosis, rhabdomyolysis', 'Propofol infusion syndrome is a rare but serious complication characterized by metabolic acidosis, hyperkalemia, rhabdomyolysis, cardiac failure, and renal failure with prolonged high-dose infusion.'),
('452b4768-e45a-568e-ba1d-87505075cc59'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which inhalational anesthetic is most commonly used for maintenance of anesthesia?', '["Halothane","Sevoflurane","Ether","Trichloroethylene","Methoxyflurane"]'::jsonb, 'Sevoflurane', 'Sevoflurane is the most commonly used inhalational anesthetic for maintenance due to its low blood-gas partition coefficient, lack of pungency, and minimal cardiovascular effects.'),
('68440c13-741d-5af6-a9e8-8b63b83aa0d8'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'The blood-gas partition coefficient determines:', '["Speed of induction and recovery","Potency of anesthetic","Analgesic effect","Muscle relaxation","Nausea and vomiting"]'::jsonb, 'Speed of induction and recovery', 'Blood-gas partition coefficient determines the speed of induction and recovery. Lower coefficient = faster induction and recovery (e.g., Desflurane < Sevoflurane < Isoflurane < Halothane).'),
('466c0e9d-a545-5441-9cf7-e36a9001ad2d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which inhalational anesthetic has the lowest blood-gas partition coefficient (fastest induction)?', '["Halothane","Isoflurane","Sevoflurane","Desflurane","Nitrous oxide"]'::jsonb, 'Desflurane', 'Desflurane has the lowest blood-gas partition coefficient (0.42) among volatile anesthetics, leading to fastest induction and recovery.'),
('dc9a6b04-eb07-529c-9926-8ece26aa32f7'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which inhalational anesthetic should NOT be used in patients with malignant hyperthermia susceptibility?', '["Nitrous oxide","Desflurane","Sevoflurane","All volatile anesthetics","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All volatile anesthetics', 'All volatile (halogenated) anesthetics can trigger malignant hyperthermia. Nitrous oxide is NOT a trigger as it is not a volatile halogenated agent.'),
('4b15cd7c-3a25-521c-870c-b6a482d32b48'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'The treatment of choice for malignant hyperthermia is:', '["Dantrolene","Diazepam","Propranolol","Phenytoin","Succinylcholine"]'::jsonb, 'Dantrolene', 'Dantrolene is the specific antidote for malignant hyperthermia. It acts by inhibiting calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum in skeletal muscle.'),
('b9eca9ea-5873-5c07-adb7-04844f7cff44'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following is NOT a trigger for malignant hyperthermia?', '["Succinylcholine","Volatile anesthetics","Nitrous oxide","All volatile anesthetics","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Nitrous oxide', 'Nitrous oxide is not associated with malignant hyperthermia. Succinylcholine and all volatile anesthetics are recognized triggers.'),
('17a64527-6489-578b-b7d5-fe54508535f1'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Etomidate is preferred in patients with:', '["Hepatic failure","Renal failure","Cardiovascular instability","Raised intracranial pressure","Bronchospasm"]'::jsonb, 'Cardiovascular instability', 'Etomidate is preferred in hemodynamically unstable patients because it has minimal effects on cardiovascular function and maintains cardiac output and blood pressure.'),
('aa691aef-fb28-513b-ad1f-495e4ad5c406'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which IV anesthetic suppresses the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis?', '["Propofol","Thiopental","Etomidate","Ketamine","Midazolam"]'::jsonb, 'Etomidate', 'Etomidate inhibits 11β-hydroxylase in the adrenal cortex, suppressing cortisol synthesis. This is a significant adverse effect, especially with prolonged infusion.'),
('766fbb99-5262-5352-877a-fef4163ce3d6'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'The most common cause of awareness during general anesthesia is:', '["Patient resistance","Inadequate depth of anesthesia","Drug interaction","Equipment failure","Metabolic imbalance"]'::jsonb, 'Inadequate depth of anesthesia', 'Inadequate depth of anesthesia is the most common cause of intraoperative awareness. This can occur due to underdosing of anesthetic agents.'),
('d2cea792-a550-5d62-87ee-e21e5e794993'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which inhalational anesthetic has a pungent odor and can cause airway irritation?', '["Sevoflurane","Halothane","Desflurane","Nitrous oxide","Ether"]'::jsonb, 'Desflurane', 'Desflurane has a pungent odor that can cause airway irritation, coughing, breath-holding, and laryngospasm. Sevoflurane is non-pungent and preferred for inhalational induction.'),
('557a8511-3c7f-57fb-9fc9-80b166b0bfe6'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following is the least soluble inhalational anesthetic in blood?', '["Halothane","Isoflurane","Sevoflurane","Desflurane","Ether"]'::jsonb, 'Desflurane', 'Desflurane has the lowest blood-gas partition coefficient (0.42), making it the least soluble in blood among volatile anesthetics.'),
('b9f9cfc3-e960-5d9c-886a-ead634a9a1cb'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which inhalational anesthetic increases cerebral metabolic rate (CMRO2)?', '["Nitrous oxide","Sevoflurane","Isoflurane","Desflurane","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Nitrous oxide', 'Nitrous oxide increases cerebral metabolic rate, which in turn increases cerebral blood flow and can raise intracranial pressure. Volatile anesthetics generally decrease CMRO2.'),
('fadc392c-b2cd-54d1-95a3-cf6732a3d2a5'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which inhalational anesthetic is a potent bronchodilator?', '["Halothane","Isoflurane","Sevoflurane","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'All volatile anesthetics are bronchodilators. Halothane was traditionally used for this purpose, but all volatile agents produce bronchodilation.'),
('94fba1f0-8740-5684-bc95-0dd8270efc24'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following is used for anesthesia in patients with pheochromocytoma?', '["Halothane","Ketamine","Propofol","Thiopental","Fentanyl"]'::jsonb, 'Propofol', 'Propofol is preferred in pheochromocytoma as it does not cause catecholamine release. Halothane and ketamine sensitize to or increase catecholamines respectively.'),
('ae5c8936-5f86-56d6-ac36-ee9e512405b5'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which local anesthetic is an ester type?', '["Lidocaine","Bupivacaine","Procaine","Ropivacaine","Mepivacaine"]'::jsonb, 'Procaine', 'Procaine is an ester-type local anesthetic. Esters are hydrolyzed by plasma cholinesterase. Amides (lidocaine, bupivacaine, ropivacaine) are metabolized in the liver.'),
('38563107-4551-5c1e-a632-a658d16e7523'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'The most potent local anesthetic is:', '["Procaine","Lidocaine","Bupivacaine","Chlorprocaine","Tetracaine"]'::jsonb, 'Bupivacaine', 'Bupivacaine is the most potent amide local anesthetic. Potency is related to lipid solubility. Bupivacaine has high lipid solubility.'),
('6eddee59-6f8b-5401-a00f-e37b136fdd41'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'The longest acting local anesthetic is:', '["Procaine","Lidocaine","Chlorprocaine","Bupivacaine","Prilocaine"]'::jsonb, 'Bupivacaine', 'Bupivacaine has the longest duration of action (4-8 hours) among commonly used local anesthetics due to high protein binding.'),
('8d4c5a14-b1c4-5d6a-a0d8-fca6f93a2697'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which local anesthetic has the shortest duration of action?', '["Procaine","Lidocaine","Bupivacaine","Chlorprocaine","Tetracaine"]'::jsonb, 'Procaine', 'Procaine has the shortest duration of action (30-60 minutes) due to rapid hydrolysis by plasma cholinesterase.'),
('d3235c0d-e544-5a9d-bf12-b98ee9fdfe64'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following local anesthetics is the most cardiotoxic?', '["Procaine","Lidocaine","Chlorprocaine","Bupivacaine","Prilocaine"]'::jsonb, 'Bupivacaine', 'Bupivacaine is the most cardiotoxic local anesthetic due to its high lipid solubility and protein binding, leading to prolonged cardiac sodium channel blockade.'),
('0c871579-fc4c-511e-aa47-af42306096ab'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'The onset of action of local anesthetics is affected by:', '["pH of solution","Concentration of drug","Presence of vasoconstrictor","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Onset of action depends on pH (alkaline solutions penetrate nerve better), concentration, and vasoconstrictors (epinephrine slows absorption but doesn''t directly affect onset).'),
('3130af06-a33a-526a-aaea-1327018ac362'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Adding epinephrine to local anesthetic solutions:', '["Prolongs duration","Reduces systemic toxicity","Improves surgical field","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Epinephrine causes vasoconstriction, which prolongs duration, reduces systemic absorption and toxicity, and provides a drier surgical field.'),
('61f1ff00-ee3b-5010-9af3-934cbd245ead'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which local anesthetic should NOT be used in patients with malignant hyperthermia?', '["Procaine","Lidocaine","Bupivacaine","Ropivacaine","Mepivacaine"]'::jsonb, 'Procaine', 'Ester-type local anesthetics (procaine) can potentially trigger malignant hyperthermia in susceptible individuals. Amides are considered safe.'),
('79837876-692f-5af5-894c-0e6e9a5d9fb6'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'The maximum recommended dose of lidocaine without epinephrine is:', '["3 mg/kg","4.5 mg/kg","7 mg/kg","10 mg/kg","15 mg/kg"]'::jsonb, '7 mg/kg', 'Maximum recommended dose of lidocaine is 4.5 mg/kg with epinephrine and 7 mg/kg without epinephrine.'),
('73af6d44-4583-5300-b1ad-d3e5cb32f3ca'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which local anesthetic causes methemoglobinemia?', '["Procaine","Lidocaine","Bupivacaine","Prilocaine","Chlorprocaine"]'::jsonb, 'Prilocaine', 'Prilocaine is metabolized to o-toluidine, which causes oxidation of hemoglobin to methemoglobin, leading to methemoglobinemia.'),
('141de899-f3ee-58c2-98d6-8a3ed26146a8'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which local anesthetic is used in epidural anesthesia for labor?', '["Procaine","Bupivacaine","Chlorprocaine","Tetracaine","Cocaine"]'::jsonb, 'Bupivacaine', 'Bupivacaine is commonly used in epidural anesthesia for labor due to its long duration of action and differential sensory block.'),
('8ac441c2-3ee1-5525-a9de-8c82096b69dd'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following is an amide-type local anesthetic?', '["Procaine","Tetracaine","Chlorprocaine","Ropivacaine","Cocaine"]'::jsonb, 'Ropivacaine', 'Ropivacaine is an amide-type local anesthetic (like lidocaine, bupivacaine, mepivacaine). Esters include procaine, tetracaine, chlorprocaine, and cocaine.'),
('64a9c2fd-506e-5847-a8f8-98490406fdc4'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'The antidote for local anesthetic systemic toxicity (LAST) is:', '["Atropine","Epinephrine","Intralipid (lipid emulsion)","Sodium bicarbonate","Calcium gluconate"]'::jsonb, 'Intralipid (lipid emulsion)', 'Intravenous lipid emulsion (Intralipid 20%) is the specific treatment for LAST. It acts as a lipid sink, sequestering the lipophilic local anesthetic from tissues.'),
('9e82144e-1468-5550-a8c7-b8f2e28254b2'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which nerve fibers are blocked first by local anesthetics?', '["Motor fibers","Pain fibers","Autonomic fibers","Touch fibers","Temperature fibers"]'::jsonb, 'Autonomic fibers', 'The order of nerve blockade is: autonomic > sensory (pain, temperature, touch) > motor. This is related to nerve fiber diameter and myelination.'),
('bb66d3ee-c535-5075-a07e-5278c66e750e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which local anesthetic is used for spinal anesthesia?', '["Procaine","Lidocaine","Bupivacaine","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Procaine, lidocaine, and bupivacaine can all be used for spinal anesthesia. Bupivacaine is most commonly used for prolonged spinal anesthesia.'),
('83c65fcf-a92e-55cc-a7c3-390c8f3f6f66'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Cocaine is unique among local anesthetics because it:', '["Blocks sodium channels","Causes vasoconstriction","Has no cardiovascular effects","Is an amide type","Is metabolized by liver"]'::jsonb, 'Causes vasoconstriction', 'Cocaine is the only local anesthetic that inhibits norepinephrine reuptake, causing vasoconstriction. All others cause vasodilation.'),
('22c22289-ab5c-52ee-a17c-b99481f7524a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following local anesthetics is used for surface (topical) anesthesia?', '["Lidocaine","Tetracaine","Benzocaine","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Lidocaine, tetracaine, and benzocaine are all used for topical anesthesia. They have appropriate脂溶性 for mucosal absorption.'),
('544cf44c-1df0-58ee-9c07-75459cac57a1'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which local anesthetic is preferred in patients with liver disease?', '["Lidocaine","Bupivacaine","Procaine","Ropivacaine","Prilocaine"]'::jsonb, 'Procaine', 'Procaine (ester type) is preferred in liver disease because esters are hydrolyzed by plasma cholinesterase, not metabolized by the liver.'),
('0f4972fe-4d64-508e-bd56-3e76f3910d93'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which local anesthetic is used in TAC (tetracaine-adrenaline-cocaine) solution?', '["Procaine","Lidocaine","Tetracaine","Bupivacaine","Chlorprocaine"]'::jsonb, 'Tetracaine', 'TAC (tetracaine-adrenaline-cocaine) is a topical anesthetic solution used for wound repair, particularly in children. Tetracaine is the primary anesthetic agent.'),
('ec7cb249-cd6f-5320-bad1-faddb629a029'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Benzodiazepines act on which receptor?', '["GABA-A receptor","GABA-B receptor","Glutamate receptor","Dopamine receptor","Serotonin receptor"]'::jsonb, 'GABA-A receptor', 'Benzodiazepines act as positive allosteric modulators at the GABA-A receptor, increasing the frequency of chloride channel opening.'),
('b7292ea4-d3c5-58b2-9955-01302eef2477'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which benzodiazepine is used as an antidote for benzodiazepine overdose?', '["Diazepam","Midazolam","Flumazenil","Chlordiazepoxide","Lorazepam"]'::jsonb, 'Flumazenil', 'Flumazenil is a competitive antagonist at the benzodiazepine binding site on the GABA-A receptor, used to reverse benzodiazepine effects.'),
('6ca5b40f-2fa6-581c-9903-476ab7ff260d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which benzodiazepine has the longest duration of action?', '["Diazepam","Lorazepam","Midazolam","Alprazolam","Temazepam"]'::jsonb, 'Diazepam', 'Diazepam has a long half-life (20-100 hours) and active metabolites (desmethyldiazepam), giving it the longest duration of action among benzodiazepines.'),
('b629c014-23c2-5857-ace3-ff3c4e7cd197'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which benzodiazepine is preferred in patients with liver disease?', '["Diazepam","Chlordiazepoxide","Lorazepam","Temazepam","Nitrazepam"]'::jsonb, 'Lorazepam', 'Lorazepam undergoes glucuronidation (Phase II metabolism) which is not affected by liver disease, making it preferred in hepatic failure.'),
('2a0833d9-2c25-5c22-a363-92f57b258c1f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Midazolam is preferred for procedural sedation because:', '["Long duration","Amnesia-inducing","Anticonvulsant properties","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Midazolam is preferred for procedural sedation due to its rapid onset, short duration, potent amnesia, and anticonvulsant properties.'),
('be676d57-1a82-5174-8601-96fe7935b32d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Barbiturates act on which receptor?', '["GABA-A receptor","GABA-B receptor","NMDA receptor","Dopamine receptor","Opioid receptor"]'::jsonb, 'GABA-A receptor', 'Barbiturates act on the GABA-A receptor by increasing the duration of chloride channel opening, unlike benzodiazepines which increase frequency.'),
('388d55a3-f05f-5f8f-9a8d-baf5e0d97887'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'The most common use of phenobarbital today is:', '["Sedation","Hypnosis","Antiepileptic","Anesthesia","Anxiolysis"]'::jsonb, 'Antiepileptic', 'Phenobarbital is primarily used as an antiepileptic drug, particularly for generalized tonic-clonic seizures and focal seizures.'),
('012bd07a-eaeb-55da-bb88-e3d6c7175bed'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which benzodiazepine is preferred for anxiety associated with depression?', '["Diazepam","Alprazolam","Chlordiazepoxide","Lorazepam","Clonazepam"]'::jsonb, 'Alprazolam', 'Alprazolam is particularly effective for anxiety with depression, especially panic disorder, due to its high potency and possible serotonin reuptake inhibition.'),
('070d6cce-61a7-527f-9061-5de564d6f178'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Zolpidem acts on which subtype of GABA-A receptor?', '["α1-containing receptors","α2-containing receptors","α3-containing receptors","α5-containing receptors","Non-selective GABA-A"]'::jsonb, 'α1-containing receptors', 'Zolpidem is a non-benzodiazepine hypnotic (Z-drug) that selectively acts on α1-containing GABA-A receptors, promoting sleep without significant anxiolytic or muscle relaxant effects.'),
('f3b6f211-94c6-5e93-b91a-5a4a21f1639f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following is a non-benzodiazepine hypnotic (Z-drug)?', '["Diazepam","Zolpidem","Phenobarbital","Chloral hydrate","Buspirone"]'::jsonb, 'Zolpidem', 'Zolpidem, zopiclone, and zaleplon are non-benzodiazepine hypnotics (Z-drugs) that act on GABA-A receptors with selectivity for α1 subunits.'),
('f9f273a3-8e2b-5ebb-8def-9375817cdce4'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Buspirone acts primarily on which receptor?', '["D2 receptor","5-HT1A receptor","GABA-A receptor","Benzodiazepine receptor","α-adrenergic receptor"]'::jsonb, '5-HT1A receptor', 'Buspirone is a 5-HT1A partial agonist used for generalized anxiety disorder. It has no sedative, muscle relaxant, or anticonvulsant effects.'),
('b1a1225e-cb26-5c70-b12a-42c5a7b919d9'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which advantage does buspirone have over benzodiazepines?', '["Faster onset","No dependence potential","More potent","Sedative effect","Amnesia"]'::jsonb, 'No dependence potential', 'Buspirone has no abuse potential or dependence liability, unlike benzodiazepines. However, it has a slower onset of action (1-2 weeks).'),
('5b16209a-685e-564f-a025-ec232f85e365'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which benzodiazepine is preferred for alcohol withdrawal?', '["Alprazolam","Lorazepam","Diazepam","Midazolam","Triazolam"]'::jsonb, 'Diazepam', 'Diazepam (or chlordiazepoxide) is preferred for alcohol withdrawal due to its long half-life and anti-seizure properties, providing a built-in taper.'),
('97f8d605-fc1e-5e00-baf7-30590af9a570'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Flumazenil can precipitate withdrawal in patients chronically dependent on:', '["Barbiturates","Alcohol","Benzodiazepines","Opioids","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Benzodiazepines', 'Flumazenil is a competitive benzodiazepine antagonist and can precipitate acute withdrawal seizures in patients chronically dependent on benzodiazepines.'),
('51fc3792-bdad-5810-aefc-067398a55381'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following has no amnestic effect?', '["Diazepam","Midazolam","Lorazepam","Buspirone","Alprazolam"]'::jsonb, 'Buspirone', 'Buspirone is a 5-HT1A agonist and does not cause amnesia. All benzodiazepines (diazepam, midazolam, lorazepam, alprazolam) cause anterograde amnesia.'),
('60cc189c-45d5-5d3f-a648-928bfcd8c8e8'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'The antidote for barbiturate poisoning is:', '["Flumazenil","Naloxone","Physostigmine","No specific antidote","Dantrolene"]'::jsonb, 'No specific antidote', 'There is no specific antidote for barbiturate poisoning. Treatment is supportive with airway management, ventilation, and hemodynamic support.'),
('d21b42aa-ea56-5064-b654-dbc3f15e6ab6'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which benzodiazepine is preferred for status epilepticus?', '["Diazepam","Lorazepam","Midazolam","Clonazepam","Alprazolam"]'::jsonb, 'Lorazepam', 'Lorazepam IV is preferred for status epilepticus due to its longer duration of anticonvulsant effect compared to diazepam.'),
('3adfc53e-0d91-5fc1-8c18-ba7e7e010fd6'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following is used for insomnia associated with anxiety?', '["Phenobarbital","Diazepam","Zolpidem","Buspirone","Alprazolam"]'::jsonb, 'Diazepam', 'Diazepam is used for insomnia associated with anxiety due to its sedative, hypnotic, anxiolytic, and muscle relaxant properties.'),
('3c748236-9fa2-5e88-8b02-e9074e00f4cb'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Benzodiazepines can cause respiratory depression when combined with:', '["Alcohol","Opioids","Barbiturates","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Benzodiazepines potentiate respiratory depression when combined with alcohol, opioids, or barbiturates. This combination can be fatal.'),
('54821294-ebdb-5fb5-9a44-304f6ed52472'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which benzodiazepine is available for oral administration only as a liquid (not tablets)?', '["Diazepam","Lorazepam","Midazolam","Alprazolam","Clonazepam"]'::jsonb, 'Midazolam', 'Midazolam oral solution is available and commonly used for procedural sedation in children. It is not typically available as tablets.'),
('4f357176-61ea-5dba-bcd9-f50e4b323010'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Phenytoin acts by:', '["Blocking voltage-gated sodium channels","Enhancing GABA-ergic transmission","Blocking calcium channels","Blocking NMDA receptors","Enhancing glutamate transmission"]'::jsonb, 'Blocking voltage-gated sodium channels', 'Phenytoin blocks voltage-gated sodium channels in their inactivated state, preventing repetitive neuronal firing and stabilizing neuronal membranes.'),
('924db20f-a0f2-55d4-88fd-9610dd874bf6'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following is a prodrug for epilepsy?', '["Phenytoin","Carbamazepine","Valproic acid","Oxcarbazepine","Levetiracetam"]'::jsonb, 'Oxcarbazepine', 'Oxcarbazepine is a prodrug that is rapidly reduced to its active metabolite 10-monohydroxy derivative (MHD), which is responsible for its antiepileptic effects.'),
('bede9a33-3fb3-5852-8dd9-ffc66d555e6b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which antiepileptic drug has the widest spectrum of activity?', '["Phenytoin","Carbamazepine","Valproic acid","Ethosuximide","Lamotrigine"]'::jsonb, 'Valproic acid', 'Valproic acid has the broadest spectrum of activity among antiepileptics, effective against almost all seizure types except absence seizures.'),
('aedf1f39-51ee-5a0b-b442-3ba703fb4ac8'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which antiepileptic drug is first-line for absence seizures?', '["Phenytoin","Carbamazepine","Valproic acid","Ethosuximide","Lamotrigine"]'::jsonb, 'Ethosuximide', 'Ethosuximide is the drug of choice for absence seizures. It blocks T-type calcium channels in thalamic neurons.'),
('8b570b2e-2847-53a7-afd9-3ad549b81b0c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Carbamazepine is most effective for which type of seizure?', '["Absence seizures","Tonic-clonic seizures","Myoclonic seizures","Atonic seizures","Infantile spasms"]'::jsonb, 'Tonic-clonic seizures', 'Carbamazepine is first-line for partial (focal) seizures and generalized tonic-clonic seizures. It is ineffective for absence seizures.'),
('98062b43-4ea6-5ef2-925d-ad78664929ec'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which antiepileptic drug can cause Stevens-Johnson syndrome?', '["Phenytoin","Lamotrigine","Carbamazepine","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Phenytoin, lamotrigine, and carbamazepine all carry risks of serious dermatologic reactions including Stevens-Johnson syndrome.'),
('5c607a54-379d-5042-82bd-5a283ec2e52a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Valproic acid acts by:', '["Blocking sodium channels","Enhancing GABA transmission","Blocking calcium channels","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Valproic acid has multiple mechanisms: blocks sodium channels, enhances GABA (by inhibiting GABA transaminase and stimulating glutamic acid decarboxylase), and blocks T-type calcium channels.'),
('e977ff3c-8ef6-5696-8d82-5ddcd79e60e2'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which antiepileptic drug is contraindicated in pregnancy due to neural tube defects?', '["Phenytoin","Carbamazepine","Valproic acid","Lamotrigine","Levetiracetam"]'::jsonb, 'Valproic acid', 'Valproic acid has the highest risk of neural tube defects (1-2%) when used in pregnancy. Folic acid supplementation is essential if valproate is used.'),
('4e398a5e-ddbb-5ff8-81c9-3137ed0a5968'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Levetiracetam acts by:', '["Binding to SV2A protein","Blocking sodium channels","Enhancing GABA","Blocking calcium channels","Blocking NMDA receptors"]'::jsonb, 'Binding to SV2A protein', 'Levetiracetam binds to synaptic vesicle protein 2A (SV2A), modulating neurotransmitter release. It has a unique mechanism of action.'),
('64e0f1ff-78ba-56ad-aabb-de087c6d4d55'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which antiepileptic drug is used for tonic-clonic seizures and trigeminal neuralgia?', '["Phenytoin","Carbamazepine","Valproic acid","Lamotrigine","Topiramate"]'::jsonb, 'Carbamazepine', 'Carbamazepine is used for both tonic-clonic seizures and trigeminal neuralgia. It is first-line for trigeminal neuralgia.'),
('e83141cf-c8c1-50d0-90ec-70edb3059eb5'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Phenytoin shows zero-order kinetics at therapeutic doses because:', '["It is highly protein bound","Hepatic metabolism is saturated","It has a narrow therapeutic index","It has active metabolites","It is rapidly absorbed"]'::jsonb, 'Hepatic metabolism is saturated', 'Phenytoin shows dose-dependent (non-linear) pharmacokinetics because its hepatic metabolism (CYP2C9, CYP2C19) becomes saturated at therapeutic concentrations.'),
('1e55cc4a-949d-5f69-86ab-7bc03e47e392'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which antiepileptic drug is used for myoclonic seizures?', '["Phenytoin","Carbamazepine","Valproic acid","Ethosuximide","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Valproic acid', 'Valproic acid is effective for myoclonic seizures. Phenytoin and carbamazepine can worsen myoclonic seizures.'),
('f4e57b2a-2e36-5ed9-9b10-8c874071ffed'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Gabapentin acts by:', '["Blocking voltage-gated calcium channels","Enhancing GABA","Blocking sodium channels","Blocking NMDA receptors","Inhibiting GABA transaminase"]'::jsonb, 'Blocking voltage-gated calcium channels', 'Gabapentin binds to the α2δ subunit of voltage-gated calcium channels, reducing calcium influx and neurotransmitter release.'),
('6494fc43-d813-50a9-8e64-9ff5869f2e91'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which antiepileptic drug is also effective for bipolar disorder?', '["Phenytoin","Carbamazepine","Valproic acid","Lamotrigine","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Carbamazepine, valproic acid, and lamotrigine are all used as mood stabilizers in bipolar disorder.'),
('a2588d86-53e7-5ef5-af37-5f4ad524e73b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Lamotrigine acts primarily by:', '["Blocking voltage-gated sodium channels","Enhancing GABA","Blocking calcium channels","Blocking NMDA receptors","Inhibiting GABA transaminase"]'::jsonb, 'Blocking voltage-gated sodium channels', 'Lamotrigine primarily blocks voltage-gated sodium channels, similar to phenytoin and carbamazepine, reducing glutamate release.'),
('2f63a69c-bf19-5515-ab53-8a459191009e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following can reduce the effectiveness of oral contraceptives?', '["Phenytoin","Carbamazepine","Phenobarbital","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Phenytoin, carbamazepine, and phenobarbital are hepatic enzyme inducers that can reduce the effectiveness of oral contraceptives by increasing their metabolism.'),
('255c917c-9aa3-50e8-a8f3-986de531430b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Ethosuximide acts by:', '["Blocking T-type calcium channels","Blocking sodium channels","Enhancing GABA","Blocking NMDA receptors","Blocking potassium channels"]'::jsonb, 'Blocking T-type calcium channels', 'Ethosuximide selectively blocks T-type calcium channels in thalamic neurons, making it specific for absence seizures.'),
('ab48cdf4-6dda-5bca-9311-5a0c7db775fa'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which antiepileptic drug can cause gingival hyperplasia?', '["Phenytoin","Carbamazepine","Valproic acid","Lamotrigine","Levetiracetam"]'::jsonb, 'Phenytoin', 'Gingival hyperplasia is a characteristic side effect of phenytoin, occurring in up to 50% of patients. Good oral hygiene can minimize this.'),
('f944ffe7-8b83-5059-b98c-4e6df6871b1c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which antiepileptic drug can cause megaloblastic anemia?', '["Phenytoin","Carbamazepine","Valproic acid","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Phenytoin, carbamazepine, and valproic acid can all interfere with folate metabolism, potentially causing megaloblastic anemia.'),
('c01417a1-54da-5304-b213-f19ec01d8aba'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Topiramate is also used for:', '["Migraine prophylaxis","Weight loss","Bipolar disorder","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Topiramate is used for migraine prophylaxis, can cause weight loss (anorexia), and is sometimes used as an adjunct in bipolar disorder.'),
('21a16da6-f38d-545a-8c6f-16591a05b4f5'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Levodopa is converted to dopamine by which enzyme?', '["MAO-B","COMT","DOPA decarboxylase","Dopamine β-hydroxylase","Tyrosine hydroxylase"]'::jsonb, 'DOPA decarboxylase', 'Levodopa is converted to dopamine by DOPA decarboxylase (aromatic L-amino acid decarboxylase) in the brain and periphery.'),
('f3437734-09c5-550d-8cbc-6bf84f4e243f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Carbidopa is combined with levodopa because it:', '["Enhances CNS entry","Inhibits peripheral DOPA decarboxylase","Inhibits COMT","Inhibits MAO-B","Blocks dopamine receptors"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibits peripheral DOPA decarboxylase', 'Carbidopa inhibits peripheral DOPA decarboxylase, preventing the conversion of levodopa to dopamine in the periphery, thereby increasing CNS availability and reducing peripheral side effects.'),
('7294a01c-cabf-548b-a51e-d06162dbe61f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following is a dopamine agonist used in Parkinson''s disease?', '["Selegiline","Entacapone","Bromocriptine","Amantadine","Trihexyphenidyl"]'::jsonb, 'Bromocriptine', 'Bromocriptine is an ergot-derived dopamine agonist used in Parkinson''s disease. Pramipexole and ropinirole are non-ergot dopamine agonists.'),
('9609079d-e512-501a-af1c-54ca47eedd4a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which drug inhibits MAO-B and is used in Parkinson''s disease?', '["Selegiline","Entacapone","Tolcapone","Amantadine","Trihexyphenidyl"]'::jsonb, 'Selegiline', 'Selegiline is an irreversible MAO-B inhibitor that prevents the breakdown of dopamine in the brain, used as adjunct therapy in Parkinson''s disease.'),
('678ed663-0ed4-53f3-a6ad-30aceab7c744'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which COMT inhibitor is used in Parkinson''s disease?', '["Selegiline","Entacapone","Amantadine","Trihexyphenidyl","Bromocriptine"]'::jsonb, 'Entacapone', 'Entacapone is a COMT inhibitor used with levodopa/carbidopa to extend the duration of levodopa action by preventing its peripheral metabolism.'),
('92e3f0ac-f041-5c79-9f2d-e65e2502e791'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Amantadine acts by:', '["Blocking muscarinic receptors","Enhancing dopamine release","NMDA receptor antagonism","MAO-B inhibition","COMT inhibition"]'::jsonb, 'NMDA receptor antagonism', 'Amantadine is an NMDA receptor antagonist. It also enhances dopamine release and blocks dopamine reuptake. It is used for dyskinesias and early Parkinson''s disease.'),
('26a1a742-e4cb-5d24-b2a6-4f09d912fac6'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Trihexyphenidyl is used in Parkinson''s disease for:', '["Tremor","Rigidity","Bradykinesia","Postural instability","Gait disturbance"]'::jsonb, 'Tremor', 'Anticholinergics (trihexyphenidyl) are most effective for tremor in Parkinson''s disease. They have limited effect on rigidity or bradykinesia.'),
('e5c05d9e-1fd8-5a74-bd5c-8a13d1bb7d52'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following is the most effective treatment for Parkinson''s disease?', '["Trihexyphenidyl","Amantadine","Levodopa","Bromocriptine","Selegiline"]'::jsonb, 'Levodopa', 'Levodopa is the most effective treatment for Parkinson''s disease, providing significant improvement in motor symptoms, especially bradykinesia and rigidity.'),
('97ac97a4-29f7-5b95-a2ee-78c768626af1'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Motor complications of long-term levodopa therapy include:', '["Wearing-off phenomenon","On-off phenomenon","Dyskinesias","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Long-term levodopa therapy leads to motor fluctuations (wearing-off, on-off) and dyskinesias due to pulsatile dopaminergic stimulation.'),
('4b032b9e-e138-5808-8f98-27c0aa1bbfee'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'The wearing-off phenomenon in Parkinson''s disease refers to:', '["Disease progression","End-of-dose deterioration","Sudden unpredictable fluctuations","Drug tolerance","Drug toxicity"]'::jsonb, 'End-of-dose deterioration', 'The wearing-off phenomenon (end-of-dose deterioration) is a predictable shortening of levodopa''s duration of action as the disease progresses.'),
('b0f4f247-541c-52c9-9947-70e656f8b605'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which dopamine agonist is associated with sudden sleep attacks?', '["Bromocriptine","Pergolide","Pramipexole","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Pramipexole', 'Pramipexole (and other dopamine agonists) can cause sudden sleep attacks, which can be dangerous, especially while driving.'),
('55daa36a-457c-5ebb-a863-27969cd16959'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which drug is used for drug-induced Parkinsonism?', '["Levodopa","Bromocriptine","Trihexyphenidyl","Selegiline","Entacapone"]'::jsonb, 'Trihexyphenidyl', 'Anticholinergics (trihexyphenidyl) are used for drug-induced parkinsonism caused by antipsychotics that block D2 receptors.'),
('88919277-9166-5209-b2c9-6bbbc300a7bf'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Tolcapone is rarely used because of:', '["Hepatotoxicity","Nephrotoxicity","Cardiotoxicity","Neurotoxicity","Pulmonary toxicity"]'::jsonb, 'Hepatotoxicity', 'Tolcapone is rarely used due to risk of fatal hepatotoxicity. Entacapone is the preferred COMT inhibitor as it has less hepatotoxic potential.'),
('4aa743b5-74fc-5541-8138-fb411f678d03'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following is an anticholinergic drug used in Parkinson''s disease?', '["Benztropine","Biperiden","Orphenadrine","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Benztropine, biperiden, and orphenadrine are all anticholinergic drugs used in Parkinson''s disease, primarily for tremor.'),
('036a6d42-17d5-5451-a281-5dfc72f47f90'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Selegiline is metabolized to:', '["Amphetamine and methamphetamine","Levodopa accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dopamine mediated by specific receptor activation","Norepinephrine without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Serotonin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Amphetamine and methamphetamine', 'Selegiline is metabolized to L-methamphetamine and L-amphetamine, which can cause insomnia and cardiovascular effects.'),
('844bc955-6a6a-5ef2-9544-fbd9c023633e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Rasagiline differs from selegiline in that it:', '["Is less selective for MAO-B","Does not produce amphetamine metabolites","Is irreversible","Is less potent","Has more side effects"]'::jsonb, 'Does not produce amphetamine metabolites', 'Rasagiline is metabolized to 1(R)-aminoindan, which has neuroprotective properties. Unlike selegiline, it does not produce amphetamine metabolites.'),
('a2ef2054-c63c-534f-96b1-7973d5185aab'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following is NOT a dopamine agonist?', '["Bromocriptine","Pramipexole","Ropinirole","Amantadine","Rotigotine"]'::jsonb, 'Amantadine', 'Amantadine is an NMDA receptor antagonist, not a dopamine agonist. Bromocriptine, pramipexole, ropinirole, and rotigotine are dopamine agonists.'),
('c6fbeee6-306c-5d45-a5ef-2d23e75ea28f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'The initial dose of levodopa should be low in elderly patients because:', '["They have reduced renal function","They are more prone to hallucinations and confusion","They have faster metabolism","They have better tolerance","Levodopa is less effective in elderly"]'::jsonb, 'They are more prone to hallucinations and confusion', 'Elderly patients are more susceptible to levodopa-induced hallucinations, confusion, and psychosis. Low starting doses with slow titration are recommended.'),
('d105feeb-ade0-570c-a8a4-32490120f085'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which enzyme inhibitor is combined with levodopa to prevent its peripheral metabolism?', '["Entacapone","Carbidopa","Selegiline","Tolcapone","Rasagiline"]'::jsonb, 'Carbidopa', 'Carbidopa inhibits peripheral DOPA decarboxylase, preventing conversion of levodopa to dopamine in the periphery, increasing CNS bioavailability.'),
('f189ecc1-c4e1-59b0-8e70-5105325cd90d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Typical antipsychotics primarily block which receptor?', '["D1 receptor","D2 receptor","5-HT2A receptor","Muscarinic receptor","Histamine receptor"]'::jsonb, 'D2 receptor', 'Typical (first-generation) antipsychotics primarily block D2 receptors in the mesolimbic pathway, which is responsible for their antipsychotic effect.'),
('671bbd68-7fae-5c61-9276-4d8934ab8828'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which atypical antipsychotic has the highest affinity for 5-HT2A receptors relative to D2?', '["Haloperidol","Chlorpromazine","Clozapine","Risperidone","Olanzapine"]'::jsonb, 'Clozapine', 'Clozapine has the highest 5-HT2A/D2 affinity ratio, which contributes to its atypical profile and reduced extrapyramidal side effects.'),
('39a76302-29a1-5b02-94b7-ca8f917f266b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'The most effective antipsychotic for treatment-resistant schizophrenia is:', '["Haloperidol","Chlorpromazine","Clozapine","Risperidone","Olanzapine"]'::jsonb, 'Clozapine', 'Clozapine is the only antipsychotic FDA-approved for treatment-resistant schizophrenia. Approximately 30% of patients unresponsive to other antipsychotics respond to clozapine.'),
('512c1e2e-e009-504b-a9e0-a5ff4a60728e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'The most serious side effect of clozapine is:', '["Extrapyramidal symptoms","Tardive dyskinesia","Agranulocytosis","Weight gain","Diabetes mellitus"]'::jsonb, 'Agranulocytosis', 'Agranulocytosis (1-2%) is the most dangerous side effect of clozapine, requiring regular blood monitoring. It can be fatal if not detected early.'),
('25d1a51d-5ed6-525c-8fb8-2df1e633cb28'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Haloperidol is most commonly associated with:', '["Sedation","Weight gain","Extrapyramidal symptoms","Hypotension","Anticholinergic effects"]'::jsonb, 'Extrapyramidal symptoms', 'Haloperidol, a typical antipsychotic, has high D2 affinity and is most commonly associated with extrapyramidal symptoms (EPS) due to nigrostriatal D2 blockade.'),
('01c0dee3-02d9-5676-9e7d-6e28d77309ae'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which antipsychotic is most likely to cause hyperprolactinemia?', '["Clozapine","Olanzapine","Quetiapine","Risperidone","Aripiprazole"]'::jsonb, 'Risperidone', 'Risperidone has strong D2 blockade in the tuberoinfundibular pathway, leading to increased prolactin levels. Clozapine and quetiapine have minimal effect on prolactin.'),
('81ff6e69-97de-50f0-a862-a05bdcd9dd72'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Tardive dyskinesia is caused by:', '["Acute D2 blockade resulting in decreased functional activity","Chronic D2 blockade leading to receptor supersensitivity","5-HT2A blockade mediated by specific receptor activation","Muscarinic blockade without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Histamine blockade leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Chronic D2 blockade leading to receptor supersensitivity', 'Tardive dyskinesia is believed to result from chronic D2 receptor blockade leading to dopamine receptor supersensitivity in the nigrostriatal pathway.'),
('749f50bf-cc51-59ac-9b8c-601d1be3e82f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following is used to treat acute dystonia caused by antipsychotics?', '["Haloperidol","Benztropine","Clozapine","Risperidone","Olanzapine"]'::jsonb, 'Benztropine', 'Anticholinergics (benztropine, diphenhydramine) are used to treat acute dystonia caused by antipsychotics.'),
('6b488f03-9768-5d3a-bfdd-e89d3daec6a1'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Neuroleptic malignant syndrome (NMS) is characterized by:', '["Hyperthermia, muscle rigidity, autonomic dysfunction","Hypothermia, muscle rigidity, autonomic dysfunction","Hyperthermia, muscle flaccidity, autonomic dysfunction","Hypothermia, muscle flaccidity, autonomic dysfunction","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperthermia, muscle rigidity, autonomic dysfunction', 'NMS is characterized by hyperthermia, severe muscle rigidity (lead-pipe), autonomic instability, altered mental status, and elevated creatine kinase.'),
('e64fe8e8-b08c-5073-b867-6d06d862d56a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'The treatment of choice for neuroleptic malignant syndrome is:', '["Anticholinergics","Benzodiazepines","Dantrolene","Bromocriptine","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'NMS is treated with dantrolene (muscle relaxant), bromocriptine (dopamine agonist), benzodiazepines (sedation), and supportive care. All may be used.'),
('d2e26f64-3418-59df-a6d0-8473650ed775'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which atypical antipsychotic has the least metabolic side effects?', '["Olanzapine","Clozapine","Quetiapine","Aripiprazole","Risperidone"]'::jsonb, 'Aripiprazole', 'Aripiprazole has the least metabolic side effects (weight gain, diabetes, dyslipidemia) among atypical antipsychotics due to partial D2 agonism.'),
('c89ee948-75f8-506e-9ae5-6b147a1a3aa3'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Aripiprazole is unique because it is a:', '["Full antagonist","Partial agonist","Full agonist","Inverse agonist","Silent antagonist"]'::jsonb, 'Partial agonist', 'Aripiprazole is a D2 partial agonist. It acts as a functional antagonist in hyperdopaminergic states and as a functional agonist in hypodopaminergic states.'),
('c67f8c3d-dac4-5043-b9b3-9a68c4e00479'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Chlorpromazine is most likely to cause:', '["Extrapyramidal symptoms","Sedation and hypotension","Tardive dyskinesia","Akathisia","Dystonia"]'::jsonb, 'Sedation and hypotension', 'Chlorpromazine has strong antihistaminic and anti-α1 adrenergic effects, causing sedation and hypotension. It has lower EPS risk than high-potency typicals.'),
('85d44ca5-9369-5888-93db-832b3539f5bd'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following is a high-potency typical antipsychotic?', '["Chlorpromazine","Thioridazine","Haloperidol","Loxapine","Molindone"]'::jsonb, 'Haloperidol', 'Haloperidol is a high-potency typical antipsychotic. High-potency drugs have more D2 blockade and higher EPS risk but less sedation and hypotension.'),
('08ddfca0-408c-5151-807f-c6702e8a5f62'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Clozapine requires regular monitoring of:', '["Blood glucose","Complete blood count","Liver function","Renal function","Thyroid function"]'::jsonb, 'Complete blood count', 'Clozapine requires weekly to monthly CBC monitoring due to the risk of agranulocytosis. This is mandatory for the first 6 months.'),
('bde1563a-b6eb-5f41-8eee-9c4935abf5da'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which antipsychotic is most effective for positive symptoms of schizophrenia?', '["Clozapine","Haloperidol","Risperidone","All typical antipsychotics","All atypical antipsychotics"]'::jsonb, 'All typical antipsychotics', 'All typical antipsychotics are equally effective for positive symptoms (hallucinations, delusions) due to D2 blockade. Atypicals may be less effective for positive symptoms.'),
('9c3c5f73-9596-5e69-9a6f-ee389889f087'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which antipsychotic can improve negative symptoms of schizophrenia?', '["Haloperidol","Chlorpromazine","Clozapine","Fluphenazine","Thioridazine"]'::jsonb, 'Clozapine', 'Clozapine is the only antipsychotic with proven efficacy for negative symptoms of schizophrenia, possibly due to its 5-HT2A antagonism and effects on glutamate.'),
('8e27556c-255c-5a60-9f49-4984a7bce723'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following is used as a depot antipsychotic injection?', '["Fluphenazine enanthate","Haloperidol decanoate","Risperidone long-acting","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Fluphenazine enanthate, haloperidol decanoate, and risperidone long-acting injection are all depot formulations for long-term antipsychotic therapy.'),
('80bcaf92-90e5-5673-94f9-ebb857dc6517'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'The therapeutic dose of antipsychotics for schizophrenia is typically reached in:', '["1-2 weeks","2-4 weeks","4-6 weeks","6-8 weeks","8-12 weeks"]'::jsonb, '4-6 weeks', 'Full therapeutic effect of antipsychotics typically takes 4-6 weeks. This is due to the time required for receptor adaptations and neuroplastic changes.'),
('678f86bc-ff56-5517-aa1c-6c8a19427b8d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which atypical antipsychotic is most sedating?', '["Aripiprazole","Ziprasidone","Quetiapine","Lurasidone","Iloperidone"]'::jsonb, 'Quetiapine', 'Quetiapine has strong antihistaminic (H1) properties, making it one of the most sedating atypical antipsychotics, often used for insomnia.'),
('edeba1ab-3b48-57f0-9526-fd456a2d1089'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'TCAs act primarily by:', '["Blocking serotonin reuptake","Blocking norepinephrine reuptake","Blocking both serotonin and norepinephrine reuptake","Inhibiting MAO","Blocking dopamine receptors"]'::jsonb, 'Blocking both serotonin and norepinephrine reuptake', 'TCAs (tricyclic antidepressants) primarily block the reuptake of both serotonin and norepinephrine, increasing their synaptic concentrations.'),
('e8210508-8755-533e-819e-129a588e5b3b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which SSRI is most commonly prescribed for depression?', '["Fluoxetine","Sertraline","Paroxetine","Citalopram","Escitalopram"]'::jsonb, 'Escitalopram', 'Escitalopram is often considered first-line due to its high selectivity for serotonin reuptake transporter, minimal drug interactions, and favorable side effect profile.'),
('bbc47888-9cdc-5b93-a930-688edda19531'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'MAO inhibitors act by:', '["Blocking serotonin reuptake","Blocking norepinephrine reuptake","Inhibiting monoamine oxidase enzyme","Blocking dopamine receptors","Enhancing GABA transmission"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibiting monoamine oxidase enzyme', 'MAO inhibitors prevent the breakdown of monoamines (serotonin, norepinephrine, dopamine) by inhibiting monoamine oxidase enzymes (MAO-A and MAO-B).'),
('2feff065-fe8d-5cf8-9b54-9b97bae4822a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'The most dangerous interaction of MAO inhibitors is with:', '["Opioids","Tyramine-containing foods","Benzodiazepines","Antihistamines","NSAIDs"]'::jsonb, 'Tyramine-containing foods', 'MAO inhibitors combined with tyramine-rich foods (cheese, wine, fermented foods) can cause hypertensive crisis due to uncontrolled norepinephrine release.'),
('9a52302d-53e5-56cc-aa32-83f24597a9f0'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which TCA has the strongest anticholinergic effects?', '["Amitriptyline","Nortriptyline","Imipramine","Desipramine","Clomipramine"]'::jsonb, 'Amitriptyline', 'Amitriptyline has the strongest anticholinergic effects among TCAs, causing dry mouth, constipation, urinary retention, and blurred vision.'),
('96706c93-94a7-52c0-a85c-1dbae305e86d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which SSRI is most activating and best for depressed patients with fatigue?', '["Fluoxetine","Sertraline","Paroxetine","Citalopram","Escitalopram"]'::jsonb, 'Fluoxetine', 'Fluoxetine is the most activating SSRI, making it suitable for depressed patients with fatigue, psychomotor retardation, and hypersomnia.'),
('bb3d9efc-ea8b-585d-8d27-650b5e90789e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which antidepressant is most effective for OCD?', '["Amitriptyline","Imipramine","Fluoxetine","Mianserin","Mirtazapine"]'::jsonb, 'Fluoxetine', 'Fluoxetine (and other SSRIs) are first-line for OCD. Clomipramine (a TCA with strong SRI activity) is also effective but has more side effects.'),
('4fa0506b-08a7-5ff3-a0cf-b1b2854cff3e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Venlafaxine is classified as:', '["TCA","SSRI","SNRI","MAOI","Atypical antidepressant"]'::jsonb, 'SNRI', 'Venlafaxine is a serotonin-norepinephrine reuptake inhibitor (SNRI). At higher doses, it also inhibits dopamine reuptake.'),
('d5935d56-5e70-50a2-8730-c244ea5eeb25'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Mirtazapine acts by:', '["Blocking α2-adrenergic autoreceptors","Blocking 5-HT2A receptors","Blocking 5-HT3 receptors","Blocking H1 receptors","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Mirtazapine has multiple mechanisms: blocks α2-autoreceptors (increasing NE/5-HT release), 5-HT2A, 5-HT3, and H1 receptors (causing sedation and weight gain).'),
('3d33a519-2f5f-584e-a0c2-8e4e27f12b48'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Bupropion is unique among antidepressants because it:', '["Inhibits serotonin reuptake resulting in decreased functional activity","Inhibits norepinephrine and dopamine reuptake","Is a MAOI mediated by specific receptor activation","Blocks 5-HT receptors without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Is a TCA leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibits norepinephrine and dopamine reuptake', 'Bupropion is a norepinephrine-dopamine reuptake inhibitor (NDRI). It is unique among antidepressants for its dopaminergic activity and lack of sexual side effects.'),
('9f5aadc5-cb0b-5ea7-a403-ec288a3480ad'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which antidepressant is most likely to cause weight loss?', '["Amitriptyline","Mirtazapine","Paroxetine","Fluoxetine","Nortriptyline"]'::jsonb, 'Fluoxetine', 'Fluoxetine can cause weight loss, especially in the short term. Most other antidepressants (TCAs, mirtazapine, paroxetine) tend to cause weight gain.'),
('59a776c4-3a05-5f90-9d58-548a72ad4fc1'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'SSRIs are contraindicated with MAO inhibitors because of the risk of:', '["Hypertensive crisis","Serotonin syndrome","Neuroleptic malignant syndrome","Tardive dyskinesia","Akathisia"]'::jsonb, 'Serotonin syndrome', 'Combining SSRIs with MAO inhibitors can cause serotonin syndrome (hyperthermia, agitation, clonus, hyperreflexia) due to excessive serotonin activity.'),
('3b449a99-8f32-5731-8a76-f56cd8fa8e97'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'The washout period between stopping an MAOI and starting an SSRI should be:', '["1 week","2 weeks","4 weeks","6 weeks","8 weeks"]'::jsonb, '2 weeks', 'A washout period of at least 2 weeks is required between stopping MAOIs and starting SSRIs to prevent serotonin syndrome.'),
('fd51126a-7fb4-59c0-a43c-a9b4eb0fe9f3'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which TCA is used for nocturnal enuresis in children?', '["Amitriptyline","Imipramine","Clomipramine","Nortriptyline","Desipramine"]'::jsonb, 'Imipramine', 'Imipramine is the TCA of choice for nocturnal enuresis. Its anticholinergic and antidiuretic effects help reduce bedwetting.'),
('b86c4be2-b4c7-5d0a-b356-d5d6a6f4dbb8'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which antidepressant is most sedating?', '["Fluoxetine","Sertraline","Mirtazapine","Bupropion","Venlafaxine"]'::jsonb, 'Mirtazapine', 'Mirtazapine is highly sedating due to its potent H1 receptor blockade. It is useful for depressed patients with insomnia and poor appetite.'),
('07c0b349-5047-5031-9a92-1d6b758f332d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which antidepressant is contraindicated in patients with seizure disorders?', '["SSRIs","Bupropion","Mirtazapine","Trazodone","Vilazodone"]'::jsonb, 'Bupropion', 'Bupropion lowers the seizure threshold and is contraindicated in patients with seizure disorders or eating disorders.'),
('702bc2cb-bc7e-5233-9c7d-aa757740005e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Trazodone is primarily used as:', '["Antidepressant","Anxiolytic","Hypnotic","Mood stabilizer","Antipsychotic"]'::jsonb, 'Hypnotic', 'Trazodone is primarily used as a hypnotic at low doses (50-100 mg) rather than as an antidepressant due to its strong antihistaminic effects.'),
('083e156e-7b4b-5898-b043-e3e815ba7825'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'The most lethal TCAs in overdose are:', '["Amitriptyline and clomipramine","Nortriptyline and desipramine","Imipramine and clomipramine","Amitriptyline and nortriptyline","All TCAs are equally toxic"]'::jsonb, 'Amitriptyline and clomipramine', 'Amitriptyline and clomipramine are the most lethal TCAs in overdose due to their strong sodium channel blockade, leading to fatal cardiac arrhythmias.'),
('4499b985-cf05-5353-9280-19d176581e7a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which antidepressant has the least drug interactions?', '["Fluoxetine","Sertraline","Escitalopram","Paroxetine","Citalopram"]'::jsonb, 'Escitalopram', 'Escitalopram has the fewest drug interactions among SSRIs as it has minimal effects on CYP enzymes, unlike fluoxetine and paroxetine.'),
('b3e76ae2-1eac-566c-8493-bf126c19bf76'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which antidepressant is preferred in patients with cardiac disease?', '["Amitriptyline","Imipramine","Sertraline","Fluoxetine","Venlafaxine"]'::jsonb, 'Sertraline', 'Sertraline is the safest antidepressant in cardiac patients (based on SADHART trial). TCAs are contraindicated due to cardiotoxicity.'),
('6431d185-16af-5293-a74f-3dbdc3f42b75'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'SSRIs can cause which sexual dysfunction?', '["Decreased libido","Erectile dysfunction","Anorgasmia","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'SSRIs commonly cause sexual dysfunction including decreased libido, erectile dysfunction, and anorgasmia in up to 40-70% of patients.'),
('42f627fd-8ae3-5b36-bc36-0d260d13e034'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following is NOT an SNRI?', '["Venlafaxine","Duloxetine","Desvenlafaxine","Milnacipran","Mirtazapine"]'::jsonb, 'Mirtazapine', 'Mirtazapine is a noradrenergic and specific serotonergic antidepressant (NaSSA), not an SNRI. Venlafaxine, duloxetine, desvenlafaxine, and milnacipran are SNRIs.'),
('71d23e29-58e9-5809-8e68-a1ec0385b71f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which antidepressant is approved for diabetic neuropathy?', '["Amitriptyline","Duloxetine","Venlafaxine","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Duloxetine', 'Duloxetine is FDA-approved for diabetic peripheral neuropathic pain. Amitriptyline and venlafaxine are also used but not FDA-approved for this indication.'),
('f38b48d9-6930-5659-8bc3-694ef5430632'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Clomipramine is particularly effective for which condition?', '["Panic disorder","Social anxiety","OCD","PTSD","GAD"]'::jsonb, 'OCD', 'Clomipramine is the most effective TCA for OCD due to its potent serotonin reuptake inhibition. It is often considered when SSRIs fail.'),
('e44add99-b568-532a-8ce2-3420c784dd43'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which antidepressant should be started at bedtime due to sedation?', '["Fluoxetine","Bupropion","Mirtazapine","Sertraline","Escitalopram"]'::jsonb, 'Mirtazapine', 'Mirtazapine is highly sedating due to H1 blockade and should be started at bedtime. This can help with insomnia in depressed patients.'),
('7041c897-019c-5d04-a033-9b7a1aa9060b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Morphine acts primarily on which receptor?', '["μ (mu) receptor","κ (kappa) receptor","δ (delta) receptor","σ (sigma) receptor","ε (epsilon) receptor"]'::jsonb, 'μ (mu) receptor', 'Morphine acts primarily on μ (mu) opioid receptors, which mediate analgesia, respiratory depression, euphoria, and physical dependence.'),
('4255cac2-517f-5079-a88a-b5dd7a9a77ad'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'The antidote for opioid overdose is:', '["Flumazenil","Naloxone","Physostigmine","Atropine","Naloxone and flumazenil"]'::jsonb, 'Naloxone', 'Naloxone is a competitive opioid receptor antagonist used to reverse opioid overdose, particularly respiratory depression.'),
('c17ac9bc-7a1a-5509-ad7c-5aa61e511458'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Codeine is used primarily as:', '["Analgesic","Antitussive","Antidiarrheal","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Codeine is used as a mild analgesic, antitussive (cough suppressant), and antidiarrheal due to its μ receptor agonist activity.'),
('c2577b92-c866-543e-9f5d-0c6c55bf9d0b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Meperidine (pethidine) is contraindicated in patients taking:', '["MAO inhibitors","SSRIs","TCAs","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'MAO inhibitors', 'Meperidine combined with MAO inhibitors can cause fatal serotonin syndrome and/or respiratory depression. This combination is absolutely contraindicated.'),
('e5c595a6-31c2-5082-9781-b56acc94639a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'The respiratory depressant effect of opioids can be reversed by:', '["Naloxone","Naltrexone","Both A and B","Neither A nor B","Flumazenil"]'::jsonb, 'Both A and B', 'Both naloxone and naltrexone are opioid antagonists that can reverse respiratory depression. Naloxone is preferred for acute reversal due to IV administration.'),
('88e274a4-7ff3-5baa-91ac-87edcd77d469'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which opioid is most commonly used for severe cancer pain?', '["Codeine","Tramadol","Morphine","Pethidine","Methadone"]'::jsonb, 'Morphine', 'Morphine is the gold standard for severe cancer pain. It is available in various formulations including immediate-release, sustained-release, and parenteral.'),
('28639f2e-e2b6-537b-af34-14e402a4273e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which opioid is used for opioid substitution therapy in addiction?', '["Morphine","Heroin","Methadone","Fentanyl","Codeine"]'::jsonb, 'Methadone', 'Methadone is a long-acting oral opioid used for maintenance therapy in opioid addiction. It prevents withdrawal and reduces cravings.'),
('b76bf61a-7d5b-5bfd-b05f-a9a7c0b5ae7e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Tramadol acts by:', '["Weak μ agonist activity","Inhibiting serotonin and norepinephrine reuptake","Both A and B","Only μ agonist activity","Only SNRI activity"]'::jsonb, 'Both A and B', 'Tramadol has dual mechanism: weak μ opioid agonist activity AND inhibition of serotonin and norepinephrine reuptake, providing both opioid and SNRI-like effects.'),
('17a8827e-8f0d-5951-a42c-38cec887d94c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which opioid agonist has mixed agonist-antagonist activity?', '["Morphine","Fentanyl","Pentazocine","Hydromorphone","Oxycodone"]'::jsonb, 'Pentazocine', 'Pentazocine is a κ agonist and weak μ antagonist. It provides analgesia through κ receptors but can precipitate withdrawal in μ-dependent patients.'),
('91db683f-6889-50bc-9e7e-2ec09bc72db4'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following is a pure μ agonist?', '["Pentazocine","Butorphanol","Nalbuphine","Morphine","Buprenorphine"]'::jsonb, 'Morphine', 'Morphine is a pure μ agonist. Pentazocine, butorphanol, and nalbuphine are mixed agonist-antagonists. Buprenorphine is a partial μ agonist.'),
('4252133e-377e-5261-a727-7632aa364cef'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Naloxone differs from naltrexone in that:', '["Naloxone has longer duration","Naltrexone is available orally","Naloxone is available orally","Naltrexone is used for acute overdose","Naloxone is used for maintenance therapy"]'::jsonb, 'Naltrexone is available orally', 'Naltrexone is available orally and has a longer duration (24-72 hours) vs naloxone (IV, 30-90 minutes). Naloxone is for acute overdose; naltrexone for maintenance.'),
('148ce6ab-3eeb-5836-8acb-359c29c99f6d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Pethidine (meperidine) is rarely used because of:', '["Neurotoxic metabolite (norpethidine)","Hepatotoxicity","Nephrotoxicity","Cardiotoxicity","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Neurotoxic metabolite (norpethidine)', 'Pethidine is rarely used due to accumulation of norpethidine, a neurotoxic metabolite that can cause seizures, especially in renal impairment.'),
('566815ae-66d9-56a0-8350-9a4df267a328'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which opioid is preferred in patients with renal failure?', '["Morphine","Pethidine","Hydromorphone","Fentanyl","Codeine"]'::jsonb, 'Hydromorphone', 'Hydromorphone is preferred in renal failure as it has no active metabolites, unlike morphine (morphine-6-glucuronide accumulates).'),
('1de643de-64cf-5ca6-bf34-44792d285bc2'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Buprenorphine is a:', '["Full μ agonist","Partial μ agonist","μ antagonist","κ agonist","δ agonist"]'::jsonb, 'Partial μ agonist', 'Buprenorphine is a partial μ agonist with a ceiling effect for respiratory depression, making it safer than full agonists in overdose.'),
('94ffb4d7-05fd-561c-b9e7-32bfd579daaf'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Methadone is used for opioid addiction because it:', '["Has long duration of action","Is orally active","Prevents withdrawal symptoms","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Methadone is long-acting, orally active, and prevents withdrawal. Its slow onset and long duration prevent the euphoric rush of short-acting opioids.'),
('31c08692-8e03-50dd-ab29-a53a33c861f9'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which opioid is used as an antitussive?', '["Morphine","Codeine","Fentanyl","Hydromorphone","Oxycodone"]'::jsonb, 'Codeine', 'Codeine is used as an antitussive at low doses (10-20 mg). Dextromethorphan is also commonly used as a non-opioid antitussive.'),
('a95c0b75-1a06-5e06-ba29-24f6329e2070'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Loperamide is used as an antidiarrheal because it:', '["Does not cross the blood-brain barrier","Is a peripheral μ agonist","Both A and B","Is a central μ agonist","Is an antagonist"]'::jsonb, 'Both A and B', 'Loperamide is a peripheral μ agonist that does not cross the BBB in normal doses, providing antidiarrheal effect without central opioid effects.'),
('6a3a3fa0-9988-5bfd-9b92-c2e5a65a9bdf'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'The maximum respiratory depressant dose of opioids can be reversed with:', '["Small dose naloxone","Large dose naloxone","Any dose of naloxone","Naloxone cannot reverse respiratory depression","Flumazenil"]'::jsonb, 'Small dose naloxone', 'Small doses of naloxone (0.04-0.4 mg) can reverse respiratory depression without completely reversing analgesia. Large doses can precipitate acute withdrawal.'),
('ca7a5f1b-660b-5e39-8f6b-a7855512e407'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which opioid is most likely to cause histamine release?', '["Fentanyl","Morphine","Hydromorphone","Oxycodone","Methadone"]'::jsonb, 'Morphine', 'Morphine causes direct histamine release from mast cells, leading to vasodilation, hypotension, flushing, and bronchospasm. Fentanyl does not cause histamine release.'),
('4ecde9c0-6d14-5946-8495-125499f1c52c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Tolerance develops to which effects of opioids?', '["Analgesia","Euphoria","Respiratory depression","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Tolerance develops to most opioid effects including analgesia, euphoria, respiratory depression, sedation, and constipation. Tolerance to constipation is minimal.'),
('556db85c-d061-5fc9-90e2-3bf1c8efb7d2'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following is NOT a sign of opioid withdrawal?', '["Yawning","Lacrimation","Mydriasis","Pupil dilation","All of the above are signs"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above are signs', 'Yawning, lacrimation, mydriasis, rhinorrhea, piloerection, diarrhea, and abdominal cramps are all classic signs of opioid withdrawal.'),
('4a47bc97-5bcc-5cd6-b02a-fa628059e0e9'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Pethidine (meperidine) is contraindicated in patients with:', '["Asthma","Epilepsy","Renal failure","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Pethidine is contraindicated in asthma (mast cell degranulation), epilepsy (norpethidine lowers seizure threshold), and renal failure (metabolite accumulation).'),
('5d0efade-30bb-5877-ad4f-30d770b66c55'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Amphetamine acts by:', '["Blocking dopamine reuptake","Enhancing dopamine release","Inhibiting MAO","Both A and B","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Amphetamine blocks dopamine reuptake, enhances dopamine release, and inhibits MAO. It also increases norepinephrine and serotonin levels.'),
('5b15c851-7b17-51fe-9097-4eafb1ab733c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Methylphenidate is primarily used for:', '["Depression","ADHD","Schizophrenia","Bipolar disorder","Anxiety"]'::jsonb, 'ADHD', 'Methylphenidate is a first-line treatment for ADHD. It blocks dopamine and norepinephrine reuptake, improving attention and reducing hyperactivity.'),
('45f6df42-6f0a-526b-accf-5d1882e326dd'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Caffeine acts primarily by:', '["Blocking adenosine receptors","Enhancing GABA","Blocking dopamine receptors","Enhancing serotonin","Blocking NMDA receptors"]'::jsonb, 'Blocking adenosine receptors', 'Caffeine is an adenosine receptor antagonist (A1 and A2A), which increases alertness, reduces fatigue, and has mild bronchodilatory effects.'),
('504f1700-4cde-5516-a7e8-a992f6b7dacd'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Theophylline is used for:', '["Asthma","COPD","Neonatal apnea","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Theophylline (a methylxanthine) is used for asthma, COPD, and neonatal apnea. It acts as a phosphodiesterase inhibitor and adenosine receptor antagonist.'),
('db0c97b7-67f2-52e9-9a56-a1fb7921b182'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Modafinil acts by:', '["Blocking dopamine reuptake","Enhancing histamine release","Enhancing orexin","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Modafinil promotes wakefulness through multiple mechanisms including dopamine reuptake inhibition, histamine release, and orexin activation.'),
('5ad41e51-72ff-509c-ae0b-dd5325606a23'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which stimulant is used for narcolepsy?', '["Amphetamine","Methylphenidate","Modafinil","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Amphetamine, methylphenidate, and modafinil are all used for narcolepsy. Modafinil is often preferred due to lower abuse potential.'),
('247f85e9-1abe-5b61-ad15-dafbbb8b9b99'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Pemoline is rarely used because of:', '["Hepatotoxicity","Nephrotoxicity","Cardiotoxicity","Neurotoxicity","Pulmonary toxicity"]'::jsonb, 'Hepatotoxicity', 'Pemoline has been withdrawn from many markets due to risk of severe hepatotoxicity. It was previously used for ADHD and narcolepsy.'),
('d1cacb0a-45cb-53ec-8594-5fafbd10b42d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Cocaine blocks the reuptake of:', '["Dopamine only","Norepinephrine only","Serotonin only","All monoamines","GABA only"]'::jsonb, 'All monoamines', 'Cocaine blocks the reuptake of dopamine, norepinephrine, and serotonin. This leads to increased synaptic concentrations and CNS stimulation.'),
('4651ec07-5a66-5cb2-80db-773a0e1ddcc7'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'The therapeutic use of cocaine in medicine is as a:', '["Stimulant resulting in decreased functional activity","Local anesthetic and vasoconstrictor","Analgesic mediated by specific receptor activation","Antidepressant without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anxiolytic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Local anesthetic and vasoconstrictor', 'Cocaine is used as a topical local anesthetic (especially ENT procedures) due to its unique ability to cause vasoconstriction and provide anesthesia.'),
('5a00d3a4-30f8-5ce6-a310-dae5fb426669'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which stimulant is most likely to cause psychosis?', '["Caffeine","Methylphenidate","Modafinil","Amphetamine","Theophylline"]'::jsonb, 'Amphetamine', 'Amphetamine is most likely to cause stimulant-induced psychosis (paranoia, hallucinations) due to its potent dopamine-releasing effects.'),
('d7f3b724-6d09-5ed3-9da2-030c0d430de3'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Strychnine is a CNS stimulant that acts by:', '["Blocking glycine receptors","Blocking GABA receptors","Enhancing dopamine","Blocking adenosine","Blocking NMDA receptors"]'::jsonb, 'Blocking glycine receptors', 'Strychnine is a competitive antagonist at glycine receptors (inhibitory) in the spinal cord, leading to uncontrolled muscle contractions and convulsions.'),
('73254a67-b7e7-5d3b-bca6-7bf183a27b1a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Nikethamide is a respiratory stimulant that acts by:', '["Directly stimulating medullary centers","Enhancing dopamine release","Blocking adenosine receptors","Enhancing GABA","Blocking NMDA receptors"]'::jsonb, 'Directly stimulating medullary centers', 'Nikethamide is a direct-acting respiratory stimulant that stimulates the medullary respiratory centers, used for respiratory depression.'),
('3fed9d1e-0c9e-52c8-9cf7-9391bd2c5fbb'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following is a xanthine derivative?', '["Caffeine","Theophylline","Theobromine","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Caffeine, theophylline, and theobromine are all methylxanthines (xanthine derivatives) with CNS stimulant, diuretic, and bronchodilator properties.'),
('21ff0d7a-5bc2-5f70-8931-3f6b8125b249'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Dextroamphetamine is used for:', '["ADHD","Narcolepsy","Obesity (short-term)","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Dextroamphetamine is used for ADHD, narcolepsy, and short-term treatment of obesity. It is a potent CNS stimulant and dopamine releaser.'),
('65b3ea42-d3c5-5e21-bcc3-ac4d5a538be7'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'The main mechanism of tolerance to amphetamines involves:', '["Receptor downregulation","Depletion of monoamine stores","Hepatic enzyme induction","Renal adaptation","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Depletion of monoamine stores', 'Tolerance to amphetamines primarily involves depletion of dopamine and norepinephrine stores in nerve terminals, reducing their releasing effect.'),
('f23069f6-bd31-5558-9fe8-8a160ae3c2f8'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which stimulant is used as an antidote for sedative overdose?', '["Caffeine","Methylphenidate","Amphetamine","Modafinil","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Caffeine', 'Caffeine sodium benzoate was historically used as a respiratory stimulant for sedative overdose, though its use has largely been replaced by more specific agents.'),
('c292ce98-a9b7-50bb-9068-920349561852'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Donepezil acts by:', '["Inhibiting acetylcholinesterase","Blocking NMDA receptors","Enhancing dopamine release","Blocking serotonin reuptake","Enhancing GABA"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibiting acetylcholinesterase', 'Donepezil is a reversible, selective acetylcholinesterase inhibitor that increases acetylcholine levels in the brain, improving cholinergic neurotransmission.'),
('4842d803-ad90-5e7d-a89d-b8a4e43f3e37'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Rivastigmine inhibits which enzymes?', '["Acetylcholinesterase only resulting in decreased functional activity","Butyrylcholinesterase only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Both acetylcholinesterase and butyrylcholinesterase","MAO-A and MAO-B without affecting the primary tissue pathway","COMT leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Both acetylcholinesterase and butyrylcholinesterase', 'Rivastigmine is a pseudo-irreversible inhibitor of both acetylcholinesterase and butyrylcholinesterase (pseudocholinesterase), providing dual enzyme inhibition.'),
('992d1e63-b5ab-55fd-aaf1-38e37a0790c5'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Memantine acts by:', '["Inhibiting acetylcholinesterase","Blocking NMDA receptors","Enhancing dopamine release","Blocking serotonin reuptake","Enhancing GABA"]'::jsonb, 'Blocking NMDA receptors', 'Memantine is a non-competitive NMDA receptor antagonist that reduces glutamate excitotoxicity, protecting neurons from damage in Alzheimer''s disease.'),
('22c0459c-31ee-5077-a735-3e43a2624b2c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which Alzheimer''s drug is available as a transdermal patch?', '["Donepezil","Rivastigmine","Galantamine","Memantine","Tacrine"]'::jsonb, 'Rivastigmine', 'Rivastigmine is available as a transdermal patch, which provides steady drug levels and reduces gastrointestinal side effects compared to oral formulations.'),
('157b0b11-d688-5ce8-b318-2b17762c843b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Tacrine is rarely used because of:', '["Hepatotoxicity","Nephrotoxicity","Cardiotoxicity","Neurotoxicity","Pulmonary toxicity"]'::jsonb, 'Hepatotoxicity', 'Tacrine (the first cholinesterase inhibitor approved for AD) is rarely used due to significant hepatotoxicity requiring regular liver function monitoring.'),
('8c573e45-0c4c-5af7-96f3-6e1df2f8ca5f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Galantamine is a:', '["Selective AChE inhibitor resulting in decreased functional activity","Dual AChE and BuChE inhibitor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","AChE inhibitor and allosteric modulator of nicotinic receptors","NMDA antagonist without affecting the primary tissue pathway","MAO-B inhibitor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'AChE inhibitor and allosteric modulator of nicotinic receptors', 'Galantamine is a reversible AChE inhibitor AND an allosteric modulator of nicotinic acetylcholine receptors, providing dual mechanism of action.'),
('eb48d36e-49bd-5fd1-9654-dbadaef58545'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following is the first drug approved for Alzheimer''s disease?', '["Donepezil","Rivastigmine","Galantamine","Memantine","Tacrine"]'::jsonb, 'Tacrine', 'Tacrine was the first cholinesterase inhibitor approved for Alzheimer''s disease (1993) but has been largely replaced by safer alternatives.'),
('e9d9bac8-5c74-5aa3-a042-20e3260fce7a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'The main side effect of cholinesterase inhibitors is:', '["Nausea and vomiting","Headache","Insomnia","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Nausea and vomiting', 'Gastrointestinal effects (nausea, vomiting, diarrhea) are the most common side effects of cholinesterase inhibitors due to increased cholinergic activity.'),
('cc1c4df7-2e8a-50e7-9409-6d548b2ff002'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Memantine is used in combination with:', '["Donepezil","Rivastigmine","Galantamine","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Memantine can be combined with any cholinesterase inhibitor (donepezil, rivastigmine, or galantamine) for moderate to severe Alzheimer''s disease.'),
('8b746492-93bb-5d7c-8fc8-108e80aa9cc0'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'The cholinergic hypothesis of Alzheimer''s disease states that:', '["There is excess acetylcholine","There is deficiency of acetylcholine","There is excess glutamate","There is deficiency of dopamine","There is excess serotonin"]'::jsonb, 'There is deficiency of acetylcholine', 'The cholinergic hypothesis proposes that cognitive decline in AD is due to loss of cholinergic neurons and reduced acetylcholine levels in the cortex and hippocampus.'),
('eb2d334b-d2b6-5a18-b971-1a2bebf50fa1'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following can worsen symptoms of Alzheimer''s disease?', '["Anticholinergics","Antihistamines","Benzodiazepines","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Anticholinergics, antihistamines, and benzodiazepines all worsen cognitive function and should be avoided in Alzheimer''s patients.'),
('f19111a6-39a3-5e75-8ae1-61de7a2a29e4'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Aducanumab is a monoclonal antibody that targets:', '["Acetylcholine","Amyloid-beta plaques","Tau tangles","Glutamate","Dopamine"]'::jsonb, 'Amyloid-beta plaques', 'Aducanumab is a monoclonal antibody that targets aggregated amyloid-beta plaques, representing a disease-modifying approach to Alzheimer''s treatment.'),
('6a15b9ca-842b-5c23-906b-78dd35cf9daf'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Lecanemab is a monoclonal antibody used for:', '["Parkinson''s disease","Alzheimer''s disease","Huntington''s disease","ALS","Multiple sclerosis"]'::jsonb, 'Alzheimer''s disease', 'Lecanemab is an anti-amyloid monoclonal antibody approved for early Alzheimer''s disease. It targets soluble amyloid-beta protofibrils.'),
('ad17fab0-4021-537b-882c-f51288407c33'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'The side effect of cholinesterase inhibitors that can be fatal in patients with peptic ulcer is:', '["Nausea resulting in decreased functional activity","Gastric acid hypersecretion","Diarrhea mediated by specific receptor activation","Bradycardia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Insomnia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Gastric acid hypersecretion', 'Cholinesterase inhibitors increase gastric acid secretion, which can exacerbate peptic ulcer disease and potentially cause GI bleeding.'),
('177406d4-80af-5e1c-aa2a-db54fe207b6d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which cholinesterase inhibitor has the longest half-life?', '["Tacrine","Donepezil","Rivastigmine","Galantamine","All have similar half-lives"]'::jsonb, 'Donepezil', 'Donepezil has the longest half-life (70 hours) among cholinesterase inhibitors, allowing once-daily dosing. Rivastigmine has the shortest (1.5-2 hours).'),
('f42847f9-b9e5-550f-a9fd-ab10e78b353a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Cholinesterase inhibitors are effective in which stage of Alzheimer''s disease?', '["Mild","Moderate","Severe","All stages","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All stages', 'Cholinesterase inhibitors are approved for mild, moderate, and severe stages of Alzheimer''s disease, though efficacy is greatest in early stages.'),
('58427728-ea53-5af5-982b-b98c9ba1e15d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following is NOT used in Alzheimer''s disease?', '["Donepezil","Memantine","Levodopa","Rivastigmine","Galantamine"]'::jsonb, 'Levodopa', 'Levodopa is used for Parkinson''s disease, not Alzheimer''s disease. Donepezil, memantine, rivastigmine, and galantamine are all used for AD.'),
('0f0e1166-900e-5057-8b03-f4f905caf138'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'What is the primary neurotransmitter deficit in Alzheimer''s disease?', '["Dopamine","Serotonin","Acetylcholine","GABA","Glutamate"]'::jsonb, 'Acetylcholine', 'Acetylcholine is the primary neurotransmitter deficit in AD due to loss of cholinergic neurons in the basal forebrain (nucleus basalis of Meynert).'),
('82b58d7f-19c6-5033-87fe-d00242cbfb78'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'The dose of donepezil for moderate to severe AD is:', '["5 mg daily","10 mg daily","23 mg daily","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, '23 mg daily', 'Donepezil is started at 5 mg daily and can be increased to 10 mg daily after 4-6 weeks. The 23 mg dose is for severe AD.'),
('107335ad-0e34-587c-9f76-10df3c05d72c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which Alzheimer''s drug has a 2-week washout period due to long half-life?', '["Tacrine","Donepezil","Rivastigmine","Galantamine","Memantine"]'::jsonb, 'Donepezil', 'Donepezil''s long half-life (70 hours) requires approximately 2 weeks for complete washout, which is important when switching medications.'),
('279f3653-87c2-581f-aeb1-be9a68240fd4'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which inhalational anesthetic decreases renal blood flow?', '["Sevoflurane","Desflurane","Isoflurane","Nitrous oxide","All volatile anesthetics"]'::jsonb, 'All volatile anesthetics', 'All volatile anesthetics decrease renal blood flow due to direct renal vasoconstriction and sympathetically mediated effects.'),
('54862356-bb47-542b-85f6-3a44b5ee4e06'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which local anesthetic is used for spinal anesthesia in day-case surgery?', '["Bupivacaine","Procaine","Lidocaine","Chlorprocaine","Tetracaine"]'::jsonb, 'Lidocaine', 'Lidocaine is used for short-duration spinal anesthesia in day-case surgery due to its intermediate duration of action and rapid recovery.'),
('40219657-a6a0-55bb-879b-25160cd4631d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which benzodiazepine is available as a buccal formulation for seizures?', '["Diazepam","Midazolam","Lorazepam","Clonazepam","Alprazolam"]'::jsonb, 'Midazolam', 'Midazolam is available as a buccal (intranasal/buccal) formulation for management of acute seizures in children and adults.'),
('52e64e34-59c9-5c7a-8946-747f92073b79'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which antiepileptic drug is preferred in women of childbearing age?', '["Valproic acid","Lamotrigine","Phenytoin","Carbamazepine","Topiramate"]'::jsonb, 'Lamotrigine', 'Lamotrigine is preferred in women of childbearing age due to its lower teratogenic risk compared to valproic acid and carbamazepine.'),
('b1a8040c-c01c-50be-b4ac-356faf87424f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which antipsychotic is available as a long-acting injection every 3 months?', '["Haloperidol","Fluphenazine","Risperidone","Paliperidone palmitate","Aripiprazole"]'::jsonb, 'Paliperidone palmitate', 'Paliperidone palmitate (Invega Sustenna/Trinza) is available as a long-acting injection given every 1-3 months for schizophrenia maintenance.'),
('5413349c-408e-55c3-9ca4-8b2226fb39a1'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which SNRI is also approved for fibromyalgia?', '["Venlafaxine","Duloxetine","Desvenlafaxine","Milnacipran","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Duloxetine', 'Duloxetine is FDA-approved for fibromyalgia. Milnacipran is also approved for fibromyalgia in some countries.'),
('50918acf-2890-57b5-b910-075e6cb7dd65'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'The ''ceiling effect'' for respiratory depression is seen with which opioid?', '["Morphine","Fentanyl","Buprenorphine","Hydromorphone","Oxycodone"]'::jsonb, 'Buprenorphine', 'Buprenorphine, as a partial μ agonist, has a ceiling effect for respiratory depression, making it safer in overdose compared to full agonists.'),
('b769b5ca-cce7-54ca-b08a-21e755c88569'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Pharmacology', 'Which of the following is used for Alzheimer''s disease and has a dual mechanism (AChE inhibitor + NMDA antagonist)?', '["Donepezil resulting in decreased functional activity","Memantine accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Memantine + Donepezil combination","Galantamine without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Rivastigmine leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Memantine + Donepezil combination', 'Memantine + Donepezil (Namzaric) combination provides dual mechanism: AChE inhibition and NMDA antagonism for moderate to severe AD.'),
('8344e5de-3c19-518c-85c3-ad6bbf099bfe'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'NSAIDs exert their analgesic effect primarily by inhibiting:', '["Lipoxygenase","Cyclooxygenase (COX)","Phospholipase A2","Caspase-1","Myeloperoxidase"]'::jsonb, 'Cyclooxygenase (COX)', 'NSAIDs inhibit COX enzymes, blocking prostaglandin synthesis from arachidonic acid.'),
('e392aae4-e90a-5b9a-bf45-03c92fbbf5ad'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Which COX isoform is constitutively expressed in most tissues?', '["COX-1","COX-2","COX-3","mPGES-1","5-LOX"]'::jsonb, 'COX-1', 'COX-1 is constitutively expressed for gastric protection, platelet aggregation, and renal blood flow.'),
('26695e56-a9c6-548c-8ed7-e5f459698cf7'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'COX-2 is primarily induced during:', '["Basal conditions","Inflammation and tissue injury","Platelet aggregation","Gastric acid secretion","Renal vasoconstriction"]'::jsonb, 'Inflammation and tissue injury', 'COX-2 is an inducible enzyme upregulated by cytokines at sites of inflammation.'),
('7797599c-4989-50af-9cd1-c0d5950efa88'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'The antiplatelet effect of low-dose aspirin is due to:', '["Irreversible acetylation of COX-1 in platelets","Reversible inhibition of COX-2","Inhibition of thromboxane synthase","Enhancement of prostacyclin","Blockade of ADP receptors"]'::jsonb, 'Irreversible acetylation of COX-1 in platelets', 'Aspirin irreversibly acetylates COX-1 Ser-530, preventing TXA2 synthesis in platelets for their entire lifespan.'),
('b52ab63f-dafe-59f0-9210-4e127f4769c4'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Aspirin-induced bronchospasm in asthmatics is mediated by:', '["Increased leukotriene synthesis due to COX blockade","Direct histamine release","IgE-mediated hypersensitivity","Complement activation","Mast cell degranulation via IgG"]'::jsonb, 'Increased leukotriene synthesis due to COX blockade', 'COX inhibition shunts arachidonic acid to lipoxygenase pathway, increasing cysteinyl leukotrienes causing bronchospasm.'),
('47436e98-308e-53da-9b19-faf5018bb8ac'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'NSAIDs are contraindicated in the third trimester because they:', '["Cause neural tube defects","Cause premature closure of the ductus arteriosus","Cause maternal hemorrhage only","Are teratogenic in all trimesters","Cause fetal renal agenesis"]'::jsonb, 'Cause premature closure of the ductus arteriosus', 'NSAIDs inhibit PGs needed to keep the ductus arteriosus patent; closure can cause fatal pulmonary hypertension.'),
('0b4fbc2b-768e-5717-b5b0-d8164df1c6d2'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'The ceiling analgesic effect of NSAIDs is reached at:', '["Maximum tolerated dose","The dose that fully inhibits COX","Any dose above 4g paracetamol","The lowest effective dose","Doses causing renal toxicity"]'::jsonb, 'The dose that fully inhibits COX', 'Once COX is maximally inhibited, increasing dose provides no additional analgesia but increases toxicity.'),
('700567be-2a2c-580d-a8f5-d55d6c979071'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Ibuprofen differs from aspirin in that it:', '["Irreversibly inhibits COX","Is a reversible COX inhibitor","Is a prodrug","Has no antiplatelet effect","Causes more GI bleeding than aspirin"]'::jsonb, 'Is a reversible COX inhibitor', 'Ibuprofen is a reversible, non-selective COX inhibitor, unlike aspirin which irreversibly acetylates COX.'),
('400329a4-e3b0-56c6-b92a-d0bc0d7127a2'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Diclofenac is classified as:', '["Indole derivative","Anthranilic acid derivative","Acetic acid derivative","Enolic acid derivative","Salicylate derivative"]'::jsonb, 'Acetic acid derivative', 'Diclofenac is a phenylacetic acid derivative, related to indomethacin in structure.'),
('bff1dce0-ef5c-5e11-9e90-86fe3ecff268'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Mefenamic acid belongs to which class?', '["Acetic acid derivatives","Anthranilic acid (fenamate) derivatives","Oxicam derivatives","Salicylates","Para-aminophenol derivatives"]'::jsonb, 'Anthranilic acid (fenamate) derivatives', 'Mefenamic acid is a fenamate used primarily for primary dysmenorrhea and short-term pain.'),
('9765226c-057f-548f-a030-79d95966a981'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Which of the following NSAIDs is a prodrug?', '["Ibuprofen","Nabumetone","Diclofenac","Ketorolac","Aspirin"]'::jsonb, 'Nabumetone', 'Nabumetone is a non-acidic prodrug converted to active 6-MNA in the liver, reducing direct GI irritation.'),
('db6993e8-5fcd-5ef6-a8af-ad96faa36aef'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Flurbiprofen belongs to which chemical class?', '["Salicylates","Acetic acid derivatives","Propionic acid derivatives","Enolic acid derivatives","Anthranilic acid derivatives"]'::jsonb, 'Propionic acid derivatives', 'Flurbiprofen is a propionic acid derivative along with ibuprofen, naproxen, and ketoprofen.'),
('152f70cf-73da-5689-8ce1-6bfba0234c10'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Indomethacin is used clinically for:', '["Osteoarthritis only","Closure of patent ductus arteriosus","Gout prophylaxis","Migraine prophylaxis","Low back pain"]'::jsonb, 'Closure of patent ductus arteriosus', 'Indomethacin is used to close a hemodynamically significant PDA in premature neonates.'),
('6cae2818-b483-57b3-bbe7-9854f3267a33'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Sulindac is a prodrug whose main advantage is:', '["Greater potency than indomethacin","Lower risk of renal prostaglandin inhibition","No GI side effects","No effect on platelets","Longer duration than piroxicam"]'::jsonb, 'Lower risk of renal prostaglandin inhibition', 'Sulindac is a sulfinyl prodrug; its inactive form is not concentrated in the renal medulla, reducing renal PG inhibition.'),
('43fefc78-b618-51ed-b58c-51c95d5f77c0'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Pethidine (meperidine) should NOT be co-administered with:', '["Paracetamol","MAO inhibitors","Antacids","Iron supplements","Vitamin C"]'::jsonb, 'MAO inhibitors', 'Meperidine + MAOIs can cause fatal serotonin syndrome with hyperthermia, rigidity, and cardiovascular collapse.'),
('ce70efcb-230b-5a89-b272-18f423872d14'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Which NSAID has the longest plasma half-life suitable for once-daily dosing?', '["Ibuprofen","Diclofenac","Naproxen","Ketorolac","Aspirin"]'::jsonb, 'Naproxen', 'Naproxen has a half-life of 12-17 hours, suitable for twice-daily or once-daily dosing.'),
('61358bc8-5ff0-527e-849c-e30771df7a44'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'The main mechanism of antipyretic action of NSAIDs is:', '["Direct cooling of the hypothalamus","Inhibition of COX-2 in the hypothalamus reducing PGE2","Increased sweating via cholinergic stimulation","Vasodilation in skin vessels","Suppression of IL-1 production"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibition of COX-2 in the hypothalamus reducing PGE2', 'NSAIDs inhibit COX-2 in the hypothalamus, reducing PGE2 synthesis and resetting the thermoregulatory set point.'),
('b1c4617b-d3ec-5d69-b141-1b6828375a7d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Nimesulide differs from other NSAIDs by:', '["Being a prodrug","Having preferential COX-2 selectivity","Being an irreversible COX inhibitor","Causing no renal effects","Being an opioid agonist"]'::jsonb, 'Having preferential COX-2 selectivity', 'Nimesulide is a preferential COX-2 inhibitor with additional antioxidant and anti-histamine properties.'),
('feab4220-05ce-5b84-9869-cd129c3431b6'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Which NSAID is contraindicated in active liver disease due to hepatotoxicity?', '["Ibuprofen","Naproxen","Nimisulide","Aspirin","Celecoxib"]'::jsonb, 'Nimisulide', 'Nimisulide carries a higher risk of hepatotoxicity and is contraindicated in active hepatic impairment.'),
('a8599505-f490-5c53-82c6-058461e5f7f6'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Meclofenamate belongs to which group of NSAIDs?', '["Salicylates","Propionic acid derivatives","Fenamates (anthranilic acid derivatives)","Oxicams","Acetic acid derivatives"]'::jsonb, 'Fenamates (anthranilic acid derivatives)', 'Meclofenamate is a fenamate NSAID used for dysmenorrhea and acute musculoskeletal pain.'),
('ea3b57a1-0221-5c69-b4ec-8756da10866d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Ketoprofen differs from ibuprofen by:', '["Being a prodrug resulting in decreased functional activity","Having a longer half-life and additional lipoxygenase inhibition","Being COX-2 selective mediated by specific receptor activation","Having no anti-inflammatory effect without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Being an opioid leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Having a longer half-life and additional lipoxygenase inhibition', 'Ketoprofen has additional weak 5-LOX inhibition alongside COX inhibition and a longer half-life than ibuprofen.'),
('1dcb8583-874f-52c9-beac-899ab901d8a4'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Nabumetone is unique among NSAIDs because it:', '["Is a non-acidic prodrug causing less direct GI irritation","Is the most potent NSAID accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Is given IV only mediated by specific receptor activation","Has no COX inhibition without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Is used only for gout leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Is a non-acidic prodrug causing less direct GI irritation', 'Nabumetone lacks a free carboxylic acid group, reducing direct topical GI mucosal damage.'),
('7ad02b43-aa77-511b-8673-0e79b63b76dd'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Phenylbutazone is an NSAID that is:', '["Safe for long-term use in all patients resulting in decreased functional activity","Reserved for severe conditions due to risk of agranulocytosis and aplastic anemia","A COX-2 selective inhibitor mediated by specific receptor activation","Used only topically without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A muscle relaxant leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Reserved for severe conditions due to risk of agranulocytosis and aplastic anemia', 'Phenylbutazone carries risk of serious blood dyscrasias and is restricted to severe refractory conditions.'),
('8ab2b434-dd21-5ca9-a1b1-a8ef33048d46'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Celecoxib is a selective inhibitor of:', '["COX-1","COX-2","5-LOX","Phospholipase A2","NF-kB"]'::jsonb, 'COX-2', 'Celecoxib is a sulfonamide-based selective COX-2 inhibitor with lower GI toxicity than non-selective NSAIDs.'),
('e137cd45-391a-5d21-9127-6cc79300a600'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'The increased cardiovascular risk of COX-2 inhibitors is attributed to:', '["Increased TXA2 relative to PGI2","Decreased TXA2 with preserved PGI2","Balanced inhibition of TXA2 and PGI2","Increased platelet aggregation","Renal sodium retention only"]'::jsonb, 'Increased TXA2 relative to PGI2', 'Selective COX-2 inhibition suppresses PGI2 while leaving TXA2 intact, shifting balance toward thrombosis.'),
('6314908b-aa72-510a-a505-044362a0d5fc'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Rofecoxib (Vioxx) was withdrawn from the market due to:', '["Hepatotoxicity","Increased risk of MI and stroke","Renal failure","GI perforation","Severe skin reactions"]'::jsonb, 'Increased risk of MI and stroke', 'Rofecoxib was withdrawn after the VIGOR trial showed significantly increased risk of MI and stroke.'),
('0465a194-f0eb-5001-b83b-3f1080d78b2c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Which COX-2 inhibitor has a sulfonamide moiety contraindicated in sulfa allergy?', '["Rofecoxib","Valdecoxib","Celecoxib","Etoricoxib","Parecoxib"]'::jsonb, 'Celecoxib', 'Celecoxib contains a sulfonamide group and may cause cross-reactive allergic reactions in sulfa hypersensitivity.'),
('cf9421d3-6d6f-53fe-9908-77154001aded'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Etoricoxib is approved for use in:', '["Rheumatoid arthritis and ankylosing spondylitis","Fever only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pediatric pain mediated by specific receptor activation","Postoperative nausea without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Neuropathic pain leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Rheumatoid arthritis and ankylosing spondylitis', 'Etoricoxib is a highly selective COX-2 inhibitor approved for OA, RA, gout, and ankylosing spondylitis.'),
('4e03d7d9-6b37-555a-a923-8aaf735eae9e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Lumiracoxib differs from other coxibs by:', '["Being a propionic acid derivative with COX-2 selectivity","Being an opioid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Being a muscle relaxant mediated by specific receptor activation","Having no COX inhibition without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Being a prodrug only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Being a propionic acid derivative with COX-2 selectivity', 'Lumiracoxib is structurally related to diclofenac but highly COX-2 selective, though withdrawn due to hepatotoxicity.'),
('fca408c6-7c83-540f-9e23-31f416b8ce01'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Paracetamol (acetaminophen) is classified as:', '["NSAID resulting in decreased functional activity","Non-opioid, non-NSAID analgesic-antipyretic","Opioid agonist mediated by specific receptor activation","COX-2 selective inhibitor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Corticosteroid leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Non-opioid, non-NSAID analgesic-antipyretic', 'Paracetamol is a para-aminophenol derivative with analgesic and antipyretic but negligible anti-inflammatory activity.'),
('048a7436-fa32-5d16-babc-f2597a1d0f99'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'The mechanism of paracetamol involves:', '["Peripheral COX-1 and COX-2 inhibition resulting in decreased functional activity","Central COX inhibition and possible endocannabinoid system involvement","Irreversible platelet COX acetylation mediated by specific receptor activation","Inhibition of 5-lipoxygenase without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Blockade of NMDA receptors leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Central COX inhibition and possible endocannabinoid system involvement', 'Paracetamol acts primarily in the CNS by inhibiting a COX variant and may modulate serotonergic and endocannabinoid systems.'),
('b0585058-ff11-57da-b0fe-0ff1884ab7a0'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'The hepatotoxic metabolite of paracetamol in overdose is:', '["Paracetamol glucuronide","NAPQI (N-acetyl-p-benzoquinone imine)","Paracetamol sulfate","p-aminophenol","Paracetamol mercapturate"]'::jsonb, 'NAPQI (N-acetyl-p-benzoquinone imine)', 'NAPQI is a reactive metabolite formed by CYP2E1; in overdose glutathione is depleted causing hepatocyte necrosis.'),
('86434b20-02db-578f-93a7-38456eb4ea07'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'The antidote for paracetamol overdose is:', '["Atropine","N-acetylcysteine (NAC)","Flumazenil","Naloxone","Deferoxamine"]'::jsonb, 'N-acetylcysteine (NAC)', 'NAC replenishes glutathione stores, detoxifies NAPQI, and is most effective within 8-10 hours of ingestion.'),
('17db10d8-bb70-5298-87b6-7eb4c0aec49d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'At therapeutic doses, paracetamol is mainly conjugated by:', '["CYP2E1 oxidation only","Glucuronidation and sulfation","Direct renal excretion","Deacetylation","Reduction by gut flora"]'::jsonb, 'Glucuronidation and sulfation', '90% of paracetamol undergoes Phase II conjugation (glucuronidation and sulfation) producing non-toxic metabolites.'),
('b5e12260-93af-54f9-bae1-e06224b7d9f1'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Paracetamol is preferred over aspirin in children because:', '["It has greater anti-inflammatory effect","It does not cause Reye''s syndrome","It is a more potent antipyretic","It inhibits platelet function","It is more effective in viral infections"]'::jsonb, 'It does not cause Reye''s syndrome', 'Aspirin in children with viral infections is associated with Reye''s syndrome; paracetamol does not carry this risk.'),
('0f002bf5-75e7-5f28-8e60-116e57e14aee'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'The enzyme primarily responsible for toxic NAPQI formation from paracetamol is:', '["CYP1A2","CYP2E1","CYP3A4","UGT1A1","SULT1A1"]'::jsonb, 'CYP2E1', 'CYP2E1 oxidizes paracetamol to hepatotoxic NAPQI; this pathway dominates in overdose when conjugation is saturated.'),
('b8a9b538-6cd6-512e-8c06-a3fe11d15be0'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Paracetamol has analgesic but not significant anti-inflammatory action because:', '["It is rapidly metabolized in the liver resulting in decreased functional activity","It requires peroxidase activity for COX inhibition which is low at inflammatory sites","It does not cross the blood-brain barrier mediated by specific receptor activation","It is a weak base without affecting the primary tissue pathway","It is excreted unchanged leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'It requires peroxidase activity for COX inhibition which is low at inflammatory sites', 'Paracetamol is a weak COX inhibitor requiring high peroxide tone; inflammatory sites have high peroxide levels reducing its efficacy.'),
('27a5c568-beb3-52c4-bee3-2b8afbbfeb19'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Maximum recommended daily dose of paracetamol for an adult is:', '["1g","2g","4g","6g","8g"]'::jsonb, '4g', 'The maximum is 4g/day for healthy adults; lower doses (2-3g/day) for liver disease or chronic alcohol use.'),
('7a484c41-640c-5548-8102-638add6a1418'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Chronic alcohol consumption increases paracetamol hepatotoxicity by:', '["Inducing CYP2E1 increasing NAPQI formation","Decreasing glutathione stores","Impairing glucuronidation","All of the above","Only A and B"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Chronic alcohol induces CYP2E1, depletes glutathione, and may impair conjugation, collectively increasing hepatotoxicity.'),
('d08ac08f-9b33-56dd-b333-32d4eb204f87'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Paracetamol does not have significant anti-inflammatory effect because its active metabolite requires:', '["High peroxide tone at inflammatory sites which is absent there","Low pH environment accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","High albumin binding mediated by specific receptor activation","Active transport into cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Enzymatic activation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'High peroxide tone at inflammatory sites which is absent there', 'Paracetamol needs low peroxide environment for COX inhibition; inflammatory sites have high peroxides reducing its activity there.'),
('2fa72e46-ac34-5b39-86a5-0b2d51818a6b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'In paracetamol overdose, the hepatotoxicity pattern is:', '["Centrilobular hepatic necrosis","Periportal steatosis","Cholestatic jaundice","Granulomatous hepatitis","Autoimmune hepatitis"]'::jsonb, 'Centrilobular hepatic necrosis', 'NAPQI causes centrilobular (zone 3) hepatic necrosis due to high CYP2E1 concentration in that zone.'),
('3b08eef1-2a0c-54f7-9bcd-656e205728ee'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'The primary analgesic receptor for morphine is:', '["mu (mu) opioid receptor","kappa (kappa) opioid receptor","delta (delta) opioid receptor","sigma (sigma) receptor","NMDA receptor"]'::jsonb, 'mu (mu) opioid receptor', 'Morphine is a full agonist at mu-opioid receptors mediating analgesia, respiratory depression, and euphoria.'),
('8b6831f0-f7c8-573d-aa31-82b3dc1eb3bf'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Opioid analgesics produce analgesia by:', '["Blocking prostaglandin synthesis resulting in decreased functional activity","Hyperpolarizing neurons and reducing neurotransmitter release in pain pathways","Blocking NMDA glutamate receptors mediated by specific receptor activation","Inhibiting sodium channels without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Activating GABA receptors leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperpolarizing neurons and reducing neurotransmitter release in pain pathways', 'Opioids open K+ channels (hyperpolarization) and close voltage-gated Ca2+ channels reducing neurotransmitter release.'),
('1223756a-f98d-5e0d-b42d-bdd54665abcb'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Tolerance to opioid analgesic effects develops because of:', '["Receptor downregulation and desensitization","Increased hepatic metabolism","Development of antibodies","Renal clearance adaptation","Gut microbiome changes"]'::jsonb, 'Receptor downregulation and desensitization', 'Chronic opioid use causes receptor phosphorylation, internalization, downregulation, and uncoupling from G-proteins.'),
('70510971-e1c0-5e74-adab-15106ad70c87'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Physical dependence on opioids is characterized by:', '["Craving and drug-seeking behavior","Withdrawal symptoms upon abrupt cessation","Tolerance to analgesic effects only","Increased pain threshold","Hepatic adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Withdrawal symptoms upon abrupt cessation', 'Physical dependence manifests as withdrawal symptoms (anxiety, sweating, diarrhea, rhinorrhea, piloerection) on cessation.'),
('0bd6b76a-907a-5733-8b82-8a956cda5546'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Which is NOT a feature of opioid withdrawal?', '["Mydriasis","Yawning","Miosis","Diarrhea","Lacrimation"]'::jsonb, 'Miosis', 'Opioid withdrawal causes mydriasis (dilated pupils), not miosis (constricted pupils) which is a feature of intoxication.'),
('172a0d45-b094-5d83-ad07-74e2556cf1ae'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Cross-tolerance among opioids means:', '["One opioid can suppress withdrawal from another","Tolerance to one opioid confers partial tolerance to others","All opioids have identical potency","Opioids cannot be used together","Tolerance develops to side effects only"]'::jsonb, 'Tolerance to one opioid confers partial tolerance to others', 'Because opioids act on the same receptor family, tolerance to one provides partial cross-tolerance to others.'),
('896f1a0b-7818-550a-93df-c5c3ff2310ec'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Opioid-induced constipation occurs because opioids:', '["Increase GI motility resulting in decreased functional activity","Decrease peristalsis and increase sphincter tone via mu-receptors in the gut","Cause direct mucosal irritation mediated by specific receptor activation","Increase fluid secretion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Stimulate serotonin receptors leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Decrease peristalsis and increase sphincter tone via mu-receptors in the gut', 'Opioids activate mu-receptors on enteric neurons, reducing peristalsis, increasing sphincter tone, and promoting fluid absorption.'),
('dc291723-013c-597f-83a5-3f385495586a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Nausea and vomiting from opioids is mainly due to stimulation of:', '["Gastric mu-receptors resulting in decreased functional activity","Chemoreceptor trigger zone (CTZ) in the area postrema","Vagal afferents mediated by specific receptor activation","Cerebellar vestibular nuclei without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cortical vomiting center leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Chemoreceptor trigger zone (CTZ) in the area postrema', 'Opioids stimulate mu and delta receptors in the CTZ (area postrema) which lacks a complete blood-brain barrier.'),
('595621ef-25c2-5d4d-a5d2-e9bc9b04652e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'The most serious acute toxicity of opioid overdose is:', '["Seizures","Respiratory depression","Hepatotoxicity","Nephrotoxicity","Cardiac arrhythmia"]'::jsonb, 'Respiratory depression', 'Severe respiratory depression is the most dangerous acute toxicity leading to hypoxia, respiratory arrest, and death.'),
('8a587fd4-055e-5b14-b6be-dc02da1324ab'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Naloxone reverses opioid effects by:', '["Partial mu-receptor agonism","Competitive mu-receptor antagonism","Non-competitive receptor blockade","Inhibiting opioid reuptake","Enhancing GABA activity"]'::jsonb, 'Competitive mu-receptor antagonism', 'Naloxone is a competitive antagonist at mu, kappa, and delta opioid receptors, rapidly reversing respiratory depression.'),
('a4d944c1-ac78-5d00-8514-349e05e14dbf'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Naloxone has a shorter duration than most opioids, meaning:', '["A single dose is always sufficient","Repeated doses or infusion may be needed","It should never be used","It is only effective IV","It has no clinical use"]'::jsonb, 'Repeated doses or infusion may be needed', 'Naloxone''s half-life (30-90 min) is shorter than many opioids; re-sedation can occur requiring repeat dosing.'),
('03ad5822-7de3-5143-b85a-64d415541400'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'The primary analgesic effect of morphine is mediated at which spinal cord site?', '["Dorsal horn","Ventral horn","Lateral horn","Central canal","Anterior median fissure"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal horn', 'Morphine acts on mu-receptors in the dorsal horn of the spinal cord, inhibiting pain signal transmission from afferent fibers.'),
('f7bd882a-73a6-5cdc-a96b-0a4c6133f443'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Endorphins, enkephalins, and dynorphins are:', '["Exogenous opioid drugs","Endogenous opioid peptides","COX inhibitors","Corticosteroids","Local anesthetics"]'::jsonb, 'Endogenous opioid peptides', 'These are endogenous neuropeptides that bind to opioid receptors (endorphins to mu, dynorphins to kappa, enkephalins to delta).'),
('827748b4-0c24-52a1-832e-404878a20d18'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Morphine is a natural alkaloid from:', '["Cannabis sativa","Papaver somniferum","Erythroxylum coca","Atropa belladonna","Digitalis purpurea"]'::jsonb, 'Papaver somniferum', 'Morphine is the principal alkaloid of opium, extracted from Papaver somniferum (opium poppy).'),
('4e877e8c-1524-5951-af70-41e24b1e2d9c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Morphine''s main metabolites are:', '["Morphine-3-glucuronide and morphine-6-glucuronide","Codeine and norcodeine accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Heroin and 6-MAM mediated by specific receptor activation","Fentanyl and norfentanyl without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Methadone and EDDP leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Morphine-3-glucuronide and morphine-6-glucuronide', 'Morphine is glucuronidated to M3G (inactive/neuroexcitatory) and M6G (more potent analgesic than morphine).'),
('21a50eb9-edb7-56ef-8993-20f69b86875d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Morphine-6-glucuronide (M6G) is:', '["An inactive metabolite","A more potent analgesic than morphine","A partial agonist","Excreted only in bile","Responsible for constipation only"]'::jsonb, 'A more potent analgesic than morphine', 'M6G is a potent mu-receptor agonist that contributes significantly to morphine''s analgesic effect, especially in renal impairment.'),
('9afdaa1a-d4d7-527b-9583-091f0570d88b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Codeine differs from morphine by:', '["Being a more potent analgesic","Having a methyl group at position 3","Being a selective kappa-agonist","Causing less constipation","Being a pure antagonist"]'::jsonb, 'Having a methyl group at position 3', 'Codeine is 3-methylmorphine, a prodrug converted to morphine by CYP2D6; it is a weaker analgesic than morphine.'),
('81c4349e-1db5-5380-9960-3de968f2942d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Codeine''s analgesic efficacy depends on:', '["Direct action on mu-receptors","Conversion to morphine by CYP2D6","Anti-inflammatory properties","Calcium channel blockade","NMDA receptor antagonism"]'::jsonb, 'Conversion to morphine by CYP2D6', 'Codeine is a prodrug; CYP2D6 converts it to morphine which provides the analgesic effect.'),
('def6a484-d61e-5273-a23a-7b848578207f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'In CYP2D6 ultra-rapid metabolizers, codeine can cause:', '["No effect resulting in decreased functional activity","Excessive morphine formation leading to respiratory depression","Decreased analgesia mediated by specific receptor activation","Hepatotoxicity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Rash leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Excessive morphine formation leading to respiratory depression', 'Ultra-rapid metabolizers convert codeine to morphine excessively, leading to high morphine levels and respiratory depression.'),
('8e384c5b-90e0-522a-a739-8c829d4fab47'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Heroin (diacetylmorphine) is:', '["A natural opioid resulting in decreased functional activity","A semi-synthetic opioid with two acetyl groups","A synthetic opioid mediated by specific receptor activation","An opioid antagonist without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A partial agonist leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A semi-synthetic opioid with two acetyl groups', 'Heroin is diacetylmorphine, rapidly converted to 6-MAM and then morphine in the brain.'),
('746666e8-e9d5-5eb8-8088-887e37b67ec8'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Fentanyl is approximately how many times more potent than morphine?', '["10 times","50 times","100 times","500 times","1000 times"]'::jsonb, '100 times', 'Fentanyl is approximately 100 times more potent than morphine due to high lipophilicity and rapid CNS penetration.'),
('1d05fe34-dc5e-52a6-8b00-a8631e3c7c0c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Fentanyl is preferred in anesthesia because of:', '["Long duration of action resulting in decreased functional activity","Rapid onset and short duration with rapid redistribution","Lack of respiratory depression mediated by specific receptor activation","Anti-inflammatory properties without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Oral bioavailability leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Rapid onset and short duration with rapid redistribution', 'Fentanyl''s high lipophilicity allows rapid onset (1-2 min IV) and short context-sensitive half-time.'),
('caee7b1e-92b4-55b8-90d8-7b81aff040a6'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Sufentanil is:', '["Less potent than fentanyl","More potent than fentanyl","Equi-potent with morphine","An opioid antagonist","An agonist-antagonist"]'::jsonb, 'More potent than fentanyl', 'Sufentanil is approximately 5-10 times more potent than fentanyl for major surgical procedures.'),
('50e19bb0-f075-57c0-8e02-2b08f90b63c8'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Remifentanil is unique because it:', '["Has the longest duration of action resulting in decreased functional activity","Is metabolized by plasma esterases giving ultra-short context-sensitive half-time","Is a partial agonist mediated by specific receptor activation","Is given only orally without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Has no respiratory depression leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Is metabolized by plasma esterases giving ultra-short context-sensitive half-time', 'Remifentanil is hydrolyzed by non-specific esterases, giving a context-sensitive half-time of 3-5 minutes.'),
('59e11507-948a-58de-a4f4-8fd6a4bb4c27'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Meperidine (pethidine) has a:', '["Natural alkaloid structure","Phenylpiperidine structure","Benzodiazepine structure","Thiopental structure","Phenothiazine structure"]'::jsonb, 'Phenylpiperidine structure', 'Meperidine is a phenylpiperidine synthetic opioid; its metabolite normeperidine causes seizures with chronic use.'),
('b20fc814-c13c-5de5-8ed8-14674a204692'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'The main reason meperidine is rarely used chronically is:', '["It is too weak","Accumulation of normeperidine causing seizures","It has no analgesic effect","It is not available","It causes only GI side effects"]'::jsonb, 'Accumulation of normeperidine causing seizures', 'Normeperidine has a long half-life and accumulates, causing irritability, tremors, and generalized seizures.'),
('c5f8f93e-f88e-5e7a-bf34-93db673a0107'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Methadone is used in opioid maintenance therapy because it:', '["Is a short-acting agonist resulting in decreased functional activity","Has a long half-life and oral bioavailability preventing withdrawal","Is an antagonist mediated by specific receptor activation","Causes euphoria without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Is a partial agonist leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Has a long half-life and oral bioavailability preventing withdrawal', 'Methadone''s long half-life (24-36 hours), oral bioavailability, and slow receptor binding prevent withdrawal.'),
('ba86b575-bf72-5e90-a7d0-b7002f21fb9e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Oral morphine is the gold standard first-line strong opioid for:', '["Mild pain only resulting in decreased functional activity","Moderate-to-severe cancer pain in the WHO analgesic ladder","Neuropathic pain mediated by specific receptor activation","Fever without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Postoperative nausea leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Moderate-to-severe cancer pain in the WHO analgesic ladder', 'Oral morphine is the first-line strong opioid for moderate-to-severe cancer pain in the WHO analgesic ladder.'),
('cafb8baa-722d-5c32-a4e3-2460d4e7689d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Tramadol is unique because it:', '["Is a pure mu-agonist resulting in decreased functional activity","Has weak opioid agonist activity and inhibits norepinephrine and serotonin reuptake","Is a COX inhibitor mediated by specific receptor activation","Is an NMDA antagonist without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Is an antagonist leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Has weak opioid agonist activity and inhibits norepinephrine and serotonin reuptake', 'Tramadol has weak mu-opioid agonist properties and additionally inhibits reuptake of NE and 5-HT.'),
('17d836df-6ba9-5df7-8c51-77fddf7b016f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Tramadol''s M1 metabolite (O-desmethyltramadol) is:', '["Inactive resulting in decreased functional activity","A more potent mu-agonist than the parent compound","An antagonist mediated by specific receptor activation","A COX inhibitor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Excreted unchanged leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A more potent mu-agonist than the parent compound', 'M1 is formed by CYP2D6 and has 200-fold greater mu-receptor affinity than tramadol.'),
('f12bfc7b-c725-5673-8fb6-adc4a757c682'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Tapentadol differs from tramadol by:', '["Being a prodrug resulting in decreased functional activity","Having stronger direct mu-opioid activity and less serotonin reuptake inhibition","Being an antagonist mediated by specific receptor activation","Being a COX inhibitor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Having longer half-life leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Having stronger direct mu-opioid activity and less serotonin reuptake inhibition', 'Tapentadol has greater direct mu-agonist potency and primarily inhibits NE reuptake rather than serotonin.'),
('9447dfdc-061f-5351-8164-e231d106db37'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Pentazocine is classified as:', '["Full mu-agonist resulting in decreased functional activity","Mixed agonist-antagonist (kappa-agonist, mu-partial agonist/antagonist)","Pure antagonist mediated by specific receptor activation","Partial agonist only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Non-opioid analgesic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mixed agonist-antagonist (kappa-agonist, mu-partial agonist/antagonist)', 'Pentazocine is a benzomorphan mixed agonist-antagonist that activates kappa-receptors and partially antagonizes mu-receptors.'),
('cdc53444-026e-5ef5-8daf-8c42017b6abe'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Butorphanol is used clinically as:', '["Oral analgesic only resulting in decreased functional activity","Intranasal or IV analgesic with agonist-antagonist properties","Muscle relaxant mediated by specific receptor activation","Antiemetic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anticonvulsant leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Intranasal or IV analgesic with agonist-antagonist properties', 'Butorphanol is a kappa-agonist/mu-partial agonist available as nasal spray and injection for moderate-to-severe pain.'),
('379f369c-051a-5857-ab60-7af7ac01da0e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Nalbuphine is:', '["A pure agonist resulting in decreased functional activity","An agonist-antagonist with ceiling effect for respiratory depression","A pure antagonist mediated by specific receptor activation","A COX inhibitor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A local anesthetic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An agonist-antagonist with ceiling effect for respiratory depression', 'Nalbuphine is a kappa-agonist/mu-antagonist with a ceiling effect for respiratory depression.'),
('fb5f7714-d688-5dce-8caa-dd88e3aead85'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Buprenorphine is a partial mu-agonist used for:', '["Acute pain only resulting in decreased functional activity","Opioid dependence maintenance and moderate-to-severe pain","Fever mediated by specific receptor activation","Inflammation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Neuropathic pain only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Opioid dependence maintenance and moderate-to-severe pain', 'Buprenorphine is used in opioid maintenance therapy (Suboxone/Subutex) and for pain; it has a ceiling for respiratory depression.'),
('d6450b1a-a072-5b5f-a953-327d77efc68e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'The ceiling effect of buprenorphine means:', '["It has no maximum analgesic dose resulting in decreased functional activity","Beyond a certain dose respiratory depression does not increase further","It is always safe in overdose mediated by specific receptor activation","It has no side effects without affecting the primary tissue pathway","It cannot cause dependence leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Beyond a certain dose respiratory depression does not increase further', 'As a partial agonist buprenorphine reaches a ceiling for respiratory depression, making it relatively safer.'),
('8e87c35e-5c7f-54f9-9420-fbf4ee66abaa'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Which opioid is available as a transdermal patch?', '["Morphine","Codeine","Fentanyl","Tramadol","Meperidine"]'::jsonb, 'Fentanyl', 'Fentanyl transdermal patches deliver drug continuously over 48-72 hours for stable chronic pain.'),
('d9921a82-9006-5efe-9ebe-557c41a4ac32'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Fentanyl transdermal patch is NOT suitable for:', '["Cancer pain","Postoperative acute pain","Chronic non-cancer pain","Opioid-tolerant patients","Elderly patients"]'::jsonb, 'Postoperative acute pain', 'Fentanyl patches have a slow onset (12-24 hours) making them unsuitable for acute pain titration.'),
('53fbcceb-4540-5ad2-89a2-72e7669c5684'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Oxycodone is a semi-synthetic opioid that is:', '["A pure antagonist resulting in decreased functional activity","A full mu-agonist more potent than codeine","A partial agonist mediated by specific receptor activation","An agonist-antagonist without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A non-opioid leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A full mu-agonist more potent than codeine', 'Oxycodone is a semi-synthetic mu-agonist approximately 1.5 times more potent than morphine.'),
('e7915bf5-b523-5554-a096-fb31dc96cccc'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Hydromorphone is:', '["A natural opioid resulting in decreased functional activity","A semi-synthetic opioid approximately 5-7 times more potent than morphine","A weak agonist mediated by specific receptor activation","An antagonist without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A local anesthetic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A semi-synthetic opioid approximately 5-7 times more potent than morphine', 'Hydromorphone is a hydrogenated ketone of morphine, a potent semi-synthetic mu-agonist.'),
('24fab42f-5c10-5d96-89d0-728fdd1ec56e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Dihydrocodeine is:', '["A prodrug for morphine resulting in decreased functional activity","A semi-synthetic opioid used for mild-to-moderate pain and cough","An antagonist mediated by specific receptor activation","A NSAID without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A local anesthetic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A semi-synthetic opioid used for mild-to-moderate pain and cough', 'Dihydrocodeine is a semi-synthetic opioid roughly equipotent to codeine, used for moderate pain and cough suppression.'),
('b12c933f-1faf-50ea-b62a-a773caab3a2d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Oxymorphone is:', '["A weak opioid resulting in decreased functional activity","A potent semi-synthetic opioid with less histamine release than morphine","An antagonist mediated by specific receptor activation","A COX inhibitor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","An NSAID leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A potent semi-synthetic opioid with less histamine release than morphine', 'Oxymorphone is highly potent with less histamine release than morphine, useful in patients with bronchospasm.'),
('b20ea7f7-0bd7-5eec-b5ae-9c4b3fbff5bd'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Naltrexone differs from naloxone by:', '["Being shorter acting resulting in decreased functional activity","Being orally active with longer duration (24-72 hours)","Being a partial agonist mediated by specific receptor activation","Being a kappa-agonist without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Having no effect on opioids leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Being orally active with longer duration (24-72 hours)', 'Naltrexone is an oral mu-antagonist with 24-72 hour duration for opioid and alcohol dependence maintenance.'),
('4a9ce182-2f98-57e9-aff5-29edfd285842'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Nalmefene is an opioid antagonist that:', '["Is only available IV resulting in decreased functional activity","Has no hepatotoxicity and longer duration than naloxone","Is a partial agonist mediated by specific receptor activation","Is used for pain without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Is a prodrug leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Has no hepatotoxicity and longer duration than naloxone', 'Nalmefene is a long-acting opioid antagonist with no hepatotoxicity, available as IV and oral formulations.'),
('a3b12b08-628b-5aa2-919c-9359632bc798'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Which opioid antagonist is used to prevent relapse in alcohol dependence?', '["Naloxone","Naltrexone","Nalbuphine","Pentazocine","Butorphanol"]'::jsonb, 'Naltrexone', 'Naltrexone blocks the rewarding effects of alcohol by antagonizing endogenous opioid release.'),
('355dbbfd-bce5-5929-8ac9-084fc77cc953'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Naloxone is most commonly administered by which route in emergency settings?', '["Oral","Intramuscular or intravenous","Subcutaneous only","Rectal only","Inhalation only"]'::jsonb, 'Intramuscular or intravenous', 'Naloxone is given IM or IV in emergencies for opioid overdose; IV provides fastest onset (1-2 minutes).'),
('18545ce9-7fd0-5f9e-81e5-db5337e6ec22'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Naloxone can precipitate withdrawal in opioid-dependent patients because:', '["It blocks all opioid receptors without tolerance","It has partial agonist activity","It enhances opioid effects","It is a prodrug","It blocks only kappa receptors"]'::jsonb, 'It blocks all opioid receptors without tolerance', 'Naloxone is a competitive antagonist that rapidly displaces opioids from receptors, precipitating acute withdrawal in dependent patients.'),
('52bc67ab-d8fc-5830-b69c-cc839af3826a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Baclofen is a centrally acting muscle relaxant that acts as:', '["GABA-A agonist","GABA-B agonist","GABA-A antagonist","Dopamine agonist","Serotonin antagonist"]'::jsonb, 'GABA-B agonist', 'Baclofen is a GABA-B agonist that inhibits spinal cord reflexes, reducing spasticity.'),
('9bf561a1-f412-5b54-9043-2506d12d45df'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Tizanidine acts by:', '["GABA-B agonism","Alpha2-adrenergic agonism in the spinal cord","Direct skeletal muscle paralysis","Calcium channel blockade","Sodium channel blockade"]'::jsonb, 'Alpha2-adrenergic agonism in the spinal cord', 'Tizanidine is a centrally acting alpha2-adrenergic agonist that reduces spasticity by inhibiting excitatory amino acid release.'),
('6a70a712-1dca-5262-ba56-2bac13af0a7a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Dantrolene acts by:', '["Central GABA enhancement","Directly inhibiting calcium release from sarcoplasmic reticulum","Blocking neuromuscular junction","Blocking voltage-gated sodium channels","Inhibiting acetylcholine release"]'::jsonb, 'Directly inhibiting calcium release from sarcoplasmic reticulum', 'Dantrolene blocks ryanodine receptors on the sarcoplasmic reticulum, preventing Ca2+ release and muscle contraction.'),
('6d7fa945-ebb3-5c0f-83d7-750ac6149d68'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Dantrolene is specifically used for:', '["Chronic low back pain","Malignant hyperthermia","Spasticity only","Fibromyalgia","Headache"]'::jsonb, 'Malignant hyperthermia', 'Dantrolene is the specific antidote for malignant hyperthermia, preventing uncontrolled calcium release from SR.'),
('13fdd4f9-24a0-5f52-931a-6e7983c2afba'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Carisoprodol is metabolized to:', '["Aspirin resulting in decreased functional activity","Meprobamate which has anxiolytic and sedative properties","Morphine mediated by specific receptor activation","Codeine without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Naproxen leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Meprobamate which has anxiolytic and sedative properties', 'Carisoprodol is metabolized to meprobamate which potentiates GABA-A activity.'),
('e2cf0be4-badd-5b89-81cb-d92cf12024b5'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Orphenadrine is a muscle relaxant with:', '["Pure anticholinergic properties","Anticholinergic and antihistaminic properties","GABA-B agonist activity","Alpha2-agonist activity","Direct muscle relaxant activity"]'::jsonb, 'Anticholinergic and antihistaminic properties', 'Orphenadrine is a diphenhydramine analogue with anticholinergic and antihistaminic properties.'),
('6746caf2-93a7-56bc-ab0d-b393408d0b10'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Cyclobenzaprine is structurally related to:', '["Baclofen","Tricyclic antidepressants","Benzodiazepines","Opioids","NSAIDs"]'::jsonb, 'Tricyclic antidepressants', 'Cyclobenzaprine is structurally similar to tricyclic antidepressants and acts centrally on brainstem and spinal cord.'),
('e016a824-eb0f-5e72-b6e0-a29322c812c2'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Metaxalone absorption is enhanced by:', '["Taking it with antacids","Taking it with food (high-fat meal)","Fasting","Combining with alcohol","IV administration"]'::jsonb, 'Taking it with food (high-fat meal)', 'Metaxalone has poor bioavailability in fasting state; a high-fat meal increases its absorption significantly.'),
('394d7d4c-8f33-5103-8eda-3d1b891c77b4'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Methocarbamol is a centrally acting muscle relaxant that:', '["Is a carbamate derivative of guaifenesin","Is a GABA-B agonist","Blocks neuromuscular junction","Is a COX inhibitor","Is an opioid"]'::jsonb, 'Is a carbamate derivative of guaifenesin', 'Methocarbamol is a carbamate derivative of guaifenesin that acts centrally to reduce skeletal muscle spasm.'),
('fca869bc-c54a-57db-89fd-63cd31d61cfe'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Chlorzoxazone is a muscle relaxant that acts:', '["On GABA-B receptors","Centrally by inhibiting polysynaptic reflexes","Peripherally on the neuromuscular junction","By blocking calcium channels","By blocking sodium channels"]'::jsonb, 'Centrally by inhibiting polysynaptic reflexes', 'Chlorzoxazone acts centrally to inhibit polysynaptic reflex pathways in the spinal cord and brainstem.'),
('ae0afcf6-bffd-5ad0-ba99-ced5a309f9c5'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Sumatriptan is a selective agonist at:', '["5-HT1A","5-HT1B/1D","5-HT2A","5-HT3","5-HT4"]'::jsonb, '5-HT1B/1D', 'Sumatriptan selectively agonizes 5-HT1B (vasoconstriction) and 5-HT1D (inhibiting neurogenic inflammation) receptors.'),
('00fb358f-08f9-5f5d-b449-deb1434d3046'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Triptans abort migraine by:', '["Blocking prostaglandin synthesis resulting in decreased functional activity","Causing cranial vasoconstriction and inhibiting neurogenic inflammation","Blocking NMDA receptors mediated by specific receptor activation","Increasing serotonin levels without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Blocking dopamine receptors leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Causing cranial vasoconstriction and inhibiting neurogenic inflammation', 'Triptans constrict dilated cranial vessels (5-HT1B) and inhibit trigeminal CGRP release (5-HT1D).'),
('4f2d99ba-27bb-5799-b45f-e40bb361d819'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Absolute contraindication to triptan use includes:', '["Hypertension resulting in decreased functional activity","Coronary artery disease or uncontrolled hypertension","Diabetes mellitus mediated by specific receptor activation","Asthma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hypothyroidism leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Coronary artery disease or uncontrolled hypertension', 'Triptans cause vasoconstriction and are contraindicated in CAD, uncontrolled HTN, and stroke.'),
('4f6789ac-bc1e-5cd2-aefd-1e9e25beeb0f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Triptans should NOT be combined with:', '["Paracetamol resulting in decreased functional activity","SSRIs or MAOIs (risk of serotonin syndrome)","Antihistamines mediated by specific receptor activation","Corticosteroids without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Antiemetics leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'SSRIs or MAOIs (risk of serotonin syndrome)', 'Combining triptans with SSRIs, SNRIs, or MAOIs increases risk of serotonin syndrome.'),
('8a79f5f2-3b05-5ef0-908e-7614e5893ea6'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'The fastest onset triptan is:', '["Oral sumatriptan","Subcutaneous sumatriptan","Nasal zolmitriptan","Oral rizatriptan","Transdermal frovatriptan"]'::jsonb, 'Subcutaneous sumatriptan', 'Subcutaneous sumatriptan has the fastest onset (10-15 min) due to 100% bioavailability.'),
('04f6373b-e22b-5b52-b155-20bc9fe71cf6'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Frovatriptan''s long half-life (26 hours) makes it useful for:', '["Acute migraine only","Menstrual migraine prophylaxis","Chronic daily headache","Cluster headache","Tension-type headache"]'::jsonb, 'Menstrual migraine prophylaxis', 'Frovatriptan''s long half-life makes it suitable for menstrual migraine prophylaxis and prevents early recurrence.'),
('fa26c7a0-4b75-50a5-9ca0-66f98f98ebb7'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Rizatriptan ODT (orally disintegrating tablet) is useful because:', '["Faster absorption when nausea prevents swallowing","Improved bioavailability accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Reduced side effects mediated by specific receptor activation","Longer duration without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pediatric use only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Faster absorption when nausea prevents swallowing', 'The ODT dissolves on the tongue without water, useful when migraine-associated nausea makes swallowing difficult.'),
('dd36b591-dccb-5abb-81b7-8fa404591cd5'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Almotriptan has the advantage of:', '["Being the most potent triptan","Fewer drug interactions due to minimal CYP450 metabolism","Being available only IV","Being contraindicated in all patients","Having the longest half-life"]'::jsonb, 'Fewer drug interactions due to minimal CYP450 metabolism', 'Almotriptan is minimally metabolized by CYP450, resulting in fewer drug-drug interactions.'),
('a96fb5db-671a-586b-a5e6-fad14ee484a1'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Ergotamine differs from triptans by:', '["Being more selective for 5-HT1B resulting in decreased functional activity","Having longer duration and binding multiple receptor types","Being safer in CAD mediated by specific receptor activation","Having faster onset without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Not being vasoconstrictive leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Having longer duration and binding multiple receptor types', 'Ergotamine interacts with multiple receptors (5-HT, alpha-adrenergic, dopamine) causing more side effects.'),
('52d58065-3a92-5068-87fb-e412d1c43475'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Ergotamine is contraindicated in:', '["Migraine with aura resulting in decreased functional activity","Pregnancy, peripheral vascular disease, and uncontrolled hypertension","Tension-type headache mediated by specific receptor activation","Children only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Patients on paracetamol leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pregnancy, peripheral vascular disease, and uncontrolled hypertension', 'Ergotamine''s potent vasoconstriction contraindicates use in pregnancy (abortifacient), PVD, and CAD.'),
('c1f112b1-0560-557e-b53c-5ec166b43e9a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Ergotamine-induced ergotism manifests as:', '["Nausea and vomiting only resulting in decreased functional activity","Peripheral vasospasm, gangrene, and CNS symptoms","Hepatotoxicity mediated by specific receptor activation","Renal failure without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Aplastic anemia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Peripheral vasospasm, gangrene, and CNS symptoms', 'Ergotism presents with peripheral vasoconstriction (cold extremities, gangrene) and CNS symptoms.'),
('f9b7077c-37f9-5f81-b7d3-115d975fc108'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Propranolol is used for migraine prophylaxis at doses of:', '["10-20mg/day","80-240mg/day in divided doses","1-5mg/day","500mg/day","5mg once weekly"]'::jsonb, '80-240mg/day in divided doses', 'Propranolol is effective at 80-240mg/day in 2-3 divided doses, reducing attack frequency by >=50%.'),
('fc82dad0-d0c6-5170-99aa-27aa73358f02'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Valproate is used for migraine prophylaxis at doses of:', '["50-100mg/day","500-1000mg/day","2-4g/day","10-20mg/day","2000-3000mg/day"]'::jsonb, '500-1000mg/day', 'Valproate at 500-1000mg/day is effective for migraine prophylaxis but is contraindicated in women of childbearing age.'),
('ea3a91d4-a69f-526e-927b-f753798b4962'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Topiramate used for migraine prophylaxis has the additional benefit of:', '["Weight gain","Weight loss","Sedation","Hypertension","Insomnia"]'::jsonb, 'Weight loss', 'Topiramate causes appetite suppression and weight loss, which may be desirable in obese migraine patients.'),
('1aa30883-7361-5755-b268-11499867cc68'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Amitriptyline for migraine prophylaxis is particularly useful in patients with:', '["Hypertension resulting in decreased functional activity","Coexisting tension-type headache or insomnia","Coronary artery disease mediated by specific receptor activation","Hepatic failure without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Asthma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Coexisting tension-type headache or insomnia', 'Amitriptyline is useful in patients with coexisting tension-type headache, insomnia, or depression.'),
('e99d80e7-ba39-583c-be6a-72ca73dfc6fc'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Gabapentin for migraine prophylaxis works by:', '["COX inhibition resulting in decreased functional activity","Binding to voltage-gated calcium channel alpha2-delta subunit","Serotonin reuptake inhibition mediated by specific receptor activation","GABA-B agonism without affecting the primary tissue pathway","5-HT1B agonism leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Binding to voltage-gated calcium channel alpha2-delta subunit', 'Gabapentin binds the alpha2-delta subunit of voltage-gated calcium channels, reducing excitatory neurotransmitter release.'),
('d151bf1e-b1d2-5822-b984-dfd402fda9c5'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'CGRP antagonists (gepants) work by:', '["Blocking 5-HT1B receptors resulting in decreased functional activity","Antagonizing CGRP receptors preventing vasodilation and neurogenic inflammation","Inhibiting COX-2 mediated by specific receptor activation","Blocking dopamine receptors without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Enhancing GABA activity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Antagonizing CGRP receptors preventing vasodilation and neurogenic inflammation', 'Gepants block CGRP receptors, preventing vasodilation and trigeminovascular activation driving migraine pain.'),
('3b00bce8-7d1a-5b5c-8eda-ee95d2534317'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Erenumab targets:', '["CGRP ligand","CGRP receptor","5-HT1B receptor","TNF-alpha","IL-6"]'::jsonb, 'CGRP receptor', 'Erenumab is a monoclonal antibody that blocks the CGRP receptor, given monthly subcutaneously for migraine prophylaxis.'),
('c5a61868-c411-5953-aede-e821495578e0'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Fremanezumab differs from erenumab by targeting:', '["The CGRP receptor","The CGRP ligand itself","5-HT1B receptor","NMDA receptor","Voltage-gated sodium channels"]'::jsonb, 'The CGRP ligand itself', 'Fremanezumab binds and neutralizes the CGLP ligand, unlike erenumab which targets the receptor.'),
('53f9283c-15a5-587d-a2f5-e1715196fa00'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'OnabotulinumtoxinA (Botox) is approved for:', '["Episodic migraine","Chronic migraine (>=15 headache days/month)","Acute migraine attack","Cluster headache only","Tension-type headache only"]'::jsonb, 'Chronic migraine (>=15 headache days/month)', 'OnabotulinumtoxinA is FDA-approved for chronic migraine, injected at 31-39 sites every 12 weeks.'),
('87c365f5-8d4b-56b2-993b-02908061173a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Naproxen is effective in acute migraine because of its:', '["COX inhibition reducing prostaglandin-mediated inflammation","Opioid activity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Triptan-like mechanism mediated by specific receptor activation","Calcium channel blockade without affecting the primary tissue pathway","GABA enhancement leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'COX inhibition reducing prostaglandin-mediated inflammation', 'Naproxen inhibits COX reducing prostaglandin-mediated inflammation and pain in acute migraine attacks.'),
('bc6a65a8-7c57-5348-ac80-169e22557cb5'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Colchicine treats acute gout by:', '["Inhibiting uric acid production resulting in decreased functional activity","Inhibiting microtubule polymerization in neutrophils reducing chemotaxis","Increasing uric acid excretion mediated by specific receptor activation","Dissolving urate crystals without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Blocking COX-2 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibiting microtubule polymerization in neutrophils reducing chemotaxis', 'Colchicine binds tubulin, preventing microtubule assembly, impairing neutrophil migration and phagocytosis of urate crystals.'),
('834f19a6-aba4-5990-abaa-25a1fd9a51b2'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Colchicine is most effective for acute gout when given:', '["72 hours after onset","Within the first 12-36 hours of an attack","After the attack subsides","Only prophylactically","Intramuscularly only"]'::jsonb, 'Within the first 12-36 hours of an attack', 'Colchicine is most effective when initiated within 12-36 hours of onset; efficacy diminishes after 36 hours.'),
('71b7622b-442d-5461-ac2c-e5047c5b8a34'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Most common dose-limiting side effect of colchicine is:', '["Hepatotoxicity","Nausea, vomiting, and diarrhea","Nephrotoxicity","Bone marrow suppression","Cardiac arrhythmia"]'::jsonb, 'Nausea, vomiting, and diarrhea', 'GI intolerance (diarrhea, nausea, vomiting) occurs in up to 80% at higher doses and is dose-limiting.'),
('d390c194-5ce2-582a-af2a-0ad941c18ea2'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Colchicine is contraindicated with:', '["Paracetamol resulting in decreased functional activity","Strong CYP3A4/P-gp inhibitors (clarithromycin, ketoconazole)","Antacids mediated by specific receptor activation","Iron supplements without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vitamin D leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Strong CYP3A4/P-gp inhibitors (clarithromycin, ketoconazole)', 'CYP3A4 and P-gp inhibitors increase colchicine levels dramatically leading to potentially fatal toxicity.'),
('a239d615-7d6c-5296-8602-2b543488c16f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Allopurinol treats gout by:', '["Increasing uric acid excretion resulting in decreased functional activity","Inhibiting xanthine oxidase reducing uric acid production","Dissolving urate crystals mediated by specific receptor activation","Blocking neutrophil migration without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inhibiting COX-2 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibiting xanthine oxidase reducing uric acid production', 'Allopurinol is a xanthine oxidase inhibitor reducing uric acid production from hypoxanthine and xanthine.'),
('b2bc40cf-269d-5010-aa28-55bcd343dd3c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Allopurinol is a mechanism-based inhibitor of xanthine oxidase meaning it:', '["Competitively binds the active site resulting in decreased functional activity","Is converted to oxypurinol which irreversibly inhibits the enzyme","Binds allosterically mediated by specific receptor activation","Enhances enzyme activity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Is an allosteric activator leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Is converted to oxypurinol which irreversibly inhibits the enzyme', 'Allopurinol is converted to oxypurinol which tightly binds the molybdenum center, irreversibly inhibiting xanthine oxidase.'),
('d383e645-2966-528a-b6cf-3832048c9616'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'The active metabolite of allopurinol is:', '["Allopurinol riboside","Oxypurinol (alloxanthine)","Hypoxanthine","Xanthine","Uric acid"]'::jsonb, 'Oxypurinol (alloxanthine)', 'Oxypurinol has a longer half-life (28 hours) providing sustained xanthine oxidase inhibition.'),
('14440f48-bed1-53d9-9b9d-a3127705338b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Febuxostat differs from allopurinol by:', '["Being a purine analog","Being a non-purine selective xanthine oxidase inhibitor","Requiring renal dose adjustment","Having more drug interactions","Being less effective"]'::jsonb, 'Being a non-purine selective xanthine oxidase inhibitor', 'Febuxostat is a non-purine selective XO inhibitor not requiring dose adjustment in mild-moderate CKD.'),
('88dbf033-dab4-54cb-8f62-44b8f9e810e9'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Allopurinol hypersensitivity syndrome includes:', '["Mild rash only resulting in decreased functional activity","Severe hypersensitivity with fever, rash, eosinophilia, hepatitis, and renal failure","GI upset mediated by specific receptor activation","Headache without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Joint pain leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Severe hypersensitivity with fever, rash, eosinophilia, hepatitis, and renal failure', 'AHS is life-threatening with severe cutaneous reactions (SJS/TEN), eosinophilia, hepatitis, and nephritis.'),
('aaf76584-f617-5249-b472-0616b8d08484'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Allopurinol hypersensitivity is strongly associated with:', '["HLA-B27","HLA-B5801","HLA-B51","HLA-B15","HLA-B8"]'::jsonb, 'HLA-B5801', 'HLA-B5801 is strongly associated with allopurinol hypersensitivity; screening is recommended in at-risk populations.'),
('ccb11ce9-b34b-5353-b4c5-afef40ef47f0'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Lesinurad acts by:', '["Inhibiting xanthine oxidase resulting in decreased functional activity","Inhibiting URAT1 transporter in the proximal tubule","Increasing renal blood flow mediated by specific receptor activation","Dissolving crystals without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Blocking COX leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibiting URAT1 transporter in the proximal tubule', 'Lesinurad inhibits URAT1, increasing uric acid excretion; used with a xanthine oxidase inhibitor.'),
('af6e4312-7032-52d4-a730-055dffac1239'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Probenecid increases uric acid excretion by:', '["Inhibiting xanthine oxidase","Blocking tubular reabsorption via URAT1 inhibition","Increasing GFR","Dissolving tophi","Inhibiting neutrophil chemotaxis"]'::jsonb, 'Blocking tubular reabsorption via URAT1 inhibition', 'Probenecid inhibits URAT1 and OAT transporters, reducing uric acid reabsorption and increasing renal excretion.'),
('f6387956-9631-5586-acfe-5cfcbee8978d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Probenecid is contraindicated in patients with:', '["Hypertension resulting in decreased functional activity","Renal uric acid stones or renal insufficiency","Diabetes mellitus mediated by specific receptor activation","Peptic ulcer disease without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Asthma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Renal uric acid stones or renal insufficiency', 'Probenecid increases urinary uric acid which can precipitate nephrolithiasis, especially in renal impairment.'),
('f216ac14-24a4-5251-a9ee-baefa5012e9b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Benzbromarone differs from probenecid by:', '["Being less potent","Being more potent and effective even in renal impairment","Being a xanthine oxidase inhibitor","Being used for acute gout","Having no effect on uric acid"]'::jsonb, 'Being more potent and effective even in renal impairment', 'Benzbromarone is a highly effective uricosuric active even with mild-moderate CKD, though hepatotoxicity limits use.'),
('bde7db42-2b86-5fc3-8661-30b74b7bafde'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Pegloticase treats severe refractory gout by:', '["Inhibiting xanthine oxidase resulting in decreased functional activity","Being a PEGylated recombinant uricase converting uric acid to allantoin","Blocking URAT1 mediated by specific receptor activation","Inhibiting neutrophils without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Dissolving crystals directly leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Being a PEGylated recombinant uricase converting uric acid to allantoin', 'Pegloticase rapidly degrades uric acid to soluble allantoin, used for chronic tophaceous gout refractory to other therapies.'),
('3a30b6d3-541a-5475-90bf-b20c62129c75'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'During initiation of allopurinol, starting dose is low because:', '["It causes immediate pain relief","Rapid lowering of urate can precipitate acute gout flares","It has no side effects at low doses","Higher doses are unavailable","It is a controlled substance"]'::jsonb, 'Rapid lowering of urate can precipitate acute gout flares', 'Starting low and titrating slowly prevents sudden urate mobilization that can trigger acute gout flares.'),
('9476d347-c2bb-5a7d-b9be-0b132aa5c3fd'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Allopurinol dose adjustment is needed in:', '["Patients with hypertension","Patients with chronic kidney disease","Patients with asthma","Young adults only","Post-surgical patients"]'::jsonb, 'Patients with chronic kidney disease', 'Allopurinol and oxypurinol are renally excreted; dose reduction in CKD prevents accumulation and hypersensitivity.'),
('25d6c603-7826-5abd-8384-0d215a214131'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Colchicine is also used prophylactically for:', '["Hypertension resulting in decreased functional activity","Familial Mediterranean fever (FMF)","Diabetes mellitus mediated by specific receptor activation","Asthma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hypothyroidism leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Familial Mediterranean fever (FMF)', 'Colchicine is the first-line prophylactic agent for Familial Mediterranean fever, preventing acute serositis attacks.'),
('28dfd8d4-cac9-55b7-b503-5def44aa523d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Gout prophylaxis with allopurinol should be continued for at least:', '["1 week resulting in decreased functional activity","3-6 months after achieving target uric acid","24 hours mediated by specific receptor activation","1 year regardless of levels without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lifelong without monitoring leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '3-6 months after achieving target uric acid', 'ULT should be continued for at least 3-6 months after achieving target urate, often long-term to prevent flare recurrence.'),
('aabcfc26-af9d-5578-86a7-93385b8fe5e6'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'The target serum uric acid level for patients on ULT is:', '["<3 mg/dL resulting in decreased functional activity","<6 mg/dL (some guidelines <5 mg/dL)","<10 mg/dL mediated by specific receptor activation","<1 mg/dL without affecting the primary tissue pathway","No target exists leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '<6 mg/dL (some guidelines <5 mg/dL)', 'Target is <6 mg/dL (some recommend <5 mg/dL for tophaceous gout) to allow crystal dissolution.'),
('549b1aa9-eb6d-5ff5-b27e-cfea96192137'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'NSAIDs used for acute gout are effective because:', '["They lower serum uric acid levels","They inhibit prostaglandin-mediated inflammation at the joint","They dissolve urate crystals","They enhance renal uric acid excretion","They block xanthine oxidase"]'::jsonb, 'They inhibit prostaglandin-mediated inflammation at the joint', 'NSAIDs inhibit COX reducing prostaglandin-mediated inflammation and pain during acute gout flares.'),
('ebfc6f7d-89fe-5fc2-8557-f68c910f2b1a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Corticosteroids are used for acute gout when:', '["NSAIDs are contraindicated","Always as first-line","Only for prophylaxis","Never appropriate","Only in children"]'::jsonb, 'NSAIDs are contraindicated', 'Intra-articular or systemic corticosteroids are alternatives when NSAIDs and colchicine are contraindicated.'),
('1ddbd5b3-613f-53ae-9927-8fd40e88e756'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'A patient on warfarin starts allopurinol. The concern is:', '["Allopurinol enhances warfarin metabolism","Allopurinol inhibits the metabolism of warfarin increasing bleeding risk","No interaction exists","Allopurinol reduces warfarin absorption","Allopurinol has no anticoagulant effect"]'::jsonb, 'Allopurinol inhibits the metabolism of warfarin increasing bleeding risk', 'Allopurinol can inhibit the metabolism of warfarin, increasing INR and bleeding risk; monitoring is essential.'),
('1751d7ca-f401-5789-8b60-af31945c554c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Capsaicin topical analgesia works by:', '["Blocking COX enzymes resulting in decreased functional activity","Depleting substance P from sensory nerve endings via TRPV1 activation","Blocking voltage-gated sodium channels mediated by specific receptor activation","Inhibiting acetylcholine release without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Enhancing GABA activity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Depleting substance P from sensory nerve endings via TRPV1 activation', 'Capsaicin activates TRPV1 receptors causing initial excitation followed by desensitization and substance P depletion.'),
('398588d6-3741-5b3b-803e-0d13183a329a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'The initial burning from capsaicin is due to:', '["Allergic reaction","Activation of TRPV1 nociceptors","Histamine release","Complement activation","IgE-mediated reaction"]'::jsonb, 'Activation of TRPV1 nociceptors', 'Capsaicin binds TRPV1 (the heat and pain receptor), causing initial burning which subsides with repeated application.'),
('3b427a0b-aeb4-5caf-b4ca-7d7f7c3f6509'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Lidocaine 5% patch is used for:', '["Muscle spasm resulting in decreased functional activity","Localized neuropathic pain (e.g. postherpetic neuralgia)","Osteoarthritis mediated by specific receptor activation","Fever without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Migraine leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Localized neuropathic pain (e.g. postherpetic neuralgia)', 'Lidocaine 5% patches provide localized sodium channel blockade in painful areas, particularly effective for PHN.'),
('9cb13375-140c-5ccb-8dc6-8d94d6b1d49e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Topical lidocaine mechanism is:', '["COX inhibition resulting in decreased functional activity","Blockade of voltage-gated sodium channels in peripheral nociceptors","Substance P depletion mediated by specific receptor activation","GABA enhancement without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Calcium channel blockade leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Blockade of voltage-gated sodium channels in peripheral nociceptors', 'Lidocaine blocks voltage-gated sodium channels in peripheral nerves, preventing nociceptive signal transmission.'),
('c1b5e3a9-be7f-590d-9004-a532f1a4abd8'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Menthol produces cooling sensation by:', '["Activating TRPV1 (heat receptor) resulting in decreased functional activity","Activating TRPM8 (cold receptor) on sensory neurons","Blocking COX mediated by specific receptor activation","Depleting substance P without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Blocking sodium channels leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Activating TRPM8 (cold receptor) on sensory neurons', 'Menthol activates TRPM8 channels (cold-sensitive) producing a cooling sensation that counteracts pain signals.'),
('db287b80-a835-540c-92ab-6d91472cf607'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Topical NSAIDs are advantageous over oral NSAIDs because:', '["They are more potent resulting in decreased functional activity","They provide local concentration with minimal systemic side effects","They work faster mediated by specific receptor activation","They are cheaper without affecting the primary tissue pathway","They can be used in pregnancy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'They provide local concentration with minimal systemic side effects', 'Topical NSAIDs achieve therapeutic concentrations locally with minimal GI/renal systemic side effects.'),
('de1acc59-a69c-5b87-8d6f-62e26dfcf7d9'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Diflunisal differs from aspirin by:', '["Being a salicylate resulting in decreased functional activity","Being a difluorophenyl derivative of salicylic acid that does not cause salicylism","Being a prodrug mediated by specific receptor activation","Being an opioid without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Having no anti-inflammatory effect leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Being a difluorophenyl derivative of salicylic acid that does not cause salicylism', 'Diflunisal is a difluorophenyl salicylate that does not undergo hepatic conjugation like salicylate, with longer duration.'),
('a3ffbcb3-8d05-5a2c-b579-09b126d6af12'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Aspirin (acetylsalicylic acid) differs from salicylic acid by:', '["Being less potent resulting in decreased functional activity","Having an acetyl group that enables irreversible COX inhibition and antiplatelet effect","Being a prodrug mediated by specific receptor activation","Having no anti-inflammatory effect without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Being used only topically leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Having an acetyl group that enables irreversible COX inhibition and antiplatelet effect', 'The acetyl group enables irreversible acetylation of COX-1, giving aspirin its unique antiplatelet property.'),
('97e30f29-8520-5af8-b03d-859e4f8bc55b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'EMLA cream contains:', '["Ephedrine, Morphine, Lidocaine, Adrenaline","Eutectic mixture of Lidocaine and Prilocaine","Epinephrine, Metronidazole, Lidocaine, Aspirin","Exogenous Morphine, Lidocaine, Adrenaline","Ethyl Methacrylate, Lidocaine, Prilocaine"]'::jsonb, 'Eutectic mixture of Lidocaine and Prilocaine', 'EMLA is a eutectic mixture of 2.5% lidocaine and 2.5% prilocaine providing topical anaesthesia for procedures.'),
('dd131f45-0262-5ef4-9c45-51a3defa5778'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Salicylic acid topical preparations are used for:', '["Acute pain resulting in decreased functional activity","Keratolytic action in warts, calluses, and psoriasis","Fever reduction mediated by specific receptor activation","Gout prophylaxis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Migraine treatment leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Keratolytic action in warts, calluses, and psoriasis', 'Salicylic acid has keratolytic properties that soften and remove excess keratin in dermatological conditions.'),
('fadbbe8f-f38d-577d-aaad-f052eea11430'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Pregabalin is used for neuropathic pain and acts by:', '["COX inhibition resulting in decreased functional activity","Binding to alpha2-delta subunit of voltage-gated calcium channels","Blocking sodium channels mediated by specific receptor activation","Inhibiting serotonin reuptake without affecting the primary tissue pathway","GABA-B agonism leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Binding to alpha2-delta subunit of voltage-gated calcium channels', 'Pregabalin binds alpha2-delta subunit of voltage-gated calcium channels reducing excitatory neurotransmitter release.'),
('605f538b-46a8-55bc-aaae-a031d27f9181'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Pregabalin differs from gabapentin by:', '["Being less potent resulting in decreased functional activity","Having better bioavailability and more predictable dose-response relationship","Being an opioid mediated by specific receptor activation","Being a COX inhibitor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Being a prodrug leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Having better bioavailability and more predictable dose-response relationship', 'Pregabalin has linear pharmacokinetics with >90% bioavailability compared to gabapentin''s saturable absorption.'),
('9068ee23-1252-5f1e-bc7d-d8bec2bade2f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Duloxetine for neuropathic pain works by:', '["COX inhibition resulting in decreased functional activity","Inhibiting both serotonin and norepinephrine reuptake","GABA-B agonism mediated by specific receptor activation","Blocking sodium channels without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mu-opioid agonism leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibiting both serotonin and norepinephrine reuptake', 'Duloxetine is an SNRI that enhances descending inhibitory pain pathways via 5-HT and NE reuptake inhibition.'),
('69750444-bbc8-5e49-ac2e-124777352f0f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Amitriptyline''s analgesic effect in neuropathic pain is independent of:', '["Its antidepressant effect","Its norepinephrine reuptake inhibition","Its sodium channel blocking property","Its anticholinergic effect","Its analgesic effect"]'::jsonb, 'Its anticholinergic effect', 'Amitriptyline''s analgesic effect is partly due to NE reuptake inhibition and sodium channel blockade, independent of anticholinergic properties.'),
('338a23b2-465c-5036-8d0e-3db1d13cd0a7'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Etoricoxib is a selective COX-2 inhibitor that:', '["Has no GI side effects resulting in decreased functional activity","Has a long half-life (22 hours) and high COX-2 selectivity","Is an opioid mediated by specific receptor activation","Is used for neuropathic pain only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Is a prodrug leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Has a long half-life (22 hours) and high COX-2 selectivity', 'Etoricoxib is a highly selective COX-2 inhibitor with long duration allowing once-daily dosing.'),
('3a74f73f-45e9-5d8a-964d-796528426645'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Aceclofenac differs from diclofenac by:', '["Being less effective resulting in decreased functional activity","Having preferential COX-2 inhibition with less GI toxicity","Being a prodrug mediated by specific receptor activation","Being an opioid without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Being used only topically leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Having preferential COX-2 inhibition with less GI toxicity', 'Aceclofenac has preferential COX-2 selectivity and less GI toxicity compared to diclofenac.'),
('4dfc84c8-3d03-5816-afde-216ab93f5eb5'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Ziconotide is a non-opioid analgesic that acts by:', '["Blocking N-type voltage-gated calcium channels in the spinal cord","Inhibiting COX accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Blocking sodium channels mediated by specific receptor activation","GABA enhancement without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mu-opioid agonism leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Blocking N-type voltage-gated calcium channels in the spinal cord', 'Ziconotide is an intrathecal N-type calcium channel blocker derived from cone snail venom, used for severe chronic pain.'),
('33060a92-0618-5220-a69a-ffe7bea12da9'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Capsaicin high-concentration (8%) patches (Qutenza) are applied by:', '["The patient at home daily resulting in decreased functional activity","Healthcare professional for 30-60 minutes every 3 months","Oral administration mediated by specific receptor activation","Intramuscular injection without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Continuous infusion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Healthcare professional for 30-60 minutes every 3 months', 'Qutenza 8% capsaicin patches are applied in-office by healthcare professionals for 30-60 min every 3 months for PHN.'),
('71182867-3e08-55b2-add9-0959a466dccd'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Nefopam is a non-opioid, non-NSAID analgesic that acts by:', '["Inhibiting serotonin and norepinephrine reuptake and NMDA receptors","Blocking COX enzymes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mu-opioid agonism mediated by specific receptor activation","Blocking sodium channels without affecting the primary tissue pathway","GABA enhancement leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibiting serotonin and norepinephrine reuptake and NMDA receptors', 'Nefopam inhibits 5-HT and NE reuptake and NMDA receptors, used for acute pain without GI or renal toxicity of NSAIDs.'),
('bbadf690-2f2d-57e2-97a4-0a5d795e4a4e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Paracetamol combination with codeine is used for:', '["Severe cancer pain resulting in decreased functional activity","Mild-to-moderate pain not responding to paracetamol alone","Neuropathic pain mediated by specific receptor activation","Gout without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Migraine prophylaxis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mild-to-moderate pain not responding to paracetamol alone', 'Codeine-paracetamol combinations provide enhanced analgesia for mild-to-moderate pain through dual mechanisms.'),
('902d5878-aae4-5197-8085-0db9786367b2'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Paracetamol combination with tramadol provides:', '["No additional benefit","Additive analgesia for moderate pain","Antipyretic synergy only","Muscle relaxation","Anti-inflammatory effect"]'::jsonb, 'Additive analgesia for moderate pain', 'The combination provides additive analgesia through different mechanisms: central COX inhibition plus weak opioid agonism.'),
('d6d37d43-47a7-52b4-b114-5cbebd76cff1'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Ketamine is used as an adjunct analgesic in:', '["Only veterinary medicine resulting in decreased functional activity","Refractory pain, especially neuropathic pain, via NMDA receptor antagonism","Postoperative nausea mediated by specific receptor activation","Hypertension without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Diabetes management leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Refractory pain, especially neuropathic pain, via NMDA receptor antagonism', 'Ketamine is an NMDA antagonist useful in refractory pain states, preventing central sensitization and wind-up phenomenon.'),
('288aad83-8cb1-5e67-abb3-fffade4f971f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Magnesium sulfate has analgesic properties through:', '["COX inhibition","NMDA receptor antagonism","Mu-opioid agonism","GABA enhancement","Sodium channel blockade"]'::jsonb, 'NMDA receptor antagonism', 'Magnesium blocks NMDA receptors in the spinal cord, reducing central sensitization in chronic pain states.'),
('09e8f749-9086-5120-94b4-c2d7925e69f3'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Dextromethorphan has analgesic adjunct properties because it:', '["Is an opioid agonist","Is an NMDA receptor antagonist","Is a COX inhibitor","Is a muscle relaxant","Is an anticonvulsant"]'::jsonb, 'Is an NMDA receptor antagonist', 'Dextromethorphan is an NMDA antagonist that may reduce central sensitization when used as analgesic adjunct.'),
('4c385271-e130-5d9f-82d8-9cd1cb7812e8'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Salsalate differs from aspirin by:', '["Being more potent resulting in decreased functional activity","Being a non-acetylated salicylate with less antiplatelet effect","Being a prodrug for paracetamol mediated by specific receptor activation","Being an opioid without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Being used only topically leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Being a non-acetylated salicylate with less antiplatelet effect', 'Salsalate is a non-acetylated salicylate that does not inhibit platelet COX-1, reducing bleeding risk.'),
('8d8ed73a-ede3-5dfa-855f-0be6b2387c60'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'The prodrug form of sulindac is converted to active sulfide by:', '["Hepatic reduction in the liver","Gut bacteria only","Renal metabolism","Spontaneous hydrolysis","Plasma esterases"]'::jsonb, 'Hepatic reduction in the liver', 'Sulindac is reduced hepatically to its active sulfide metabolite which inhibits COX at inflammatory sites.'),
('42f95e02-86b6-5c39-9990-1a28c2fdd54f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Naproxen has the lowest cardiovascular risk among NSAIDs because:', '["It is COX-2 selective resulting in decreased functional activity","Sustained COX-2 inhibition balances TXA2 and PGI2","It has no COX-1 effect mediated by specific receptor activation","It is a prodrug without affecting the primary tissue pathway","It has different metabolism leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sustained COX-2 inhibition balances TXA2 and PGI2', 'Naproxen''s long half-life provides consistent COX-2 inhibition, maintaining a favorable TXA2/PGI2 balance.'),
('96f6451c-2318-526c-b944-d8502d355eea'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Which opioid is used for opioid withdrawal management in maintenance therapy?', '["Morphine resulting in decreased functional activity","Methadone or buprenorphine","Codeine mediated by specific receptor activation","Fentanyl without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pethidine leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Methadone or buprenorphine', 'Methadone (full agonist) and buprenorphine (partial agonist) are used in opioid maintenance therapy for addiction treatment.'),
('6d058b41-b119-5bfe-ba67-fcc28ec4b5db'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'The WHO analgesic ladder for cancer pain recommends:', '["Step 1: Weak opioid only resulting in decreased functional activity","Step 1: Non-opioid, Step 2: Weak opioid, Step 3: Strong opioid","Step 1: Strong opioid, Step 2: Surgery mediated by specific receptor activation","All patients start on morphine without affecting the primary tissue pathway","NSAIDs are never used leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Step 1: Non-opioid, Step 2: Weak opioid, Step 3: Strong opioid', 'The WHO ladder progresses from non-opioid to weak opioid to strong opioid based on pain severity.'),
('e969405e-09c8-5bf6-a459-bb4fac87a2fd'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Leflunomide (DMARD) inhibits:', '["DHODH","DHFR","JAK enzymes","TNF-alpha","IL-6"]'::jsonb, 'DHODH', 'Leflunomide blocks de novo pyrimidine synthesis in activated lymphocytes by inhibiting DHODH.'),
('336e4179-553e-50cc-a28f-2a362cde73b6'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Capsaicin cream (0.025-0.075%) provides pain relief through:', '["Blocking voltage-gated sodium channels resulting in decreased functional activity","Depleting substance P from peripheral sensory neurons via TRPV1","Inhibiting COX enzymes mediated by specific receptor activation","Blocking calcium channels without affecting the primary tissue pathway","GABA enhancement leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Depleting substance P from peripheral sensory neurons via TRPV1', 'Repeated capsaicin application depletes substance P from C-fiber nociceptors, reducing pain transmission.'),
('3d9bc4f1-d722-5d4e-9505-d2f70aa6e434'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Which enzyme converts arachidonic acid to prostaglandins?', '["Lipoxygenase","Cyclooxygenase","Phospholipase A2","Caspase-1","Myeloperoxidase"]'::jsonb, 'Cyclooxygenase', 'Cyclooxygenase (COX) converts arachidonic acid (released by PLA2) to PGH2, the precursor of all prostaglandins.'),
('871ba39d-4f00-54f7-ad01-8bc4902635d6'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Corticosteroids reduce inflammation by:', '["Inhibiting COX-2 directly","Inducing lipocortin-1 which inhibits phospholipase A2","Blocking histamine receptors","Inhibiting serotonin reuptake","Blocking NMDA receptors"]'::jsonb, 'Inducing lipocortin-1 which inhibits phospholipase A2', 'Corticosteroids induce lipocortin-1 (annexin A1) which inhibits PLA2, blocking arachidonic acid release at the earliest step.'),
('8a044e2e-2842-5ccf-a43d-3496de02cf42'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Flurbiprofen used in migraine prophylaxis acts by:', '["Blocking 5-HT receptors resulting in decreased functional activity","COX inhibition reducing prostaglandin-mediated neurogenic inflammation","NMDA antagonism mediated by specific receptor activation","GABA enhancement without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Calcium channel blockade leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'COX inhibition reducing prostaglandin-mediated neurogenic inflammation', 'Flurbiprofen inhibits COX reducing prostaglandin-mediated neurogenic inflammation in trigeminovascular pathways.'),
('3657f700-2fef-55c2-bd5c-03fc769481db'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Zolmitriptan nasal spray provides rapid migraine relief because:', '["It bypasses first-pass metabolism for faster systemic absorption","It directly blocks COX in the brain accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","It dissolves urate crystals mediated by specific receptor activation","It relaxes smooth muscle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","It blocks dopamine receptors leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'It bypasses first-pass metabolism for faster systemic absorption', 'Nasal absorption bypasses first-pass metabolism and GI absorption delay, providing rapid systemic availability.'),
('cbccf6e8-ac24-5f1f-b05a-2da5649ef60c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Naratriptan has slower onset than sumatriptan because:', '["It is a weaker 5-HT1B agonist","It has slower absorption and lower lipophilicity","It is only available IV","It is an antagonist","It works on different receptors"]'::jsonb, 'It has slower absorption and lower lipophilicity', 'Naratriptan has slower absorption and somewhat lower lipophilicity compared to sumatriptan, resulting in delayed onset.'),
('b53748d9-669c-56c3-8de5-28593001353b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Aprepitant is an antiemetic that works by:', '["Blocking 5-HT3 receptors","Blocking NK1 (substance P) receptors in the CTZ","Blocking dopamine receptors","Blocking histamine receptors","Inhibiting COX"]'::jsonb, 'Blocking NK1 (substance P) receptors in the CTZ', 'Aprepitant is an NK1 receptor antagonist that blocks substance P in the area postrema, effective for chemotherapy-induced emesis.'),
('6df506b9-562c-5cad-9e74-068a1882cc5e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Pantoprazole co-prescribed with NSAIDs reduces:', '["NSAID efficacy","NSAID-induced gastric ulcer risk","Renal toxicity of NSAIDs","Hepatic metabolism of NSAIDs","Platelet inhibition by NSAIDs"]'::jsonb, 'NSAID-induced gastric ulcer risk', 'PPIs like pantoprazole reduce gastric acid secretion, lowering the risk of NSAID-induced gastropathy.'),
('894a2fc9-da71-5d64-bacf-e9cf0a09e649'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'The main difference between morphine and codeine in terms of potency is:', '["They are equipotent resulting in decreased functional activity","Morphine is approximately 10 times more potent than codeine as analgesic","Codeine is more potent than morphine mediated by specific receptor activation","Morphine has no analgesic effect without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Codeine is used for severe pain only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Morphine is approximately 10 times more potent than codeine as analgesic', 'Morphine is approximately 10 times more potent than codeine; codeine is a prodrug requiring CYP2D6 conversion.'),
('25a08f6e-0c8d-5eaf-8eb9-08faa882e375'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Fentanyl is highly lipophilic which accounts for:', '["Slow onset of action","Rapid CNS penetration and fast onset of analgesia","Low potency","Poor oral bioavailability only","Lack of respiratory depression"]'::jsonb, 'Rapid CNS penetration and fast onset of analgesia', 'Fentanyl''s high lipophilicity enables rapid blood-brain barrier penetration giving fast onset (1-2 min IV).'),
('07c5a1d9-eac0-5cfe-8d93-e9d561b4aa15'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Methadone''s long half-life is primarily due to:', '["Renal retention resulting in decreased functional activity","Enterohepatic recirculation and slow receptor dissociation","Low potency mediated by specific receptor activation","Rapid metabolism without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Poor absorption leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Enterohepatic recirculation and slow receptor dissociation', 'Methadone undergoes enterohepatic recirculation and has slow dissociation from mu-receptors, prolonging its duration.'),
('ef73eb39-3ad7-527f-a92f-367a326a56bb'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'The primary metabolite of tramadol responsible for mu-receptor activity is:', '["O-desmethyltramadol (M1)","N-desmethyltramadol","Tramadol N-oxide","M2 metabolite","Unchanged tramadol"]'::jsonb, 'O-desmethyltramadol (M1)', 'M1 (O-desmethyltramadol) formed by CYP2D6 has 200-fold greater mu-receptor affinity than the parent compound.'),
('d0bc9b7e-b1f4-5cad-bfed-c2794aac191c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Naloxone''s rapid onset makes it ideal for:', '["Chronic pain management","Emergency reversal of opioid overdose","Migraine prophylaxis","Gout treatment","Muscle spasm"]'::jsonb, 'Emergency reversal of opioid overdose', 'Naloxone''s rapid IV onset (1-2 minutes) makes it the drug of choice for emergency opioid overdose reversal.'),
('75a3cc14-6976-5d4a-aa3c-19b233173556'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Allopurinol should be started simultaneously with NSAID or colchicine prophylaxis because:', '["It reduces the dose of other drugs needed","Allopurinol initiation can trigger acute gout flares","It enhances absorption of other drugs","It prevents drug interactions","It has no effect on flare risk"]'::jsonb, 'Allopurinol initiation can trigger acute gout flares', 'Starting allopurinol can mobilize urate crystals triggering flares; co-prescription of anti-inflammatory prophylaxis is essential.'),
('2b1b1716-2d06-50e7-adab-514be6c03d87'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'The analgesic ladder step 2 for moderate cancer pain uses:', '["NSAIDs alone resulting in decreased functional activity","Weak opioids (codeine, tramadol) +/- non-opioid","Strong opioids immediately mediated by specific receptor activation","Antidepressants only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anticonvulsants only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Weak opioids (codeine, tramadol) +/- non-opioid', 'Step 2 adds weak opioids (codeine 30-60mg q4-6h or tramadol 50-100mg q4-6h) to non-opioid therapy.'),
('89fb6a6a-a318-54bc-bb25-3bc916f227ac'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Colchicine''s mechanism in FMF prophylaxis involves:', '["Blocking IL-1 beta directly resulting in decreased functional activity","Inhibiting microtubule-dependent assembly of the NALP3 inflammasome","Blocking TNF-alpha mediated by specific receptor activation","Inhibiting COX-2 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Enhancing GABA leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibiting microtubule-dependent assembly of the NALP3 inflammasome', 'Colchicine inhibits microtubule-dependent inflammasome assembly, reducing IL-1beta production in FMF.'),
('c0c8d8f2-805b-549f-9c63-ef5376a07988'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Which non-opioid analgesic is absolutely contraindicated in children under 16 with viral illness?', '["Paracetamol","Aspirin","Ibuprofen","Naproxen","Diclofenac"]'::jsonb, 'Aspirin', 'Aspirin in children with viral infections (influenza, varicella) can cause Reye''s syndrome involving hepatic encephalopathy.'),
('55a854d2-a247-59d5-aa40-e7ca4751c9f2'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Rofecoxib and valdecoxib were withdrawn because of:', '["Hepatotoxicity resulting in decreased functional activity","Cardiovascular thrombotic events (MI, stroke)","Renal failure mediated by specific receptor activation","GI perforation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anaphylaxis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cardiovascular thrombotic events (MI, stroke)', 'Both coxibs were withdrawn due to increased cardiovascular risk from selective COX-2 inhibition disrupting TXA2/PGI2 balance.'),
('bc2a3955-d114-52ca-8679-b3b835a213ae'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'A patient on long-term morphine develops increasing constipation. The best approach is:', '["Increase morphine dose","Switch to a different opioid","Add prophylactic laxatives","Discontinue opioids","Ignore it"]'::jsonb, 'Add prophylactic laxatives', 'Opioid-induced constipation does not develop tolerance; prophylactic laxatives should be prescribed from the start.'),
('166021f3-efd4-5d93-ab2c-712c7cd4c483'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Buprenorphine''s ceiling effect means:', '["It cannot cause respiratory depression at any dose resulting in decreased functional activity","At high doses, respiratory depression plateaus and does not worsen","It has no analgesic ceiling mediated by specific receptor activation","It is safe in all overdoses without affecting the primary tissue pathway","It has no withdrawal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'At high doses, respiratory depression plateaus and does not worsen', 'As a partial agonist, buprenorphine reaches a ceiling for respiratory depression, providing a safety advantage.'),
('9d1e6e8c-108c-54f9-bcf5-5f5da432e586'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Topiramate''s carbonic anhydrase inhibition side effect can cause:', '["Weight gain resulting in decreased functional activity","Metabolic acidosis and kidney stones","Hypertension mediated by specific receptor activation","Sedation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Insomnia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Metabolic acidosis and kidney stones', 'Topiramate inhibits carbonic anhydrase, potentially causing metabolic acidosis and nephrolithiasis as side effects.'),
('362e9a43-54f0-5f71-9389-204b0598ad49'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Colchicine is used prophylactically at a dose of:', '["1.2mg hourly until pain resolves","0.5-0.6mg once or twice daily","5mg daily","10mg daily","100mg daily"]'::jsonb, '0.5-0.6mg once or twice daily', 'Colchicine prophylaxis uses low dose (0.5-0.6mg once or twice daily) to prevent flares while minimizing GI side effects.'),
('b16919ec-856a-5ba5-9380-48e719da9e64'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Valproate contraindication in pregnancy is due to risk of:', '["Cardiac defects only resulting in decreased functional activity","Neural tube defects (spina bifida) in the fetus","Cleft palate only mediated by specific receptor activation","Limb defects only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Renal agenesis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Neural tube defects (spina bifida) in the fetus', 'Valproate is a known teratogen causing neural tube defects (spina bifida) with highest risk in first trimester exposure.'),
('40fa3d5b-fcc2-5c37-a340-c4eb32dfe56f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'In the arachidonic acid cascade, phospholipase A2 is inhibited by:', '["NSAIDs resulting in decreased functional activity","Corticosteroids (via lipocortin-1)","Triptans mediated by specific receptor activation","Opioids without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Antihistamines leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Corticosteroids (via lipocortin-1)', 'Corticosteroids induce lipocortin-1 (annexin A1) which inhibits PLA2, blocking arachidonic acid release at the earliest step.'),
('37e542e9-b129-5073-8a6a-493a132b9d48'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Diazepam as a muscle relaxant acts by:', '["GABA-B agonism resulting in decreased functional activity","Enhancing GABA-A chloride channel activity centrally","Blocking calcium channels mediated by specific receptor activation","Alpha2-agonism without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Direct muscle relaxation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Enhancing GABA-A chloride channel activity centrally', 'Diazepam enhances GABA-A receptor activity, increasing chloride conductance, producing central muscle relaxation and sedation.'),
('575b0c6e-c0bb-5fe4-a5cd-6941dc53b22a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Phenytoin is sometimes used off-label as a muscle relaxant because it:', '["Blocks voltage-gated sodium channels reducing repetitive neuronal firing","Is a GABA-A agonist accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inhibits COX mediated by specific receptor activation","Blocks calcium channels without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Is an opioid leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Blocks voltage-gated sodium channels reducing repetitive neuronal firing', 'Phenytoin blocks voltage-gated sodium channels, reducing repetitive firing in motor neurons.'),
('2b49dfb2-2b6b-522b-8d02-6dc3fa8b2b9b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Oral sumatriptan bioavailability is approximately:', '["100%","50%","14%","5%","80%"]'::jsonb, '14%', 'Oral sumatriptan has 14% bioavailability due to extensive first-pass metabolism, compared to 100% for SC route.'),
('b74c0c6d-6c0c-5f16-ab29-b22db2cb3131'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'CGRP is released from:', '["Cholinergic nerves resulting in decreased functional activity","Trigeminal nerve endings in cranial blood vessels","Sympathetic nerves mediated by specific receptor activation","Enteric neurons without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Motor neurons leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Trigeminal nerve endings in cranial blood vessels', 'CGRP is co-released with substance P from trigeminal C-fibers innervating cranial meningeal vessels during migraine.'),
('805f697e-6c64-5eee-b2a2-319dce6f91b9'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Ergotamine has a long duration of action because:', '["It binds irreversibly to receptors resulting in decreased functional activity","It has high lipid solubility with slow receptor dissociation","It is a prodrug mediated by specific receptor activation","It is excreted very slowly without affecting the primary tissue pathway","It blocks calcium channels leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'It has high lipid solubility with slow receptor dissociation', 'Ergotamine''s high lipid solubility and slow receptor dissociation contribute to its prolonged duration of action.'),
('04a28c3b-875b-5501-ac16-b66bb5e34927'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'The maximum daily dose of aspirin for anti-inflammatory purposes is:', '["325mg","1g","4-6g","10g","15g"]'::jsonb, '4-6g', 'High-dose aspirin (4-6g/day in divided doses) is needed for full anti-inflammatory effect in rheumatic conditions.'),
('797e7369-0f7b-5bba-8f2d-cd685ed2bf18'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Diclofenac sodium vs diclofenac potassium:', '["Sodium form is for oral use, potassium for topical","Sodium form has faster absorption suitable for acute pain","They are identical in every way","Potassium form is for injection only","Sodium form is contraindicated in hypertension"]'::jsonb, 'Sodium form has faster absorption suitable for acute pain', 'Diclofenac potassium has faster absorption, making it more suitable for acute pain, while sodium is more commonly used.'),
('d12ae284-2776-5622-b330-9b3484fe0fac'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Low-dose aspirin (75-325mg) for cardiovascular prevention works by:', '["Reversibly inhibiting platelet COX-1 resulting in decreased functional activity","Irreversibly inhibiting platelet COX-1 throughout the platelet lifespan","Blocking ADP receptors mediated by specific receptor activation","Inhibiting thromboxane synthase without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Enhancing prostacyclin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Irreversibly inhibiting platelet COX-1 throughout the platelet lifespan', 'Low-dose aspirin irreversibly acetylates COX-1 in anucleate platelets for their entire 7-10 day lifespan.'),
('2fb07e86-de17-53c7-be0c-94b8a295273c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Celecoxib requires dose adjustment in patients with:', '["Hypertension resulting in decreased functional activity","Moderate hepatic impairment (Child-Pugh B)","Mild renal impairment only mediated by specific receptor activation","Asthma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Diabetes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Moderate hepatic impairment (Child-Pugh B)', 'Celecoxib is hepatically metabolized by CYP2C9; dose reduction is needed in moderate hepatic impairment.'),
('5d22bc8c-954b-5272-a777-8aaa571be7f7'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Colchicine-induced myopathy is a risk especially when combined with:', '["Paracetamol resulting in decreased functional activity","Statins (HMG-CoA reductase inhibitors)","Antihistamines mediated by specific receptor activation","Antacids without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vitamin C leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Statins (HMG-CoA reductase inhibitors)', 'Colchicine + statin combination increases risk of myopathy due to shared effects on microtubule function and muscle membrane stability.'),
('bbd78896-887a-5e79-b4c5-adb27acf7b27'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Rizatriptan 10mg ODT provides faster relief than oral tablet because:', '["Higher dose in ODT formulation resulting in decreased functional activity","Buccal absorption bypasses GI absorption delay and first-pass metabolism","ODT contains additional analgesic mediated by specific receptor activation","It dissolves directly into the brain without affecting the primary tissue pathway","It is an IV formulation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Buccal absorption bypasses GI absorption delay and first-pass metabolism', 'Buccal/sublingual absorption avoids GI transit time and some first-pass metabolism, accelerating onset.'),
('fbd1958e-055b-567a-969b-d8d5e56ae42f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Methadone causes QT prolongation due to:', '["Blocking hERG potassium channels","COX inhibition","Mu-receptor activation","GABA enhancement","NMDA antagonism"]'::jsonb, 'Blocking hERG potassium channels', 'Methadone blocks hERG (IKr) potassium channels in cardiac myocytes, prolonging QT interval and risking torsades de pointes.'),
('84715516-c863-5f79-bc1f-9def192a0ed5'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Pregabalin is a Schedule V controlled substance because:', '["It is an opioid resulting in decreased functional activity","It has abuse potential and can cause euphoria","It is extremely potent mediated by specific receptor activation","It is a prodrug without affecting the primary tissue pathway","It causes no side effects leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'It has abuse potential and can cause euphoria', 'Pregabalin has recognized abuse potential with euphoria in some patients, leading to Schedule V classification.'),
('504347a5-0065-579b-8e29-127d9ca844ac'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'The kappa-opioid receptor primarily mediates:', '["Euphoria and respiratory depression","Analgesia with dysphoria and sedation","Nausea and vomiting","Constipation","Tolerance"]'::jsonb, 'Analgesia with dysphoria and sedation', 'Kappa receptor activation produces analgesia (especially spinal) with dysphoria and sedation rather than euphoria.'),
('fc5cc821-4f42-50c7-9da0-a2e75c63485e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Capsaicin''s TRPV1 receptor is also activated by:', '["Cold temperature resulting in decreased functional activity","Heat above 43 degrees Celsius and protons (acidic pH)","Light pressure only mediated by specific receptor activation","Sound waves without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Magnetic fields leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Heat above 43 degrees Celsius and protons (acidic pH)', 'TRPV1 is a polymodal nociceptor activated by heat (>43C), protons (acidic pH), capsaicin, and endovanilloids.'),
('52369f11-09c7-55d6-86f2-4b460c324534'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 1)', 'Analgesics - Analgesics', 'Amitriptyline''s analgesic dose for neuropathic pain is typically:', '["150-300mg (antidepressant dose) resulting in decreased functional activity","10-75mg at bedtime (lower than antidepressant dose)","500mg daily mediated by specific receptor activation","1g daily without affecting the primary tissue pathway","5mg weekly leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '10-75mg at bedtime (lower than antidepressant dose)', 'Neuropathic pain doses of amitriptyline (10-75mg) are typically lower than antidepressant doses, reducing anticholinergic side effects.'),
('61d5005c-7a5d-56ee-b310-41fb1c4e71f8'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Which class of antihypertensives works by inhibiting the angiotensin converting enzyme?', '["ARBs","ACE inhibitors","Beta-blockers","Calcium channel blockers","Direct vasodilators"]'::jsonb, 'ACE inhibitors', 'ACE inhibitors inhibit ACE, preventing conversion of angiotensin I to angiotensin II, reducing vasoconstriction and aldosterone secretion.'),
('e8ffc1da-ee71-53b9-af06-85cb8b84049a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Which of the following is an absolute contraindication to ACE inhibitors?', '["Diabetes mellitus","History of angioedema","Hyperlipidemia","Osteoarthritis","Asthma"]'::jsonb, 'History of angioedema', 'ACE inhibitors are absolutely contraindicated in patients with a history of angioedema related to prior ACE inhibitor use.'),
('f678e8b8-ffbc-573b-836e-23bcb8c7848b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'The most common side effect of ACE inhibitors is:', '["Hyperkalemia","Persistent dry cough","Angioedema","Hypotension","Renal failure"]'::jsonb, 'Persistent dry cough', 'Persistent dry cough occurs in 10-20% of patients due to accumulation of bradykinin and substance P.'),
('e8b7af43-9d2c-540f-9a33-82a403ceb322'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'ARBs act by blocking which receptor?', '["AT1 receptor","AT2 receptor","Beta-1 receptor","Alpha-1 receptor","Dopamine receptor"]'::jsonb, 'AT1 receptor', 'ARBs selectively block the AT1 receptor, preventing angiotensin II from exerting vasoconstrictive effects.'),
('835ecd5b-466d-5490-9313-ba8a941c8d01'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Losartan is an example of which class of drugs?', '["ACE inhibitor","ARB","Beta-blocker","CCB","Diuretic"]'::jsonb, 'ARB', 'Losartan is the first commercially available ARB, used for hypertension and diabetic nephropathy.'),
('773ae282-aff4-5543-ba43-31294985db9b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Which ARB also has a uricosuric effect lowering serum uric acid?', '["Losartan","Valsartan","Irbesartan","Candesartan","Telmisartan"]'::jsonb, 'Losartan', 'Losartan has a unique uricosuric effect, beneficial in hypertensive patients with hyperuricemia.'),
('1ab3cf79-af58-5fe1-9218-91a1d70dbabb'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Beta-blockers lower blood pressure by all EXCEPT:', '["Decreasing cardiac output","Reducing renin release","Central sympatholytic action","Direct vasodilation","Blocking cardiac beta-1 receptors"]'::jsonb, 'Direct vasodilation', 'Conventional beta-blockers do not cause direct vasodilation. Some newer agents have vasodilatory properties but this is not a class effect.'),
('93ba4d3b-3b13-5d38-ad52-af3872ec8435'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Propranolol is a non-selective beta-blocker that blocks:', '["Only beta-1","Only beta-2","Both beta-1 and beta-2","Alpha-1 and beta-1","Alpha-2 and beta-1"]'::jsonb, 'Both beta-1 and beta-2', 'Propranolol antagonizes both beta-1 (cardiac) and beta-2 (bronchial, vascular smooth muscle) receptors.'),
('c6ab6e74-7e4a-5380-a59a-847481c82841'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Metoprolol is preferred over propranolol in patients requiring cardioselective blockade because:', '["It blocks alpha receptors","It is beta-1 selective at therapeutic doses","It has intrinsic sympathomimetic activity","It blocks calcium channels","It is a diuretic"]'::jsonb, 'It is beta-1 selective at therapeutic doses', 'Metoprolol is beta-1 selective (cardioselective) at therapeutic doses, causing fewer bronchospastic side effects.'),
('98536c0a-8f1f-5ecd-96d2-1fc2ae216939'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Carvedilol differs from conventional beta-blockers in that it also blocks:', '["Alpha-1 receptors","Calcium channels","Potassium channels","Sodium channels","H+ K+ ATPase"]'::jsonb, 'Alpha-1 receptors', 'Carvedilol is a non-selective beta-blocker with additional alpha-1 blocking activity, causing vasodilation.'),
('6a1a2c05-162f-5773-a95c-4da4d54fd0dd'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Nebivolol is unique among beta-blockers because it:', '["Has intrinsic sympathomimetic activity","Causes vasodilation via nitric oxide potentiation","Is a potent diuretic","Blocks alpha receptors","Is a pure vasoconstrictor"]'::jsonb, 'Causes vasodilation via nitric oxide potentiation', 'Nebivolol is a highly beta-1 selective blocker that enhances endothelial nitric oxide release, causing vasodilation.'),
('1bd8bc19-48a0-5285-a36c-8f721928009b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Pindolol and acebutolol are examples of beta-blockers with:', '["Alpha-blocking activity","Intrinsic sympathomimetic activity (ISA)","Membrane stabilizing activity only","Nitric oxide releasing property","Calcium channel blocking activity"]'::jsonb, 'Intrinsic sympathomimetic activity (ISA)', 'Pindolol and acebutolol have ISA (partial agonist activity), causing less bradycardia at rest.'),
('603f35c9-f594-5786-985e-3554a7083837'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Labetalol is used in hypertensive emergencies because it blocks:', '["Only beta-1 receptors","Only alpha-1 receptors","Both alpha-1 and beta receptors","Calcium channels","ACE"]'::jsonb, 'Both alpha-1 and beta receptors', 'Labetalol blocks both alpha-1 and beta receptors, providing arterial and venous dilation plus reduced cardiac output.'),
('224c58bb-19c4-5e28-b85f-14fccad7b387'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Amlodipine belongs to which class of calcium channel blockers?', '["Phenylalkylamine","Benzothiazepine","Dihydropyridine","Piperazine derivative","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Dihydropyridine', 'Amlodipine is a dihydropyridine CCB acting primarily on vascular smooth muscle causing vasodilation.'),
('8798f0d4-4403-54dd-8dee-b33680bef48f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Verapamil is contraindicated in patients with:', '["Hypertension","Stable angina","Heart failure with reduced ejection fraction","Atrial fibrillation rate control","Raynaud''s phenomenon"]'::jsonb, 'Heart failure with reduced ejection fraction', 'Verapamil has strong negative inotropic effects and is contraindicated in HFrEF.'),
('73d3def9-53b4-57ef-8665-b8a7998c0295'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Nifedipine immediate-release formulation can cause:', '["Reflex bradycardia","Reflex tachycardia","Heart block","Bradycardia","No reflex response"]'::jsonb, 'Reflex tachycardia', 'Immediate-release nifedipine causes rapid vasodilation leading to reflex sympathetic activation and tachycardia.'),
('48d4e82e-852b-5901-8c3e-4c9d41c0764d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'The drug of choice for hypertensive emergency in pregnancy is:', '["Enalapril","Losartan","Hydralazine","Atenolol","Nifedipine"]'::jsonb, 'Hydralazine', 'Hydralazine is the traditional drug of choice for hypertensive emergencies in pregnancy. Labetalol and nifedipine are alternatives.'),
('f5ecaf40-7d80-5d9c-83f1-290b5662df65'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Methyldopa works centrally by:', '["Blocking alpha-1 receptors","Stimulating alpha-2 receptors","Blocking beta receptors","Inhibiting ACE","Blocking calcium channels"]'::jsonb, 'Stimulating alpha-2 receptors', 'Methyldopa is converted to alpha-methylnorepinephrine, stimulating central alpha-2 receptors, reducing sympathetic outflow.'),
('cd777975-ceda-59e4-9c7b-de754dfb0feb'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Hydralazine acts by:', '["Blocking alpha-1 receptors","Direct relaxation of arteriolar smooth muscle","Inhibiting ACE","Blocking calcium channels","Stimulating beta-2 receptors"]'::jsonb, 'Direct relaxation of arteriolar smooth muscle', 'Hydralazine causes direct relaxation of arteriolar smooth muscle, decreasing peripheral resistance.'),
('7d604ed0-5701-5bce-bb58-593cb4bbaeed'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Spironolactone is an antagonist of which receptor?', '["Angiotensin II receptor","Aldosterone receptor","Beta-1 receptor","Calcium channel","ACE"]'::jsonb, 'Aldosterone receptor', 'Spironolactone is a potassium-sparing diuretic that acts as a competitive aldosterone antagonist.'),
('9b1416f1-b8a8-5c17-9cb1-e81e3181722b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Furosemide acts on which part of the nephron?', '["Proximal convoluted tubule","Loop of Henle (thick ascending limb)","Distal convoluted tubule","Collecting duct","Glomerulus"]'::jsonb, 'Loop of Henle (thick ascending limb)', 'Furosemide inhibits the Na-K-2Cl cotransporter in the thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle.'),
('e8ea4122-d1ce-53c3-9c8d-5a5bc2c5774e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Mannitol reduces intracranial pressure because it:', '["Is a potent vasodilator","Creates an osmotic gradient drawing fluid from brain tissue","Blocks CSF production","Causes cerebral vasoconstriction","Inhibits sympathetic nervous system"]'::jsonb, 'Creates an osmotic gradient drawing fluid from brain tissue', 'Mannitol increases plasma osmolality, drawing interstitial fluid from brain tissue into the vascular compartment.'),
('b1e2b4df-f00b-50ea-9e99-a7bd6b32494b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Which diuretic is most appropriate as first-line for hypertension?', '["Furosemide","Hydrochlorothiazide","Spironolactone","Acetazolamide","Mannitol"]'::jsonb, 'Hydrochlorothiazide', 'Thiazide diuretics are recommended as first-line antihypertensives in uncomplicated hypertension.'),
('e9fe5353-b6c7-52d0-b074-71605c54df86'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Sodium nitroprusside is used in hypertensive emergencies because it:', '["Is a beta-blocker resulting in decreased functional activity","Relaxes both arteriolar and venous smooth muscle","Is an ACE inhibitor mediated by specific receptor activation","Is a diuretic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Blocks calcium channels leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Relaxes both arteriolar and venous smooth muscle', 'Sodium nitroprusside releases nitric oxide, causing balanced arterial and venous dilation.'),
('7f088ce0-f0eb-5740-8ca1-bb7e916cac36'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Nitroprusside toxicity results in accumulation of which ion?', '["Sodium","Cyanide","Potassium","Calcium","Magnesium"]'::jsonb, 'Cyanide', 'Nitroprusside is metabolized to cyanide, which can accumulate in toxicity.'),
('99b8d9a1-6f1d-593a-bee9-594dfd4ee57b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Clonidine works by:', '["Blocking beta-1 receptors","Stimulating central alpha-2 receptors","Blocking alpha-1 receptors","Inhibiting ACE","Blocking calcium channels"]'::jsonb, 'Stimulating central alpha-2 receptors', 'Clonidine stimulates central alpha-2 adrenergic receptors, reducing sympathetic outflow.'),
('03345067-4fc0-5ccf-9d33-6573dac2bfe7'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'The major side effect of clonidine on abrupt withdrawal is:', '["Hyperkalemia","Rebound hypertension","Angioedema","Gynecomastia","Persistent cough"]'::jsonb, 'Rebound hypertension', 'Abrupt discontinuation of clonidine can cause rebound sympathetic hyperactivity with severe hypertension.'),
('00499506-1668-5867-8c21-425a73b1439d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Reserpine works by depleting catecholamine stores from:', '["Adrenal medulla resulting in decreased functional activity","Postganglionic sympathetic nerve terminals","Brain cortex mediated by specific receptor activation","Kidney without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Liver leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Postganglionic sympathetic nerve terminals', 'Reserpine inhibits VMAT, depleting norepinephrine from postganglionic sympathetic nerve terminals.'),
('96c51acf-8bf4-5752-bcae-fa3f8063cc49'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Reserpine is associated with which psychiatric side effect?', '["Mania","Depression","Anxiety","Psychosis","Euphoria"]'::jsonb, 'Depression', 'Reserpine depletes central monoamines and can cause severe depression.'),
('d72f8cc4-ad71-59dd-9d4e-0ee7dfc28471'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Eplerenone differs from spironolactone in having:', '["Greater diuretic potency resulting in decreased functional activity","Greater selectivity for mineralocorticoid receptor with less anti-androgen effects","Alpha-blocking activity mediated by specific receptor activation","Beta-blocking activity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","ACE inhibiting activity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Greater selectivity for mineralocorticoid receptor with less anti-androgen effects', 'Eplerenone is more selective with fewer anti-androgenic side effects than spironolactone.'),
('0e3a13f0-a56e-5b67-8ed7-30f7b2e8ebe5'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Chlorthalidone is preferred over hydrochlorothiazide because it:', '["Has fewer electrolyte disturbances","Has a longer duration of action and greater efficacy","Is less expensive","Is more potent but shorter acting","Has no effect on glucose"]'::jsonb, 'Has a longer duration of action and greater efficacy', 'Chlorthalidone has a longer half-life and greater 24-hour BP lowering efficacy compared to HCTZ.'),
('7e4c720e-c0ba-5439-bc1c-b6c2fa907815'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Which drugs are recommended first-line for hypertension in diabetic patients with proteinuria?', '["Beta-blockers","ACE inhibitors or ARBs","Alpha-blockers","Direct vasodilators","Centrally acting sympatholytics"]'::jsonb, 'ACE inhibitors or ARBs', 'ACE inhibitors or ARBs are first-line in diabetic patients with proteinuria due to renoprotective effects.'),
('17e59f7b-6954-580f-b4ae-1617490e9ac7'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Ethacrynic acid is safe in patients allergic to:', '["Penicillin","Sulfonamides","ACE inhibitors","Statins","Beta-blockers"]'::jsonb, 'Sulfonamides', 'Ethacrynic acid is the only loop diuretic not containing a sulfonamide group.'),
('4433e969-8442-5298-b710-14e167a382bc'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'The antidote for digoxin toxicity in life-threatening situations is:', '["Atropine resulting in decreased functional activity","Digoxin-specific antibody fragments (Digibind)","Calcium gluconate mediated by specific receptor activation","Insulin-glucose without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Magnesium sulfate leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Digoxin-specific antibody fragments (Digibind)', 'Digoxin-specific antibody fragments bind free digoxin, preventing toxicity.'),
('b5898cbe-1b87-5494-b6e2-8bd4aa8f7c5d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'According to Vaughan Williams classification, Class I antiarrhythmics block:', '["Potassium channels","Sodium channels","Calcium channels","Potassium and calcium channels","Beta receptors"]'::jsonb, 'Sodium channels', 'Class I antiarrhythmics block voltage-gated sodium channels, slowing phase 0 depolarization.'),
('c65161fe-aed9-54c9-aa86-11209536961b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Quinidine belongs to which subclass of Class I antiarrhythmics?', '["IA","IB","IC","II","III"]'::jsonb, 'IA', 'Quinidine is Class IA, moderately blocking sodium channels and prolonging action potential duration.'),
('f4aa9552-be79-560a-beb7-63f6c582d22d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Lidocaine is a Class IB antiarrhythmic that primarily acts on:', '["Atrial tissue resulting in decreased functional activity","Ventricular tissue and ischemic myocardium","AV node mediated by specific receptor activation","SA node without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Purkinje fibers only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ventricular tissue and ischemic myocardium', 'Lidocaine preferentially binds to inactivated sodium channels in ventricular tissue and ischemic myocardium.'),
('5986e587-4338-5be0-a193-a2d03a940e86'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Flecainide is a Class IC antiarrhythmic that:', '["Has minimal effect on conduction velocity resulting in decreased functional activity","Markedly slows conduction with minimal effect on repolarization","Prolongs repolarization mediated by specific receptor activation","Blocks beta receptors without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Blocks calcium channels leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Markedly slows conduction with minimal effect on repolarization', 'Class IC agents cause marked slowing of conduction velocity with relatively little effect on action potential duration.'),
('629be2a7-7a4e-5563-a5a3-c86e1baa6d51'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'The CAST trial demonstrated that flecainide increases mortality in patients with:', '["Hypertension","Post-MI ventricular arrhythmias","Atrial fibrillation","Heart failure","Angina"]'::jsonb, 'Post-MI ventricular arrhythmias', 'CAST trial showed flecainide increased mortality in post-MI asymptomatic ventricular arrhythmias.'),
('434f7ce3-80c0-52bc-a649-5154aa7755d7'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Amiodarone is classified primarily as which Vaughan Williams class?', '["IA resulting in decreased functional activity","IB accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","IC mediated by specific receptor activation","III without affecting the primary tissue pathway","It has properties of multiple classes"]'::jsonb, 'It has properties of multiple classes', 'Amiodarone has properties of all four classes but is primarily Class III (potassium channel blocker).'),
('c851b2ef-2fb8-5692-9664-711224d0b9aa'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'The most dangerous long-term side effect of amiodarone is:', '["Hypertension","Pulmonary fibrosis","Diabetes","Renal failure","Peptic ulcer"]'::jsonb, 'Pulmonary fibrosis', 'Pulmonary fibrosis occurs in 1-5% of patients on long-term amiodarone and can be fatal.'),
('6eac7c6f-38d3-5277-9c7f-04169b3d2652'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Amiodarone-induced thyrotoxicosis occurs because amiodarone:', '["Is a thyroid hormone analogue","Contains 37% iodine by weight","Stimulates TSH release","Blocks T4 to T3 conversion only","Is a direct thyroid antagonist"]'::jsonb, 'Contains 37% iodine by weight', 'Amiodarone contains approximately 37% iodine by weight, causing either thyrotoxicosis or hypothyroidism.'),
('78964514-01f6-51b8-8831-53f8d32e507c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Amiodarone can cause corneal microdeposits visible as:', '["Hypopyon resulting in decreased functional activity","Vortex keratopathy (corneal verticillata)","Band keratopathy mediated by specific receptor activation","Keratoconus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Arcus senilis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Vortex keratopathy (corneal verticillata)', 'Amiodarone causes vortex keratopathy in nearly all patients on long-term therapy.'),
('f7b257f3-7695-5908-a9f8-f1f2ffec518f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Dofetilide is a Class III antiarrhythmic that blocks:', '["Sodium channels resulting in decreased functional activity","Rapid component of delayed rectifier potassium channels (IKr)","L-type calcium channels mediated by specific receptor activation","Fast sodium channels without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Beta receptors leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Rapid component of delayed rectifier potassium channels (IKr)', 'Dofetilide selectively blocks IKr, prolonging repolarization and refractoriness.'),
('fcea84f5-aaef-58cc-a55c-b85c51396c99'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Sotalol has which additional property beyond Class III activity?', '["Class I activity","Class II (beta-blocking) activity","Class IV activity","Phosphodiesterase inhibition","ACE inhibition"]'::jsonb, 'Class II (beta-blocking) activity', 'Sotalol is Class III with non-selective beta-blocking (Class II) activity.'),
('ca554e90-f8a0-57af-b980-c5c094046dce'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Verapamil belongs to which Vaughan Williams class?', '["Class I","Class II","Class III","Class IV","Class V"]'::jsonb, 'Class IV', 'Verapamil is a Class IV antiarrhythmic blocking L-type calcium channels.'),
('60c050c5-bdfe-5d94-864b-0060546b8165'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Verapamil is effective in treating:', '["Ventricular tachycardia","Supraventricular tachycardia","Ventricular fibrillation","Asystole","Torsades de pointes"]'::jsonb, 'Supraventricular tachycardia', 'Verapamil slows AV nodal conduction and is effective in SVT including AVNRT and AVRT.'),
('20319b8d-1171-5a02-9953-34026d1893d5'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Adenosine terminates SVT by:', '["Blocking sodium channels resulting in decreased functional activity","Hyperpolarizing AV nodal cells via potassium channel activation","Blocking calcium channels mediated by specific receptor activation","Blocking beta receptors without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Blocking sodium and potassium channels leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperpolarizing AV nodal cells via potassium channel activation', 'Adenosine activates IKAcH and inhibits calcium channels in the AV node.'),
('58f9be66-5198-5e7d-ad11-56820b51c024'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'The half-life of adenosine is approximately:', '["10 minutes","10 seconds","10 hours","10 milliseconds","10 days"]'::jsonb, '10 seconds', 'Adenosine has an extremely short half-life of approximately 10 seconds.'),
('46cc43e5-c1af-5d3b-867e-f2f7c29dcd10'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Digoxin slows heart rate by:', '["Blocking sodium channels","Increasing vagal tone and slowing AV nodal conduction","Blocking calcium channels","Blocking potassium channels","Blocking beta receptors"]'::jsonb, 'Increasing vagal tone and slowing AV nodal conduction', 'Digoxin increases vagal tone and directly slows AV nodal conduction.'),
('89919a06-c39a-59e5-9dce-991b13fc02e6'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Procainamide prolongs the QT interval because it:', '["Blocks potassium channels resulting in decreased functional activity","Is a Class IA agent that also blocks potassium channels","Blocks calcium channels mediated by specific receptor activation","Stimulates beta receptors without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inhibits ACE leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Is a Class IA agent that also blocks potassium channels', 'Procainamide blocks both sodium and potassium channels, prolonging action potential duration.'),
('5af2133e-18a1-565b-b2cf-6d8ea2d47ec4'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Tocainide is an oral analogue of:', '["Quinidine","Lidocaine","Flecainide","Amiodarone","Verapamil"]'::jsonb, 'Lidocaine', 'Tocainide is an oral analogue of lidocaine with similar Class IB properties.'),
('45ffb33b-02b6-5382-bc94-ce17072836da'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Propafenone is a Class IC agent with additional:', '["Beta-blocking properties","Alpha-blocking properties","Calcium channel blocking properties","Potassium channel blocking properties","ACE inhibiting properties"]'::jsonb, 'Beta-blocking properties', 'Propafenone has mild beta-blocking activity, which should be considered in asthma or COPD.'),
('87f256c8-24a3-5fde-9498-8c150b06f5c1'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Torsades de pointes is best treated with:', '["Digoxin resulting in decreased functional activity","Magnesium sulfate and overdrive pacing","Verapamil mediated by specific receptor activation","Amiodarone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lidocaine leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Magnesium sulfate and overdrive pacing', 'Torsades de pointes is treated with IV magnesium sulfate and temporary overdrive pacing.'),
('b99fe8c7-26b2-5566-839e-716d852a64dc'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'The drug of choice for arrhythmias in Wolff-Parkinson-White syndrome is:', '["Verapamil","Digoxin","Procainamide","Adenosine","Beta-blockers"]'::jsonb, 'Procainamide', 'Procainamide (or amiodarone) is used in WPW. Verapamil, digoxin, adenosine are contraindicated.'),
('c9edea7e-5355-5b10-9080-dea98e1a6f85'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Ivabradine acts by:', '["Blocking calcium channels","Selective inhibition of If current in SA node","Blocking beta receptors","Blocking potassium channels","Blocking sodium channels"]'::jsonb, 'Selective inhibition of If current in SA node', 'Ivabradine selectively inhibits the pacemaker If current, reducing heart rate.'),
('caeecc8f-19e5-5730-b69a-1025ca806055'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Ranolazine works by:', '["Blocking beta receptors","Blocking late sodium current","Blocking calcium channels","Releasing nitric oxide","Blocking potassium channels"]'::jsonb, 'Blocking late sodium current', 'Ranolazine inhibits the late sodium current, reducing intracellular calcium overload.'),
('ed54d4da-328a-5deb-b245-eba2d3685d10'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Which drug is contraindicated in Brugada syndrome?', '["Amiodarone resulting in decreased functional activity","Beta-blockers accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Class IC antiarrhythmics (flecainide)","Lidocaine without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Magnesium leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Class IC antiarrhythmics (flecainide)', 'Class IC antiarrhythmics worsen the Brugada ECG pattern and can precipitate ventricular fibrillation.'),
('f85ef330-0aa6-5d04-9b1a-87e43559b41b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Adenosine is contraindicated in patients taking:', '["Digoxin","Dipyridamole","Aspirin","Statins","Metformin"]'::jsonb, 'Dipyridamole', 'Dipyridamole inhibits adenosine uptake, potentiating its effects.'),
('2310139a-eedc-57dc-9812-0bb1d9187291'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Procainamide can cause a lupus-like syndrome characterized by:', '["Photosensitivity and rash resulting in decreased functional activity","Arthralgia, myalgia, serositis, and anti-histone antibodies","Renal failure mediated by specific receptor activation","Hepatitis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pneumonitis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Arthralgia, myalgia, serositis, and anti-histone antibodies', 'Procainamide causes drug-induced lupus with arthralgia, pleuritis, pericarditis, and positive anti-histone antibodies.'),
('1ebf62ea-28cb-59ab-9c39-d47cdbb46225'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Digoxin inhibits which enzyme in cardiac myocytes?', '["ACE","Na+/K+ ATPase","Phosphodiesterase","HMG-CoA reductase","Cyclooxygenase"]'::jsonb, 'Na+/K+ ATPase', 'Digoxin inhibits Na+/K+ ATPase, increasing intracellular calcium via Na+/Ca2+ exchanger.'),
('fd5630f6-873c-554b-844c-7c630a92ff4c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'The therapeutic index of digoxin is:', '["Wide","Narrow","Very wide","Non-existent","Variable but always safe"]'::jsonb, 'Narrow', 'Digoxin has a narrow therapeutic index (0.5-2.0 ng/mL).'),
('e5f94aef-3670-516f-b419-a1aa570b076e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Hypokalemia increases digoxin toxicity risk because:', '["Potassium competes with digoxin for Na+/K+ ATPase binding","Hypokalemia increases digoxin absorption","Hypokalemia decreases digoxin excretion","Potassium is an antidote","Hypokalemia increases protein binding"]'::jsonb, 'Potassium competes with digoxin for Na+/K+ ATPase binding', 'Potassium and digoxin compete for binding to Na+/K+ ATPase. Hypokalemia increases digoxin binding.'),
('c9bc6bf0-5bcd-5fb3-bf11-f76b0d782f77'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Digoxin toxicity typically presents with all EXCEPT:', '["Visual disturbances (yellow-green halos)","Nausea and vomiting","Hyperkalemia","Bradyarrhythmias","Bidirectional ventricular tachycardia"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperkalemia', 'In chronic toxicity, hypokalemia is seen. In acute poisoning, hyperkalemia can occur.'),
('dd42ce20-9d33-50f4-a4b6-71e22deff389'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'ACE inhibitors are first-line in HFrEF because they:', '["Increase heart rate resulting in decreased functional activity","Reduce preload and afterload, and prevent adverse remodeling","Are positive inotropes mediated by specific receptor activation","Block potassium channels without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Increase myocardial oxygen demand leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Reduce preload and afterload, and prevent adverse remodeling', 'ACE inhibitors reduce preload and afterload, inhibit aldosterone, and prevent adverse cardiac remodeling.'),
('1e78243c-bfae-59d5-8823-c6610e0ed849'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Sacubitril/valsartan (ARNI) is superior to ACE inhibitors because sacubitril:', '["Inhibits ACE resulting in decreased functional activity","Inhibits neprilysin, increasing natriuretic peptides","Blocks angiotensin II receptors mediated by specific receptor activation","Blocks beta receptors without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Is a diuretic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibits neprilysin, increasing natriuretic peptides', 'Sacubitril inhibits neprilysin, preventing degradation of natriuretic peptides.'),
('8bdd08c0-1304-5cbf-832b-f8d09eadccad'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Spironolactone reduces mortality in heart failure as shown in which trial?', '["RALES","MERIT-HF","CIBIS-II","CONSENSUS","SOLVD"]'::jsonb, 'RALES', 'RALES demonstrated spironolactone reduced mortality by 30% in severe heart failure.'),
('ac9ad8e5-59f2-5db7-a531-19b26e9f55b5'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Hydralazine/isosorbide dinitrate is particularly beneficial in:', '["White patients resulting in decreased functional activity","Self-identified African American patients","Young patients mediated by specific receptor activation","Diabetic patients without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Elderly patients leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Self-identified African American patients', 'A-HeFT trial showed benefit particularly in self-identified African American patients with HFrEF.'),
('c6dc666b-d0a7-505e-866b-c1f5a02695c6'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'In acute decompensated heart failure, the initial drug for symptom relief is:', '["Digoxin resulting in decreased functional activity","Intravenous loop diuretic (furosemide)","ACE inhibitor mediated by specific receptor activation","Beta-blocker without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Spironolactone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Intravenous loop diuretic (furosemide)', 'IV loop diuretics are first-line for acute decompensated heart failure with congestion.'),
('8d1e63fb-7ed1-5bb8-a50a-e34e279c8325'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Dobutamine is a positive inotrope that acts primarily on:', '["Alpha-1 receptors","Beta-1 receptors","Beta-2 receptors","Dopamine receptors","Muscarinic receptors"]'::jsonb, 'Beta-1 receptors', 'Dobutamine has primary beta-1 agonist activity, increasing cardiac contractility and output.'),
('d64f1554-d839-509f-b391-dd3da6ec2e52'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Milrinone is a PDE-3 inhibitor that causes:', '["Vasodilation and positive inotropy","Vasoconstriction accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Negative inotropy mediated by specific receptor activation","Bradycardia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","AV block leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Vasodilation and positive inotropy', 'Milrinone inhibits PDE-3, increasing cAMP in cardiac and vascular smooth muscle.'),
('b8c62f99-292d-55be-92d3-c1853a81cafb'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Nesiritide is a recombinant form of:', '["Angiotensin II","Aldosterone","B-type natriuretic peptide (BNP)","Vasopressin","Erythropoietin"]'::jsonb, 'B-type natriuretic peptide (BNP)', 'Nesiritide is recombinant BNP, causing vasodilation and natriuresis.'),
('7fb28386-d9a4-5324-b36c-91c5d8ac775f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'The therapeutic range of serum digoxin for heart failure is:', '["0.1-0.5 ng/mL","0.5-2.0 ng/mL","2.0-5.0 ng/mL","5.0-10.0 ng/mL","10.0-20.0 ng/mL"]'::jsonb, '0.5-2.0 ng/mL', 'The therapeutic range for digoxin in heart failure is 0.5-2.0 ng/mL.'),
('6b081a71-82be-5fbd-9e55-764e3e1953a6'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Carvedilol, bisoprolol, and metoprolol succinate are recommended in HFrEF because:', '["They are pure beta-1 blockers","They have demonstrated mortality benefit in landmark trials","They are short-acting","They have intrinsic sympathomimetic activity","They are only used in NYHA class IV"]'::jsonb, 'They have demonstrated mortality benefit in landmark trials', 'These beta-blockers have shown mortality benefit in large RCTs (COPERNICUS, CIBIS-II, MERIT-HF).'),
('e6262ac6-e073-5865-ab2c-428c37834c9a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Ivabradine is indicated in HFrEF when heart rate remains above:', '["50 bpm","60 bpm","70 bpm","80 bpm","90 bpm"]'::jsonb, '70 bpm', 'Ivabradine is indicated when HR is >=70 bpm despite maximally tolerated beta-blocker.'),
('89764047-4dd5-52d1-8b81-de25c055e7d7'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Vericiguat is a newer heart failure drug that acts as:', '["ACE inhibitor resulting in decreased functional activity","Soluble guanylate cyclase stimulator","Beta-blocker mediated by specific receptor activation","Diuretic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Aldosterone antagonist leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Soluble guanylate cyclase stimulator', 'Vericiguat stimulates soluble guanylate cyclase, increasing cGMP.'),
('18d94acd-f77b-5b60-8e7b-60403b17b549'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'The key electrolyte disturbance to monitor with loop diuretics in HF is:', '["Hypernatremia","Hypokalemia and hypomagnesemia","Hyperkalemia","Hypercalcemia","Hypermagnesemia"]'::jsonb, 'Hypokalemia and hypomagnesemia', 'Loop diuretics cause significant potassium and magnesium wasting.'),
('633e99e9-03c6-5dec-ba68-36e9f13a7ead'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Digoxin is primarily indicated in HFrEF for:', '["All stages of heart failure","Symptom relief in HFrEF with atrial fibrillation","Mortality reduction","Prevention of arrhythmias","Reducing hospitalizations"]'::jsonb, 'Symptom relief in HFrEF with atrial fibrillation', 'Digoxin reduces HF hospitalizations but not mortality, most beneficial with coexistent AF.'),
('5a9964a6-4eae-5847-beb3-6c378381d585'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Digoxin is CONTRAINDICATED in which arrhythmia?', '["Atrial fibrillation with rapid ventricular response","Supraventricular tachycardia","Ventricular fibrillation","High-grade AV block","Paroxysmal SVT"]'::jsonb, 'High-grade AV block', 'Digoxin is contraindicated in high-degree AV block as it can worsen conduction disturbance.'),
('fded6c42-0e41-588d-9326-620da8f44b9a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'In acute HF with severe pulmonary edema, which combination is most effective?', '["IV furosemide + IV nitroglycerin","Oral digoxin","Oral spironolactone","Sublingual beta-blocker","Inhaled bronchodilator"]'::jsonb, 'IV furosemide + IV nitroglycerin', 'IV furosemide (diuresis) + IV nitroglycerin (venodilation) is most effective for acute pulmonary edema.'),
('79cfeecc-0bf5-5ff1-b0c1-167e0dbf7793'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Nitroglycerin acts primarily by:', '["Blocking calcium channels","Releasing nitric oxide causing venodilation","Blocking beta receptors","Blocking sodium channels","Inhibiting ACE"]'::jsonb, 'Releasing nitric oxide causing venodilation', 'Nitroglycerin releases NO, activating guanylate cyclase, increasing cGMP, causing smooth muscle relaxation.'),
('d11fd55f-7abd-50fa-bd4c-2a92d106cc6e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'The primary mechanism by which nitrates relieve angina is:', '["Coronary vasodilation resulting in decreased functional activity","Reduction in preload reducing myocardial oxygen demand","Increased heart rate mediated by specific receptor activation","Increased contractility without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Increased afterload leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Reduction in preload reducing myocardial oxygen demand', 'Nitrates primarily reduce preload by venodilation, decreasing ventricular wall stress.'),
('886a01fc-1a27-5852-b6ba-dc76744f32c8'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Sublingual nitroglycerin typically relieves angina within:', '["1-2 minutes","30-60 minutes","2-3 hours","24 hours","1 week"]'::jsonb, '1-2 minutes', 'Sublingual nitroglycerin is rapidly absorbed and relieves angina within 1-3 minutes.'),
('b2bf40e0-b7db-57b0-8e5d-f75d07df6d8f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'To prevent nitrate tolerance, a nitrate-free interval of 8-12 hours is:', '["Recommended","Not necessary","Only for IV nitrates","Only for sublingual nitrates","Only for transdermal patches"]'::jsonb, 'Recommended', 'A nitrate-free interval helps prevent tolerance by allowing sulfhydryl group replenishment.'),
('841f53be-1dc6-53a9-b1cb-9291b079f2ff'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Beta-blockers relieve angina by:', '["Causing coronary vasodilation resulting in decreased functional activity","Reducing heart rate and contractility, decreasing myocardial oxygen demand","Blocking sodium channels mediated by specific receptor activation","Blocking calcium channels without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Releasing nitric oxide leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Reducing heart rate and contractility, decreasing myocardial oxygen demand', 'Beta-blockers reduce HR, contractility, and BP, decreasing myocardial oxygen demand.'),
('eb0c93e7-9a45-5dcb-a245-c1be420cba54'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Beta-blockers should be avoided in which type of angina?', '["Stable angina","Vasospastic (Prinzmetal) angina","Unstable angina","Post-MI angina","Exertional angina"]'::jsonb, 'Vasospastic (Prinzmetal) angina', 'Beta-blockers can worsen vasospastic angina by leaving alpha-mediated coronary vasoconstriction unopposed.'),
('76847066-a9c1-5a91-8445-93f380e4ac5f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Verapamil should NOT be combined with beta-blockers because of:', '["Hyperkalemia resulting in decreased functional activity","Severe bradycardia and heart block","Hypokalemia mediated by specific receptor activation","Hyperglycemia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hyperlipidemia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Severe bradycardia and heart block', 'Both slow AV conduction and reduce contractility. Combining can cause cardiac arrest.'),
('83ff748d-da2a-530e-ab0c-8d3b1e0b20d0'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Nicorandil is an antianginal that acts by:', '["Blocking calcium channels resulting in decreased functional activity","Opening potassium channels and releasing nitric oxide","Blocking beta receptors mediated by specific receptor activation","Blocking sodium channels without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inhibiting ACE leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Opening potassium channels and releasing nitric oxide', 'Nicorandil is a hybrid with nitrate-like activity and potassium channel opening activity.'),
('0b9fd94a-45cd-5964-b807-b0d08cb88897'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Ranolazine is particularly useful in angina that is:', '["Only at rest","Refractory to traditional antianginals","Only with exercise","Only in the morning","Only in winter"]'::jsonb, 'Refractory to traditional antianginals', 'Ranolazine is indicated for chronic angina refractory to traditional antianginals.'),
('d58bf967-84e6-5bb1-9ab7-df9eb443a595'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'The most common side effect of sublingual nitroglycerin is:', '["Bradycardia resulting in decreased functional activity","Headache due to meningeal vasodilation","Hypokalemia mediated by specific receptor activation","Hyperglycemia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Renal failure leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Headache due to meningeal vasodilation', 'Headache is most common, caused by meningeal vasodilation.'),
('488f2ec7-4215-5511-bb3c-9fc6fe65cc91'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Nitrates should NOT be combined with PDE-5 inhibitors (e.g., sildenafil) because of:', '["Reduced efficacy of nitrates","Severe, potentially fatal hypotension","Increased bleeding risk","Hyperkalemia","Renal failure"]'::jsonb, 'Severe, potentially fatal hypotension', 'PDE-5 inhibitors prevent cGMP degradation, potentiating nitrate-induced vasodilation causing severe hypotension.'),
('086ba187-bb9b-56a9-8567-19ac44d10bb2'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Amlodipine in angina reduces myocardial oxygen demand by:', '["Decreasing heart rate","Decreasing afterload (peripheral vasodilation)","Decreasing contractility","Increasing preload","Increasing coronary blood flow only"]'::jsonb, 'Decreasing afterload (peripheral vasodilation)', 'Amlodipine reduces afterload by peripheral arterial vasodilation.'),
('edcb3312-c4c7-5acf-815c-b5ac7196c7a5'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Diltiazem differs from verapamil in that it:', '["Has greater negative inotropic effect","Has less negative inotropic effect","Is a pure vasodilator","Has beta-blocking activity","Is a Class III antiarrhythmic"]'::jsonb, 'Has less negative inotropic effect', 'Diltiazem has less negative inotropic effect than verapamil.'),
('2b4eedd8-81f5-53e9-99e3-6d4134ee7dc5'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Beta-blockers should be withdrawn gradually because:', '["They are addictive resulting in decreased functional activity","Abrupt withdrawal can cause rebound angina or MI due to upregulated beta receptors","They cause sedation mediated by specific receptor activation","They cause weight gain without affecting the primary tissue pathway","They cause sexual dysfunction leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Abrupt withdrawal can cause rebound angina or MI due to upregulated beta receptors', 'Abrupt withdrawal can cause rebound sympathetic hyperactivity with angina, hypertension, and MI.'),
('e9a38b67-bd1c-5c23-b336-b5a88bb1d21f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Dipyridamole is used in myocardial perfusion imaging because it:', '["Increases heart rate resulting in decreased functional activity","Causes coronary vasodilation, revealing reduced coronary flow reserve","Increases blood pressure mediated by specific receptor activation","Decreases heart rate without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Causes vasoconstriction leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Causes coronary vasodilation, revealing reduced coronary flow reserve', 'Dipyridamole causes coronary vasodilation, revealing perfusion defects in stenotic areas.'),
('998da8ca-0177-59b7-a395-9652a9007d13'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'In vasospastic angina, the drug of choice is:', '["Beta-blockers","Calcium channel blockers","Digoxin","ACE inhibitors","Diuretics"]'::jsonb, 'Calcium channel blockers', 'CCBs are first-line for vasospastic angina by relaxing coronary arterial smooth muscle.'),
('d1354fe9-f5ab-5b4e-b444-929f582be0fa'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Acute coronary vasospasm is treated with:', '["Oral aspirin","IV nitroglycerin","Oral beta-blocker","IV furosemide","Oral digoxin"]'::jsonb, 'IV nitroglycerin', 'IV nitroglycerin is first-line for acute coronary vasospasm.'),
('124a58cd-bfb7-595c-a67a-02eb1cd9af9e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Molsidomine is an antianginal that is a:', '["Direct nitric oxide donor (no sulfhydryl group needed)","Beta-blocker accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Calcium channel blocker mediated by specific receptor activation","Potassium channel opener without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Phosphodiesterase inhibitor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Direct nitric oxide donor (no sulfhydryl group needed)', 'Molsidomine directly releases nitric oxide without requiring sulfhydryl groups.'),
('071cb341-ae10-55ea-a664-4d41ebb163a9'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Ivabradine in angina reduces oxygen demand by:', '["Reducing blood pressure","Reducing heart rate","Reducing contractility","Vasodilation","Reducing preload"]'::jsonb, 'Reducing heart rate', 'Ivabradine selectively reduces heart rate by inhibiting the If current.'),
('4092235f-aee5-5598-99b7-70b0be22fbf6'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'The coronary steal phenomenon can be caused by:', '["Beta-blockers","Dipyridamole","Nitrates","CCBs","Digoxin"]'::jsonb, 'Dipyridamole', 'Dipyridamole dilates normal coronaries more than stenotic ones, diverting blood from ischemic zones.'),
('9a6b57b0-4695-51ab-858e-a474056c42c1'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Statins lower cholesterol by inhibiting:', '["ACAT","HMG-CoA reductase","Lipoprotein lipase","Hepatic lipase","Cholesterol ester transfer protein"]'::jsonb, 'HMG-CoA reductase', 'Statins competitively inhibit HMG-CoA reductase, upregulating LDL receptors.'),
('121ecccd-2233-5323-a59f-dafbaff999d7'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'The most common side effect of statins is:', '["Hepatotoxicity resulting in decreased functional activity","Myopathy (myalgia, myositis, rarely rhabdomyolysis)","Nephrotoxicity mediated by specific receptor activation","Pancreatitis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Gallstones leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Myopathy (myalgia, myositis, rarely rhabdomyolysis)', 'Myopathy occurs in 5-10% of patients. Rhabdomyolysis is rare but serious.'),
('8ef6f3a1-3974-5674-b0f9-f002f9e4fca9'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Pravastatin is unique among statins because it is:', '["A prodrug","Hydrophilic (not lipophilic)","Short-acting","More potent than atorvastatin","A natural statin from red yeast rice"]'::jsonb, 'Hydrophilic (not lipophilic)', 'Pravastatin is hydrophilic, relying on active hepatic uptake, resulting in less muscle penetration.'),
('55e6d819-322b-5616-bb75-12911c1c3b83'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Fibrates lower triglycerides primarily by activating:', '["PPAR-alpha","PPAR-gamma","LXR","FXR","SREBP"]'::jsonb, 'PPAR-alpha', 'Fibrates activate PPAR-alpha, increasing lipoprotein lipase activity and reducing VLDL.'),
('b4f33ce1-833a-52f1-9485-8f1e8c9d45b6'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'The main indication for fibrates is:', '["Elevated LDL cholesterol","Severe hypertriglyceridemia","Low HDL cholesterol","Hypertension","Diabetes mellitus"]'::jsonb, 'Severe hypertriglyceridemia', 'Fibrates are primarily used for severe hypertriglyceridemia (TG >500 mg/dL).'),
('1b52874a-b8e9-5fea-87ce-aa32f66a5452'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Gemfibrozil should NOT be combined with which statin due to rhabdomyolysis risk?', '["Pravastatin","Lovastatin","Atorvastatin","Rosuvastatin","Pitavastatin"]'::jsonb, 'Lovastatin', 'Gemfibrozil inhibits OATP1B1, significantly increasing lovastatin levels and rhabdomyolysis risk.'),
('38c1052a-fc4f-5063-8e45-b4e296c74eca'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Ezetimibe lowers cholesterol by:', '["Inhibiting HMG-CoA reductase","Blocking cholesterol absorption at NPC1L1","Increasing bile acid excretion","Activating PPAR-alpha","Inhibiting CETP"]'::jsonb, 'Blocking cholesterol absorption at NPC1L1', 'Ezetimibe selectively inhibits NPC1L1 at the intestinal brush border.'),
('9ad09129-8138-533b-88c8-4ddb82c2a6a7'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Cholestyramine is a bile acid sequestrant that:', '["Increases cholesterol absorption resulting in decreased functional activity","Binds bile acids in the intestine, preventing reabsorption","Inhibits HMG-CoA reductase mediated by specific receptor activation","Activates PPAR-alpha without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Blocks NPC1L1 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Binds bile acids in the intestine, preventing reabsorption', 'Bile acid sequestrants bind bile acids, forcing the liver to use cholesterol to make more bile acids.'),
('76c1d874-5dc1-5006-85ce-a82b5d8c3773'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'The major side effect of bile acid sequestrants is:', '["Myopathy resulting in decreased functional activity","GI disturbances (constipation, bloating) and can increase triglycerides","Hepatotoxicity mediated by specific receptor activation","Pancreatitis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Nephrotoxicity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'GI disturbances (constipation, bloating) and can increase triglycerides', 'Bile acid sequestrants cause GI side effects and can raise triglycerides.'),
('3e07f7b6-b647-5fed-abcb-e7278c4fd985'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'PCSK9 inhibitors lower LDL by:', '["Inhibiting HMG-CoA reductase","Preventing PCSK9-mediated degradation of LDL receptors","Blocking cholesterol absorption","Binding bile acids","Activating PPAR-alpha"]'::jsonb, 'Preventing PCSK9-mediated degradation of LDL receptors', 'PCSK9 inhibitors prevent PCSK9 from degrading LDL receptors, increasing hepatic LDL clearance.'),
('d3f47489-d6f1-5131-b16d-d884b6848e43'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Niacin lowers lipids primarily by:', '["Inhibiting HMG-CoA reductase resulting in decreased functional activity","Inhibiting lipolysis in adipose tissue, reducing VLDL production","Blocking cholesterol absorption mediated by specific receptor activation","Binding bile acids without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Activating PPAR-alpha leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibiting lipolysis in adipose tissue, reducing VLDL production', 'Niacin inhibits hormone-sensitive lipase, reducing free fatty acid delivery to the liver.'),
('37e33f6b-ff6b-5475-a5c8-2e08304edccc'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'The most common side effect of niacin is:', '["Myopathy resulting in decreased functional activity","Flushing (prostaglandin-mediated)","Hepatotoxicity mediated by specific receptor activation","Hypoglycemia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Nephrotoxicity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Flushing (prostaglandin-mediated)', 'Niacin-induced flushing is mediated by prostaglandins. Aspirin can reduce this.'),
('0f240331-6fd5-5e50-99f0-5ac889f47ff4'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Rosuvastatin is the most potent statin because it:', '["Is a prodrug resulting in decreased functional activity","Has the highest affinity for HMG-CoA reductase binding","Is the longest acting mediated by specific receptor activation","Is hydrophilic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Has the best bioavailability leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Has the highest affinity for HMG-CoA reductase binding', 'Rosuvastatin has the highest binding affinity for HMG-CoA reductase.'),
('4d316c80-b1fa-5c82-a7b3-c76be86cbeeb'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Statins should be avoided in pregnancy because:', '["They are ineffective resulting in decreased functional activity","They are teratogenic (cholesterol needed for fetal development)","They cause hypoglycemia mediated by specific receptor activation","They cause hypertension without affecting the primary tissue pathway","They cause hyperkalemia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'They are teratogenic (cholesterol needed for fetal development)', 'Statins are FDA category X, potentially teratogenic.'),
('b54e40fb-d6c2-5fdc-8630-2bf2f9d1ca59'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'The primary goal of statin therapy in secondary prevention is:', '["Raise HDL","Reduce LDL cholesterol to <70 mg/dL","Raise triglycerides","Reduce blood pressure","Control heart rate"]'::jsonb, 'Reduce LDL cholesterol to <70 mg/dL', 'In secondary prevention, target is LDL <70 mg/dL (NCEP) or <55 mg/dL (ESC).'),
('2dae8377-6aa3-5e0d-8df7-12c4a1763b1f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'CETP inhibitors were developed to:', '["Lower LDL resulting in decreased functional activity","Raise HDL by inhibiting cholesteryl ester transfer protein","Lower triglycerides mediated by specific receptor activation","Raise LDL without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lower blood pressure leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Raise HDL by inhibiting cholesteryl ester transfer protein', 'CETP inhibitors block cholesteryl ester transfer from HDL to VLDL/LDL.'),
('288d7e2a-0aac-5d78-b65d-5b155346b50b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'The landmark 4S trial demonstrated that simvastatin:', '["Had no cardiovascular benefit resulting in decreased functional activity","Reduced cardiovascular mortality and morbidity in CAD patients","Increased mortality mediated by specific receptor activation","Only lowered cholesterol without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Was too toxic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Reduced cardiovascular mortality and morbidity in CAD patients', '4S showed simvastatin reduced coronary events by 42% and total mortality by 30%.'),
('568babe4-e18b-5cf1-82e6-4624ada7ddb0'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Ezetimibe can be safely combined with statins because it acts on:', '["A different pathway (intestinal cholesterol absorption)","Same enzyme as statins","Similar pathway causing excessive LDL reduction","Pathway that causes rhabdomyolysis","Pathway that increases triglycerides"]'::jsonb, 'A different pathway (intestinal cholesterol absorption)', 'Ezetimibe works by inhibiting intestinal cholesterol absorption, complementary to statins.'),
('d11a818e-8685-5541-9bac-f461e2a2eabb'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Bempedoic acid inhibits cholesterol synthesis at:', '["ACAT","ATP-citrate lyase (ACL)","HMG-CoA synthase","Squalene synthase","Lipoprotein lipase"]'::jsonb, 'ATP-citrate lyase (ACL)', 'Bempedoic acid inhibits ATP-citrate lyase, upstream of HMG-CoA reductase.'),
('f058c8a7-b12c-5e88-9bfc-6c3d9de5a231'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Bempedoic acid is particularly useful in patients who are:', '["Statins intolerant","Only hypertensive","Only diabetic","Only obese","Only elderly"]'::jsonb, 'Statins intolerant', 'Bempedoic acid is an alternative for statin-intolerant patients.'),
('b5adec5c-a3fb-5176-8159-cb2dece6087a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Lp(a) is an independent cardiovascular risk factor that is:', '["Highly responsive to statins resulting in decreased functional activity","Primarily genetically determined and poorly responsive to most therapy","Only elevated in diabetics mediated by specific receptor activation","A measure of HDL without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A measure of triglycerides leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Primarily genetically determined and poorly responsive to most therapy', 'Lp(a) levels are 90% genetically determined with minimal response to most lipid-lowering therapy.'),
('6b8837fe-4c6d-5dda-9253-4f9593d008dc'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Icosapent ethyl (purified EPA) is indicated for patients with:', '["Very high LDL only resulting in decreased functional activity","Elevated triglycerides on statin therapy to reduce cardiovascular risk","Low HDL only mediated by specific receptor activation","Hypertension without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Diabetes only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Elevated triglycerides on statin therapy to reduce cardiovascular risk', 'Icosapent ethyl is indicated as adjunctive therapy with elevated TG on statins (REDUCE-IT trial).'),
('f62bb0a8-8aeb-5532-a07e-92225b98d7c4'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Atorvastatin should be dosed:', '["Morning only","Evening only","Any time of day (long half-life)","Bedtime only","Before breakfast"]'::jsonb, 'Any time of day (long half-life)', 'Atorvastatin has a long half-life (14 hours) and can be taken at any time.'),
('52c96a96-10fb-546a-8561-2c17eaa89baf'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Red yeast rice contains a natural statin called:', '["Atorvastatin","Lovastatin (monacolin K)","Pravastatin","Simvastatin","Rosuvastatin"]'::jsonb, 'Lovastatin (monacolin K)', 'Red yeast rice contains monacolin K, chemically identical to lovastatin.'),
('f791cdd6-1e02-5c4a-9da3-203d4a84eaf1'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Fenofibrate reduces triglycerides and can also modestly:', '["Increase LDL cholesterol resulting in decreased functional activity","Reduce LDL cholesterol (especially small dense LDL)","Reduce blood pressure mediated by specific receptor activation","Increase blood sugar without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Reduce heart rate leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Reduce LDL cholesterol (especially small dense LDL)', 'Fenofibrate reduces TG, modestly reduces small dense LDL, and raises HDL.'),
('72ed207a-4be6-53fb-90e6-9d4aa3df8c8a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Heparin enhances the activity of which antithrombin?', '["Antithrombin II","Antithrombin III (AT-III)","Protein C","Protein S","Tissue factor pathway inhibitor"]'::jsonb, 'Antithrombin III (AT-III)', 'Heparin binds AT-III, accelerating inhibition of thrombin and Factor Xa by 1000-fold.'),
('5586eb14-7c04-5ce9-8b70-a7c9abfa7bbc'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Unfractionated heparin primarily inhibits which factors?', '["Factor VII only","Factor IIa and Factor Xa","Factors XI and XII","Factor V only","Factor VIII only"]'::jsonb, 'Factor IIa and Factor Xa', 'UFH inhibits both Factor IIa and Factor Xa (ratio 1:1) via antithrombin III.'),
('f9a1c932-bafd-502a-b3a9-156c5f597889'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Low molecular weight heparins preferentially inhibit:', '["Factor IIa","Factor Xa","Factor VII","Factor V","Factor VIII"]'::jsonb, 'Factor Xa', 'LMWH preferentially inhibit Factor Xa due to shorter heparin chains.'),
('22bd1980-dda2-5184-a996-fdd6da0720e3'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'The antidote for heparin overdose is:', '["Vitamin K","Protamine sulfate","Tranexamic acid","Fresh frozen plasma","Calcium gluconate"]'::jsonb, 'Protamine sulfate', 'Protamine sulfate binds and neutralizes heparin.'),
('b197523c-b30e-5e2a-bb7c-16bd0676e2e2'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (HIT) is mediated by:', '["Direct platelet destruction","IgG antibodies against PF4-heparin complex","Bone marrow suppression","Immune complex deposition","Complement activation"]'::jsonb, 'IgG antibodies against PF4-heparin complex', 'HIT Type II involves IgG antibodies against PF4-heparin complex.'),
('41783707-b801-5a1c-af28-e4ee19ea1642'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'The drug of choice for treating acute HIT is:', '["Warfarin resulting in decreased functional activity","Heparin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Argatroban or bivalirudin","Aspirin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Clopidogrel leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Argatroban or bivalirudin', 'Direct thrombin inhibitors (argatroban, bivalirudin) are used in acute HIT.'),
('0ccca2c6-55e5-5b38-95f4-b00d680314b6'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Warfarin inhibits synthesis of which vitamin K-dependent factors?', '["Factors II, VII, IX, X, and proteins C and S","Factors V and VIII accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Factors XI and XII mediated by specific receptor activation","Fibrinogen without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Factor XIII leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Factors II, VII, IX, X, and proteins C and S', 'Warfarin inhibits VKORC1, preventing gamma-carboxylation of factors II, VII, IX, X, and proteins C and S.'),
('dca6a0ae-3003-5ae9-b9cb-d1801ea0deda'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Warfarin effect is monitored by:', '["aPTT","PT (prothrombin time) / INR","Bleeding time","Platelet count","Fibrinogen level"]'::jsonb, 'PT (prothrombin time) / INR', 'Warfarin is monitored by PT/INR. Target INR 2.0-3.0 for most indications.'),
('ad4646f7-4c3d-59c8-941b-b38d44d4de28'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'The antidote for warfarin toxicity is:', '["Protamine sulfate","Vitamin K (phytonadione)","Tranexamic acid","Calcium gluconate","Naloxone"]'::jsonb, 'Vitamin K (phytonadione)', 'Vitamin K reverses warfarin by restoring synthesis of vitamin K-dependent factors.'),
('e8ff1e3f-f3de-57ac-99af-3a496224869d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Protein C is depleted before factors II, VII, IX, X because protein C has:', '["A longer half-life","A shorter half-life (6-8 hours)","No relationship to vitamin K","A longer onset of action","No role in coagulation"]'::jsonb, 'A shorter half-life (6-8 hours)', 'Protein C has a shorter half-life, leading to transient hypercoagulability when warfarin is initiated.'),
('fd16dd76-4c94-5977-a155-31d0c90f76a6'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Apixaban is a DOAC that directly inhibits:', '["Thrombin (Factor IIa)","Factor Xa","Factor VII","Factor IX","Factor V"]'::jsonb, 'Factor Xa', 'Apixaban is a direct Factor Xa inhibitor.'),
('55da9c4e-e12d-56de-a1c3-8a4333e83af8'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Dabigatran is a DOAC that directly inhibits:', '["Factor Xa","Thrombin (Factor IIa)","Factor VII","Factor IX","Antithrombin III"]'::jsonb, 'Thrombin (Factor IIa)', 'Dabigatran is a direct thrombin inhibitor.'),
('3aa8d018-66bb-5f3e-8b26-7fec54b03baf'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'The specific antidote for dabigatran is:', '["Vitamin K","Protamine sulfate","Idarucizumab","Andexanet alfa","Tranexamic acid"]'::jsonb, 'Idarucizumab', 'Idarucizumab specifically binds and neutralizes dabigatran.'),
('828d8e07-d6d5-5d89-8465-24412282620e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'The specific antidote for Factor Xa inhibitors is:', '["Vitamin K","Protamine sulfate","Idarucizumab","Andexanet alfa","Tranexamic acid"]'::jsonb, 'Andexanet alfa', 'Andexanet alfa acts as a decoy, binding Factor Xa inhibitors.'),
('424d0a03-7abf-5a85-ab52-355de18b1301'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Unlike warfarin, DOACs generally do NOT require:', '["Monitoring of PT/INR","Dose adjustment in renal impairment","Avoidance of grapefruit juice","Heparin bridging","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Monitoring of PT/INR', 'DOACs have predictable pharmacokinetics and do not require routine monitoring.'),
('65c80bbb-f8f2-5b79-8734-136631d75013'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Rivaroxaban should be taken with food because:', '["Food increases its bioavailability significantly","Food reduces its side effects","Food prevents GI bleeding","Food increases its excretion","Food has no effect"]'::jsonb, 'Food increases its bioavailability significantly', 'Rivaroxaban bioavailability is 100% with food vs 66% fasting.'),
('0f355367-dcb3-57e6-86ae-b83eaf0b4094'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Heparin is preferred over warfarin in pregnancy because heparin:', '["Is more effective","Does not cross the placenta","Is cheaper","Has fewer side effects","Is taken orally"]'::jsonb, 'Does not cross the placenta', 'Heparin does not cross the placenta. Warfarin is teratogenic.'),
('5731c360-97d5-5b9e-a342-44515c26ba31'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Fondaparinux selectively inhibits:', '["Thrombin resulting in decreased functional activity","Factor Xa (via antithrombin III)","Factor VII mediated by specific receptor activation","Factor IX without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Factor V leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Factor Xa (via antithrombin III)', 'Fondaparinux is a synthetic pentasaccharide that selectively inhibits Factor Xa.'),
('a535c0a8-f83f-5211-b96e-412504125b2e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'The target INR for warfarin in atrial fibrillation is:', '["1.0-1.5","2.0-3.0","3.0-4.0","4.0-5.0","5.0-6.0"]'::jsonb, '2.0-3.0', 'Target INR is 2.0-3.0 for AF. Higher target (2.5-3.5) for mechanical heart valves.'),
('7f6af55b-b467-51ba-b23b-11ca101d5d1d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'HIT typically occurs when platelet count falls by:', '["10%","20-50% from baseline","90%","No change","Increases"]'::jsonb, '20-50% from baseline', 'HIT is diagnosed when platelets fall by 20-50% from baseline, usually 5-10 days after initiation.'),
('4d3edc9b-60ee-5ebe-a679-8065293dffaa'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'In warfarin therapy, vitamin K-rich foods should be:', '["Avoided completely","In consistent amounts day-to-day","In large amounts daily","Only once a week","Only during bleeding"]'::jsonb, 'In consistent amounts day-to-day', 'Vitamin K-rich foods should be consumed consistently to maintain stable INR.'),
('39afb9af-1841-5013-aef2-34d34ae04d46'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Metronidazole interacts with warfarin by:', '["Increasing warfarin effect via CYP2C9 inhibition","Decreasing warfarin effect","No interaction","Causing bleeding directly","Causing hyperkalemia"]'::jsonb, 'Increasing warfarin effect via CYP2C9 inhibition', 'Metronidazole inhibits CYP2C9, reducing warfarin metabolism and increasing its effect.'),
('7adc4806-14b1-55e2-8cdb-8bb2b36d93d0'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Protamine is less effective in reversing LMWH because:', '["LMWH is more potent","Protamine binds less effectively to shorter LMWH chains","LMWH has a longer half-life","LMWH is excreted by kidneys","LMWH is not cleared by the liver"]'::jsonb, 'Protamine binds less effectively to shorter LMWH chains', 'Protamine binds less effectively to LMWH (shorter chains) than to UFH.'),
('f11089fa-3726-5a21-a104-fa47a6be9d43'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Aspirin inhibits platelet aggregation by:', '["Blocking ADP receptors resulting in decreased functional activity","Irreversibly inhibiting COX-1, reducing thromboxane A2 synthesis","Blocking GPIIb/IIIa receptors mediated by specific receptor activation","Inhibiting phosphodiesterase without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Blocking thrombin receptors leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Irreversibly inhibiting COX-1, reducing thromboxane A2 synthesis', 'Aspirin irreversibly acetylates COX-1, preventing TXA2 synthesis.'),
('b023a2ac-ba09-50ae-8a9c-fa45dc4cb0a9'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Aspirin''s antiplatelet effect lasts for the platelet lifespan because:', '["It has a long half-life resulting in decreased functional activity","COX-1 inhibition is irreversible and platelets cannot synthesize new COX-1","It accumulates in tissues mediated by specific receptor activation","It inhibits platelet production without affecting the primary tissue pathway","It enhances platelet clearance leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'COX-1 inhibition is irreversible and platelets cannot synthesize new COX-1', 'Platelets are anucleate and cannot synthesize new COX-1.'),
('68fe11b1-c752-59b3-b47e-73a58902c643'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Clopidogrel is a prodrug requiring activation by:', '["CYP2D6","CYP2C19","CYP3A4","CYP1A2","CYP2E1"]'::jsonb, 'CYP2C19', 'Clopidogrel requires hepatic bioactivation by CYP2C19.'),
('ed1d1dcf-5143-5ba7-beb5-e7e412f87322'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Ticagrelor differs from clopidogrel and prasugrel in that it is:', '["A prodrug","A reversible P2Y12 antagonist","An irreversible P2Y12 antagonist","A COX inhibitor","A GPIIb/IIIa antagonist"]'::jsonb, 'A reversible P2Y12 antagonist', 'Ticagrelor is a direct-acting, reversible P2Y12 antagonist.'),
('45c66d81-e6d2-5acf-892d-0cf25fb03f61'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Prasugrel has faster onset than clopidogrel because:', '["It does not require hepatic activation","It is activated more efficiently by CYP enzymes","It is given IV","It binds directly to P2Y12","It has a longer half-life"]'::jsonb, 'It is activated more efficiently by CYP enzymes', 'Prasugrel is more efficiently activated with less genetic variability.'),
('79a27994-2203-57fe-beb0-ca86529004e6'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Prasugrel is CONTRAINDICATED in patients with:', '["Diabetes","History of stroke or TIA","Hypertension","Atrial fibrillation","Hyperlipidemia"]'::jsonb, 'History of stroke or TIA', 'Prasugrel is contraindicated in prior stroke/TIA due to increased intracranial hemorrhage risk.'),
('02866e31-e5dd-5081-afd3-024dffd10633'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Abciximab blocks which platelet receptor?', '["P2Y12","GPIIb/IIIa","COX-1","PAR-1","EP3"]'::jsonb, 'GPIIb/IIIa', 'Abciximab is a monoclonal antibody fragment binding GPIIb/IIIa receptors.'),
('6c4f7201-2233-5088-b986-35460fde90f3'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Eptifibatide is a GPIIb/IIIa inhibitor that is:', '["A monoclonal antibody","A cyclic heptapeptide","A small molecule","A thienopyridine","A nitrate"]'::jsonb, 'A cyclic heptapeptide', 'Eptifibatide is a cyclic heptapeptide containing the KGD sequence.'),
('fdcfd80f-3e1a-5269-8554-17ce3e9bd57a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Tirofiban is a GPIIb/IIIa inhibitor that is:', '["A monoclonal antibody","A cyclic peptide","A non-peptide small molecule","A thienopyridine","A nitrate"]'::jsonb, 'A non-peptide small molecule', 'Tirofiban is a non-peptide small molecule GPIIb/IIIa inhibitor.'),
('d5558bae-334c-55ae-8a12-4570a4eaf8e5'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Dipyridamole inhibits platelet aggregation by:', '["Blocking GPIIb/IIIa","Inhibiting phosphodiesterase, increasing cAMP","Blocking P2Y12","Irreversibly inhibiting COX-1","Blocking thrombin receptors"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibiting phosphodiesterase, increasing cAMP', 'Dipyridamole inhibits PDE and blocks adenosine uptake, increasing cAMP.'),
('10b681e2-cc97-5899-af95-ef96ec856a32'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Aspirin is the drug of choice for secondary prevention of:', '["Only stroke resulting in decreased functional activity","MI, ischemic stroke, and peripheral arterial disease","Only MI mediated by specific receptor activation","Only PAD without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'MI, ischemic stroke, and peripheral arterial disease', 'Aspirin is cornerstone for secondary prevention of atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease.'),
('e711e125-d64d-5415-8f78-5a6b92800ce8'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'DAPT with aspirin + clopidogrel is standard after:', '["CABG only","PCI with stent placement","Valve replacement","Heart failure","Arrhythmia ablation"]'::jsonb, 'PCI with stent placement', 'DAPT is standard for 6-12 months after PCI with drug-eluting stent.'),
('8b81518c-5257-545a-96dd-d349538f73b2'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'The most significant risk of DAPT is:', '["Heart failure","Bleeding","Hypertension","Arrhythmias","Renal failure"]'::jsonb, 'Bleeding', 'Bleeding is the major complication of DAPT.'),
('9134d7ba-0a3c-598b-be4e-b233c8c7de05'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Cangrelor is a GPIIb/IIIa inhibitor that is:', '["Oral resulting in decreased functional activity","Intravenous with ultra-short duration","Subcutaneous mediated by specific receptor activation","Transdermal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inhaled leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Intravenous with ultra-short duration', 'Cangrelor is IV with rapid onset and very short half-life (3-6 minutes).'),
('4ff8bdee-4d2f-5933-8a86-eb357511df88'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Ticagrelor causes dyspnea because it:', '["Causes bronchospasm resulting in decreased functional activity","Inhibits adenosine reuptake, increasing adenosine levels","Blocks beta-2 receptors mediated by specific receptor activation","Causes pulmonary fibrosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Causes pulmonary embolism leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibits adenosine reuptake, increasing adenosine levels', 'Ticagrelor inhibits adenosine reuptake, stimulating pulmonary C-fibers.'),
('e64cb5f4-b11f-534f-a86a-6d3e345f4937'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Aspirin should NOT be used for primary prevention in patients over 70 because:', '["It is ineffective","Bleeding risk outweighs cardiovascular benefit","It causes too much pain","It is too expensive","It interacts with food"]'::jsonb, 'Bleeding risk outweighs cardiovascular benefit', 'In elderly (>70), bleeding risk with aspirin for primary prevention outweighs benefit (ASPREE trial).'),
('e94a7665-b0e0-5250-8d70-4c948bfed60e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'In ACS, the loading dose of clopidogrel is:', '["25 mg","75 mg","300-600 mg","1000 mg","150 mg"]'::jsonb, '300-600 mg', 'Loading dose of 300-600 mg provides more rapid platelet inhibition.'),
('578756d2-6eea-50b6-b8c5-89836a309b00'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Prasugrel provides more consistent platelet inhibition because:', '["It has a longer half-life resulting in decreased functional activity","It has fewer genetic polymorphisms affecting activation","It is not a prodrug mediated by specific receptor activation","It is given IV without affecting the primary tissue pathway","It is a GPIIb/IIIa inhibitor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'It has fewer genetic polymorphisms affecting activation', 'Clopidogrel activation is affected by CYP2C19 polymorphisms. Prasugrel has less variability.'),
('5d590d28-d322-555d-b11e-fb266d0956b3'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Vorapaxar is an antiplatelet drug that acts as a:', '["P2Y12 antagonist","PAR-1 antagonist","COX-1 inhibitor","GPIIb/IIIa antagonist","PDE-3 inhibitor"]'::jsonb, 'PAR-1 antagonist', 'Vorapaxar blocks PAR-1 (thrombin receptor), blocking thrombin-mediated platelet activation.'),
('05edd866-f1dd-5ae7-b813-3c68da994c85'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Vorapaxar is CONTRAINDICATED in patients with:', '["Diabetes","History of stroke or TIA","Hypertension","Atrial fibrillation","Renal failure"]'::jsonb, 'History of stroke or TIA', 'Vorapaxar is contraindicated in prior stroke/TIA due to increased intracranial bleeding risk.'),
('4c0a2b46-58d6-5ebd-bc8d-21cbc072dea6'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'The GPIIb/IIIa receptor is important because it is:', '["The receptor for von Willebrand factor only resulting in decreased functional activity","The final common pathway for platelet aggregation (binds fibrinogen)","A collagen receptor mediated by specific receptor activation","An ADP receptor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A thromboxane receptor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The final common pathway for platelet aggregation (binds fibrinogen)', 'GPIIb/IIIa is the final common pathway for platelet aggregation, binding fibrinogen.'),
('7b7b29fe-da74-537e-a260-774d0d79604c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Dipyridamole + aspirin (Aggrenox) is primarily used for:', '["Acute MI resulting in decreased functional activity","Secondary prevention of stroke","Heart failure mediated by specific receptor activation","Hypertension without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Diabetes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Secondary prevention of stroke', 'The combination is primarily used for secondary prevention of ischemic stroke.'),
('7d8da1a0-1d69-5c40-a47b-7dec1ce47d91'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'The maintenance dose of clopidogrel is:', '["25 mg daily","75 mg daily","300 mg daily","600 mg daily","150 mg daily"]'::jsonb, '75 mg daily', 'Standard maintenance dose is 75 mg daily.'),
('9e20bd50-ea41-54ce-a02b-d6a41b2870ad'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Aspirin-induced asthma is characterized by:', '["Bronchodilation resulting in decreased functional activity","Triad of asthma, nasal polyps, and aspirin sensitivity","Improved lung function mediated by specific receptor activation","No respiratory symptoms without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only nasal congestion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Triad of asthma, nasal polyps, and aspirin sensitivity', 'AERD is characterized by asthma, nasal polyposis, and aspirin/NSAID sensitivity.'),
('17b001c3-a26e-5275-b527-aeab69b85c9d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Streptokinase is obtained from:', '["Human plasma","Streptococcus species","Recombinant technology","Staphylococcus species","E. coli"]'::jsonb, 'Streptococcus species', 'Streptokinase is produced by beta-hemolytic streptococci. It is antigenic.'),
('988bb45b-df14-5a90-8cb7-d4921a94471e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Alteplase (tPA) is:', '["Non-fibrin-specific","Fibrin-specific (acts on clot-bound plasminogen)","Obtained from bacteria","An antigenic protein","Given subcutaneously only"]'::jsonb, 'Fibrin-specific (acts on clot-bound plasminogen)', 'Alteplase preferentially activates plasminogen bound to fibrin within thrombi.'),
('0625a9ba-a5cc-55ce-b9ad-d2665be9b4cc'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'The fibrin-specific thrombolytic agents are:', '["Streptokinase and urokinase","Alteplase, tenecteplase, and reteplase","Streptokinase only","Heparin and warfarin","Aspirin and clopidogrel"]'::jsonb, 'Alteplase, tenecteplase, and reteplase', 'Alteplase, tenecteplase, and reteplase are fibrin-specific.'),
('d80181be-ff6b-5a2c-9e3b-9731813ff16d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Tenecteplase differs from alteplase in having:', '["Shorter half-life and fibrin specificity","Longer half-life allowing single-bolus dosing","Less fibrin specificity","More antigenicity","Oral formulation"]'::jsonb, 'Longer half-life allowing single-bolus dosing', 'Tenecteplase has a longer half-life (20 min vs 4 min), allowing single-bolus administration.'),
('c66bf5fb-77c2-52f1-85ae-8b7bd88a7c23'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Reteplase is administered as:', '["Single bolus","Two bolus doses given 30 minutes apart","Continuous infusion over 90 minutes","Subcutaneous injection","Intramuscular injection"]'::jsonb, 'Two bolus doses given 30 minutes apart', 'Reteplase is given as two 10-unit IV bolus injections 30 minutes apart.'),
('697042a2-a934-58d1-9729-c45bd0d5034f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'The main advantage of streptokinase is that it is:', '["More effective than alteplase","Cheaper and non-antigenic","Cheaper (though antigenic)","Fibrin-specific","Given as a single bolus"]'::jsonb, 'Cheaper (though antigenic)', 'Streptokinase is significantly cheaper, making it preferred in resource-limited settings.'),
('83175fdb-fd72-524b-b996-e958635352d9'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'The time window for thrombolysis in acute MI is ideally within:', '["1 hour resulting in decreased functional activity","3 hours (up to 12 hours in selected patients)","24 hours mediated by specific receptor activation","1 week without affecting the primary tissue pathway","No time limit leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '3 hours (up to 12 hours in selected patients)', 'Most effective within 3 hours but can be considered up to 12 hours.'),
('1ca80e80-e26e-5f86-87b6-6ed73a853427'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Absolute contraindications to thrombolysis include all EXCEPT:', '["Active internal bleeding","Recent ischemic stroke (3 months)","Uncontrolled hypertension","Elevated troponin","Intracranial neoplasm"]'::jsonb, 'Elevated troponin', 'Elevated troponin is NOT a contraindication—it is diagnostic for MI.'),
('df52f141-9607-5115-bd29-b20eca4c5ff6'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Anistreplase (APSAC) is:', '["Fibrin-specific resulting in decreased functional activity","A streptokinase-plasminogen complex that is acylated (prodrug)","Recombinant tPA mediated by specific receptor activation","Urokinase without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A direct plasmin activator leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A streptokinase-plasminogen complex that is acylated (prodrug)', 'Anistreplase is an acylated complex activated in vivo, allowing bolus dosing.'),
('33196a39-4ee0-5aa3-9ad8-716151da9a85'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Urokinase differs from streptokinase in that it is:', '["Antigenic resulting in decreased functional activity","Non-antigenic (obtained from human sources or recombinant)","Fibrin-specific mediated by specific receptor activation","A bacterial product without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Given as single bolus only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Non-antigenic (obtained from human sources or recombinant)', 'Urokinase is non-antigenic unlike streptokinase.'),
('f8e77252-17bb-5c3d-bb99-27777dd03d60'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'PCI is preferred over thrombolysis in STEMI when:', '["Available within 120 minutes of first medical contact","Not available within 120 minutes","Patient has contraindications to thrombolysis","Both A and C","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Both A and C', 'Primary PCI is preferred when available within 120 min or when thrombolysis is contraindicated.'),
('f961b095-b29c-5419-a8ae-716d5041f329'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'The most feared complication of thrombolytic therapy is:', '["Hypotension","Intracranial hemorrhage","Allergic reaction","Fever","Nausea"]'::jsonb, 'Intracranial hemorrhage', 'Intracranial hemorrhage occurs in 0.5-1% and can be fatal.'),
('0281fdb0-a332-54eb-94a0-2f9d99885fc7'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Reperfusion arrhythmias after thrombolysis are usually:', '["Life-threatening VF","Benign and self-limiting","Complete heart block","Atrial fibrillation","VT requiring treatment"]'::jsonb, 'Benign and self-limiting', 'Reperfusion arrhythmias (e.g., AIVR) are typically benign and self-limiting.'),
('98232264-c03b-5e76-94ab-7627f27337fd'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Tenecteplase is administered as:', '["Continuous infusion over 90 minutes","Single IV bolus over 5-10 seconds","Two bolus doses 30 minutes apart","Subcutaneous injection","Intramuscular injection"]'::jsonb, 'Single IV bolus over 5-10 seconds', 'Tenecteplase is given as a single weight-based IV bolus.'),
('2f314c78-56f4-56c8-9243-cea37d8bf5e1'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Alteplase for acute MI is administered as:', '["Single bolus resulting in decreased functional activity","Weight-based regimen over 90 minutes (accelerated regimen)","Continuous infusion over 24 hours mediated by specific receptor activation","Subcutaneous injection without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Intramuscular injection leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Weight-based regimen over 90 minutes (accelerated regimen)', 'Alteplase for STEMI: 15 mg bolus, then 0.75 mg/kg over 30 min, then 0.5 mg/kg over 60 min.'),
('e95500f8-d869-5c4c-b789-d364911431c7'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Fibrinolytic therapy in acute ischemic stroke is most effective within:', '["1 hour resulting in decreased functional activity","4.5 hours of symptom onset","12 hours mediated by specific receptor activation","24 hours without affecting the primary tissue pathway","No time limit leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '4.5 hours of symptom onset', 'IV alteplase is approved within 4.5 hours of symptom onset.'),
('fb388b64-c14d-5650-9c09-ebf9d2c75aa4'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Alteplase dose for acute ischemic stroke is:', '["100 mg resulting in decreased functional activity","0.9 mg/kg (max 90 mg)","150 mg mediated by specific receptor activation","50 mg without affecting the primary tissue pathway","200 mg leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '0.9 mg/kg (max 90 mg)', 'Standard dose is 0.9 mg/kg (max 90 mg).'),
('d28a8c23-bd35-5274-828b-5cea41c4b7af'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Tenecteplase for acute ischemic stroke is being studied at doses of:', '["0.1 mg/kg","0.25 mg/kg (max 25 mg)","0.9 mg/kg","1.0 mg/kg","2.0 mg/kg"]'::jsonb, '0.25 mg/kg (max 25 mg)', 'Low-dose tenecteplase (0.25 mg/kg, max 25 mg) is being studied.'),
('4dfe1f82-6783-597c-9ee6-cdb144fcf000'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Urokinase is commonly used for catheter-directed thrombolysis because:', '["It is fibrin-specific resulting in decreased functional activity","It is non-antigenic and can be administered locally via catheter","It is given as single bolus mediated by specific receptor activation","It is the cheapest agent without affecting the primary tissue pathway","It is the most effective agent leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'It is non-antigenic and can be administered locally via catheter', 'Urokinase is non-antigenic and suitable for catheter-directed thrombolysis.'),
('33de5592-e678-5024-bd88-a2eb8b62b975'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'After successful thrombolysis in STEMI, routine follow-up angiography is recommended within:', '["24-48 hours resulting in decreased functional activity","3-24 hours (routine early angiography)","2 weeks mediated by specific receptor activation","1 month without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Not recommended leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '3-24 hours (routine early angiography)', 'Routine early angiography (3-24 hours) is recommended after successful thrombolysis.'),
('8dfe1f0e-6d64-5113-b0cd-0cd96ac8c2b3'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Fibrinolytic therapy is most beneficial in STEMI when given within the first hour because:', '["Clots are fresher and more susceptible to lysis","There is less myocardial necrosis","Side effects are reduced","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Early thrombolysis is most effective with fresher clots, less necrosis, and better benefit-risk.'),
('60c2ba28-cb27-5905-a53c-379b72a36203'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Retavase (reteplase) is a deletion mutant of alteplase that lacks:', '["The kringle 1 domain","The finger domain and kringle 1 domain","The EGF domain","The protease domain","All domains"]'::jsonb, 'The finger domain and kringle 1 domain', 'Reteplase lacks the finger and kringle 1 domains, giving longer half-life.'),
('bf892136-c009-5741-addb-817a68431a0a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Streptokinase causes hypotension because it:', '["Causes vasodilation directly","Can activate complement and kinin systems","Causes cardiac depression","Reduces blood volume","Causes bradycardia"]'::jsonb, 'Can activate complement and kinin systems', 'Streptokinase can activate complement and kallikrein-kinin systems, causing vasodilation.'),
('9f844cd4-59bd-560e-a4a2-b6ff24c00346'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Alteplase dose for stroke is given as:', '["100 mg bolus","10% as bolus, remainder infused over 60 minutes","Continuous infusion over 4 hours","Single 50 mg bolus","150 mg over 90 minutes"]'::jsonb, '10% as bolus, remainder infused over 60 minutes', 'For stroke: 10% of dose as bolus, remainder infused over 60 minutes.'),
('2724f012-7c37-5ce2-9d52-ccfcb565fa7d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Norepinephrine is the first-line vasopressor for septic shock because it:', '["Is a pure alpha-1 agonist resulting in decreased functional activity","Primarily causes vasoconstriction with mild beta-1 stimulation maintaining cardiac output","Is a beta-2 agonist mediated by specific receptor activation","Is a dopamine agonist without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Blocks alpha receptors leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Primarily causes vasoconstriction with mild beta-1 stimulation maintaining cardiac output', 'Norepinephrine has strong alpha-1 and mild beta-1 activity, raising MAP while maintaining cardiac output.'),
('4dfbf209-570a-5075-b4ef-a3d9571794fd'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Epinephrine at therapeutic doses stimulates:', '["Alpha-1 only","Beta-1 and beta-2","Alpha-1, beta-1, and beta-2","Dopamine receptors","Muscarinic receptors"]'::jsonb, 'Alpha-1, beta-1, and beta-2', 'Epinephrine stimulates alpha-1 (vasoconstriction), beta-1 (inotropy), and beta-2 (bronchodilation).'),
('955cdcbe-c80c-5d11-a66f-7b457d50da03'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Dopamine at low doses (1-5 mcg/kg/min) primarily stimulates:', '["Alpha-1 receptors","Beta-1 receptors","Dopamine-1 receptors (renal vasodilation)","Beta-2 receptors","Muscarinic receptors"]'::jsonb, 'Dopamine-1 receptors (renal vasodilation)', 'Low-dose dopamine stimulates dopamine-1 receptors in renal vasculature.'),
('ca5b20e3-110c-5dfe-b526-0bc3bbdf240d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Dopamine at moderate doses (5-10 mcg/kg/min) primarily stimulates:', '["Dopamine-1 receptors resulting in decreased functional activity","Beta-1 receptors (increased cardiac contractility)","Alpha-1 receptors mediated by specific receptor activation","Beta-2 receptors without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Muscarinic receptors leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Beta-1 receptors (increased cardiac contractility)', 'Moderate-dose dopamine stimulates beta-1 receptors, increasing HR and contractility.'),
('f1f234d3-3d33-5c2b-9f2e-59e1033e4006'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Dopamine at high doses (>10 mcg/kg/min) primarily stimulates:', '["Dopamine-1 receptors","Beta-1 receptors","Alpha-1 receptors (vasoconstriction)","Beta-2 receptors","Muscarinic receptors"]'::jsonb, 'Alpha-1 receptors (vasoconstriction)', 'High-dose dopamine primarily stimulates alpha-1 receptors, causing vasoconstriction.'),
('0d129e61-8c05-5016-b787-640b4be497da'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Dobutamine is primarily used in shock with:', '["Hypertension resulting in decreased functional activity","Cardiac dysfunction/low cardiac output","Bradycardia only mediated by specific receptor activation","Tachycardia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hypothermia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cardiac dysfunction/low cardiac output', 'Dobutamine is a beta-1 agonist (positive inotrope) used in cardiogenic shock.'),
('8ed90173-4a5e-5d78-9720-935695571af0'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'The key difference between dobutamine and dopamine is that dobutamine:', '["Is primarily a vasopressor resulting in decreased functional activity","Is primarily a positive inotrope with less chronotropic effect","Has more alpha-1 activity mediated by specific receptor activation","Is used for hypertension without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Is an antiarrhythmic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Is primarily a positive inotrope with less chronotropic effect', 'Dobutamine primarily stimulates beta-1 with less chronotropic effect and mild beta-2 vasodilation.'),
('fd90e9e6-78a7-5ddf-ac09-5dad15b8b633'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Vasopressin is used in septic shock as second-line because it:', '["Stimulates beta receptors resulting in decreased functional activity","Acts on V1 receptors causing vasoconstriction (catecholamine-resistant)","Is a calcium channel blocker mediated by specific receptor activation","Blocks alpha receptors without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Is a diuretic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Acts on V1 receptors causing vasoconstriction (catecholamine-resistant)', 'Vasopressin acts on V1 receptors causing vasoconstriction in catecholamine-resistant shock.'),
('d6d65317-2353-530c-86e0-aa2b2f5f71e1'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Phenylephrine is a pure alpha-1 agonist used in:', '["Cardiogenic shock resulting in decreased functional activity","Vasodilatory shock with tachycardia","Heart failure mediated by specific receptor activation","Bradycardia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Asthma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Vasodilatory shock with tachycardia', 'Phenylephrine raises BP by vasoconstriction without directly increasing heart rate.'),
('8dce440e-3611-50b2-aeda-522592a5adfe'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Milrinone is particularly useful in cardiogenic shock because:', '["It is a pure vasopressor resulting in decreased functional activity","It is an inodilator (positive inotrope + vasodilator)","It causes vasoconstriction mediated by specific receptor activation","It reduces heart rate without affecting the primary tissue pathway","It is a diuretic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'It is an inodilator (positive inotrope + vasodilator)', 'Milrinone inhibits PDE-3, causing both positive inotropy and vasodilation.'),
('d6a22e8b-e019-5d56-b79f-e72a984d374b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'The primary treatment of anaphylactic shock is:', '["Dopamine","Intramuscular epinephrine","Steroids","Antihistamines","IV fluids only"]'::jsonb, 'Intramuscular epinephrine', 'IM epinephrine (0.5 mg of 1:1000) is the first-line treatment for anaphylaxis.'),
('ad5b083a-5c2e-55e8-a25f-31d1fc48c775'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'In cardiogenic shock, the goal is to:', '["Increase afterload resulting in decreased functional activity","Reduce preload and improve cardiac output with inotropes","Increase heart rate only mediated by specific receptor activation","Reduce cardiac output without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cause vasoconstriction leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Reduce preload and improve cardiac output with inotropes', 'Treatment focuses on reducing preload (diuretics, vasodilators) and improving cardiac output (inotropes).'),
('70d8e8ce-122d-52e6-ae23-d14625ebd055'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Dobutamine in cardiogenic shock primarily:', '["Increases peripheral resistance resulting in decreased functional activity","Increases cardiac contractility and cardiac output","Causes bradycardia mediated by specific receptor activation","Reduces blood pressure without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Is a vasopressor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Increases cardiac contractility and cardiac output', 'Dobutamine is a positive inotrope increasing cardiac contractility and output.'),
('066f65c5-f036-517c-b892-cc4d4ed4bd9d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Vasopressin is used in septic shock at doses of approximately:', '["0.01-0.04 units/min","1-10 units/min","50-100 units/min","0.1-0.5 mcg/kg/min","5-10 mg/min"]'::jsonb, '0.01-0.04 units/min', 'Vasopressin is used at low doses (0.01-0.04 units/min) in septic shock.'),
('6cbc9f75-b2cf-5c8e-9242-09851996a519'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Norepinephrine dosing in septic shock typically ranges:', '["0.01-0.1 mcg/kg/min resulting in decreased functional activity","0.1-2 mcg/kg/min (titrated to MAP >=65 mmHg)","10-50 mcg/kg/min mediated by specific receptor activation","100-500 mcg/kg/min without affecting the primary tissue pathway","5-10 mg/min leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '0.1-2 mcg/kg/min (titrated to MAP >=65 mmHg)', 'Norepinephrine is typically titrated from 0.1 to 2 mcg/kg/min targeting MAP >=65 mmHg.'),
('af48d121-58b3-573d-bf76-e5bbf0f07200'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Epinephrine in anaphylaxis is given:', '["Intravenously at high dose resulting in decreased functional activity","Intramuscularly into the anterolateral thigh","Subcutaneously mediated by specific receptor activation","Orally without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inhalationally leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Intramuscularly into the anterolateral thigh', 'Epinephrine is given IM (0.3-0.5 mg of 1:1000) into the anterolateral thigh.'),
('82c2aa92-ce25-5104-9f9e-61744f9d02fd'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'In distributive shock (sepsis), the primary hemodynamic problem is:', '["Low cardiac output","Vasodilation with relative hypovolemia","Cardiac tamponade","Tension pneumothorax","Pulmonary embolism"]'::jsonb, 'Vasodilation with relative hypovolemia', 'Distributive shock is characterized by vasodilation, decreased SVR, and relative hypovolemia.'),
('6e9eec32-fd8f-5058-b84f-a6150cf93964'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Dopamine in cardiogenic shock can be used because it:', '["Only causes vasoconstriction resulting in decreased functional activity","Increases cardiac contractility (beta-1) at moderate doses","Reduces heart rate mediated by specific receptor activation","Is a pure vasodilator without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Has no cardiac effects leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Increases cardiac contractility (beta-1) at moderate doses', 'Dopamine at moderate doses increases contractility via beta-1 stimulation.'),
('8d25eb01-88d5-5249-a20b-275bf39b2f25'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'The target MAP in septic shock management is:', '["50 mmHg","65 mmHg or higher","80 mmHg","100 mmHg","120 mmHg"]'::jsonb, '65 mmHg or higher', 'Current guidelines target MAP >=65 mmHg in septic shock.'),
('16abd9f9-2a24-597c-8d85-ea85004bc283'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'In hypovolemic shock, the primary treatment is:', '["Vasopressors resulting in decreased functional activity","Fluid resuscitation and blood transfusion","Inotropes mediated by specific receptor activation","Diuretics without affecting the primary tissue pathway","ACE inhibitors leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fluid resuscitation and blood transfusion', 'Volume replacement (crystalloids, colloids, blood products) is the cornerstone of hypovolemic shock treatment.'),
('9f013332-3dbe-5d06-8b2b-b8fb82c2d1c1'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Epinephrine in cardiac arrest is given IV at a dose of:', '["0.01 mg","0.1 mg (1 mL of 1:10,000)","1 mg (10 mL of 1:10,000)","10 mg","100 mg"]'::jsonb, '1 mg (10 mL of 1:10,000)', 'Epinephrine is given as 1 mg IV every 3-5 minutes during cardiac arrest.'),
('e88048f8-e8f1-509f-9ffb-a440b17a5dc9'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Norepinephrine has more alpha-1 than beta-1 activity, making it:', '["More of an inotrope than a vasopressor","More of a vasopressor than an inotrope","Equal inotrope and vasopressor","A pure inotrope","A pure vasodilator"]'::jsonb, 'More of a vasopressor than an inotrope', 'Norepinephrine is predominantly an alpha-1 agonist (vasopressor) with some beta-1 (inotrope) activity.'),
('78ef3f89-c5a7-5999-8e6b-9630e6d5b560'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'The SURVIVE Sepsis Campaign recommends norepinephrine as first-line because:', '["It is the cheapest vasopressor","It has the best evidence for efficacy in septic shock","It has no side effects","It is given orally","It is the only vasopressor available"]'::jsonb, 'It has the best evidence for efficacy in septic shock', 'Norepinephrine has the strongest evidence base for efficacy in septic shock.'),
('65b55146-9bd8-55c3-9e27-c6153132976c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Vasopressin can be added to norepinephrine in septic shock to:', '["Replace norepinephrine completely resulting in decreased functional activity","Reduce the required dose of norepinephrine (catecholamine-sparing)","Increase heart rate mediated by specific receptor activation","Cause vasodilation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Reduce cardiac output leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Reduce the required dose of norepinephrine (catecholamine-sparing)', 'Vasopressin is catecholamine-sparing and can be added to reduce norepinephrine dose.'),
('1e72d7e0-f021-5400-9bec-b8b30e3f120b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Dobutamine should be used with caution in shock because:', '["It is a vasopressor resulting in decreased functional activity","It can cause hypotension via beta-2 mediated vasodilation","It increases afterload mediated by specific receptor activation","It causes bradycardia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","It is a diuretic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'It can cause hypotension via beta-2 mediated vasodilation', 'Dobutamine has some beta-2 activity causing vasodilation, which can worsen hypotension.'),
('676c532c-ab0b-5306-be46-c11dfef74467'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Epinephrine in severe sepsis/septic shock can be used when:', '["Norepinephrine is unavailable resulting in decreased functional activity","As a second-line agent when norepinephrine is insufficient","Never mediated by specific receptor activation","Only in cardiac arrest without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only in anaphylaxis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'As a second-line agent when norepinephrine is insufficient', 'Epinephrine can be used as second-line or adjunct in severe sepsis.'),
('2e6f61f1-68f6-5467-8c91-34bb6be4a4ad'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Phenylephrine is used in shock with:', '["Bradycardia resulting in decreased functional activity","Tachycardia (when increasing HR is undesirable)","Low cardiac output mediated by specific receptor activation","Heart failure without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Arrhythmias leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tachycardia (when increasing HR is undesirable)', 'Phenylephrine is useful when tachycardia is undesirable.'),
('ae92d58a-df0b-519f-9370-d71d9afef85b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'In anaphylactic shock, IM epinephrine should be repeated every:', '["1 minute resulting in decreased functional activity","5-15 minutes if no improvement","60 minutes mediated by specific receptor activation","4 hours without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Never repeat leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '5-15 minutes if no improvement', 'IM epinephrine can be repeated every 5-15 minutes if symptoms persist.'),
('1b2636af-e189-5dd2-995c-187fdcc0832e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular Blood - CV Drugs', 'Vasopressin acts on which receptor in vascular smooth muscle?', '["Beta-1","V1 receptors","Alpha-1","M3 muscarinic","D1 receptors"]'::jsonb, 'V1 receptors', 'Vasopressin acts on V1 receptors on vascular smooth muscle, causing vasoconstriction.'),
('8df94dbc-4e77-575b-888f-e81ef47c53de'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which of the following PPIs is most commonly used for GERD?', '["Omeprazole","Lansoprazole","Pantoprazole","Rabeprazole","Esomeprazole"]'::jsonb, 'Omeprazole', 'Omeprazole is the most commonly prescribed PPI for GERD due to its efficacy, availability, and cost-effectiveness.'),
('3665dca1-ef59-5b67-902d-ee5f9d3910b3'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'PPIs exert their antisecretory effect by:', '["H2 receptor blockade","Irreversible inhibition of H+/K+ ATPase","Muscarinic receptor blockade","Prostaglandin synthesis","Gastrin receptor antagonism"]'::jsonb, 'Irreversible inhibition of H+/K+ ATPase', 'PPIs irreversibly inhibit the H+/K+ ATPase (proton pump) on gastric parietal cells, reducing acid secretion.'),
('4d3e14e6-9d0b-5316-a423-a99bb10a33e0'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which PPI has the least cytochrome P450 interactions?', '["Omeprazole","Esomeprazole","Lansoprazole","Pantoprazole","Rabeprazole"]'::jsonb, 'Pantoprazole', 'Pantoprazole has the least CYP450 interactions, making it safer for patients on multiple medications.'),
('853c6cb3-de55-5e1d-87f0-4e9d74d6396d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Mechanism of action of sucralfate in peptic ulcer disease:', '["Proton pump inhibition","H2 receptor blockade","Forms a protective barrier over ulcer at acidic pH","Stimulates prostaglandin synthesis","Neutralizes gastric acid"]'::jsonb, 'Forms a protective barrier over ulcer at acidic pH', 'Sucralfate polymerizes in acidic pH to form a protective paste that adheres to ulcer base, protecting it from acid and pepsin.'),
('a9ec0321-33ed-55ce-afa5-4595f2a92c26'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Misoprostol is a synthetic analog of:', '["Histamine","Acetylcholine","PGE1 (Prostaglandin E1)","PGE2","PGI2"]'::jsonb, 'PGE1 (Prostaglandin E1)', 'Misoprostol is a synthetic PGE1 analog that increases bicarbonate and mucus secretion while decreasing acid secretion.'),
('4b9be1b3-dd03-509c-814a-a2ba7c2a3d21'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which of the following is NOT an indication for misoprostol?', '["NSAID-induced gastric ulcer prophylaxis","NSAID-induced duodenal ulcer","Peptic ulcer disease","Gastric cancer prevention","High-risk patients on NSAIDs"]'::jsonb, 'Gastric cancer prevention', 'Misoprostol is indicated for NSAID-induced ulcer prophylaxis but has no role in gastric cancer prevention.'),
('385baccf-a2a9-5ef5-b34e-5ca365f85534'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Common side effect of misoprostol that limits its use:', '["Constipation resulting in decreased functional activity","Diarrhea and abdominal cramps","Headache mediated by specific receptor activation","Dry mouth without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Dizziness leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Diarrhea and abdominal cramps', 'Diarrhea and abdominal cramps are common side effects of misoprostol, limiting patient compliance.'),
('c554b235-2fb3-5253-83de-dc64c9da0b75'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which H2 blocker has the longest duration of action?', '["Cimetidine","Ranitidine","Famotidine","Nizatidine","Roxatidine"]'::jsonb, 'Famotidine', 'Famotidine has the longest duration of action (up to 12 hours) among H2 blockers.'),
('4c5eab21-3e25-5d09-b301-eaf3f84c9307'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Cimetidine inhibits which cytochrome P450 enzyme?', '["CYP1A2","CYP2C9","CYP3A4","CYP2D6","CYP2E1"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4', 'Cimetidine is a potent inhibitor of CYP3A4, leading to significant drug interactions.'),
('52935539-2893-54ab-9870-576a5b004ab5'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which antacid contains aluminum and can cause constipation?', '["Magnesium hydroxide","Sodium bicarbonate","Aluminum hydroxide","Calcium carbonate","Magaldrate"]'::jsonb, 'Aluminum hydroxide', 'Aluminum hydroxide can cause constipation as a side effect, unlike magnesium-containing antacids which cause diarrhea.'),
('a909c98c-a670-5225-9c4d-98105873b4d7'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Ranitidine was withdrawn from market due to contamination with:', '["Lead resulting in decreased functional activity","Cadmium accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","NDMA (N-nitrosodimethylamine)","Mercury without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Arsenic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'NDMA (N-nitrosodimethylamine)', 'Ranitidine was recalled due to NDMA contamination, a probable human carcinogen found in the drug substance.'),
('1d3f27d8-e9e1-5531-9f97-1b32f2d331a6'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which drug increases lower esophageal sphincter pressure?', '["Omeprazole","Domperidone","Cimetidine","Misoprostol","Ranitidine"]'::jsonb, 'Domperidone', 'Domperidone is a prokinetic that increases LES pressure and promotes gastric emptying.'),
('2298aa3c-1f55-5348-a3ba-2b2d1d620db2'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'First-line treatment for H. pylori eradication includes:', '["PPI alone","PPI + 2 antibiotics (triple therapy)","H2 blocker + antibiotic","Antacid + antibiotic","Sucralfate + antibiotic"]'::jsonb, 'PPI + 2 antibiotics (triple therapy)', 'Triple therapy (PPI + clarithromycin + amoxicillin or metronidazole) is the standard first-line treatment for H. pylori.'),
('e9a03157-f862-5a34-a0e3-e777fe9b88a5'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which of the following is a potassium-competitive acid blocker (P-CAB)?', '["Omeprazole","Vonoprazan","Pantoprazole","Lansoprazole","Esomeprazole"]'::jsonb, 'Vonoprazan', 'Vonoprazan is aP-CAB that competitively blocks the proton pump, offering faster and more sustained acid suppression.'),
('64ae6a31-aadd-5c1b-951f-48ae9a46b7de'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'PPIs are activated in:', '["Alkaline pH resulting in decreased functional activity","Neutral pH accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Acidic pH of parietal cell canaliculus","All pH environments without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only in blood leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Acidic pH of parietal cell canaliculus', 'PPIs are prodrugs that are activated in the acidic environment of the parietal cell canaliculus.'),
('269e4910-824e-56c6-b0c3-93f2039a3648'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which of the following is a mucosal protectant?', '["Omeprazole","Cimetidine","Colloidal bismuth subcitrate","Ranitidine","Famotidine"]'::jsonb, 'Colloidal bismuth subcitrate', 'Colloidal bismuth subcitrate forms a protective barrier over ulcers and has antibacterial activity against H. pylori.'),
('c180993b-a5c5-5657-905b-dfcfbcc8e7ed'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Long-term PPI use is associated with increased risk of:', '["Hypoglycemia","Hypomagnesemia","Hyperkalemia","Hyperglycemia","Hypercalcemia"]'::jsonb, 'Hypomagnesemia', 'Long-term PPI use can cause hypomagnesemia by impairing intestinal magnesium absorption.'),
('9ac48cc7-7edf-5fca-94ee-2e8b2ac58a1b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Drug of choice for Zollinger-Ellison syndrome:', '["Ranitidine","High-dose omeprazole","Antacids","Misoprostol","Sucralfate"]'::jsonb, 'High-dose omeprazole', 'High-dose PPIs (especially omeprazole) are the treatment of choice for Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.'),
('9783d137-5f39-5ec9-baa0-79d750b1ee91'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Cytoprotective action of prostaglandins in the stomach includes:', '["Increased mucus secretion","Increased bicarbonate secretion","Maintained mucosal blood flow","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Prostaglandins protect the gastric mucosa by increasing mucus and bicarbonate secretion and maintaining mucosal blood flow.'),
('f078ba50-5305-5161-80dc-1eded0f2abbe'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which antacid can cause milk-alkali syndrome?', '["Aluminum hydroxide","Magnesium hydroxide","Calcium carbonate","Sodium bicarbonate","Magaldrate"]'::jsonb, 'Calcium carbonate', 'Excessive use of calcium carbonate antacids can cause milk-alkali syndrome characterized by hypercalcemia, metabolic alkalosis, and renal impairment.'),
('1497f095-4dd0-5fdf-86ac-0b37568e99a5'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Famotidine is approximately how many times more potent than cimetidine?', '["2 times","5 times","7-20 times","50 times","100 times"]'::jsonb, '7-20 times', 'Famotidine is approximately 7-20 times more potent than cimetidine in acid suppression.'),
('bec84065-1461-5007-a373-bfacf2437e51'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Cimetidine can cause gynecomastia due to:', '["H2 receptor blockade","Antiandrogenic effect","Increased prolactin","Estrogen release","Progesterone effect"]'::jsonb, 'Antiandrogenic effect', 'Cimetidine has antiandrogenic properties and can bind to androgen receptors, causing gynecomastia and impotence.'),
('179aac2d-0655-5e1c-a9a7-55cb15851760'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which drug is absolutely contraindicated in pregnancy?', '["Pantoprazole","Lansoprazole","Misoprostol","Ranitidine","Omeprazole"]'::jsonb, 'Misoprostol', 'Misoprostol is contraindicated in pregnancy as it can cause uterine contractions and miscarriage.'),
('bdc4f78e-84ef-52aa-9149-3fad908eb15c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Bismuth subsalicylate is used in:', '["Peptic ulcer disease","Traveler''s diarrhea","GERD","Zollinger-Ellison syndrome","Gastric cancer"]'::jsonb, 'Traveler''s diarrhea', 'Bismuth subsalicylate is effective against traveler''s diarrhea and is also used in H. pylori quadruple therapy.'),
('fea6bb60-ee70-50a9-8368-9b1e4f399e07'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Mechanism of action of H2 receptor blockers:', '["Block histamine at H1 receptors resulting in decreased functional activity","Competitive block of histamine at H2 receptors on parietal cells","Block acetylcholine receptors mediated by specific receptor activation","Block gastrin receptors without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inhibit proton pump leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Competitive block of histamine at H2 receptors on parietal cells', 'H2 blockers competitively block histamine at H2 receptors on gastric parietal cells, reducing acid secretion.'),
('c844c6b4-ca10-5c02-a825-bead0bacced2'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which drug is used for stress ulcer prophylaxis in ICU patients?', '["Antacids","H2 blockers or PPIs","Misoprostol","Sucralfate","Bismuth"]'::jsonb, 'H2 blockers or PPIs', 'H2 blockers or PPIs are used for stress ulcer prophylaxis in critically ill patients at risk for GI bleeding.'),
('0ffe1e53-98fa-5ef6-b451-b4e04298781d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which PPI is available as a combination with sodium bicarbonate for immediate effect?', '["Esomeprazole","Omeprazole","Pantoprazole","Lansoprazole","Rabeprazole"]'::jsonb, 'Esomeprazole', 'Esomeprazole is available in combination with sodium bicarbonate (Vimovo) to provide immediate and sustained acid suppression.'),
('870e66f8-bc4f-55d1-9c8f-e4625441215c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which drug is used to heal NSAID-induced ulcers while continuing NSAID therapy?', '["Antacids","PPIs","H2 blockers","Sucralfate","Misoprostol"]'::jsonb, 'PPIs', 'PPIs can heal NSAID-induced ulcers while NSAID therapy is continued, making them first-line for this indication.'),
('e3b25e48-b2c7-5649-826d-4e8c75768915'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Gastric acid secretion is primarily stimulated by all EXCEPT:', '["Histamine","Acetylcholine","Gastrin","Somatostatin","All stimulate acid secretion"]'::jsonb, 'Somatostatin', 'Somatostatin inhibits acid secretion, while histamine, acetylcholine, and gastrin stimulate it.'),
('a40eae9e-0141-5ade-ac2b-0963b8a15cf3'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Mechanism of sucralfate requires:', '["Alkaline pH","Acidic pH for polymerization","Neutral pH","No pH requirement","Basic environment"]'::jsonb, 'Acidic pH for polymerization', 'Sucralfate requires an acidic pH (<4) to polymerize and form a protective paste over ulcers.'),
('39f629e9-a073-5ca8-ac5c-a2838592417f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Metoclopramide acts as a prokinetic primarily by:', '["Blocking D2 receptors resulting in decreased functional activity","Blocking 5-HT4 receptors accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Stimulating 5-HT4 receptors and blocking D2 receptors","Blocking 5-HT3 receptors without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Blocking M1 receptors leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Stimulating 5-HT4 receptors and blocking D2 receptors', 'Metoclopramide stimulates 5-HT4 receptors and blocks D2 receptors, increasing acetylcholine release and promoting gastric motility.'),
('679185d3-c78b-549d-855b-fd17d41d37d1'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which prokinetic drug can cross the blood-brain barrier?', '["Domperidone","Metoclopramide","Erythromycin","Prucalopride","Cisapride"]'::jsonb, 'Metoclopramide', 'Metoclopramide crosses the BBB and can cause extrapyramidal side effects due to central D2 receptor blockade.'),
('72955176-0c1d-50a3-a078-6de253aad6b3'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Domperidone does NOT cross the blood-brain barrier effectively because:', '["It is too lipophilic","It is a substrate of P-glycoprotein efflux pump","It is metabolized in the liver","It is excreted by kidneys","It binds to plasma proteins"]'::jsonb, 'It is a substrate of P-glycoprotein efflux pump', 'Domperidone is a substrate of P-glycoprotein which actively transports it out of the brain, preventing CNS accumulation.'),
('97f12405-d44c-53e8-a5c9-f53c03dbc2f1'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Extrapyramidal side effects of metoclopramide can be treated with:', '["Atropine","Diazepam","Diphenhydramine","Ondansetron","Metoprolol"]'::jsonb, 'Diphenhydramine', 'Anticholinergic drugs like diphenhydramine can treat acute extrapyramidal reactions by blocking dopamine in the nigrostriatal pathway.'),
('6f2e646a-71ec-5047-a28c-d0ccc69bad5e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Erythromycin acts as a prokinetic by acting as an agonist at:', '["5-HT4 receptors","Motilin receptors","D2 receptors","M3 receptors","NK1 receptors"]'::jsonb, 'Motilin receptors', 'Erythromycin is a motilin receptor agonist that stimulates gastric motility and is used as a prokinetic agent.'),
('a3861cfa-1142-50ca-bb8b-15c87c38f528'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which prokinetic drugs are associated with QT prolongation?', '["Metoclopramide","Domperidone","Erythromycin","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Metoclopramide, domperidone, and erythromycin can all prolong the QT interval and should be used with caution.'),
('d79e09e7-f0ee-5620-81fc-1d2303a22486'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Metoclopramide is contraindicated in:', '["Gastroparesis","Chemotherapy-induced nausea","Intestinal obstruction","GERD","Postoperative nausea"]'::jsonb, 'Intestinal obstruction', 'Metoclopramide is contraindicated in intestinal obstruction as it increases motility which can worsen obstruction.'),
('cf767680-ff06-5815-9f5c-08d7ca4264af'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Tardive dyskinesia from metoclopramide is:', '["Always reversible","Usually irreversible","Mild and self-limiting","Only occurs in elderly","Dose-independent"]'::jsonb, 'Usually irreversible', 'Tardive dyskinesia from metoclopramide is usually irreversible and may persist even after discontinuation.'),
('7f5706d3-1177-55cd-92da-d1e53d01b47d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'The dose-limiting side effect of metoclopramide is:', '["Diarrhea","Extrapyramidal symptoms","Hypotension","Bradycardia","Seizures"]'::jsonb, 'Extrapyramidal symptoms', 'Extrapyramidal symptoms (EPS) including acute dystonia and tardive dyskinesia are the dose-limiting side effects of metoclopramide.'),
('68995364-950a-5415-a1dc-f83514665e60'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Domperidone is preferred over metoclopramide in children because:', '["It is more effective","It has fewer CNS side effects","It tastes better","It is cheaper","It has no side effects"]'::jsonb, 'It has fewer CNS side effects', 'Domperidone has fewer CNS side effects as it does not cross the BBB, making it safer for pediatric use.'),
('7c36a19c-7161-528a-8458-b6cc3596e3fd'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Prucalopride acts as an agonist at which receptor?', '["5-HT3","5-HT4 (selective)","D2","Motilin","NK1"]'::jsonb, '5-HT4 (selective)', 'Prucalopride is a selective 5-HT4 receptor agonist used for chronic constipation in women.'),
('2adb215e-25a1-544e-9118-48c9b03dcb6e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Metoclopramide increases prolactin levels by:', '["Stimulating 5-HT4 receptors","Blocking D2 receptors in anterior pituitary","Inhibiting GABA","Stimulating histamine receptors","Blocking muscarinic receptors"]'::jsonb, 'Blocking D2 receptors in anterior pituitary', 'Metoclopramide blocks D2 receptors in the anterior pituitary, removing tonic inhibition of prolactin release.'),
('adc6e513-733d-5e72-8aac-d975cf29c295'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'The prokinetic effect of metoclopramide is most pronounced in:', '["Esophagus resulting in decreased functional activity","Stomach and proximal duodenum","Terminal ileum mediated by specific receptor activation","Colon without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Rectum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Stomach and proximal duodenum', 'Metoclopramide''s prokinetic effect is most pronounced in the stomach and proximal duodenum, enhancing gastric emptying.'),
('809d44f7-8f41-5b94-a8a2-844d675fbc9e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which drug can cause galactorrhea as a side effect?', '["Domperidone","Metoclopramide","Erythromycin","Prucalopride","Ranitidine"]'::jsonb, 'Metoclopramide', 'Metoclopramide can cause hyperprolactinemia leading to galactorrhea, gynecomastia, and menstrual irregularities.'),
('c162bd43-9562-57d3-a884-4f2ea0cc0582'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which prokinetic is available as a transdermal patch?', '["Metoclopramide","Domperidone","Erythromycin","Cisapride","Prucalopride"]'::jsonb, 'Metoclopramide', 'Metoclopramide is available as a transdermal patch (Reglan) for continuous delivery over 3 days.'),
('4e241417-0465-51b7-a42e-2e070355d953'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Domperidone is available in which formulations?', '["Only oral","Oral and IV","Oral, IV, and rectal","Oral and topical","Only IV"]'::jsonb, 'Oral, IV, and rectal', 'Domperidone is available in oral (tablets, suspension), IV, and rectal formulations.'),
('3f5d9b0f-d968-5d4a-929f-1aaa82fc88d3'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which prokinetic is NOT associated with significant extrapyramidal side effects?', '["Metoclopramide","Domperidone","Chlorpromazine","Prochlorperazine","Haloperidol"]'::jsonb, 'Domperidone', 'Domperidone has minimal risk of EPS as it does not cross the BBB significantly.'),
('da5d0b47-deb7-585d-a68a-3b58f9ebf603'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which of the following is a rescue medication for acute dystonic reactions?', '["Metoclopramide","Promethazine","IV diphenhydramine","Domperidone","Atropine"]'::jsonb, 'IV diphenhydramine', 'IV diphenhydramine or benztropine is the treatment of choice for acute dystonic reactions caused by dopamine antagonists.'),
('c69a8676-9424-515b-a0d9-0a370e58a898'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Ondansetron blocks which receptor?', '["D2 receptors","5-HT3 receptors","NK1 receptors","H1 receptors","M1 receptors"]'::jsonb, '5-HT3 receptors', 'Ondansetron is a selective 5-HT3 receptor antagonist used for chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting.'),
('321656d3-7229-588d-bb62-3ec438a9558b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Aprepitant blocks which receptor?', '["5-HT3 resulting in decreased functional activity","D2 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","NK1 (substance P)","H1 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","5-HT4 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'NK1 (substance P)', 'Aprepitant is a selective NK1 receptor antagonist used for delayed CINV in combination with ondansetron and dexamethasone.'),
('68712d3f-3050-5b2a-af6b-0c1de524cab9'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which antiemetic is most effective for acute chemotherapy-induced vomiting?', '["Metoclopramide","Prochlorperazine","Ondansetron (5-HT3 antagonist)","Dexamethasone","Lorazepam"]'::jsonb, 'Ondansetron (5-HT3 antagonist)', '5-HT3 antagonists like ondansetron are most effective for acute CINV (within 24 hours of chemotherapy).'),
('2c65a621-d646-56e3-a344-18d9b245d89b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Promethazine is an antagonist at all these receptors EXCEPT:', '["H1","D2","M1","5-HT3","Alpha-1"]'::jsonb, '5-HT3', 'Promethazine blocks H1, D2, M1, and alpha-1 receptors but has no significant 5-HT3 antagonism.'),
('96135bee-79cb-50a9-a139-643016e86a88'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which antiemetic is drug of choice for motion sickness?', '["Ondansetron","Scopolamine (transdermal)","Aprepitant","Granisetron","Palonosetron"]'::jsonb, 'Scopolamine (transdermal)', 'Scopolamine (anticholinergic) is the drug of choice for motion sickness prevention, available as transdermal patch.'),
('497924bb-78a5-50df-8a22-19099a231499'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Dexamethasone as an antiemetic is effective in:', '["Acute CINV only","Delayed CINV only","Both acute and delayed CINV","Motion sickness","Vestibular nausea"]'::jsonb, 'Both acute and delayed CINV', 'Dexamethasone is effective in both acute and delayed CINV and is used in combination with other antiemetics.'),
('85363d1f-ccc0-5f2e-97d5-70e3cf8e3685'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Palonosetron differs from ondansetron by:', '["Shorter half-life resulting in decreased functional activity","Longer half-life (25-30 hours) and receptor binding","No 5-HT3 activity mediated by specific receptor activation","D2 antagonism without affecting the primary tissue pathway","NK1 antagonism leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Longer half-life (25-30 hours) and receptor binding', 'Palonosetron has a longer half-life and enhanced receptor binding, providing prolonged antiemetic protection.'),
('3fd5505c-11b5-5a62-918d-b03a22a4bed0'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Benzodiazepines are used as antiemetics to:', '["Directly block vomiting center resulting in decreased functional activity","Provide anxiolysis and anticipatory nausea control","Block 5-HT3 receptors mediated by specific receptor activation","Block D2 receptors without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Stimulate appetite leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Provide anxiolysis and anticipatory nausea control', 'Benzodiazepines like lorazepam reduce anxiety and control anticipatory nausea and vomiting before chemotherapy.'),
('6d34a8c8-84e8-5467-ba72-60a49141274c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'The only FDA-approved antiemetic specifically for pregnancy-related nausea is:', '["Ondansetron resulting in decreased functional activity","Promethazine accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Metoclopramide mediated by specific receptor activation","Doxylamine-pyridoxine (Bendectin)","Meclizine leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Doxylamine-pyridoxine (Bendectin)', 'Doxylamine-pyridoxine (Bendectin) is the only FDA-approved antiemetic specifically for pregnancy-related nausea.'),
('786f03d0-515c-5b18-b228-f0b83bff8e50'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'The chemoreceptor trigger zone (CTZ) is located in:', '["Vestibular apparatus","Area postrema of medulla","Cerebral cortex","Hypothalamus","Cerebellum"]'::jsonb, 'Area postrema of medulla', 'The CTZ is located in the area postrema on the floor of the 4th ventricle, outside the BBB, and responds to circulating emetogens.'),
('adc89dc6-dc4b-526f-9df8-bebd4f66f57c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Granisetron is approximately how many times more potent than ondansetron?', '["2 times","5 times","10 times","50 times","100 times"]'::jsonb, '10 times', 'Granisetron is approximately 10 times more potent than ondansetron at the 5-HT3 receptor.'),
('76a1aa21-659d-50ef-b81e-d4697f4c8893'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which combination is used for highly emetogenic chemotherapy?', '["Ondansetron + dexamethasone","Ondansetron + aprepitant + dexamethasone","Metoclopramide alone","Prochlorperazine + dexamethasone","Ondansetron alone"]'::jsonb, 'Ondansetron + aprepitant + dexamethasone', 'Triple therapy with ondansetron + aprepitant + dexamethasone is standard for highly emetogenic chemotherapy.'),
('56e03651-6f36-5e00-b07c-8acbd4d9bc8b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'The vomiting center is located in:', '["Area postrema","Nucleus tractus solitarius","Medullary reticular formation","Cerebellum","Hypothalamus"]'::jsonb, 'Medullary reticular formation', 'The vomiting center is a neuronal pool in the medullary reticular formation that coordinates the vomiting reflex.'),
('b211c61d-e961-5533-a3a8-e186b7227ced'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Why are 5-HT3 antagonists NOT effective for delayed CINV?', '["Wrong mechanism resulting in decreased functional activity","Serotonin release is transient, occurring mainly in acute phase","Receptor upregulation mediated by specific receptor activation","Drug tolerance without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Poor bioavailability leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Serotonin release is transient, occurring mainly in acute phase', 'Serotonin release from enterochromaffin cells is transient and occurs mainly in the acute phase (0-24 hours).');
insert into fcps_mcq_stage (id, subject_name, chapter_name, question, options, correct_answer, explanation) values
('3175b874-e7e7-509a-a691-bffe5aee4588'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Meclizine is preferred over dimenhydrinate for motion sickness because:', '["It is more potent resulting in decreased functional activity","Longer duration of action (12-24 hours vs 4-6 hours)","Fewer side effects mediated by specific receptor activation","Better absorption without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Available IV leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Longer duration of action (12-24 hours vs 4-6 hours)', 'Meclizine has a longer duration of action (12-24 hours) compared to dimenhydrinate (4-6 hours).'),
('00545530-7b40-5e72-9387-16c00abf2081'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Prochlorperazine acts primarily by blocking:', '["5-HT3 receptors","D2 receptors in CTZ","NK1 receptors","H1 receptors","M1 receptors"]'::jsonb, 'D2 receptors in CTZ', 'Prochlorperazine is a phenothiazine that blocks D2 receptors in the chemoreceptor trigger zone.'),
('cbf4525a-986f-56eb-8b6e-7149495c30a3'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which antiemetic is a NK1 receptor antagonist available IV?', '["Ondansetron","Lorazepam","Fosaprepitant","Dexamethasone","Prochlorperazine"]'::jsonb, 'Fosaprepitant', 'Fosaprepitant is the IV prodrug of aprepitant, a selective NK1 receptor antagonist.'),
('860537fa-46b0-5b73-a833-943496018205'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Dimenhydrinate is an effective antiemetic mainly for:', '["Chemotherapy-induced vomiting","Postoperative nausea","Motion sickness and vestibular vertigo","Hyperemesis gravidarum","Gastroparesis"]'::jsonb, 'Motion sickness and vestibular vertigo', 'Dimenhydrinate (Dramamine) is an antihistamine effective for motion sickness and vestibular disorders.'),
('ce0a16eb-ef21-50b1-ace4-d0873ab98a7b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which antiemetic drug should be AVOIDED in young females due to tardive dyskinesia risk?', '["Ondansetron","Metoclopramide","Aprepitant","Dexamethasone","Lorazepam"]'::jsonb, 'Metoclopramide', 'Metoclopramide can cause tardive dyskinesia, particularly risky in young females who are more susceptible.'),
('ba07217c-2d36-52e9-a2a0-d9c04f926a65'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which of the following is a stimulant laxative?', '["Psyllium","Docusate","Bisacodyl","Lactulose","Polyethylene glycol"]'::jsonb, 'Bisacodyl', 'Bisacodyl is a stimulant laxative that acts by stimulating the myenteric plexus and increasing water secretion.'),
('6dd07053-ce0f-5a8e-8fa0-16956b71b41c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Osmotic laxatives work by:', '["Stimulating peristalsis","Drawing water into the intestinal lumen","Lubricating the stool","Softening the stool","Increasing fiber content"]'::jsonb, 'Drawing water into the intestinal lumen', 'Osmotic laxatives like PEG and lactulose retain water in the intestinal lumen, softening stool.'),
('677bf0f2-7b2b-5492-8276-ca0d1959326e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Lactulose is contraindicated in:', '["Constipation","Hepatic encephalopathy","Galactosemia","Diabetes","Hypertension"]'::jsonb, 'Galactosemia', 'Lactulose contains galactose and lactose, making it contraindicated in galactosemia.'),
('a456ee56-2471-5588-91f6-e8fef3e74339'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Loperamide acts as an antidiarrheal by:', '["Adsorbing toxins","Peripheral mu-opioid receptor agonism","Inhibiting motility centrally","Binding bile acids","Reducing secretion"]'::jsonb, 'Peripheral mu-opioid receptor agonism', 'Loperamide is a peripheral mu-opioid agonist that acts on the myenteric plexus to reduce motility and increase transit time.'),
('45a42e0d-d1cc-59d7-95d9-b9e329f0bdd0'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which antidiarrheal agent binds toxins in the gut?', '["Loperamide","Bismuth subsalicylate","Kaolin-pectin","Diphenoxylate","Racecadotril"]'::jsonb, 'Kaolin-pectin', 'Kaolin-pectin is an adsorbent that binds toxins and bacteria in the GI tract, reducing diarrhea.'),
('07f4250f-9fbe-5c28-aa3b-638bdf5bcc4b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which laxative is preferred in pregnancy?', '["Bisacodyl","Senna","Polyethylene glycol (PEG)","Castor oil","Magnesium citrate"]'::jsonb, 'Polyethylene glycol (PEG)', 'Polyethylene glycol is considered safe in pregnancy as it is not absorbed systemically.'),
('ec8f0c5c-62d0-55cb-94c8-9dc52ff04f26'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Castor oil works by:', '["Osmotic effect resulting in decreased functional activity","Release of ricinoleic acid in the small intestine","Stimulating colonic motility mediated by specific receptor activation","Lubricating stool without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Blocking water absorption leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Release of ricinoleic acid in the small intestine', 'Castor oil is hydrolyzed to ricinoleic acid in the small intestine, which irritates the intestinal mucosa and stimulates peristalsis.'),
('77d858dc-36a7-543d-9725-25dc4a698a46'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Docusate sodium is classified as:', '["Stimulant laxative","Osmotic laxative","Stool softener/surfactant","Bulk-forming laxative","Lubricant laxative"]'::jsonb, 'Stool softener/surfactant', 'Docusate is a surfactant/stool softener that allows water and fat to penetrate stool, making it easier to pass.'),
('2f993785-0ad8-5bbb-a96a-75deefb688e7'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which bulk-forming laxative is derived from Plantago ovata?', '["Methylcellulose","Polycarbophil","Psyllium","Carrageenan","Agar"]'::jsonb, 'Psyllium', 'Psyllium (Isabgol) is a bulk-forming laxative derived from Plantago ovata seeds, used for chronic constipation.'),
('ef88d31f-792f-507b-a66c-37212ba2d16f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which antidiarrheal drug inhibits intestinal secretion without affecting motility?', '["Loperamide","Racecadotril (acetorphan)","Bismuth subsalicylate","Kaolin-pectin","Diphenoxylate"]'::jsonb, 'Racecadotril (acetorphan)', 'Racecadotril inhibits enkephalinase, reducing intestinal fluid secretion without affecting motility.'),
('bb78ffcd-a4c5-552f-b36e-089b9006cbd9'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which laxative should be avoided in patients with renal failure?', '["PEG resulting in decreased functional activity","Lactulose accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Magnesium-containing laxatives","Bisacodyl without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Psyllium leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Magnesium-containing laxatives', 'Magnesium-containing laxatives can cause hypermagnesemia in patients with renal impairment.'),
('3ede9e07-bb13-5cee-a387-e91a56b41336'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Diphenoxylate is combined with atropine to:', '["Increase efficacy resulting in decreased functional activity","Prevent abuse by causing unpleasant effects at high doses","Reduce side effects mediated by specific receptor activation","Improve absorption without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Enhance taste leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Prevent abuse by causing unpleasant effects at high doses', 'Atropine is added to diphenoxylate to deter abuse by causing unpleasant anticholinergic effects at high doses.'),
('4f462091-eb49-5275-afbb-7be5d722fa02'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Psyllium works best when taken with:', '["A small amount of water resulting in decreased functional activity","Large amounts of water (200-300 mL per dose)","Juice mediated by specific receptor activation","Milk without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Without fluids leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Large amounts of water (200-300 mL per dose)', 'Psyllium requires adequate water intake to work effectively and prevent intestinal obstruction.'),
('8db62c98-ab8a-527c-93ca-51d2f37a7712'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which laxative can cause hypokalemia with chronic use?', '["PEG","Lactulose","Bisacodyl (stimulant)","Psyllium","Docusate"]'::jsonb, 'Bisacodyl (stimulant)', 'Stimulant laxatives like bisacodyl can cause potassium loss in stool, leading to hypokalemia with chronic use.'),
('374d922b-2999-55b8-8c4a-11f735439478'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Racecadotril is preferred over loperamide in children because:', '["It is more effective","It does not cause constipation","It tastes better","It is cheaper","It has no side effects"]'::jsonb, 'It does not cause constipation', 'Racecadotril reduces secretion without affecting motility, so it does not cause constipation like loperamide.'),
('a7e0bc49-ad06-5d1a-a9b9-f1ef43c4c72c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Bismuth subsalicylate can cause which harmless side effect?', '["Black tongue and stool","White tongue","Red eyes","Yellow skin","Blue nails"]'::jsonb, 'Black tongue and stool', 'Bismuth subsalicylate can cause harmless blackening of the tongue and stool due to bismuth sulfide formation.'),
('d6ccf6e7-7d26-5210-a5b7-f8cb57fb2d47'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which laxative is the gold standard for bowel preparation before colonoscopy?', '["Psyllium","Bisacodyl","High-volume PEG solution","Docusate","Lactulose"]'::jsonb, 'High-volume PEG solution', 'High-volume PEG solutions (GoLYTELY) are the gold standard for bowel preparation before colonoscopy.'),
('dee6d9df-2151-5a0a-a9a1-111b261e30a1'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Senna contains active compounds called:', '["Anthraquinones (sennosides)","Flavonoids accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Alkaloids mediated by specific receptor activation","Terpenes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Steroids leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anthraquinones (sennosides)', 'Senna contains anthraquinone glycosides (sennosides) that stimulate the myenteric plexus.'),
('70da93ae-0a67-5b96-86b7-8182a7b7af5f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which antidiarrheal is NOT an opioid?', '["Loperamide","Diphenoxylate","Racecadotril","Codeine","Tramadol"]'::jsonb, 'Racecadotril', 'Racecadotril is an enkephalinase inhibitor, not an opioid, and works by reducing intestinal secretion.'),
('26b0fef1-730c-5a29-aee5-5168a007d600'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which laxative is available as a suppository for acute relief?', '["PEG","Bisacodyl","Psyllium","Docusate","Lactulose"]'::jsonb, 'Bisacodyl', 'Bisacodyl is available as suppositories for rapid relief of constipation (onset 15-60 minutes).'),
('d121355d-e0f1-593e-bc25-70ebe1e704fb'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Mesalazine (5-ASA) is the drug of choice for:', '["Crohn''s disease","Mild to moderate ulcerative colitis","Irritable bowel syndrome","Diverticulitis","Celiac disease"]'::jsonb, 'Mild to moderate ulcerative colitis', '5-ASA compounds are first-line for mild to moderate ulcerative colitis, both for induction and maintenance of remission.'),
('b699225c-7dcf-54ed-a43d-bdf169174ca9'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Sulfasalazine consists of 5-ASA linked to:', '["Folic acid","Sulfapyridine","Mesalamine","Olsalazine","Balsalazide"]'::jsonb, 'Sulfapyridine', 'Sulfasalazine is a prodrug consisting of 5-ASA linked to sulfapyridine by an azo bond, cleaved by colonic bacteria.'),
('a8e05cb2-64ca-5ee5-9417-6004848f2eb1'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Adalimumab is a monoclonal antibody against:', '["TNF-alpha","IL-6","IL-12/23","Integrins","JAK"]'::jsonb, 'TNF-alpha', 'Adalimumab is a fully human monoclonal antibody against TNF-alpha used in moderate to severe IBD.'),
('0dd8bf52-003c-54af-9176-03b720984962'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which biologic is a selective gut integrin antagonist?', '["Infliximab resulting in decreased functional activity","Adalimumab accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Vedolizumab (anti-alpha4beta7)","Ustekinumab without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Natalizumab leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Vedolizumab (anti-alpha4beta7)', 'Vedolizumab selectively blocks alpha4beta7 integrin, preventing lymphocyte trafficking specifically to gut tissue.'),
('3d546311-a84c-5614-adb7-a259d982fa9e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Azathioprine is a prodrug converted to:', '["Methotrexate","6-mercaptopurine (6-MP)","Cyclosporine","Mycophenolate","Tacrolimus"]'::jsonb, '6-mercaptopurine (6-MP)', 'Azathioprine is converted to 6-mercaptopurine (6-MP) which inhibits purine synthesis in lymphocytes.'),
('0c789024-d412-5a23-94cc-1324d70168cd'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which drug requires TPMT testing before initiation?', '["Methotrexate","Infliximab","Azathioprine/6-MP","Mesalazine","Corticosteroids"]'::jsonb, 'Azathioprine/6-MP', 'TPMT (thiopurine methyltransferase) deficiency increases risk of severe myelosuppression with azathioprine/6-MP.'),
('82e7111e-8e54-5e4f-bc8e-62cb716ca24c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Infliximab is administered:', '["Orally","Subcutaneously","Intravenously","Intramuscularly","Rectally"]'::jsonb, 'Intravenously', 'Infliximab is a chimeric monoclonal antibody administered as IV infusion at 0, 2, and 6 weeks, then every 8 weeks.'),
('0c3b8115-cc14-5612-b01e-c6a00dfcfd68'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Budesonide is preferred over prednisolone in IBD because:', '["It is more potent resulting in decreased functional activity","High first-pass metabolism reduces systemic side effects","It is cheaper mediated by specific receptor activation","It has fewer side effects locally without affecting the primary tissue pathway","It is available IV leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'High first-pass metabolism reduces systemic side effects', 'Budesonide has approximately 90% first-pass hepatic metabolism, reducing systemic corticosteroid side effects.'),
('f0e203a3-4179-5ae9-a10d-41a613da81e7'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which biologic targets IL-12/23 (p40 subunit)?', '["Infliximab","Adalimumab","Ustekinumab","Vedolizumab","Tofacitinib"]'::jsonb, 'Ustekinumab', 'Ustekinumab is a monoclonal antibody against the p40 subunit shared by IL-12 and IL-23.'),
('88e3ba78-9902-5fc7-a528-648459fba7a5'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Topical 5-ASA (enemas) is used for:', '["Crohn''s disease","Distal ulcerative colitis","Irritable bowel syndrome","Diverticulitis","Celiac disease"]'::jsonb, 'Distal ulcerative colitis', '5-ASA enemas are effective for distal UC and left-sided colitis, delivering medication directly to affected areas.'),
('79076d68-4f14-52ca-aeef-4fea38c83b53'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Methotrexate in IBD acts by:', '["Blocking TNF-alpha resulting in decreased functional activity","Inhibiting dihydrofolate reductase (DHFR)","Blocking IL-6 mediated by specific receptor activation","Inhibiting JAK without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Blocking integrins leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibiting dihydrofolate reductase (DHFR)', 'Methotrexate inhibits DHFR, reducing folate availability and suppressing lymphocyte proliferation.'),
('c417c100-384e-5818-97fd-83f1d8f85097'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Tofacitinib is an inhibitor of:', '["TNF-alpha","Janus kinase (JAK1/JAK3)","IL-12/23","Integrins","NF-kappaB"]'::jsonb, 'Janus kinase (JAK1/JAK3)', 'Tofacitinib is a JAK inhibitor (primarily JAK1/JAK3) used for ulcerative colitis.'),
('09d34d52-fab7-5774-aaca-238e38aa6dca'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Cyclosporine in IBD is used for:', '["Mild UC resulting in decreased functional activity","Moderate UC accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Acute severe steroid-refractory UC (rescue therapy)","Crohn''s disease without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Maintenance therapy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Acute severe steroid-refractory UC (rescue therapy)', 'IV cyclosporine is used as rescue therapy for acute severe UC refractory to IV corticosteroids.'),
('211e1215-a9fc-5628-a613-50246e205c9d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Rectal 5-ASA suppositories are effective for:', '["Right-sided colitis","UC limited to rectum (proctitis)","Crohn''s disease","Irritable bowel syndrome","Diverticulitis"]'::jsonb, 'UC limited to rectum (proctitis)', '5-ASA suppositories are first-line for UC limited to the rectum (proctitis).'),
('b2f98b51-8a5c-5cdf-a2e4-0db6cb953283'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which biologic is associated with progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy (PML)?', '["Infliximab","Adalimumab","Natalizumab","Vedolizumab","Ustekinumab"]'::jsonb, 'Natalizumab', 'Natalizumab (anti-alpha4 integrin) is associated with PML due to JC virus reactivation, limiting its use.'),
('58a3b7d1-df0e-50cc-9a7b-8ade31a729c1'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Maintenance therapy with 5-ASA in UC reduces relapse rate by approximately:', '["10%","30%","50%","70%","90%"]'::jsonb, '70%', '5-ASA maintenance therapy reduces UC relapse rates by approximately 70% compared to placebo.'),
('82efffea-1834-5cf6-b218-04feacb37187'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Crohn''s disease most commonly affects:', '["Rectum resulting in decreased functional activity","Terminal ileum and colon","Duodenum mediated by specific receptor activation","Esophagus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Stomach leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Terminal ileum and colon', 'Crohn''s disease most commonly affects the terminal ileum and colon, with skip lesions and transmural inflammation.'),
('d8ebc5ad-1dd7-5228-ae2b-29830a210000'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which drug is used for fistulizing Crohn''s disease?', '["Mesalazine","Sulfasalazine","Infliximab","Lactulose","Prednisolone"]'::jsonb, 'Infliximab', 'Infliximab is effective for closing fistulas in Crohn''s disease and is FDA-approved for this indication.'),
('86bb0149-a2c1-55de-9bbe-437cf648fb7f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which IBD drug should NOT be combined with live vaccines?', '["Mesalazine","Prednisolone at low dose","Biologics (e.g., infliximab)","Loperamide","Ondansetron"]'::jsonb, 'Biologics (e.g., infliximab)', 'Biologics like infliximab suppress immunity and live vaccines are contraindicated during treatment.'),
('b4a5fc33-0d34-5a21-b63e-1533de07675a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which drug is used for mild to moderate Crohn''s disease?', '["Budesonide","Prednisolone","Metronidazole","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Budesonide, prednisolone, and metronidazole are all used for mild to moderate Crohn''s disease.'),
('e74cd832-3820-5fb1-a1bd-0ab98ecbc7e9'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Ulcerative colitis is characterized by:', '["Transmural inflammation resulting in decreased functional activity","Skip lesions accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Continuous mucosal inflammation starting from rectum","Granulomas without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fistulae leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Continuous mucosal inflammation starting from rectum', 'UC is characterized by continuous mucosal inflammation starting from the rectum and extending proximally.'),
('e03531f0-6d56-5c40-9f3e-1f02627d98dc'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Combination of infliximab with azathioprine increases risk of:', '["Infections and lymphoma","GI bleeding","Nephrotoxicity","Pancreatitis","Cardiotoxicity"]'::jsonb, 'Infections and lymphoma', 'Combining infliximab with azathioprine increases risk of serious infections and lymphoma, though combination therapy is sometimes used carefully.'),
('f7f728cb-11f4-500a-842a-ea6f9ff0ed27'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which drug is first-line for maintaining remission in Crohn''s disease?', '["Prednisolone","Azathioprine","5-ASA","Antibiotics","Opioids"]'::jsonb, 'Azathioprine', 'Azathioprine/6-MP are steroid-sparing agents used as first-line for maintaining remission in Crohn''s disease.'),
('485a5175-db42-55bc-a64d-a6ff01897fb8'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Ursodeoxycholic acid (UDCA) is used for:', '["Gallstone dissolution","Primary biliary cholangitis (PBC)","Both A and B","Peptic ulcer disease","GERD"]'::jsonb, 'Both A and B', 'UDCA is used for dissolving small radiolucent gallstones and is first-line for primary biliary cholangitis.'),
('9559b3fd-e418-5b14-bebb-d340350306b6'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Cholestyramine works by:', '["Inhibiting bile acid synthesis resulting in decreased functional activity","Binding bile acids in the intestine (bile acid sequestrant)","Stimulating bile flow mediated by specific receptor activation","Dissolving gallstones without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Protecting hepatocytes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Binding bile acids in the intestine (bile acid sequestrant)', 'Cholestyramine is a bile acid sequestrant that binds bile acids in the intestine, preventing their enterohepatic reabsorption.'),
('68b2ff49-70a7-5bc8-81c7-1a1ed7e30247'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'UDCA dissolves gallstones by:', '["Dissolving calcium deposits","Reducing cholesterol secretion in bile","Increasing bile acid production","Stimulating gallbladder contraction","Inhibiting hepatic cholesterol synthesis"]'::jsonb, 'Reducing cholesterol secretion in bile', 'UDCA reduces cholesterol saturation of bile by decreasing cholesterol secretion, allowing gallstone dissolution.'),
('b626a1da-7287-5f4d-9387-4a88bfbc8fd6'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which type of gallstones can be dissolved by UDCA?', '["Pigment gallstones","Cholesterol gallstones < 10mm","Calcified gallstones","Mixed gallstones with calcium","All gallstones"]'::jsonb, 'Cholesterol gallstones < 10mm', 'UDCA dissolves small (<10mm) cholesterol-rich gallstones by reducing cholesterol secretion in bile.'),
('5ee89867-2c27-5b0d-81fd-cddfaeebb8b1'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Cholestyramine causes deficiency of:', '["Vitamin C resulting in decreased functional activity","Vitamin D only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Vitamin K only mediated by specific receptor activation","All fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, K)","Vitamin B12 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'All fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, K)', 'Bile acid sequestrants can bind fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, K), potentially causing deficiency with long-term use.'),
('61fdeb9f-2bf5-52da-bc29-eafcb37d37f1'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Obeticholic acid is a potent agonist of:', '["FXR (Farnesoid X Receptor)","PPAR-gamma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","LXR mediated by specific receptor activation","VDR without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CAR leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'FXR (Farnesoid X Receptor)', 'Obeticholic acid is a selective FXR agonist used for PBC in patients who respond inadequately to UDCA.'),
('4f560617-1844-5d83-b435-dcb456852561'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'UDCA side effects include:', '["Weight gain","Diarrhea","Hair loss","Hypertension","Hyperglycemia"]'::jsonb, 'Diarrhea', 'Diarrhea is the most common side effect of UDCA, occurring in approximately 5% of patients.'),
('4a6182d8-e813-542a-b3c0-48e21fa7d55d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Ursodeoxycholic acid is naturally found in:', '["Human bile as major component resulting in decreased functional activity","Bear bile (primary therapeutic source)","Cow bile mediated by specific receptor activation","Sheep bile without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Synthetic only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bear bile (primary therapeutic source)', 'UDCA was originally isolated from bear bile and constitutes only about 1-5% of human bile acids.'),
('2b207ed6-e51f-5fb5-a478-e312c374bdf8'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Cholestyramine should be taken:', '["With meals resulting in decreased functional activity","1 hour before or 4-6 hours after other drugs","At bedtime only mediated by specific receptor activation","On empty stomach with fruit juice without affecting the primary tissue pathway","With milk leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '1 hour before or 4-6 hours after other drugs', 'Cholestyramine can bind many drugs in the gut, so it should be taken 1 hour before or 4-6 hours after other medications.'),
('d6dcba89-cc26-5568-bb0f-0f81e76204c8'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Gallstone dissolution with UDCA typically takes:', '["1-2 weeks","1-2 months","6-24 months","5 years","It never dissolves stones"]'::jsonb, '6-24 months', 'Gallstone dissolution with UDCA typically takes 6-24 months of continuous therapy.'),
('13b28dd6-6721-5461-b594-5ec0200943da'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Primary biliary cholangitis is characterized by:', '["Destruction of intrahepatic bile ducts","Gallstones accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pancreatitis mediated by specific receptor activation","GERD without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Peptic ulcer leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Destruction of intrahepatic bile ducts', 'PBC is an autoimmune disease characterized by destruction of small intrahepatic bile ducts, leading to cholestasis.'),
('60c0488d-0ff7-5a69-a45a-07595ba95ddd'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'UDCA and cholestyramine should be given at different times because:', '["They have different mechanisms","Cholestyramine binds and reduces absorption of UDCA","They cause drug interactions in liver","They have different half-lives","No reason"]'::jsonb, 'Cholestyramine binds and reduces absorption of UDCA', 'Cholestyramine can bind UDCA in the gut and reduce its absorption; they should be given 2-4 hours apart.'),
('5748092e-ab91-5af7-ab35-3084e36b5e17'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which drug is used for pruritus associated with cholestasis?', '["UDCA","Cholestyramine","Rifaximin","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Cholestyramine, rifaximin, and UDCA are all used for pruritus associated with cholestasis.'),
('ee699409-0e27-5ef6-a189-88f0990c2488'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'The most common type of gallstone in Western countries is:', '["Pigment stones","Cholesterol stones","Calcium stones","Bilirubin stones","Mixed stones"]'::jsonb, 'Cholesterol stones', 'Cholesterol gallstones are the most common type in Western countries, comprising approximately 80% of gallstones.'),
('a7df83b0-aa3b-5db8-9876-3518e002355b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Pancreatic enzyme replacement therapy is indicated for:', '["Acute pancreatitis resulting in decreased functional activity","Chronic pancreatitis with exocrine insufficiency","Diabetes mellitus mediated by specific receptor activation","Gallstones without affecting the primary tissue pathway","GERD leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Chronic pancreatitis with exocrine insufficiency', 'Pancreatic enzyme replacement is indicated for pancreatic exocrine insufficiency (e.g., chronic pancreatitis, cystic fibrosis).'),
('8f25cd17-4c12-5ca4-8464-9076868aa3ba'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Pancrelipase contains:', '["Lipase only","Amylase only","Protease only","Lipase, amylase, and protease","Insulin only"]'::jsonb, 'Lipase, amylase, and protease', 'Pancrelipase contains pancreatic lipase, amylase, and protease to replace deficient exocrine enzymes.'),
('3d668cd7-006c-5914-b981-fd9313a3983b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Pancreatic enzymes should be taken:', '["On an empty stomach","Before meals","With meals and snacks","At bedtime only","Once daily in the morning"]'::jsonb, 'With meals and snacks', 'Pancreatic enzymes should be taken with meals and snacks to effectively digest food as it enters the duodenum.'),
('bcfe1b78-1b19-5a2a-bef2-3d39054866cc'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'High-dose pancreatic enzymes in children with CF can cause:', '["Hyperglycemia","Fibrosing colonopathy","Hypoglycemia","Peptic ulcer","Gallstones"]'::jsonb, 'Fibrosing colonopathy', 'High-dose pancreatic enzymes (>10,000 lipase units/kg/meal) have been associated with fibrosing colonopathy in children with cystic fibrosis.'),
('8019eafa-19c1-5cd8-a83f-3eca7ac689f6'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Enteric-coated pancreatic enzymes are designed to:', '["Improve taste","Prevent inactivation by gastric acid","Increase absorption","Reduce side effects","Enhance absorption"]'::jsonb, 'Prevent inactivation by gastric acid', 'Enteric coating protects enzymes from gastric acid inactivation, allowing them to release in the alkaline duodenum.'),
('0da26dca-cd51-5594-a733-6c7cb001500b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which enzyme deficiency primarily causes steatorrhea?', '["Amylase","Lipase","Protease","Nucleases","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Lipase', 'Lipase deficiency causes fat malabsorption and steatorrhea (fatty, foul-smelling stools).'),
('37919eeb-c1f9-546e-bb1e-c3e8276f8e1c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Creon is a brand name for:', '["Insulin resulting in decreased functional activity","Pancrelipase (pancreatic enzymes)","Omeprazole mediated by specific receptor activation","Metformin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lactulose leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pancrelipase (pancreatic enzymes)', 'Creon is a brand name for pancrelipase, a porcine-derived pancreatic enzyme replacement.'),
('97d5017c-ca2b-5fd9-b5d0-463e459ec6c2'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Pancreatic enzyme supplementation in cystic fibrosis primarily improves:', '["Lung function","Nutritional status and growth","Pancreatic function recovery","Liver function","Kidney function"]'::jsonb, 'Nutritional status and growth', 'Pancreatic enzymes improve fat and protein absorption, leading to better nutrition and growth in CF patients.'),
('8efb1743-0597-5752-8f74-e17b7dbe61b1'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Enteric-coated enzyme microspheres should NOT be crushed because:', '["They taste bad resulting in decreased functional activity","Crushing destroys the acid-protective enteric coating","They are expensive mediated by specific receptor activation","They become toxic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","They become inactive immediately leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Crushing destroys the acid-protective enteric coating', 'Enteric-coated microspheres should not be crushed as it destroys the acid-protective coating, releasing enzymes prematurely in stomach.'),
('e42b3f20-0f43-52ea-818a-4f9699b698c2'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'The dose of pancreatic enzymes is adjusted based on:', '["Body weight only resulting in decreased functional activity","Steatorrhea control and nutritional status","Age only mediated by specific receptor activation","Gender only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Blood glucose levels leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Steatorrhea control and nutritional status', 'Pancreatic enzyme dose is titrated based on response (steatorrhea control) and nutritional status.'),
('cd01ad98-f244-5b82-85eb-2c61d00f7cb3'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which condition causes pancreatic exocrine insufficiency?', '["Chronic pancreatitis","Cystic fibrosis","Pancreatic cancer","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Chronic pancreatitis, cystic fibrosis, and pancreatic cancer all cause pancreatic exocrine insufficiency.'),
('40dfbcd0-6190-55cd-bf7e-d5c70409e494'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Pancreatic enzymes can reduce absorption of which nutrients?', '["Iron and folic acid","Vitamin B12 only","Calcium only","Vitamin C only","All vitamins"]'::jsonb, 'Iron and folic acid', 'High-dose pancreatic enzymes may reduce absorption of iron and folic acid, requiring supplementation.'),
('0a194f5f-4a16-5b0b-b8e3-e355cc858684'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'The most important enzyme in pancreatic replacement is:', '["Trypsin","Lipase","Amylase","Chymotrypsin","Elastase"]'::jsonb, 'Lipase', 'Lipase is the primary enzyme responsible for fat digestion and the most important component of replacement therapy.'),
('fdf485ff-9abd-5964-bcb5-594a259e31b8'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Orlistat works by inhibiting:', '["Gastric acid secretion","Pancreatic lipase","Intestinal absorption of all nutrients","Appetite centrally","Metabolism"]'::jsonb, 'Pancreatic lipase', 'Orlistat is a pancreatic lipase inhibitor that reduces dietary fat absorption by approximately 30%.'),
('56eed7b3-7261-5065-b3f3-6eee08030c23'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'The most common side effect of orlistat is:', '["Headache resulting in decreased functional activity","Steatorrhea, oily stools, and flatulence","Nausea mediated by specific receptor activation","Dizziness without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Insomnia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Steatorrhea, oily stools, and flatulence', 'Steatorrhea with oily spotting, flatus, and fecal urgency are the most common side effects of orlistat.'),
('1b8db823-3b7b-567c-8e1d-6410ffb2521b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Phentermine is classified as:', '["SSRI resulting in decreased functional activity","Sympathomimetic amine (anorectic)","Benzodiazepine mediated by specific receptor activation","Opioid without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Antipsychotic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sympathomimetic amine (anorectic)', 'Phentermine is a sympathomimetic amine that suppresses appetite by stimulating norepinephrine release.'),
('b03310f3-20a8-5cca-a724-dfbb78280fda'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Liraglutide for obesity is administered:', '["Orally","Subcutaneous injection","Intravenous","Intramuscular","Transdermal"]'::jsonb, 'Subcutaneous injection', 'Liraglutide (Saxenda) is a GLP-1 agonist administered as a daily subcutaneous injection for weight management.'),
('e3cfde56-a33c-535f-8e3c-39c2cd035471'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Orlistat can reduce absorption of which vitamins?', '["Vitamin C resulting in decreased functional activity","Fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, K)","Vitamin B12 mediated by specific receptor activation","Folic acid without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vitamin C and B12 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, K)', 'Orlistat reduces absorption of fat-soluble vitamins; supplementation is recommended with long-term use.'),
('1299d01a-9407-5a7e-bba5-928001827486'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'GLP-1 agonists approved or being studied for obesity include:', '["Liraglutide","Semaglutide","Dulaglutide","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Liraglutide, semaglutide, and dulaglutide are GLP-1 agonists approved or being studied for obesity management.'),
('984bfb88-f08f-58b8-b940-6bd5302e9f49'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Orlistat is available OTC at what dose?', '["10 mg","40 mg","60 mg (Alli)","120 mg","240 mg"]'::jsonb, '60 mg (Alli)', 'Low-dose orlistat (Alli, 60mg) is available OTC while higher dose (120mg, Xenical) requires prescription.'),
('e205e335-2ac6-5aa5-8ecc-c3439a69b2ca'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Phentermine should NOT be used for more than:', '["1 week","1 month","12 weeks","6 months","1 year"]'::jsonb, '12 weeks', 'Phentermine is approved for short-term use (up to 12 weeks) due to tolerance development and dependence potential.'),
('ce34f59e-2262-51ab-9388-e0973ff76d5a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Naltrexone/bupropion combination (Contrave) works on:', '["Hypothalamic appetite centers only","Mesolimbic reward system only","Both appetite regulation and reward pathways","Only gut","Only pancreas"]'::jsonb, 'Both appetite regulation and reward pathways', 'This combination acts on both hypothalamic appetite regulation and mesolimbic reward pathways to reduce food intake.'),
('1a6bacd9-0b93-556d-934d-a7dc8e44701e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which anti-obesity drug carries risk of valvular heart disease and was withdrawn?', '["Orlistat resulting in decreased functional activity","Phentermine accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lorcaserin (withdrawn from market)","Liraglutide without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Semaglutide leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lorcaserin (withdrawn from market)', 'Lorcaserin (5-HT2C agonist) was withdrawn due to increased risk of cardiac valvulopathy and cancer.'),
('4f1f4d15-f2b5-5187-a501-1afb50892b97'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Semaglutide for obesity (Wegovy) is administered:', '["Orally resulting in decreased functional activity","Once-weekly subcutaneous injection","Daily injection mediated by specific receptor activation","Transdermal patch without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Intramuscularly leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Once-weekly subcutaneous injection', 'Semaglutide (Wegovy) is administered as a once-weekly subcutaneous injection for weight management.'),
('5ef63488-eea4-52f2-944d-7b417664af8e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Orlistat is most effective when combined with:', '["High-fat diet","Low-fat diet","High-protein diet","Low-carb diet","Fasting"]'::jsonb, 'Low-fat diet', 'Orlistat is most effective and has fewer GI side effects when combined with a low-fat diet (30% or less calories from fat).'),
('11bf126e-3c01-5f6a-90ec-43d18206484d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Phentermine works primarily by:', '["Blocking serotonin receptors","Releasing norepinephrine from nerve terminals","Blocking dopamine receptors","Inhibiting GABA","Stimulating serotonin release"]'::jsonb, 'Releasing norepinephrine from nerve terminals', 'Phentermine releases norepinephrine from nerve terminals, suppressing appetite through hypothalamic stimulation.'),
('b203d7e2-6661-50d7-94c0-1a3a55ca3d09'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Bariatric surgery is generally recommended for BMI:', '[">25 resulting in decreased functional activity",">30 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms",">35 with comorbidities or >40",">50 without affecting the primary tissue pathway",">60 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '>35 with comorbidities or >40', 'Bariatric surgery is recommended for BMI >= 35 with obesity-related comorbidities or BMI >= 40.'),
('5eb3fd9a-90e5-5fb4-b526-ee6f8401031a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'The most effective long-term treatment for severe obesity is:', '["Diet alone","Exercise alone","Medications","Bariatric surgery","All are equally effective"]'::jsonb, 'Bariatric surgery', 'Bariatric surgery produces the most significant and sustained weight loss in severe obesity.'),
('3ef6e585-4959-5fe9-8b65-13ef7551b622'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Phentermine/topiramate (Qsymia) works by:', '["Suppressing appetite only","Suppressing appetite and increasing satiety","Blocking fat absorption","Increasing metabolism only","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Suppressing appetite and increasing satiety', 'Phentermine suppresses appetite while topiramate increases satiety through multiple neurologic mechanisms.'),
('d70d662a-63b2-5844-96d2-13a8c554509b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'All prescription anti-obesity drugs are contraindicated in:', '["Diabetes","Pregnancy","Hypertension","Elderly","Children"]'::jsonb, 'Pregnancy', 'All prescription anti-obesity drugs are contraindicated in pregnancy due to lack of safety data.'),
('556f5bc3-bcf6-577a-90fb-f9b7f1af8f0a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Weight loss with orlistat is typically:', '["1-2% body weight","5-10% body weight","20-30% body weight","40-50% body weight","0%"]'::jsonb, '5-10% body weight', 'Orlistat typically produces 5-10% weight loss when combined with diet and exercise over 1 year.'),
('7d3e27f4-1d9d-54e3-ac25-3c9542dfdae7'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which weight loss drug is a combination of naltrexone and bupropion?', '["Qsymia","Contrave","Xenical","Saxenda","Wegovy"]'::jsonb, 'Contrave', 'Contrave is a combination of naltrexone (opioid antagonist) and bupropion (dopamine/norepinephrine reuptake inhibitor).'),
('0a033e35-d9db-5bfb-b878-ff27fa66bfb3'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Phentermine is structurally similar to:', '["Amphetamine","Diazepam","Omeprazole","Metformin","Aspirin"]'::jsonb, 'Amphetamine', 'Phentermine is a sympathomimetic amine structurally similar to amphetamine, which is why it is a controlled substance.'),
('e7e3d9a9-37f4-509f-b983-486b6df4763c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which of the following PPIs is a prodrug activated in acidic pH?', '["Omeprazole","Lansoprazole","Pantoprazole","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'All PPIs are prodrugs activated in the acidic parietal cell canaliculus.'),
('9a55ebd5-5c26-5341-8bf2-8a9d023e71fe'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which PPI has the highest oral bioavailability?', '["Omeprazole","Lansoprazole","Esomeprazole","Pantoprazole","Rabeprazole"]'::jsonb, 'Esomeprazole', 'Esomeprazole (S-enantiomer of omeprazole) has higher bioavailability due to reduced first-pass metabolism.'),
('349a6301-866e-555a-9c04-6dd04cbf9afc'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Metoclopramide crosses the blood-brain barrier because it is:', '["Large molecule","Lipophilic","Hydrophilic","Protein-bound","Highly ionized"]'::jsonb, 'Lipophilic', 'Metoclopramide is lipophilic, allowing it to cross the BBB and cause CNS side effects like EPS.'),
('2eef7b64-f06d-54b7-8bfa-5860137b5086'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Lactulose works in hepatic encephalopathy by:', '["Osmotic effect drawing water into colon","Conversion to organic acids lowering colonic pH","Trapping ammonia in colon as ammonium ions","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Lactulose is osmotically active, metabolized to organic acids lowering pH, and traps ammonia as ammonium ions.'),
('09f7c055-f416-54c9-a33b-194bdb7278c2'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'First-line treatment for mild UC includes:', '["Prednisolone resulting in decreased functional activity","5-ASA (mesalazine) oral and/or topical","Azathioprine mediated by specific receptor activation","Infliximab without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cyclosporine leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '5-ASA (mesalazine) oral and/or topical', '5-ASA compounds are first-line for mild to moderate UC, both for induction and maintenance.'),
('2678a105-a506-5a88-a03f-b2bc5a1d2872'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Infliximab is classified as:', '["Small molecule drug","Chimeric monoclonal antibody","Receptor antagonist","Kinase inhibitor","Peptide analog"]'::jsonb, 'Chimeric monoclonal antibody', 'Infliximab is a chimeric (mouse-human) monoclonal antibody against TNF-alpha, administered IV.'),
('4095f2d0-47d5-5ef2-8025-4428732b8f98'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'UDCA is preferred in which type of gallstones for dissolution?', '["Pigment stones","Small cholesterol stones (<10mm)","Calcified stones","Mixed stones with calcium","Large stones (>2cm)"]'::jsonb, 'Small cholesterol stones (<10mm)', 'UDCA is effective for small (<10mm) cholesterol-rich gallstones, not for pigment or calcified stones.'),
('94e0ced2-dcec-562a-a739-ca2ef1f852ce'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which biologic is administered subcutaneously (not IV)?', '["Infliximab resulting in decreased functional activity","Adalimumab accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Vedolizumab mediated by specific receptor activation","Both B and some formulations of C","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Both B and some formulations of C', 'Adalimumab is SC; vedolizumab is available IV and subcutaneous formulations in some regions.'),
('7854fd4d-29cc-5b8d-87de-0916e7e60d0e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Tofacitinib (JAK inhibitor) carries black box warnings for:', '["Infections","Venous thromboembolism","Malignancy","All of the above","None"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'JAK inhibitors carry black box warnings for serious infections, VTE, and malignancy.'),
('edd96a97-92a4-527a-be6d-9a23208453c8'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Prucalopride is indicated for:', '["Chronic constipation (primarily in women)","Acute diarrhea accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","IBD mediated by specific receptor activation","GERD without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Gallstones leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Chronic constipation (primarily in women)', 'Prucalopride is a selective 5-HT4 agonist indicated for chronic idiopathic constipation.'),
('6b7b59a6-2aa2-5a05-a5a6-1e766ee9ec1a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'The main mechanism of loperamide is:', '["Central opioid effect","Peripheral mu-opioid agonism in gut","Serotonin antagonism","Dopamine blockade","Calcium channel blockade"]'::jsonb, 'Peripheral mu-opioid agonism in gut', 'Loperamide acts peripherally on mu-opioid receptors in the gut, not centrally, so it doesn''t cause analgesia.'),
('37e98555-5f14-56d7-b409-678fe4ecd717'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Bulk-forming laxatives include:', '["Bisacodyl","Psyllium (Isabgol)","Lactulose","PEG","Docusate"]'::jsonb, 'Psyllium (Isabgol)', 'Psyllium is a bulk-forming laxative that absorbs water and forms a gel, increasing stool bulk and promoting peristalsis.'),
('4fa84027-e2b9-5615-b254-7dd46bb01f43'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which drug can cause orange-red discoloration of urine?', '["Rifampin","Phenazopyridine","Metoclopramide","Omeprazole","Aspirin"]'::jsonb, 'Phenazopyridine', 'Phenazopyridine (urinary tract analgesic) causes harmless orange-red discoloration of urine.'),
('1db16356-b025-5e14-865e-3a4c284d60ff'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which IBD drug requires monitoring for hepatotoxicity?', '["Mesalazine","Azathioprine","Both A and B","Prednisolone","Infliximab"]'::jsonb, 'Both A and B', 'Both mesalazine and azathioprine can cause hepatotoxicity and require LFT monitoring.'),
('2386a3eb-ae01-59a2-b9cb-dc91d00369aa'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Pantoprazole has the least effect on which CYP enzymes?', '["CYP2C19 and CYP3A4","CYP1A2","CYP2D6","CYP2E1","CYP2B6"]'::jsonb, 'CYP2C19 and CYP3A4', 'Pantoprazole has minimal inhibition of CYP2C19 and CYP3A4, making it the safest PPI for drug interactions.'),
('0d8bc7c7-c957-51db-8685-460ee2c895ea'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which drug is used for opioid-induced constipation without reversing central analgesia?', '["Loperamide","Methylnaltrexone","Bisacodyl","PEG","Docusate"]'::jsonb, 'Methylnaltrexone', 'Methylnaltrexone is a peripheral mu-opioid antagonist specifically for opioid-induced constipation.'),
('4aa07ba3-e8a8-53d5-bdf1-ceb9837a5b60'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Domperidone is mainly used for:', '["Diarrhea resulting in decreased functional activity","Nausea, vomiting, and gastroparesis","Constipation mediated by specific receptor activation","GERD without affecting the primary tissue pathway","IBD leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Nausea, vomiting, and gastroparesis', 'Domperidone is a prokinetic and antiemetic used for nausea, vomiting, and gastroparesis.'),
('31a45b83-41c3-53bb-a3e3-e79c938ab10f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'The drug of choice for chemotherapy-induced acute emesis is:', '["Metoclopramide","5-HT3 antagonist (e.g., ondansetron)","Promethazine","Dexamethasone alone","Lorazepam alone"]'::jsonb, '5-HT3 antagonist (e.g., ondansetron)', '5-HT3 antagonists like ondansetron are first-line for acute CINV (within 24 hours).'),
('230967d3-69b5-5dbd-91f8-082a83caa463'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Lactulose dose for hepatic encephalopathy is titrated to achieve:', '["2-3 soft stools per day","Diarrhea","Constipation","No stools","1 hard stool per day"]'::jsonb, '2-3 soft stools per day', 'Lactulose dose is titrated to achieve 2-3 soft stools per day to optimize ammonia excretion.'),
('d1f0eb31-6ddc-52a2-a59f-45d4a087c9cc'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which drug increases gastric motility and is used for diabetic gastroparesis?', '["Omeprazole","Metoclopramide","Cimetidine","Ranitidine","Antacids"]'::jsonb, 'Metoclopramide', 'Metoclopramide is a prokinetic agent that increases gastric motility and emptying in gastroparesis.'),
('496ecd6c-2bda-5561-83be-14623e86b0ce'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Misoprostol is contraindicated in women of childbearing age not using contraception because:', '["It causes weight gain resulting in decreased functional activity","It can cause uterine contractions and miscarriage","It causes headache mediated by specific receptor activation","It causes diarrhea without affecting the primary tissue pathway","It causes constipation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'It can cause uterine contractions and miscarriage', 'Misoprostol can cause uterine contractions and miscarriage, making it contraindicated in women not using contraception.'),
('83fe400e-21f2-5743-a1ba-79b5fc26848c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Cholestyramine can cause:', '["Constipation","Diarrhea","Abdominal cramps","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Cholestyramine commonly causes GI effects including constipation, diarrhea, and abdominal cramps.'),
('1956aae7-e388-5cbc-88d7-c86f0daa4484'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'The half-life of omeprazole is approximately:', '["0.5 hours resulting in decreased functional activity","1 hour accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","2-3 hours (but duration of action much longer)","12 hours without affecting the primary tissue pathway","24 hours leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '2-3 hours (but duration of action much longer)', 'Omeprazole has a plasma half-life of approximately 1-2 hours, but its duration of action is much longer due to irreversible binding to proton pump.'),
('5eb01e7c-9f98-5c68-852c-8d80a621c706'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Misoprostol is used for postpartum hemorrhage because it:', '["Constricts uterine smooth muscle","Increases uterine tone and sustained contraction","Reduces bleeding","Both A and C","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Both A and C', 'Misoprostol (PGE1 analog) constricts uterine smooth muscle, causing sustained contraction that reduces postpartum hemorrhage.'),
('6e58575d-04b9-5220-a7a2-bce8608ab338'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'The primary site of action of sucralfate is:', '["Esophagus resulting in decreased functional activity","Stomach (at ulcer site in acidic pH)","Duodenum mediated by specific receptor activation","Colon without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Small intestine leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Stomach (at ulcer site in acidic pH)', 'Sucralfate acts primarily in the stomach where it polymerizes at pH <4 to form a protective barrier over ulcers.'),
('bb55e49f-bd59-5040-b67e-27e018e2fd78'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which antiemetic should be given slowly IV to prevent hypotension?', '["Ondansetron","Metoclopramide","Prochlorperazine","Promethazine","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'All these antiemetics can cause hypotension if given rapidly IV and should be administered slowly.'),
('a7de0707-2396-5b6c-9215-ebbc48483294'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'The mainstay of treatment for symptomatic gallstones is:', '["UDCA resulting in decreased functional activity","Cholestyramine accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Laparoscopic cholecystectomy","Antibiotics without affecting the primary tissue pathway","PPIs leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Laparoscopic cholecystectomy', 'Laparoscopic cholecystectomy is the definitive treatment for symptomatic gallstones.'),
('e6d314bf-b3a4-532e-ab8e-3d3f8803b224'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Octreotide is a synthetic analog of:', '["Insulin","Somatostatin","Growth hormone","Glucagon","Thyroxine"]'::jsonb, 'Somatostatin', 'Octreotide is a synthetic somatostatin analog used for acromegaly, variceal bleeding, and carcinoid syndrome.'),
('d8e927a7-a974-5d4a-b9b6-8c5e04afc8f7'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'The main mechanism of 5-ASA in IBD is:', '["Immunosuppression resulting in decreased functional activity","Local anti-inflammatory effect in gut mucosa","Antibacterial mediated by specific receptor activation","Antispasmodic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Analgesic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Local anti-inflammatory effect in gut mucosa', '5-ASA (mesalazine) has local anti-inflammatory effects in the gut mucosa, inhibiting prostaglandin and leukotriene synthesis.'),
('e9bbb54a-cd0e-566a-8b89-ad04b6c15f81'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Omeprazole is metabolized primarily by:', '["CYP2C19 and CYP3A4","CYP1A2","CYP2D6","CYP2E1","CYP2B6"]'::jsonb, 'CYP2C19 and CYP3A4', 'Omeprazole is metabolized primarily by CYP2C19 and CYP3A4, leading to significant drug interactions.'),
('85656008-6117-5ec8-bc5a-e7e8c49f1f43'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'The most important drug interaction of metoclopramide is with:', '["Antacids resulting in decreased functional activity","Levodopa (in Parkinson''s disease)","Antibiotics mediated by specific receptor activation","Antihistamines without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vitamins leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Levodopa (in Parkinson''s disease)', 'Metoclopramide can worsen Parkinson''s disease by blocking dopamine receptors, counteracting levodopa.'),
('249af5f9-0439-58ec-a808-7037311265d0'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Racecadotril is used in:', '["Adult diarrhea resulting in decreased functional activity","Pediatric diarrhea (preferred over loperamide)","Chronic constipation mediated by specific receptor activation","IBD without affecting the primary tissue pathway","GERD leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pediatric diarrhea (preferred over loperamide)', 'Racecadotril is preferred in children because it reduces secretion without affecting motility, avoiding constipation.'),
('35c334fa-96f6-5c17-ae20-4deff201e83d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which drug is used for portal hypertensive bleeding?', '["Octreotide","Terlipressin","Propranolol (for prophylaxis)","All of the above","None"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Octreotide, terlipressin, and propranolol are all used in management of portal hypertensive bleeding.'),
('69fe1160-b46d-5cc5-b5f9-c4d4de01ebf2'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Bismuth subsalicylate is used in H. pylori:', '["Triple therapy resulting in decreased functional activity","Quadruple therapy (bismuth quadruple)","Dual therapy mediated by specific receptor activation","Monotherapy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Not used leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Quadruple therapy (bismuth quadruple)', 'Bismuth subsalicylate is part of bismuth quadruple therapy (PPI + bismuth + metronidazole + tetracycline).'),
('ff8089bc-8458-51a5-b62c-0ed2bbc35911'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Erythromycin as a prokinetic is most useful in:', '["Chronic gastroparesis resulting in decreased functional activity","Acute hospitalized gastroparesis (IV)","GERD mediated by specific receptor activation","IBS without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Constipation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Acute hospitalized gastroparesis (IV)', 'Erythromycin IV is most useful for acute gastroparesis in hospitalized patients.'),
('f564e07e-5e46-52fd-b139-da392d042c67'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which drug for obesity reduces fat absorption?', '["Orlistat","Phentermine","Liraglutide","Semaglutide","Naltrexone/bupropion"]'::jsonb, 'Orlistat', 'Orlistat is a pancreatic lipase inhibitor that reduces dietary fat absorption by about 30%.'),
('f9dab58c-4114-5ff5-9bec-a7dade2e3950'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'The main advantage of budesonide over prednisolone is:', '["More potent resulting in decreased functional activity","90% first-pass metabolism reduces systemic effects","Cheaper mediated by specific receptor activation","Available IV without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fewer drug interactions leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '90% first-pass metabolism reduces systemic effects', 'Budesonide has approximately 90% first-pass hepatic metabolism, significantly reducing systemic corticosteroid side effects.'),
('2faf6f0e-cbb0-5bfc-9541-e74fa7f6c1ce'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'The main indication for erythromycin as a prokinetic (not antibiotic) is:', '["GERD","Gastroparesis","IBS","Constipation","Diarrhea"]'::jsonb, 'Gastroparesis', 'Erythromycin is used as a prokinetic for gastroparesis, particularly acute cases in hospitalized patients.'),
('e7f638fe-1b48-5efc-9448-1a7c4e3a1977'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Linaclotide works by:', '["Guanylate cyclase-C agonist (increases intestinal secretion)","Chloride channel activator accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","5-HT4 agonist mediated by specific receptor activation","Opioid agonist without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anticholinergic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Guanylate cyclase-C agonist (increases intestinal secretion)', 'Linaclotide is a guanylate cyclase-C agonist that increases intestinal fluid secretion and transit.'),
('82e4e16f-4b4a-5b4d-b3dd-78947206bfa7'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Ustekinumab after initial IV induction is maintained by:', '["IV every 8 weeks","SC every 8 weeks","Oral daily","IM monthly","Transdermal"]'::jsonb, 'SC every 8 weeks', 'After IV induction, ustekinumab is maintained as SC injection every 8 weeks.'),
('f46facb5-5324-5a19-8656-ba5ae063c3a9'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which drug for IBD requires monitoring of TPMT before initiation?', '["Mesalazine","Infliximab","Azathioprine","Prednisolone","Budesonide"]'::jsonb, 'Azathioprine', 'TPMT testing before azathioprine prevents severe myelosuppression in TPMT-deficient patients.'),
('63cf5df2-2b79-5398-a56c-ad18729e7724'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which of the following is a stool softener?', '["Bisacodyl","Docusate sodium","Senna","PEG","Lactulose"]'::jsonb, 'Docusate sodium', 'Docusate is a surfactant stool softener that allows water and fat to penetrate stool.'),
('59e81cef-bf38-59d1-9686-41a7081c1dfe'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which drug is used for diabetic gastroparesis?', '["Metoclopramide","Domperidone","Erythromycin","All of the above","None"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Metoclopramide, domperidone, and erythromycin are all used for diabetic gastroparesis.'),
('5937a682-abc8-5360-a29d-805fa760b5cd'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Prednisolone in IBD is used for:', '["Mild disease maintenance","Moderate to severe disease flares","Mild disease induction","All of the above","None"]'::jsonb, 'Moderate to severe disease flares', 'Corticosteroids are used for moderate-severe IBD flares but not for maintenance due to side effects.'),
('57ddfb03-5afc-5f32-ba32-12811087b342'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which biologic is a fully human monoclonal antibody (not chimeric)?', '["Infliximab","Adalimumab","Both","Neither","All biologics"]'::jsonb, 'Adalimumab', 'Adalimumab is fully human; infliximab is chimeric (mouse-human).'),
('1c0faf9d-a879-5d5c-9900-569c833959bd'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'The main advantage of esomeprazole over omeprazole is:', '["Higher potency due to better bioavailability","Fewer side effects accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lower cost mediated by specific receptor activation","Better taste without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Available IV only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Higher potency due to better bioavailability', 'Esomeprazole (S-enantiomer) has higher bioavailability and more potent acid suppression than omeprazole.'),
('23aeffd0-74c5-57e3-bc0a-5038cb7163ae'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Domperidone should be avoided with:', '["Antacids","QT-prolonging drugs","Antibiotics","Antivirals","Antifungals"]'::jsonb, 'QT-prolonging drugs', 'Domperidone prolongs QT interval and should be avoided with other QT-prolonging drugs due to arrhythmia risk.'),
('a1e46a3d-2dee-5fa9-ab30-2042da0d5a5f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Lubiprostone is a:', '["Chloride channel activator","5-HT4 agonist","Guanylate cyclase-C agonist","Opioid agonist","Anticholinergic"]'::jsonb, 'Chloride channel activator', 'Lubiprostone is a chloride channel activator that increases chloride and fluid secretion in the intestine.'),
('7be4df13-7b64-5cdf-acfe-0bbe3d12854e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Vedolizumab is gut-selective because it blocks:', '["TNF-alpha everywhere resulting in decreased functional activity","alpha4beta7 integrin (gut-specific lymphocyte trafficking)","IL-12/23 mediated by specific receptor activation","JAK1 only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All integrins leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'alpha4beta7 integrin (gut-specific lymphocyte trafficking)', 'Vedolizumab selectively blocks alpha4beta7 integrin, preventing lymphocyte trafficking specifically to gut tissue.'),
('134c3fd0-461c-5e9f-b9b4-63447d06d976'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'The main mechanism of metoclopramide''s antiemetic effect is:', '["5-HT4 agonism","D2 receptor antagonism in CTZ","5-HT3 antagonism","NK1 antagonism","H1 antagonism"]'::jsonb, 'D2 receptor antagonism in CTZ', 'Metoclopramide blocks D2 receptors in the chemoreceptor trigger zone, providing its antiemetic effect.'),
('aefe4b0c-7b95-53e1-ae62-67d1ed4030b5'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which drug for IBD is used for both Crohn''s and UC?', '["Mesalazine (more effective in UC)","Infliximab","Azathioprine","All of the above","None"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Mesalazine, infliximab, and azathioprine can all be used for both Crohn''s disease and UC.'),
('8010380c-8574-5b4e-9368-05388cc61046'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Dexamethasone as an antiemetic in chemotherapy is given:', '["Only on day 1 resulting in decreased functional activity","Days 1-4 for delayed CINV prevention","Weekly mediated by specific receptor activation","Monthly without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Not used leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Days 1-4 for delayed CINV prevention', 'Dexamethasone is given on days 1-4 for prevention of both acute and delayed CINV.'),
('296fab3b-d1b9-5b90-854c-23b77ccec3a8'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'The main side effect of linaclotide is:', '["Headache","Diarrhea","Nausea","Dizziness","Rash"]'::jsonb, 'Diarrhea', 'Diarrhea is the most common side effect of linaclotide, occurring in about 20% of patients.'),
('5b6882b8-799d-57e3-8b6c-5c999f99275a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Rifaximin is used for:', '["Traveler''s diarrhea","Hepatic encephalopathy","IBS with diarrhea","All of the above","None"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Rifaximin is a non-absorbable antibiotic used for traveler''s diarrhea, hepatic encephalopathy prophylaxis, and IBS-D.'),
('77051437-aec8-511a-aecf-e67bfb1db5c4'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'The main difference between metoclopramide and domperidone is:', '["Metoclopramide crosses BBB causing EPS; domperidone doesn''t","Both cross BBB equally","Neither crosses BBB","Domperidone crosses BBB, metoclopramide doesn''t","Same mechanism and side effects"]'::jsonb, 'Metoclopramide crosses BBB causing EPS; domperidone doesn''t', 'Metoclopramide crosses BBB causing EPS; domperidone is aP-gp substrate that doesn''t cross BBB significantly.'),
('7518394f-d974-5ae4-8560-06503c3e04b9'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'The main adverse effect of long-term corticosteroids in IBD is:', '["Immunosuppression","Metabolic effects (diabetes, osteoporosis)","Growth retardation in children","All of the above","None"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Long-term corticosteroids cause immunosuppression, metabolic effects, growth retardation, and many other adverse effects.'),
('4c3040f3-abce-50f7-81bc-88fb3dc692a8'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Propranolol for portal hypertension works by:', '["Reducing portal pressure via beta-blockade","Blocking acid secretion","Increasing gut motility","Binding bile acids","Dissolving clots"]'::jsonb, 'Reducing portal pressure via beta-blockade', 'Propranolol (non-selective beta-blocker) reduces portal pressure by decreasing splanchnic blood flow and cardiac output.'),
('12701750-9060-5a85-b079-bf21d19b89e9'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'The main side effect of everolimus in pNETs is:', '["Stomatitis","Infection","Pneumonitis","All of the above","None"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Everolimus commonly causes stomatitis, infections, and pneumonitis in patients with pancreatic neuroendocrine tumors.'),
('ea269a64-5034-55a4-aea4-d3e8af4218df'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Telotristat for carcinoid syndrome works by:', '["Inhibiting tryptophan hydroxylase (reducing serotonin synthesis)","Blocking somatostatin receptors accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inhibiting mTOR mediated by specific receptor activation","Blocking TNF-alpha without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inhibiting JAK leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibiting tryptophan hydroxylase (reducing serotonin synthesis)', 'Telotristat inhibits tryptophan hydroxylase, the rate-limiting enzyme in serotonin synthesis.'),
('6226ae75-759f-54f2-bddb-522843c8f7bd'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Carvedilol for portal hypertension is preferred because it:', '["Has both alpha and beta blocking activity","Reduces portal pressure more effectively than propranolol","Is better tolerated","All of the above","None"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Carvedilol has both alpha and beta blocking activity, providing more effective portal pressure reduction.'),
('65a12b84-b54e-547f-9c07-c7c535b25e72'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'The main contraindication for PPIs in long-term use includes:', '["Hypomagnesemia","Hypocalcemia","Hyperkalemia","Hypernatremia","Hyponatremia"]'::jsonb, 'Hypomagnesemia', 'Long-term PPI use can cause hypomagnesemia, which may also contribute to hypocalcemia and hypokalemia.'),
('6fb507c9-d16b-5d79-9ce2-83d00ce9b9c3'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Balsalazide is a prodrug that delivers:', '["5-ASA directly to colon","Sulfapyridine","Mesalamine to stomach","Prednisolone","Azathioprine"]'::jsonb, '5-ASA directly to colon', 'Balsalazide is a 5-ASA prodrug linked to an inert carrier, delivering 5-ASA to the colon.'),
('1fa3df4d-3169-5594-92e8-bd82c9daf711'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which drug is used for constipation-predominant IBS in women?', '["Linaclotide","Lubiprostone","Alosetron (5-HT3 antagonist, limited use)","All of the above","None"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Linaclotide, lubiprostone, and alosetron are all used for IBS-C, with alosetron limited to severe cases in women.'),
('b9a34eba-7ff4-54d0-b1fc-dd4b8e200d2c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'The main difference between stimulant and osmotic laxatives is:', '["Mechanism of action","Speed of onset","Risk of dependence with chronic stimulant use","All of the above","None"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'They differ in mechanism, onset speed, and stimulant laxatives carry risk of dependence with chronic use.'),
('321d8a63-90d7-59e5-9583-81ac552f78cb'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which drug is used for Barrett''s esophagus?', '["High-dose PPIs","H2 blockers","Antacids","Sucralfate","Misoprostol"]'::jsonb, 'High-dose PPIs', 'High-dose PPIs are used for Barrett''s esophagus to suppress acid and reduce progression risk to dysplasia/cancer.'),
('47bab599-1245-528a-bf5e-282503978867'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'The main mechanism of UDCA in PBC is:', '["Dissolving gallstones resulting in decreased functional activity","Immunomodulatory and choleretic (protecting bile ducts)","Antibacterial mediated by specific receptor activation","Antispasmodic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Analgesic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Immunomodulatory and choleretic (protecting bile ducts)', 'UDCA has immunomodulatory and choleretic properties that protect bile ducts and improve bile flow in PBC.'),
('49a8f6a4-ea06-5533-8e9f-0e6ab4f32509'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Acarbose (used for diabetes) can also cause:', '["Flatulence and bloating","Hypoglycemia","Weight gain","Constipation","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Flatulence and bloating', 'Acarbose inhibits alpha-glucosidase, causing undigested carbohydrates to reach colon and be fermented, causing flatulence.'),
('f2df92c2-5004-5065-a0aa-57c659497487'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'The most important aspect of managing gallstone disease is:', '["Dissolving stones resulting in decreased functional activity","Preventing complications (cholecystitis, pancreatitis)","Pain control only mediated by specific receptor activation","Antibiotics without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Dietary changes only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Preventing complications (cholecystitis, pancreatitis)', 'Management focuses on preventing complications like cholecystitis, cholangitis, and pancreatitis.'),
('08d9684e-44e7-5e8f-b176-767bf16281d9'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which drug is used for esophageal candidiasis in immunocompromised patients?', '["Fluconazole","Nystatin oral suspension","Clotrimazole troches","All of the above","None"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Fluconazole, nystatin, and clotrimazole are all used for esophageal candidiasis depending on severity.'),
('2245e277-22ce-5d9d-a5ef-54076954046e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'The main advantage of valacyclovir over acyclovir is:', '["Better bioavailability and less frequent dosing","More potent accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fewer side effects mediated by specific receptor activation","Lower cost without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Available IV leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Better bioavailability and less frequent dosing', 'Valacyclovir has better oral bioavailability (55% vs 15-30%) and requires less frequent dosing than acyclovir.'),
('35275493-111c-59b8-8c4f-eba42a2818db'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Imatinib for GIST works by inhibiting:', '["KIT (CD117) and PDGFRA tyrosine kinases","VEGF receptor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","EGFR mediated by specific receptor activation","HER2 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","BCR-ABL only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'KIT (CD117) and PDGFRA tyrosine kinases', 'Imatinib inhibits KIT (CD117), PDGFRA, and BCR-ABL tyrosine kinases, effective in GIST with KIT mutations.'),
('adc2ed4c-092f-5f49-b7e4-003d20e77386'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Sorafenib for hepatocellular carcinoma is a:', '["Multi-kinase inhibitor (VEGFR, PDGFR, RAF)","TNF-alpha inhibitor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","JAK inhibitor mediated by specific receptor activation","5-HT3 antagonist without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Proton pump inhibitor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Multi-kinase inhibitor (VEGFR, PDGFR, RAF)', 'Sorafenib is a multi-kinase inhibitor targeting VEGFR, PDGFR, and RAF kinases, used for advanced HCC.'),
('05f81ddc-4233-50e7-8b7f-0ce6271bc033'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'The most common cause of pancreatitis worldwide is:', '["Alcohol","Gallstones","Drugs","Hyperlipidemia","Trauma"]'::jsonb, 'Gallstones', 'Gallstones and alcohol are the two most common causes, with gallstones being most common worldwide.'),
('8ade180e-d95c-5377-9a76-a390e31348d3'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'FOLFIRINOX for pancreatic cancer includes:', '["5-FU, leucovorin, irinotecan, oxaliplatin","Gemcitabine alone","5-FU and cisplatin","Gemcitabine and paclitaxel","Capecitabine and oxaliplatin"]'::jsonb, '5-FU, leucovorin, irinotecan, oxaliplatin', 'FOLFIRINOX is a combination of 5-FU, leucovorin, irinotecan, and oxaliplatin for advanced pancreatic cancer.'),
('3de28359-73ad-560c-b203-45c41258a507'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'The main side effect of gemcitabine is:', '["Myelosuppression","Flu-like symptoms","Hepatotoxicity","All of the above","None"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Gemcitabine commonly causes myelosuppression, flu-like symptoms, and hepatotoxicity.'),
('1396136e-00d2-516d-a7a6-3b8c4d692004'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Phentermine is contraindicated with:', '["MAO inhibitors (risk of hypertensive crisis)","Antacids accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Antibiotics mediated by specific receptor activation","Antihistamines without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vitamins leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'MAO inhibitors (risk of hypertensive crisis)', 'Phentermine is contraindicated with MAO inhibitors due to risk of hypertensive crisis.'),
('2376058d-7113-57a1-b786-263232d25fda'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which drug is used for achalasia?', '["Nifedipine (calcium channel blocker)","Isosorbide dinitrate","Botulinum toxin injection","All of the above","None"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Calcium channel blockers, nitrates, and botulinum toxin are all used for achalasia management.'),
('4d50dec8-4b0a-5bac-89d0-a64eeaa2ec7e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'The main indication for pancreatic enzyme replacement is:', '["Acute pancreatitis resulting in decreased functional activity","Chronic pancreatic exocrine insufficiency","Diabetes mediated by specific receptor activation","Celiac disease without affecting the primary tissue pathway","GERD leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Chronic pancreatic exocrine insufficiency', 'Pancreatic enzyme replacement is indicated for chronic pancreatic exocrine insufficiency causing malabsorption.'),
('ac38a954-a57c-5c52-921e-156a87884026'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which of the following can cause drug-induced pancreatitis?', '["Azathioprine","Valproic acid","Didanosine","All of the above","None"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Multiple drugs including azathioprine, valproic acid, and didanosine can cause drug-induced pancreatitis.'),
('96dd751c-616a-59ca-aeb8-8c13cb005dda'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'The main mechanism of nifedipine in achalasia is:', '["Smooth muscle relaxation via calcium channel blockade","Reducing LES pressure accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anticholinergic effect mediated by specific receptor activation","All of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Smooth muscle relaxation via calcium channel blockade', 'Nifedipine relaxes smooth muscle by blocking calcium channels, reducing LES pressure in achalasia.'),
('5cef2364-c784-56ab-aaf6-5504034ff9a5'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which biologic for IBD is available as both IV and SC formulations?', '["Infliximab","Adalimumab","Vedolizumab","Ustekinumab","All"]'::jsonb, 'Vedolizumab', 'Vedolizumab is available as both IV infusion and SC injection in various markets.'),
('d15073bd-d5af-5b61-a433-78b276f7f422'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'The main side effect of methylnaltrexone is:', '["Abdominal pain","Diarrhea","Nausea","Dizziness","Headache"]'::jsonb, 'Diarrhea', 'Diarrhea is the most common side effect of methylnaltrexone, occurring in up to 30% of patients.'),
('b3ddbfca-821e-5654-9854-284ea6ae55ca'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Which drug is used for fecal incontinence?', '["Low-dose loperamide","Bisacodyl","PEG","Psyllium","Docusate"]'::jsonb, 'Low-dose loperamide', 'Low-dose loperamide can increase anal sphincter tone and is used for fecal incontinence.'),
('c85ca5c1-d744-56c7-97d5-2d1fea5afbce'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'The most common benign liver tumor is:', '["Hepatic hemangioma","Focal nodular hyperplasia","Hepatic adenoma","All equally common","None"]'::jsonb, 'Hepatic hemangioma', 'Hepatic hemangioma is the most common benign liver tumor, found in up to 5% of the population.'),
('11edc064-2c0e-5a72-a1a4-4e0c658c711b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'The main complication of hepatic adenoma (>5cm) is:', '["Hemorrhage","Malignant transformation","Both A and B","Infection","Rupture only"]'::jsonb, 'Both A and B', 'Hepatic adenomas >5cm carry risk of both hemorrhage and malignant transformation to HCC.'),
('da162b3f-0a16-5e1e-a6f2-488aa581b861'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Treatment of echinococcal (hydatid) cyst includes:', '["Albendazole resulting in decreased functional activity","PAIR procedure accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Surgical excision mediated by specific receptor activation","All of the above depending on stage","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above depending on stage', 'Treatment depends on cyst stage: albendazole, PAIR, or surgery.'),
('9b985a0d-4e4d-5055-a3e5-37392c580cde'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'Hepatic angiosarcoma is associated with exposure to:', '["Vinyl chloride","Thorotrast","Arsenic","All of the above","None"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Hepatic angiosarcoma is associated with vinyl chloride, thorotrast, and arsenic exposure.'),
('33ab95e3-2b09-598c-a819-70fc402f77c8'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'AFP (alpha-fetoprotein) is a tumor marker for:', '["Hepatocellular carcinoma","Hepatoblastoma","Germ cell tumors","All of the above","None"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'AFP is elevated in HCC, hepatoblastoma, and germ cell tumors.'),
('78ca783a-cb8c-50cc-b505-11db5b6aae48'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'The main side effect of cholestyramine is:', '["Liver damage resulting in decreased functional activity","Constipation (most common)","Diarrhea mediated by specific receptor activation","Headache without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Nausea leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Constipation (most common)', 'Constipation is the most common side effect of cholestyramine, occurring in up to 20% of patients.'),
('b3c82d9d-392a-551b-b138-7de5c34ef765'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Hepatobiliary - GIT Drugs', 'The most common cause of death in acute severe pancreatitis is:', '["Infection","Multi-organ failure","Hemorrhage","Pseudocyst","Diabetes"]'::jsonb, 'Multi-organ failure', 'Multi-organ failure (especially respiratory and renal) is the most common cause of death in acute severe pancreatitis.'),
('c147e88c-a7e8-5299-8f5c-a8b19bacd8ee'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Salbutamol primarily acts on which receptor?', '["Beta-1 adrenergic receptor","Beta-2 adrenergic receptor","Alpha-1 adrenergic receptor","Muscarinic M3 receptor","Histamine H1 receptor"]'::jsonb, 'Beta-2 adrenergic receptor', 'Salbutamol (albuterol) is a selective beta-2 adrenergic agonist used as a bronchodilator. It causes relaxation of bronchial smooth muscle.'),
('7222b380-261c-5050-af1b-d1c01115d4d5'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Which is the most commonly used short-acting beta-2 agonist (SABA) for acute bronchospasm?', '["Salmeterol","Formoterol","Salbutamol","Budesonide","Ipratropium"]'::jsonb, 'Salbutamol', 'Salbutamol (albuterol) is the most commonly used SABA for acute relief of bronchospasm. It has rapid onset of action (5-15 minutes) and duration of 3-6 hours.'),
('e01b0be1-224f-58bf-b930-ab7da400bd00'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'The onset of action of nebulized salbutamol is approximately:', '["1-2 minutes","5-15 minutes","30-45 minutes","1-2 hours","3-4 hours"]'::jsonb, '5-15 minutes', 'Nebulized salbutamol has an onset of action of approximately 5-15 minutes, making it ideal for acute bronchospasm relief.'),
('f4ae8691-618c-5221-8475-2223d9a6c6ff'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Which side effect is most commonly associated with systemic beta-2 agonists?', '["Bradycardia","Hypotension","Tremor and tachycardia","Constipation","Miosis"]'::jsonb, 'Tremor and tachycardia', 'Systemic beta-2 agonists cause tremor (due to beta-2 stimulation on skeletal muscle) and tachycardia (due to some beta-1 activity or reflex tachycardia).'),
('de51b4c1-8575-55d7-85dc-33ddbcc880cd'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Terbutaline is preferred over salbutamol in which clinical situation?', '["Acute asthma attack","Preterm labor suppression","COPD exacerbation","Pulmonary edema","Cystic fibrosis"]'::jsonb, 'Preterm labor suppression', 'Terbutaline is a beta-2 agonist used as a tocolytic agent to suppress preterm labor by relaxing uterine smooth muscle.'),
('c2f2dfb3-f044-5b7e-b3f0-f525961cfc6f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Metaproterenol differs from salbutamol in that it:', '["Is more selective for beta-2 receptors","Has no beta-1 activity","Is less selective for beta-2 receptors","Has a longer duration of action","Is only available as an inhaled formulation"]'::jsonb, 'Is less selective for beta-2 receptors', 'Metaproterenol is a non-selective beta agonist with significant beta-1 activity, making it less selective than salbutamol for beta-2 receptors.'),
('04ad9d16-1322-53da-83f0-7e09004fcd63'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Salmeterol is classified as a:', '["Short-acting beta-2 agonist (SABA)","Long-acting beta-2 agonist (LABA)","Anticholinergic bronchodilator","Methylxanthine","Leukotriene receptor antagonist"]'::jsonb, 'Long-acting beta-2 agonist (LABA)', 'Salmeterol is a LABA with duration of action of approximately 12 hours. It should not be used as monotherapy for asthma.'),
('8d02c323-5a68-5142-910f-deccd6f8ee40'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Formoterol differs from salmeterol in that formoterol has:', '["Slower onset of action","No intrinsic activity","Intrinsic partial agonist activity","Shorter duration of action","Greater beta-1 selectivity"]'::jsonb, 'Intrinsic partial agonist activity', 'Formoterol has intrinsic partial agonist activity, unlike salmeterol. This means formoterol can produce some bronchodilation even without beta-2 receptor stimulation.'),
('8622f225-f94c-5bce-8ae9-7524f21b3736'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Which LABA is available in a combination inhaler with budesonide as a single maintenance inhaler?', '["Salmeterol","Formoterol","Indacaterol","Olodaterol","Vilanterol"]'::jsonb, 'Formoterol', 'Formoterol is combined with budesonide in a single inhaler (e.g., Symbicort) for maintenance therapy of asthma and COPD.'),
('603271e6-c803-5a10-91c7-cf7174ce4c1a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Which LABA has a duration of action of 24 hours and is used once daily?', '["Salmeterol","Formoterol","Indacaterol","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Indacaterol', 'Indacaterol is an ultra-LABA with 24-hour duration of action allowing once-daily dosing, providing better patient compliance.'),
('b27ccb5c-1ef7-5ab1-8d41-89f2288750f3'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'The SMART (Symbicort Maintenance and Reliever Therapy) concept uses which combination?', '["Budesonide/formoterol as both maintenance and reliever","Fluticasone/salmeterol as both maintenance and reliever","Beclomethasone/salbutamol as both maintenance and reliever","Mometasone/formoterol as both maintenance and reliever","Fluticasone/formoterol as both maintenance and reliever"]'::jsonb, 'Budesonide/formoterol as both maintenance and reliever', 'The SMART concept uses budesonide/formoterol (Symbicort) as both maintenance therapy and as-needed reliever therapy, reducing the need for separate SABA use.'),
('8f1f4ba8-7122-59c2-96d6-31c97762f1dc'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Which of the following LABAs is NOT currently available in the United States?', '["Salmeterol","Formoterol","Indacaterol","Arformoterol","Carmoterol"]'::jsonb, 'Carmoterol', 'Carmoterol is not currently available in the United States. The other LABAs listed are approved for clinical use.'),
('ae70be0b-1ed5-5bf6-8ddf-79b6f265fcd1'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Ipratropium bromide is an antagonist at which receptor?', '["Beta-2 adrenergic receptor","Muscarinic M3 receptor","Histamine H1 receptor","Leukotriene receptor","Adenosine receptor"]'::jsonb, 'Muscarinic M3 receptor', 'Ipratropium is a short-acting muscarinic antagonist (SAMA) that blocks M3 receptors on bronchial smooth muscle, causing bronchodilation.'),
('8aa7219f-9f48-59a9-a07f-461b1e5ab92f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Which anticholinergic is used as a SAMA (short-acting muscarinic antagonist)?', '["Tiotropium","Glycopyrrolate","Ipratropium","Darifenacin","Solifenacin"]'::jsonb, 'Ipratropium', 'Ipratropium is the prototypical SAMA used for acute bronchospasm relief. Tiotropium and glycopyrrolate are long-acting muscarinic antagonists (LAMA).'),
('b85829c8-7d7b-529e-a566-ceec2a91c45c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Tiotropium bromide is classified as a:', '["SAMA","LAMA","SABA","LABA","Methylxanthine"]'::jsonb, 'LAMA', 'Tiotropium is a long-acting muscarinic antagonist (LAMA) with 24-hour duration of action, used once daily for COPD maintenance therapy.'),
('6a68a74b-929f-54a7-ae27-8149a1628d1f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'The onset of action of ipratropium when given by nebulizer is:', '["1-2 minutes","15-30 minutes","1-2 hours","4-6 hours","12-24 hours"]'::jsonb, '15-30 minutes', 'Ipratropium has a slower onset (15-30 minutes) compared to salbutamol but provides bronchodilation for 4-6 hours.'),
('e93b4918-2cca-5f1c-9e5e-6d3de0fe223c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Which combination is available as a dual bronchodilator inhaler for COPD?', '["Tiotropium + salmeterol","Ipratropium + formoterol","Tiotropium + olodaterol","Glycopyrrolate + salbutamol","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'All listed combinations are available as dual bronchodilator inhalers combining LAMA + LABA for COPD maintenance therapy.'),
('26d5955d-1097-50dd-b104-033728fb43fc'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Aclidinium is characterized by:', '["Once-daily dosing","Twice-daily dosing","Three times daily dosing","Four times daily dosing","Only intravenous administration"]'::jsonb, 'Twice-daily dosing', 'Aclidinium is a LAMA that requires twice-daily dosing due to its shorter duration of action compared to tiotropium.'),
('51bf5ac4-3a9d-515b-84f0-1a11de3f72fa'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'The primary mechanism of action of theophylline in asthma is:', '["Beta-2 adrenergic stimulation","Phosphodiesterase inhibition","Muscarinic receptor blockade","Leukotriene synthesis inhibition","Mast cell stabilization"]'::jsonb, 'Phosphodiesterase inhibition', 'Theophylline inhibits phosphodiesterase (PDE), increasing intracellular cAMP, leading to bronchodilation. It also has anti-inflammatory effects and acts as an adenosine receptor antagonist.'),
('51b6b409-a3f2-58d6-b320-9ab4da87cfe1'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'The therapeutic plasma concentration of theophylline is:', '["1-5 mcg/mL","5-10 mcg/mL","10-20 mcg/mL","20-30 mcg/mL","30-40 mcg/mL"]'::jsonb, '10-20 mcg/mL', 'The therapeutic range of theophylline is 10-20 mcg/mL. Levels above 20 mcg/mL increase the risk of toxicity including seizures and cardiac arrhythmias.'),
('4d76a142-873c-5480-912f-9606c4d5079d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Which of the following drugs inhibits the metabolism of theophylline?', '["Rifampin","Phenytoin","Ciprofloxacin","Phenobarbital","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Ciprofloxacin', 'Ciprofloxacin is a potent inhibitor of theophylline metabolism via CYP1A2, leading to increased theophylline levels and risk of toxicity.'),
('aa1e908d-6b0a-5ab6-9bd0-5c2703f5ec53'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Aminophylline is:', '["The sodium salt of theophylline","The ethylenediamine salt of theophylline","A synthetic derivative of caffeine","A selective PDE4 inhibitor","A methylxanthine with no bronchodilator activity"]'::jsonb, 'The ethylenediamine salt of theophylline', 'Aminophylline is the ethylenediamine salt of theophylline, which is more water-soluble and used for parenteral administration.'),
('bed2ef46-eaf4-5092-bf94-18f088c03393'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Theophylline has the narrowest therapeutic index due to its potential to cause:', '["Nephrotoxicity","Hepatotoxicity","Seizures and cardiac arrhythmias","Bone marrow suppression","Pulmonary fibrosis"]'::jsonb, 'Seizures and cardiac arrhythmias', 'Theophylline toxicity can cause life-threatening seizures and cardiac arrhythmias, which is why therapeutic drug monitoring is essential.'),
('51ccb421-78a7-58e3-984a-a43d0e35a203'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Caffeine is chemically related to theophylline and acts primarily as:', '["A bronchodilator","A central nervous system stimulant","A diuretic","An anti-inflammatory agent","A mucolytic agent"]'::jsonb, 'A central nervous system stimulant', 'Caffeine is a methylxanthine that primarily acts as a CNS stimulant. It is also used in apnea of prematurity as a respiratory stimulant.'),
('bf6125bd-69f1-5cf0-aa9e-ca5a293bc5a9'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Which of the following is an ultra-selective PDE4 inhibitor used for COPD?', '["Theophylline","Roflumilast","Aminophylline","Dyphylline","Enprofylline"]'::jsonb, 'Roflumilast', 'Roflumilast is an ultra-selective PDE4 inhibitor used as add-on therapy for severe COPD with chronic bronchitis phenotype.'),
('da1e6519-4646-5405-af7f-50b2159619fa'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Which inhaled corticosteroid has the highest oral bioavailability when swallowed?', '["Beclomethasone dipropionate","Budesonide","Fluticasone propionate","Fluticasone furoate","Mometasone furoate"]'::jsonb, 'Budesonide', 'Budesonide has relatively higher oral bioavailability (11%) compared to fluticasone propionate (1%), making it more prone to systemic effects if swallowed.'),
('8bbb3207-e746-5a5d-b5ab-e36728825c0f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Which ICS has the lowest oral bioavailability, minimizing systemic effects?', '["Beclomethasone dipropionate","Budesonide","Fluticasone propionate","Triamcinolone acetonide","Flunisolide"]'::jsonb, 'Fluticasone propionate', 'Fluticasone propionate has very low oral bioavailability (1%), meaning most of the swallowed drug is not absorbed, minimizing systemic side effects.'),
('19803915-12f3-5fa9-92f0-62cdfb74e8a2'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'The most common local side effect of inhaled corticosteroids is:', '["Pneumonia","Oral candidiasis","Adrenal suppression","Osteoporosis","Cataracts"]'::jsonb, 'Oral candidiasis', 'Oral candidiasis (thrush) is the most common local side effect of ICS, which can be prevented by using a spacer and rinsing the mouth after inhalation.'),
('936d10a1-231d-53fd-8abe-42a7ba4be0a1'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Beclomethasone dipropionate is a prodrug that is converted to its active metabolite by:', '["Hepatic CYP450 enzymes","Esterases in the lungs","Plasma esterases","Gut bacteria","Renal enzymes"]'::jsonb, 'Esterases in the lungs', 'Beclomethasone dipropionate (BDP) is a prodrug that is converted to its active metabolite, beclomethasone-17-monopropionate (17-BMP), by esterases in the lungs.'),
('65031e3e-a0cb-5126-8164-32e5abae3495'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Which ICS is available as a dry powder inhaler (DPI) in combination with formoterol?', '["Fluticasone propionate","Fluticasone furoate","Budesonide","Mometasone furoate","Beclomethasone dipropionate"]'::jsonb, 'Budesonide', 'Budesonide is available in combination with formoterol (Symbicort) as a dry powder inhaler for maintenance therapy.'),
('ad4ee9ee-77b5-5d97-9acc-dcfe03fdbf9b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Fluticasone furoate differs from fluticasone propionate in that:', '["It has lower potency","It has longer duration of action allowing once-daily dosing","It is only available as an MDI","It has higher oral bioavailability","It cannot be combined with LABAs"]'::jsonb, 'It has longer duration of action allowing once-daily dosing', 'Fluticasone furoate has a longer duration of action compared to fluticasone propionate, allowing once-daily dosing when combined with vilanterol.'),
('0d6f9437-c47e-5638-afb2-cb4c834d16ba'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Ciclesonide is unique among ICS because it:', '["Has the highest potency","Is activated in the lungs, not the airways","Has zero oral bioavailability","Is only available as a nebulized solution","Cannot cause adrenal suppression"]'::jsonb, 'Is activated in the lungs, not the airways', 'Ciclesonide is a prodrug activated by esterases specifically in the lungs (not airways), producing its active metabolite deep in the lung tissue.'),
('a8ba5c70-49f2-536b-a83a-e9f97c56078d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'The use of inhaled corticosteroids at high doses for prolonged periods may cause:', '["Diabetes mellitus resulting in decreased functional activity","Adrenal suppression and growth retardation in children","Hypothyroidism mediated by specific receptor activation","Hypogonadism without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hyperkalemia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Adrenal suppression and growth retardation in children', 'High-dose ICS can cause systemic effects including adrenal suppression and growth retardation in children, though these effects are less than with systemic corticosteroids.'),
('5dc5d2f2-fe6f-5445-93a7-10d7af6aa88e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Which ICS has the highest lung deposition when delivered via MDI with spacer?', '["Beclomethasone dipropionate","Budesonide","Fluticasone propionate","Mometasone furoate","Flunisolide"]'::jsonb, 'Beclomethasone dipropionate', 'Beclomethasone dipropionate (especially the HFA formulation) has good lung deposition when used with a spacer, though newer formulations may vary.'),
('80e8cc70-2c0b-50a7-8e85-6d8269b22db1'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'The main anti-inflammatory mechanism of ICS in asthma is:', '["Inhibition of phosphodiesterase","Blockade of leukotriene receptors","Suppression of multiple inflammatory genes and mediators","Stabilization of mast cell membranes","Beta-2 receptor stimulation"]'::jsonb, 'Suppression of multiple inflammatory genes and mediators', 'ICS suppress inflammation by inhibiting transcription factors (NF-κB, AP-1), reducing cytokine production, and decreasing inflammatory cell recruitment.'),
('3a8ba3ff-035e-544b-8a28-f05023daa5cd'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Montelukast is a:', '["5-lipoxygenase inhibitor","Leukotriene receptor antagonist","Phospholipase A2 inhibitor","Cyclooxygenase inhibitor","Thromboxane receptor antagonist"]'::jsonb, 'Leukotriene receptor antagonist', 'Montelukast is a leukotriene receptor antagonist (LTRA) that blocks the CysLT1 receptor, preventing the action of cysteinyl leukotrienes (LTC4, LTD4, LTE4).'),
('9cd422c7-4081-58d8-9b93-7709f6344e7e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Zileuton inhibits which enzyme in the leukotriene pathway?', '["Phospholipase A2","Cyclooxygenase","5-Lipoxygenase","Leukotriene A4 hydrolase","Leukotriene C4 synthase"]'::jsonb, '5-Lipoxygenase', 'Zileuton is a 5-lipoxygenase inhibitor that blocks the conversion of arachidonic acid to leukotrienes, inhibiting both leukotriene B4 and cysteinyl leukotriene synthesis.'),
('0e9324f2-d349-57ee-8c79-0630b481f2fd'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Which leukotriene modifier requires liver function monitoring?', '["Montelukast","Zafirlukast","Zileuton","Pranlukast","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Zileuton', 'Zileuton requires regular liver function monitoring due to risk of hepatotoxicity, which is not a significant concern with montelukast or zafirlukast.'),
('02a32b80-53dc-50e9-876f-7f384b2d7652'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Montelukast is FDA-approved for the treatment of:', '["Asthma prophylaxis only","Asthma prophylaxis and allergic rhinitis","COPD only","Acute asthma attacks","Exercise-induced bronchospasm only"]'::jsonb, 'Asthma prophylaxis and allergic rhinitis', 'Montelukast is approved for prophylaxis of asthma and relief of symptoms of seasonal and perennial allergic rhinitis.'),
('e76adb62-5c37-50b3-89cd-941247908f07'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'The leukotriene pathway produces mediators from which substrate?', '["Cyclooxygenase pathway resulting in decreased functional activity","Lipoxygenase pathway acting on arachidonic acid","Cytochrome P450 pathway mediated by specific receptor activation","Lysosomal enzymes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Complement cascade leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lipoxygenase pathway acting on arachidonic acid', 'Leukotrienes are produced from arachidonic acid via the 5-lipoxygenase pathway. Arachidonic acid is released from membrane phospholipids by phospholipase A2.'),
('a887b828-ed81-5cdf-8037-3ca28054da86'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Zafirlukast is contraindicated in patients taking:', '["Beta-blockers","Warfarin","ACE inhibitors","Calcium channel blockers","Diuretics"]'::jsonb, 'Warfarin', 'Zafirlukast inhibits CYP2C9 and can increase warfarin levels, leading to increased INR and risk of bleeding. Warfarin dose adjustment is needed.'),
('9a6606c4-a902-5192-a20b-4baea424f979'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Which leukotriene is primarily responsible for bronchoconstriction in asthma?', '["Leukotriene B4","Leukotriene A4","Leukotriene C4","Leukotriene E4","Leukotriene D4"]'::jsonb, 'Leukotriene C4', 'Leukotriene C4 (and its metabolites LTD4 and LTE4) are cysteinyl leukotrienes that cause potent bronchoconstriction, mucus secretion, and airway edema in asthma.'),
('373fa3e9-24c0-563a-a86d-a2d3538d9e91'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Montelukast is most effective in which type of asthma?', '["Aspirin-induced asthma","Allergic (eosinophilic) asthma","Exercise-induced bronchospasm","Occupational asthma","All of the above equally"]'::jsonb, 'Allergic (eosinophilic) asthma', 'Montelukast is most effective in allergic (eosinophilic) asthma, though it also helps with exercise-induced bronchospasm and aspirin-sensitive asthma.'),
('2688393f-ef55-5ca4-8a16-93c2ebea1132'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Cromolyn sodium prevents bronchoconstriction by:', '["Blocking histamine receptors","Stabilizing mast cell membranes to prevent mediator release","Inhibiting phosphodiesterase","Stimulating beta-2 receptors","Blocking leukotriene receptors"]'::jsonb, 'Stabilizing mast cell membranes to prevent mediator release', 'Cromolyn sodium stabilizes mast cell membranes, preventing degranulation and release of histamine, leukotrienes, and other inflammatory mediators.'),
('6e7b99a5-8176-5284-9d2d-b95b1089ee9d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Nedocromil sodium differs from cromolyn in that it:', '["Is less potent resulting in decreased functional activity","Has additional anti-inflammatory effects on inflammatory cells","Is available only as oral medication mediated by specific receptor activation","Acts as a bronchodilator without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Has a shorter duration of action leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Has additional anti-inflammatory effects on inflammatory cells', 'Nedocromil has additional anti-inflammatory effects beyond mast cell stabilization, including inhibiting eosinophil activation and sensory nerve activation.'),
('29602fb0-565f-5ca9-b220-fd573445ac6c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'The main limitation of cromolyn sodium is:', '["High cost","Necessity for frequent dosing (4 times daily)","Severe systemic side effects","Inability to prevent exercise-induced bronchospasm","Drug interactions with corticosteroids"]'::jsonb, 'Necessity for frequent dosing (4 times daily)', 'Cromolyn requires frequent dosing (typically 4 times daily), which reduces patient compliance compared to once or twice daily medications.'),
('ebc61219-6a69-5735-9743-6abf32bcc5a5'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Cromolyn sodium is available in which formulation for asthma?', '["Oral tablets only","Inhaled solution via nebulizer","Intravenous injection","Rectal suppository","Transdermal patch"]'::jsonb, 'Inhaled solution via nebulizer', 'Cromolyn is available as an inhaled solution for nebulization and as an MDI. It must be inhaled to reach the lungs for local mast cell stabilization.'),
('2c9bb46b-617c-5649-9e17-2a96a933f725'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Ketotifen is a mast cell stabilizer that also has:', '["Beta-2 agonist activity","Antihistamine (H1 blocker) activity","Anticholinergic activity","Corticosteroid activity","Phosphodiesterase inhibitor activity"]'::jsonb, 'Antihistamine (H1 blocker) activity', 'Ketotifen is a dual-action drug with both mast cell stabilizing and H1 antihistamine properties, used for allergic conditions and asthma prophylaxis.'),
('50759a5a-a1e8-546d-8a77-c970e6c804e5'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'The primary clinical use of cromolyn sodium is:', '["Acute asthma relief resulting in decreased functional activity","Prophylaxis of asthma and allergic conditions","COPD maintenance mediated by specific receptor activation","Pulmonary fibrosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pneumocystis pneumonia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Prophylaxis of asthma and allergic conditions', 'Cromolyn is used prophylactically, not for acute relief. It must be started before allergen exposure and is effective for prevention of asthma symptoms.'),
('d3265784-9c9a-5f74-8845-5e58be3aa776'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Acetylcysteine (NAC) acts as a mucolytic by:', '["Increasing water content of mucus","Breaking disulfide bonds in mucus glycoproteins","Stimulating surfactant production","Inhibiting mucus secretion","Increasing ciliary motility"]'::jsonb, 'Breaking disulfide bonds in mucus glycoproteins', 'Acetylcysteine breaks disulfide bonds in mucoprotein, reducing mucus viscosity and making it easier to expectorate.'),
('1c1e7fad-46e4-54f0-b3bc-4a7e755131d6'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Acetylcysteine is also used as an antidote in which poisoning?', '["Iron poisoning","Acetaminophen (paracetamol) poisoning","Aspirin poisoning","Opioid poisoning","Benzodiazepine poisoning"]'::jsonb, 'Acetaminophen (paracetamol) poisoning', 'NAC is the specific antidote for acetaminophen poisoning. It replenishes glutathione stores and prevents hepatotoxicity from the toxic metabolite NAPQI.'),
('571ca468-ddf0-5c2f-9791-234c4636f243'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Guaifenesin is classified as a(n):', '["Antitussive","Expectorant","Mucolytic","Decongestant","Bronchodilator"]'::jsonb, 'Expectorant', 'Guaifenesin is an expectorant that increases the volume and reduces viscosity of respiratory tract secretions, facilitating their removal.'),
('f9762fc8-c1e9-5fc6-acdd-0219edb1df87'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'The mechanism of action of guaifenesin involves:', '["Direct mucolytic action","Increasing respiratory tract fluid secretion","Suppressing the cough center","Beta-2 receptor stimulation","Mast cell stabilization"]'::jsonb, 'Increasing respiratory tract fluid secretion', 'Guaifenesin increases the volume of respiratory tract fluid secretion, which helps to thin mucus and facilitate expectoration.'),
('7309edba-c69f-524c-86c3-d1dfede7018d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Ambroxol is a metabolite of which mucolytic drug?', '["Acetylcysteine","Bromhexine","Guaifenesin","Carbocisteine","Erdosteine"]'::jsonb, 'Bromhexine', 'Ambroxol is an active metabolite of bromhexine. It has mucolytic, expectorant, and additional anti-inflammatory and antioxidant properties.'),
('d2d34674-6b8f-527f-aecf-9de48535dff9'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Bromhexine acts as a mucolytic by:', '["Breaking disulfide bonds","Increasing serous gland secretions","Depolymerizing mucopolysaccharides","Inhibiting mucus glycoprotein synthesis","Stimulating surfactant production"]'::jsonb, 'Depolymerizing mucopolysaccharides', 'Bromhexine depolymerizes mucopolysaccharide fibers in mucus, reducing its viscosity and elasticity, making expectoration easier.'),
('43d76a6c-9c11-5a30-a7e4-8905203fee8c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Carbocisteine acts as a mucolytic by:', '["Breaking disulfide bonds in mucus","Normalizing the composition of bronchial mucus","Increasing fluid secretion","Inhibiting mucus production","Stimulating ciliary motility"]'::jsonb, 'Normalizing the composition of bronchial mucus', 'Carbocisteine normalizes the composition of bronchial mucus by regulating sialomucin and fucomucin production, improving mucus rheology.'),
('8c9dd5a9-b61c-5f3b-9a43-048f2a8fa3b3'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Erdosteine is a mucolytic that:', '["Has no antioxidant properties","Has both mucolytic and antioxidant properties","Is only available as an inhaled formulation","Cannot be used in COPD","Requires frequent dosing"]'::jsonb, 'Has both mucolytic and antioxidant properties', 'Erdosteine has both mucolytic activity (breaking disulfide bonds) and antioxidant properties, making it useful in chronic respiratory diseases.'),
('efa44a58-b9a9-5b6e-9a44-d53d2b2c95ba'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Codeine is an antitussive that acts by:', '["Blocking peripheral cough receptors","Acting on the cough center in the medulla","Dilating bronchioles","Stabilizing mast cells","Breaking down mucus"]'::jsonb, 'Acting on the cough center in the medulla', 'Codeine acts centrally on the cough center in the medulla, raising the threshold for the cough reflex, making it an effective antitussive.'),
('52c38649-d002-5fed-9169-51a4343f3b12'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Dextromethorphan is preferred over codeine as an antitussive because:', '["It is more potent","It has no opioid dependence potential","It has a longer duration of action","It can be used in children of all ages","It has anti-inflammatory properties"]'::jsonb, 'It has no opioid dependence potential', 'Dextromethorphan is a non-narcotic antitussive with no significant opioid dependence potential, making it safer than codeine for OTC use.'),
('755c8bc5-3efb-5f84-9bfa-11416ab2ab95'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Noscapine is an antitussive that:', '["Is an opioid derivative","Has no significant analgesic or addictive properties","Acts primarily as a bronchodilator","Is only available by prescription","Has anticholinergic effects"]'::jsonb, 'Has no significant analgesic or addictive properties', 'Noscapine is a phthalideisoquinoline alkaloid from opium with antitussive action but no significant analgesic, respiratory depressant, or addictive properties.'),
('78905935-55cf-5388-a3a1-b351cd4f5a16'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Benzonatate is an antitussive that acts by:', '["Suppressing the cough center in the medulla","Anesthetizing stretch receptors in the lungs","Blocking histamine receptors","Inhibiting phosphodiesterase","Stabilizing mast cells"]'::jsonb, 'Anesthetizing stretch receptors in the lungs', 'Benzonatate is a non-narcotic antitussive that anesthetizes stretch receptors in the respiratory tract, reducing the cough reflex.'),
('74055cdb-7af0-5efb-8148-00591e7ca518'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Which antitussive should be avoided in children under 2 years of age?', '["Dextromethorphan","Benzonatate","Codeine","All opioid-derived antitussives","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'All opioid-derived antitussives (including codeine) and dextromethorphan should be avoided in children under 2 years due to risk of respiratory depression and death.'),
('e9a0ea56-a3ee-5cc0-bb10-c27421bd861c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Fosphenytoin has been shown to have antitussive properties and acts by:', '["Blocking sodium channels","Suppressing the cough center","Anesthetizing airway receptors","Blocking calcium channels","Enhancing GABA activity"]'::jsonb, 'Blocking sodium channels', 'Fosphenytoin (as phenytoin) blocks voltage-gated sodium channels in airway sensory nerves, reducing cough reflex excitability.'),
('76954356-6bf9-52d5-b2c7-8e4e9acb0dce'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Nebulized lidocaine can be used as a topical antitussive for:', '["Chronic cough resulting in decreased functional activity","Post-extubation cough and procedural cough suppression","Asthma attacks mediated by specific receptor activation","COPD exacerbations without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bronchiectasis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Post-extubation cough and procedural cough suppression', 'Nebulized lidocaine provides topical anesthesia to airway receptors and is used for post-extubation cough and to suppress cough during bronchoscopy.'),
('b41b848d-8fbf-5343-b9ce-df5757c79a22'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'According to GINA guidelines, Step 1 asthma therapy for adults includes:', '["As-needed ICS-formoterol","Daily low-dose ICS plus SABA","Daily medium-dose ICS","Daily LABA plus ICS","As-needed SABA only"]'::jsonb, 'As-needed ICS-formoterol', 'GINA 2023 recommends as-needed low-dose ICS-formoterol as the preferred reliever for Step 1-2 asthma, replacing SABA-only therapy.'),
('ed809a4b-1d4d-57fb-b750-b87138d4d7b4'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'At Step 2 of asthma therapy, the preferred controller is:', '["Leukotriene receptor antagonist","Low-dose inhaled corticosteroid","Long-acting beta-2 agonist","Theophylline","Cromolyn sodium"]'::jsonb, 'Low-dose inhaled corticosteroid', 'Low-dose ICS is the preferred controller at Step 2, either as daily low-dose ICS or as-needed low-dose ICS-formoterol.'),
('ba3da599-4b33-5b97-9a6e-51aecb191643'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Step 3 asthma therapy typically involves:', '["Low-dose ICS plus LABA","Medium-dose ICS plus LABA","High-dose ICS plus LABA","ICS plus LTRA","ICS plus theophylline"]'::jsonb, 'Low-dose ICS plus LABA', 'Step 3 therapy is low-dose ICS plus LABA, which provides better asthma control than increasing ICS dose alone.'),
('8260c59d-39a8-5c18-b2f4-1d19cd053383'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'When Step 3 therapy is not controlling asthma, Step 4 options include:', '["Medium to high-dose ICS-LABA plus LAMA","Low-dose ICS plus LTRA","Theophylline plus cromolyn","SABA plus anticholinergic","Systemic corticosteroids"]'::jsonb, 'Medium to high-dose ICS-LABA plus LAMA', 'Step 4 includes medium to high-dose ICS-LABA plus LAMA (tiotropium) as add-on therapy for uncontrolled asthma.'),
('a4b107dd-4944-5615-ade3-c57a1e0bfaa3'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Step 5 therapy for severe asthma includes which biologic therapy option?', '["Anti-IgE therapy (omalizumab)","Anti-IL5 therapy (mepolizumab)","Anti-IL4R therapy (dupilumab)","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Step 5 includes biologic therapies such as anti-IgE (omalizumab), anti-IL5 (mepolizumab, reslizumab), and anti-IL4R (dupilumab) based on phenotype.'),
('d425a6cd-866c-50ca-9948-001c4e7418fb'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'The SMART concept (Smart Maintenance and Reliever Therapy) recommends:', '["Using SABA as both maintenance and reliever","Using ICS-formoterol as both maintenance and reliever","Using LABA alone as reliever","Using anticholinergics as reliever","Using theophylline as reliever"]'::jsonb, 'Using ICS-formoterol as both maintenance and reliever', 'SMART uses low-dose budesonide-formoterol as both maintenance (twice daily) and reliever (as needed), which has been shown to reduce asthma exacerbations.'),
('141a06fd-7b17-57c2-a8d6-fb98ef1c5d4d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'The preferred reliever therapy according to current GINA guidelines is:', '["SABA alone","ICS-formoterol","Ipratropium","Theophylline","Leukotriene modifier"]'::jsonb, 'ICS-formoterol', 'GINA now recommends as-needed ICS-formoterol as the preferred reliever over SABA alone to reduce exacerbation risk.'),
('2bc8b3ba-70db-564e-8604-4f145a127ec1'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Omalizumab (anti-IgE) is indicated for which asthma phenotype?', '["Non-allergic asthma","Allergic asthma with elevated IgE","Exercise-induced asthma only","Aspirin-induced asthma","Occupational asthma only"]'::jsonb, 'Allergic asthma with elevated IgE', 'Omalizumab is indicated for moderate-to-severe allergic asthma with elevated serum IgE levels that are inadequately controlled on ICS.'),
('24010af1-6b52-5cb7-bacf-8f3f12cdb69d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Mepolizumab (anti-IL5) is most effective in which asthma phenotype?', '["Neutrophilic asthma","Eosinophilic asthma","Paucigranulocytic asthma","Allergic asthma","Aspirin-sensitive asthma"]'::jsonb, 'Eosinophilic asthma', 'Mepolizumab targets IL-5, which is crucial for eosinophil survival and activation, making it most effective in eosinophilic asthma.'),
('c232dd9f-1305-5b23-942e-c1d8f770bec2'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Dupilumab targets which cytokine receptor?', '["IL-5 receptor","IgE receptor","IL-4 receptor alpha subunit","IL-13 receptor","IL-17 receptor"]'::jsonb, 'IL-4 receptor alpha subunit', 'Dupilumab blocks the IL-4 receptor alpha subunit, inhibiting both IL-4 and IL-13 signaling, which are key type 2 inflammatory cytokines in asthma.'),
('31eee184-b74a-5c69-9c80-f7a8e9a3d944'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Tezepelumab is a biologic therapy that targets:', '["IL-5 resulting in decreased functional activity","IgE accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","TSLP (thymic stromal lymphopoietin)","IL-4 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","IL-13 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'TSLP (thymic stromal lymphopoietin)', 'Tezepelumab is an anti-TSLP antibody that blocks TSLP, an epithelial cytokine involved in type 2 and non-type 2 inflammation.'),
('28abb1bb-7f2d-5e3c-a436-dbcf722fc1df'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Which step in asthma therapy recommends adding a LAMA as add-on?', '["Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 1"]'::jsonb, 'Step 4', 'LAMA (tiotropium) is added at Step 4 as an add-on to ICS-LABA for patients with inadequately controlled asthma.'),
('cb4d511a-a53c-5726-a873-f46464a5f9d2'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Benralizumab (anti-IL5R) differs from mepolizumab in that it:', '["Is less effective","Induces direct apoptosis of eosinophils via ADCC","Targets IgE instead of IL-5","Has a shorter duration of action","Cannot be used in severe asthma"]'::jsonb, 'Induces direct apoptosis of eosinophils via ADCC', 'Benralizumab binds to IL-5Rα and recruits NK cells to induce direct eosinophil apoptosis via antibody-dependent cell-mediated cytotoxicity (ADCC).'),
('61d63c9e-cf2b-52b9-af82-4f79edfca590'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'For Step 5 asthma with persistent eosinophilia despite biologic therapy, which option may be considered?', '["Oral corticosteroids","Methotrexate","Cyclosporine","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Oral corticosteroids, methotrexate, and cyclosporine may be considered for severe asthma at Step 5 when biologics are not available or effective.'),
('229eb9f9-74c2-5ebc-a8c5-6e8e55fa77fd'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Theophylline is considered at which step of asthma therapy?', '["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5"]'::jsonb, 'Step 4', 'Theophylline may be considered at Step 4 as add-on therapy, though it is used less frequently now due to its narrow therapeutic index.'),
('dffb9c2b-9190-5eab-b74b-f385130179f2'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'The cornerstone of pharmacotherapy for COPD is:', '["Inhaled corticosteroids","Bronchodilators (LABA and/or LAMA)","Phosphodiesterase-4 inhibitors","Mucolytics","Systemic corticosteroids"]'::jsonb, 'Bronchodilators (LABA and/or LAMA)', 'Bronchodilators (LABA and/or LAMA) are the cornerstone of COPD pharmacotherapy, providing symptom relief and reducing exacerbations.'),
('0bf8744d-d8a4-515f-adb2-81083a524a5a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'According to GOLD guidelines, initial pharmacotherapy for Group A COPD includes:', '["SABA as needed","LABA or LAMA","ICS-LABA combination","LABA-LAMA combination","Triple therapy"]'::jsonb, 'LABA or LAMA', 'Group A COPD (mild symptoms, low exacerbation risk) is treated with a bronchodilator (LABA or LAMA) as needed.'),
('7d5ffe3c-ad08-5724-8847-5618a51996dd'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Triple therapy in COPD combines which three drug classes?', '["LABA + LAMA + ICS","LABA + LAMA + PDE4 inhibitor","LABA + ICS + mucolytic","LAMA + ICS + LTRA","LABA + ICS + anticholinergic"]'::jsonb, 'LABA + LAMA + ICS', 'Triple therapy combines LABA + LAMA + ICS for patients with frequent exacerbations despite dual bronchodilator therapy and blood eosinophilia.'),
('3fe87fd6-54e9-5b12-a813-0aa7800935fd'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Roflumilast is indicated for which COPD phenotype?', '["Emphysema-predominant","Chronic bronchitis with frequent exacerbations","Young patients with early COPD","COPD with asthma overlap","Mild COPD"]'::jsonb, 'Chronic bronchitis with frequent exacerbations', 'Roflumilast (PDE4 inhibitor) is indicated for severe COPD with chronic bronchitis phenotype and frequent exacerbations despite inhaled therapy.'),
('61aa79aa-f014-5de3-8e95-2159217daf11'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'The main mechanism of roflumilast in COPD is:', '["Bronchodilation resulting in decreased functional activity","Anti-inflammatory effects via PDE4 inhibition","Mucolytic action mediated by specific receptor activation","Antitussive action without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Surfactant production leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anti-inflammatory effects via PDE4 inhibition', 'Roflumilast inhibits PDE4, reducing neutrophilic inflammation and mucus hypersecretion in the airways of COPD patients.'),
('7c9d2140-4aa7-5c25-8c03-e811209a208b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Which inhaled therapy is recommended first-line for COPD patients with frequent exacerbations and eosinophilia?', '["LABA alone","LAMA alone","ICS-containing regimen","PDE4 inhibitor","Mucolytics"]'::jsonb, 'ICS-containing regimen', 'ICS-containing regimens are preferred for COPD patients with frequent exacerbations and elevated blood eosinophils (>300 cells/μL).'),
('626c95c7-0720-5b4a-a05b-cf782c3b9855'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Azithromycin prophylaxis in COPD is recommended for:', '["All COPD patients resulting in decreased functional activity","Former smokers with frequent exacerbations despite optimal inhaler therapy","COPD patients with pneumonia mediated by specific receptor activation","Mild COPD without affecting the primary tissue pathway","COPD patients on supplemental oxygen leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Former smokers with frequent exacerbations despite optimal inhaler therapy', 'Long-term low-dose azithromycin prophylaxis is recommended for former smokers with frequent exacerbations despite optimal inhaler therapy.'),
('10f8c8a0-1c78-534c-83a6-34996e428fc9'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'N-acetylcysteine (NAC) in COPD is used as a:', '["Bronchodilator resulting in decreased functional activity","Mucolytic with antioxidant properties","Corticosteroid mediated by specific receptor activation","Antitussive without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Leukotriene modifier leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mucolytic with antioxidant properties', 'High-dose NAC (1200 mg/day) is used as a mucolytic with antioxidant properties that may reduce exacerbations in COPD.'),
('c31b8865-794b-54ea-9763-fd74faf2ecd7'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Carbocisteine in COPD is used to:', '["Relieve bronchospasm resulting in decreased functional activity","Reduce mucus viscosity and exacerbation frequency","Suppress cough mediated by specific receptor activation","Prevent pneumonia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Reduce inflammation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Reduce mucus viscosity and exacerbation frequency', 'Carbocisteine normalizes mucus composition and reduces exacerbation frequency in chronic bronchitis and COPD.'),
('ae54ebde-8f6a-50db-9a75-0c61a472d3b3'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Which COPD therapy has been shown to reduce mortality in alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency?', '["Inhaled corticosteroids resulting in decreased functional activity","Alpha-1 antitrypsin augmentation therapy","Bronchodilators mediated by specific receptor activation","PDE4 inhibitors without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mucolytics leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Alpha-1 antitrypsin augmentation therapy', 'Alpha-1 antitrypsin augmentation therapy (IV pooled human AAT) is the only specific treatment for AAT deficiency and may slow lung function decline.'),
('a59bdb98-2626-5708-a0fd-d8d08ccdb58e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Inhaler technique education is critical in COPD because:', '["It determines drug delivery to the lungs","Poor technique increases systemic side effects","It affects clinical outcomes","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Proper inhaler technique is essential for effective drug delivery, minimizing systemic side effects, and achieving optimal clinical outcomes in COPD.'),
('f1c617d7-54ec-5fa9-8944-3b02a63db0a7'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Which delivery device typically provides the highest lung deposition in COPD?', '["Metered-dose inhaler (MDI) without spacer","Metered-dose inhaler (MDI) with spacer","Dry powder inhaler (DPI)","Soft-mist inhaler","Nebulizer"]'::jsonb, 'Nebulizer', 'Nebulizers typically provide the highest lung deposition in COPD patients, especially those with poor inhaler technique or severe disease.'),
('46c022af-4707-5938-9a43-dc4cf8ba2778'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'The GOLD ABCD assessment tool classifies COPD based on:', '["Spirometry only","Symptom burden and exacerbation history","Age and smoking status","Comorbidities only","Oxygen saturation"]'::jsonb, 'Symptom burden and exacerbation history', 'The GOLD ABCD tool classifies COPD based on symptom burden (mMRC or CAT score) and exacerbation history (frequency and severity).'),
('2b3004ef-5f58-56d8-bef4-ae21e15269bd'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Which COPD medication has anticholinergic side effects that may worsen urinary retention?', '["Salmeterol","Tiotropium","Budesonide","Roflumilast","N-acetylcysteine"]'::jsonb, 'Tiotropium', 'Tiotropium and other LAMAs have anticholinergic effects that can worsen urinary retention, particularly in men with BPH.'),
('8a2c46db-ebe2-5184-8d58-f5d76f511cc3'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Pulmonary surfactant is primarily produced by:', '["Clara cells","Type II alveolar pneumocytes","Type I alveolar pneumocytes","Macrophages","Endothelial cells"]'::jsonb, 'Type II alveolar pneumocytes', 'Type II alveolar pneumocytes are responsible for synthesis, storage, and secretion of pulmonary surfactant.'),
('37faeaa7-67ae-5888-84c0-8d2002b6b1c8'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'The main function of pulmonary surfactant is to:', '["Facilitate gas exchange resulting in decreased functional activity","Reduce surface tension at the air-liquid interface","Fight infection mediated by specific receptor activation","Produce mucus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Transport oxygen leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Reduce surface tension at the air-liquid interface', 'Surfactant reduces surface tension at the air-liquid interface in alveoli, preventing atelectasis and reducing the work of breathing.'),
('a3197329-b38b-5412-89a8-db3bdd9fa14a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Exogenous surfactant is indicated in:', '["Adult respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS) resulting in decreased functional activity","Neonatal respiratory distress syndrome (NRDS)","Asthma mediated by specific receptor activation","COPD without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bronchiectasis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Neonatal respiratory distress syndrome (NRDS)', 'Exogenous surfactant replacement therapy is primarily indicated for NRDS in premature infants to prevent and treat respiratory distress.'),
('065a903b-afeb-5434-88f6-60c29cfde7eb'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Which surfactant preparation is derived from porcine lung extracts?', '["Beractant (Survanta)","Poractant alfa (Curosurf)","Calfactant (Infasurf)","Bovactant","Alveofact"]'::jsonb, 'Poractant alfa (Curosurf)', 'Poractant alfa (Curosurf) is a natural surfactant derived from porcine lung extracts, containing phospholipids and surfactant proteins B and C.'),
('bc5e6a75-e8a0-531e-91b5-81fdd439623d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Beractant (Survanta) is derived from which source?', '["Porcine lungs","Bovine lungs","Synthetic production","Human amniotic fluid","Recombinant technology"]'::jsonb, 'Bovine lungs', 'Beractant (Survanta) is a bovine-derived surfactant containing phospholipids, neutral lipids, and surfactant proteins B and C.'),
('68ce477f-445a-56ae-ba45-b463c6724399'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Lucinactant (Surfaxin) is unique among surfactants because it:', '["Is animal-derived resulting in decreased functional activity","Contains a synthetic peptide mimicking surfactant protein B","Is only used in adults mediated by specific receptor activation","Has no phospholipids without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Is administered orally leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Contains a synthetic peptide mimicking surfactant protein B', 'Lucinactant is a synthetic surfactant containing KL4 peptide, which mimics the function of surfactant protein B, providing surface tension reduction.'),
('f58513cb-8d3d-5bff-b60a-6f8b4eab6a24'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'The dose of surfactant for NRDS is typically:', '["0.5 mL/kg","1-2 mL/kg","5 mL/kg","10 mL/kg","20 mL/kg"]'::jsonb, '1-2 mL/kg', 'The standard dose of surfactant for NRDS is 1-2 mL/kg administered intratracheally, which can be repeated if needed.'),
('9b3a2fc1-2149-5819-bb52-d41829115867'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'A major complication of rapid surfactant administration is:', '["Hypothermia resulting in decreased functional activity","Reflex bradycardia and transient hypoxemia","Hyperglycemia mediated by specific receptor activation","Hypertension without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Seizures leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Reflex bradycardia and transient hypoxemia', 'Rapid surfactant administration can cause transient hypoxemia, bradycardia, and airway obstruction. Slow administration with proper positioning is recommended.'),
('65cdfdf3-70fd-5715-8352-cdf34d564476'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'The major phospholipid component of pulmonary surfactant is:', '["Phosphatidylethanolamine resulting in decreased functional activity","Phosphatidylcholine (dipalmitoylphosphatidylcholine - DPPC)","Phosphatidylserine mediated by specific receptor activation","Sphingomyelin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Phosphatidylinositol leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Phosphatidylcholine (dipalmitoylphosphatidylcholine - DPPC)', 'Dipalmitoylphosphatidylcholine (DPPC) is the major phospholipid component of surfactant, comprising approximately 80% of surfactant phospholipids.'),
('a3244f5b-7054-5667-967b-9029298e80ad'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Surfactant proteins A and D are important for:', '["Surface tension reduction","Innate immune defense in the lungs","Mucus production","Ciliary function","Alveolar macrophage migration"]'::jsonb, 'Innate immune defense in the lungs', 'SP-A and SP-D are collectins involved in innate immune defense, opsonization of pathogens, and regulation of inflammatory responses in the lungs.'),
('993efef4-001c-5e62-afb3-75ad573cf6dd'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'In ARDS, surfactant dysfunction occurs due to:', '["Increased surfactant production","Inactivation of surfactant by protein-rich edema fluid","Decreased alveolar surface tension","Increased type II cell activity","Enhanced surfactant clearance"]'::jsonb, 'Inactivation of surfactant by protein-rich edema fluid', 'In ARDS, the protein-rich edema fluid inactivates surfactant, and inflammatory mediators damage type II cells, leading to surfactant dysfunction.'),
('d566964e-3287-5143-925a-b776e55c1eda'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Calfactant is a surfactant preparation that:', '["Is fully synthetic","Is derived from bovine lung lavage","Contains only phospholipids","Is used only in adults","Has no surface tension reducing properties"]'::jsonb, 'Is derived from bovine lung lavage', 'Calfactant (Infasurf) is derived from bovine lung lavage and contains phospholipids and natural surfactant proteins.'),
('f2ab5ebf-d0e8-5301-83b7-deb341c11bc3'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Epoprostenol (Flolan) is used for pulmonary arterial hypertension and acts as:', '["Endothelin receptor antagonist","Prostacyclin analog","Phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitor","Soluble guanylate cyclase stimulator","Calcium channel blocker"]'::jsonb, 'Prostacyclin analog', 'Epoprostenol is a synthetic prostacyclin (PGI2) analog that causes vasodilation and inhibits platelet aggregation in pulmonary arteries.'),
('47a1698a-9197-50f1-97df-26455d365989'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Sildenafil in pulmonary hypertension acts by:', '["Blocking endothelin receptors","Inhibiting phosphodiesterase-5 (PDE5)","Stimulating prostacyclin production","Blocking calcium channels","Inhibiting angiotensin converting enzyme"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibiting phosphodiesterase-5 (PDE5)', 'Sildenafil inhibits PDE5, preventing cGMP degradation in pulmonary vascular smooth muscle, leading to vasodilation.'),
('087274ba-3169-5130-97ed-244f90769180'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Bosentan is classified as a:', '["PDE5 inhibitor","Endothelin receptor antagonist (dual ETA/ETB)","Prostacyclin analog","Soluble guanylate cyclase stimulator","Nitric oxide donor"]'::jsonb, 'Endothelin receptor antagonist (dual ETA/ETB)', 'Bosentan is a dual endothelin receptor antagonist that blocks both ETA and ETB receptors, reducing pulmonary vasoconstriction and remodeling.'),
('80501eda-52c2-508b-ab8b-601dcd5192b9'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Ambrisentan differs from bosentan in that ambrisentan is:', '["A dual endothelin receptor antagonist","A selective ETA receptor antagonist","A PDE5 inhibitor","A prostacyclin analog","An endothelin receptor agonist"]'::jsonb, 'A selective ETA receptor antagonist', 'Ambrisentan is a selective ETA receptor antagonist, which may provide more targeted blockade of endothelin-1 mediated vasoconstriction.'),
('30bb8b81-1bc8-598f-902f-7735bb952007'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Macitentan is an endothelin receptor antagonist that differs from bosentan by:', '["Having less selectivity for ETA","Being more tissue-penetrant with dual ETA/ETB blockade","Being a PDE5 inhibitor","Being a prostacyclin analog","Having a shorter half-life"]'::jsonb, 'Being more tissue-penetrant with dual ETA/ETB blockade', 'Macitentan is a tissue-penetrant dual endothelin receptor antagonist with improved pharmacokinetic properties compared to bosentan.'),
('a4ae1417-f15f-560c-8889-88eac0cc2406'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Treprostinil is a prostacyclin analog that can be administered by which routes?', '["Intravenous only resulting in decreased functional activity","Subcutaneous, intravenous, inhaled, and oral","Oral only mediated by specific receptor activation","Intramuscular only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Topical only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Subcutaneous, intravenous, inhaled, and oral', 'Treprostinil is unique among prostacyclins in that it can be administered subcutaneously, intravenously, inhaled, and orally.'),
('3c8ba73c-94e4-55a0-95ad-8cdaa3008438'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Iloprost is a prostacyclin analog that is administered by:', '["Oral route","Intravenous infusion","Nebulized inhalation","Subcutaneous injection","Intramuscular injection"]'::jsonb, 'Nebulized inhalation', 'Iloprost is administered via nebulized inhalation, allowing direct delivery to the pulmonary vasculature with fewer systemic effects.'),
('d42da967-d4d1-5863-8866-e5f3b0d8d9fe'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Riociguat is classified as a:', '["PDE5 inhibitor","Soluble guanylate cyclase (sGC) stimulator","Endothelin receptor antagonist","Prostacyclin analog","Nitric oxide donor"]'::jsonb, 'Soluble guanylate cyclase (sGC) stimulator', 'Riociguat directly stimulates soluble guanylate cyclase (sGC) independently of nitric oxide and also sensitizes sGC to endogenous NO.'),
('5e5d13b3-0083-5f98-a874-ed6f2853a0ce'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Riociguat is indicated for which conditions?', '["Pulmonary arterial hypertension only","Chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension (CTEPH) and PAH","Left heart failure only","COPD-associated pulmonary hypertension only","Interstitial lung disease only"]'::jsonb, 'Chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension (CTEPH) and PAH', 'Riociguat is indicated for both inoperable CTEPH and PAH, making it unique among pulmonary hypertension therapies.'),
('372ca98e-a89a-5353-8335-1daf46eedeae'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Selexipag acts as an:', '["Endothelin receptor antagonist resulting in decreased functional activity","Oral prostacyclin receptor (IP receptor) agonist","PDE5 inhibitor mediated by specific receptor activation","sGC stimulator without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Calcium channel blocker leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Oral prostacyclin receptor (IP receptor) agonist', 'Selexipag is an oral selective IP receptor agonist that mimics the effects of prostacyclin, providing vasodilation and anti-proliferative effects.'),
('32dad707-8c29-5035-b372-6ab5f078c1f4'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Tadalafil in pulmonary hypertension is distinguished from sildenafil by:', '["Being a different drug class","Having a longer half-life allowing once-daily dosing","Being administered only intravenously","Having less PDE5 selectivity","Being less effective"]'::jsonb, 'Having a longer half-life allowing once-daily dosing', 'Tadalafil has a longer half-life (17.5 hours) compared to sildenafil (4 hours), allowing once-daily dosing for pulmonary hypertension.'),
('12898caf-5565-59b8-9ac7-6333c010522d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Calcium channel blockers are effective in PAH only in patients who:', '["Have severe disease resulting in decreased functional activity","Show acute vasoreactivity during right heart catheterization","Have CTEPH mediated by specific receptor activation","Have left heart disease without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Are on combination therapy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Show acute vasoreactivity during right heart catheterization', 'Calcium channel blockers (nifedipine, diltiazem) are only effective in the 5% of PAH patients who demonstrate acute vasoreactivity during catheterization.'),
('b8f98322-1158-52d4-973b-d728fcb72e23'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Inhaled nitric oxide (iNO) in pulmonary hypertension acts by:', '["Blocking endothelin receptors","Stimulating soluble guanylate cyclase to increase cGMP","Inhibiting PDE5","Activating prostacyclin receptors","Blocking calcium channels"]'::jsonb, 'Stimulating soluble guanylate cyclase to increase cGMP', 'Inhaled nitric oxide activates soluble guanylate cyclase, increasing cGMP production in pulmonary vascular smooth muscle, causing selective pulmonary vasodilation.'),
('c1bf44ce-00f9-55d6-8aa3-a578fde395dd'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Epoprostenol has a very short half-life of approximately:', '["1-2 minutes","5-10 minutes","30-60 minutes","2-4 hours","12-24 hours"]'::jsonb, '1-2 minutes', 'Epoprostenol has an extremely short half-life of 1-5 minutes, requiring continuous IV infusion via a central venous catheter.'),
('66b23675-a6b5-5660-adc6-86d5c79aafa6'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'The main advantage of inhaled prostacyclin analogs in PAH is:', '["Longer duration of action resulting in decreased functional activity","Selective pulmonary vasodilation with minimal systemic effects","Oral bioavailability mediated by specific receptor activation","Lower cost without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fewer drug interactions leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Selective pulmonary vasodilation with minimal systemic effects', 'Inhaled prostacyclin analogs (iloprost, treprostinil) provide selective pulmonary vasodilation, minimizing systemic hypotension.'),
('76905347-72c5-50c7-9bf0-afadb67c553d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'A serious side effect of endothelin receptor antagonists (bosentan, macitentan) is:', '["Pulmonary edema resulting in decreased functional activity","Hepatotoxicity requiring LFT monitoring","Nephrotoxicity mediated by specific receptor activation","Bone marrow suppression without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pancreatitis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hepatotoxicity requiring LFT monitoring', 'ERAs, particularly bosentan, can cause dose-dependent hepatotoxicity, requiring regular LFT monitoring. Macitentan has less hepatotoxicity.'),
('6bd88d7c-e2fa-595c-b312-7d32bc12b380'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'PDE5 inhibitors are contraindicated with which medication class?', '["Calcium channel blockers","Nitrates","Diuretics","Anticoagulants","Antibiotics"]'::jsonb, 'Nitrates', 'PDE5 inhibitors combined with nitrates cause severe hypotension due to synergistic effects on cGMP-mediated vasodilation.'),
('75b1c63e-54e8-5c14-8d1e-797ccc0a0f90'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Ambrisentan has a lower risk of hepatotoxicity compared to bosentan because:', '["It is not metabolized by the liver","It has less inhibition of bile salt export pump (BSEP)","It has a shorter half-life","It is given at lower doses","It is not an ERA"]'::jsonb, 'It has less inhibition of bile salt export pump (BSEP)', 'Ambrisentan causes less inhibition of the bile salt export pump (BSEP) compared to bosentan, resulting in lower hepatotoxicity risk.'),
('d5286274-a32b-502e-9e08-2339a5ca981a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Combination therapy in PAH may include:', '["ERA + PDE5 inhibitor","PDE5 inhibitor + prostacyclin analog","ERA + prostacyclin analog","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Combination therapy targeting different pathways (ERA + PDE5i, PDE5i + prostacyclin, triple therapy) is increasingly used in PAH management.'),
('1b2f747d-9f9e-52eb-aa86-8c8ec3842e21'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'The PATHWAY-2 trial demonstrated the efficacy of which combination in PAH?', '["Bosentan + sildenafil","Ambrisentan + tadalafil","Macitentan + riociguat","Selexipag + bosentan","Epoprostenol + sildenafil"]'::jsonb, 'Ambrisentan + tadalafil', 'The PATHWAY-2 trial demonstrated that combination therapy with ambrisentan (ERA) + tadalafil (PDE5i) improved exercise capacity in PAH.'),
('8e197e50-dff1-5820-b5e8-5e164b6ba235'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Riociguat should NOT be used concomitantly with:', '["Diuretics","PDE5 inhibitors","Anticoagulants","Calcium channel blockers","Antibiotics"]'::jsonb, 'PDE5 inhibitors', 'Riociguat should not be used with PDE5 inhibitors due to additive hypotensive effects and lack of proven additional benefit.'),
('a4350165-5a29-596d-84a0-c9eedd46a457'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Beraprost is an oral prostacyclin analog primarily used in PAH in which country?', '["United States","Japan","Germany","United Kingdom","India"]'::jsonb, 'Japan', 'Beraprost is an oral prostacyclin analog primarily used in Japan for treatment of PAH, though it is not widely available elsewhere.'),
('00d571aa-b4d1-5ed1-8b3b-ec9485416c8e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'The GINA 2023 guidelines recommend against using which therapy as monotherapy in asthma?', '["Low-dose ICS","As-needed SABA","LTRA","Cromolyn","Theophylline"]'::jsonb, 'As-needed SABA', 'GINA 2023 recommends against SABA-only treatment due to increased risk of severe exacerbations and asthma-related death.'),
('72cfa7df-f09e-55a2-b21f-9ef67f28f256'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Asthma-COPD overlap (ACO) is best treated with:', '["ICS-LABA combination","LAMA alone","SABA as needed","PDE4 inhibitor","Mucolytics"]'::jsonb, 'ICS-LABA combination', 'ICS-LABA combination is recommended for ACO as it addresses both the asthmatic and COPD components of the overlap.'),
('76839f29-be91-501d-8860-ba856aa62d06'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Short-acting beta-2 agonists should not be used more than how many days per week for optimal asthma control?', '["1 day","2 days","3 days","5 days","7 days"]'::jsonb, '2 days', 'Using SABA more than 2 days per week indicates poor asthma control and the need for step-up therapy with ICS.'),
('e69a5af0-6be6-554a-b654-eb11f5ad50fe'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Which drug class is most effective for preventing exercise-induced bronchoconstriction when taken before exercise?', '["Antihistamines","SABA (salbutamol)","Anticholinergics","Mucolytics","Expectorants"]'::jsonb, 'SABA (salbutamol)', 'SABA taken 15-30 minutes before exercise is the most effective prevention for exercise-induced bronchoconstriction.'),
('9df2a523-99c0-5fd7-84c9-ff25025aa442'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease (AERD) is best managed with:', '["Avoiding all NSAIDs","LTRA (montelukast) as add-on therapy","High-dose ICS","Both A and B","Desensitization therapy only"]'::jsonb, 'Both A and B', 'AERD management includes avoiding aspirin/NSAIDs and adding LTRA (montelukast) therapy. Aspirin desensitization may also be considered.'),
('a1ec3c38-3ef0-5071-b360-607d28f7956b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'A spacer device with an MDI is particularly important in which patient population?', '["Adults with good technique","Young children and elderly patients","Athletes","Healthcare professionals","All patients equally"]'::jsonb, 'Young children and elderly patients', 'Spacers improve drug delivery and reduce oral candidiasis risk, and are especially important for young children and elderly patients with poor coordination.'),
('9163b3f0-5a02-57f9-9acf-0f50a865c432'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Chronic use of SABA without ICS in asthma is associated with:', '["Improved outcomes resulting in decreased functional activity","Increased risk of severe exacerbations and mortality","Decreased airway inflammation mediated by specific receptor activation","Bronchodilation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","No adverse effects leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Increased risk of severe exacerbations and mortality', 'Regular SABA use without ICS increases the risk of severe exacerbations, asthma-related hospitalization, and mortality due to underlying untreated inflammation.'),
('5fd51ae9-1f7d-5e05-a623-a6c1392af6cb'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Montelukast was given a boxed warning by the FDA in 2020 for:', '["Hepatotoxicity","Neuropsychiatric adverse events","Cardiac arrhythmias","Renal failure","Pancreatitis"]'::jsonb, 'Neuropsychiatric adverse events', 'The FDA issued a boxed warning for montelukast due to reports of serious neuropsychiatric events including depression, suicidal ideation, and hallucinations.'),
('629b657b-609a-52e5-9622-d17829c8834d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Theophylline enhances diaphragmatic contractility, which is beneficial in:', '["Asthma resulting in decreased functional activity","COPD accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Neuromuscular diseases causing respiratory failure","Pulmonary fibrosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bronchiectasis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Neuromuscular diseases causing respiratory failure', 'Theophylline enhances diaphragmatic contractility, which can be beneficial in neuromuscular diseases (e.g., myasthenia gravis, muscular dystrophy) causing respiratory failure.'),
('5ebc1f6b-bfa0-55f1-bee4-517a81a0263c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Which medication is contraindicated in patients with severe asthma because it may precipitate fatal asthma attacks?', '["Montelukast resulting in decreased functional activity","Aspirin (in aspirin-sensitive asthma)","Ipratropium mediated by specific receptor activation","Budesonide without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Formoterol leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Aspirin (in aspirin-sensitive asthma)', 'Aspirin and other NSAIDs can precipitate severe, potentially fatal bronchospasm in patients with aspirin-sensitive asthma (AERD).'),
('4e09778f-a584-5344-a687-8bcfc0fe0645'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Nebulized magnesium sulfate is used as an add-on therapy in which condition?', '["Mild asthma","Severe acute asthma exacerbation","COPD maintenance","Pulmonary fibrosis","Bronchiectasis"]'::jsonb, 'Severe acute asthma exacerbation', 'Nebulized magnesium sulfate is used as add-on therapy in severe acute asthma exacerbations unresponsive to initial bronchodilator therapy.'),
('34d9919e-11d5-5eef-a47c-cfefcc968552'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Heliox (helium-oxygen mixture) is used in severe asthma because it:', '["Directly dilates bronchioles resulting in decreased functional activity","Reduces airway resistance and work of breathing due to lower density","Has anti-inflammatory properties mediated by specific receptor activation","Stimulates surfactant production without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Increases oxygen delivery to tissues leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Reduces airway resistance and work of breathing due to lower density', 'Heliox reduces air turbulence and work of breathing due to helium''s lower density compared to nitrogen, improving ventilation in severe bronchospasm.'),
('de68444d-821d-56ae-be89-ee0472551c78'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Ketamine in severe asthma is used because it has:', '["Bronchodilator and sedative properties","Anti-inflammatory properties","Antitussive properties","Mucolytic properties","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Bronchodilator and sedative properties', 'Ketamine provides both bronchodilation (via catecholamine release and direct smooth muscle relaxation) and sedation, making it useful in severe asthma requiring intubation.'),
('5ce7986b-6b8c-59c4-9b25-ef6e8b780290'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'In status asthmaticus, the first-line systemic corticosteroid is:', '["Hydrocortisone","Methylprednisolone","Dexamethasone","Prednisolone","All are equally appropriate"]'::jsonb, 'All are equally appropriate', 'All listed systemic corticosteroids are appropriate for status asthmaticus. Choice depends on availability, route, and clinical scenario.'),
('b5f42347-8294-586d-aa78-be24d121243d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Ipratropium bromide is particularly useful in COPD exacerbations when combined with:', '["Ipratropium alone","Salbutamol (nebulized)","Budesonide","Theophylline","Montelukast"]'::jsonb, 'Salbutamol (nebulized)', 'Ipratropium combined with salbutamol (Combivent) provides dual bronchodilation (anticholinergic + beta-2 agonist) for COPD exacerbations.'),
('131c2d93-7bce-56b0-98aa-455bbc422c7c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'The most important step before initiating biologic therapy in asthma is:', '["Determining IgE levels resulting in decreased functional activity","Confirming diagnosis and optimizing inhaler technique and adherence","Starting oral corticosteroids mediated by specific receptor activation","Performing bronchoscopy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Checking for infections leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Confirming diagnosis and optimizing inhaler technique and adherence', 'Before starting biologics, it is essential to confirm asthma diagnosis, optimize inhaler technique, and assess medication adherence.'),
('b5c4dce8-a278-55fb-be3e-c14c8932ec32'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Which biologic therapy for asthma targets IL-5 receptor alpha and can be administered every 4-8 weeks?', '["Omalizumab","Mepolizumab","Benralizumab","Dupilumab","Tezepelumab"]'::jsonb, 'Benralizumab', 'Benralizumab targets IL-5 receptor alpha and is administered every 4 weeks for the first 3 doses, then every 8 weeks.'),
('4b1b8ac2-b03a-5d31-af58-4fc072d1829a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Zileuton is less commonly used than montelukast because:', '["It is less effective resulting in decreased functional activity","It requires four times daily dosing and LFT monitoring","It is more expensive mediated by specific receptor activation","It has more drug interactions without affecting the primary tissue pathway","It is not available leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'It requires four times daily dosing and LFT monitoring', 'Zileuton requires four times daily dosing (or sustained-release twice daily) and regular liver function monitoring, reducing compliance compared to once-daily montelukast.'),
('5efd2e9e-3349-5f49-b7d7-480fe834a7da'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Which inhaled corticosteroid is available as a pressurized metered-dose inhaler with HFA propellant as an extra-fine formulation?', '["Budesonide","Fluticasone propionate","Beclomethasone dipropionate","Mometasone furoate","Ciclesonide"]'::jsonb, 'Beclomethasone dipropionate', 'Beclomethasone dipropionate is available as an extra-fine HFA formulation (QVAR) that provides smaller particles and potentially better lung deposition.'),
('90c81c90-6da9-5a1e-9801-15d97fa7f555'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'The main mechanism of action of antitussives like codeine is:', '["Peripheral cough receptor blockade","Central depression of cough center in medulla","Bronchodilation","Mucus thinning","Anti-inflammatory action"]'::jsonb, 'Central depression of cough center in medulla', 'Codeine and related opioid antitussives act centrally on the cough center in the medulla oblongata, raising the cough threshold.'),
('b0c963d9-b3ee-520f-96c6-a1e707e068b6'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Dextromethorphan in high doses may cause:', '["Severe respiratory depression","Serotonin syndrome, especially with SSRIs","Hepatic failure","Renal failure","Bone marrow suppression"]'::jsonb, 'Serotonin syndrome, especially with SSRIs', 'High-dose dextromethorphan can cause serotonin syndrome, particularly when combined with serotonergic drugs like SSRIs, SNRIs, or MAOIs.'),
('b23f9b02-555f-54bb-a831-c5926f121a29'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Nebulized hypertonic saline is used in which respiratory condition?', '["Asthma","Cystic fibrosis","COPD","Pulmonary fibrosis","Pneumonia"]'::jsonb, 'Cystic fibrosis', 'Nebulized hypertonic saline (7%) is used in cystic fibrosis to improve mucociliary clearance by drawing water into the airway surface liquid.'),
('c4eff8cb-123e-5739-be29-040d0c49954b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Dornase alfa (Pulmozyme) is a mucolytic used in cystic fibrosis that:', '["Breaks disulfide bonds resulting in decreased functional activity","Is a recombinant human DNase that degrades extracellular DNA in sputum","Increases fluid secretion mediated by specific receptor activation","Stabilizes mast cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Acts as an expectorant leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Is a recombinant human DNase that degrades extracellular DNA in sputum', 'Dornase alfa is a recombinant human DNase that cleaves extracellular DNA released by degenerating neutrophils, reducing sputum viscosity in CF.'),
('8f5137eb-ec40-508c-963d-cfe5add3166d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Bronchopulmonary dysplasia (BPD) in premature infants is primarily treated with:', '["Antibiotics resulting in decreased functional activity","Corticosteroids (dexamethasone or budesonide)","Diuretics mediated by specific receptor activation","Surfactant without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bronchodilators leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Corticosteroids (dexamethasone or budesonide)', 'Corticosteroids (dexamethasone or inhaled budesonide) are used to reduce airway inflammation and prevent/treat BPD in premature infants.'),
('b2289f25-7419-540a-897c-f3d961831eaa'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Caffeine citrate is used in neonates for:', '["Apnea of prematurity","Bronchopulmonary dysplasia","Neonatal jaundice","Necrotizing enterocolitis","Intraventricular hemorrhage"]'::jsonb, 'Apnea of prematurity', 'Caffeine citrate is a respiratory stimulant used to treat and prevent apnea of prematurity in premature neonates.'),
('fd25449f-c648-57b9-b019-1e6666a34cf5'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Which drug is used for acute bronchospasm in the emergency department as a nebulized solution?', '["Salmeterol","Formoterol","Salbutamol (albuterol)","Indacaterol","Vilanterol"]'::jsonb, 'Salbutamol (albuterol)', 'Nebulized salbutamol (albuterol) is the standard treatment for acute bronchospasm in the emergency department.'),
('4529b35f-f577-5a7b-80e8-2ceb3968250f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'In the management of acute severe asthma, IV magnesium sulfate is used because it:', '["Is a direct bronchodilator resulting in decreased functional activity","Causes smooth muscle relaxation including bronchial smooth muscle","Has anti-inflammatory effects mediated by specific receptor activation","Reduces mucus secretion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Stimulates beta-2 receptors leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Causes smooth muscle relaxation including bronchial smooth muscle', 'IV magnesium sulfate causes smooth muscle relaxation by blocking calcium entry, providing bronchodilation in severe acute asthma.'),
('22a7c496-4617-5fa7-8560-aaf82a5031b5'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Which of the following is NOT a step in the GINA asthma treatment pathway?', '["Assess asthma control","Check inhaler technique and adherence","Treat comorbidities","Start biologic therapy at Step 1","Adjust therapy based on control"]'::jsonb, 'Start biologic therapy at Step 1', 'Biologic therapy is introduced at Step 5 of the GINA pathway, not at Step 1. Steps 1-2 focus on ICS-based therapy.'),
('063ddc6a-0d2c-5665-9ef1-1e48b6da8484'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'The SMART approach to asthma management stands for:', '["Standard Management and Relief Therapy","Symbicort Maintenance and Reliever Therapy","Short-acting Maintenance and Reliever Therapy","Salmeterol Maintenance and Reliever Therapy","SABA Maintenance and Reliever Therapy"]'::jsonb, 'Symbicort Maintenance and Reliever Therapy', 'SMART stands for Symbicort Maintenance and Reliever Therapy, using budesonide-formoterol for both maintenance and as-needed reliever.'),
('63b54db7-519a-5b1f-938a-98f527b89f72'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Bronchiectasis is primarily managed with:', '["Bronchodilators resulting in decreased functional activity","Airway clearance techniques and antibiotics for exacerbations","Inhaled corticosteroids alone mediated by specific receptor activation","Leukotriene modifiers without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mast cell stabilizers leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Airway clearance techniques and antibiotics for exacerbations', 'Bronchiectasis management focuses on airway clearance techniques (chest physiotherapy, oscillatory devices) and antibiotics for acute exacerbations.'),
('4bac9b81-bd8b-5943-b244-64977327aab8'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Inhaled tobramycin (TOBI) is used in cystic fibrosis for:', '["Routine prophylaxis resulting in decreased functional activity","Treatment of Pseudomonas aeruginosa pulmonary infections","Bronchodilation mediated by specific receptor activation","Mucolytic effect without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anti-inflammatory effect leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Treatment of Pseudomonas aeruginosa pulmonary infections', 'Inhaled tobramycin (TOBI) is used to treat Pseudomonas aeruginosa pulmonary infections in CF patients, providing high local antibiotic concentrations.'),
('9d0aa5cb-bc5f-5f13-8854-b25a0ed7b21c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Aztreonam lysine (Cayston) is an inhaled antibiotic used in CF for:', '["MRSA infections","Pseudomonas aeruginosa infections","Gram-positive infections","Fungal infections","Viral infections"]'::jsonb, 'Pseudomonas aeruginosa infections', 'Inhaled aztreonam lysine (Cayston) is an aminoglycoside antibiotic used for Pseudomonas aeruginosa infections in CF patients.'),
('9067a4c6-bdff-52b6-8910-160dadf7344f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Dornase alfa should NOT be mixed with which drug in the nebulizer?', '["Salbutamol","Ipratropium","Budesonide","Tobramycin","Hypertonic saline"]'::jsonb, 'Hypertonic saline', 'Dornase alfa should not be mixed with hypertonic saline in the same nebulizer as it may reduce the efficacy of both drugs.'),
('171cc3e8-087b-562a-b9b8-968866e98c4c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'In COPD, the main pathophysiological mechanism addressed by bronchodilators is:', '["Airway inflammation resulting in decreased functional activity","Smooth muscle tone and reversible airflow limitation","Alveolar destruction mediated by specific receptor activation","Mucus hypersecretion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pulmonary vascular remodeling leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Smooth muscle tone and reversible airflow limitation', 'Bronchodilators primarily address smooth muscle tone, reducing air trapping and improving expiratory flow in COPD.'),
('ee7bda92-7289-54ca-b610-439f52c6b3f4'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'The main difference between asthma and COPD in terms of reversibility is:', '["Asthma has no reversibility resulting in decreased functional activity","COPD has complete reversibility accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Asthma typically shows significant reversibility; COPD shows limited reversibility","Both have equal reversibility without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Neither has reversibility leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Asthma typically shows significant reversibility; COPD shows limited reversibility', 'Asthma is characterized by significant reversible airflow obstruction, while COPD has limited but some reversibility, particularly in early disease.'),
('d42a16e6-0b8a-5b21-9608-7781e0c1a89b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Which inhaler device requires the patient to coordinate inspiration with actuation?', '["Dry powder inhaler (DPI)","Metered-dose inhaler (MDI)","Nebulizer","Soft-mist inhaler","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Metered-dose inhaler (MDI)', 'MDIs require the patient to coordinate actuation (pressing the canister) with inspiration, which can be challenging for some patients.'),
('82ea8f6a-aa85-5141-a6fc-c99ae4a3802e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Dry powder inhalers (DPIs) differ from MDIs in that DPIs are:', '["Actuated by shaking","Breath-actuated, requiring no coordination","Only available as rescue inhalers","Always used with a spacer","Less portable than MDIs"]'::jsonb, 'Breath-actuated, requiring no coordination', 'DPIs are breath-actuated, meaning the drug is delivered when the patient inhales, eliminating the need for coordination between actuation and inspiration.'),
('8d12915e-0eda-5577-a76d-621c8cfd0e07'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'The Respimat soft-mist inhaler provides which advantage over MDIs?', '["Higher lung deposition","Slower-moving plume for easier inhalation","No propellant needed","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'The Respimat provides higher lung deposition, a slower-moving plume (requiring less inspiratory effort), and is propellant-free.'),
('75b8ac90-9c59-5a61-982b-4dc62e4f33af'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Spacer devices with MDIs reduce which side effect of inhaled corticosteroids?', '["Systemic side effects only","Oropharyngeal candidiasis","Cough","Bronchospasm","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Oropharyngeal candidiasis', 'Spacers reduce oropharyngeal deposition of ICS, significantly decreasing the risk of oral candidiasis and dysphonia.'),
('d099aecb-e5d2-5955-a485-2cba06dcd805'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'The BODE index in COPD includes which four parameters?', '["BMI, Obstruction, Dyspnea, Exercise capacity","Blood pressure, Oxygen, Duration, Exercise","Bronchodilator response, Obstruction, Diffusion, Exacerbations","BMI, Oxygen saturation, Disease duration, Exercise","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'BMI, Obstruction, Dyspnea, Exercise capacity', 'The BODE index includes Body mass index, Obstruction (FEV1), Dyspnea (mMRC), and Exercise capacity (6MWD) to predict mortality in COPD.'),
('bfe1ff4a-f9e9-59c3-a2b2-af98145bf131'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Pulmonary rehabilitation in COPD includes pharmacotherapy combined with:', '["Exercise training, education, and behavior modification","Supplemental oxygen only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Surgical intervention mediated by specific receptor activation","Antibiotic therapy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bronchodilator therapy only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Exercise training, education, and behavior modification', 'Pulmonary rehabilitation is a comprehensive program combining exercise training, education, and behavior modification to improve physical and psychological condition.'),
('d07de725-39e8-5863-8c51-794cfd81b1c7'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Long-term oxygen therapy (LTOT) in COPD is indicated when PaO2 is:', '["≤55 mmHg or ≤59 mmHg with cor pulmonale","≤70 mmHg accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","≤80 mmHg mediated by specific receptor activation","≤90 mmHg without affecting the primary tissue pathway","≤40 mmHg leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '≤55 mmHg or ≤59 mmHg with cor pulmonale', 'LTOT is indicated for COPD patients with PaO2 ≤55 mmHg or ≤59 mmHg with evidence of cor pulmonale, polycythemia, or pulmonary hypertension.'),
('7944a483-1871-5458-bf95-7a1ac218c738'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Acetylcysteine should NOT be used concurrently with which medication due to potential inactivation?', '["Salbutamol resulting in decreased functional activity","Activated charcoal (for poisoning)","Budesonide mediated by specific receptor activation","Theophylline without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ipratropium leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Activated charcoal (for poisoning)', 'Activated charcoal can adsorb acetylcysteine, potentially reducing its efficacy when used for acetaminophen poisoning.'),
('a1e65cfc-f5f5-59ac-8018-be108e5eaa9e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'The primary mechanism of corticosteroids in reducing airway inflammation in asthma involves:', '["Direct bronchodilation resulting in decreased functional activity","Inhibition of inflammatory gene transcription and promotion of anti-inflammatory genes","Mast cell stabilization mediated by specific receptor activation","Leukotriene receptor blockade without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Beta-2 receptor stimulation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibition of inflammatory gene transcription and promotion of anti-inflammatory genes', 'Corticosteroids bind to glucocorticoid receptors, inhibiting pro-inflammatory transcription factors (NF-κB, AP-1) and upregulating anti-inflammatory genes.'),
('51cef7fd-8fdf-558c-9b92-4c0c30e80c1a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Tachyphylaxis to beta-2 agonists refers to:', '["Allergic reaction to the drug","Decreased bronchodilator response with regular use","Increased drug efficacy over time","Drug tolerance with no clinical significance","Development of resistance to antibiotics"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased bronchodilator response with regular use', 'Tachyphylaxis refers to the diminished bronchodilator response to beta-2 agonists with regular use, which is one reason why SABA-only therapy is not recommended.'),
('102d5b0a-1a41-590c-80a0-b7936cab3648'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Which of the following is a LABA that is NOT available as a single-agent inhaler?', '["Salmeterol","Formoterol","Vilanterol","Indacaterol","Olodaterol"]'::jsonb, 'Vilanterol', 'Vilanterol is only available in combination products (with fluticasone furoate or umeclidinium) and not as a single-agent inhaler.'),
('e5b66b73-762b-5ae5-9ba2-163aa05ad24c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Theophylline toxicity can be precipitated by which of the following?', '["Ciprofloxacin","Smoking cessation","High-protein diet","Exercise","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Ciprofloxacin', 'Ciprofloxacin inhibits theophylline metabolism (CYP1A2), leading to increased levels and toxicity risk. Smoking cessation also increases theophylline levels.'),
('09f3be37-de55-5373-88bd-a8c56d190a82'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Beclomethasone dipropionate HFA formulation differs from CFC formulation in that:', '["It has larger particle size resulting in decreased functional activity","It has smaller particle size with potentially better lung deposition","It is less potent mediated by specific receptor activation","It requires more frequent dosing without affecting the primary tissue pathway","It has more systemic effects leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'It has smaller particle size with potentially better lung deposition', 'HFA-BDP has a smaller mass median aerodynamic diameter (MMAD) than CFC-BDP, resulting in potentially higher lung deposition and greater potency.'),
('ca35f248-0847-5a11-bc59-00338d139bd3'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Montelukast dosing in children aged 6-14 years is:', '["2 mg once daily","4 mg once daily","5 mg once daily (chewable tablet)","10 mg once daily","20 mg once daily"]'::jsonb, '5 mg once daily (chewable tablet)', 'Montelukast is dosed at 5 mg (chewable tablet) once daily for children aged 6-14 years, taken in the evening.'),
('1240bcc8-78b4-5b35-8c7c-386f46ffe040'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Zafirlukast should be taken:', '["On an empty stomach","With food (to improve absorption)","At bedtime only","With antacids","On an empty stomach with grapefruit juice"]'::jsonb, 'With food (to improve absorption)', 'Zafirlukast absorption is enhanced when taken with food, so it is recommended to take it with meals.'),
('c9d783e7-652a-545b-b2c3-6ed0531e5b86'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Which drug is used for bronchopulmonary spasm during anesthesia?', '["Theophylline","Salbutamol","Ipratropium","Both B and C","Montelukast"]'::jsonb, 'Both B and C', 'Both salbutamol and ipratropium (anticholinergic) are used intraoperatively to manage bronchospasm during anesthesia.'),
('0d36203e-eb68-5e05-97bb-731776f006e6'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Pulmonary arterial hypertension (PAH) is classified as a WHO Group:', '["1","2","3","4","5"]'::jsonb, '1', 'PAH is classified as WHO Group 1 pulmonary hypertension, which includes idiopathic, heritable, drug-induced, and associated forms.'),
('3cb2943d-44b8-5e0d-a093-db0677660cb8'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Left heart disease causes which type of pulmonary hypertension?', '["Group 1 (PAH)","Group 2 (PH due to left heart disease)","Group 3 (PH due to lung disease)","Group 4 (CTEPH)","Group 5 (unclear/multifactorial)"]'::jsonb, 'Group 2 (PH due to left heart disease)', 'Left heart disease (systolic or diastolic dysfunction, valvular disease) causes Group 2 pulmonary hypertension.'),
('00f08164-f50c-5b76-b6f0-c29522e65c9f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension (CTEPH) is classified as WHO Group:', '["1","2","3","4","5"]'::jsonb, '4', 'CTEPH is classified as WHO Group 4 pulmonary hypertension, resulting from unresolved pulmonary emboli causing vascular obstruction and remodeling.'),
('31227d4d-5bbb-5009-98d4-8161c0778ecd'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Surgical treatment of CTEPH is:', '["Lung transplantation","Pulmonary endarterectomy (PEA)","Balloon pulmonary angioplasty (BPA)","Both B and C depending on disease distribution","No surgical treatment available"]'::jsonb, 'Both B and C depending on disease distribution', 'PEA is the definitive treatment for central CTEPH, while BPA may be used for distal disease not amenable to PEA.'),
('38699396-c887-522f-94d2-677fdf9d742c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Prostacyclin pathway dysregulation in PAH is characterized by:', '["Increased prostacyclin production","Decreased prostacyclin and increased thromboxane A2","No change in prostacyclin levels","Increased thromboxane A2 only","Decreased thromboxane A2"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased prostacyclin and increased thromboxane A2', 'In PAH, there is decreased prostacyclin (vasodilator, anti-aggregatory) and increased thromboxane A2 (vasoconstrictor, pro-aggregatory), creating a prothrombotic, vasoconstricted state.'),
('79125df5-93bf-5406-8c4d-057032aa852c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Endothelin-1 levels in PAH patients are:', '["Decreased","Normal","Elevated","Undetectable","Variable"]'::jsonb, 'Elevated', 'Endothelin-1, a potent vasoconstrictor and smooth muscle mitogen, is elevated in PAH patients, contributing to pulmonary vasoconstriction and vascular remodeling.'),
('d83c3121-669a-592e-9fbb-867daa6c13fe'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Nitric oxide bioavailability is reduced in PAH due to:', '["Increased NO synthase activity","Increased oxidative stress and reduced eNOS function","Increased guanylate cyclase activity","Increased cGMP degradation","Decreased superoxide production"]'::jsonb, 'Increased oxidative stress and reduced eNOS function', 'Oxidative stress reduces eNOS function and increases NO scavenging by superoxide, decreasing NO bioavailability in PAH.'),
('f86d17ea-b9a5-50c4-97b1-5d71857e0597'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Pulmonary vascular remodeling in PAH involves:', '["Smooth muscle cell proliferation and migration","Endothelial dysfunction","Inflammation and fibrosis","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'PAH involves a complex interplay of smooth muscle proliferation, endothelial dysfunction, inflammation, fibrosis, and in-situ thrombosis.'),
('b709b239-8e16-5109-ad56-ebea132c9209'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Inhaled nitric oxide selectively dilates pulmonary vessels because:', '["It is rapidly inactivated in the systemic circulation","It requires hemoglobin for activation","It is rapidly bound and inactivated by hemoglobin in pulmonary capillary blood","It only acts on pulmonary vascular smooth muscle","It has higher affinity for pulmonary receptors"]'::jsonb, 'It is rapidly bound and inactivated by hemoglobin in pulmonary capillary blood', 'Inhaled NO is rapidly bound and inactivated by hemoglobin as it diffuses from alveoli to pulmonary capillaries, providing selective pulmonary vasodilation.'),
('e5273be4-19d3-59ae-889c-c749541723bd'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Sildenafil in pulmonary hypertension can cause which significant side effect?', '["Nephrotoxicity","Visual disturbances (cyanopsia)","Hearing loss","Alopecia","Photosensitivity"]'::jsonb, 'Visual disturbances (cyanopsia)', 'Sildenafil can cause visual disturbances including blue-tinted vision (cyanopsia) due to inhibition of PDE6 in retinal photoreceptors.'),
('555ab171-c2a4-52db-b548-acabd903ce37'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Bosentan requires monitoring of which laboratory parameter due to hepatotoxicity risk?', '["Serum creatinine","Liver function tests (LFTs)","Complete blood count","Coagulation profile","Thyroid function tests"]'::jsonb, 'Liver function tests (LFTs)', 'Bosentan requires regular LFT monitoring (monthly initially) due to dose-dependent hepatotoxicity risk.'),
('9bfd9347-9c3d-50fe-ac8f-3e36add9e2a8'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Bosentan is contraindicated in pregnancy because it is:', '["Teratogenic","Hepatotoxic","Nephrotoxic","Cardiotoxic","Neurotoxic"]'::jsonb, 'Teratogenic', 'Bosentan is teratogenic (Category X) and must not be used during pregnancy. Women of childbearing potential must use reliable contraception.'),
('1fbf46d3-29d0-53e8-b15b-7670cec0e930'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Selexipag acts on which receptor?', '["EP receptor (prostaglandin E receptor)","IP receptor (prostacyclin receptor)","FP receptor (prostaglandin F receptor)","TP receptor (thromboxane receptor)","DP receptor (prostaglandin D receptor)"]'::jsonb, 'IP receptor (prostacyclin receptor)', 'Selexipag selectively activates the IP (prostacyclin) receptor, mimicking the vasodilatory and anti-proliferative effects of endogenous prostacyclin.'),
('50b2c696-0283-56f7-bd07-7d30cf0496e9'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'The half-life of epoprostenol is approximately 3-5 minutes, which necessitates:', '["Oral dosing resulting in decreased functional activity","Continuous intravenous infusion via a central line","Intermittent intravenous bolus mediated by specific receptor activation","Subcutaneous injection without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inhaled administration leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Continuous intravenous infusion via a central line', 'The extremely short half-life of epoprostenol requires continuous IV infusion via a central venous catheter with a ambulatory infusion pump.'),
('e5518d47-80a8-5c1e-86f9-c0124c0a6280'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Treprostinil has a longer half-life than epoprostenol, allowing for:', '["Continuous IV infusion only resulting in decreased functional activity","Subcutaneous, intravenous, inhaled, and oral administration","Once-weekly dosing mediated by specific receptor activation","Intramuscular administration without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Topical application leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Subcutaneous, intravenous, inhaled, and oral administration', 'Treprostinil''s longer half-life (4 hours) allows for multiple routes of administration including subcutaneous, IV, inhaled, and oral formulations.'),
('add0cfde-9ba2-5a8c-9a4e-3bfb85a63a45'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'Macitentan achieved a reduction in morbidity/mortality in the SERAPHIN trial. The primary endpoint included:', '["6-minute walk distance only","Morbidity and mortality events","WHO functional class improvement","Reduction in pulmonary vascular resistance","Improvement in NT-proBNP levels"]'::jsonb, 'Morbidity and mortality events', 'The SERAPHIN trial demonstrated that macitentan reduced the composite of morbidity and mortality events in PAH patients.'),
('410b47ab-10e1-5b36-945f-e155cffa2ca2'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'The GRIPHON trial evaluated which drug for PAH?', '["Bosentan","Ambrisentan","Selexipag","Riociguat","Macitentan"]'::jsonb, 'Selexipag', 'The GRIPHON trial demonstrated that selexipag reduced the risk of morbidity and mortality events in PAH patients.'),
('0445e369-71f3-5f6f-a1be-e423cb529968'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Drugs', 'The COMPASS-2 trial evaluated which combination therapy for PAH?', '["Bosentan + sildenafil","Ambrisentan + tadalafil","Macitentan + riociguat","Selexipag + bosentan","Epoprostenol + sildenafil"]'::jsonb, 'Bosentan + sildenafil', 'The COMPASS-2 trial evaluated bosentan added to sildenafil in PAH, though it did not meet its primary endpoint of reducing clinical worsening.'),
('0d8e07fe-7028-51f9-9cef-7c9831a378dc'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Which insulin analog has the longest duration of action?', '["Regular insulin","NPH insulin","Insulin glargine","Insulin lispro","Insulin aspart"]'::jsonb, 'Insulin glargine', 'Insulin glargine is a long-acting analog with duration 24 hours, providing basal insulin coverage.'),
('26b6caa0-5c33-5d81-93e3-adb8554b5b05'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Metformin primarily acts by:', '["Stimulating insulin secretion resulting in decreased functional activity","Increasing hepatic glucose production accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Decreasing hepatic glucose output and improving insulin sensitivity","Inhibiting alpha-glucosidase without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Blocking SGLT2 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Decreasing hepatic glucose output and improving insulin sensitivity', 'Metformin reduces hepatic glucose production and enhances peripheral insulin sensitivity via AMPK activation.'),
('a2548489-2dfb-5fbc-bed9-6f71cfffc697'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Which oral hypoglycemic is contraindicated in renal impairment due to lactic acidosis risk?', '["Glipizide","Glyburide","Metformin","Pioglitazone","Acarbose"]'::jsonb, 'Metformin', 'Metformin is contraindicated in severe renal impairment (eGFR <30) due to accumulation and risk of lactic acidosis.'),
('78d2f26f-2e24-5ebe-a10a-d15a631ed119'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Sulfonylureas lower blood glucose by:', '["Inhibiting alpha-glucosidase","Blocking glucose absorption","Stimulating insulin secretion from pancreatic beta cells","Increasing insulin sensitivity","Inhibiting renal glucose reabsorption"]'::jsonb, 'Stimulating insulin secretion from pancreatic beta cells', 'Sulfonylureas close ATP-sensitive K+ channels on beta cells, leading to depolarization and insulin release.'),
('bc1bf53f-9239-54e0-8429-88926a43c611'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Which is a selective SGLT2 inhibitor?', '["Acarbose","Miglitol","Empagliflozin","Sitagliptin","Exenatide"]'::jsonb, 'Empagliflozin', 'Empagliflozin is an SGLT2 inhibitor that blocks renal glucose reabsorption, promoting glycosuria.'),
('b690c327-e9d2-5539-829b-9a5246f99ffb'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'DPP-4 inhibitors work by:', '["Increasing insulin secretion directly resulting in decreased functional activity","Inactivating incretin hormones accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Preventing breakdown of incretin hormones, increasing insulin and decreasing glucagon","Stimulating GLP-1 receptor directly without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Blocking glucose absorption leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Preventing breakdown of incretin hormones, increasing insulin and decreasing glucagon', 'DPP-4 inhibitors prevent degradation of endogenous GLP-1 and GIP, enhancing glucose-dependent insulin secretion.'),
('3e037a6e-00b4-547d-ab04-fa81feea6f88'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Which GLP-1 receptor agonist is administered once weekly?', '["Exenatide","Liraglutide","Dulaglutide","Lixisenatide","Albiglutide"]'::jsonb, 'Dulaglutide', 'Dulaglutide is a once-weekly GLP-1 receptor agonist for type 2 diabetes.'),
('bfdc62a1-04f5-5010-8b72-52a85bc60c36'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Pioglitazone belongs to which class?', '["Sulfonylureas","Biguanides","Thiazolidinediones","DPP-4 inhibitors","Alpha-glucosidase inhibitors"]'::jsonb, 'Thiazolidinediones', 'Pioglitazone is a thiazolidinedione that activates PPAR-gamma, improving insulin sensitivity.'),
('fce18637-80ba-51f9-aa00-2ed4a9fedbb0'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Common adverse effect of thiazolidinediones?', '["Hypoglycemia","Weight gain and fluid retention","Lactic acidosis","Pancreatitis","Vitamin B12 deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Weight gain and fluid retention', 'Thiazolidinediones can cause weight gain, edema, and exacerbation of heart failure due to fluid retention.'),
('cdc195e1-f132-5a25-9028-56206cd23335'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Which insulin is considered ''peakless''?', '["Regular insulin","NPH insulin","Insulin glargine","Insulin lispro","Insulin aspart"]'::jsonb, 'Insulin glargine', 'Insulin glargine has a flat pharmacokinetic profile, providing steady basal insulin without significant peaks.'),
('f458a01d-6487-54e3-9b73-3084fa234293'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Acarbose lowers blood glucose by:', '["Stimulating insulin release","Inhibiting alpha-glucosidase in the small intestine","Increasing insulin sensitivity","Blocking renal glucose reabsorption","Inhibiting hepatic glucose production"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibiting alpha-glucosidase in the small intestine', 'Acarbose inhibits alpha-glucosidase enzymes, delaying carbohydrate digestion and glucose absorption.'),
('ba9ec12e-2be1-5ed4-a28e-500762ac3312'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Which is a sulfonylurea?', '["Metformin","Glipizide","Pioglitazone","Sitagliptin","Empagliflozin"]'::jsonb, 'Glipizide', 'Glipizide is a second-generation sulfonylurea that stimulates insulin secretion.'),
('7282fca5-bd11-5c17-a6fa-9851d0c7e494'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'GLP-1 receptor agonists have which effect?', '["Increase glucagon secretion","Delay gastric emptying and promote satiety","Directly stimulate insulin secretion independent of glucose","Cause weight gain","Increase hepatic glucose output"]'::jsonb, 'Delay gastric emptying and promote satiety', 'GLP-1 agonists delay gastric emptying, promote satiety, and enhance glucose-dependent insulin secretion.'),
('73f2e768-8028-5905-9b86-b2f18d82adb1'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Risk of sulfonylurea therapy?', '["Weight loss","Hypoglycemia","Lactic acidosis","Pancreatic cancer","Renal tubular acidosis"]'::jsonb, 'Hypoglycemia', 'Sulfonylureas can cause hypoglycemia, especially in elderly or those with renal impairment.'),
('a384ffd5-0936-5a18-bb62-75105e013840'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Metformin is classified as a:', '["Sulfonylurea","Biguanide","Thiazolidinedione","DPP-4 inhibitor","GLP-1 agonist"]'::jsonb, 'Biguanide', 'Metformin is the only biguanide used clinically for type 2 diabetes.'),
('11f6b889-3ec3-5e8b-a197-0191cfc3f395'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Which of the following is NOT a incretin-based therapy?', '["Sitagliptin","Exenatide","Liraglutide","Metformin","Dulaglutide"]'::jsonb, 'Metformin', 'Metformin is a biguanide, not an incretin-based therapy. DPP-4 inhibitors and GLP-1 agonists are incretin-based.'),
('50ef1f2f-c9fe-5e85-9c95-0dee080c8b25'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Which drug in the MECHANISM causes hypoglycemia LEAST?', '["Glipizide","Metformin","Pioglitazone","Glyburide","Glimepiride"]'::jsonb, 'Metformin', 'Metformin rarely causes hypoglycemia when used alone as it does not stimulate insulin secretion.'),
('f1d6f958-5244-5c24-b65b-7ddd0d24e750'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Canagliflozin is an example of which class?', '["DPP-4 inhibitor","GLP-1 agonist","SGLT2 inhibitor","Alpha-glucosidase inhibitor","Thiazolidinedione"]'::jsonb, 'SGLT2 inhibitor', 'Canagliflozin is an SGLT2 inhibitor that promotes urinary glucose excretion.'),
('771ea9af-0cd5-584f-be89-1c2f068602d7'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Which of the following GLP-1 agonists is approved for cardiovascular risk reduction?', '["Exenatide","Liraglutide","Dulaglutide","Lixisenatide","Albiglutide"]'::jsonb, 'Liraglutide', 'Liraglutide (LEADER trial) showed cardiovascular benefit in type 2 diabetes patients.'),
('f8284feb-2801-579b-a712-f85440d6be72'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Repaglinide belongs to which class?', '["Sulfonylureas","Meglitinides","Thiazolidinediones","Alpha-glucosidase inhibitors","Biguanides"]'::jsonb, 'Meglitinides', 'Repaglinide is a meglitinide that stimulates short-acting insulin secretion.'),
('ece64ea9-0218-599f-8eb9-1d1793e3d44d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Which drug is used to treat hypothyroidism?', '["Methimazole","Propylthiouracil","Levothyroxine","Lithium","Iodide"]'::jsonb, 'Levothyroxine', 'Levothyroxine is synthetic T4 used for thyroid hormone replacement in hypothyroidism.'),
('d8512ed1-7c63-57f4-9900-fd3312f10c14'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Methimazole acts by:', '["Inhibiting thyroid hormone synthesis by blocking thyroid peroxidase","Blocking TSH receptors accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inhibiting peripheral conversion of T4 to T3 mediated by specific receptor activation","Destroying thyroid tissue without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inhibiting iodine uptake leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibiting thyroid hormone synthesis by blocking thyroid peroxidase', 'Methimazole inhibits thyroid peroxidase, preventing iodination and coupling of thyroid hormones.'),
('fc37eca9-976c-55e2-b78e-a5ddd41701dd'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Propylthiouracil differs from methimazole in that PTU:', '["Is more potent","Also inhibits peripheral conversion of T4 to T3","Is administered once daily","Has a shorter half-life","Is preferred in pregnancy"]'::jsonb, 'Also inhibits peripheral conversion of T4 to T3', 'PTU inhibits both thyroid hormone synthesis and peripheral conversion of T4 to T3.'),
('b0adc4a7-c48c-5576-9360-ddf95b96efd0'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Which antithyroid drug is preferred in first trimester of pregnancy?', '["Methimazole","Propylthiouracil","Carbimazole","Iodide","Lithium"]'::jsonb, 'Propylthiouracil', 'PTU is preferred in the first trimester due to lower risk of teratogenicity compared to methimazole.'),
('482170b7-1900-5098-9bbf-1827750b859f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Levothyroxine should be taken:', '["With food resulting in decreased functional activity","At bedtime accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","On an empty stomach, 30-60 minutes before breakfast","With calcium supplements without affecting the primary tissue pathway","With iron supplements leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'On an empty stomach, 30-60 minutes before breakfast', 'Levothyroxine absorption is enhanced when taken on an empty stomach, separated from food, calcium, or iron.'),
('717c4f33-40a4-5fe6-8964-40464419271b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Side effect of methimazole?', '["Hypothyroidism","Agranulocytosis","Hypoglycemia","Osteoporosis","Renal failure"]'::jsonb, 'Agranulocytosis', 'Methimazole can cause agranulocytosis, a rare but serious adverse effect requiring monitoring.'),
('0c7de4e4-ba5a-5c9f-8d9e-085386ddfe9d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Metformin first-line for T2DM because:', '["Reduces hepatic glucose production","Stimulates insulin secretion","Inhibits alpha-glucosidase","Increases urinary glucose excretion","Inhibits DPP-4"]'::jsonb, 'Reduces hepatic glucose production', 'Activates AMPK, reduces hepatic glucose output.'),
('d335854f-21d3-5795-a6fd-8d26d972e062'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'In thyroid storm, which drug inhibits peripheral conversion of T4 to T3?', '["Levothyroxine","Methimazole","Propylthiouracil","Iodide","Beta-blockers"]'::jsonb, 'Propylthiouracil', 'PTU is used in thyroid storm because it inhibits peripheral conversion of T4 to T3 in addition to blocking synthesis.'),
('1fe825e4-dbbf-59a8-9424-344bc16ea968'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Which blocks thyroid hormone synthesis preoperatively?', '["Levothyroxine","Methimazole","Iodide (Lugol''s solution)","Lithium","Dexamethasone"]'::jsonb, 'Iodide (Lugol''s solution)', 'Iodide (Lugol''s solution or SSKI) is given preoperatively to reduce thyroid vascularity and hormone synthesis.'),
('45d298fa-1019-5daf-b6b1-06346847483d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Most useful thyroid function test for monitoring levothyroxine replacement?', '["Total T4","Free T4","TSH","Total T3","Free T3"]'::jsonb, 'TSH', 'TSH is the most sensitive marker for monitoring levothyroxine replacement therapy.'),
('5a685d8a-9c46-5ca8-b27a-ccb89a4dee82'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Methimazole is contraindicated in:', '["Graves'' disease","Toxic multinodular goiter","First trimester of pregnancy","Thyroid storm","Solitary toxic adenoma"]'::jsonb, 'First trimester of pregnancy', 'Methimazole is contraindicated in the first trimester due to risk of aplasia cutis.'),
('90c6181d-d142-505f-8507-69fe8b393872'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Side effect of propylthiouracil?', '["Aplasia cutis resulting in decreased functional activity","Hepatotoxicity (cholestatic and hepatocellular)","Pancreatitis mediated by specific receptor activation","Pulmonary fibrosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Nephrotic syndrome leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hepatotoxicity (cholestatic and hepatocellular)', 'PTU can cause both cholestatic and hepatocellular liver injury, which can be severe.'),
('34d6a6f7-3192-56e5-a3f6-afd4209bba61'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Beta-blockers in hyperthyroidism are used to:', '["Reduce thyroid hormone synthesis","Block peripheral conversion of T4 to T3","Control sympathetic symptoms like tachycardia and tremor","Increase thyroid hormone binding","Stimulate TSH release"]'::jsonb, 'Control sympathetic symptoms like tachycardia and tremor', 'Beta-blockers provide symptomatic relief of adrenergic symptoms in hyperthyroidism.'),
('69827b2b-4be2-5608-8965-6188d60b9702'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Amiodarone-induced thyrotoxicosis type 1 is treated with:', '["Methimazole alone","Propylthiouracil alone","Antithyroid drugs with perchlorate","Radioactive iodine","Thyroidectomy"]'::jsonb, 'Antithyroid drugs with perchlorate', 'Type 1 amiodarone-induced thyrotoxicosis (excess hormone synthesis) is treated with antithyroid drugs and iodide uptake inhibitors.'),
('865188d3-bb6e-51c5-aaa8-ad6465822674'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Which of the following is NOT a thionamide antithyroid drug?', '["Methimazole","Propylthiouracil","Carbimazole","Iodide","Tapazole"]'::jsonb, 'Iodide', 'Iodide is not a thionamide. Methimazole, PTU, and carbimazole are thionamides that inhibit thyroid peroxidase.'),
('0e5e30ca-1bd5-59ed-97a3-f286e6cefd6a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Corticosteroids primarily act by:', '["Inhibiting cyclooxygenase resulting in decreased functional activity","Blocking histamine receptors accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Binding to intracellular receptors and modulating gene transcription","Inhibiting calcium channels without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Blocking sodium channels leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Binding to intracellular receptors and modulating gene transcription', 'Corticosteroids bind to intracellular glucocorticoid receptors, translocate to nucleus, and alter gene transcription.'),
('45fae5ba-ec20-54e3-92a6-ac929514d197'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Mineralocorticoid effect of corticosteroids?', '["Anti-inflammatory action","Sodium and water retention","Immunosuppression","Protein catabolism","Hyperglycemia"]'::jsonb, 'Sodium and water retention', 'Mineralocorticoid effects include sodium and water retention, potassium excretion, and hypertension.'),
('56fd94f3-90e0-5dca-a3b5-62d42653656e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Prednisone is converted to its active form in the:', '["Liver","Kidney","Lungs","Intestine","Spleen"]'::jsonb, 'Liver', 'Prednisone is a prodrug that is converted to prednisolone (active form) by hepatic 11-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase.'),
('3d53e02c-1b75-5a5b-ac41-9a187d624911'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Which corticosteroid has the least mineralocorticoid activity?', '["Cortisol","Cortisone","Fludrocortisone","Dexamethasone","Aldosterone"]'::jsonb, 'Dexamethasone', 'Dexamethasone is a potent glucocorticoid with negligible mineralocorticoid activity.'),
('91e24fc6-059f-5607-be6d-7bfe6b2ad2d8'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Cushing''s syndrome can be caused by:', '["Long-term low-dose inhaled corticosteroids","Long-term systemic corticosteroid therapy","Topical corticosteroid use","Nasal corticosteroid sprays","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Long-term systemic corticosteroid therapy', 'Systemic corticosteroid use for prolonged periods can cause iatrogenic Cushing''s syndrome.'),
('8b59b350-3ec4-56ba-9496-12d8232b185c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Common adverse effect of long-term corticosteroid use?', '["Hypoglycemia","Osteoporosis","Hyperkalemia","Weight loss","Hypotension"]'::jsonb, 'Osteoporosis', 'Long-term corticosteroid use causes osteoporosis due to decreased osteoblast activity and increased bone resorption.'),
('a499b52e-9942-57f6-80bd-a4693e9dd199'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Steroid withdrawal syndrome can include:', '["Hyperglycemia resulting in decreased functional activity","Adrenal insufficiency and hypotension","Weight gain mediated by specific receptor activation","Cushingoid features without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hypokalemia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Adrenal insufficiency and hypotension', 'Abrupt withdrawal of chronic steroids can cause adrenal crisis with hypotension, fatigue, and electrolyte imbalance.'),
('b3dbc3b6-73af-5a46-996b-a118e552015b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Contraindication to systemic corticosteroids?', '["Rheumatoid arthritis","Active peptic ulcer disease","Asthma exacerbation","Autoimmune hemolytic anemia","Inflammatory bowel disease"]'::jsonb, 'Active peptic ulcer disease', 'Active peptic ulcer disease is a relative contraindication due to increased risk of GI bleeding and perforation.'),
('e8848237-de1b-5a17-8e24-151c1ba6917b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Inhaled corticosteroids in asthma are used to:', '["Relieve acute bronchospasm","Provide long-term control of airway inflammation","Replace systemic steroids completely","Treat infection-triggered asthma","Prevent exercise-induced bronchospasm"]'::jsonb, 'Provide long-term control of airway inflammation', 'Inhaled corticosteroids are the mainstay for long-term control of persistent asthma by reducing airway inflammation.'),
('c50d7b43-9a57-5d1e-ba9d-dd724c998bb8'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Side effect of inhaled corticosteroids?', '["Hypertension","Oral candidiasis","Hyperglycemia","Osteoporosis","Adrenal suppression"]'::jsonb, 'Oral candidiasis', 'Oral candidiasis is a local side effect of inhaled corticosteroids; rinsing mouth after use reduces risk.'),
('50d847e8-e739-5594-89f3-250d9c2a9639'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Which corticosteroid is used for mineralocorticoid replacement in Addison''s disease?', '["Prednisone","Dexamethasone","Fludrocortisone","Methylprednisolone","Hydrocortisone"]'::jsonb, 'Fludrocortisone', 'Fludrocortisone is a synthetic mineralocorticoid used for mineralocorticoid replacement in primary adrenal insufficiency.'),
('55a4cdae-252a-53f2-b57c-358377ba8daf'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Pulse methylprednisolone therapy is used in:', '["Mild asthma resulting in decreased functional activity","Severe autoimmune conditions like lupus nephritis","Routine allergies mediated by specific receptor activation","Mild eczema without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Osteoarthritis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Severe autoimmune conditions like lupus nephritis', 'High-dose IV methylprednisolone is used for severe autoimmune/inflammatory conditions.'),
('0bfdee87-36f8-5970-a9b5-5c96b7a1d1b0'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Feature of Cushing''s syndrome?', '["Weight loss","Moon face and buffalo hump","Hypotension","Hyperkalemia","Hypoglycemia"]'::jsonb, 'Moon face and buffalo hump', 'Cushing''s syndrome is characterized by central obesity, moon facies, dorsocutaneous fat pad (buffalo hump).'),
('b0caa8f3-29d5-52fb-af76-c66913233bbd'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Which of the following is a steroid-sparing agent used in autoimmune diseases?', '["Aspirin","Methotrexate","Acetaminophen","Ibuprofen","Naproxen"]'::jsonb, 'Methotrexate', 'Methotrexate is a DMARD that can reduce the need for corticosteroids in autoimmune diseases.'),
('bafd3875-42e7-5c12-886e-6bd6cb68b13d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Which corticosteroid is equivalent to 5mg prednisone?', '["20mg hydrocortisone","5mg prednisolone","0.75mg dexamethasone","20mg cortisone","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'All listed doses are equivalent to 5mg prednisone: hydrocortisone 20mg, prednisolone 5mg, dexamethasone 0.75mg, cortisone 25mg.'),
('dbdf6557-30e1-56bf-bedb-b3214deef8f4'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'ACTH stimulation test is used to diagnose:', '["Cushing''s syndrome","Addison''s disease","Pheochromocytoma","Conn''s syndrome","Congenital adrenal hyperplasia"]'::jsonb, 'Addison''s disease', 'ACTH stimulation test assesses adrenal cortex function; inadequate cortisol response suggests Addison''s disease.'),
('62c015c1-3b11-5a05-9592-d0049b8fda88'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'In primary adrenal insufficiency, which hormone is elevated?', '["Cortisol","ACTH","Aldosterone","DHEA","Androstenedione"]'::jsonb, 'ACTH', 'In primary adrenal insufficiency, low cortisol leads to loss of negative feedback, elevating ACTH.'),
('2b76236d-0cca-59df-aafb-44e0064feee7'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Ketoconazole is used in the treatment of:', '["Addison''s disease","Cushing''s syndrome","Hypothyroidism","Hyperthyroidism","Hypogonadism"]'::jsonb, 'Cushing''s syndrome', 'Ketoconazole inhibits steroidogenesis and is used in the treatment of Cushing''s syndrome.'),
('f541724e-cb64-5730-87f5-486c1e244038'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Adrenal enzyme inhibitor used in Cushing''s syndrome?', '["Metformin","Metyrapone","Spironolactone","Fludrocortisone","Levothyroxine"]'::jsonb, 'Metyrapone', 'Metyrapone inhibits 11-beta-hydroxylase, reducing cortisol synthesis.'),
('5d2c1551-bfca-5b68-b8d5-ec53533a39d8'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Used to treat hyperaldosteronism (Conn''s syndrome)?', '["Lisinopril","Spironolactone","Amlodipine","Metoprolol","Furosemide"]'::jsonb, 'Spironolactone', 'Spironolactone is an aldosterone antagonist used to treat primary hyperaldosteronism.'),
('77d80217-cf1e-50ef-a538-b6c6fdd8308a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Used to treat an adrenal crisis?', '["Oral prednisone","IV hydrocortisone and IV normal saline","IM dexamethasone","Oral fludrocortisone","IV methimazole"]'::jsonb, 'IV hydrocortisone and IV normal saline', 'Adrenal crisis requires immediate IV hydrocortisone and aggressive IV normal saline for resuscitation.'),
('31e5cfde-030e-5c78-be12-b3789383775d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Mifepristone acts as a:', '["Glucocorticoid receptor antagonist","Mineralocorticoid receptor agonist","Glucocorticoid receptor agonist","Mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist","Androgen receptor agonist"]'::jsonb, 'Glucocorticoid receptor antagonist', 'Mifepristone is a competitive antagonist at glucocorticoid receptors used in Cushing''s syndrome.'),
('14378218-73a2-53f5-96dc-b27263f5921c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Congenital adrenal hyperplasia (21-hydroxylase deficiency) is treated with:', '["Hydrocortisone and fludrocortisone","Methimazole accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Spironolactone mediated by specific receptor activation","Levothyroxine without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Octreotide leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hydrocortisone and fludrocortisone', '21-hydroxylase deficiency requires glucocorticoid (hydrocortisone) and mineralocorticoid (fludrocortisone) replacement.'),
('a24af5f3-448c-5480-a325-7866166aec34'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Pheochromocytoma is treated with:', '["Beta-blockers alone resulting in decreased functional activity","Alpha-blockers (phenoxybenzamine) followed by beta-blockers","Calcium channel blockers alone mediated by specific receptor activation","ACE inhibitors without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Direct vasodilators leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Alpha-blockers (phenoxybenzamine) followed by beta-blockers', 'Alpha-blockade is initiated first to prevent unopposed alpha stimulation; beta-blockers are added later.'),
('2255b2a9-153c-5627-88a6-06efb31c3f01'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Preoperative preparation in pheochromocytoma?', '["Propranolol first resulting in decreased functional activity","Phenoxybenzamine (alpha-blocker) for 10-14 days","Nifedipine alone mediated by specific receptor activation","Clonidine alone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Methyldopa leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Phenoxybenzamine (alpha-blocker) for 10-14 days', 'Phenoxybenzamine is used for preoperative alpha-blockade for 10-14 days before surgery.'),
('f1d923f8-4ff1-5293-bea3-aac90d2879fe'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Mitotane is used in the treatment of:', '["Hypothyroidism","Adrenocortical carcinoma","Pheochromocytoma","Thyroid storm","Graves'' disease"]'::jsonb, 'Adrenocortical carcinoma', 'Mitotane is an adrenolytic agent used in adrenocortical carcinoma.'),
('e93afc58-c83a-52b1-bdb4-c28f78de153f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Which of the following is NOT a glucocorticoid?', '["Prednisone","Dexamethasone","Fludrocortisone","Hydrocortisone","Methylprednisolone"]'::jsonb, 'Fludrocortisone', 'Fludrocortisone is primarily a mineralocorticoid, not a glucocorticoid.'),
('05744ae2-a062-5c81-821d-f767a25f9776'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Oral contraceptive pills primarily prevent pregnancy by:', '["Killing sperm resulting in decreased functional activity","Inhibiting ovulation via negative feedback on FSH and LH","Thickening cervical mucus only mediated by specific receptor activation","Preventing implantation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Causing tubal spasm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibiting ovulation via negative feedback on FSH and LH', 'Combined OCPs inhibit ovulation by suppressing FSH and LH via negative feedback.'),
('e33181d2-2a42-58d6-96a9-80c217f86b2f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Progestin-only contraceptive method?', '["Combined OCP resulting in decreased functional activity","Depo-Provera (medroxyprogesterone acetate)","Transdermal patch mediated by specific receptor activation","Vaginal ring without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ethinyl estradiol pill leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Depo-Provera (medroxyprogesterone acetate)', 'Depo-Provera is a progestin-only injectable contraceptive given every 3 months.'),
('a06d8aec-b099-593a-ac7b-0650d552590f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Tamoxifen is a SERM used in:', '["Breast cancer (ER-positive)","Osteoporosis","Menopausal symptoms","Endometriosis","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Breast cancer (ER-positive)', 'Tamoxifen is an antiestrogen used in ER-positive breast cancer treatment and prevention.'),
('a7fed391-5a2b-51cc-805c-d0cba7c986d2'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Side effect of tamoxifen?', '["Osteoporosis","Endometrial hyperplasia/cancer","Weight loss","Hypoglycemia","Hyperkalemia"]'::jsonb, 'Endometrial hyperplasia/cancer', 'Tamoxifen has estrogenic effects on the uterus, increasing risk of endometrial hyperplasia and cancer.'),
('ccd8a186-24da-51de-a03c-d65efac0316f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Clomiphene citrate is used for:', '["Treatment of breast cancer resulting in decreased functional activity","Ovulation induction in anovulatory infertility","Treatment of endometriosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Prostate cancer without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hirsutism leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ovulation induction in anovulatory infertility', 'Clomiphene is a SERM that stimulates ovulation by blocking estrogen negative feedback at the hypothalamus.'),
('981d4ac6-5a1f-574e-94d6-9fef8b16138d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Androgen deprivation therapy is the mainstay of treatment for:', '["Breast cancer","Prostate cancer","Ovarian cancer","Lung cancer","Colorectal cancer"]'::jsonb, 'Prostate cancer', 'Androgen deprivation (via GnRH agonists/antagonists or orchiectomy) is primary treatment for advanced prostate cancer.'),
('8da8778a-cc0a-5d8b-9e44-9199dff7a19a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'GnRH agonists initially cause a ''flare'' phenomenon because:', '["They immediately suppress LH and FSH","They initially stimulate LH/FSH release before downregulation","They block androgen receptors","They inhibit 5-alpha-reductase","They have direct antiandrogen effects"]'::jsonb, 'They initially stimulate LH/FSH release before downregulation', 'GnRH agonists initially stimulate (flare) gonadotropin release before causing pituitary desensitization.'),
('d5a48fad-454d-55a7-ba3c-98f662cdbc7e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Finasteride is used in:', '["Female pattern hair loss","Benign prostatic hyperplasia","Both A and B","Neither A nor B","Prostate cancer only"]'::jsonb, 'Both A and B', 'Finasteride (5-alpha-reductase inhibitor) is used for BPH and male/female pattern hair loss.'),
('6b96a029-bce7-5dd0-90d7-4e8e858ab150'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Spironolactone is used in the treatment of:', '["Hirsutism in women","Acne","Female pattern hair loss","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Spironolactone is an antiandrogen used for hirsutism, acne, and female pattern hair loss.'),
('32a1946a-fdc6-5218-9279-609bb993be13'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Estrogen replacement therapy in menopause primarily alleviates:', '["Hot flashes and vaginal atrophy","Osteoporosis only","Cardiovascular disease","Breast cancer risk","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Hot flashes and vaginal atrophy', 'Estrogen replacement relieves vasomotor symptoms (hot flashes) and urogenital atrophy.'),
('56351a06-c0a1-5c61-b1d0-66cc7564c0be'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Mifepristone is also used as a:', '["Emergency contraceptive","Medical abortion pill (with misoprostol)","Treatment for endometriosis","Fertility drug","Treatment for menorrhagia"]'::jsonb, 'Medical abortion pill (with misoprostol)', 'Mifepristone is a progesterone receptor antagonist used with misoprostol for medical abortion.'),
('fd13f9d2-37e0-5e1b-aa0a-fc9dd137db92'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Danazol is used in:', '["Endometriosis","Fibrocystic breast disease","Hereditary angioedema","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Danazol is a synthetic androgen used for endometriosis, fibrocystic breast disease, and hereditary angioedema.'),
('def30606-f05c-57eb-b764-d81ed8094fae'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Testosterone replacement therapy is indicated in:', '["Male hypogonadism","Delayed puberty in males","Gender-affirming therapy","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Testosterone is used for male hypogonadism, delayed puberty, and gender-affirming therapy.'),
('f99910f3-c13a-5ec2-ae89-c9dc7ffa9fdb'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Which of the following is an aromatase inhibitor?', '["Letrozole","Anastrozole","Exemestane","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Letrozole, anastrozole, and exemestane are aromatase inhibitors used in ER+ breast cancer.'),
('ab159445-61cb-53ac-8571-cb8d6aa74624'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Aromatase inhibitors are contraindicated in:', '["Premenopausal women","Postmenopausal women","Men","Children","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Premenopausal women', 'Aromatase inhibitors are ineffective in premenopausal women due to ongoing ovarian estrogen production.'),
('1894b534-a1f7-54c6-b705-8cfd8ef33e35'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Bisphosphonates work by:', '["Stimulating osteoblast activity","Inhibiting osteoclast-mediated bone resorption","Increasing calcium absorption","Stimulating PTH release","Blocking RANKL"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibiting osteoclast-mediated bone resorption', 'Bisphosphonates bind to hydroxyapatite and inhibit osteoclast function, reducing bone resorption.'),
('144c1ce2-3001-5339-b1da-676814134837'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Alendronate should be taken:', '["With food resulting in decreased functional activity","On an empty stomach with water, remaining upright for 30 minutes","At bedtime mediated by specific receptor activation","With calcium supplements without affecting the primary tissue pathway","With milk leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'On an empty stomach with water, remaining upright for 30 minutes', 'Alendronate must be taken on an empty stomach with water, and the patient must remain upright for 30 minutes to prevent esophageal irritation.'),
('0ffc9e5b-dd9d-5e21-82d2-7e0d71fe5d23'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Serious adverse effect of bisphosphonates?', '["Hypocalcemia","Osteonecrosis of the jaw","Hyperkalemia","Hypoglycemia","Tinnitus"]'::jsonb, 'Osteonecrosis of the jaw', 'Osteonecrosis of the jaw (ONJ) is a rare but serious complication, especially with IV bisphosphonates.'),
('c3eb29f0-1736-59ab-ba57-f6f5ee3efbc9'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Denosumab acts by:', '["Inhibiting osteoclast formation by blocking RANKL","Stimulating osteoblasts","Inhibiting calcium absorption","Stimulating PTH","Blocking vitamin D receptors"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibiting osteoclast formation by blocking RANKL', 'Denosumab is a monoclonal antibody against RANKL, preventing osteoclast formation and activity.'),
('324c227b-9a5f-5961-bd4d-1b78f2742782'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Teriparatide (PTH 1-34) is used in osteoporosis because it:', '["Inhibits bone resorption","Stimulates new bone formation","Blocks calcium absorption","Increases renal calcium excretion","Inhibits osteoclasts"]'::jsonb, 'Stimulates new bone formation', 'Teriparatide is a recombinant PTH that stimulates osteoblast activity and new bone formation.'),
('963101c9-bb3c-57cb-aeb1-a1a836dc5a92'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Contraindication to teriparatide?', '["Osteoporosis","Paget''s disease of bone","Hypercalcemia","Both B and C","Renal stones"]'::jsonb, 'Both B and C', 'Teriparatide is contraindicated in Paget''s disease, hypercalcemia, and unexplained alkaline phosphatase elevation.'),
('784c2d82-d210-5420-a801-27e31325bc7e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Calcitonin is used in osteoporosis because it:', '["Stimulates osteoblasts resulting in decreased functional activity","Inhibits osteoclast activity and has analgesic properties","Increases calcium absorption mediated by specific receptor activation","Stimulates vitamin D production without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inhibits PTH leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibits osteoclast activity and has analgesic properties', 'Calcitonin inhibits osteoclast activity and has analgesic properties, useful in postmenopausal osteoporosis.'),
('128d55d5-ee0e-5e2e-b373-8fe1a5a867ca'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Romosozumab is a monoclonal antibody that targets:', '["RANKL","Sclerostin","PTH receptor","Estrogen receptor","Calcitonin receptor"]'::jsonb, 'Sclerostin', 'Romosozumab is an anti-sclerostin antibody that stimulates bone formation and reduces resorption.'),
('a21f8857-6e99-5e10-ac2c-dabe637e0fef'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Used for glucocorticoid-induced osteoporosis?', '["Calcium and vitamin D","Bisphosphonates","Both A and B","Tamoxifen","Spironolactone"]'::jsonb, 'Both A and B', 'Calcium/vitamin D and bisphosphonates are first-line for prevention/treatment of glucocorticoid-induced osteoporosis.'),
('8f4b7cc9-19d8-555c-adad-59505e29a036'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Alendronate is indicated for:', '["Postmenopausal osteoporosis","Glucocorticoid-induced osteoporosis","Paget''s disease of bone","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Alendronate is approved for postmenopausal osteoporosis, glucocorticoid-induced osteoporosis, and Paget''s disease.'),
('d3a1ffbf-c847-5f1d-b0f0-3314e7e8d6d2'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Hypercalcemia can be treated with:', '["Calcium supplementation resulting in decreased functional activity","IV bisphosphonates (zoledronic acid)","Estrogen mediated by specific receptor activation","Levothyroxine without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Insulin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'IV bisphosphonates (zoledronic acid)', 'IV bisphosphonates (zoledronic acid) are used to treat hypercalcemia of malignancy.'),
('9f790e6c-7f2c-5fe8-84a0-983b3ce3db08'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Which of the following is a risk factor for osteoporosis?', '["Smoking","Excessive alcohol intake","Low body weight","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Smoking, excessive alcohol, low body weight, family history, and age are risk factors for osteoporosis.'),
('a266637e-e2a3-52c0-adef-4083446c128f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Oxytocin is used in obstetrics to:', '["Preterm labor induction resulting in decreased functional activity","Labor induction and control of postpartum hemorrhage","Treatment of dysmenorrhea mediated by specific receptor activation","Suppression of lactation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Treatment of fibroids leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Labor induction and control of postpartum hemorrhage', 'Oxytocin is used for labor induction and to control postpartum hemorrhage by stimulating uterine contractions.'),
('99ed675b-2873-5db7-ab1f-1ad1f0ae17a2'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Desmopressin (DDAVP) is used in:', '["Central diabetes insipidus","Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus","SIADH","Hypertension","Heart failure"]'::jsonb, 'Central diabetes insipidus', 'Desmopressin is a synthetic ADH analog used in central diabetes insipidus.'),
('6186b437-3e7a-51ca-9deb-7329c0a7aefd'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Side effect of desmopressin?', '["Hyponatremia","Hyperkalemia","Hypoglycemia","Hypocalcemia","Hypercalcemia"]'::jsonb, 'Hyponatremia', 'Desmopressin can cause water retention and dilutional hyponatremia if fluid intake is not restricted.'),
('f5fb8dac-8c36-5bc8-96d1-0bf347d1db70'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Oxytocin antagonists (atosiban) are used to:', '["Induce labor","Inhibit preterm labor (tocolytic)","Control postpartum hemorrhage","Treat dysmenorrhea","Suppress lactation"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibit preterm labor (tocolytic)', 'Atosiban is an oxytocin receptor antagonist used as a tocolytic to delay preterm labor.'),
('caf992b7-0221-5c26-9f9c-953a32f72b96'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Desmopressin is also used in the treatment of:', '["Hemophilia A and von Willebrand disease","Diabetes mellitus","Hypothyroidism","Addison''s disease","Cushing''s syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'Hemophilia A and von Willebrand disease', 'Desmopressin releases vWF and factor VIII, used in mild hemophilia A and von Willebrand disease.'),
('bb67c1d2-eb44-5e00-bc9e-85f349dcccdf'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Contraindication to desmopressin?', '["Central diabetes insipidus","Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus","Habitual polydipsia","Both B and C","Hemophilia"]'::jsonb, 'Both B and C', 'Desmopressin is ineffective in nephrogenic DI and can cause hyponatremia in habitual polydipsia.'),
('1125f3e8-6f6e-5e35-b191-c8005d992296'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Carbetocin is a long-acting analog of:', '["ADH","Oxytocin","ACTH","TSH","FSH"]'::jsonb, 'Oxytocin', 'Carbetocin is a long-acting oxytocin analog used for prevention of postpartum hemorrhage.'),
('6d9600da-7730-5b7f-bfbe-67c7aa96de54'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Vasopressin (ADH) is released from the:', '["Anterior pituitary","Posterior pituitary","Hypothalamus","Thyroid gland","Adrenal gland"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior pituitary', 'ADH (vasopressin) is synthesized in hypothalamus and stored/released from posterior pituitary.'),
('69b9df0a-9f6b-596d-892d-56ecb2d0e1cc'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Vasopressin is used in the treatment of:', '["Diabetes insipidus","Esophageal variceal bleeding","Septic shock (vasopressor)","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Vasopressin is used in DI, esophageal varices (to reduce portal pressure), and as a vasopressor in shock.'),
('0ffc4b42-ab9b-5133-beb3-f8f4beb2dc56'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Labetalol is used in obstetrics for:', '["Postpartum hypertension","Preeclampsia/eclampsia","Both A and B","Induction of labor","Prevention of preterm labor"]'::jsonb, 'Both A and B', 'Labetalol is a combined alpha-beta blocker used for hypertension in pregnancy, including preeclampsia.'),
('1c0fb5ed-3fbb-5f1a-9594-110ebe9b4d1e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Magnesium sulfate is used in obstetrics to:', '["Prevent eclampsia seizures","Induce labor","Control postpartum hemorrhage","Treat preterm labor","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Prevent eclampsia seizures', 'Magnesium sulfate is the drug of choice for prevention and treatment of eclamptic seizures.'),
('a23679b1-7309-560e-98bc-f920c3930d98'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Terlipressin is a synthetic analog of:', '["Oxytocin","Vasopressin","ACTH","TSH","Growth hormone"]'::jsonb, 'Vasopressin', 'Terlipressin is a synthetic vasopressin analog used in hepatorenal syndrome and variceal bleeding.'),
('dd45bcd7-94b5-53f2-b215-c59a451ac26b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Mainstay of treatment for central diabetes insipidus?', '["Thiazide diuretics","Desmopressin (DDAVP)","Amiloride","Carbamazepine","Chlorpropamide"]'::jsonb, 'Desmopressin (DDAVP)', 'Desmopressin is the mainstay for central DI, replacing deficient ADH.'),
('74c7ac7f-40f0-56b0-92d3-4ba052bab282'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'In nephrogenic diabetes insipidus, which may be used?', '["Desmopressin resulting in decreased functional activity","Hydrochlorothiazide (thiazide diuretic)","Furosemide mediated by specific receptor activation","Spironolactone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mannitol leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hydrochlorothiazide (thiazide diuretic)', 'Thiazide diuretics paradoxically reduce urine volume in nephrogenic DI by inducing mild volume depletion.'),
('aa1f2ca0-b291-5833-a4e2-6fdf588c11a0'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Amiloride is useful in nephrogenic diabetes insipidus caused by:', '["Lithium use","Hypercalcemia","Hypokalemia","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Lithium use', 'Amiloride blocks lithium entry into collecting duct cells, helpful in lithium-induced nephrogenic DI.'),
('811f9d3f-492c-5e39-bc5e-59d14f5c20b9'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Patient with polyuria, polydipsia, dilute urine. Water deprivation test shows failure to concentrate urine. Consistent with:', '["Diabetes mellitus","Diabetes insipidus","SIADH","Primary polydipsia","Heart failure"]'::jsonb, 'Diabetes insipidus', 'Diabetes insipidus is characterized by inability to concentrate urine, leading to polyuria and polydipsia.'),
('535fc656-9b43-5b47-9857-09af0d7df4a7'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Desmopressin is available in which formulations?', '["Intranasal spray","Oral tablets","Subcutaneous injection","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Desmopressin is available as intranasal spray, oral tablet, and subcutaneous injection.'),
('de736309-f7d4-51ac-858f-9fa96909c110'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Which can cause nephrogenic diabetes insipidus?', '["Lithium","Hypercalcemia","Hypokalemia","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Lithium, hypercalcemia, hypokalemia, and chronic kidney disease can all cause nephrogenic DI.'),
('83e740aa-ae24-5af2-93a4-617393e83d15'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'In water deprivation test, a patient with central DI will:', '["Concentrate urine after desmopressin","Fail to concentrate urine even after desmopressin","Concentrate urine without desmopressin","Have high urine osmolality at baseline","Have low serum sodium"]'::jsonb, 'Concentrate urine after desmopressin', 'In central DI, urine concentrates after desmopressin administration due to restored ADH effect.'),
('5dd3709b-d937-5a97-9cea-a07dc841c0fa'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Which can mimic central diabetes insipidus?', '["Primary polydipsia","Nephrogenic DI","Gestational DI","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Primary polydipsia', 'Primary (psychogenic) polydipsia can mimic DI with dilute urine, but water deprivation test helps differentiate.'),
('0ee0d8d6-495e-5ca2-b1ab-c4f373f48c91'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Gestational diabetes insipidus is caused by:', '["Increased renal clearance of ADH by placental vasopressinase","Defect in V2 receptors accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lithium use mediated by specific receptor activation","Hypercalcemia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hypokalemia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Increased renal clearance of ADH by placental vasopressinase', 'Gestational DI is due to placental production of vasopressinase that degrades ADH.'),
('e549f926-7cb1-537d-9cc2-dfd7201a7be9'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Gestational diabetes insipidus is treated with:', '["Desmopressin (resistant to vasopressinase)","Thiazide diuretics accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Amiloride mediated by specific receptor activation","Furosemide without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Spironolactone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Desmopressin (resistant to vasopressinase)', 'Desmopressin is resistant to placental vasopressinase and is the treatment of choice for gestational DI.'),
('59942071-c817-57f5-9594-a3a1e2d53921'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Recombinant human growth hormone (rhGH) is indicated in:', '["Growth hormone deficiency in children","Turner syndrome","Chronic renal insufficiency in children","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'rhGH is approved for GH deficiency, Turner syndrome, and chronic renal insufficiency in children.'),
('5028dce9-ce5c-561f-81c1-e6ad90731456'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Side effect of growth hormone therapy?', '["Hypoglycemia","Edema","Arthralgia","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'GH therapy can cause hypoglycemia, edema, arthralgia, and increased insulin resistance.'),
('d324b347-5f65-53af-bb23-adef8442d6be'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Somatostatin analogs (octreotide) are used in:', '["Acromegaly","Neuroendocrine tumors","Variceal bleeding","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Octreotide is used in acromegaly, neuroendocrine tumors, and variceal bleeding.'),
('e55e1cfe-c5ab-5ed7-a018-0d10f4b9005e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Pegvisomant is a growth hormone receptor antagonist used in:', '["GH deficiency","Acromegaly","Cushing''s syndrome","Hypothyroidism","Diabetes insipidus"]'::jsonb, 'Acromegaly', 'Pegvisomant blocks GH receptors, reducing IGF-1 levels in acromegaly.'),
('2933cbc5-4f6e-5394-9e8d-32383a8af1c2'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Which is used to diagnose acromegaly?', '["Oral glucose tolerance test (suppression test)","Water deprivation test","ACTH stimulation test","Thyroid function tests","Dexamethasone suppression test"]'::jsonb, 'Oral glucose tolerance test (suppression test)', 'In acromegaly, GH fails to suppress after oral glucose load (normal suppression occurs).'),
('7f0bce45-e4ec-5177-9452-f93cb6c316d3'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Cabergoline is used in acromegaly because it:', '["Blocks GH receptors","Inhibits GH secretion (dopamine agonist)","Stimulates IGF-1 production","Blocks somatostatin receptors","Inhibits TSH"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibits GH secretion (dopamine agonist)', 'Cabergoline (dopamine agonist) can reduce GH levels in some patients with acromegaly.'),
('d37b0527-d1a7-542c-a028-b3f394c0049d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'GH deficiency in adults can cause:', '["Increased body fat (especially central)","Decreased muscle mass","Reduced bone mineral density","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Adult GH deficiency causes increased central adiposity, decreased muscle mass, reduced bone density, and fatigue.'),
('266b5b82-c775-59db-917e-2eaa8963c278'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Recombinant IGF-1 (mecasermin) is used in:', '["GH insensitivity syndrome (Laron syndrome)","Acromegaly accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cushing''s syndrome mediated by specific receptor activation","Diabetes mellitus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hypothyroidism leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'GH insensitivity syndrome (Laron syndrome)', 'Mecasermin (recombinant IGF-1) is used in GH insensitivity syndrome where GH receptors are defective.'),
('70c6847e-fb18-50bf-93f3-90ed109e765d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Side effects of somatostatin analogs include:', '["Gallstones","Hyperglycemia","GI disturbances","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Octreotide can cause gallstones, hyperglycemia, and GI side effects like nausea and diarrhea.'),
('173ab796-d9b7-585a-a421-e133c7f10638'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Transsphenoidal surgery is the primary treatment for:', '["GH-secreting pituitary adenoma","Prolactinoma","Non-functioning pituitary adenoma","Cushing''s disease","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'GH-secreting pituitary adenoma', 'Transsphenoidal surgery is first-line for GH-secreting adenomas in acromegaly.'),
('66a47f21-e181-5f11-a218-802ae2ab63d8'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Acromegaly is associated with increased risk of:', '["Colon polyps and colorectal cancer","Cardiovascular disease","Sleep apnea","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Acromegaly increases risk of colon polyps/cancer, cardiovascular disease, and sleep apnea.'),
('b5300336-7669-5258-a7eb-4ba1fb793d80'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Laron syndrome is characterized by:', '["GH insensitivity due to GH receptor defect","GH excess accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Somatostatin excess mediated by specific receptor activation","IGF-1 receptor defect without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'GH insensitivity due to GH receptor defect', 'Laron syndrome is GH insensitivity due to mutations in GH receptor, leading to low IGF-1 despite high GH.'),
('8c83ef40-58c4-5378-ab1f-efa21aef4693'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'GH excess in childhood (before epiphyseal closure) causes:', '["Gigantism","Acromegaly","Dwarfism","Cushing''s syndrome","Addison''s disease"]'::jsonb, 'Gigantism', 'GH excess before epiphyseal closure causes gigantism; after closure, it causes acromegaly.'),
('9948199a-2b78-5b07-87e5-43d6942e706c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Prolactinomas are treated with:', '["Dopamine agonists (cabergoline, bromocriptine)","Surgery (transsphenoidal)","Radiation","All of the above (depending on size/resistance)","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above (depending on size/resistance)', 'Prolactinomas are primarily treated with dopamine agonists, with surgery and radiation for resistant cases.'),
('0ce5532a-0a36-5937-85d6-6194ffbfeae4'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Hypopituitarism can result from:', '["Pituitary adenomas","Pituitary surgery or radiation","Sheehan syndrome (postpartum pituitary necrosis)","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Hypopituitarism can result from tumors, surgery, radiation, Sheehan syndrome, or infiltrative diseases.'),
('3293a542-4a25-57fd-8707-287850f99c58'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Saxagliptin is an example of which class?', '["GLP-1 agonist","DPP-4 inhibitor","SGLT2 inhibitor","Thiazolidinedione","Meglitinide"]'::jsonb, 'DPP-4 inhibitor', 'Saxagliptin is a DPP-4 inhibitor that prevents degradation of incretin hormones.'),
('6e6d123a-13ac-582a-8602-8e5d091a262b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Which of the following is NOT an SGLT2 inhibitor?', '["Empagliflozin","Canagliflozin","Dapagliflozin","Sitagliptin","Ertugliflozin"]'::jsonb, 'Sitagliptin', 'Sitagliptin is a DPP-4 inhibitor, not an SGLT2 inhibitor.'),
('973266de-8bc2-5399-a40d-a086681c8ba5'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Metformin contraindicated in:', '["Severe renal impairment (eGFR<30)","Mild hepatic impairment accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","HTN mediated by specific receptor activation","Obesity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","PCOS leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Severe renal impairment (eGFR<30)', 'Risk of lactic acidosis.'),
('ea489d4c-c035-5cda-b8a7-8c5cab676945'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Which of the following can cause drug-induced hypothyroidism?', '["Amiodarone","Lithium","Interferon-alpha","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Amiodarone, lithium, and interferon-alpha can all cause thyroid dysfunction.'),
('20694e36-cfad-54c6-89e4-d6fbb665a50b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Which of the following is a mineralocorticoid?', '["Cortisol","Cortisone","Aldosterone","Dexamethasone","Prednisone"]'::jsonb, 'Aldosterone', 'Aldosterone is the primary endogenous mineralocorticoid.'),
('0e0ff8d5-a75a-5379-b12e-daf9e86e180f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Inhaled corticosteroids are preferred over systemic corticosteroids in asthma because:', '["They have fewer systemic side effects","They are more effective","They are cheaper","They can be used in pregnancy","They cure asthma"]'::jsonb, 'They have fewer systemic side effects', 'Inhaled corticosteroids deliver medication directly to the airways with minimal systemic absorption.'),
('083dcc33-3a3a-5f20-8b0a-321e20a40e1e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Which test is used to diagnose pheochromocytoma?', '["Plasma free metanephrines","24-hour urinary catecholamines","Both A and B","Neither A nor B","Plasma cortisol"]'::jsonb, 'Both A and B', 'Plasma free metanephrines and 24-hour urinary catecholamines are used to diagnose pheochromocytoma.'),
('ea90eeeb-c2a1-510f-bfd9-c8585fd72a7b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Conn''s syndrome is characterized by:', '["Hypertension and hypokalemia","Hypotension and hyperkalemia","Normal blood pressure","Hyperglycemia","Hypothyroidism"]'::jsonb, 'Hypertension and hypokalemia', 'Conn''s syndrome (primary hyperaldosteronism) presents with hypertension and hypokalemia.'),
('09694a62-e048-5bbd-9dd8-25edfc7888dc'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Levonorgestrel emergency contraceptive is effective when taken within:', '["24 hours","48 hours","72 hours","120 hours","1 week"]'::jsonb, '72 hours', 'Levonorgestrel emergency contraceptive is most effective when taken within 72 hours of unprotected intercourse.'),
('6554dd2e-ff02-5317-97cc-ccd41a8132c4'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Which of the following is a GnRH agonist?', '["Leuprolide","Degarelix","Cyproterone","Bicalutamide","Flutamide"]'::jsonb, 'Leuprolide', 'Leuprolide is a GnRH agonist used for prostate cancer, endometriosis, and precocious puberty.'),
('bf4eb9e7-3d13-5192-ae85-ed8424563d82'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Zoledronic acid is administered:', '["Orally","Intravenously once yearly","Intramuscularly","Subcutaneously","Topically"]'::jsonb, 'Intravenously once yearly', 'Zoledronic acid is an intravenous bisphosphonate given once yearly for osteoporosis.'),
('79d3fbbd-9480-5c06-8857-23ccbfea3413'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Which of the following is a SERM used for osteoporosis?', '["Tamoxifen","Raloxifene","Clomiphene","Fulvestrant","Aromatase inhibitor"]'::jsonb, 'Raloxifene', 'Raloxifene is a SERM that reduces vertebral fracture risk in osteoporosis.'),
('1e8eedf6-c160-5195-87a8-c084345fbe62'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Which of the following is an oxytocin receptor agonist?', '["Atosiban","Carbetocin","Desmopressin","Vasopressin","Terlipressin"]'::jsonb, 'Carbetocin', 'Carbetocin is a long-acting oxytocin receptor agonist.'),
('842a8ca0-cc03-53d6-bb2b-bb079c8b6ab4'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Desmopressin nasal spray is used for:', '["Central diabetes insipidus","Bedwetting (nocturnal enuresis)","Hemophilia A","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Desmopressin nasal spray is used for central DI, nocturnal enuresis, and mild hemophilia A.'),
('9e7ce7ca-ff59-5732-9721-a0bb956e1e15'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Primary polydipsia is characterized by:', '["Excessive water intake causing dilute urine","ADH deficiency","ADH resistance","Increased thirst due to hyperglycemia","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Excessive water intake causing dilute urine', 'Primary polydipsia is excessive water intake that suppresses ADH, causing dilute urine.'),
('daffa349-3c78-54f1-a2fd-f5e0de2f8580'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'In nephrogenic diabetes insipidus, desmopressin will:', '["Concentrate urine","Have no effect on urine concentration","Increase urine output","Cause hyponatremia","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Have no effect on urine concentration', 'Desmopressin is ineffective in nephrogenic DI because the kidneys cannot respond to ADH.'),
('649aa1ca-ba43-5f3b-ab5c-947c7ce67abd'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Which of the following is a somatostatin analog?', '["Octreotide","Pasireotide","Lanreotide","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Octreotide, pasireotide, and lanreotide are all somatostatin analogs.'),
('c0d6f2e2-0afa-5ec2-87b5-95ff20adbb08'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Acromegaly is caused by excess secretion of:', '["ACTH resulting in decreased functional activity","GH (usually from pituitary adenoma)","TSH mediated by specific receptor activation","Prolactin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","FSH leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'GH (usually from pituitary adenoma)', 'Acromegaly is caused by excess growth hormone, usually from a pituitary adenoma.'),
('de079bcb-281d-5e14-b2ca-6f1369dabbbe'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Sheehan syndrome is postpartum pituitary necrosis caused by:', '["Hypotension and hemorrhage during delivery","Autoimmune destruction accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pituitary tumor mediated by specific receptor activation","Infection without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Radiation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hypotension and hemorrhage during delivery', 'Sheehan syndrome results from ischemic pituitary necrosis due to severe hypotension/hemorrhage during delivery.'),
('bc1a02cc-eefd-569d-a46d-6bee2e1f0ca2'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Which of the following is a dopamine agonist?', '["Cabergoline","Bromocriptine","Ropinirole","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Cabergoline, bromocriptine, and ropinirole are all dopamine agonists.'),
('a1affc43-810c-5f50-a3ec-3260350b570e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'In Cushing''s disease, the most common pituitary adenoma size is:', '["Microadenoma","Macroadenoma","Giant adenoma","They are all the same size","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Microadenoma', 'Most ACTH-secreting adenomas in Cushing''s disease are microadenomas (<10mm).'),
('e4716110-945d-5831-ac87-495df5f00543'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Inferior petrosal sinus sampling (IPSS) is used to:', '["Diagnose Cushing''s syndrome","Differentiate pituitary from ectopic ACTH","Treat pituitary adenomas","None of the above","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Differentiate pituitary from ectopic ACTH', 'IPSS differentiates pituitary from ectopic ACTH source in Cushing''s syndrome.'),
('9154cc19-8d17-552a-aaf8-2ad195f73225'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Ectopic ACTH syndrome is most commonly caused by:', '["Small cell lung cancer","Carcinoid tumors","Pheochromocytoma","Medullary thyroid carcinoma","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Small cell lung cancer', 'Small cell lung cancer is the most common cause of ectopic ACTH syndrome.'),
('792514dd-fade-56cf-b367-cbec00cbee0b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'In ACTH-dependent Cushing''s syndrome, ACTH levels are:', '["Low","Normal or elevated","Undetectable","Variable","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Normal or elevated', 'ACTH-dependent Cushing''s has normal or elevated ACTH (pituitary or ectopic source).'),
('20379f53-54a6-5d8e-9ea0-e37d45fdd5a3'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'In ACTH-independent Cushing''s syndrome, ACTH levels are:', '["Low or undetectable","Elevated","Normal","Variable","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Low or undetectable', 'ACTH-independent Cushing''s (adrenal source) has suppressed/undetectable ACTH.'),
('1f9fa8f6-cab3-523d-9471-821a4933ec58'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Pituitary apoplexy is a medical emergency characterized by:', '["Sudden headache, visual loss, and ophthalmoplegia","Gradual vision changes over months accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Chronic headache mediated by specific receptor activation","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sudden headache, visual loss, and ophthalmoplegia', 'Pituitary apoplexy is sudden hemorrhage/infarction in pituitary adenoma causing acute symptoms.'),
('a6047f18-0bb5-5207-ad9c-5184d7ee4236'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Treatment of pituitary apoplexy includes:', '["High-dose IV corticosteroids","Urgent surgical decompression","Both A and B","Neither A nor B","Observation only"]'::jsonb, 'Both A and B', 'Pituitary apoplexy requires IV steroids and urgent surgical decompression if visual loss occurs.'),
('8ee9b43e-11a0-53ad-8b23-4e5cf8add57c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'In primary adrenal insufficiency, hyperkalemia occurs because:', '["Aldosterone deficiency causes potassium retention","Cortisol deficiency causes potassium retention","ACTH excess causes potassium retention","None of the above","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Aldosterone deficiency causes potassium retention', 'Aldosterone deficiency impairs renal potassium excretion, causing hyperkalemia.'),
('8c28886e-cb9a-5fe5-ab2a-c8bbea64b389'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'In primary adrenal insufficiency, hyponatremia occurs because:', '["Aldosterone deficiency causes sodium wasting","Cortisol deficiency causes ADH excess (water retention)","Both A and B","Neither A nor B","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Both A and B', 'Hyponatremia in primary AI is due to both sodium wasting (aldosterone deficiency) and water retention (cortisol deficiency).'),
('b941b24f-bd7a-5db2-9149-28ed1c2f4025'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'In Addison''s disease, skin hyperpigmentation occurs because:', '["High ACTH and MSH (from POMC cleavage)","High cortisol accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Low aldosterone mediated by specific receptor activation","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'High ACTH and MSH (from POMC cleavage)', 'ACTH is derived from POMC, which also yields MSH, causing skin hyperpigmentation.'),
('3d2e4abb-a60f-5963-ba57-09af66ace312'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Tertiary adrenal insufficiency is most commonly caused by:', '["Autoimmune disease","Chronic exogenous glucocorticoid use","Pituitary surgery","None of the above","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Chronic exogenous glucocorticoid use', 'Chronic glucocorticoid use suppresses hypothalamic CRH, leading to tertiary AI upon withdrawal.'),
('f1ace544-7a91-5299-b267-8cde614efba5'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Congenital adrenal hyperplasia (CAH) is caused by:', '["Enzyme defects in cortisol synthesis","ACTH receptor mutations","Cortisol receptor mutations","None of the above","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Enzyme defects in cortisol synthesis', 'CAH is caused by autosomal recessive enzyme defects in adrenal steroidogenesis.'),
('60334f20-dd9a-528f-91ce-53a0e6ef59c5'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'In 21-hydroxylase deficiency, there is:', '["Deficient cortisol and aldosterone, excess androgens","Excess cortisol accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Deficient androgens mediated by specific receptor activation","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Deficient cortisol and aldosterone, excess androgens', '21-hydroxylase deficiency leads to cortisol and aldosterone deficiency with androgen excess.'),
('49a68be5-1163-5f8d-abe1-74af0e73b7dc'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'In 11-hydroxylase deficiency, there is:', '["Deficient cortisol, excess androgens and 11-deoxycorticosterone (hypertension)","Deficient cortisol and aldosterone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Excess cortisol mediated by specific receptor activation","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Deficient cortisol, excess androgens and 11-deoxycorticosterone (hypertension)', '11-hydroxylase deficiency causes cortisol deficiency, androgen excess, and hypertension from 11-deoxycorticosterone.'),
('39a7e6d8-14d8-5636-bc0b-d35bbfb507bd'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Sulfonylureas work by:', '["Stimulating insulin release from beta cells","Blocking insulin receptors","Inhibiting hepatic glucose production","Enhancing peripheral uptake","Inhibiting DPP-4"]'::jsonb, 'Stimulating insulin release from beta cells', 'Close K-ATP channels on beta cells -> depolarisation -> insulin release.'),
('9bd910b0-439c-56b9-93f3-c65cfb00dee5'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Apparent mineralocorticoid excess is caused by:', '["11-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase type 2 deficiency","Aldosterone excess accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cortisol excess mediated by specific receptor activation","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '11-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase type 2 deficiency', 'AME is caused by deficiency of 11-beta-HSD2, which normally inactivates cortisol in the kidney.'),
('17492f21-d96f-566f-be32-c9fb701435a8'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Glucocorticoid-remediable aldosteronism is caused by:', '["Chimeric CYP11B1/CYP11B2 gene","CYP21A2 mutation","CYP11B1 mutation","None of the above","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Chimeric CYP11B1/CYP11B2 gene', 'GRA is caused by a chimeric gene fusing CYP11B1 (ACTH-responsive) with CYP11B2 (aldosterone-producing).'),
('8b0ba2de-543f-5631-ad73-4fedaaa2bc67'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Which of the following is used for ovulation induction?', '["Clomiphene citrate","Letrozole","Gonadotropins","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Clomiphene, letrozole, and gonadotropins are all used for ovulation induction.'),
('713c135f-659d-52d8-b961-4e58298db702'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Etonogestrel implant (Nexplanon) provides contraception for:', '["1 month","3 months","3 years","5 years","10 years"]'::jsonb, '3 years', 'Etonogestrel implant provides contraception for up to 3 years.'),
('f4c3900a-e7d9-51ba-b170-8b592f9cf377'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Levonorgestrel IUS (Mirena) provides contraception and:', '["Reduces menorrhagia","Treats endometrial hyperplasia","Reduces dysmenorrhea","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Levonorgestrel IUS provides contraception, reduces heavy menstrual bleeding, and treats endometrial hyperplasia.'),
('8cd15ebf-c618-5896-9e58-248aea9ed264'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Which of the following is a GnRH antagonist?', '["Leuprolide","Degarelix","Goserelin","Triptorelin","Buserelin"]'::jsonb, 'Degarelix', 'Degarelix is a GnRH antagonist used in prostate cancer.'),
('6483e2d1-ba65-5455-8bed-039276dbe86a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Relugolix is a:', '["GnRH antagonist (oral)","GnRH agonist","5-alpha-reductase inhibitor","Antiandrogen","Aromatase inhibitor"]'::jsonb, 'GnRH antagonist (oral)', 'Relugolix is an oral GnRH antagonist approved for prostate cancer.'),
('714e9aaa-3e2c-50ef-9844-5829d5932413'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Bicalutamide is dosed at higher dose (150 mg) for:', '["Prostate cancer monotherapy","BPH accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Acne mediated by specific receptor activation","Hirsutism without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Prostate cancer monotherapy', 'Bicalutamide 150 mg daily is used as monotherapy in locally advanced prostate cancer.'),
('76336e6e-4d08-53ba-b399-1983686a9d36'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Nilutamide is a nonsteroidal antiandrogen used in:', '["Prostate cancer (with orchiectomy)","Breast cancer accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hirsutism mediated by specific receptor activation","Acne without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Prostate cancer (with orchiectomy)', 'Nilutamide is an antiandrogen used in combination with orchiectomy for metastatic prostate cancer.'),
('c4c520ec-f40f-5c87-ab0b-e5f1047a6bca'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Enzalutamide is a:', '["5-alpha-reductase inhibitor","CYP17A1 inhibitor","Androgen receptor antagonist","GnRH agonist","Aromatase inhibitor"]'::jsonb, 'Androgen receptor antagonist', 'Enzalutamide is a potent androgen receptor antagonist used in castration-resistant prostate cancer.'),
('9183da06-21e6-540f-9cf0-6a361dc1dcca'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Apalutamide is a:', '["5-alpha-reductase inhibitor","CYP17A1 inhibitor","Androgen receptor antagonist","GnRH agonist","Aromatase inhibitor"]'::jsonb, 'Androgen receptor antagonist', 'Apalutamide is an androgen receptor antagonist used in non-metastatic castration-resistant prostate cancer.'),
('4be84bdd-40ac-5ec7-8c75-c3fb5e58747d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Darolutamide is a:', '["5-alpha-reductase inhibitor","CYP17A1 inhibitor","Androgen receptor antagonist","GnRH agonist","Aromatase inhibitor"]'::jsonb, 'Androgen receptor antagonist', 'Darolutamide is a novel androgen receptor antagonist with less CNS penetration.'),
('c7fe7968-000f-5c10-9120-95d25ae5796b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Abiraterone is a:', '["5-alpha-reductase inhibitor","CYP17A1 inhibitor (androgen synthesis inhibitor)","Androgen receptor antagonist","GnRH agonist","Aromatase inhibitor"]'::jsonb, 'CYP17A1 inhibitor (androgen synthesis inhibitor)', 'Abiraterone inhibits CYP17A1, blocking androgen synthesis in testes, adrenal glands, and prostate tumors.'),
('059f13ce-ffc9-5afd-8f83-7673b1e0974f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Fulvestrant is a:', '["SERM resulting in decreased functional activity","Selective estrogen receptor downregulator (SERD)","Aromatase inhibitor mediated by specific receptor activation","GnRH agonist without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Progestin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Selective estrogen receptor downregulator (SERD)', 'Fulvestrant is a SERD that degrades estrogen receptors, used in ER-positive breast cancer.'),
('75fdaa5c-1061-5fd5-827b-fa563350145d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Toremifene is used in:', '["ER-positive breast cancer","Osteoporosis","Menopausal symptoms","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'ER-positive breast cancer', 'Toremifene is a SERM used in ER-positive metastatic breast cancer.'),
('58e74c56-f44e-5125-a2da-7b3f10dc8e5e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Ulipristal acetate is a selective progesterone receptor modulator used for:', '["Emergency contraception","Uterine fibroids","Endometriosis","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Ulipristal acetate is used for emergency contraception, fibroid management, and endometriosis.'),
('2a49f617-8461-5144-9fcd-74da10397efc'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrine System - Endocrine Drugs', 'Mifepristone (RU-486) is a:', '["Progesterone receptor antagonist","Glucocorticoid receptor antagonist","Both A and B","Neither A nor B","Estrogen receptor antagonist"]'::jsonb, 'Both A and B', 'Mifepristone is both a progesterone and glucocorticoid receptor antagonist.'),
('b806a1b7-1daf-5b52-a35a-a92b64994350'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Methotrexate acts primarily by inhibiting which enzyme?', '["Dihydrofolate reductase","Thymidylate synthase","DNA gyrase","Topoisomerase I","RNA polymerase"]'::jsonb, 'Dihydrofolate reductase', 'Methotrexate is a folate analogue that competitively inhibits dihydrofolate reductase (DHFR), preventing conversion of dihydrofolate to tetrahydrofolate needed for thymidylate and purine synthesis.'),
('bfd31c5a-2817-529e-8bbb-d38ca3159028'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'The antidote for methotrexate toxicity is:', '["Mesna","Leucovorin (folinic acid)","N-acetylcysteine","Dexrazoxane","Fomepizole"]'::jsonb, 'Leucovorin (folinic acid)', 'Leucovorin (folinic acid) bypasses the DHFR block by providing reduced folates directly and is used as rescue therapy after high-dose methotrexate.'),
('b01f75b0-8f87-5e7a-a476-a4e6406634e1'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Methotrexate is contraindicated in which condition?', '["Rheumatoid arthritis","Psoriasis","Pregnancy","Asthma","Hypertension"]'::jsonb, 'Pregnancy', 'Methotrexate is teratogenic and abortifacient, and is absolutely contraindicated in pregnancy.'),
('63341a22-2c9c-5948-b00e-d49fcab329af'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', '5-Fluorouracil (5-FU) inhibits which enzyme in pyrimidine synthesis?', '["Thymidylate synthase","Dihydrofolate reductase","Ribonucleotide reductase","Xanthine oxidase","Adenosine deaminase"]'::jsonb, 'Thymidylate synthase', '5-FU is converted to FdUMP, which forms a ternary complex with thymidylate synthase and N5,N10-methylene-THF, inhibiting conversion of dUMP to dTMP.'),
('f56d9e39-0a91-5405-a143-edefda908f2a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Palmar-plantar erythrodysesthesia (hand-foot syndrome) is characteristic of:', '["Cyclophosphamide resulting in decreased functional activity","Vincristine accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","5-Fluorouracil (and capecitabine)","Methotrexate without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bleomycin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '5-Fluorouracil (and capecitabine)', 'Hand-foot syndrome is a distinctive toxicity of 5-FU and its oral prodrug capecitabine, characterized by redness, swelling, and pain of palms and soles.'),
('637e30b7-fd3a-58a7-97c6-4e23055f2007'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Cytarabine (Ara-C) is primarily used in the treatment of:', '["Breast cancer","Acute myeloid leukemia (AML)","Testicular cancer","Thyroid cancer","Renal cell carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 'Acute myeloid leukemia (AML)', 'Cytarabine is a cornerstone of AML induction and consolidation therapy. It inhibits DNA polymerase and is most effective in S-phase.'),
('8587ba9d-9030-5966-9653-4e24a7f14509'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'The major dose-limiting toxicity of cytarabine is:', '["Cardiotoxicity resulting in decreased functional activity","Nephrotoxicity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Myelosuppression (bone marrow suppression)","Pulmonary fibrosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Neurotoxicity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Myelosuppression (bone marrow suppression)', 'Cytarabine causes severe myelosuppression as its dose-limiting toxicity. High doses can also cause cerebellar toxicity.'),
('b3413a62-559b-5054-8aa7-73df9e4d74db'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Gemcitabine is a deoxycytidine analogue that inhibits:', '["RNA polymerase","Ribonucleotide reductase","DNA topoisomerase II","Thymidylate synthase","Dihydrofolate reductase"]'::jsonb, 'Ribonucleotide reductase', 'Gemcitabine is incorporated into DNA causing chain termination and also inhibits ribonucleotide reductase, depleting deoxynucleotide pools.'),
('5fdbbadb-0f46-5e58-b5b1-9243df47408b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Pemetrexed inhibits multiple folate-dependent enzymes including:', '["Thymidylate synthase, DHFR, and GARFT","Only dihydrofolate reductase","Only thymidylate synthase","RNA polymerase only","Topoisomerase I only"]'::jsonb, 'Thymidylate synthase, DHFR, and GARFT', 'Pemetrexed is a multitargeted antifolate that inhibits TS, DHFR, and GARFT, blocking both pyrimidine and purine synthesis.'),
('e44e2279-443d-5bee-8292-27848ae2f527'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Pemetrexed is most commonly used for which malignancy?', '["Colon cancer resulting in decreased functional activity","Mesothelioma and non-small cell lung cancer (non-squamous)","Ovarian cancer mediated by specific receptor activation","Glioblastoma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hepatocellular carcinoma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mesothelioma and non-small cell lung cancer (non-squamous)', 'Pemetrexed is the standard of care for malignant pleural mesothelioma (with cisplatin) and is used in non-squamous NSCLC.'),
('497510db-1b68-518d-9a25-5df0cf72ee09'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Vitamin supplementation required before pemetrexed:', '["Vitamin B12 and folic acid","Vitamin C only","Vitamin D only","Vitamin K only","Vitamin B6 only"]'::jsonb, 'Vitamin B12 and folic acid', 'Folic acid and vitamin B12 supplementation is mandatory before and during pemetrexed therapy to reduce myelosuppression, mucositis, and skin rash.'),
('a390ef91-7f53-5ef1-ae3b-dfa0f2d45dcc'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'High-dose methotrexate can cause which renal complication?', '["Nephrotic syndrome resulting in decreased functional activity","Acute kidney injury due to crystal precipitation in renal tubules","Renal artery stenosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Chronic interstitial nephritis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Renal papillary necrosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Acute kidney injury due to crystal precipitation in renal tubules', 'High-dose methotrexate can precipitate in acidic urine within renal tubules, causing AKI. Alkalinization and hydration prevent this.'),
('d451b437-3b4b-5fc7-8a1b-a557e6ea5d8d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Capecitabine is an oral prodrug converted to which active metabolite?', '["Methotrexate","5-Fluorouracil","Cytarabine","Gemcitabine","Pemetrexed"]'::jsonb, '5-Fluorouracil', 'Capecitabine is preferentially converted to 5-FU in tumor tissue by thymidine phosphorylase, which is more highly expressed in tumors.'),
('e9d26dda-3cb4-5dbe-8857-3353f543821b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Which antimetabolite is used as a disease-modifying agent in rheumatoid arthritis?', '["5-Fluorouracil","Methotrexate","Cytarabine","Gemcitabine","Pemetrexed"]'::jsonb, 'Methotrexate', 'Low-dose weekly methotrexate is the anchor drug in rheumatoid arthritis with anti-inflammatory properties through increased adenosine release.'),
('aeca1ed9-a632-50bf-86e9-4a51ea2fdd7a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Which of the following is NOT an antimetabolite?', '["Methotrexate","5-Fluorouracil","Cyclophosphamide","Gemcitabine","Pemetrexed"]'::jsonb, 'Cyclophosphamide', 'Cyclophosphamide is an alkylating agent (nitrogen mustard derivative), not an antimetabolite.'),
('41e59818-346c-57c1-b3ec-e321abc1cd5b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'The mechanism of action of cytarabine involves:', '["Inhibition of DHFR resulting in decreased functional activity","Inhibition of DNA polymerase and incorporation into DNA","Alkylation of DNA bases mediated by specific receptor activation","Inhibition of topoisomerase I without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Microtubule disruption leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibition of DNA polymerase and incorporation into DNA', 'Cytarabine (Ara-C) is phosphorylated to Ara-CTP which inhibits DNA polymerase and is incorporated into DNA, causing chain termination (S-phase specific).'),
('fd1f9090-5a83-59da-b928-5b4456fdcec9'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Mucositis is a common dose-limiting toxicity of which class?', '["Antimetabolites","Vinca alkaloids","Platinum compounds only","Monoclonal antibodies","Hormonal agents"]'::jsonb, 'Antimetabolites', 'Antimetabolites (5-FU, methotrexate, capecitabine) commonly cause mucositis because they affect rapidly dividing mucosal cells.'),
('7055d305-f628-5d18-a2bf-ca3a7c76b2a3'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Topical 5-fluorouracil is used for which skin condition?', '["Melanoma resulting in decreased functional activity","Actinic keratoses and superficial basal cell carcinoma","Invasive squamous cell carcinoma mediated by specific receptor activation","Dermatofibrosarcoma protuberans without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Kaposi sarcoma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Actinic keratoses and superficial basal cell carcinoma', 'Topical 5-FU (5% cream) is widely used for actinic keratoses, superficial basal cell carcinoma, and Bowen disease.'),
('917aa4d7-0295-54f2-9b12-c64047905a06'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'TMP-SMX should be avoided with methotrexate because:', '["It reduces MTX absorption","It competes for renal tubular secretion and increases MTX toxicity","It enhances MTX efficacy too much","It causes pharmacodynamic antagonism","It converts MTX to inactive form"]'::jsonb, 'It competes for renal tubular secretion and increases MTX toxicity', 'Both TMP-SMX and MTX are secreted by the organic acid transport system. Co-administration reduces renal clearance of MTX.'),
('c6df5355-c67e-51eb-a8b8-97676fecc954'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Gemcitabine + cisplatin is a standard regimen for which cancer?', '["Ovarian cancer","Bladder (urothelial) cancer","Prostate cancer","Thyroid cancer","Melanoma"]'::jsonb, 'Bladder (urothelial) cancer', 'Gemcitabine plus cisplatin (GC) is a standard first-line chemotherapy regimen for advanced/metastatic urothelial cancer.'),
('27cdcb21-ecb9-5ec3-affc-52c7134d35bd'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Chronic low-dose methotrexate causes which hepatic toxicity?', '["Hepatic adenoma","Hepatic fibrosis and cirrhosis","Hemochromatosis","Budd-Chiari syndrome","Peliosis hepatis"]'::jsonb, 'Hepatic fibrosis and cirrhosis', 'Chronic low-dose methotrexate can cause hepatic steatosis, fibrosis, and cirrhosis. Regular LFT monitoring is recommended.'),
('173b7a05-3339-5970-ab28-e81b0438a60c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Cyclophosphamide is a prodrug that requires activation by which organ?', '["Kidneys","Liver (cytochrome P450)","Lungs","Spleen","Bone marrow"]'::jsonb, 'Liver (cytochrome P450)', 'Cyclophosphamide is an inactive prodrug requiring hepatic CYP450-mediated oxidation to form 4-hydroxycyclophosphamide, which decomposes to active phosphoramide mustard.'),
('eb98b813-9e2d-53c1-9e0c-d5f4bd7bcaa1'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Hemorrhagic cystitis is a characteristic toxicity of:', '["Methotrexate resulting in decreased functional activity","Cyclophosphamide and ifosfamide","Vincristine mediated by specific receptor activation","Doxorubicin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cisplatin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cyclophosphamide and ifosfamide', 'Cyclophosphamide and ifosfamide produce acrolein as a toxic metabolite, excreted in urine causing hemorrhagic cystitis. Mesna is the uroprotective agent.'),
('fcf859f2-a350-58b9-9af5-e70b4ad78f23'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'The uroprotective agent for cyclophosphamide-induced hemorrhagic cystitis is:', '["Leucovorin resulting in decreased functional activity","Mesna (2-mercaptoethane sulfonate sodium)","Dexrazoxane mediated by specific receptor activation","Amifostine without affecting the primary tissue pathway","N-acetylcysteine leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mesna (2-mercaptoethane sulfonate sodium)', 'Mesna binds to acrolein in the urinary tract, forming a non-toxic conjugate, preventing hemorrhagic cystitis.'),
('2a0920e9-f733-57e9-b6b8-252d02150fee'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Which alkylating agent is used as oral maintenance therapy for CLL?', '["Cyclophosphamide","Chlorambucil","Ifosfamide","Busulfan","Procarbazine"]'::jsonb, 'Chlorambucil', 'Chlorambucil is an oral nitrogen mustard traditionally used for CLL, well-tolerated in elderly patients.'),
('e505b46e-d9fe-59e8-a4b0-1c3afe9aea5e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Temozolomide is the drug of choice for which brain tumor?', '["Meningioma","Glioblastoma multiforme","Pituitary adenoma","Vestibular schwannoma","Craniopharyngioma"]'::jsonb, 'Glioblastoma multiforme', 'Temozolomide is the standard of care for glioblastoma, used with concurrent and adjuvant radiotherapy (Stupp protocol).'),
('de635a5f-a932-5cb2-b322-f99b83265382'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Temozolomide methylation of which DNA position is associated with better response?', '["O6-methylguanine","N7-methylguanine","N3-methyladenine","N1-methyladenine","N2-methylguanine"]'::jsonb, 'O6-methylguanine', 'Tumors with methylated MGMT promoter respond better because they cannot repair the O6-methylguanine lesion produced by temozolomide.'),
('c02f84a8-ec03-544e-be04-c17a975ef18e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Ifosfamide differs from cyclophosphamide primarily by:', '["Being administered IV only","Requiring different activating enzyme","Greater neurotoxicity and requiring mesna for uroprotection","Not causing myelosuppression","Being a non-alkylating agent"]'::jsonb, 'Greater neurotoxicity and requiring mesna for uroprotection', 'Ifosfamide has greater neurotoxicity (encephalopathy) and always requires mesna co-administration.'),
('8e3927b5-ee4a-57b2-8343-5048ede5c341'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Melphalan is primarily used in the treatment of:', '["Lung cancer","Multiple myeloma","Colon cancer","Pancreatic cancer","Thyroid cancer"]'::jsonb, 'Multiple myeloma', 'Melphalan (L-phenylalanine mustard) is a key agent in multiple myeloma, often combined with prednisone (MP regimen).'),
('c956f7c1-581d-567b-be15-0fc8c2781ec7'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'The major dose-limiting toxicity of most alkylating agents is:', '["Nephrotoxicity resulting in decreased functional activity","Myelosuppression (bone marrow suppression)","Pulmonary fibrosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Neurotoxicity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cardiotoxicity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Myelosuppression (bone marrow suppression)', 'Myelosuppression is the dose-limiting toxicity of most alkylating agents. WBCs are usually the earliest affected.'),
('a8e22e08-f0d2-582e-9caa-3d6cb567de35'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'High-dose cyclophosphamide can cause which cardiac toxicity?', '["Dilated cardiomyopathy","Hemorrhagic myocarditis","Pericarditis","Valvular heart disease","Coronary artery spasm"]'::jsonb, 'Hemorrhagic myocarditis', 'High-dose cyclophosphamide can cause hemorrhagic myocarditis and cardiac failure, especially with prior radiation/anthracyclines.'),
('737e1f17-998e-5b09-a367-59083708694f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Busulfan causes which characteristic skin discoloration?', '["Yellow","Blue-green (bronze)","Red","Purple","Black"]'::jsonb, 'Blue-green (bronze)', 'Busulfan can cause bronze/blue-green discoloration of the skin and is also associated with pulmonary fibrosis (busulfan lung).'),
('2fd0aaa8-fa5c-5f75-a398-2cd8f1c1d797'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Alkylating agents are classified as:', '["Cell cycle specific (CCS)","Cell cycle non-specific (CCNS)","Phase-specific only (G1)","Phase-specific only (S)","Phase-specific only (M)"]'::jsonb, 'Cell cycle non-specific (CCNS)', 'Alkylating agents are cell cycle non-specific, killing cells in any phase including resting G0 cells.'),
('ce0f264b-3793-558a-ad78-01a6cc716a55'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'The mechanism of action of alkylating agents involves:', '["Inhibition of topoisomerase","Covalent binding (alkylation) of DNA causing cross-linking","Disruption of microtubule assembly","Intercalation between DNA base pairs","Inhibition of RNA polymerase"]'::jsonb, 'Covalent binding (alkylation) of DNA causing cross-linking', 'Alkylating agents transfer alkyl groups to DNA (primarily N7 of guanine), forming intrastrand and interstrand cross-links.'),
('abd3133b-9423-53ec-8735-0a000e4f1c26'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Cyclophosphamide is also used as immunosuppressant in:', '["Type 1 diabetes resulting in decreased functional activity","Severe lupus nephritis and granulomatosis with polyangiitis","Hypertension mediated by specific receptor activation","Asthma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Osteoarthritis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Severe lupus nephritis and granulomatosis with polyangiitis', 'Cyclophosphamide is used in severe lupus nephritis, granulomatosis with polyangiitis, and severe rheumatoid vasculitis.'),
('e5c9e2eb-9c30-556f-8947-a62faf0dd6b7'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Secondary malignancy (AML/MDS) is a long-term risk of which class?', '["Antimetabolites","Alkylating agents","Vinca alkaloids","Taxanes","Platinum compounds"]'::jsonb, 'Alkylating agents', 'Alkylating agents cause dose-related, delayed-onset secondary malignancies (AML/MDS), typically 2-10 years after treatment.'),
('e6ce14b6-267e-5375-9026-18f67ed896eb'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'BuCy (busulfan + cyclophosphamide) is used for:', '["Breast cancer resulting in decreased functional activity","Myeloablative conditioning before bone marrow transplant","Lung cancer mediated by specific receptor activation","Colon cancer without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ovarian cancer leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Myeloablative conditioning before bone marrow transplant', 'BuCy is a classic myeloablative conditioning regimen before allogeneic bone marrow transplant.'),
('ecc7bb96-4402-5c8b-ac92-018c49be1e01'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Ifosfamide encephalopathy is caused by which metabolite?', '["Acrolein","Chloroacetaldehyde","Phosphoramide mustard","4-hydroxycyclophosphamide","Carboxyphosphamide"]'::jsonb, 'Chloroacetaldehyde', 'Ifosfamide produces chloroacetaldehyde as a neurotoxic metabolite (unlike cyclophosphamide), causing encephalopathy.'),
('3bcabcb9-7700-5038-8033-08cd91e68036'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Bendamustine has properties of both an alkylating agent and:', '["Antimetabolite (purine-like benzimidazole ring)","Antitumor antibiotic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Vinca alkaloid mediated by specific receptor activation","Taxane without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Platinum compound leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Antimetabolite (purine-like benzimidazole ring)', 'Bendamustine is a unique nitrogen mustard with a benzimidazole ring, giving it properties of both an alkylating agent and antimetabolite.'),
('11df235e-110a-5e57-86f5-6d62baf9213e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Temozolomide is an oral prodrug of which active metabolite?', '["MTIC (3-methyl-(triazen-1-yl)imidazole-4-carboxamide)","Phosphoramide mustard accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Acrolein mediated by specific receptor activation","Chloroacetaldehyde without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Melphalan leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'MTIC (3-methyl-(triazen-1-yl)imidazole-4-carboxamide)', 'Temozolomide is an imidazotetrazine prodrug that spontaneously hydrolyzes to MTIC, the active alkylating species.'),
('61363462-bd0b-5f6e-b308-ca346ec83ea0'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Lomustine (CCNU) is notable for:', '["Good CNS penetration","Causing nephrotoxicity","Causing peripheral neuropathy","Causing cardiotoxicity","Being administered IV only"]'::jsonb, 'Good CNS penetration', 'Lomustine is a lipophilic nitrosourea with excellent CNS penetration, used for brain tumors and Hodgkin lymphoma.'),
('70fcd7fa-a135-5efe-89b8-1da6cd0998e8'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Mechlorethamine (nitrogen mustard) is used topically for:', '["Melanoma resulting in decreased functional activity","Early-stage mycosis fungoides (CTCL)","Basal cell carcinoma mediated by specific receptor activation","Psoriasis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Actinic keratoses leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Early-stage mycosis fungoides (CTCL)', 'Mechlorethamine is used topically for early-stage mycosis fungoides (cutaneous T-cell lymphoma).'),
('a9e2d149-31c7-581b-987b-0cd084319d01'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Busulfan + cyclophosphamide conditioning is used primarily for:', '["Solid tumors resulting in decreased functional activity","Leukemias and lymphomas before BMT","Brain tumors mediated by specific receptor activation","Lung cancer without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Breast cancer leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Leukemias and lymphomas before BMT', 'BuCy is a myeloablative conditioning regimen used before allogeneic BMT for leukemias (AML, CML) and lymphomas.'),
('c885c367-6b43-56e8-85e4-80a345d24d3d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Cyclophosphamide causes which reproductive toxicity in males?', '["Permanent sterility always","Azoospermia (may be reversible with time)","No reproductive effects","Only affects libido","Only affects testosterone"]'::jsonb, 'Azoospermia (may be reversible with time)', 'Cyclophosphamide causes azoospermia in males, which may be reversible months to years after stopping therapy. Sperm banking should be offered.'),
('47f96920-8330-5366-a17e-5bffebe4ab02'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Doxorubicin''s dose-limiting toxicity is:', '["Nephrotoxicity resulting in decreased functional activity","Cumulative cardiotoxicity (dilated cardiomyopathy)","Pulmonary fibrosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Hepatotoxicity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Neurotoxicity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cumulative cardiotoxicity (dilated cardiomyopathy)', 'Doxorubicin causes dose-dependent, cumulative cardiotoxicity leading to dilated cardiomyopathy. Lifetime dose limit is 450-550 mg/m2.'),
('335bb683-e403-55be-8ac1-52d4e7e5295c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Dexrazoxane protects against doxorubicin cardiotoxicity by:', '["Inhibiting topoisomerase II","Chelating iron and preventing free radical formation in the heart","Enhancing doxorubicin clearance","Increasing doxorubicin uptake into tumors","Blocking DNA intercalation"]'::jsonb, 'Chelating iron and preventing free radical formation in the heart', 'Dexrazoxane is an iron chelator that removes iron from cardiac myocytes, preventing iron-mediated free radical formation.'),
('0e0d53a9-a65a-578b-8fa2-dfe07fbcc4f2'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Bleomycin''s major dose-limiting toxicity is:', '["Nephrotoxicity","Pulmonary fibrosis","Cardiotoxicity","Hepatotoxicity","Myelosuppression"]'::jsonb, 'Pulmonary fibrosis', 'Bleomycin causes pulmonary fibrosis as its dose-limiting toxicity, cumulative and dose-dependent (risk increases above 400 units).'),
('3ac023b2-2824-5f05-a287-8f2032376807'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Bleomycin is most commonly used for:', '["Breast cancer","Testicular cancer and Hodgkin lymphoma","Colon cancer","Pancreatic cancer","Hepatocellular carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 'Testicular cancer and Hodgkin lymphoma', 'Bleomycin is a key component of BEP for testicular cancer and ABVD for Hodgkin lymphoma.'),
('9bd4e92f-1a9f-57af-b176-b139b759a5fb'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Bleomycin produces free radicals by:', '["Iron-mediated Fenton reaction after binding Fe2+","Cytochrome P450 activation","Direct DNA intercalation","Inhibition of superoxide dismutase","Xanthine oxidase activation"]'::jsonb, 'Iron-mediated Fenton reaction after binding Fe2+', 'Bleomycin binds Fe2+ and reacts with O2 to produce superoxide and hydroxyl radicals, causing DNA strand breaks.'),
('bba943dc-d7cf-5c02-a4b1-4516e6f9fb41'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Bleomycin pulmonary toxicity is increased by all EXCEPT:', '["High oxygen concentration (FiO2 >25%)","Concurrent chest radiation","Renal impairment","High cumulative dose (>400 units)","Concurrent leucovorin"]'::jsonb, 'Concurrent leucovorin', 'Bleomycin pulmonary toxicity is increased by high-dose oxygen, chest radiation, renal failure, and high cumulative dose. Leucovorin does not affect it.'),
('6c4b88b7-67e3-5a3c-a605-32e4f26a1f97'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Dactinomycin (actinomycin D) is used primarily in:', '["Breast cancer resulting in decreased functional activity","Gestational trophoblastic disease and Wilms tumor","Lung cancer mediated by specific receptor activation","Pancreatic cancer without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Colon cancer leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Gestational trophoblastic disease and Wilms tumor', 'Dactinomycin intercalates DNA and is used in gestational trophoblastic disease, Wilms tumor, and rhabdomyosarcoma.'),
('f6b2b1fb-390f-528e-9bd0-b8a72561d023'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Mitomycin C is used topically in ophthalmology for:', '["Cataract surgery resulting in decreased functional activity","Glaucoma filtration surgery (to prevent scarring)","Corneal transplant rejection mediated by specific receptor activation","Diabetic retinopathy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Conjunctivitis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Glaucoma filtration surgery (to prevent scarring)', 'Mitomycin C is applied during glaucoma filtration surgery to inhibit fibroblast proliferation and prevent scarring.'),
('ccb1ca85-f761-5337-a499-b4545f32dc8c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Mitomycin C can cause which hematologic toxicity?', '["Polycythemia resulting in decreased functional activity","Microangiopathic hemolytic anemia (MAHA) with thrombocytopenia","Sickle cell crisis mediated by specific receptor activation","Megaloblastic anemia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Aplastic anemia only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Microangiopathic hemolytic anemia (MAHA) with thrombocytopenia', 'Mitomycin C can cause thrombotic microangiopathy (TMA) with MAHA and thrombocytopenia, similar to TTP/HUS.'),
('490b2175-4f70-5b2a-a126-637b96df1dfa'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Doxorubicin must NEVER be administered via which route?', '["Intravenous","Intravesical","Intrathecal","Intra-arterial","Subcutaneous"]'::jsonb, 'Intrathecal', 'Doxorubicin must NEVER be given intrathecal - accidental intrathecal injection has been fatal.'),
('23b3bfb4-e632-5945-9f81-5950f80f34ff'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'The red urine after doxorubicin administration is due to:', '["Hematuria resulting in decreased functional activity","The red-orange color of the drug being excreted in urine","Rhabdomyolysis mediated by specific receptor activation","Hemolytic anemia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hepatotoxicity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The red-orange color of the drug being excreted in urine', 'Doxorubicin has a characteristic red-orange color that causes urine to turn red/orange after administration. This is normal drug excretion.'),
('67a6823b-25ba-5f16-bb77-d7607d6dbdee'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Flagellate skin pigmentation is a characteristic toxicity of:', '["Doxorubicin","Bleomycin","Vincristine","Methotrexate","Cisplatin"]'::jsonb, 'Bleomycin', 'Bleomycin causes distinctive flagellate (whip-like) hyperpigmentation of the skin, particularly in areas of friction.'),
('f081be8c-c2b4-5ad7-96f2-0b4511105b65'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Doxorubicin is also known by which trade name?', '["Cytoxan","Adriamycin","Velban","Platinol","Taxol"]'::jsonb, 'Adriamycin', 'Doxorubicin is commonly known as Adriamycin. Cyclophosphamide=Cytoxan, vinblastine=Velban, cisplatin=Platinol, paclitaxel=Taxol.'),
('4b48f2c3-5812-51cb-86d2-3074b22c3258'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'DLCO monitoring during bleomycin therapy is important because:', '["It measures kidney function resulting in decreased functional activity","It is the most sensitive early indicator of bleomycin pulmonary toxicity","It measures liver function mediated by specific receptor activation","It measures cardiac function without affecting the primary tissue pathway","It measures bone marrow reserve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'It is the most sensitive early indicator of bleomycin pulmonary toxicity', 'DLCO is the most sensitive early indicator of bleomycin pulmonary toxicity. Treatment should stop if DLCO drops >20-30% from baseline.'),
('bad41861-6527-5531-8284-43f189fa275b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'PEGylated liposomal doxorubicin was developed to:', '["Increase efficacy resulting in decreased functional activity","Reduce cardiotoxicity and alter pharmacokinetics","Reduce cost mediated by specific receptor activation","Allow oral administration without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Eliminate alopecia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Reduce cardiotoxicity and alter pharmacokinetics', 'PEGylated liposomal doxorubicin reduces cardiotoxicity and enhances tumor delivery through the EPR effect.'),
('375bd6eb-5a6b-5aa2-9f76-f1e12825dc64'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Hand-foot syndrome is MORE common with which doxorubicin formulation?', '["Conventional doxorubicin","PEGylated liposomal doxorubicin","Both equally","Neither causes it","Only at very high doses"]'::jsonb, 'PEGylated liposomal doxorubicin', 'Hand-foot syndrome is more common with PEGylated liposomal doxorubicin, but cardiotoxicity is significantly reduced.'),
('d00d863d-45f4-5d9a-90ea-81c47e291bad'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Epirubicin differs from doxorubicin in that it:', '["Is not an anthracycline resulting in decreased functional activity","Has 4''-epimer of daunosamine sugar, allowing higher cumulative doses","Causes more cardiotoxicity mediated by specific receptor activation","Is not used in breast cancer without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Has a different mechanism leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Has 4''-epimer of daunosamine sugar, allowing higher cumulative doses', 'Epirubicin differs from doxorubicin only in the 4''-hydroxyl configuration on the sugar, allowing faster elimination and less cardiotoxicity.'),
('ec024bef-e5e7-5717-9ab0-ccbf61914109'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Mitoxantrone differs from doxorubicin by:', '["Being an anthracenedione with less cardiotoxicity","Being a completely different drug class","Causing more pulmonary fibrosis","Being only for solid tumors","Not intercalating DNA"]'::jsonb, 'Being an anthracenedione with less cardiotoxicity', 'Mitoxantrone is an anthracenedione with less cardiotoxicity, used in AML and metastatic breast cancer.'),
('0c59187c-510b-5443-b76e-19887c2b3471'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Vincristine''s major dose-limiting toxicity is:', '["Myelosuppression","Peripheral neuropathy (dose-limiting)","Nephrotoxicity","Cardiotoxicity","Pulmonary fibrosis"]'::jsonb, 'Peripheral neuropathy (dose-limiting)', 'Vincristine causes dose-dependent peripheral neuropathy (sensorimotor) as its dose-limiting toxicity.'),
('56765f6a-16d0-561d-a087-175ad96496c2'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Vincristine and vinblastine are derived from which plant?', '["Pacific yew tree","Periwinkle plant (Catharanthus roseus)","Mayapple (Podophyllum peltatum)","Foxglove","Opium poppy"]'::jsonb, 'Periwinkle plant (Catharanthus roseus)', 'Vinca alkaloids are derived from the Madagascar periwinkle plant (Catharanthus roseus).'),
('d6cd4fcf-efe5-5c33-b5e0-3a685f2bc21d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Vinblastine''s dose-limiting toxicity differs from vincristine because:', '["Vinblastine causes more neurotoxicity","Vinblastine causes more myelosuppression (leukopenia)","Vinblastine causes more nephrotoxicity","Vinblastine has no dose-limiting toxicity","Vinblastine causes more cardiac toxicity"]'::jsonb, 'Vinblastine causes more myelosuppression (leukopenia)', 'While vincristine''s DLT is neuropathy, vinblastine''s DLT is myelosuppression (leukopenia). This is an important clinical distinction.'),
('b9fe0ec5-84a4-54a3-a815-efb6cc0d7b61'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Paclitaxel belongs to which class?', '["Vinca alkaloids","Taxanes (microtubule stabilizers)","Epipodophyllotoxins","Alkylating agents","Antimetabolites"]'::jsonb, 'Taxanes (microtubule stabilizers)', 'Paclitaxel is a taxane that stabilizes microtubules, preventing depolymerization. This is opposite to vinca alkaloids.'),
('6bf9e891-246d-5380-8335-493bcaafdfbd'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Paclitaxel requires premedication because of:', '["Nephrotoxicity resulting in decreased functional activity","Hypersensitivity/anaphylaxis reactions (Cremophor EL vehicle)","Hepatotoxicity mediated by specific receptor activation","Pulmonary fibrosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cerebellar ataxia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hypersensitivity/anaphylaxis reactions (Cremophor EL vehicle)', 'Paclitaxel (especially Cremophor EL formulation) causes severe hypersensitivity. Premedication with dexamethasone, diphenhydramine, and H2-blockers is mandatory.'),
('942dd498-c379-5cae-8e7a-63155bd51eaa'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Etoposide inhibits which topoisomerase enzyme?', '["Topoisomerase I","Topoisomerase II","Topoisomerase III","DNA gyrase","RNA polymerase"]'::jsonb, 'Topoisomerase II', 'Etoposide (VP-16) inhibits topoisomerase II by stabilizing the DNA-enzyme cleavable complex, causing double-strand breaks.'),
('658c3a91-3fab-523e-9049-ab73a59e4ffd'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Etoposide is a key component of which regimen for testicular cancer?', '["CHOP resulting in decreased functional activity","BEP (bleomycin, etoposide, cisplatin)","ABVD mediated by specific receptor activation","FOLFOX without affecting the primary tissue pathway","MVAC leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'BEP (bleomycin, etoposide, cisplatin)', 'BEP is the standard curative regimen for metastatic testicular cancer, achieving >90% cure rates.'),
('1ed316f3-09da-5a9a-9d75-182955711f10'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Irinotecan inhibits which topoisomerase?', '["Topoisomerase I","Topoisomerase II","Topoisomerase III","DNA gyrase","Reverse transcriptase"]'::jsonb, 'Topoisomerase I', 'Irinotecan (CPT-11) is a camptothecin derivative that inhibits topoisomerase I. It is a prodrug converted to SN-38.'),
('1589e49d-797a-5c10-a94f-1381da6eab02'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Irinotecan causes which characteristic early syndrome?', '["Alopecia resulting in decreased functional activity","Acute cholinergic syndrome (diarrhea, salivation)","Peripheral neuropathy mediated by specific receptor activation","Cardiotoxicity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Nephrotoxicity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Acute cholinergic syndrome (diarrhea, salivation)', 'Irinotecan causes early-onset cholinergic syndrome with diarrhea, cramping, and salivation. Atropine is used for acute cholinergic syndrome.'),
('367e1f90-de13-5527-89e6-481bae38c33e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Topotecan inhibits which enzyme and is used for:', '["Topoisomerase I; ovarian cancer and SCLC","Topoisomerase II; testicular cancer","DHFR; breast cancer","Thymidylate synthase; colon cancer","Ribonucleotide reductase; pancreatic cancer"]'::jsonb, 'Topoisomerase I; ovarian cancer and SCLC', 'Topotecan is a camptothecin derivative inhibiting topoisomerase I, used for relapsed ovarian cancer and SCLC.'),
('8b097eba-c466-5fba-8ccc-bb54dfca12b5'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Vinca alkaloids work by:', '["Stabilizing microtubules","Inhibiting microtubule polymerization (assembly)","Disrupting DNA replication","Inhibiting protein synthesis","Inhibiting RNA transcription"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibiting microtubule polymerization (assembly)', 'Vinca alkaloids bind tubulin and inhibit its polymerization into microtubules, disrupting the mitotic spindle and arresting cells in metaphase.'),
('d85121dc-0d24-5adc-be69-4ca383045074'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Paclitaxel stabilizes microtubules, leading to:', '["Inhibition of mitotic spindle disassembly and M phase arrest","Enhanced microtubule depolymerization","Increased DNA synthesis","Enhanced RNA transcription","Inhibition of protein synthesis"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibition of mitotic spindle disassembly and M phase arrest', 'Paclitaxel hyperstabilizes microtubules, preventing normal disassembly needed for mitotic spindle function. Cells arrest in M phase.'),
('c6e76cc6-3ee0-5983-a0cb-5956206798e2'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'The dose-limiting toxicity of paclitaxel is:', '["Nephrotoxicity resulting in decreased functional activity","Neutropenia (dose-limiting) and peripheral neuropathy","Cardiotoxicity mediated by specific receptor activation","Pulmonary fibrosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hepatotoxicity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Neutropenia (dose-limiting) and peripheral neuropathy', 'Paclitaxel''s DLT is neutropenia. Peripheral neuropathy is also common and dose-limiting in some protocols.'),
('efe2f12d-695b-598a-8837-aa35369b5e40'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Vincristine should NEVER be given intrathecally because:', '["It causes seizures resulting in decreased functional activity","It causes fatal ascending demyelinating neuropathy","It causes blindness mediated by specific receptor activation","It causes deafness without affecting the primary tissue pathway","It causes renal failure leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'It causes fatal ascending demyelinating neuropathy', 'Accidental intrathecal vincristine is almost always fatal, causing ascending demyelinating neuropathy and death.'),
('23a371ac-09a8-5cd6-b7c6-b91cc321edad'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Docetaxel differs from paclitaxel in that:', '["It is a semi-synthetic taxane from European yew needles","It does not cause myelosuppression","It is not a microtubule stabilizer","It is used only for lung cancer","It does not require premedication"]'::jsonb, 'It is a semi-synthetic taxane from European yew needles', 'Docetaxel is a semi-synthetic taxane from European yew (Taxus baccata) needles with greater potency and different side effects.'),
('ee7f1022-48c3-55b6-9940-8458235af361'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Etoposide is derived from which natural product?', '["Pacific yew bark","Podophyllotoxin (from mayapple)","Vinca rosea","Streptomyces species","Penicillium species"]'::jsonb, 'Podophyllotoxin (from mayapple)', 'Etoposide is a semi-synthetic glycoside derivative of podophyllotoxin, obtained from the mayapple plant.'),
('808f3c25-2e57-5ab6-afde-4cb85977d7ba'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Irinotecan-induced late diarrhea can be managed with:', '["Atropine","High-dose loperamide","Ondansetron","Dexamethasone","Ranitidine"]'::jsonb, 'High-dose loperamide', 'Early-onset diarrhea (<24h) is cholinergic and responds to atropine. Late-onset diarrhea (>24h) is managed with high-dose loperamide.'),
('b69f441a-9a64-5fa4-bb85-7b0a51639cd8'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Vincristine causes which electrolyte abnormality?', '["Hyperkalemia","SIADH causing hyponatremia","Hypercalcemia","Hypomagnesemia","Hyperphosphatemia"]'::jsonb, 'SIADH causing hyponatremia', 'Vincristine can cause SIADH leading to hyponatremia due to effects on hypothalamic or pituitary function.'),
('202264e6-b9e6-5c0a-9fd7-aeae97d864f7'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Vinca alkaloids are cell cycle specific for which phase?', '["G0 phase","S phase","M phase (mitosis)","G1 phase","G2 phase"]'::jsonb, 'M phase (mitosis)', 'Vinca alkaloids are M-phase specific, disrupting the mitotic spindle and arresting cells in metaphase.'),
('15c441c0-b2a7-5321-873f-da9099fa5192'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Paclitaxel is specifically active in which phase?', '["S phase","G1 phase","M phase (mitosis)","G0 phase","G2 phase"]'::jsonb, 'M phase (mitosis)', 'Paclitaxel is M-phase specific. By hyperstabilizing microtubules, it prevents spindle disassembly and arrests cells in metaphase.'),
('ec4d16f3-289f-5277-80b3-c79e6a696802'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Etoposide is most effective in which cell cycle phases?', '["G0 and G1","S and G2 phases","M phase only","All phases equally","G1 only"]'::jsonb, 'S and G2 phases', 'Etoposide is cell cycle-specific, primarily active in S and G2 phases when DNA is being replicated and cells prepare for mitosis.'),
('c1606f26-14cd-562d-9df5-4c603216b9e9'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'UGT1A1 polymorphism affects toxicity of which drug?', '["Doxorubicin resulting in decreased functional activity","Irinotecan (slow metabolizers at higher risk)","Vincristine mediated by specific receptor activation","Cisplatin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bleomycin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Irinotecan (slow metabolizers at higher risk)', 'UGT1A1*28 reduces glucuronidation of SN-38, increasing active metabolite levels and risk of severe diarrhea/neutropenia.'),
('ee5de067-7286-53f8-945b-2cadf3d45db7'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'ABVD regimen for Hodgkin lymphoma contains which vinca alkaloid?', '["Vincristine","Vinblastine","Vinorelbine","Vindesine","Vinflunine"]'::jsonb, 'Vinblastine', 'ABVD = Adriamycin, Bleomycin, Vinblastine, Dacarbazine. It is the standard chemotherapy for Hodgkin lymphoma.'),
('2dc83b5c-be05-5c88-ad69-1d5fc9b027a5'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Irinotecan is a prodrug activated by carboxylesterase to:', '["VP-16 resulting in decreased functional activity","SN-38 (active topoisomerase I inhibitor)","Phosphoramide mustard mediated by specific receptor activation","FdUMP without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ara-CTP leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'SN-38 (active topoisomerase I inhibitor)', 'Irinotecan (CPT-11) is converted by carboxylesterases to SN-38, which is 100-1000x more potent as a topoisomerase I inhibitor.'),
('f0b6e63f-2817-5f69-a8e0-d739f7d8126e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Vincristine neuropathy is managed by:', '["Dose reduction or discontinuation","There is no specific reversal agent","Leucovorin rescue","Mesna","Dexrazoxane"]'::jsonb, 'Dose reduction or discontinuation', 'Vincristine neuropathy is managed by dose reduction, discontinuation, or switching agents. There is no specific reversal agent.'),
('58aa29c2-19ca-52d8-92a1-4c229bb57107'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Cisplatin''s major dose-limiting toxicity is:', '["Myelosuppression","Nephrotoxicity","Cardiotoxicity","Pulmonary fibrosis","Hepatotoxicity"]'::jsonb, 'Nephrotoxicity', 'Cisplatin''s DLT is nephrotoxicity. Aggressive hydration with saline and mannitol is used to prevent renal damage.'),
('205b04e4-9baf-51ad-8a51-add418afb087'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Aggressive hydration with cisplatin is used to:', '["Prevent allergic reactions resulting in decreased functional activity","Prevent nephrotoxicity by maintaining high urine output","Enhance drug absorption mediated by specific receptor activation","Prevent hepatotoxicity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Reduce myelosuppression leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Prevent nephrotoxicity by maintaining high urine output', 'Cisplatin causes renal tubular damage worsened by dehydration. Aggressive hydration maintains high urine flow.'),
('70a7d758-0a70-5bb7-8623-d4a215b7702c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Cisplatin causes which characteristic ototoxicity?', '["Tinnitus and high-frequency sensorineural hearing loss","Conductive hearing loss","Low-frequency hearing loss only","Vertigo only without hearing loss","Complete deafness in one ear only"]'::jsonb, 'Tinnitus and high-frequency sensorineural hearing loss', 'Cisplatin causes dose-dependent, irreversible ototoxicity with tinnitus and progressive high-frequency sensorineural hearing loss.'),
('c46dbf8a-bd03-559d-b89e-a29c9d1c2fd8'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Carboplatin dosing is based on which formula?', '["BSA-based dosing only","Calvert formula (target AUC and GFR)","Ideal body weight","Liver function tests","Serum albumin level"]'::jsonb, 'Calvert formula (target AUC and GFR)', 'Carboplatin uses the Calvert formula: Dose (mg) = Target AUC x (GFR + 25), accounting for renal clearance.'),
('1c2a165a-a98a-52c4-a4b4-b1bb18f37530'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Which platinum compound has less nephrotoxicity but more myelosuppression than cisplatin?', '["Oxaliplatin","Carboplatin","Nedaplatin","Heptaplatin","Lobaplatin"]'::jsonb, 'Carboplatin', 'Carboplatin has significantly less nephrotoxicity and ototoxicity but more myelosuppression (particularly thrombocytopenia).'),
('d8178b67-9546-5f90-9552-283018c9ea2a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Oxaliplatin is a key component of which regimen for colon cancer?', '["FOLFIRI","FOLFOX and CAPOX","FOLFIRI plus bevacizumab","ABVD","CHOP"]'::jsonb, 'FOLFOX and CAPOX', 'Oxaliplatin is used in FOLFOX and CAPOX regimens for colorectal cancer, effective unlike cisplatin in this histology.'),
('b697f3d5-3c23-5205-a596-a3fa0d363c91'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Oxaliplatin causes a unique neuropathy characterized by:', '["Numbness only in feet resulting in decreased functional activity","Cold-triggered laryngopharyngeal dysesthesia and chronic sensory neuropathy","Motor weakness mediated by specific receptor activation","Central neurotoxicity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Autonomic neuropathy only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cold-triggered laryngopharyngeal dysesthesia and chronic sensory neuropathy', 'Oxaliplatin causes unique acute cold-triggered neuropathy (tingling in hands, throat) and chronic cumulative sensory neuropathy.'),
('cc8a7c4e-9e1c-54fa-8e29-4fd03f01f269'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Cisplatin is a key component of which regimen for testicular cancer?', '["CHOP resulting in decreased functional activity","BEP (bleomycin, etoposide, cisplatin)","ABVD mediated by specific receptor activation","FOLFOX without affecting the primary tissue pathway","MVAC leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'BEP (bleomycin, etoposide, cisplatin)', 'BEP is the gold standard curative regimen for metastatic testicular cancer, with cure rates exceeding 90%.'),
('e7cc7644-8bdb-5c88-8a4e-f6336982ab9e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Cisplatin causes which electrolyte disturbances?', '["Hypokalemia and hypomagnesemia","Hyperkalemia and hypercalcemia","Hypernatremia and hyperchloremia","Hypocalcemia and hyperphosphatemia","No electrolyte disturbances"]'::jsonb, 'Hypokalemia and hypomagnesemia', 'Cisplatin causes renal magnesium wasting (hypomagnesemia) and potassium wasting (hypokalemia) due to tubular damage.'),
('3d81cfd9-8703-5e9e-bd52-79772c400511'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Cisplatin is classified as:', '["Cell cycle specific (S-phase only)","Cell cycle non-specific (acts in all phases)","Cell cycle specific (M-phase only)","Cell cycle specific (G1-phase only)","Cell cycle specific (G2-phase only)"]'::jsonb, 'Cell cycle non-specific (acts in all phases)', 'Cisplatin is cell cycle non-specific, forming DNA adducts and cross-links regardless of cell cycle phase.'),
('ee9959d6-04a2-5b4a-a071-670f00073024'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Hypersensitivity reactions are most common with which platinum compound?', '["Cisplatin resulting in decreased functional activity","Carboplatin (especially after >6 cycles)","Oxaliplatin mediated by specific receptor activation","All equally without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None cause hypersensitivity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Carboplatin (especially after >6 cycles)', 'Carboplatin has the highest rate of hypersensitivity reactions, particularly after multiple cycles (>6).'),
('d2ac222e-c773-5a70-88c7-a2a9c935e8c8'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'The mechanism of action of platinum compounds involves:', '["Inhibition of microtubule assembly resulting in decreased functional activity","Formation of intrastrand and interstrand DNA cross-links","Inhibition of topoisomerase mediated by specific receptor activation","Inhibition of DHFR without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Alkylation of RNA leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Formation of intrastrand and interstrand DNA cross-links', 'Platinum compounds form reactive complexes that bind DNA, creating intrastrand and interstrand cross-links at N7 of guanine/adenine.'),
('11122ab1-8936-5d88-9d05-f2294e6fdd3c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Cisplatin nephrotoxicity is prevented by:', '["Dextrose infusion resulting in decreased functional activity","Aggressive normal saline hydration with mannitol diuresis","Fluid restriction mediated by specific receptor activation","Acetazolamide without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Spironolactone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Aggressive normal saline hydration with mannitol diuresis', 'Cisplatin nephrotoxicity is prevented by aggressive hydration with normal saline (urine output >100-150 mL/h) with mannitol.'),
('a16df32d-a452-5f42-b1a9-8e10c0ae09f4'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Oxaliplatin differs from cisplatin in that:', '["It is more nephrotoxic resulting in decreased functional activity","It is effective against cisplatin-resistant tumors and less nephrotoxic","It requires more aggressive hydration mediated by specific receptor activation","It causes more ototoxicity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","It is not a platinum compound leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'It is effective against cisplatin-resistant tumors and less nephrotoxic', 'Oxaliplatin is active against cisplatin-resistant tumors, has less nephrotoxicity/ototoxicity, and is effective in colorectal cancer.'),
('bcccf551-d080-5e24-a80e-110713fd36d2'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Cisplatin ototoxicity is exacerbated by concurrent:', '["Leucovorin","Aminoglycoside antibiotics","Metformin","Aspirin","Omeprazole"]'::jsonb, 'Aminoglycoside antibiotics', 'Aminoglycosides have additive ototoxicity with cisplatin. Concurrent use significantly increases hearing loss risk.'),
('f02d13cd-8a7a-5615-a205-ec1a918f6ef6'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Cisplatin neurotoxicity presents as:', '["Upper motor neuron signs resulting in decreased functional activity","Peripheral sensory neuropathy (dose-dependent, cumulative)","Cranial nerve palsies mediated by specific receptor activation","Seizures without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Encephalopathy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Peripheral sensory neuropathy (dose-dependent, cumulative)', 'Cisplatin causes dose-dependent cumulative peripheral sensory neuropathy, typically beginning in the feet. It may be irreversible.'),
('3cf534b9-f9a2-5081-82fb-3ab0bbbccb6a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Carboplatin''s main dose-limiting toxicity is:', '["Nephrotoxicity","Thrombocytopenia (dose-limiting)","Ototoxicity","Neurotoxicity","Cardiotoxicity"]'::jsonb, 'Thrombocytopenia (dose-limiting)', 'Carboplatin''s DLT is thrombocytopenia. Unlike cisplatin, nephrotoxicity and ototoxicity are minimal.'),
('d96f9898-6f5e-5e3a-bf44-58e839cdf053'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Cisplatin is most emetogenic. Prevention requires:', '["Metoclopramide alone resulting in decreased functional activity","5-HT3 antagonist + NK1 antagonist + dexamethasone","Antihistamine alone mediated by specific receptor activation","Benzodiazepine alone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Antacid alone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '5-HT3 antagonist + NK1 antagonist + dexamethasone', 'For highly emetogenic cisplatin, combination antiemetic therapy with 5-HT3 antagonists, NK1 antagonists (aprepitant), and dexamethasone is standard.'),
('6e02f425-d0ac-540f-80a4-e5b29ee5e1d5'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Nedaplatin is primarily used in which region?', '["United States resulting in decreased functional activity","Japan (approved for head/neck, lung, esophageal, bladder cancers)","United Kingdom mediated by specific receptor activation","Australia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Canada leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Japan (approved for head/neck, lung, esophageal, bladder cancers)', 'Nedaplatin is a second-generation platinum compound approved primarily in Japan for head/neck, lung, esophageal, and bladder cancers.'),
('6dcab8bb-1026-5ebe-b872-c900569f78ef'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Which of the following is a cell cycle non-specific (CCNS) agent?', '["Vincristine","Methotrexate","Cisplatin","5-Fluorouracil","Cytarabine"]'::jsonb, 'Cisplatin', 'Cisplatin is cell cycle non-specific. Antimetabolites and vinca alkaloids are cell cycle-specific.'),
('4decf8f3-efea-5f39-b4bb-0c37fb940d07'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Vincristine is specifically active in which cell cycle phase?', '["G0 phase","S phase","M phase (mitosis)","G1 phase","G2 phase"]'::jsonb, 'M phase (mitosis)', 'Vinca alkaloids are M-phase specific, disrupting the mitotic spindle and arresting cells in metaphase.'),
('63be07eb-d40d-5be6-8653-9a78460f2e38'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', '5-Fluorouracil is most active in which cell cycle phase?', '["G0 phase","S phase (DNA synthesis)","M phase","G1 phase","G2 phase"]'::jsonb, 'S phase (DNA synthesis)', 'Antimetabolites like 5-FU are cell cycle-specific for S phase, interfering with DNA synthesis.'),
('f9f7f1f0-0685-5051-87c4-904eb1b78578'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Cyclophosphamide is classified as:', '["Cell cycle specific (S-phase)","Cell cycle non-specific","Phase-specific (M-phase)","Phase-specific (G1-phase)","Phase-specific (G2-phase)"]'::jsonb, 'Cell cycle non-specific', 'Alkylating agents like cyclophosphamide are cell cycle non-specific, killing cells in any phase including G0.'),
('8ba87e1d-5f3a-53df-a122-aaaaff8dae5f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Combination chemotherapy often combines CCS and CCNS agents because:', '["It reduces overall toxicity resulting in decreased functional activity","CCNS agents kill G0 cells while CCS agents kill actively dividing cells","It prevents all drug resistance mediated by specific receptor activation","It eliminates dose adjustments without affecting the primary tissue pathway","It always improves cure rates leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CCNS agents kill G0 cells while CCS agents kill actively dividing cells', 'Combining CCS and CCNS agents provides complementary cell kill: CCNS agents eliminate quiescent G0 cells while CCS agents target proliferating cells.'),
('71d51722-7130-51c9-b77f-b99e2ce8c4f3'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'G0 (quiescent) cells are resistant to which type of agents?', '["Cell cycle non-specific agents","Cell cycle-specific agents","Both CCS and CCNS agents","None -- all agents kill G0 cells","Alkylating agents only"]'::jsonb, 'Cell cycle-specific agents', 'G0 cells are resistant to cell cycle-specific agents because these drugs require cells to be actively dividing.'),
('5b3ce6f0-3c4b-5c95-a81c-6825c9340c98'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Which of the following is a cell cycle-specific agent active in S phase?', '["Doxorubicin","Cyclophosphamide","Cytarabine","Cisplatin","Bleomycin"]'::jsonb, 'Cytarabine', 'Cytarabine is an S-phase specific antimetabolite that inhibits DNA polymerase. The others are cell cycle non-specific.'),
('9dc0e317-edcd-5f1c-8ed8-beb7b1fea62b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Bleomycin is classified as:', '["Cell cycle specific (S-phase only)","Cell cycle non-specific","Cell cycle specific (M-phase only)","Phase-specific (G1 phase)","Phase-specific (G2 phase)"]'::jsonb, 'Cell cycle non-specific', 'Bleomycin is considered cell cycle non-specific, although it has some preference for G2 and M phases.'),
('42187a79-15a6-5647-a963-bf5c4d731262'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Gemcitabine is cell cycle-specific for which phase?', '["G0 phase","S phase (DNA synthesis)","M phase","G1 phase","G2 phase"]'::jsonb, 'S phase (DNA synthesis)', 'Gemcitabine is an antimetabolite that is cell cycle-specific for S phase, inhibiting DNA synthesis.'),
('335c173c-ddb5-525b-ac9b-61872a5c4d86'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'The concept of cell cycle specificity is important because:', '["CCNS agents can kill resting (G0) tumor cells","CCS agents kill all tumor cells regardless of phase","CCNS agents are always more effective","CCS agents never cause resistance","CCNS agents have no side effects"]'::jsonb, 'CCNS agents can kill resting (G0) tumor cells', 'CCNS agents can kill cells in G0 (resting) phase, which is important because many tumor cells are quiescent.'),
('3c5b262d-ed05-50e6-9cd5-906f44500250'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Paclitaxel and vincristine both affect microtubules but differ because:', '["Both stabilize microtubules","Paclitaxel stabilizes while vincristine inhibits polymerization","Both inhibit polymerization","Both have no effect on microtubules","Vincristine stabilizes while paclitaxel depolymerizes"]'::jsonb, 'Paclitaxel stabilizes while vincristine inhibits polymerization', 'Paclitaxel hyperstabilizes microtubules (preventing disassembly) while vincristine inhibits polymerization (preventing assembly). Both arrest cells in M phase.'),
('d0d16800-abc1-5086-ae70-76b405fa10b8'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Etoposide is cell cycle-specific for which phases?', '["G0 and G1","S and G2 phases","M only","All phases","G1 only"]'::jsonb, 'S and G2 phases', 'Etoposide is primarily active in S and G2 phases, when topoisomerase II is most active during DNA replication.'),
('0ed232fa-2ab4-57b0-8056-ea2f42282951'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Ifosfamide is classified as:', '["Cell cycle-specific antimetabolite","Cell cycle non-specific alkylating agent","Cell cycle-specific (M phase) agent","Topoisomerase inhibitor","Antitumor antibiotic"]'::jsonb, 'Cell cycle non-specific alkylating agent', 'Ifosfamide, like cyclophosphamide, is an alkylating agent that is cell cycle non-specific.'),
('8b637d01-16ad-5697-919b-5e0ff40b6206'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Resistance to methotrexate commonly involves:', '["Increased DHFR expression (gene amplification)","Decreased drug efflux","Enhanced drug activation","Decreased DNA repair","Increased apoptosis"]'::jsonb, 'Increased DHFR expression (gene amplification)', 'The most common mechanism of methotrexate resistance is amplification of the DHFR gene, leading to increased enzyme levels.'),
('7b26ee22-3b18-5c9c-be10-2f40ebdb5a21'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'MDR (multidrug resistance) is mediated by:', '["P-glycoprotein (MDR1/ABCB1) drug efflux pump","Increased DNA repair","Decreased drug activation","Enhanced apoptosis","Decreased growth factor receptors"]'::jsonb, 'P-glycoprotein (MDR1/ABCB1) drug efflux pump', 'MDR1/P-glycoprotein is an ATP-dependent efflux pump that actively pumps drugs out of cells, reducing intracellular concentration.'),
('8e0dd7e7-62dc-5343-a938-8cb8087568e0'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Resistance to cisplatin commonly involves:', '["Increased drug influx","Enhanced DNA repair and increased glutathione","Decreased DNA content","Enhanced apoptosis","Decreased glutathione levels"]'::jsonb, 'Enhanced DNA repair and increased glutathione', 'Cisplatin resistance involves enhanced DNA repair (especially NER), increased glutathione, decreased drug uptake, and increased efflux.'),
('a5eddfa4-be92-5ce3-b721-297d3e30e067'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'P-glycoprotein confers resistance to which drugs?', '["Alkylating agents only resulting in decreased functional activity","Natural product drugs (anthracyclines, vinca alkaloids, taxanes)","Antimetabolites only mediated by specific receptor activation","Platinum compounds only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All drugs equally leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Natural product drugs (anthracyclines, vinca alkaloids, taxanes)', 'P-gp confers resistance primarily to hydrophobic natural product drugs: anthracyclines, vinca alkaloids, and taxanes.'),
('68e262e5-5d31-5652-83cf-96dcfacd3a35'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Thymidylate synthase overexpression causes resistance to:', '["Vincristine","5-Fluorouracil","Cyclophosphamide","Cisplatin","Bleomycin"]'::jsonb, '5-Fluorouracil', 'Overexpression of thymidylate synthase is a major mechanism of 5-FU resistance. More enzyme is present than can be inhibited by FdUMP.'),
('393ff6d5-e268-5921-8416-adf8df3b4e54'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'MGMT is a resistance mechanism for:', '["Doxorubicin","Temozolomide","Vincristine","5-Fluorouracil","Paclitaxel"]'::jsonb, 'Temozolomide', 'MGMT directly removes the O6-methyl group from O6-methylguanine. Tumors with unmethylated MGMT promoters have active MGMT and resist temozolomide.'),
('2a93ac52-d940-5d07-9106-14cad534f8c7'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Gemcitabine resistance commonly involves decreased:', '["Thymidine kinase resulting in decreased functional activity","Deoxycytidine kinase (dCK) -- the activating enzyme","Ribonucleotide reductase mediated by specific receptor activation","DNA polymerase without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Topoisomerase leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Deoxycytidine kinase (dCK) -- the activating enzyme', 'Gemcitabine requires activation by deoxycytidine kinase (dCK). Loss of dCK is a major resistance mechanism.'),
('3e3c7d7d-46dd-58d4-bae7-945323dd6ca3'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Cyclophosphamide resistance involves:', '["Decreased CYP450 activation","Increased aldehyde dehydrogenase (ALDH) activity","Both A and B","Enhanced drug absorption","Decreased protein binding"]'::jsonb, 'Both A and B', 'Cyclophosphamide resistance involves decreased hepatic CYP450 activation and increased ALDH activity (inactivates 4-hydroxycyclophosphamide).'),
('72709158-9143-5653-aeb1-7606bf059984'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Topoisomerase I mutations cause resistance to:', '["Vincristine","Irinotecan and topotecan","Cyclophosphamide","Methotrexate","Cisplatin"]'::jsonb, 'Irinotecan and topotecan', 'Mutations in topoisomerase I that reduce drug-enzyme binding confer resistance to camptothecins (irinotecan, topotecan).'),
('fce0bfa1-a77d-582e-9081-fbe57abcb74e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Glutathione overexpression causes resistance to:', '["Antimetabolites resulting in decreased functional activity","Alkylating agents and platinum compounds","Vinca alkaloids mediated by specific receptor activation","Taxanes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Topoisomerase inhibitors leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Alkylating agents and platinum compounds', 'Glutathione conjugates with alkylating agents and platinum compounds, inactivating them and facilitating their efflux.'),
('5c793619-91ea-5a2b-9fc0-a4ba30990328'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Decreased CTR1 (copper transporter 1) causes resistance to:', '["Doxorubicin","Cisplatin","Vincristine","Methotrexate","5-FU"]'::jsonb, 'Cisplatin', 'Cisplatin enters cells through copper transporters (particularly CTR1). Decreased CTR1 reduces cisplatin uptake.'),
('b0e78380-ed8e-5eec-bcf8-de6c28ff42b5'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Hypoxia contributes to drug resistance by:', '["Increasing drug activation resulting in decreased functional activity","Reducing delivery and promoting G0 arrest (resistance to CCS agents)","Enhancing apoptosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Increasing drug uptake without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Decreasing DNA repair leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Reducing delivery and promoting G0 arrest (resistance to CCS agents)', 'Tumor hypoxia reduces drug delivery and promotes quiescence (G0 arrest), making cells resistant to cell cycle-specific agents.'),
('e72434ea-2dc1-5236-acfb-632216ec48f1'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Loss of mismatch repair (MMR) causes resistance to:', '["Cyclophosphamide","Temozolomide","Vincristine","Paclitaxel","Bleomycin"]'::jsonb, 'Temozolomide', 'Loss of MMR (MLH1/MSH2) allows cells to tolerate O6-methylguanine lesions without apoptosis, conferring resistance to temozolomide.'),
('e7ebe38f-314d-5757-b508-0cd987da8941'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Enhanced DNA repair is a common resistance mechanism for:', '["Antimetabolites resulting in decreased functional activity","DNA-damaging agents (alkylating agents, platinum compounds)","Microtubule inhibitors mediated by specific receptor activation","Antibiotics that don''t damage DNA without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hormonal agents leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'DNA-damaging agents (alkylating agents, platinum compounds)', 'Enhanced NER and BER can repair DNA damage caused by alkylating agents and platinum compounds, reducing cytotoxic effect.'),
('ff56581c-b816-53e1-a32f-d5f6c247bfd9'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Deoxycytidine kinase (dCK) deficiency causes resistance to:', '["Methotrexate","Cytarabine and gemcitabine","5-FU","Pemetrexed","Capecitabine"]'::jsonb, 'Cytarabine and gemcitabine', 'Both cytarabine and gemcitabine require activation by dCK. Deficiency prevents phosphorylation to active forms.'),
('4e3c497d-8cff-5945-9cbc-4d0db39c61e5'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Drug efflux pumps reduce drug efficacy by:', '["Increasing drug activation resulting in decreased functional activity","Actively pumping drugs out of cells, reducing intracellular concentration","Enhancing drug uptake mediated by specific receptor activation","Increasing DNA repair without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Decreasing apoptosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Actively pumping drugs out of cells, reducing intracellular concentration', 'Efflux transporters (P-gp, MRP, BCRP) use ATP to actively transport drugs out of cancer cells.'),
('a8fb2dcc-c9bb-5605-a322-c74a6ca65d1e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'P-glycoprotein overexpression can be overcome by:', '["Increasing the dose of the affected drug","Using P-gp inhibitors (experimental)","Using non-P-gp substrate drugs","All of the above strategies have been tried","There is no way to overcome MDR"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above strategies have been tried', 'Multiple strategies have been tried: dose escalation, P-gp inhibitors (clinical trials disappointing), and non-P-gp substrate drugs.'),
('113432e1-66a9-574c-a659-3feb114de93b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'The dose-limiting toxicity of vincristine is:', '["Myelosuppression","Peripheral neuropathy","Nephrotoxicity","Cardiotoxicity","Pulmonary fibrosis"]'::jsonb, 'Peripheral neuropathy', 'Vincristine''s DLT is peripheral neuropathy (sensorimotor).'),
('f464e641-6ffa-5679-a9c8-efcda93df51c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'The dose-limiting toxicity of doxorubicin is:', '["Nephrotoxicity","Cumulative cardiotoxicity","Neurotoxicity","Pulmonary fibrosis","Hepatotoxicity"]'::jsonb, 'Cumulative cardiotoxicity', 'Doxorubicin''s DLT is cumulative cardiotoxicity. Lifetime cumulative dose limit is 450-550 mg/m2.'),
('4e150aee-2882-5b8a-8dcc-08b659b7b667'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'The dose-limiting toxicity of cisplatin is:', '["Myelosuppression","Nephrotoxicity","Neurotoxicity","Cardiotoxicity","Hepatotoxicity"]'::jsonb, 'Nephrotoxicity', 'Cisplatin''s DLT is nephrotoxicity.'),
('4efac23a-f26f-50ab-a440-17f2b893bd52'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'The dose-limiting toxicity of bleomycin is:', '["Nephrotoxicity","Pulmonary fibrosis","Cardiotoxicity","Myelosuppression","Neurotoxicity"]'::jsonb, 'Pulmonary fibrosis', 'Bleomycin''s DLT is pulmonary fibrosis, risk increases above 400 units total dose.'),
('f5f3be92-d8ca-54b1-a25d-4bd1a77a9bc6'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'The dose-limiting toxicity of carboplatin is:', '["Nephrotoxicity resulting in decreased functional activity","Thrombocytopenia (myelosuppression)","Neurotoxicity mediated by specific receptor activation","Ototoxicity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cardiotoxicity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thrombocytopenia (myelosuppression)', 'Carboplatin''s DLT is myelosuppression, particularly thrombocytopenia.'),
('c1219e87-8ae3-5aa7-b461-89f9441c15ed'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'The dose-limiting toxicity of methotrexate at standard doses is:', '["Cardiotoxicity","Myelosuppression and mucositis","Pulmonary fibrosis","Neurotoxicity","Ototoxicity"]'::jsonb, 'Myelosuppression and mucositis', 'At standard doses, methotrexate''s DLTs are myelosuppression and mucositis.'),
('651fbe84-178f-5168-940a-568f6502b2bd'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'The dose-limiting toxicity of cyclophosphamide is:', '["Cardiotoxicity resulting in decreased functional activity","Myelosuppression (particularly leukopenia)","Nephrotoxicity mediated by specific receptor activation","Pulmonary fibrosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Neurotoxicity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Myelosuppression (particularly leukopenia)', 'Cyclophosphamide''s DLT is myelosuppression (nadir at 8-14 days).'),
('9e679a61-f612-5059-bb54-71dcaa8482c0'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'The dose-limiting toxicity of 5-fluorouracil is:', '["Nephrotoxicity","Mucositis and diarrhea (GI toxicity)","Cardiotoxicity","Pulmonary fibrosis","Neurotoxicity"]'::jsonb, 'Mucositis and diarrhea (GI toxicity)', '5-FU''s DLTs are mucositis and diarrhea due to its effect on rapidly dividing GI mucosal cells.'),
('235ea007-db39-5e29-9520-5f8e3b3c4d77'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'The dose-limiting toxicity of etoposide is:', '["Cardiotoxicity","Myelosuppression (leukopenia)","Nephrotoxicity","Pulmonary fibrosis","Hepatotoxicity"]'::jsonb, 'Myelosuppression (leukopenia)', 'Etoposide''s DLT is myelosuppression (leukopenia, nadir 7-14 days).'),
('7c5f0b13-6dde-5bc8-928d-274782d924a4'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'The dose-limiting toxicity of irinotecan is:', '["Cardiotoxicity resulting in decreased functional activity","Diarrhea (early cholinergic and late severe) and myelosuppression","Nephrotoxicity mediated by specific receptor activation","Pulmonary fibrosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Neurotoxicity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Diarrhea (early cholinergic and late severe) and myelosuppression', 'Irinotecan''s DLTs are diarrhea (early cholinergic and late-onset) and neutropenia.'),
('7a5bd650-c4d1-5c6c-bcd8-b5dbdfb497ec'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'The dose-limiting toxicity of temozolomide is:', '["Nephrotoxicity","Lymphopenia and thrombocytopenia","Cardiotoxicity","Pulmonary fibrosis","Neurotoxicity"]'::jsonb, 'Lymphopenia and thrombocytopenia', 'Temozolomide''s DLTs are lymphopenia (predisposing to PML) and thrombocytopenia.'),
('e13e1f2e-ec9f-5f93-97df-e4babc6cdb99'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'The dose-limiting toxicity of vinblastine is:', '["Peripheral neuropathy","Myelosuppression (leukopenia)","Nephrotoxicity","Cardiotoxicity","Pulmonary fibrosis"]'::jsonb, 'Myelosuppression (leukopenia)', 'Vinblastine''s DLT is myelosuppression (leukopenia), unlike vincristine whose DLT is neuropathy.'),
('eb9d8963-6203-5268-b184-215a8ee44742'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Pulmonary fibrosis as DLT is characteristic of:', '["Doxorubicin","Bleomycin","Vincristine","Methotrexate","Cisplatin"]'::jsonb, 'Bleomycin', 'Bleomycin has pulmonary fibrosis as its characteristic DLT.'),
('0b22bcde-2f64-57f3-8ab7-b55397a72307'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Nephrotoxicity as PRIMARY DLT is characteristic of:', '["Carboplatin","Cisplatin","Oxaliplatin","Doxorubicin","Vincristine"]'::jsonb, 'Cisplatin', 'Cisplatin has nephrotoxicity as its primary DLT.'),
('0839b181-6696-5c3d-80c8-f64b6af9aeee'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Peripheral neuropathy as DLT is seen with:', '["Vincristine and cisplatin","Bleomycin and doxorubicin","5-FU and methotrexate","Cyclophosphamide and ifosfamide","Gemcitabine and cytarabine"]'::jsonb, 'Vincristine and cisplatin', 'Both vincristine (dose-limiting) and cisplatin (cumulative) cause peripheral neuropathy.'),
('85c5476a-3453-5122-b4ce-992de3716c81'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'The nadir of myelosuppression for most chemotherapy agents occurs at:', '["1-2 days","7-14 days","21-28 days","Immediately","4-6 weeks"]'::jsonb, '7-14 days', 'Most chemotherapy agents cause myelosuppression with a nadir at 7-14 days after administration.'),
('80d6928a-aef3-5221-8152-e0c4b3cc7067'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Mucositis as DLT is most common with:', '["Antimetabolites (5-FU, methotrexate)","Alkylating agents accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Platinum compounds mediated by specific receptor activation","Vinca alkaloids without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Taxanes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Antimetabolites (5-FU, methotrexate)', 'Antimetabolites (5-FU, methotrexate, capecitabine) commonly cause mucositis because they affect rapidly dividing mucosal cells.'),
('f9bd51ec-6744-53a7-9784-1039c3a09998'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Leucovorin rescue after high-dose methotrexate works by:', '["Inhibiting methotrexate absorption","Providing reduced folates that bypass the DHFR block","Enhancing methotrexate excretion","Binding to methotrexate in the blood","Inhibiting renal tubular secretion"]'::jsonb, 'Providing reduced folates that bypass the DHFR block', 'Leucovorin is already in the reduced folate form and does not require DHFR, bypassing the metabolic block.'),
('7659367b-6694-5dd8-a2b5-0d568b07c298'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Mesna works by:', '["Binding to methotrexate resulting in decreased functional activity","Binding to acrolein in the urinary tract, preventing hemorrhagic cystitis","Enhancing renal clearance of cisplatin mediated by specific receptor activation","Protecting the heart from doxorubicin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Preventing peripheral neuropathy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Binding to acrolein in the urinary tract, preventing hemorrhagic cystitis', 'Mesna contains a thiol group that binds to acrolein in the urinary tract, forming a non-toxic conjugate.'),
('36229b6d-333b-5bd5-bc1c-9cddebe0b37b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Dexrazoxane is used as cardioprotective agent with:', '["Methotrexate","Doxorubicin (anthracyclines)","Vincristine","Cisplatin","Bleomycin"]'::jsonb, 'Doxorubicin (anthracyclines)', 'Dexrazoxane is an iron chelator that prevents iron-mediated free radical damage to the heart caused by anthracyclines.'),
('78802904-f8fa-50e3-abf0-ad437d77c1c4'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Amifostine reduces which toxicity?', '["Doxorubicin cardiotoxicity","Cisplatin nephrotoxicity and radiation xerostomia","Methotrexate hepatotoxicity","Vincristine neurotoxicity","Bleomycin pulmonary toxicity"]'::jsonb, 'Cisplatin nephrotoxicity and radiation xerostomia', 'Amifostine is a prodrug that reduces cisplatin nephrotoxicity and radiation-induced xerostomia.'),
('3bd14ecd-22cd-542a-b475-5603fccd4523'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'High-dose methotrexate protocols require:', '["Urine alkalinization, hydration, and leucovorin rescue","Urine acidification and no rescue","Fluid restriction and diuretics","No special measures","Only leucovorin without hydration"]'::jsonb, 'Urine alkalinization, hydration, and leucovorin rescue', 'High-dose MTX requires: (1) aggressive hydration, (2) urine alkalinization (NaHCO3 to pH >7), and (3) leucovorin rescue.'),
('a902588f-25ee-5a58-a388-1f3b2b49e53b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Leucovorin enhances 5-FU efficacy because:', '["It reduces 5-FU toxicity resulting in decreased functional activity","It stabilizes the FdUMP-thymidylate synthase ternary complex","It prevents hand-foot syndrome mediated by specific receptor activation","It reduces mucositis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","It prevents myelosuppression leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'It stabilizes the FdUMP-thymidylate synthase ternary complex', 'Leucovorin provides N5,N10-methylene-THF which stabilizes the FdUMP-thymidylate synthase ternary complex, enhancing 5-FU.'),
('05e2bcaf-ab04-5441-8830-ad9893bad8f0'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Glucarpidase is used for:', '["Cisplatin toxicity resulting in decreased functional activity","High-dose methotrexate toxicity (when leucovorin insufficient)","Doxorubicin extravasation mediated by specific receptor activation","Vincristine neurotoxicity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bleomycin pulmonary toxicity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'High-dose methotrexate toxicity (when leucovorin insufficient)', 'Glucarpidase cleaves methotrexate into inactive metabolites. Used for life-threatening MTX levels when leucovorin rescue is insufficient.'),
('38d31f81-e22a-5bc2-9f9a-84669203f979'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Dexrazoxane works by:', '["Enhancing doxorubicin metabolism","Chelating iron, preventing free radical formation in cardiac tissue","Binding to doxorubicin in bloodstream","Enhancing renal excretion","Inhibiting topoisomerase II in heart"]'::jsonb, 'Chelating iron, preventing free radical formation in cardiac tissue', 'Dexrazoxane enters cardiac cells and hydrolyzes to ADR-925, an iron chelator that prevents free radical formation.'),
('c71f7c42-6a87-5c23-b899-6654f262676e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Sodium thiosulfate is used for:', '["Methotrexate overdose","Cisplatin extravasation","Doxorubicin overdose","Vincristine overdose","Bleomycin overdose"]'::jsonb, 'Cisplatin extravasation', 'Sodium thiosulfate is used topically for cisplatin extravasation, forming a non-toxic complex with cisplatin.'),
('df4cb92f-b1e4-532d-a996-8d98e58d7ef1'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'The timing of leucovorin rescue after high-dose MTX is critical because:', '["Leucovorin is only effective within 2 hours resulting in decreased functional activity","Leucovorin must be started before MTX clearance to prevent irreversible toxicity","Leucovorin is not effective after 24 hours mediated by specific receptor activation","Leucovorin is only needed on day 7 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Timing does not matter leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Leucovorin must be started before MTX clearance to prevent irreversible toxicity', 'Leucovorin rescue must be started within 24-36 hours of high-dose MTX and continued until MTX levels are safe.'),
('5b01b83f-f736-50bd-a3f5-f15a5d01df7f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Dexrazoxane should only be used when doxorubicin dose exceeds:', '["100 mg/m2","300 mg/m2","500 mg/m2","1000 mg/m2","Any dose"]'::jsonb, '300 mg/m2', 'Dexrazoxane is generally recommended for patients who have received >300 mg/m2 doxorubicin.'),
('64f85403-e7bc-59da-a7bd-e45fa366c3b6'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Mesna dosing schedule typically involves:', '["Single dose before chemotherapy","Divided doses at 0, 4, and 8 hours after ifosfamide","Continuous infusion for 24 hours","Weekly injections","Single dose after completion"]'::jsonb, 'Divided doses at 0, 4, and 8 hours after ifosfamide', 'Mesna is typically given in divided doses (0, 4, 8 hours after ifosfamide) to maintain urinary thiol levels.'),
('ccb60ac8-2514-55f4-bbd9-574c89f30863'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Amifostine is selectively protective because:', '["It is only absorbed by tumor cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Normal tissues have higher alkaline phosphatase, activating it more efficiently","It is degraded by tumor cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Tumor cells cannot take it up without affecting the primary tissue pathway","It only works in hypoxic tissues leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Normal tissues have higher alkaline phosphatase, activating it more efficiently', 'Amifostine is activated by alkaline phosphatase, more abundant in normal tissues than tumors, providing selective protection.'),
('f5be0857-4f89-5264-bc85-2ae556af0ef3'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Urine alkalinization with NaHCO3 is used with high-dose MTX because:', '["It enhances MTX absorption resulting in decreased functional activity","MTX is more soluble in alkaline urine, preventing crystalluria","It inhibits MTX metabolism mediated by specific receptor activation","It enhances MTX renal clearance without affecting the primary tissue pathway","It prevents hepatotoxicity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'MTX is more soluble in alkaline urine, preventing crystalluria', 'Methotrexate is poorly soluble in acidic urine. Alkalinizing urine (pH >7.0) dramatically increases MTX solubility.'),
('1983f0a4-b9a4-5e72-886b-52cd53364470'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Mesna dose is approximately what percentage of cyclophosphamide dose?', '["20%","60-100%","10%","200%","5%"]'::jsonb, '60-100%', 'Mesna is typically given at 60-100% of the cyclophosphamide/ifosfamide dose (divided into 3-4 doses).'),
('b6e4a369-0774-5474-860e-a4a08bb2bf44'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Which rescue agent is used for MTX but NOT for 5-FU toxicity?', '["Leucovorin (rescues from MTX, enhances 5-FU)","Mesna accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dexrazoxane mediated by specific receptor activation","Amifostine without affecting the primary tissue pathway","N-acetylcysteine leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Leucovorin (rescues from MTX, enhances 5-FU)', 'Leucovorin rescues from MTX by bypassing DHFR block. With 5-FU, leucovorin actually enhances toxicity (therapeutically desired).'),
('5eb4e980-ce4b-579e-aa4b-1ec4fc7cfa2c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Folinic acid is another name for:', '["Folic acid","Leucovorin","Methotrexate","Vitamin B12","Trimethoprim"]'::jsonb, 'Leucovorin', 'Folinic acid (5-formyl-THF) is another name for leucovorin. It is the reduced, active form of folic acid.'),
('13c48b0f-fbc2-56f8-927b-e876006601d4'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Rasburicase is used for tumor lysis syndrome prophylaxis because it:', '["Inhibits uric acid production","Enzymatically converts uric acid to soluble allantoin","Enhances renal excretion of uric acid","Alkalinizes urine","Binds to uric acid in blood"]'::jsonb, 'Enzymatically converts uric acid to soluble allantoin', 'Rasburicase is a recombinant urate oxidase that catalyzes oxidation of uric acid to allantoin, 5-10x more soluble.'),
('b6246403-6647-55c3-99dd-af74f15d89b1'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Rasburicase is contraindicated in:', '["Diabetes resulting in decreased functional activity","G6PD deficiency (risk of hemolysis)","Hypertension mediated by specific receptor activation","Asthma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Renal stones leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'G6PD deficiency (risk of hemolysis)', 'Rasburicase is contraindicated in G6PD deficiency because it generates hydrogen peroxide, causing hemolysis.'),
('6363bc1d-1161-5675-b7e7-fb334f6c12cf'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Palonosetron is preferred over first-gen 5-HT3 antagonists because:', '["It is cheaper resulting in decreased functional activity","Longer half-life and greater receptor affinity for delayed emesis","Fewer side effects mediated by specific receptor activation","Given intramuscularly without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Works faster leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Longer half-life and greater receptor affinity for delayed emesis', 'Palonosetron has longer half-life (40h vs 4h) and higher 5-HT3 receptor binding affinity, more effective for delayed emesis.'),
('097daaf8-f9fc-5cea-b4ce-238dfaccb9b2'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Aprepitant (Emend) is a NK1 receptor antagonist that:', '["Blocks serotonin receptors","Blocks substance P (NK1) receptors in the vomiting center","Blocks dopamine receptors","Blocks histamine receptors","Blocks acetylcholine receptors"]'::jsonb, 'Blocks substance P (NK1) receptors in the vomiting center', 'Aprepitant blocks NK1 receptors in the area postrema and vomiting center, providing delayed emesis control.'),
('fa3b615f-2b88-5e30-9f45-23806c655b6b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Tumor lysis syndrome prophylaxis includes:', '["Aggressive IV hydration and allopurinol (or rasburicase)","Fluid restriction and NSAIDs","Diuretics and acetaminophen","Antibiotics and antiemetics","Blood transfusion only"]'::jsonb, 'Aggressive IV hydration and allopurinol (or rasburicase)', 'TLS prophylaxis includes aggressive hydration, allopurinol (blocks xanthine oxidase), and rasburicase for high-risk patients.'),
('1552de02-cd66-58dd-a309-113949ac3292'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'G-CSF (filgrastim) is used with chemotherapy to:', '["Prevent nephrotoxicity resulting in decreased functional activity","Reduce duration and severity of chemotherapy-induced neutropenia","Prevent cardiotoxicity mediated by specific receptor activation","Prevent mucositis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Prevent alopecia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Reduce duration and severity of chemotherapy-induced neutropenia', 'G-CSF stimulates neutrophil production, reducing the duration and severity of chemotherapy-induced neutropenia.'),
('1c763a8d-d302-550c-adaa-06065dfc3cb6'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Epoetin alfa is used with chemotherapy to:', '["Prevent infection resulting in decreased functional activity","Reduce chemotherapy-induced anemia and transfusion requirements","Prevent thrombocytopenia mediated by specific receptor activation","Prevent neutropenia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Prevent mucositis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Reduce chemotherapy-induced anemia and transfusion requirements', 'Epoetin alfa stimulates RBC production, reducing chemotherapy-induced anemia and transfusion needs.'),
('6be5dc2e-f453-537d-89e8-959757fb921b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Oprelvekin (IL-11) is used to:', '["Prevent anemia","Prevent severe thrombocytopenia","Prevent neutropenia","Prevent infection","Prevent mucositis"]'::jsonb, 'Prevent severe thrombocytopenia', 'Oprelvekin (recombinant IL-11) stimulates megakaryocyte maturation and platelet production.'),
('a4182c54-def8-507a-bca0-d3c146b5760f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Dexrazoxane is dosed at what ratio to doxorubicin?', '["1:1 resulting in decreased functional activity","10:1 (dexrazoxane:doxorubicin)","1:10 mediated by specific receptor activation","5:1 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","1:5 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '10:1 (dexrazoxane:doxorubicin)', 'Dexrazoxane is typically dosed at 10:1 ratio to doxorubicin (e.g., 500 mg/m2 for 50 mg/m2 doxorubicin).'),
('4dee5efe-8236-5db2-88e5-1398b5906479'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Which chemotherapy agent is most emetogenic?', '["Methotrexate","Cisplatin (highly emetogenic)","Vincristine","Bleomycin","Vinblastine"]'::jsonb, 'Cisplatin (highly emetogenic)', 'Cisplatin is the most emetogenic chemotherapeutic agent (>90% emesis risk without antiemetics).'),
('9b1ca6df-c079-5d4d-9114-6b07894083ab'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'ABVD regimen for Hodgkin lymphoma contains:', '["Adriamycin, bleomycin, vinblastine, dacarbazine","Adriamycin, bleomycin, vincristine, dacarbazine","Adriamycin, bleomycin, vinblastine, doxorubicin","Adriamycin, busulfan, vinblastine, dacarbazine","Adriamycin, bleomycin, vincristine, etoposide"]'::jsonb, 'Adriamycin, bleomycin, vinblastine, dacarbazine', 'ABVD = Adriamycin (doxorubicin), Bleomycin, Vinblastine, Dacarbazine.'),
('9cf27d1a-220b-5e5f-baae-94216289a68b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'CHOP regimen includes:', '["Cyclophosphamide, doxorubicin, vincristine, prednisone","Cyclophosphamide, methotrexate, vincristine, prednisone","Cyclophosphamide, doxorubicin, etoposide, prednisone","Cyclophosphamide, doxorubicin, vincristine, procarbazine","Cyclophosphamide, bleomycin, vincristine, prednisone"]'::jsonb, 'Cyclophosphamide, doxorubicin, vincristine, prednisone', 'CHOP = Cyclophosphamide, Hydroxydaunorubicin (doxorubicin), Oncovin (vincristine), Prednisone.'),
('e9acbc47-1f7d-568d-8e72-a09a14bac7f5'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Cytarabine conjunctivitis is prevented by:', '["Oral antihistamines","Prophylactic corticosteroid eye drops","Antibiotic eye drops","Artificial tears only","Avoiding light"]'::jsonb, 'Prophylactic corticosteroid eye drops', 'Cytarabine causes hemorrhagic conjunctivitis, preventable with prophylactic corticosteroid eye drops during high-dose therapy.'),
('9aafa7a9-1708-56f1-8117-62fc9c861404'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Alopecia is most common with which agents?', '["Alkylating agents and taxanes","Antimetabolites only","Platinum compounds only","Hormonal agents","None cause alopecia"]'::jsonb, 'Alkylating agents and taxanes', 'Alopecia is most common with doxorubicin, cyclophosphamide, paclitaxel, docetaxel, and etoposide.'),
('b6320e45-e3da-5cec-9e3b-3a3dac62ddc5'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Dacarbazine (DTIC) is primarily used in:', '["Breast cancer resulting in decreased functional activity","Hodgkin lymphoma (ABVD) and sarcomas","Colon cancer mediated by specific receptor activation","Lung cancer without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pancreatic cancer leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hodgkin lymphoma (ABVD) and sarcomas', 'Dacarbazine is used in ABVD for Hodgkin lymphoma and in soft tissue sarcomas and melanoma.'),
('234049a7-984f-5dfd-8695-c7f040a7af14'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Procarbazine causes which important drug interaction?', '["Enhances warfarin effect","Disulfiram-like reaction with alcohol (MAO inhibition)","Causes hyperglycemia with insulin","Reduces oral contraceptive effectiveness only","Increases absorption of all drugs"]'::jsonb, 'Disulfiram-like reaction with alcohol (MAO inhibition)', 'Procarbazine inhibits MAO, causing a disulfiram-like reaction with alcohol (flushing, nausea, hypotension).'),
('660e96b3-04c6-548d-8127-f0f604a29adf'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'L-Asparaginase is most effective against leukemias that:', '["Cannot synthesize glutamine resulting in decreased functional activity","Cannot synthesize asparagine (require exogenous asparagine)","Cannot synthesize glycine mediated by specific receptor activation","Cannot synthesize leucine without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cannot synthesize methionine leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cannot synthesize asparagine (require exogenous asparagine)', 'L-Asparaginase depletes circulating asparagine. ALL cells lack asparagine synthetase and depend on exogenous supply.'),
('46e27db8-854d-5be0-a451-5f9d93760997'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Pentostatin is used primarily for:', '["AML","Hairy cell leukemia","ALL","Lymphoma","Myeloma"]'::jsonb, 'Hairy cell leukemia', 'Pentostatin inhibits adenosine deaminase, leading to accumulation of toxic deoxyadenosine. Highly effective in hairy cell leukemia.'),
('1b1023a8-11b8-55f6-b0b1-2a676f8749a8'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Cladribine is primarily used for:', '["Breast cancer","Hairy cell leukemia","Colon cancer","Lung cancer","Pancreatic cancer"]'::jsonb, 'Hairy cell leukemia', 'Cladribine is a purine analogue resistant to adenosine deaminase, highly effective in hairy cell leukemia.'),
('bf37861a-3da7-5ddd-a217-d7975ffe535d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Hydroxyurea inhibits ribonucleotide reductase and is used in:', '["Only cancer treatment resulting in decreased functional activity","CML, sickle cell disease (increases HbF), and essential thrombocythemia","Only sickle cell disease mediated by specific receptor activation","Only CML without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only essential thrombocythemia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CML, sickle cell disease (increases HbF), and essential thrombocythemia', 'Hydroxyurea inhibits ribonucleotide reductase, used in CML, sickle cell disease (increases fetal hemoglobin), and myeloproliferative disorders.'),
('20a0049c-37a8-5c65-8342-8f2033444e39'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Fludarabine is primarily used for:', '["AML","CLL","Breast cancer","Lung cancer","Colon cancer"]'::jsonb, 'CLL', 'Fludarabine is a purine nucleoside analogue key in CLL treatment, used in FCR regimen.'),
('22ebde4f-1b5c-5f89-b67a-29ac9079b35c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'FOLFOX regimen consists of:', '["5-FU, leucovorin, oxaliplatin","5-FU, leucovorin, cisplatin","5-FU, leucovorin, carboplatin","5-FU, methotrexate, oxaliplatin","5-FU, etoposide, oxaliplatin"]'::jsonb, '5-FU, leucovorin, oxaliplatin', 'FOLFOX = FOLinic acid + FU + OX (oxaliplatin), standard for colorectal cancer.'),
('efb5dc43-c489-591d-b976-ffdd9d8d7adc'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'FOLFIRI regimen consists of:', '["5-FU, leucovorin, irinotecan","5-FU, leucovorin, etoposide","5-FU, leucovorin, ifosfamide","5-FU, methotrexate, irinotecan","5-FU, cisplatin, irinotecan"]'::jsonb, '5-FU, leucovorin, irinotecan', 'FOLFIRI = FOLinic acid + FU + IRINotecan, standard for metastatic colorectal cancer.'),
('d6a034a9-0960-5634-a3bf-cbf97de9b775'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Dose-limiting toxicity of chlorambucil is:', '["Cardiotoxicity resulting in decreased functional activity","Myelosuppression (thrombocytopenia and leukopenia)","Nephrotoxicity mediated by specific receptor activation","Neurotoxicity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pulmonary fibrosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Myelosuppression (thrombocytopenia and leukopenia)', 'Chlorambucil''s DLT is myelosuppression.'),
('287f4554-ec84-59b1-b243-aa65355cb3a7'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Melphalan oral absorption is best when:', '["Taken with food resulting in decreased functional activity","Taken on an empty stomach (1h before or 2h after meals)","Taken at bedtime mediated by specific receptor activation","Taken with antibiotics without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Taken with antacids leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Taken on an empty stomach (1h before or 2h after meals)', 'Oral melphalan should be taken on an empty stomach for consistent bioavailability.'),
('64337dee-8ed8-565d-9cd4-1e087eb1e666'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Intrathecal chemotherapy is used for:', '["Liver metastases resulting in decreased functional activity","Prevention and treatment of CNS leukemia/lymphoma","Lung metastases mediated by specific receptor activation","Bone metastases without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Kidney metastases leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Prevention and treatment of CNS leukemia/lymphoma', 'Intrathecal chemotherapy (MTX, Ara-C, hydrocortisone) is used to treat and prevent meningeal leukemia/lymphoma.'),
('823256b6-3413-5d3c-b108-8274353dfd64'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Intravesical BCG is used for:', '["Lung cancer resulting in decreased functional activity","Superficial (non-muscle invasive) bladder cancer","Prostate cancer mediated by specific receptor activation","Kidney cancer without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Testicular cancer leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superficial (non-muscle invasive) bladder cancer', 'BCG (immunotherapy) and mitomycin C are standard intravesical agents for superficial bladder cancer.'),
('84cc0ef1-5b6d-5600-b5ea-7c8c7b850e42'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'High-dose MTX with leucovorin rescue is key for:', '["Breast cancer","Osteosarcoma and CNS lymphoma","Colon cancer","Lung cancer","Pancreatic cancer"]'::jsonb, 'Osteosarcoma and CNS lymphoma', 'High-dose MTX with leucovorin rescue is a cornerstone of osteosarcoma (MAP regimen) and primary CNS lymphoma.'),
('4922d8a7-0eae-51e3-a26d-adb3d4f0b127'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Gemcitabine + nab-paclitaxel is used for:', '["Lung cancer","Pancreatic cancer","Ovarian cancer","Breast cancer","Colon cancer"]'::jsonb, 'Pancreatic cancer', 'Gemcitabine + nab-paclitaxel (Abraxane) is a standard first-line regimen for metastatic pancreatic cancer.'),
('998d097b-99c8-5774-bcdc-5b5c304ed498'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Cisplatin + etoposide (EP regimen) is used for:', '["Breast cancer","Small cell lung cancer (SCLC)","Colon cancer","Ovarian cancer","Pancreatic cancer"]'::jsonb, 'Small cell lung cancer (SCLC)', 'Cisplatin + etoposide (EP) is a standard regimen for small cell lung cancer.'),
('d79be74f-0b7c-538b-8635-4113321a928a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Cisplatin + pemetrexed is used for:', '["Squamous cell lung cancer","Non-squamous NSCLC and mesothelioma","Small cell lung cancer","Colon cancer","Breast cancer"]'::jsonb, 'Non-squamous NSCLC and mesothelioma', 'Cisplatin + pemetrexed is standard for non-squamous NSCLC and malignant pleural mesothelioma.'),
('96fe74a1-492d-5576-9d4d-9fade34f3d80'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Cyclophosphamide is also used as immunosuppressant in which nephrotic syndrome?', '["Minimal change disease resulting in decreased functional activity","Frequently relapsing and steroid-dependent nephrotic syndrome","Membranous nephropathy mediated by specific receptor activation","Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","IgA nephropathy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Frequently relapsing and steroid-dependent nephrotic syndrome', 'Cyclophosphamide is a steroid-sparing immunosuppressive agent in frequently relapsing and steroid-dependent nephrotic syndrome in children.'),
('076b82b1-627b-5e2d-ae0d-8d5736891858'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Bleomycin causes which skin finding besides flagellate pigmentation?', '["Scleroderma-like skin changes","Raynaud phenomenon","Both A and B","Only alopecia","No skin changes"]'::jsonb, 'Both A and B', 'Bleomycin can cause scleroderma-like skin changes (fibrosis, thickening) and Raynaud phenomenon, in addition to flagellate pigmentation.'),
('556b033d-d804-55e5-98df-7551bb016843'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Methotrexate at high doses can cause which neurotoxicity?', '["Peripheral neuropathy only resulting in decreased functional activity","Acute encephalopathy (seizures, confusion, coma) -- especially with IT use","Cranial nerve palsies only mediated by specific receptor activation","No neurotoxicity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Chronic dementia only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Acute encephalopathy (seizures, confusion, coma) -- especially with IT use', 'High-dose IV or intrathecal methotrexate can cause acute encephalopathy with seizures, confusion, and coma.'),
('076ff7d7-0e9a-5fec-9449-2f5fe571fb26'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Thiotepa is used in high-dose conditioning for BMT because it:', '["Is less toxic than other alkylating agents","Has good CNS penetration and myeloablative properties","Only affects tumor cells","Has no bone marrow suppression","Is administered orally"]'::jsonb, 'Has good CNS penetration and myeloablative properties', 'Thiotepa has good CNS penetration and potent myeloablative properties, useful in conditioning regimens for BMT.'),
('0db578a0-a00b-5382-9126-fd4e590980b1'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Altretamine (hexamethylmelamine) is used for:', '["Lung cancer","Refractory ovarian cancer","Breast cancer","Colon cancer","Liver cancer"]'::jsonb, 'Refractory ovarian cancer', 'Altretamine is used in recurrent/refractory ovarian cancer, often in combination.'),
('6ee91846-45d7-5dac-bbf7-117739d96c70'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Vincristine causes which characteristic reflex change?', '["Increased deep tendon reflexes","Loss of deep tendon reflexes (areflexia)","No change in reflexes","Babinski sign","Clonus"]'::jsonb, 'Loss of deep tendon reflexes (areflexia)', 'Vincristine causes loss of deep tendon reflexes (areflexia) as part of peripheral neuropathy, often the earliest sign.'),
('a4e0534d-1887-5c2d-9429-2c1ba5ce9a4e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Ifosfamide encephalopathy may respond to:', '["Thiamine and methylene blue (experimental)","Leucovorin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mesna mediated by specific receptor activation","Dexrazoxane without affecting the primary tissue pathway","N-acetylcysteine leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thiamine and methylene blue (experimental)', 'Thiamine and methylene blue have been tried for ifosfamide encephalopathy, though evidence is limited.'),
('20a2e59b-64f0-5549-937c-d480ab3634d5'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'The lifetime cumulative dose limit for doxorubicin is approximately:', '["100-200 mg/m2","450-550 mg/m2","800-1000 mg/m2","1500-2000 mg/m2","No limit exists"]'::jsonb, '450-550 mg/m2', 'The traditional lifetime cumulative dose limit for doxorubicin is 450-550 mg/m2, above which cardiotoxicity risk increases significantly.'),
('61be28a9-10d9-5937-8f93-dca33d1c2f66'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Etoposide causes which characteristic dermatologic toxicity?', '["Flagellate pigmentation","Alopecia (very common)","Hand-foot syndrome","Scleroderma","Photosensitivity"]'::jsonb, 'Alopecia (very common)', 'Etoposide commonly causes alopecia (very frequent) and can also cause hypotension if infused too rapidly.'),
('ff024b0c-409c-5d47-950a-239fdb8364fc'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Etoposide can cause hypotension when:', '["Given orally","Infused too rapidly intravenously","Given with food","Combined with cisplatin","Given at night"]'::jsonb, 'Infused too rapidly intravenously', 'Etoposide IV infusion can cause hypotension if administered too rapidly. It should be infused over 30-60 minutes.'),
('ee46bf20-7cde-5863-90c1-fa10c3b8eafb'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Vincristine neuropathy is characterized by:', '["Pure motor neuropathy","Sensorimotor neuropathy with areflexia","Pure sensory neuropathy","Mononeuropathy","Autonomic neuropathy only"]'::jsonb, 'Sensorimotor neuropathy with areflexia', 'Vincristine causes sensorimotor peripheral neuropathy with loss of deep tendon reflexes, paresthesias, and possible motor weakness.'),
('e2302e9b-3e9e-56bb-8a98-ebede9e4ce63'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Cisplatin causes which GI side effect besides nausea/vomiting?', '["Constipation","Diarrhea","Pancreatitis","Hepatitis","Gastric ulceration"]'::jsonb, 'Diarrhea', 'Cisplatin can cause diarrhea in addition to severe nausea and vomiting, plus electrolyte wasting through renal tubular damage.'),
('99d450d4-39a4-5e52-9fba-90d57939c510'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'Mitomycin C is sometimes combined with cisplatin for:', '["Breast cancer resulting in decreased functional activity","Gastric and pancreatic cancer (in some regimens)","Lung cancer mediated by specific receptor activation","Prostate cancer without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thyroid cancer leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Gastric and pancreatic cancer (in some regimens)', 'Mitomycin C has been used in gastric cancer and pancreatic cancer regimens, though its use has declined with newer agents.'),
('47970195-40c5-5996-942d-4a31f503bacc'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'PEGylated liposomal doxorubicin trade names include:', '["Adriamycin only","Doxil/Caelyx","Ellence only","Idamycin only","Myocet only"]'::jsonb, 'Doxil/Caelyx', 'PEGylated liposomal doxorubicin is marketed as Doxil (US) or Caelyx (Europe/other).'),
('11bd5b60-aa20-5a93-94d9-0a4ffaa83ad9'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 1', 'The FOLFOX regimen is a standard treatment for:', '["Testicular cancer","Colorectal cancer","Hodgkin lymphoma","Glioblastoma","Pancreatic cancer"]'::jsonb, 'Colorectal cancer', 'FOLFOX (5-FU, leucovorin, oxaliplatin) is standard for adjuvant and metastatic colorectal cancer.'),
('3028f7e1-2a44-5886-b21b-1d94b896bcab'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'A 30-year-old female with Pseudomonas bacteremia is treated with gentamicin. The patient develops tinnitus, vertigo, and rising creatinine. (Chemo Case #1)', '["Ototoxicity and nephrotoxicity","Myelosuppression and cardiotoxicity","Hepatotoxicity and tendon rupture","Hemolytic anemia","Pulmonary fibrosis and neuropathy"]'::jsonb, 'Ototoxicity and nephrotoxicity', 'Aminoglycosides are classically associated with ototoxicity and nephrotoxicity.'),
