('af3b6d0b-227b-5fe7-9b2d-1871b63b0852'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'A 45-year-old mechanic on aminoglycoside therapy develops hearing loss and kidney dysfunction. (Chemo Case #68)', '["Hemolytic anemia","Hepatotoxicity and tendon rupture","Pulmonary fibrosis and neuropathy","Ototoxicity and nephrotoxicity","Myelosuppression and cardiotoxicity"]'::jsonb, 'Ototoxicity and nephrotoxicity', 'Aminoglycosides are classically associated with ototoxicity and nephrotoxicity.'),
('ad615686-1686-5443-9d47-cfb84ca6b2e4'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'In the study of Chemotherapy Pt2, Vinca alkaloids work by:', '["Stabilizing microtubules","Inhibiting microtubule polymerization (assembly)","Disrupting DNA replication","Inhibiting protein synthesis","Inhibiting RNA transcription"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibiting microtubule polymerization (assembly)', 'Vinca alkaloids bind tubulin and inhibit its polymerization into microtubules, disrupting the mitotic spindle and arresting cells in metaphase.'),
('3bf7e56a-f195-525c-811e-12291c069c34'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Chemotherapy Pt2: Dexrazoxane should only be used when doxorubicin dose exceeds:', '["100 mg/m2","300 mg/m2","500 mg/m2","1000 mg/m2","Any dose"]'::jsonb, '300 mg/m2', 'Dexrazoxane is generally recommended for patients who have received >300 mg/m2 doxorubicin.'),
('c6f64722-f77b-565f-b50f-55f77055a130'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Chemotherapy Pt2: Irinotecan-induced late diarrhea can be managed with:', '["Atropine","High-dose loperamide","Ondansetron","Dexamethasone","Ranitidine"]'::jsonb, 'High-dose loperamide', 'Early-onset diarrhea (<24h) is cholinergic and responds to atropine. Late-onset diarrhea (>24h) is managed with high-dose loperamide.'),
('81c25c02-fe11-5073-89c2-186797dcf2fd'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'A 40-year-old female presents with a fever, headache, and a macular rash on the wrists and ankles following a tick bite (Rocky Mountain spotted fever). The patient is started on doxycycline. What is the mechanism of action of this drug?', '["Inhibits DNA-dependent RNA polymerase","Inhibits bacterial cell wall synthesis by binding to D-Ala-D-Ala","Binds to the 30S ribosomal subunit, preventing aminoacyl-tRNA binding","Binds to the 50S ribosomal subunit, blocking transpeptidation","Inhibits topoisomerase II (DNA gyrase)"]'::jsonb, 'Binds to the 30S ribosomal subunit, preventing aminoacyl-tRNA binding', 'Doxycycline is a tetracycline. Tetracyclines bind reversibly to the 30S ribosomal subunit in bacteria, blocking the binding of aminoacyl-tRNA to the A site, thereby inhibiting protein synthesis.'),
('b4820134-0c9d-59da-bd00-83a196169c51'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Chemotherapy Pt2: Paclitaxel requires premedication because of:', '["Nephrotoxicity resulting in decreased functional activity","Hypersensitivity/anaphylaxis reactions (Cremophor EL vehicle)","Hepatotoxicity mediated by specific receptor activation","Pulmonary fibrosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cerebellar ataxia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hypersensitivity/anaphylaxis reactions (Cremophor EL vehicle)', 'Paclitaxel (especially Cremophor EL formulation) causes severe hypersensitivity. Premedication with dexamethasone, diphenhydramine, and H2-blockers is mandatory.'),
('869afa3b-1306-5214-9bd5-b600a241faa5'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'A 65-year-old female with a severe Pseudomonas aeruginosa bacteremia is treated with high-dose gentamicin. After several days of therapy, the patient complains of ringing in the ears (tinnitus) and unsteady gait, and labs show a rising serum creatinine. Which organ toxicities are most classically associated with this class of antibiotic?', '["Ototoxicity and nephrotoxicity","Myelosuppression and cardiac toxicity","Hepatotoxicity and tendon rupture","Hemolytic anemia and red man syndrome","Pulmonary fibrosis and peripheral neuropathy"]'::jsonb, 'Ototoxicity and nephrotoxicity', 'Aminoglycosides (like gentamicin) accumulate in renal proximal tubule cells and the endolymph/perilymph of the inner ear, leading to acute tubular necrosis (nephrotoxicity) and destruction of sensory hair cells (ototoxicity, presenting as vestibular/auditory deficits).'),
('fc00d720-1e34-5ea0-b975-7c8c38b53fba'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'Ciltacabtagene autoleucel (Carvykti) targets which antigen for multiple myeloma?', '["CD19 resulting in decreased functional activity","CD20 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","BCMA (B-cell maturation antigen)","CD33 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","HER2 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'BCMA (B-cell maturation antigen)', 'Cilta-cel is an anti-BCMA CAR-T therapy for relapsed/refractory multiple myeloma.'),
('a9550430-3a1b-5bd1-95ad-3cb12fa1a6f5'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'Cytokine release syndrome (CRS) in CAR-T therapy is characterized by:', '["Hypotension, fever, and elevated cytokines","Neutropenia only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anemia mediated by specific receptor activation","Thrombocytopenia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Skin rash only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hypotension, fever, and elevated cytokines', 'CRS presents with fever, hypotension, hypoxia, and elevated IL-6, IL-10, and IFN-gamma.'),
('606bc01a-868b-545c-806b-a48c6fac23df'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'A 65-year-old male presents with a localized skin infection. Culturing reveals Staphylococcus aureus that is resistant to penicillin G. Which bacterial mechanism is primarily responsible for this resistance?', '["Production of beta-lactamase (penicillinase)","Ribosomal methylation","Alteration of penicillin-binding proteins (PBPs)","Active efflux pumps","Mutated DNA gyrase"]'::jsonb, 'Production of beta-lactamase (penicillinase)', 'Most S. aureus strains are resistant to penicillin G due to plasmid-encoded beta-lactamase, which hydrolyzes the beta-lactam ring. Methicillin resistance (MRSA) is instead mediated by altered penicillin-binding proteins (PBP2a encoded by mecA).'),
('8c5ebec3-529b-5c25-a6eb-fa080d6c838a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'Immune effector cell-associated neurotoxicity syndrome (ICANS) after CAR-T presents with:', '["Headache only resulting in decreased functional activity","Confusion, aphasia, seizures, cerebral edema","Peripheral neuropathy mediated by specific receptor activation","Hearing loss without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Visual loss leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Confusion, aphasia, seizures, cerebral edema', 'ICANS is a neurotoxicity syndrome causing encephalopathy, potentially progressing to cerebral edema.'),
('7b770d14-d804-527c-a559-6939d6b7b90a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'CAR-T cell therapy requires which manufacturing step?', '["Using allogeneic cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Leukapheresis, T-cell expansion, genetic engineering, and re-infusion","Direct intratumoral injection mediated by specific receptor activation","IV immunoglobulin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Stem cell transplant leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Leukapheresis, T-cell expansion, genetic engineering, and re-infusion', 'CAR-T manufacturing involves collecting patient T-cells, engineering them, expanding in culture, then re-infusing.'),
('98141434-b218-5124-9828-c10af362ae20'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'Tumor lysis syndrome (TLS) risk after CAR-T therapy is due to:', '["Direct drug toxicity resulting in decreased functional activity","Rapid tumor cell killing by engineered T-cells","Infection mediated by specific receptor activation","Autoimmunity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Drug allergy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Rapid tumor cell killing by engineered T-cells', 'Rapid lysis of tumor cells releases intracellular contents, causing TLS (hyperkalemia, hyperuricemia).'),
('9abb2990-a0c8-5d28-ad45-49e74b3ddd22'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'Which CAR-T therapy is approved for relapsed/refractory follicular lymphoma?', '["Tisagenlecleucel","Axicabtagene ciloleucel and tisagenlecleucel","Ciltacabtagene autoleucel","Brexucabtagene autoleucel","Lisocabtagene maraleucel"]'::jsonb, 'Axicabtagene ciloleucel and tisagenlecleucel', 'Both axi-cel and tisa-cel are approved for relapsed/refractory follicular lymphoma.'),
('1444e0aa-41ee-5d9a-9a13-6c91bb8de039'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'A 50-year-old female presents with a localized skin infection. Culturing reveals Staphylococcus aureus that is resistant to penicillin G. Which bacterial mechanism is primarily responsible for this resistance?', '["Mutated DNA gyrase","Ribosomal methylation","Production of beta-lactamase (penicillinase)","Alteration of penicillin-binding proteins (PBPs)","Active efflux pumps"]'::jsonb, 'Production of beta-lactamase (penicillinase)', 'Most S. aureus strains are resistant to penicillin G due to plasmid-encoded beta-lactamase, which hydrolyzes the beta-lactam ring. Methicillin resistance (MRSA) is instead mediated by altered penicillin-binding proteins (PBP2a encoded by mecA).'),
('008fa90d-fe8b-55f9-b319-1160ee53c428'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'Tamoxifen is the standard of care for:', '["Pre-menopausal ER+ breast cancer","Post-menopausal only","ER- breast cancer","Triple-negative breast cancer","HER2+ breast cancer"]'::jsonb, 'Pre-menopausal ER+ breast cancer', 'Tamoxifen is the standard endocrine therapy for pre-menopausal women with ER+ breast cancer.'),
('69affd8b-0c32-56ad-9a4a-2aa20f8b4433'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'A 25-year-old male with a severe Pseudomonas aeruginosa bacteremia is treated with high-dose gentamicin. After several days of therapy, the patient complains of ringing in the ears (tinnitus) and unsteady gait, and labs show a rising serum creatinine. Which organ toxicities are most classically associated with this class of antibiotic?', '["Pulmonary fibrosis and peripheral neuropathy","Ototoxicity and nephrotoxicity","Hepatotoxicity and tendon rupture","Myelosuppression and cardiac toxicity","Hemolytic anemia and red man syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'Ototoxicity and nephrotoxicity', 'Aminoglycosides (like gentamicin) accumulate in renal proximal tubule cells and the endolymph/perilymph of the inner ear, leading to acute tubular necrosis (nephrotoxicity) and destruction of sensory hair cells (ototoxicity, presenting as vestibular/auditory deficits).'),
('152af992-8dd8-5afc-9905-632360a8f2da'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'A 60-year-old female presents with a fever, headache, and a macular rash on the wrists and ankles following a tick bite (Rocky Mountain spotted fever). The patient is started on doxycycline. What is the mechanism of action of this drug?', '["Inhibits DNA-dependent RNA polymerase","Inhibits bacterial cell wall synthesis by binding to D-Ala-D-Ala","Inhibits topoisomerase II (DNA gyrase)","Binds to the 30S ribosomal subunit, preventing aminoacyl-tRNA binding","Binds to the 50S ribosomal subunit, blocking transpeptidation"]'::jsonb, 'Binds to the 30S ribosomal subunit, preventing aminoacyl-tRNA binding', 'Doxycycline is a tetracycline. Tetracyclines bind reversibly to the 30S ribosomal subunit in bacteria, blocking the binding of aminoacyl-tRNA to the A site, thereby inhibiting protein synthesis.'),
('40fc62cb-4458-5f7b-a923-ab8712879560'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'Aromatase inhibitors are indicated in:', '["Pre-menopausal women","Post-menopausal women with ER+ breast cancer","All breast cancers","ER- breast cancer","Triple-negative breast cancer"]'::jsonb, 'Post-menopausal women with ER+ breast cancer', 'AIs are first-line for post-menopausal ER+ breast cancer by blocking peripheral estrogen synthesis.'),
('79d4ad87-16c2-5d54-b78b-63f483ca34ac'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'Aromatase inhibitors cause which characteristic side effect?', '["Hepatotoxicity resulting in decreased functional activity","Musculoskeletal symptoms and osteoporosis","Nephrotoxicity mediated by specific receptor activation","Cardiomyopathy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Neuropathy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Musculoskeletal symptoms and osteoporosis', 'AIs cause arthralgia, bone loss, and fractures due to estrogen deprivation.'),
('90f2a87d-5c94-501d-b368-1244d5676a02'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'A 30-year-old female with a severe Pseudomonas aeruginosa bacteremia is treated with high-dose gentamicin. After several days of therapy, the patient complains of ringing in the ears (tinnitus) and unsteady gait, and labs show a rising serum creatinine. Which organ toxicities are most classically associated with this class of antibiotic?', '["Hemolytic anemia and red man syndrome","Hepatotoxicity and tendon rupture","Pulmonary fibrosis and peripheral neuropathy","Ototoxicity and nephrotoxicity","Myelosuppression and cardiac toxicity"]'::jsonb, 'Ototoxicity and nephrotoxicity', 'Aminoglycosides (like gentamicin) accumulate in renal proximal tubule cells and the endolymph/perilymph of the inner ear, leading to acute tubular necrosis (nephrotoxicity) and destruction of sensory hair cells (ototoxicity, presenting as vestibular/auditory deficits).'),
('22077bfe-cbff-5397-96df-b91436ad7adc'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'In the study of Chemotherapy Pt2, Aggressive hydration with cisplatin is used to:', '["Prevent allergic reactions resulting in decreased functional activity","Prevent nephrotoxicity by maintaining high urine output","Enhance drug absorption mediated by specific receptor activation","Prevent hepatotoxicity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Reduce myelosuppression leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Prevent nephrotoxicity by maintaining high urine output', 'Cisplatin causes renal tubular damage worsened by dehydration. Aggressive hydration maintains high urine flow.'),
('b0d4568c-5445-5ba1-a4e1-6cfc4982bd99'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Chemotherapy Pt2: The mechanism of action of platinum compounds involves:', '["Inhibition of microtubule assembly resulting in decreased functional activity","Formation of intrastrand and interstrand DNA cross-links","Inhibition of topoisomerase mediated by specific receptor activation","Inhibition of DHFR without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Alkylation of RNA leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Formation of intrastrand and interstrand DNA cross-links', 'Platinum compounds form reactive complexes that bind DNA, creating intrastrand and interstrand cross-links at N7 of guanine/adenine.'),
('7400fce3-dc8d-50d9-9ba0-02145104f744'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'A 45-year-old male with a severe Pseudomonas aeruginosa bacteremia is treated with high-dose gentamicin. After several days of therapy, the patient complains of ringing in the ears (tinnitus) and unsteady gait, and labs show a rising serum creatinine. Which organ toxicities are most classically associated with this class of antibiotic?', '["Myelosuppression and cardiac toxicity","Ototoxicity and nephrotoxicity","Hepatotoxicity and tendon rupture","Hemolytic anemia and red man syndrome","Pulmonary fibrosis and peripheral neuropathy"]'::jsonb, 'Ototoxicity and nephrotoxicity', 'Aminoglycosides (like gentamicin) accumulate in renal proximal tubule cells and the endolymph/perilymph of the inner ear, leading to acute tubular necrosis (nephrotoxicity) and destruction of sensory hair cells (ototoxicity, presenting as vestibular/auditory deficits).'),
('03fa9e4a-b36d-5c1f-b57c-c1f364dedb35'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of Chemotherapy Pt2, The dose-limiting toxicity of carboplatin is:', '["Nephrotoxicity resulting in decreased functional activity","Thrombocytopenia (myelosuppression)","Neurotoxicity mediated by specific receptor activation","Ototoxicity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cardiotoxicity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thrombocytopenia (myelosuppression)', 'Carboplatin''s DLT is myelosuppression, particularly thrombocytopenia.'),
('db9d6f46-4d65-519c-a026-df6d2043ecd2'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'A 70-year-old female with a severe Pseudomonas aeruginosa bacteremia is treated with high-dose gentamicin. After several days of therapy, the patient complains of ringing in the ears (tinnitus) and unsteady gait, and labs show a rising serum creatinine. Which organ toxicities are most classically associated with this class of antibiotic?', '["Ototoxicity and nephrotoxicity","Myelosuppression and cardiac toxicity","Pulmonary fibrosis and peripheral neuropathy","Hepatotoxicity and tendon rupture","Hemolytic anemia and red man syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'Ototoxicity and nephrotoxicity', 'Aminoglycosides (like gentamicin) accumulate in renal proximal tubule cells and the endolymph/perilymph of the inner ear, leading to acute tubular necrosis (nephrotoxicity) and destruction of sensory hair cells (ototoxicity, presenting as vestibular/auditory deficits).'),
('d51ba47e-0a8e-5682-9060-d54b9ed6e95f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'A 35-year-old female presents with a fever, headache, and a macular rash on the wrists and ankles following a tick bite (Rocky Mountain spotted fever). The patient is started on doxycycline. What is the mechanism of action of this drug?', '["Inhibits DNA-dependent RNA polymerase","Binds to the 50S ribosomal subunit, blocking transpeptidation","Inhibits topoisomerase II (DNA gyrase)","Inhibits bacterial cell wall synthesis by binding to D-Ala-D-Ala","Binds to the 30S ribosomal subunit, preventing aminoacyl-tRNA binding"]'::jsonb, 'Binds to the 30S ribosomal subunit, preventing aminoacyl-tRNA binding', 'Doxycycline is a tetracycline. Tetracyclines bind reversibly to the 30S ribosomal subunit in bacteria, blocking the binding of aminoacyl-tRNA to the A site, thereby inhibiting protein synthesis.'),
('d7a9b331-b878-5a80-b78f-09b51bf7ce5a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'GnRH antagonists (degarelix) differ from agonists by:', '["Causing initial flare resulting in decreased functional activity","Directly blocking GnRH receptors without initial testosterone surge","Being oral formulations mediated by specific receptor activation","Being less effective without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Being more expensive without benefit leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Directly blocking GnRH receptors without initial testosterone surge', 'GnRH antagonists like degarelix directly block GnRH receptors, avoiding the testosterone flare seen with agonists.'),
('572d2658-450f-52fc-b2c5-08c53c471123'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'Nilutamide is an anti-androgen used in combination with surgical castration for:', '["Breast cancer","Metastatic prostate cancer","Lung cancer","Colon cancer","Lymphoma"]'::jsonb, 'Metastatic prostate cancer', 'Nilutamide is used with orchiectomy in metastatic prostate cancer (combined androgen blockade).'),
('c0c53dec-685e-5541-8477-913a99598212'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'Fulvestrant is a selective estrogen receptor degrader (SERD) that:', '["Stimulates estrogen receptors resulting in decreased functional activity","Destroys estrogen receptors, used in ER+ metastatic breast cancer","Inhibits aromatase mediated by specific receptor activation","Blocks progesterone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inhibits HER2 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Destroys estrogen receptors, used in ER+ metastatic breast cancer', 'Fulvestrant degrades the estrogen receptor and is used for advanced ER+ breast cancer after progression on tamoxifen/AIs.'),
('c0c7519b-946a-5a5d-a906-f81376dc9398'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'Toremifene is a SERM similar to tamoxifen used in:', '["Prostate cancer","Post-menopausal ER+ breast cancer","Lung cancer","All breast cancers","Liver cancer"]'::jsonb, 'Post-menopausal ER+ breast cancer', 'Toremifene is a chlorinated tamoxifen analog approved for metastatic ER+ breast cancer.'),
('42c8ee69-5039-5c34-8bd6-8f8c97d51668'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'Megestrol acetate is a progestational agent used in:', '["Prostate cancer resulting in decreased functional activity","Appetite stimulation and endometrial cancer","Breast cancer only mediated by specific receptor activation","All cancers without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Leukemia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Appetite stimulation and endometrial cancer', 'Megestrol acetate is used for appetite stimulation in cancer cachexia and in endometrial cancer.'),
('8dc75e22-8f8b-508b-8bff-3dce6ee210bf'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Chemotherapy Pt2: High-dose MTX with leucovorin rescue is key for:', '["Breast cancer","Osteosarcoma and CNS lymphoma","Colon cancer","Lung cancer","Pancreatic cancer"]'::jsonb, 'Osteosarcoma and CNS lymphoma', 'High-dose MTX with leucovorin rescue is a cornerstone of osteosarcoma (MAP regimen) and primary CNS lymphoma.'),
('e28434ab-661e-5f82-a834-0bcde65c24bb'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'Bicalutamide versus flutamide in prostate cancer:', '["Bicalutamide is more hepatotoxic","Bicalutamide has less GI toxicity and is taken once daily","Flutamide is more potent","Both have equal side effects","Bicalutamide requires TID dosing"]'::jsonb, 'Bicalutamide has less GI toxicity and is taken once daily', 'Bicalutamide is better tolerated with lower hepatotoxicity and convenient once-daily dosing.'),
('3e63b65e-903a-5688-be15-c48e52cd1a51'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'Amphotericin B exerts antifungal activity by:', '["Inhibiting ergosterol synthesis resulting in decreased functional activity","Binding ergosterol and forming pores in the fungal cell membrane","Inhibiting cell wall synthesis mediated by specific receptor activation","Inhibiting fungal DNA synthesis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Blocking protein synthesis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Binding ergosterol and forming pores in the fungal cell membrane', 'Amphotericin B binds ergosterol in the fungal membrane, forming pores leading to cell death.'),
('34dab3f8-16d8-5179-9446-72b3f06e0d7a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'Liposomal amphotericin B (AmBisome) has:', '["Greater nephrotoxicity than conventional resulting in decreased functional activity","Less nephrotoxicity and infusion-related reactions than conventional","Narrower spectrum mediated by specific receptor activation","Oral formulation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","No antifungal activity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Less nephrotoxicity and infusion-related reactions than conventional', 'Liposomal formulation reduces nephrotoxicity and infusion reactions while maintaining efficacy.'),
('1e98f306-6da3-52bf-9911-42c0b84c3139'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'Amphotericin B is the drug of choice for:', '["Oral thrush resulting in decreased functional activity","Mucormycosis and severe invasive fungal infections","Dermatophyte infection mediated by specific receptor activation","Nail fungus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vaginal candidiasis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mucormycosis and severe invasive fungal infections', 'Amphotericin B is first-line for life-threatening infections including mucormycosis.'),
('565f9b91-4948-5993-8c73-bd459f25fc48'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Chemotherapy Pt2: Nephrotoxicity as PRIMARY DLT is characteristic of:', '["Carboplatin","Cisplatin","Oxaliplatin","Doxorubicin","Vincristine"]'::jsonb, 'Cisplatin', 'Cisplatin has nephrotoxicity as its primary DLT.'),
('2d1705ae-dfbf-53a1-8ee0-01b8ba50b9e3'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'A 45-year-old female presents with a localized skin infection. Culturing reveals Staphylococcus aureus that is resistant to penicillin G. Which bacterial mechanism is primarily responsible for this resistance?', '["Active efflux pumps","Alteration of penicillin-binding proteins (PBPs)","Production of beta-lactamase (penicillinase)","Ribosomal methylation","Mutated DNA gyrase"]'::jsonb, 'Production of beta-lactamase (penicillinase)', 'Most S. aureus strains are resistant to penicillin G due to plasmid-encoded beta-lactamase, which hydrolyzes the beta-lactam ring. Methicillin resistance (MRSA) is instead mediated by altered penicillin-binding proteins (PBP2a encoded by mecA).'),
('bfd1dce9-8738-5615-ab09-1605390775cf'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'Fluconazole inhibits which enzyme in fungi?', '["Glucan synthase","14-alpha-demethylase (CYP51)","Squalene epoxidase","DNA polymerase","RNA polymerase"]'::jsonb, '14-alpha-demethylase (CYP51)', 'Azoles including fluconazole inhibit 14-alpha-demethylase, blocking ergosterol synthesis.'),
('26fc45cf-6a6a-5cea-9770-561b25e520b2'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'Voriconazole is first-line for:', '["Cryptococcal meningitis","Invasive aspergillosis","Mucormycosis","Dermatophyte infection","Vaginal candidiasis"]'::jsonb, 'Invasive aspergillosis', 'Voriconazole is the first-line treatment for invasive aspergillosis per guidelines.'),
('694c776d-903b-5dd9-a870-b38211f1870b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'A 45-year-old male presents with a fever, headache, and a macular rash on the wrists and ankles following a tick bite (Rocky Mountain spotted fever). The patient is started on doxycycline. What is the mechanism of action of this drug?', '["Binds to the 30S ribosomal subunit, preventing aminoacyl-tRNA binding","Inhibits bacterial cell wall synthesis by binding to D-Ala-D-Ala","Inhibits topoisomerase II (DNA gyrase)","Binds to the 50S ribosomal subunit, blocking transpeptidation","Inhibits DNA-dependent RNA polymerase"]'::jsonb, 'Binds to the 30S ribosomal subunit, preventing aminoacyl-tRNA binding', 'Doxycycline is a tetracycline. Tetracyclines bind reversibly to the 30S ribosomal subunit in bacteria, blocking the binding of aminoacyl-tRNA to the A site, thereby inhibiting protein synthesis.'),
('e7a7910d-05a9-579f-9315-57708788fc21'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Chemotherapy Pt2: Topical 5-fluorouracil is used for which skin condition?', '["Melanoma resulting in decreased functional activity","Actinic keratoses and superficial basal cell carcinoma","Invasive squamous cell carcinoma mediated by specific receptor activation","Dermatofibrosarcoma protuberans without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Kaposi sarcoma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Actinic keratoses and superficial basal cell carcinoma', 'Topical 5-FU (5% cream) is widely used for actinic keratoses, superficial basal cell carcinoma, and Bowen disease.'),
('799a5d34-a7d3-590b-9cd7-c7b57e69695e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'Isavuconazole is approved for:', '["Only aspergillosis","Invasive aspergillosis and mucormycosis","Only candidiasis","Only cryptococcosis","Only dermatophytosis"]'::jsonb, 'Invasive aspergillosis and mucormycosis', 'Isavuconazole is approved for invasive aspergillosis and mucormycosis with less QT prolongation than voriconazole.'),
('919d7f2b-ae56-56d7-aa81-a96eb6fb577f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'A 25-year-old male presents with a localized skin infection. Culturing reveals Staphylococcus aureus that is resistant to penicillin G. Which bacterial mechanism is primarily responsible for this resistance?', '["Active efflux pumps","Alteration of penicillin-binding proteins (PBPs)","Mutated DNA gyrase","Production of beta-lactamase (penicillinase)","Ribosomal methylation"]'::jsonb, 'Production of beta-lactamase (penicillinase)', 'Most S. aureus strains are resistant to penicillin G due to plasmid-encoded beta-lactamase, which hydrolyzes the beta-lactam ring. Methicillin resistance (MRSA) is instead mediated by altered penicillin-binding proteins (PBP2a encoded by mecA).'),
('1c626982-dfc2-5cc7-afe8-eb49df339d3d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'Caspofungin belongs to which antifungal class?', '["Azoles resulting in decreased functional activity","Echinocandins (inhibits beta-1,3-glucan synthase)","Polyenes mediated by specific receptor activation","Allylamines without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Antimetabolites leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Echinocandins (inhibits beta-1,3-glucan synthase)', 'Caspofungin is an echinocandin that inhibits beta-1,3-glucan synthase, disrupting the fungal cell wall.'),
('6e024cb4-cceb-53b0-979e-bd81ee555405'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'In the study of Chemotherapy Pt2, Alkylating agents are classified as:', '["Cell cycle specific (CCS)","Cell cycle non-specific (CCNS)","Phase-specific only (G1)","Phase-specific only (S)","Phase-specific only (M)"]'::jsonb, 'Cell cycle non-specific (CCNS)', 'Alkylating agents are cell cycle non-specific, killing cells in any phase including resting G0 cells.'),
('a8b9a19e-e2e8-5001-8ebb-f912bc5bf614'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'A 30-year-old male with a severe Pseudomonas aeruginosa bacteremia is treated with high-dose gentamicin. After several days of therapy, the patient complains of ringing in the ears (tinnitus) and unsteady gait, and labs show a rising serum creatinine. Which organ toxicities are most classically associated with this class of antibiotic?', '["Hemolytic anemia and red man syndrome","Ototoxicity and nephrotoxicity","Pulmonary fibrosis and peripheral neuropathy","Myelosuppression and cardiac toxicity","Hepatotoxicity and tendon rupture"]'::jsonb, 'Ototoxicity and nephrotoxicity', 'Aminoglycosides (like gentamicin) accumulate in renal proximal tubule cells and the endolymph/perilymph of the inner ear, leading to acute tubular necrosis (nephrotoxicity) and destruction of sensory hair cells (ototoxicity, presenting as vestibular/auditory deficits).'),
('bcd3bb0d-227b-5e59-ba10-3ce07d70dfa0'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Chemotherapy Pt2: Dexrazoxane works by:', '["Enhancing doxorubicin metabolism","Chelating iron, preventing free radical formation in cardiac tissue","Binding to doxorubicin in bloodstream","Enhancing renal excretion","Inhibiting topoisomerase II in heart"]'::jsonb, 'Chelating iron, preventing free radical formation in cardiac tissue', 'Dexrazoxane enters cardiac cells and hydrolyzes to ADR-925, an iron chelator that prevents free radical formation.'),
('f0d1ac9f-9d52-5fa5-a68f-6b43263399cc'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of Chemotherapy Pt2, Dexrazoxane protects against doxorubicin cardiotoxicity by:', '["Inhibiting topoisomerase II","Chelating iron and preventing free radical formation in the heart","Enhancing doxorubicin clearance","Increasing doxorubicin uptake into tumors","Blocking DNA intercalation"]'::jsonb, 'Chelating iron and preventing free radical formation in the heart', 'Dexrazoxane is an iron chelator that removes iron from cardiac myocytes, preventing iron-mediated free radical formation.'),
('aef222a2-df75-5980-b6ac-32ba18db3c86'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'Terbinafine inhibits:', '["14-alpha-demethylase","Squalene epoxidase","Glucan synthase","DNA polymerase","RNA polymerase"]'::jsonb, 'Squalene epoxidase', 'Terbinafine inhibits squalene epoxidase, leading to ergosterol deficiency and toxic squalene accumulation.'),
('032389f8-aa06-5ac9-ad02-2f9e5d64125d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'A 40-year-old female with a severe Pseudomonas aeruginosa bacteremia is treated with high-dose gentamicin. After several days of therapy, the patient complains of ringing in the ears (tinnitus) and unsteady gait, and labs show a rising serum creatinine. Which organ toxicities are most classically associated with this class of antibiotic?', '["Myelosuppression and cardiac toxicity","Ototoxicity and nephrotoxicity","Hemolytic anemia and red man syndrome","Pulmonary fibrosis and peripheral neuropathy","Hepatotoxicity and tendon rupture"]'::jsonb, 'Ototoxicity and nephrotoxicity', 'Aminoglycosides (like gentamicin) accumulate in renal proximal tubule cells and the endolymph/perilymph of the inner ear, leading to acute tubular necrosis (nephrotoxicity) and destruction of sensory hair cells (ototoxicity, presenting as vestibular/auditory deficits).'),
('1d1d3e15-0306-59ad-9855-dde1a2918f2b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'A 25-year-old female presents with a localized skin infection. Culturing reveals Staphylococcus aureus that is resistant to penicillin G. Which bacterial mechanism is primarily responsible for this resistance?', '["Active efflux pumps","Ribosomal methylation","Alteration of penicillin-binding proteins (PBPs)","Mutated DNA gyrase","Production of beta-lactamase (penicillinase)"]'::jsonb, 'Production of beta-lactamase (penicillinase)', 'Most S. aureus strains are resistant to penicillin G due to plasmid-encoded beta-lactamase, which hydrolyzes the beta-lactam ring. Methicillin resistance (MRSA) is instead mediated by altered penicillin-binding proteins (PBP2a encoded by mecA).'),
('d96326da-116e-51fd-80d9-2bc9c3835a4d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'Anidulafungin is an echinocandin with:', '["Hepatic metabolism resulting in decreased functional activity","Non-hepatic elimination (chemical degradation)","Renal clearance mediated by specific receptor activation","Oral bioavailability without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CYP450 metabolism leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Non-hepatic elimination (chemical degradation)', 'Anidulafungin is chemically degraded (not metabolized hepatically), safe in liver disease.'),
('233af52a-98cb-5167-8904-7a906d5d9b4d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of Chemotherapy Pt2, Deoxycytidine kinase (dCK) deficiency causes resistance to:', '["Methotrexate","Cytarabine and gemcitabine","5-FU","Pemetrexed","Capecitabine"]'::jsonb, 'Cytarabine and gemcitabine', 'Both cytarabine and gemcitabine require activation by dCK. Deficiency prevents phosphorylation to active forms.'),
('f9c4106e-21c6-53f3-991c-b0d5dfa018c8'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Chemotherapy Pt2: Etoposide is a key component of which regimen for testicular cancer?', '["CHOP resulting in decreased functional activity","BEP (bleomycin, etoposide, cisplatin)","ABVD mediated by specific receptor activation","FOLFOX without affecting the primary tissue pathway","MVAC leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'BEP (bleomycin, etoposide, cisplatin)', 'BEP is the standard curative regimen for metastatic testicular cancer, achieving >90% cure rates.'),
('35045a0e-d33a-53df-ac78-c6aadb19fd8c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'A 55-year-old male presents with a localized skin infection. Culturing reveals Staphylococcus aureus that is resistant to penicillin G. Which bacterial mechanism is primarily responsible for this resistance?', '["Active efflux pumps","Ribosomal methylation","Production of beta-lactamase (penicillinase)","Mutated DNA gyrase","Alteration of penicillin-binding proteins (PBPs)"]'::jsonb, 'Production of beta-lactamase (penicillinase)', 'Most S. aureus strains are resistant to penicillin G due to plasmid-encoded beta-lactamase, which hydrolyzes the beta-lactam ring. Methicillin resistance (MRSA) is instead mediated by altered penicillin-binding proteins (PBP2a encoded by mecA).'),
('9ea08890-e3a8-5826-ad5d-34288af024d9'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Chemotherapy Pt2: PEGylated liposomal doxorubicin trade names include:', '["Adriamycin only","Doxil/Caelyx","Ellence only","Idamycin only","Myocet only"]'::jsonb, 'Doxil/Caelyx', 'PEGylated liposomal doxorubicin is marketed as Doxil (US) or Caelyx (Europe/other).'),
('dbbb7b79-44dc-5041-98e6-3efcefd9d91e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Chemotherapy Pt2: Etoposide is derived from which natural product?', '["Pacific yew bark","Podophyllotoxin (from mayapple)","Vinca rosea","Streptomyces species","Penicillium species"]'::jsonb, 'Podophyllotoxin (from mayapple)', 'Etoposide is a semi-synthetic glycoside derivative of podophyllotoxin, obtained from the mayapple plant.'),
('6a7b0d77-0ce0-50e2-8747-88d8e38a0fe2'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'Acyclovir requires initial phosphorylation by:', '["Cellular kinases","Viral thymidine kinase (TK)","Viral DNA polymerase","Viral RNA polymerase","Alkaline phosphatase"]'::jsonb, 'Viral thymidine kinase (TK)', 'Acyclovir is selectively phosphorylated by viral TK, conferring selectivity for infected cells.'),
('4498c770-5b94-5140-81c0-f05cb445aa0b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Chemotherapy Pt2: Doxorubicin''s dose-limiting toxicity is:', '["Nephrotoxicity resulting in decreased functional activity","Cumulative cardiotoxicity (dilated cardiomyopathy)","Pulmonary fibrosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Hepatotoxicity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Neurotoxicity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cumulative cardiotoxicity (dilated cardiomyopathy)', 'Doxorubicin causes dose-dependent, cumulative cardiotoxicity leading to dilated cardiomyopathy. Lifetime dose limit is 450-550 mg/m2.'),
('699bc9da-69b9-5585-9729-4691ba8ea4e0'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'A 55-year-old male with a severe Pseudomonas aeruginosa bacteremia is treated with high-dose gentamicin. After several days of therapy, the patient complains of ringing in the ears (tinnitus) and unsteady gait, and labs show a rising serum creatinine. Which organ toxicities are most classically associated with this class of antibiotic?', '["Myelosuppression and cardiac toxicity","Pulmonary fibrosis and peripheral neuropathy","Ototoxicity and nephrotoxicity","Hemolytic anemia and red man syndrome","Hepatotoxicity and tendon rupture"]'::jsonb, 'Ototoxicity and nephrotoxicity', 'Aminoglycosides (like gentamicin) accumulate in renal proximal tubule cells and the endolymph/perilymph of the inner ear, leading to acute tubular necrosis (nephrotoxicity) and destruction of sensory hair cells (ototoxicity, presenting as vestibular/auditory deficits).'),
('b96eaeb5-0e70-5e65-8c74-26c2b83eedfe'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Chemotherapy Pt2: Gemcitabine resistance commonly involves decreased:', '["Thymidine kinase resulting in decreased functional activity","Deoxycytidine kinase (dCK) -- the activating enzyme","Ribonucleotide reductase mediated by specific receptor activation","DNA polymerase without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Topoisomerase leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Deoxycytidine kinase (dCK) -- the activating enzyme', 'Gemcitabine requires activation by deoxycytidine kinase (dCK). Loss of dCK is a major resistance mechanism.'),
('3dae47f8-6772-563b-bb0b-364847d98909'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Chemotherapy Pt2: Irinotecan inhibits which topoisomerase?', '["Topoisomerase I","Topoisomerase II","Topoisomerase III","DNA gyrase","Reverse transcriptase"]'::jsonb, 'Topoisomerase I', 'Irinotecan (CPT-11) is a camptothecin derivative that inhibits topoisomerase I. It is a prodrug converted to SN-38.'),
('5e7d5cfa-f432-5ef3-9598-8e2d2278166c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of Chemotherapy Pt2, Doxorubicin must NEVER be administered via which route?', '["Intravenous","Intravesical","Intrathecal","Intra-arterial","Subcutaneous"]'::jsonb, 'Intrathecal', 'Doxorubicin must NEVER be given intrathecal - accidental intrathecal injection has been fatal.'),
('d210d44f-6383-5ca6-812a-13ae783864f7'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'A 40-year-old male presents with a fever, headache, and a macular rash on the wrists and ankles following a tick bite (Rocky Mountain spotted fever). The patient is started on doxycycline. What is the mechanism of action of this drug?', '["Binds to the 30S ribosomal subunit, preventing aminoacyl-tRNA binding","Inhibits DNA-dependent RNA polymerase","Inhibits topoisomerase II (DNA gyrase)","Inhibits bacterial cell wall synthesis by binding to D-Ala-D-Ala","Binds to the 50S ribosomal subunit, blocking transpeptidation"]'::jsonb, 'Binds to the 30S ribosomal subunit, preventing aminoacyl-tRNA binding', 'Doxycycline is a tetracycline. Tetracyclines bind reversibly to the 30S ribosomal subunit in bacteria, blocking the binding of aminoacyl-tRNA to the A site, thereby inhibiting protein synthesis.'),
('00ee2265-bb9a-5a84-918e-ecd549cb2275'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'A 65-year-old female presents with a localized skin infection. Culturing reveals Staphylococcus aureus that is resistant to penicillin G. Which bacterial mechanism is primarily responsible for this resistance?', '["Mutated DNA gyrase","Production of beta-lactamase (penicillinase)","Ribosomal methylation","Active efflux pumps","Alteration of penicillin-binding proteins (PBPs)"]'::jsonb, 'Production of beta-lactamase (penicillinase)', 'Most S. aureus strains are resistant to penicillin G due to plasmid-encoded beta-lactamase, which hydrolyzes the beta-lactam ring. Methicillin resistance (MRSA) is instead mediated by altered penicillin-binding proteins (PBP2a encoded by mecA).'),
('d05bcea0-f6fb-5f57-9168-e4b2e9afbb5e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'A 40-year-old male with a severe Pseudomonas aeruginosa bacteremia is treated with high-dose gentamicin. After several days of therapy, the patient complains of ringing in the ears (tinnitus) and unsteady gait, and labs show a rising serum creatinine. Which organ toxicities are most classically associated with this class of antibiotic?', '["Hemolytic anemia and red man syndrome","Myelosuppression and cardiac toxicity","Hepatotoxicity and tendon rupture","Pulmonary fibrosis and peripheral neuropathy","Ototoxicity and nephrotoxicity"]'::jsonb, 'Ototoxicity and nephrotoxicity', 'Aminoglycosides (like gentamicin) accumulate in renal proximal tubule cells and the endolymph/perilymph of the inner ear, leading to acute tubular necrosis (nephrotoxicity) and destruction of sensory hair cells (ototoxicity, presenting as vestibular/auditory deficits).'),
('0c63077e-d670-5bb6-b67a-9367218937ca'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'A 35-year-old male with a severe Pseudomonas aeruginosa bacteremia is treated with high-dose gentamicin. After several days of therapy, the patient complains of ringing in the ears (tinnitus) and unsteady gait, and labs show a rising serum creatinine. Which organ toxicities are most classically associated with this class of antibiotic?', '["Myelosuppression and cardiac toxicity","Hepatotoxicity and tendon rupture","Ototoxicity and nephrotoxicity","Pulmonary fibrosis and peripheral neuropathy","Hemolytic anemia and red man syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'Ototoxicity and nephrotoxicity', 'Aminoglycosides (like gentamicin) accumulate in renal proximal tubule cells and the endolymph/perilymph of the inner ear, leading to acute tubular necrosis (nephrotoxicity) and destruction of sensory hair cells (ototoxicity, presenting as vestibular/auditory deficits).'),
('0b34247c-5576-577a-af57-4c2a411fb4d6'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'Foscarnet''s major toxicity is:', '["Cardiotoxicity resulting in decreased functional activity","Nephrotoxicity (requires adequate hydration)","Hepatotoxicity mediated by specific receptor activation","Neuropathy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pulmonary fibrosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Nephrotoxicity (requires adequate hydration)', 'Foscarnet causes nephrotoxicity; hydration and close monitoring of renal function are essential.'),
('7bf453de-c55d-5789-8d6b-5eb5cc988bdd'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Chemotherapy Pt2: Pemetrexed inhibits multiple folate-dependent enzymes including:', '["Thymidylate synthase, DHFR, and GARFT","Only dihydrofolate reductase","Only thymidylate synthase","RNA polymerase only","Topoisomerase I only"]'::jsonb, 'Thymidylate synthase, DHFR, and GARFT', 'Pemetrexed is a multitargeted antifolate that inhibits TS, DHFR, and GARFT, blocking both pyrimidine and purine synthesis.'),
('044b6427-1501-5f3d-a853-8f740f0a3750'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'In the study of Chemotherapy Pt2, Methotrexate acts primarily by inhibiting which enzyme?', '["Dihydrofolate reductase","Thymidylate synthase","DNA gyrase","Topoisomerase I","RNA polymerase"]'::jsonb, 'Dihydrofolate reductase', 'Methotrexate is a folate analogue that competitively inhibits dihydrofolate reductase (DHFR), preventing conversion of dihydrofolate to tetrahydrofolate needed for thymidylate and purine synthesis.'),
('e91a4b4b-cd19-5384-956f-21149f591963'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'Cidofovir is given with probenecid to:', '["Increase absorption resulting in decreased functional activity","Reduce nephrotoxicity by inhibiting renal tubular secretion","Increase potency mediated by specific receptor activation","Reduce cost without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Prevent hypotension leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Reduce nephrotoxicity by inhibiting renal tubular secretion', 'Probenecid inhibits renal tubular secretion of cidofovir, reducing nephrotoxicity.'),
('5529166d-cca5-59fd-87b2-1fb07526d2bb'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'Oseltamivir (Tamiflu) is activated to:', '["Oseltamivir resulting in decreased functional activity","Oseltamivir carboxylate (active neuraminidase inhibitor)","Zanamivir mediated by specific receptor activation","Peramivir without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Baloxavir leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Oseltamivir carboxylate (active neuraminidase inhibitor)', 'Oseltamivir is a prodrug converted by hepatic esterases to the active oseltamivir carboxylate.'),
('c26688bb-0dd1-5218-ac78-74d37d3ac617'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'Oseltamivir inhibits which influenza enzyme?', '["M2 ion channel resulting in decreased functional activity","Neuraminidase (preventing viral release)","RNA polymerase mediated by specific receptor activation","Protease without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Integrase leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Neuraminidase (preventing viral release)', 'Oseltamivir inhibits neuraminidase, preventing release of new virions from infected cells.'),
('4dd2e790-ab95-5354-97a6-4a871ceda57d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'A 50-year-old male with a severe Pseudomonas aeruginosa bacteremia is treated with high-dose gentamicin. After several days of therapy, the patient complains of ringing in the ears (tinnitus) and unsteady gait, and labs show a rising serum creatinine. Which organ toxicities are most classically associated with this class of antibiotic?', '["Hepatotoxicity and tendon rupture","Ototoxicity and nephrotoxicity","Hemolytic anemia and red man syndrome","Myelosuppression and cardiac toxicity","Pulmonary fibrosis and peripheral neuropathy"]'::jsonb, 'Ototoxicity and nephrotoxicity', 'Aminoglycosides (like gentamicin) accumulate in renal proximal tubule cells and the endolymph/perilymph of the inner ear, leading to acute tubular necrosis (nephrotoxicity) and destruction of sensory hair cells (ototoxicity, presenting as vestibular/auditory deficits).'),
('530700ec-efe2-58ad-a129-3adb31916fbf'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'In the study of Chemotherapy Pt2, Paclitaxel requires premedication because of:', '["Nephrotoxicity resulting in decreased functional activity","Hypersensitivity/anaphylaxis reactions (Cremophor EL vehicle)","Hepatotoxicity mediated by specific receptor activation","Pulmonary fibrosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cerebellar ataxia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hypersensitivity/anaphylaxis reactions (Cremophor EL vehicle)', 'Paclitaxel (especially Cremophor EL formulation) causes severe hypersensitivity. Premedication with dexamethasone, diphenhydramine, and H2-blockers is mandatory.'),
('84d76ddb-4b38-5eb6-b7b8-c2cb81492c7e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'Baloxavir is given as:', '["Intranasal","Single oral dose","IV","Inhaled","5-day course"]'::jsonb, 'Single oral dose', 'Baloxavir marboxil is given as a single oral dose, unlike oseltamivir''s 5-day course.'),
('26f78af6-366c-556d-a757-5a136ace7cae'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'A 40-year-old male presents with a localized skin infection. Culturing reveals Staphylococcus aureus that is resistant to penicillin G. Which bacterial mechanism is primarily responsible for this resistance?', '["Production of beta-lactamase (penicillinase)","Alteration of penicillin-binding proteins (PBPs)","Active efflux pumps","Mutated DNA gyrase","Ribosomal methylation"]'::jsonb, 'Production of beta-lactamase (penicillinase)', 'Most S. aureus strains are resistant to penicillin G due to plasmid-encoded beta-lactamase, which hydrolyzes the beta-lactam ring. Methicillin resistance (MRSA) is instead mediated by altered penicillin-binding proteins (PBP2a encoded by mecA).'),
('afe4a72f-930e-5c6b-b8a8-377a9732f897'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'Tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (TDF) is a nucleotide analog active against:', '["Only HSV","HBV and HIV","HCV","CMV","Influenza"]'::jsonb, 'HBV and HIV', 'TDF is active against both HBV and HIV, used in combination antiretroviral therapy.'),
('523abc80-7f6a-54c6-bfc9-39775ebe3e40'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'A 50-year-old female presents with a fever, headache, and a macular rash on the wrists and ankles following a tick bite (Rocky Mountain spotted fever). The patient is started on doxycycline. What is the mechanism of action of this drug?', '["Inhibits bacterial cell wall synthesis by binding to D-Ala-D-Ala","Inhibits topoisomerase II (DNA gyrase)","Binds to the 30S ribosomal subunit, preventing aminoacyl-tRNA binding","Binds to the 50S ribosomal subunit, blocking transpeptidation","Inhibits DNA-dependent RNA polymerase"]'::jsonb, 'Binds to the 30S ribosomal subunit, preventing aminoacyl-tRNA binding', 'Doxycycline is a tetracycline. Tetracyclines bind reversibly to the 30S ribosomal subunit in bacteria, blocking the binding of aminoacyl-tRNA to the A site, thereby inhibiting protein synthesis.'),
('547da9c2-17c5-53ed-9c17-8c2e9cd0fdd9'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'A 45-year-old female with a severe Pseudomonas aeruginosa bacteremia is treated with high-dose gentamicin. After several days of therapy, the patient complains of ringing in the ears (tinnitus) and unsteady gait, and labs show a rising serum creatinine. Which organ toxicities are most classically associated with this class of antibiotic?', '["Ototoxicity and nephrotoxicity","Pulmonary fibrosis and peripheral neuropathy","Hemolytic anemia and red man syndrome","Myelosuppression and cardiac toxicity","Hepatotoxicity and tendon rupture"]'::jsonb, 'Ototoxicity and nephrotoxicity', 'Aminoglycosides (like gentamicin) accumulate in renal proximal tubule cells and the endolymph/perilymph of the inner ear, leading to acute tubular necrosis (nephrotoxicity) and destruction of sensory hair cells (ototoxicity, presenting as vestibular/auditory deficits).'),
('28939de1-fb19-5918-82ae-9cf96906b7cc'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'A 60-year-old female presents with a localized skin infection. Culturing reveals Staphylococcus aureus that is resistant to penicillin G. Which bacterial mechanism is primarily responsible for this resistance?', '["Active efflux pumps","Mutated DNA gyrase","Production of beta-lactamase (penicillinase)","Alteration of penicillin-binding proteins (PBPs)","Ribosomal methylation"]'::jsonb, 'Production of beta-lactamase (penicillinase)', 'Most S. aureus strains are resistant to penicillin G due to plasmid-encoded beta-lactamase, which hydrolyzes the beta-lactam ring. Methicillin resistance (MRSA) is instead mediated by altered penicillin-binding proteins (PBP2a encoded by mecA).'),
('4c2637ed-b195-53ee-b74d-f50e75e6c6bb'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Chemotherapy Pt2: BuCy (busulfan + cyclophosphamide) is used for:', '["Breast cancer resulting in decreased functional activity","Myeloablative conditioning before bone marrow transplant","Lung cancer mediated by specific receptor activation","Colon cancer without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ovarian cancer leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Myeloablative conditioning before bone marrow transplant', 'BuCy is a classic myeloablative conditioning regimen before allogeneic bone marrow transplant.'),
('2749e493-c480-5973-8572-ddc0dce631e7'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'Metronidazole (Flagyl) exerts antiprotozoal activity by:', '["Inhibiting folate synthesis resulting in decreased functional activity","Reduced nitro group causing DNA damage in anaerobic organisms","Inhibiting protein synthesis mediated by specific receptor activation","Disrupting cell membranes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inhibiting DNA gyrase leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Reduced nitro group causing DNA damage in anaerobic organisms', 'Metronidazole is reduced by anaerobic organisms, generating free radicals that damage DNA.'),
('29346606-1716-5380-96ee-9574d4f4da20'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'Metronidazole is effective against all EXCEPT:', '["Giardia lamblia","Entamoeba histolytica","Trichomonas vaginalis","Plasmodium falciparum","Clostridium difficile"]'::jsonb, 'Plasmodium falciparum', 'Metronidazole has no significant activity against Plasmodium species.'),
('f7d3694f-d784-5cee-8044-7dd0c5dc1e92'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'A 60-year-old female with a severe Pseudomonas aeruginosa bacteremia is treated with high-dose gentamicin. After several days of therapy, the patient complains of ringing in the ears (tinnitus) and unsteady gait, and labs show a rising serum creatinine. Which organ toxicities are most classically associated with this class of antibiotic?', '["Hemolytic anemia and red man syndrome","Myelosuppression and cardiac toxicity","Ototoxicity and nephrotoxicity","Pulmonary fibrosis and peripheral neuropathy","Hepatotoxicity and tendon rupture"]'::jsonb, 'Ototoxicity and nephrotoxicity', 'Aminoglycosides (like gentamicin) accumulate in renal proximal tubule cells and the endolymph/perilymph of the inner ear, leading to acute tubular necrosis (nephrotoxicity) and destruction of sensory hair cells (ototoxicity, presenting as vestibular/auditory deficits).'),
('2664c89e-12c7-546d-aa83-e453896cbdfe'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'Chloroquine exerts antimalarial action by:', '["Inhibiting DNA gyrase resulting in decreased functional activity","Inhibiting heme polymerization in the parasite food vacuole","Blocking protein synthesis mediated by specific receptor activation","Inhibiting folate metabolism without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Disrupting cell membranes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibiting heme polymerization in the parasite food vacuole', 'Chloroquine accumulates in the parasite food vacuole, preventing toxic heme conversion to hemozoin.'),
('f7727893-1564-561b-a55d-214c49161d8c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'Chloroquine resistance in P. falciparum is due to:', '["DHFR mutations resulting in decreased functional activity","PfCRT (chloroquine resistance transporter) mutations","16S rRNA mutations mediated by specific receptor activation","Efflux pump mutations in bacteria without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Target modification leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'PfCRT (chloroquine resistance transporter) mutations', 'Mutations in PfCRT on the food vacuole membrane allow chloroquine efflux, reducing drug accumulation.'),
('8fa81388-de34-5e6e-b77c-1ad0eb0045b4'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of Chemotherapy Pt2, Temozolomide is an oral prodrug of which active metabolite?', '["MTIC (3-methyl-(triazen-1-yl)imidazole-4-carboxamide)","Phosphoramide mustard accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Acrolein mediated by specific receptor activation","Chloroacetaldehyde without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Melphalan leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'MTIC (3-methyl-(triazen-1-yl)imidazole-4-carboxamide)', 'Temozolomide is an imidazotetrazine prodrug that spontaneously hydrolyzes to MTIC, the active alkylating species.'),
('6806c89f-b669-5318-9f62-02868792b409'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Chemotherapy Pt2: Dexrazoxane is dosed at what ratio to doxorubicin?', '["1:1 resulting in decreased functional activity","10:1 (dexrazoxane:doxorubicin)","1:10 mediated by specific receptor activation","5:1 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","1:5 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '10:1 (dexrazoxane:doxorubicin)', 'Dexrazoxane is typically dosed at 10:1 ratio to doxorubicin (e.g., 500 mg/m2 for 50 mg/m2 doxorubicin).'),
('6475c053-cb2c-52b5-8dba-097e3a117e8c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'Artemisinin-based combination therapies (ACTs) are recommended because:', '["Artemisinin is cheaper resulting in decreased functional activity","They reduce resistance development by combining rapid-acting and长效 drugs","Artemisinin has long half-life mediated by specific receptor activation","Partner drug acts faster without affecting the primary tissue pathway","They are oral only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'They reduce resistance development by combining rapid-acting and长效 drugs', 'ACTs combine fast-acting artemisinins with longer-acting partner drugs to delay resistance.'),
('0225829e-f4e7-5ea3-a517-730dfab32765'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Chemotherapy Pt2: Combination chemotherapy often combines CCS and CCNS agents because:', '["It reduces overall toxicity resulting in decreased functional activity","CCNS agents kill G0 cells while CCS agents kill actively dividing cells","It prevents all drug resistance mediated by specific receptor activation","It eliminates dose adjustments without affecting the primary tissue pathway","It always improves cure rates leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CCNS agents kill G0 cells while CCS agents kill actively dividing cells', 'Combining CCS and CCNS agents provides complementary cell kill: CCNS agents eliminate quiescent G0 cells while CCS agents target proliferating cells.'),
('a7617262-0551-5e7c-a396-47e9a33b2c20'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Chemotherapy Pt2: The dose-limiting toxicity of irinotecan is:', '["Cardiotoxicity resulting in decreased functional activity","Diarrhea (early cholinergic and late severe) and myelosuppression","Nephrotoxicity mediated by specific receptor activation","Pulmonary fibrosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Neurotoxicity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Diarrhea (early cholinergic and late severe) and myelosuppression', 'Irinotecan''s DLTs are diarrhea (early cholinergic and late-onset) and neutropenia.'),
('e292e3e5-36b8-5867-a394-4a1f415dea7a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'Pentamidine causes characteristic hypoglycemia because:', '["Inhibits insulin resulting in decreased functional activity","Stimulates insulin release from beta-cells","Inhibits glucagon mediated by specific receptor activation","Affects liver without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Blocks glucose absorption leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Stimulates insulin release from beta-cells', 'Pentamidine stimulates insulin release from pancreatic beta-cells, causing hypoglycemia.'),
('35252df7-1ae0-5beb-bb44-a67b0cfd8d34'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'A 55-year-old female with a severe Pseudomonas aeruginosa bacteremia is treated with high-dose gentamicin. After several days of therapy, the patient complains of ringing in the ears (tinnitus) and unsteady gait, and labs show a rising serum creatinine. Which organ toxicities are most classically associated with this class of antibiotic?', '["Ototoxicity and nephrotoxicity","Hepatotoxicity and tendon rupture","Pulmonary fibrosis and peripheral neuropathy","Myelosuppression and cardiac toxicity","Hemolytic anemia and red man syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'Ototoxicity and nephrotoxicity', 'Aminoglycosides (like gentamicin) accumulate in renal proximal tubule cells and the endolymph/perilymph of the inner ear, leading to acute tubular necrosis (nephrotoxicity) and destruction of sensory hair cells (ototoxicity, presenting as vestibular/auditory deficits).'),
('bc90dcae-c0b1-5e95-82ed-23de395f8869'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'A 70-year-old male with a severe Pseudomonas aeruginosa bacteremia is treated with high-dose gentamicin. After several days of therapy, the patient complains of ringing in the ears (tinnitus) and unsteady gait, and labs show a rising serum creatinine. Which organ toxicities are most classically associated with this class of antibiotic?', '["Pulmonary fibrosis and peripheral neuropathy","Hemolytic anemia and red man syndrome","Hepatotoxicity and tendon rupture","Ototoxicity and nephrotoxicity","Myelosuppression and cardiac toxicity"]'::jsonb, 'Ototoxicity and nephrotoxicity', 'Aminoglycosides (like gentamicin) accumulate in renal proximal tubule cells and the endolymph/perilymph of the inner ear, leading to acute tubular necrosis (nephrotoxicity) and destruction of sensory hair cells (ototoxicity, presenting as vestibular/auditory deficits).'),
('8a7d8e1a-8199-5b80-b611-08529468697b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Chemotherapy Pt2: Dexrazoxane is dosed at what ratio to doxorubicin?', '["1:1 resulting in decreased functional activity","10:1 (dexrazoxane:doxorubicin)","1:10 mediated by specific receptor activation","5:1 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","1:5 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '10:1 (dexrazoxane:doxorubicin)', 'Dexrazoxane is typically dosed at 10:1 ratio to doxorubicin (e.g., 500 mg/m2 for 50 mg/m2 doxorubicin).'),
('73004f7b-2ff1-520f-b354-51d3d8138b89'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'A 35-year-old male presents with a fever, headache, and a macular rash on the wrists and ankles following a tick bite (Rocky Mountain spotted fever). The patient is started on doxycycline. What is the mechanism of action of this drug?', '["Inhibits DNA-dependent RNA polymerase","Binds to the 30S ribosomal subunit, preventing aminoacyl-tRNA binding","Inhibits bacterial cell wall synthesis by binding to D-Ala-D-Ala","Binds to the 50S ribosomal subunit, blocking transpeptidation","Inhibits topoisomerase II (DNA gyrase)"]'::jsonb, 'Binds to the 30S ribosomal subunit, preventing aminoacyl-tRNA binding', 'Doxycycline is a tetracycline. Tetracyclines bind reversibly to the 30S ribosomal subunit in bacteria, blocking the binding of aminoacyl-tRNA to the A site, thereby inhibiting protein synthesis.'),
('f68cde38-6093-54b7-b8fe-c8b83fc24a85'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'Doxycycline for malaria prophylaxis is given:', '["Weekly resulting in decreased functional activity","Daily (starting 1-2 days before, during, and 4 weeks after travel)","Monthly mediated by specific receptor activation","As a single dose without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only during travel leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Daily (starting 1-2 days before, during, and 4 weeks after travel)', 'Doxycycline 100 mg daily is taken 1-2 days before, throughout, and 4 weeks after malaria exposure.'),
('1fe7b58b-cf95-5354-9698-02846de315d9'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'Metronidazole is the DOC for:', '["Malaria resulting in decreased functional activity","Trichomonas vaginalis and Giardia lamblia","Aspergillosis mediated by specific receptor activation","HSV encephalitis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tuberculosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Trichomonas vaginalis and Giardia lamblia', 'Metronidazole is first-line for trichomoniasis and giardiasis.'),
('edd524e9-0e70-5c36-a293-a4465ca2ca77'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'In the study of Chemotherapy Pt2, Mitoxantrone differs from doxorubicin by:', '["Being an anthracenedione with less cardiotoxicity","Being a completely different drug class","Causing more pulmonary fibrosis","Being only for solid tumors","Not intercalating DNA"]'::jsonb, 'Being an anthracenedione with less cardiotoxicity', 'Mitoxantrone is an anthracenedione with less cardiotoxicity, used in AML and metastatic breast cancer.'),
('a8c55b90-efa6-5e96-b620-a456db823ff2'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'Paromomycin is an aminoglycoside used for:', '["Bacterial infections resulting in decreased functional activity","Visceral leishmaniasis and intestinal protozoa","Malaria mediated by specific receptor activation","Fungal infections without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Viral infections leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Visceral leishmaniasis and intestinal protozoa', 'Paromomycin is used for visceral leishmaniasis (with miltefosine) and intestinal amoebiasis.'),
('e48a08de-2c78-5ff4-b5fa-0d0e2604f89d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'A 60-year-old male with a severe Pseudomonas aeruginosa bacteremia is treated with high-dose gentamicin. After several days of therapy, the patient complains of ringing in the ears (tinnitus) and unsteady gait, and labs show a rising serum creatinine. Which organ toxicities are most classically associated with this class of antibiotic?', '["Ototoxicity and nephrotoxicity","Pulmonary fibrosis and peripheral neuropathy","Myelosuppression and cardiac toxicity","Hepatotoxicity and tendon rupture","Hemolytic anemia and red man syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'Ototoxicity and nephrotoxicity', 'Aminoglycosides (like gentamicin) accumulate in renal proximal tubule cells and the endolymph/perilymph of the inner ear, leading to acute tubular necrosis (nephrotoxicity) and destruction of sensory hair cells (ototoxicity, presenting as vestibular/auditory deficits).'),
('f4423933-7418-58c6-9bfd-cd6d6ad795f7'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of Chemotherapy Pt2, Hypersensitivity reactions are most common with which platinum compound?', '["Cisplatin resulting in decreased functional activity","Carboplatin (especially after >6 cycles)","Oxaliplatin mediated by specific receptor activation","All equally without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None cause hypersensitivity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Carboplatin (especially after >6 cycles)', 'Carboplatin has the highest rate of hypersensitivity reactions, particularly after multiple cycles (>6).'),
('d02eb73f-eef2-52d4-b7e0-41030b22544b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'Eflornithine (DFMO) is used for:', '["Malaria resulting in decreased functional activity","Trypanosoma brucei gambiense sleeping sickness","Leishmaniasis mediated by specific receptor activation","Chagas disease without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Toxoplasmosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Trypanosoma brucei gambiense sleeping sickness', 'Eflornithine inhibits ornithine decarboxylase and is used for West African trypanosomiasis.'),
('103f5991-af71-5a42-a986-aa628bbe54e2'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'In the study of Chemotherapy Pt2, Chronic low-dose methotrexate causes which hepatic toxicity?', '["Hepatic adenoma","Hepatic fibrosis and cirrhosis","Hemochromatosis","Budd-Chiari syndrome","Peliosis hepatis"]'::jsonb, 'Hepatic fibrosis and cirrhosis', 'Chronic low-dose methotrexate can cause hepatic steatosis, fibrosis, and cirrhosis. Regular LFT monitoring is recommended.'),
('5beba27d-fb5b-5741-866d-4bc642637848'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Chemotherapy Pt2: Gemcitabine is a deoxycytidine analogue that inhibits:', '["RNA polymerase","Ribonucleotide reductase","DNA topoisomerase II","Thymidylate synthase","Dihydrofolate reductase"]'::jsonb, 'Ribonucleotide reductase', 'Gemcitabine is incorporated into DNA causing chain termination and also inhibits ribonucleotide reductase, depleting deoxynucleotide pools.'),
('4a918c7f-5ddf-5d7c-86e7-9480d122cf9a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Chemotherapy Pt2: Which of the following is NOT an antimetabolite?', '["Methotrexate","5-Fluorouracil","Cyclophosphamide","Gemcitabine","Pemetrexed"]'::jsonb, 'Cyclophosphamide', 'Cyclophosphamide is an alkylating agent (nitrogen mustard derivative), not an antimetabolite.'),
('94890772-84a7-5080-b062-c704ab568da2'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'Mebendazole exerts anthelmintic action by:', '["Paralyzing the worm","Inhibiting tubulin polymerization (glucose uptake)","Blocking neuromuscular transmission","Inhibiting DNA synthesis","Disrupting cell membranes"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibiting tubulin polymerization (glucose uptake)', 'Mebendazole binds beta-tubulin, inhibiting microtubule formation and glucose uptake in helminths.'),
('db9f2a9e-c484-54ff-a431-39d92432ef80'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'Mebendazole is effective against:', '["Tapeworm only resulting in decreased functional activity","Roundworm, pinworm, whipworm, hookworm (broad-spectrum nematode)","Fluke only mediated by specific receptor activation","Protozoa without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ectoparasites leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Roundworm, pinworm, whipworm, hookworm (broad-spectrum nematode)', 'Mebendazole is a broad-spectrum anthelmintic effective against most intestinal nematodes.'),
('2401e02c-96c9-5833-8424-7f25cba7aa44'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'A 65-year-old male presents with a fever, headache, and a macular rash on the wrists and ankles following a tick bite (Rocky Mountain spotted fever). The patient is started on doxycycline. What is the mechanism of action of this drug?', '["Inhibits topoisomerase II (DNA gyrase)","Inhibits bacterial cell wall synthesis by binding to D-Ala-D-Ala","Binds to the 50S ribosomal subunit, blocking transpeptidation","Binds to the 30S ribosomal subunit, preventing aminoacyl-tRNA binding","Inhibits DNA-dependent RNA polymerase"]'::jsonb, 'Binds to the 30S ribosomal subunit, preventing aminoacyl-tRNA binding', 'Doxycycline is a tetracycline. Tetracyclines bind reversibly to the 30S ribosomal subunit in bacteria, blocking the binding of aminoacyl-tRNA to the A site, thereby inhibiting protein synthesis.'),
('03fb2db8-e94a-5fc2-ab85-526cd2b7358d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'A 55-year-old male presents with a fever, headache, and a macular rash on the wrists and ankles following a tick bite (Rocky Mountain spotted fever). The patient is started on doxycycline. What is the mechanism of action of this drug?', '["Binds to the 50S ribosomal subunit, blocking transpeptidation","Inhibits bacterial cell wall synthesis by binding to D-Ala-D-Ala","Inhibits DNA-dependent RNA polymerase","Inhibits topoisomerase II (DNA gyrase)","Binds to the 30S ribosomal subunit, preventing aminoacyl-tRNA binding"]'::jsonb, 'Binds to the 30S ribosomal subunit, preventing aminoacyl-tRNA binding', 'Doxycycline is a tetracycline. Tetracyclines bind reversibly to the 30S ribosomal subunit in bacteria, blocking the binding of aminoacyl-tRNA to the A site, thereby inhibiting protein synthesis.'),
('62e0c100-8be0-5cef-baa7-2c4197d90868'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Chemotherapy Pt2: Oxaliplatin differs from cisplatin in that:', '["It is more nephrotoxic resulting in decreased functional activity","It is effective against cisplatin-resistant tumors and less nephrotoxic","It requires more aggressive hydration mediated by specific receptor activation","It causes more ototoxicity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","It is not a platinum compound leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'It is effective against cisplatin-resistant tumors and less nephrotoxic', 'Oxaliplatin is active against cisplatin-resistant tumors, has less nephrotoxicity/ototoxicity, and is effective in colorectal cancer.'),
('1de4e07d-7a17-5e82-8931-76be592c2a5a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of Chemotherapy Pt2, The dose-limiting toxicity of cyclophosphamide is:', '["Cardiotoxicity resulting in decreased functional activity","Myelosuppression (particularly leukopenia)","Nephrotoxicity mediated by specific receptor activation","Pulmonary fibrosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Neurotoxicity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Myelosuppression (particularly leukopenia)', 'Cyclophosphamide''s DLT is myelosuppression (nadir at 8-14 days).'),
('69619d41-83af-53cb-969a-5c90e6102120'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'Albendazole is teratogenic and:', '["Safe in pregnancy","Contraindicated in pregnancy (Category D)","Safe in first trimester","Given during delivery","Safe with precautions"]'::jsonb, 'Contraindicated in pregnancy (Category D)', 'Albendazole is teratogenic and contraindicated; use mebendazole (which has minimal absorption) in pregnancy.'),
('0c069875-4dc5-516a-93b4-4e6abb14397d'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'In the study of Chemotherapy Pt2, Melphalan oral absorption is best when:', '["Taken with food resulting in decreased functional activity","Taken on an empty stomach (1h before or 2h after meals)","Taken at bedtime mediated by specific receptor activation","Taken with antibiotics without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Taken with antacids leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Taken on an empty stomach (1h before or 2h after meals)', 'Oral melphalan should be taken on an empty stomach for consistent bioavailability.'),
('3e8c411e-1187-5402-bc0d-8a40f944119c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Chemotherapy Pt2: Nedaplatin is primarily used in which region?', '["United States resulting in decreased functional activity","Japan (approved for head/neck, lung, esophageal, bladder cancers)","United Kingdom mediated by specific receptor activation","Australia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Canada leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Japan (approved for head/neck, lung, esophageal, bladder cancers)', 'Nedaplatin is a second-generation platinum compound approved primarily in Japan for head/neck, lung, esophageal, and bladder cancers.'),
('d7ef7992-a41b-564f-829e-e1ee4dec0162'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Chemotherapy Pt2: Vincristine is specifically active in which cell cycle phase?', '["G0 phase","S phase","M phase (mitosis)","G1 phase","G2 phase"]'::jsonb, 'M phase (mitosis)', 'Vinca alkaloids are M-phase specific, disrupting the mitotic spindle and arresting cells in metaphase.'),
('c36006a9-5ca5-5a92-b40e-51b2ca0a9a89'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of Chemotherapy Pt2, Mechlorethamine (nitrogen mustard) is used topically for:', '["Melanoma resulting in decreased functional activity","Early-stage mycosis fungoides (CTCL)","Basal cell carcinoma mediated by specific receptor activation","Psoriasis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Actinic keratoses leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Early-stage mycosis fungoides (CTCL)', 'Mechlorethamine is used topically for early-stage mycosis fungoides (cutaneous T-cell lymphoma).'),
('c2e846ee-72e0-5836-8c6c-aa65c096f206'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Chemotherapy Pt2: Leucovorin rescue after high-dose methotrexate works by:', '["Inhibiting methotrexate absorption","Providing reduced folates that bypass the DHFR block","Enhancing methotrexate excretion","Binding to methotrexate in the blood","Inhibiting renal tubular secretion"]'::jsonb, 'Providing reduced folates that bypass the DHFR block', 'Leucovorin is already in the reduced folate form and does not require DHFR, bypassing the metabolic block.'),
('a441eea0-6c64-59e4-8c41-ddc108b40ce0'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'Ivermectin should be avoided in:', '["Healthy adults resulting in decreased functional activity","Children under 15 kg (risk of Mazzotti reaction in heavy O. volvulus infection)","Elderly mediated by specific receptor activation","Pregnant women only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Diabetics leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Children under 15 kg (risk of Mazzotti reaction in heavy O. volvulus infection)', 'Ivermectin is avoided in children <15 kg and can cause severe Mazzotti reactions in heavy onchocerciasis.'),
('784f3300-22f5-55cd-ad76-1398ac3f4e28'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Chemotherapy Pt2: Mitomycin C is used topically in ophthalmology for:', '["Cataract surgery resulting in decreased functional activity","Glaucoma filtration surgery (to prevent scarring)","Corneal transplant rejection mediated by specific receptor activation","Diabetic retinopathy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Conjunctivitis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Glaucoma filtration surgery (to prevent scarring)', 'Mitomycin C is applied during glaucoma filtration surgery to inhibit fibroblast proliferation and prevent scarring.'),
('89e11d51-c20e-5653-b6c7-4d18b3baf425'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'DEC is contraindicated in areas co-endemic with:', '["Malaria resulting in decreased functional activity","Onchocerciasis (risk of severe Mazzotti reaction)","Schistosomiasis mediated by specific receptor activation","Leishmaniasis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Trypanosomiasis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Onchocerciasis (risk of severe Mazzotti reaction)', 'DEC causes severe Mazzotti reactions (fever, hypotension, encephalopathy) in O. volvulus co-infection.'),
('b00c7051-de14-572a-bf1f-4c8655241bd1'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'A 30-year-old female presents with a fever, headache, and a macular rash on the wrists and ankles following a tick bite (Rocky Mountain spotted fever). The patient is started on doxycycline. What is the mechanism of action of this drug?', '["Inhibits bacterial cell wall synthesis by binding to D-Ala-D-Ala","Inhibits topoisomerase II (DNA gyrase)","Binds to the 50S ribosomal subunit, blocking transpeptidation","Binds to the 30S ribosomal subunit, preventing aminoacyl-tRNA binding","Inhibits DNA-dependent RNA polymerase"]'::jsonb, 'Binds to the 30S ribosomal subunit, preventing aminoacyl-tRNA binding', 'Doxycycline is a tetracycline. Tetracyclines bind reversibly to the 30S ribosomal subunit in bacteria, blocking the binding of aminoacyl-tRNA to the A site, thereby inhibiting protein synthesis.'),
('df5d6e35-7fdb-5876-b503-8a51652ae41c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'Pyrantel pamoate is effective against:', '["Tapeworm resulting in decreased functional activity","Pinworm, roundworm, hookworm (nematodes)","Flukes mediated by specific receptor activation","Protozoa without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ectoparasites leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pinworm, roundworm, hookworm (nematodes)', 'Pyrantel pamoate causes depolarizing neuromuscular blockade in nematodes, effective against common intestinal worms.'),
('f06b2baf-7821-5a5a-9d2e-4a231c725e68'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'In the study of Chemotherapy Pt2, Irinotecan inhibits which topoisomerase?', '["Topoisomerase I","Topoisomerase II","Topoisomerase III","DNA gyrase","Reverse transcriptase"]'::jsonb, 'Topoisomerase I', 'Irinotecan (CPT-11) is a camptothecin derivative that inhibits topoisomerase I. It is a prodrug converted to SN-38.'),
('807e01a9-9d66-54ea-9a74-c228d704b185'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Chemotherapy Pt2: 5-Fluorouracil (5-FU) inhibits which enzyme in pyrimidine synthesis?', '["Thymidylate synthase","Dihydrofolate reductase","Ribonucleotide reductase","Xanthine oxidase","Adenosine deaminase"]'::jsonb, 'Thymidylate synthase', '5-FU is converted to FdUMP, which forms a ternary complex with thymidylate synthase and N5,N10-methylene-THF, inhibiting conversion of dUMP to dTMP.'),
('254b6165-edbf-53cc-b7cb-c421cceecf98'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Chemotherapeutic Agents - Chemotherapy Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Chemotherapy Pt2: Mesna dosing schedule typically involves:', '["Single dose before chemotherapy","Divided doses at 0, 4, and 8 hours after ifosfamide","Continuous infusion for 24 hours","Weekly injections","Single dose after completion"]'::jsonb, 'Divided doses at 0, 4, and 8 hours after ifosfamide', 'Mesna is typically given in divided doses (0, 4, 8 hours after ifosfamide) to maintain urinary thiol levels.'),
('11761726-edd8-5199-b272-96bd765dc91b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Hematologic Coagulation - Hematologic Drugs', '4T score 0-3 indicates:', '["High probability","Intermediate","Low probability (HIT unlikely)","Diagnostic","Indeterminate"]'::jsonb, 'Low probability (HIT unlikely)', 'Low probability (NPV >95%); 4-5 intermediate; 6-8 high.'),
('59b43135-b8ac-596c-9bd3-36bd3655f21e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Hematologic Coagulation - Hematologic Drugs', '4T score includes Thrombocytopenia, Timing, Thrombosis, and:', '["Temperature","Treatment response","Other causes (absence of)","Total IgG","Thrombin generation"]'::jsonb, 'Other causes (absence of)', '4T: Thrombocytopenia, Timing, Thrombosis, absence of Other causes.'),
('d9513ebc-dd94-51f9-90c3-126e774a817b'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Hematologic Coagulation - Hematologic Drugs', '4T scoring system includes EXCEPT:', '["Thrombocytopenia","Timing","Thrombosis/sequelae","Temperature","Absence of other causes"]'::jsonb, 'Temperature', '4T: Thrombocytopenia, Timing, Thrombosis, absence of Other causes.'),
('8dd0fdec-1f1d-5307-b02a-2edcd12860d6'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Hematologic Coagulation - Hematologic Drugs', 'ACS with AF stable >1 year: current guidelines suggest:', '["Triple therapy indefinitely resulting in decreased functional activity","Single anticoagulant (DOAC preferred) without antiplatelet","DAPT mediated by specific receptor activation","Aspirin only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","No therapy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Single anticoagulant (DOAC preferred) without antiplatelet', 'After 1 year: single anticoagulant (DOAC) without antiplatelet.'),
('d9e9d511-cbec-5c23-bbf6-ebeee83be313'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Hematologic Coagulation - Hematologic Drugs', 'AERD aspirin desensitization by:', '["Avoiding forever resulting in decreased functional activity","Gradual dose escalation under supervision","IV aspirin only mediated by specific receptor activation","Alternative NSAIDs without affecting the primary tissue pathway","No treatment leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Gradual dose escalation under supervision', 'Gradual escalation induces tolerance in 60-70% of AERD patients.'),
('0a984ecd-1620-5a40-9499-17572874e669'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Hematologic Coagulation - Hematologic Drugs', 'AERD involves:', '["Asthma worsening with aspirin via COX-1 inhibition shunting to leukotrienes","Allergic reaction accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pulmonary embolism mediated by specific receptor activation","Pneumonia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fibrosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Asthma worsening with aspirin via COX-1 inhibition shunting to leukotrienes', 'COX-1 inhibition shifts arachidonic acid to leukotriene synthesis.'),
('7bbb2b15-6ce5-5d6a-98c9-1122e588dc51'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Hematologic Coagulation - Hematologic Drugs', 'APC resistance test measures:', '["Protein C levels resulting in decreased functional activity","aPTT ratio with/without APC (functional Factor V Leiden assay)","Thrombin generation mediated by specific receptor activation","Platelet function without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fibrinogen levels leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'aPTT ratio with/without APC (functional Factor V Leiden assay)', 'Low ratio = APC-resistant factor Va (Factor V Leiden).'),
('99af8b1f-b144-5100-b397-5125e50aff24'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Hematologic Coagulation - Hematologic Drugs', 'APS diagnosed when antibody positive on how many occasions >=12 weeks apart:', '["1","2","3","4","5"]'::jsonb, '2', 'Positive on 2+ occasions at least 12 weeks apart plus clinical criteria.'),
('55ed70ae-fd82-567c-ac5e-32d7b540d2c4'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Hematologic Coagulation - Hematologic Drugs', 'APS warfarin target INR typically:', '["1.5-2.0","2.0-3.0","2.5-3.5","3.0-4.0","4.0-5.0"]'::jsonb, '2.5-3.5', 'APS with thrombosis: target 2.5-3.5 (higher than standard).'),
('f976b750-07e9-5dcf-89d2-678aa179b0c7'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Hematologic Coagulation - Hematologic Drugs', 'AT III concentrate used in:', '["HIT only resulting in decreased functional activity","AT III deficiency with thrombosis/surgery","All heparin patients mediated by specific receptor activation","DIC only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Stroke prevention leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'AT III deficiency with thrombosis/surgery', 'Indicated for AT III deficiency with thrombosis or during surgery.'),
('bc4868f4-5a3a-5813-8301-581797850566'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Hematologic Coagulation - Hematologic Drugs', 'AT III deficiency predisposes to:', '["Bleeding","Venous thromboembolism","Thrombocytopenia","Anemia","Aplastic anemia"]'::jsonb, 'Venous thromboembolism', 'AT III is natural anticoagulant; deficiency increases VTE risk.'),
('24b8443b-7301-51c4-a690-52aa380996c4'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Hematologic Coagulation - Hematologic Drugs', 'Active B12 coenzyme forms:', '["Cyanocobalamin/hydroxocobalamin","Methylcobalamin/adenosylcobalamin","Nitrosocobalamin","Chlorocobalamin","Ferricobalamin"]'::jsonb, 'Methylcobalamin/adenosylcobalamin', 'Methylcobalamin (methionine synthase) and adenosylcobalamin (methylmalonyl-CoA mutase).'),
('1d8d58b7-e67d-5f62-a2cf-48d8c7ed512c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Hematologic Coagulation - Hematologic Drugs', 'Acute iron poisoning treated with:', '["Deferoxamine","N-acetylcysteine","Succimer","Dimercaprol","Penicillamine"]'::jsonb, 'Deferoxamine', 'Deferoxamine is chelator of choice for acute iron poisoning.'),
('71e7ba89-7247-5c49-b527-89f0a5d02fa5'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Hematologic Coagulation - Hematologic Drugs', 'Acute warfarin reversal with serious bleeding:', '["Vitamin K alone","FFP or PCC +/- IV vitamin K","Protamine","TXA alone","Desmopressin"]'::jsonb, 'FFP or PCC +/- IV vitamin K', 'FFP/PCC provides immediate factor replacement + IV vitamin K.'),
('e0668665-5203-570e-a47e-533caf0fb831'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Hematologic Coagulation - Hematologic Drugs', 'African Americans need higher warfarin because:', '["Lower VKORC1 resulting in decreased functional activity","Higher VKORC1 -1639G frequency and CYP2C9*1 (normal)","CYP2C9*3 mediated by specific receptor activation","von Willebrand disease without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Poor absorption leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Higher VKORC1 -1639G frequency and CYP2C9*1 (normal)', 'Higher G allele + CYP2C9*1 = resistance = higher doses.'),
('b5f08e22-54ad-56cd-9bce-948e56e79a27'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Hematologic Coagulation - Hematologic Drugs', 'Alteplase for ischemic stroke within:', '["1 hour","4.5 hours","24 hours","72 hours","1 week"]'::jsonb, '4.5 hours', 'IV alteplase approved within 4.5 hours of symptom onset.'),
('2e9ac72f-864e-5a91-89fa-1233b4e5868a'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Hematologic Coagulation - Hematologic Drugs', 'Alteplase is a:', '["Anticoagulant resulting in decreased functional activity","Fibrin-specific thrombolytic (plasminogen activator)","Antiplatelet mediated by specific receptor activation","Antifibrinolytic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Heparin analogue leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fibrin-specific thrombolytic (plasminogen activator)', 'rt-PA preferentially activates plasminogen bound to fibrin.'),
('b86abb17-e276-5ce7-8333-c3c688012ae6'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Hematologic Coagulation - Hematologic Drugs', 'Aminocaproic acid (EACA) classified as:', '["Thrombolytic","Antifibrinolytic (lysine analogue)","Anticoagulant","Antiplatelet","Vitamin K antagonist"]'::jsonb, 'Antifibrinolytic (lysine analogue)', 'Antifibrinolytic inhibiting plasminogen activation.'),
('5a9d7489-8586-596d-98e5-ac3c22139496'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Hematologic Coagulation - Hematologic Drugs', 'Aminocaproic acid available in:', '["IV and oral only","IV, oral, and topical","Oral only","IV only","Topical only"]'::jsonb, 'IV, oral, and topical', 'IV, oral, and topical formulations available.'),
('8885bb50-4911-52c5-bc0e-0ac343000030'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Hematologic Coagulation - Hematologic Drugs', 'Aminocaproic acid half-life approximately:', '["30 min","2 hours","8 hours","24 hours","48 hours"]'::jsonb, '2 hours', '2 hours vs TXA 3 hours; requires more frequent dosing.'),
('77b3b52f-e434-5f1e-a708-f6846f1b5451'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Hematologic Coagulation - Hematologic Drugs', 'Amiodarone-warfarin interaction causes:', '["Decreased effect resulting in decreased functional activity","Significantly increased effect requiring major dose reduction","No interaction mediated by specific receptor activation","Increased metabolism without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Decreased absorption leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Significantly increased effect requiring major dose reduction', 'Inhibits both CYP2C9 and CYP3A4; 30-50% dose reduction typically needed.'),
('dd341a0c-4576-57e3-9466-849e0569c8eb'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Hematologic Coagulation - Hematologic Drugs', 'Anaphylaxis risk with:', '["Alteplase","Streptokinase","Tenecteplase","Reteplase","Urokinase"]'::jsonb, 'Streptokinase', 'Bacterial protein; avoid if used previous 6 months.'),
('c24bc156-7b51-5c58-9019-8d7095e0af6f'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Hematologic Coagulation - Hematologic Drugs', 'Andexanet alfa reverses:', '["Dabigatran","Warfarin","Direct Xa inhibitors","Heparin","Aspirin"]'::jsonb, 'Direct Xa inhibitors', 'Modified recombinant factor Xa acting as decoy receptor for Xa inhibitors.'),
('27480dcb-2466-51d3-9fc0-42a2c8842800'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Hematologic Coagulation - Hematologic Drugs', 'Andexanet alfa works by:', '["Inhibiting absorption resulting in decreased functional activity","Decoy receptor (modified Xa) binding sequestering Xa inhibitors","Increasing metabolism mediated by specific receptor activation","Blocking absorption without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Enhancing excretion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Decoy receptor (modified Xa) binding sequestering Xa inhibitors', 'Modified recombinant factor Xa binding rivaroxaban/apixaban/edoxaban as decoy.'),
('b2064de3-4579-56f9-902d-1293e07f64fe'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Hematologic Coagulation - Hematologic Drugs', 'Anti-Xa monitoring recommended for LMWH in:', '["All patients routinely resulting in decreased functional activity","Obesity, renal impairment, pregnancy, extreme weight","Never mediated by specific receptor activation","Pediatric only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Elderly only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Obesity, renal impairment, pregnancy, extreme weight', 'Recommended when standard dosing may be unreliable.'),
('ce5e8231-7266-59c4-8a80-beb2df829916'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Hematologic Coagulation - Hematologic Drugs', 'Anticoagulant with no specific reversal agent (until recently):', '["Heparin","Warfarin","Dabigatran","Apixaban","All have agents"]'::jsonb, 'Apixaban', 'Until andexanet alfa; dabigatran had idarucizumab earlier.'),
('a651e0f7-3e1f-5bf0-80c5-8d911f462657'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Hematologic Coagulation - Hematologic Drugs', 'Antifibrinolytic mechanism similar to endogenous:', '["Protein C","Protein S","Alpha-2 antiplasmin","Thrombomodulin","Antithrombin III"]'::jsonb, 'Alpha-2 antiplasmin', 'Similar to alpha-2 antiplasmin inhibiting plasminogen activation/plasmin.'),
('15451fdc-69f3-531b-a95e-f74a09dce5ac'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Hematologic Coagulation - Hematologic Drugs', 'Antifibrinolytics useful in hemophilia for:', '["Joint bleeds resulting in decreased functional activity","Mucosal bleeding (dental, epistaxis, menorrhagia)","Intracranial hemorrhage mediated by specific receptor activation","Muscle hematomas without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All types leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mucosal bleeding (dental, epistaxis, menorrhagia)', 'Mucosa has high fibrinolytic activity.'),
('8aa51ab4-1641-5683-a70a-0d8e6f075787'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Hematologic Coagulation - Hematologic Drugs', 'Antiphospholipid syndrome characterized by:', '["Bleeding tendency resulting in decreased functional activity","Thrombosis + antiphospholipid antibodies (LA, aCL, anti-B2GP1)","Low platelets only mediated by specific receptor activation","Anemia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Leukocytosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thrombosis + antiphospholipid antibodies (LA, aCL, anti-B2GP1)', 'Arterial/venous thrombosis + persistent antiphospholipid antibodies.');
insert into fcps_mcq_stage (id, subject_name, chapter_name, question, options, correct_answer, explanation) values
('03f09e42-4f5b-56a2-9fdd-ee33d26843b3'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Hematologic Coagulation - Hematologic Drugs', 'Apixaban dose reduced to 2.5mg BD when >=2 of: age>=80, weight<=60kg, Cr>=:', '["1.0 mg/dL","1.5 mg/dL","2.0 mg/dL","3.0 mg/dL","0.5 mg/dL"]'::jsonb, '1.5 mg/dL', 'Apixaban reduction requires >=2 of: age>=80, weight<=60kg, Cr>=1.5 mg/dL.'),
('7fb66a7b-6717-5b96-bb2a-186b1f12d7ca'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Hematologic Coagulation - Hematologic Drugs', 'Apixaban primarily metabolized by:', '["CYP2C19","CYP3A4","CYP2D6","Renal only","Not metabolized"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4', 'Primarily CYP3A4 with some CYP2C19; P-gp substrate.'),
('6c2f572e-a4a1-52d0-b491-dbd6f73a977c'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Hematologic Coagulation - Hematologic Drugs', 'Aprotinin withdrawn due to concerns about:', '["Inefficiency resulting in decreased functional activity","Increased mortality and renal failure risk","Cost mediated by specific receptor activation","Allergic reactions only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Availability leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Increased mortality and renal failure risk', 'Withdrawn 2007; observational data suggested increased mortality/renal failure.'),
('c8be1afa-bb62-5878-9187-3ca11232d883'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Hematologic Coagulation - Hematologic Drugs', 'Argatroban differs from dabigatran: argatroban is:', '["Oral resulting in decreased functional activity","IV only, primarily hepatically cleared","Xa inhibitor mediated by specific receptor activation","Prodrug without affecting the primary tissue pathway","P2Y12 inhibitor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'IV only, primarily hepatically cleared', 'IV DTI, hepatic clearance (useful in renal impairment).'),
('467a655e-86a0-51c1-993f-f21eb0ee24bd'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Hematologic Coagulation - Hematologic Drugs', 'Aspirin anti-inflammatory dose typically:', '["75-100 mg","325-650 mg q4-6h","1-2g q4-6h","5-10g daily","50 mg"]'::jsonb, '1-2g q4-6h', '1-2g every 4-6 hours (3-6g/day); higher than antiplatelet doses.'),
('05c24dc7-c0ec-5ffd-a967-3035e9266b67'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Hematologic Coagulation - Hematologic Drugs', 'Aspirin anti-inflammatory doses cause:', '["Hepatotoxicity resulting in decreased functional activity","Tinnitus/hearing loss (salicylism)","Nephrotic syndrome mediated by specific receptor activation","Pancreatitis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tinnitus/hearing loss (salicylism)', 'Salicylism (tinnitus, hearing loss, dizziness) at higher doses.'),
('961871bf-adeb-5c6a-b722-1c1659a588a4'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Hematologic Coagulation - Hematologic Drugs', 'Aspirin effect lasts approximately:', '["1-2 days","7-10 days","1 month","3 months","1 year"]'::jsonb, '7-10 days', 'Platelet lifespan 7-10 days; irreversible inhibition persists for cell life.'),
('4bcd42f6-6ab6-5714-abc9-acff40458ed1'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Hematologic Coagulation - Hematologic Drugs', 'Aspirin effect lasts platelet lifespan because:', '["Stored in granules resulting in decreased functional activity","Irreversible inhibition; platelets cannot make new COX-1","Long half-life mediated by specific receptor activation","Regenerates in 24h without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Binds fibrinogen leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Irreversible inhibition; platelets cannot make new COX-1', 'Platelets lack nuclei; cannot synthesize new COX-1.'),
('a5c46c65-69e5-5dda-8d4b-af542950393e'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Hematologic Coagulation - Hematologic Drugs', 'Aspirin inhibits platelets by:', '["Reversibly inhibiting COX-1 resulting in decreased functional activity","Irreversibly acetylating COX-1, blocking TXA2","Blocking ADP mediated by specific receptor activation","Inhibiting PDE without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Blocking GPIIb/IIIa leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Irreversibly acetylating COX-1, blocking TXA2', 'Irreversible COX-1 acetylation permanently blocks TXA2 for platelet lifespan.'),
('f4854527-3a5a-55aa-9d36-10a356bbfb29'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Hematologic Coagulation - Hematologic Drugs', 'Aspirin loading dose ACS:', '["81 mg","162-325 mg","500 mg","50 mg","10 mg"]'::jsonb, '162-325 mg', '162-325 mg chewed for rapid absorption; maintenance 75-100 mg.'),
('a589368f-c6be-5b58-bd21-93fdc6483bee'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Hematologic Coagulation - Hematologic Drugs', 'Aspirin maintenance dose:', '["325 mg","75-100 mg","500 mg","500 mg BD","10 mg"]'::jsonb, '75-100 mg', '75-100 mg daily; higher doses increase bleeding without benefit.'),
('64534330-d425-52fe-b9b4-99d437396605'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Hematologic Coagulation - Hematologic Drugs', 'Aspirin resistance measured by:', '["PT/INR resulting in decreased functional activity","Platelet function tests (VerifyNow, TEG, aggregometry)","aPTT mediated by specific receptor activation","Serum aspirin level without affecting the primary tissue pathway","COX-1 gene testing leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Platelet function tests (VerifyNow, TEG, aggregometry)', 'Platelet function testing showing persistent TXA2-mediated activation.'),
('1819138f-5568-50a6-9cbe-89208f434352'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Hematologic Coagulation - Hematologic Drugs', 'Aspirin unique antiplatelet properties:', '["Antiviral only resulting in decreased functional activity","Antipyretic, analgesic, anti-inflammatory (COX inhibition in tissues)","Antibacterial only mediated by specific receptor activation","Antifungal only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Antiparasitic only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Antipyretic, analgesic, anti-inflammatory (COX inhibition in tissues)', 'COX inhibition provides antipyretic, analgesic, anti-inflammatory beyond antiplatelet.'),
('f29da735-fc8f-5736-8846-8a4ba2354dd0'::uuid, 'Pharmacology (Paper 2)', 'Hematologic Coagulation - Hematologic Drugs', 'Aspirin with thrombolytics in STEMI because:', '["Enhances thrombolysis resulting in decreased functional activity","Prevents re-occlusion at thrombolytic site","Reduces bleeding mediated by specific receptor activation","Replaces heparin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Prevents allergy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Prevents re-occlusion at thrombolytic site', 'Prevents platelet-mediated re-occlusion.'),
