('9d71a65c-d021-5851-87b5-f214bcd5bc55'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Virology - Virology Part 2', 'Measles virus genome:', '["Single-stranded DNA","Double-stranded DNA","Single-stranded RNA (-sense)","Single-stranded RNA (+sense)","Double-stranded RNA"]'::jsonb, 'Single-stranded RNA (-sense)', 'Measles is a negative-sense ssRNA virus.'),
('f3b0b651-be59-5101-b57a-5a6c72407418'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Virology - Virology Part 2', 'Measles Koplik spots:', '["Skin rash","Buccal mucosa enanthem","Eye conjunctiva","Palate lesions","Tongue lesions"]'::jsonb, 'Buccal mucosa enanthem', 'Koplik spots are pathognomonic white spots on buccal mucosa.'),
('e889de67-5184-5154-9e39-ad7ff3127c05'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Virology - Virology Part 2', 'Measles SSPE:', '["Acute complication resulting in decreased functional activity","Rare fatal degenerative CNS disease years later","Common mediated by specific receptor activation","Only in vaccinated without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only in adults leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Rare fatal degenerative CNS disease years later', 'SSPE is a rare, fatal, progressive encephalitis occurring years after infection.'),
('cd80b29f-23d8-5323-8b1b-705c0405ca39'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Virology - Virology Part 2', 'Measles immune amnesia:', '["Does not occur","Destroys pre-existing immune memory","Enhances immunity","Only in children","Only in adults"]'::jsonb, 'Destroys pre-existing immune memory', 'Measles can erase immune memory, increasing susceptibility to other infections.'),
('0612e39c-ac9f-5947-b77a-47078a61b654'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Virology - Virology Part 2', 'Rubella congenital syndrome:', '["Does not occur resulting in decreased functional activity","Deafness, cataracts, cardiac defects","Only deafness mediated by specific receptor activation","Only cardiac without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only cataracts leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Deafness, cataracts, cardiac defects', 'CRS includes deafness, cataracts, cardiac defects, and intellectual disability.'),
('81d4d731-34c8-5770-9a72-63f5266e3b5b'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Virology - Virology Part 2', 'Rubella vaccine strategy:', '["Only children resulting in decreased functional activity","Girlss before childbearing age (MMR)","Only pregnant women mediated by specific receptor activation","Only elderly without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Not needed leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Girlss before childbearing age (MMR)', 'Rubella vaccination targets girls to prevent CRS.'),
('60d495b8-c675-5413-bdd0-be7a4f9afed2'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Virology - Virology Part 2', 'Rotavirus vaccine type:', '["Inactivated","Live attenuated (oral)","Subunit","mRNA","Viral vector"]'::jsonb, 'Live attenuated (oral)', 'Rotavirus vaccines (RotaTeq, Rotarix) are live attenuated oral vaccines.'),
('8507d0ef-bd1f-5822-8a1a-9c6dd22dd580'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Virology - Virology Part 2', 'Rotavirus most severe age group:', '["Neonates","6 months - 2 years","5-10 years","Adults","Elderly"]'::jsonb, '6 months - 2 years', 'Severe rotavirus gastroenteritis peaks in infants and young children.'),
('154725a7-39f5-5cff-b6f5-44ef12adf62e'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Virology - Virology Part 2', 'Norovirus common name:', '["Stomach flu","Winter vomiting bug","Travelers diarrhea","Food poisoning","Stomach bug"]'::jsonb, 'Winter vomiting bug', 'Norovirus is known as the winter vomiting bug due to seasonal outbreaks.'),
('c023b598-5fce-58d1-8e03-091fd6c72daf'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Virology - Virology Part 2', 'Norovirus outbreaks setting:', '["Homes only","Cruise ships, hospitals, schools","Only hospitals","Only schools","Only cruise ships"]'::jsonb, 'Cruise ships, hospitals, schools', 'Norovirus causes outbreaks in closed settings with close contact.'),
('11c24352-539d-52b4-bc93-30b9ab0574db'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Virology - Virology Part 2', 'A 70-year-old male presents for evaluation of chronic fatigue. Lab studies reveal the presence of HBsAg and HBeAg in the serum, with negative anti-HBs. Anti-HBc IgG is positive. What is the clinical status of this patient?', '["Chronic hepatitis B with low infectivity","Acute hepatitis B infection","Immunity due to vaccination","Recovery from past infection","Chronic hepatitis B with active viral replication"]'::jsonb, 'Chronic hepatitis B with active viral replication', 'The persistence of HBsAg for > 6 months indicates chronic HBV. The presence of HBeAg indicates high viral replication and high infectivity. Recovery/immunity would be marked by positive anti-HBs antibodies.'),
('145e2e72-386d-5f6c-a926-f35b8d0e4d52'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Virology - Virology Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Virology Pt2: HBsAg positivity for >6 months indicates:', '["Acute infection","Chronic infection","Resolution","Immunity","Carrier only"]'::jsonb, 'Chronic infection', 'HBsAg persistence >6 months defines chronic HBV infection.'),
('089e68fb-ef29-540a-a44f-5477da26e8db'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Virology - Virology Part 2', 'HPV and warts:', '["No warts","HPV 6, 11 cause genital warts","HPV 16, 18 cause warts","All types cause warts","Only in children"]'::jsonb, 'HPV 6, 11 cause genital warts', 'HPV 6 and 11 cause 90% of genital warts.'),
('d92f264a-daf9-5c89-a237-f319c45411ec'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Virology - Virology Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Virology Pt2: HDV is a:', '["DNA virus","Complete virus","Retrovirus","Defective RNA (needs HBsAg)","Picornavirus"]'::jsonb, 'Defective RNA (needs HBsAg)', 'HDV is a defective RNA virus requiring HBV surface antigen for replication.'),
('5c5ffd41-9e4f-590d-bbd0-e768d35c0197'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Virology - Virology Part 2', 'In the study of Virology Pt2, CMV pneumonitis in transplant:', '["No treatment needed","Ganciclovir + immunoglobulin","Acyclovir only","Observation only","Antibiotics"]'::jsonb, 'Ganciclovir + immunoglobulin', 'CMV pneumonitis requires ganciclovir + CMV immunoglobulin.'),
('2e8e2982-1a22-5065-8805-00942001ac7d'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Virology - Virology Part 2', 'Adenovirus common manifestation:', '["Hepatitis only resulting in decreased functional activity","Pharyngoconjunctival fever, keratoconjunctivitis","Only pneumonia mediated by specific receptor activation","Only gastroenteritis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only cystitis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pharyngoconjunctival fever, keratoconjunctivitis', 'Adenovirus causes pharyngoconjunctival fever and epidemic keratoconjunctivitis.'),
('0a18e14f-d91b-5546-acb4-0c44eb930fe9'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Virology - Virology Part 2', 'Adenovirus transmission:', '["Blood only resulting in decreased functional activity","Respiratory, fecal-oral, direct contact","Vector-borne only mediated by specific receptor activation","Sexual only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vertical only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Respiratory, fecal-oral, direct contact', 'Adenovirus transmits via respiratory, fecal-oral, and direct contact routes.'),
('7f2b0e93-f425-5c9b-bfd0-78bdc7a1f7c0'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Virology - Virology Part 2', 'Hantavirus transmission:', '["Mosquito bite","Inhalation of rodent excreta","Human-to-human","Tick bite","Foodborne"]'::jsonb, 'Inhalation of rodent excreta', 'Hantavirus transmits via aerosolized rodent urine, feces, saliva.'),
('d49521f0-1c8b-5d36-b301-c0b96e22048d'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Virology - Virology Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Virology Pt2: Kaposi sarcoma is caused by:', '["HSV resulting in decreased functional activity","EBV accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CMV mediated by specific receptor activation","HHV-8 in AIDS/transplant","VZV leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'HHV-8 in AIDS/transplant', 'KS is caused by HHV-8, especially in AIDS and transplant patients.'),
('c0c08b49-89e5-5705-a874-c877f56df56b'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Virology - Virology Part 2', 'Lassa fever transmission:', '["Mosquito bite","Contact with Mastomys rat excreta","Human-to-human only","Tick bite","Airborne only"]'::jsonb, 'Contact with Mastomys rat excreta', 'Lassa fever transmits via contact with infected Mastomys rat excreta.'),
('ea42fd13-6995-52c7-8a00-4599b80cea2e'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Virology - Virology Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Virology Pt2: HIV Bictegravir class:', '["NRTI","NNRTI","INSTI","Protease inhibitor","Entry inhibitor"]'::jsonb, 'INSTI', 'Bictegravir is a newer INSTI, component of Biktarvy.'),
('9d2e3720-4040-5cb8-af7a-d86139b4d191'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Virology - Virology Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Virology Pt2: HIV genome is:', '["dsDNA","ssDNA","dsRNA","ssRNA (diploid)","ssRNA-"]'::jsonb, 'ssRNA (diploid)', 'HIV has two copies of positive-sense ssRNA (diploid genome).'),
('637406de-19e7-59a6-9731-fb4e23244c6f'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Virology - Virology Part 2', 'Ebola virus persistence:', '["Never persists resulting in decreased functional activity","Can persist in immunoprivileged sites","Lifetime in blood mediated by specific receptor activation","Only in skin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only in nails leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Can persist in immunoprivileged sites', 'Ebola can persist in eyes, CNS, testes after recovery.'),
('d66809e0-72da-5535-948c-f87b4d681802'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Virology - Virology Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Virology Pt2: HAV does NOT progress to:', '["Acute hepatitis","Chronic hepatitis","Cirrhosis","Liver failure","Both B and C"]'::jsonb, 'Both B and C', 'HAV does NOT cause chronic hepatitis or cirrhosis.'),
('2de06d0f-7fb4-5c34-bd4b-fecf9ddfb944'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Virology - Virology Part 2', 'Nipah virus:', '["Mosquito-borne","Bat-borne, can cause encephalitis","Rodent-borne only","Bird-borne only","Human-to-human only"]'::jsonb, 'Bat-borne, can cause encephalitis', 'Nipah virus originates from fruit bats and causes encephalitis.'),
('b8621069-7a7c-506a-bc27-2ec06e056a85'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Virology - Virology Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Virology Pt2: HIV CD4/CD8 ratio inversion:', '["Normal resulting in decreased functional activity","Indicates immune activation/dysfunction","Only in AIDS mediated by specific receptor activation","Only with treatment without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Never occurs leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Indicates immune activation/dysfunction', 'CD4/CD8 inversion (<1) indicates immune activation/dysfunction.'),
('79b67f77-3c7d-562f-ae59-c106f83cdff8'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Virology - Virology Part 2', 'Hendra virus:', '["Human-to-human common resulting in decreased functional activity","Horse-borne zoonosis (paramyxovirus)","Mosquito-borne mediated by specific receptor activation","Tick-borne without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Waterborne leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Horse-borne zoonosis (paramyxovirus)', 'Hendra virus is a bat-borne paramyxovirus affecting horses and humans.'),
('87a9bda8-ea36-5616-9d50-8ac83bc47dc4'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Virology - Virology Part 2', 'A 30-year-old female with diagnosed HIV infection presents with progressive dyspnea, fever, and a nonproductive cough. CD4 count is 120 cells/mcL. Chest X-ray reveals bilateral interstitial infiltrates. Silver stain of bronchoalveolar lavage fluid shows cup-shaped cysts. What is the most likely pathogen?', '["Pneumocystis jirovecii","Cryptococcus neoformans","Mycobacterium tuberculosis","Cytomegalovirus","Streptococcus pneumoniae"]'::jsonb, 'Pneumocystis jirovecii', 'Pneumocystis pneumonia (PCP) is a classic opportunistic infection in HIV patients with CD4 counts < 200 cells/mcL. Silver staining showing cup-shaped cysts in lavage fluid is diagnostic for Pneumocystis jirovecii.'),
('3aeb24e4-4fbb-5dfb-a780-f6b82c5a55de'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Virology - Virology Part 2', 'RSV vaccine target population:', '["All ages resulting in decreased functional activity","Infants and elderly (high-risk groups)","Only children mediated by specific receptor activation","Only adults without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Not available leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Infants and elderly (high-risk groups)', 'RSV vaccines target infants and elderly at highest risk.'),
('e04a4e3c-e8a3-5159-bff2-6cc4470816c1'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Virology - Virology Part 2', 'In the study of Virology Pt2, CMV mononucleosis vs EBV mononucleosis:', '["Identical presentation","Heterophile antibody positive in CMV","Heterophile antibody negative in CMV","CMV causes jaundice, EBV does not","EBV causes rash, CMV does not"]'::jsonb, 'Heterophile antibody negative in CMV', 'CMV mononucleosis is heterophile-negative; EBV is heterophile-positive.'),
('1eda5f7c-d7fc-5fd0-893b-bc6f1580dc31'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Virology - Virology Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Virology Pt2: HDV coinfection with HBV:', '["Always chronic","Usually self-limited","Fatal","Mild","Asymptomatic"]'::jsonb, 'Usually self-limited', 'HDV coinfection with HBV usually results in self-limited disease.'),
('cea7e6be-e00e-5eea-a51c-6be8e59972d5'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Virology - Virology Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Virology Pt2: HIV is NOT transmitted by:', '["Sexual contact","Needle sharing","Casual contact","Blood transfusion","Breastfeeding"]'::jsonb, 'Casual contact', 'HIV is NOT transmitted by casual contact (hugging, sharing utensils).'),
('99583a54-6986-5e89-b0f6-a83e4e25ce9b'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Virology - Virology Part 2', 'SARS-CoV-2 variants of concern:', '["No variants resulting in decreased functional activity","Alpha, Beta, Delta, Omicron, etc.","Only Delta mediated by specific receptor activation","Only Omicron without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only Alpha leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Alpha, Beta, Delta, Omicron, etc.', 'Multiple VOCs emerged including Alpha, Beta, Delta, Omicron.'),
('5567c4ff-39fd-5490-bf68-42d4ac42793f'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Virology - Virology Part 2', 'Omicron variant characteristics:', '["More severe than Delta","More transmissible, less severe","Less transmissible","Same as original","More virulent"]'::jsonb, 'More transmissible, less severe', 'Omicron is more transmissible but generally causes less severe disease.'),
('5904f068-045e-506a-882e-914de1055d0a'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Virology - Virology Part 2', 'In the study of Virology Pt2, HCV cirrhosis development time:', '["1-2 years","5-10 years","10-20 years","20-30 years","Never"]'::jsonb, '20-30 years', 'HCV-related cirrhosis typically develops over 20-30 years.'),
('e370f71b-d26c-56c7-8fa8-77ff5ad1e6e5'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Virology - Virology Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Virology Pt2: HAV is an enveloped virus:', '["Yes","No","Sometimes","Only in liver cells","Only in blood"]'::jsonb, 'No', 'HAV is a non-enveloped virus.'),
('27094b63-09cf-5184-ab47-42c44757b0b6'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Virology - Virology Part 2', 'Influenza pandemic potential:', '["Only H1N1 resulting in decreased functional activity","Novel subtypes against which population has no immunity","Only H5N1 mediated by specific receptor activation","Only H3N2 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only influenza B leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Novel subtypes against which population has no immunity', 'Pandemics arise from novel subtypes with no pre-existing immunity.'),
('76b0a827-df7e-5b3b-a7e2-691461648eaf'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Virology - Virology Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of Virology Pt2, HEV and organ transplantation:', '["No risk","High risk of chronic HEV","Only risk in liver transplant","Only risk in kidney transplant","Only risk in bone marrow transplant"]'::jsonb, 'High risk of chronic HEV', 'Solid organ transplant recipients are at high risk of chronic HEV.'),
('8a1efb01-56f9-51e3-8e7b-6a23629e7bde'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Virology - Virology Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Virology Pt2: HCV liver fibrosis assessment:', '["Liver biopsy only","Fibroscan (transient elastography)","ALT level","AST/ALT ratio only","Imaging only"]'::jsonb, 'Fibroscan (transient elastography)', 'Fibroscan is a non-invasive method to assess liver fibrosis.'),
('bb0c9cf7-37dc-5818-a810-57a99888bd41'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Virology - Virology Part 2', 'COVID-19 mRNA vaccine mechanism:', '["Contains live virus","Encodes spike protein mRNA","Contains inactivated virus","Contains viral vector","Contains protein subunit"]'::jsonb, 'Encodes spike protein mRNA', 'mRNA vaccines encode spike protein, translated by host cells.'),
('a114840b-c315-5e0c-85cd-77e09ad602ef'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Virology - Virology Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Virology Pt2: HIV CCR5 tropic virus:', '["Uses CXCR4 resulting in decreased functional activity","Uses CCR5 co-receptor (macrophage-tropic)","No co-receptor needed mediated by specific receptor activation","Uses CD4 only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Uses CCR3 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Uses CCR5 co-receptor (macrophage-tropic)', 'R5 virus uses CCR5, predominantly in early infection.'),
('1615af95-903c-5b7e-9c11-acd637fe382d'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Virology - Virology Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Virology Pt2: HEV is NOT transmitted by:', '["Contaminated water","Food (undercooked pork)","Blood transfusion","Respiratory droplets","Person-to-person (rare)"]'::jsonb, 'Respiratory droplets', 'HEV is not transmitted by respiratory droplets.'),
('ebf689fd-2632-5488-9816-455a0cec2087'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Virology - Virology Part 2', 'Influenza neuraminidase function:', '["Cell attachment","Viral release from cells","Viral replication","Viral assembly","Immune evasion"]'::jsonb, 'Viral release from cells', 'NA cleaves sialic acid to release new virions from cells.'),
('04253997-6623-5612-808e-36b3cfc6145d'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Virology - Virology Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Virology Pt2: HBV vaccine response in elderly:', '["Same as young adults","Lower response rate","Higher response rate","No response","Contraindicated"]'::jsonb, 'Lower response rate', 'Elderly have lower seroconversion rates to HBV vaccine.'),
('ef2a61f7-9dec-5b71-b618-8f1c4ae2d470'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Virology - Virology Part 2', 'In the study of Virology Pt2, HBV core antibody (anti-HBc) after recovery:', '["Disappears","Persists lifelong","Appears only during infection","Only IgM persists","Converts to anti-HBs"]'::jsonb, 'Persists lifelong', 'Anti-HBc (total) persists lifelong after HBV infection.'),
('1fe0c64e-59a7-529b-9859-f1983079f300'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Virology - Virology Part 2', 'COVID-19 and MIS-C:', '["Does not occur resulting in decreased functional activity","Multisystem inflammatory syndrome in children","Only in adults mediated by specific receptor activation","Only in elderly without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only in infants leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Multisystem inflammatory syndrome in children', 'MIS-C is a hyperinflammatory syndrome occurring weeks after COVID-19 in children.'),
('3d775de8-bd91-5604-8966-36b636d1827b'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Infection with Enterobius vermicularis (pinworm) characteristically causes:', '["Malabsorption","Nocturnal perianal itching","Iron-deficiency anemia","Invasive dysentery","Persistent cough"]'::jsonb, 'Nocturnal perianal itching', 'Female pinworms migrate to the perianal skin at night to lay eggs, causing intense nocturnal pruritus ani. Enterobius does not cause malabsorption, anemia or dysentery.'),
('a2732bbd-5172-5ac2-bc1c-0afe394b86e4'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Which drug is the preferred tissue amebicide for invasive amebiasis?', '["Metronidazole","Albendazole","Iodoquinol","Diloxanide furoate","Paromomycin"]'::jsonb, 'Metronidazole', 'Metronidazole is the drug of choice for invasive intestinal and extraintestinal amebiasis. Luminal agents such as iodoquinol, diloxanide furoate and paromomycin only clear intraluminal cysts.'),
('9f855395-25b1-50da-9087-89d38d3a71cf'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Kala-azar (visceral leishmaniasis) typically presents with:', '["Lymphadenopathy alone","Generalized rash","Fever, splenomegaly, and pancytopenia","Mucosal ulceration","Skin ulcers"]'::jsonb, 'Fever, splenomegaly, and pancytopenia', 'Leishmania donovani invades the reticuloendothelial system, producing prolonged irregular fever, massive splenomegaly and pancytopenia from marrow infiltration.'),
('ee20ccc4-ba16-5fbb-aa97-599d1a1d5464'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Taenia solium is which type of tapeworm?', '["Fish tapeworm resulting in decreased functional activity","Dog tapeworm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pork tapeworm (its eggs cause cysticercosis)","Beef tapeworm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Dwarf tapeworm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pork tapeworm (its eggs cause cysticercosis)', 'Taenia solium is the pork tapeworm. Ingesting its eggs causes larval tissue infection (cysticercosis), while the adult worm is acquired from undercooked pork.'),
('80419e78-efda-5740-9d0d-faf945132866'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Leishmania species are transmitted by which vector?', '["Sandfly (Phlebotomus)","Blackfly","Mosquito","Flea","Tsetse fly"]'::jsonb, 'Sandfly (Phlebotomus)', 'Promastigotes are injected when an infected female sandfly takes a blood meal. No other listed insect transmits Leishmania.'),
('72eafc70-ce51-53dd-8901-e5b9e33d2a0c'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'The Scotch tape (cellophane tape) test is used primarily to diagnose:', '["Trichuris trichiura","Strongyloides stercoralis","Hookworm","Ascaris lumbricoides","Enterobius vermicularis"]'::jsonb, 'Enterobius vermicularis', 'Pinworm eggs are deposited perianally at night; pressing adhesive tape on the perianal skin in the morning captures the eggs and confirms enterobiasis.'),
('87542726-4df2-52be-abe4-f0c84955dc5b'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Chronic Schistosoma mansoni infection characteristically causes:', '["Portal hypertension (pipestem fibrosis)","Carcinoma of the bladder","Cercarial dermatitis","Katayama fever only","Hepatocellular carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 'Portal hypertension (pipestem fibrosis)', 'S. mansoni eggs lodge in portal venules and produce granulomatous periportal (Symmers pipestem) fibrosis, causing presinusoidal portal hypertension. Bladder carcinoma is linked to S. haematobium.'),
('f6c5c63d-b309-5797-91cf-4433471c0743'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'PAIR (Puncture, Aspirate, Inject scolecidal agent, Re-aspirate) is a therapeutic alternative to surgery in the management of:', '["Amebic liver abscess resulting in decreased functional activity","Neurocysticercosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hydatid cyst (Echinococcus granulosus)","Ascariasis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Giardiasis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hydatid cyst (Echinococcus granulosus)', 'PAIR, done under albendazole cover, is an accepted non-resective treatment for selected liver hydatid cysts caused by Echinococcus granulosus.'),
('418fe3e9-6a46-5551-9982-78b7ac4883c9'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Ascaris lumbricoides is best described as:', '["Rare in humans","A trematode","Eradicated by vector control","A cestode","The most common intestinal nematode"]'::jsonb, 'The most common intestinal nematode', 'Ascaris lumbricoides is the most prevalent soil-transmitted intestinal nematode, infecting over one billion people worldwide.'),
('7b486d58-2739-5709-89cf-07e73f6fcd1a'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'The eggs of Schistosoma mansoni are characterized by:', '["A prominent lateral spine","A terminal spine","Barrel shape with bipolar plugs","A thin mammillated shell","A small operculum"]'::jsonb, 'A prominent lateral spine', 'A lateral spine is the hallmark of S. mansoni eggs; S. haematobium eggs carry a terminal spine, and barrel-shaped eggs with bipolar plugs belong to Trichuris.'),
('880921af-aef0-5e91-9b7b-55ebd293a8b2'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'The main role of primaquine in malaria therapy is:', '["Chemoprophylaxis","Cure of acute falciparum malaria","Relapse prevention (kills hypnozoites)","Treatment of schistosomiasis","Suppression of fever"]'::jsonb, 'Relapse prevention (kills hypnozoites)', 'Primaquine eradicates dormant hepatic hypnozoites of P. vivax and P. ovale, giving radical cure and preventing relapse; it is contraindicated in G6PD deficiency.'),
('d9196997-de7b-5217-9473-b98fb121a2be'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Trichuris trichiura is diagnosed by finding which eggs in stool?', '["Barrel-shaped eggs with bipolar plugs","Oval eggs with a lateral spine","Eggs with a terminal spine","Large operculated eggs","Eggs with a mammillated coat"]'::jsonb, 'Barrel-shaped eggs with bipolar plugs', 'The whipworm egg is barrel-shaped with a translucent mucus plug at each end; finding it in stool is the diagnostic method of choice.'),
('487984bc-6eda-526c-873e-d3a3c947cd20'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Which drug is the treatment of choice for strongyloidiasis?', '["Albendazole","Praziquantel","Ivermectin","Diethylcarbamazine","Metronidazole"]'::jsonb, 'Ivermectin', 'Ivermectin is the most effective agent against Strongyloides stercoralis and is preferred for both simple and disseminated disease.'),
('9cddb61e-4d07-544c-a26b-fe1a368a618b'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'The drug of choice for Strongyloides stercoralis infection, including hyperinfection syndrome, is:', '["Ivermectin","Albendazole","Praziquantel","Metronidazole","Diethylcarbamazine"]'::jsonb, 'Ivermectin', 'Ivermectin is the preferred treatment because it kills intestinal larvae and the migrating filariform larvae responsible for hyperinfection.'),
('d2aff2d0-e9f4-521a-9bae-a0780b2ab257'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'The biological vector transmitting visceral leishmaniasis (kala-azar) is the:', '["Phlebotomus sandfly","Blackfly","Anopheles mosquito","Xenopsylla flea","Tsetse fly"]'::jsonb, 'Phlebotomus sandfly', 'Visceral leishmaniasis is transmitted by the bite of infected female sandflies of the genus Phlebotomus.'),
('47ba0597-dc1e-5bde-adf9-8a68ae15d4f6'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Wuchereria bancrofti (lymphatic filariasis) is transmitted by the bite of:', '["Culex mosquito","Tsetse fly","Sandfly","Flea","Blackfly"]'::jsonb, 'Culex mosquito', 'Bancroftian filariasis is spread by mosquitoes, principally Culex quinquefasciatus (also Aedes and Anopheles).'),
('a821f25f-6bbf-5353-bfe9-bf17d8345079'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Microfilariae of Wuchereria bancrofti appear in peripheral blood with which periodicity?', '["Diurnal","Seasonal","Nocturnal","Annual","Irregular (aperiodic)"]'::jsonb, 'Nocturnal', 'W. bancrofti microfilariae are nocturnally periodic, so blood for diagnosis should be drawn at night (10 pm to 2 am).'),
('e1a0e8da-231e-5ee5-9bef-f4d32267015c'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Schistosomiasis is diagnosed definitively by demonstrating:', '["Imaging findings resulting in decreased functional activity","Blood smear accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","PCR mediated by specific receptor activation","Serology without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Characteristic eggs in urine or stool"]'::jsonb, 'Characteristic eggs in urine or stool', 'Demonstration of species-specific eggs in urine or stool is the diagnostic standard for schistosomiasis.'),
('d489b7e8-d508-57b5-b0d1-856894d0217e'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Enterobius vermicularis (pinworm) is diagnosed by:', '["Scotch tape (cellophane tape) test","Blood smear","Stool for barrel-shaped eggs","Serology","Radiology"]'::jsonb, 'Scotch tape (cellophane tape) test', 'The adhesive tape test performed on perianal skin in the early morning captures pinworm eggs laid at night.'),
('3a4ec688-df74-5f38-9924-3a23178795c2'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'How is hookworm infection transmitted to humans?', '["Skin penetration by filariform larvae","Ingestion of eggs","Vector bite","Blood transfusion","Inhalation of dust"]'::jsonb, 'Skin penetration by filariform larvae', 'Infective filariform larvae in contaminated soil penetrate intact skin, usually of the bare foot, to initiate infection.'),
('25269619-45d5-57dc-946b-6baa6d196405'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'The portal of entry of hookworm larvae into the human host is:', '["Vector inoculation","Intact skin","Inhalation","Blood transfusion","Ingestion of eggs"]'::jsonb, 'Intact skin', 'Hookworm filariform larvae actively bore through intact skin; ground itch at the entry site is a common feature.'),
('6b31006d-b237-58ee-97f5-b6be6f396419'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'The drug of choice for giardiasis is:', '["Praziquantel","Metronidazole","Diethylcarbamazine","Ivermectin","Chloroquine"]'::jsonb, 'Metronidazole', 'Metronidazole is first-line therapy for Giardia lamblia infection; tinidazole is an equally effective alternative.'),
('509a1e79-0a21-5a69-8460-0b415cb37454'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'The preferred tissue amebicide for invasive amebiasis is:', '["Albendazole","Iodoquinol","Paromomycin","Diloxanide furoate","Metronidazole"]'::jsonb, 'Metronidazole', 'Metronidazole kills trophozoites in tissues and is the drug of choice for invasive intestinal and hepatic amebiasis.'),
('c69a08c1-a1ca-5430-a01a-c16816623636'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Strongyloides stercoralis is diagnosed by finding in stool:', '["Scotch tape test","Serology","Larvae (rhabditiform)","Eggs","Blood smear"]'::jsonb, 'Larvae (rhabditiform)', 'Strongyloides eggs hatch in the gut, so rhabditiform larvae rather than eggs are found in stool.'),
('c8825975-d290-509c-a52d-bb3333d21654'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'The infectious form of Giardia lamblia that transmits infection is the:', '["Spore resulting in decreased functional activity","Trophozoite accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cyst containing four nuclei","Oocyst without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Egg leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cyst containing four nuclei', 'The mature Giardia cyst contains four nuclei and survives in the environment; it excysts in the duodenum to release trophozoites.'),
('cf56a723-0ffb-5015-abda-ff7d227781f3'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'The Scotch tape test is used for the diagnosis of:', '["Trichuris trichiura","Enterobius vermicularis","Hookworm","Ascaris lumbricoides","Strongyloides stercoralis"]'::jsonb, 'Enterobius vermicularis', 'Perianal eggs laid by the female pinworm at night are captured on the adhesive tape for microscopy.'),
('8ae5370a-ff6f-59d0-ace2-9f966aa9bdd2'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Trophozoites of Entamoeba histolytica characteristically contain:', '["Ingested red blood cells in the cytoplasm","Cilia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A single flagellum mediated by specific receptor activation","Spores without affecting the primary tissue pathway","An axostyle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ingested red blood cells in the cytoplasm', 'Erythrophagocytosis (ingested RBCs) distinguishes invasive E. histolytica trophozoites from the non-invasive E. dispar.'),
('cb29c22a-2a9d-5cb8-a805-3113485727bb'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Trichuris trichiura (whipworm) is transmitted by:', '["Ingestion of embryonated eggs","Blood transfusion","Vector bite","Skin penetration","Inhalation"]'::jsonb, 'Ingestion of embryonated eggs', 'Whipworm eggs embryonate in soil and are ingested on contaminated hands, food or water; there is no tissue migration.'),
('29b737e6-8b0a-5721-a4a9-93d4e08c04cf'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Kala-azar characteristically presents with:', '["Fever, massive splenomegaly, and pancytopenia","Rash only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mucosal lesions only mediated by specific receptor activation","Dermatitis herpetiformis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Skin ulcers only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fever, massive splenomegaly, and pancytopenia', 'RE cell invasion by L. donovani causes high fever, huge spleen and marrow suppression with pancytopenia.'),
('4bb22753-e49a-5afc-a9e1-a03576e26548'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Leishmania donovani is the causative agent of:', '["Visceral leishmaniasis (kala-azar)","Cutaneous leishmaniasis","Mucocutaneous leishmaniasis","Chagas disease","Tropical splenomegaly syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'Visceral leishmaniasis (kala-azar)', 'L. donovani parasitizes reticuloendothelial cells and causes visceral leishmaniasis; cutaneous and mucocutaneous forms are caused by other species.'),
('c527bc38-5e39-56d8-aef5-7028fbc075d7'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'The liver injury characteristic of Schistosoma mansoni is:', '["Katayama fever resulting in decreased functional activity","Carcinoma of the bladder accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Generalized cirrhosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Schistosomal periportal (pipestem) fibrosis","Fatty liver leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Schistosomal periportal (pipestem) fibrosis', 'Eggs trapped in portal venules cause granulomatous periportal fibrosis (Symmers pipestem fibrosis), not true cirrhosis.'),
('1e80b9f2-736f-5ff0-9138-13b3576d4bb5'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'A major complication of heavy Ascaris lumbricoides infection is:', '["Bowel obstruction","Blindness","Liver abscess","Elephantiasis","Hydrocele"]'::jsonb, 'Bowel obstruction', 'A tangled bolus of adult worms can mechanically obstruct the small intestine, particularly in children with heavy loads.'),
('8cbb5305-7e31-56a9-9a1a-796e8fbb0a7b'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'The diagnostic finding in stool for strongyloidiasis is:', '["Eggs with bipolar plugs","Larvae","Serology","Scotch tape test","Blood smear"]'::jsonb, 'Larvae', 'Strongyloides is identified by rhabditiform larvae in stool because eggs hatch within the intestine.'),
('da2ec0ad-989d-5a64-83b8-267c751d6a1e'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Bowel obstruction is a recognized complication of heavy infection with:', '["Enterobius vermicularis","Strongyloides stercoralis","Trichuris trichiura","Ascaris lumbricoides","Ancylostoma duodenale"]'::jsonb, 'Ascaris lumbricoides', 'The large adult worms of Ascaris form an obstructing mass in the gut lumen, classically in children.'),
('e98f117e-3a4a-5fe0-9dc8-0b7d74f8ca17'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Hydatid cyst disease (Echinococcus granulosus) is diagnosed by:', '["CT only resulting in decreased functional activity","Ultrasound combined with serology","PCR alone mediated by specific receptor activation","Stool examination without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Blood smear leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ultrasound combined with serology', 'Imaging shows the cystic lesion while hydatid-specific serology (ELISA) confirms the diagnosis; stool examination is unhelpful as humans are dead-end hosts.'),
('67a638cc-59ff-5758-8295-3047b51f8cc6'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Leishmania donovani infection results in:', '["Visceral leishmaniasis (kala-azar)","All clinical forms","Mucocutaneous leishmaniasis","No clinical disease","Cutaneous leishmaniasis"]'::jsonb, 'Visceral leishmaniasis (kala-azar)', 'L. donovani causes visceral leishmaniasis; mucocutaneous disease is due to L. braziliensis and cutaneous disease to L. tropica/major.'),
('df141a8f-35e0-5ae2-b991-9c0e852d349b'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Hookworm infection is diagnosed by demonstrating in stool:', '["Serology","PCR","Blood smear","Scotch tape test","Oval, thin-shelled eggs"]'::jsonb, 'Oval, thin-shelled eggs', 'Hookworm ova are oval with a thin shell and clear space; they are detected on stool microscopy.'),
('24a4461c-b0a0-54e4-8fc4-bd5524f98d17'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'The species causing visceral leishmaniasis (kala-azar) is:', '["Leishmania tropica","Leishmania braziliensis","Leishmania donovani","Trypanosoma cruzi","Leishmania major"]'::jsonb, 'Leishmania donovani', 'L. donovani is the classic agent of visceral leishmaniasis in the Indian subcontinent and East Africa.'),
('32091cbc-8ccd-55d0-a465-3f7642fdb970'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Echinococcus granulosus infection is diagnosed with the help of:', '["Ultrasound and hydatid serology","CT only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Stool examination mediated by specific receptor activation","PCR only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Blood smear leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ultrasound and hydatid serology', 'Ultrasonography depicts the cyst (often with daughter cysts) and serology detects anti-hydatid antibodies.'),
('83b63763-25d2-5a7e-bf85-246957b6fda0'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'A microscopic feature of the invasive trophozoite of Entamoeba histolytica is:', '["Nucleus resulting in decreased functional activity","Ingested RBCs in the cytoplasm","Cilia mediated by specific receptor activation","Spores without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Flagella leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ingested RBCs in the cytoplasm', 'Erythrophagocytosis by trophozoites is the hallmark that separates invasive E. histolytica from E. dispar.'),
('0788c23a-2707-5097-b0fa-26ccd9143c7d'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Schistosomiasis is confirmed by demonstrating:', '["Characteristic eggs in urine or stool","Blood smear accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Serology mediated by specific receptor activation","PCR only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Imaging only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Characteristic eggs in urine or stool', 'Finding species-specific eggs in urine or stool is the definitive, direct diagnostic test for schistosomiasis.'),
('31f48db1-51b7-5921-b1dd-942edcaf1dec'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Echinococcus granulosus in humans produces:', '["Fish tapeworm infection","Beef tapeworm infection","Hydatid cyst (liver/lung)","Pork tapeworm infection","Dwarf tapeworm infection"]'::jsonb, 'Hydatid cyst (liver/lung)', 'Ingested Echinococcus eggs develop into hydatid cysts, most often in the liver and lungs.'),
('52bbd0a6-a443-52cb-b37e-ecc24b976e04'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'First-line treatment for Giardia lamblia is:', '["Metronidazole","Praziquantel","Diethylcarbamazine","Ivermectin","Chloroquine"]'::jsonb, 'Metronidazole', 'Metronidazole is the standard first-line drug for giardiasis.'),
('8a35ce91-ad3d-5b93-b47e-6753ecc23fc8'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Wuchereria bancrofti causes which disease?', '["Anemia","Diarrhea","Lymphatic filariasis","Liver abscess","Malaria"]'::jsonb, 'Lymphatic filariasis', 'Adult W. bancrofti worms live in lymphatics causing lymphatic filariasis; chronic lymphatic obstruction may produce elephantiasis.'),
('09dba848-6e7c-5502-a4b8-0400360e8777'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Portal hypertension of the presinusoidal type is a complication of chronic infection with:', '["Schistosoma haematobium","Trypanosoma cruzi","Schistosoma mansoni","Fasciola hepatica","Clonorchis sinensis"]'::jsonb, 'Schistosoma mansoni', 'S. mansoni eggs trapped in portal venules produce Symmers pipestem fibrosis leading to presinusoidal portal hypertension.'),
('df75f8e1-c351-50f1-8a95-d2cba1219491'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Which drug is used to treat strongyloidiasis?', '["Diethylcarbamazine","Ivermectin","Praziquantel","Metronidazole","Albendazole"]'::jsonb, 'Ivermectin', 'Ivermectin is the drug of choice for Strongyloides stercoralis infection.'),
('d5e25a20-13e5-52ae-94f0-021252bbe141'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Kala-azar classically manifests as:', '["Fever, splenomegaly, and pancytopenia","Rash accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mucosal disease mediated by specific receptor activation","Lymphadenopathy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Skin ulcers leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fever, splenomegaly, and pancytopenia', 'Visceral leishmaniasis presents with prolonged fever, massive splenomegaly and pancytopenia due to RE system invasion.'),
('8aef4015-fa64-58fb-9bb7-d3479a89e003'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Taenia solium is a:', '["Pork tapeworm (causes cysticercosis)","Fish tapeworm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Beef tapeworm mediated by specific receptor activation","Dwarf tapeworm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Dog tapeworm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pork tapeworm (causes cysticercosis)', 'T. solium adult is acquired from undercooked pork; its eggs cause cysticercosis via the fecal-oral route.'),
('89b6b47e-5456-5b36-83b5-5d63ab71c28f'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Which findings best describe kala-azar?', '["Lymphadenopathy resulting in decreased functional activity","Fever, splenomegaly, and pancytopenia","Skin ulcers mediated by specific receptor activation","Mucosal lesions without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Rash leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fever, splenomegaly, and pancytopenia', 'The classic triad of visceral leishmaniasis reflects parasitization of the spleen, liver and bone marrow.'),
('63d494be-9e37-5184-9038-aa18cc1db821'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Microfilariae of W. bancrofti in peripheral blood show which periodicity?', '["Nocturnal","Seasonal","None","Diurnal","Annual"]'::jsonb, 'Nocturnal', 'W. bancrofti microfilariae are nocturnally periodic because the vector mosquito bites at night.'),
('c9fe19a3-c067-5f55-b8ad-56c669190ce3'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Leishmania is transmitted by which insect?', '["Sandfly (Phlebotomus)","Blackfly","Mosquito","Tsetse fly","Flea"]'::jsonb, 'Sandfly (Phlebotomus)', 'Transmission is by the bite of infected female Phlebotomus (Old World) or Lutzomyia (New World) sandflies.'),
('aa3cea4e-42aa-54ae-badf-d6c7c32e3575'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Echinococcus granulosus (hydatid) infection is confirmed by:', '["Ultrasound plus serology","PCR","CT only","Stool examination","Blood smear"]'::jsonb, 'Ultrasound plus serology', 'Cyst morphology on ultrasound plus hydatid-specific serology gives the diagnosis with high confidence.'),
('2373a85a-2b06-5be5-b92e-09ac3fe705b2'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Bancroftian filariasis may ultimately lead to:', '["Lymphatic filariasis (elephantiasis)","Malaria accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Typhus mediated by specific receptor activation","Pneumonia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Diarrhea leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lymphatic filariasis (elephantiasis)', 'Chronic lymphatic obstruction by adult W. bancrofti worms causes lymphedema and elephantiasis.'),
('a5bb0bd2-400f-54fb-8df5-52b76dd50259'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Hookworm infection is caused by which parasites?', '["Enterobius vermicularis","Ancylostoma duodenale and Necator americanus","Trichuris trichiura","Ascaris lumbricoides","Strongyloides stercoralis"]'::jsonb, 'Ancylostoma duodenale and Necator americanus', 'The two hookworm species cause iron-deficiency anemia by chronic blood loss from the intestinal wall.'),
('3a179a4b-5ce7-5299-b6ab-f95bea2b840e'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'The vector of lymphatic filariasis is:', '["Mosquito (Culex/Anopheles/Aedes)","Tsetse fly accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sandfly mediated by specific receptor activation","Flea without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Blackfly leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mosquito (Culex/Anopheles/Aedes)', 'W. bancrofti and B. malayi are transmitted by mosquitoes, principally Culex quinquefasciatus.'),
('631cb7f7-7361-5fe9-9937-7e3007bc68f3'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Amebic liver abscess is treated with:', '["Metronidazole (aspiration if needed)","Drainage only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pancreatic enzymes mediated by specific receptor activation","Antipyretics without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Surgery only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Metronidazole (aspiration if needed)', 'Metronidazole is the drug of choice for amebic liver abscess; aspiration is reserved for large or ruptured abscesses.'),
('846fa7b2-b762-50bc-9c29-37ad847df5ea'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Trichuris trichiura is commonly called the:', '["Whipworm (heavy infection causes bloody diarrhea and prolapse)","Hookworm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pinworm mediated by specific receptor activation","Threadworm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Groundworm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Whipworm (heavy infection causes bloody diarrhea and prolapse)', 'The whipworm''s whip-shaped body and heavy infections that cause dysentery and rectal prolapse are classic features.'),
('cef8a303-ac93-514a-911b-4972b19667fe'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Toxoplasma gondii acquired during pregnancy may cause:', '["Congenital toxoplasmosis (hydrocephalus, chorioretinitis)","Spina bifida accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cardiac defects mediated by specific receptor activation","Cleft palate without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Limb defects leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Congenital toxoplasmosis (hydrocephalus, chorioretinitis)', 'Primary maternal infection crosses the placenta and causes congenital toxoplasmosis, classically hydrocephalus, chorioretinitis and intracranial calcification.'),
('36b52740-13e7-5a1a-9d8f-0a1c8629650f'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'In strongyloidiasis, the diagnostic stage found in stool is the:', '["Rhabditiform larva (not eggs)","Egg accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Scotch tape mediated by specific receptor activation","Blood smear without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Serology only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Rhabditiform larva (not eggs)', 'Strongyloides eggs hatch in the gut, so the stool contains rhabditiform larvae rather than eggs.'),
('316551a6-f141-5ba3-a6b9-9202d871476d'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Schistosoma mansoni infection is a cause of:', '["Cercarial dermatitis resulting in decreased functional activity","Bladder cancer accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Portal hypertension (pipestem fibrosis)","Katayama fever without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All of these leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Portal hypertension (pipestem fibrosis)', 'Chronic S. mansoni causes periportal (pipestem) fibrosis and presinusoidal portal hypertension; the other listed features are not the defining late complication.'),
('f55d8aa2-cd97-584a-9528-9d468e69983e'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Trichuris trichiura eggs seen in stool are:', '["Barrel-shaped with bipolar plugs","Blood smear accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Scotch tape mediated by specific receptor activation","Serology without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Radiology leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Barrel-shaped with bipolar plugs', 'Whipworm eggs are barrel-shaped (lemon-shaped) with a translucent bipolar mucus plug.'),
('22b158fe-f5e4-50ab-b378-1f9f9cef1cc1'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Echinococcus granulosus in humans causes:', '["Dog tapeworm infection","Pork tapeworm infection","Hydatid cyst (liver/lung)","Fish tapeworm infection","Beef tapeworm infection"]'::jsonb, 'Hydatid cyst (liver/lung)', 'Humans are accidental intermediate hosts; ingested eggs develop into hydatid cysts, chiefly in the liver.'),
('959aac19-2be5-5eb6-9033-d3daa4fb7564'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Hookworm ova in stool are best described as:', '["Blood smear","PCR","Serology","Oval, thin-shelled eggs","Scotch tape test"]'::jsonb, 'Oval, thin-shelled eggs', 'Hookworm eggs are oval, thin-shelled and contain a segmented embryo on fresh stool examination.'),
('5357bd35-9755-5221-9b33-43e3e7c95de6'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Uncomplicated falciparum malaria should be treated with:', '["ACT (artemisinin-based combination therapy)","Primaquine only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Chloroquine only mediated by specific receptor activation","Metronidazole without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Albendazole leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'ACT (artemisinin-based combination therapy)', 'WHO first-line treatment for uncomplicated P. falciparum malaria is ACT to overcome resistance and prevent emergence of artemisinin monotherapy resistance.'),
('9f0ef4f1-9546-5660-a0b0-969951c0c08d'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'The drug of choice for hyperinfection strongyloidiasis is:', '["Albendazole","Praziquantel","Ivermectin","Diethylcarbamazine","Metronidazole"]'::jsonb, 'Ivermectin', 'Ivermectin is preferred for strongyloidiasis, including hyperinfection caused by autoinfection in the immunocompromised.'),
('0b0569f8-696b-5f6f-9515-207e73976b23'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'The most common organ involved in hydatid disease is the:', '["Hydatid cyst in liver/lung","Fish tapeworm infection","Beef tapeworm infection","Pork tapeworm infection","Dwarf tapeworm infection"]'::jsonb, 'Hydatid cyst in liver/lung', 'The liver is the most common site of E. granulosus hydatid cysts, followed by the lungs.'),
('68911f52-de9b-5178-b362-80712fdbf9d4'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'The sandfly (Phlebotomus) transmits which infection?', '["Mosquito","Blackfly","Tsetse fly","Leishmaniasis","Flea"]'::jsonb, 'Leishmaniasis', 'Phlebotomus sandflies transmit Leishmania promastigotes during blood feeding.'),
('4cbf54bb-ec59-558e-9d65-2a60ccba29ff'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Echinococcus granulosus infection is best confirmed by:', '["Stool exam resulting in decreased functional activity","CT only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Blood smear mediated by specific receptor activation","Ultrasound plus serology","PCR leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ultrasound plus serology', 'Combined imaging and hydatid serology is the standard diagnostic approach for echinococcosis.'),
('003ebea0-45e5-5888-9354-6f5089c08844'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Microfilariae of W. bancrofti show which periodicity in blood?', '["Diurnal","Annual","Seasonal","Nocturnal","Aperiodic"]'::jsonb, 'Nocturnal', 'W. bancrofti microfilariae are nocturnally periodic; night blood sampling yields the diagnosis.'),
('812a1077-2726-5560-8381-a638f636376a'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'The Scotch tape test diagnoses:', '["Ascaris","Strongyloides","Enterobius vermicularis","Hookworm","Trichuris"]'::jsonb, 'Enterobius vermicularis', 'The cellophane tape test on perianal skin in the morning detects pinworm eggs.'),
('f84d7ea8-6da5-55d9-8f71-d3c01292bf27'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Plasmodium malariae causes which pattern of fever?', '["Malignant tertian","Benign tertian","Quartan (every 72 hours)","Ovale","Tertian"]'::jsonb, 'Quartan (every 72 hours)', 'P. malariae''s 72-hour erythrocytic cycle produces quartan malaria; it may also cause nephrotic syndrome.'),
('5726024b-2598-5136-8310-cb9179986090'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Chronic lymphatic obstruction following bancroftian filariasis produces:', '["Lymphatic filariasis (elephantiasis)","Malaria accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Typhus mediated by specific receptor activation","Pneumonia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Diarrhea leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lymphatic filariasis (elephantiasis)', 'Recurrent lymphangitis and lymphatic obstruction by W. bancrofti lead to hydrocele and elephantiasis.'),
('a5de3383-ff31-519f-9cf5-7c2e4e1450fb'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Which drug is indicated for strongyloidiasis?', '["Ivermectin","Metronidazole","Albendazole","Diethylcarbamazine","Praziquantel"]'::jsonb, 'Ivermectin', 'Ivermectin is the treatment of choice for Strongyloides stercoralis.'),
('52245d7c-4672-594a-b8ef-b472034a88f9'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Taenia saginata is diagnosed by identifying gravid proglottids with:', '["Eggs only","Serology only","More than 12 uterine branches","Fewer than 12 uterine branches","A scolex with hooks"]'::jsonb, 'More than 12 uterine branches', 'T. saginata gravid proglottids have more than 12 lateral uterine branches, whereas T. solium has fewer than 12.'),
('27f995e6-5723-5d4c-85c5-c684ea285340'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'The tape test is used for the diagnosis of:', '["Ascaris","Strongyloides","Hookworm","Enterobius vermicularis","Trichuris"]'::jsonb, 'Enterobius vermicularis', 'Enterobius eggs laid perianally at night are detected by adhesive tape applied in the morning.'),
('84c1cd8d-8ddb-5504-8616-31666b29a66e'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Echinococcus granulosus produces what lesion in humans?', '["Hydatid cyst (liver/lung)","Fish tapeworm infection","Pork tapeworm infection","Beef tapeworm infection","Dwarf tapeworm infection"]'::jsonb, 'Hydatid cyst (liver/lung)', 'Humans are intermediate hosts; the larval form forms unilocular hydatid cysts, usually hepatic.'),
('314a91b9-9de7-567d-968d-dfa0f1eaa1f7'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Hookworm disease with iron-deficiency anemia is caused by:', '["Trichuris trichiura","Ancylostoma duodenale and Necator americanus","Enterobius vermicularis","Strongyloides stercoralis","Ascaris lumbricoides"]'::jsonb, 'Ancylostoma duodenale and Necator americanus', 'Blood loss from the mucosa attached by hookworms causes progressive iron-deficiency anemia.'),
('1a4e4dc8-87b5-5d55-ba39-2fc255dd932f'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Primaquine is used to achieve:', '["Radical cure of P. vivax/ovale (kills hypnozoites)","Acute falciparum cure accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Schistosomiasis cure mediated by specific receptor activation","Amoebiasis cure without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Helminth clearance leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Radical cure of P. vivax/ovale (kills hypnozoites)', 'Primaquine eradicates hepatic hypnozoites of P. vivax and P. ovale, preventing relapse.'),
('7a557658-940c-5f31-895a-5ebdf7cb137f'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'The gelatin-tape test is the diagnostic test of choice for:', '["Hookworm","Enterobius vermicularis","Strongyloides","Ascaris","Trichuris"]'::jsonb, 'Enterobius vermicularis', 'The Scotch tape preparation on the perianal area in the early morning detects pinworm eggs.'),
('98b8abd3-a9bf-51b7-bb1d-72f7765a4bbd'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'A complication of heavy Ascaris infection is:', '["Liver abscess","Hydrocele","Blindness","Elephantiasis","Bowel obstruction"]'::jsonb, 'Bowel obstruction', 'A worm bolus in the small intestine causes mechanical bowel obstruction, especially in children.'),
('95ce677d-8533-5bdb-864b-8080fac9b304'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Hookworm infection is diagnosed by finding in stool:', '["Blood smear","Scotch tape test","Oval, thin-shelled eggs","PCR","Serology"]'::jsonb, 'Oval, thin-shelled eggs', 'The characteristic oval, thin-shelled hookworm eggs are seen on stool microscopy.'),
('5a2c52ad-b2b6-5504-b902-eed6ec35085d'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'The parasites responsible for hookworm infection are:', '["Enterobius vermicularis","Trichuris trichiura","Ancylostoma duodenale and Necator americanus","Strongyloides stercoralis","Ascaris lumbricoides"]'::jsonb, 'Ancylostoma duodenale and Necator americanus', 'These two hookworm species attach to the intestinal mucosa and cause chronic blood-loss anemia.'),
('29d354fa-064d-5e08-9363-7a4216d76e77'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Echinococcus granulosus is diagnosed by:', '["Ultrasound plus serology","PCR","Stool examination","CT only","Blood smear"]'::jsonb, 'Ultrasound plus serology', 'Ultrasonography shows the cyst and serology confirms Echinococcus-specific antibodies.'),
('57b658e5-66f1-5953-946d-bf32d3fd10e1'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Taenia solium is:', '["Pork tapeworm (causes cysticercosis)","Fish tapeworm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Beef tapeworm mediated by specific receptor activation","Dwarf tapeworm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Dog tapeworm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pork tapeworm (causes cysticercosis)', 'T. solium is the pork tapeworm; ingestion of its eggs produces cysticercosis.'),
('a8a919fd-936b-5712-a9d5-bbbb82ab2f99'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Schistosoma mansoni eggs are best described as:', '["Laterally spined","Terminally spined","Barrel-shaped","Bipolar plugged","Mammillated"]'::jsonb, 'Laterally spined', 'The lateral spine of S. mansoni eggs distinguishes them from the terminal-spined eggs of S. haematobium.'),
('1e9effb7-e512-586d-88b8-8c6124785258'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Whipworm is acquired by:', '["Inhalation","Skin penetration","Ingesting embryonated eggs","Vector bite","Blood transfusion"]'::jsonb, 'Ingesting embryonated eggs', 'Trichuris eggs embryonate in soil and infect humans when swallowed with contaminated food or water.'),
('2d8b4c46-e5d9-57ed-90ff-5abe4d32f826'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Echinococcus granulosus commonly causes cysts in the:', '["Liver (and lung)","Fish muscularis","Beef muscle","Pork muscle","Dwarf intestine"]'::jsonb, 'Liver (and lung)', 'Hydatid cysts of E. granulosus are most often hepatic, with pulmonary cysts the second commonest.'),
('4078a912-1cf3-5f9f-b29f-aaf23b655e3f'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'The diagnosis of hydatid cyst is usually made by:', '["Ultrasound plus serology","CT only","Stool examination","PCR only","Blood smear"]'::jsonb, 'Ultrasound plus serology', 'Imaging of the cyst and hydatid-specific antibody detection together confirm the diagnosis.'),
('87afc2c6-d89a-5227-b97f-a4a9fc7b996c'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Filariasis is transmitted by:', '["Culex mosquito","Tsetse fly","Sandfly","Flea","Blackfly"]'::jsonb, 'Culex mosquito', 'Bancroftian filariasis is mainly transmitted by Culex quinquefasciatus.'),
('fb55ff3e-e38e-5f6f-a00e-0cf8a0acabe5'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Taenia saginata is the:', '["Fish tapeworm","Dog tapeworm","Beef tapeworm (no cysticercosis)","Dwarf tapeworm","Pork tapeworm"]'::jsonb, 'Beef tapeworm (no cysticercosis)', 'T. saginata is the beef tapeworm; unlike T. solium, its eggs do not cause human cysticercosis.'),
('df2b793f-ed40-594d-80ec-023ae1c7f9ce'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'First-line therapy for giardiasis is:', '["Metronidazole","Praziquantel","Diethylcarbamazine","Ivermectin","Chloroquine"]'::jsonb, 'Metronidazole', 'Giardia lamblia infection is treated with metronidazole as first-line therapy.'),
('2874a8a2-11e4-57a6-af40-b39faa3af46d'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Hydatid disease is diagnosed by:', '["Ultrasound plus serology","CT only","Stool examination","PCR only","Blood smear"]'::jsonb, 'Ultrasound plus serology', 'Typical cyst morphology on ultrasound coupled with hydatid serology is diagnostic.'),
('97a8f861-13c1-54fc-9bef-5092d3e544a7'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'The infectious form of Giardia lamblia is the:', '["Oocyst","Trophozoite","Spore","Cyst (four nuclei)","Egg"]'::jsonb, 'Cyst (four nuclei)', 'The mature, four-nucleated cyst is infectious; trophozoites are too fragile to transmit disease.'),
('55469326-2505-5247-9687-1251229ac77c'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'DEC (diethylcarbamazine) is the drug of choice for:', '["Amebiasis","Lymphatic filariasis","Malaria","Leishmaniasis","Schistosomiasis"]'::jsonb, 'Lymphatic filariasis', 'Diethylcarbamazine is the drug of choice for lymphatic filariasis caused by W. bancrofti and B. malayi.'),
('743baf71-fc45-56c3-8f14-0918dedda4b5'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Taenia saginata is diagnosed by identifying proglottids with:', '["Both patterns","Eggs only","Serology only","More than 12 uterine branches","Fewer than 12 uterine branches"]'::jsonb, 'More than 12 uterine branches', 'Gravid proglottids of T. saginata contain more than 12 lateral uterine branches, unlike T. solium.'),
('da8da997-6e1b-5d71-8054-91b374e595d2'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'S. mansoni eggs in stool have a characteristic:', '["Lateral spine","Terminal spine","Barrel shape","Bipolar plugs","Thick mammillated coat"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral spine', 'The presence of a lateral spine is the defining feature of the S. mansoni egg.'),
('5feeb4db-5c87-5395-a946-ccb80bed5347'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Ascaris lumbricoides is:', '["Rare","Most common nematode","A cestode","Eradicated","A trematode"]'::jsonb, 'Most common nematode', 'Ascaris is the commonest intestinal nematode infection worldwide.'),
('6961e694-7c32-5abb-9954-fa7da44c723b'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Taenia solium is the:', '["Fish tapeworm resulting in decreased functional activity","Dwarf tapeworm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pork tapeworm (causes cysticercosis)","Beef tapeworm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Dog tapeworm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pork tapeworm (causes cysticercosis)', 'T. solium, the pork tapeworm, causes cysticercosis when its eggs are ingested.'),
('e0e2ba1c-e6f1-5453-b006-110144f71d9b'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'The late hepatic complication of Schistosoma mansoni is:', '["Katayama fever resulting in decreased functional activity","Portal hypertension (pipestem fibrosis)","Bladder cancer mediated by specific receptor activation","Cercarial dermatitis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All of these leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Portal hypertension (pipestem fibrosis)', 'Fibrosis around eggs in portal triads produces periportal (Symmers) fibrosis causing portal hypertension.'),
('ce4113c0-2ab4-5a5e-b08c-a4e74a2f6bd4'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Loeffler syndrome (transient pulmonary infiltrates and eosinophilia) is caused by larval migration of:', '["Ascaris lumbricoides through the lungs","Enterobius vermicularis","Taenia solium","Trichuris trichiura","Giardia lamblia"]'::jsonb, 'Ascaris lumbricoides through the lungs', 'Ascaris larvae migrate through the lungs causing transient infiltrates with eosinophilia (Loeffler syndrome).'),
('734a2910-3dc5-5ae2-9271-86379974a4ad'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Elephantiasis is the result of:', '["Chronic lymphatic obstruction","Bone deformity","Acute muscle spasm","Vitamin deficiency","Skin infection"]'::jsonb, 'Chronic lymphatic obstruction', 'Long-standing lymphatic obstruction by filarial worms produces lymphedema and elephantiasis.'),
('3905be11-dcd9-50a1-998c-94d8f82e4bdb'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Schistosomiasis is confirmed by finding:', '["Eggs in urine or stool","Blood smear","Serology","PCR only","Imaging only"]'::jsonb, 'Eggs in urine or stool', 'Demonstration of characteristic schistosome eggs in urine or stool is the definitive diagnosis.'),
('a81d94b5-3616-5976-b5ab-00b7739aa2b6'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Leishmaniasis is transmitted by:', '["Sandfly (Phlebotomus)","Blackfly","Mosquito","Flea","Tsetse fly"]'::jsonb, 'Sandfly (Phlebotomus)', 'Female sandflies of the genus Phlebotomus transmit Leishmania promastigotes.'),
('b19daa39-2afd-54e9-aebb-2e7d1f1823d6'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Bancroftian filariasis causes:', '["Lymphatic filariasis (elephantiasis)","Malaria accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Typhus mediated by specific receptor activation","Pneumonia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Diarrhea leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lymphatic filariasis (elephantiasis)', 'Adult worms in lymphatics produce lymphatic filariasis with chronic elephantiasis.'),
('2787808a-e8c2-50be-83ba-1d6dab13966e'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Trichuris trichiura, the whipworm, causes:', '["Hookworm disease","Pinworm infection","Strongyloidiasis","Ascariasis","Dysentery with rectal prolapse"]'::jsonb, 'Dysentery with rectal prolapse', 'Heavy whipworm infection causes bloody diarrhea (dysentery) and rectal prolapse, especially in children.'),
('74c666c0-514e-52fb-a0bb-fe7d969074e9'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'The agent that eradicates hepatic hypnozoites of P. vivax and P. ovale is:', '["Prophylaxis","Acute falciparum drugs","Primaquine (radical cure)","Chloroquine","None"]'::jsonb, 'Primaquine (radical cure)', 'Primaquine kills latent liver hypnozoites of vivax and ovale malaria, effecting radical cure.'),
('6179b3bd-09db-556f-ba0a-12af88d09105'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'The drug of choice for strongyloidiasis including hyperinfection is:', '["Ivermectin","Albendazole","Praziquantel","Metronidazole","Diethylcarbamazine"]'::jsonb, 'Ivermectin', 'Ivermectin is the treatment of choice for Strongyloides, including hyperinfection syndrome.'),
('5105a151-304e-5e86-907f-6f2b55201f37'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Toxoplasmosis acquired in pregnancy may cause:', '["Congenital toxoplasmosis (hydrocephalus, chorioretinitis)","Spina bifida accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cardiac defects mediated by specific receptor activation","Cleft palate without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Limb defects leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Congenital toxoplasmosis (hydrocephalus, chorioretinitis)', 'Transplacental transmission of Toxoplasma gondii causes congenital toxoplasmosis, classically hydrocephalus and chorioretinitis.'),
('82352cc3-797e-5696-9a2a-493b83d867c9'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'S. mansoni eggs possess:', '["Lateral spine","Terminal spine","Barrel shape","Bipolar plugs","Thick mammillated coat"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral spine', 'S. mansoni eggs carry a lateral spine, distinguishing them from the terminal-spined S. haematobium eggs.'),
('2b803b9f-a842-58e9-b1d8-5133cb72a142'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'The tissue amebicide of choice for invasive amebiasis is:', '["Paromomycin","Iodoquinol","Albendazole","Metronidazole","Diloxanide furoate"]'::jsonb, 'Metronidazole', 'Metronidazole is the drug of choice for invasive tissue amebiasis.'),
('1f3ee6b2-9914-5ca7-8050-589529451d13'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Whipworm infection is diagnosed by:', '["Scotch tape","Radiology","Blood smear","Serology","Barrel-shaped eggs in stool"]'::jsonb, 'Barrel-shaped eggs in stool', 'Trichuris is diagnosed by the characteristic barrel-shaped eggs with bipolar plugs in stool.'),
('37d7aacd-4480-54ad-aeb1-99d969ba2de0'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Loeffler syndrome is associated with migration of:', '["Brain resulting in decreased functional activity","Skin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Gut mediated by specific receptor activation","Hepatic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ascaris larvae through the lungs"]'::jsonb, 'Ascaris larvae through the lungs', 'Trans-pulmonary passage of Ascaris larvae produces transient eosinophilic infiltrates (Loeffler syndrome).'),
('5a38a13a-69f1-5988-9793-125c87bcd466'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Giardia lamblia causes:', '["Giardiasis (watery diarrhea with malabsorption)","Leishmaniasis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ascariasis mediated by specific receptor activation","Malaria without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Amebic dysentery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Giardiasis (watery diarrhea with malabsorption)', 'Giardia colonizes the upper small intestine causing foul diarrhea and malabsorption.'),
('71d2c857-4a40-5e89-85a7-c7a5845544be'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'DEC (diethylcarbamazine) is used for:', '["Amebiasis","Leishmaniasis","Schistosomiasis","Malaria","Lymphatic filariasis"]'::jsonb, 'Lymphatic filariasis', 'Diethylcarbamazine is the treatment of choice for lymphatic filariasis.'),
('f82722a3-bb6e-55af-992b-95646a6be015'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Enterobius vermicularis characteristically causes:', '["Diarrhea","Abdominal pain","Anemia","Nocturnal perianal itching","Malabsorption"]'::jsonb, 'Nocturnal perianal itching', 'Pinworm infection presents with nocturnal perianal pruritus from perianal egg deposition.'),
('f81aa5aa-0b81-5d99-9a7f-a02a6a675a00'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Leishmaniasis is spread by:', '["Sandfly (Phlebotomus)","Blackfly","Mosquito","Flea","Tsetse fly"]'::jsonb, 'Sandfly (Phlebotomus)', 'Phlebotomus sandflies are the biological vectors of all forms of leishmaniasis.'),
('b0424305-1935-5541-a313-29ba1b02ed1e'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Leishmania donovani is the cause of:', '["Visceral leishmaniasis (kala-azar)","Cutaneous leishmaniasis","Mucocutaneous leishmaniasis","Chagas disease","Toxoplasmosis"]'::jsonb, 'Visceral leishmaniasis (kala-azar)', 'L. donovani parasitizes RF cells causing kala-azar; cutaneous form is due to L. tropica/major.'),
('eee00bf4-f846-5ce1-bfe0-623a682794b7'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Entamoeba histolytica causes:', '["Amebic dysentery and liver abscess","Leishmaniasis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Giardiasis mediated by specific receptor activation","Malaria without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ascariasis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Amebic dysentery and liver abscess', 'Invasive E. histolytica causes dysentery and hematogenous spread to the liver forming abscesses.'),
('f82fd2c6-b11a-58e5-addc-a4f809ac788e'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Bancroftian filariasis manifests as:', '["Lymphatic filariasis (elephantiasis)","Malaria accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Typhus mediated by specific receptor activation","Pneumonia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Diarrhea leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lymphatic filariasis (elephantiasis)', 'W. bancrofti blocks lymphatics producing lymphedema and elephantiasis.'),
('669a7eb4-0e6e-5332-998b-515e438bc8f9'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Trophozoites of E. histolytica contain:', '["Flagella resulting in decreased functional activity","Cilia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Nucleus mediated by specific receptor activation","Ingested RBCs in the cytoplasm","Spores leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ingested RBCs in the cytoplasm', 'Erythrophagocytosis by the trophozoite is the hallmark distinguishing E. histolytica from E. dispar.'),
('fee2d701-dd90-5863-a31c-1ec4d75c93bd'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Which is true of Ascaris lumbricoides?', '["It is the most common nematode worldwide","A trematode accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Rare in humans mediated by specific receptor activation","Eradicated without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A cestode leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'It is the most common nematode worldwide', 'Ascaris lumbricoides infects about one billion people globally.'),
('a33d07f3-43d5-5474-8612-3bfada92a168'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Strongyloidiasis is diagnosed by finding in the stool:', '["Serology","Eggs","Scotch tape","Larvae (rhabditiform)","Blood smear"]'::jsonb, 'Larvae (rhabditiform)', 'Strongyloides eggs hatch immediately; stool contains motile rhabditiform larvae.'),
('d7354fea-5fad-5d86-9638-f33efe20a309'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'A key feature of Strongyloides stercoralis is:', '["Autoinfection (internal reinfection)","Gut only","Autoinfection plus hyperinfection","Self-limited","Skin only"]'::jsonb, 'Autoinfection plus hyperinfection', 'Strongyloides autoinfects the host; in the immunocompromised this leads to hyperinfection syndrome.'),
('575e131b-61c0-55e9-92e2-1276bdfdd9a8'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'First-line treatment of Giardia lamblia is:', '["Metronidazole","Praziquantel","Diethylcarbamazine","Ivermectin","Chloroquine"]'::jsonb, 'Metronidazole', 'Metronidazole is the first-line therapy for giardiasis.'),
('071213d9-291d-5117-8abd-f43ac167c4f1'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'The vector of filariasis is:', '["Culex mosquito","Tsetse fly","Sandfly","Flea","Blackfly"]'::jsonb, 'Culex mosquito', 'Mosquitoes, particularly Culex, transmit lymphatic filariasis.'),
('3c1caaef-0a35-5197-8b5b-c6871f23ce7c'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Taeniasis (adult tapeworm infection) is treated with:', '["Metronidazole","Praziquantel","Albendazole","Diethylcarbamazine","Ivermectin"]'::jsonb, 'Praziquantel', 'Praziquantel is the drug of choice for intestinal taeniasis.'),
('0e09aab2-8779-5e86-92c3-985691005b9c'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Primaquine is used for:', '["Radical cure of P. vivax/ovale (kills hypnozoites)","Acute falciparum only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Schistosomiasis mediated by specific receptor activation","Amoebiasis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Helminths leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Radical cure of P. vivax/ovale (kills hypnozoites)', 'Primaquine eradicates hypnozoites of vivax and ovale malaria producing a radical cure.'),
('d6888eb4-325d-5bcf-8cb2-cdd857614c6a'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Wuchereria bancrofti infection leads to:', '["Lymphatic filariasis (elephantiasis)","Malaria accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Typhus mediated by specific receptor activation","Pneumonia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Diarrhea leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lymphatic filariasis (elephantiasis)', 'W. bancrofti, a lymphatic parasite, causes elephantiasis through chronic lymphatic obstruction.'),
('21cc3947-7dd7-5816-92d9-c10c525c36b1'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'PKDL refers to:', '["Cutaneous leishmaniasis","Mucocutaneous leishmaniasis","Tropical splenomegaly","Post-kala-azar dermal leishmaniasis","Skin form of VL"]'::jsonb, 'Post-kala-azar dermal leishmaniasis', 'PKDL is a dermal sequel of visceral leishmaniasis appearing after treatment, with hypopigmented macules and nodules.'),
('9f67a738-5d2a-58a6-8bf0-b33e25935e55'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Trichuris diagnosis is based on:', '["Barrel-shaped eggs with bipolar plugs","Blood smear accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Scotch tape mediated by specific receptor activation","Serology without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Radiology leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Barrel-shaped eggs with bipolar plugs', 'The barrel-shaped, bipolar-plugged eggs of T. trichiura are diagnostic in stool.'),
('dc5a306f-2136-50cf-b47d-b3a30b9eec72'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Artemisinin is used in malaria as:', '["Radical cure resulting in decreased functional activity","Prophylaxis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Monotherapy mediated by specific receptor activation","ACT for uncomplicated falciparum malaria","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'ACT for uncomplicated falciparum malaria', 'Artemisinin-based combination therapy (ACT) is the WHO standard for uncomplicated falciparum malaria.'),
('f229f85e-9434-55a9-8461-5e232c425d00'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Malaria is confirmed by:', '["RDT only resulting in decreased functional activity","PCR accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Serology mediated by specific receptor activation","Culture without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thick and thin blood smear"]'::jsonb, 'Thick and thin blood smear', 'Microscopy of Giemsa-stained thick and thin blood films is the mainstay of malaria diagnosis.'),
('0ba279d3-0286-5b47-9cbb-3ef3f4849490'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'The infectious form of Giardia is:', '["Spore","Cyst (four nuclei)","Oocyst","Egg","Trophozoite"]'::jsonb, 'Cyst (four nuclei)', 'The mature, four-nucleate cyst is the infectious, environmentally resistant stage of Giardia.'),
('d108c575-69b7-5575-8b4c-c975573bc509'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'A drug used for radical cure of vivax/ovale malaria is:', '["Radical cure of P. vivax/ovale (kills hypnozoites)","Acute falciparum only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Schistosomiasis mediated by specific receptor activation","Amoebiasis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Helminths leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Radical cure of P. vivax/ovale (kills hypnozoites)', 'The drug, primaquine, kills hypnozoites and provides radical cure of vivax/ovale malaria.'),
('3106f598-ef17-53fa-a5bc-5039dea6b851'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Loeffler syndrome is produced by:', '["Hepatic resulting in decreased functional activity","Brain accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Skin mediated by specific receptor activation","Ascaris larvae migrating through the lungs","Gut infection leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ascaris larvae migrating through the lungs', 'Larvae passing through the pulmonary circulation evoke transient eosinophilic pneumonitis.'),
('874fc8db-c2ae-5467-9872-b5159c300e52'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'The periodicity of W. bancrofti microfilariae is:', '["Diurnal","Annual","Seasonal","Nocturnal","Aperiodic"]'::jsonb, 'Nocturnal', 'W. bancrofti microfilariae are nocturnally periodic; blood taken at night demonstrates them best.'),
('23d6d479-3017-5a81-a212-742ec1564157'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Hookworm larvae enter the human host by:', '["Blood","Ingestion","Skin penetration","Inhalation","Vector bite"]'::jsonb, 'Skin penetration', 'Infective filariform larvae penetrate intact skin, classically of the bare foot.'),
('d43bc597-07a3-546a-9139-2bfa5a62add4'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Hookworm infection is acquired through:', '["Skin penetration by filariform larvae","Ingestion of eggs accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Vector bite mediated by specific receptor activation","Blood transfusion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inhalation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Skin penetration by filariform larvae', 'Filariform larvae in contaminated soil penetrate the skin to initiate hookworm infection.'),
('a96e2ae3-2546-52f7-80d0-3f9de90f93f0'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'E. histolytica trophozoites contain:', '["Ingested RBCs in the cytoplasm","Cilia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Flagella only mediated by specific receptor activation","Spores without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Nucleus only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ingested RBCs in the cytoplasm', 'Ingested erythrocytes in the cytoplasm are the hallmark of invasive E. histolytica trophozoites.'),
('e0a1faa7-ebae-53fb-aed0-1617d09f8fd3'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'First-line drug for giardiasis is:', '["Metronidazole","Praziquantel","Diethylcarbamazine","Ivermectin","Chloroquine"]'::jsonb, 'Metronidazole', 'Metronidazole is first-line treatment of Giardia lamblia infection.'),
('8b87af9c-3d75-505f-9f1d-7f5b3f8991cd'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Hookworm larvae infect humans by:', '["Larvae penetrating the skin","Blood accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inhalation mediated by specific receptor activation","Vector without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ingestion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Larvae penetrating the skin', 'Hookworm filariform larvae actively penetrate unbroken skin.'),
('e19cb4fa-cc88-54df-a7fd-4835d26f8cb4'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Giardia lamblia is transmitted by:', '["Spore","Oocyst","Cyst (four nuclei)","Trophozoite","Egg"]'::jsonb, 'Cyst (four nuclei)', 'The mature four-nucleate cyst is the infectious form of Giardia.'),
('4929c763-8f86-5f8b-9b17-993aaca99e58'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Echinococcus granulosus infection is diagnosed by:', '["Ultrasound plus serology","CT only","Stool examination","PCR only","Blood smear"]'::jsonb, 'Ultrasound plus serology', 'Ultrasound outlines the hydatid cyst and serology confirms the species-specific diagnosis.'),
('7ef55cbe-852c-5bbc-87b6-e1b08872b29f'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Leishmania is transmitted by:', '["Mosquito","Tsetse fly","Flea","Sandfly (Phlebotomus)","Blackfly"]'::jsonb, 'Sandfly (Phlebotomus)', 'Phlebotomus (and Lutzomyia) sandflies transmit Leishmania promastigotes.'),
('e6428a43-dbbf-562c-8b22-1e881d96f334'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'A characteristic feature of Strongyloides stercoralis is:', '["Autoinfection","Skin only","Gut only","Autoinfection plus hyperinfection","Self-limited infection"]'::jsonb, 'Autoinfection plus hyperinfection', 'Internal autoinfection permits persistence; immunosuppression leads to hyperinfection.'),
('92b0d2d6-d121-5b5d-846d-471fb7928d41'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Pinworm is diagnosed by:', '["Scotch tape test","Blood smear","Barrel-shaped eggs in stool","Serology","Radiology"]'::jsonb, 'Scotch tape test', 'Perianal adhesive tape sampling detects pinworm eggs deposited at night.'),
('52d6cc4d-d822-5083-bd18-a13df197b846'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Trichuris trichiura is the:', '["Strongyloides resulting in decreased functional activity","Ascaris accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pinworm mediated by specific receptor activation","Whipworm (bloody diarrhea)","Hookworm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Whipworm (bloody diarrhea)', 'The whipworm, T. trichiura, causes bloody diarrhea and rectal prolapse in heavy infection.'),
('faa50aee-98c2-52a4-b897-87697f0a3e26'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Strongyloidiasis is treated with:', '["Praziquantel","Diethylcarbamazine","Metronidazole","Albendazole","Ivermectin"]'::jsonb, 'Ivermectin', 'Ivermectin is the drug of choice for Strongyloides stercoralis.'),
('8454d548-7dfd-57e2-8fe9-b1836ebed71b'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Enterobius vermicularis causes:', '["Pinworm (perianal itching)","Malabsorption accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pain mediated by specific receptor activation","Anemia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Diarrhea leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pinworm (perianal itching)', 'Enterobius infection classically produces nocturnal perianal itching.'),
('ae46c310-4ca4-5b09-97ce-0fd44918d0ef'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'The treatment of choice for giardiasis is:', '["Metronidazole","Praziquantel","Diethylcarbamazine","Ivermectin","Chloroquine"]'::jsonb, 'Metronidazole', 'Giardia lamblia responds best to metronidazole.'),
('4dae1b7d-231a-548c-98be-c90611351e34'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'S. mansoni results in:', '["Bladder cancer resulting in decreased functional activity","Katayama fever accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","HCC mediated by specific receptor activation","Portal hypertension (pipestem fibrosis)","Cercarial dermatitis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Portal hypertension (pipestem fibrosis)', 'Chronic S. mansoni causes periportal fibrosis with presinusoidal portal hypertension.'),
('e722de52-3ec4-5ebb-8d2f-09e1649e78ac'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'The pinworm is best diagnosed by:', '["Scotch tape test","Blood smear","Barrel-shaped eggs in stool","Serology","Radiology"]'::jsonb, 'Scotch tape test', 'The Scotch tape test on perianal skin in the morning identifies pinworm eggs.'),
('79368455-3ade-52d9-bc54-9df6cd556447'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Taenia solium (incomplete name "solium") is the:', '["Dog tapeworm resulting in decreased functional activity","Beef tapeworm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dwarf tapeworm mediated by specific receptor activation","Fish tapeworm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pork tapeworm (causes cysticercosis)"]'::jsonb, 'Pork tapeworm (causes cysticercosis)', 'T. solium is the pork tapeworm; its eggs cause cysticercosis in humans.'),
('ad51685c-c061-5564-ae45-408e21e2371b'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'The infectious stage of Giardia is:', '["Egg","Oocyst","Trophozoite","Spore","Cyst (four nuclei)"]'::jsonb, 'Cyst (four nuclei)', 'The mature tetranucleate cyst infects new hosts by surviving gastric passage and excysting.'),
('d32a3177-0045-5515-80d5-d8a6e0f35241'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Ascaris complication includes:', '["Bowel obstruction","Hydrocele","Liver abscess","Elephantiasis","Blindness"]'::jsonb, 'Bowel obstruction', 'Heavy Ascaris loads can mechanically obstruct the small intestine.'),
('11f586cf-8fdd-5942-8d53-054a5d24ced8'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Leishmania donovani causes:', '["Visceral leishmaniasis (kala-azar)","Cutaneous leishmaniasis","Mucocutaneous leishmaniasis","Chagas disease","All forms"]'::jsonb, 'Visceral leishmaniasis (kala-azar)', 'L. donovani invades RE cells producing kala-azar; mucosal forms are caused by other species.'),
('70b6fff3-b6e2-5635-8ea6-9d00c97c913b'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Echinococcus granulosus produces:', '["Fish tapeworm","Pork tapeworm","Hydatid cyst (liver/lung)","Beef tapeworm","Dog tapeworm"]'::jsonb, 'Hydatid cyst (liver/lung)', 'The larval form of E. granulosus forms hydatid cysts chiefly in the liver and lungs.'),
('8197cf24-78aa-5c99-be91-ab2359c5e1be'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Schistosoma haematobium is associated with:', '["Portal hypertension","Bladder cancer (squamous cell)","HCC","Cirrhosis","Cholangiocarcinoma"]'::jsonb, 'Bladder cancer (squamous cell)', 'S. haematobium causes calcification and squamous cell carcinoma of the urinary bladder.'),
('3bbdbc46-d899-5c33-ad9d-e44eb3edc6af'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Toxoplasma infection during pregnancy may cause:', '["Congenital toxoplasmosis (hydrocephalus, chorioretinitis)","Spina bifida accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cardiac defects mediated by specific receptor activation","Cleft palate without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Limb defects leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Congenital toxoplasmosis (hydrocephalus, chorioretinitis)', 'Maternal primary infection can be transmitted transplacentally causing congenital toxoplasmosis.'),
('8865600f-aabb-5de9-89f2-23df21eabdee'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Trichuris trichiura is transmitted by:', '["Inhalation resulting in decreased functional activity","Blood accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Skin penetration mediated by specific receptor activation","Vector without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ingestion of embryonated eggs"]'::jsonb, 'Ingestion of embryonated eggs', 'Whipworm eggs embryonate in soil and infect when ingested with contaminated food or water.'),
('55aa18d5-29da-5739-8fca-677e51bd8a07'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Echinococcus granulosus commonly causes:', '["Hydatid cyst (liver/lung)","Fish tapeworm infection","Beef tapeworm infection","Pork tapeworm infection","Dwarf tapeworm infection"]'::jsonb, 'Hydatid cyst (liver/lung)', 'E. granulosus larval cysts (hydatid) most often develop in the liver, then lungs.'),
('fb44d22b-90e3-5f12-9554-f325088e8f28'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Loeffler syndrome is caused by:', '["Gut resulting in decreased functional activity","Ascaris larvae migrating through the lungs","Skin mediated by specific receptor activation","Brain without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hepatic infection leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ascaris larvae migrating through the lungs', 'Pulmonary passage of Ascaris larvae triggers transient eosinophilic infiltrates (Loeffler syndrome).'),
('3f369b16-218e-538c-87d8-26a8f68f5a48'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'The tissue amebicide of choice is:', '["Diloxanide furoate","Paromomycin","Metronidazole","Iodoquinol","Albendazole"]'::jsonb, 'Metronidazole', 'Metronidazole is the tissue amebicide of choice for invasive amebiasis.'),
('b6d42ee3-5d6f-504c-a024-2286c91c965b'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Pinworm infection is diagnosed by:', '["Scotch tape test","Blood smear","Barrel-shaped eggs in stool","Serology","Radiology"]'::jsonb, 'Scotch tape test', 'The Scotch (cellophane) tape test on the perianal skin detects pinworm eggs.'),
('a3e7cc9c-7ea2-5b28-85d9-e0775446b8f0'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Kala-azar presents with:', '["Fever, splenomegaly, and pancytopenia","Rash only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mucosal lesions mediated by specific receptor activation","Lymphadenopathy only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Skin ulcers leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fever, splenomegaly, and pancytopenia', 'Kala-azar manifests with prolonged fever, splenomegaly and pancytopenia from RE invasion.'),
('4a3c7fe6-d205-596c-a9b9-eede612bb16c'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Toxoplasmosis during pregnancy may produce:', '["Congenital toxoplasmosis (hydrocephalus, chorioretinitis)","Spina bifida accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cardiac defects mediated by specific receptor activation","Cleft palate without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Limb defects leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Congenital toxoplasmosis (hydrocephalus, chorioretinitis)', 'Toxoplasma crosses the placenta causing congenital toxoplasmosis characterized by hydrocephalus and chorioretinitis.'),
('814d28ba-ff21-52e9-b083-5b659cdbf0ea'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'The infectious form of Giardia lamblia is:', '["Egg","Trophozoite","Oocyst","Spore","Cyst (four nuclei)"]'::jsonb, 'Cyst (four nuclei)', 'The mature four-nucleate Giardia cyst is the infectious, transmissible stage.'),
('a802991e-7da8-5111-8628-fddff26b80ae'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Schistosomiasis is diagnosed by demonstrating:', '["Eggs in urine or stool","Blood smear","Serology","PCR only","Imaging only"]'::jsonb, 'Eggs in urine or stool', 'Species-specific eggs in urine or stool are the diagnostic hallmark of schistosomiasis.'),
('a98bb6dd-78ed-5de7-8cf0-ea731ffb910d'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'S. mansoni chronic infection leads to:', '["Portal hypertension (pipestem fibrosis)","Katayama fever accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bladder cancer mediated by specific receptor activation","Cercarial dermatitis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All of these leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Portal hypertension (pipestem fibrosis)', 'Egg-associated periportal fibrosis causes presinusoidal portal hypertension in chronic S. mansoni.'),
('cc60fdd7-3376-56cc-99fd-20d76fa14deb'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Heavy Ascaris infection can cause:', '["Bowel obstruction","Blindness","Liver abscess","Elephantiasis","Hydrocele"]'::jsonb, 'Bowel obstruction', 'Worm bolus can cause mechanical intestinal obstruction in heavy ascariasis.'),
('4cd3fbbf-a20a-58db-9fb0-2f52d54b59ba'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'S. mansoni eggs are:', '["Laterally spined","Terminally spined","Barrel-shaped","Bipolar-plugged","Mammillated"]'::jsonb, 'Laterally spined', 'S. mansoni eggs bear a lateral spine distinguishing them from terminal-spined S. haematobium.'),
('0a4ae8f5-5a69-52d9-b2fc-5647d2fa32f3'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Artemisinin is used as:', '["Prophylaxis resulting in decreased functional activity","ACT for uncomplicated falciparum malaria","Monotherapy mediated by specific receptor activation","Radical cure without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'ACT for uncomplicated falciparum malaria', 'Artemisinin is given as combination therapy (ACT) for uncomplicated falciparum malaria per WHO.'),
('cb3437ad-1cf2-501b-b199-ef20d5732a18'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Plasmodium malariae produces which fever pattern?', '["Ovale","Tertian","Quartan (every 72 hours)","Malignant tertian","Benign tertian"]'::jsonb, 'Quartan (every 72 hours)', 'P. malariae has a 72-hour cycle producing quartan malaria.'),
('bc170fa2-000a-5be5-b190-58f41e5d6131'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Taeniasis is treated with:', '["Ivermectin","Metronidazole","Diethylcarbamazine","Albendazole","Praziquantel"]'::jsonb, 'Praziquantel', 'Praziquantel is the drug of choice for intestinal taeniasis.'),
('12dbd45e-e950-5107-b475-732a93254832'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Strongyloidiasis is diagnosed by finding in stool:', '["Serology","Larvae (rhabditiform)","Scotch tape","Blood smear","Eggs"]'::jsonb, 'Larvae (rhabditiform)', 'Rhabditiform larvae, not eggs, are found in Strongyloides infection.'),
('ada728ef-b0b4-5293-9e2f-c11bf926b1d4'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Hookworm is diagnosed by finding in stool:', '["Oval, thin-shelled eggs","Scotch tape","PCR","Blood smear","Serology"]'::jsonb, 'Oval, thin-shelled eggs', 'Hookworm eggs are oval and thin-shelled on stool microscopy.'),
('bdb1cdfc-aae4-5879-b8eb-45bd95e7bae5'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Enterobius (pinworm) is diagnosed by:', '["Scotch tape test","Blood smear","Barrel-shaped eggs in stool","Serology","Radiology"]'::jsonb, 'Scotch tape test', 'Perianal tape sampling in the morning captures pinworm eggs.'),
('98e148f2-469d-5a0b-a42d-bd100518b7a6'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Giardiasis is treated with:', '["Metronidazole","Praziquantel","Chloroquine","Diethylcarbamazine","Ivermectin"]'::jsonb, 'Metronidazole', 'Metronidazole is the treatment of choice for giardiasis.'),
('4a33131a-e301-52a1-9086-8126db12b313'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Pinworm infection is confirmed by:', '["Scotch tape test","Blood smear","Barrel-shaped eggs in stool","Serology","Radiology"]'::jsonb, 'Scotch tape test', 'Adhesive tape pressed on the perianal skin early morning detects pinworm eggs.'),
('bf2b6cea-92f5-5f5b-8a02-5ed2e669c7d7'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'The first-line drug for Giardia lamblia is:', '["Metronidazole","Praziquantel","Diethylcarbamazine","Ivermectin","Chloroquine"]'::jsonb, 'Metronidazole', 'Metronidazole is first-line for giardiasis.'),
('95411c27-8bfb-5f95-91b7-2599e56780ec'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Giardia lamblia is a cause of:', '["Leishmaniasis resulting in decreased functional activity","Ascariasis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Malaria mediated by specific receptor activation","Giardiasis (diarrhea with malabsorption)","Amebic dysentery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Giardiasis (diarrhea with malabsorption)', 'Giardia causes enteritis with watery diarrhea and malabsorption.'),
('9df1d1f0-763b-50ea-84aa-ddbee7f102f5'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Taenia saginata is diagnosed by proglottids with:', '["Fewer than 12 uterine branches","Serology only","Both patterns","Eggs only","More than 12 uterine branches"]'::jsonb, 'More than 12 uterine branches', 'T. saginata gravid proglottids contain more than 12 lateral uterine branches.'),
('3ff017d7-1a43-5469-a35b-beb291991d96'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'The Scotch tape test is used to diagnose:', '["Enterobius vermicularis (pinworm)","Trichuris trichiura","Hookworm","Ascaris lumbricoides","Strongyloides stercoralis"]'::jsonb, 'Enterobius vermicularis (pinworm)', 'The adhesive tape test on the perianal area detects pinworm eggs.'),
('51ca2564-1850-569f-bd0e-26163d9d5584'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Hookworm is diagnosed by:', '["Oval, thin-shelled eggs in stool","Blood smear accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Scotch tape mediated by specific receptor activation","Serology without affecting the primary tissue pathway","PCR leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Oval, thin-shelled eggs in stool', 'Oval thin-shelled hookworm eggs are identified in stool.'),
('bd6a8e6d-9e3d-520c-a9dc-27a8e3e4e73e'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Chloroquine resistance is most prominent in:', '["P. vivax","P. malariae","P. falciparum","P. knowlesi","P. ovale"]'::jsonb, 'P. falciparum', 'Chloroquine resistance is a major problem in Plasmodium falciparum.'),
('e95bf0b0-3958-58c6-b903-b17eb50fc16c'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'W. bancrofti microfilariae show which periodicity?', '["Nocturnal","Seasonal","Annual","None","Diurnal"]'::jsonb, 'Nocturnal', 'W. bancrofti microfilariae are nocturnally periodic.'),
('f49ef8ba-b20c-52be-b835-dfde3eba117a'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Malaria is diagnosed by:', '["PCR resulting in decreased functional activity","Culture accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thick and thin blood smear","RDT only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Serology leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thick and thin blood smear', 'Giemsa-stained thick and thin blood films are the gold standard for malaria diagnosis.'),
('d91060d6-c53d-5862-9b51-b0d584a89491'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Plasmodium falciparum causes:', '["Benign tertian","Knowlesi","Ovale infection","Malignant tertian malaria","Quartan fever"]'::jsonb, 'Malignant tertian malaria', 'P. falciparum produces malignant (subtertian) tertian malaria with the most severe disease.'),
('1aa6c6d3-1007-50a4-afd5-d512154706c3'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Plasmodium vivax produces which fever pattern?', '["Quartan fever (every 72 hours)","Malignant tertian","Daily fever","Subtertian","Benign tertian malaria (48-hour cycle)"]'::jsonb, 'Benign tertian malaria (48-hour cycle)', 'P. vivax has a 48-hour erythrocytic cycle causing benign tertian malaria; hypnozoites in the liver cause relapses.'),
('6f919efe-e223-5f34-8d99-18f98b88213b'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'S. mansoni eggs are best described as:', '["Laterally spined","Terminally spined","Barrel-shaped","Bipolar-plugged","Mammillated"]'::jsonb, 'Laterally spined', 'The lateral spine is the characteristic feature of S. mansoni eggs, distinguishing them from the terminal-spined eggs of S. haematobium.'),
('ba365f93-4f51-5eae-9497-847c8403428c'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Schistosomiasis is diagnosed by:', '["Eggs in urine or stool","Blood smear","Serology","PCR only","Imaging only"]'::jsonb, 'Eggs in urine or stool', 'Finding species-specific eggs in urine/stool is the diagnostic confirmation.'),
('f2c58bb5-d476-5ee2-8ab4-b5c8e073c6dd'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Trichuris trichiura is diagnosed by:', '["Scotch tape resulting in decreased functional activity","Blood smear accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Radiology mediated by specific receptor activation","Serology without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Barrel-shaped eggs with bipolar plugs in stool"]'::jsonb, 'Barrel-shaped eggs with bipolar plugs in stool', 'Diagnosis is by the characteristic barrel-shaped whipworm eggs with bipolar plugs in stool.'),
('437c06ab-a744-58e7-ae12-e56e39b57cdb'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Hookworm is caused by:', '["Trichuris trichiura","Ancylostoma duodenale and Necator americanus","Ascaris lumbricoides","Enterobius vermicularis","Strongyloides stercoralis"]'::jsonb, 'Ancylostoma duodenale and Necator americanus', 'Hookworm disease is caused by Ancylostoma duodenale and Necator americanus.'),
('43047c20-4275-56e6-a490-30782bef03be'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Malaria is transmitted by the bite of:', '["Female Anopheles mosquito","Culex mosquito","Sandfly","Male Anopheles mosquito","Aedes mosquito"]'::jsonb, 'Female Anopheles mosquito', 'Only female Anopheles mosquitoes transmit Plasmodium by injecting sporozoites during a blood meal.'),
('07865d9c-b1b8-57e9-92a0-27a825d188e5'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Taenia solium is diagnosed (vs T. saginata) by gravid proglottids with:', '["Serology only","More than 12 uterine branches","Eggs only","Both patterns","Fewer than 12 uterine branches"]'::jsonb, 'Fewer than 12 uterine branches', 'T. solium gravid proglottids have fewer than 12 lateral uterine branches, unlike T. saginata with more than 12.'),
('fa275c70-4b15-5f61-9b2c-f505a947fa40'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Toxoplasma infection in pregnancy may cause:', '["Congenital toxoplasmosis (hydrocephalus, chorioretinitis)","Spina bifida accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cardiac defects mediated by specific receptor activation","Cleft palate without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Limb defects leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Congenital toxoplasmosis (hydrocephalus, chorioretinitis)', 'Toxoplasma can cross the placenta causing congenital toxoplasmosis.'),
('9cd0bec3-24e3-59cc-9098-5766bcff1e56'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Neurocysticercosis is caused by:', '["Brain cysts of Taenia solium larvae (seizures)","Meningitis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dementia mediated by specific receptor activation","Stroke without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Parkinsonism leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Brain cysts of Taenia solium larvae (seizures)', 'Larval cysts of Taenia solium in the brain (neurocysticercosis) classically present with seizures.'),
('ff9d3b97-67c0-5345-bebd-dc88030d123e'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Neurocysticercosis presents with:', '["Dementia resulting in decreased functional activity","Meningitis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Seizures (brain cysts of T. solium)","Parkinsonism without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Stroke leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Seizures (brain cysts of T. solium)', 'Taenia solium larval cysts in the brain cause epilepsy.'),
('398efdb1-fa53-5a6c-bd67-b35374a49702'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Leishmania donovani is associated with:', '["Visceral leishmaniasis (kala-azar)","Mucocutaneous leishmaniasis","Chagas disease","All forms","Cutaneous leishmaniasis"]'::jsonb, 'Visceral leishmaniasis (kala-azar)', 'L. donovani causes visceral leishmaniasis.'),
('d12ac32e-79ce-536d-8c6b-c0fa9cac95d1'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Uncomplicated falciparum malaria is treated with:', '["ACT (artemisinin-based combination therapy)","Primaquine only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Chloroquine only mediated by specific receptor activation","Metronidazole without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Albendazole leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'ACT (artemisinin-based combination therapy)', 'ACT is the WHO first-line for uncomplicated P. falciparum malaria.'),
('bd37e8dd-cbb3-55bd-8c8d-dcf2bdae24ef'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'The drug of choice for strongyloidiasis is:', '["Albendazole","Diethylcarbamazine","Metronidazole","Praziquantel","Ivermectin"]'::jsonb, 'Ivermectin', 'Ivermectin is the treatment of choice for Strongyloides stercoralis infection.'),
('521ad85f-7025-5c49-9bc6-c028d8882a24'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'T. solium gravid proglottids have:', '["More than 12 uterine branches","Fewer than 12 uterine branches","Serology only","Both patterns","Eggs only"]'::jsonb, 'Fewer than 12 uterine branches', 'T. solium proglottids have fewer than 12 lateral uterine branches, unlike T. saginata.'),
('6cdac24e-2f43-5d04-ae46-639317f09198'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Visceral leishmaniasis (kala-azar) is treated with:', '["Liposomal amphotericin B","Ivermectin","Chloroquine","Metronidazole","Penicillin"]'::jsonb, 'Liposomal amphotericin B', 'Liposomal amphotericin B is the drug of choice for visceral leishmaniasis.'),
('699ce655-1986-5bef-ab0a-61cbd4b3a3ee'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Taenia solium gravid proglottids have how many uterine branches?', '["Both patterns","More than 12 branches","Fewer than 12 branches","Serology only","Eggs only"]'::jsonb, 'Fewer than 12 branches', 'T. solium proglottids have fewer than 12 lateral uterine branches.'),
('53098734-ee0a-5761-8071-900dd390f71c'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'Elephantiasis results from:', '["Chronic lymphatic obstruction","Bone deformity","Acute infection","None","Skin ulcer"]'::jsonb, 'Chronic lymphatic obstruction', 'Filarial lymphatic obstruction causes chronic lymphedema (elephantiasis).'),
('2645f4ec-bce8-505e-98fd-ff328bc01f54'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'A 35-year-old man with amebic liver abscess should be treated with:', '["Broad-spectrum antibiotics","Observation only","Immediate surgery","Surgical drainage only","Metronidazole (aspiration if needed)"]'::jsonb, 'Metronidazole (aspiration if needed)', 'Amebic liver abscess is treated with metronidazole; aspiration is reserved for large or complicated abscesses.'),
('ae4f5682-7f81-53c2-82bd-a14ae27c1230'::uuid, 'General Pathology Microbiology Immunology (Paper 2)', 'Parasitology - Parasitology', 'The ideal management of an amebic liver abscess is:', '["Antibiotics only resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Surgery only mediated by specific receptor activation","Drainage only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Metronidazole (aspiration if needed)"]'::jsonb, 'Metronidazole (aspiration if needed)', 'Metronidazole is the drug of choice for amebic liver abscess, with aspiration reserved for giant or complicated cysts.'),
('e963b56c-fc1f-5607-a279-696fbdcda405'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Which measure reflects the number of new cases of a disease in a population over a specified time period?', '["Mortality rate","Prevalence","Attack rate","Incidence","Case fatality rate"]'::jsonb, 'Incidence', 'Incidence counts new cases arising during a defined period, whereas prevalence counts all existing cases; it is therefore the measure of new disease occurrence.'),
('194150f3-69ea-5c28-b396-7f1defa069d0'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'A survey of 20,000 individuals finds the prevalence of a disease to be 2%. Prevalence is best defined as:', '["Number of cases/Person-time","Number of new cases/Total population at risk","Number of deaths/Total population","Number of new cases/Population at risk","Number of existing cases/Total population"]'::jsonb, 'Number of existing cases/Total population', 'Prevalence describes the existing cases (old and new) in a population at a point in time, calculated as the number of existing cases divided by the total population.'),
('afe5af3e-52c1-5cbb-96d1-de002f730ee2'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Which study design is best for studying rare diseases?', '["Case-control study","Cohort study","Ecological study","RCT","Cross-sectional study"]'::jsonb, 'Case-control study', 'Case-control studies begin with cases and select controls, so they can efficiently study rare outcomes without following a huge population over time.'),
('22738117-5857-5bcb-8156-3880e2daa60a'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'The odds ratio is the measure of association used in which study design?', '["Cross-sectional study","Cohort study","RCT","Quasi-experimental study","Case-control study"]'::jsonb, 'Case-control study', 'Because incidence cannot be measured directly, case-control studies estimate the strength of association using the odds ratio.'),
('3b291859-a725-531f-a781-43fa3ebe0a62'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'A cohort study reports a relative risk of 2.0 for developing disease in the exposed group. What does this mean?', '["Exposed individuals have twice the risk of disease compared to unexposed","The disease causes twice the exposure accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The study is invalid mediated by specific receptor activation","The risk is halved without affecting the primary tissue pathway","No association exists leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Exposed individuals have twice the risk of disease compared to unexposed', 'Relative risk compares disease incidence in the exposed and unexposed groups; a value of 2.0 means exposed individuals develop the disease at twice the rate of the unexposed.'),
('b2b7d222-5598-58bb-bb7f-06fe47412276'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'In a 2x2 table for a case-control study, the odds ratio is calculated as:', '["a/(a+b) / c/(c+d)","(a+d)/(b+c)","ad/bc","bc/ad","(a+b)/(c+d)"]'::jsonb, 'ad/bc', 'The odds ratio is the cross-product of the 2x2 table, ad/bc, which equals the odds of exposure among cases divided by the odds of exposure among controls.'),
('deb6339e-fbf2-5912-89a5-173621ea5a85'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'A case-control study is most susceptible to which type of bias?', '["Recall bias","Detection bias","Procedure bias","Lead-time bias","Length-time bias"]'::jsonb, 'Recall bias', 'Cases may recall past exposures more vividly or differently than controls, producing recall bias, the classic information bias of retrospective case-control studies.'),
('9766f88f-2bf9-59a5-b87e-ce9c4b890d8a'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Which study design is the gold standard for evaluating therapeutic interventions?', '["Case-control study","Cohort study","Randomized controlled trial","Ecological study","Cross-sectional study"]'::jsonb, 'Randomized controlled trial', 'By randomizing treatment allocation, the randomized controlled trial balances known and unknown confounders and is the gold standard for evaluating interventions.'),
('51d951a4-6f8f-5db8-91ad-d23e2e46e06b'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'The purpose of randomization in an RCT is to:', '["Reduce cost resulting in decreased functional activity","Minimize confounding by balancing known and unknown confounders","Eliminate the need for blinding mediated by specific receptor activation","Ensure equal group sizes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Increase sample size leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Minimize confounding by balancing known and unknown confounders', 'Random allocation tends to balance both measured and unmeasured confounders across groups, allowing outcome differences to be attributed to the intervention.'),
('8489d912-3180-5b8c-b0f1-94ce590a5fb7'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Which type of blinding involves neither participants nor researchers knowing treatment allocation?', '["Unblinded","Triple-blind","Open-label","Single-blind","Double-blind"]'::jsonb, 'Double-blind', 'In a double-blind trial both the participants and the researchers involved in care and outcome assessment are unaware of which treatment was given.'),
('0160aa0c-0630-574e-8a96-aebf2df54a72'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Intention-to-treat analysis is characterized by:', '["Analyzing patients according to the treatment they actually received resulting in decreased functional activity","Analyzing only those who completed the study accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Analyzing all patients according to their original group allocation regardless of protocol deviations","Per-protocol analysis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Excluding dropouts leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Analyzing all patients according to their original group allocation regardless of protocol deviations', 'Intention-to-treat analysis preserves randomization by analyzing all patients in the group to which they were originally allocated, even after cross-over or withdrawal.'),
('eaa91481-2e1f-55b2-ba7c-c1b83a200cd3'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'The number needed to treat (NNT) is calculated as:', '["RRR/100","1/(CER - EER)","ARR/100","CER - EER","1/RRR"]'::jsonb, '1/(CER - EER)', 'NNT is 1 divided by the absolute risk reduction (ARR = CER − EER), i.e., 1/(CER − EER), the number of patients needed to treat to prevent one adverse event.'),
('abba9a2b-9ed4-5cbf-bb2e-d40fe499eae7'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Number needed to harm (NNH) is calculated as:', '["1/ARR","1/OR","1/(EER - CER)","1/RRR","1/(CER - EER)"]'::jsonb, '1/(EER - CER)', 'NNH is 1 divided by the absolute increase in adverse events caused by the intervention, calculated as 1/(EER − CER).'),
('0ec0db59-47a1-593c-b315-608d373342c5'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'A screening test with 90% sensitivity means:', '["There is a 10% false positive rate","90% of negative tests are true negatives","90% of those without disease test negative","90% of positive tests are true positives","90% of those with disease test positive"]'::jsonb, '90% of those with disease test positive', 'Sensitivity is the probability of a positive test given the disease is present, so 90% sensitivity means 90% of diseased individuals test positive.'),
('630bcf3a-9871-5805-998f-b2f82d76a45f'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'A screening test with 90% specificity means:', '["There is a 10% false negative rate","90% of those without disease test negative","90% of negative tests are true negatives","90% of positive tests are true positives","90% of those with disease test positive"]'::jsonb, '90% of those without disease test negative', 'Specificity is the probability of a negative test among people without the disease, so 90% specificity means 90% of non-diseased individuals test negative.'),
('23e688ff-2d2f-5848-a916-e83050159055'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Positive predictive value depends on:', '["Both sensitivity and specificity","Specificity only","Sensitivity only","Prevalence of the disease","Sample size"]'::jsonb, 'Prevalence of the disease', 'PPV is the chance that a positive test truly indicates disease; for fixed sensitivity and specificity it rises as disease prevalence rises.'),
('de92696b-5ca1-50d4-96ee-01a609485a2d'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'As the prevalence of a disease decreases, the positive predictive value of a screening test:', '["Increases","Decreases","Remains unchanged","Increases, then decreases","Cannot be determined from this information"]'::jsonb, 'Decreases', 'PPV is the proportion of positive results that are true positives; when the disease becomes rarer, proportionally more positive results are false positives, so PPV decreases.'),
('c9311e71-885f-519e-8adb-31674d8f2ed7'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Which bias occurs when screening detects a disease earlier but does not actually prolong survival?', '["Length-time bias","Recall bias","Selection bias","Detection bias","Lead-time bias"]'::jsonb, 'Lead-time bias', 'Lead-time bias inflates apparent survival because diagnosis is advanced to an earlier point, lengthening measured survival time without genuinely extending life.'),
('0514ff36-6641-5058-97af-58ed021e3265'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Length-time bias in screening refers to:', '["Screening detecting slow-progressing cases preferentially","Overdiagnosis of harmless disease","Patients recalling exposures differently","Diagnosis being made earlier than usual","Healthy volunteers having better outcomes"]'::jsonb, 'Screening detecting slow-progressing cases preferentially', 'Slow-progressing tumors remain detectable for longer, so screening preferentially identifies less aggressive cases, producing length-time bias.'),
('46b0c452-3ec1-5310-86ca-58cee7539855'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'The ROC curve plots:', '["Sensitivity vs Specificity","Sensitivity vs 1 - Specificity","Prevalence vs Accuracy","PPV vs NPV","True positives vs False negatives"]'::jsonb, 'Sensitivity vs 1 - Specificity', 'The receiver-operating-characteristic curve plots sensitivity (true-positive rate) against 1 − specificity (false-positive rate) across all threshold values.'),
('2eaa1cf8-3ac8-5ba6-8d98-e14a7f1c1c15'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'The area under the ROC curve (AUC) of 1.0 indicates:', '["Perfect discrimination between diseased and non-diseased","Test is better than random accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Test is better than chance mediated by specific receptor activation","Moderate accuracy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Worthless test leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Perfect discrimination between diseased and non-diseased', 'An AUC of 1.0 means the test separates diseased from non-diseased individuals perfectly, with no overlap in their test results.'),
('3b4ff9e6-8f50-5421-88ae-b7dd23096224'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'An AUC of 0.5 on ROC analysis indicates:', '["Discrimination no better than chance","Good discrimination","Perfect discrimination","Excellent discrimination","Poor discrimination"]'::jsonb, 'Discrimination no better than chance', 'An AUC of 0.5 equals the diagonal of the ROC plot, meaning the test discriminates no better than random chance.'),
('fd57603d-66ae-5522-bcbe-4bf9a8a18bdb'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Which measure of association is used in cross-sectional studies?', '["Prevalence ratio","Hazard ratio","Odds ratio","Relative risk","Attributable risk"]'::jsonb, 'Prevalence ratio', 'Cross-sectional studies measure existing disease, so association is expressed as a prevalence ratio (or prevalence odds ratio) rather than an incidence-based measure.'),
('7f725cb0-ea81-5413-a118-c86326e4a8ea'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Attributable risk (risk difference) is calculated as:', '["ad/bc","Incidence in exposed - Incidence in unexposed","Incidence in exposed / Incidence in unexposed","a/(a+b) / c/(c+d)","(Incidence in exposed - Incidence in unexposed) / Incidence in exposed"]'::jsonb, 'Incidence in exposed - Incidence in unexposed', 'Attributable risk is the absolute difference in incidence between exposed and unexposed groups, quantifying the excess disease burden due to the exposure.'),
('f54e7a14-5128-5b0e-acd0-2308792cff4f'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Population attributable risk measures:', '["Overall disease incidence resulting in decreased functional activity","Proportion of disease in the total population attributable to the exposure","Effect of confounding mediated by specific receptor activation","Risk difference in exposed without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Risk in exposed compared to unexposed leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Proportion of disease in the total population attributable to the exposure', 'Population attributable risk estimates the proportion of all cases in the population that could be prevented if the exposure were eliminated.'),
('52706d0c-c89c-5666-bab3-c307dc4ff798'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Which study design is most prone to selection bias?', '["Ecological study","Cross-sectional study","RCT","Cohort study","Case-control study"]'::jsonb, 'Case-control study', 'Selection of cases and controls is highly vulnerable to differential participation, making the case-control design most prone to selection bias.'),
('2c9cc864-1f40-5485-ace9-339113cfb4f6'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Confounding is best controlled by which method in the analysis phase?', '["Blinding resulting in decreased functional activity","Matching accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Restriction mediated by specific receptor activation","Randomization without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Stratification & multivariable analysis"]'::jsonb, 'Stratification & multivariable analysis', 'Stratification and multivariable regression adjust for confounders during analysis; randomization, matching, and restriction act at the design stage.'),
('de0d42ba-81a8-5ceb-95b9-51173a9b2477'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Matching in a case-control study helps control:', '["Information bias","Selection bias","Confounding","Recall bias","Measurement bias"]'::jsonb, 'Confounding', 'Matching cases and controls on variables such as age and sex balances the confounder across groups and reduces its distorting influence.'),
('0dd2c391-dda5-5e4e-976d-a5d7d7ecbd3f'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Restriction as a method to control confounding involves:', '["Using regression models","Randomly allocating subjects to groups","Limiting the study to a single category of the confounding variable","Matching pairs of subjects","Selecting controls with similar characteristics as cases"]'::jsonb, 'Limiting the study to a single category of the confounding variable', 'Restriction confines enrollment to one level of a confounder (e.g., only non-smokers), removing variation in that variable and thereby its confounding effect.'),
('b3db58fe-df0c-57db-bc2f-dc3321688f03'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'The Hawthorne effect refers to:', '["True treatment effect resulting in decreased functional activity","Subjects changing behavior because they know they are being observed","Placebo effect mediated by specific receptor activation","Recall bias without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Regression to the mean leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Subjects changing behavior because they know they are being observed', 'The Hawthorne effect is a change in behavior caused by participants knowing they are under observation, which can bias intervention studies.'),
('0e982bd7-b9ed-5e43-b9d3-efd7e0961731'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Regression to the mean refers to:', '["Observer bias resulting in decreased functional activity","Subjects dropping out of study accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Extreme values tending to be followed by less extreme values on repeat measurement","Systematic error in measurement without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Recall of past events being inaccurate leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Extreme values tending to be followed by less extreme values on repeat measurement', 'Regression to the mean is the statistical tendency for extreme initial measurements to move toward the average on repeat testing, which can mimic a treatment effect.'),
('243914da-9455-56e8-b84b-2992de119d45'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'The placebo effect is best defined as:', '["A statistical artifact","The effect observed only in the control group","An adverse drug reaction","The effect of the patient''s expectation of benefit","The true pharmacological effect of a drug"]'::jsonb, 'The effect of the patient''s expectation of benefit', 'The placebo effect is a genuine response that arises from the patient''s expectation that an inactive treatment will produce benefit.'),
('a760ad8c-6362-548e-8b89-2b663dfddb50'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Which type of bias occurs when the outcome assessment is influenced by knowledge of exposure?', '["Lead-time bias","Selection bias","Recall bias","Observer/detection bias","Attrition bias"]'::jsonb, 'Observer/detection bias', 'Observer (detection) bias occurs when the assessor''s knowledge of exposure or group assignment influences how outcomes are measured or recorded.'),
('6fc1c525-a2ae-5d87-be9b-95a821a858cc'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Berkson''s bias is a type of:', '["Confounding resulting in decreased functional activity","Information bias accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Measurement bias mediated by specific receptor activation","Recall bias without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Selection bias in hospital-based studies"]'::jsonb, 'Selection bias in hospital-based studies', 'Berkson''s bias arises in hospital-based studies because the chance of hospitalization is higher when two conditions coexist, distorting the observed association.'),
('6b4940d5-a73d-52c1-b324-4122141ef79f'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'What is the effect of non-differential misclassification?', '["Can bias in either direction","Biases away from the null","Only affects sensitivity","Only affects specificity","Biases toward the null"]'::jsonb, 'Biases toward the null', 'Non-differential misclassification, in which classification errors occur equally in both groups, dilutes the true association and shifts the estimate toward the null.'),
('b9f7ecbd-52bf-5d66-a321-19e32aaee697'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'What is differential misclassification?', '["Error that only affects controls","Error from sampling","Error from random measurement","Error depends on exposure or disease status","Error equally likely in all groups"]'::jsonb, 'Error depends on exposure or disease status', 'In differential misclassification the probability of misclassification differs according to exposure or disease status, so associations can be biased in either direction.'),
('3629a942-2cc7-5ea4-ae7a-64bd3d23bc29'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'McNemar''s test is used in what type of study design?', '["Unmatched cohort","Cross-sectional studies","Paired or matched case-control studies","Independent samples","RCT with parallel groups"]'::jsonb, 'Paired or matched case-control studies', 'McNemar''s test analyzes discordant pairs, making it the appropriate test for paired or matched case-control data.'),
('5d79bc77-586a-50ad-b384-782af7b88119'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Which design is most efficient for studying multiple exposures and a single disease?', '["Cohort study","RCT","Cross-sectional study","Case-control study","Ecological study"]'::jsonb, 'Case-control study', 'Case-control studies start from one disease outcome and then assess many past exposures, making them efficient for multiple exposures and a single disease.'),
('0c06764b-d5c8-5ff3-856c-c066fd3c5329'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Which design is most efficient for studying multiple outcomes of a single exposure?', '["Cross-sectional study","RCT","Cohort study","Case report","Case-control study"]'::jsonb, 'Cohort study', 'Cohort studies follow an exposed group forward and can record multiple outcomes from the single exposure, making them efficient for this purpose.'),
('588fbda2-72a7-533d-8d38-8969d8c94c47'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Ecological fallacy refers to:', '["Inferring individual-level associations from group-level data","Confounding by indication accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Systematic error in measurement mediated by specific receptor activation","Error from misclassification without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bias from non-response leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inferring individual-level associations from group-level data', 'The ecological fallacy is the error of assuming that associations seen at the group level also hold for the individuals within those groups.'),
('cece85c3-49f7-51a2-8c67-6a38003d347f'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Which study design is most suitable for studying disease trends over time?', '["Case-control study","Cohort study","Ecological study","Cross-sectional study","Longitudinal study"]'::jsonb, 'Ecological study', 'Ecological studies analyze aggregate population data collected over successive time periods, making them well suited for describing secular trends of disease.'),
('b2c3e495-2e2b-5a41-829e-23668f98bb8b'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'A nested case-control study is characterized by:', '["Cases and controls selected from hospitals","Matching on all variables","Random selection of controls from general population","Prospective enrollment only","Cases and controls selected from within a cohort"]'::jsonb, 'Cases and controls selected from within a cohort', 'In a nested case-control design, cases arising during follow-up and their controls are drawn from within an existing cohort.'),
('a3db9687-a519-5cc4-9093-6b63d2a65fb0'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Which measure is calculated as the total number of cases divided by the total population at a point in time?', '["Attack rate","Cumulative incidence","Incidence","Prevalence","Incidence density"]'::jsonb, 'Prevalence', 'Prevalence is the count of existing cases divided by the total population at a specific point in time; incidence, by contrast, counts only new cases.'),
('5989cf20-f050-53fe-ac1c-ae442772fb8d'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Incidence density (person-time rate) uses which denominator?', '["Total population","Total population at risk","Person-time at risk","Number of new cases","Total number of cases"]'::jsonb, 'Person-time at risk', 'Incidence density uses the sum of person-time at risk contributed by each subject as its denominator, accounting for different durations of follow-up.'),
('04c827ed-1d48-5785-9fd2-c8cb30b10b69'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'The relationship between incidence (I) and prevalence (P) for a stable population with duration (D) is:', '["P = I × D","P = D / I","P = I - D","P = I + D","P = I / D"]'::jsonb, 'P = I × D', 'In a stable population, prevalence approximately equals incidence multiplied by the average disease duration, that is, P = I × D.'),
('61c5ce85-7678-5b2c-a22b-5260858dcc58'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Attack rate in a foodborne outbreak investigation is a type of:', '["Mortality rate","Case fatality","Incidence proportion/risk","Prevalence","Incidence density"]'::jsonb, 'Incidence proportion/risk', 'Attack rate is the proportion of exposed people who become ill over the short outbreak period, which is an incidence proportion (risk).'),
('5449e447-9e68-596a-8249-fc5970ad6ce3'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Case fatality rate is a measure of:', '["Vaccine efficacy","Disease severity","Disease frequency","Disease transmissibility","Exposure effect"]'::jsonb, 'Disease severity', 'Case fatality is the proportion of diagnosed cases who die from the disease, reflecting how severe or lethal the disease is.'),
('21814d89-935b-5ec0-940a-d5bed7edd5fb'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Which type of bias arises when participants drop out of a study differentially between groups?', '["Recall bias","Attrition bias","Selection bias","Lead-time bias","Detection bias"]'::jsonb, 'Attrition bias', 'Attrition bias results from differential loss to follow-up between study groups, leaving the groups no longer comparable in ways related to the outcome.'),
('6a8d14ff-0d7b-595c-95cb-26af5241525b'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Which bias occurs when healthy volunteers are more likely to participate in a study?', '["Lead-time bias","Recall bias","Selection bias","Length-time bias","Information bias"]'::jsonb, 'Selection bias', 'When healthier individuals volunteer preferentially, study participants no longer represent the target population, introducing selection bias.'),
('24102317-ee1d-52a1-8d83-5a5d2415d083'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'What does the kappa statistic measure?', '["Sample size adequacy","Prevalence of disease","Effect size","Correlation between two variables","Agreement beyond chance between raters"]'::jsonb, 'Agreement beyond chance between raters', 'Cohen''s kappa measures inter-rater agreement after removing agreement expected by chance alone.'),
('8d93625e-e4c2-55d9-914e-2662b2ce1445'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'A kappa value of 0.8 in a study indicates:', '["Moderate agreement","Substantial/strong agreement","Slight agreement","Fair agreement","Poor agreement"]'::jsonb, 'Substantial/strong agreement', 'Kappa values of 0.61-0.80 represent substantial agreement. A kappa of 0.8 therefore indicates substantial or strong agreement beyond chance.'),
('12e9189b-fef9-54b6-9b0e-1f5bcd39a237'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Which screening program is recommended for cervical cancer screening?', '["Chest X-ray","FOBT","PSA testing","Pap smear","Mammography"]'::jsonb, 'Pap smear', 'The Pap smear (cytology) detects precancerous cervical lesions and is the recommended screening modality for cervical cancer.'),
('8b6811b6-de44-5f09-9f56-50f5e2fd468b'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Mammography is the recommended screening modality for which cancer?', '["Cervical cancer","Lung cancer","Breast cancer","Prostate cancer","Colorectal cancer"]'::jsonb, 'Breast cancer', 'Mammography is used to screen asymptomatic women for breast cancer. It detects early lesions and reduces breast cancer mortality.'),
('816223d9-ad02-5ed3-9045-d74a0e05f31c'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'The recommended screening modality for colorectal cancer is:', '["Pap smear","Colonoscopy/FOBT","CT scan","Mammography","PSA"]'::jsonb, 'Colonoscopy/FOBT', 'Fecal occult blood testing (FOBT) and colonoscopy are the recommended screening modalities for colorectal cancer.'),
('74371d69-2318-58e7-9abb-9c49b8ae8e2a'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Which test is used for screening prostate cancer?', '["Chest X-ray","MRI","Pap smear","PSA","Colonoscopy"]'::jsonb, 'PSA', 'Prostate-specific antigen (PSA) is the blood test used for screening prostate cancer.'),
('12b49e8e-fd86-5bf6-b24c-974cac1bcb16'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Lead-time bias can falsely inflate which measure?', '["NPV","Sensitivity","PPV","Specificity","Survival time"]'::jsonb, 'Survival time', 'Lead-time bias moves the diagnosis earlier without prolonging life, so survival measured from diagnosis appears longer. This falsely inflates apparent survival time.'),
('7cee1868-b2ad-505b-b583-6cc8a98463f4'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Which study design is best for establishing the temporal sequence between exposure and disease?', '["Case-control study","Cohort study","Ecological study","Case series","Cross-sectional study"]'::jsonb, 'Cohort study', 'A cohort study follows exposed and unexposed groups forward in time, allowing exposure to be documented before disease develops. This directly establishes temporality.'),
('a14fe8cb-5ad5-5a2c-9bc8-b289315a696f'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'A cohort study that looks backward using existing records is called:', '["Prospective cohort","Nested case-control","Ambispective cohort","Historical/retrospective cohort","Case-cohort study"]'::jsonb, 'Historical/retrospective cohort', 'A historical or retrospective cohort uses existing records to reconstruct exposure and outcome status for a cohort assembled in the past.'),
('82407301-b0c8-5521-bd1a-7e25655ffcbb'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'The Framingham Heart Study is an example of a:', '["Cross-sectional study","Case-control study","RCT","Prospective cohort study","Case series"]'::jsonb, 'Prospective cohort study', 'The Framingham Heart Study has followed an initially healthy adult population forward over time to detect cardiovascular disease. It is the classic prospective cohort study.'),
('c3622721-e6f9-538a-a249-5cbb565d4970'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Confounding by indication occurs when:', '["Treatment groups are balanced","There is differential loss to follow-up","Treatment assignment is influenced by prognosis","Subjects recall exposure differently","Exposure and disease are measured simultaneously"]'::jsonb, 'Treatment assignment is influenced by prognosis', 'Confounding by indication arises when the decision to give treatment is influenced by the patient''s prognosis or disease severity. This makes the treated and untreated groups unequal at baseline.'),
('71be2ff2-af77-518c-af41-39293e1a7e9c'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Which method of randomization ensures equal group sizes in an RCT?', '["Block randomization","Cluster randomization","Simple randomization","Systematic randomization","Stratified randomization"]'::jsonb, 'Block randomization', 'Block randomization allocates subjects in blocks of fixed size so that each block contains equal numbers assigned to each arm. This guarantees balanced group sizes throughout the trial.'),
('82b4ef89-7b69-5bea-8b2a-71a9cb4a05bd'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Stratified randomization ensures:', '["Equal group sizes","Blinding of participants","Concealment of allocation","Standardized treatment protocols","Balance of important prognostic factors across groups"]'::jsonb, 'Balance of important prognostic factors across groups', 'Stratified randomization randomizes within strata of important prognostic variables. This balances these risk factors across treatment arms.'),
('f4e1b18f-e9f8-5995-b7e7-2532d32ad224'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Allocation concealment aims to prevent:', '["Attrition bias","Recall bias","Selection bias at enrollment","Performance bias","Measurement bias"]'::jsonb, 'Selection bias at enrollment', 'Allocation concealment keeps the upcoming treatment assignment hidden from investigators and participants during enrollment. This prevents selection bias by avoiding predictable allocation.'),
('bc880cef-b91f-53f3-99a4-e6078c1ceb52'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Which ethical principle is most relevant to informed consent?', '["Justice","Non-maleficence","Veracity","Beneficence","Autonomy"]'::jsonb, 'Autonomy', 'Informed consent respects the participant''s right to make voluntary, informed decisions about participation. It is grounded in the ethical principle of autonomy.'),
('6a05596f-133a-595e-affe-91b52b24c863'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Ap-value of 0.03 in a hypothesis test indicates:', '["The null hypothesis is definitely true","Weak evidence against the null hypothesis","Strong evidence to accept the null hypothesis","The result is clinically insignificant","Strong evidence against the null hypothesis at alpha = 0.05"]'::jsonb, 'Strong evidence against the null hypothesis at alpha = 0.05', 'Ap-value of 0.03 is below the conventional alpha of 0.05, so the null hypothesis is rejected. This provides statistically significant evidence against the null hypothesis.'),
('271af0be-56de-5194-8dad-2a8fe3242047'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'A new diagnostic test for diabetes has a sensitivity of 95% and a specificity of 90%. In a population with a disease prevalence of 10%, the positive predictive value (PPV) is approximately:', '["90%","95%","50%","69%","72%"]'::jsonb, '50%', 'PPV = (prevalence x sensitivity) / [(prevalence x sensitivity) + (1 - prevalence) x (1 - specificity)] = 0.095 / 0.185, which is about 51%. The closest option is 50%.'),
('5c7355e0-f74e-538f-a4df-321364b18779'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Which formula correctly calculates the positive likelihood ratio (LR+) of a diagnostic test?', '["LR+ = sens/(1-spec)","LR- = (1-sens)/spec","LR- = sens/spec","LR+ = sens x spec","LR+ = spec/(1-sens)"]'::jsonb, 'LR+ = sens/(1-spec)', 'The positive likelihood ratio is defined as sensitivity divided by (1 - specificity). It indicates how much the probability of disease increases after a positive test.'),
('9f1fd143-2581-544d-829d-f70de012a597'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'A likelihood ratio of 10 means:', '["The test is useless","The disease is 10 times more likely after a positive test","The test has 10% accuracy","The disease is 10 times less likely after a positive test","The false positive rate is 10%"]'::jsonb, 'The disease is 10 times more likely after a positive test', 'A positive likelihood ratio of 10 increases the post-test odds of disease ten-fold. The disease is therefore 10 times more likely after a positive test than before it.'),
('dcf50974-b604-51a5-8f3c-24a1a80676ee'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'What is the purpose of a run-in period in an RCT?', '["To identify and exclude non-compliant participants before randomization","To extend the follow-up period accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","To reduce costs mediated by specific receptor activation","To unblind participants without affecting the primary tissue pathway","To increase sample size leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'To identify and exclude non-compliant participants before randomization', 'A run-in period tests participants on the treatment or placebo before randomization. Non-compliant or intolerant participants are excluded, which improves adherence in the trial.'),
('dfd3b626-10e3-59da-8ed2-142a2054141d'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'What does a negative predictive value of 95% mean?', '["95% of those who test negative are disease-free","5% of positive tests are false","95% of those with disease test negative","95% of disease is detected","95% of tests are correct"]'::jsonb, '95% of those who test negative are disease-free', 'Negative predictive value is the proportion of negative test results that are truly disease-free. An NPV of 95% means 95% of negative results occur in people without the disease.'),
('767f5bcf-eb4e-547c-9de2-758db35f5910'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Screening for a disease with very low prevalence will result in:', '["Low PPV","Low specificity","High PPV","High accuracy","High sensitivity"]'::jsonb, 'Low PPV', 'As prevalence falls, most positive results are false positives even when sensitivity and specificity are high. This produces a low positive predictive value.'),
('f658737c-1f65-5ccb-9caa-bc0f75c69168'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Which bias results from selecting controls that are more health-conscious than the general population?', '["Recall bias resulting in decreased functional activity","Lead-time bias accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Length bias mediated by specific receptor activation","Selection bias in case-control studies","Procedure bias leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Selection bias in case-control studies', 'Choosing controls who are more health-conscious than the source population distorts the exposure distribution. This is a form of selection bias in case-control studies.'),
('6145bfab-9d2e-5e0d-9a2d-cf22703b62cb'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'What is the primary advantage of a crossover RCT?', '["Washout period needed resulting in decreased functional activity","Requires a larger sample accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Each subject serves as their own control, eliminating between-subject variability","Easier to blind without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ethical concerns leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Each subject serves as their own control, eliminating between-subject variability', 'In a crossover design each participant receives both treatments in sequence. Comparing within subjects removes between-subject variability and reduces the required sample size.'),
('f851bb07-d63a-5ea2-8045-0b8c440a9538'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'What is a major disadvantage of the crossover design?', '["Requires a large sample","Fewer outcome measures","Complex randomization","Increased cost","Carry-over effect of treatment"]'::jsonb, 'Carry-over effect of treatment', 'The effect of the first treatment may persist into the second period. This carry-over effect can confound the results unless a sufficiently long washout period is used.'),
('e1482258-50f4-5e23-8afb-5c214612b013'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'The Hawthorne effect is best minimized by:', '["Using a control group that is blinded","Increasing sample size","Using placebo","Random allocation","Concealing the study hypothesis from participants"]'::jsonb, 'Concealing the study hypothesis from participants', 'The Hawthorne effect is the change in participant behavior that occurs because they know they are being observed. Concealing the study hypothesis reduces this behavioral change.'),
('160cc823-35dd-52d1-b261-528906f04257'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Population-based case-control studies are generally preferred over hospital-based studies because:', '["They are easier to conduct","They provide more detailed data","They are less prone to selection bias","They are cheaper","They have larger sample sizes"]'::jsonb, 'They are less prone to selection bias', 'Hospital-based controls may not represent the population that produced the cases. Population-based case-control studies recruit controls from the same general population, reducing selection bias.'),
('079aa956-cd49-5d8f-ba99-65e63ed774a8'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'The Mantel-Haenszel method is used for:', '["Calculating sample size resulting in decreased functional activity","ROC analysis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pooling stratified data to get an adjusted odds ratio","Measuring kappa without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Calculating sensitivity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pooling stratified data to get an adjusted odds ratio', 'The Mantel-Haenszel method combines estimates across strata of a confounder. It produces a summary odds ratio adjusted for the stratifying variable.'),
('16bbd2df-6008-58ae-95f7-3dbaa367fe9c'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'If a test has 100% sensitivity, what is the false negative rate?', '["0%","100%","50%","25%","Depends on prevalence"]'::jsonb, '0%', 'The false negative rate equals 1 - sensitivity. With 100% sensitivity no diseased person is missed, so the false negative rate is 0%.'),
('dbb30981-6ffe-5877-8bbb-611782bef29b'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'If a screening test has 100% specificity, what is its false positive rate?', '["0%","100%","Cannot be determined","10%","50%"]'::jsonb, '0%', 'The false positive rate equals 1 - specificity. A specificity of 100% means no non-diseased person tests positive, so the false positive rate is 0%.'),
('8bb904df-7536-5527-862c-38fead41c292'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'The cut-off value for a diagnostic test influences:', '["Only disease prevalence","Only sensitivity","Both sensitivity and specificity","Neither sensitivity nor specificity","Only specificity"]'::jsonb, 'Both sensitivity and specificity', 'Moving the cut-off point trades sensitivity against specificity. Raising the threshold lowers sensitivity and raises specificity, while lowering it has the opposite effect.'),
('07dea7a4-cc56-5877-9359-d9a22dc15519'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'A test with high sensitivity is most useful for:', '["Predicting prognosis","Confirming disease","Monitoring treatment","Detecting disease stage","Ruling out disease (screening)"]'::jsonb, 'Ruling out disease (screening)', 'A highly sensitive test rarely misses disease, so a negative result reliably excludes it. High-sensitivity tests are therefore best for screening and ruling out disease.'),
('b9baabcc-fdb1-5ec4-8399-b5175b1e2a80'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'A test with high specificity is most useful for:', '["Screening","Ruling in a disease (confirmatory)","Assessing prognosis","Incidence studies","Population surveys"]'::jsonb, 'Ruling in a disease (confirmatory)', 'A highly specific test produces few false positives, so a positive result confirms disease. High-specificity tests are therefore best for ruling in a diagnosis.'),
('2a739bf2-c569-5b63-b0ca-f634412db885'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'The absolute risk reduction (ARR) is calculated as:', '["EER/CER","CER/EER","CER - EER","CER + EER","(CER - EER)/CER"]'::jsonb, 'CER - EER', 'Absolute risk reduction is the arithmetic difference between the control event rate and the experimental event rate (CER - EER). It expresses the treatment benefit as a percentage.'),
('089e049f-7356-5f5a-aeea-f598bb52c4e1'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'The relative risk reduction (RRR) is calculated as:', '["CER - EER","EER - CER","EER/CER","1/RR","(CER - EER)/CER"]'::jsonb, '(CER - EER)/CER', 'Relative risk reduction is the absolute risk reduction divided by the control event rate, i.e. (CER - EER)/CER. It expresses the proportional reduction in risk conferred by the treatment.'),
('506be1d2-0f32-5f69-a77c-90b3d9b15dc8'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'If the control event rate (CER) is 20% and the experimental event rate (EER) is 15%, the absolute risk reduction (ARR) is:', '["5%","25%","75%","33%","20%"]'::jsonb, '5%', 'ARR = CER - EER = 20% - 15% = 5%. It is the absolute difference in the event rate between the control and treatment groups.'),
('7ed3217e-b15a-58ef-8ce9-b4f481fed536'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Which study design is most susceptible to the ecological fallacy?', '["RCT","Cross-sectional study","Case-control study","Cohort study","Ecological study"]'::jsonb, 'Ecological study', 'Ecological studies use group-level data and cannot link exposure to outcome at the individual level. Associations found at the group level may not hold for individuals, producing the ecological fallacy.'),
('833e6add-1af0-5992-8ba4-f5d9c078a518'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'The purpose of a washout period in a crossover trial is to:', '["Increase treatment effect resulting in decreased functional activity","Extend study duration accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Reduce cost mediated by specific receptor activation","Eliminate carry-over effects from the first treatment phase","Improve compliance leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Eliminate carry-over effects from the first treatment phase', 'The washout period is an interval with no treatment between the two crossover phases. It allows the effect of the first treatment to clear before the second is given.'),
('b623e31e-2f46-5fa3-8e1c-16470ea356cd'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'A factorial RCT is designed to test:', '["A single intervention","Two or more interventions simultaneously in the same trial","Multiple outcomes","Two interventions tested sequentially in separate trials","A single intervention at a fixed dose"]'::jsonb, 'Two or more interventions simultaneously in the same trial', 'A factorial design assigns participants to combinations of two or more interventions within one trial. This allows several treatments and their interactions to be evaluated efficiently.'),
('430a0c80-e25f-5ad7-a0df-c6796ea662f4'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Equivalence trials aim to show:', '["Treatment is better than placebo","Two treatments are similar within a specified margin","Non-inferiority with superiority","One treatment is not worse than the other by a margin","One treatment is superior"]'::jsonb, 'Two treatments are similar within a specified margin', 'An equivalence trial tests whether a new treatment''s effect lies within a pre-specified margin on either side of the standard treatment. Its goal is demonstrating similarity rather than superiority.'),
('f553afa9-2c02-5dfc-9f6c-4b84b739a9d2'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Non-inferiority trials aim to show:', '["New treatment is cost-effective resulting in decreased functional activity","New treatment is not worse than standard by more than a pre-specified margin","New treatment is better than standard mediated by specific receptor activation","Placebo is better without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Both treatments are identical leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'New treatment is not worse than standard by more than a pre-specified margin', 'A non-inferiority trial seeks to prove that the new treatment is not unacceptably worse than the standard comparator. The acceptable amount of inferiority is set by a pre-specified margin.'),
('43d024f5-028e-53d0-9d6d-522eb3e79b13'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'The null hypothesis in a superiority trial states:', '["There is no difference between groups","The new treatment is better","Treatments are equivalent","There is a significant difference between groups","One treatment is non-inferior"]'::jsonb, 'There is no difference between groups', 'The null hypothesis assumes no difference in outcome between the treatment groups. It is rejected only when the observed difference is unlikely to be due to chance.'),
('9f6dc4f5-9e57-54cc-b185-143bb2357f19'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Random error is reduced by:', '["Increasing sample size","Restriction","Randomization","Using blinding","Improving measurement accuracy"]'::jsonb, 'Increasing sample size', 'Random error arises from sampling variation and is reduced by increasing the sample size. Larger samples give more precise estimates with narrower confidence intervals.'),
('a8bdbe73-6992-5f95-97a5-df41b994f0c6'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Systematic error is also known as:', '["Random error","Bias","Precision","Chance","Confounding"]'::jsonb, 'Bias', 'Systematic error is a consistent, non-random deviation of results from the truth. It is called bias and cannot be reduced by increasing the sample size.'),
('9c514612-2017-5282-83b9-11e42ce6e0b6'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Precision in research refers to:', '["Lack of bias resulting in decreased functional activity","Reproducibility and lack of random error","Generalizability mediated by specific receptor activation","Truth without affecting the primary tissue pathway","External validity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Reproducibility and lack of random error', 'Precision reflects the degree of random error in an estimate. A precise result is reproducible and gives narrow confidence intervals.'),
('4c7c7181-4c8c-5e84-9d84-247a9f82c364'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Validity in research refers to:', '["Freedom from systematic error (bias)","Reproducibility accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sample size mediated by specific receptor activation","Low p-value without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Large effect size leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Freedom from systematic error (bias)', 'Validity indicates how closely a measurement reflects the truth, which requires freedom from systematic error or bias. It is distinct from precision.'),
('bc27c1ae-c85f-522b-89af-69f916538cf5'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Internal validity refers to:', '["Correlation between variables","Precision of measurement","Inter-rater reliability","Generalizability to other populations","Whether study results are true for the study population"]'::jsonb, 'Whether study results are true for the study population', 'Internal validity is the extent to which the conclusions drawn from a study are correct for the people actually studied. It requires that observed associations are not due to bias or confounding.'),
('02ba5eae-1499-5fe0-95c0-c0177b44ae35'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'External validity refers to:', '["Freedom from bias resulting in decreased functional activity","Accuracy of measurement accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Precision mediated by specific receptor activation","Reproducibility without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Generalizability of results to other populations"]'::jsonb, 'Generalizability of results to other populations', 'External validity is the degree to which study findings apply beyond the study sample to other populations and settings. It depends on how representative the participants are.'),
('76b63ac3-7a43-5ef0-9008-925fa6d9cf51'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Convenience sampling is a type of:', '["Stratified sampling","Probability sampling","Cluster sampling","Simple random sampling","Non-probability sampling"]'::jsonb, 'Non-probability sampling', 'Convenience sampling selects participants who are readily available, so not every member of the population has a known chance of selection. It is a non-probability sampling method.'),
('6d0295be-5255-5324-8f1f-89772e03d2a0'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Snowball sampling is useful for studying:', '["Hospital inpatients","Healthy volunteers","Rare or hard-to-reach populations","College students","The general population"]'::jsonb, 'Rare or hard-to-reach populations', 'Snowball sampling recruits initial participants who then refer further eligible subjects. It is well suited to rare, hidden, or otherwise hard-to-reach populations.'),
('c45e6b37-85c2-5c3d-aa1c-7684013d1608'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Stratified sampling ensures:', '["Convenient selection","Simple random selection","Representation of subgroups in the population","Ease of data collection","Each element has equal chance"]'::jsonb, 'Representation of subgroups in the population', 'Stratified sampling divides the population into subgroups and samples from each stratum. This guarantees that relevant subgroups are represented in the sample.'),
('5d297c0e-773d-511c-82db-b36cffff4510'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Cluster sampling is most appropriate when:', '["Population is naturally divided into groups and a complete list is unavailable","Rare outcomes are studied accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","High precision is needed mediated by specific receptor activation","Sample size is small without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Population is homogeneous leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Population is naturally divided into groups and a complete list is unavailable', 'In cluster sampling the population is divided into naturally occurring groups and all members of selected clusters are studied, which is useful when no complete list of individuals exists. It is practical and economical but generally less precise than simple random sampling.'),
('28edfad8-68ea-50b6-b61e-e4d4ef3f0c84'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'The sampling frame is:', '["The questionnaire resulting in decreased functional activity","The sample selected for study accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The target population mediated by specific receptor activation","The list of all elements in the population from which the sample is drawn","The study protocol leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The list of all elements in the population from which the sample is drawn', 'The sampling frame is the actual list of all elements in the population from which the sample is drawn. The sample itself is selected from this list, whereas the target population is the larger population of interest.'),
('e990e87a-d338-5075-ab3f-0017b1c54635'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Sampling error is:', '["Error in measurement resulting in decreased functional activity","Error in data entry accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Error due to studying a sample rather than the whole population","Error in analysis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Error due to systematic bias leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Error due to studying a sample rather than the whole population', 'Sampling error is the chance variation that arises because a sample, not the entire population, is studied. It is random in nature and decreases as the sample size increases.'),
('28972c82-aefe-53e0-b70b-977824270748'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Standard error of the mean is calculated as:', '["√SD/n","√(SD/n)","SD/√n","SD × n","SD/n"]'::jsonb, 'SD/√n', 'The standard error of the mean is the standard deviation of the sample mean and is calculated as the population standard deviation divided by the square root of the sample size (SD/√n). It decreases as the sample size increases.'),
('3d8ab9b8-a235-5af7-b396-f2a61bd3236a'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'As sample size increases, what happens to the precision of a study estimate?', '["Increases","Decreases","Stays the same","Doubles","Becomes zero"]'::jsonb, 'Increases', 'Larger sample sizes reduce random (sampling) error, producing narrower confidence intervals and greater precision. Precision therefore increases while the standard error falls.'),
('87b24585-a610-5d27-9b03-2d277ae215c3'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Type I error is:', '["Setting alpha too low","Correct decision","Accepting a false null hypothesis (false negative)","Rejecting a true null hypothesis (false positive)","Insufficient power"]'::jsonb, 'Rejecting a true null hypothesis (false positive)', 'A Type I error occurs when a true null hypothesis is wrongly rejected, producing a false positive conclusion. Its probability is denoted alpha and is conventionally set at 0.05.'),
('edab2686-12fb-5e99-a78b-66cd90093b8e'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Type II error is:', '["Setting alpha too high resulting in decreased functional activity","Failing to reject a false null hypothesis (false negative)","Increasing power mediated by specific receptor activation","Correctly rejecting H0 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Rejecting a true null hypothesis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Failing to reject a false null hypothesis (false negative)', 'A Type II error occurs when a false null hypothesis is not rejected, producing a false negative result. Its probability is denoted beta, and power equals 1 − beta.'),
('47b00fef-0eee-5e24-b047-1f3eceaf435c'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'The probability of Type I error is denoted by:', '["P-value","Beta","1 - beta","Power","Alpha"]'::jsonb, 'Alpha', 'The probability of a Type I error is the significance level, denoted by alpha and conventionally set at 0.05. Beta denotes the probability of a Type II error.'),
('e2e592a4-7f9c-5e2b-826e-4e2c6a30ddd3'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'The probability of Type II error is denoted by:', '["Beta","1 - alpha","Alpha","P-value","Power"]'::jsonb, 'Beta', 'The probability of a Type II error is denoted by beta. Statistical power, the probability of avoiding this error, is calculated as 1 − beta.'),
('2a6105c9-1275-5b22-8c8d-81e2e6b4f10b'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Statistical power is calculated as:', '["Alpha","1 - Beta","P-value","1 - Alpha","Beta"]'::jsonb, '1 - Beta', 'Statistical power is the probability of correctly rejecting a false null hypothesis and is calculated as 1 − beta. It increases with sample size and effect size.'),
('85d866f7-009b-5972-9910-6a029557be2f'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'In a study, alpha = 0.05 and beta = 0.20. What is the power of the study?', '["80%","20%","5%","95%","50%"]'::jsonb, '80%', 'Power is calculated as 1 − beta, so with beta = 0.20 the power is 0.80 (80%). Alpha (0.05) specifies the Type I error rate and does not feature in this calculation.'),
('fdc400fb-f01a-5693-bfc1-95e7f0240d01'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Power of a study can be increased by:', '["Increasing Type I error rate","Increasing sample size","Decreasing sample size","Decreasing effect size","Reducing study duration"]'::jsonb, 'Increasing sample size', 'Larger samples reduce the standard error and increase the ability to detect a true effect, thereby raising statistical power. Smaller samples and smaller effect sizes lower power.'),
('19b59b39-3149-5a9b-94fb-a48e5125f096'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'The effect size in a study refers to:', '["The sample size resulting in decreased functional activity","The p-value accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The standard deviation mediated by specific receptor activation","The alpha level without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The magnitude of the difference or association being studied"]'::jsonb, 'The magnitude of the difference or association being studied', 'Effect size quantifies the magnitude of the difference or association under investigation, independent of sample size. Larger effect sizes require smaller samples to detect and contribute to greater power.'),
('bb8141b6-e49e-5c86-abc7-4eaf2216eebe'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'The p-value is defined as:', '["The probability of observing results as extreme or more extreme than those observed if the null hypothesis is true","The probability of Type II error accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The probability that the null hypothesis is true mediated by specific receptor activation","The probability that the alternative hypothesis is true without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The effect size leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The probability of observing results as extreme or more extreme than those observed if the null hypothesis is true', 'The p-value is the probability of obtaining results as extreme as, or more extreme than, those observed if the null hypothesis were true. Ap-value below the chosen alpha (conventionally 0.05) leads to rejection of the null hypothesis.'),
('d12cb3a4-7e81-5d99-bc98-ff51381b6593'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Clinical significance differs from statistical significance because:', '["Only RCTs have clinical significance","Statistical significance is clinically meaningful","They are the same concept","A statistically significant result may be clinically unimportant","Clinical significance uses p-values"]'::jsonb, 'A statistically significant result may be clinically unimportant', 'Statistical significance reflects only whether the finding is unlikely under the null hypothesis, whereas clinical significance reflects whether the effect is large enough to matter in practice. A large study can yield a statistically significant yet clinically trivial difference.'),
('82d73e2b-8a0a-58c4-b028-4dec528fd899'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'A 95% confidence interval (CI) means that:', '["If the study were repeated many times 95% of CIs would contain the true value","95% of sample means fall within this interval","There is a 95% chance the null hypothesis is false","95% chance that the true value lies within the interval","The interval contains 95% of the data"]'::jsonb, 'If the study were repeated many times 95% of CIs would contain the true value', 'A 95% CI is an estimate of uncertainty such that, if the study were repeated many times, about 95% of intervals constructed in the same way would contain the true population value. It does not itself place a 95% probability on a single fixed interval.'),
('37c348f0-24fd-545a-9337-939f3eb3e628'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'If the 95% confidence interval for an odds ratio includes 1.0, the result is:', '["Statistically significant at p < 0.05","Highly significant","Clinically significant","Not statistically significant at p < 0.05","Evidence of a protective effect"]'::jsonb, 'Not statistically significant at p < 0.05', 'An odds ratio of 1.0 represents no association, so a 95% CI spanning 1.0 is consistent with the null hypothesis of no effect. Such a result is statistically non-significant at the 0.05 level.'),
('50f24ea0-de28-57a3-8397-8154155485ae'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'A confounding variable is:', '["A variable that only affects the outcome","Random error","A variable that is intermediate in the causal pathway between exposure and outcome","A variable associated with both exposure and outcome but not on the causal pathway","A variable that only affects the exposure"]'::jsonb, 'A variable associated with both exposure and outcome but not on the causal pathway', 'A confounder is associated with both the exposure and the outcome and is not an intermediate step on the causal pathway. Unless controlled, it distorts the observed exposure-outcome association.'),
('0a0f829b-def0-5f2c-801a-a5ae683b48d4'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Which of the following is NOT a method to control confounding?', '["Randomization","Placebo","Stratification","Restriction","Matching"]'::jsonb, 'Placebo', 'Randomization, restriction, matching, and stratification are established methods for controlling confounding. Placebo is a control substance used in trials and does not address confounding.'),
('e4fd3ac7-037b-5312-93ea-492cc09cf6cc'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Standardization is used to:', '["Reduce bias in measurement","Control confounding by age","Increase sample size","Improve recall","Blind the observer"]'::jsonb, 'Control confounding by age', 'Standardization adjusts rates so that comparisons are unaffected by differences in age structure between populations. It thereby controls confounding by age.'),
('665983ba-ae29-56fa-9ebf-b29c53da091e'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Direct standardization requires:', '["Age-specific rates in the study population and a standard population","Only the crude rate","The standard population''s age distribution","Age-specific rates in both populations and a standard population","Population census of both areas"]'::jsonb, 'Age-specific rates in the study population and a standard population', 'Direct standardization applies the age-specific rates of the study population to the age distribution of a standard population. The resulting rate is compared across populations free of age-structure differences.'),
('4dc5fdf1-085d-5feb-a336-faf06d2df27e'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Indirect standardization calculates:', '["Standardized mortality ratio (SMR)","Prevalence","Crude mortality rate","Incidence rate","Attack rate"]'::jsonb, 'Standardized mortality ratio (SMR)', 'Indirect standardization applies the age-specific rates of a standard population to the study population''s age structure and expresses observed deaths as a ratio of expected deaths, yielding the standardized mortality ratio (SMR).'),
('93138803-7df4-586d-a166-fa6c639b8fe5'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'A standardized mortality ratio (SMR) greater than 1 indicates:', '["Fewer deaths than expected","Data error","More deaths than expected","Equal observed and expected deaths","Deaths only in older age groups"]'::jsonb, 'More deaths than expected', 'An SMR is the ratio of observed to expected deaths, so a value greater than 1 means the population experienced more deaths than expected. An SMR below 1 indicates fewer deaths and a value of 1 equals expectation.'),
('288cabf7-cac5-5579-9592-3d3d0ec6a73a'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Which study design is the strongest observational design for causal inference?', '["Cohort","Case-control","Cross-sectional","Case series","Ecological"]'::jsonb, 'Cohort', 'Cohort studies follow groups forward in time from exposure to outcome, establishing temporality and permitting direct measurement of incidence and relative risk. They therefore provide the strongest evidence among observational designs.'),
('639fc102-7850-52f2-b366-a1eb36ba295c'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Bradford Hill criteria for causation include all of the following EXCEPT:', '["Specificity","Temporality","P-value","Strength of association","Consistency"]'::jsonb, 'P-value', 'The Bradford Hill criteria include strength, consistency, specificity, temporality, biological gradient, plausibility, coherence, experiment, and analogy. The p-value is a statistical measure and is not among these criteria.'),
('a28f624e-d0f1-503a-855e-d407373fe8e9'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Temporality in the Bradford Hill criteria means:', '["Exposure must precede outcome","The association is consistent across studies","There is a dose-response","The association is strong","Disease is rare"]'::jsonb, 'Exposure must precede outcome', 'Temporality requires that the cause precede the effect. It is an essential criterion because a factor occurring after the outcome could not be causal.'),
('1eeda784-447a-53cb-bf22-7c6da8e6527e'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'A dose-response relationship according to Bradford Hill means:', '["Association is specific","Results are biologically plausible","Association is consistent","Exposure precedes outcome","More exposure leads to more disease"]'::jsonb, 'More exposure leads to more disease', 'A dose-response (biological gradient) relationship exists when increasing levels of exposure are associated with increasing risk or severity of disease. Such a gradient strengthens the case for causation.'),
('38a878bf-9b18-5f89-96f1-cdf1b31123ae'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Biological plausibility as a causation criterion means:', '["Other explanations are ruled out","Association is consistent","Exposure precedes disease","Results are specific","There is a reasonable biological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'There is a reasonable biological mechanism', 'Biological plausibility is satisfied when a credible biological mechanism can explain the observed association between exposure and disease. It is supportive of causation rather than a necessary proof.'),
('e842e157-1ce3-5c82-a28d-ede62be069be'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Which of the following is NOT a measure of disease frequency?', '["Odds ratio","Mortality rate","Attack rate","Incidence","Prevalence"]'::jsonb, 'Odds ratio', 'Incidence, prevalence, attack rate, and mortality rate all quantify the frequency of disease in a population. The odds ratio is a measure of association comparing exposed and unexposed groups, not a frequency measure.'),
('17a97b7e-b2e6-5aac-936a-e8e693d2bb12'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'The proportional mortality ratio (PMR) is:', '["Total deaths / Population","Deaths from a specific cause / Total deaths","Cases of disease / Population","Deaths from a specific cause / Population","Deaths from a specific cause / Cases of that disease"]'::jsonb, 'Deaths from a specific cause / Total deaths', 'The PMR expresses deaths from a specific cause as a proportion of all deaths in the population. It shows the relative contribution of a cause to overall mortality.'),
('34c03c1a-b33d-5924-84f7-b5e6f7bc79d7'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Years of potential life lost (YPLL) measures:', '["Incidence of disease","Total deaths","Quality of life","Deaths at all ages","Deaths before a reference age"]'::jsonb, 'Deaths before a reference age', 'YPLL quantifies the years of life lost from deaths occurring before a chosen reference age. It weights premature deaths more heavily and is used to prioritize causes affecting younger people.'),
('3e43632b-6a50-5c84-869f-f7d4e723ed45'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'The Disability-Adjusted Life Year (DALY) is the sum of:', '["Incidence + Prevalence","Morbidity + Mortality","YLD + YLL","YLD - YLL","Direct + Indirect costs"]'::jsonb, 'YLD + YLL', 'A DALY combines years of life lost due to premature death (YLL) with years lived with disability (YLD). One DALY represents one lost year of healthy life.'),
('ebe8e766-efe3-5bec-a36c-0ce784ad3898'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'The Quality-Adjusted Life Year (QALY) measures:', '["Health expenditure","Incidence rate","Crude mortality","Disability burden","Quantity of life adjusted for quality"]'::jsonb, 'Quantity of life adjusted for quality', 'A QALY assigns weights to health states and combines survival duration with health-related quality of life. One QALY equals one year of life in full health.'),
('de49b78b-db10-50bf-bf30-b17288c60893'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Cost-effectiveness analysis compares:', '["Costs vs monetary benefits","Costs of different programs","Multiple outcomes with costs","Costs in monetary terms vs outcomes in natural units","Quality of life gains"]'::jsonb, 'Costs in monetary terms vs outcomes in natural units', 'Cost-effectiveness analysis measures costs in monetary units and outcomes in natural units such as life years gained or cases prevented. The result is an incremental cost-effectiveness ratio.'),
('fc77b730-ebc0-5275-890e-599bc72c24b2'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Cost-benefit analysis expresses outcomes in:', '["Natural units (life years)","DALYs","Monetary terms","Clinical events","QALYs"]'::jsonb, 'Monetary terms', 'Cost-benefit analysis values both costs and outcomes in monetary units, allowing a direct net-benefit comparison. Expressing health benefits in money is a defining feature of this method.'),
('35dffc53-4a4e-5d20-a6f4-29d968ef5c8f'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Cost-utility analysis uses which outcome measure?', '["Life years gained","QALYs or DALYs","Clinical events","Monetary units","Survival rate"]'::jsonb, 'QALYs or DALYs', 'Cost-utility analysis measures outcomes as quality-adjusted life years (QALYs) or disability-adjusted life years (DALYs). These utility measures combine survival length with health-related quality of life.'),
('39dee2de-3063-5a08-9e7e-a27c4f08eee3'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'ICER stands for:', '["Incremental Cost-Effectiveness Ratio","Independent Cost-Effectiveness Ratio","International Cost Evaluation Ratio","Index of Cost-Effectiveness Research","Incremental Cost-Equity Ratio"]'::jsonb, 'Incremental Cost-Effectiveness Ratio', 'The ICER is the difference in cost between two interventions divided by the difference in their health outcomes. It expresses the additional cost per additional unit of outcome gained.'),
('d1db2a73-37c7-51ee-aa29-ed154a8b7619'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'The most cost-effective intervention is the one with:', '["Lowest outcome","Highest cost","Lowest ICER","Greatest resource use","Highest ICER"]'::jsonb, 'Lowest ICER', 'Cost-effectiveness is assessed by the incremental cost-effectiveness ratio, so a lower ICER means more health outcome per unit of cost. The intervention with the lowest ICER is considered most cost-effective.'),
('4b5ac230-f9f6-537f-8412-7081897671f5'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Which type of economic evaluation considers only costs?', '["Cost-minimization analysis","Cost-utility analysis","Cost-effectiveness analysis","Cost-benefit analysis","Sensitivity analysis"]'::jsonb, 'Cost-minimization analysis', 'Cost-minimization analysis compares only the costs of alternatives whose health outcomes are assumed equivalent. The other listed evaluations incorporate outcomes as well as costs.'),
('1a7f26f5-bb22-5c88-9de2-839eeb9f60d8'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Discrete (selective) screening refers to:', '["Opportunistic screening","Screening multiple diseases","Screening at multiple time points","Screening high-risk groups only","Screening entire population"]'::jsonb, 'Screening high-risk groups only', 'Selective (discrete) screening is applied to subgroups at increased risk of a disease rather than to the whole population. It concentrates resources on those most likely to benefit.'),
('f59e4522-5d13-51e6-9d93-e08ae714ce0d'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Mass screening refers to:', '["Screening of entire population irrespective of risk","Diagnostic testing accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Confirmatory testing mediated by specific receptor activation","Screening of high-risk groups without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Monitoring treatment leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Screening of entire population irrespective of risk', 'Mass screening applies a screening test to the entire population regardless of individual risk. In contrast, selective screening targets only high-risk groups.'),
('d8f650b6-6acd-5813-ae58-74c11dee6867'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Opportunistic screening is performed when:', '["Screening camps are organized","Individual visits healthcare provider for another reason","Screening is mandated by law","Only high-risk groups are invited","Systematic population screening is done"]'::jsonb, 'Individual visits healthcare provider for another reason', 'Opportunistic screening occurs when a test is offered to an individual who is already seeing a health care provider for an unrelated reason. It is distinct from systematic, population-based screening.'),
('36d16697-cd04-5e15-83ab-389fb5c00edd'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'The Wilson-Jungner criteria for a screening program include all of the following EXCEPT:', '["The test must be 100% accurate","The condition should be an important health problem","There should be an accepted treatment","The natural history should be understood","The test should be acceptable to population"]'::jsonb, 'The test must be 100% accurate', 'The Wilson-Jungner criteria require that the condition be important, its natural history understood, an accepted treatment exist, and the test be acceptable, but they do not demand 100% accuracy. Screening tests are imperfect by design.'),
('c945fd5b-4657-5038-aa99-a0fba48adfd2'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Screening should NOT be implemented when:', '["Test is acceptable","Natural history is poorly understood","Costs are reasonable","Disease is important","Effective treatment exists"]'::jsonb, 'Natural history is poorly understood', 'A screening program requires that the natural history of the disease be well understood so that early detection changes the outcome. If the natural history is poorly understood, screening cannot be justified.'),
('71d84b7e-f5ff-5af6-bd70-13af84ef157a'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Overdiagnosis in screening refers to:', '["Falsely diagnosing disease","Detecting disease earlier","Missing early disease","Missing a case of disease","Detecting disease that would not have caused symptoms"]'::jsonb, 'Detecting disease that would not have caused symptoms', 'Overdiagnosis occurs when screening detects disease that would never have caused symptoms or harm during the person''s lifetime. Those affected undergo treatment they cannot benefit from.'),
('d4fd8ff8-fce5-5ebb-b421-37b76e1e3526'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'The difference between screening and diagnostic tests is that:', '["Diagnostic tests are for asymptomatic individuals resulting in decreased functional activity","Screening is performed on symptomatic individuals accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Screening is for asymptomatic individuals; diagnostic tests confirm disease in symptomatic individuals","Screening is more accurate without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Diagnostic tests are cheaper leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Screening is for asymptomatic individuals; diagnostic tests confirm disease in symptomatic individuals', 'Screening is applied to asymptomatic individuals to detect early disease, whereas diagnostic tests are used in symptomatic or screen-positive individuals to confirm or exclude disease. Screening tests are generally less definitive and less accurate than diagnostic tests.'),
('dd271b17-c012-5ef2-aac7-e72b71bfa029'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Which of the following is a potential harm of screening?', '["False positives leading to unnecessary anxiety","Cost to healthcare system","All of the above","False reassurance from false negatives","Overdiagnosis"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Screening can cause false positives with consequent anxiety and further testing, false negatives with false reassurance, overdiagnosis of harmless disease, and financial costs to the health care system. All listed items are recognized harms.'),
('e85b8307-c040-5c09-bbdc-3d8c8c53391e'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Lead-time bias is particularly problematic in evaluating screening programs for:', '["Acute infectious diseases resulting in decreased functional activity","Genetic disorders accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Poisoning mediated by specific receptor activation","Trauma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cancers with long pre-clinical phase"]'::jsonb, 'Cancers with long pre-clinical phase', 'Lead-time bias arises when screening detects disease earlier in its natural history, lengthening apparent survival without actually prolonging life. It is especially relevant to cancers with a long pre-clinical, detectable phase.'),
('c04955d4-ba60-56b0-875f-ed149f054a73'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Length-time bias in screening results from:', '["Overrepresentation of slow-growing cancers among screen-detected cases","Rapid progression of screen-detected cases","Fast-growing cancers being detected early","Screening detecting more aggressive cancers","Equal detection of all cancer types"]'::jsonb, 'Overrepresentation of slow-growing cancers among screen-detected cases', 'Length-time bias occurs because screening intervals detect slow-growing cancers more often than fast-growing ones, overrepresenting indolent tumors with better outcomes. This makes screening appear more beneficial than it truly is.'),
('e38938bf-d4d9-54e6-aa97-cd7c9f37f778'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'What type of study is a cross-sectional study?', '["Interventional resulting in decreased functional activity","Observational with data collected at a single point in time","Experimental mediated by specific receptor activation","Longitudinal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Prospective leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Observational with data collected at a single point in time', 'A cross-sectional study is observational and collects exposure and outcome data at one point in time. It measures prevalence and cannot establish temporality, unlike longitudinal or prospective designs.'),
('7c5a17c5-be64-5bf1-b33b-d7f435e8964f'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Prevalence-incidence bias (Neyman bias) occurs in:', '["Ecological studies","RCTs","Cohort studies","Case-control studies","Cross-sectional studies"]'::jsonb, 'Case-control studies', 'Neyman (prevalence-incidence) bias arises when prevalent rather than incident cases are sampled, so survivors with milder disease are overrepresented. It is classically described in case-control studies that recruit prevalent cases.'),
('e8c0f2d5-4d95-5e39-824b-c15306f09509'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'The healthy worker effect is seen in:', '["Occupational cohort studies","Case-control studies","Clinical trials","General population studies","RCTs"]'::jsonb, 'Occupational cohort studies', 'The healthy worker effect describes the lower morbidity and mortality of employed persons compared with the general population. It is characteristically observed in occupational cohort studies because workers are healthier at baseline.'),
('170bfba3-a755-578f-992b-3ec1edfcb1fb'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Detection bias can be minimized by:', '["Increasing sample size","Randomization","Restriction","Using placebo","Blinding of outcome assessors"]'::jsonb, 'Blinding of outcome assessors', 'Detection (ascertainment) bias occurs when the outcome is assessed differently between groups. Blinding the outcome assessors prevents their expectations from influencing how outcomes are detected and classified.'),
('c8774497-8cb6-5d98-bf7b-a90eedca1228'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Performance bias refers to:', '["Errors in outcome measurement resulting in decreased functional activity","Patient dropouts accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Differences in care provided aside from the intervention being studied","Recall of past events without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Loss of participants during follow-up leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Differences in care provided aside from the intervention being studied', 'Performance bias results from systematic differences in care or co-interventions given to groups other than the intervention under study. Blinding participants and care providers helps reduce it.'),
('80ff30ae-c737-5d4b-8a03-51135da8e134'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Which is NOT a type of selection bias?', '["Non-response bias","Healthy worker effect","Admission rate bias (Berkson''s bias)","Volunteer bias","Recall bias"]'::jsonb, 'Recall bias', 'Recall bias is an information bias, since it arises from differential reporting of past exposures rather than from how participants are selected. The other four options all originate in the process of selecting study subjects.'),
('84934c56-caf8-5797-8547-a8059372fe4c'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Recall bias is a type of:', '["Selection bias","Random error","Confounding","Information bias","Sampling bias"]'::jsonb, 'Information bias', 'Recall bias results from inaccurate or differential recollection of exposure between groups, so it is classified as an information (observation) bias.'),
('e16e0d6a-08c8-5723-9693-94a92177adc9'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Interviewer bias is a type of:', '["Information bias","Selection bias","Sampling error","Ecological fallacy","Confounding"]'::jsonb, 'Information bias', 'Interviewer bias occurs when data are collected or recorded differently between groups by the interviewer. It therefore belongs to the information (observation) bias category.'),
('c4649a35-29bf-53e9-adf6-05e9a5eb9c28'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'What is the most effective way to reduce interviewer bias?', '["Matching","Randomization","Restriction","Standardized questionnaire","Large sample"]'::jsonb, 'Standardized questionnaire', 'A standardized, structured questionnaire ensures that questions, prompts, and recording are identical for all participants. This minimizes interviewer-induced differences in how information is obtained.'),
('a69570a7-3959-5426-a484-563c52530e8c'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Publication bias refers to:', '["Studies with negative results published more frequently","Only large trials are published","Only RCTs are published","Studies with positive results are more likely to be published","Only high-quality studies are published"]'::jsonb, 'Studies with positive results are more likely to be published', 'Publication bias is the tendency for studies with positive or statistically significant results to be submitted and published while negative studies remain unpublished. It is commonly assessed with a funnel plot.'),
('eb60b22f-3879-5101-a8f0-81e1574a97d5'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'A funnel plot is used to detect:', '["Confounding","Selection bias","Measurement error","Publication bias","Recall bias"]'::jsonb, 'Publication bias', 'A funnel plot graphs each study''s effect estimate against its precision. An asymmetric plot or a missing region of studies suggests publication (small-study) bias.'),
('87dd0d38-11c9-5400-b390-7fda16032999'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Sensitivity analysis in a systematic review assesses:', '["Effect size resulting in decreased functional activity","Statistical significance accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Publication bias mediated by specific receptor activation","Whether results are robust to different analytical decisions","Heterogeneity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Whether results are robust to different analytical decisions', 'Sensitivity analysis repeats the analysis under altered decisions, such as including or excluding low-quality studies or changing assumptions. If the pooled result stays similar, the conclusions are considered robust.'),
('19e9d6b7-ee04-5173-b2d8-b5a816f64af8'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Heterogeneity in meta-analysis refers to:', '["Lack of blinding","Small sample sizes","Variability in study results beyond chance","Reporting bias","P-value of pooled estimate"]'::jsonb, 'Variability in study results beyond chance', 'Heterogeneity is the variability in effect estimates between studies that exceeds what chance alone would produce. It is usually quantified using the I-squared statistic or the Q test.'),
('0cb4457a-ab6a-55a5-a786-d3511f068666'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'I² statistic measures:', '["Accuracy","Heterogeneity","Precision","Publication bias","Effect size"]'::jsonb, 'Heterogeneity', 'The I-squared statistic expresses the percentage of total variation between study results that is due to genuine heterogeneity rather than sampling error.'),
('bf50acf6-dc4b-5c04-b7b5-6459e7c68373'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'An I² value of 75% indicates:', '["Low heterogeneity","Negative heterogeneity","Moderate heterogeneity","Complete homogeneity","Substantial heterogeneity"]'::jsonb, 'Substantial heterogeneity', 'Conventional thresholds grade I-squared values of 50-90% as substantial heterogeneity. A value of 75% therefore indicates substantial heterogeneity among the pooled studies.'),
('631ad468-7dc5-5237-941b-bf39513878db'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Fixed-effect meta-analysis assumes:', '["One true effect common to all studies","Random effects are present","Effect is zero","High heterogeneity","True effect varies across studies"]'::jsonb, 'One true effect common to all studies', 'The fixed-effect model assumes all studies estimate the same single underlying effect, and that observed differences are due only to sampling error.'),
('e7a93ec2-06a8-5435-8859-f4b94d0bbb6a'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Random-effects meta-analysis assumes:', '["Effect is zero resulting in decreased functional activity","No heterogeneity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","True effects vary across studies following a distribution","Single true effect without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All studies identical leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'True effects vary across studies following a distribution', 'The random-effects model assumes that the true effect differs between studies and that these effects follow a distribution around an average. It is used when between-study heterogeneity is expected.'),
('4b935987-d230-5fbc-bb20-af44ebd6af6e'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'A forest plot in meta-analysis displays:', '["Individual study results and pooled estimate","Study quality accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Publication bias mediated by specific receptor activation","Confounding variables without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sample size leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Individual study results and pooled estimate', 'A forest plot shows each included study''s effect estimate with its confidence interval and the overall pooled estimate, usually drawn as a diamond.'),
('19f9a114-f62b-51af-b5f4-cec4eb2c09cc'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'In which design does the investigator assign the exposure?', '["Experimental study","Cross-sectional study","Case-control study","Cohort study","Observational study"]'::jsonb, 'Experimental study', 'An experimental study is defined by the investigator actively assigning the exposure or intervention to participants, in contrast to observational designs where exposures are merely observed.'),
('8a840dd2-b4f0-5876-bbc5-5447cffc7f9d'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'The target population is:', '["The population to which results are intended to be generalized","The sample actually studied accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The control group mediated by specific receptor activation","The sampling frame without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The exposed group leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The population to which results are intended to be generalized', 'The target population is the entire group of people to whom the investigator wishes to generalize the study findings. It differs from the sample actually studied and from the sampling frame.'),
('62837640-7a46-5e4f-8138-770a7408ec50'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'External validity is threatened when:', '["Randomization is used resulting in decreased functional activity","Sample is representative accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Study population differs significantly from the target population","Confounding is controlled without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Study is well-designed leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Study population differs significantly from the target population', 'External validity depends on how well the study sample represents the target population. When the two differ substantially, applying the results beyond the study population is not justified.'),
('a14b83e5-5d57-5beb-97d0-c93fa13e60ee'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'In a population of 500 000 people, 50 new cases of a disease are diagnosed in one year. The incidence rate is best expressed as:', '["Cases per 1000 population","Cases per 1000 births","Cases per 1000 women","Cases per 1000 person-years","Percentage of population affected"]'::jsonb, 'Cases per 1000 person-years', 'An incidence rate uses person-time as the denominator because each person contributes a different period at risk. The result is therefore expressed as cases per person-time, such as per 1000 person-years.'),
('446c1b26-46d7-5f86-9d36-290a621613d0'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Cumulative incidence (risk) ranges from:', '["0 to 1 (or 0% to 100%)","0 to -1","0 to 1000","0 to infinity","-1 to +1"]'::jsonb, '0 to 1 (or 0% to 100%)', 'Cumulative incidence is the proportion of a defined population that develops the disease over a period, so it can only range from 0 to 1 (0% to 100%).'),
('5bb03448-4fd0-5691-9589-5d307c9fd9ba'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'The attributable fraction in exposed (AFE) is calculated as:', '["(Ie - Iu)/Ie","(Ie - Iu)/Iu","Ie/Iu","(Iu - Ie)/Ie","1 - RR"]'::jsonb, '(Ie - Iu)/Ie', 'The attributable fraction in the exposed is the proportion of disease among exposed individuals that is due to the exposure, computed as (Ie - Iu)/Ie, where Ie is the incidence in the exposed and Iu in the unexposed.'),
('4edea5f7-a8fa-544d-b273-5835487e8959'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Population attributable fraction (PAF) depends on:', '["Both RR and exposure prevalence","RR only","Sensitivity","Specificity","Prevalence of exposure in the population"]'::jsonb, 'Both RR and exposure prevalence', 'The population attributable fraction depends on both the strength of the exposure-disease association (RR) and the prevalence of exposure in the population, because it estimates the disease that could be eliminated if the exposure were removed.'),
('0c01adc6-7859-5810-8dab-ef4098b8cfae'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'If RR = 2 and exposure prevalence = 20%, the PAF is approximately:', '["16.7%","10%","20%","33.3%","40%"]'::jsonb, '16.7%', 'PAF = Pe(RR - 1)/[1 + Pe(RR - 1)] = 0.2(1)/[1 + 0.2(1)] = 0.2/1.2, which is approximately 16.7%.'),
('00f639f5-9ddf-519e-8c2d-7e2848627dee'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Which of the following is NOT an observational study?', '["Case series","Cohort","RCT","Cross-sectional","Case-control"]'::jsonb, 'RCT', 'An RCT is an experimental design in which the investigator assigns the intervention. Case series, cohort, cross-sectional, and case-control designs are all observational.'),
('f877528f-4f89-56d9-8696-e12b16fc802a'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'The main advantage of an RCT over observational studies is:', '["Easier to conduct resulting in decreased functional activity","Better control of confounding by randomization","Fewer ethical concerns mediated by specific receptor activation","Longer follow-up without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lower cost leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Better control of confounding by randomization', 'Randomization balances both known and unknown confounders across treatment groups. This gives RCTs superior control of confounding compared with observational studies.'),
('40385115-d229-52fc-b77c-d74183905c25'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'A cluster RCT randomizes:', '["Subgroups/clusters rather than individuals","Only treatment assignments","Individuals","Time periods","Organs within individuals"]'::jsonb, 'Subgroups/clusters rather than individuals', 'In a cluster randomized trial, whole groups such as communities, schools, or clinics are randomized to the interventions instead of individual participants.'),
('1e0e1904-2c35-5429-8637-1e5e7f788a09'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'A pragmatic RCT aims to assess:', '["Safety only","Efficacy under ideal conditions","Effectiveness in real-world conditions","Cost only","Biological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Effectiveness in real-world conditions', 'Pragmatic trials measure effectiveness, or how well an intervention works under routine real-world conditions with usual care and flexible protocols.'),
('d12a845a-4084-51c3-a5e0-d43ce9844d6c'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'An explanatory RCT aims to assess:', '["Real-world effectiveness","Cost-effectiveness","Long-term outcomes","Patient satisfaction","Efficacy under ideal circumstances"]'::jsonb, 'Efficacy under ideal circumstances', 'Explanatory (efficacy) trials test whether an intervention works under ideal, tightly controlled conditions, typically with selected participants and strict protocols.'),
('e8ff5fa8-2306-57f9-a498-bd2415e6afb5'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Equipoise refers to:', '["Equal sample sizes resulting in decreased functional activity","Genuine uncertainty about which treatment is superior","Balance between groups mediated by specific receptor activation","Bias in allocation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Treatment equivalence leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Genuine uncertainty about which treatment is superior', 'Equipoise is the genuine state of uncertainty in the medical community about whether one treatment is better than another. It is the ethical justification for randomizing patients in a trial.'),
('f44ea8bd-d506-555c-a819-7338633b2b3d'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Clinical equipoise is an ethical prerequisite for:', '["Observational studies","Case reports","RCTs","Case series","Systematic reviews"]'::jsonb, 'RCTs', 'Randomizing a patient is ethical only when genuine uncertainty exists about which treatment is superior. Clinical equipoise is therefore a required ethical condition for conducting an RCT.'),
('ad31ef96-36fd-5286-918a-f0d4f5934b56'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'The Hawthorne effect is a threat to:', '["Blinding","Randomization","Allocation concealment","External validity","Internal validity"]'::jsonb, 'Internal validity', 'The Hawthorne effect changes participants'' behavior simply because they know they are being observed. This can alter outcomes independently of the intervention and thus threatens the internal validity of a study.'),
('8418bb6b-6d86-5941-9266-abb88a65efd0'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Which of the following is a continuous variable?', '["Blood pressure (mmHg)","Number of children","Smoking status","Blood group","Gender"]'::jsonb, 'Blood pressure (mmHg)', 'A continuous variable can take any numerical value within a range, and blood pressure in mmHg is measured on a continuous scale. The other options are counts or categorical variables.'),
('8fecdf02-ccab-5ba6-9aa4-a1bdd2bdda59'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Which of the following is an ordinal variable?', '["Gender resulting in decreased functional activity","Blood group accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Eye color mediated by specific receptor activation","Educational level (primary/secondary/tertiary)","Marital status leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Educational level (primary/secondary/tertiary)', 'Educational level categories follow a meaningful order but with unequal intervals, which defines an ordinal variable. Gender, blood group, eye color, and marital status are nominal variables.'),
('e34a9245-5559-5ddf-83ef-0da5f779ea97'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Which of the following is a nominal variable?', '["Ranking","Blood group (A/B/AB/O)","Height","Pain score (0-10)","Temperature in Celsius"]'::jsonb, 'Blood group (A/B/AB/O)', 'Blood group categories (A/B/AB/O) have no intrinsic order, making it a nominal variable. Height and temperature are continuous, while ranking and pain score are ordinal.'),
('9d761440-7558-5e52-a1ae-0a031ff8e2f4'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'A person-time denominator is most appropriate for:', '["Chronic diseases with variable follow-up times","Case reports accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Acute epidemics mediated by specific receptor activation","Screening programs without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cross-sectional surveys leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Chronic diseases with variable follow-up times', 'Person-time accounts for each subject''s actual duration of follow-up. It is most appropriate when studying chronic diseases in cohorts with unequal or varying follow-up times.'),
('0079751f-8cbe-5a11-b1cc-46d740f48f8d'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'The standardized mortality ratio is an example of:', '["Odds ratio","Risk ratio","Direct standardization","Prevalence ratio","Indirect standardization"]'::jsonb, 'Indirect standardization', 'The standardized mortality ratio applies a study population''s age-specific rates to a standard population to obtain expected deaths, and is the classic measure of indirect standardization.'),
('daa9a6de-3457-5d2b-b120-8b0d594e5df4'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'What does a 95% confidence interval for a RR of 1.5 (1.1 to 2.0) indicate?', '["There is a protective effect resulting in decreased functional activity","The sample is too small accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The true RR is exactly 1.5 mediated by specific receptor activation","The result is statistically significant and the association is positive","The result is not statistically significant leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The result is statistically significant and the association is positive', 'Because the confidence interval (1.1 to 2.0) lies entirely above 1.0, the null value is excluded and the association is statistically significant with an increased risk.'),
('a62eb544-ac10-5b01-8db9-f256eee3526e'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'What does a RR of 0.5 indicate?', '["50% increased risk","Half the sample needed","Double the risk","50% decreased risk (protective effect)","No association"]'::jsonb, '50% decreased risk (protective effect)', 'A relative risk below 1 indicates a protective association. A RR of 0.5 means the risk in the exposed group is half that in the unexposed group, a 50% reduction in risk.'),
('0a74cfe2-5e2a-5597-85f8-778845836c4f'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Misclassification bias is a type of:', '["Confounding","Random error","Information bias","Selection bias","Sampling error"]'::jsonb, 'Information bias', 'Misclassification assigns subjects to the wrong exposure or disease category, so it is an information (observation) bias rather than a selection bias.'),
('37c37d81-1924-565d-8129-a8cfa4a892c6'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Differential misclassification of exposure in case-control studies typically:', '["Increases specificity","Biases away from null","Biases toward null","Eliminates the association","Reduces sample size"]'::jsonb, 'Biases away from null', 'When exposure misclassification differs between cases and controls, the effect estimate is usually distorted away from the null. This can exaggerate or create a spurious association.'),
('f40efa0b-29fb-5f56-8c1c-8917c1fde4af'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Non-differential misclassification of exposure typically:', '["Increases power","Causes bias","Biases away from null","Increases effect size","Biases toward null"]'::jsonb, 'Biases toward null', 'Non-differential misclassification occurs at similar rates in both groups and typically dilutes the true association, biasing the estimate toward the null and reducing its magnitude.'),
('afc86db2-26f9-523c-977e-570591671537'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'What is the purpose of phase I clinical trials?', '["Effectiveness","Post-marketing surveillance","Safety and dosage","Safety in large population","Efficacy"]'::jsonb, 'Safety and dosage', 'Phase I trials enroll small numbers of healthy volunteers to establish the drug''s safety, tolerability, and dosage range in humans before efficacy is formally tested.'),
('59813606-7300-5d6b-acf6-781ce4265e8e'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Phase II clinical trials primarily assess:', '["Cost-effectiveness","Post-marketing surveillance","Dosage","Efficacy and side effects","Long-term safety"]'::jsonb, 'Efficacy and side effects', 'Phase II trials give the drug to a small group of affected patients to obtain preliminary evidence of efficacy and to characterize short-term side effects.'),
('8df23128-c9bd-5314-9e9e-23916a5d717f'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Phase III clinical trials primarily assess:', '["Safety only resulting in decreased functional activity","Mechanism of action accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Efficacy compared to standard treatment","Small sample safety without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Dosage leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Efficacy compared to standard treatment', 'Phase III trials compare the new treatment against the standard of care in large populations to confirm efficacy and monitor adverse reactions before approval.'),
('1c3e9f46-f748-5310-86b4-bde8d902c497'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Phase IV trials are conducted:', '["During preclinical phase","For initial dose finding","To determine mechanism","Before drug approval","After drug marketing to monitor long-term safety"]'::jsonb, 'After drug marketing to monitor long-term safety', 'Phase IV (post-marketing) studies are conducted after a drug is approved to monitor long-term safety, rare adverse effects, and real-world effectiveness in the wider population.'),
('4ddd24e3-3ec5-50aa-8c3c-32b859e8a8c7'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Case series are useful for:', '["Controlling bias","Establishing causality","Generalizing to population","Generating hypotheses","Testing hypotheses"]'::jsonb, 'Generating hypotheses', 'Case series describe the clinical features and outcomes of a group of patients and are most useful for generating hypotheses. They lack a comparison group and cannot establish causality or test hypotheses.'),
('0b21285a-b474-5432-b178-93d1ea59f82e'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'What is the most appropriate study design for a rare exposure?', '["Cohort study","Case-control study","RCT","Case report","Cross-sectional study"]'::jsonb, 'Cohort study', 'A cohort study assembles participants according to exposure status, so exposed individuals can be recruited directly and followed for outcomes. This makes it the most efficient design when the exposure is rare.'),
('e6392043-b4d3-57b3-b2a6-418485b81423'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'For a rare disease, the odds ratio approximates:', '["Risk difference","Attributable risk","Relative risk","Prevalence ratio","Hazard ratio"]'::jsonb, 'Relative risk', 'When the disease is rare, the odds ratio closely approximates the relative risk. The odds ratio can therefore be used as an estimate of the relative risk in case-control studies of rare diseases.'),
('3c3abbf8-4602-5461-b994-ee8cd91e934f'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'The rare disease assumption states that:', '["Prevalence is high","Incidence equals prevalence","OR approximates RR when disease is rare","RR approximates OR when disease is common","The disease is fatal"]'::jsonb, 'OR approximates RR when disease is rare', 'When the outcome is uncommon, the odds ratio obtained from a case-control study approximates the relative risk. The approximation improves as the disease prevalence approaches zero.'),
('25d60712-43ba-5256-856d-21a221e6dfc9'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'A number needed to treat (NNT) of 10 means that:', '["Ten times more effective than placebo","Number needed to harm is 10","10% of patients benefit","Treatment is 10% effective","10 patients must be treated to prevent one adverse outcome"]'::jsonb, '10 patients must be treated to prevent one adverse outcome', 'NNT equals 1 divided by the absolute risk reduction. An NNT of 10 means 10 patients must receive the treatment to prevent one additional adverse outcome compared with the control group.'),
('fb06ae4d-6bd9-5eff-849b-9b6c20508c54'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'The number needed to treat (NNT) is influenced by:', '["Patient population","Follow-up duration","Baseline risk (CER)","Effect size","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'NNT equals 1 divided by the absolute risk reduction, so it depends on the baseline risk in the treated population, the duration of follow-up, and the magnitude of the treatment effect.'),
('7a96d0ed-6d27-5610-b8fa-aeba30eaa49b'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Which bias occurs when both cases and controls have equal accuracy of recall but one group recalls one past exposure more completely than the other?', '["Non-differential recall bias","Differential recall bias","Detection bias","Confounding","Selection bias"]'::jsonb, 'Differential recall bias', 'Differential recall bias occurs when cases and controls have comparable accuracy but the completeness of recall differs systematically between the groups. It can produce a spurious association between exposure and disease.'),
('412296e0-313a-52f5-b312-f175011f0234'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'A case definition must be:', '["Standardized and specific to the study","Unclear accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Vague mediated by specific receptor activation","Broad without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Changing during the study leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Standardized and specific to the study', 'A case definition must be standardized, objective, and specific to the study. Vague, broad, or changing criteria reduce the validity and comparability of case ascertainment.'),
('5855c772-7a7a-5ea5-ade3-ee383c04e00c'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Which study design provides the strongest evidence for causation?', '["Cohort","Case-control","Meta-analysis of RCTs","Expert opinion","Case series"]'::jsonb, 'Meta-analysis of RCTs', 'Systematic reviews and meta-analyses of randomized controlled trials sit at the top of the evidence hierarchy. They synthesize multiple RCTs, reducing random error and bias.'),
('b8ced09e-08a2-55b5-9e0c-c3a800441c81'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Arrange the following study designs and evidence sources in order of level of evidence, highest to lowest:', '["Expert opinion > Case series > Case-control","Meta-analysis of RCTs > RCT > Cohort > Case-control > Case series","All equal","RCT > Cohort > Case-control > Case series > Expert opinion","Case series > Cohort > RCT"]'::jsonb, 'Meta-analysis of RCTs > RCT > Cohort > Case-control > Case series', 'Evidence is ranked by design validity: meta-analyses of RCTs, single RCTs, cohort studies, case-control studies, and finally case series. Expert opinion ranks below these designs.'),
('7a64cfc4-d2ae-5c94-81c8-4713d9c4d9c5'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Which statement about the p-value is correct?', '["P-value measures the probability that H0 is true","P-value depends on sample size","P-value < 0.05 guarantees clinical significance","Larger p-values indicate stronger evidence against H0","P-value is the effect size"]'::jsonb, 'P-value depends on sample size', 'For a fixed effect size, larger samples produce smaller p-values. The p-value is a conditional probability about the observed data under the null hypothesis, not the probability that H0 is true.'),
('22a5b217-fd32-5be1-976d-2743b9628bd9'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Why might a statistically significant result fail to be clinically significant?', '["Very large sample can detect trivial differences","Confounding accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","High variability mediated by specific receptor activation","Low power without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Small sample leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Very large sample can detect trivial differences', 'A very large sample gives high power, so even a trivially small effect may reach statistical significance. Statistical significance reflects the likelihood of the data under the null hypothesis, not the clinical importance of the effect.'),
('a8639bed-473f-53a6-9f8a-22a78d1cc481'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'A one-tailed test is appropriately used:', '["When any difference in either direction is of interest","When the sample is small","When only a difference in one specific direction is of interest","When the sample is large","When using paired data only"]'::jsonb, 'When only a difference in one specific direction is of interest', 'A one-tailed test is justified only when a deviation in a single predetermined direction is scientifically plausible. Testing for any difference in either direction requires a two-tailed test.'),
('a3963200-a978-5dbe-bee6-474878fe3ca0'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Compared with a one-tailed test, a two-tailed test is:', '["More conservative (less likely to reject H0 if only one direction is true)","Only appropriate for non-parametric tests accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only appropriate for large samples mediated by specific receptor activation","Centered around zero without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Less conservative leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'More conservative (less likely to reject H0 if only one direction is true)', 'A two-tailed test splits alpha across both tails, so it is more conservative and less likely to reject the null when a true effect exists in only one direction. A one-tailed test concentrates all alpha in a single tail.'),
('98e7cc2b-b142-53e2-aeae-5317c89e019a'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'A paired t-test is appropriate when:', '["Comparing proportions","Same subjects measured twice (pre-post)","Comparing more than two groups","Two independent groups are compared","Analyzing categorical data"]'::jsonb, 'Same subjects measured twice (pre-post)', 'The paired t-test is used when observations are dependent, such as measuring the same subjects before and after an intervention. Each subject serves as its own control.'),
('02f7996f-5d36-519c-9ffa-b29347971d85'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'An unpaired (independent-samples) t-test is used for:', '["Same subjects before-after","Matched pairs","More than two groups","Comparing two independent groups","Correlated data"]'::jsonb, 'Comparing two independent groups', 'The independent-samples t-test compares the means of two separate, unrelated groups. It requires continuous data that are approximately normally distributed with similar variances.'),
('b3203961-0c09-5c38-aba0-6fd77e4cfa71'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'ANOVA is used to compare:', '["Two groups","More than two groups","Correlation","Two paired groups","Proportions"]'::jsonb, 'More than two groups', 'ANOVA compares the means of three or more groups while controlling the overall type I error. It extends the two-sample t-test to multiple groups.'),
('cf144647-abd9-5a5c-b4a8-6237634a3d71'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'One-way ANOVA compares:', '["Two independent variables","Survival curves","One variable across multiple groups","Correlation between variables","Repeated measures"]'::jsonb, 'One variable across multiple groups', 'One-way ANOVA compares one continuous outcome across three or more groups defined by a single categorical factor. It tests for an overall difference among the group means.'),
('e36076ef-e4a0-5ed4-96f2-984e999ce64b'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Two-way ANOVA assesses:', '["Correlation matrices","Repeated measures only","Effect of one factor","Effect of two factors and their interaction","Non-linear relationships"]'::jsonb, 'Effect of two factors and their interaction', 'Two-way ANOVA examines the simultaneous effect of two independent factors on an outcome and whether they interact. It can detect effect modification between the factors.'),
('b1256854-c7c0-508d-9c15-11c616c30df1'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'The Kruskal-Wallis test is the non-parametric equivalent of:', '["Chi-square test","One-way ANOVA","Paired t-test","McNemar''s test","Two-way ANOVA"]'::jsonb, 'One-way ANOVA', 'The Kruskal-Wallis test is the non-parametric analogue of one-way ANOVA, comparing independent groups using ranks. It does not assume a normal distribution of the data.'),
('ccf46155-e458-52cb-8528-d9bd05dff04f'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'The Mann-Whitney U test is the non-parametric equivalent of:', '["Paired t-test","ANOVA","Chi-square test","Independent t-test","Wilcoxon test"]'::jsonb, 'Independent t-test', 'The Mann-Whitney U test compares the ranks of two independent samples. It is the non-parametric counterpart of the independent-samples t-test.'),
('0d160625-1f44-56c0-8fd3-ff5f1ae674ff'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'The Wilcoxon signed-rank test is the non-parametric equivalent of:', '["Spearman correlation","Independent t-test","Paired t-test","Chi-square","ANOVA"]'::jsonb, 'Paired t-test', 'The Wilcoxon signed-rank test analyzes paired or matched data on an ordinal or non-normal continuous scale. It is the non-parametric counterpart of the paired t-test.'),
('b0f4178a-4d91-5ec1-818e-9c315b267900'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'The chi-square test is used for:', '["Comparing proportions/categorical data","Comparing medians accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Regression mediated by specific receptor activation","Correlation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Comparing means leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Comparing proportions/categorical data', 'The chi-square test compares observed and expected frequencies in categorical data. It tests whether two categorical variables are independent.'),
('23d44f42-8bd4-5370-924b-5ca2f8d801b0'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Fisher''s exact test is preferred over the chi-square test when:', '["Sample is large","Comparing means","Data is continuous","Expected cell frequencies < 5","Data is ordinal"]'::jsonb, 'Expected cell frequencies < 5', 'The chi-square approximation becomes unreliable when expected cell frequencies fall below 5. Fisher''s exact test computes the exact probability and is preferred in this setting.'),
('83d2d93c-90a3-59fc-b808-236cd22e0c7d'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'McNemar''s test is used for:', '["Continuous data analysis","Comparing means","Multiple groups","Paired or matched categorical data","Independent proportions"]'::jsonb, 'Paired or matched categorical data', 'McNemar''s test analyzes paired or matched binary outcomes in a 2x2 table. It compares discordant pairs, such as before-after responses on the same subjects.'),
('d8ef52e7-810f-581e-a2c0-1f8dc961e744'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'The Pearson correlation coefficient ranges from:', '["0 to 100","0 to 1","1 to 10","-1 to +1","-∞ to +∞"]'::jsonb, '-1 to +1', 'Pearson''sr is a standardized measure of linear association bounded between -1 and +1. Values of -1 and +1 indicate perfect negative and perfect positive linear relationships, respectively.'),
('5412e6b0-fe47-5ea5-b591-9195112feeab'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'A Pearson correlation coefficient of 0 indicates:', '["Perfect positive correlation","Strong correlation","Weak correlation","Perfect negative correlation","No linear correlation"]'::jsonb, 'No linear correlation', 'A Pearson coefficient of 0 indicates no linear relationship between the two variables. A non-linear relationship may still be present.'),
('cca6c7e2-bd1d-5a38-8079-afaec759dd7a'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Spearman rank correlation is used for:', '["Monotonic relationships (not necessarily linear)","Linear relationships only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Multiple outcomes mediated by specific receptor activation","Binary variables without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Categorical data only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Monotonic relationships (not necessarily linear)', 'Spearman''s rho is based on ranks and measures monotonic association regardless of linearity. It does not require normally distributed data.'),
('90d13b51-5ad5-51ed-bf98-5ff2178a2aef'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Simple linear regression models the relationship between:', '["Multiple dependent variables","Categorical outcomes","Multiple independent variables","One dependent and one independent variable","Survival data"]'::jsonb, 'One dependent and one independent variable', 'Simple linear regression predicts one continuous outcome from a single continuous predictor. It models a straight-line relationship between the two variables.'),
('4c9a1bae-127e-52a1-8042-c2b52be8f873'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Multiple linear regression includes:', '["Only categorical outcomes","Time-to-event data","One independent variable","Multiple independent variables","Only categorical predictors"]'::jsonb, 'Multiple independent variables', 'Multiple linear regression includes two or more independent variables predicting one continuous outcome. It allows simultaneous adjustment for several predictors.'),
('2db6f847-6d89-52fc-9f5e-3aead26c4bec'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Logistic regression is used when the outcome is:', '["Nominal only","Binary/categorical","Continuous","Ordinal only","Time-to-event"]'::jsonb, 'Binary/categorical', 'Logistic regression models a binary outcome such as the presence versus absence of disease. It estimates the probability of the outcome on a logit scale.'),
('d56e7d4a-e033-58bf-8f06-c93ff7152334'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Logistic regression provides:', '["Relative risks resulting in decreased functional activity","Risk differences accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Regression coefficients and odds ratios","Prevalence without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Incidence rates leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Regression coefficients and odds ratios', 'Logistic regression produces regression coefficients whose exponentiation yields odds ratios. It models the log odds of a binary outcome.'),
('a07df225-d6c4-560e-9f51-6b7208d93bf0'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Cox proportional hazards regression is used for:', '["Time-to-event (survival) data","Cross-sectional data","Correlation","Linear outcomes","Categorical outcomes"]'::jsonb, 'Time-to-event (survival) data', 'Cox proportional hazards regression analyzes time-to-event data while accounting for censoring. It estimates hazard ratios for predictor variables.'),
('1ae7964f-784a-58b0-b620-6f3b7e46dff5'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'The hazard ratio derived from Cox regression is most similar to:', '["Risk difference","Kappa","Prevalence ratio","Odds ratio","Relative risk"]'::jsonb, 'Relative risk', 'The hazard ratio compares the event rate (hazard) per unit time between exposed and unexposed groups. It is interpreted like a relative risk.'),
('4678de92-d9fc-58de-8255-1d7ec3ab8095'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'The Kaplan-Meier method estimates:', '["Incidence rate","Prevalence","Mean survival","Survival probability over time","Odds ratio"]'::jsonb, 'Survival probability over time', 'The Kaplan-Meier method estimates the probability of surviving past each observed event time while accounting for censoring. It produces a step-function survival curve.'),
('8cf4d54b-3cc7-5314-8727-c0539f3587e2'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'The log-rank test compares:', '["Means between groups","Correlation","Sample means","Proportions between groups","Survival distributions between groups"]'::jsonb, 'Survival distributions between groups', 'The log-rank test compares complete survival distributions between two or more groups. It detects differences in the timing of events across the full follow-up period.'),
('9eefdc9b-9a72-5a6b-9e07-7c8933336bf9'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Censoring in survival analysis occurs when:', '["Patient has the outcome resulting in decreased functional activity","Patient dies accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Data is missing mediated by specific receptor activation","Event of interest is not observed for some subjects during follow-up","Study is completed leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Event of interest is not observed for some subjects during follow-up', 'Censoring occurs when the event of interest has not occurred by the last follow-up for a subject. Such subjects still contribute partial information up to the censoring time.'),
('3c8fb888-59be-569e-a28d-a34836c103d6'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Right censoring, the most common type of censoring, occurs when:', '["Missing baseline data resulting in decreased functional activity","Data entry error accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Subject experienced event left of time zero mediated by specific receptor activation","Event occurred before study start without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Subject is lost to follow-up or study ends before event occurs"]'::jsonb, 'Subject is lost to follow-up or study ends before event occurs', 'Right censoring occurs when a subject is lost to follow-up, withdraws, or the study ends before the event takes place. The true event time is later than the censoring time.'),
('1b4af591-5bd4-5ed3-8137-a69157362b83'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Left truncation in survival analysis means that:', '["Missing outcome","Excluded from analysis","Subject was lost early","Event occurred before time zero","Subject entered study after time zero"]'::jsonb, 'Subject entered study after time zero', 'Left truncation occurs when subjects enter observation some time after the study''s time origin. Individuals who experienced the event before entry are never observed.'),
('d280b3b1-208c-5313-a9e3-63ba6176e9cc'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'The proportional hazards assumption in Cox regression means that:', '["Hazards are equal","Risk is equal in groups","Hazard ratio is constant over time","Baseline hazard is zero","The hazard ratio varies linearly with time"]'::jsonb, 'Hazard ratio is constant over time', 'Under proportional hazards, the ratio of hazards between two groups remains constant over the follow-up period. The survival curves of the groups do not cross.'),
('a8b8393c-0e01-56be-87dd-b1c74e40fd6a'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'The number needed to screen is:', '["Number of false positives resulting in decreased functional activity","Number screened to prevent one death or adverse outcome","Number of true positives mediated by specific receptor activation","Number needed to treat without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sample size needed leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Number screened to prevent one death or adverse outcome', 'The number needed to screen is the number of people who must be invited for screening to prevent one death or adverse outcome over a defined period. It combines screening accuracy with the effectiveness of earlier intervention.'),
('dd066b8d-75de-54dc-8ca7-b6739c7d9f34'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'In a screening test, missing a case (failing to detect the disease) represents which type of error?', '["False negative","True positive","False positive","True negative","Type I error"]'::jsonb, 'False negative', 'A false negative means the test misses a case, delaying diagnosis and treatment. Such errors are generally considered more harmful than false positives in screening programs.'),
('95e9baaf-dfd1-51d3-805e-037cb03fde6b'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'What happens when you lower the threshold for a positive test?', '["Sensitivity decreases","Sensitivity increases","Specificity increases","Specificity is unchanged","Both sensitivity and specificity increase"]'::jsonb, 'Sensitivity increases', 'Lowering the threshold for a positive test captures more diseased subjects, so sensitivity rises. The trade-off is a drop in specificity and more false positives.'),
('64bb79e3-39e6-5743-a58e-eb97d35376a3'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Raising the cut-off for a positive test leads to:', '["Both sensitivity and specificity increase","Higher sensitivity","Lower sensitivity","Lower specificity","Higher specificity"]'::jsonb, 'Higher specificity', 'Raising the cut-off means the test is positive only at more extreme values, so fewer healthy subjects are misclassified. Specificity increases while sensitivity decreases.'),
('a76a75ab-1d99-53f6-a3e1-8cbd5f3d9a4c'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Youden''s index is calculated as:', '["Sensitivity + Specificity - 1","Sensitivity ÷ Specificity","Sensitivity × (1 - Specificity)","Sensitivity × Specificity","Specificity ÷ (1 - Sensitivity)"]'::jsonb, 'Sensitivity + Specificity - 1', 'Youden''s index equals sensitivity plus specificity minus one. It ranges from 0 (no discriminating ability) to 1 (perfect test) and is used to select an optimal cut-off.'),
('88f94cd4-7dc2-5ba5-9c38-42b1c00b70b3'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'An ROC curve is constructed by plotting:', '["Prevalence vs 1-Prevalence resulting in decreased functional activity","Sensitivity vs 1-Specificity at various cut-offs","PPV vs NPV mediated by specific receptor activation","TP vs FP without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sensitivity vs Specificity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sensitivity vs 1-Specificity at various cut-offs', 'An ROC curve plots sensitivity (true-positive rate) against 1-specificity (false-positive rate) across all possible thresholds. It summarizes the trade-off between sensitivity and specificity.'),
('3726a6c1-a487-5d8d-beed-9eebca959481'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'The best cut-off value on an ROC curve is closest to the:', '["Bottom-left corner","Top-right corner","Top-left corner","Center","Bottom-right corner"]'::jsonb, 'Top-left corner', 'The optimal threshold is the point closest to the top-left corner, where both sensitivity and specificity are maximized. The area under the curve summarizes overall diagnostic accuracy.'),
('8c9ba25b-3633-5c7c-946c-7a2ee4ee4871'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Pre-test probability of disease is best estimated by the:', '["NPV","PPV","Prevalence","Sensitivity","Specificity"]'::jsonb, 'Prevalence', 'Pre-test probability is the probability of disease before testing, best estimated by the disease prevalence in the relevant population.'),
('bdede9aa-04f3-53ab-a2b9-83dfe213d87b'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'The post-test probability after a positive test equals the:', '["Prevalence","NPV","PPV","Sensitivity","1 - Specificity"]'::jsonb, 'PPV', 'Among those who test positive, the post-test probability of disease is the positive predictive value. The PPV depends on sensitivity, specificity, and pre-test probability.'),
('3bd204d2-e0e3-558e-903c-535537d4c5d7'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Bayes theorem combines:', '["Multiple outcomes resulting in decreased functional activity","Sample sizes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sensitivity with specificity to get accuracy mediated by specific receptor activation","Two different tests without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pre-test probability with likelihood ratios to get post-test probability"]'::jsonb, 'Pre-test probability with likelihood ratios to get post-test probability', 'Bayes theorem updates the pre-test probability of disease using the likelihood ratio of the test result. It yields the post-test probability.'),
('18ccf7b3-6cfb-5735-8909-ebe30513a184'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'The Fagan nomogram is used for:', '["Comparing two treatments resulting in decreased functional activity","Determining post-test probability using pre-test probability and LR","Calculating sample size mediated by specific receptor activation","Assessing heterogeneity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Measuring kappa leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Determining post-test probability using pre-test probability and LR', 'The Fagan nomogram graphs post-test probability from the pre-test probability and the likelihood ratio. It provides a quick visual estimate without calculation.'),
('83196ea6-a762-5fc9-8d61-9c7d1f7a8874'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'If the pre-test probability is 20% and the positive likelihood ratio (LR+) is 10, the post-test probability is approximately:', '["25%","50%","70%","90%","95%"]'::jsonb, '70%', 'Pre-test odds are 0.20/0.80 = 0.25; multiplying by an LR+ of 10 gives post-test odds of 2.5. Converting to probability: 2.5/(1 + 2.5) = 0.714, approximately 70%.'),
('6bfcfde6-b5bc-50fd-9a02-71a449f7fc88'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Performing many statistical tests (multiple testing) increases the risk of:', '["Selection bias","Type I error","Confounding","Measurement error","Type II error"]'::jsonb, 'Type I error', 'Each statistical test carries a probability of a false-positive conclusion (type I error). Performing many tests inflates the experiment-wise type I error unless multiplicity correction is applied.'),
('89917a03-a8e5-5eff-872e-f96b91537242'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'The Bonferroni correction is used to adjust:', '["P-value by multiplying by number of comparisons resulting in decreased functional activity","P-value threshold by dividing alpha by number of comparisons","Confidence interval width mediated by specific receptor activation","Effect size without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sample size leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'P-value threshold by dividing alpha by number of comparisons', 'The Bonferroni correction controls the family-wise error rate by dividing the alpha level by the number of comparisons, lowering the P-value threshold required for significance.'),
('4ab69077-0b4b-5406-87d3-10814a83b0f6'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Interim analysis in clinical trials may require:', '["Stopping rules and alpha spending function","Longer follow-up accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Blinding mediated by specific receptor activation","Placebo group without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Increasing sample size leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Stopping rules and alpha spending function', 'Repeated interim looks at accumulating data inflate the overall type I error. Pre-specified stopping rules and an alpha spending function apportion the alpha across interim and final analyses to control this error.'),
('28837076-166e-5419-ae24-4b37131195b8'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'The Data Safety Monitoring Board (DSMB) is responsible for:', '["Patient recruitment","Writing the paper","Monitoring safety and interim results","Randomization","Data analysis"]'::jsonb, 'Monitoring safety and interim results', 'The DSMB independently monitors accumulating safety data and interim efficacy results during a trial, and can recommend stopping, modifying, or continuing the study.'),
('b648de89-ed86-5908-aa3c-ef0d1bc420ff'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Per-protocol analysis includes:', '["Only controls","Subjects with missing data","Only the treatment group","Only those who completed the protocol as planned","All randomized subjects"]'::jsonb, 'Only those who completed the protocol as planned', 'Per-protocol analysis restricts the sample to participants who adhered to the protocol as planned, excluding those with major protocol violations.'),
('577a1494-6023-525e-a95d-e13077bb8c00'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Per-protocol analysis can introduce bias because:', '["It preserves randomization resulting in decreased functional activity","It reduces sample size accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","It uses ITT approach mediated by specific receptor activation","It includes all subjects without affecting the primary tissue pathway","It excludes non-compliers who may differ from compliers"]'::jsonb, 'It excludes non-compliers who may differ from compliers', 'Excluding non-compliers removes the protection of randomization, and non-compliers may systematically differ from compliers in prognosis, introducing selection bias.'),
('495a86fe-1fa2-5223-abf0-0e3b09318f6f'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Subgroup analyses should be:', '["The primary analysis","Ignored","Performed regardless of planning","Pre-specified and interpreted cautiously","Used to replace main analysis"]'::jsonb, 'Pre-specified and interpreted cautiously', 'Subgroup analyses are prone to chance findings and false positives, so they should be pre-specified and interpreted cautiously, with the main analysis carrying the trial''s conclusion.'),
('dbbd5d9c-095d-5079-ae78-2864ddfb6778'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Effect modification (interaction) occurs when:', '["The third variable is a mediator","The effect of exposure differs across levels of a third variable","The third variable is a collider","The third variable is a confounder","The variable is continuous"]'::jsonb, 'The effect of exposure differs across levels of a third variable', 'Effect modification occurs when the association between exposure and outcome differs across levels of a third variable, so strata-specific estimates are reported separately.'),
('0ec479bf-aee3-5c72-b604-56bef4903f3c'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Interaction on an additive scale means:', '["RR is constant resulting in decreased functional activity","OR is constant accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Risk difference is not constant across strata","Risk ratio is constant without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Risk difference is not constant across strata', 'On the additive scale, interaction means the risk difference is not constant across strata, so the combined effect differs from the sum of the individual effects.'),
('515eac79-3437-5b27-a4c4-375de9f3d01d'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Interaction on a multiplicative scale means:', '["Risk difference is constant","RR varies across strata","RR is constant across strata","OR varies across strata","Synergy only"]'::jsonb, 'RR varies across strata', 'Multiplicative interaction means the relative risk (or odds ratio) varies across strata, so the effect measure is not homogeneous across levels of the third variable.'),
('e7f8f36e-2a23-5ceb-adac-a9cfcb6e9541'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Confounding differs from effect modification because confounding:', '["Does not affect associations","Is beneficial","Is a bias to be controlled","Increases effect size","Is a natural phenomenon to be described"]'::jsonb, 'Is a bias to be controlled', 'Confounding is a distortion of the exposure-disease association caused by an extraneous variable; it is a bias to be controlled in design or analysis, whereas effect modification is a real effect to be reported.'),
('2e733160-259a-54cd-be20-feefeb0ef18b'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'The gold standard for diagnosis is:', '["Predictive value","LR+","Screening test","Reference standard","New diagnostic test"]'::jsonb, 'Reference standard', 'The reference (criterion) standard is the best available test used to establish the true presence or absence of disease, against which new diagnostic tests are compared.'),
('5a8ddcd0-d58b-5d36-aadb-2bd2fdfb3683'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Diagnostic accuracy studies compare:', '["Two treatments resulting in decreased functional activity","Two screening tests accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","New test results against the reference standard","Prevalence rates without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Two populations leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'New test results against the reference standard', 'Diagnostic accuracy studies compare the results of a new test against the reference (gold) standard to measure sensitivity, specificity, and predictive values.'),
('2bf99bb8-09dc-514a-ad61-dbaea36150d0'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'The STARD statement provides guidelines for:', '["Observational studies","Reporting RCTs","Animal studies","Reporting diagnostic accuracy studies","Systematic reviews"]'::jsonb, 'Reporting diagnostic accuracy studies', 'STARD (Standards for Reporting Diagnostic Accuracy Studies) provides a checklist for complete and transparent reporting of diagnostic accuracy studies.'),
('7af04631-cff7-5912-b0b8-a8b17d6cc506'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'The CONSORT statement provides guidelines for:', '["Economic evaluations","Case reports","Observational studies","Diagnostic studies","Reporting RCTs"]'::jsonb, 'Reporting RCTs', 'CONSORT (Consolidated Standards of Reporting Trials) provides guidelines for transparent reporting of randomized controlled trials.'),
('03a5000a-b54b-5529-9af3-830fe717154d'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'The PRISMA statement provides guidelines for:', '["Systematic reviews and meta-analyses","Diagnostic studies","Observational studies","RCTs","Qualitative research"]'::jsonb, 'Systematic reviews and meta-analyses', 'PRISMA provides guidelines for reporting systematic reviews and meta-analyses, including search strategies and study selection.'),
('377f2334-2375-5f1c-bbb7-1767c723dd12'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'The STROBE statement provides guidelines for:', '["Diagnostic studies resulting in decreased functional activity","Qualitative research accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","RCTs mediated by specific receptor activation","Observational studies in epidemiology","Case reports leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Observational studies in epidemiology', 'STROBE (Strengthening the Reporting of Observational Studies in Epidemiology) provides reporting guidance for cohort, case-control, and cross-sectional studies.'),
('73a660a6-7d28-5537-ae8d-711fc82eb660'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Intra-rater reliability refers to:', '["Specificity resulting in decreased functional activity","Sensitivity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Consistency of same rater over repeated measurements","Agreement between different raters without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Agreement beyond chance leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Consistency of same rater over repeated measurements', 'Intra-rater reliability is the degree of agreement when the same rater measures or rates the same subjects on repeated occasions.'),
('a9f257c8-5846-5c74-b89d-b8804b104b99'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Inter-rater reliability refers to:', '["Consistency of same rater","Validity","Sensitivity","Agreement between different raters","Specificity"]'::jsonb, 'Agreement between different raters', 'Inter-rater reliability is the degree of agreement between two or more different raters measuring or rating the same subjects.'),
('aadfc813-2b5b-5440-8c6d-22c686de639d'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Test-retest reliability assesses:', '["Criterion validity","Consistency of a measure over time","Agreement between raters","Internal consistency","Split-half reliability"]'::jsonb, 'Consistency of a measure over time', 'Test-retest reliability assesses the stability of a measure by repeating it on the same subjects at two time points and correlating the results.'),
('e40e2c52-86f6-5bb8-8651-9bb94de69994'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Cronbach''s alpha measures:', '["Inter-rater reliability","Internal consistency (of items in a scale)","Test-retest reliability","Criterion validity","Content validity"]'::jsonb, 'Internal consistency (of items in a scale)', 'Cronbach''s alpha measures internal consistency, that is, how closely related a set of items are as a group within a scale.'),
('ea43845a-778c-5947-a9ba-a5ed1cd9477e'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'A Cronbach''s alpha greater than 0.7 generally indicates:', '["Excellent reliability","Poor internal consistency","Low validity","Acceptable internal consistency","Poor test-retest"]'::jsonb, 'Acceptable internal consistency', 'A Cronbach''s alpha above 0.7 is conventionally regarded as indicating acceptable internal consistency of a scale.'),
('796e3538-beb9-5ad2-9796-8ac36e351d8e'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Content validity refers to:', '["Measure appears valid","Measure correlates with gold standard","Measure is stable","Measure predicts future outcome","Measure covers all aspects of the concept"]'::jsonb, 'Measure covers all aspects of the concept', 'Content validity indicates the extent to which a measure adequately covers all relevant aspects of the concept it intends to measure.'),
('eb5060c1-62fc-53f6-92f1-e01aca31a06c'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Criterion validity is assessed by:', '["Expert opinion resulting in decreased functional activity","Factor analysis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Test-retest mediated by specific receptor activation","Comparing measure to a gold standard","Cronbach''s alpha leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Comparing measure to a gold standard', 'Criterion validity is assessed by comparing the measure against a gold standard or established criterion, with high correlation indicating concurrent or predictive validity.'),
('1c4faa60-ddc2-5556-9196-f958068ca8d7'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Construct validity refers to:', '["Comparison with gold standard resulting in decreased functional activity","Content coverage accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Stability over time mediated by specific receptor activation","Whether the measure correlates with theoretical constructs","Inter-rater agreement leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Whether the measure correlates with theoretical constructs', 'Construct validity concerns whether a measure relates to other variables and theoretical constructs in a manner consistent with the underlying theory.'),
('499dd34a-a43e-5e95-ae75-7bf67bec0266'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Face validity means:', '["Measure correlates with gold standard","Measure is reliable","Measure internal consistency","Measure appears to measure what it intends","Measure covers all concepts"]'::jsonb, 'Measure appears to measure what it intends', 'Face validity is a subjective judgment of whether the items of a measure appear, on their face, to measure what they are intended to measure.'),
('3e31b3a0-5c32-545d-ab89-951d969793e6'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Which of the following is the strongest predictor of a positive screening test result?', '["Low specificity","High sensitivity","Low disease prevalence","High test specificity","High disease prevalence"]'::jsonb, 'High disease prevalence', 'Positive predictive value rises with increasing disease prevalence, so a high prevalence is the strongest predictor that a positive screening result is a true positive.'),
('302bdf49-cc4a-5f9c-a6fb-31cba3033e81'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Attributable risk percent (AR%) is calculated as:', '["(Ie - Iu)/Ie × 100","(Ie - Iu)/Iu × 100","RR - 1 × 100","Iu/Ie × 100","Ie/Iu × 100"]'::jsonb, '(Ie - Iu)/Ie × 100', 'Attributable risk percent is the proportion of disease in exposed individuals attributable to the exposure, computed as (Ie - Iu)/Ie × 100.'),
('b5378d44-cdac-5e5e-83f3-722361b167a7'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'The etiologic fraction (attributable fraction) is another term for:', '["Prevalence ratio","Relative risk","Attributable risk in exposed","Odds ratio","Standardized mortality ratio"]'::jsonb, 'Attributable risk in exposed', 'The etiologic or attributable fraction among the exposed is the proportion of cases among exposed persons caused by the exposure, equivalent to the attributable risk in the exposed expressed as a fraction.'),
('3122c680-3f92-52f0-9194-f46808518545'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'The population etiologic fraction is high when:', '["Risk difference is low","Exposure is common and RR is moderate","Attributable risk is zero","Exposure is rare and RR is high","Prevalence is low"]'::jsonb, 'Exposure is common and RR is moderate', 'The population etiologic (attributable) fraction depends on both the prevalence of the exposure and the strength of its association; it is high when the exposure is common even if the relative risk is only moderate.'),
('e2490a64-1deb-5a7f-8cfa-789ae177588c'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Which of the following is a probability sampling method?', '["Purposive sampling","Snowball sampling","Convenience sampling","Simple random sampling","Quota sampling"]'::jsonb, 'Simple random sampling', 'Simple random sampling gives every member of the population a known and equal probability of selection, making it a probability sampling method.'),
('7c89b04a-2f01-5142-8d67-0a79b812160d'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Quota sampling is a type of:', '["Cluster sampling","Stratified random sampling","Simple random sampling","Probability sampling","Non-probability sampling"]'::jsonb, 'Non-probability sampling', 'Quota sampling selects participants non-randomly to match population proportions on chosen characteristics, so it is a non-probability sampling method.'),
('8793a3bf-669e-5a6f-8bff-1eb4e12ba7d4'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Purposive sampling is also known as:', '["Judgmental sampling","Systematic sampling","Random sampling","Probability sampling","Stratified sampling"]'::jsonb, 'Judgmental sampling', 'Purposive sampling selects participants based on the researcher''s judgment of who is most informative, hence it is also called judgmental sampling.'),
('060533ef-7167-5531-98fc-543d47e0d501'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Which sampling method divides the population into strata and randomly samples within each?', '["Systematic","Simple random","Cluster","Snowball","Stratified random"]'::jsonb, 'Stratified random', 'Stratified random sampling divides the population into strata and draws a random sample from within each stratum.'),
('738b89cc-2c96-5bea-89cf-a57d56777543'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Cluster sampling is:', '["Selecting subjects conveniently","Selecting volunteers","Selecting based on quotas","Selecting every kth subject","Selecting groups rather than individuals randomly"]'::jsonb, 'Selecting groups rather than individuals randomly', 'Cluster sampling randomly selects groups (clusters) and then measures all or a random subset of individuals within the selected clusters.'),
('818b10e9-b4c1-5a98-80fa-2e2f1e191b1d'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'The design effect in cluster sampling accounts for:', '["Confounding","Smaller sample size","Loss of efficiency due to clustering","Increased precision","Selection bias"]'::jsonb, 'Loss of efficiency due to clustering', 'The design effect quantifies the loss of statistical efficiency (increase in variance) that results from sampling clusters rather than independent individuals.'),
('b7d3190a-9842-58b7-acf7-f1d636721fd1'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'The sample size needed for a study depends on all of the following EXCEPT:', '["Bias of measurement","Expected effect size","Desired power","Variability of outcome","Alpha level"]'::jsonb, 'Bias of measurement', 'Sample size is determined by the expected effect size, power, alpha level, and variability of the outcome; measurement bias is a validity issue and is not used in the sample size formula.'),
('f3b1be03-7347-503d-9fe1-bfc945210bf1'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'A larger sample size is needed when:', '["Power is low","Variability is small","Alpha is high","Effect size is small","Effect size is large"]'::jsonb, 'Effect size is small', 'Detecting a small effect requires a larger sample, because smaller effect sizes produce smaller test statistics and lower power at a fixed sample size.'),
('c6176d64-92e2-5f96-a98e-5978ac74331d'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'What is the relationship between power and sample size?', '["No relationship","Sample size depends only on effect size","Inverse (larger n = lower power)","Direct (larger n = higher power)","Power depends only on alpha"]'::jsonb, 'Direct (larger n = higher power)', 'Power and sample size are directly related: increasing the sample size increases the power of a study to detect a given effect.'),
('68be0098-15a3-5695-9811-1c159dba61b9'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Meta-analysis is a:', '["Narrative review resulting in decreased functional activity","Systematic review without pooling accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Primary study mediated by specific receptor activation","Qualitative research without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Systematic review with statistical pooling of results"]'::jsonb, 'Systematic review with statistical pooling of results', 'Meta-analysis is a systematic review that quantitatively pools the results of included studies using statistical methods to produce a combined estimate.'),
('0a9cce04-82ef-5fe7-86a9-4bcda0b657c2'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Methods for detecting publication bias include:', '["All of the above","Begg''s test","Funnel plot","Trim and fill","Egger''s test"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Funnel plots, Egger''s and Begg''s tests, and trim-and-fill procedures are all methods used to detect and assess publication bias in a meta-analysis.'),
('7194c759-6b96-52ef-a85d-699c2dec4226'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Sensitivity analysis in a meta-analysis may involve:', '["Removing one study at a time and re-analyzing","Changing the effect measure","Including/excluding specific studies","Using different statistical models","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Sensitivity analysis tests the robustness of a meta-analysis by repeating the analysis under different assumptions, such as removing one study at a time, changing the effect measure, or using different statistical models.'),
('c1f21613-893e-57ff-bb30-017ee30fc0c8'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'A narrative (traditional) review differs from a systematic review because it:', '["Uses statistical pooling","Has transparent methods","Pre-registered","Is comprehensive","May be subjective and prone to bias"]'::jsonb, 'May be subjective and prone to bias', 'A narrative review lacks a systematic, reproducible search strategy and may be subjective and prone to selection bias, unlike a systematic review.'),
('7f968420-0450-517f-8673-d8727b0a6236'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'A research question in the PICO format includes all of the following EXCEPT:', '["Comparison","Outcome","Population","Intervention","Cost"]'::jsonb, 'Cost', 'PICO includes Population, Intervention, Comparison, and Outcome; cost is not one of the PICO elements.'),
('cbf04a64-a7e9-5992-997b-8f4c9f4b0abf'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'PICOT includes which additional element?', '["Cost","Design","Setting","Treatment","Time"]'::jsonb, 'Time', 'PICOT adds a Time (timeframe) element to the standard PICO components.'),
('462fd5ee-901e-52d1-9ac3-edacb99f5576'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'Evidence-based medicine integrates:', '["Research evidence","Best available evidence","Clinical expertise","All of the above","Patient values"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Evidence-based medicine integrates best available research evidence with clinical expertise and patient values and preferences.'),
('f6f0a590-1ada-504f-b26d-0d10723e53a6'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Epidemiology - Epidemiology', 'In a survey of 20,000 individuals, the prevalence of a disease is 2%. Prevalence is best defined as:', '["Number of cases/Person-time","Number of new cases/Total population at risk","Number of deaths/Total population","Number of healthy people/Total population","Number of existing cases/Total population"]'::jsonb, 'Number of existing cases/Total population', 'Prevalence measures existing disease burden by dividing the number of existing cases at a point or period by the total population at risk; the other options describe incidence or mortality.'),
('41e2c10a-a783-5c06-8eed-8bfc24032b0e'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'What type of data is blood group (A/B/AB/O)?', '["Ordinal","Nominal","Continuous","Ratio","Interval"]'::jsonb, 'Nominal', 'Blood groups (A, B, AB, O) are categories with no natural order or ranking among them, which defines nominal data.'),
('6c95d604-1894-57c0-8c04-421d943b88f7'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'What type of data is educational level (illiterate/primary/secondary/graduate)?', '["Interval","Ordinal","Continuous","Ratio","Nominal"]'::jsonb, 'Ordinal', 'Educational levels have a natural order but unequal intervals between categories, which defines ordinal data.'),
('80b28bb9-1c14-5da0-bb78-6a1e186dbac6'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'What type of data is temperature in Celsius?', '["Ordinal","Interval","Ratio","Continuous","Nominal"]'::jsonb, 'Interval', 'Celsius temperature has equal intervals between units but an arbitrary zero point (0 to C does not mean absence of temperature), which defines interval data.'),
('9076b267-9928-5c94-b2a8-2a1c08cce2c3'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'What type of data is height in centimeters?', '["Ratio","Continuous","Ordinal","Interval","Nominal"]'::jsonb, 'Ratio', 'Height in centimeters has equal intervals and a true zero point (0 cm means no height), so ratios are meaningful; this defines ratio data.'),
('71ebf27d-e89e-54bb-8d39-5d8f7e88812c'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'What type of data is a pain score on a VAS 0-10?', '["Ordinal","Ratio","Interval","Continuous","Nominal"]'::jsonb, 'Ordinal', 'A VAS 0-10 pain score ranks pain severity, but the intervals between scores are not equal, so it is ordinal data.'),
('85e2c41e-ea13-5982-bd92-0234cc6f7a2a'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'What type of data is the number of children in a family?', '["Discrete","Ratio","Nominal","Interval","Ordinal"]'::jsonb, 'Ratio', 'The number of children is a count with a true zero (no children), allowing meaningful ratios, so it is ratio data even though it is discrete.'),
('6c5a97d2-18cb-53c9-b253-dbb9af1c691c'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Which measure of central tendency is most appropriate for skewed data?', '["Mode","Variance","Mean","Median","Range"]'::jsonb, 'Median', 'The median is resistant to extreme values and therefore represents the center of skewed distributions better than the mean.'),
('552ba8c8-6897-59e3-867d-d3cc39ff46a2'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The mean is most appropriate for which type of data?', '["Skewed data resulting in decreased functional activity","Normally distributed interval/ratio","Binary data mediated by specific receptor activation","Nominal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ordinal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Normally distributed interval/ratio', 'The mean uses the actual numerical values and is a valid measure of central tendency when data are normally distributed and measured on an interval or ratio scale.'),
('d3d663ae-b3a2-53a1-bf02-5af2453bd768'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The mode is the only measure of central tendency that can be used for:', '["Interval data","Ratio data","Nominal data","Continuous data","Ordinal data"]'::jsonb, 'Nominal data', 'The mode is the category that occurs most frequently and is the only measure of central tendency applicable to nominal (categorical) data.'),
('05c09749-2caa-5fa7-a41b-f7f3964ba869'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'In a positively (right) skewed distribution, the relationship between the mean, median, and mode is:', '["Mean < Median < Mode","Mode < Median < Mean","Median < Mean < Mode","Mode < Mean < Median","Mean = Median = Mode"]'::jsonb, 'Mode < Median < Mean', 'In a positively skewed distribution the tail extends to the right, pulling the mean upward, so the order is mode < median < mean.'),
('fcf50a76-b8b4-5512-a949-47086ac53eab'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'In a negatively (left) skewed distribution:', '["Mode < Median < Mean","Median < Mode < Mean","Mode < Mean < Median","Mean < Median < Mode","Mean = Median = Mode"]'::jsonb, 'Mean < Median < Mode', 'In a negatively skewed distribution the tail extends to the left, pulling the mean downward, so the order is mean < median < mode.'),
('c1facb82-4b11-5015-a2a5-8b3810bfcbf0'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'In a normal distribution the mean, median, and mode are:', '["Median > Mode","Mean > Median","Equal","Mode > Mean","Different"]'::jsonb, 'Equal', 'A normal distribution is symmetric and unimodal, so the mean, median, and mode coincide at the center.'),
('66322249-14c4-507c-b214-648d9516c7b9'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The interquartile range (IQR) contains what percentage of the data?', '["50%","99%","75%","95%","68%"]'::jsonb, '50%', 'The interquartile range spans from the 25th to the 75th percentile, containing the middle 50% of observations.'),
('79314066-6199-5684-8dce-6075a21bc8cb'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The range is calculated as:', '["Standard deviation squared","Variance","Maximum - Minimum","Mean - Median","Q3 - Q1"]'::jsonb, 'Maximum - Minimum', 'The range is the simplest measure of dispersion, calculated as the largest value minus the smallest value.'),
('466887d6-e35b-50cc-8a33-0a57962f6a39'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Variance is defined as:', '["Sum of deviations from mean","Average of squared deviations from mean","Average of absolute deviations from mean","Square root of standard deviation","Mean of the data"]'::jsonb, 'Average of squared deviations from mean', 'Variance quantifies spread by averaging the squared deviations of each value from the mean.'),
('8c2d65c1-5335-5a59-8140-e82c825217fd'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Standard deviation is:', '["Square of variance","Average deviation","Square root of variance","Median deviation","Mean deviation"]'::jsonb, 'Square root of variance', 'The standard deviation is the positive square root of the variance, expressing dispersion in the original units of the data.'),
('69267c34-e8aa-504a-a44e-0011849eff7c'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Standard error of the mean (SEM) is:', '["SD/n","√SD/n","SD/√n","SD × √n","√(SD/n)"]'::jsonb, 'SD/√n', 'The standard error of the mean estimates how far the sample mean is likely to be from the population mean and is SD divided by the square root of the sample size.'),
('85f2cafc-8c13-5d7c-9845-2ab426b5b693'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'As the sample size increases, the standard error of the mean (SEM):', '["Decreases","Increases","Remains constant","Fluctuates randomly","Becomes equal to SD"]'::jsonb, 'Decreases', 'Because SEM = SD/√n, increasing the sample size reduces the standard error, making the sample mean a more precise estimate.'),
('bcb6b0bf-fc1f-541e-8bfa-154fd5e1aa91'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The coefficient of variation (CV) is calculated as:', '["(Range/Mean) × 100%","(SD/Mean) × 100%","(Mean/SD) × 100%","(Variance/Mean) × 100%","(SD/√n) × 100%"]'::jsonb, '(SD/Mean) × 100%', 'The coefficient of variation standardizes dispersion relative to the mean and is expressed as (SD/Mean) × 100%.'),
('5e09d465-081f-53ac-80ff-c223907659d9'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The coefficient of variation is useful for:', '["Assessing skewness resulting in decreased functional activity","Comparing means accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Calculating sample size mediated by specific receptor activation","Measuring central tendency without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Comparing variability between datasets with different units"]'::jsonb, 'Comparing variability between datasets with different units', 'The coefficient of variation is a relative measure of dispersion, so it allows comparison of variability across datasets that have different units or means.'),
('ea4db662-e279-57a3-a6c5-037aad2d61fe'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'A researcher measures cholesterol in a large population and the data follow a normal distribution. The normal distribution is characterized by:', '["Mean = Median = Mode","Asymptotic tails","68% within ±1 SD","95% within ±2 SD","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'The normal distribution is symmetric with mean = median = mode, asymptotic tails, and fixed percentages of values within each standard deviation; all listed properties apply.'),
('a77fe981-b67c-55d8-bc63-7fe170561713'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Approximately what percentage of values lie within ±2 SD of the mean in a normal distribution?', '["68%","50%","95%","75%","99%"]'::jsonb, '95%', 'In a normal distribution approximately 95% of observations fall within two standard deviations (±2 SD) of the mean.'),
('9786fbb5-e3ab-57ee-9ebd-7c6a6db78d2c'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Approximately what percentage of values lies within ±3 SD of the mean in a normal distribution?', '["75%","99.7%","68%","95%","99%"]'::jsonb, '99.7%', 'In a normal distribution approximately 99.7% of observations fall within three standard deviations (±3 SD) of the mean.'),
('b27217a1-e72c-53b7-aa2e-84e83b7a027c'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The Z-score (standard score) is calculated as:', '["(x - μ)/√σ","(x - μ)/σ²","(μ - x)/σ","(x - x̄)/SEM","(x - μ)/σ"]'::jsonb, '(x - μ)/σ', 'The Z-score expresses a value as the number of standard deviations it lies from the mean and is (x − μ)/σ.'),
('618a20ef-1a72-5c7d-b550-f9e6e22be04b'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'AZ-score of +1.96 corresponds to what percentile in a standard normal distribution?', '["99th","75th","50th","95th","97.5th"]'::jsonb, '97.5th', 'AZ-score of +1.96 cuts off the upper 2.5% of the standard normal distribution, so it marks the 97.5th percentile.'),
('cca8ae10-3526-5b77-b629-892160d47217'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'What Z-score corresponds to the 95th percentile?', '["0.67","1.96","1.65","2.58","1.28"]'::jsonb, '1.65', 'AZ-score of +1.65 leaves 5% in the upper tail of the standard normal distribution and therefore corresponds to the 95th percentile.'),
('d69c741f-9a34-5781-9357-30b62ca2ea38'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'What Z-score corresponds to the 99th percentile?', '["2.33","1.96","2.58","3.00","1.65"]'::jsonb, '2.33', 'AZ-score of +2.33 leaves 1% in the upper tail of the standard normal distribution and therefore corresponds to the 99th percentile.'),
('5d619750-6fb4-51cc-bce1-eaef908831d9'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'What percentage of the normal distribution lies below Z = 0?', '["95%","75%","68%","50%","25%"]'::jsonb, '50%', 'The standard normal distribution is symmetric around Z = 0, placing exactly 50% of the values below the mean.'),
('60c55036-2052-5fe5-8953-e91978d0cb18'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Skewness measures:', '["Asymmetry of distribution","Central tendency","Peakedness of distribution","Spread of distribution","Tail thickness"]'::jsonb, 'Asymmetry of distribution', 'Skewness quantifies the asymmetry of a distribution, describing whether the data trail off more to one side.'),
('d8242196-0cdc-585d-9a55-1b41518c48a2'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Positive skewness indicates:', '["Flat distribution","Long tail on the right","Long tail on the left","Symmetric distribution","Peaked distribution"]'::jsonb, 'Long tail on the right', 'Positive skewness produces a long tail on the right side of the distribution, with the mean pulled toward that tail.'),
('98f32845-18b8-519a-bb6e-2a936b0d2265'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Negative skewness indicates:', '["Long tail on the left","Long tail on the right","Symmetric distribution","Flat distribution","Bimodal distribution"]'::jsonb, 'Long tail on the left', 'Negative skewness produces a long tail on the left side of the distribution, with the mean pulled toward that tail.'),
('3eb2d753-0d84-53ac-8d70-0b9298adac89'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Kurtosis measures:', '["Asymmetry resulting in decreased functional activity","Variability accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Spread mediated by specific receptor activation","Peakedness/flatness of a distribution","Central tendency leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Peakedness/flatness of a distribution', 'Kurtosis describes the peakedness or flatness of a distribution relative to a normal distribution.'),
('654e1d24-18df-5cf2-be2e-97bbcd2e5ee0'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'A leptokurtic distribution has:', '["Zero peakedness","Low peakedness (thin tails)","High peakedness (fat tails)","Normal peakedness","Negative peakedness"]'::jsonb, 'High peakedness (fat tails)', 'A leptokurtic distribution has a sharp central peak and heavier (fat) tails compared with the normal distribution.'),
('9771b7ba-25c2-5af4-bd92-d5cf4304f113'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'A platykurtic distribution has:', '["High peakedness","Normal peakedness","Low peakedness (flat)","Zero peakedness","Negative kurtosis"]'::jsonb, 'Low peakedness (flat)', 'A platykurtic distribution is flatter, with a lower central peak and thinner tails than the normal distribution.'),
('80c5c670-c221-55bb-8a9a-bd6c1aa20d1b'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'A mesokurtic distribution refers to:', '["Normal kurtosis (like normal distribution)","Low kurtosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","High kurtosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Negative kurtosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Zero kurtosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Normal kurtosis (like normal distribution)', 'A mesokurtic distribution has moderate kurtosis equivalent to that of the standard normal distribution.'),
('b28695de-f536-5301-8025-701f1680452d'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'A point estimate provides:', '["The standard error","A range of values for a parameter","A single value as best estimate of a population parameter","The probability of the estimate","The confidence interval"]'::jsonb, 'A single value as best estimate of a population parameter', 'A point estimate uses the sample statistic, such as the sample mean, as a single numerical best estimate of the population parameter.'),
('6ca3b063-6b0d-57e2-877c-3d74bbd302e1'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'A confidence interval provides:', '["The standard deviation resulting in decreased functional activity","A single value estimate accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The exact population parameter mediated by specific receptor activation","The p-value without affecting the primary tissue pathway","An interval estimate with a specified level of confidence"]'::jsonb, 'An interval estimate with a specified level of confidence', 'A confidence interval provides a range of plausible values for the population parameter together with a specified confidence level.'),
('97257a2a-8981-5869-a36e-e903240f792b'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The width of a confidence interval depends on:', '["All of the above","Only sample size","Sample size","Confidence level","Variability"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Confidence interval width is determined jointly by the sample size, the confidence level, and the variability of the data, so all of the above factors apply.'),
('b29cdd97-edb7-5181-8aad-e554e9aaf1de'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'If the sample size increases, the width of a confidence interval:', '["Increases","Fluctuates","Decreases","Remains the same","Becomes zero"]'::jsonb, 'Decreases', 'Because the standard error decreases with larger samples, the width of the confidence interval narrows as the sample size increases.'),
('29d4d5f5-2d55-5f07-bc46-27715d660bb9'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'What happens to statistical power as the sample size increases?', '["Increases","Decreases","Stays the same","Becomes zero","Doubles automatically"]'::jsonb, 'Increases', 'Statistical power, the ability to detect a true effect when one exists, rises as the sample size increases.'),
('c58a3a47-1f7a-5fb7-ab8b-43797e6b249f'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The probability of an event ranges between:', '["0 and 100","-∞ and +∞","0 and 10","-1 and 1","0 and 1"]'::jsonb, '0 and 1', 'Probability is expressed on a scale from 0 (impossible) to 1 (certain), inclusive.'),
('b4cd7b91-b37b-50f8-98d8-28b71990407a'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'If two events A and B are independent, P(A and B) =', '["P(A) × P(B)","P(A) / P(B)","P(A) + P(B)","P(A) + P(B) - P(A and B)","P(A) - P(B)"]'::jsonb, 'P(A) × P(B)', 'When events are independent the probability that both occur is the product of their individual probabilities.'),
('cc4840a8-7ca8-5960-ad89-865d23920882'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'If two events A and B are mutually exclusive, P(A or B) =', '["P(A) - P(B)","P(A) + P(B)","P(A) + P(B) - P(A and B)","P(A) / P(B)","P(A) × P(B)"]'::jsonb, 'P(A) + P(B)', 'When events are mutually exclusive they cannot occur together, so the probability of either occurring is the sum of their individual probabilities.'),
('139b92f2-596a-5d04-a424-bc376c8344f0'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Bayes theorem is used to calculate:', '["Sample size resulting in decreased functional activity","Type I error accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Prior probability mediated by specific receptor activation","Posterior probability given new evidence","Power leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior probability given new evidence', 'Bayes theorem updates a prior probability into a posterior probability once new information or evidence becomes available.'),
('b6e1671f-00c2-5406-b7ae-8039327435de'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The Bayes theorem formula is:', '["P(BA) × P(A) / P(B)","P(A)×P(B)/P(AB)","P(A)+P(B)/P(A∩B)","P(A∩B)/P(BA)","P(A) × P(B)"]'::jsonb, 'P(BA) × P(A) / P(B)', 'Bayes theorem, P(BA) × P(A) / P(B), computes the posterior probability of A given new evidence B.'),
('afe70e24-f59b-59db-999d-4db63608a2a3'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'P(AB) is the probability of:', '["A given B","A or B","A complement","B given A","A and B together"]'::jsonb, 'A given B', 'P(AB) denotes the conditional probability of event A occurring given that event B has occurred.'),
('14623a97-4f70-5227-a344-c22bce498d1d'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The sensitivity of a test equals:', '["P(D+ T+)","P(T- D-)","P(T+ D-)","P(T+ D+)","P(D- T-)"]'::jsonb, 'P(T+ D+)', 'Sensitivity, the ability of a test to identify disease, equals the probability of a positive test result among those with the disease, P(T+ D+).'),
('9bae305d-25bc-5a09-a4e3-3a458671825a'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The specificity of a test equals:', '["P(T+ D+)","P(T+ D-)","P(T- D-)","P(D+ T+)","P(D- T-)"]'::jsonb, 'P(T- D-)', 'Specificity, the ability of a test to identify non-disease, equals the probability of a negative test result among those without the disease, P(T− D−).'),
('65e5a0e5-12f2-5897-bffa-9d33dbf2438a'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The positive predictive value (PPV) of a test equals:', '["P(D+ T+)","P(T+ D+)","P(D- T-)","P(T- D-)","P(T+ D-)"]'::jsonb, 'P(D+ T+)', 'The positive predictive value is the probability that a positive test result indicates actual disease, P(D+ T+).'),
('102d0b93-d002-5b7b-a8b4-b74127703c33'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The negative predictive value (NPV) of a test equals:', '["P(T+ D-)","P(D- T-)","P(T- D-)","P(T+ D+)","P(D+ T+)"]'::jsonb, 'P(D- T-)', 'The negative predictive value is the probability that a negative test result reliably excludes disease, P(D− T−).'),
('9c9de2ed-5a98-521d-b455-b69c3381b317'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'What does the null hypothesis (H0) typically state?', '["There is a difference between groups","There is no difference or no association","There is an association between variables","The alternative hypothesis is true","The effect size is large"]'::jsonb, 'There is no difference or no association', 'The null hypothesis states there is no difference, no effect, or no association in the population. Only when H0 is rejected is there statistical evidence to support the alternative hypothesis.'),
('b38930ac-d99f-5a0c-bf92-8cff914841ee'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'What does the alternative hypothesis (H1) typically state?', '["There is a difference or association between the variables","There is no difference between the groups","The null hypothesis is true","The p-value is small","The effect size is large"]'::jsonb, 'There is a difference or association between the variables', 'The alternative hypothesis states that a difference or association exists between groups or variables in the population. It is the claim the study seeks evidence for and is the opposite of the null hypothesis.'),
('cfbb8a50-0789-52ed-9951-201677fb5c0c'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The alpha (α) significance level is commonly set at:', '["0.10","0.01","0.50","0.001","0.05"]'::jsonb, '0.05', 'The alpha level is the probability of a type I error and is conventionally set at 0.05. Stricter thresholds such as 0.01 are reserved for confirmatory analyses or multiple testing.'),
('c566da46-d2e6-5086-870b-ce25ad5ea455'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Beta (β), the probability of a type II error, is commonly set at:', '["0.10","0.05","0.20","0.50","0.01"]'::jsonb, '0.20', 'Beta is conventionally set at 0.20, giving a study power (1 − β) of 80%. Power is the probability of detecting an effect when one truly exists.'),
('4181af45-5e4c-5ed0-9e02-5b65b4be0943'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'If the p-value is less than the alpha level (p < α), the correct decision is to:', '["Increase the sample size","Conclude that the effect exists","Accept H1 as proven","Reject H0","Accept H0"]'::jsonb, 'Reject H0', 'Ap-value below alpha means the observed result is unlikely under the null hypothesis, so H0 is rejected and the finding is called statistically significant.'),
('32460d44-82e6-5952-a287-9a97f2c7b805'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'If the p-value is greater than the alpha level (p > α), the correct decision is to:', '["Conclude that H0 is false resulting in decreased functional activity","Reject H0 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Increase the power of the study mediated by specific receptor activation","Fail to reject H0 (insufficient evidence to reject)","Accept H1 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fail to reject H0 (insufficient evidence to reject)', 'Ap-value above alpha provides insufficient evidence to reject the null hypothesis. The result is described as not statistically significant, though it does not prove H0 true.'),
('0d98dceb-12b8-54f6-9845-159f2a7abf71'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'A one-sample t-test compares the mean of a sample with:', '["Another sample mean","The median","A known population mean","Zero","The mode"]'::jsonb, 'A known population mean', 'A one-sample t-test compares the sample mean to a specified value, usually a known or hypothesized population mean. It does not require a second comparison group.'),
('e6b224d6-2dc6-5d0c-8a4a-150c896aa114'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'An independent (unpaired) t-test compares the means of:', '["Two independent groups","The same group measured twice","Three or more groups","Matched pairs","One group versus a population mean"]'::jsonb, 'Two independent groups', 'An independent t-test contrasts the means of two separate, unrelated groups, such as treatment and placebo arms. Measurements are assumed independent across groups.'),
('1833e4c2-674c-5b09-bb1f-48419e156f30'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'A paired t-test is most appropriate when:', '["Correlation between two variables is assessed","Proportions are compared between two groups","The same subjects are measured before and after","Two independent groups are compared","Three or more groups are compared"]'::jsonb, 'The same subjects are measured before and after', 'A paired t-test analyses measurements taken on the same subjects, typically before and after an intervention. Matching each subject to itself removes between-subject variability.'),
('a755184e-22d2-5fa5-ac58-a80e9bfca739'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Which of the following is NOT an assumption required for an independent t-test?', '["A categorical dependent variable","Normality of the data","Equal variances (for the pooled t-test)","Independence of observations","Random sampling"]'::jsonb, 'A categorical dependent variable', 'The independent t-test requires a continuous, normally distributed outcome with independent observations and, for the pooled version, equal variances. A categorical outcome is not an assumption, and such data would instead need chi-square or logistic methods.'),
('9d1cf97b-da10-5823-9f06-58cc623e11aa'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Which test is used to compare the means of three or more independent groups?', '["Wilcoxon rank-sum test","ANOVA","McNemar''s test","t-test","Chi-square test"]'::jsonb, 'ANOVA', 'ANOVA compares the means of three or more independent groups in a single analysis. At-test is limited to two groups, while the chi-square and McNemar tests handle categorical data.'),
('b293ab3c-c585-5461-b9c2-a0fa3f019fdf'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'One-way ANOVA involves:', '["Only dependent variables","Several independent variables","One independent variable (factor)","Two independent variables","Repeated measurements of the same subjects"]'::jsonb, 'One independent variable (factor)', 'One-way ANOVA has a single independent variable (factor) with three or more levels. It tests whether the group means differ under this one factor.'),
('e09a7392-c922-5a62-815d-63276bd0b9d6'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'A two-way ANOVA can assess:', '["Only one main effect","Two main effects and their interaction","Only post-hoc comparisons","Only the interaction between the variables","Only repeated measures"]'::jsonb, 'Two main effects and their interaction', 'Two-way ANOVA models two independent variables and can test both main effects together with their interaction. This is its advantage over running two separate one-way ANOVAs.'),
('93c14609-b407-5fa7-ba0d-7d41210d21ac'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Post-hoc tests after ANOVA are needed when:', '["ANOVA is not significant resulting in decreased functional activity","The data are non-normal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The sample size is small mediated by specific receptor activation","ANOVA is significant (to identify which groups differ)","Variances are unequal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'ANOVA is significant (to identify which groups differ)', 'A significant ANOVA shows that some groups differ but not which ones. Post-hoc tests such as Tukey''s HSD compare group pairs while controlling the type I error rate.'),
('3d42d501-775e-5a30-ae7d-99b7078e8f80'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Tukey''s HSD is a:', '["Non-parametric test","Pre-test for at-test","Sample size calculation","Measure of correlation","Post-hoc test for ANOVA"]'::jsonb, 'Post-hoc test for ANOVA', 'Tukey''s honestly significant difference (HSD) compares all pairs of group means after a significant ANOVA. It adjusts for multiple comparisons to keep the family-wise error rate controlled.'),
('d07d9821-e14a-57f9-bbbc-77468ff570bf'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The Bonferroni correction for multiple comparisons involves:', '["Doubling the p-value","Increasing statistical power","Dividing the alpha level by the number of comparisons","Adjusting the sample size","Inverting the alpha level"]'::jsonb, 'Dividing the alpha level by the number of comparisons', 'The Bonferroni correction divides the alpha level by the number of comparisons, making each individual test harder to pass. This controls the overall type I error rate at the cost of statistical power.'),
('803e13cc-218f-59b3-b5ee-2102b4685071'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The Mann-Whitney U test is used to:', '["Assess correlation resulting in decreased functional activity","Fit a regression model accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Compare the distributions of two independent groups (non-parametric)","Compare paired data without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Compare three or more groups leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Compare the distributions of two independent groups (non-parametric)', 'The Mann-Whitney U test compares two independent groups without a normality assumption. It is the non-parametric alternative to the independent t-test.'),
('037df8e7-9562-5830-9026-bc948a4c6fca'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The Wilcoxon signed-rank test is the non-parametric alternative to the:', '["Pearson correlation","Chi-square test","Independent t-test","One-way ANOVA","Paired t-test"]'::jsonb, 'Paired t-test', 'The Wilcoxon signed-rank test analyses paired measurements without a normality assumption. It is the non-parametric counterpart of the paired t-test.'),
('21761217-20b3-539f-8347-2688d7fae159'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The Kruskal-Wallis test is the non-parametric alternative to:', '["One-way ANOVA","Chi-square test","Repeated measures ANOVA","Paired t-test","Two-way ANOVA"]'::jsonb, 'One-way ANOVA', 'The Kruskal-Wallis test compares three or more independent groups using ranks. It is the non-parametric alternative to one-way ANOVA.'),
('3e0955c3-fa82-574d-b2ec-865472c24a77'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The Friedman test is the non-parametric alternative to:', '["t-test","Chi-square test","One-way ANOVA","Repeated measures ANOVA","Two-way ANOVA"]'::jsonb, 'Repeated measures ANOVA', 'The Friedman test analyses three or more related (repeated) measurements without a normality assumption. It is the non-parametric alternative to repeated-measures ANOVA.'),
('d272bf4d-e8b2-5c9a-acf1-0bc711f708f8'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The chi-square (χ²) test is used to:', '["Compare means resulting in decreased functional activity","Perform survival analysis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Measure correlation mediated by specific receptor activation","Assess the association between categorical variables","Compare medians leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Assess the association between categorical variables', 'The chi-square test compares observed counts with counts expected under independence to test the association between two categorical variables. It is not suited to continuous outcomes.'),
('623461ad-618a-5e08-bb29-485bd838a0fb'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The chi-square test requires:', '["An observed frequency of at least 5 in every cell","Paired data","An expected frequency of at least 5 in every cell","Continuous data","A normal distribution"]'::jsonb, 'An expected frequency of at least 5 in every cell', 'A chi-square test is valid when the expected frequency in each cell is at least 5. When this condition fails, Fisher''s exact test is preferred.'),
('afd83ea8-dc38-5836-88f5-a1bdcab9c6c3'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Fisher''s exact test is used instead of the chi-square test when:', '["The data are normally distributed","Expected frequencies are small (< 5)","Variances are unequal","The sample is very large","The data are paired"]'::jsonb, 'Expected frequencies are small (< 5)', 'When expected cell frequencies fall below 5, the chi-square approximation becomes unreliable. Fisher''s exact test computes the exact probability of the observed table.'),
('070058bb-49d3-5061-8714-382ee90d20ea'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'McNemar''s test is the paired version of the:', '["Wilcoxon test","Mann-Whitney test","t-test","Chi-square test","ANOVA"]'::jsonb, 'Chi-square test', 'McNemar''s test compares two related categorical measurements, such as results before and after treatment on the same subjects. It is the paired analogue of the chi-square test.'),
('8749942e-874f-5735-a56e-421de7aaea36'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The Pearson correlation coefficient (r) measures:', '["The strength of a linear relationship between two continuous variables","Agreement between raters accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Non-linear relationships mediated by specific receptor activation","The difference between two means without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Causal effect leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The strength of a linear relationship between two continuous variables', 'Pearson''sr ranges from -1 to +1 and describes the strength and direction of a linear relationship between two continuous variables. It cannot capture non-linear or causal associations.'),
('3e4cde81-a7f8-5e06-acad-a90689f3c551'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'A Pearson correlation coefficient (r) of 0.8 indicates:', '["Perfect correlation","Strong positive correlation","Negative correlation","Moderate positive correlation","Weak positive correlation"]'::jsonb, 'Strong positive correlation', 'An r of 0.8 shows a strong positive linear relationship, meaning the variables tend to increase together. Values close to +1 approach a perfect positive association.'),
('8d6bf89c-54bc-5d14-af74-c67fc3458833'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'A Pearson correlation coefficient (r) of -0.7 indicates:', '["Weak negative correlation","Moderate negative correlation","Strong negative correlation","Positive correlation","No correlation"]'::jsonb, 'Strong negative correlation', 'An r of -0.7 shows a strong negative linear relationship, in which one variable tends to fall as the other rises. A magnitude of 0.7 is conventionally graded as strong.'),
('91309646-7e1a-5a97-a025-e090d7fae2db'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Spearman rank correlation is based on:', '["Percentiles","Ranks of the data","Differences from the mean","Standardized scores","Actual measurement values"]'::jsonb, 'Ranks of the data', 'Spearman''s coefficient is computed from the ranks of the observations rather than the raw values. Ranking makes the test robust to outliers and non-normal data.'),
('a59d3af2-7d64-50e1-9561-63aa0a694559'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Spearman correlation is most appropriate when:', '["The data are categorical","Independent groups are compared","The dependent variable is binary","The relationship is monotonic but not necessarily linear","The data are normally distributed"]'::jsonb, 'The relationship is monotonic but not necessarily linear', 'Spearman''s test handles monotonic associations that need not be linear, as well as ordinal or non-normal data. When the association is strictly linear and normal, Pearson''sr is used instead.'),
('83665bc9-3a5a-5cd1-b75b-af205809db34'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The equation for simple linear regression is:', '["Y = a + bX + cX²","log Y = a + bX","Y = a + bX²","Y = a/X + b","Y = a + bX"]'::jsonb, 'Y = a + bX', 'Simple linear regression models the outcome as Y = a + bX, with a the intercept and b the slope. The listed alternatives include quadratic, logarithmic, and reciprocal forms.'),
('46aea47e-f81f-5111-b3f0-6c1598496a4d'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'In linear regression, the slope (b) represents the:', '["Y-intercept resulting in decreased functional activity","R-squared accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Standard error mediated by specific receptor activation","Correlation coefficient without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Change in Y for a one-unit change in X"]'::jsonb, 'Change in Y for a one-unit change in X', 'The slope b gives the predicted change in the outcome Y for each one-unit increase in the predictor X. The intercept a is the predicted Y when X equals zero.'),
('52bb4346-1f71-5041-bb80-5ed090824950'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The R-squared value in regression represents the:', '["Intercept resulting in decreased functional activity","Correlation coefficient accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Slope mediated by specific receptor activation","Proportion of variance in Y explained by X","P-value leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Proportion of variance in Y explained by X', 'R-squared is the proportion of variance in the outcome that the predictors explain. It ranges from 0 to 1, with values near 1 indicating a good fit.'),
('eafd9822-1fb5-5b75-909f-a13667a04b8b'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'An R² value ranging from 0 to 1 indicates:', '["The proportion of variation explained","Statistical significance accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Effect size mediated by specific receptor activation","Correlation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sample size leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The proportion of variation explained', 'R² expresses the proportion of total variation in the outcome accounted for by the model. It measures explained variation rather than statistical significance.'),
('f21d1fe0-342f-5add-8422-5436269285cc'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Multiple linear regression extends simple regression to include:', '["Non-linear relationships","Multiple predictors","Categorical outcomes","Multiple outcomes","Time-to-event data"]'::jsonb, 'Multiple predictors', 'Multiple regression models one continuous outcome against two or more predictor variables. Each coefficient is estimated while adjusting for the other predictors.'),
('ecdc9776-814f-58d9-a453-abc668b8abd6'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Logistic regression is used when the outcome variable is:', '["Binary (dichotomous)","Ordinal with many levels","Time-to-event","Nominal with more than two levels","Continuous"]'::jsonb, 'Binary (dichotomous)', 'Logistic regression models a binary outcome, such as disease present or absent, and estimates the probability of the event. Continuous and time-to-event outcomes require other regression models.'),
('2d2d1b12-0c8a-58aa-8fb7-433a5cac35cb'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'In logistic regression, the link function is the:', '["Square root","Log","Inverse","Logit","Identity"]'::jsonb, 'Logit', 'Logistic regression links the probability of the outcome to the linear predictor through the logit (log-odds) function. This keeps the predicted probabilities within 0 and 1.'),
('abecfa7b-7669-5d67-9c0e-1466ec2d8c7d'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The odds ratio of a predictor in logistic regression is obtained by:', '["Taking the coefficient itself","Squaring the coefficient","Inverting the coefficient","Taking the log of the coefficient","Exponentiating the coefficient (e^β)"]'::jsonb, 'Exponentiating the coefficient (e^β)', 'The logistic coefficient is a change in log-odds, so exponentiating it (e to the power β) yields the odds ratio. The odds ratio gives the change in odds per unit increase in the predictor.'),
('2a73bea1-adc2-5a82-821e-6555d8600e29'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The proportional hazards assumption states that:', '["Events occur at a constant rate","Censoring is absent","The hazard changes over time","The baseline hazard is zero","The hazard ratio is constant over time"]'::jsonb, 'The hazard ratio is constant over time', 'Proportional hazards means the hazard ratio between two groups stays constant across follow-up time. If the ratio changes over time, Cox regression may be invalid.'),
('30e20695-78d1-5d10-899e-ed03cb874822'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The hazard ratio (HR) from a Cox regression is:', '["Always positive","Always between 0 and 1","Always negative","Always between -1 and +1","Always equal to the p-value"]'::jsonb, 'Always positive', 'A hazard ratio is the ratio of two hazards and therefore can only be positive, from above 0 to infinity. An HR of 1 means no effect, values below 1 are protective, and values above 1 indicate increased risk.'),
('8e7a48f3-b605-55b4-8e13-21730bb937bc'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'In a Kaplan-Meier survival curve, the Y-axis represents:', '["Time resulting in decreased functional activity","Number of events accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cumulative survival probability","Number at risk without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hazard rate leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cumulative survival probability', 'The Y-axis of a Kaplan-Meier plot shows cumulative survival probability, from 1.0 down to 0, while the X-axis shows follow-up time. Each drop in the curve marks an event.'),
('c9acc015-d911-5195-bc7c-90d4008dc3a1'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The log-rank test is used to:', '["Estimate survival probability","Calculate a hazard ratio","Compare survival between two or more groups","Assess the proportional hazards assumption","Test for normality"]'::jsonb, 'Compare survival between two or more groups', 'The log-rank test compares the whole survival curves of two or more groups over follow-up time. It is a non-parametric test that requires no distributional assumption about survival times.'),
('53f7ded1-0c6c-5ba7-82fa-f5b77902f0f0'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'On a Kaplan-Meier curve, censored observations are indicated by:', '["Tick marks on the curve","A vertical line","A dotted line","Step-downs in the curve","A dashed line"]'::jsonb, 'Tick marks on the curve', 'Censored observations are marked with small perpendicular tick marks placed along the curve. Vertical drops in the curve, by contrast, represent the occurrence of events.'),
('0e766beb-eb3c-5d54-84a9-14a7566ab0ed'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Right censoring occurs when:', '["Some data are missing","The event occurs before study entry","The subject is excluded from analysis","A subject is lost to follow-up or the event is not observed by study end","The subject experiences the event at time zero"]'::jsonb, 'A subject is lost to follow-up or the event is not observed by study end', 'Right censoring means the event has not been observed by the end of follow-up, often because the subject is lost or the study ends first. The true survival time is known only to exceed the observed time.'),
('11cdb081-2d2a-5626-bd4d-17e1f55648d8'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Simple random sampling requires:', '["Selection of convenient, easily accessible subjects","Equal probability of selection for every subject","Allocation of subjects in proportion to subgroup size","Selection of whole clusters of subjects","Inclusion of only the most severely affected subjects"]'::jsonb, 'Equal probability of selection for every subject', 'Simple random sampling gives every member of the population an equal and independent chance of selection, typically from a complete sampling frame. Convenience, quota, cluster, and stratified schemes do not constitute simple random sampling.'),
('30adbdb5-52c5-5206-bcad-10c41846e328'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Systematic sampling selects:', '["Random elements independently","Volunteers","Every kth element after a random start","Quota samples","Convenient elements"]'::jsonb, 'Every kth element after a random start', 'Systematic sampling advances through an ordered list taking every kth element after a random starting point. It is faster than simple random sampling but sensitive to periodic patterns in the list.'),
('fa2e40fa-2e03-5f4d-81cd-8bc4bd746b64'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'A disadvantage of systematic sampling is that:', '["It is expensive","A large sample is required","Analysis is complex","A sampling frame is required","Periodicity in the list can bias the sample"]'::jsonb, 'Periodicity in the list can bias the sample', 'If the sampling frame shows a periodic pattern that coincides with the sampling interval, systematic sampling captures the same repeating subset of the population and introduces bias.'),
('d651a598-3718-51f4-aa70-2b5b8fe3b061'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'In stratified sampling, allocation of the sample proportional to stratum size is called:', '["Proportional allocation","Optimal allocation","Simple allocation","Neyman allocation","Equal allocation"]'::jsonb, 'Proportional allocation', 'Proportional allocation sizes each stratum sample in proportion to its population share, so larger strata contribute more subjects. Neyman allocation instead weights strata by their variance.'),
('8c384b11-05de-5fe8-a277-f3d6576d5e4f'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Cluster sampling is most useful when:', '["List is easily available resulting in decreased functional activity","Geographic dispersion makes simple random sampling impractical","The population is homogeneous mediated by specific receptor activation","Precision is critical without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sample size is small leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Geographic dispersion makes simple random sampling impractical', 'Cluster sampling is preferred when the population is geographically dispersed, so listing every individual would be impractical or impossible. It is more feasible and cheaper than simple random sampling in such settings.'),
('d1b3e520-0222-57cf-b00e-58156f03aca2'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The main disadvantage of cluster sampling is:', '["Requires full list resulting in decreased functional activity","Expensive accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Time-consuming mediated by specific receptor activation","Lower precision per sample size compared to SRS (design effect)","Complex to organize leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lower precision per sample size compared to SRS (design effect)', 'Members within a cluster tend to be similar, so cluster sampling provides less statistical precision per subject than simple random sampling. This loss in efficiency is quantified by the design effect.'),
('2b636e12-9334-5173-90d1-bc31b2c99b9f'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Non-probability sampling includes:', '["Stratified","Systematic","Cluster","Simple random","Convenience"]'::jsonb, 'Convenience', 'Convenience sampling selects subjects who are easiest to reach and does not rely on random selection, so it is a non-probability method. Stratified, systematic, cluster, and simple random sampling are all probability methods.'),
('50253a06-0356-56dc-87f8-bb3ac4b62f39'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Snowball sampling is particularly useful for:', '["Hidden populations (e.g. drug users)","Children","General population","College students","Hospital patients"]'::jsonb, 'Hidden populations (e.g. drug users)', 'Snowball sampling relies on early participants referring others, making it practical for hidden or hard-to-reach groups. Such populations lack a usable sampling frame.'),
('6c514451-ef7b-5f70-a1ae-2d98f67ea8c4'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Convenience sampling is:', '["Likely to be representative","Probability-based","Highly generalizable","Prone to selection bias","Unbiased"]'::jsonb, 'Prone to selection bias', 'Convenience sampling recruits subjects because they are readily available, which does not reflect the target population. The resulting samples are prone to selection bias and poor generalizability.'),
('42c87229-c852-5c16-9424-2c6b00daee10'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Quota sampling is similar to stratified sampling but:', '["Quota is non-random (judgmental selection within strata)","Quota uses clusters accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Quota is random mediated by specific receptor activation","Quota is probability-based without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Quota has strata leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Quota is non-random (judgmental selection within strata)', 'In quota sampling, the investigator selects subjects within each stratum using judgment rather than random choice. This distinguishes it from stratified sampling, where selection within strata is random.'),
('04ab8649-4223-5b95-bcea-450506e5807d'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Purposive sampling selects subjects based on:', '["Snowball referrals","Specific predefined criteria","Convenience","Volunteering","Random selection"]'::jsonb, 'Specific predefined criteria', 'Purposive (judgmental) sampling enrols participants who meet predefined characteristics relevant to the research question. It is chosen deliberately rather than by random or convenience methods.'),
('f334d262-c12d-5b75-aad5-d20a95f4de05'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Sampling error is the difference between:', '["Sample and target population","Two population parameters","Sample statistic and population parameter","Two sample means","Observed and expected values"]'::jsonb, 'Sample statistic and population parameter', 'Sampling error arises because a sample is an incomplete picture of the population. It is the discrepancy between the estimate from the sample (statistic) and the true population value (parameter).'),
('2c67e387-978d-5933-ae88-8b2df473b947'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Non-sampling error includes all of the following EXCEPT:', '["Interviewer bias","Data entry error","Non-response error","Measurement error","Sampling variability"]'::jsonb, 'Sampling variability', 'Non-sampling errors occur during data collection and processing, including interviewer bias, data entry error, non-response, and measurement error. Sampling variability is part of sampling error, not non-sampling error.'),
('284bec1c-8af9-5232-a850-dc62a96e1b5b'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Measurement error can be reduced by:', '["Random selection","Using standardized instruments and training","Taking multiple samples","Cluster sampling","Increasing sample size"]'::jsonb, 'Using standardized instruments and training', 'Standardized instruments and trained personnel improve the reliability of data collection and reduce error in measurement. This directly targets the source of measurement error.'),
('e70ee39a-5573-57bf-a19e-e1357140c2a9'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Non-response bias occurs when:', '["Questionnaire is flawed resulting in decreased functional activity","Interviewer is biased accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Respondents differ systematically from non-respondents","Sample is too small without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Data entry is incorrect leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Respondents differ systematically from non-respondents', 'Non-response bias occurs when people who do not take part differ in important ways from those who do. A low response rate allows such systematic differences to distort the results.'),
('344808ec-99b2-5b2c-a685-ca434c009f07'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'A response rate below which percentage raises concern about non-response bias?', '["80%","70%","50%","90%","60%"]'::jsonb, '60%', 'A response rate below 60% is generally considered inadequate and raises concern about non-response bias. Lower participation increases the risk that responders differ systematically from non-responders.'),
('de0ecebe-afe0-57aa-89b5-f23a8d6fbc88'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Berkson''s bias is observed in:', '["Ecological studies","RCTs","Cohort studies","Hospital-based case-control studies","Population-based studies"]'::jsonb, 'Hospital-based case-control studies', 'Berkson''s bias arises because hospitalized cases and controls are more likely to have multiple concurrent conditions, distorting the apparent association between exposures and disease. It is specific to hospital-based case-control studies.'),
('34ac7320-168d-5d37-a6dc-be835a2afc7d'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Neyman bias (prevalence-incidence bias) occurs when:', '["Using prevalent instead of incident cases","Hospital controls","Using population controls","Matching is used","Using incident cases"]'::jsonb, 'Using prevalent instead of incident cases', 'Prevalent cases include only survivors, so exposures and factors related to survival can be misrepresented. Neyman bias occurs when prevalent rather than incident cases are enrolled.'),
('13aa8624-c33b-59b1-a7f1-b68556de6fbd'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Sample size estimation depends on all of the following EXCEPT:', '["Desired precision","Power (1-beta)","Effect size expected","Significance level (alpha)","P-value expected"]'::jsonb, 'P-value expected', 'Sample size is determined by desired precision, power (1-β), expected effect size, and the significance level (α). The p-value is an outcome of the analysis, not an input for the calculation.'),
('98e94e9b-6621-50bd-806b-5ca18ddb233b'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'If the expected effect size is small, the sample size needed is:', '["Larger","Zero","Smaller","Unchanged","Doubled"]'::jsonb, 'Larger', 'Smaller effects are harder to detect, so more subjects are needed to achieve adequate power. Sample size is inversely related to the square of the effect size.'),
('206dd28a-d036-5ab1-aa64-bfb31a97acf5'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Increasing power from 80% to 90% will:', '["Decrease required sample size","Decrease alpha","Not affect sample size","Increase required sample size","Increase effect size"]'::jsonb, 'Increase required sample size', 'Raising power lowers the chance of a type II error and therefore demands a larger sample. A sample sized for 80% power is too small to achieve 90% power.'),
('149f8792-d946-5c92-91f7-86ccce73d2e9'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The null hypothesis significance testing approach was developed by:', '["Karl Pearson","Jerzy Neyman and Egon Pearson","Galton","Ronald Fisher","Bradford Hill"]'::jsonb, 'Jerzy Neyman and Egon Pearson', 'Neyman and Pearson formalized hypothesis testing as a decision procedure contrasting a null and an alternative hypothesis. Their framework introduced control of type I and type II errors.'),
('eadaf7a4-ee5c-534d-b90e-369b13d9ed42'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Which of the following is NOT affected by sample size?', '["Confidence interval width","Point estimate","P-value","Standard error","Power"]'::jsonb, 'Point estimate', 'The point estimate is the single value computed from the observed data and does not systematically depend on how many subjects are sampled. In contrast, the standard error, confidence interval width, power, and p-value all change with sample size.'),
('a62b2e6a-e431-5362-887e-71e7c37a9dec'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The Central Limit Theorem states that:', '["Variance equals mean resulting in decreased functional activity","The population is normal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Median equals mean mediated by specific receptor activation","The sampling distribution of the mean approaches normal as n increases regardless of population distribution","The sample must be large leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The sampling distribution of the mean approaches normal as n increases regardless of population distribution', 'For a sufficiently large sample, the sampling distribution of the mean becomes approximately normal even if the underlying population is not. This holds regardless of the shape of the population distribution.'),
('46818df7-6776-505e-ac22-b89ff66ea7b6'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The Central Limit Theorem applies when the sample size is typically:', '["n ≥ 30","n ≥ 100","n ≥ 1000","n < 10","n = any size"]'::jsonb, 'n ≥ 30', 'A sample size of about 30 or more is the usual rule of thumb for the sampling distribution of the mean to be approximately normal. This holds even when the parent population is not normally distributed.'),
('886429fd-68f5-5514-98bc-c8c2f5bd1110'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'In a normal distribution, what percentage of values fall within one standard deviation of the mean?', '["68%","95%","99.7%","50%","25%"]'::jsonb, '68%', 'In a normal distribution about 68% of values lie within ±1 standard deviation of the mean. The corresponding figures for ±2 and ±3 SD are 95% and 99.7%.'),
('2f39c39e-16a9-55d0-83ed-9a3b25651bc9'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'In the standard normal distribution, the mean and standard deviation are:', '["Mean = 1 SD = 0","Mean = 0 SD = 1","Mean = 1 SD = 1","Mean = 50 SD = 10","Mean = 0 SD = 0"]'::jsonb, 'Mean = 0 SD = 1', 'The standard normal (Z) distribution is defined with a mean of 0 and a standard deviation of 1. Any normally distributed variable can be converted to it by standardizing.'),
('ab328114-faab-590b-ae46-475643156698'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'If a variable is transformed to Z-scores, the mean of the Z-scores is:', '["10","0","1","-1","50"]'::jsonb, '0', 'Subtracting the mean and dividing by the SD re-centers every score so the standardized values have mean 0. Each Z-score then expresses how many SDs a value lies from the original mean.'),
('4ab212e6-269b-5f77-ab69-25ff805c1bd7'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'If a variable is transformed to Z-scores, the SD of the Z-scores is:', '["1","Variable-dependent","0","-1","10"]'::jsonb, '1', 'Standardizing a distribution scales it so its spread equals 1 SD. Because Z = (X − mean)/SD, dividing by the SD makes the resulting standard deviation exactly 1.'),
('da63abf0-82af-5bf7-bfe0-1ddb9a9a4dae'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The 68-95-99.7 rule applies to:', '["Nominal data","Skewed distribution","Any distribution","Normal distribution","Ordinal data"]'::jsonb, 'Normal distribution', 'The 68-95-99.7 rule describes the percentages of values within 1, 2, and 3 SD of the mean only for a normal distribution. Non-normal and skewed data do not follow these proportions.'),
('f76a7424-be7e-5a63-8ee1-33ef122ebf4b'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'What proportion of a normal distribution lies beyond Z = 1.96 in both tails?', '["2.5%","0.5%","5%","1%","10%"]'::jsonb, '5%', 'Each tail beyond Z = 1.96 contains 2.5%, so the two tails combined contain 5%. This is why ±1.96 defines the 95% confidence interval.'),
('c79d759d-91e1-5e73-9d09-334104534138'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'What proportion of a normal distribution lies beyond Z = 2.58 in both tails?', '["5%","10%","1%","0.5%","2.5%"]'::jsonb, '1%', 'Each tail beyond Z = 2.58 contains 0.5%, giving 1% for both tails together. This value sets the 99% confidence limit (α = 0.01).'),
('cf261fc4-8524-5e8f-9fb5-2dabc6eb9ad8'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The t-distribution is similar to the normal distribution but with:', '["A flatter peak","Left skewed only","Thicker tails (more variability)","Thinner tails","Same shape"]'::jsonb, 'Thicker tails (more variability)', 'The t-distribution has heavier tails than the normal distribution because it is used when the population SD is unknown and estimated from the sample. This extra variability in the tails is most marked at small sample sizes.'),
('a60516cc-ccdd-52a7-820a-ecc85e70867d'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'As the degrees of freedom increase, the t-distribution approaches:', '["Standard normal","F-distribution","Exponential","Chi-square","Uniform"]'::jsonb, 'Standard normal', 'With larger samples the SD estimate becomes more precise, so the t-distribution converges toward the standard normal. At large degrees of freedom the two distributions are very close.'),
('2faa185b-d378-5c39-9022-f8be3a650d80'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The F-distribution is used in:', '["Regression","Correlation","t-test","ANOVA","Chi-square test"]'::jsonb, 'ANOVA', 'ANOVA compares the variance between groups with the variance within groups using the F-test. The ratio of two variances follows the F-distribution.'),
('541495a1-c7f4-533b-b8d1-095a1760a947'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The chi-square distribution is used in:', '["All of the above","Contingency table analysis","Testing variances","Goodness-of-fit tests","Comparing the means of two groups"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'The chi-square distribution underlies contingency-table tests of association, goodness-of-fit tests, and tests concerning variances. Comparing two group means uses the t-test or ANOVA family, not chi-square.'),
('0f7b9421-fca2-56c9-bd29-f5e3ce8d71b5'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The degrees of freedom for a chi-square test of independence in an r × c table is:', '["r×c","(r-1)(c-1)","rc-1","r+c-2","(r-1)+(c-1)"]'::jsonb, '(r-1)(c-1)', 'The degrees of freedom equal (number of rows − 1) × (number of columns − 1). This reflects the cells that are free to vary once the marginal totals are fixed.'),
('419339d1-aec1-569a-9478-21a0e1bf21ab'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'In a 2 × 2 contingency table, the degrees of freedom for chi-square is:', '["4","1","2","0","3"]'::jsonb, '1', 'For a 2 × 2 table, df = (2 − 1) × (2 − 1) = 1. Only one cell is free to vary after the row and column marginal totals are fixed.'),
('7c314719-51bf-5f4b-83e6-ddf24e0173b8'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The observed frequency in a chi-square test is:', '["The actual count in the table","The theoretical frequency","Expected frequency","The column total","The row total"]'::jsonb, 'The actual count in the table', 'The observed frequency is the actual number of subjects counted in each cell of the table. It is compared against the expected frequency, which is calculated under the null hypothesis.'),
('357a6ecf-cfa5-5a1d-bd3c-ede80031df8d'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The expected frequency in a chi-square test is calculated as:', '["Row total × Column total / Grand total","Grand total/Row total","(Row total + Column total)/2","Observed/Total","Row total/Column total"]'::jsonb, 'Row total × Column total / Grand total', 'The expected frequency for a cell is the product of its row and column totals divided by the grand total. It represents the count that would be expected if the two variables were independent.'),
('98862513-e9dc-5273-9c2c-40e63dcc1cfd'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Parametric tests require the assumption of:', '["Equal variances (for some)","None of the above","Normal distribution","All of the above","Independence"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Parametric tests assume normally distributed data, independence of observations, and, for many, equal variances across groups. Non-normal or dependent data may require non-parametric alternatives.');
insert into fcps_mcq_stage (id, subject_name, chapter_name, question, options, correct_answer, explanation) values
('ccccddc2-b4ef-5344-9e99-fb450264edc8'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Non-parametric tests:', '["Can be used with ordinal data","Make fewer assumptions about data distribution","Require normally distributed data","Are less powerful when parametric assumptions hold","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Non-parametric tests make minimal assumptions about the underlying distribution and can handle ordinal data. They are generally less powerful than parametric tests when parametric assumptions are satisfied.'),
('23e786e2-587d-5d62-8047-cee067a6fff7'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Which test would you use for paired ordinal data?', '["Mann-Whitney","Chi-square","Paired t-test","Kruskal-Wallis","Wilcoxon signed-rank test"]'::jsonb, 'Wilcoxon signed-rank test', 'The Wilcoxon signed-rank test compares two related (paired) groups on an ordinal scale. A paired t-test is unsuitable because it requires continuous, normally distributed differences.'),
('ae2e17d6-e5f9-5e5d-8d2b-c5b2092f22f7'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Which test is used to compare three independent groups on a continuous, non-normally distributed outcome?', '["Chi-square","Kruskal-Wallis","One-way ANOVA","Friedman test","McNemar"]'::jsonb, 'Kruskal-Wallis', 'The Kruskal-Wallis test is the non-parametric analogue of one-way ANOVA for three or more independent groups. It works on ranks and does not require normality.'),
('040118e5-425b-5584-82c6-c0c96d62490d'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Which test is used to compare three paired groups on ordinal data?', '["Wilcoxon","Chi-square","Kruskal-Wallis","Friedman test","One-way repeated measures ANOVA"]'::jsonb, 'Friedman test', 'The Friedman test is the non-parametric analogue of repeated-measures ANOVA for three or more related groups. It is appropriate for paired ordinal data.'),
('e159ed86-5ed1-5d6b-8766-b96588782e7b'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The range of Spearman rho is:', '["-1 to +1","-1 to 0","0 to infinity","0 to 1","-∞ to +∞"]'::jsonb, '-1 to +1', 'Spearman''s rho measures rank correlation and, like Pearson''sr, ranges from −1 to +1. Values near ±1 indicate a perfect monotonic relationship.'),
('5feb399c-6232-5ba9-b91d-6505f3df9c4b'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Which correlation coefficient measures the strength of a monotonic relationship?', '["Pearson","Point-biserial","Phi coefficient","Spearman","Cramér''sV"]'::jsonb, 'Spearman', 'Spearman''s rho assesses rank-based correlation, capturing monotonic rather than strictly linear relationships. Pearson''sr measures linear association and assumes continuous, roughly normal data.'),
('a9888689-6519-5e9e-b210-eea45567d65e'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The phi coefficient is used for correlation between:', '["One binary and one continuous","Ordinal variables","Two continuous variables","Ranked data","Two binary variables"]'::jsonb, 'Two binary variables', 'Phi measures the association between two dichotomous (binary) variables. It is derived from a 2 × 2 contingency table.'),
('d13944b0-10a8-5caa-b290-0516739a3ebc'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Point-biserial correlation measures association between:', '["Two continuous variables","Ordinal data","Two binary variables","One binary and one continuous variable","Ranked data"]'::jsonb, 'One binary and one continuous variable', 'The point-biserial coefficient quantifies correlation between a dichotomous variable and a continuous variable. It is a special case of Pearson''sr.'),
('4d4fdff0-45be-5464-9142-27625e55f0da'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Cramér''sV measures association between:', '["Two continuous variables","Ordinal data","Two nominal variables","Ranks","One binary and one continuous"]'::jsonb, 'Two nominal variables', 'Cramér''sV is based on the chi-square statistic and gauges the strength of association between two nominal variables. It can be used with tables larger than 2 × 2.'),
('8a27dea1-d29c-5cc0-b08f-185177b82d2c'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'ANOVA assumptions include:', '["Normality of residuals","Independence","All groups must have equal sample sizes","All of the above","Homogeneity of variances"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'ANOVA requires independent observations, normally distributed residuals, and homogeneity of variances across groups. Equal group sizes are not a requirement because ANOVA handles unbalanced designs.'),
('fde99383-9f7c-5636-b3c0-4d17b3e6e977'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Homogeneity of variances can be tested using:', '["t-test","Durbin-Watson test","Kolmogorov-Smirnov test","Shapiro-Wilk test","Levene''s test"]'::jsonb, 'Levene''s test', 'Levene''s test assesses whether group variances are equal, a key assumption for ANOVA and t-tests. It is more robust to non-normality than the F-test for variances.'),
('eac57231-b96a-57cf-8978-42b1d8c98332'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Normality can be tested using:', '["Levene''s test","F-test","Bartlett''s test","Shapiro-Wilk test","Durbin-Watson test"]'::jsonb, 'Shapiro-Wilk test', 'The Shapiro-Wilk test evaluates whether a sample came from a normal distribution. A significant p-value indicates departure from normality.'),
('b65f20b8-1f33-5a31-a755-fdb9395394cc'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The Kolmogorov-Smirnov test can test:', '["Independence","Correlation","Equality of variances","Normality","Randomness"]'::jsonb, 'Normality', 'The Kolmogorov-Smirnov test compares a sample distribution against a reference distribution, commonly to assess normality. It compares cumulative proportions rather than means or variances.'),
('7989abbd-aa3c-56ba-bf65-ed5a28c72811'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'If data are non-normal and the sample size is small, which type of test should be used?', '["Parametric test","Non-parametric test","Transform to categorical","Ignore the violation","Any test"]'::jsonb, 'Non-parametric test', 'Parametric tests assume normality, which cannot be safely assumed with small samples and non-normal data. Non-parametric tests make no distributional assumptions and are preferred in this setting.'),
('8da4b61d-ba42-5591-ac07-7f6b47912dc3'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Data transformation (e.g., log transformation) is used to:', '["Decrease bias","Increase power","Make data more normal","Increase sample size","Reduce variability"]'::jsonb, 'Make data more normal', 'Log and other transformations reduce skewness so the data approximate a normal distribution. This permits analysis with parametric tests that require the normality assumption.'),
('ff53af5e-89f7-5f76-8a55-29f71e89dfbe'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Log transformation is particularly useful for:', '["Uniform data","Normal data","Binomial data","Positively skewed data","Negatively skewed data"]'::jsonb, 'Positively skewed data', 'Logarithmic transformation compresses the long right tail of positively skewed data, pulling the distribution toward symmetry. It does not correct negatively skewed data and is unnecessary for already normal data.'),
('63527988-7624-5486-b641-16fe39e9f22c'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The null hypothesis (H0) tested by the Shapiro-Wilk test is that:', '["Data is normal","Variances are unequal","Variances are equal","Data is not normal","Mean is zero"]'::jsonb, 'Data is normal', 'The Shapiro-Wilk test assesses normality, and its null hypothesis states that the data follow a normal distribution. Rejecting H0 indicates significant departure from normality.'),
('ff60ffa5-5619-5f1d-b341-fb5fabf4d950'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'In meta-analysis, a fixed-effect model assumes that:', '["Studies share a common effect size","Small study effects","Publication bias is present","Effects vary randomly","High heterogeneity"]'::jsonb, 'Studies share a common effect size', 'A fixed-effect model assumes one true underlying effect size shared by all included studies, with observed differences attributed solely to sampling error. A random-effects model instead allows effects to vary between studies.'),
('ef1552a5-8cf1-5d1e-9ae3-969195ba77ce'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'In a random-effects meta-analysis model:', '["Results are fixed","All studies have same effect","Effects vary across studies following a distribution","Heterogeneity is zero","Studies are weighted by their sample size"]'::jsonb, 'Effects vary across studies following a distribution', 'A random-effects model assumes the true effect differs across studies and follows a distribution, incorporating both within-study and between-study variance. It is used when heterogeneity among studies is expected.'),
('f8978495-927c-5cfb-8d13-ed5dc8e42595'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'What does I² = 0% mean in a meta-analysis?', '["Test is invalid","Negative heterogeneity","Maximum heterogeneity","Very high heterogeneity","No heterogeneity (all variation due to chance)"]'::jsonb, 'No heterogeneity (all variation due to chance)', 'I² estimates the proportion of total variation across studies due to true differences rather than chance. An I² of 0% indicates no apparent heterogeneity, with all observed variation attributable to sampling error.'),
('2a392ffc-a404-558e-ad65-0d2911ee17de'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Dichotomizing a continuous variable leads to:', '["Reduced bias","Increased precision","Loss of information","Better interpretation","Loss of statistical power"]'::jsonb, 'Loss of statistical power', 'Cutting a continuous variable at a threshold discards within-category information and reduces the variance retained, thereby decreasing statistical power. It can also misclassify subjects near the cut-point.'),
('2842bf3c-a934-5d1e-99b9-90eda6ee9dce'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Regression to the mean is most likely when:', '["Subjects are randomly selected","Data is normally distributed","Subjects are selected based on extreme values","Sample is large","Correlation is high"]'::jsonb, 'Subjects are selected based on extreme values', 'When subjects are selected because they have extreme values, a repeat measurement tends to fall closer to the population mean even without any real change. Selection on extremes therefore makes regression to the mean likely.'),
('fe944921-0f48-586c-b4b7-f174ca30103f'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Which graphical plot is used to check the normality assumption of data?', '["Scatter plot","Bar chart","Q-Q plot","Box plot","Histogram"]'::jsonb, 'Q-Q plot', 'AQ-Q plot graphs observed quantiles against the quantiles of a theoretical normal distribution. Points following a straight diagonal line indicate data consistent with normality.'),
('afe9e62f-f340-5fd8-ba3f-9712398ae95e'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'In aQ-Q plot, data points that follow a straight diagonal line suggest:', '["Outliers","Heteroscedasticity","Non-normal data","Normal data","Skewness"]'::jsonb, 'Normal data', 'Systematic alignment of observed quantiles with the line of identity indicates the data are normally distributed. Deviation from the line implies departure from normality.'),
('af7df1fd-5013-5e5e-93a7-7efa368bb873'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'A box plot displays:', '["Mean and SD","All data points","Proportions","Median IQR and outliers","Mean and range"]'::jsonb, 'Median IQR and outliers', 'A box plot summarizes the median, the interquartile range (box), and whiskers extending to non-outlier limits, with points beyond plotted as outliers. It is a compact visual summary of a distribution.'),
('fc4743f4-5bb5-5531-9f9e-f2a56a7c7b02'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The line inside the box of a box plot represents the:', '["Q3","Q1","Mean","Median","Mode"]'::jsonb, 'Median', 'The central line within the box marks the median (50th percentile) of the distribution. The box itself spans Q1 to Q3, that is, the interquartile range.'),
('3fb28b1f-312c-5df9-80cb-a5ec8adbf284'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The whiskers of a box plot typically extend to:', '["Data range","±2 SD","Minimum and maximum","±1 SD","1.5 × IQR beyond Q1 and Q3"]'::jsonb, '1.5 × IQR beyond Q1 and Q3', 'Conventionally, whiskers extend to the most extreme data point within 1.5 times the interquartile range beyond Q1 and Q3. Observations outside this are plotted individually as outliers.'),
('c8b91965-af3d-5b7b-a7a8-d1d2e41fdc56'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'An outlier in a box plot is a point located beyond:', '["1.5 × IQR from Q1 or Q3","Minimum","2×IQR","Maximum","Mean ± 2SD"]'::jsonb, '1.5 × IQR from Q1 or Q3', 'Points lying more than 1.5 times the interquartile range beyond the first or third quartile are flagged as outliers. This convention identifies observations that are unusually extreme relative to the central bulk of the data.'),
('dfeee099-cd18-5263-9031-4467018af54e'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'A histogram is used for:', '["Comparing means resulting in decreased functional activity","Displaying distribution of continuous data","Testing hypotheses mediated by specific receptor activation","Correlation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Agreement leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Displaying distribution of continuous data', 'A histogram shows the frequency distribution of a continuous variable by dividing its range into bins. It is used to visualize the shape, central tendency, spread, and skewness of the data.'),
('3bf41ada-8837-501f-a675-b94af23879ff'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'A bar chart is appropriate for:', '["Correlation","Survival data","Regression","Continuous data","Categorical data"]'::jsonb, 'Categorical data', 'Bar charts use separated bars to display the frequencies or proportions of categorical groups. Unlike histograms, their bars represent discrete categories rather than a continuous scale.'),
('f4b5cc68-e4cd-58a7-8d63-4d7defc224bb'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'A scatter plot is used to display:', '["Correlation matrix","Frequencies over time","Relationship between two continuous variables","Proportions","Distribution of one variable"]'::jsonb, 'Relationship between two continuous variables', 'A scatter plot plots pairs of numeric values on two axes to reveal the direction, strength, and form of an association. It is the standard first step before correlation or regression analysis.'),
('639fb63c-b0a7-53ed-bb9f-ea6b9ace3f7d'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'A pie chart is used to show:', '["Trends over time","Comparison of means","Proportions of a whole","Correlation","Distribution"]'::jsonb, 'Proportions of a whole', 'A pie chart divides a circle into slices whose areas represent the proportion each category contributes to the total. It is suited to displaying parts of a whole with few categories.'),
('842176f5-b581-559b-bb09-d3a6a1c266e3'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The arithmetic mean is particularly sensitive to:', '["Outliers","Mode","Median","Variance","Sample size"]'::jsonb, 'Outliers', 'Because every value contributes to its calculation, a single extreme observation can pull the mean substantially. The median is more robust to outliers and is preferred for skewed data.'),
('4a6bdb49-a952-5920-82df-96e821110bac'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The median is preferred over the mean when the data are:', '["Symmetric","Skewed or has outliers","Interval","Normally distributed","Continuous"]'::jsonb, 'Skewed or has outliers', 'The median depends only on the position of the middle value and is unaffected by the magnitude of extreme observations. It therefore better represents the center of skewed or outlier-contaminated data.'),
('5d77d03d-79e0-53bf-bde4-e4e37d1be63a'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The mode is the only meaningful measure of central tendency for:', '["Ratio data","Interval data","Continuous data","Nominal data","Ordinal data"]'::jsonb, 'Nominal data', 'Nominal categories have no natural order, so the arithmetic operations required for the mean or median are meaningless. The mode, which identifies the most frequent category, is the only applicable measure of central tendency.'),
('9b88555c-620e-5932-a31b-9551d06ddf1b'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The range is best described as:', '["Equal to SD","Preferred for all data","Equal to IQR","Robust to outliers","Sensitive to outliers"]'::jsonb, 'Sensitive to outliers', 'The range equals the difference between the maximum and minimum values and depends entirely on the two extremes. A single outlier can therefore greatly inflate the range.'),
('84572876-5101-5103-8b69-393fdb794781'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The interquartile range is preferred over the range because it is:', '["Larger","Not affected by outliers","Affected by outliers","Smaller","Easier to calculate"]'::jsonb, 'Not affected by outliers', 'The IQR spans the middle 50% of observations (Q3 minus Q1) and ignores extreme values. It is therefore more robust than the range, which relies only on the minimum and maximum.'),
('2e5f4674-b862-5a12-961e-fb4b004047d7'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The variance is expressed in:', '["Ratio","Original units","Squared units","Same as mean","Standard deviation units"]'::jsonb, 'Squared units', 'Variance is the mean of squared deviations from the mean, so its units are the square of the original measurement units. The standard deviation, its square root, returns to the original units.'),
('96bfc6d1-6526-5284-86dd-8bc851c4878f'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The standard deviation of a data set is expressed in:', '["Proportion","Squared units","Z-scores","Original units","Percentage"]'::jsonb, 'Original units', 'The standard deviation equals the square root of the variance, restoring the original measurement units of the data. This makes it directly interpretable alongside the mean.'),
('91041196-f920-55ae-bfdd-9611108c64d1'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The standard error of the mean (SEM) is always:', '["Equal to variance","Smaller than SD","Negative","Equal to SD","Larger than SD"]'::jsonb, 'Smaller than SD', 'Because SEM equals SD divided by the square root of the sample size, it is smaller than the standard deviation whenever the sample has more than one subject. As sample size increases, the SEM decreases and the estimate of the mean becomes more precise.'),
('c07508c4-4d52-5f09-9248-ac299cc3c15d'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The coefficient of variation (CV) is most useful when:', '["Distribution is skewed resulting in decreased functional activity","Sample is small accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Comparing variability of variables measured in different units","Correlation is high without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Data is normal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Comparing variability of variables measured in different units', 'CV expresses the standard deviation as a percentage of the mean, making it a scale-free measure of relative variability. It therefore permits comparisons between variables or groups with different units or very different magnitudes.'),
('5f7fde8a-2f04-50e9-9ff3-f20067fb5077'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'For a binary variable coded 0 and 1, the mean represents the:', '["Variance","Mode","Proportion of 1s","Average value","Median"]'::jsonb, 'Proportion of 1s', 'Summing the values of a 0/1 variable counts the number of ones, and dividing by the sample size yields the proportion of ones. The mean of a binary variable is therefore a proportion.'),
('c1c1db09-f095-5445-a7db-d69c9e34023e'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Dummy variables are used in regression to represent:', '["Outcome variables","Categorical predictors","Ranks","Continuous predictors","Time variables"]'::jsonb, 'Categorical predictors', 'Dummy coding converts categorical predictors into a series of 0/1 indicator variables for use in regression. This allows the categorical influence on an outcome to be estimated numerically.'),
('6c594786-020c-5c4c-8071-02d9d6b38681'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The logit function is the link function used in:', '["Linear regression","Poisson regression","Cox regression","ANOVA","Logistic regression"]'::jsonb, 'Logistic regression', 'Logistic regression models the natural logarithm of the odds (logit) of a binary outcome as a linear function of predictors. The logit transformation keeps predicted probabilities between 0 and 1.'),
('4b13dc8d-ef75-58bd-90ee-23a9b61a3591'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Poisson regression is used to model:', '["Time-to-event","Count data (number of events)","Continuous data","Binary outcomes","Ordinal data"]'::jsonb, 'Count data (number of events)', 'Poisson regression models count outcomes, such as the number of events per unit of time or per person, and assumes the variance approximately equals the mean. It is the standard approach for event counts and incidence rates.'),
('5ffd87ee-f844-5b0c-a083-e39a7e5e5033'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The incidence rate ratio (IRR) obtained from Poisson regression is analogous to the:', '["Mean difference","Risk ratio","Odds ratio","Correlation","Hazard ratio"]'::jsonb, 'Risk ratio', 'The IRR compares incidence rates between exposure groups and is interpreted on the same scale as a risk ratio, with a value of 1 indicating no association. Both are ratio measures of effect for comparative rates.'),
('86d50fb8-912f-52a8-a201-135c3c573d04'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'In multiple regression, multicollinearity refers to:', '["Outliers resulting in decreased functional activity","Non-linearity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Non-normal residuals mediated by specific receptor activation","High correlation among predictor variables","Heteroscedasticity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'High correlation among predictor variables', 'Multicollinearity exists when predictor variables are highly correlated with one another. It inflates the variance of coefficient estimates and makes it difficult to separate the individual effects of predictors.'),
('6ba88f06-63a4-5f96-bdae-040954f5215d'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The variance inflation factor (VIF) is used to detect:', '["Outliers","Normality","Multicollinearity","Heteroscedasticity","Independence"]'::jsonb, 'Multicollinearity', 'The VIF quantifies how much multicollinearity inflates the variance of a regression coefficient estimate. A VIF above about 5 or 10 is commonly taken as evidence of problematic multicollinearity.'),
('cf380344-1a5b-521f-a677-4d4583316b6f'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Heteroscedasticity in regression means that the residuals have:', '["Constant variance","Non-constant variance of residuals","Normal residuals","Independent residuals","Zero residuals"]'::jsonb, 'Non-constant variance of residuals', 'Heteroscedasticity occurs when the variance of the residuals is not constant across levels of the predicted values or predictors. It violates the homoscedasticity assumption and can distort the standard errors.'),
('892ca79c-e037-5968-9182-2546a19992b3'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'In linear regression, homoscedasticity means that the residuals have:', '["Outcome is binary","Predictors are independent","Residuals are normal","Residuals have zero mean","Residuals have constant variance"]'::jsonb, 'Residuals have constant variance', 'Homoscedasticity is the assumption that the variance of the residuals is constant across all levels of the predicted values. Violation of this assumption is called heteroscedasticity.'),
('52a0bbe2-24cd-5a42-b1af-477d0100a10b'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'In regression, residuals are defined as:', '["Observed - predicted values","Mean - observed values","Log of observed","Observed values/mean","Predicted - observed values"]'::jsonb, 'Observed - predicted values', 'A residual is the difference between the observed outcome and the value predicted by the regression model (observed minus predicted). Examining residuals helps assess model fit and the validity of assumptions.'),
('9122569f-e495-5bf9-81bf-947ac66cf5e1'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Studentized residuals are used to detect:', '["Multicollinearity","Heteroscedasticity","Non-normality","Independence","Outliers in regression"]'::jsonb, 'Outliers in regression', 'Studentized residuals divide each residual by its estimated standard error, identifying observations that are unusually far from the model fit. Large studentized residuals flag potential outliers and influential points.'),
('27cb9e6a-0cb1-5284-93f0-da9093d6d0eb'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Cook''s distance measures the:', '["Multicollinearity resulting in decreased functional activity","Heteroscedasticity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Prediction accuracy mediated by specific receptor activation","Influence of each data point on regression coefficients","Normality leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Influence of each data point on regression coefficients', 'Cook''s distance quantifies how much the regression coefficients change when a single observation is removed. Large values identify influential points that disproportionately determine the fitted model.'),
('db040d30-00c3-53fb-8b81-7558c082a9d9'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The Hamilton Depression Rating Scale (HAMD) total score is best classified as:', '["Ratio","Continuous","Interval","Nominal","Ordinal"]'::jsonb, 'Ordinal', 'Rating scale total scores rank subjects on an ordered continuum, but the intervals between successive scores are not equal, so they are treated as ordinal data. Parametric analyses may be applied only when distributional assumptions hold.'),
('e87ef7dd-9484-52fc-8c10-76da7be0e360'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The APGAR score is best classified as:', '["Continuous","Ratio","Interval","Nominal","Ordinal"]'::jsonb, 'Ordinal', 'The APGAR score is the sum of ordered component ratings (each 0 to 2 across five domains), producing ranked scores with unequal intervals. It is therefore treated as ordinal data.'),
('9dfc0610-12fd-53dd-bcf1-e7d6a0d069c7'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS) score is best classified as:', '["Interval","Continuous","Ratio","Ordinal","Nominal"]'::jsonb, 'Ordinal', 'The GCS is the sum of ordered subscale scores for eye, verbal, and motor responses, with the intervals between total scores not being equal. It is conventionally regarded as ordinal data.'),
('7ea8f6d5-7556-593f-b84d-4eebfdb88fcd'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Temperature measured in Kelvin is best classified as:', '["Ratio","Continuous","Ordinal","Nominal","Interval"]'::jsonb, 'Ratio', 'The Kelvin scale has a true absolute zero point, so ratios of values are meaningful and Kelvin temperature is ratio data. In contrast, Celsius and Fahrenheit scales lack a true zero and are interval data.'),
('47ed2cb7-92bf-5f71-a9e0-e7bf26fc588d'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Year of birth is best classified as:', '["Nominal","Interval","Ratio","Ordinal","Continuous"]'::jsonb, 'Interval', 'Year of birth has equal intervals but no true zero point, as the chosen zero is arbitrary, so it is interval data. Adding or subtracting years is meaningful, but ratios such as ''twice a year'' are not.'),
('a26e1cc6-4f43-5732-b337-f2b7a0d8c615'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Which t-test is used to compare blood pressure before and after treatment in the same patients?', '["Paired t-test","Independent t-test","Unpaired t-test","One-sample t-test","Two-sample t-test"]'::jsonb, 'Paired t-test', 'Measurements taken twice on the same subjects are paired, so the paired t-test is used to compare the mean of the before-after differences. It accounts for the correlation between the repeated readings.'),
('55c87d7c-20a2-52a9-b17f-10d437516b53'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Which t-test compares the mean BMI between males and females?', '["McNemar","Independent t-test","ANOVA","One-sample t-test","Paired t-test"]'::jsonb, 'Independent t-test', 'Males and females constitute two independent groups, so the independent (unpaired) t-test is used to compare their mean BMI. A paired test would be appropriate only for measurements taken on the same subjects.'),
('2c7f32a4-3dcd-53e2-90ae-8520e9e9926b'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Cohen''sd, a measure of effect size, is calculated as:', '["(Mean1 - Mean2)/Pooled SD","SD/√n","Mean1/Mean2","(Mean1 - Mean2)/SEM","(Median1 - Median2)/IQR"]'::jsonb, '(Mean1 - Mean2)/Pooled SD', 'Cohen''sd standardizes the difference between two group means by the pooled standard deviation, giving an effect size independent of sample size. Values around 0.2, 0.5, and 0.8 indicate small, medium, and large effects.'),
('c403453e-6588-5982-9a5a-4e4b13c49a94'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'A Cohen''sd value of 0.8 is conventionally considered:', '["Medium effect","Negligible effect","Very large effect","Large effect","Small effect"]'::jsonb, 'Large effect', 'Cohen''s conventions define 0.2 as a small, 0.5 as a medium, and 0.8 as a large effect size. Ad of 0.8 therefore represents a large standardized mean difference.'),
('01b00aba-c00f-5086-bb90-c61f842baf8d'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The number needed to treat (NNT) is a measure of:', '["Clinical relevance and effect","Precision","Statistical significance","Effect size","Sample size"]'::jsonb, 'Clinical relevance and effect', 'The NNT is the number of patients who need to be treated to prevent one additional adverse outcome, reflecting the clinical magnitude of a treatment''s effect. It translates statistical findings into a clinically interpretable figure.'),
('22d6cde7-d026-5941-8655-62694cae165d'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'In a clinical trial the number needed to treat (NNT) is 5. This means that:', '["1 in 5 patients benefit from treatment","5 events are prevented","5 patients must be treated for one to benefit","5% of patients benefit from treatment","The treatment is 5 times more effective than placebo"]'::jsonb, '5 patients must be treated for one to benefit', 'NNT is the reciprocal of the absolute risk reduction (1/ARR). An NNT of 5 means 5 patients must receive the treatment for one additional patient to benefit from it.'),
('e4068027-7cad-5b56-a7f1-656e5e3db669'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'An absolute risk reduction (ARR) of 10% corresponds to a number needed to treat (NNT) of:', '["10","20","1","5","100"]'::jsonb, '10', 'NNT is the reciprocal of the absolute risk reduction expressed as a proportion: 1/0.10 = 10. Ten patients must be treated to prevent one additional outcome.'),
('ca04adda-b4f0-505f-997d-ab05cae05492'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'A relative risk (RR) of 1.0 means that:', '["Risk is equal in both groups","Risk is decreased","Risk is doubled","Risk is increased","Risk is halved"]'::jsonb, 'Risk is equal in both groups', 'A relative risk of 1.0 indicates no difference in the risk of the outcome between the exposed and unexposed groups.'),
('d35b38c6-b809-5a9f-b212-fb276dfde9c4'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'An odds ratio (OR) of 1.5 with a 95% confidence interval of 0.9-2.5 indicates:', '["A protective effect","Significant decreased odds","Statistically significant increased odds","Non-significant increased odds","No association between exposure and disease"]'::jsonb, 'Non-significant increased odds', 'The 95% confidence interval (0.9 to 2.5) includes 1.0, so the elevated odds ratio of 1.5 is not statistically significant.'),
('a021a8c5-c241-58dd-844f-50bd2fb56dad'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The correlation between height and weight is an example of:', '["Confounding","Interaction","Independence","Association","Causation"]'::jsonb, 'Association', 'Correlation is a statistical measure of association between two continuous variables. Correlation does not by itself establish causation.'),
('ec95b682-4e3b-51a5-ae66-c55cc8747c1d'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Stratified analysis (Mantel-Haenszel) is used to:', '["Test normality","Measure kappa","Assess and adjust for confounding","Assess publication bias","Calculate sample size"]'::jsonb, 'Assess and adjust for confounding', 'The Mantel-Haenszel method pools data across strata and compares crude with adjusted estimates to detect and remove the influence of confounding.'),
('80a4ead2-13d3-5c9e-899f-4a56576905cf'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'A case-control study reports an odds ratio of 1.8 for the association between exposure and disease. The Mantel-Haenszel odds ratio is a:', '["Prevalence OR resulting in decreased functional activity","Logistic regression OR accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Crude OR mediated by specific receptor activation","Adjusted OR (weighted average across strata)","Simple OR leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Adjusted OR (weighted average across strata)', 'The Mantel-Haenszel odds ratio combines stratum-specific odds ratios into a single weighted average, thereby providing an estimate adjusted for the stratifying variables.'),
('63fd4ae0-658e-5949-9c64-79fddeb212e6'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'If the crude OR differs from the adjusted OR, this suggests that:', '["Heterogeneity is present","Confounding is present","Effect modification is present","Selection bias is present","Random variation is present"]'::jsonb, 'Confounding is present', 'A material difference between the crude and adjusted estimates indicates that a confounder has distorted the crude association, and adjustment has removed its influence.'),
('7e4277ac-f817-5767-a4d5-f828080f8085'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Standardization is a method used to:', '["Adjust for age differences between populations","Reduce measurement error","Reduce recall bias","Blind the observer","Increase sample size"]'::jsonb, 'Adjust for age differences between populations', 'Standardization applies a common reference population so that differences in age structure do not distort comparisons of rates between populations.'),
('9ea4e50c-1a27-546b-b19d-d7e632104705'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The standardized mortality ratio (SMR) uses:', '["Risk ratios","Indirect standardization","Correlation","Crude rates","Direct standardization"]'::jsonb, 'Indirect standardization', 'The SMR compares observed deaths in the study population with expected deaths derived by applying standard (reference) rates, which is indirect standardization.'),
('e310bcd4-e6bf-50d1-b434-d158f463aa33'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'An age-standardized rate allows comparison of:', '["Population sizes resulting in decreased functional activity","Absolute numbers of cases accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Time trends mediated by specific receptor activation","Populations with different age structures","Geographic areas leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Populations with different age structures', 'Age-standardization removes the effect of differing age composition, permitting valid comparison of rates across populations with different age structures.'),
('9d0a2e6c-a559-5a5e-bc05-e9ba0fb6e681'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Indirect standardization is preferred when:', '["All ages are equally represented resulting in decreased functional activity","Comparing two studies accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Standard population rates are unknown mediated by specific receptor activation","Age-specific rates in the study population are unstable (small numbers)","Data are complete leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Age-specific rates in the study population are unstable (small numbers)', 'Indirect standardization is used when the study population''s stratum-specific rates are unreliable, often because of small numbers, by applying standard rates to it.'),
('02a89b06-ab25-5bd1-b63c-20f0af8280c0'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Randomization (random allocation) is a method to control for:', '["Unknown confounders","Known confounders","Measurement error","Known confounders only","Both known and unknown confounders"]'::jsonb, 'Both known and unknown confounders', 'Random allocation distributes both known and unknown confounding variables evenly between groups by chance, so their influence is balanced across the arms.'),
('02d2d11a-02bd-554c-b1d2-4cf681679978'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The main purpose of matching is to:', '["Eliminate selection bias resulting in decreased functional activity","Improve measurement accuracy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Control confounding by equalizing confounders across groups","Reduce cost without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Increase sample size leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Control confounding by equalizing confounders across groups', 'Matching selects controls comparable to cases for characteristics such as age and sex, so these confounders are distributed equally across the groups.'),
('054121df-c533-5eed-9882-8a129ee18368'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'In 1:1 matched case-control studies, the analysis uses:', '["Chi-square test","Paired t-test","ANOVA","McNemar''s test","Unpaired t-test"]'::jsonb, 'McNemar''s test', '1:1 matched case-control data form paired dichotomous observations, which are compared using McNemar''s test for paired proportions.'),
('1514759e-a46d-5a02-a27d-80a6f0caa9e4'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'What is the consequence of overmatching?', '["Better control of confounding","Smaller sample needed","Loss of efficiency","Increased precision","Reduced bias"]'::jsonb, 'Loss of efficiency', 'Overmatching on a variable related to exposure but not disease reduces the number of usable pairs and lowers precision, causing a loss of study efficiency.'),
('e1830797-a613-5331-85b1-f13c6904c148'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Overmatching can occur when matching is done on:', '["Occupation resulting in decreased functional activity","Age accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Gender mediated by specific receptor activation","An intermediate variable on the causal pathway","Socioeconomic status leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An intermediate variable on the causal pathway', 'Matching on a variable that lies on the causal pathway between exposure and disease removes part of the true effect of the exposure, which is overmatching.'),
('6ff439f5-0a4c-55de-ae61-9fa2df985403'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Individual matching matches each case to:', '["Random controls resulting in decreased functional activity","Hospital controls accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Population controls mediated by specific receptor activation","One or more specific controls with similar characteristics","A group of controls leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'One or more specific controls with similar characteristics', 'In individual matching, each case is paired with one or more specific controls who share prescribed characteristics such as age and sex.'),
('37903e8a-3605-5d83-ae2c-6fd726b37417'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Frequency matching (group matching) ensures that:', '["Controls are from hospitals resulting in decreased functional activity","Controls are randomly selected accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The overall distribution of the matching factor is similar in cases and controls","Matched pairs analysis is used without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Each case has a matched control leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The overall distribution of the matching factor is similar in cases and controls', 'Frequency (group) matching ensures that the aggregate proportion of the matching factor is similar in cases and controls, without individual pairing.'),
('59faa4b2-af76-5466-8424-a55e35a6dd3d'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'A diagnostic test has 60% sensitivity and 80% specificity. If it is applied to 1000 subjects of whom 200 are diseased, how many true positives are detected?', '["120","160","200","80","400"]'::jsonb, '120', 'True positives equal sensitivity multiplied by the number of diseased subjects: 0.60 × 200 = 120.'),
('5c5d1ec8-d8e3-5b3b-b9e8-19d6c5c4eb97'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Which measure of disease frequency is most useful for assessing the burden of chronic disease in a community?', '["Case fatality","Incidence density","Prevalence","Incidence","Attack rate"]'::jsonb, 'Prevalence', 'Prevalence reflects the proportion of existing cases at a point in time and best represents the total burden of chronic disease in a population.'),
('fc14c750-773f-59c7-94ff-8dcaec92375f'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'A survey of 20 000 individuals finds the prevalence of a disease to be 5%. For acute diseases of short duration, incidence and prevalence are related such that:', '["They are similar","Incidence = 0","Prevalence > Incidence","Prevalence = 0","Prevalence < Incidence"]'::jsonb, 'Prevalence < Incidence', 'Prevalence depends on both incidence and disease duration. For short-duration acute diseases, prevalence is lower relative to incidence.'),
('7a941fa4-61d3-5caf-9448-947ef86176d1'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'A survey of 2000 individuals finds that 250 have a particular condition. When disease duration is long, prevalence relative to incidence is:', '["Inverse","Higher","Lower","Equal","Unrelated"]'::jsonb, 'Higher', 'Prevalence increases as disease duration lengthens; for chronic diseases of long duration, prevalence is high relative to incidence.'),
('6bf00cc5-f059-5e26-9976-970227ee24e8'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Which type of error involves accepting a false null hypothesis (false negative result)?', '["Measurement error","Type I error","Type II error","Systematic error","Sampling error"]'::jsonb, 'Type II error', 'A Type II error (beta) is failing to reject the null hypothesis when it is false, producing a false-negative conclusion.'),
('3bd81ad5-a256-572b-97b6-fe04189f0a03'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Which type of error involves rejecting a true null hypothesis (false positive result)?', '["Type I error","Random error","Confounding","Sampling error","Type II error"]'::jsonb, 'Type I error', 'A Type I error (alpha) is rejecting the null hypothesis when it is true, producing a false-positive conclusion.'),
('f24171d2-9405-5d7d-b084-f1838a8a23df'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'If alpha = 0.05, the probability of a Type I error is:', '["10%","95%","2.5%","5%","1%"]'::jsonb, '5%', 'Alpha is defined as the probability of a Type I error. An alpha of 0.05 therefore corresponds to a 5% risk of a false-positive result.'),
('292e3b59-3d27-573f-93c9-47bf289994db'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'If beta = 0.10, the power of the study is:', '["90%","95%","99%","10%","80%"]'::jsonb, '90%', 'Power equals 1 − beta. With beta = 0.10, power = 1 − 0.10 = 0.90, or 90%.'),
('1b4cd1b8-7529-5ea8-b236-1dce72fb49f0'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Statistical power depends on all of the following EXCEPT:', '["Alpha level","Bias","Effect size","Variability","Sample size"]'::jsonb, 'Bias', 'Power is determined by the alpha level, effect size, variability, and sample size. Bias does not influence statistical power.'),
('20ff6b65-aa35-5b35-901e-8158cd07cb26'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'A study with 80% power means there is:', '["A 20% chance of reaching the correct conclusion","An 80% chance of detecting a true effect if it exists","An 80% chance that subjects respond to treatment","An 80% chance of a Type I error","A 20% chance of a Type I error"]'::jsonb, 'An 80% chance of detecting a true effect if it exists', 'Power is the probability of detecting a true effect if it exists. Eighty percent power means the study would detect a genuine effect 80% of the time.'),
('c3210655-5535-5d08-9317-14b96ba8bcd7'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'To detect a smaller effect size while maintaining the same power, you need:', '["Double the alpha","A larger sample","A smaller sample","The same sample","A smaller alpha level"]'::jsonb, 'A larger sample', 'A smaller effect is more difficult to detect, so a larger sample size is required to preserve the same statistical power.'),
('4d9ba5ab-42a8-55bf-92e4-75642d540a66'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The relationship between alpha and beta is generally:', '["Inverse (higher alpha = lower beta)","Direct (higher alpha = higher beta)","The sum of alpha and beta equals 1","Alpha = Beta","Alpha = 1 − Beta"]'::jsonb, 'Inverse (higher alpha = lower beta)', 'Raising alpha increases the chance of a Type I error and decreases the probability of a Type II error (beta), so the two are inversely related.'),
('ede310e4-feb9-5761-8664-aa37a663d95b'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Compared with two-tailed tests, one-tailed tests have power to detect an effect in the specified direction:', '["Same","Variable","Unrelated","Lower","Higher"]'::jsonb, 'Higher', 'By concentrating the rejection region in a single direction, a one-tailed test allocates more power to detecting an effect in the specified direction.'),
('32ab45e9-ccd7-56a5-a0b4-541cd087e285'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'When parametric assumptions are met, non-parametric tests generally have power than parametric tests:', '["Same","Lower","Variable","Double","Higher"]'::jsonb, 'Lower', 'Non-parametric tests make fewer distributional assumptions and are less efficient, so they have lower statistical power than parametric tests when those assumptions hold.'),
('9d0fca9f-2e05-5ee3-a1bc-060c2f53bdd2'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'An intention-to-treat analysis tends to:', '["Analyze only subjects who completed the study","Analyze only per-protocol subjects","Underestimate the treatment effect","Preserve randomization","Overestimate the treatment effect"]'::jsonb, 'Preserve randomization', 'Intention-to-treat analyzes all randomized subjects according to their original allocation, thereby preserving the comparability achieved by randomization.'),
('b10c004c-bb8f-5c7d-882a-3aee982e58d4'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'An as-treated analysis analyzes subjects according to:', '["Original allocation","Completion status","Treatment actually received","Gender","Age"]'::jsonb, 'Treatment actually received', 'An as-treated (on-treatment) analysis groups subjects by the treatment they actually received rather than the treatment they were allocated to.'),
('ece6c9a8-71c2-5c97-92c6-aa5c5c03423f'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Which analysis is most conservative and typically preferred for superiority trials?', '["On-treatment","Completer","As-treated","Per-protocol","Intention-to-treat"]'::jsonb, 'Intention-to-treat', 'Intention-to-treat preserves randomization and generally yields a more conservative estimate of treatment effect, so it is the preferred primary analysis for superiority trials.'),
('36c82a0c-c96d-5de7-8de2-b03112e85297'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Subgroup analyses should be interpreted cautiously because:', '["There is often a lack of power for detecting interactions","Subgroup findings may arise by chance","Multiple testing increases the risk of a Type I error","Post-hoc subgroups may be exploratory","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Subgroup analyses typically have limited power for interaction effects, inflate the Type I error rate through multiple testing, and are often exploratory, so all of the listed concerns apply.'),
('a9120743-36be-5560-9e1f-69c041eacc2b'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Which graph is most appropriate for showing the relationship between two continuous variables?', '["Box plot","Scatter plot","Pie chart","Bar chart","Histogram"]'::jsonb, 'Scatter plot', 'A scatter plot displays pairs of values from two continuous variables, revealing the direction and strength of their relationship.'),
('669ccfa3-ceaa-50a4-a717-b1ce0cfb3aee'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Which graph displays the median, interquartile range (IQR), and potential outliers?', '["Scatter plot","Bar chart","Box plot","Histogram","Q-Q plot"]'::jsonb, 'Box plot', 'A box plot summarizes the median, interquartile range, and any values that fall beyond the whiskers, which are flagged as potential outliers.'),
('e855268a-2afb-5425-939e-5efeb24c5cfd'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Which graph is used to assess the normality of data?', '["Pie chart","Histogram or Q-Q plot","Box plot","Scatter plot","Bar chart"]'::jsonb, 'Histogram or Q-Q plot', 'A histogram or a quantile-quantile (Q-Q) plot compares the observed data distribution with the expected normal distribution, allowing normality to be assessed visually.'),
('ffb7d01a-7276-5b7d-b79c-201398d9d86f'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Which graph is used to compare frequencies across categories?', '["Bar chart","Histogram","Scatter plot","Box plot","Line graph"]'::jsonb, 'Bar chart', 'A bar chart displays counts or frequencies for each category, making comparisons between categorical groups straightforward.'),
('78892ac6-0dd8-5497-b454-c472cc64de44'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Which non-parametric test compares two independent groups?', '["Kruskal-Wallis test","McNemar test","Mann-Whitney U test","Friedman test","Wilcoxon signed-rank test"]'::jsonb, 'Mann-Whitney U test', 'The Mann-Whitney U test is the non-parametric analogue of the unpaired t-test, comparing two independent groups based on ranks.'),
('aec822b3-b382-5af4-8ad9-7f19b6d808fc'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Which non-parametric test compares two paired groups?', '["Friedman test","Mann-Whitney U test","Wilcoxon signed-rank test","Kruskal-Wallis test","Chi-square test"]'::jsonb, 'Wilcoxon signed-rank test', 'The Wilcoxon signed-rank test is the non-parametric analogue of the paired t-test, comparing two related groups using the ranks of their differences.'),
('ab6ea2a7-78b8-5ab5-8c50-0da8fc48fd67'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Which non-parametric test compares three or more independent groups?', '["Friedman test","Wilcoxon signed-rank test","Kruskal-Wallis test","Mann-Whitney U test","McNemar test"]'::jsonb, 'Kruskal-Wallis test', 'The Kruskal-Wallis test is the non-parametric analogue of one-way ANOVA, comparing three or more independent groups.'),
('931e0221-6d3e-5a80-b68d-d454616b3b8c'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Which non-parametric test compares three or more paired groups?', '["Friedman test","Chi-square test","Kruskal-Wallis test","Mann-Whitney U test","Wilcoxon signed-rank test"]'::jsonb, 'Friedman test', 'The Friedman test is the non-parametric analogue of repeated-measures ANOVA, comparing three or more related (paired) groups.'),
('394acbd0-8c93-5bc8-8324-170f04b86de4'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The Spearman rank correlation coefficient is denoted by:', '["τ (tau)","φ (phi)","ρ (rho)","Cramér''sV","r"]'::jsonb, 'ρ (rho)', 'Spearman rank correlation is conventionally denoted by the Greek letter rho (ρ), in contrast to r for Pearson correlation.'),
('18557d6d-43f9-5f73-9b5b-f26ca851fd1b'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Kendall''s tau is an alternative to:', '["Pearson correlation","The t-test","Spearman correlation","ANOVA","The chi-square test"]'::jsonb, 'Spearman correlation', 'Both Kendall''s tau and Spearman''s rho are rank-based measures of association between two variables, so tau is an alternative to Spearman correlation.'),
('3824ed37-cb66-52f6-9d47-ff2982bf6dd2'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'In a study that follows 200 patients with cancer to estimate survival, which plot shows survival probability over time?', '["Bar chart","Histogram","Box plot","Scatter plot","Kaplan-Meier curve"]'::jsonb, 'Kaplan-Meier curve', 'The Kaplan-Meier curve estimates the probability of survival (or another event-free outcome) over time while accounting for censored observations.'),
('d81d7aa2-8a80-53ee-857c-3c0be2261526'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'In a Kaplan-Meier survival curve, a steeper drop at a given time indicates:', '["Higher event rate at that time","Censoring","Lower event rate at that time","Better prognosis","Normal survival"]'::jsonb, 'Higher event rate at that time', 'A steep vertical drop in a Kaplan-Meier curve represents the occurrence of events (such as deaths) at that time point. A larger drop therefore reflects a higher event rate during that interval.'),
('4fe49766-2d29-5a47-ad60-567d4a112a94'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The Nelson-Aalen estimator is an alternative to:', '["Chi-square test","Kaplan-Meier for survival estimation","Cox regression","Logistic regression","Linear regression"]'::jsonb, 'Kaplan-Meier for survival estimation', 'The Nelson-Aalen estimator non-parametrically estimates the cumulative hazard function, from which the survival function can be derived. It is used in the same settings as the Kaplan-Meier method.'),
('6780a363-5f68-584c-ae5a-42b01c828b13'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Which of the following is NOT a method used to handle missing data?', '["Last observation carried forward","Intention-to-treat analysis","Complete case analysis","Multiple imputation","Mean imputation"]'::jsonb, 'Intention-to-treat analysis', 'LOCF, complete case analysis, and imputation are accepted approaches for handling missing data. Intention-to-treat is an analytical principle for randomized trials, not a missing-data technique.'),
('6fa11c1c-c070-508e-bdba-b311e0a880af'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Multiple imputation involves:', '["Deleting missing cases resulting in decreased functional activity","Replacing with mean accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Creating a single imputed dataset mediated by specific receptor activation","Creating multiple complete datasets and combining results","Ignoring missing data leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Creating multiple complete datasets and combining results', 'In multiple imputation, several imputed datasets are generated, each is analyzed separately, and the results are pooled using Rubin''s rules. Pooling accounts for the uncertainty introduced by the imputed values.'),
('138bdb10-f919-5297-9ede-1029fb2bf9aa'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Last observation carried forward (LOCF) is used in:', '["Case-control studies","Cross-sectional studies","Longitudinal studies with dropouts","Cohort studies","Diagnostic studies"]'::jsonb, 'Longitudinal studies with dropouts', 'In longitudinal studies, LOCF fills a participant''s missing follow-up values using their last measured value. It is common but can introduce bias when dropout is not random.'),
('d3c64ec5-724c-5810-ac66-a1017313aee0'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Complete case analysis (listwise deletion) can cause bias when:', '["Sample is large","Missing data depends on observed values (MAR)","Data is MCAR (missing completely at random)","Missing data is MNAR (missing not at random)","Missing values are replaced by the mean"]'::jsonb, 'Missing data depends on observed values (MAR)', 'Complete case analysis gives unbiased estimates only when data are missing completely at random (MCAR). When missingness depends on observed values (MAR), the analyzed sample is no longer representative and results become biased.'),
('b81c19b8-cb67-5cfd-9da4-5261d39946ab'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The sample size for a study should be determined based on:', '["Number of patients available","Funding available","Primary research question and analysis","Past studies","Convenience"]'::jsonb, 'Primary research question and analysis', 'Sample size must be calculated from the primary research question, expected effect size, and planned analysis to ensure adequate power. Funding and convenience should not determine the sample size.'),
('1596bb1b-d59f-5591-958c-fd908c45bd31'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Which software is commonly used for statistical analysis in medical research?', '["SPSS resulting in decreased functional activity","STATA accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","R mediated by specific receptor activation","SAS without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'SPSS, STATA, R, and SAS are all widely used statistical programs in medical research. Therefore every listed option is correct.'),
('0bf15a70-e5a3-56f7-87fe-f0bd238ae32f'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The purpose of a pilot study is to:', '["Generalize results","Provide definitive results","Replace the main study","Test the main hypothesis","Test feasibility and refine study procedures"]'::jsonb, 'Test feasibility and refine study procedures', 'A pilot study evaluates the feasibility of recruitment, procedures, and measurements and helps refine the protocol before the main study. It is not intended to provide definitive results.'),
('b10cc181-0f48-5202-b1ca-0bc01e89e247'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Error bars in graphs typically represent:', '["Mean ± SEM resulting in decreased functional activity","Mean ± SD accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","95% CI mediated by specific receptor activation","Median ± IQR without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All of the above depending on context"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above depending on context', 'Error bars can convey the mean ± SD, the mean ± SEM, a 95% CI, or the median ± IQR, depending on what the author chooses. Since any of these may be displayed, the meaning depends on the stated convention.'),
('fd5c9d2d-c292-5c3d-9a9a-9a91c5d41755'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The difference between the standard deviation (SD) and the standard error of the mean (SEM) is that:', '["SD measures precision, SEM measures variability resulting in decreased functional activity","SD = SEM × n accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","SD measures variability in data, SEM measures precision of the mean estimate","SD < SEM without affecting the primary tissue pathway","They are the same leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'SD measures variability in data, SEM measures precision of the mean estimate', 'The SD describes the spread of individual observations in the sample, while the SEM (SD divided by the square root of n) quantifies the precision of the estimated mean. The SEM is therefore always smaller than the SD.'),
('cb91c278-528b-5972-87e9-99791f275499'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Statistical inference involves:', '["Describing sample characteristics resulting in decreased functional activity","Summarizing data accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Data entry mediated by specific receptor activation","Drawing conclusions about population from sample data","Collecting data leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Drawing conclusions about population from sample data', 'Statistical inference uses sample data to draw conclusions about the underlying population through estimation and hypothesis testing. Simply describing or summarizing the sample is descriptive rather than inferential.'),
('338be062-6256-5111-924a-fc86a637cb0d'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Descriptive statistics include:', '["Mean, median, mode, SD, range","Chi-square test","Regression","t-test and ANOVA","Survival analysis"]'::jsonb, 'Mean, median, mode, SD, range', 'Measures of central tendency (mean, median, mode) and dispersion (SD, range) summarize the data at hand. Chi-square, t-test, ANOVA, and regression are inferential techniques.'),
('0bbc20c9-c8e9-5d01-8ced-279d3e5e79b7'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Which of the following are examples of inferential statistics?', '["R-squared","Standard deviation","Percentage","t-tests, ANOVA, regression","Mean and median"]'::jsonb, 't-tests, ANOVA, regression', 't-tests, ANOVA, and regression draw conclusions about populations from sample data. The SD, mean, median, and percentages are descriptive measures.'),
('4cb353e8-bb7a-5ff0-98c8-c19e7e46b0aa'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Which of the following is a distribution-free (non-parametric) test?', '["ANOVA","Linear regression","Pearson correlation","t-test","Wilcoxon signed-rank test"]'::jsonb, 'Wilcoxon signed-rank test', 'The Wilcoxon signed-rank test makes no assumption about the underlying distribution of the data, making it non-parametric. ANOVA, t-test, Pearson correlation, and linear regression are parametric methods.'),
('788707c5-d499-5c4e-90e2-8a97ac4a9b96'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The null hypothesis of a one-way ANOVA is that:', '["All group means differ","Group variances are equal","All group means are equal","At least one group mean differs","The group mean equals zero"]'::jsonb, 'All group means are equal', 'ANOVA tests the null hypothesis that all group means are equal. Rejection of this null hypothesis implies that at least one group mean differs from the others.'),
('8738f709-6a17-5198-b89e-01be5021f370'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'A significant ANOVA result indicates that:', '["The null hypothesis is true","Group variances are unequal","At least one group differs from the others","The median differs","All groups differ from each other"]'::jsonb, 'At least one group differs from the others', 'A significant ANOVA only shows that not all group means are equal, so the null hypothesis is rejected. Post-hoc tests are required to identify which specific groups differ.'),
('70ead3b2-2bd4-59f0-bed9-9c1dfa7b39f6'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The ANOVA F-statistic is calculated as:', '["Mean1 - Mean2","Within-group variance / Between-group variance","Between-group variance / Within-group variance","Total variance / Between-group variance","SD1/SD2"]'::jsonb, 'Between-group variance / Within-group variance', 'The F-statistic is the ratio of between-group variance to within-group variance. Large between-group variability relative to within-group variability produces a large F value.'),
('d3139d91-d761-5ca8-8151-b631ba2464de'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'If most of the variation is between groups (large F), the p-value is likely to be:', '["Depends on the sample","Equal to one","Small (significant)","Large (non-significant)","Equal to zero"]'::jsonb, 'Small (significant)', 'A large F reflects large between-group differences relative to within-group variability, which makes the result statistically significant with a small p-value.'),
('f8f9a7e8-9f85-5a2d-b639-298171f8b758'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'In repeated measures ANOVA, the same subjects are measured:', '["Only once resulting in decreased functional activity","Independently accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Randomly mediated by specific receptor activation","At multiple time points or conditions","In different groups leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'At multiple time points or conditions', 'Repeated measures ANOVA is used when the same participants are assessed on several occasions or under several conditions. This approach accounts for the correlation between a subject''s repeated observations.'),
('2834aea7-faa1-51e5-805f-29cae1779650'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The sphericity assumption in repeated measures ANOVA means that:', '["Normal distribution resulting in decreased functional activity","Equal variances of differences between conditions","Equal group sizes mediated by specific receptor activation","Independence without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Constant mean leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Equal variances of differences between conditions', 'Sphericity requires that the variances of the differences between all pairs of conditions are equal. A violation inflates the F-test, and a correction such as Greenhouse-Geisser is then used.'),
('56cbdd2c-736a-522b-b3cd-7c2b2830a799'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Mauchly''s test is used to test:', '["Homogeneity of variances","Independence","Normality","Outliers","Sphericity"]'::jsonb, 'Sphericity', 'Mauchly''s test assesses the sphericity assumption in repeated measures ANOVA. A significant result indicates that the sphericity assumption has been violated.'),
('ee5cd6dc-b5f7-5225-9213-d77309816c6d'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'If Mauchly''s test is significant, you should use:', '["t-test","Non-parametric test","Standard ANOVA","Greenhouse-Geisser correction","Ignore it"]'::jsonb, 'Greenhouse-Geisser correction', 'When sphericity is violated, the degrees of freedom are adjusted using the Greenhouse-Geisser (or Huynh-Feldt) correction. This adjustment yields a valid F-test for the repeated measures ANOVA.'),
('13aeeb04-9c7b-535e-9c85-c4cd9cb96467'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Which method is used to compare two groups on a binary outcome while adjusting for covariates?', '["ANOVA","Linear regression","Paired t-test","Logistic regression","Chi-square test"]'::jsonb, 'Logistic regression', 'Logistic regression models the probability of a binary outcome and can adjust for multiple covariates simultaneously. ANOVA, t-tests, and chi-square cannot accommodate covariate adjustment in this manner.'),
('d4efcd38-271f-5cbf-8266-bd319786a229'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Which method compares the means of two groups while adjusting for baseline differences?', '["t-test resulting in decreased functional activity","Chi-square accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","ANCOVA (Analysis of Covariance)","Logistic regression without affecting the primary tissue pathway","ANOVA leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'ANCOVA (Analysis of Covariance)', 'ANCOVA combines ANOVA with regression to compare group means while statistically controlling for one or more baseline covariates. It adjusts for baseline imbalance between the groups.'),
('51b31c72-e9e9-5a23-a272-88ef3c6a274a'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'ANCOVA combines:', '["t-test and chi-square","ANOVA and regression","Regression and correlation","Logistic and linear","Survival and regression"]'::jsonb, 'ANOVA and regression', 'ANCOVA merges the principles of ANOVA and regression: it tests group mean differences while regressing out the effect of covariates.'),
('24db83b5-e7fe-5508-b6d5-785c8f58b57e'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The propensity score is used to:', '["Increase sample size resulting in decreased functional activity","Test normality accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Reduce confounding in observational studies","Increase power without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Measure correlation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Reduce confounding in observational studies', 'The propensity score is the conditional probability of receiving treatment given observed covariates. Matching or weighting by it balances treated and untreated groups and reduces confounding in observational studies.'),
('5dacbd51-2d9d-53d4-8a84-3fd2b1ff6954'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Propensity score matching matches treated and untreated subjects with:', '["Similar confounders","Similar exposures","Similar outcomes","Similar ages","Similar propensity scores"]'::jsonb, 'Similar propensity scores', 'Propensity score matching pairs treated and untreated subjects who have similar probabilities of receiving treatment given their covariates. This creates comparable groups in non-randomized studies.'),
('fd8a00a8-8c81-5bfc-9e45-7a62d0c3175a'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Instrumental variable analysis is used to:', '["Control for measured confounding only","Control for unmeasured confounding","Increase sample size","Reduce measurement error","Improve blinding"]'::jsonb, 'Control for unmeasured confounding', 'Instrumental variable analysis can produce unbiased effect estimates using an instrument related to the exposure but independent of unmeasured confounders. It is a key approach when randomization is infeasible.'),
('94aa7cd3-0eaa-5623-9532-b0e14c805b69'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'A valid instrument must:', '["Be strongly associated with the exposure","Affect the outcome only through the exposure (exclusion restriction)","Be independent of unmeasured confounders","Have no direct effect on the outcome","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Instrumental variable validity requires relevance (association with the exposure), independence from confounders, and the exclusion restriction (no effect on the outcome except through the exposure). All three conditions are required.'),
('b8314573-5f21-567b-9989-eb1c2bfe911a'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Difference-in-differences (DID) analysis is used in:', '["Cross-sectional studies","Quasi-experimental studies","RCTs","Case series","Case-control studies"]'::jsonb, 'Quasi-experimental studies', 'DID compares changes over time between treated and untreated groups while controlling for pre-existing trends. It is commonly applied when randomization is not possible, as in quasi-experimental designs.'),
('c1d5e65e-6130-5ad0-97c6-ab1559e0ed20'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Broken line (piecewise) regression is used when:', '["Relationship is non-linear throughout resulting in decreased functional activity","Sample is small accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Data is categorical mediated by specific receptor activation","Relationship is linear without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The relationship between X and Y changes at a threshold point"]'::jsonb, 'The relationship between X and Y changes at a threshold point', 'Piecewise regression fits separate linear segments joined at a breakpoint, modeling data in which the X-Y relationship changes at a threshold. It is used when a single straight-line model is inadequate.'),
('75d1d0d7-2ab8-5a04-a760-d2fdf7ef4e80'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Receiver operating characteristic (ROC) curve analysis is used to:', '["Compare group means resulting in decreased functional activity","Assess survival accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Test correlation mediated by specific receptor activation","Measure agreement without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Assess diagnostic test performance across cut-offs"]'::jsonb, 'Assess diagnostic test performance across cut-offs', 'The ROC curve plots sensitivity against 1 - specificity across all possible cut-offs of a diagnostic test. It is used to evaluate test accuracy, compare tests, and select an optimal threshold.'),
('dfd479a0-e6cd-58f5-a805-e81ce37dc88b'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'A Bland-Altman plot is used to:', '["Compare means resulting in decreased functional activity","Test correlation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Assess agreement between two measurement methods","Assess normality without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Display distributions leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Assess agreement between two measurement methods', 'The Bland-Altman plot displays the difference between two measurements against their mean for each subject. It is the standard method for quantifying agreement and bias between measurement methods.'),
('ebd6915c-8c2d-5332-a72e-59e587d48f73'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'In a Bland-Altman plot, if most points lie within the limits of agreement:', '["Correlation is high","There is poor agreement","One method is biased","There is systematic error","The two methods agree sufficiently"]'::jsonb, 'The two methods agree sufficiently', 'The Bland-Altman limits of agreement (mean difference ± 1.96 SD) define the range within which 95% of the differences fall. If most points lie within them, the two methods agree sufficiently for clinical use.'),
('9d3a86eb-2922-5f6a-96b5-4468a8e64d3b'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The kappa statistic measures:', '["Correlation between variables","Effect size","Sample size adequacy","Agreement beyond chance between raters","Difference between means"]'::jsonb, 'Agreement beyond chance between raters', 'Cohen''s kappa quantifies inter-rater agreement after correcting for the agreement expected by chance alone. A value of 1 indicates perfect agreement, and 0 indicates agreement no better than chance.'),
('a45d8257-e090-52af-9132-61160a611621'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'A kappa value of 0 indicates:', '["Substantial agreement","Agreement equivalent to chance","Perfect disagreement between raters","Perfect agreement","Poor agreement"]'::jsonb, 'Agreement equivalent to chance', 'A kappa of 0 means that the observed agreement is no better than what chance alone would predict. It does not necessarily imply systematic disagreement.'),
('15d41639-531c-5690-bcd4-cfb6bc7f9fa5'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'A kappa value of 1 indicates:', '["Moderate agreement","Slight agreement","Perfect agreement","Agreement by chance","Poor agreement"]'::jsonb, 'Perfect agreement', 'A kappa of 1 occurs when the raters are in complete agreement. Because kappa is corrected for chance, perfect observed agreement yields the maximum possible score of 1.'),
('6081247e-0750-5d0b-84c3-b443de353acc'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'A kappa value of 0.4 generally indicates:', '["Fair to moderate agreement","Almost perfect agreement","Poor agreement","Substantial agreement","Perfect agreement"]'::jsonb, 'Fair to moderate agreement', 'Under the Landis and Koch scale, kappa values of 0.21-0.40 are considered fair and 0.41-0.60 are moderate. A value of 0.4 is therefore interpreted as fair to moderate agreement.'),
('3042df5c-d90c-5703-b44b-a3441b8bd3ed'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Weighted kappa is used when:', '["Data is continuous resulting in decreased functional activity","Disagreements have different levels of severity","There are more than 2 raters mediated by specific receptor activation","Sample is small without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Data is nominal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Disagreements have different levels of severity', 'Weighted kappa assigns partial credit to disagreements according to their severity, making it suitable for ordinal ratings. Simple unweighted kappa treats all disagreements as equally serious.'),
('dda32716-4b5a-5514-9bee-506df6b94022'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The intraclass correlation coefficient (ICC) is used for:', '["Inter-rater reliability for continuous measures","Ordinal data accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Binary outcomes mediated by specific receptor activation","Nominal data without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Survival data leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inter-rater reliability for continuous measures', 'The ICC measures reliability or agreement for continuous or quantitative measurements obtained across raters. Kappa, by contrast, is used for categorical ratings.'),
('8ecfd348-41b0-5f05-9cf6-daf84c12073c'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'ICC values range from:', '["0 to 1","0 to 100","1 to 10","-∞ to +∞","-1 to 1"]'::jsonb, '0 to 1', 'The ICC is conventionally reported on a 0 to 1 scale, with values closer to 1 indicating stronger reliability. This differs from Pearson''sr, which ranges from -1 to 1.'),
('aa4c3788-f83d-5c5b-aa59-cec414ed84c1'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'An ICC greater than 0.75 indicates:', '["Negative reliability","Moderate reliability","Good to excellent reliability","Poor reliability","No reliability"]'::jsonb, 'Good to excellent reliability', 'In common interpretive guidelines, ICC values above 0.75 represent good to excellent reliability, while values of 0.5-0.75 indicate moderate reliability. Values below 0.5 reflect poor reliability.'),
('97ef093f-1b55-5f9b-8a35-99327a530b7c'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The standard deviation of a sampling distribution is called the:', '["Range","Standard error","Variance","Mean","Standard deviation"]'::jsonb, 'Standard error', 'The standard deviation of a statistic''s sampling distribution is termed its standard error. For the mean, it equals the population SD divided by the square root of the sample size.'),
('43727893-209e-5a0d-a2e1-9be9cc8438dc'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The standard deviation of the sampling distribution of the mean depends on:', '["Population mean only","Population size","Significance level","Population SD and sample size","Sample mean only"]'::jsonb, 'Population SD and sample size', 'The standard error of the mean is computed as the population (or sample) SD divided by the square root of the sample size. It does not depend on the mean, population size, or significance level.'),
('f837404a-8769-591a-aedd-a48dd06a74c3'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The bootstrap method is used to:', '["Increase sample size resulting in decreased functional activity","Increase power accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Test normality mediated by specific receptor activation","Reduce bias without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Estimate standard errors and CIs without parametric assumptions"]'::jsonb, 'Estimate standard errors and CIs without parametric assumptions', 'The bootstrap repeatedly resamples with replacement from the observed data to approximate the sampling distribution of a statistic. It provides standard errors and confidence intervals without relying on parametric assumptions.'),
('b2669677-a15e-5968-a001-a8616528a790'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The jackknife is a resampling method similar to the bootstrap, but:', '["Each resample omits one observation at a time","It samples with replacement","It uses parametric assumptions","It is Bayesian","It is for categorical data"]'::jsonb, 'Each resample omits one observation at a time', 'The jackknife generates resamples by systematically deleting one observation at a time. Unlike the bootstrap, it does not sample with replacement.'),
('92c9a668-75d5-5111-a1dc-5832d4c88865'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'A study follows 200 patients with heart failure to estimate survival. The most appropriate method to compare survival between two groups is:', '["t-test","ANOVA","Chi-square test","Linear regression","Log-rank test"]'::jsonb, 'Log-rank test', 'The log-rank test compares survival distributions between groups while handling censored observations. It is the standard test for comparing Kaplan-Meier survival curves.'),
('28f45ba8-25ce-5e45-a009-2f0bf7212759'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'The most appropriate method to identify independent predictors of a binary outcome is:', '["Linear regression","Kaplan-Meier","ANOVA","Logistic regression","Chi-square"]'::jsonb, 'Logistic regression', 'Logistic regression models the probability of a binary outcome and estimates the independent effect of each predictor after adjustment. It is the standard method for identifying independent predictors of a binary outcome.'),
('2cfd2566-ae39-50ca-88e5-22ed6fbb709a'::uuid, 'Research Biostatistics (Paper 1)', 'Biostatistics - Biostatistics', 'Which type of error occurs when we reject a true null hypothesis?', '["Type II error (beta)","Measurement error","Sampling error","Type I error (alpha)","Confounding"]'::jsonb, 'Type I error (alpha)', 'A Type I error (alpha) is the rejection of a true null hypothesis, a false positive. In contrast, a Type II error (beta) is failing to reject a false null hypothesis.'),
('191ba71f-1999-5381-9e73-c1cc6cba532d'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Which ethical principle respects a patient''s right to self-determination?', '["Confidentiality","Justice","Autonomy","Beneficence","Non-maleficence"]'::jsonb, 'Autonomy', 'Autonomy recognizes a patient''s right to self-determination and to make informed decisions about their own care. The other four principles address harm, benefit, fairness, and privacy rather than self-governance.'),
('ee405489-9a84-52c9-b2f3-b2af9b18e577'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Passive euthanasia refers to:', '["Withholding or withdrawing life-sustaining treatment","Administering lethal injection accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Assisted suicide mediated by specific receptor activation","Pain relief only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Withholding antibiotics leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Withholding or withdrawing life-sustaining treatment', 'Passive euthanasia is the withholding or withdrawing of life-sustaining treatment so that death results from the underlying disease process. Lethal injection and assisted suicide involve deliberate acts aimed at ending life, which are distinct concepts.'),
('d1b92172-b694-5849-8b99-6c5bbb3a8748'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Non-maleficence is best described as:', '["Fidelity","Being just","Doing no harm","Respecting autonomy","Doing good"]'::jsonb, 'Doing no harm', 'Non-maleficence means avoiding harm to the patient, captured by the maxim ''primum non nocere''. Doing good describes beneficence, while fidelity, justice, and autonomy represent separate principles.'),
('f08bebd8-1387-53f1-bccb-86123163d3ec'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'A hospital must decide how to allocate scarce resources among multiple patients with different needs. Justice in medical ethics refers to:', '["Truth-telling resulting in decreased functional activity","Fair distribution of healthcare resources","Doing good mediated by specific receptor activation","Respecting patient choices without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Confidentiality leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fair distribution of healthcare resources', 'Justice requires the fair and equitable allocation of healthcare resources, especially when they are scarce and must be rationed among competing patients. Truth-telling, doing good, respecting choices, and confidentiality reflect other ethical duties.'),
('b550b29f-9650-51b0-9f4c-ea044f7b3ba2'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Which of the following is NOT a component of informed consent?', '["Alternatives","Voluntary participation","Physician''s personal opinion","Nature of the procedure","Risks and benefits"]'::jsonb, 'Physician''s personal opinion', 'Informed consent requires disclosure of the nature of the procedure, its risks and benefits, and reasonable alternatives, along with voluntary agreement. A physician''s personal opinion is not a required element of disclosure.'),
('0e4e74d1-9c93-58cf-abad-5b489cfcf8a2'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Capacity to consent requires the ability to:', '["All of the above","Communicate a choice","Appreciate the situation","Understand relevant information","Reason about options"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Capacity is assessed through four abilities: communicating a choice, understanding relevant information, appreciating the situation, and reasoning about options. Because every listed ability is required, all of the above is correct.'),
('3a78bd80-a1cf-592f-bf5c-774a77285ebf'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Competence is a determination, while capacity is a determination:', '["Clinical; ethical","Legal; medical","Ethical; legal","Medical; legal","Moral; clinical"]'::jsonb, 'Legal; medical', 'Competence is a legal determination made by a court, whereas capacity is a clinical judgment made by a physician. The two terms are related but not interchangeable.'),
('9cfc186d-d179-583a-a281-313e037995e9'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Which is NOT an exception to patient confidentiality?', '["Legal requirement (e.g. notifiable diseases)","Futility of treatment","Serious harm to third parties","Child protection concerns","Patient consent"]'::jsonb, 'Futility of treatment', 'Confidentiality may be breached for notifiable disease reporting, to protect third parties from serious harm, for child protection, and with patient consent. Futility of treatment is not a recognized exception to confidentiality.'),
('1f1e6be4-a120-5b59-9beb-15f430b01b04'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The duty to warn a third party at serious risk is established in which case?', '["Belmont Report","Bolam vs Friern","Tarasoff v Regents of California","Hippocratic Oath","Roe v Wade"]'::jsonb, 'Tarasoff v Regents of California', 'Tarasoff v Regents of the University of California established that a clinician has a duty to warn an identifiable third party who faces a serious risk of harm. The Belmont Report and Bolam relate to research ethics and the standard of care respectively.'),
('08d8ce26-2022-542a-9a19-59fc73daec88'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Active euthanasia involves:', '["Refusing treatment","Withdrawing life support","Withholding treatment","Providing pain relief that may hasten death","Administering a lethal substance to end life"]'::jsonb, 'Administering a lethal substance to end life', 'Active euthanasia is a deliberate act, such as giving a lethal injection, that directly ends the patient''s life. Withholding or withdrawing treatment and pain relief describe passive measures rather than an active act.'),
('c09ab83d-8bac-57b8-827c-604d4687a3a9'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Voluntary euthanasia is performed:', '["When patient is unconscious","On family request","Without the patient''s consent","By court order","At the patient''s competent request"]'::jsonb, 'At the patient''s competent request', 'Voluntary euthanasia is carried out only at the explicit request of a competent patient who gives informed consent. Euthanasia without consent is classified as involuntary or non-voluntary.'),
('b110fc92-f3c5-5005-bf37-ae6a9bbdf1d1'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Involuntary euthanasia refers to:', '["Competent patient unwilling resulting in decreased functional activity","Euthanasia without patient consent (against will)","Court ordered mediated by specific receptor activation","Patient requests death without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Family consent only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Euthanasia without patient consent (against will)', 'Involuntary euthanasia is performed on a competent patient who is unwilling or has not consented, meaning it is done against their wishes. It is ethically and legally distinct from voluntary euthanasia.'),
('4fe07744-94bf-55de-aaff-1c7a2f9b33eb'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Non-voluntary euthanasia applies to:', '["Patient who refuses","Patient who cannot consent (e.g. coma)","Competent patient","Minor patient","Psychiatric patient"]'::jsonb, 'Patient who cannot consent (e.g. coma)', 'Non-voluntary euthanasia applies when the patient lacks capacity to consent, such as someone in a coma. Refusing patients retain capacity and instead fit the definition of involuntary euthanasia.'),
('de860224-d868-52b5-b86a-7b643e9fbca6'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Physician-assisted suicide refers to:', '["The physician provides the means but the patient self-administers","It is a passive act accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The physician directly administers the lethal drug mediated by specific receptor activation","It requires consent without affecting the primary tissue pathway","It is a legal act leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The physician provides the means but the patient self-administers', 'In physician-assisted suicide, the physician supplies the means, such as a prescription, and the patient performs the final act of self-administration. Direct lethal administration by the physician would instead constitute active euthanasia.'),
('c9b124a6-5e29-5671-86bb-696743408b6a'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Which country was the first to legalize euthanasia?', '["Belgium","Switzerland","USA","Netherlands","Canada"]'::jsonb, 'Netherlands', 'The Netherlands became the first country to formally legalize euthanasia in 2002. Belgium followed in the same year, while assisted suicide has remained more limited in Switzerland.'),
('0c5b563c-6dce-53b6-9844-5549011013af'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The principle of double effect refers to:', '["An action with both good effect (pain relief) and foreseeable bad effect (respiratory depression)","Two ethical principles conflicting accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Patient and family consent mediated by specific receptor activation","Two treatments for same condition without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Two physicians treating one patient leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An action with both good effect (pain relief) and foreseeable bad effect (respiratory depression)', 'The principle of double effect permits an action with a good intended effect, such as pain relief, even when a foreseeable but unintended harm, such as respiratory depression, may follow. It does not refer to two treatments, physicians, or conflicting principles.'),
('4d5a1d88-4970-58fb-89f2-6476a0b8691e'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Brain death is defined as:', '["Persistent vegetative state resulting in decreased functional activity","Coma for 24 hours accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Loss of consciousness mediated by specific receptor activation","Irreversible loss of all brain functions including brainstem","Cerebral death only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Irreversible loss of all brain functions including brainstem', 'Brain death requires the irreversible loss of all brain function, including the brainstem, which governs consciousness and spontaneous respiration. A persistent vegetative state and transient coma preserve brainstem function and are not brain death.'),
('57fd62ce-d177-577c-83f0-af6fd6a45f5e'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Physician-assisted suicide is characterized by:', '["Physician provides the means; the patient self-administers","Physician directly administers the lethal drug","Withholding treatment","Withdrawing life support","Refusing treatment"]'::jsonb, 'Physician provides the means; the patient self-administers', 'In physician-assisted suicide, the physician provides the means and the patient makes the final decision and performs the final self-administering act. Direct administration of a lethal drug by the physician constitutes active euthanasia.'),
('97cc23b9-8487-5b71-abff-92a3809155cd'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Which ethical principle requires fair distribution of healthcare resources?', '["Confidentiality","Justice","Autonomy","Non-maleficence","Beneficence"]'::jsonb, 'Justice', 'Justice is the ethical principle that requires fair and equitable distribution of healthcare resources. Confidentiality concerns privacy, autonomy concerns self-determination, and beneficence and non-maleficence concern doing good and avoiding harm.'),
('558c6077-f297-597b-959f-83922362a252'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Non-maleficence means:', '["Doing no harm","Doing good","Being just","Respecting autonomy","Maintaining confidentiality"]'::jsonb, 'Doing no harm', 'Non-maleficence means avoiding harm, commonly expressed as ''primum non nocere''. Doing good describes beneficence, while the other options reflect separate ethical principles.'),
('6db1fd79-4a4c-5248-a69a-219fa53ae09f'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'IRB/IEC review is required for:', '["Research involving human subjects","Medical education","Literature review","Clinical case reports","Routine clinical care"]'::jsonb, 'Research involving human subjects', 'Institutional review boards and institutional ethics committees must review research involving human subjects to protect their rights, safety, and welfare. Routine care, education, literature reviews, and case reports generally do not require such approval.'),
('3b370e23-aa4d-5acf-87a7-109a1b4ca62b'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The Tarasoff case established:', '["Duty to warn third party at serious risk","Patient confidentiality","Right to refuse treatment","Informed consent","Euthanasia principles"]'::jsonb, 'Duty to warn third party at serious risk', 'Tarasoff established that clinicians have a duty to warn an identifiable third party who faces a serious risk of harm, overriding confidentiality in that situation. The other options concern separate legal and ethical doctrines.'),
('51c4e380-05bc-54fc-920b-0424dbeeef38'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The principle of double effect states that:', '["An action with both good effect (pain relief) and foreseeable bad effect (respiratory depression)","Two ethical principles are in conflict accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Two treatments for the same condition are combined mediated by specific receptor activation","Two physicians treat one patient without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Two consent forms are required leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An action with both good effect (pain relief) and foreseeable bad effect (respiratory depression)', 'The principle of double effect holds that an action with a good intended effect, such as palliative pain relief, may be permitted even when it has a foreseeable but unintended harmful effect, such as respiratory depression. It does not describe paired treatments, physicians, or consent forms.'),
('28db147b-c0ba-5c67-94ea-a0df05714da7'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The Belmont Report identified which three basic ethical principles for human research?', '["Non-maleficence, autonomy, justice","Respect for persons, beneficence, justice","Honesty, integrity, fairness","Autonomy, beneficence, justice","Fidelity, respect, confidentiality"]'::jsonb, 'Respect for persons, beneficence, justice', 'The Belmont Report articulated three basic ethical principles for research involving human subjects: respect for persons, beneficence, and justice. Although autonomy overlaps with respect for persons, the Report''s stated trio is respect for persons, beneficence, and justice.'),
('b51764d5-3fdd-5909-bd4d-95e3d638daec'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Vulnerable populations in research include all EXCEPT:', '["Healthy adult volunteers","Children","Medical students","Pregnant women","Prisoners"]'::jsonb, 'Healthy adult volunteers', 'Children, medical students, pregnant women, and prisoners are considered vulnerable populations because of limited autonomy or coercion risk. Healthy adult volunteers who can give independent consent are not inherently vulnerable.'),
('d296b532-4721-5d70-a889-35a2a3594ec5'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Therapeutic misconception in research occurs when:', '["Researchers misunderstand the protocol resulting in decreased functional activity","Data is fabricated accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Trial is unblinded mediated by specific receptor activation","Subjects believe research will provide personal therapeutic benefit","Consent is not obtained leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Subjects believe research will provide personal therapeutic benefit', 'Therapeutic misconception occurs when research participants incorrectly believe that the trial procedures are designed for their personal therapeutic benefit rather than to answer a scientific question. It does not refer to protocol errors, fabrication, or unblinding.'),
('94478dd3-1a99-52b8-becd-cc6376e216b8'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The Bolitho modification of the Bolam test holds that:', '["Medical opinion must be logical and reasonable","Any opinion suffices","Only majority opinion counts","Only expert testimony matters","Medical opinion is not needed"]'::jsonb, 'Medical opinion must be logical and reasonable', 'Under Bolitho, a court can reject a body of medical opinion if it does not withstand logical analysis, so the opinion relied upon must be logical and reasonable. It does not accept just any opinion or limit proof to expert testimony.'),
('247e5186-0937-5ae9-933d-98ca3364e534'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The use of placebo in clinical trials is unethical when:', '["Placebo has side effects","Trial is double-blind","Study is small","Proven effective treatment exists","Patient prefers placebo"]'::jsonb, 'Proven effective treatment exists', 'Placebo use is unethical when a proven effective treatment exists, because withholding it would deprive participants of known benefit. Double-blinding, small sample size, and placebo side effects do not by themselves make placebo use unethical.'),
('4abbd6b3-aa0f-57a3-9dfa-8c84fca16e96'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Medical negligence requires all EXCEPT:', '["Patient consent","Breach of duty","Damages","Duty of care","Causation"]'::jsonb, 'Patient consent', 'Medical negligence requires four elements: a duty of care, breach of that duty, causation, and damages. Patient consent is not an element of a negligence claim.'),
('371e49ef-4fd8-515b-9eae-e3cf10159d50'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The standard of care in medical negligence is determined by:', '["What a reasonably competent physician would do","Cost of treatment","Patient expectation","Physician''s personal standard","Best available evidence anywhere"]'::jsonb, 'What a reasonably competent physician would do', 'The standard of care is set by what a reasonably competent physician would do in similar circumstances, as reflected in the Bolam test. It is not defined by cost, patient expectation, or a physician''s personal preference.'),
('739884ea-7bf1-5979-9d3c-d3b3bfbcf4bb'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Confidentiality exceptions include:', '["Notifiable diseases, serious harm to third parties, child protection","Only notifiable diseases accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only court orders mediated by specific receptor activation","Only patient consent without affecting the primary tissue pathway","No exceptions leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Notifiable diseases, serious harm to third parties, child protection', 'Confidentiality can be breached for notifiable disease reporting, to protect third parties from serious harm, and for child protection concerns. These situations override the duty of secrecy, so confidentiality is not absolute.'),
('dc84061f-e2d3-54e7-a7a5-1161e087e3b0'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Res ipsa loquitur means:', '["The thing speaks for itself","Let the buyer beware","To err is human","The patient is right","Caveat emptor"]'::jsonb, 'The thing speaks for itself', 'Res ipsa loquitur means ''the thing speaks for itself'' and allows negligence to be inferred from the circumstances. ''Let the buyer beware'' and ''caveat emptor'' are the same consumer-law maxim, which is not what this doctrine describes.'),
('7ac8386e-0b9d-56a9-af72-9a8a00af4321'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Resource allocation in healthcare primarily involves which ethical principle?', '["Beneficence","Autonomy","Justice","Non-maleficence","Confidentiality"]'::jsonb, 'Justice', 'Resource allocation is primarily a matter of justice, which requires the fair and equitable distribution of limited healthcare resources. The other principles address benefit, harm, self-determination, and privacy rather than fair distribution.'),
('60d70380-8046-5f2d-9640-bc66cc8791ed'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'When a physician makes decisions for a patient without consulting them, this is called:', '["Benevolence","Paternalism","Informed consent","Shared decision making","Empathy"]'::jsonb, 'Paternalism', 'Paternalism occurs when a physician makes decisions for a patient without consulting them or obtaining their input, overriding the patient''s autonomy. Shared decision making and informed consent instead emphasize patient involvement.'),
('d8645c7e-1069-5fb2-909a-96383960dcaf'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Which doctor-patient relationship model involves the physician providing information and the patient making the decision?', '["Informative","Deliberative","Cooperative","Paternalistic","Interpretive"]'::jsonb, 'Informative', 'In the informative (consumer) model, the physician provides all relevant information and the patient makes the final decision. The deliberative and interpretive models involve more of the physician''s input, while the paternalistic model gives the physician control.'),
('12416843-602b-5d4e-819b-e62391a2ab05'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The Belmont Report identified:', '["Respect for persons, beneficence, justice","Only autonomy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only non-maleficence mediated by specific receptor activation","Only beneficence without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only justice leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Respect for persons, beneficence, justice', 'The Belmont Report articulated three basic ethical principles for research involving human subjects: respect for persons, beneficence, and justice. It did not identify a single principle alone.'),
('185e1c2a-5008-5b2f-8280-256feba3d339'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Informed consent requires:', '["Understanding, voluntariness, capacity, disclosure","Only physician decision","Only patient signature","Only treatment information","Only risk disclosure"]'::jsonb, 'Understanding, voluntariness, capacity, disclosure', 'Valid informed consent requires that the patient has capacity, receives adequate disclosure of information, understands that information, and consents voluntarily. A signature alone or isolated disclosure is insufficient without capacity and understanding.'),
('3868a3ab-5713-5ae2-8665-2a5f6fb7f7a6'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Standard of care in medical negligence is determined by:', '["Cost of treatment","Patient expectation","What a reasonably competent physician would do","Physician''s personal standard","Best available evidence anywhere"]'::jsonb, 'What a reasonably competent physician would do', 'The standard of care is defined by what a reasonably competent physician would do in the same circumstances. Cost, patient expectation, and a physician''s personal preference do not set the legal standard of care.'),
('b1e42e57-290b-5c87-815e-bcd6f24e6c59'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Autonomy in medical ethics means:', '["Respecting patient''s right to self-determination","Doing no harm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Being just mediated by specific receptor activation","Doing good without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Maintaining confidentiality leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Respecting patient''s right to self-determination', 'Autonomy is the principle of respecting the patient''s right to self-determination and to make informed decisions about their care. Doing no harm, being just, doing good, and maintaining confidentiality describe other ethical duties.'),
('12b20c59-557e-5762-ac4e-f47129c62640'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The Bolitho modification of the Bolam test adds that:', '["The medical opinion must be logical and reasonable","Any opinion suffices","Only majority opinion counts","Only expert testimony matters","No medical opinion is needed"]'::jsonb, 'The medical opinion must be logical and reasonable', 'The Bolitho modification holds that a medical opinion relied upon under Bolam must withstand logical analysis and be reasonable; a court may reject an illogical body of opinion. It does not accept any opinion or dispense with the need for one.'),
('e9137eca-fddd-5bb7-b928-9399090a42f3'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The Consumer Protection Act applies to:', '["Medical services","Only hospitals","Only goods","Only products","Only private doctors"]'::jsonb, 'Medical services', 'The Consumer Protection Act treats medical care as a service, so it applies to medical services provided to patients as consumers. It is not limited to hospitals, goods, products, or private practitioners alone.'),
('1f73e25f-ecc8-5a30-b28b-e1ef9ed7ee7c'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Justice in medical ethics refers to:', '["Fair distribution of healthcare resources","Doing no harm","Respecting autonomy","Being honest","Maintaining confidentiality"]'::jsonb, 'Fair distribution of healthcare resources', 'Justice requires the fair and equitable distribution of healthcare resources and equal treatment of patients. Doing no harm, respecting autonomy, honesty, and confidentiality reflect other ethical principles.'),
('99d24eda-1c82-5ad1-be8a-e2947bd53ca3'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Duty of care arises when:', '["A doctor-patient relationship is established","Patient is in hospital","Physician is on call","Hospital has emergency room","Patient pays fees"]'::jsonb, 'A doctor-patient relationship is established', 'A duty of care arises once a doctor-patient relationship is established, regardless of location, payment, or call status. It is the establishment of the relationship that creates the legal duty.'),
('fbf7b9f3-f765-5e1c-9369-adac6c641047'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Beneficence means:', '["Act in the patient''s best interest","Do no harm","Be just","Respect autonomy","Maintain confidentiality"]'::jsonb, 'Act in the patient''s best interest', 'Beneficence is the obligation to act for the benefit of the patient and to promote their wellbeing. Do no harm defines non-maleficence, while the remaining choices reflect other ethical principles.'),
('6061c04b-72b5-5d13-b15c-b09fd345d6fe'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'A competent patient scheduled for a high-risk procedure decides to decline it. Which ethical principle best supports respecting that decision?', '["Beneficence","Justice","Non-maleficence","Autonomy","Fidelity"]'::jsonb, 'Autonomy', 'Autonomy gives competent patients the right to accept or refuse any medical intervention, including a high-risk procedure. Respecting the patient''s informed refusal honors self-determination even when beneficence would favor treatment.'),
('b914fe23-3024-5027-b529-cf9b1968243b'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Which of the following is NOT a required element of informed consent before an elective surgical procedure?', '["Disclosure of diagnosis","Prognosis without treatment","Cost of treatment to physician","Risks and benefits","Description of the proposed treatment"]'::jsonb, 'Cost of treatment to physician', 'For valid informed consent the physician must disclose the diagnosis, the proposed treatment, its risks and benefits, and the likely outcome of no treatment. A physician''s own cost of treatment is not a required element of disclosure.'),
('0484283d-46e1-51e9-b048-831b50cb5b30'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'For a scheduled elective surgical procedure, informed consent should be obtained by:', '["Verbal consent only","Both verbal discussion and written documentation","Not needed in emergencies","Written consent only","Required only for surgical procedures"]'::jsonb, 'Both verbal discussion and written documentation', 'Informed consent requires both a verbal discussion of the procedure, risks, benefits and alternatives and written documentation signed by the patient. Written documentation alone or verbal consent alone is not considered complete.'),
('16e611f0-1ae5-518b-b90d-bffd124b74f2'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Therapeutic misconception occurs when:', '["Research subjects believe research provides personal therapeutic benefit","The researcher misunderstands the protocol accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Research data is fabricated mediated by specific receptor activation","Consent is not obtained without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The trial is unblinded leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Research subjects believe research provides personal therapeutic benefit', 'Therapeutic misconception describes research participants who believe the study is designed for their personal therapeutic benefit. This mistaken belief threatens the validity of consent in clinical research.'),
('f33ed4c8-5314-591c-aea5-88ecd7b1b83d'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The use of a placebo in a clinical trial is unethical when:', '["Proven effective treatment exists","The trial is double-blind","The study is small","The patient prefers placebo","Side effects are mild"]'::jsonb, 'Proven effective treatment exists', 'When a proven effective treatment exists, withholding it in favour of a placebo is unethical and violates the Declaration of Helsinki. Placebo control is acceptable only when no proven therapy exists or there is a sound methodological justification.'),
('8e832948-7495-5d4d-b573-79080c9bc42c'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Physician-assisted suicide is best defined as a situation in which:', '["Physician provides means, patient self-administers","Physician directly administers a lethal drug","Withholding life-sustaining treatment","Withdrawing life support","Refusing treatment"]'::jsonb, 'Physician provides means, patient self-administers', 'Physician-assisted suicide occurs when the physician provides the means, such as a lethal prescription, and the patient performs the final act himself. Direct administration of a lethal drug by the physician constitutes euthanasia, not assisted suicide.'),
('9f0e4dbb-ac6e-5fe5-9317-204068b6f539'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'A living will is a type of:', '["Research consent","Informed consent","Insurance document","Confidentiality agreement","Advance directive"]'::jsonb, 'Advance directive', 'A living will is a written advance directive that records treatment preferences in case of future incapacity. It guides end-of-life decisions without a surrogate.'),
('51f7a67a-5d1d-54bc-bdff-900eabfbf87e'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Medical negligence requires all of the following EXCEPT:', '["Patient consent","Breach of duty","Damages","Duty of care","Causation"]'::jsonb, 'Patient consent', 'Negligence requires the four elements of duty of care, breach of that duty, causation and damages. Patient consent is not one of these elements.'),
('e834f0e9-8a3d-5391-98e0-882ded936cb3'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Which ethical principle requires truthfulness in interactions with patients?', '["Justice","Beneficence","Veracity","Non-maleficence","Autonomy"]'::jsonb, 'Veracity', 'Veracity is the ethical principle requiring honesty and truthfulness in patient care. It underpins trust in the physician-patient relationship.'),
('dc41f285-abb7-567b-87c4-b876c3ca4a07'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Withholding the truth from a patient may be justified by:', '["Cost concerns","Family request","Patient request","Physician convenience","Therapeutic privilege"]'::jsonb, 'Therapeutic privilege', 'Therapeutic privilege permits withholding information when disclosure would cause the patient serious harm. It is a narrow exception and does not cover family requests or physician convenience.'),
('78d06a94-6ab7-59ed-92b5-3caa2958a125'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Confidentiality may be breached without the patient''s consent when:', '["An insurance company requests records","A lawyer requests records","There is serious risk of harm to a third party","A researcher requests data","Family members request information"]'::jsonb, 'There is serious risk of harm to a third party', 'Confidentiality may be overridden when there is a serious, imminent risk of harm to an identifiable third party, giving rise to a duty to warn. Ordinary requests from insurers, lawyers, researchers or family do not justify breaching confidence.'),
('4449b6ab-9b2f-5e27-a68a-fa73f8e919e9'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The case of Tarasoff v Regents of California established the physician''s:', '["Duty to warn third parties at risk","Right to die","Legality of euthanasia","Standard of informed consent","Definition of futility"]'::jsonb, 'Duty to warn third parties at risk', 'Tarasoff established that a physician or therapist has a duty to warn identifiable third parties who face a serious threat of violence. This duty overrides confidentiality.'),
('33ddb61b-bfde-5225-aee4-4d4b3306cbca'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'In cases of suspected child abuse, reporting confidential information is:', '["Only if the parent consents","Mandatory by law","Optional","Not allowed","Unethical"]'::jsonb, 'Mandatory by law', 'Suspected child abuse and neglect must be reported to child protection authorities, and such reporting is mandatory by law. Parental consent is not required.'),
('deaef90b-9642-56b9-8926-08b4917b4d83'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Physicians caring for patients with notifiable diseases are required to:', '["Obtain patient consent for reporting","Treat without reporting","Only report if the disease is severe","Report to public health authorities","Maintain confidentiality"]'::jsonb, 'Report to public health authorities', 'Notifiable diseases must be reported to public health authorities regardless of patient consent. Statutory reporting obligations are a well-recognized exception to confidentiality.'),
('a0371823-7d5e-5672-a9db-7b650e552046'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Which of the following best describes the ''slippery slope'' argument against the legalization of euthanasia?', '["It is expensive resulting in decreased functional activity","Physicians will profit accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Euthanasia is painful mediated by specific receptor activation","Legalizing euthanasia will lead to unacceptable practices","Patients will choose death leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Legalizing euthanasia will lead to unacceptable practices', 'The slippery slope argument holds that legalizing euthanasia in limited circumstances would gradually expand to unacceptable practices and deaths that are neither voluntary nor desired. It is a standard objection in the euthanasia debate.'),
('97ecfd87-21e7-51a4-adbe-2cfe08155095'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'In medical negligence law, the standard of care is determined by:', '["What a reasonably competent physician would do","Cost of treatment","Patient expectation","The physician''s personal standard","Best available evidence anywhere"]'::jsonb, 'What a reasonably competent physician would do', 'The Bolam test defines the standard of care as that which a reasonably competent physician in the same field would provide. The standard is judged against responsible medical opinion, not cost or the best evidence available anywhere.'),
('01212b60-d6a7-5d3f-b731-f5be4b886376'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Which of the following statements is most consistent with an argument in favour of physician-assisted death for a terminally ill patient?', '["Only the physician decides resulting in decreased functional activity","The family decides accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Life is worth preserving mediated by specific receptor activation","When quality is intolerably poor death may be preferable","Suffering builds character leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'When quality is intolerably poor death may be preferable', 'This statement reflects a quality-of-life argument that when suffering is severe and quality of life is intolerably poor, death may be preferable to continued suffering. It is commonly used to support physician-assisted dying.'),
('3022fb2b-b99c-588a-a785-f24e04de6cb4'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The primary goal of palliative care in a patient with advanced cancer is:', '["Making a diagnosis only","Prolonging life at all costs","Experimental treatments","Curing the disease","Symptom relief and quality of life"]'::jsonb, 'Symptom relief and quality of life', 'Palliative care focuses on relieving symptoms and improving quality of life in advanced or terminal disease. Disease cure and life prolongation at all costs are not its primary goals.'),
('f7f0d775-cd46-5b45-9222-0bc835a8b45f'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The concept of total pain in palliative care includes:', '["Physical pain only resulting in decreased functional activity","Physical psychological social and spiritual pain","Nociceptive pain mediated by specific receptor activation","Chronic pain without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cancer pain leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Physical psychological social and spiritual pain', 'Total pain, as described by Cicely Saunders, encompasses physical, psychological, social and spiritual suffering. Palliative assessment must address all of these dimensions.'),
('e8cf4ac3-4feb-5de6-9c8e-f2b5e8705ad1'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The Belmont Report identified which three basic ethical principles for research involving human subjects?', '["Respect for persons, beneficence, justice","Autonomy only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Non-maleficence only mediated by specific receptor activation","Beneficence only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Justice only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Respect for persons, beneficence, justice', 'The Belmont Report established three basic ethical principles for human research: respect for persons, beneficence and justice. These principles underlie informed consent, risk-benefit assessment and fair subject selection.'),
('6a796e81-8e18-5ac4-b113-9e9019de1818'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'A Do Not Resuscitate (DNR) order requires:', '["The physician''s unilateral decision","Hospital policy only","Nurse approval","Pharmacist input","Patient or family consent"]'::jsonb, 'Patient or family consent', 'A DNR order should be made together with the patient or, when the patient lacks capacity, with the family or an appropriate surrogate. It is not a unilateral physician decision.'),
('37e2481d-9877-57f1-81f8-6e890836b87f'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Which of the following is a core component of end-of-life (palliative) care?', '["Surgery","Vaccination","Blood transfusion","Organ transplantation","Pain management at end of life"]'::jsonb, 'Pain management at end of life', 'Aggressive pain management, guided by the principle of double effect, is a core component of end-of-life care. Surgery, vaccination, transfusion and transplantation are not defining features of palliative care.'),
('5ea54967-5bd2-5179-ad37-c3107abee9a2'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'When a patient or family demands a treatment that the physician considers futile, the physician should:', '["Explain why treatment is futile and attempt consensus","Consult an ethics committee","Ignore the request","Provide the treatment","Refuse and discharge the patient"]'::jsonb, 'Explain why treatment is futile and attempt consensus', 'The physician should explain why the treatment is futile and involve the patient and family in seeking consensus. Unilaterally providing or refusing futile treatment, or ignoring the request, fails to respect shared decision-making.'),
('6e7db41d-f99a-5b40-aab9-3cffbe7ecb01'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The four-box method of ethical decision-making considers:', '["Boxes A, B, C and D resulting in decreased functional activity","Patient, family, physician and hospital accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Medical indications patient preferences quality of life and contextual features","Legal, clinical, ethical and social without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cost-effectiveness, safety and equity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Medical indications patient preferences quality of life and contextual features', 'Jonsen''s four-box method structures ethical analysis into medical indications, patient preferences, quality of life and contextual features. It provides a systematic framework for clinical ethical decisions.'),
('75a84acb-7b1f-5317-8c72-245d49d3b1c4'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Which ethical principle most directly respects a patient''s right to self-determination?', '["Non-maleficence","Justice","Confidentiality","Beneficence","Autonomy"]'::jsonb, 'Autonomy', 'Autonomy respects a patient''s right to self-determination and to make informed decisions about their own care. It underlies informed consent and refusal of treatment.'),
('b9f038da-0119-5e77-94dc-0b5ef5a9ab4e'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Do competent adult patients have the right to refuse treatment even if it may result in death?', '["Only if the family agrees","No, never","Only if the physician agrees","Yes always","Only if the patient is terminal"]'::jsonb, 'Yes always', 'A competent adult may refuse any treatment, including life-sustaining treatment, even where refusal may result in death. This right does not depend on agreement from the family, the physician or terminal status.'),
('961549b9-6970-5bc4-88a5-f759fd721c78'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Consent to treatment in children is governed by:', '["Parental permission","Only parental consent","Gillick competence (UK)","All of the above depending on jurisdiction","Emancipated minor laws"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above depending on jurisdiction', 'Consent for treatment in children is governed variously by parental permission, Gillick competence and emancipated minor laws depending on the jurisdiction. Therefore all of the mechanisms listed may apply.'),
('0696649a-b114-5a7a-827a-e528a0ad0102'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Gillick competence refers to:', '["Physician authority resulting in decreased functional activity","Parental rights accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Research consent mediated by specific receptor activation","Legal age of consent without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A child''s capacity to consent to treatment"]'::jsonb, 'A child''s capacity to consent to treatment', 'Gillick competence is the legal principle that a child under 16 may consent to treatment when they have sufficient understanding and intelligence. Capacity is assessed on the individual child in each case.'),
('c482d376-6a98-5d35-abde-4fdaea53866f'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Exceptions to patient confidentiality include:', '["Notifiable diseases, serious harm to third parties, child protection","Only notifiable diseases accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only court orders mediated by specific receptor activation","Only patient consent without affecting the primary tissue pathway","No exceptions leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Notifiable diseases, serious harm to third parties, child protection', 'Confidentiality has exceptions including statutory reporting of notifiable diseases, serious harm to third parties and child protection duties. These obligations override the general duty of secrecy.'),
('75aa02e9-7930-5163-9627-99ebc0a549b1'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'In medical law, competence is a determination, whereas capacity is a assessment:', '["Legal, medical","Medical, legal","Ethical, legal","Clinical, ethical","Moral, clinical"]'::jsonb, 'Legal, medical', 'Competence is a legal status determined by a court, whereas capacity is a clinical assessment made by a physician. A competent adult is presumed to have capacity.'),
('26260552-8059-5634-82c4-fefa3cbb19cb'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The legal maxim res ipsa loquitur means:', '["The thing speaks for itself","Let the buyer beware","To err is human","The patient is right","Caveat emptor"]'::jsonb, 'The thing speaks for itself', 'Res ipsa loquitur literally means ''the thing speaks for itself''. It allows negligence to be inferred when an injury would not ordinarily occur in the absence of someone''s negligence.'),
('58ead296-b872-5d8a-a5d7-d90726f76e5d'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'In the traditional paternalistic model of the physician-patient relationship, the physician:', '["Provides information only","Engages in dialogue","Follows the patient''s wishes","Delegates decisions to the family","Acts as a guardian making decisions for the patient"]'::jsonb, 'Acts as a guardian making decisions for the patient', 'In the paternalistic model the physician acts as a guardian, making decisions believed to be in the patient''s best interest with limited patient input. Modern practice has shifted toward shared and autonomy-based models.'),
('515aee8c-9e64-52f9-b139-de5f04d5daf3'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The consumerist (informative) model of the physician-patient relationship is also called the:', '["Engineering model","Paternalistic model","Cooperative model","Deliberative model","Interpretive model"]'::jsonb, 'Engineering model', 'The consumerist or informative model, in which the physician supplies factual information and the patient decides, is also called the engineering model. Deliberative and interpretive models emphasize discussion and interpretation of values.'),
('959dc2bf-d453-5a65-916b-d3b5a9d04533'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The biopsychosocial model of health and illness integrates:', '["Only physical resulting in decreased functional activity","Only biological and social accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only psychological mediated by specific receptor activation","Biological, psychological and social factors","Only genetic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Biological, psychological and social factors', 'The biopsychosocial model, proposed by George Engel, integrates biological, psychological and social factors in understanding health and illness. It rejects reducing illness to physical or genetic causes alone.'),
('8bf6004a-d100-5745-a329-a83bfc993337'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Non-compliance with treatment is best addressed by:', '["Discharging the patient","Prescribing more medication","Coercion","Ignoring it","Exploring patient beliefs and barriers"]'::jsonb, 'Exploring patient beliefs and barriers', 'Addressing non-compliance begins with exploring the patient''s beliefs, concerns and practical barriers to adherence. Coercion, ignoring the problem or prescribing more medication without such inquiry is ineffective.'),
('c7a92665-f10e-5ada-9a6f-e5ec0676b44f'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The use of placebos in clinical practice raises ethical concerns primarily about:', '["Cost","Safety","Availability","Deception and autonomy","Effectiveness"]'::jsonb, 'Deception and autonomy', 'Using placebos in ordinary clinical practice involves deception, which undermines informed consent and patient autonomy. These concerns underlie ethical objections to placebo use outside controlled research.'),
('30ac54fc-3fff-5f75-be30-8f8084d2d633'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The concept of therapeutic misconception is most relevant to:', '["Nursing","Pharmacy","Surgery","Clinical trials","Clinical practice"]'::jsonb, 'Clinical trials', 'Therapeutic misconception, in which participants mistake research procedures for personal therapy, is a central concern in clinical trials. It threatens the validity of informed consent in research.'),
('48f97d3d-897c-5262-a1db-4edb2e008b02'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'A conflict of interest arises when:', '["Patient disagrees with physician","Physician''s primary duty is compromised by secondary interests","Family conflicts with patient","Two treatments are equally effective","Insurance denies coverage"]'::jsonb, 'Physician''s primary duty is compromised by secondary interests', 'A conflict of interest exists when a physician''s primary duty to the patient is compromised by secondary interests, such as financial gain or personal relationships. Such conflicts should be disclosed and managed.'),
('bbb148c6-29d3-53f5-af0a-d604e3b01552'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Gifts from pharmaceutical companies to physicians raise ethical concerns primarily about:', '["Conflict of interest","Regulation","Patient safety","Effectiveness","Cost"]'::jsonb, 'Conflict of interest', 'Gifts and payments from pharmaceutical companies can bias prescribing and create conflicts of interest in the physician''s primary duty to the patient. Transparency and policies limiting such gifts help manage these risks.'),
('97a16646-ec59-5ac6-aa55-7c2903ea189c'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The physician-industry relationship should be:', '["Ignored","Prohibited entirely","Disclosed and regulated","Only for research","Encouraged without limits"]'::jsonb, 'Disclosed and regulated', 'Relationships between physicians and industry should be transparent, disclosed and regulated to avoid undue influence. Total prohibition is impractical, but unrecognized conflicts can bias decisions.'),
('19b9e15e-d59e-5812-938a-7d7898443c0f'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Professional boundaries in the physician-patient relationship ensure that:', '["Socializing is encouraged resulting in decreased functional activity","Physician and patient are friends accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Long-term relationships are formed mediated by specific receptor activation","The therapeutic relationship remains focused on patient needs","Gifts are accepted leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The therapeutic relationship remains focused on patient needs', 'Professional boundaries keep the physician-patient relationship focused on the patient''s therapeutic needs and prevent exploitation. Social friendship, gift acceptance or other dual relationships can compromise clinical objectivity.'),
('90b79eb1-692c-5cc0-8037-716ab3387ff2'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Universal health coverage means:', '["Only rich people get care resulting in decreased functional activity","The government supplies all care accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Everyone can access needed health services without financial hardship","Private insurance for all without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only emergency care is covered leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Everyone can access needed health services without financial hardship', 'Universal health coverage ensures that everyone can obtain needed health services without suffering financial hardship. It addresses access, quality and financial risk protection.'),
('07fde380-ce61-5cfa-a87e-0647da213fcc'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'For which of the following is QALY (Quality-Adjusted Life Year) used?', '["Diagnosis resulting in decreased functional activity","Research accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Treatment mediated by specific receptor activation","Nursing without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Resource allocation decisions"]'::jsonb, 'Resource allocation decisions', 'The QALY combines length and quality of life into a single measure, allowing cost-effectiveness of different interventions to be compared. It is a standard tool used in health technology assessment and resource allocation.'),
('c0a632a2-f345-5a18-a215-1aa9cfd70694'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Which of the following actions constitutes active euthanasia?', '["Administering a lethal substance to end life","Withholding treatment","Withdrawing life support","Providing pain relief","Refusing treatment"]'::jsonb, 'Administering a lethal substance to end life', 'Active euthanasia involves a deliberate positive act, such as administering a lethal injection, that is intended to cause the patient''s death. It is distinct from withholding or withdrawing treatment, which are forms of passive euthanasia.'),
('3808ec1d-2e85-5cfb-beea-f651a7748be4'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Which principle is described by the rule of rescue?', '["Preventive care resulting in decreased functional activity","Tendency to prioritize identifiable individuals over statistical lives","Favoring young patients mediated by specific receptor activation","Ignoring patients without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Saving people in immediate danger leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tendency to prioritize identifiable individuals over statistical lives', 'The rule of rescue describes the strong human tendency to allocate resources toward identifiable individuals in immediate danger, even when the same resources could save more ''statistical'' lives elsewhere. It is frequently discussed in the allocation of scarce medical resources.'),
('3b284d15-68e5-53b6-80f8-eaabf899ce13'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'According to the Bolam test, the standard of care is determined by:', '["What a reasonably competent physician would do","Cost of treatment","Patient expectation","Physician''s personal standard","Best evidence anywhere"]'::jsonb, 'What a reasonably competent physician would do', 'Under the Bolam test, a doctor is not negligent if they acted in accordance with a responsible body of medical opinion. The benchmark is the practice of a reasonably competent physician in the same field.'),
('98df6a67-0446-56b8-aeba-dc28b3bfe025'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The Tarasoff case established which principle in medical law?', '["Duty to warn third party at serious risk","Patient confidentiality","Right to refuse treatment","Informed consent","Euthanasia principles"]'::jsonb, 'Duty to warn third party at serious risk', 'In Tarasoff v. Regents of the University of California, the court held that a therapist has a duty to warn or protect identifiable third parties threatened with serious harm by a patient. This duty may override the patient''s expectation of confidentiality.'),
('39b90f21-8edd-56cc-8a85-aa5a834f053d'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Deontological ethics emphasizes:', '["Consequences only resulting in decreased functional activity","Emotions accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Duties and rules regardless of consequences","Virtues without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Intuitions leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Duties and rules regardless of consequences', 'Deontology judges the morality of an action by adherence to duties, rules, and obligations rather than by its outcomes. Kantian ethics, which holds that certain acts are right or wrong in themselves, is the classic example.'),
('dd2bfdd9-0d0e-5a2f-9336-cc96b18d6520'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The principle of non-maleficence means:', '["Do no harm","Do good","Be just","Respect autonomy","Maintain confidentiality"]'::jsonb, 'Do no harm', 'Non-maleficence obliges clinicians to avoid causing harm to their patients, summarized by the maxim primum non nocere. It is distinct from beneficence, which is the positive duty to act for the patient''s good.'),
('b4017e42-1ef8-551e-b99b-27076f649dba'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The four principles approach to medical ethics was proposed by:', '["Beauchamp and Childress","John Gregory","Hippocrates","Thomas Percival","Amartya Sen"]'::jsonb, 'Beauchamp and Childress', 'Tom Beauchamp and James Childress introduced the four-principles framework of autonomy, beneficence, non-maleficence, and justice in Principles of Biomedical Ethics. It remains a widely used structure for analyzing ethical problems in medicine.'),
('e34b27d5-c76e-5018-9916-11af7f236d9a'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Public health ethics often prioritizes which of the following over individual autonomy?', '["Profit","Tradition","Physician authority","Community benefit","Patient privacy"]'::jsonb, 'Community benefit', 'Public health ethics weighs collective goods, such as preventing spread of disease, against individual autonomy. Measures like vaccination mandates or reporting obligations may be justified when they protect the community.'),
('4bd5ff5b-2538-505a-ab71-7a43629f4d30'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Mandatory vaccination programs raise tensions between which two values?', '["Safety and efficacy","Cost and benefit","Research and practice","Public health benefit and individual autonomy","Prevention and treatment"]'::jsonb, 'Public health benefit and individual autonomy', 'Mandatory vaccination seeks herd protection for the population while limiting the individual''s freedom to choose. The central ethical conflict is thus between collective public health benefit and respect for personal autonomy.'),
('77b9d1fd-3c01-50c2-865d-9a81d2417861'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Quarantine and isolation for infectious diseases are justified by:', '["Public health necessity","Individual autonomy","Hospital policy","Physician order","Patient request"]'::jsonb, 'Public health necessity', 'Restricting movement of exposed or infected individuals is ethically acceptable when it is necessary to prevent transmission of a serious communicable disease to the community. Such measures must be the least restrictive effective option.'),
('b79ad5f1-6a1e-5ccc-aad6-40f1234a8316'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'According to Mill''s harm principle, which of the following is permissible?', '["Individuals have absolute freedom","All restrictions are wrong","Only emergency restrictions allowed","State can restrict liberty to prevent harm to others","Physicians decide"]'::jsonb, 'State can restrict liberty to prevent harm to others', 'Mill''s harm principle holds that society may interfere with an individual''s liberty only to prevent harm to other people. It justifies state action against conduct that injures others while protecting self-regarding conduct from coercion.'),
('f9178f79-ea41-55ce-81b5-8077864f95c1'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Ethical research involving children requires:', '["Parental permission and child assent when possible","Court approval","Only child''s consent","Only parental permission","Written consent of both parents"]'::jsonb, 'Parental permission and child assent when possible', 'Because children cannot legally give informed consent, researchers must obtain parental permission and, in addition, the child''s assent whenever the child is capable of understanding. This dual requirement respects both the child''s developing autonomy and the parents'' authority.'),
('ca8998d8-ec79-5ae8-8118-7cf54bd2449a'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The standard of care in negligence is determined by:', '["What a reasonably competent physician would do","Cost of treatment","Patient expectation","Physician''s personal standard","Best evidence anywhere"]'::jsonb, 'What a reasonably competent physician would do', 'The standard of care is judged against the conduct of a reasonably competent physician practicing in the same specialty. This objective professional benchmark, derived from the Bolam test, is what a court compares the doctor''s conduct against.'),
('0733ced9-4581-5207-b70b-340d1b445178'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The concept of standard of care in research means:', '["Placebo is acceptable","Treatment can be substandard","Participants may receive any care they request","Participants should not receive inferior care","Only experimental group gets care"]'::jsonb, 'Participants should not receive inferior care', 'The standard of care principle holds that research participants should not receive care that is inferior to the established standard in their community. This prevents exploitation and ensures participants are not worse off than comparable non-participants.'),
('9f9b8910-eaeb-5ff6-bb4b-bbae25b2d655'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Post-trial access to treatment is an ethical obligation because:', '["Only for successful trials resulting in decreased functional activity","Only drug company decides accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","It depends on cost mediated by specific receptor activation","It is not required without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Participants contributed to research and may benefit"]'::jsonb, 'Participants contributed to research and may benefit', 'Participants who contributed to the research should have reasonable access to interventions found to be beneficial after the trial ends. This expectation acknowledges their contribution to advancing knowledge and avoids abandoning them when the study concludes.'),
('80dc42e5-ee35-5149-a6a2-0b4c3dfcaf77'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Compensation for research-related injury is:', '["Only for major injury","Voluntary","Ethically required","Not needed","Family responsibility"]'::jsonb, 'Ethically required', 'Researchers and sponsors have an ethical obligation to compensate participants who suffer harm as a result of their participation in research. This responsibility exists because participants accept risk that primarily benefits society.'),
('3e2d2708-4681-5471-b65e-6c36c0dd501c'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Protecting data confidentiality in research requires:', '["Avoiding research","Secure storage for at least ten years","Anonymization or de-identification of data","Publishing names","Sharing data with anyone"]'::jsonb, 'Anonymization or de-identification of data', 'To protect participant confidentiality, identifying information should be removed or replaced with codes so that data cannot be traced back to individuals. De-identification minimizes the risk of unauthorized disclosure while allowing the research to proceed.'),
('9c2c2f9c-ac23-5fb6-8f21-577e074deba1'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Fraud in research includes:', '["Disagreement resulting in decreased functional activity","Peer review accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fabrication, falsification, and plagiarism","Honest error without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bias leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fabrication, falsification, and plagiarism', 'Research fraud comprises fabrication (inventing data), falsification (manipulating or misrepresenting data), and plagiarism (using others'' work without attribution). These are intentional misrepresentations, unlike honest error or legitimate disagreement.'),
('f8be5680-516a-5010-85e2-ac508cdb6d3b'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Ghost authorship occurs when:', '["Author does not contribute","A named author contributed nothing","All authors are listed","An unnamed person makes substantial contribution","Only editors"]'::jsonb, 'An unnamed person makes substantial contribution', 'Ghost authorship is the failure to list a person who made a substantial intellectual contribution to a manuscript. It misrepresents the true authorship of research, often hiding industry influence or undisclosed contributions.'),
('72c7e1ae-f6c4-5029-8282-82e891a27e72'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Valid informed consent requires:', '["Understanding, voluntariness, capacity, disclosure","Only physician decision","Only patient signature","Only treatment information","Only risks disclosure"]'::jsonb, 'Understanding, voluntariness, capacity, disclosure', 'Informed consent is valid only when the patient has the capacity to decide, receives adequate disclosure of relevant information, understands it, and gives consent voluntarily. The central elements are disclosure, understanding, voluntariness, and capacity.'),
('c0bbeccd-8281-5333-9a8a-f6c44355a091'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Peer review of manuscripts should be:', '["Confidential, objective, and constructive","Public accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Subjective mediated by specific receptor activation","Biased without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Rushed leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Confidential, objective, and constructive', 'Peer review is most effective when reviewers assess manuscripts confidentially, objectively, and constructively. This rigor helps editors judge scientific validity, originality, and significance while protecting reviewers'' identity.'),
('60695ec3-1503-583b-bf33-0ff335e07fef'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Which of the following is the primary ethical obligation in peer review?', '["Speed","Quantity","Friendship","Profit","Confidentiality"]'::jsonb, 'Confidentiality', 'Reviewers must keep the content of manuscripts and the review process confidential. Maintaining confidentiality preserves impartiality, protects unpublished findings, and is a core obligation in the peer review process.'),
('153d3ea7-2bff-5673-a83c-45264a8869ca'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'According to the Bolitho modification of the Bolam test:', '["Medical opinion must be logical and reasonable","Any opinion suffices","Only majority opinion counts","Only expert testimony matters","Opinion not needed"]'::jsonb, 'Medical opinion must be logical and reasonable', 'Bolitho restated that although a responsible body of medical opinion is the benchmark, that opinion must withstand logical analysis. A court can reject expert opinion that is illogical, ill-founded, or unreasonable, even if supported by practitioners.'),
('2f94b7b0-b709-5772-b803-023fa23e5822'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Physician-assisted suicide involves:', '["Physician provides means, patient self-administers","Physician directly administers lethal drug","Withholding treatment","Withdrawing life support","Refusing treatment"]'::jsonb, 'Physician provides means, patient self-administers', 'In physician-assisted suicide the physician supplies the means, such as a prescription, but the patient performs the final act of self-administration. This distinguishes it from active euthanasia, in which the physician directly causes death.'),
('e5223a22-a129-5664-98e5-3541a2d8226e'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The Institutional Animal Care and Use Committee (IACUC) oversees:', '["Animal research ethics","Pharmacy","Human research ethics","Hospital administration","Clinical trials"]'::jsonb, 'Animal research ethics', 'The IACUC reviews and approves protocols involving animals, ensuring humane treatment, adequate anesthesia and analgesia, and justification of animal numbers. It serves as the oversight body for the ethical conduct of animal research.'),
('b56032ad-055e-50d2-a941-6f2466d05a70'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Ethical issues in genetic testing include:', '["Discrimination","Stigmatization","None of the above","Privacy and confidentiality","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Genetic testing raises concerns about genetic discrimination by insurers or employers, stigmatization of individuals and families, and threats to privacy and confidentiality of genetic information. Together these are well-recognized ethical issues in genetic testing.'),
('6485501c-f5ac-5979-9c56-aacd71377ea1'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Informed consent for genetic testing should include:', '["Genetic counseling","Only individual results","Cost only","Treatment options only","Implications for family members"]'::jsonb, 'Implications for family members', 'Genetic test results frequently have implications not only for the patient but also for blood relatives, who may share risk. Consent must therefore cover disclosure of familial implications so the patient understands the consequences of testing.'),
('7390c3b7-cdc4-5ad6-9f4e-6ff747fb0e19'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Non-directive counseling in genetics means:', '["Screening only resulting in decreased functional activity","Providing information without directing patient''s decision","Recommending testing mediated by specific receptor activation","Recommending termination without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Telling patient what to do leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Providing information without directing patient''s decision', 'Non-directive genetic counseling gives the individual accurate, balanced information and support while leaving reproductive and testing decisions entirely to that person. The counselor refrains from steering the decision toward any particular outcome.'),
('f1173b5c-8eb1-5239-9716-f85ea9702d5c'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Pre-implantation genetic diagnosis (PGD) raises ethical concerns about:', '["Infertility resulting in decreased functional activity","Cancer accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Infectious disease mediated by specific receptor activation","Disease treatment without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Selection of embryos and designer babies"]'::jsonb, 'Selection of embryos and designer babies', 'PGD allows embryos to be screened for genetic traits before implantation, which raises concerns about selection of embryos and the potential for ''designer babies''. Critics worry that testing may extend beyond disease prevention toward selecting non-medical characteristics.'),
('08bbe75c-5155-563f-a526-00118228c281'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Cloning for reproductive purposes is widely considered:', '["Standard practice resulting in decreased functional activity","Ethical accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Unethical due to safety and dignity concerns","Legal everywhere without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Required leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Unethical due to safety and dignity concerns', 'Reproductive cloning is widely regarded as unethical because of the low efficiency and high risk of abnormalities, and because it raises serious concerns about human dignity and autonomy. It is prohibited in nearly all jurisdictions.'),
('acdfb30c-2dd8-59ff-a4fd-06c711859f1f'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The WHO Guiding Principles on Organ Transplantation prohibit:', '["Organ trafficking and transplant tourism","Only living donation","Voluntary unpaid donation","Only cadaveric donation","All transplantation"]'::jsonb, 'Organ trafficking and transplant tourism', 'The WHO Guiding Principles prohibit the purchase and sale of organs, organ trafficking, and transplant tourism, which exploit vulnerable donors. Voluntary, unpaid donation by consenting adults or the next of kin of deceased donors remains the acceptable basis for transplantation.'),
('d6d05a70-4a63-5fff-aaf0-03c728201244'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The allocation of scarce organs should be based on:', '["Race","Social status","Ability to pay","Age only","Medical urgency and benefit"]'::jsonb, 'Medical urgency and benefit', 'Ethical allocation of scarce organs uses objective criteria of medical urgency and expected benefit to the recipient. Allocation by race, social status, ability to pay, or age alone is discriminatory and unjust.'),
('d6b352fa-002b-5e9c-928d-f36201861065'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Ethical issues in psychiatry include:', '["Competence assessment","Involuntary commitment","Informed consent for treatment","All of the above","Confidentiality with dangerous patients"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Psychiatric practice raises ethical issues including assessment of competence, involuntary commitment, ensuring informed consent for treatment, and balancing confidentiality against the duty to protect when a patient is dangerous. All of these are recognized ethical concerns in psychiatry.'),
('b0b262f8-ecb4-5d4f-8d24-b39685527411'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'A physician''s conscientious objection allows the physician to:', '["Refuse all patients resulting in decreased functional activity","Ignore patient needs accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only religious objections allowed mediated by specific receptor activation","Refuse to provide a legally permitted treatment on moral grounds","Force beliefs on patients leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Refuse to provide a legally permitted treatment on moral grounds', 'Conscientious objection permits a physician to decline a legally permitted intervention, such as abortion, because it conflicts with personal moral or religious convictions. The physician remains obliged to ensure the patient has timely access to care, such as through referral.'),
('0d835594-d6e9-5896-89ab-c1f3952d3fc6'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The ethical principles for human research identified by the Belmont Report are:', '["Respect for persons, beneficence, justice","Only autonomy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only non-maleficence mediated by specific receptor activation","Only beneficence without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only justice leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Respect for persons, beneficence, justice', 'The Belmont Report sets out three fundamental ethical principles for research with human subjects: respect for persons, beneficence, and justice. The other options list only single principles.'),
('2130a1a2-0691-503c-a38e-b9bc196e15ec'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The Bolitho modification of the Bolam principle holds that the responsible body of medical opinion:', '["Medical opinion must be logical and reasonable","Any opinion suffices","Only majority opinion counts","Only expert testimony matters","Opinion not needed"]'::jsonb, 'Medical opinion must be logical and reasonable', 'Under Bolam, a doctor is not negligent if supported by a responsible body of medical opinion. Bolitho adds that the court may reject that opinion if it cannot withstand logical analysis.'),
('46d21015-5cb5-5810-a369-9a275c7d9dc7'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Ethical issues in cosmetic surgery include:', '["None of the above","Informed consent","All of the above","Unrealistic expectations","Patient autonomy vs medical necessity"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Cosmetic surgery raises several ethical concerns, including failure to obtain genuine informed consent, the challenge of unrealistic patient expectations, and tension between patient autonomy and medical necessity.'),
('29ed33ec-c227-5fc4-a67c-02c8c9616914'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The right to healthcare is recognized in:', '["Nowhere","Only poor countries","Only rich countries","UDHR and national constitutions","Only some countries"]'::jsonb, 'UDHR and national constitutions', 'The right to health is recognized in the Universal Declaration of Human Rights and is protected in many national constitutions around the world.'),
('9461e25f-0338-5ad2-b703-fccec664f5cd'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The essential medicines concept ensures:', '["Only brand names resulting in decreased functional activity","Free medicines for all accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only generic medicines mediated by specific receptor activation","Only expensive medicines without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Access to effective affordable essential medicines"]'::jsonb, 'Access to effective affordable essential medicines', 'The WHO essential medicines concept aims to ensure that safe, effective, and affordable medicines for priority health conditions are accessible to all who need them.'),
('17d02e6f-d627-5335-8b41-acc5507f5b22'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Competence is a determination, capacity is :', '["Legal, medical","Medical, legal","Ethical, legal","Clinical, ethical","Moral, clinical"]'::jsonb, 'Legal, medical', 'Competence is legal status; capacity is clinical assessment.'),
('d225d66d-49e5-5448-9d15-43dfccb21cb8'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'A claim of medical negligence requires all of the following EXCEPT:', '["Patient consent","Breach of duty","Damages","Duty of care","Causation"]'::jsonb, 'Patient consent', 'Civil negligence requires four elements: a duty of care, breach of that duty, causation, and damages. A patient''s consent is not an element of negligence.'),
('8840bb73-bbff-55be-b5f1-8de5de8a754b'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Learning health systems integrate:', '["Research and clinical care seamlessly","Finance accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only research mediated by specific receptor activation","Only clinical care without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Administration leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Research and clinical care seamlessly', 'Learning health systems continuously integrate research with routine clinical care so that day-to-day practice generates knowledge and improves care.'),
('8ebba77d-4453-56eb-85c3-75c4fcf640d4'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Maldistribution of healthcare workers primarily raises:', '["Only economic issues resulting in decreased functional activity","Only personal choice accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Justice concerns about fair distribution of health workers","Ethical issues without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only legal issues leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Justice concerns about fair distribution of health workers', 'Inequitable distribution of health workers between regions and facilities raises ethical concerns about justice and the fair allocation of healthcare resources.'),
('294aa44d-d7b7-51c4-b745-fb95be386ce5'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Social media use by physicians raises concerns about:', '["Friendship resulting in decreased functional activity","Time management accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Marketing mediated by specific receptor activation","Entertainment without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Professional boundaries and confidentiality"]'::jsonb, 'Professional boundaries and confidentiality', 'Social media use by physicians can blur professional boundaries and risk breaching patient confidentiality when personal and professional roles overlap.'),
('e64ce2f3-a2e4-5995-ac92-e6ef4ae50e2e'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Online physician rating sites raise concerns about:', '["Confidentiality","Accuracy and fairness","All of the above","Professionalism","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Online rating sites raise concerns about the accuracy and fairness of posted evaluations, protection of patient confidentiality, and professionalism of public commentary about physicians. All of these apply.'),
('cdf8a02f-1abc-5354-ad5c-961a77f05b54'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The duty to report impaired colleagues is an ethical obligation based on:', '["Revenge resulting in decreased functional activity","Personal grudge accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Competition mediated by specific receptor activation","Jealousy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Patient safety and professional responsibility"]'::jsonb, 'Patient safety and professional responsibility', 'Physicians have a professional duty to protect patients from harm, which includes reporting colleagues whose impairment from illness or substance use endangers patient safety.'),
('61ede5a8-2d15-59c6-a64d-d3fb65295b0f'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Self-treatment and treating family members is generally discouraged because:', '["Patient prefers it","Objectivity is compromised","It saves money","It is convenient","It is legal"]'::jsonb, 'Objectivity is compromised', 'Treating oneself or close family members impairs clinical objectivity, making it difficult to assess problems and to communicate about them in a detached, professional manner.'),
('762befdc-d7ac-5b1d-b7c5-7a0f68d583f8'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Negligence per se means that:', '["A mere error in clinical judgment resulting in decreased functional activity","Patient is at fault accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A dangerous act mediated by specific receptor activation","The physician acted correctly without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The act is considered negligent because it violates a statute"]'::jsonb, 'The act is considered negligent because it violates a statute', 'Negligence per se treats a statutory violation as negligence without requiring separate proof of the applicable care standard. The claimant must still show the violation caused the harm.'),
('600e4cad-909d-556f-979a-b361b6364247'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Locum tenens physicians have the same:', '["Lesser standard resulting in decreased functional activity","Reduced duty accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Duty of care and standard of care as regular physicians","Limited liability without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Scope of practice leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Duty of care and standard of care as regular physicians', 'Locum tenens physicians provide care as temporary replacements and are held to the same duty of care and standard of care as the regularly assigned physicians.'),
('b6cc9716-ab40-5ebf-8ef6-08342934635b'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'A competent adult scheduled for a high-risk procedure must provide informed consent. Which of the following best describes the right to refuse such a procedure?', '["The patient''s decision to decline treatment after understanding the risks and benefits","The family''s refusal of consent on the patient''s behalf","A refusal made without information about the procedure","Written documentation of the refusal","The physician''s refusal to perform the procedure"]'::jsonb, 'The patient''s decision to decline treatment after understanding the risks and benefits', 'Informed consent implies the right of informed refusal: a competent adult who understands the risks, benefits, and alternatives may decline a high-risk procedure. Such a refusal is ethically valid even if the physician disagrees.'),
('fb1a0ad4-4981-5bca-af24-755a63e4db20'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'In most jurisdictions, the age at which an individual may give independent medical consent is:', '["12 years resulting in decreased functional activity","18 years (or 16 in some jurisdictions)","10 years mediated by specific receptor activation","21 years without affecting the primary tissue pathway","25 years leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '18 years (or 16 in some jurisdictions)', 'The legal age of majority for independent medical consent is generally 18 years, though some jurisdictions permit consent at 16 years for certain decisions.'),
('685dc152-2dc2-5632-83a2-0ca6a1f375a7'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'A minor may give independent consent to medical treatment when they:', '["They live independently or are married or have children","They prefer their own physician","They are over 16 years of age","Their parents are divorced","They file a petition with the court"]'::jsonb, 'They live independently or are married or have children', 'Minors who live independently or who are married or have children are generally regarded as emancipated. Emancipated minors may legally consent to their own medical treatment.'),
('34dc084a-2183-5291-bc35-ba1145d09093'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Parental refusal of life-saving treatment for a child may be overridden by:', '["Physician''s decision resulting in decreased functional activity","Court order on child protection grounds","Parental rights mediated by specific receptor activation","Child''s wish without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hospital policy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Court order on child protection grounds', 'When parental refusal would put a child at serious risk, providers may seek a court order allowing the necessary treatment on child-protection grounds.'),
('21d8c826-2f92-58ad-85a4-fc99a6356bac'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'CPR may be withheld when:', '["All of the above","It is medically futile","A valid do-not-resuscitate order exists","The competent patient requests no resuscitation","The patient''s surrogate declines resuscitation on the patient''s behalf"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'CPR may be withheld when it would be medically futile, when a valid do-not-resuscitate order exists, or when a competent patient or an appropriate surrogate declines resuscitation. Each of these situations permits withholding, so the combined answer applies.'),
('49f94eb0-4ea1-5cb3-b355-6ddd234a9f96'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The concept of medical futility is controversial because:', '["It is clear resulting in decreased functional activity","It is purely scientific accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","It is ethical mediated by specific receptor activation","It may involve value judgments about quality of life","Everyone agrees on definition leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'It may involve value judgments about quality of life', 'Judgements about medical futility mix scientific facts with value judgments about quality of life and expected benefit, which is why the concept is contested.'),
('a82d3817-59e0-5e58-bc2a-7a07f5fc3508'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The landmark case that recognized an adult patient''s right to determine what shall be done with their own body is:', '["Schloendorff v Society of New York Hospital","Plessy v Ferguson accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Brown v Board of Education mediated by specific receptor activation","Miranda v Arizona without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Roe v Wade leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Schloendorff v Society of New York Hospital', 'In Schloendorff v Society of New York Hospital the court recognized that every adult has the right to determine what is done to their own body, a foundation of informed consent.'),
('879be8f2-f25d-50c7-a927-26817356a5a4'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The case of Canterbury v Spence established:', '["The reasonable patient standard for disclosure","Written consent for every procedure","Paternalism as the guiding model","The reasonable physician standard","Confidentiality of the physician-patient relationship"]'::jsonb, 'The reasonable patient standard for disclosure', 'Canterbury v Spence established the ''reasonable patient'' standard, requiring physicians to disclose whatever a reasonable patient would want to know before consenting.'),
('4cb7f9d6-6174-599d-8264-d2e4173f50b7'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The reasonable patient standard is now more widely accepted because:', '["It is easier resulting in decreased functional activity","It is faster accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","It protects physicians mediated by specific receptor activation","It costs less without affecting the primary tissue pathway","It respects patient autonomy more fully"]'::jsonb, 'It respects patient autonomy more fully', 'The reasonable patient standard emphasizes disclosure of the information a patient would consider material, so it gained acceptance because it respects patient autonomy more fully.'),
('d5ac80ea-845c-5ae3-8b6a-929f1e4938af'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The subjective patient standard means:', '["What any physician would disclose","Minimum information","What any patient would want","A standard list of disclosures","Disclose what this particular patient would want to know"]'::jsonb, 'Disclose what this particular patient would want to know', 'Under the subjective patient standard, the clinician must disclose the information that this particular patient would want to know, reflecting that individual patient''s needs.'),
('40dd5552-88b6-5417-8d15-97010a98d266'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Establishing causation in medical negligence requires showing that:', '["The physician intended harm","The hospital is liable","But for the breach the harm would not have occurred","The patient contributed to the outcome","An unrelated cause produced the harm"]'::jsonb, 'But for the breach the harm would not have occurred', 'Causation is shown by the ''but for'' test, requiring proof that, but for the physician''s breach of duty, the harm would not have occurred.'),
('23d9a6db-232e-5066-867b-ac1fcbf29f96'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The Bolitho modification requires:', '["Any opinion is acceptable resulting in decreased functional activity","Only textbook medicine accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Patient''s opinion mediated by specific receptor activation","Numerical majority without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The professional opinion to be capable of logical analysis"]'::jsonb, 'The professional opinion to be capable of logical analysis', 'Bolitho requires that the responsible body of medical opinion supporting the doctor''s conduct be capable of withstanding logical analysis, allowing courts to reject illogical opinions.'),
('a9852cd1-6d4a-570a-80c2-bd47b2651f67'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Damages awarded in a medical negligence claim can include:', '["Punishment of the physician","Pain and suffering, medical expenses and lost income","Only medical expenses","Only pain and suffering","An apology by the physician"]'::jsonb, 'Pain and suffering, medical expenses and lost income', 'Damages in a negligence action compensate the claimant''s losses, which may include medical expenses, loss of earnings, and pain and suffering.'),
('956cc8fc-1843-571f-add2-68be25dc8d74'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Medical malpractice is a type of:', '["Moral wrong","Contract breach","Administrative violation","Crime","Tort (civil wrong)"]'::jsonb, 'Tort (civil wrong)', 'Medical malpractice is a tort, a civil wrong committed by a healthcare professional that injures a patient through breach of professional duty.'),
('41211336-c13b-59ec-8c18-01bb078cf7f0'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Contributory negligence by the patient:', '["Bars all claims","Applies only to criminal cases","Is a complete defense","May reduce damages in some jurisdictions","Is ignored"]'::jsonb, 'May reduce damages in some jurisdictions', 'In comparative-fault jurisdictions, a patient''s contributory negligence does not bar recovery entirely but reduces the damages in proportion to the patient''s share of responsibility.'),
('d3b25fe8-9cf0-5ac1-86ee-5907106b59fa'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Sorry laws in some jurisdictions:', '["Ignore apologies resulting in decreased functional activity","Require apologies accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Protect apologies from being used as evidence of liability","Prohibit apologies without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mandate compensation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Protect apologies from being used as evidence of liability', 'Sorry laws protect a physician''s apology or expression of sympathy from being admitted in court as evidence of liability, encouraging disclosure while preserving legal accountability.'),
('8be8730d-43c2-5116-8054-85de39984d02'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Enterprise liability in healthcare means that:', '["The nurse bears sole liability","Coverage is provided for all employees","The hospital is liable for all care provided","The physician bears sole liability","The patient is liable"]'::jsonb, 'The hospital is liable for all care provided', 'Under enterprise liability, the healthcare entity or hospital bears responsibility for the overall quality of care delivered within its system rather than each individual professional.'),
('d0ab706a-5bd2-5cd9-bfbc-73449751b609'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Patient education and consent materials should be written at a reading level that is:', '["Medical school level","Any level","Legal level","5th-6th grade level","College level"]'::jsonb, '5th-6th grade level', 'Consent and patient education materials should be written at a reading level most patients can understand, commonly around a 5th to 6th grade level.'),
('309dce09-169b-5bab-8dd2-5a0b531e88a6'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The use of interpreters in healthcare is:', '["Not needed resulting in decreased functional activity","Only for consent accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","An ethical and legal requirement for language access","Optional without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only for surgery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An ethical and legal requirement for language access', 'Providing language access through qualified interpreters is both an ethical duty that permits informed decisions and a legal requirement when the patient''s language differs from the provider''s.'),
('1da32519-db56-5464-a748-dd538f913df5'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Health literacy refers to:', '["Education level resulting in decreased functional activity","Medical knowledge accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Reading ability mediated by specific receptor activation","IQ without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ability to obtain, process, and understand health information"]'::jsonb, 'Ability to obtain, process, and understand health information', 'Health literacy is the capacity to obtain, process, and understand basic health information needed to make appropriate health decisions. It goes beyond reading ability or education alone.'),
('783d0350-076b-5020-a2e6-9c3e856f0e13'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The corporate practice of medicine doctrine generally prohibits:', '["Physicians teaching resulting in decreased functional activity","Physicians forming groups accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Physicians doing research mediated by specific receptor activation","Physicians employed by hospitals without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Non-physician corporations employing physicians to practice medicine"]'::jsonb, 'Non-physician corporations employing physicians to practice medicine', 'The corporate practice of medicine doctrine generally prevents lay corporations from employing physicians to provide medical services, limiting the practice of medicine to licensed professionals.'),
('9972641e-4d1c-5556-a8c1-8673a2c0696d'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Peer review of physician performance should be:', '["Retaliatory resulting in decreased functional activity","Public accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Selective mediated by specific receptor activation","Confidential, protected, and constructive","Punitive leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Confidential, protected, and constructive', 'Peer review evaluates a colleague''s practice for quality assurance. It must be confidential, protected from legal disclosure, and constructive so that reviewers are candid and clinicians can improve without fear of retaliation.'),
('8b65d5bd-f9e1-5e25-b5a6-aee555a47841'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'False imprisonment in healthcare means:', '["Confidentiality breach","Refusing treatment","Leaving against advice","Hospital discharge","Unlawful confinement or restraint of a patient"]'::jsonb, 'Unlawful confinement or restraint of a patient', 'False imprisonment is the intentional, unlawful confinement or restraint of a person without lawful justification. In healthcare it may occur when patients are detained against their will without legal authority.'),
('91caa2bc-1902-52d8-88e9-838865a0b7b6'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Advance care planning involves:', '["Insurance planning resulting in decreased functional activity","Deciding only the code status accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Writing a will mediated by specific receptor activation","Discussion about goals and preferences for future care","Financial planning leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Discussion about goals and preferences for future care', 'Advance care planning is an ongoing conversation about a patient''s values, goals, and preferences for future care, especially for times when the patient cannot speak for themselves. It informs, but is not the same as, a living will or financial planning.'),
('2e559afa-2b25-52c2-a694-83b544c318b6'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The importance of advance care planning is to:', '["Save money resulting in decreased functional activity","Increase profit accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ensure care aligns with patient values when they lack capacity","Comply with law without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Reduce hospital stay leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ensure care aligns with patient values when they lack capacity', 'Advance care planning ensures that future care reflects the patient''s own values and preferences at a time when they lack capacity. This preserves autonomy and reduces unwanted or non-beneficial treatment.'),
('2eb2ffea-04a1-53d2-833c-4742717ca20a'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Physician Orders for Life-Sustaining Treatment (POLST) are:', '["Medical orders based on patient preferences","Consent forms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Advance directives mediated by specific receptor activation","Living wills without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Insurance forms leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Medical orders based on patient preferences', 'A POLST is a set of medical orders, usually completed with a physician, that translate a patient''s preferences about life-sustaining treatment into actionable orders that follow the patient across care settings. Unlike advance directives, they bind emergency personnel directly.'),
('68a51e70-3401-58eb-a398-63cd72dec267'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Capacity assessment must be performed when a patient:', '["Is confused","Has dementia","Refuses recommended treatment","Is unconscious","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Capacity should be assessed whenever there is doubt about a patient''s ability to make a specific decision, including confusion, cognitive impairment, refusal of recommended treatment, or unconsciousness. Each situation raises concern that the patient cannot decide autonomously.'),
('7572dc78-aeae-55be-816f-f78ecf02b234'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The four abilities required for decision-making capacity are:', '["Reading, writing, arithmetic, and speaking","Understanding, appreciation, reasoning, and expressing a choice","Hearing, seeing, feeling, and thinking","Sight, hearing, touch, and taste","Consciousness, memory, intelligence, and attention"]'::jsonb, 'Understanding, appreciation, reasoning, and expressing a choice', 'Decision-making capacity requires four abilities: understanding relevant information, appreciating its significance for oneself, reasoning through the options, and communicating a choice. These are tested for each specific decision.'),
('076528c2-6ad7-5358-91db-1a3ef08cf563'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'A patient who cannot make medical decisions requires:', '["A court hearing","Hospital discharge","Forced treatment","A surrogate decision-maker","Treatment without consent"]'::jsonb, 'A surrogate decision-maker', 'When a patient lacks capacity, decisions are made by a surrogate, whether an appointed agent, next of kin, or court-appointed guardian. The surrogate should apply the substituted judgment or best interest standard.'),
('d0c91e66-fff1-5d34-8a97-358370b867ee'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The substituted judgment standard requires a surrogate to decide:', '["Based on what the patient would have wanted","What is cheapest","What the family wants","What the physician recommends","In the patient''s best interest"]'::jsonb, 'Based on what the patient would have wanted', 'Substituted judgment asks the surrogate to make the decision the patient would have made, drawing on their known values and prior statements. It differs from the best interest standard, which focuses on what benefits the patient most.'),
('42ade065-fd28-5323-a8fe-473577cdbd1a'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The best interest standard requires a surrogate to:', '["Consider cost","Follow what the patient would have wanted","Follow physician preference","Decide based on what benefits the patient most","Follow family preference"]'::jsonb, 'Decide based on what benefits the patient most', 'The best interest standard directs the surrogate to choose the option that most promotes the patient''s welfare and wellbeing. It is used when the patient''s own wishes are unknown.'),
('351d4773-4685-51d2-9585-0bfa796243a5'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Euthanasia differs from physician-assisted suicide (PAS) because in PAS:', '["The procedure is more painful resulting in decreased functional activity","PAS is illegal everywhere accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Euthanasia is self-administered mediated by specific receptor activation","Euthanasia is legal everywhere without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The patient self-administers the lethal drug at their own initiation"]'::jsonb, 'The patient self-administers the lethal drug at their own initiation', 'In physician-assisted suicide the physician provides the means, typically a lethal prescription, but the patient performs the final act of self-administration. In euthanasia the physician directly administers the lethal intervention.'),
('b29e6919-bbf4-52e8-b53f-03fc12eb98f4'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The concept of a good death (dying well) includes:', '["Freedom from pain, dignity, and closure with loved ones","Only prolonging life accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only comfort mediated by specific receptor activation","Only privacy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only pain relief leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Freedom from pain, dignity, and closure with loved ones', 'A good death typically involves relief from suffering, preservation of dignity and control, and the opportunity for closure with family and loved ones. It reflects physical, psychological, and social dimensions of dying well.'),
('8438ddd4-a27f-5a2b-9592-6061a6d7cf60'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Burnout in healthcare professionals is most characterized by which combination of features?', '["Emotional exhaustion, depersonalization, and reduced accomplishment","Relocation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Financial wealth mediated by specific receptor activation","Retirement without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Job promotion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Emotional exhaustion, depersonalization, and reduced accomplishment', 'Burnout is a syndrome of emotional exhaustion, depersonalization or cynicism toward patients, and a reduced sense of personal accomplishment. It is an occupational hazard linked to chronic workplace stress.'),
('c66ea39d-7e27-56e1-9cd5-cc82ca00165a'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'An error disclosure conversation should include:', '["Defensiveness resulting in decreased functional activity","Blame shifting accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lying mediated by specific receptor activation","Minimizing without affecting the primary tissue pathway","What happened, why it happened, an apology, and a plan to prevent recurrence"]'::jsonb, 'What happened, why it happened, an apology, and a plan to prevent recurrence', 'Honest disclosure of a medical error includes explaining what happened and why, apologizing, and describing how recurrence will be prevented. Such transparency supports patient trust and is ethically and legally expected.'),
('ccf92b65-599b-5995-8c8e-7c046989f068'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Patient safety culture emphasizes:', '["Learning from errors, system improvement, and transparency","Punishment for errors accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Secrecy mediated by specific receptor activation","Individual blame without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Compliance leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Learning from errors, system improvement, and transparency', 'A positive safety culture treats errors as opportunities to improve systems rather than to assign blame. It is characterized by openness, transparency, and a shared commitment to harm prevention.'),
('240b208f-d8e7-5112-b660-1a2292e7dc7d'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Disclosure of adverse events to patients is:', '["Optional","Only required if the patient asks","Discouraged","Prohibited","Ethically and often legally required"]'::jsonb, 'Ethically and often legally required', 'Clinicians have an ethical and, in many jurisdictions, a legal duty to disclose adverse events honestly and promptly. Withholding information about harm undermines trust and the patient''s ability to make informed decisions.'),
('99ef35e5-7beb-5add-98df-cfcc9b79a57b'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The concept of never events refers to:', '["Routine complications resulting in decreased functional activity","Serious preventable adverse events that should never occur","Petty errors mediated by specific receptor activation","Minor mistakes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Events that inevitably happen leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Serious preventable adverse events that should never occur', 'Never events are serious, largely preventable patient safety events, such as wrong-site surgery or retained instruments, that should never occur. Their occurrence signals a serious system failure.'),
('8225e865-7695-5935-bf3a-d190a8145e16'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'No-fault compensation for medical injury means:', '["Compensation only if negligence is proven","Compensation to cover care costs","Compensation without proving negligence","The patient assumes the risk","Compensation only if the injury was intentional"]'::jsonb, 'Compensation without proving negligence', 'In a no-fault system, patients injured by medical care receive compensation without having to prove negligence. The scheme trades individual blame for broader, more efficient compensation of harm.'),
('73dc7b6c-b43a-57eb-8e13-76f0e9e86bcf'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Human factors engineering in healthcare aims to:', '["Reduce staff resulting in decreased functional activity","Increase workload accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Blame individuals mediated by specific receptor activation","Automate all care without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Design systems that reduce error and improve safety"]'::jsonb, 'Design systems that reduce error and improve safety', 'Human factors engineering applies knowledge of human capabilities and limitations to design safer work systems, equipment, and processes. It reduces error by making it harder for well-intentioned people to make mistakes.'),
('07079656-556e-5d37-8f02-4ce110039e44'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Checklists in healthcare are used to:', '["Replace training resulting in decreased functional activity","Standardize critical processes and reduce errors","Slow down care mediated by specific receptor activation","Increase paperwork without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Confuse staff leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Standardize critical processes and reduce errors', 'Checklists standardize key steps and improve reliability in high-risk processes, as with the WHO Surgical Safety Checklist. They reduce omissions and errors without replacing clinical judgment or training.'),
('e403bd39-6fb1-551d-93c1-463de2293b92'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The Bolitho modification of the Bolam test states that:', '["Medical opinion must be logical and reasonable","Any opinion suffices","Only majority opinion counts","Only expert testimony matters","Opinion is not needed"]'::jsonb, 'Medical opinion must be logical and reasonable', 'Under Bolam, practice is judged against a responsible body of medical opinion. Bolitho added that the court may reject that opinion if it does not withstand logical analysis, so the standard must be both responsible and logically defensible.'),
('423427f1-867f-5ade-8765-f891c745b6e5'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'TeamSTEPPS is a framework to:', '["Reduce costs resulting in decreased functional activity","Treat patients accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Improve team communication and patient safety","Step down care without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Train surgeons leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Improve team communication and patient safety', 'TeamSTEPPS (Team Strategies and Tools to Enhance Performance and Patient Safety) is an evidence-based teamwork system that enhances communication, situation monitoring, and mutual support among healthcare professionals. It aims to improve the quality and safety of care.'),
('07c03dcd-f1fb-5a8e-8a78-862a9bd9d8b1'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The hierarchy gradient in healthcare teams can:', '["Cause harm only in emergencies","Reduce errors","Inhibit open communication if too steep","Improve communication","Improve team morale"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibit open communication if too steep', 'A steep hierarchy makes junior staff reluctant to voice concerns, which can silence safety-relevant information. Flattening the gradient encourages speaking up and improves patient safety.'),
('0849ca8d-d72d-55ec-b234-9087aaa4728a'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Speaking up in healthcare means:', '["Complaining","Ignoring concerns","Only physicians speak","Remaining silent","Voicing concerns about patient safety"]'::jsonb, 'Voicing concerns about patient safety', 'Speaking up refers to voicing safety concerns, questions, or disagreements before harm reaches a patient. It is a core element of psychological safety and effective teamwork.'),
('a88744fe-f305-5e2d-ac39-15aee84a662d'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'A patient safety culture in which most errors are attributed to individual misconduct rather than system factors reflects a:', '["Tendency to blame individuals rather than systems","Correct identification of root causes","Appropriate disciplinary action","Impartial investigation","System thinking"]'::jsonb, 'Tendency to blame individuals rather than systems', 'A blame-oriented culture focuses on punishing the individual clinician instead of analyzing the latent system factors that enable errors. Moving away from individual blame toward system improvement enhances reporting and safety.'),
('13170d07-89ae-5b4e-8f5c-814c384458a6'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Just culture in patient safety distinguishes between:', '["Only reward resulting in decreased functional activity","Financial rewards accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Human error, at-risk behavior, and reckless behavior","Cultural differences without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Punishment for all errors leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Human error, at-risk behavior, and reckless behavior', 'A just culture differentiates human error, managed by support and system redesign, from at-risk behavior, managed by coaching, and reckless behavior, managed by discipline. This balance preserves accountability while encouraging reporting.'),
('e9c4a82e-2388-5853-9e20-70a961384a68'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Human error should be managed by:', '["Fines resulting in decreased functional activity","Termination accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Punishment mediated by specific receptor activation","System redesign and support","Suspension leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'System redesign and support', 'Because human error is unintentional, it is best managed by improving systems and supporting the clinician rather than by punishment. Disciplinary responses should be reserved for reckless or intentional misconduct.'),
('2ff71ae2-f94c-5a7d-a426-cffa7468fb16'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'At-risk behavior should be managed by:', '["Ignoring it resulting in decreased functional activity","Rewarding it accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Punishment mediated by specific receptor activation","Termination without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Coaching and removing incentives for risky behavior"]'::jsonb, 'Coaching and removing incentives for risky behavior', 'At-risk behavior occurs when the person does not recognize the risk their actions carry. It is managed by coaching and by removing workplace incentives that encourage shortcuts, rather than by punishment, which is reserved for reckless behavior.'),
('a9c6a957-6d6f-591b-a37c-3f187f639cb3'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Physician-assisted suicide:', '["The physician provides the means and the patient self-administers","The physician directly administers the lethal drug accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Withholding treatment mediated by specific receptor activation","Withdrawing life support without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Refusing treatment leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The physician provides the means and the patient self-administers', 'In physician-assisted suicide the clinician supplies the means, such as a lethal prescription, but the patient performs the final self-administering act. If the physician administers the lethal agent directly, the act is euthanasia.'),
('d643abf5-944a-5c2b-8907-2573cd3f31b9'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The Dax Cowart case illustrates the ethical principle of:', '["Justice resulting in decreased functional activity","Beneficence accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Autonomy and the right to refuse treatment despite others'' wishes","Confidentiality without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Futility leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Autonomy and the right to refuse treatment despite others'' wishes', 'Dax Cowart, a burn patient, repeatedly refused treatment that was continued against his will because clinicians judged it beneficial. The case highlights the tension between beneficence and a patient''s autonomy to refuse even life-saving care.'),
('37f3b7b3-e97b-582e-a39e-32537fcbffea'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The Terri Schiavo case raised issues about:', '["Stem cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Abortion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Animal research mediated by specific receptor activation","Withdrawal of life support in a persistent vegetative state, surrogacy, and legal disputes","Euthanasia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Withdrawal of life support in a persistent vegetative state, surrogacy, and legal disputes', 'The Terri Schiavo case centered on whether life support could be withdrawn from a patient in a persistent vegetative state and who had authority to decide when family members disagreed. It highlighted surrogate decision-making and the role of the courts.'),
('6e6a810c-2c31-56e6-8c3c-af6ec84f9ca7'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The Baby Doe regulations in the US relate to:', '["Adoption resulting in decreased functional activity","Treatment of disabled newborns","Child abuse mediated by specific receptor activation","Abortion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vaccination leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Treatment of disabled newborns', 'The Baby Doe regulations protect disabled newborns from discrimination by requiring that they receive medically indicated treatment. They were designed to prevent the withholding of life-sustaining care solely on the basis of disability.'),
('d2d3b6c6-cbdc-522f-9e19-5509bd0145f2'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'In the case of Charlie Gard the ethical tension was between:', '["Parental wishes and medical opinion about futility","Research versus treatment goals accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Two treatments mediated by specific receptor activation","Cost of care without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Location of treatment leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Parental wishes and medical opinion about futility', 'The dispute centered on whether continued life-sustaining treatment was futile, with parents seeking further interventions while clinicians judged them not in the child''s best interests. Courts ultimately supported the medical opinion on futility.'),
('dc90ce03-0c5e-52b4-98cc-6deeea15868e'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The ordinary versus extraordinary means distinction in medical ethics refers to:', '["Relative cost","Traditional vs new treatments","Proportionate vs disproportionate interventions","Surgery vs medication","Natural vs artificial"]'::jsonb, 'Proportionate vs disproportionate interventions', 'The distinction turns on whether an intervention is proportionate and offers reasonable hope of benefit for the patient, not on whether it is conventional or novel. Ordinary (proportionate) measures are obligatory, whereas extraordinary (disproportionate) measures may be ethically forgone.'),
('2b77768c-a21f-5156-ac39-9924433cbe2d'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The WHO definition of health is:', '["Absence of disability resulting in decreased functional activity","Absence of disease accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Physical fitness mediated by specific receptor activation","Long life without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Complete physical, mental and social well-being; not merely the absence of disease"]'::jsonb, 'Complete physical, mental and social well-being; not merely the absence of disease', 'The World Health Organization defines health as a state of complete physical, mental and social well-being, not merely the absence of disease or infirmity. This definition broadened health beyond a purely biomedical, disease-free model.'),
('b95efa25-3154-5423-ae85-a2c5a15fe8fe'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Positive health emphasizes:', '["Treatment resulting in decreased functional activity","Disease absence accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Wellness and optimal functioning","Prevention without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Diagnosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Wellness and optimal functioning', 'Positive health focuses on well-being and optimal functioning rather than the mere absence of disease. It emphasizes assets such as coping ability, resilience and social connectedness.'),
('1b81a7a4-8365-5094-a6dd-4fa026f7a669'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The biomedical model focuses on:', '["Emotions resulting in decreased functional activity","Society accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Whole person mediated by specific receptor activation","Culture without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Disease as deviation from biological norms"]'::jsonb, 'Disease as deviation from biological norms', 'The biomedical model reduces health and illness to deviations from biological norms and measurable pathophysiological parameters. It privileges disease processes over psychosocial and cultural dimensions.'),
('5cffecec-2cc6-5c23-bedf-5f6ec8641c29'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Empathy differs from sympathy because empathy involves:', '["Pity resulting in decreased functional activity","Excessive emotion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Indifference mediated by specific receptor activation","Helplessness without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Understanding patient''s experience without merging feelings (feeling with vs feeling for)"]'::jsonb, 'Understanding patient''s experience without merging feelings (feeling with vs feeling for)', 'Empathy is understanding and sharing the patient''s perspective while retaining appropriate emotional distance. Sympathy entails feeling for the patient and risks merging the clinician''s emotions with the patient''s.'),
('283f5014-3346-5c9c-a0d5-ad6258270490'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Emotional intelligence in physicians includes:', '["Medical knowledge only resulting in decreased functional activity","Self-awareness, self-regulation, empathy and social skills","Technical skill mediated by specific receptor activation","IQ only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Physical strength leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Self-awareness, self-regulation, empathy and social skills', 'Emotional intelligence comprises recognizing and regulating one''s own emotions, empathizing with others and navigating social interactions. These skills support communication, teamwork and resilience in clinical practice.'),
('04149ce6-58a0-530d-9767-2dc5c4aaa8b9'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Resilience in healthcare professionals means:', '["Avoidance resulting in decreased functional activity","Ability to adapt and recover from adversity","Perfection mediated by specific receptor activation","Denial without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Absence of stress leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ability to adapt and recover from adversity', 'Resilience is the capacity to adapt positively and recover when facing adversity, stress or burnout. Protective factors include social support, self-care and structured reflection rather than avoidance.'),
('922405da-783a-5585-b980-b69541a4451a'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Mindfulness in clinical practice involves:', '["Daydreaming resulting in decreased functional activity","Present-moment non-judgmental awareness","Rushing mediated by specific receptor activation","Multi-tasking without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Distraction leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Present-moment non-judgmental awareness', 'Mindfulness is a state of attentive, non-judgmental awareness of the present moment. It helps clinicians regulate stress and improves attentiveness during clinical encounters.'),
('98647d8e-0fd3-5f57-8f85-8311be0ee6db'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The healing relationship is characterized by:', '["Detachment resulting in decreased functional activity","Control accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Efficiency mediated by specific receptor activation","Authority without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Trust, empathy and collaborative goal-setting"]'::jsonb, 'Trust, empathy and collaborative goal-setting', 'A healing relationship rests on trust, empathy and shared goal-setting between clinician and patient. These elements strengthen engagement, communication and adherence to care plans.'),
('a4df3779-a4bd-5bed-98f2-e6868e1cbe21'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Sense of coherence has three components:', '["Understanding, action, purpose","Thinking, feeling, doing","Physical, mental, social","Comprehensibility, manageability, meaningfulness","Body, mind, spirit"]'::jsonb, 'Comprehensibility, manageability, meaningfulness', 'Antonovsky''s sense of coherence comprises comprehensibility (making sense of events), manageability (having resources to cope) and meaningfulness (seeing life as worth engaging). These components foster resilience and health.'),
('08df8926-32e6-57b3-9bc0-a3b26ca3651d'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Motivational interviewing is a method to:', '["Resolve ambivalence and enhance intrinsic motivation for change","Threaten accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Force compliance mediated by specific receptor activation","Dictate change without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Persuade leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Resolve ambivalence and enhance intrinsic motivation for change', 'Motivational interviewing is a collaborative, patient-centered approach that resolves ambivalence and strengthens intrinsic motivation for behavior change. It deliberately avoids confrontation, coercion and direct persuasion.'),
('67d151ae-2a0f-5c09-9fc8-48395def8096'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The stages of change model includes:', '["Denial, anger, bargaining, depression, acceptance resulting in decreased functional activity","Awakening, struggle, resolution accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Start, middle, end mediated by specific receptor activation","Precontemplation, contemplation, preparation, action, maintenance, termination","Acute, chronic, recovery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Precontemplation, contemplation, preparation, action, maintenance, termination', 'The transtheoretical model describes change as a sequence of precontemplation, contemplation, preparation, action and maintenance (with termination). Progress through the stages is typically non-linear.'),
('99cb8a5f-f3fe-5346-85ae-dabc835be587'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The Bolitho modification of the Bolam principle states that:', '["Medical opinion must be logical and reasonable","Any opinion suffices","Only the majority opinion counts","Only expert testimony matters","Opinion is not needed"]'::jsonb, 'Medical opinion must be logical and reasonable', 'Bolitho held that a body of medical opinion is only defensible if it is logically sustainable, allowing courts to reject expert opinion lacking a reasonable basis. This qualifies the Bolam test''s reliance on professional custom.'),
('2c53d217-dd64-5a3f-83de-984d426c003d'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Self-management support helps patients with chronic disease to:', '["Visit the doctor weekly","Give up","Rely on family","Only take medication","Manage their condition day-to-day"]'::jsonb, 'Manage their condition day-to-day', 'Self-management support equips patients with the knowledge, skills and confidence to manage their chronic condition day-to-day. It emphasizes patient activation and partnership rather than clinician control.'),
('9b174a0a-1e89-5864-8a4b-ffd49c2a87ee'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The patient-centered medical home provides:', '["Surgery only resulting in decreased functional activity","Specialty only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only emergency care mediated by specific receptor activation","Only primary care without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Comprehensive, coordinated, accessible patient-centered care"]'::jsonb, 'Comprehensive, coordinated, accessible patient-centered care', 'A patient-centered medical home is a primary care model delivering comprehensive, coordinated, accessible and patient-centered services. It emphasizes continuity, team-based care and enhanced access.'),
('6b1ca33b-c5b4-5817-ac08-160d31f78e9b'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Integrated care means:', '["Coordination of physical and behavioral health services","Only pharmacy services accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Separate services mediated by specific receptor activation","Only medical services without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only mental health services leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Coordination of physical and behavioral health services', 'Integrated care coordinates physical, behavioral and social services so that care is delivered as a coherent whole. Fragmented and siloed services undermine this model.'),
('56aa3f23-1501-5321-a393-90a51c549197'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Interprofessional education (IPE) is:', '["Learning together to promote collaborative practice","Solo learning accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lectures only mediated by specific receptor activation","Self-study without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Online learning only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Learning together to promote collaborative practice', 'IPE brings students from two or more professional groups together to learn with, from and about one another. Its goal is to foster collaborative practice and team-based patient care.'),
('600384b0-94b3-5ce6-b91f-cc2ec4c95068'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The role of a patient advocate is to:', '["Represent the physician","Support and represent patient''s interests","Represent the family","Represent insurance companies","Represent the hospital"]'::jsonb, 'Support and represent patient''s interests', 'A patient advocate supports and represents the patient''s interests within the healthcare system. The advocate helps patients navigate care and ensures their voice is heard.'),
('6cd5bf61-710f-5e47-9d62-a6d955a31f50'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Narrative medicine emphasizes:', '["Treatment resulting in decreased functional activity","Understanding patient''s illness narrative and meaning","Only biomedical facts mediated by specific receptor activation","Testing without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Diagnosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Understanding patient''s illness narrative and meaning', 'Narrative medicine attends to the patient''s illness story, context and subjective meaning rather than only biomedical facts. It aims to deepen understanding and strengthen the clinician-patient relationship.'),
('5c1d91be-f3bc-55fd-91ac-1673968731c4'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The sick role (Parsons) includes:', '["Exemption from normal roles and obligation to seek help","Need for treatment accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Independence mediated by specific receptor activation","Continued responsibility without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Self-care leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Exemption from normal roles and obligation to seek help', 'Parsons'' sick role exempts the ill person from normal social obligations while imposing the duty to seek competent help and cooperate with treatment. Illness thus carries both rights and responsibilities.'),
('3b340b39-27ca-5f84-bcd8-e41d81072bb5'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Illness behavior refers to:', '["Physical symptoms resulting in decreased functional activity","Treatment accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hospitalization mediated by specific receptor activation","How individuals perceive, evaluate and respond to symptoms","Diagnosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'How individuals perceive, evaluate and respond to symptoms', 'Illness behavior describes how individuals perceive, evaluate and respond to symptoms. It shapes the decision to seek or delay care and is influenced by culture and social context.'),
('532c8baa-7c26-5ba5-b5ec-53d66dc47ccb'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Delay in seeking care can be due to:', '["Quick action resulting in decreased functional activity","Appraisal delay, illness delay, behavioral delay and medical delay","Fear of diagnosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Financial constraints only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Access barriers only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Appraisal delay, illness delay, behavioral delay and medical delay', 'Help-seeking delay comprises appraisal delay, illness delay, behavioral delay and medical (scheduling) delay. Reducing the pathway to care therefore requires attention to psychosocial and clinician factors as well as access and cost.'),
('4b718eb4-8bf1-5c2d-b793-05be6a343d51'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Social determinants of health include:', '["Only healthcare resulting in decreased functional activity","Only genetics accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only lifestyle mediated by specific receptor activation","Income, education, housing, environment and social support","Only age leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Income, education, housing, environment and social support', 'Social determinants are the conditions in which people are born, grow, live, work and age, including income, education, housing and social support. These factors structure health inequalities beyond healthcare and genetics alone.'),
('ea35416b-ac75-5df9-8b66-5196e7adda5f'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Health equity means:', '["Absence of disease resulting in decreased functional activity","All diseases cured accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Equal outcomes mediated by specific receptor activation","Everyone has fair opportunity to attain full health potential","Everyone gets same treatment leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Everyone has fair opportunity to attain full health potential', 'Health equity implies that everyone has a fair opportunity to reach their full health potential, free of avoidable disadvantage. It differs from equality of treatment, which gives identical care regardless of differing need.'),
('32c754ae-c6c3-52cf-9091-a9bb76d556d9'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The social gradient in health means:', '["Only education matters resulting in decreased functional activity","Health improves with each step up socioeconomic ladder","Only rich are healthy mediated by specific receptor activation","Determined entirely by genetics without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only poor are sick leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Health improves with each step up socioeconomic ladder', 'The social gradient describes how health improves with each step up the socioeconomic ladder, not only between rich and poor. The gradient persists even in affluent societies with universally accessible services.'),
('d7cbf54c-b8ae-55c5-8444-fcdd664321b5'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The Whitehall studies demonstrated:', '["Direct genetic causation of disease","Only lifestyle factors","Only genetic factors","Only healthcare access","Social gradient in health among British civil servants"]'::jsonb, 'Social gradient in health among British civil servants', 'The Whitehall studies of British civil servants showed a clear social gradient in health and mortality across employment grade. Lower grade was associated with worse health outcomes independently of traditional risk factors.'),
('0e09e8eb-7558-555f-a566-dcdefb7646e0'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The concept of allostatic load refers to:', '["Infection risk resulting in decreased functional activity","Exercise accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Genetic predisposition mediated by specific receptor activation","Wear and tear on the body from chronic stress","Diet leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Wear and tear on the body from chronic stress', 'Allostatic load denotes the cumulative physiological wear and tear resulting from chronic or repeated stress. It reflects dysregulation across neuroendocrine and cardiovascular systems and predicts adverse health outcomes.'),
('359d6921-1fd1-5146-8313-60fbda66f2d6'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Adverse childhood experiences (ACEs) are associated with:', '["Increased risk of chronic disease in adulthood","Only childhood effects","Effects limited to childhood","Genetic effects","Positive outcomes"]'::jsonb, 'Increased risk of chronic disease in adulthood', 'ACEs such as abuse, neglect and household dysfunction increase the risk of chronic disease, mental illness and early mortality in adulthood. The effect is dose-dependent and mediated partly by stress physiology and health behaviors.'),
('4a8bcfac-2515-54e3-9e13-c8bf95dc708d'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Placebo use is unethical when:', '["Proven effective treatment exists","Trial is double-blind","Study is small","Patient prefers placebo","Side effects are mild"]'::jsonb, 'Proven effective treatment exists', 'Withholding a proven effective treatment to give a placebo denies patients established benefit and violates beneficence. Placebo-controlled designs are only ethical when no proven therapy exists or when its use is justified and appropriately safeguarded.'),
('10a05eff-a575-5f1a-a77b-d058af5bb444'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'Post-traumatic growth refers to:', '["Return to baseline function resulting in decreased functional activity","Only negative outcomes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Positive psychological change after trauma","Only recovery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Denial leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Positive psychological change after trauma', 'Post-traumatic growth is positive psychological change arising from struggling with highly challenging life circumstances. It includes improved relationships, new possibilities and a changed sense of self.'),
('f329eecc-077c-5973-8c22-da753e1ad873'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The nocebo effect is:', '["Negative outcome from a placebo","Positive expectation","Allergic reaction","Negative effect from expectation of harm","Side effect"]'::jsonb, 'Negative effect from expectation of harm', 'The nocebo effect is an adverse outcome produced by negative expectations rather than the pharmacological action of the treatment. Expectation, conditioning and anxiety all contribute to it.'),
('662a942e-f4ea-5b0b-bf1d-9be4cced0b1c'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Medical Ethics - Medical Ethics', 'The placebo effect is best explained by which of the following mechanisms?', '["Purely psychological factors resulting in decreased functional activity","Expectation, conditioning and neurobiological mechanisms","Deception mediated by specific receptor activation","Coincidence without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Purely biological factors leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Expectation, conditioning and neurobiological mechanisms', 'The placebo effect arises through expectation of benefit, conditioning and changes in neurobiological pathways such as endogenous opioid and dopamine systems. It is not primarily deception or coincidence.'),
('c458ff73-1342-5500-bfce-80675fb1f32c'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'The Calgary-Cambridge guide provides a:', '["Framework for medical interview skills","Ethics code accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Statistical test mediated by specific receptor activation","Communication model without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Research method leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Framework for medical interview skills', 'The Calgary-Cambridge guide structures the medical interview into stages, including initiating the session, gathering information, and planning, and is used to teach and assess consultation and communication skills.'),
('b0b55be9-43d8-5ac8-984e-196dc9446bcf'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'The maxim ''res ipsa loquitur'' means:', '["The thing speaks for itself","Let the buyer beware","To err is human","The patient is right","Caveat emptor"]'::jsonb, 'The thing speaks for itself', 'Res ipsa loquitur, literally "the thing speaks for itself," allows negligence to be inferred from circumstances in which the injury would not ordinarily occur without negligence, for example a sponge left in the abdomen after surgery.'),
('b39ced75-2a25-576e-b4ee-5655ae38eca4'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Bodily movements and gestures during communication are known as:', '["Chronemics","Proxemics","Paralanguage","Haptics","Kinesics"]'::jsonb, 'Kinesics', 'Kinesics is the study of body movements, gestures, posture, and facial expression in communication. It is one of the major categories of non-verbal communication.'),
('7b86c54a-4e1f-5d76-bf1a-778f1332bf2a'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'The study of personal space and distance in communication is:', '["Kinesics","Chronemics","Paralanguage","Occulesics","Proxemics"]'::jsonb, 'Proxemics', 'Proxemics is the study of how people use and perceive personal space and interpersonal distance during interaction. It classifies distance into intimate, personal, social, and public zones.'),
('767be12a-9fe8-5529-a6f3-7bc133458344'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'In proxemics, the intimate zone is:', '["6-10 feet","4-12 feet","0-1.5 feet","12+ feet","1.5-4 feet"]'::jsonb, '0-1.5 feet', 'The intimate zone (about 0-1.5 feet) is reserved for the closest interactions such as whispering and physical contact. The personal (1.5-4 feet), social (4-12 feet), and public (12+ feet) zones lie progressively further out.'),
('a4234345-58f5-5a18-ad29-72d050f273df'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Paralanguage refers to:', '["Personal space resulting in decreased functional activity","Facial expressions accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Eye contact mediated by specific receptor activation","Body language without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Voice qualities, tone, pitch, volume, and speech rate"]'::jsonb, 'Voice qualities, tone, pitch, volume, and speech rate', 'Paralanguage encompasses the non-lexical vocal features of speech, including tone, pitch, volume, and rate, which modify the meaning of the spoken words.'),
('1d5da223-ed72-53cf-b228-3bb7683e76e3'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Competence is a determination, whereas capacity is a determination:', '["Legal, medical","Medical, legal","Ethical, legal","Clinical, ethical","Moral, clinical"]'::jsonb, 'Legal, medical', 'Competence is a legal determination made by a court, while decision-making capacity is a clinical assessment performed by physicians. A patient can lack capacity without being formally adjudicated incompetent.'),
('87f009c2-d6e2-5c19-b34f-817b140573b8'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Touch (haptics) in the clinical setting should be:', '["Only for children resulting in decreased functional activity","Used appropriately with sensitivity to context and culture","Avoided entirely mediated by specific receptor activation","Used only in emergencies without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Rough and quick leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Used appropriately with sensitivity to context and culture', 'Haptics is the use of touch in communication. In medicine, touch should be used judiciously and with sensitivity to the patient''s condition, personal boundaries, and cultural norms surrounding physical contact.'),
('49085bb7-4bee-5fc0-b2f1-4cecd7726e17'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Which communication skill is used when delivering difficult medical information?', '["Breaking bad news","Obtaining consent","History taking","Prescribing","Physical examination"]'::jsonb, 'Breaking bad news', 'Breaking bad news is the specific communication skill used to deliver serious or distressing medical information such as a cancer diagnosis. Structured approaches such as SPIKES guide the process.'),
('19962a9a-c9e9-5396-b33c-735952b5e064'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'The first step in the SPIKES protocol for breaking bad news is:', '["Getting Invitation resulting in decreased functional activity","Responding to Emotions accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Giving Knowledge mediated by specific receptor activation","Assessing Perception without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Setting up the interview (private quiet setting)"]'::jsonb, 'Setting up the interview (private quiet setting)', 'SPIKES begins with ''S'', setting up the interview, which involves arranging a private, quiet setting with adequate time and ensuring key people are present. The later steps are Perception, Invitation, Knowledge, Emotions, and Strategy.'),
('6dc32f22-93e7-5aab-aab6-9b158da35c75'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'In the SPIKES protocol, assessing the patient''s Perception involves:', '["Warning shot resulting in decreased functional activity","Finding out what the patient already knows","Giving information mediated by specific receptor activation","Setting the scene without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Discussing treatment leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Finding out what the patient already knows', 'The ''P'' of SPIKES explores what the patient already knows or believes about their condition before the news is delivered. This allows information to be pitched and tailored appropriately.'),
('b3ed1492-0daf-5264-b96f-1cc04dbba365'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'The Belmont Report identified the following ethical principles for human research:', '["Respect for persons, beneficence, justice","Only autonomy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only non-maleficence mediated by specific receptor activation","Only beneficence without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only justice leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Respect for persons, beneficence, justice', 'The Belmont Report (1979) set out three principles for human subjects research: respect for persons, beneficence, and justice. These underpin informed consent, risk-benefit assessment, and fair subject selection.'),
('62576f29-187b-5c87-8a97-df7ebbfdf81b'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'The ABCDE model for breaking bad news stands for:', '["Ask Believe Confirm Decide Explain resulting in decreased functional activity","Assessment Briefing Communication Decision Evaluation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Be Careful During Examination mediated by specific receptor activation","Advance preparation Build therapeutic environment Communicate well Deal with reactions Encourage emotions","Attend Before Care During End leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Advance preparation Build therapeutic environment Communicate well Deal with reactions Encourage emotions', 'The ABCDE model guides breaking bad news through advance preparation, building a therapeutic environment, communicating well, dealing with patient reactions, and encouraging and validating emotions.'),
('6c0e0ffe-2cd2-5a5c-bdf2-1ea0e3eb60c2'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Which components are part of the ABCDE approach to breaking bad news?', '["Advance preparation building environment communicating dealing with reactions and encouraging","Only communication accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only support mediated by specific receptor activation","Only information without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Assessment only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Advance preparation building environment communicating dealing with reactions and encouraging', 'The ABCDE approach comprises advance preparation, building a supportive environment, effective communication, dealing with reactions, and encouraging emotions. It offers a structured sequence for sharing difficult news.'),
('31af4dd6-9180-513a-84b1-c5e520688cad'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'When a cancer diagnosis is to be delivered, the information should be:', '["Prohibited resulting in decreased functional activity","Only if family demands accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Discouraged mediated by specific receptor activation","Mandatory without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Offered to the patient (patient preference respected)"]'::jsonb, 'Offered to the patient (patient preference respected)', 'Autonomy-based practice favours offering the diagnosis to the patient directly while respecting a patient who does not wish to be informed. Family involvement is welcomed only with the patient''s agreement.'),
('8389df5b-5317-5d11-8095-afcc023a9c11'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Patient-centered counseling emphasizes:', '["Physician''s agenda resulting in decreased functional activity","The patient''s perspective concerns and preferences","Protocol adherence mediated by specific receptor activation","Quick consultation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Standard advice leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The patient''s perspective concerns and preferences', 'Patient-centered counseling prioritises the patient''s perspective, concerns, and preferences rather than imposing the clinician''s agenda. Shared decision-making is built on this foundation.'),
('946f6e86-1123-5329-bf50-41e18290f7b8'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Motivational interviewing (MI) is a style used for:', '["Advising resulting in decreased functional activity","Directing patient accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Confronting mediated by specific receptor activation","Persuading without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Resolving ambivalence and strengthening intrinsic motivation"]'::jsonb, 'Resolving ambivalence and strengthening intrinsic motivation', 'Motivational interviewing aims to resolve ambivalence and strengthen the patient''s intrinsic motivation for change. It uses a collaborative, non-confrontational style.'),
('b98e0f41-cbcd-5eea-8274-868a99371561'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'The spirit of motivational interviewing includes:', '["Partnership acceptance compassion and evocation","Directing accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Authority mediated by specific receptor activation","Judgment without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Confrontation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Partnership acceptance compassion and evocation', 'The spirit of motivational interviewing combines partnership, acceptance, compassion, and evocation. These attitudes foster a collaborative and non-judgmental environment for change.'),
('ccfc613d-c9ef-5f5f-a756-ce1f93d28f86'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'In motivational interviewing, OARS stands for:', '["Organize Assess Review Schedule","Open questions Affirmations Reflections Summaries","Observe Analyze React Solve","Orient Arrange Refer Support","Offer Advice Respond Support"]'::jsonb, 'Open questions Affirmations Reflections Summaries', 'OARS is a core skill set in motivational interviewing: open questions, affirmations, reflections, and summaries. These techniques build rapport and elicit change talk.'),
('bdf8630e-b553-5282-91b8-c811fcdde5a3'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Change talk in motivational interviewing refers to:', '["Threats resulting in decreased functional activity","Family pressure accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Patient''s own statements about desire ability reasons and need for change","External reasons without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Physician''s advice leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Patient''s own statements about desire ability reasons and need for change', 'Change talk comprises the patient''s own statements expressing desire, ability, reasons, and need for change. The clinician seeks to elicit and reinforce these arguments for change.'),
('9db93387-1e55-529d-b499-9c9023e3a35b'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'The cognitive-behavioral approach focuses on:', '["Only behaviors resulting in decreased functional activity","Connections between thoughts feelings and behaviors","Only feelings mediated by specific receptor activation","Only thoughts without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Unconscious conflicts leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Connections between thoughts feelings and behaviors', 'Cognitive-behavioral therapy targets the connections between thoughts, feelings, and behaviors. Changing maladaptive thoughts and behaviors is used to relieve psychological distress.'),
('d6cee3cd-6f83-51fc-9e9d-2288d4099689'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Grief counseling aims to:', '["Eliminate grief resulting in decreased functional activity","Medicate accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Replace the deceased mediated by specific receptor activation","Facilitate adaptation to loss and prevent complicated grief","Reverse loss leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Facilitate adaptation to loss and prevent complicated grief', 'Grief counseling helps people adapt to a loss and aims to prevent complicated or prolonged grief. It supports normal mourning rather than eliminating or reversing the loss.'),
('2d5d9666-fd51-59a9-b6e3-f955ce49f7a0'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'In physician-assisted suicide:', '["Physician provides means, patient self-administers","Physician directly administers lethal drug","Withholding treatment","Withdrawing life support","Refusing treatment"]'::jsonb, 'Physician provides means, patient self-administers', 'Physician-assisted suicide involves the physician supplying the means of death while the patient performs the final act of self-administration. This distinguishes it from active euthanasia, in which the physician administers the lethal agent.'),
('ab24c7c1-3d68-5ec7-b8e1-54c40e849717'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Reflection in active listening means:', '["Interrupting resulting in decreased functional activity","Analyzing accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Judging mediated by specific receptor activation","Diagnosing without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Repeating back what the patient said to show understanding"]'::jsonb, 'Repeating back what the patient said to show understanding', 'Reflection involves repeating or paraphrasing the patient''s words back to them to demonstrate understanding and confirm accuracy. It reassures the patient that their concerns have been heard.'),
('711e63ee-2a2f-535a-a287-7cddfb940435'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Summarizing in medical interviews serves to:', '["End the interview resulting in decreased functional activity","Waste time accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Repeat everything mediated by specific receptor activation","Consolidate information show understanding and transition to next phase","Confuse the patient leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Consolidate information show understanding and transition to next phase', 'A summary consolidates the information gathered, demonstrates that the clinician has understood the patient, and signals a transition to the next phase of the consultation. It also allows the patient to correct any misunderstanding.'),
('a9897000-d91b-57bc-8d37-b59f72b05e6f'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Placebo use in clinical trials is unethical when:', '["Proven effective treatment exists","Trial is double-blind","Study is small","Patient prefers placebo","Side effects are mild"]'::jsonb, 'Proven effective treatment exists', 'A placebo control is unethical when an effective treatment already exists, because withholding proven therapy would deprive participants of it. In such cases an active-comparator design should be used.'),
('b85d6a6f-faf2-50c1-93cb-15d3fc549cff'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'The Tarasoff case established the:', '["Duty to warn third party at serious risk","Patient confidentiality","Right to refuse treatment","Informed consent","Euthanasia principles"]'::jsonb, 'Duty to warn third party at serious risk', 'In Tarasoff v Regents of the University of California, the court held that a clinician who determines that a patient poses a serious threat to an identifiable person has a duty to warn that person. This is a recognised exception to confidentiality.'),
('794323ac-041c-5531-b2b1-b5e2e0aafc9a'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Language barriers in healthcare should be addressed by:', '["Ignoring the barrier","Writing notes","Using gestures","Using professional medical interpreters","Using family members as interpreters"]'::jsonb, 'Using professional medical interpreters', 'Professional medical interpreters provide accurate and confidential interpretation and reduce error compared with untrained family members. Using trained interpreters also protects patient privacy and dignity.'),
('80feb406-b745-5035-a2c7-cd1482b65628'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Health literacy barriers should be addressed by:', '["Assumed understanding","Fast speech","Complex medical terms","Written instructions only","Using plain language and teach-back method"]'::jsonb, 'Using plain language and teach-back method', 'Low health literacy is managed by using plain, jargon-free language and checking understanding with the teach-back method. Verbal and written information should be tailored to the patient''s reading level.'),
('0b805722-4946-50e4-8a4a-fe82b34b50cb'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'The teach-back method involves:', '["Teaching a new skill resulting in decreased functional activity","Asking patient to explain in their own words what was discussed","Giving instructions mediated by specific receptor activation","Exam preparation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Testing knowledge leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Asking patient to explain in their own words what was discussed', 'Teach-back asks the patient to restate the information in their own words to confirm understanding. Gaps revealed by this can then be re-explained before the consultation ends.'),
('40dbaff6-c71b-518b-a5dc-50683885c019'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Patient interview skills include:', '["Only diagnosis resulting in decreased functional activity","Only history taking accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only physical exam mediated by specific receptor activation","Only treatment without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Rapport building data gathering and planning"]'::jsonb, 'Rapport building data gathering and planning', 'Clinical interview skills span the whole consultation, from building rapport and gathering information to explaining and planning. Structured frameworks such as the Calgary-Cambridge guide organise these phases.'),
('2de43ad1-b276-5152-9205-96f50fa98154'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Agenda setting at the beginning of a consultation involves:', '["Rushing","Only physician''s agenda","Limiting discussion to the presenting complaint","Only patient''s agenda","Eliciting all patient concerns and prioritizing"]'::jsonb, 'Eliciting all patient concerns and prioritizing', 'Agenda setting elicits the full range of the patient''s concerns early in the consultation and helps prioritise them collaboratively. This prevents late-appearing concerns and improves consultation efficiency.'),
('b9eb5650-d71e-501e-983e-87f3834ace49'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Picking up non-verbal cues from patients requires:', '["Observation and attention to body language, voice and expression","Only listening accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Note-taking mediated by specific receptor activation","Only asking questions without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Computer use leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Observation and attention to body language, voice and expression', 'Non-verbal cues such as posture, facial expression, tone of voice and gesture convey emotion and distress that words may miss. Detecting them depends on observation and attention to body language, voice and expression. Listening, note-taking or questioning alone cannot capture this channel.'),
('2ff78d2c-e0d8-5fdf-a6ec-65ffef011d64'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'In medical interviews, signposting means:', '["Telling the patient what you are going to do next","Road directions accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Silence mediated by specific receptor activation","Writing notes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pointing to body parts leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Telling the patient what you are going to do next', 'Signposting is a communication technique that announces and links the next part of the interview, for example: ''Now I am going to examine you''. It gives the consultation structure and reduces patient anxiety by making progress predictable.'),
('2cb4574a-9442-59a1-a75a-e6ecd05faa3d'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Transition statements help to:', '["Move smoothly between phases of the consultation","Start the consultation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Prescribe mediated by specific receptor activation","End the consultation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Diagnose leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Move smoothly between phases of the consultation', 'Transition statements bridge one phase of the interview and the next, such as moving from history to examination. They create smooth flow but do not themselves initiate, terminate, prescribe or diagnose.'),
('217672a8-e991-512a-a8d4-1ccffd092903'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Facilitative responses in listening include:', '["Encouraging continuation (e.g. ''uh-huh'', ''go on'')","Judging accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Leading mediated by specific receptor activation","Interrupting without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Silence leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Encouraging continuation (e.g. ''uh-huh'', ''go on'')', 'Facilitative responses are minimal verbal encouragers such as ''uh-huh'' and ''go on'' that invite the patient to continue. Judging, leading and interrupting disrupt the patient''s narrative, and silence does not actively encourage.'),
('49e3126d-8ba6-51e1-8028-a8d3f390656f'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'The explanatory model of illness (Kleinman) explores:', '["What the patient thinks caused their illness","Only treatment accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only diagnosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Only symptoms without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only prognosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'What the patient thinks caused their illness', 'Kleinman''s explanatory model asks about the patient''s beliefs concerning the cause, nature, expected course and treatment of their illness. Understanding the patient''s perceived cause is central to culturally sensitive care.'),
('0bcdc9ff-8561-571c-a86b-180ed2ee8ac9'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Decision aids are tools used to:', '["Help patients make informed decisions consistent with their values","Replace the physician accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Treat the patient mediated by specific receptor activation","Confuse the patient without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Diagnose the patient leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Help patients make informed decisions consistent with their values', 'Decision aids present the risks, benefits and alternatives of treatment in a balanced format to support shared decision-making. They complement clinician guidance rather than replace it, and neither treat nor diagnose.'),
('ff0a4903-aec0-5e70-8383-91eac4c1ea45'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Cultural competence includes:', '["Awareness of one''s own cultural biases, knowledge of other cultures and cross-cultural skills","Only skills accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only awareness mediated by specific receptor activation","Language only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only knowledge leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Awareness of one''s own cultural biases, knowledge of other cultures and cross-cultural skills', 'Cultural competence combines self-awareness of personal bias, knowledge about cultural groups and the skills to deliver respectful cross-cultural care. Any single component on its own is insufficient.'),
('6962bfc8-c760-534b-9398-f50cb8a8fe5f'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Cultural humility differs from cultural competence by emphasizing:', '["Ongoing self-reflection and lifelong learning rather than mastery","Final achievement accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Permanent expertise mediated by specific receptor activation","Mastery of cultures without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Exhaustive knowledge leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ongoing self-reflection and lifelong learning rather than mastery', 'Cultural humility frames the clinician as a lifelong learner who continually reflects on personal bias and power. Cultural competence, by contrast, can imply reaching a finite endpoint of mastery.'),
('6ef8e9c8-9678-5094-85ba-1673597360d2'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Dealing with an angry patient involves:', '["Remaining calm, acknowledging the anger and exploring its causes","Getting defensive accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Arguing back mediated by specific receptor activation","Ignoring the patient without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Leaving the room leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Remaining calm, acknowledging the anger and exploring its causes', 'Staying calm, acknowledging the patient''s anger and exploring what triggered it de-escalates conflict and preserves rapport. Defensiveness, argument or withdrawal would escalate the situation.'),
('e017f9e6-7753-519d-99ea-78c3f8f5037b'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Communicating with a depressed patient requires:', '["Empathic listening, instilling hope and appropriate referral","Medication only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ignoring the low mood mediated by specific receptor activation","Telling the patient to cheer up without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A quick fix leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Empathic listening, instilling hope and appropriate referral', 'Depressed patients need empathic, non-judgemental listening and realistic hope, with referral for specialist care and consideration of medication. Dismissive advice to ''cheer up'' or avoidance invalidates the patient''s experience.'),
('2664d6aa-acab-55fc-9887-bafa1a34cc00'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Good telephone consultation skills include:', '["Clear structure, active listening and documentation","Rushing the caller accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Prescribing without assessment mediated by specific receptor activation","Only prescribing without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Avoiding documentation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Clear structure, active listening and documentation', 'Without visual cues, safe telephone consultations depend on a clear structure, active listening and contemporaneous documentation. Rushing, prescribing without assessment or skipping documentation increases risk.'),
('72313ccb-1030-53d6-9f8a-b81e8917fccc'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'The legal maxim ''res ipsa loquitur'' means:', '["The thing speaks for itself","Let the buyer beware","To err is human","The patient is right","Caveat emptor"]'::jsonb, 'The thing speaks for itself', 'Res ipsa loquitur means ''the thing speaks for itself'', a rule that lets negligence be inferred where the harm would not normally occur without carelessness and the defendant controlled the situation. Caveat emptor (''let the buyer beware'') is a separate, unrelated maxim.'),
('c8f904ca-4bee-5127-a3b8-1e192d20aa27'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'HIPAA (or the local equivalent) regulations require:', '["Protection of patient health information privacy","Sharing information freely","Keeping only paper records","Keeping only electronic records","Public disclosure of all records"]'::jsonb, 'Protection of patient health information privacy', 'Privacy laws such as HIPAA require safeguarding protected health information and restricting its use and disclosure to what is permitted. They do not mandate a particular record format or open sharing.'),
('3e5b0fa3-3f17-564a-a876-8d27036373fd'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Closed-loop communication in teams ensures:', '["Confirmation that the message was received and understood","Multiple messages accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Email only mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the sender speaks without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Messages sent without confirmation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Confirmation that the message was received and understood', 'Closed-loop communication confirms each message by having the receiver repeat it back and the sender verify it. This reduces miscommunication and is critical in emergency and team settings.'),
('7c833ea9-e130-56e5-bb70-8be20230fb48'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Competence is a determination, whereas capacity is a assessment.', '["Legal, medical","Medical, legal","Ethical, legal","Clinical, ethical","Moral, clinical"]'::jsonb, 'Legal, medical', 'Competence is a legal status decided by a court, whereas capacity is a medical or clinical assessment performed at the bedside for a specific decision. Legal competence is presumed unless removed by a court.'),
('44315c3f-4476-5d2c-81ac-0b5aa657a1a9'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Speaking up about safety concerns is:', '["Essential for patient safety","Punished accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Optional mediated by specific receptor activation","Discouraged without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Dangerous leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Essential for patient safety', 'Speaking up about errors, risks or concerns is a core patient-safety behaviour expected of every member of the team. It is supported rather than punished, and is a professional duty rather than an optional act.'),
('af0f9583-5fcc-5cb3-a71b-d3feb786f471'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'The PASS model for speaking up stands for:', '["Probe, Alert, State and Seek","Protect, Assess, Support and Secure","Present, Ask, State and Support","Push, Alert, See and Stop","Patient, Safe, Speak"]'::jsonb, 'Probe, Alert, State and Seek', 'PASS is an advocacy tool for speaking up: Probe the situation, Alert the person to the concern, State the concern directly and Seek their response. The other letter series are not part of this model.'),
('e343561c-88d5-5610-8990-1a8e83005ae8'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'The GRRRR listening model stands for:', '["Greet, Respect, Review, Respond and Reflect","Greet, Ask, Repeat and Reflect","Gather, Respect, Respond and Review","Great, Respond, Review and Repeat","Good, Respond, Review and React"]'::jsonb, 'Greet, Respect, Review, Respond and Reflect', 'GRRRR is a model for listening during conflict: Greet, Respect, Review, Respond and Reflect. It structures respectful, empathic responses. The other acronyms are not the standard sequence.'),
('ecc23240-d1df-59d2-99ac-c3cacff68772'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Conflict resolution in healthcare teams requires:', '["Open communication, respect and finding common ground","Aggression accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Avoidance mediated by specific receptor activation","Blame without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Escalation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Open communication, respect and finding common ground', 'Constructive conflict resolution depends on open communication, mutual respect and identifying shared goals. Aggression, avoidance, blame and escalation all damage team functioning and patient safety.'),
('2e628abf-9ac9-567d-b575-74a438e2a89c'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'The Two-Challenge Rule in advocacy means that:', '["If the concern is ignored, the team member challenges twice and then escalates","A concern should be raised once and then dropped","Only physicians are allowed to challenge","The original plan is accepted without questioning","One challenge is always enough"]'::jsonb, 'If the concern is ignored, the team member challenges twice and then escalates', 'The Two-Challenge Rule lets any team member state a safety concern clearly at least twice; if it is still ignored, the concern is escalated up the chain of command. This protects patients even in strict hierarchies.'),
('74b03f06-f471-5dbe-bbf1-19f83f741f1d'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'The WHO framework for interprofessional collaboration includes:', '["Shared goals, clear roles, mutual respect, effective communication and teamwork","Only communication accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only clear roles mediated by specific receptor activation","Only shared goals without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only mutual respect leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Shared goals, clear roles, mutual respect, effective communication and teamwork', 'Interprofessional collaboration requires combined elements: shared goals, defined roles, mutual respect, effective communication and teamwork. No single element on its own constitutes collaboration.'),
('ee06f539-e9e6-51f4-9009-63bed744d01f'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'The Bolitho modification of the Bolam test means that:', '["Medical opinion must be logical and reasonable","Any medical opinion suffices","Only the majority opinion counts","Only expert testimony matters","No medical opinion is needed"]'::jsonb, 'Medical opinion must be logical and reasonable', 'Bolitho added that a court may reject a body of medical opinion that is illogical or not defensible on the evidence. This makes the standard depend on rational justification rather than customary practice alone.'),
('55061646-a34d-5982-8536-91b82e7d8d63'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Relationship-centered care extends patient-centered care by also including:', '["The importance of relationships (patient, provider, team and community)","Only the community accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the physician mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the family without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only the patient leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The importance of relationships (patient, provider, team and community)', 'Relationship-centered care recognises that health and healing depend on relationships among the patient, provider, team and broader community. It broadens the focus of patient-centered care beyond the individual patient-clinician encounter.'),
('140b674b-c55d-5614-9df7-12825ab8538f'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Building rapport with patients involves:', '["Warm greeting, active listening and showing interest","Rushing the consultation","Multi-tasking during the consultation","Using notes only","Excessive formality"]'::jsonb, 'Warm greeting, active listening and showing interest', 'Rapport develops from warmth, attentive listening and genuine interest in the patient as a person. Rushing, multi-tasking, hiding behind notes and excessive formality all undermine connection.'),
('b89de355-0ae6-5f7c-9589-954bbe7540ce'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'A therapeutic alliance is defined as:', '["A collaborative relationship directed at therapeutic goals","Friendship accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A contract mediated by specific receptor activation","An authority relationship without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Passive care leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A collaborative relationship directed at therapeutic goals', 'The therapeutic alliance is the working partnership between clinician and patient built on agreement about goals, tasks and trust. It differs from friendship, a formal contract, authority or passive care.'),
('6c57f4b8-5195-5bab-84e5-02f206ad3b1d'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Fostering patient engagement involves:', '["Inviting questions and encouraging participation","Quick visits","Answering only closed questions","The physician doing all the talking","Keeping the patient passive"]'::jsonb, 'Inviting questions and encouraging participation', 'Engagement grows when clinicians invite questions and encourage patients to participate actively in decisions. Rushed visits, closed-ended questioning, one-way talk and passivity all reduce engagement.'),
('5ceaaf5e-87e4-5413-8621-fe1571ce7150'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Non-adherence to treatment may result from:', '["Poor communication, misunderstanding of side effects or lack of trust","Poverty accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Unavailability of the drug mediated by specific receptor activation","Ignorance without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Patient stubbornness leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Poor communication, misunderstanding of side effects or lack of trust', 'Adherence is often undermined by poor understanding of the regimen, fear of side effects and distrust of the clinician, all of which better communication can address. Labeling the patient as stubborn or ignorant is unhelpful.'),
('feb6c8fd-84ac-5a87-9d21-d05ebf728a55'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'In the Health Belief Model, cues to action are:', '["Triggers that prompt health behaviour, such as symptoms, media or advice","Reasons for illness accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Treatments mediated by specific receptor activation","Side effects without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cost leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Triggers that prompt health behaviour, such as symptoms, media or advice', 'In the Health Belief Model, cues to action are internal or external triggers such as symptoms, media campaigns or professional advice that motivate a person to act on their perceived threat and beliefs. They are distinct from perceived susceptibility, severity, benefits and barriers.'),
('a703457b-22f7-5164-8d5d-1201ccb8b15c'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Self-efficacy refers to:', '["Belief about disease severity resulting in decreased functional activity","Social support accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Confidence in one''s ability to perform a behavior","Knowledge without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Perception of risk leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Confidence in one''s ability to perform a behavior', 'Self-efficacy is the individual''s belief in their capacity to execute behaviors necessary to produce specific outcomes. It is a central construct in social cognitive theory and strongly influences how patients approach goals and tasks.'),
('3023efec-97ef-5609-9705-9553c6fade0b'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'The Theory of Planned Behavior includes:', '["Self-efficacy resulting in decreased functional activity","Only control accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Attitude, subjective norm, and perceived behavioral control","Only attitude without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only norms leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Attitude, subjective norm, and perceived behavioral control', 'The Theory of Planned Behavior holds that behavioral intention, the immediate antecedent of action, is determined by attitude toward the behavior, subjective norm, and perceived behavioral control.'),
('1bead6db-7e74-5adc-abac-b40cc1dddeaf'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'The Transtheoretical Model (Stages of Change) includes:', '["Acute, subacute, and chronic","Precontemplation, contemplation, preparation, action, and maintenance","Denial, anger, bargaining, depression, acceptance","Primary, secondary, and tertiary","Assessment, diagnosis, and treatment"]'::jsonb, 'Precontemplation, contemplation, preparation, action, and maintenance', 'The Transtheoretical Model describes behavior change as progressing through precontemplation, contemplation, preparation, action, and maintenance, with relapse possible between stages. The denial-to-acceptance sequence is the Kubler-Ross grief model.'),
('d10aefc9-54d4-5bca-a10a-389716a1f24e'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Competence is a determination, whereas capacity is :', '["Legal, medical","Medical, legal","Ethical, legal","Clinical, ethical","Moral, clinical"]'::jsonb, 'Legal, medical', 'Competence is a legal status determined by a court or statute, whereas capacity is a clinical assessment made by a clinician of a patient''s ability to make a specific decision.'),
('af732710-ad49-57cf-a63c-04cb404d1b85'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'The action stage involves:', '["Planning resulting in decreased functional activity","Not thinking about change accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Considering change mediated by specific receptor activation","Maintenance without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Making specific overt changes in behavior"]'::jsonb, 'Making specific overt changes in behavior', 'In the Transtheoretical Model the action stage is defined by making specific, overt modifications of behavior, typically within the past six months. The earlier stages involve intention rather than visible change.'),
('345f0feb-7547-507f-a8c8-b0cd3ca346d9'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'The maintenance stage involves:', '["Taking initial steps toward change","Actively modifying behavior","Planning","Working to sustain behavior change over time","Relapse"]'::jsonb, 'Working to sustain behavior change over time', 'Maintenance is the stage of working to sustain behavior change over time, generally after about six months of successful change. Initial behavior modification belongs to the action stage.'),
('01d45889-89e3-5cce-8926-b97ffd93933e'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Relapse in the stages of change is:', '["Rare resulting in decreased functional activity","Common and part of the change process (can recycle)","Punishment mediated by specific receptor activation","Failure without affecting the primary tissue pathway","End of process leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Common and part of the change process (can recycle)', 'Relapse is common and considered a normal part of the change process rather than failure. Patients typically recycle through the stages when attempting change again.'),
('1ecd9a57-d483-5946-877c-b91d8995aa96'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Theories of health behavior help physicians understand:', '["Why patients behave as they do and how to promote change","Diagnosis only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Treatment only mediated by specific receptor activation","Surgery only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Medication only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Why patients behave as they do and how to promote change', 'Behavioral theories explain the determinants of health-related behavior and provide frameworks for designing interventions to promote behavior change. They go beyond diagnosis or treatment alone.'),
('1e34e50b-8a42-5641-81d7-dedd41de5688'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Patient-reported outcome measures (PROMs) assess:', '["Lab results resulting in decreased functional activity","Chart review accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Health status, quality of life, and symptoms from the patient''s perspective","Physician''s assessment without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Imaging leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Health status, quality of life, and symptoms from the patient''s perspective', 'PROMs are standardized questionnaires completed by patients that capture health status, quality of life, and symptoms directly from the patient''s perspective. They do not rely on laboratory, imaging, or clinician-based assessment.'),
('fcfd88ac-eb3d-5052-92c2-ba9b2f80089f'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'The standard of care is determined by:', '["What a reasonably competent physician would do","Cost of treatment","Patient expectation","Physician''s personal standard","Best evidence anywhere"]'::jsonb, 'What a reasonably competent physician would do', 'Under the Bolam test the standard of care is what a reasonably competent physician in the same speciality and circumstances would do. It is not set by cost, personal standards, or the patient''s expectations.'),
('0d844174-75fe-5fc3-83a1-44c453ad185e'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Low-context cultures emphasize:', '["Indirectness resulting in decreased functional activity","Non-verbal cues accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Touch mediated by specific receptor activation","Explicit verbal communication and directness","Sharing leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Explicit verbal communication and directness', 'In low-context cultures meaning is conveyed primarily through explicit verbal communication and directness, with less reliance on non-verbal cues and shared context. High-context cultures rely more on indirect cues.'),
('b2895fac-ca72-5621-bec9-d5d022f632dd'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Collectivist cultures prioritize:', '["Self-interest resulting in decreased functional activity","Privacy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Independence mediated by specific receptor activation","Individual autonomy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Family and community decisions over the individual"]'::jsonb, 'Family and community decisions over the individual', 'In collectivist cultures group goals and family or community interests take precedence over individual preferences, so healthcare decisions commonly involve family members. Individualist cultures instead emphasize personal autonomy.'),
('4cf6a92e-2e35-56c5-b0e8-37b5b490e596'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Individualist cultures emphasize:', '["Community resulting in decreased functional activity","Family accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Personal autonomy and individual decision-making","Group harmony without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Obligation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Personal autonomy and individual decision-making', 'Individualist cultures value personal autonomy and independent decision-making, with the individual expected to make their own healthcare choices. Group harmony and obligation are characteristic of collectivist cultures.'),
('1371d86d-17e4-55d7-8fd1-63f5b201f6c6'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Spirituality and religion influence healthcare decisions about:', '["Life support, blood transfusion, and end-of-life care","Only exercise accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only medication mediated by specific receptor activation","Only diet without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only surgery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Life support, blood transfusion, and end-of-life care', 'Religious and spiritual beliefs frequently shape decisions about life support, blood transfusion, and end-of-life care, such as refusal of blood products by Jehovah''s Witnesses. Their influence spans multiple domains, not a single one.'),
('43c4616d-7186-5df5-b6d1-0057e882354e'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Advance care planning discussions should consider:', '["Only prognosis resulting in decreased functional activity","Only medication accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only age mediated by specific receptor activation","Patient''s values, cultural beliefs, and spiritual beliefs","Only diagnosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Patient''s values, cultural beliefs, and spiritual beliefs', 'Advance care planning must incorporate the patient''s personal values, cultural background, and spiritual beliefs along with their treatment preferences. Considering only biomedical facts would be incomplete.'),
('26b370aa-627b-53de-ae7f-74e84a95676c'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'The LEARN model for cross-cultural communication stands for:', '["Learn, Evaluate, Assess, Refer, Note","Look, Explain, Ask, Respond, Note","Listen, Empathize, Apologize, Resolve, Negotiate","Lead, Engage, Advise, Refer, Notify","Listen, Explain, Acknowledge, Recommend, Negotiate"]'::jsonb, 'Listen, Explain, Acknowledge, Recommend, Negotiate', 'The LEARN model guides cross-cultural care through Listen, Explain, Acknowledge, Recommend, and Negotiate. The correct sequence is an established mnemonic for addressing cultural differences in the consultation.'),
('6235e394-5c1e-58ff-acac-f2214bb89ca1'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'The ETHNIC framework for cultural assessment stands for:', '["Elicit, Treat, Handle, Negotiate, Integrate, Consult","Evaluate, Treat, Heal, Navigate, Inform, Counsel","Explanation, Treatment, Healers, Negotiate, Intervention, Collaboration","Explore, Treat, Help, Negotiate, Implement, Care","Educate, Train, Help, Notify, Inform, Counsel"]'::jsonb, 'Explanation, Treatment, Healers, Negotiate, Intervention, Collaboration', 'The ETHNIC framework explores the patient''s explanation of illness, treatment preferences, use of healers, negotiation, intervention, and collaboration. It structures culturally sensitive assessment in the clinical encounter.'),
('2fbb6402-c641-5ee3-aa69-a7d8073cb49d'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Use of interpreters should include:', '["Speaking to the interpreter resulting in decreased functional activity","Speaking to the patient rather than the interpreter and using short segments","Complex language mediated by specific receptor activation","Jokes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Using family members leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Speaking to the patient rather than the interpreter and using short segments', 'Effective interpreter-mediated care involves speaking directly to the patient, not the interpreter, and delivering information in short segments so nothing is lost in translation. Family members are not professional interpreters.'),
('6b344db7-de07-55ea-8883-6cfbeb66b1e1'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Telephone interpreting services provide:', '["Access to trained interpreters by phone for multiple languages","Only in emergency departments","Available only in hospitals","Limited to a small number of languages","Lower quality than in-person interpreting"]'::jsonb, 'Access to trained interpreters by phone for multiple languages', 'Telephone interpreting connects clinicians to trained professional interpreters by phone across many languages, enabling timely interpretation where in-person interpreters are unavailable.'),
('5bcb656d-2cae-5fbe-834b-21c65c7e016f'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Working with medical interpreters requires:', '["Briefing before the session and debriefing after","Using family members as interpreters","Speaking only to the interpreter","Using complex medical jargon","Omitting preparation time"]'::jsonb, 'Briefing before the session and debriefing after', 'Good practice includes briefing the interpreter before the consultation about its purpose and debriefing afterward to review what occurred and address concerns. This improves accuracy of interpretation.'),
('19374652-65c0-5ca4-a11a-448f2bf85d44'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Low health literacy is associated with:', '["Better outcomes resulting in decreased functional activity","Better adherence accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","More visits mediated by specific receptor activation","More medication use without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Poorer health outcomes, less preventive care, and higher hospitalization"]'::jsonb, 'Poorer health outcomes, less preventive care, and higher hospitalization', 'Low health literacy is linked to poorer health outcomes, reduced use of preventive services, and higher rates of hospitalization and medication errors. It does not improve outcomes or adherence.'),
('5e253284-3caa-5992-ac1e-c965968cdac8'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'The Newest Vital Sign is a tool to assess:', '["Depression","Pain","Health literacy","Cognition","Nutrition"]'::jsonb, 'Health literacy', 'The Newest Vital Sign is a brief screening instrument that assesses health literacy using questions about a nutrition facts label. It does not measure mood, pain, cognition, or diet.'),
('6dccc9fd-6893-5c1a-b753-3cf9f56c28f9'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'The teach-back method is effective for:', '["Teaching a procedure resulting in decreased functional activity","Testing memory accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Patient education mediated by specific receptor activation","Confirming patient understanding of health information","Exam preparation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Confirming patient understanding of health information', 'Teach-back asks patients to restate health information in their own words, confirming that they understood it. It verifies comprehension rather than memory or educational delivery alone.'),
('3c170a6e-b148-5008-ac96-24b09e87c8bd'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Universal precautions for health literacy assume:', '["Only non-English speakers resulting in decreased functional activity","Only illiterate patients accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only elderly patients mediated by specific receptor activation","Only some patients without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All patients may have difficulty understanding health information"]'::jsonb, 'All patients may have difficulty understanding health information', 'Universal precautions for health literacy assume that all patients, regardless of education or language, may have difficulty understanding health information. Clinicians therefore simplify communication for everyone rather than singling out groups.'),
('d611de9e-2373-52d3-a7d8-9c381efc6eb2'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Awkward questions from patients should be met with:', '["Dismissal resulting in decreased functional activity","Lying accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Avoidance mediated by specific receptor activation","Honest, direct answers in a respectful tone","Defensiveness leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Honest, direct answers in a respectful tone', 'Awkward or challenging questions should be answered honestly and directly while maintaining a respectful, non-defensive tone. Dismissal, avoidance, and lying damage trust.'),
('4a459b6e-67f6-513d-8976-3ca36ad1b93a'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Continuity of care improves:', '["Only compliance resulting in decreased functional activity","Patient satisfaction, trust, and communication","Only appointments mediated by specific receptor activation","Only access without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only cost leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Patient satisfaction, trust, and communication', 'Continuity of care improves patient satisfaction, trust, and communication and reduces unnecessary admissions and tests. Its benefits span multiple domains rather than a single one.'),
('44713eaf-393b-54ac-a0cf-39572712283a'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Patients with somatization benefit from:', '["Specialist referrals resulting in decreased functional activity","Regular scheduled visits and avoiding unnecessary tests","Surgery mediated by specific receptor activation","More tests without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Medication leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Regular scheduled visits and avoiding unnecessary tests', 'Somatizing patients benefit from regular scheduled visits and avoidance of unnecessary investigations, which reduces reinforcement of symptoms. Repeated testing and surgery can perpetuate the illness behavior.'),
('6f5e5a7e-3824-5be4-a28b-ad62ee0cfe3e'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'The FIFE model stands for:', '["Feelings, Identify, Focus, Explain","Feelings, Information, Function, Evaluation","Feelings, Ideas, Function, Expectations","Function, Illness, Facts, Evaluation","Facts, Ideas, Feelings, Expectations"]'::jsonb, 'Feelings, Ideas, Function, Expectations', 'The FIFE model explores the patient''s illness experience through Feelings, Ideas, Function, and Expectations. It is a framework for eliciting the patient''s perspective in patient-centred care.'),
('1a12466c-557b-5ee5-a277-02d6b8cf61c8'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Open disclosure of adverse events involves:', '["Defensiveness resulting in decreased functional activity","Blame and silence accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Minimization mediated by specific receptor activation","Full, honest explanation, apology, and support","Secrecy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Full, honest explanation, apology, and support', 'Open disclosure requires a full, honest explanation of the adverse event, a sincere apology, and ongoing support for the patient and family. Concealment, blame, and defensiveness are contrary to this duty.'),
('83ce7e80-250a-5124-8981-97542a86d908'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'The double effect principle refers to:', '["An action with both a good effect (pain relief) and a foreseeable bad effect (respiratory depression)","Two ethical principles in conflict accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Two treatments used together mediated by specific receptor activation","Two physicians jointly managing care without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Two consent forms signed leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An action with both a good effect (pain relief) and a foreseeable bad effect (respiratory depression)', 'The double effect principle permits an action with an intended good effect even when it has a foreseeable but unintended bad effect, provided the bad effect is not intended and is proportioned to the good, as with opioid analgesia at the end of life.'),
('8d574a30-1905-548a-8450-893d12f65313'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Expressing regret after an adverse event uses:', '["Legal language resulting in decreased functional activity","Excuses accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Defensiveness mediated by specific receptor activation","Empathic apology without blaming individuals","Blame leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Empathic apology without blaming individuals', 'Expressing regret involves an empathic apology that acknowledges the harm without assigning blame to individuals or admitting legal liability. Excuses, defensiveness, and blame undermine the disclosure.'),
('4b09924c-e7e9-5ce7-aa8f-8a14daa54329'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'The standard of care in a legal claim is determined by:', '["What a reasonably competent physician would do","Cost of treatment","Patient expectation","Physician''s personal standard","Best evidence anywhere"]'::jsonb, 'What a reasonably competent physician would do', 'The standard of care is what a reasonably competent physician in the same field would do, traditionally assessed under the Bolam test. Cost, patient expectation, or an individual physician''s personal standard do not set the expected level of care.'),
('f8078a1b-a5ad-54d5-b275-72b62ea76fba'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Negotiating the agenda in a consultation involves:', '["Agreeing on which concerns will be addressed in the consultation","Patient sets all agenda accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Nurse sets agenda mediated by specific receptor activation","Physician sets all agenda without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Clerk sets agenda leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Agreeing on which concerns will be addressed in the consultation', 'Negotiating the agenda is a shared process in which clinician and patient agree on the concerns to be covered in the consultation. It is not set unilaterally by any single party.'),
('dea434ba-51af-5184-8426-7b22641ff290'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'A complete medical consultation involves:', '["Only diagnosis resulting in decreased functional activity","Only history accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only treatment mediated by specific receptor activation","Building relationship gathering information managing problems","Only rapport leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Building relationship gathering information managing problems', 'A consultation combines building the relationship with gathering information and managing problems. Focusing on a single element such as diagnosis, history, treatment, or rapport omits the other core tasks.'),
('3d59d4eb-ccd4-5d8d-831b-2d64d292d992'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Tarasoff case established:', '["Duty to warn third party at serious risk","Patient confidentiality","Right to refuse treatment","Informed consent","Euthanasia principles"]'::jsonb, 'Duty to warn third party at serious risk', 'Tarasoff v Regents of California.'),
('f859cb75-2e85-5f85-a235-7a73f36ba3a1'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'The hidden curriculum in medical education refers to:', '["Books resulting in decreased functional activity","Exams accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Formal curriculum mediated by specific receptor activation","Informal learning about attitudes values and behaviors","Lectures leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Informal learning about attitudes values and behaviors', 'The hidden curriculum is the informal learning of attitudes, values, and behaviours transmitted through the clinical environment rather than the formal syllabus. Books, exams, lectures, and the formal curriculum are explicit forms of teaching.'),
('92f3b331-ee6e-5409-a944-c6b6b30d56a4'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Professionalism includes:', '["Only attendance resulting in decreased functional activity","Only grades accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Altruism accountability excellence duty honor integrity respect","Only skills without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only knowledge leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Altruism accountability excellence duty honor integrity respect', 'Professionalism encompasses altruism, accountability, excellence, duty, honour, integrity, and respect. It extends beyond attendance, grades, skills, or knowledge considered in isolation.'),
('2a0ee2c6-daae-5517-bb3b-db9aebf1b252'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'The APPEAL mnemonic for professional conduct stands for:', '["Professional Prepare Engage Act Listen","Attend Prepare Present Engage Act Lead","Appearance Punctuality Preparation Engagement Accountability Language","Ask Prepare Present Evaluate Assess Learn","Appearance Professionalism Prepare Engage Act Lead"]'::jsonb, 'Appearance Punctuality Preparation Engagement Accountability Language', 'APPEAL stands for Appearance, Punctuality, Preparation, Engagement, Accountability, and Language. It is a mnemonic used to remind trainees of key points of professional conduct.'),
('31bab1ee-771a-5af8-89fb-85890cdc16fe'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Reflective practice involves:', '["Analyzing experiences to improve learning and performance","Passive learning accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Memorization mediated by specific receptor activation","Repetition without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Rote learning leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Analyzing experiences to improve learning and performance', 'Reflective practice is an active process of analyzing experiences to improve learning and performance. Passive learning, memorization, repetition, and rote learning do not involve this deliberate review.'),
('2461d9c0-2dc9-5d8c-94f1-1f3ec02a374e'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'The Gibbs reflective cycle includes:', '["Do review plan resulting in decreased functional activity","Description feelings evaluation analysis conclusion action plan","Study practice test mediated by specific receptor activation","Observe think act without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Prepare present evaluate leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Description feelings evaluation analysis conclusion action plan', 'The Gibbs reflective cycle comprises description, feelings, evaluation, analysis, conclusion, and an action plan. It provides a structured framework for reflecting on an experience.'),
('83af6ecd-6efe-538a-9086-a096b104473c'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Feedback in medical education should be:', '["General vague delayed resulting in decreased functional activity","Critical accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Punitive mediated by specific receptor activation","Specific timely focused on behavior and constructive","Judgmental leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Specific timely focused on behavior and constructive', 'Effective feedback is specific, timely, focused on behaviour rather than the person, and constructive. General, vague, delayed, punitive, or judgmental feedback is less useful for learning.'),
('f2079d37-3a50-5c88-9f4e-f1d0f34f88fa'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Pendleton''s rules for giving feedback include:', '["Ask learner what went well first then what could be improved","Observer comments first accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only negative mediated by specific receptor activation","Only positive without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Criticize first leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ask learner what went well first then what could be improved', 'Under Pendleton''s rules the learner first identifies what went well and then what could be improved, before the observer adds comments. The learner leads the review rather than receiving criticism first.'),
('ac28f1a3-f50f-535d-8393-38d7be41b117'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'The feedback sandwich involves:', '["Criticism resulting in decreased functional activity","Only negative accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Praise only mediated by specific receptor activation","Only positive without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Positive-constructive-positive structure"]'::jsonb, 'Positive-constructive-positive structure', 'The feedback sandwich structures feedback as positive, constructive, and positive. Criticism alone, negativity, or praise without constructive content does not provide this balanced structure.'),
('e565afa1-f620-53d2-9791-9523e957ac79'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Educational supervision aims to:', '["Administration resulting in decreased functional activity","Evaluation only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Punishment mediated by specific receptor activation","Support trainee development and ensure patient safety","Assessment only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Support trainee development and ensure patient safety', 'Educational supervision supports trainee development while safeguarding patient care. It is not primarily administrative, punitive, or limited to evaluation and assessment.'),
('0170b441-b1c0-54f9-ac2b-22af9af0bb4a'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'The one-minute preceptor model includes:', '["Get commitment probe evidence teach general rules reinforce what was right correct mistakes","One exam accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","One answer mediated by specific receptor activation","One minute lecture without affecting the primary tissue pathway","One question leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Get commitment probe evidence teach general rules reinforce what was right correct mistakes', 'The one-minute preceptor uses five microskills: get a commitment, probe for supporting evidence, teach general rules, reinforce what was done right, and correct mistakes. It is a structured teaching framework for clinical encounters.'),
('4ad19cc4-2fc6-53a9-9e7b-ce320d2f78dd'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'The SNAPPS model for learner presentations stands for:', '["Situation Narrow Assess Plan Present Summarize","State Narrow Analyze Probe Practice Summarize","State Note Apply Plan Present Select","Summarize Narrow Analyze Probe Plan Select","Summarize Note Assess Plan Present Select"]'::jsonb, 'Summarize Narrow Analyze Probe Plan Select', 'SNAPPS stands for Summarize, Narrow the differential, Analyze the differential, Probe the preceptor, Plan management, and Select a case for discussion. It helps learners expose their clinical reasoning during presentations.'),
('a11ddb79-429c-5117-b4c4-40f6bfbe2cbc'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Providing hope while being honest is described as:', '["Avoidance","Withholding truth","Despair","False hope","Balanced realistic optimism"]'::jsonb, 'Balanced realistic optimism', 'Balanced realistic optimism allows the clinician to give honest information while sustaining hope. Avoidance, withholding truth, despair, and false hope each fail to reconcile honesty with encouragement.'),
('13f17ab9-1698-568b-9e50-0adfcb298575'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Communicating prognosis requires:', '["Only negative resulting in decreased functional activity","Only statistics accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ignoring prognosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Sensitivity honesty and allowing for hope","Only positive leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sensitivity honesty and allowing for hope', 'Communicating prognosis requires sensitivity, honesty, and leaving room for hope. Conveying only statistics, only negative or positive messages, or avoiding the topic undermines the discussion.'),
('47c8bcb8-d18f-516a-aef4-b92b75ca1f50'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Discussing code status (DNR) requires:', '["Only physician decision resulting in decreased functional activity","Rushing accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only patient mediated by specific receptor activation","Only family without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Understanding patient values and explaining options clearly"]'::jsonb, 'Understanding patient values and explaining options clearly', 'A code status discussion requires understanding the patient''s values and explaining the options clearly. It is a shared decision and should not be rushed or made unilaterally by any single party.'),
('f975a5cd-7dfe-5d30-bb79-9511d45b8aa7'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Goals of care conversations explore:', '["Only costs resulting in decreased functional activity","What matters most to the patient given their health situation","Only treatment mediated by specific receptor activation","Only prognosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only medications leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'What matters most to the patient given their health situation', 'Goals-of-care conversations explore what matters most to the patient given their health situation. They are not limited to costs, treatment options, prognosis, or medications.'),
('1740afb8-b5c7-5b3c-8d5e-5905a5332ba5'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Discussing transition to hospice requires:', '["Abrupt discussion resulting in decreased functional activity","Sensitive honest conversation about prognosis and options","Postponing mediated by specific receptor activation","Withholding without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only family leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sensitive honest conversation about prognosis and options', 'Transition to hospice should be discussed in a sensitive, honest conversation covering prognosis and available options. Abrupt, postponed, or withheld discussions fail the patient and family.'),
('3d112b3a-40cc-5ad4-9b1a-cfa724dcefad'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Exceptions to medical confidentiality include:', '["Notifiable diseases, serious harm to third parties, child protection","Only notifiable diseases accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only court orders mediated by specific receptor activation","Only patient consent without affecting the primary tissue pathway","No exceptions leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Notifiable diseases, serious harm to third parties, child protection', 'Confidentiality is not absolute; it may be breached for notifiable diseases, serious harm to third parties, and child protection. These public-interest duties override the duty of confidentiality in specific circumstances.'),
('5bf691df-b384-551e-a86c-002125656be3'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Default options in advance care planning can:', '["Have effect resulting in decreased functional activity","Always beneficial accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Influence choices because people tend to stick with defaults","Confuse patients without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Always harmful leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Influence choices because people tend to stick with defaults', 'Defaults influence choices because people tend to stick with the pre-set option (status quo bias). This behavioural effect is relevant when designing advance care planning documents.'),
('2d37e87c-dc4e-58ff-a381-0b9a721f45bd'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Framing effects in risk communication refer to:', '["How information is presented (positive vs negative framing) affects perception","Only negative framing works accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Facts are objective mediated by specific receptor activation","Only positive framing works without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The framing effect does not influence perception leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'How information is presented (positive vs negative framing) affects perception', 'Framing effects describe how the same information, presented positively or negatively, changes how it is perceived. The way risk is presented therefore matters even when the underlying facts are identical.'),
('07724a59-1b03-578e-8253-21ea1f62ae75'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Using absolute risk rather than relative risk helps patients:', '["Exaggerate the difference resulting in decreased functional activity","Minimize benefit accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Understand the true magnitude of benefit without exaggeration","Confuse patients without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Exaggerate benefit leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Understand the true magnitude of benefit without exaggeration', 'Absolute risk reduction conveys the true magnitude of a treatment''s benefit, whereas relative risk reduction tends to look larger and can exaggerate benefit. Patients can therefore gauge the actual difference a treatment makes.'),
('c2b0f0eb-0ec1-56f2-abfd-d634552615ba'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Number needed to treat (NNT) is useful for communicating:', '["Mechanism resulting in decreased functional activity","Statistical significance accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Clinical benefit in practical terms","Cost without affecting the primary tissue pathway","P-value leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Clinical benefit in practical terms', 'The number needed to treat expresses how many patients must receive treatment for one to benefit from it. It conveys clinical benefit in practical terms that patients can understand.'),
('52164e9d-3763-5ef9-a82e-f98dfa40bca8'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Physician-assisted suicide is defined by:', '["Physician provides means, patient self-administers","Physician directly administers lethal drug","Withholding treatment","Withdrawing life support","Refusing treatment"]'::jsonb, 'Physician provides means, patient self-administers', 'In physician-assisted suicide the physician provides the means but the patient performs the final self-administering act. Direct administration of the lethal drug by the physician constitutes active euthanasia instead.'),
('d60e1918-6afd-57d5-a38c-4c415e7a54b7'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Patient information leaflets should be written at:', '["5th-6th grade reading level","12th grade level","Medical level","Expert level","College level"]'::jsonb, '5th-6th grade reading level', 'Patient information leaflets should be written at a 5th-6th grade reading level so that most readers can understand them. Higher grade-level text reduces comprehension.'),
('fa1fd21f-a048-56ef-a269-751cab855e77'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Readability formulas such as Flesch-Kincaid measure:', '["Cultural appropriateness","Reading grade level of text","Patient satisfaction","Scientific validity","Medical accuracy"]'::jsonb, 'Reading grade level of text', 'Readability formulas such as Flesch-Kincaid estimate the reading grade level of a text from word and sentence length. They do not assess cultural appropriateness, satisfaction, validity, or medical accuracy.'),
('fd14a261-1efe-56ca-9ae2-3b8bf1ae0eda'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Methods to check patient understanding include:', '["Handouts","Teach-back and chunk and check","Repetition","Lecturing","Only asking do you understand?"]'::jsonb, 'Teach-back and chunk and check', 'Teach-back and chunk-and-check actively verify understanding by asking the patient to restate information. Handouts and simple yes/no questions such as ''Do you understand?'' do not reliably confirm comprehension.'),
('eca1c7b2-b521-5d96-9bd4-87b7c145d4ab'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'The chunk and check method means:', '["Checking understanding only at the end of the discussion resulting in decreased functional activity","Rapid delivery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Give all information at once mediated by specific receptor activation","One lecture without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Give small information then check understanding before continuing"]'::jsonb, 'Give small information then check understanding before continuing', 'Chunk and check means giving a small amount of information and then checking understanding before moving on. Delivering everything at once or delaying the check until the end defeats its purpose.'),
('4cdab723-adcf-5e7f-8af3-e7ec358c75cd'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'The BATHE technique questions stand for:', '["Behavior Assessment Treatment Health Education","Basic Assessment Therapeutic Help End","Background Assessment Treatment Health Evaluation","Background Affect Trouble Handling Empathy","Basic Affect Treatment Handling Empathy"]'::jsonb, 'Background Affect Trouble Handling Empathy', 'BATHE stands for Background, Affect, Trouble, Handling, and Empathy. It is a structured psychosocial assessment technique used during consultations.'),
('f2d9d218-4886-5c2d-9a03-a0c2652c96bf'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Which communication skill involves understanding the patient''s perspective?', '["Background","Handling","Trouble","Empathy","Affect"]'::jsonb, 'Empathy', 'Empathy involves understanding and acknowledging the patient''s perspective and feelings. Background, handling, trouble, and affect are components of the BATHE technique rather than the skill of perceiving the patient''s viewpoint.'),
('4bdfcfa9-0eb8-5a2d-88f0-746755360a3b'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Responding to patient emotions requires:', '["Naming the emotion and exploring respectfully","Dismissing the emotion","Minimizing the emotion","Ignoring the emotion","Rushing the consultation"]'::jsonb, 'Naming the emotion and exploring respectfully', 'Empathic responses name the feelings the patient is expressing and explore them non-judgmentally instead of dismissing, minimizing or ignoring them.'),
('40097373-8858-5242-8150-d727673adbcb'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'The NURSE mnemonic for responding to emotion stands for:', '["Naming Understanding Respecting Supporting Exploring","Nurturing Understanding Respect Supporting Empathy","Understanding Really Supporting Empathy","Naming Using Respect Supporting Empathy","Not Using Rude Speaking Expressions"]'::jsonb, 'Naming Understanding Respecting Supporting Exploring', 'NURSE is a structured framework for empathic communication: Naming the emotion, Understanding, Respecting, Supporting and Exploring.'),
('c8325f64-bd57-5e7f-b316-f631387e356d'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Validating patient emotions means:', '["Acknowledging the legitimacy of their feelings","Agreeing with their viewpoint","Ignoring their feelings","Disagreeing with their viewpoint","Dismissing their feelings"]'::jsonb, 'Acknowledging the legitimacy of their feelings', 'Validation acknowledges that the patient''s feelings are reasonable and understandable; it does not require the clinician to agree with the patient''s beliefs.'),
('6583e28f-15ac-5014-8a50-1aa7a4edfd1a'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'The patient-centered interview includes:', '["Both the disease (biomedical) and illness (patient experience) perspectives","Only family accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only physician mediated by specific receptor activation","Only patient experience without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only biomedical leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Both the disease (biomedical) and illness (patient experience) perspectives', 'Patient-centered interviewing integrates the biomedical view of disease with the patient''s illness experience, including their ideas, concerns and expectations.'),
('a60f9c0c-b113-5f1c-88d3-fab7dd9cce48'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'The Calgary-Cambridge guide for the physical examination includes:', '["Explaining what you will do and obtaining consent","Performing the examination without any explanation","Obtaining consent without explaining the procedure","Thanking the patient only","Rushing through the examination"]'::jsonb, 'Explaining what you will do and obtaining consent', 'Before the physical examination the clinician should explain the steps, obtain consent and attend to the patient''s comfort and dignity throughout.'),
('1729754e-5a38-5416-84fa-519c9608e334'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Planning and shared decision making in the Calgary-Cambridge guide includes:', '["Involving the patient in decisions and checking understanding","Prescribing medication only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Recommending treatment only mediated by specific receptor activation","Following only the physician''s plan without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Making a referral only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Involving the patient in decisions and checking understanding', 'Shared decision making presents options, discusses risks and benefits, and involves the patient in the choice while checking their comprehension of the plan.'),
('4f03ed34-a270-576c-bc8d-09f4950bdd99'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Closing the session in the Calgary-Cambridge guide includes:', '["Forward planning, safety netting and final check","Leaving the room abruptly accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Prescribing medication mediated by specific receptor activation","Saying goodbye without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Writing the notes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Forward planning, safety netting and final check', 'Closure involves summarizing, agreeing a follow-up plan, providing safety-netting advice about when to seek help, and a final check of patient understanding.'),
('0fa8335c-f522-5d17-a322-f57132a9af5f'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Follow-up planning should include:', '["When, where and under what circumstances to seek care","A single follow-up date","Advice to return only if symptoms worsen","Decisions made only by the physician","Vague advice"]'::jsonb, 'When, where and under what circumstances to seek care', 'Effective follow-up specifies clearly when and where to return and the circumstances that should prompt the patient to seek care promptly.'),
('302e7270-fc7b-552f-90fd-456a4933e600'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'The patient''s perspective should be elicited throughout the consultation:', '["At all stages","Only in history","Only at start","Only at end","Only if patient asks"]'::jsonb, 'At all stages', 'The patient''s ideas, concerns and expectations should be explored continuously across the entire consultation, not confined to one phase.'),
('8eb2faef-af3d-5fce-ae7a-0652863fd8ac'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Patients with limited English proficiency should have:', '["Professional interpreter services","Gestures","Family interpreter","Written translation","An ad hoc untrained interpreter"]'::jsonb, 'Professional interpreter services', 'Professional interpreters are preferred because family members and ad hoc interpreters risk misinterpreting, omitting information and breaching confidentiality.'),
('5e34c503-bd8c-5fb0-a71f-6ad1ba72b409'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Talking to patients with hearing impairment:', '["Face the patient directly, ensure good lighting and minimize background noise","Speak from behind accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Whisper mediated by specific receptor activation","Turn away without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Shout leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Face the patient directly, ensure good lighting and minimize background noise', 'Facing the patient, using good lighting and reducing background noise facilitate lip-reading and make best use of any residual hearing.'),
('2820a583-e1e8-57a2-a98e-562135ae4de2'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Talking to patients with visual impairment:', '["Identify yourself, describe the surroundings and use specific language","Speak loudly accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ignore the patient mediated by specific receptor activation","Do not identify yourself without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Use vague language leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Identify yourself, describe the surroundings and use specific language', 'Announcing who you are, describing the environment and using concrete language orient the visually impaired patient and reduce anxiety.'),
('419563ec-a76e-5fc0-9528-f065ca43972d'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Talking to children requires:', '["Age-appropriate language involving child and parent","Using toys only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ignoring the child mediated by specific receptor activation","Talking only to the parent without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Using complex language leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Age-appropriate language involving child and parent', 'Communication with children uses developmentally appropriate language and engages both the child and parent to build trust and gather information.'),
('1496e899-d2be-508b-833d-947de3f70192'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Talking to adolescents requires:', '["Confidentiality reassurance and a respectful, non-judgmental approach","An authoritarian approach accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Keeping a parent present throughout mediated by specific receptor activation","A judgmental approach without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A dismissive approach leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Confidentiality reassurance and a respectful, non-judgmental approach', 'Adolescents respond to clear confidentiality limits set early, together with a respectful, non-judgmental style that encourages disclosure of sensitive issues.'),
('3f544826-ffae-5f29-8d21-ae993618d0f6'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'The HEADSS assessment for adolescents covers:', '["Home Education Activities Drugs Sexuality Safety","History Exam Assessment Diagnosis Surgery","Symptoms Signs Treatment Follow-up","Housing Education Activities Diet Smoking Sleep","Health Education Assessment Development Social Status"]'::jsonb, 'Home Education Activities Drugs Sexuality Safety', 'HEADSS is a psychosocial screening tool for adolescents covering Home, Education/employment, Activities, Drugs, Sexuality and suicide/Safety.'),
('049bb39d-cb53-5d8f-ac8d-20f6d840efb4'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'The HEEADSSS assessment adds:', '["Strengths and Spirituality","Activity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Social mediated by specific receptor activation","Education without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Health leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Strengths and Spirituality', 'HEEADSSS expands on HEADSS by explicitly screening eating habits, sleep, strengths and spirituality as additional protective and risk domains for young people.'),
('ebabec3d-56f1-51a5-b4df-9a240283e573'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Communicating with patients from different socioeconomic backgrounds requires:', '["Awareness of literacy and resources without assumptions","Ignoring the patient accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Making assumptions about education mediated by specific receptor activation","Stereotyping the patient without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Being judgmental leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Awareness of literacy and resources without assumptions', 'Care avoids stereotyping and instead recognizes the patient''s literacy level and social resources, adapting explanations accordingly.'),
('5dd2711a-0f0b-5d7e-9643-5016c4069b85'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Patients'' decision-making preferences should be:', '["Elicited and respected (may vary from active to passive)","Ignored","Assumed to prefer active participation","Assumed to prefer passive participation","Assumed to be the same"]'::jsonb, 'Elicited and respected (may vary from active to passive)', 'Patients vary from wanting full autonomy to preferring a passive role, so their decision-making preference should be elicited and respected rather than assumed.'),
('79b2696a-d155-57e0-87ee-a9043ba48b89'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'The control preferences scale identifies:', '["How much control patients want in decision-making","Education accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Health literacy mediated by specific receptor activation","Income without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Knowledge leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'How much control patients want in decision-making', 'The control preferences scale measures the degree of control a patient wishes to exercise in medical decision-making, rather than knowledge or literacy.'),
('0d14ecd5-a2e8-518c-a80b-0995d298ffca'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'The double effect principle describes:', '["An action with both a good effect (pain relief) and a foreseeable bad effect (respiratory depression)","Two ethical principles conflicting accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Two treatments mediated by specific receptor activation","Two physicians without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Two consent forms leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An action with both a good effect (pain relief) and a foreseeable bad effect (respiratory depression)', 'Double effect permits an action with a good intended effect even if it carries a foreseeable but unintended harmful effect, provided the harm is not intended.'),
('f25240c8-62d0-5834-907b-0e6d71061d49'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Communicating with families in the intensive care unit requires:', '["Regular updates, empathy and support for decision-making","Only clinical updates accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Limiting family involvement mediated by specific receptor activation","One-time communication without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Family involvement in decisions leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Regular updates, empathy and support for decision-making', 'Families of ICU patients need frequent honest updates delivered with empathy, plus practical and emotional support while they participate in decisions.'),
('4798eb4b-6ffc-5a0d-b40f-ec5ed7cf9e2a'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Family meetings in the ICU should have:', '["Clear structure, all relevant team members and support for the family","Attendance by the nurse only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Attendance by the physician only mediated by specific receptor activation","No one attending without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Attendance by the family only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Clear structure, all relevant team members and support for the family', 'Effective ICU family conferences are structured, include the relevant members of the care team and provide the family with information, questions and emotional support.'),
('a08b6720-07b9-55c1-a8bb-c3c9626d2233'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Helping relationships as a process of change refer to:', '["Self-reevaluation","Using social support for change","Information","Environment control","Commitment"]'::jsonb, 'Using social support for change', 'Helping relationships involve drawing on the support of caring people, such as family, friends, or counselors, to initiate and sustain behavior change.'),
('5958a518-a6dd-5744-b563-fa817144fd3d'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Shared decision making with families in the ICU involves:', '["Regular meetings, discussion of goals and conflict resolution","The physician decides alone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The family decides alone mediated by specific receptor activation","Only during emergencies without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A single one-time discussion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Regular meetings, discussion of goals and conflict resolution', 'Shared decision making in ICU uses regular meetings to align goals, share information, and resolve conflict among clinicians and family.'),
('9cc58200-2008-5b91-b52a-5c6844977d6d'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Standard of care is determined by:', '["What a reasonably competent physician would do","Cost of treatment","Patient expectation","Physician''s personal standard","Best evidence anywhere"]'::jsonb, 'What a reasonably competent physician would do', 'The standard of care is set by the conduct of a reasonably competent practitioner in the same specialty, as established by the Bolam test.'),
('9b8ad502-bded-5a2e-a0e6-1a41705c7eac'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Organ donation request communication should be:', '["Separate from death notification and by trained staff","Done by any staff member","Done without training","Combined with death notification","Done immediately after death"]'::jsonb, 'Separate from death notification and by trained staff', 'Best practice separates the death notification from the donation request and uses trained staff, which improves family decision quality and consent rates.'),
('bd2b994a-fb4c-5b64-8181-73e1f1267ff2'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'The Ask-Tell-Ask model proceeds as:', '["Ask patient''s understanding, Tell information, Ask for patient''s interpretation","Ask Ignore Ask accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hide Tell Hide mediated by specific receptor activation","Tell Ignore Ask without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tell Ask Tell leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ask patient''s understanding, Tell information, Ask for patient''s interpretation', 'Ask-Tell-Ask first explores what the patient already knows, then delivers information, and finally checks how the patient understands it.'),
('a5ada79a-2b92-5751-beb6-c9ab1d5f329b'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'The REMAP framework for goals of care stands for:', '["Reframe Expect emotions Map Align Plan","Reflect Expect Manage Align Proceed","Review Evaluate Map Assess Plan","Respond Evaluate Manage Align Plan","Reframe Educate Manage Align Proceed"]'::jsonb, 'Reframe Expect emotions Map Align Plan', 'REMAP structures goals-of-care conversations as Reframe, Expect emotion, Map values, Align, and Plan.'),
('05697a4d-9917-5aae-9268-60e23526aae7'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Reframing in goals of care conversations means:', '["Shifting from what treatments to what goals and what matters","Starting a new treatment accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Repeating the same frame mediated by specific receptor activation","Making a referral without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Giving a new diagnosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Shifting from what treatments to what goals and what matters', 'Reframing moves the conversation from specific interventions toward the patient''s broader goals and what matters most to them.'),
('6a1d4953-26f5-582a-9ce3-28af682a46de'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Mapping patient values means:', '["Exploring what the patient values most in their life","Geography accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Body mapping mediated by specific receptor activation","Genetics without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Family tree leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Exploring what the patient values most in their life', 'Mapping values elicits what the patient cares about most, which then guides what treatment goals should be aligned to.'),
('97ae3c72-2b12-5268-ad3e-206f9abb707f'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Aligning with patient values means:', '["Showing how the plan respects their expressed values","Ignoring them accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Disagreeing mediated by specific receptor activation","Repeating without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Forcing leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Showing how the plan respects their expressed values', 'Aligning connects the proposed plan explicitly to the values the patient has expressed, so recommendations fit what matters to them.'),
('a5fc0768-998f-53a8-b298-c56f3b59bc5c'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'A 60-year-old female with advanced heart failure is transitioned to palliative care. Which intervention most enhances meaning and dignity at the end of life?', '["Structured intervention to enhance meaning and dignity at the end of life","Only physical care accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only medication mediated by specific receptor activation","Psychological support without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only pain relief leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Structured intervention to enhance meaning and dignity at the end of life', 'Dignity therapy is a brief structured psychotherapy that reduces existential distress and enhances meaning, purpose and dignity at the end of life.'),
('e8fc44b9-8160-5850-b957-14fd3ddeb9ff'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'The dual process model of grief (Stroebe & Schut) describes:', '["Oscillation between loss-oriented and restoration-oriented coping","A linear process accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Focus on loss only mediated by specific receptor activation","A single stage without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A fixed sequence leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Oscillation between loss-oriented and restoration-oriented coping', 'The dual process model holds that grieving alternates between confronting the loss (loss orientation) and attending to ongoing life demands (restoration orientation).'),
('a925354e-9b8d-5d98-a622-5c757f6e0788'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'The continuing bonds model of grief suggests that:', '["Maintaining connection with the deceased can be healthy","It is always pathological","Letting go is necessary","A brief connection is expected","Forgetting the deceased"]'::jsonb, 'Maintaining connection with the deceased can be healthy', 'The continuing bonds model proposes that ongoing inner connection with the deceased is a normal and often adaptive part of grieving.'),
('15eb6b7a-cdbf-515b-ad73-bc6348ba1ed0'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Complicated grief treatment involves:', '["Targeted psychotherapy for prolonged intense grief","Standard counseling only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Medication alone mediated by specific receptor activation","Bereavement support groups only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Support only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Targeted psychotherapy for prolonged intense grief', 'Complicated grief is treated with a specifically designed psychotherapy targeting prolonged, intense grief (complicated grief therapy) rather than generic support.'),
('a826761e-bba3-55ad-9055-aae61d20888b'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Disenfranchised grief refers to:', '["Grief not socially recognized or validated","Complicated grief accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Childhood grief mediated by specific receptor activation","Normal grief without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Grief of any intensity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Grief not socially recognized or validated', 'Disenfranchised grief is loss that is not openly acknowledged or socially supported, such as after stigmatized or unrecognized relationships.'),
('e5e9e3e6-d47e-58c9-bfed-efad3fd35484'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Communicating with parents of ill children requires:', '["Honesty, empathy and partnership","Focusing on the child only","Focusing on the parents only","Focusing on the disease only","Focusing on treatment only"]'::jsonb, 'Honesty, empathy and partnership', 'Parents are helped by truthful information delivered with empathy, treating them as partners in their child''s care.'),
('dc66356a-8e34-5261-b099-730cb08ce3d4'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'The legal doctrine established by the Tarasoff case is:', '["Duty to warn third party at serious risk","Patient confidentiality","Right to refuse treatment","Informed consent","Euthanasia principles"]'::jsonb, 'Duty to warn third party at serious risk', 'In Tarasoff v Regents of the University of California (1976), the court held that a psychotherapist has a legal duty to warn an identifiable third party at serious risk of harm from a patient. This duty overrides usual confidentiality obligations.'),
('2b16d90e-7870-5d7a-914e-830266f56f4c'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'In medical law, the standard of care is determined by:', '["What a reasonably competent physician would do","Cost of treatment","Patient expectation","Physician''s personal standard","Best evidence anywhere"]'::jsonb, 'What a reasonably competent physician would do', 'The Bolam test states that a physician is not negligent if they practice in accordance with a responsible body of medical opinion. The standard of care is therefore judged against the conduct of a reasonably competent peer, not cost, expectations, or personal preference.'),
('cb8ce7fa-c85f-5597-afd3-e3f001a2b21d'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Siblings of chronically ill children most need:', '["Ice resulting in decreased functional activity","Information and attention to their concerns","Exclusion mediated by specific receptor activation","Only activities without affecting the primary tissue pathway","information leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Information and attention to their concerns', 'Siblings of ill children commonly experience anxiety, jealousy, and a sense of neglect. Providing age-appropriate information and dedicated attention addresses these concerns and supports their adjustment.'),
('32d51cbb-f6ef-522b-8fa7-ac552748ccfe'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Grandparent involvement in pediatric illness can be:', '["Only helpful resulting in decreased functional activity","Supportive but needs clear communication","Ignored mediated by specific receptor activation","Only problematic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Excluded leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Supportive but needs clear communication', 'Grandparents often provide practical and emotional support when a grandchild is ill, but their involvement must be coordinated through clear communication with parents and the care team. This prevents conflicting instructions and reduces family stress.'),
('5562dc75-c6f4-5bfe-8f9a-984dbe434b62'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Whole person care means:', '["Addressing all dimensions of patient''s being (body mind spirit context)","Only social accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only physical mediated by specific receptor activation","Only spiritual without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only mental leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Addressing all dimensions of patient''s being (body mind spirit context)', 'Whole person care addresses the physical, psychological, social, and spiritual dimensions of the patient together. Restricting care to any single dimension ignores the interconnected aspects of health and illness.'),
('e0c325b5-cbf1-5f42-a8ec-b5530bf7a354'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Integrative medicine combines:', '["Only conventional resulting in decreased functional activity","Conventional and evidence-based complementary approaches","evidence mediated by specific receptor activation","Only alternative without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Eclectic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Conventional and evidence-based complementary approaches', 'Integrative medicine deliberately combines conventional treatments with evidence-based complementary therapies. It does not simply replace conventional care with unproven alternative methods.'),
('4906bf85-3ae2-5184-8497-2cc41936bbc5'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Complementary therapies should be discussed with patients to:', '["Endorse all resulting in decreased functional activity","Dismiss them accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Prohibit mediated by specific receptor activation","Ensure safety and avoid harmful interactions","Ignore leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ensure safety and avoid harmful interactions', 'Discussing complementary therapies allows the clinician to identify potential interactions with prescribed drugs and to discourage unsafe products. This protects the patient while respecting their choices.'),
('75c6b225-d6f6-5927-96d0-d3662f9e38f8'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Non-maleficence in medical ethics means:', '["Do no harm","Do good","Be just","Respect autonomy","Maintain confidentiality"]'::jsonb, 'Do no harm', 'Non-maleficence obliges clinicians to avoid causing harm and is encapsulated in the maxim ''first, do no harm.'' It is one of the four pillars of biomedical ethics alongside beneficence, autonomy, and justice.'),
('41e85d44-db67-5294-a66d-2c527c922346'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Use of a placebo is unethical when:', '["Proven effective treatment exists","Trial is double-blind","Study is small","Patient prefers placebo","Side effects are mild"]'::jsonb, 'Proven effective treatment exists', 'A placebo must not be used when a proven, effective treatment is available, because withholding it deprives the patient of benefit and violates beneficence and informed consent. In such a situation placebo use is deceptive and clinically harmful.'),
('10f62766-f270-5f87-88ad-10e987fc62c0'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'When the patient is not ready to hear the prognosis, the physician should:', '["Force discussion resulting in decreased functional activity","Respect their readiness and offer to discuss later","Inform family only mediated by specific receptor activation","Discharge without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Withhold all leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Respect their readiness and offer to discuss later', 'Patients have the right to control how much information they receive and when. Respecting their readiness and offering to revisit the discussion later preserves autonomy and trust without abandoning them.'),
('2a42f9ac-4299-51a7-8721-98818171b8f1'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'In serious illness, the concept of hope typically evolves from:', '["Only denial resulting in decreased functional activity","hope for cure accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","hope mediated by specific receptor activation","Only acceptance without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hope for cure to hope for quality time and meaningful moments"]'::jsonb, 'Hope for cure to hope for quality time and meaningful moments', 'In serious illness, hope often shifts from wishing for a cure toward hoping for symptom control, meaningful time, and valued moments with loved ones. This reframing supports realistic, patient-centered goals.'),
('08fd8785-d05d-5209-922c-f9382918c722'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Adaptive hope in serious illness involves:', '["Denial resulting in decreased functional activity","False hope accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Avoidance mediated by specific receptor activation","Despair without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hoping for realistic achievable goals"]'::jsonb, 'Hoping for realistic achievable goals', 'Adaptive hope focuses on achievable goals that remain possible despite the illness, such as comfort, dignity, and completing personal priorities. It differs from false hope or denial because it is grounded in the patient''s actual situation.'),
('63d7842b-663d-5597-b65b-4c775609a762'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'The ABCDE model of breaking bad news consists of which sequence of steps?', '["Attend Before Care During resulting in decreased functional activity","Assessment Briefing Communication Decision accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Be Careful During Exams mediated by specific receptor activation","Advance preparation Building environment Communicating Dealing with reactions Encouraging","Ask Believe Confirm Decide leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Advance preparation Building environment Communicating Dealing with reactions Encouraging', 'The ABCDE approach to breaking bad news comprises Advance preparation, Building a supportive environment, Communicating clearly, Dealing with reactions, and Encouraging and validating emotions. It provides a structured framework for difficult conversations.'),
('a3e19777-7252-5220-8b11-3f1c47404b6a'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Advance preparation for delivering bad news includes:', '["Rushing in resulting in decreased functional activity","Multiple interruptions accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Unprepared mediated by specific receptor activation","preparation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Knowing patient facts arranging privacy and preparing emotionally"]'::jsonb, 'Knowing patient facts arranging privacy and preparing emotionally', 'Advance preparation involves reviewing the patient''s clinical facts, anticipating questions, arranging privacy, and preparing oneself emotionally. This groundwork allows the news to be delivered accurately and compassionately.'),
('d51b2553-49e8-5de5-8e14-508745883219'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Building the environment for delivering bad news means providing:', '["Private comfortable setting with tissues seating","Public area accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Noisy place mediated by specific receptor activation","Standing without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Same as usual leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Private comfortable setting with tissues seating', 'A private, comfortable setting with seating and tissues helps the patient receive bad news with dignity and reduces distress. The physical environment shapes how the conversation is experienced.'),
('1758ad6c-661e-55ce-922d-31bc10eb33a0'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'In the ABCDE model of breaking bad news, dealing with the patient''s reactions involves:', '["Rush resulting in decreased functional activity","Allow silence listen and acknowledge emotions","Talk through silence mediated by specific receptor activation","Ignore tears without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Change subject leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Allow silence listen and acknowledge emotions', 'Allowing silence, listening actively, and acknowledging the patient''s emotional responses are central to dealing with reactions. Rushing through or deflecting emotion prevents the patient from processing the news.'),
('cb5f9291-3a77-5596-b31a-59823544be8b'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'In the ABCDE model, encouraging emotions means:', '["Minimizing resulting in decreased functional activity","Labeling accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ignoring mediated by specific receptor activation","Validating and supporting emotional expression","Shutting down leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Validating and supporting emotional expression', 'Encouraging emotions means validating and supporting the patient''s emotional expression rather than minimizing or suppressing it. This fosters trust and allows genuine feelings to be addressed.'),
('08c374a6-1511-530a-8186-dad8606088d4'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'The Invitation step of the SPIKES protocol involves:', '["Only family","Tell all without asking","Assume they want nothing","Assume they want everything","Ask how much the patient wants to know"]'::jsonb, 'Ask how much the patient wants to know', 'The Invitation step of SPIKES asks how much detail the patient wishes to know. Tailoring the disclosure to the patient''s stated preference respects their autonomy and readiness.'),
('0e0ab6cc-31be-5396-add2-c4ab675b12e8'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'A wish statement in a serious illness conversation is:', '["There is nothing we can do","Everything is fine","You will be fine","Stop worrying","I wish things were different"]'::jsonb, 'I wish things were different', 'Wish statements such as ''I wish things were different'' express empathy and align the clinician with the patient without offering false reassurance. They acknowledge shared regret and the reality of the situation.'),
('d561d5ad-f01c-5463-8a0d-8d6fc1f85800'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'When a patient refuses to hear bad news, the physician should:', '["Respect their wish and offer to speak with a family member or later","Inform family only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Document refusal mediated by specific receptor activation","Force information without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ignore leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Respect their wish and offer to speak with a family member or later', 'When a patient declines to receive information, the physician should respect that wish and offer to speak with a family member or to revisit the topic later. Forcing disclosure undermines autonomy and trust.'),
('0d2be321-bebf-5c82-950f-671103e36933'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Family presence during the breaking of bad news requires:', '["Only if dying","Family request","present","Patient''s permission","present (alternative wording)"]'::jsonb, 'Patient''s permission', 'Family members should only be present when the patient gives permission. The patient''s preferences, not family requests or the clinician''s convenience, determine who attends the conversation.'),
('45c1448f-ef07-5d15-bf7b-35bd1616a408'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'In the SPIKES protocol, which component involves arranging a private, unhurried setting for the discussion?', '["Emotions","Knowledge","Setting up the interview","Invitation","Perception"]'::jsonb, 'Setting up the interview', 'In the SPIKES protocol, ''Setting up the interview'' involves arranging a private, quiet, and unhurried environment with attention to seating and minimizing interruptions. It is the first step before assessing perception or delivering information.'),
('bbdbb3af-7552-544e-b05f-eba3b85489a7'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'A warning shot when delivering bad news is illustrated by which statement?', '["What brings you here resulting in decreased functional activity","I''m afraid I have some difficult news","Hello mediated by specific receptor activation","How are you without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Goodbye leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'I''m afraid I have some difficult news', 'A warning shot phrases difficult news with a gentle lead-in such as ''I''m afraid I have some difficult news,'' giving the patient a moment to prepare. It softens the impact without diminishing the seriousness of the message.'),
('9dde18af-d07a-528c-b4d0-91915b873acc'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'The period immediately after giving bad news should include:', '["New topic resulting in decreased functional activity","Rapid speech accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Leaving the room mediated by specific receptor activation","Next patient without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Silence to allow patient to process"]'::jsonb, 'Silence to allow patient to process', 'After delivering bad news, pausing in silence gives the patient time to absorb the information and respond emotionally. Rushing to a new topic or leaving the room prevents the patient from processing what they have heard.'),
('2f16bb52-1ac0-5017-94c5-49812d80e7db'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'After delivering bad news, the physician should arrange:', '["Referral only resulting in decreased functional activity","Arranging support and future contact","Surgery mediated by specific receptor activation","follow-up without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Discharge leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Arranging support and future contact', 'After delivering bad news, the physician should offer practical and emotional support, arrange follow-up contact, and identify the next steps. This continuity reassures the patient that they will not be abandoned.'),
('f6d7783d-0308-5901-a8bc-688e9d6e1bce'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Situation monitoring in healthcare teams means:', '["Ongoing awareness of environment and team performance","Only monitoring patient accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Individual work mediated by specific receptor activation","monitoring without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only monitoring equipment leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ongoing awareness of environment and team performance', 'Situation monitoring is the continuous awareness of the clinical environment, the patient''s condition, and the performance of team members. It allows the team to detect changes early and respond before errors occur.'),
('4929df32-e81f-5a91-b7a8-82546ff5a66d'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Mutual support within healthcare teams involves:', '["Helping others and advocating for safety","Individual work accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Competition mediated by specific receptor activation","Blame without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Isolation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Helping others and advocating for safety', 'Mutual support means team members assist one another and advocate for patient safety, including speaking up when a colleague''s action may be unsafe. It fosters collaboration rather than competition, blame, or isolation.'),
('6753e60a-5a1d-566a-8a4e-099cf770113e'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'The legal elements of medical negligence include all of the following EXCEPT:', '["Patient consent","Breach of duty","Damages","Duty of care","Causation"]'::jsonb, 'Patient consent', 'The four elements of negligence are duty of care, breach of duty, causation, and damages. Patient consent is not one of these elements and is therefore the exception.'),
('a2931e52-8bf3-5f65-ac64-6c1a7be3dce6'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'An effective healthcare team is characterized by:', '["Flexible only resulting in decreased functional activity","Authoritarian only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Clear roles shared goals mutual respect and effective communication","structure without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Loose only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Clear roles shared goals mutual respect and effective communication', 'Effective healthcare teams are characterized by clear roles, shared goals, mutual respect, and effective communication. These attributes promote coordination and reduce the risk of error.'),
('344fa081-3074-5d08-88e9-adfc9869b146'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Debriefing after procedures or clinical events involves:', '["Structured reflection to identify what went well and what to improve","Only positive accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Blame mediated by specific receptor activation","review without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Punishment leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Structured reflection to identify what went well and what to improve', 'Debriefing is a structured, non-punitive review of what went well and what could be improved after a procedure or event. It supports learning and team improvement without assigning blame.'),
('17b27e89-cdce-5ea6-a076-eb7fa05337cc'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'The Belmont Report identified which set of ethical principles for human research?', '["Respect for persons, beneficence, justice","Only autonomy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only non-maleficence mediated by specific receptor activation","Only beneficence without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only justice leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Respect for persons, beneficence, justice', 'The Belmont Report (1979) identified three fundamental ethical principles for research involving human subjects: respect for persons, beneficence, and justice. These principles underpin informed consent, risk-benefit assessment, and fair subject selection.'),
('f8b3d1ca-8e2f-5d94-ae3e-d201f4d03b44'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Microaggressions in healthcare communication are:', '["Overt aggression resulting in decreased functional activity","Subtle slights that communicate bias and disrespect","Compliments mediated by specific receptor activation","Professionalism without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Direct insults leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Subtle slights that communicate bias and disrespect', 'Microaggressions are subtle verbal or nonverbal slights that communicate bias, disrespect, or hostility toward members of marginalized groups. They are distinct from overt aggression or direct insults.'),
('b620a028-939a-529e-8b6f-0fd3cf50e620'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Strategies to mitigate implicit bias in clinical care include:', '["Mindfulness perspective-taking and structured decision-making","Only training accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Embracing bias mediated by specific receptor activation","Ice bias without affecting the primary tissue pathway","strategy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mindfulness perspective-taking and structured decision-making', 'Strategies to reduce implicit bias include mindfulness, perspective-taking, and the use of structured decision-making tools. These approaches interrupt automatic associations and promote more objective clinical judgments.'),
('9932d1e8-bab3-5564-a3c7-6b9d2aed9ec7'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'Physician-assisted suicide is best described as:', '["Physician provides means, patient self-administers","Physician directly administers lethal drug","Withholding treatment","Withdrawing life support","Refusing treatment"]'::jsonb, 'Physician provides means, patient self-administers', 'Physician-assisted suicide occurs when the physician provides the means, such as a prescription, and the patient performs the final act themselves. It differs from active euthanasia, in which the physician directly administers the lethal drug.'),
('594234f2-7388-55c4-ac07-8dcd36715cb7'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'In adults, the spinal cord typically ends at which vertebral level?', '["S1-S2","L3-L4","L1-L2","T10-T11","T12-L1"]'::jsonb, 'L1-L2', 'In adults, the spinal cord terminates at the conus medullaris at the L1-L2 vertebral level. This is why the lumbar cistern from L2 downward is a safe site for lumbar puncture.'),
('8118ea39-8b76-586c-8c49-df67aa9e0286'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Communication Skills - Communication Skills', 'The sciatic nerve is formed by which nerve roots?', '["L5-S2 nerve roots","L2-L4 nerve roots","S1-S3 nerve roots","L4-S3 nerve roots","L3-S1 nerve roots"]'::jsonb, 'L4-S3 nerve roots', 'The sciatic nerve arises from the L4, L5, S1, S2, and S3 nerve roots. It is the largest nerve in the body and exits the pelvis through the greater sciatic foramen.'),
('2a2014a6-e22c-55ad-998a-576ca0782f85'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'The biopsychosocial model was proposed by:', '["McWhinney","Balint","Osler","Engel","Szasz & Hollender"]'::jsonb, 'Engel', 'George Engel introduced the biopsychosocial model as a framework integrating biological, psychological and social factors in health and disease. It contrasts with the strictly biomedical model.'),
('1e40fa31-42d3-56ad-8ba7-eb79630879b7'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'The biomedical model focuses primarily on:', '["Cultural factors","Biological mechanisms of disease","Social factors","Psychological factors","Spiritual factors"]'::jsonb, 'Biological mechanisms of disease', 'The biomedical model explains illness through measurable biological deviations and excludes psychological and social dimensions. It treats disease as a purely organic process.'),
('5e082c36-148d-5212-aea4-7286f478e4a4'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'The general adaptation syndrome describing the non-specific response to prolonged stressors was described by:', '["Cannon","Lazarus","Holmes & Rahe","Engel","Selye"]'::jsonb, 'Selye', 'Hans Selye described the general adaptation syndrome with its alarm, resistance and exhaustion stages. Cannon had earlier described the fight-or-flight response, while Lazarus proposed the transactional model of stress.'),
('1b66d6aa-eb26-5644-a957-4d8bbc9f3376'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'The three stages of the general adaptation syndrome are:', '["Alarm, resistance, exhaustion","Shock, bargaining, recovery","Alarm, denial, acceptance","Acute, subacute, chronic","Denial, anger, depression"]'::jsonb, 'Alarm, resistance, exhaustion', 'Selye''s general adaptation syndrome progresses through alarm (initial sympathetic activation), resistance (adaptation to the stressor) and exhaustion (depletion of resources). Shock, bargaining and anger belong to stage models of grief, not to this syndrome.'),
('bd0f4261-f072-5251-8db0-521315a3bf1f'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'The alarm stage of the general adaptation syndrome involves:', '["Denial resulting in decreased functional activity","Depletion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Coping adaptation mediated by specific receptor activation","Recovery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Initial sympathetic nervous system activation (fight or flight)"]'::jsonb, 'Initial sympathetic nervous system activation (fight or flight)', 'The alarm stage is the immediate response to a stressor, marked by sympathetic activation and epinephrine release that prepare the body for fight or flight. Depletion and adaptation belong to later stages of the syndrome.'),
('24935932-1b86-57a3-84eb-53a141184be6'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'The resistance stage of the general adaptation syndrome involves:', '["Initial shock resulting in decreased functional activity","Adaptation and coping with ongoing stressor","Exhaustion mediated by specific receptor activation","Recovery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Collapse leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Adaptation and coping with ongoing stressor', 'During the resistance stage the body adapts and copes with a persistent stressor while maintaining apparently normal function. Exhaustion follows when adaptive resources are depleted.'),
('144b2355-d3bb-5fbb-a163-2d4f6cb83829'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Cannon described which stress response?', '["General Adaptation Syndrome","Transactional model","Fight or flight","Problem-focused coping","Appraisal process"]'::jsonb, 'Fight or flight', 'Walter Cannon first described the fight-or-flight response as the sympathetic reaction to an acute threat. Selye later extended this work into the general adaptation syndrome.'),
('0dd6a399-19de-5260-9161-3c911981aff0'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Acute stress is characterized by:', '["Long duration low intensity resulting in decreased functional activity","Short duration intense sympathetic response","Exhaustion mediated by specific receptor activation","Adaptation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Chronic fatigue leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Short duration intense sympathetic response', 'Acute stress is brief but intense, producing sympathetic activation with tachycardia and raised catecholamines. Chronic stress, by contrast, is prolonged and lower in intensity.'),
('05c4c4ad-42e4-5e3a-8022-d0160fc6d8b9'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Allostasis refers to:', '["Recovery resulting in decreased functional activity","Stress vulnerability accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Homeostasis mediated by specific receptor activation","Stress resistance without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The process of achieving stability through physiological change"]'::jsonb, 'The process of achieving stability through physiological change', 'Allostasis is the active process by which the body achieves stability through change in response to demands. It differs from homeostasis, which maintains fixed set points.'),
('c1010522-5cec-5f9a-941f-1d34c653381e'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'The Holmes and Rahe Social Readjustment Rating Scale measures:', '["Depression","Personality","Life events as stressors","Coping style","Social support"]'::jsonb, 'Life events as stressors', 'The scale quantifies stress by assigning life-change units to recent life events. Higher cumulative scores correlate with an increased risk of subsequent illness.'),
('17c1887d-94d4-53e1-8546-4a0379e50fb9'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Illness behaviour refers to:', '["Treatment behavior resulting in decreased functional activity","Help-seeking only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Recovery mediated by specific receptor activation","Compliance without affecting the primary tissue pathway","How individuals monitor and respond to symptoms"]'::jsonb, 'How individuals monitor and respond to symptoms', 'Illness behaviour describes how people monitor bodily symptoms, interpret them and act on them. It precedes and differs from sick-role behaviour and help-seeking.'),
('7cac6d56-2593-5a83-9276-b4437fbe381e'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'The stages of illness behaviour include:', '["Only recovery resulting in decreased functional activity","Only treatment accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only symptom experience mediated by specific receptor activation","Symptom experience, sick role assumption, medical care contact, dependent role, recovery","Only diagnosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Symptom experience, sick role assumption, medical care contact, dependent role, recovery', 'Illness behaviour proceeds from symptom experience and tentative sick-role assumption to contacting medical care, assuming the dependent role and finally recovery. Such stage models describe the social response to symptoms.'),
('57ea9309-a4ea-5e88-8ed2-b475fcf5a5a4'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Parsons'' sick role has which features?', '["Exemption only resulting in decreased functional activity","Only obligations accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Isolation mediated by specific receptor activation","Exemption from normal roles and obligation to seek help and get well","Only rights leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Exemption from normal roles and obligation to seek help and get well', 'Parsons'' sick role grants the patient exemption from normal social responsibilities. In return, the patient is obliged to seek competent help and work toward recovery.'),
('e169a9fe-70ab-5754-b992-9988dac6bd05'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Somatization is:', '["Normal illness resulting in decreased functional activity","Factitious disorder accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Physical symptoms arising from psychological distress","Malingering without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Organic disease leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Physical symptoms arising from psychological distress', 'Somatization presents psychological distress through physical symptoms without an adequate organic basis. The symptoms are produced unconsciously, unlike in malingering.'),
('daa11232-fbb7-51b3-8bc0-31ea6720d44b'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Somatization differs from malingering because somatization is:', '["Unconscious not intentional (malingering is intentional deception)","Intentional accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Conscious mediated by specific receptor activation","Faking without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Voluntary leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Unconscious not intentional (malingering is intentional deception)', 'Somatization symptoms arise unconsciously from psychological distress, whereas malingering involves deliberate feigning for external gain. Factitious disorders involve intentional illness production without obvious external reward.'),
('9d994abd-d18f-5a60-b81a-1f39e24d2639'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'The Bolitho modification of the Bolam test:', '["Medical opinion must be logical and reasonable","Any opinion suffices","Only majority opinion counts","Only expert testimony matters","Opinion not needed"]'::jsonb, 'Medical opinion must be logical and reasonable', 'Bolitho ruled that the court may reject a body of medical opinion that is not logical or defensible. It qualifies the Bolam test, which otherwise relies on customary responsible practice.'),
('4e4f9800-7a7b-5b14-ac2d-2fabf825cf38'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Munchausen syndrome is:', '["Severe factitious disorder with hospital wandering and pseudologia fantastica","Anxiety accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Somatization mediated by specific receptor activation","Psychosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Depression leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Severe factitious disorder with hospital wandering and pseudologia fantastica', 'Munchausen syndrome is the severe form of factitious disorder in which patients fabricate illness and wander between hospitals. It is characterised by pathological lying (pseudologia fantastica) with no external incentive.'),
('550f25dc-dc08-5954-bc3b-b1daa29cb0d3'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Exceptions to confidentiality include:', '["Notifiable diseases, serious harm to third parties, child protection","Only notifiable diseases accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only court orders mediated by specific receptor activation","Only patient consent without affecting the primary tissue pathway","No exceptions leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Notifiable diseases, serious harm to third parties, child protection', 'Confidentiality may be breached for notifiable diseases, to protect third parties from serious harm and for child protection. The Tarasoff duty and mandatory reporting obligations override confidentiality.'),
('75459364-05d3-5ab6-8875-76eeed73c9d8'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'The doctor-patient relationship models of Szasz and Hollender include:', '["Cooperative-resistive resulting in decreased functional activity","Passive-active accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hierarchical mediated by specific receptor activation","Dependent-independent without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Active-passive, guidance-cooperation, mutual participation"]'::jsonb, 'Active-passive, guidance-cooperation, mutual participation', 'Szasz and Hollender described three models of the doctor-patient relationship: active-passive, guidance-cooperation and mutual participation. They correspond to the patient''s degree of involvement in the relationship.'),
('aefb1c40-34ed-5055-b360-4d4dbac88190'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'The active-passive model (Szasz and Hollender) is appropriate for:', '["Prevention","Long-term care","Chronic illness","Emergency/unconscious patient","Health promotion"]'::jsonb, 'Emergency/unconscious patient', 'The active-passive model suits situations where the patient is unable to participate, such as an unconscious or emergency patient. The physician acts while the patient remains passive.'),
('0c50b6a1-17ed-5b26-b854-84092559bf4d'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'The guidance-cooperation model is appropriate for:', '["Terminal illness resulting in decreased functional activity","Unconscious patient accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Acute illness (patient seeks help and follows advice)","Prevention without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Chronic illness leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Acute illness (patient seeks help and follows advice)', 'In the guidance-cooperation model the physician instructs and the patient cooperates, which suits acute illness. The patient seeks help and can follow the advice given.'),
('79dd7c36-d300-57cf-bd5c-32724d67167e'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'The mutual participation model is appropriate for:', '["Trauma resulting in decreased functional activity","Emergency accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Acute illness mediated by specific receptor activation","Chronic illness management and health promotion","Surgery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Chronic illness management and health promotion', 'Mutual participation is a partnership suited to chronic illness and health promotion. The physician and patient share responsibility for treatment decisions.'),
('b94cc2d7-665c-573a-96fe-9107ba60bdc2'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'The degree to which a hypertensive patient''s behaviour matches the treatment recommendations agreed with the physician is called:', '["Agreement on plan resulting in decreased functional activity","Satisfaction accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Shared decision making mediated by specific receptor activation","Understanding without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The extent to which patient follows agreed treatment recommendations"]'::jsonb, 'The extent to which patient follows agreed treatment recommendations', 'Adherence is the extent to which a patient''s behaviour, including medication taking, follows the agreed treatment recommendations. It is distinct from shared decision making and satisfaction, which do not by themselves define it.'),
('198d5ca8-941a-5757-bb72-3031de965cb8'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Factors affecting adherence include:', '["Only beliefs resulting in decreased functional activity","Only cost accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only complexity mediated by specific receptor activation","Only side effects without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Complexity of regimen, side effects, understanding, beliefs, cost, support"]'::jsonb, 'Complexity of regimen, side effects, understanding, beliefs, cost, support', 'Adherence is influenced by regimen complexity, side effects, patient understanding and beliefs, cost and social support. No single factor alone determines adherence.'),
('e92e8493-f626-588b-99ea-cb1cba2b5133'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'DALE (Disability-Adjusted Life Expectancy) measures:', '["Disease prevalence resulting in decreased functional activity","Healthcare cost accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Healthy life expectancy adjusted for disability","Mortality without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Total life expectancy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Healthy life expectancy adjusted for disability', 'DALE adjusts life expectancy for years lived with disability to state the expected years of healthy life. It is a summary measure of population health also known as healthy life expectancy.'),
('9a64dcec-07e7-53cf-a2c8-e8903de26c44'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'In the health belief model, perceived severity is:', '["Belief about seriousness of consequences","Belief about benefit","Belief about barriers","Confidence","Belief about risk"]'::jsonb, 'Belief about seriousness of consequences', 'Perceived severity is the person''s belief about the seriousness of an illness and its consequences. It combines with perceived susceptibility to shape the perceived threat of disease.'),
('209e8ce6-be43-5400-82ea-3922ebfcfb43'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'In the health belief model, perceived benefits are:', '["Confidence resulting in decreased functional activity","Cues to action accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Barriers mediated by specific receptor activation","Belief about risk without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Belief that action will reduce risk or severity"]'::jsonb, 'Belief that action will reduce risk or severity', 'Perceived benefits reflect the individual''s belief that a recommended action will reduce the threat of disease. They are weighed against perceived barriers when deciding whether to act.'),
('0f0d7864-8a1b-51be-929e-27ac3a8b0153'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'In the health belief model, perceived barriers are:', '["Risk resulting in decreased functional activity","Benefits accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Belief about negative aspects of recommended action","Severity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cues leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Belief about negative aspects of recommended action', 'Perceived barriers are beliefs about the costs or drawbacks of a recommended action that may deter it. Lowering barriers while raising perceived benefits encourages behaviour change.'),
('f3b670e0-54f3-58de-af5f-7a5ddddefcd8'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Self-efficacy (Bandura) is:', '["Belief about treatment resulting in decreased functional activity","Social support accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Confidence in doctor mediated by specific receptor activation","Belief in one''s ability to perform a behavior","Belief about disease leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Belief in one''s ability to perform a behavior', 'Self-efficacy is Bandura''s construct for a person''s belief in their ability to perform a specific behavior, and it is a core determinant of health behavior change.'),
('4b2d00a5-bb34-5062-80a9-7711cbdac8e6'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Psychological aspects of cancer include:', '["Symptoms only resulting in decreased functional activity","Denial, depression, anxiety, and adjustment disorders","Treatment only mediated by specific receptor activation","Pain only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cost only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Denial, depression, anxiety, and adjustment disorders', 'Cancer patients commonly experience denial, depression, anxiety, and adjustment disorders in response to diagnosis, treatment, and prognosis, and these reactions may coexist and change over the illness course.'),
('da02a877-9782-50db-9b3d-e09ca97053ec'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Denial in cancer patients may be:', '["Adaptive resulting in decreased functional activity","Adaptive or maladaptive depending on context and duration","Only late mediated by specific receptor activation","Only early without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Maladaptive leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Adaptive or maladaptive depending on context and duration', 'Brief, early denial can protect a patient from overwhelming distress and allow gradual acceptance, but prolonged denial may impair treatment adherence and become maladaptive.'),
('fb2352ba-59a9-5804-b7e4-3ab7967af14b'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Depression in cancer patients:', '["Only in terminal stage resulting in decreased functional activity","Common and often underdiagnosed and undertreated","Not treatable mediated by specific receptor activation","Rare without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Easily diagnosed leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Common and often underdiagnosed and undertreated', 'Depression is common in cancer patients but is frequently underdiagnosed and undertreated; overlapping physical symptoms of cancer can mask its recognition.'),
('754db668-5447-51c1-8805-50724ee8a42a'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Anxiety in cancer patients is related to:', '["Only cost resulting in decreased functional activity","Uncertainty, treatment side effects, and fear of recurrence","Only prognosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Only diagnosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only treatment leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Uncertainty, treatment side effects, and fear of recurrence', 'Anxiety in cancer patients arises from diagnostic and prognostic uncertainty, concerns about treatment side effects, and fear of recurrence, and it may interfere with coping and treatment adherence.'),
('686873ab-1adf-5ba1-a439-62e33f6f6b6c'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'A 60-year-old female reports significant work-related stress affecting her sleep. This presentation is most characteristic of which personality trait?', '["Extraversion resulting in decreased functional activity","Conscientiousness accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Optimism mediated by specific receptor activation","Agreeableness without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Negative affectivity and social inhibition"]'::jsonb, 'Negative affectivity and social inhibition', 'The combination of negative affectivity and social inhibition defines type D personality, which is associated with chronic work stress, poor sleep, and adverse cardiovascular outcomes.'),
('af8346b6-2e4f-527b-b266-39139d2389bf'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Anxiety in heart disease may manifest as:', '["Only hyperventilation resulting in decreased functional activity","Only chest pain accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only fear mediated by specific receptor activation","Chest pain, palpitations, fear of exercise, and hyperventilation","Only palpitations leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Chest pain, palpitations, fear of exercise, and hyperventilation', 'Anxiety in cardiac patients may present with chest pain, palpitations, fear of exercise, and hyperventilation, symptoms that overlap with and can mimic cardiac disease.'),
('8cfe1a17-9c69-50e2-b09e-13c82417199b'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Cardiac rehabilitation includes:', '["Monitoring only resulting in decreased functional activity","Diet only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Exercise, education, and psychological support","Medication only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Surgery only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Exercise, education, and psychological support', 'Comprehensive cardiac rehabilitation programs combine supervised exercise, patient education, and psychological support to improve recovery and reduce morbidity after cardiac events.'),
('4c563d4f-4660-539c-b0c8-95998d017749'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'The denial stage in the Kübler-Ross model is characterized by:', '["Name of stage","This is happening","Shock","This cannot be happening","Not me"]'::jsonb, 'This cannot be happening', 'In Kübler-Ross''s model, the denial stage is characterized by refusal to accept the diagnosis, expressed in thoughts such as ''This cannot be happening to me.'''),
('6867d97c-d85f-5cab-8f1e-63978c578e98'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'The anger stage in the Kübler-Ross model is characterized by:', '["I accept","Just let it be","What can I do","Not me","Why me? It''s not fair!"]'::jsonb, 'Why me? It''s not fair!', 'In the anger stage, the patient expresses frustration and asks ''Why me?'', often directing anger at others, including family and healthcare staff.'),
('2fec6df4-7274-53bb-b665-66ecdca13fa1'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'The bargaining stage in the Kübler-Ross model is characterized by:', '["If I do this then maybe...","Why me accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","What can I do mediated by specific receptor activation","Not you without affecting the primary tissue pathway","For you leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'If I do this then maybe...', 'In the bargaining stage, the patient attempts to negotiate a postponement or alteration of death, often expressed as ''If I do this, then maybe...''.'),
('b36738e9-2aac-5c61-97c5-ea0ca31df810'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Competence is a determination, while capacity is .', '["Legal, medical","Medical, legal","Ethical, legal","Clinical, ethical","Moral, clinical"]'::jsonb, 'Legal, medical', 'Competence is a legal determination made by a court, whereas capacity is a clinical assessment by a physician of a patient''s ability to make specific decisions.'),
('563a13a4-41ef-598a-b05d-d7f4649f35be'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Grief is:', '["Natural emotional response to loss","Unnatural accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only about death mediated by specific receptor activation","Only pathological without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Complicated leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Natural emotional response to loss', 'Grief is the natural emotional response to loss and may follow bereavement, loss of health, or relationship loss; it is a normal process rather than a disorder.'),
('f88e967c-cdf8-59c4-be17-25e0d271219c'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Normal grief typically includes:', '["Only denial resulting in decreased functional activity","Only crying accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sadness, yearning, preoccupation with the deceased, and gradual acceptance","Only sadness without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only anger leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sadness, yearning, preoccupation with the deceased, and gradual acceptance', 'Normal grief is characterized by sadness, yearning for the deceased, and preoccupation with the loss, followed by gradual acceptance over time.'),
('416fffa0-f6ca-5c69-9444-194d1c6be571'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Anticipatory grief occurs:', '["Only in children resulting in decreased functional activity","Only in accidents accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","After sudden death mediated by specific receptor activation","Before an expected loss (e.g., terminal diagnosis)","Only in elderly leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Before an expected loss (e.g., terminal diagnosis)', 'Anticipatory grief occurs before an expected loss, such as after a terminal diagnosis, when the individual begins mourning while the person is still alive.'),
('fee5cc2b-8258-5a5a-adbd-19dd13144e7c'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'The Bolitho modification of Bolam states that:', '["Medical opinion must be logical and reasonable","Any opinion suffices","Only majority opinion counts","Only expert testimony matters","Opinion not needed"]'::jsonb, 'Medical opinion must be logical and reasonable', 'The Bolitho case modified Bolam by requiring that the medical opinion put forward must be logical and capable of withstanding logical analysis, allowing a court to reject an illogical body of opinion.'),
('f2cfaa6e-1e70-51f4-bdbd-81c463bcbbef'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Worden''s tasks of mourning include:', '["Only first task resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Accept reality of loss, work through pain, adjust to environment without the deceased, and find connection while moving on","Only last task mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Cure without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Deny, avoid, forget, ignore, or replace leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Accept reality of loss, work through pain, adjust to environment without the deceased, and find connection while moving on', 'Worden''s tasks of mourning are to accept the reality of the loss, work through the pain of grief, adjust to an environment without the deceased, and find an enduring connection while moving on.'),
('7564d5fb-3c3c-5678-b4d5-914703d44eba'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'After losing a family member, a 70-year-old male presents with sleep disturbance, decreased appetite, and sadness. His grief alternates between processing the loss and managing the practical demands of daily life. Which grief model best describes this pattern?', '["One process resulting in decreased functional activity","Oscillation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Avoidance only mediated by specific receptor activation","Oscillation between loss-oriented and restoration-oriented coping","Linear stages leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Oscillation between loss-oriented and restoration-oriented coping', 'The dual-process model of grief describes oscillation between loss-oriented coping (processing the loss) and restoration-oriented coping (attending to practical demands of life), which characterizes adaptive grieving.'),
('d0d89165-bd2e-54ed-acab-93f479ff286d'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Family homeostasis means:', '["Rigidity resulting in decreased functional activity","Families strive for stability and balance","Constant change mediated by specific receptor activation","Disorganization without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Chaos leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Families strive for stability and balance', 'Family homeostasis refers to the tendency of families to maintain stability and balance through established patterns of interaction, which illness can disturb.'),
('11bb975b-723a-542b-960a-002158c87a04'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'The family life cycle includes which stages?', '["Only aging resulting in decreased functional activity","Only parenting accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only marriage mediated by specific receptor activation","Leaving home, forming a couple, family with adolescents, launching children, and later life","Infancy, childhood, adolescence, adulthood leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Leaving home, forming a couple, family with adolescents, launching children, and later life', 'The family life cycle describes predictable stages, including leaving home, forming a couple, families with adolescents, launching children, and the later-life family, each with distinct tasks.'),
('22c73218-fc57-53d7-8e3d-333decc47d3b'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Caregiving burden refers to:', '["Only emotional resulting in decreased functional activity","Only social accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Physical, emotional, and financial strain of caring for an ill relative","Only physical burden without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only financial leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Physical, emotional, and financial strain of caring for an ill relative', 'Caregiving burden encompasses the physical, emotional, and financial strain experienced by family members caring for an ill relative.'),
('a7a6cee3-a420-5f21-9b5d-9d35f3d7472c'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'The concept of illness in the family recognizes that:', '["Only patient suffers resulting in decreased functional activity","Only children accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Family unaffected mediated by specific receptor activation","Only caregivers without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Illness affects all family members, not just the patient"]'::jsonb, 'Illness affects all family members, not just the patient', 'Illness in a family rarely affects only the patient; the stress of chronic or serious illness alters roles, routines, and relationships across all family members.'),
('5e3dbcb2-ae31-5e07-8680-fb68b28f31e5'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Perceived threat in the Health Belief Model combines:', '["Self-efficacy and cues","Benefits and barriers","Attitudes and norms","Beliefs and values","Perceived susceptibility and severity"]'::jsonb, 'Perceived susceptibility and severity', 'Perceived threat in the Health Belief Model is the product of perceived susceptibility to the illness and perceived severity of its consequences.'),
('ae633215-bbda-52ad-8195-5a68330c0d39'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Cues to action in the Health Belief Model are:', '["Barriers resulting in decreased functional activity","Self-efficacy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Benefits mediated by specific receptor activation","Norms without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Triggers that prompt health behavior (e.g., symptoms, media, advice)"]'::jsonb, 'Triggers that prompt health behavior (e.g., symptoms, media, advice)', 'Cues to action in the Health Belief Model are internal or external triggers, such as symptoms, media messages, or advice from health professionals, that prompt the health behavior.'),
('942c6ee1-08b3-5e89-a8e3-17f6c4df7f2f'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Perceived behavioral control in the Theory of Planned Behavior is:', '["Attitude resulting in decreased functional activity","Knowledge accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Confidence mediated by specific receptor activation","Norm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Perception of ease or difficulty of performing behavior"]'::jsonb, 'Perception of ease or difficulty of performing behavior', 'Perceived behavioral control in the Theory of Planned Behavior is the individual''s perception of how easy or difficult performing the behavior will be, including their confidence in overcoming obstacles.'),
('600f16ab-4eda-53f5-b182-923bc93cafd3'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'The precontemplation stage of the transtheoretical model is characterized by:', '["Taking action resulting in decreased functional activity","Planning change accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Maintaining change mediated by specific receptor activation","Ready to change without affecting the primary tissue pathway","No intention to change (often unaware of the problem)"]'::jsonb, 'No intention to change (often unaware of the problem)', 'In the precontemplation stage, the person has no intention to change within the foreseeable future and is often unaware that a problem exists.'),
('49573ee4-1b76-570d-a44a-264c3c7d79de'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'The contemplation stage of the transtheoretical model is characterized by:', '["Maintaining resulting in decreased functional activity","Intention accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ready to act mediated by specific receptor activation","Taking action without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Aware of the problem and considering change but ambivalent"]'::jsonb, 'Aware of the problem and considering change but ambivalent', 'In the contemplation stage, a person is aware of the problem and considering change but remains ambivalent, having not yet committed to action.'),
('f5bf822f-f153-5f33-91a6-d796f596b83c'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'In the transtheoretical model, which stage is a person in when they have made specific overt behavioral changes?', '["Contemplating resulting in decreased functional activity","Preparing accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Taking action mediated by specific receptor activation","Has made specific overt behavioral changes","Maintaining leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Has made specific overt behavioral changes', 'In the transtheoretical model, a person in the action stage has made specific, overt behavioral changes within the past six months.'),
('74849bfb-a431-5c84-9291-75326b200ef3'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'In the Transtheoretical Model, the termination stage is characterized by:', '["Action resulting in decreased functional activity","Behavior is fully integrated and no risk of relapse","Relapse mediated by specific receptor activation","Maintenance without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Precontemplation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Behavior is fully integrated and no risk of relapse', 'In the termination stage the new behavior has become fully integrated so that there is no risk of relapsing to the old pattern, whereas precontemplation, action, and maintenance describe earlier phases of change.'),
('dc8ea205-be3b-500d-ae82-afef580cdfae'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Decisional balance in the Transtheoretical Model refers to:', '["Weighing pros and cons of change","Outcome accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cons only mediated by specific receptor activation","For and against only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pros only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Weighing pros and cons of change', 'Decisional balance is the individual''s weighing of the perceived benefits (pros) against the perceived costs (cons) of changing the behavior.'),
('7f3bf169-13c4-5a2b-8220-f16d0bf55dd4'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Processes of change in the Transtheoretical Model are:', '["Outcomes only resulting in decreased functional activity","Covert and overt activities used to progress through stages","Stages only mediated by specific receptor activation","Only overt without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only covert leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Covert and overt activities used to progress through stages', 'Processes of change are the covert (cognitive/emotional) and overt (behavioral) strategies people use to move from one stage of change to the next.'),
('5daa9759-3c60-517b-865e-e8142c97b4fc'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Stimulus control as a process of change involves:', '["Helping relationship resulting in decreased functional activity","Social liberation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Emotional release mediated by specific receptor activation","Avoiding triggers and creating cues for healthy behavior","Awareness leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Avoiding triggers and creating cues for healthy behavior', 'Stimulus control modifies the environment so that cues for the unhealthy habit are removed while reminders that prompt the desired behavior are added.'),
('5e95ce72-f629-5233-96d3-98cdf59eaa0d'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'In the study of Psychosocial Aspects, Human error should be managed by:', '["Fines resulting in decreased functional activity","Termination accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Punishment mediated by specific receptor activation","System redesign and support","Suspension leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'System redesign and support', 'Because human error is unintentional, it is best managed by improving systems and supporting the clinician rather than by punishment. Disciplinary responses should be reserved for reckless or intentional misconduct.'),
('a3cff90d-2acf-51e9-8597-c9d15e107357'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Adherence, compared with compliance, is:', '["Active patient agreement with treatment plan (vs passive following orders)","Passive following accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Forced treatment mediated by specific receptor activation","Same thing without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Opposite leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Active patient agreement with treatment plan (vs passive following orders)', 'Adherence implies the patient actively agrees with and participates in the treatment plan, whereas compliance suggests passively following the doctor''s instructions.'),
('89a361a2-048e-5862-a2aa-9b294a872043'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Factors affecting medication compliance include:', '["Only side effects resulting in decreased functional activity","Only number accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Number of medications frequency side effects understanding cost","Only cost without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only frequency leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Number of medications frequency side effects understanding cost', 'Compliance is influenced by multiple variables, including the number and dosing frequency of medications, side effects, the patient''s understanding, and the cost of treatment.'),
('73aac262-fce5-5384-a7cf-0e185d0a7a86'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Strategies to improve adherence include:', '["Only cost reduction resulting in decreased functional activity","Only simplification accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Simplifying regimen patient education addressing beliefs reminders","Only reminders without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only education leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Simplifying regimen patient education addressing beliefs reminders', 'Adherence improves when the regimen is simplified, patients are educated, their health beliefs are addressed, and reminder systems are used.'),
('392b6a14-7b13-5005-87cd-e10087f259ba'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'The Belmont Report identified which set of basic ethical principles for human research?', '["Respect for persons, beneficence, justice","Only autonomy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only non-maleficence mediated by specific receptor activation","Only beneficence without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only justice leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Respect for persons, beneficence, justice', 'The Belmont Report named respect for persons, beneficence, and justice as the three foundational principles governing research with human subjects.'),
('a78404e8-37f9-56b1-9c7b-745cbb068848'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Dependency in hospitalized patients refers to:', '["Self-reliance","Autonomy","Independence","Increased reliance on healthcare providers","Normal for context but maladaptive if excessive"]'::jsonb, 'Increased reliance on healthcare providers', 'Hospitalization increases a patient''s reliance on staff for care; some dependency is expected in this setting but can become maladaptive when it impairs recovery or discharge.'),
('a64c214d-4197-5637-9671-1453ccd00133'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Effects of institutionalization include:', '["Loss of initiative passivity and dependence from long institutional stay","Autonomy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Adaptation mediated by specific receptor activation","Independence without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Coping leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Loss of initiative passivity and dependence from long institutional stay', 'Prolonged institutional care commonly produces apathy, loss of initiative, passivity, and dependence as the person adapts to the institutional routine.'),
('40fe430b-02f1-5fe2-9031-bf3ad6cfbac6'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'ICU psychosis (delirium) is characterized by:', '["Acute confusion disorientation and hallucinations in ICU setting","Dementia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anxiety mediated by specific receptor activation","Chronic psychosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Depression leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Acute confusion disorientation and hallucinations in ICU setting', 'ICU delirium presents with the acute onset of confusion, disorientation, and perceptual disturbances during intensive care, unlike chronic conditions such as dementia or psychosis.'),
('df10a38c-28ab-506c-947f-f71916475583'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Sleep deprivation in the hospital affects:', '["Only immunity resulting in decreased functional activity","Only mood accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Recovery immune function cognition and mood","Only cognition without affecting the primary tissue pathway","No effect leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Recovery immune function cognition and mood', 'Inadequate sleep impairs recovery, immune function, cognition, and mood, so protecting sleep is clinically important for hospitalized patients.'),
('b0b5e79d-455f-59c6-8792-44b1e811bca5'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Sensory deprivation or overload in the ICU:', '["Helps recovery resulting in decreased functional activity","Reduces pain accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Contributes to delirium and psychological distress","Has no psychological impact without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Improves sleep leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Contributes to delirium and psychological distress', 'Both excessive and insufficient sensory input in the ICU disrupt perception and orientation, increasing the risk of delirium and psychological distress.'),
('da9c4433-890f-557c-b10f-a4e90b7cf5f0'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'The sick role provides:', '["Social rejection resulting in decreased functional activity","Exemption from normal responsibilities and expectation to seek help","Guaranteed cure mediated by specific receptor activation","Stigma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Continued work leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Exemption from normal responsibilities and expectation to seek help', 'According to Parsons''s sick role, the ill person is excused from normal duties but is expected to seek competent help and to work toward recovery.'),
('d705762b-0b7c-5ca0-b000-13c6e70302c8'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'A criticism of the sick role concept is that:', '["It is perfect resulting in decreased functional activity","It is universally accepted by all cultures accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Everyone accepts it mediated by specific receptor activation","It is too passive and does not apply to chronic illness","It applies to all leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'It is too passive and does not apply to chronic illness', 'Critics argue that the sick role casts the patient as passive and fits acute illness poorly, since chronic illness requires long-term adaptation rather than a brief recovery period.'),
('12244b94-b527-58ee-94a2-f629430dda42'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Illness behavior can be influenced by:', '["Only personality resulting in decreased functional activity","Personality culture social support and prior experience","Only social support mediated by specific receptor activation","Only prior experience without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only culture leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Personality culture social support and prior experience', 'Illness behavior is shaped by multiple interacting factors, including personality, cultural norms, available social support, and prior experience of illness.'),
('a53c08bc-3a90-525d-ae10-f3b73f7a02a3'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Illness denial may:', '["Strengthen the doctor-patient relationship","Improve adherence","Shorten the duration of illness","Delay treatment and worsen outcomes in some contexts","Have no impact on prognosis"]'::jsonb, 'Delay treatment and worsen outcomes in some contexts', 'Denial can be temporarily protective but often delays help-seeking and worsens outcomes when it prevents the patient from accepting necessary treatment.'),
('de12fadd-b1b4-5daf-978a-f08f781d4bd3'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Health anxiety is:', '["Worry about health that may be excessive or disproportionate","Delusion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Acceptance of one''s health status mediated by specific receptor activation","Psychosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Concern limited to a single screening result leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Worry about health that may be excessive or disproportionate', 'Health anxiety involves persistent worry about having a serious illness that is out of proportion to the real risk and that persists despite reassurance.'),
('64f16e5c-971a-5d81-a416-74c11ff66189'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Beneficence in medical ethics means:', '["Act in the patient''s best interest","Do no harm","Be just","Respect autonomy","Maintain confidentiality"]'::jsonb, 'Act in the patient''s best interest', 'Beneficence obliges the physician to act for the good of the patient and to promote the patient''s welfare.'),
('34ce1086-81bb-5f2c-ae10-61cbce3771a1'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'The gate control theory of pain (Melzack and Wall) proposes:', '["Simple reflex resulting in decreased functional activity","A purely psychological explanation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only brain involvement mediated by specific receptor activation","Spinal gate mechanism modulating pain transmission","Direct pathway leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Spinal gate mechanism modulating pain transmission', 'Melzack and Wall proposed that signals in the spinal cord can open or close a gate, modulating the transmission of pain signals to the brain.'),
('8227ac51-9a14-590f-99c9-f77584813304'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'The gate control theory locates the gate for pain modulation in the:', '["Brainstem as gate resulting in decreased functional activity","Cortex as gate accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thalamus as gate mediated by specific receptor activation","Substantia gelatinosa in spinal cord as gate","Periphery as gate leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Substantia gelatinosa in spinal cord as gate', 'The gate is proposed to lie in the substantia gelatinosa of the dorsal horn of the spinal cord, where incoming afferent signals are modulated before transmission.'),
('887b39bc-4588-5b96-9b90-8c676ea43e59'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Factors that close the gate and reduce pain include:', '["Focus on pain resulting in decreased functional activity","Boredom accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Distraction relaxation attention elsewhere","Fear without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anxiety leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Distraction relaxation attention elsewhere', 'Psychological states such as distraction, relaxation, and shifting attention away from pain increase inhibitory input and close the gate, reducing perceived pain.'),
('9b78afb4-278d-515c-901d-14b376c7093b'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Factors that open the gate and increase pain include:', '["Confidence resulting in decreased functional activity","Safety accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Distraction mediated by specific receptor activation","Anxiety fear depression focus on pain","Relaxation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anxiety fear depression focus on pain', 'Emotional states such as anxiety, fear, depression, and intense focus on the pain open the gate, amplifying pain perception.'),
('e1e89957-6678-53ca-9293-0f3694e261d7'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'The standard of care in medical practice is determined by:', '["What a reasonably competent physician would do","Cost of treatment","Patient expectation","Physician''s personal standard","Best evidence anywhere"]'::jsonb, 'What a reasonably competent physician would do', 'The standard of care is judged against what a reasonably competent physician with similar training would do in the same circumstances.'),
('95da45b5-c2d2-576d-83b8-68ebede57ef2'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Phantom limb pain is:', '["Not real resulting in decreased functional activity","Psychiatric accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Imagined pain mediated by specific receptor activation","Pain perceived in amputated limb from altered central processing","Only psychological leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pain perceived in amputated limb from altered central processing', 'Phantom limb pain is genuine pain experienced in a missing limb and is attributed to altered central nervous system processing after amputation.'),
('be234603-4a99-59f6-b5d8-7f5465c4ba3f'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'The placebo effect in pain involves:', '["Deception only resulting in decreased functional activity","Expectation conditioning and endogenous opioid release","Only psychology mediated by specific receptor activation","A random chemical reaction without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Imagination leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Expectation conditioning and endogenous opioid release', 'Placebo analgesia is mediated by the patient''s expectation of relief, prior conditioning, and the release of endogenous opioids.'),
('864e5174-9b99-5e44-b2f1-1bf04879f61e'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'The nocebo effect in pain refers to:', '["Positive expectation","Indifference to treatment","Negative expectation increases pain perception","The same as the placebo effect","A purely physical effect with no psychological basis"]'::jsonb, 'Negative expectation increases pain perception', 'The nocebo effect is the worsening of symptoms or pain caused by negative expectations, the opposite counterpart of the placebo effect.'),
('ea29d0aa-4264-5e2e-b8b7-a1d8ca254784'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Factors influencing the placebo response include:', '["Only medication resulting in decreased functional activity","Only relationship accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only conditioning mediated by specific receptor activation","Expectation conditioning doctor-patient relationship","Only expectation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Expectation conditioning doctor-patient relationship', 'Placebo responses are influenced by the patient''s expectations, prior conditioning, and the quality of the doctor-patient relationship.'),
('f7875fb5-9212-5c97-b863-f337dad41dd9'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Cultural competence involves:', '["Only skills resulting in decreased functional activity","Only language accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Skills knowledge and attitudes for cross-cultural care","Only attitudes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only knowledge leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Skills knowledge and attitudes for cross-cultural care', 'Cultural competence combines awareness, knowledge, and skills that enable effective and respectful healthcare across different cultural backgrounds.'),
('7b1ee91c-ba8b-597e-994b-dc9eb78d4d07'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Socioeconomic status affects health through:', '["Only education resulting in decreased functional activity","Only healthcare accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only income mediated by specific receptor activation","Only occupation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Material resources psychosocial stress and health behaviors"]'::jsonb, 'Material resources psychosocial stress and health behaviors', 'Socioeconomic status influences health through material resources, exposure to psychosocial stress, and health-related behaviors that differ between social strata.'),
('dc49dbad-7b0c-55b1-a48d-20db0e725ce2'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'The Black Report (UK) identified which explanations for health inequalities?', '["Artefact social selection material cultural/behavioral","Only selection accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only artefact mediated by specific receptor activation","Only cultural without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only material leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Artefact social selection material cultural/behavioral', 'The Black Report explained health inequalities through four frameworks: artefact, social selection, material, and cultural/behavioral explanations.'),
('24345da3-a0bb-50c0-96e2-692803a279a9'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'The material explanation for health inequalities holds that health differences result from:', '["Selection resulting in decreased functional activity","Lifestyle choices accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Artefact mediated by specific receptor activation","Culture without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Health differences due to income housing and working conditions"]'::jsonb, 'Health differences due to income housing and working conditions', 'Material explanations attribute health inequalities to differences in income, housing, and working conditions rather than to selection or lifestyle alone.'),
('8eaaba1c-1af5-5860-ae66-e7535e03dcb4'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'The social selection explanation for health inequalities proposes that:', '["Material conditions resulting in decreased functional activity","Behavior accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Health determines social position (healthy move up ill move down)","Culture without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Artefact leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Health determines social position (healthy move up ill move down)', 'Social selection holds that good health enables upward social mobility, whereas ill health leads to downward mobility, so health determines social position.'),
('4e2589f4-f88c-5474-8a69-f6671960460b'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'The artefact explanation for health inequalities holds that:', '["Real inequalities resulting in decreased functional activity","Behavioral accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Social selection mediated by specific receptor activation","Inequalities are measurement artifacts not real","Material leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inequalities are measurement artifacts not real', 'The artefact explanation maintains that observed health differences largely reflect biases in measurement and data collection rather than genuine inequalities.'),
('c4b3bebb-ae22-5ef7-a288-e25cd19c6da1'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Health inequalities are differences in health that are:', '["Individual only","Natural","Inevitable","Avoidable unjust and systematic","Unrelated to social factors"]'::jsonb, 'Avoidable unjust and systematic', 'Health inequalities are systematic and avoidable differences in health between population groups that are judged to be unfair and unjust.'),
('e3be24fa-2803-55a7-8a1e-d9b4dc168acd'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Life expectancy differs between social classes due to:', '["Only behavior resulting in decreased functional activity","Only luck accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only healthcare mediated by specific receptor activation","Accumulated advantage/disadvantage across life course","Only genetics leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Accumulated advantage/disadvantage across life course', 'Social class differences in life expectancy result from the lifelong accumulation of advantages or disadvantages across material, educational, and occupational domains. No single factor such as behavior, healthcare, or genetics explains the gradient alone.'),
('12b8e6a6-f838-55ee-9dd5-53ea88cfba13'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'The inverse care law (Tudor Hart, 1971) states that:', '["Need determines supply resulting in decreased functional activity","Equal care distribution accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Availability of medical care varies inversely with population need","Care matches need without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Rich get less care leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Availability of medical care varies inversely with population need', 'Tudor Hart observed that those with the greatest health needs tend to receive the least medical care. Services concentrate where resources already exist rather than where the burden of disease is highest.'),
('9932cc76-5c42-5e03-aec7-06c678ba82f3'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'The mental health treatment gap refers to:', '["Over-treatment resulting in decreased functional activity","Large proportion with mental illness untreated especially in low-income countries","Equal treatment mediated by specific receptor activation","Universal access without affecting the primary tissue pathway","High rates of treatment in high-income countries leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Large proportion with mental illness untreated especially in low-income countries', 'The treatment gap is the large proportion of people with mental illness who never receive care, most marked in low-income countries. It results from scarce mental health professionals, stigma, and limited service access.'),
('fd98f694-d3ef-5b75-a4d1-5b90d64a39dc'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Stigma of disease includes:', '["Only stereotypes resulting in decreased functional activity","Only labeling accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Labeling, stereotyping, separation, status loss, and discrimination","Only discrimination without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only separation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Labeling, stereotyping, separation, status loss, and discrimination', 'Stigma operates through interrelated components: labeling, stereotyping, separation of groups, status loss, and discrimination. A single element alone is insufficient to define stigma.'),
('87c68de5-1793-51d2-b7d2-232f2c9b0af8'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Stigma can be:', '["Neutral resulting in decreased functional activity","Enacted (overt discrimination) or felt (internalized shame)","Only felt mediated by specific receptor activation","Positive without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only enacted leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Enacted (overt discrimination) or felt (internalized shame)', 'Enacted stigma is overt discrimination by others, whereas felt stigma is internalized shame and fear of rejection. Both forms operate in chronic and mental illness.'),
('9477d7df-f052-5a4d-be11-cd59e8d6e2a6'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Stigma of mental illness leads to:', '["Increased help-seeking resulting in decreased functional activity","Better outcomes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Faster recovery mediated by specific receptor activation","Delay in seeking care, social isolation, and discrimination","Social inclusion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Delay in seeking care, social isolation, and discrimination', 'Stigma deters disclosure and help-seeking and isolates patients from their social networks. It also drives discrimination in employment, housing, and healthcare.'),
('1c18f674-d64d-5de9-b607-3bc574279d1b'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Stigma of epilepsy includes:', '["Unrestricted driving privileges resulting in decreased functional activity","Complete freedom from work restrictions accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Full inclusion mediated by specific receptor activation","Social exclusion, employment discrimination, and refusal of surgery","Equal opportunities leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Social exclusion, employment discrimination, and refusal of surgery', 'People with epilepsy face social exclusion, workplace discrimination, and in some settings denial of surgical treatment. These barriers stem from public misconceptions about the condition.'),
('28182789-ac87-5266-9d0f-9267a2d8a8fd'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Stigma of HIV/AIDS includes:', '["Blame, moral judgment, and discrimination","Full support","Moral approval and enhanced social standing","Equal treatment","Sympathy without judgment"]'::jsonb, 'Blame, moral judgment, and discrimination', 'HIV/AIDS stigma commonly involves blaming the affected person and applying moral judgments about their behavior. This fuels discrimination in families, workplaces, and healthcare.'),
('aa0c9000-e893-5d42-9471-d184493a5fa0'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Stigma of cancer varies across cultures and may involve:', '["Blame, shame, and social isolation in some cultures","Uniform expression in all cultures","Universal acceptance","Improved social standing","Absence of any psychological impact"]'::jsonb, 'Blame, shame, and social isolation in some cultures', 'In some cultures cancer is linked to moral blame and shame, prompting concealment of the diagnosis. This can produce social isolation and delayed presentation for treatment.'),
('13e64830-9d2b-5d1c-b9e6-520958371edd'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'The labeling theory of stigma (Scheff) holds that:', '["A neutral description of behavior resulting in decreased functional activity","Society labels deviant behavior and individuals internalize the label","A biologically predetermined trait mediated by specific receptor activation","An innate characteristic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Positive feedback for the individual leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Society labels deviant behavior and individuals internalize the label', 'Scheff proposed that society labels deviant behavior and the labeled individual gradually internalizes that identity. The internalized label shapes self-concept and may reinforce further deviance.'),
('e65b9f7b-56da-524a-8871-aaa9f18c0f74'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Social support includes:', '["Only financial resulting in decreased functional activity","Only information accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Emotional, informational, and practical support from the social network","Only practical without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only emotional leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Emotional, informational, and practical support from the social network', 'Social support comprises emotional, informational, and practical (instrumental) components provided through the social network. Considering a single type alone misrepresents its full nature.'),
('b9172f65-183f-59b1-a4e7-ef76be222f5d'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Lack of social support is associated with:', '["Better health","Reduced mortality","Increased mortality and morbidity","Stronger immunity","Improved coping"]'::jsonb, 'Increased mortality and morbidity', 'Low social support and isolation are associated with increased all-cause mortality and morbidity. Support networks buffer stress and promote healthier behaviors.'),
('534d5f8f-6eb9-5867-a8b7-9481da70efc2'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Social networks affect health through:', '["Social influence, engagement, access, and support","Only engagement accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only access mediated by specific receptor activation","Only support without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only influence leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Social influence, engagement, access, and support', 'Social networks influence health through social support, behavioral influence, social engagement, and access to resources. These pathways act together rather than in isolation.'),
('b9f73c50-e6ef-506a-8722-5ed8b51cef33'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Type A personality is characterized by:', '["Calmness resulting in decreased functional activity","Competitiveness, time urgency, and hostility","Patience mediated by specific receptor activation","Contentment without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Relaxation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Competitiveness, time urgency, and hostility', 'The Type A behavior pattern includes competitiveness, chronic time urgency, and hostility. The hostility component in particular carries cardiovascular risk.'),
('e4dde1d0-1a55-5a21-9845-9fddf027353a'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Type A personality is associated with:', '["Better outcomes resulting in decreased functional activity","Protective effect accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lower risk mediated by specific receptor activation","Reduced blood pressure without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Increased risk of coronary heart disease"]'::jsonb, 'Increased risk of coronary heart disease', 'Type A behavior, especially its hostility component, is associated with a higher risk of coronary heart disease. Mechanisms include heightened autonomic reactivity to stress.'),
('fe149ccf-ba98-51a3-af2e-7b5f1b5f588f'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Which of the following characteristics describes a Type B personality?', '["Aggressiveness resulting in decreased functional activity","Relaxed, easygoing, and less competitive","Impatience mediated by specific receptor activation","Hostility without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Time urgency leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Relaxed, easygoing, and less competitive', 'Type B personality is characterized by a relaxed, easygoing, and non-competitive style. It contrasts with Type A behavior and does not carry the associated cardiovascular risk.'),
('e27d561c-897d-56e3-a15f-57fa26742c12'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'The hostility component of Type A personality is most strongly linked to:', '["Longevity","Happiness","Friendship","Success","Coronary heart disease"]'::jsonb, 'Coronary heart disease', 'Among Type A traits, hostility is the strongest predictor of coronary heart disease. Hostile individuals show exaggerated cardiovascular responses to stress.'),
('3fd6d043-aa83-5b6f-a9cd-d0760fcf14ef'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Hardiness (Kobasa) includes:', '["Dependence resulting in decreased functional activity","Weakness accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Commitment, control, and challenge","Passivity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vulnerability leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Commitment, control, and challenge', 'Psychological hardiness comprises commitment, control, and challenge. These three attitudes buffer the negative effects of stressful life events.'),
('140d3cff-ff9b-57ac-ab2e-b7f3045297ec'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Which of the following describes the commitment component of hardiness (Kobasa)?', '["Being involved and finding meaning in life events","Disengagement accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Avoidance mediated by specific receptor activation","Cynicism without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Indifference leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Being involved and finding meaning in life events', 'Commitment is the hardiness component of staying involved and finding meaning in life events. It is one of the three hardiness attitudes together with control and challenge.'),
('1bca1e73-d0a4-55b1-8bb7-456a91d4a916'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Which of the following describes the control component of hardiness (Kobasa)?', '["Passivity resulting in decreased functional activity","Fatalism accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dependence mediated by specific receptor activation","Helplessness without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Belief that one can influence events"]'::jsonb, 'Belief that one can influence events', 'Control is the hardiness attitude of believing one can influence events rather than feeling helpless. It reduces the perceived threat of stressors.'),
('d5f0e381-db43-54e4-95b3-eebeb538071f'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Which of the following describes the challenge component of hardiness (Kobasa)?', '["Stability resulting in decreased functional activity","Threat accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Risk avoidance mediated by specific receptor activation","Comfort without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Viewing change as opportunity for growth"]'::jsonb, 'Viewing change as opportunity for growth', 'Challenge is the hardiness attitude that regards change as an opportunity for growth. Together with commitment and control it enhances resistance to stress.'),
('a9a4bc68-d0b0-5e44-83a6-afcd888e3351'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Meaningfulness in the sense of coherence refers to:', '["Meaninglessness resulting in decreased functional activity","Despair accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Belief that life makes sense and challenges are worthy of investment","Alienation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Disengagement leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Belief that life makes sense and challenges are worthy of investment', 'Meaningfulness is the motivational component of the sense of coherence, the belief that life is worth engaging. It combines with comprehensibility and manageability in Antonovsky''s model.'),
('e8e22594-5b12-5561-992d-baaad54479c9'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Resilience refers to:', '["Vulnerability resulting in decreased functional activity","Ability to adapt positively despite adversity","Passivity mediated by specific receptor activation","Fragility without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Helplessness leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ability to adapt positively despite adversity', 'Resilience is the capacity to adapt positively and maintain function in the face of adversity. It results from individual, family, and community protective factors.'),
('0094f95e-21f2-5fb5-9e7f-f9e4ad7aeaee'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Protective factors in resilience include:', '["Only self-efficacy resulting in decreased functional activity","Only temperament accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only intelligence mediated by specific receptor activation","Only social support without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Good cognitive skills, easy temperament, self-efficacy, and social support"]'::jsonb, 'Good cognitive skills, easy temperament, self-efficacy, and social support', 'Resilience is supported by multiple interacting protective factors, including cognitive skills, easy temperament, self-efficacy, and social support. No single factor is sufficient.'),
('8cf7c942-2b3e-5c31-b100-51961f66ddbc'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Risk factors in resilience research include:', '["Only parental illness resulting in decreased functional activity","Only poverty accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only genetics mediated by specific receptor activation","Low birth weight, difficult temperament, parental mental illness, and poverty","Only temperament leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Low birth weight, difficult temperament, parental mental illness, and poverty', 'Risk factors such as low birth weight, difficult temperament, parental mental illness, and poverty increase vulnerability to poor outcomes. They are cumulative and often coexist.'),
('f3b469bb-a346-5497-8889-b50b87b2b6e1'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Chronic pain syndrome involves:', '["Psychological only resulting in decreased functional activity","Pain beyond healing with significant functional impairment","Surgery only mediated by specific receptor activation","Tissue damage only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Acute pain only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pain beyond healing with significant functional impairment', 'Chronic pain syndrome is pain that persists beyond expected healing and is accompanied by substantial functional and psychological impairment. It is no longer proportional to tissue damage.'),
('a0322508-5627-5433-ba79-296ad7598e5a'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Psychosocial factors in chronic pain include:', '["Only depression resulting in decreased functional activity","Only catastrophizing accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only anxiety mediated by specific receptor activation","Only fear without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Depression, anxiety, catastrophizing, and fear avoidance"]'::jsonb, 'Depression, anxiety, catastrophizing, and fear avoidance', 'Depression, anxiety, catastrophizing, and fear-avoidance behavior maintain and amplify chronic pain. These factors contribute to disability beyond the tissue pathology.'),
('31610282-a69f-5c57-a381-8ef2d40d98cf'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Pain catastrophizing is:', '["Exaggerated negative orientation to pain (rumination, magnification, helplessness)","Ignoring pain accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Positive thinking mediated by specific receptor activation","Distraction without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mindfulness leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Exaggerated negative orientation to pain (rumination, magnification, helplessness)', 'Catastrophizing is an exaggerated negative appraisal of pain involving rumination, magnification, and helplessness. It predicts greater pain intensity and disability.'),
('cdb94f0c-c4b8-510c-96eb-5419c46bb7e0'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'The fear-avoidance model of chronic pain proposes that:', '["Recovery resulting in decreased functional activity","Activity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Confrontation mediated by specific receptor activation","Exercise without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fear of pain leads to avoidance and disability"]'::jsonb, 'Fear of pain leads to avoidance and disability', 'The model holds that catastrophic interpretation of pain creates fear, which drives avoidance of activity and progressive disability. Breaking this cycle is a key treatment target.'),
('b59fb1b0-b5da-5d95-a365-0b83711812a6'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'CBT for chronic pain includes:', '["Only medication resulting in decreased functional activity","Only rest accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only surgery mediated by specific receptor activation","Only avoidance without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Identifying and changing maladaptive thoughts and behaviors"]'::jsonb, 'Identifying and changing maladaptive thoughts and behaviors', 'Cognitive behavioral therapy targets the maladaptive thoughts and behaviors that amplify pain-related distress. It combines cognitive restructuring with graded behavioral activation.'),
('a83cbcb8-5061-5de7-a1d6-71942f973672'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Graded exposure therapy for pain:', '["Rest only resulting in decreased functional activity","Avoidance accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Medication mediated by specific receptor activation","Gradual approach to feared activities reducing avoidance","Surgery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Gradual approach to feared activities reducing avoidance', 'Graded exposure systematically reintroduces feared activities in small steps, allowing fear to extinguish. It directly counteracts the avoidance that maintains chronic pain disability.'),
('c70cd6d9-bd07-570a-ae97-6e1725ff65f1'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Sleep hygiene interventions for chronic pain:', '["Only exercise resulting in decreased functional activity","Only diet accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only medication mediated by specific receptor activation","Only relaxation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Regular sleep schedule avoiding caffeine and screens before bed"]'::jsonb, 'Regular sleep schedule avoiding caffeine and screens before bed', 'Sleep hygiene involves a regular sleep schedule and avoiding caffeine and screen use before bed. Better sleep reduces pain sensitivity and daytime fatigue.'),
('045d45f7-0b41-5141-8716-8c282e557f89'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Central sensitization in fibromyalgia:', '["Only psychological resulting in decreased functional activity","Increased central nervous system sensitivity to stimuli","Imaginary mediated by specific receptor activation","Localized nerve injury without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Peripheral damage leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Increased central nervous system sensitivity to stimuli', 'Central sensitization is heightened responsiveness of the central nervous system to incoming nociceptive input, amplifying pain perception. It accounts for widespread pain in fibromyalgia despite little peripheral damage.'),
('d6955bf1-6fc6-5db8-9bf8-3bed3a50de9d'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Catastrophizing in fibromyalgia is associated with:', '["Worse pain and disability","Recovery","Pain reduction","Better outcomes","Improved psychosocial function"]'::jsonb, 'Worse pain and disability', 'Catastrophizing involves rumination, magnification, and helplessness about pain. It consistently predicts greater pain severity, disability, and reduced quality of life in fibromyalgia.'),
('11a22ec6-f3fd-5daf-98c7-cad00ec1f03a'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'The ''fighting spirit'' coping style in cancer patients is associated with:', '["Some studies suggest better outcomes","Only for some cancers","Not real","Worse outcomes","Guaranteed cure"]'::jsonb, 'Some studies suggest better outcomes', 'Fighting spirit reflects active, optimistic confrontation of the illness. Some studies associate it with better psychological adjustment and outcomes, although evidence on survival is mixed.'),
('408cc907-fe93-5b5e-8e9e-61a4371b8bfe'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'The psychosocial needs of cancer patients include:', '["Information, emotional support, practical help and rehabilitation","Only treatment accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only medical needs mediated by specific receptor activation","Only cure without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only pain relief leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Information, emotional support, practical help and rehabilitation', 'Psychosocial care addresses information, emotional support, practical help, and rehabilitation. These components support coping, adaptation, and quality of life alongside medical treatment.'),
('5f6c2e11-ff53-5eeb-82fd-2bb274005915'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'In medical ethics, justice refers to:', '["Fair distribution of healthcare resources","Doing no harm","Respecting autonomy","Being honest","Maintaining confidentiality"]'::jsonb, 'Fair distribution of healthcare resources', 'Justice concerns the fair, equitable allocation of limited healthcare resources across patients and populations, ensuring that like cases are treated alike.'),
('95f320d6-0280-52a6-96c0-870d530b1b16'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'The EORTC QLQ-C30 is a:', '["Quality of life questionnaire for cancer patients","Coping scale accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pain scale mediated by specific receptor activation","Anxiety scale without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Depression scale leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Quality of life questionnaire for cancer patients', 'The EORTC QLQ-C30 is a validated, self-reported instrument measuring quality of life in cancer patients across physical, emotional, social, and role domains.'),
('c7dd3bb4-59d7-5c4b-a2e5-49d8c2d34c03'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Establishing medical negligence requires all of the following EXCEPT:', '["Patient consent","Breach of duty","Damages","Duty of care","Causation"]'::jsonb, 'Patient consent', 'Medical negligence requires a duty of care, breach of that duty, causation, and resultant damages. Patient consent is not one of these four elements.'),
('a7483546-253c-57e1-bab9-42a2d2791c5b'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'The distress thermometer is used for:', '["Rapid screening of distress in cancer patients","Pain assessment accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Depression diagnosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Assessment of coping without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anxiety diagnosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Rapid screening of distress in cancer patients', 'The distress thermometer is a single-item visual analogue screening tool that rapidly identifies clinically significant psychosocial distress in cancer patients.'),
('811ad8c8-4b9c-56aa-9699-9ede96fbd0aa'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Psychosocial interventions in cancer include:', '["CBT, support groups, psychoeducation and relaxation therapy","Only radiation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only surgery mediated by specific receptor activation","Only chemotherapy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only medication leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CBT, support groups, psychoeducation and relaxation therapy', 'Psychosocial oncology interventions include cognitive-behavioral therapy, support groups, psychoeducation, and relaxation training, which reduce distress and improve coping.'),
('b491ffc0-46e4-50f0-a904-a10648b8cd62'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'The concept of total pain (Dame Cicely Saunders) encompasses:', '["Physical, psychological, social and spiritual pain","Only social pain accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only psychological pain mediated by specific receptor activation","Only physical pain without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only spiritual pain leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Physical, psychological, social and spiritual pain', 'Dame Cicely Saunders described total pain as multidimensional suffering comprising physical, psychological, social, and spiritual components that require holistic assessment.'),
('918e805e-d72d-531b-b431-76747a1bb148'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Palliative care aims to:', '["Improve quality of life for patients with life-limiting illness and their families","Only symptom management accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only hospital care mediated by specific receptor activation","Prolong life at all costs without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cure the disease leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Improve quality of life for patients with life-limiting illness and their families', 'Palliative care improves quality of life for patients with life-limiting illness and their families through early assessment and management of physical, psychosocial, and spiritual problems.'),
('83cf5cc6-b1d7-52f2-9968-579e208108ce'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Hospice care is best described as:', '["End-of-life care focused on comfort rather than cure","Hospital care accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Acute care mediated by specific receptor activation","Rehabilitation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Curative care leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'End-of-life care focused on comfort rather than cure', 'Hospice provides palliative care for people in the final phase of terminal illness, prioritizing comfort, symptom control, and quality of life rather than cure.'),
('cd296639-a035-5734-89fd-6d4594aed172'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'The Liverpool Care Pathway was:', '["A framework for end-of-life care in the last hours or days (now discontinued)","Cancer treatment accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Surgery mediated by specific receptor activation","Chemotherapy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Rehabilitation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A framework for end-of-life care in the last hours or days (now discontinued)', 'The Liverpool Care Pathway standardized end-of-life care for the final hours or days of life. It was discontinued in the UK after concerns about inappropriate use.'),
('b8ee829b-9430-5f91-a52e-47052e7dd754'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'The concept of a good death includes:', '["Pain-free, dignified, peaceful, with loved ones present","Prolonged life accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Curative treatment mediated by specific receptor activation","Fighting to the end without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Denial of death leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pain-free, dignified, peaceful, with loved ones present', 'A good death is generally regarded as one that is pain-free, dignified, peaceful, and occurring with loved ones present while respecting the patient''s wishes.'),
('5f3a5d99-aaff-50fb-b28d-61b0431ce8df'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Physician-assisted suicide (PAS) is best defined as:', '["Physician provides lethal prescription, patient self-administers","Withholding treatment accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Terminal sedation mediated by specific receptor activation","Physician administers lethal injection without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Withdrawing care leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Physician provides lethal prescription, patient self-administers', 'In physician-assisted suicide the physician supplies a lethal prescription, but the patient themselves ingests the medication. This contrasts with euthanasia, where the physician administers the lethal substance.'),
('5a0e7aa6-4feb-52a4-bd54-511d85430445'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Euthanasia is best defined as:', '["Physician directly administers a lethal substance","Painkiller overdose","Withdrawing treatment","Withholding treatment","Patient self-administers the medication"]'::jsonb, 'Physician directly administers a lethal substance', 'Euthanasia involves the physician directly administering a lethal substance to end the patient''s life. It differs from physician-assisted suicide, in which the patient takes the medication themselves.'),
('e86ff0ea-6629-5163-8371-44cfef964ef5'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Regarding the legal status of physician-assisted suicide and euthanasia around the world, which statement is correct?', '["Only European countries allow them","They are legal in all countries","They are legal in all states","They are illegal everywhere","Netherlands, Belgium, Canada and Colombia have legalized them"]'::jsonb, 'Netherlands, Belgium, Canada and Colombia have legalized them', 'Euthanasia or assisted dying is legalized in a limited number of jurisdictions, including the Netherlands, Belgium, Canada, and Colombia. Legal status varies widely across countries and regions.'),
('4c7578ee-8e10-5555-a611-99a7b0924036'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'According to the principle of double effect, administering analgesia that may hasten death is acceptable when:', '["The intent is pain relief and death is an unintended side effect","Sedation is illegal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Direct killing is acceptable mediated by specific receptor activation","Analgesia is dangerous without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pain is acceptable and untreated leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The intent is pain relief and death is an unintended side effect', 'The double effect principle permits an act with both good and foreseeable bad effects when the good effect is intended. Providing pain relief that may hasten death is ethical if the intent is analgesic, not lethal.'),
('46f4556c-a533-5ee4-9ba3-d8fa3805ae95'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'The case of Terri Schiavo involved:', '["A prolonged legal dispute over withdrawing artificial nutrition and hydration in persistent vegetative state","Physician-assisted suicide accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Terminal sedation mediated by specific receptor activation","Organ donation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Active euthanasia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A prolonged legal dispute over withdrawing artificial nutrition and hydration in persistent vegetative state', 'Terri Schiavo was in a persistent vegetative state, and her case centered on a long legal battle over whether to remove artificial nutrition and hydration. It highlighted disputes about surrogate decision-making and end-of-life withdrawal of supports.'),
('856ebcd1-30c5-5445-9582-9aac4b65de8c'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Do-not-resuscitate (DNR) orders:', '["Imposed by the physician alone","Imposed by the family alone","Decided by the nurse","Should be discussed with the patient or surrogate","Are mandatory for the elderly"]'::jsonb, 'Should be discussed with the patient or surrogate', 'A DNR order should be discussed with the patient when capable, or with their surrogate decision-maker, and aligned with the patient''s values and prognosis. It should not be imposed unilaterally by staff or applied solely on the basis of age.'),
('5239ee6f-f7ad-545c-8d72-d79f4f88bf6f'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'The death rattle in the terminal phase is best described as:', '["A sign of pain resulting in decreased functional activity","Drowning accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Choking mediated by specific receptor activation","A sign of distress without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Noisy breathing from secretions, not painful"]'::jsonb, 'Noisy breathing from secretions, not painful', 'Death rattle is noisy breathing caused by secretions pooling in the airways during the final hours of life. It is not painful and does not signify choking or drowning, and the dying patient is usually unconscious.'),
('4d49dbcf-441f-5319-aa85-ffe882fce95c'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Terminal agitation in the dying patient:', '["Should be left untreated","Requires transfer to the ICU","Is best ignored","Is managed with physical restraint","May require sedation for comfort"]'::jsonb, 'May require sedation for comfort', 'Terminal agitation can be distressing and may respond poorly to simple measures, so pharmacological sedation is sometimes needed to keep the dying patient comfortable. Physical restraint is not an accepted comfort measure.'),
('89aa4db8-ab61-5108-9beb-b3dcca383111'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Prolonged grief disorder is characterized by:', '["Persistent intense yearning and preoccupation with the deceased for more than 12 months","Post-traumatic stress disorder accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Normal grief mediated by specific receptor activation","Generalized anxiety without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Major depression leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Persistent intense yearning and preoccupation with the deceased for more than 12 months', 'Prolonged grief disorder involves disabling grief that persists beyond 12 months after the loss in adults, marked by intense yearning and preoccupation. It is distinguishable from normal bereavement and from depression or PTSD.'),
('7de44775-e297-511a-a7b0-292397ebcdd9'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Which statement correctly contrasts grief counseling with grief therapy?', '["Counseling supports normal grief; therapy treats complicated grief","They are identical practices accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Counseling is for all bereaved people mediated by specific receptor activation","Therapy is for all bereaved people without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Neither is ever indicated leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Counseling supports normal grief; therapy treats complicated grief', 'Grief counseling supports people through uncomplicated bereavement, whereas grief therapy is directed at complicated or prolonged grief that impairs function. The target populations differ based on the presence of complicated grief.'),
('6dbebede-19ab-5017-8c09-8c2e37133f07'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'The Belmont Report identified which ethical principles for research involving human subjects?', '["Respect for persons, beneficence, and justice","Only autonomy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only non-maleficence mediated by specific receptor activation","Only beneficence without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only justice leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Respect for persons, beneficence, and justice', 'The Belmont Report set out three basic ethical principles for research using human subjects: respect for persons, beneficence, and justice. These underlie informed consent, risk-benefit evaluation, and fair subject selection.'),
('e423c38b-12a5-595c-ae22-e67768568870'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'According to the dual process model of grief, adaptive grieving involves:', '["Oscillation between loss orientation and restoration orientation","Only focusing on the loss","Passing through fixed linear stages","Constant avoidance of loss-related emotion","Only focusing on rebuilding life"]'::jsonb, 'Oscillation between loss orientation and restoration orientation', 'The dual process model of grief describes adaptive coping as oscillating between loss-oriented behavior, which confronts the loss, and restoration-oriented behavior, which attends to rebuilding daily life. Alternation between the two is how people adjust to bereavement.'),
('ed7912ac-1e2e-5629-b57d-110eed9cb582'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Meaning reconstruction in grief refers to:', '["Finding new meaning and identity after loss","Speeding up the grieving process","Replacing the deceased person","Achieving complete emotional detachment","Forgetting the loss"]'::jsonb, 'Finding new meaning and identity after loss', 'Meaning reconstruction is the process by which bereaved people rebuild a meaningful life narrative and a revised identity after a loss. Grief work is seen as an active effort to restore coherence and meaning following bereavement.'),
('68b86269-a3bd-53d0-ad3a-528f5666fe6d'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Family involvement in end-of-life decisions should:', '["Include the family in discussions while respecting the patient''s prior wishes","Be limited to the patient only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Be excluded from all discussions mediated by specific receptor activation","Be decided solely by the family without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Override the patient''s prior wishes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Include the family in discussions while respecting the patient''s prior wishes', 'Families should be included in end-of-life discussions for support and information, but decisions must honor the patient''s expressed or documented wishes for care. The patient''s prior directives take precedence over family preferences.'),
('f40a8f5d-91e5-527c-bacc-a848cd36a401'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Cultural variations in death and dying include differences in:', '["Rituals, beliefs about the afterlife, and truth-telling customs","Only religious practice accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only geographical location mediated by specific receptor activation","Whether death occurs at all without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only grief intensity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Rituals, beliefs about the afterlife, and truth-telling customs', 'Cultures differ in death rituals, beliefs about the afterlife, and customs around disclosing prognoses to dying patients. Recognizing these variations is essential to providing culturally competent end-of-life care.'),
('8f93857e-7ed3-5f99-b664-949a0bc7c481'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Minority stress theory (Meyer) holds that:', '["Stigma, prejudice and discrimination cause chronic stress in minority groups","Minority identity has only positive effects","Stress arises solely from genetic factors","The theory applies only to mental illness","Stigma has no effect on health"]'::jsonb, 'Stigma, prejudice and discrimination cause chronic stress in minority groups', 'Meyer''s minority stress theory proposes that stigma, prejudice, and discrimination create chronic, elevated stress in minority populations, which increases their risk of mental and physical health problems. This stress arises from chronic social conditions, not intrinsic qualities.'),
('3b2c1a78-aad8-5cd7-80fa-d255f5d6969a'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Resilience in LGBTQ+ populations is supported by:', '["Community connection, identity pride, and social support","Only heightened vulnerability accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Concealing one''s identity mediated by specific receptor activation","Only risk factors without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Social isolation and stigma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Community connection, identity pride, and social support', 'Despite minority stress, LGBTQ+ individuals show resilience through community connection, identity pride, and supportive social relationships. These protective factors buffer the health effects of stigma and discrimination.'),
('6448b166-6c4e-5375-9f65-2b10f3725295'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'In medical negligence law, the phrase ''res ipsa loquitur'' means:', '["The thing speaks for itself","Let the buyer beware","To err is human","The patient is always right","Caveat emptor"]'::jsonb, 'The thing speaks for itself', 'Res ipsa loquitur (''the thing speaks for itself'') is a legal doctrine that allows negligence to be inferred from the circumstances of an injury that would not ordinarily occur without negligence. It shifts the burden of explanation to the defendant.'),
('45069cca-9b66-5ca4-bcdd-2e4eb9c35ee1'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Resilience to adverse childhood experiences (ACEs) can be promoted by:', '["Safe, stable, nurturing relationships and communities","Only medication accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A single counseling session mediated by specific receptor activation","Only formal therapy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Removing the child from the home leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Safe, stable, nurturing relationships and communities', 'Protective factors such as safe, stable, nurturing relationships and supportive communities buffer the effects of adverse childhood experiences. These conditions support healthy development and reduce the long-term health consequences of ACEs.'),
('f41058f8-4570-5b6f-ab78-07ab8fb6fe67'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'The Barker hypothesis (fetal origins of adult disease) proposes that:', '["Low birth weight is linked to adult cardiovascular disease and diabetes","Adult disease is environmental only","Adverse effects are limited to childhood","Adult disease is genetic only","Fetal environment has no later effect"]'::jsonb, 'Low birth weight is linked to adult cardiovascular disease and diabetes', 'The Barker hypothesis links undernutrition in fetal life, reflected by low birth weight, to an increased risk of adult cardiovascular disease and type 2 diabetes. It demonstrates that intrauterine conditions can program later adult disease.'),
('e9930c4d-5524-57d3-b5c5-c56ca8fca1e9'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Brain death defined as:', '["Irreversible loss of all brain functions including brainstem","Persistent vegetative state accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Coma for 24 hours mediated by specific receptor activation","Cerebral death only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Loss of consciousness leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Irreversible loss of all brain functions including brainstem', 'Complete cessation of all brain function.'),
('f3256a23-d771-5b40-8d7e-ec37b3915e3c'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Social epidemiology studies:', '["Social determinants and distribution of health in populations","Only biology accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only individual behavior mediated by specific receptor activation","Only genetics without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only healthcare delivery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Social determinants and distribution of health in populations', 'Social epidemiology examines how social conditions—such as income, education, and neighborhood—shape the distribution of health and disease across populations. It focuses on societal factors rather than purely individual risks.'),
('549d4719-6a44-5b3c-99b2-d6ab88ae170c'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'The ecological model of health behavior describes health as influenced by multiple interacting levels. Which set of levels does it specify?', '["Only policy resulting in decreased functional activity","Only community accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Individual, interpersonal, organizational, community, policy levels","Only interpersonal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only individual leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Individual, interpersonal, organizational, community, policy levels', 'The ecological model integrates individual, interpersonal, organizational, community, and policy levels of influence on health behavior. Each level represents a potential target for intervention.'),
('985050b0-c671-582c-b18d-406d920f1479'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'In medical law, the Latin term "res ipsa loquitur" means:', '["The thing speaks for itself","Let the buyer beware","To err is human","The patient is right","Caveat emptor"]'::jsonb, 'The thing speaks for itself', 'Res ipsa loquitur allows negligence to be inferred when the circumstances indicate that harm would not ordinarily occur without negligence. "Caveat emptor" means "let the buyer beware."'),
('b24989f4-ce80-51f7-ba89-ea58d703da80'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Health promotion aims to:', '["Prevent disease only resulting in decreased functional activity","Diagnose only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Enable people to increase control over their health","Manage only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Treat disease only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Enable people to increase control over their health', 'The Ottawa Charter defines health promotion as the process of enabling people to increase control over and improve their health. It emphasizes empowerment rather than treatment alone.'),
('3f641cef-882e-56fa-b6ed-ea24f1e9137f'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Tertiary prevention aims to:', '["Diagnose resulting in decreased functional activity","Prevent disease accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Reduce complications and improve function in established disease","Screen without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Treat early leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Reduce complications and improve function in established disease', 'Tertiary prevention reduces complications, disability, and mortality while improving function in people with established disease, for example through rehabilitation. Primary prevention aims to prevent disease onset.'),
('e4573b48-cf85-5096-9b74-0c5fde255d8e'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Quaternary prevention refers to:', '["Overdiagnosis resulting in decreased functional activity","Overtreatment accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Defensive medicine mediated by specific receptor activation","Too much medication without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Protect patients from unnecessary medical interventions"]'::jsonb, 'Protect patients from unnecessary medical interventions', 'Quaternary prevention protects patients from unnecessary or harmful medical interventions, reducing overdiagnosis and overtreatment. It is a core principle of rational, patient-centered healthcare.'),
('af88ae32-c403-5746-996f-6fa06a2084c6'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'The whole-person approach to patient care means:', '["Only treatment resulting in decreased functional activity","Only disease accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Treating the person not just the disease","Only prognosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only symptoms leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Treating the person not just the disease', 'The biopsychosocial approach considers the psychological and social dimensions of illness in addition to the biological disease. Management therefore addresses the person, not merely the diagnosis.'),
('bfc612d0-ef11-5b8b-bc73-2f1dd12fcaa9'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Patient autonomy and shared decision making reflect:', '["Respecting patient as capable decision-making partner","Physician authority accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Patient passivity mediated by specific receptor activation","Paternalism without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Family control leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Respecting patient as capable decision-making partner', 'Shared decision making treats the patient as an informed, capable partner whose values and preferences shape management decisions. It stands in contrast to paternalism, in which the physician decides alone.'),
('b78ced61-78eb-5e8e-9c03-a4286a8c8b37'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'The Expert Patient Programme is based on the idea that:', '["Medication only resulting in decreased functional activity","Patient as passive accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Symptom management only mediated by specific receptor activation","Chronic disease patients as experts in their own condition","Physician expert only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Chronic disease patients as experts in their own condition', 'The Expert Patient Programme trains people with chronic conditions to manage their own health using their lived experience. Patients become active partners in care rather than passive recipients.'),
('4753b08c-9b8e-57bd-b0c7-ed6f4f11710d'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Peer support groups provide:', '["Professional therapy resulting in decreased functional activity","Medication accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Diagnosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Medical treatment without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Shared experience mutual support and coping strategies"]'::jsonb, 'Shared experience mutual support and coping strategies', 'Peer support groups offer emotional support, information, and practical coping strategies drawn from shared lived experience. They complement rather than replace professional therapy or medication.'),
('15121f96-7c92-59c5-9ff9-3aa264fa3c25'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Patient activation refers to:', '["Dependency resulting in decreased functional activity","Knowledge skills and confidence for managing health","Illiteracy mediated by specific receptor activation","Compliance without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Passive receipt of care leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Knowledge skills and confidence for managing health', 'Patient activation describes the knowledge, skills, and confidence a person has to manage their own health. Higher activation is associated with better self-management and outcomes.'),
('9dd30da3-c4c9-56f4-865c-3a367cd53dbc'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'The legal doctrine "res ipsa loquitur" means:', '["The thing speaks for itself","Let the buyer beware","To err is human","The patient is right","Caveat emptor"]'::jsonb, 'The thing speaks for itself', 'Res ipsa loquitur allows negligence to be inferred from circumstances that would not ordinarily produce harm without fault. It shifts the burden of explanation to the defendant.'),
('2b9355c1-7bbb-5726-85b6-67b0de0efc50'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'In medical ethics, beneficence means:', '["Act in the patient''s best interest","Do no harm","Be just","Respect autonomy","Maintain confidentiality"]'::jsonb, 'Act in the patient''s best interest', 'Beneficence obliges the clinician to act for the benefit of the patient. "Do no harm" expresses non-maleficence, a separate and distinct principle.'),
('7efa24b1-4729-5cb4-9588-7902e57be990'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Social prescribing involves:', '["Only surgery resulting in decreased functional activity","Only medication accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Linking patients to community resources and non-medical support","Only referral to specialist without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only medical treatment leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Linking patients to community resources and non-medical support', 'Social prescribing connects patients to non-medical community resources, such as exercise groups or befriending services, to address social determinants of health. It complements rather than replaces clinical care.'),
('75c851b1-febe-58e4-86dd-acdd28801678'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Health coaching is a patient-centered approach to:', '["Passive education resulting in decreased functional activity","Lecturing accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Empower patients to achieve health goals through skill building","Fear tactics without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Physician direction leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Empower patients to achieve health goals through skill building', 'Health coaching builds patients'' skills and confidence to set and achieve their own health goals. It emphasizes partnership and self-efficacy rather than lecturing, fear, or coercion.'),
('d7a046b2-8d5a-5207-bebf-e3d7fab915ef'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Which of the following may justify breaching patient confidentiality?', '["Notifiable diseases, serious harm to third parties, child protection","Only notifiable diseases accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only court orders mediated by specific receptor activation","Only patient consent without affecting the primary tissue pathway","No exceptions leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Notifiable diseases, serious harm to third parties, child protection', 'Confidentiality may be overridden for statutory reporting of notifiable diseases, to protect third parties from serious harm, and for safeguarding children and vulnerable people. These disclosures are narrow, justified exceptions.'),
('0548aebd-0e26-5472-a406-8cc44302257f'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Which statement best describes the patient experience?', '["Only visit resulting in decreased functional activity","Only treatment accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","From appointment booking to follow-up","Only physician without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only hospital leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'From appointment booking to follow-up', 'The patient experience spans the entire care journey, from booking an appointment to post-discharge follow-up, and includes every interaction with the healthcare system. It is not limited to a single visit or treatment episode.'),
('6ce7619e-568a-57a4-94c5-01633be38fc8'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'The patient journey mapping tool:', '["Patient location resulting in decreased functional activity","Doctor location accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Nurse schedule mediated by specific receptor activation","Visualizes patient''s experience through the healthcare system","Map of hospital leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Visualizes patient''s experience through the healthcare system', 'Patient journey mapping visually documents the patient''s steps, touchpoints, emotions, and interactions across their care pathway. It is used to identify gaps and improve the experience of care.'),
('a3b7b8ef-e132-5b74-b8b6-bb3b30a55c50'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Person-reported experience measures (PREMs) capture:', '["Symptoms resulting in decreased functional activity","Clinical data accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Survival mediated by specific receptor activation","Feedback on experience of care from patient perspective","Health outcomes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Feedback on experience of care from patient perspective', 'PREMs collect patients'' reports of their experience of care, such as communication, waiting times, and involvement in decisions. They are distinct from symptoms, clinical data, and health outcomes.'),
('7672e50e-20cd-5904-8ad9-c700bfbe49c3'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'The distress thermometer asks patients to rate their distress on a scale of:', '["1-3 resulting in decreased functional activity","1-100 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","1-5 mediated by specific receptor activation","0-10 (0 no distress 10 extreme distress)","0-100 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '0-10 (0 no distress 10 extreme distress)', 'The distress thermometer is an 11-point visual scale from 0 (no distress) to 10 (extreme distress), with scores of 4 or more indicating clinically significant distress. It is widely used in oncology.'),
('2474012b-1813-52f3-ad06-81b51d02040b'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'In the Kubler-Ross model of grief, which stage is characterized by sadness and withdrawal?', '["Anxiety","Depression","Function","Cognition","Pain"]'::jsonb, 'Depression', 'In the Kubler-Ross model, the depression stage involves sadness, withdrawal, and a sense of loss. The five stages are denial, anger, bargaining, depression, and acceptance.'),
('dbe894d3-f2fe-526e-b461-4cddf27904cb'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'The AUDIT questionnaire is used to screen for:', '["Dementia","Alcohol use disorders","Anxiety","Depression","Pain"]'::jsonb, 'Alcohol use disorders', 'The Alcohol Use Disorders Identification Test (AUDIT) is a WHO 10-item screening tool for harmful drinking and alcohol use disorders. It is not used to screen for mood or cognitive disorders.'),
('b390fbd4-c71a-55b2-9edc-1353b2a0a46d'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'The Edinburgh Postnatal Depression Scale (EPDS) screens for:', '["Baby blues","Pain","Postpartum psychosis","Anxiety","Postpartum depression"]'::jsonb, 'Postpartum depression', 'The EPDS is a 10-item self-report questionnaire used to detect postnatal depression in the weeks after childbirth. It does not diagnose puerperal psychosis or the transient "baby blues."'),
('3871a469-78fe-5639-92e3-d42a89c29a59'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'The Hamilton Depression Rating Scale (HAM-D) is best described as a:', '["Pain scale","Self-report","Clinician-rated depression scale","Anxiety scale","Cognition scale"]'::jsonb, 'Clinician-rated depression scale', 'The HAM-D is a 17-item observer-rated scale completed by a clinician to measure the severity of depression. In contrast, instruments such as the BDI rely on self-report.'),
('59ce5a3c-3a0b-5b8d-b909-52d0be83a228'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Placebo use in clinical trials is considered unethical when:', '["Proven effective treatment exists","Trial is double-blind","Study is small","Patient prefers placebo","Side effects are mild"]'::jsonb, 'Proven effective treatment exists', 'The Declaration of Helsinki holds that placebo should not be used when an established effective intervention exists, except in justified circumstances such as minor conditions. Withholding proven treatment would deny patients effective care.'),
('317ea825-de79-5cfa-ab06-83ec663c1851'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'The Multidimensional Health Locus of Control (MHLC) scales assess:', '["Depression resulting in decreased functional activity","Pain accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Coping mediated by specific receptor activation","Beliefs about control over health (internal chance powerful others)","Anxiety leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Beliefs about control over health (internal chance powerful others)', 'The MHLC measures the extent to which individuals believe their health is controlled internally, by chance, or by powerful others. This internal-chance-powerful-others dimension is the scale''s core construct.'),
('d315d584-b8f5-516c-82f8-f32549c597ba'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'The COPE inventory assesses:', '["Stress resulting in decreased functional activity","Anxiety accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Coping strategies (problem-focused emotion-focused avoidant)","Depression without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Personality leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Coping strategies (problem-focused emotion-focused avoidant)', 'The COPE inventory measures the coping strategies people use in response to stress, grouped into problem-focused, emotion-focused, and avoidant dimensions. It does not measure clinical symptoms or personality traits.'),
('9f843560-149e-578b-8527-bea310dc993b'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'The Social Readjustment Rating Scale (SRRS) of Holmes and Rahe measures:', '["Anxiety resulting in decreased functional activity","Depression accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Life stress from major life events (LCUs)","Pain without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Coping leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Life stress from major life events (LCUs)', 'The SRRS assigns Life Change Units to major life events and sums them to quantify stress. Higher scores are associated with an increased risk of subsequent illness.'),
('dd1b6086-f123-5317-8f8c-e91a935e80b7'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'The brachial plexus is formed by the nerve roots:', '["C4-C8 nerve roots","C5-C8 nerve roots","C6-T2 nerve roots","C4-T1 nerve roots","C5-T1 nerve roots"]'::jsonb, 'C5-T1 nerve roots', 'The brachial plexus is formed by the anterior rami of C5, C6, C7, C8, and T1, which converge into trunks, divisions, cords, and terminal branches. Roots outside C5-T1 do not contribute to the plexus.'),
('470a55c9-bee9-53d1-8153-77946f1f482a'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'The femoral nerve arises from the nerve roots:', '["L3-L5 nerve roots","L5-S2 nerve roots","L1-L3 nerve roots","L2-L4 nerve roots","L4-S1 nerve roots"]'::jsonb, 'L2-L4 nerve roots', 'The femoral nerve originates from the posterior divisions of the ventral rami of L2, L3, and L4. Together with the obturator nerve it supplies the anterior compartment of the thigh.'),
('671cd543-e57a-540b-be3b-2273af919082'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'The obturator nerve arises from the nerve roots:', '["L1-L3 nerve roots","L4-S1 nerve roots","L2-L4 nerve roots","L5-S2 nerve roots","L3-L5 nerve roots"]'::jsonb, 'L2-L4 nerve roots', 'The obturator nerve arises from the anterior divisions of the L2-L4 ventral rami. It innervates the adductor muscles of the medial thigh.'),
('060e5589-6f94-5681-94f3-1406f186960c'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Which researcher described the general adaptation syndrome with alarm, resistance, and exhaustion stages?', '["Cannon","Lazarus","Holmes & Rahe","Engel","Selye"]'::jsonb, 'Selye', 'Hans Selye described the general adaptation syndrome, in which the body responds to stress through alarm, resistance, and exhaustion stages. Cannon, Lazarus, and Holmes & Rahe are associated with other stress-related concepts.'),
('34db4016-5442-5835-a8f3-fa8402842cea'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'The sequential stages of alarm, resistance, and exhaustion in the stress response were proposed by:', '["Cannon","Lazarus","Holmes & Rahe","Engel","Selye"]'::jsonb, 'Selye', 'The general adaptation syndrome, comprising the alarm, resistance, and exhaustion phases, was introduced by Hans Selye. This model forms a foundation of stress research.'),
('47634b03-a8cc-53c2-bb44-a671c445c83d'::uuid, 'Behavioural Science Medical Ethics (Paper 1)', 'Psychosocial Aspects - Psychosocial Aspects', 'Which researcher is credited with the general adaptation syndrome describing the body''s response to stress?', '["Cannon","Lazarus","Holmes & Rahe","Engel","Selye"]'::jsonb, 'Selye', 'Selye''s general adaptation syndrome describes the body''s three-stage response to sustained stress: alarm, resistance, and exhaustion. The other names are linked to fight-or-flight, appraisal, or life-event stress models.');
